(window.CORPUS_PARTS=window.CORPUS_PARTS||{})[0]=[[0,7.68,4.4,"Hello and welcome to 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil."],[0,12.08,1.36,"And I'm Pippa."],[0,13.44,4.96,"Apps like Duolingo, Babbel and Busuu all aim to help you learn a language,"],[0,18.4,1.64,"but do they really help?"],[0,20.04,1.92,"We'll be trying to answer that question"],[0,21.96,2.24,"in today's episode of 6 Minute English,"],[0,24.2,3.68,"and as usual we'll be learning some useful new vocabulary too."],[0,27.88,3.76,"Yes, and don't forget to head to our website for a transcript of this episode"],[0,31.64,2.6,"and a free worksheet to test what you've learnt."],[0,34.24,2.96,"It's all at bbclearningenglish.com."],[0,37.2,3.0,"OK. Neil, let's start with a quiz question."],[0,40.2,4.28,"Which of these is not a language you can learn using Duolingo?"],[0,44.48,4.84,"Is it: a) Latin, b) Klingon, or c) Elvish?"],[0,49.32,3.4,"I'm going to say c) Elvish."],[0,52.72,2.76,"Well, we'll find out at the end of the programme."],[0,55.48,3.36,"Now, people might be familiar with the app Duolingo."],[0,58.84,2.28,"And then there are other apps which say they can help"],[0,61.12,3.84,"you learn a language quickly – Bussuu and Babbel, for example."],[0,64.96,3.28,"And listeners might be familiar with how they tend to work."],[0,68.24,3.84,"You get short lessons which test you on a repeated set of words."],[0,72.08,3.76,"Right. And many of these apps say they focus on teaching language"],[0,75.84,2.88,"for situations and implicit learning,"],[0,78.72,3.4,"rather than directly teaching language rules such as grammar."],[0,82.12,4.4,"If something is implicit, it's suggested but not communicated directly."],[0,86.52,4.24,"Yes. So, if you think about how children learn to speak their first language,"],[0,90.76,3.08,"it's mostly implicit – they don't have lessons."],[0,93.84,2.28,"But can this work for adults?"],[0,96.12,4.2,"BBC Radio 4 programme Sliced Bread has been investigating the evidence"],[0,100.32,1.88,"behind language learning apps."],[0,102.2,2.72,"Presenter Greg Foot spoke to language learning expert"],[0,104.92,4.4,"Professor Elizabeth Wonnacott, or Liz, about implicit learning."],[0,109.32,3.36,"There is a very big difference between being a child"],[0,112.68,3.52,"who gets constantly exposed to this world of language,"],[0,116.2,3.36,"compared with trying to learn a language a few hours a week."],[0,119.56,4.2,"There is quite a bit of evidence that some explicit teaching of,"],[0,123.76,2.64,"for example, grammar and vocabulary is helpful."],[0,126.4,3.84,"Got you. So, it would be helpful to try to mimic what children do,"],[0,130.24,1.72,"which is full immersion,"],[0,131.96,3.88,"but realistically you're not going to get to those levels."],[0,135.84,3.64,"Liz says that while children learn their first language implicitly"],[0,139.48,2.88,"by being constantly surrounded by the language,"],[0,142.36,3.36,"adults are often learning for only a few hours a week."],[0,145.72,2.84,"Yes. So, for adults trying to learn a new language,"],[0,148.56,3.56,"research has shown that some explicit teaching is helpful."],[0,152.12,2.2,"Explicit is the opposite of implicit."],[0,154.32,3.88,"So, for example, explicit grammar teaching would be somebody explaining"],[0,158.2,1.68,"a grammar rule to you."],[0,159.88,3.76,"Right. So, adults can't realistically achieve full immersion"],[0,163.64,1.84,"in a language like children do."],[0,165.48,3.12,"Immersion means being completely surrounded by something."],[0,168.6,3.92,"OK. So, a mixture of the implicit teaching of the apps and"],[0,172.52,3.96,"some explicit teaching – for example, learning grammar rules – is useful."],[0,176.48,2.36,"But Neil, there's limited research about"],[0,178.84,3.32,"just how effective these apps can be at teaching you a language."],[0,182.16,4.92,"Indeed, Liz Wonnacott has seen one study she thinks could be interesting though,"],[0,187.08,3.72,"which compares two groups of Spanish speakers learning English –"],[0,190.8,4.76,"one using an app and one attending classes – for a similar amount of time."],[0,195.56,4.12,"She explains more to BBC Radio 4 programme Sliced Bread."],[0,199.68,3.6,"The key finding was that in some of the tests, written grammar tests,"],[0,203.28,3.08,"the Duolingo learners actually improved a bit more,"],[0,206.36,1.84,"while actually in a test where they were looking"],[0,208.2,3.92,"at listening the classroom learners appeared to improve more."],[0,212.12,2.16,"So, it's really promising,"],[0,214.28,3.44,"but of course it's one particular language, in one particular app,"],[0,217.72,2.2,"against one particular school."],[0,219.92,1.64,"Liz says the key finding"],[0,221.56,4.12,"from the research is that the app users improved more on written tests,"],[0,225.68,3.68,"but the classroom learners improved more on listening tests."],[0,229.36,4.08,"A key finding is the most important conclusion of a piece of research."],[0,233.44,4.6,"Liz says the study is promising, which means it shows signs of being positive."],[0,238.04,3.2,"Yes. However, Liz points out the limits of the study."],[0,241.24,3.76,"It's only looking at one particular language, one particular app"],[0,245.0,2.32,"and one particular language school."],[0,247.32,4.16,"The repetition of 'one particular' here means specific or individual,"],[0,251.48,2.4,"and Liz would like to see research on more apps,"],[0,253.88,3.56,"more types of teaching and also more different languages."],[0,257.44,2.92,"Apps can help us learn some parts of a language, but"],[0,260.36,4.28,"in this study they weren't as successful at improving listening skills,"],[0,264.64,4.68,"and so Liz concludes that combining learning in apps with some other ways"],[0,269.32,3.92,"of studying is the best way to try to improve your language skills."],[0,273.24,1.76,"Probably moving forwards,"],[0,275.0,3.8,"some combination of human teaching and these platforms to give us"],[0,278.8,3.44,"that repetition is probably going to be the right direction."],[0,282.24,3.56,"As teachers, we'd also suggest doing things that help you improve"],[0,285.8,3.32,"different skills – speaking, listening, reading and writing."],[0,289.12,3.6,"So, listening to podcasts or trying to practise speaking with"],[0,292.72,4.36,"a language exchange partner could be things to combine with learning in apps."],[0,297.08,1.68,"Find what works for you."],[0,298.76,3.96,"I think it's time to find out the answer to the quiz question, Neil."],[0,302.72,4.36,"I asked you which language you can't learn on Duolingo..."],[0,307.08,2.68,"and you were right. It's c) Elvish,"],[0,309.76,2.04,"but you can learn Latin and Klingon,"],[0,311.8,3.64,"the fictional language from the TV series Star Trek, on Duolingo."],[0,315.44,2.4,"And if you did want to improve your Elvish,"],[0,317.84,2.56,"we did find an app specifically for that."],[0,320.4,3.6,"Right. Let's recap the vocabulary we learned in this episode."],[0,324.0,3.8,"'Implicit' means suggested, but not communicated directly."],[0,327.8,2.64,"'Explicit' is the opposite of 'implicit'."],[0,330.44,2.8,"It means something is communicated directly."],[0,333.24,3.64,"'Immersion' is the state of being fully surrounded by something."],[0,336.88,4.08,"The key finding of a research study is the most important conclusion"],[0,340.96,1.0,"of the research."],[0,341.96,4.2,"And the phrase 'one particular' means specific or individual."],[0,346.16,2.24,"Once again, our six minutes are up."],[0,348.4,2.72,"Test what you've learned with the worksheet on our website."],[0,351.12,3.4,"And if you're interested to hear the full episode of Sliced Bread"],[0,354.52,3.0,"about language-learning apps and the science behind them,"],[0,357.52,2.56,"there's a link in the notes below this episode."],[0,360.08,2.6,"Try listening on 0.5 speed"],[0,362.68,3.56,"or in short sections if you find it too difficult to keep up."],[0,366.24,0.6,"Bye for now!"],[0,366.84,1.4,"Goodbye!"],[1,0.21,0.75,"Hi Becca."],[1,0.96,0.75,"Hi Neil."],[1,1.71,1.0,"How are you?"],[1,2.71,2.63,"Have you got any trips coming up soon?"],[1,5.34,4.17,"I have. I'm going to Montenegro, which is exciting."],[1,9.51,2.21,"I've never been there before,"],[1,11.72,4.46,"but it means that I have to go to an airport and I have to fly."],[1,16.18,1.17,"I don't like flying."],[1,17.35,3.88,"But we're talking about airports today in this episode of Real Easy English."],[1,21.23,4.8,"We are. But people need to know that to get a transcript and a worksheet"],[1,26.03,4.75,"for this episode, they need to go to our website, bbclearningenglish.com."],[1,30.78,1.58,"Absolutely."],[1,38.37,3.71,"So, you'll be going to an airport for your holiday,"],[1,42.08,2.17,"but what happens at an airport, Neil?"],[1,44.25,3.42,"One of the most important things is check in."],[1,47.67,6.01,"Now, 'to check in' is to officially tell the airline that you're going to fly."],[1,53.68,3.13,"Normally, now, we do this online,"],[1,56.81,3.63,"but you can still check in at the airport, I think."],[1,60.44,1.08,"Yes, you can. Yeah."],[1,61.52,2.33,"So, there will be check-in gates."],[1,63.85,1.58,"It is very important."],[1,65.44,1.88,"It is, because they give you"],[1,67.32,1.88,"a boarding pass then, don't they?"],[1,69.19,0.58,"They do."],[1,69.78,5.76,"The boarding pass is the document that lets you get on the plane"],[1,75.53,4.71,"and also tells you what seat that you have on the plane as well."],[1,80.25,3.88,"So, you've checked in and you've got your boarding pass."],[1,84.13,1.67,"What happens next?"],[1,85.79,3.21,"Well, then you need to go through security,"],[1,89.01,4.71,"and that's when they check you to make sure you haven't got anything"],[1,93.72,2.75,"that you shouldn't have on a plane."],[1,96.47,3.96,"I hate airport security."],[1,100.43,6.59,"Even though I know that I'm not carrying anything that I'm not supposed to have,"],[1,107.02,2.46,"it still makes me feel a bit stressed."],[1,109.48,1.67,"Yeah, I know what you mean."],[1,111.15,2.71,"You feel guilty even if you haven't done anything."],[1,113.86,2.0,"Exactly!"],[1,115.87,5.59,"Yeah, airport security is one of the reasons I don't really enjoy airports."],[1,121.45,2.0,"Really? You don't like airports?"],[1,123.46,1.0,"I don't like airports."],[1,124.46,2.17,"I enjoy flying but..."],[1,126.63,3.42,"yeah, airports can be a bit stressful for me."],[1,130.05,5.46,"Well, I'm the exact opposite of you because I quite like airports,"],[1,135.51,2.71,"but I really don't like flying."],[1,138.22,2.21,"What do you like about airports?"],[1,140.43,4.5,"I think because when you finally get to the airport,"],[1,144.94,3.96,"it's somebody else's problem for you to travel."],[1,148.9,6.46,"So, it's my responsibility to get to the airport but once I'm there,"],[1,155.36,1.29,"I can relax."],[1,156.66,4.25,"OK. And have you ever been in a situation"],[1,160.91,3.17,"where you relaxed a bit too much"],[1,164.08,3.42,"and almost missed a flight?"],[1,167.5,5.71,"Yes, I have missed a flight once and it was very stressful."],[1,173.22,5.05,"I was travelling in Ireland and I had a flight in Dublin,"],[1,178.26,3.09,"and I was in a different city – in Cork."],[1,181.35,3.63,"And I missed the train from Cork to Dublin"],[1,184.98,4.5,"and so I couldn't get to the airport on time."],[1,189.48,1.58,"So, you did miss that flight?"],[1,191.07,0.92,"I missed it."],[1,191.98,1.42,"Oh no!"],[1,193.4,3.5,"So, Becca, have you ever missed a flight?"],[1,196.91,3.92,"Thankfully, no, I haven't missed a flight."],[1,200.83,3.09,"I was very close to missing a flight..."],[1,203.91,2.5,"I was just relaxing."],[1,206.41,4.42,"I had travelled from Vietnam and I was really tired,"],[1,210.84,4.13,"so I wasn't really listening carefully."],[1,214.97,7.3,"Well, they didn't call my name, so I was really stressed trying to find the gate,"],[1,222.26,1.42,"but luckily I caught it."],[1,223.68,2.84,"I think I was the last person to board the plane,"],[1,226.52,3.46,"and that's always a little bit embarrassing as well."],[1,229.98,1.17,"You know what, Becca?"],[1,231.15,5.84,"I think if you get your name read out on the loudspeaker in an airport,"],[1,236.99,2.25,"that's really embarrassing"],[1,239.24,3.09,"because everyone in the airport is thinking,"],[1,242.32,2.13,"Who's that person?"],[1,244.45,2.42,"That person who's going to miss their flight!"],[1,246.87,3.29,"And you don't want to be that person."],[1,255.88,5.0,"OK. Becca, it's time to recap the vocabulary that we used in this episode,"],[1,260.89,1.58,"starting with 'check in'."],[1,262.47,4.55,"'Check in' is the verb, which means you officially tell the plane company"],[1,267.02,2.25,"that you plan to get on the plane,"],[1,269.27,2.5,"and you give them your bags as well."],[1,271.77,4.59,"And at the airport you do this at the check-in desk."],[1,276.36,4.09,"Yes. And at the check-in desk, that's where you get your boarding pass,"],[1,280.45,3.17,"and that's the ticket that you need to get on the plane."],[1,283.62,4.17,"And then we heard the expression 'go through security'."],[1,287.79,4.25,"If you go through security, you go through that area where they check"],[1,292.04,4.63,"and make sure you have nothing with you that should not be on a plane."],[1,296.67,4.84,"Yes. Going through security – my least favourite thing about airports."],[1,301.51,3.0,"After security, you wait at the boarding gate"],[1,304.51,4.38,"and this is the area that you wait at before you get on the plane."],[1,308.89,1.63,"If you catch a flight,"],[1,310.52,3.29,"it means you successfully get on the plane and fly somewhere,"],[1,313.81,2.0,"but if you miss a flight,"],[1,315.81,5.88,"like I did in Ireland, it means that you were too late to get on the plane."],[1,321.7,3.38,"Well, Neil, that's it for this week's episode of Real Easy English,"],[1,325.07,3.88,"but we'll be back next week with another episode."],[1,328.95,2.54,"Don't forget that you can download a free worksheet"],[1,331.5,3.34,"on our website, bbclearningenglish.com."],[1,334.83,0.83,"Goodbye for now."],[1,335.67,0.92,"Bye!"],[2,0.08,4.6,"Hello and welcome to 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil."],[2,4.68,1.4,"And I'm Georgie."],[2,6.08,1.92,"Find the vocabulary, definitions"],[2,8.0,6.12,"and a free worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com."],[2,14.12,4.0,"So, Georgie we're going to talk about something a little different today."],[2,18.12,2.0,"We're talking about itching."],[2,20.12,5.0,"Now an itch is that unpleasant feeling you get on your skin"],[2,25.12,3.12,"that you have to scratch with your fingers."],[2,28.24,2.96,"What sort of things make you itch?"],[2,31.2,4.44,"Well, funny you should ask, because I'm actually wearing quite an itchy jumper"],[2,35.64,4.08,"at the moment and it's making me want to scratch my skin. What about you?"],[2,39.72,5.16,"Well, recently I was bitten by a mosquito and,"],[2,44.88,3.96,"as you know, when you get bitten by a mosquito, it's really itchy."],[2,48.84,2.28,"I had to scratch loads and loads."],[2,51.12,1.64,"Oh, I hate it when that happens."],[2,52.76,1.24,"It was horrible."],[2,54.0,4.48,"In this podcast, we're going to hear from some experts about why we itch"],[2,58.48,2.9,"and how scratching can be good for us."],[2,61.38,4.32,"And as usual, we'll be learning some useful new vocabulary."],[2,65.7,3.76,"OK. Well, we're going to start with a quiz question of course, Georgie."],[2,69.46,2.2,"It's a bit of a different one today."],[2,71.66,5.28,"Dr Henning Holle studies the psychology of itching,"],[2,76.94,2.62,"and he played some scratching sounds"],[2,79.56,4.38,"to the guests on the BBC World Service programme CrowdScience."],[2,83.94,4.36,"Now the question is, what is being scratched?"],[2,88.3,8.28,"You have three choices: a) an arm, b) a beard, or c) some jeans."],[2,96.58,2.02,"OK. Well, let's listen..."],[2,106.86,4.32,"Well, that sounded a bit hairy, so I'm going to go with b) a beard."],[2,111.18,2.68,"Well, we'll find out at the end of the programme."],[2,113.86,4.64,"So, Neil, I mentioned that my jumper was a bit itchy"],[2,118.5,3.7,"but sometimes I feel itchy for no particular reason."],[2,122.2,1.74,"So, what's going on there?"],[2,123.94,1.68,"Well, that's something that"],[2,125.62,4.96,"the listeners of BBC World Service programme CrowdScience also wondered."],[2,130.58,2.04,"They wanted to know the answer to that."],[2,132.62,3.04,"The presenter, Anand Jagatia,"],[2,135.66,5.68,"spoke to itching expert Sonja Ständer to find out more. Let's listen."],[2,141.34,2.28,"There's lots of science to unpack here. Sonja,"],[2,143.62,1.96,"what actually is itchiness?"],[2,145.58,4.36,"Yeah. So, by scratching the skin, we remove parasites or plants"],[2,149.94,3.0,"or anything that does not belong on the skin."],[2,152.94,7.36,"And so we start to scratch at the very same area where we itch and remove it."],[2,160.3,4.64,"And it can no longer damage the skin and harm ourselves."],[2,164.94,4.52,"Anand says there's a lot of science to unpack about itchiness."],[2,169.46,3.0,"'Unpack' here means explain."],[2,172.46,4.32,"Sonja explains that when our skin feels itchy, we scratch it,"],[2,176.78,5.46,"and that helps to get rid of parasites and other harmful things on our skin."],[2,182.24,4.16,"Parasites are animals or plants that live on another animal"],[2,186.4,2.2,"and feed from them."],[2,188.6,3.48,"So, Neil, itching is our skin's way of protecting itself"],[2,192.08,1.96,"against harmful parasites."],[2,194.04,2.48,"And I have to say, all of this talk of itching"],[2,196.52,3.24,"and scratching is making me feel a bit itchy right now."],[2,199.76,4.44,"Well, that's interesting because Dr Henning Holle wanted to study"],[2,204.2,5.84,"whether just thinking about itching makes us more likely to feel itchy."],[2,210.04,4.12,"Henning and his team studied what happened when people watched videos"],[2,214.16,1.96,"of itching and scratching."],[2,216.12,5.16,"Let's listen to him speaking to BBC World Service programme CrowdScience."],[2,221.28,3.12,"And what we found in this study was, yes,"],[2,224.4,4.0,"these scratching videos indeed made people feel itchier."],[2,228.4,4.32,"They also started to scratch themselves more spontaneously."],[2,232.72,4.52,"So, the answer is basically yes, you can catch a scratch."],[2,237.24,4.08,"In Henning's study, when people watched videos of scratching,"],[2,241.32,3.24,"they scratched themselves spontaneously."],[2,244.56,3.68,"If you do something spontaneously, you do it without being asked"],[2,248.24,1.92,"or without thinking."],[2,250.16,4.68,"So, Henning concludes that yes, you can catch a scratch,"],[2,254.84,2.28,"just like you might catch an illness."],[2,257.12,4.4,"'Catch' in this context means get from someone else."],[2,261.52,4.76,"OK. Georgie, I think it's time for the answer to my quiz question."],[2,266.28,3.52,"We had a clip of people scratching"],[2,269.8,4.8,"and you had to decide what is being scratched."],[2,274.6,2.72,"I said that was a beard."],[2,277.32,3.76,"OK. Well, let's see if the people who are listening to this"],[2,281.08,3.48,"on the programme CrowdScience agreed."],[2,284.56,3.52,"It sounds like hairy skin so I would go for the scalp."],[2,288.08,2.72,"Yeah, I think it's beard or something because it does sound"],[2,290.8,1.12,"like there's hair there."],[2,291.92,3.0,"It is definitely hairy skin and Anand was spot on."],[2,294.92,2.96,"So, it is indeed a beard scratch that we were listening to."],[2,297.88,0.46,"Wow!"],[2,298.34,1.38,"So I was right!"],[2,299.72,2.12,"You were. Congratulations!"],[2,301.84,3.5,"OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we learned in this programme,"],[2,305.34,4.52,"starting with 'itch', which is an unpleasant feeling on your skin."],[2,309.86,4.52,"And something can be described using the adjective 'itchy'."],[2,314.38,3.76,"When you feel an itch, you often scratch it with your fingers"],[2,318.14,3.16,"by rubbing your nails on the skin."],[2,321.3,6.08,"If you unpack an idea or scientific concept, you explain it in clear detail."],[2,327.38,6.12,"A parasite is an animal or plant that lives on another animal and feeds on it."],[2,333.5,3.84,"If you do something spontaneously, you do it without being asked"],[2,337.34,1.72,"or without thinking."],[2,339.06,5.04,"'Catch' can be used to mean get from someone else, such as an illness."],[2,344.1,2.76,"For example, you can catch a cold."],[2,346.86,3.84,"Once again, our six minutes are up, but you can test what you've learned"],[2,350.7,4.08,"with the worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com."],[2,354.78,1.88,"Thank you for joining us and goodbye."],[2,356.66,1.34,"Goodbye."],[3,0.12,2.08,"From BBC Learning English."],[3,2.2,4.52,"This is Learning English from the News our podcast about the news headlines."],[3,6.72,0.84,"In this programme,"],[3,7.56,4.4,"hundreds of thousands of schoolchildren protest across France."],[3,16.36,2.2,"Hello, I'm Pippa. And I'm Phil."],[3,18.56,2.84,"In this programme, we look at one big news story"],[3,21.4,3.68,"and the vocabulary in the headlines that will help you understand it."],[3,25.08,3.56,"You can find all the vocabulary and headlines from this episode,"],[3,28.64,4.36,"as well as a worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com."],[3,33.0,3.52,"So, let's hear more about this story, Phil."],[3,40.8,0.48,"Yes."],[3,41.28,2.68,"School pupils in France have been taking part"],[3,43.96,2.24,"in protests against the government."],[3,46.2,1.92,"People say that they are protesting"],[3,48.12,2.84,"because their schools are not being well-funded."],[3,50.96,2.2,"They say that there aren't enough teachers"],[3,53.16,2.92,"and that school buildings are in a bad condition."],[3,56.08,4.48,"Since protests began, over 6,000 people have been arrested"],[3,60.56,3.28,"and a number of students and police officers have been hurt."],[3,63.84,2.92,"The French government has said that it will review the issues"],[3,66.76,1.84,"that students are protesting about."],[3,68.6,1.88,"Let's have our first headline."],[3,70.48,3.2,"This is from PBS, an American broadcaster."],[3,73.68,3.2,"Student protests grip France as Prime Minister"],[3,76.88,2.68,"Lecornu calls crisis meeting."],[3,79.56,2.48,"That headline again from PBS."],[3,82.04,5.4,"Student protests grip France as Prime Minister Lecornu calls crisis meeting."],[3,87.44,3.24,"This headline is about the impact of these protests."],[3,90.68,4.48,"In the article, the writer mentions the crisis meetings of politicians,"],[3,95.16,2.48,"hundreds of arrests and injuries,"],[3,97.64,3.32,"and the impact it could have on future elections."],[3,100.96,2.68,"We're going to learn the word 'grip'."],[3,103.64,4.0,"Yes, literally 'grip' means to hold onto something strongly."],[3,107.64,4.16,"If you grip something, you hold it so tightly it won't come out of your hands."],[3,111.8,3.4,"There are also some objects that can grip things. Paint"],[3,115.2,2.04,"or glue grips onto things,"],[3,117.24,3.56,"it doesn't move off or car tires grip the road."],[3,120.8,1.28,"They don't slip."],[3,122.08,2.84,"In this story, 'grip' is a metaphor."],[3,124.92,5.0,"If an event grips a country or a city, then it holds on to people's attention."],[3,129.92,4.04,"Many people are affected by the protests or are interested in them."],[3,133.96,2.8,"It's not just protests that can grip a country."],[3,136.76,5.0,"Emotions like fear or excitement can grip an entire nation."],[3,145.12,4.0,"We've had 'grip' - hold on to people's attention."],[3,151.16,1.0,"For example,"],[3,152.16,4.32,"the country was gripped by excitement after their team reached the final"],[3,156.48,1.92,"for the first time."],[3,162.68,4.6,"This is Learning English from the News, our podcast about the news headlines."],[3,167.28,3.32,"Today we're talking about school protests in France."],[3,170.6,4.08,"The French government reported that at least 24 schools had been burnt"],[3,174.68,2.08,"or damaged in the protests."],[3,176.76,4.8,"One protester in Lens had their hand blown off by a police grenade."],[3,181.56,4.84,"Another protest in a Paris suburb led to a protester having their jaw broken."],[3,186.4,1.96,"Let's have another headline."],[3,188.36,3.28,"This is from The Irish Times, an Irish newspaper."],[3,191.64,3.8,"Inside the school protests in France that have spiralled"],[3,195.44,2.52,"into arson and street violence."],[3,197.96,3.28,"And that headline, again from The Irish Times, an Irish newspaper."],[3,201.24,3.72,"Inside the school protests in France that have spiralled"],[3,204.96,2.28,"into arson and street violence."],[3,207.24,3.64,"This headline is about the way that protests have led to arson -"],[3,210.88,3.24,"that's setting things on fire, and street violence."],[3,214.12,2.68,"We're going to learn the word 'spiral'."],[3,216.8,1.64,"A spiral is a shape."],[3,218.44,1.68,"So, think of a snail shell"],[3,220.12,4.4,"or what happens when you get a string and wind it round in circles."],[3,224.52,3.16,"The important thing is that each circle is a little bit bigger"],[3,227.68,2.96,"than the last one, or above the previous one."],[3,230.64,3.88,"We also use 'spiral' as a verb, and often metaphorically."],[3,234.52,3.08,"If something spirals, then it gets worse, more serious"],[3,237.6,2.56,"or out of control as it develops."],[3,240.16,3.8,"In this story, what started as protests have got more serious"],[3,243.96,3.68,"and now include things like arson and street violence."],[3,247.64,2.92,"A common phrase is 'spiral out of control',"],[3,250.56,2.32,"which means that things are hard to stop."],[3,252.88,6.48,"Crowd behaviour, violence, riots, and fire are all things that can spiral."],[3,263.08,5.12,"We've had 'spiral' - get worse, often in an uncontrolled way."],[3,271.12,4.28,"For example, house prices have been spiralling recently."],[3,275.4,2.4,"They're becoming unaffordable."],[3,280.64,3.52,"This is Learning English from the News from BBC Learning English."],[3,284.16,2.96,"We're talking about schoolchildren protesting in France."],[3,287.12,3.4,"Many schools across France have been closed by the protests,"],[3,290.52,1.6,"which have been getting bigger."],[3,292.12,2.88,"There has been disagreement between the French education ministry"],[3,295.0,4.32,"and trade unions about the exact number of schools that have been closed."],[3,299.32,2.32,"Our next headline is about school closures"],[3,301.64,2.04,"and is from earlier in the week."],[3,303.68,2.0,"This is from Al Jazeera."],[3,305.68,5.64,"France to shutter up to 500 schools as more student protests called."],[3,311.32,4.64,"That headline again from Al Jazeera, an international news organisation."],[3,315.96,4.92,"France to shutter up to 500 schools as more student protests called."],[3,320.88,2.0,"This headline is about school closures"],[3,322.88,3.24,"and we're going to look at the word 'shutter'."],[3,326.12,4.16,"'Shutter' is another word being used metaphorically. Literally,"],[3,330.28,2.96,"a shutter is something solid that you put across a window"],[3,333.24,3.8,"or a door to stop light getting in, or to stop it being broken."],[3,337.04,2.76,"You can probably see the word 'shut' in there."],[3,339.8,4.08,"And 'shutter', as a verb means close the shutters"],[3,343.88,4.16,"or metaphorically to close a business or institution."],[3,348.04,4.28,"For example, local authorities might tell people to shutter their businesses"],[3,352.32,1.72,"if a severe storm is coming."],[3,354.04,3.12,"In this story, schools have been shuttered during the protest"],[3,357.16,1.56,"because of safety fears."],[3,358.72,3.56,"So, in this context, 'shutter' means to close temporarily,"],[3,362.28,3.4,"but 'shutter' can also mean to close something down permanently."],[3,365.68,3.32,"For example, after 50 years of running his business,"],[3,369.0,2.96,"he shuttered the shop when he retired."],[3,373.48,5.44,"We've had 'shutter' - to close something, often temporarily."],[3,378.92,2.16,"For example, the city was quiet"],[3,381.08,3.64,"as many of the businesses had been shuttered for the holidays."],[3,384.72,2.96,"That's it for this episode of Learning English from the News."],[3,387.68,3.36,"We'll be back next week with another news story."],[3,391.04,3.72,"And if you like our podcasts, sign up to our email newsletter"],[3,394.76,1.76,"to get the latest from BBC"],[3,396.52,4.76,"Learning English every week. There's a link in the notes below this episode."],[3,401.28,2.28,"Bye for now. Goodbye."],[4,6.56,1.16,"Pass the Parcel,"],[4,7.72,2.12,"read by me, Will Sharpe."],[4,17.52,3.0,"Today is Lila's birthday party."],[4,21.0,2.76,"\"Hey, kids, are you ready for Pass the Parcel?\""],[4,23.76,2.96,"\"Yeah!\" \"Yeah!\" \"Yeah!\""],[4,26.72,3.04,"Lila's mum asks Lucky's dad to lead the game."],[4,30.32,3.6,"\"You have to stop on each kid once,\" she explains."],[4,33.92,3.8,"\"So everyone gets a prize,\" adds Bluey's mum."],[4,37.72,4.16,"\"What?! When I was a kid, there was only one present in the…\""],[4,41.88,2.48,"\"Start the music!\" the kids shout."],[4,44.36,2.28,"\"Okay, okay,\" Lucky's dad says."],[4,46.64,3.24,"\"But that is not how you play Pass the Parcel.\""],[4,50.72,2.16,"Lucky's dad starts the music."],[4,52.88,3.56,"\"Okay, and… stop,\" he says,"],[4,56.44,1.56,"landing on Bingo."],[4,58.0,2.12,"\"Ooh, it's a bracelet!\""],[4,61.08,2.96,"Next, the music stops on Pom Pom."],[4,64.04,1.64,"\"A lollipop!\""],[4,65.72,2.6,"Then it stops on Lila."],[4,68.32,2.16,"\"You got a bracelet too!\""],[4,70.96,2.6,"Lucky's dad tries to stop on Missy next."],[4,73.56,3.92,"But the parcel accidentally lands on Pom Pom again!"],[4,77.76,2.44,"\"Oh no, that's for Missy.\""],[4,81.6,2.8,"\"But it stopped on me, Lucky's dad!\""],[4,84.4,3.0,"\"Ugh, I'm not cut out for this.\""],[4,87.92,2.16,"Today is Chucky's birthday."],[4,90.08,4.36,"His mum is preparing Pass the Parcel with a present in every layer."],[4,94.44,3.4,"But Lucky's dad says she only needs one in the middle."],[4,98.32,2.72,"\"What about everyone else?\" Lucky's mum asks."],[4,101.04,2.88,"\"They'll have to suck it up,\" he says."],[4,103.92,3.0,"\"We're raising a nation of squibs!\""],[4,107.2,2.6,"\"Fine, but you're handling it!\""],[4,113.04,1.64,"\"Ready for Pass the Parcel?\""],[4,114.68,2.36,"\"Yeah!\" \"Yeah!\" \"Yeah!\""],[4,117.04,2.96,"\"We're going to mix this up a bit, kids,\" Lucky's dad says."],[4,120.0,1.44,"\"You're gonna love it!\""],[4,123.0,2.28,"\"See now, I'm not looking."],[4,125.28,3.32,"I'm just going to push this button any time…\""],[4,129.12,2.68,"The music stops on Bingo."],[4,131.8,2.0,"\"I can't find the present.\""],[4,133.8,3.48,"Lucky's dad explains that they're playing the proper way,"],[4,137.28,3.0,"with just one big present in the middle."],[4,140.36,3.28,"\"So, I don't get a present?\" Bingo asks."],[4,143.64,3.0,"\"No, 'cause just like in real life, you…\""],[4,147.52,1.8,"But Bingo doesn't hear the rest."],[4,149.32,1.76,"\"Wah!\""],[4,151.08,2.88,"\"Oh boy. Um, she'll be right.\""],[4,155.04,3.0,"\"This is how you're supposed to play it!\" Lucky's dad says."],[4,158.04,2.2,"He keeps going, using his rules."],[4,161.04,3.0,"\"Wah!\" \"Wah!\""],[4,164.96,3.36,"\"Lucky's dad, do I not win anything?\""],[4,169.24,1.84,"He tries to make it better."],[4,171.08,2.68,"\"It's okay! You won five bucks!\""],[4,174.76,2.32,"\"Here, take one of these.\""],[4,177.08,2.16,"\"Hey! Those are my presents!\""],[4,179.24,1.56,"\"Argh!\""],[4,181.64,3.0,"Finally, the music stops on Jasper."],[4,185.28,3.0,"He wins the big present in the middle!"],[4,188.48,1.56,"\"Oh, yeah!\""],[4,191.32,3.76,"On the drive home, Mum asks Bingo if she had fun at the party."],[4,195.08,1.8,"\"Yes,\" says Bingo."],[4,196.88,1.72,"\"Except for Pass the Parcel."],[4,198.6,2.68,"I don't like Lucky's dad's rules.\""],[4,201.28,3.08,"\"Yeah,\" says Mum, \"it's hard when you don't win.\""],[4,204.36,2.64,"\"Yes, it is,\" Bingo replies."],[4,207.0,2.32,"\"Maybe next time,\" says Mum."],[4,212.2,3.32,"The following weekend is Jasper's birthday party."],[4,215.52,3.16,"He wants to play Pass the Parcel Lucky's dad's way."],[4,219.48,2.84,"\"With only one present in the middle?\" his mum asks."],[4,222.32,1.96,"\"Yeah!\" says Jasper."],[4,224.52,2.88,"\"Honey, that was a bit of a disaster.\""],[4,227.4,3.56,"\"No, it was awesome. Pleeeease!\""],[4,232.08,2.88,"At the party, Jasper's mum announces"],[4,234.96,3.36,"that Pass the Parcel will be played by Lucky's dad's rules."],[4,238.8,1.76,"\"They're not my rules!\""],[4,240.56,2.64,"\"Oh, what?\" \"Aw!\""],[4,243.2,2.96,"\"Let's just do our best, okay?\" she says."],[4,246.88,2.0,"The music starts…"],[4,257.72,1.68,"…and stops on Bingo."],[4,259.4,1.92,"\"Ooh, it's me!\" she says."],[4,263.04,1.84,"But there's no present."],[4,264.88,3.04,"\"Sorry, Bingo.\" \"Aww.\""],[4,269.52,1.44,"The music keeps going…"],[4,271.28,1.4,"…until…"],[4,273.36,1.88,"…it stops on Lila."],[4,275.24,2.32,"There's no present for her either."],[4,277.56,1.72,"\"Sorry, Lila.\""],[4,279.28,1.8,"The music starts again."],[4,282.04,2.48,"Finally, it lands on Missy,"],[4,284.52,2.72,"who wins the big present in the middle."],[4,287.24,1.24,"\"Whoa!\""],[4,288.48,2.32,"\"I was so close!\""],[4,290.8,2.44,"Everyone is excited,"],[4,293.24,1.96,"except Bingo."],[4,295.2,2.52,"\"Maybe next time, Bingo.\""],[4,300.24,2.84,"Today is Buddy's birthday party."],[4,303.08,3.32,"\"Ready for Pass the Parcel?\" Buddy's mum asks everyone."],[4,306.4,2.48,"\"Yeah!\" \"Yeah!\" \"Yeah!\""],[4,309.72,3.6,"\"We're doing Lucky's dad's rules!\" Buddy tells his friends."],[4,313.32,3.12,"Everyone cheers, but Bingo isn't sure."],[4,317.16,1.88,"\"Hooray!\" \"Hooray!\""],[4,320.16,1.52,"The music starts."],[4,322.08,2.56,"\"Please let me win this time!\""],[4,326.88,1.96,"The parcel gets passed,"],[4,330.76,2.8,"but it doesn't stop on Bingo."],[4,333.56,1.36,"\"It's me!\""],[4,335.52,1.56,"Lila wins!"],[4,337.08,2.96,"\"Bingo, look – a bubble shooter!\""],[4,341.8,2.24,"\"Ooh!\" \"Wow!\""],[4,344.04,2.12,"\"I love Lucky dad's rules.\""],[4,349.32,4.32,"\"I'm sorry you didn't win Pass the Parcel,\" Mum says to Bingo on the way home."],[4,353.64,2.88,"\"It's okay, I don't mind,\" says Bingo."],[4,356.52,1.76,"\"Really?\" Mum asks."],[4,358.28,1.48,"\"Yeah!\" says Bingo."],[4,359.76,3.12,"\"When Lila is happy, I'm happy."],[4,362.88,1.32,"Maybe next time.\""],[4,366.04,1.52,"There are more parties…"],[4,368.56,3.2,"…but Bingo still doesn't win Pass the Parcel."],[4,373.52,2.44,"\"You know what, Bingo?\" Mum tells her."],[4,382.84,2.64,"\"I think you're getting quite good at losing.\""],[4,386.96,3.2,"It's almost time for Bluey's birthday party."],[4,392.96,3.32,"\"How do you want to do Pass the Parcel tomorrow?\""],[4,396.28,2.0,"\"I'll let Bingo choose.\""],[4,399.44,2.44,"\"Really? Thanks, Bluey!\""],[4,404.88,1.2,"At Bluey's party,"],[4,406.08,3.32,"Dad asks Lucky's dad to do the honours for Pass the Parcel."],[4,410.36,1.96,"\"Righto!\" says Lucky's dad."],[4,412.32,1.96,"\"Which rules are we playing, Bluey?\""],[4,414.28,3.2,"\"I left it up to Bingo to choose,\" Bluey replies."],[4,418.24,4.76,"\"Well, Bingo, whatever way is okay with me,\" says Lucky's dad."],[4,423.0,0.92,"\"What?!\""],[4,423.92,2.6,"\"Ah, let 'em have some fun.\""],[4,426.52,2.12,"He starts the music."],[4,428.64,2.0,"When the music stops on Lila,"],[4,430.64,2.36,"everyone sees that it's…"],[4,433.0,1.8,"\"Lucky's dad's rules!\""],[4,437.88,2.28,"They keep playing until…"],[4,440.16,1.56,"Bingo wins!"],[4,442.08,2.32,"\"Birdie kite!\""],[4,445.2,1.8,"The end."],[5,6.88,1.04,"Bluey Bedroom,"],[5,7.92,1.88,"read by me, Tan France."],[5,10.8,2.48,"- Er, asleep! (BINGO SNORES)"],[5,13.28,1.28,"- Awake! - Ahh!"],[5,14.56,1.68,"- Asleep! - (BINGO SNORES)"],[5,16.24,1.0,"- Awake! - Ahh!"],[5,17.24,1.32,"- Asleep! Awake! - Ahh!"],[5,18.56,1.8,"- Asleep, awake, asleep, awake, asleep, awake! - (BINGO GIGGLES)"],[5,21.2,3.04,"Mum and Dad are clearing out the spare room."],[5,24.24,2.28,"Bluey and Bingo are helping."],[5,26.52,1.48,"\"That one can go.\""],[5,28.0,1.44,"\"Yes, boss.\""],[5,30.72,1.96,"Mum looks at the cot."],[5,32.68,2.72,"\"Why are you crying?\" Bluey asks."],[5,35.4,2.64,"\"Oh,\" says Mum with a sniffle,"],[5,38.04,2.48,"\"it's just this room, sweetheart.\""],[5,40.52,3.16,"\"How can a room make you cry?\" Bluey asks."],[5,44.2,3.72,"\"Well, I guess it's not the room. It's who's been in it.\""],[5,49.2,3.8,"\"This was your first bed,\" Mum tells Bluey."],[5,53.0,2.2,"\"Yeah, then I moved into my room.\""],[5,55.52,2.64,"\"And then it was Bingo's first bed.\""],[5,58.36,3.04,"\"Then she moved into my room,\" says Bluey."],[5,62.16,3.0,"\"And now it's no one's,\" Mum finishes sadly."],[5,67.4,2.76,"Bluey and Bingo come up with a plan."],[5,70.16,1.84,"\"Mum,\" says Bluey,"],[5,72.0,2.68,"\"can the spare room be my bedroom?"],[5,74.68,1.56,"And Bingo can stay in that room.\""],[5,76.6,3.28,"\"Then we'll each have our own bedroom,\" adds Bingo."],[5,81.36,3.0,"\"Okay,\" Mum says. \"As long as you're sure.\""],[5,85.08,1.56,"\"Hooray!\""],[5,90.36,2.52,"While Dad moves Bluey's bed into her new room,"],[5,92.88,3.0,"Bluey and Bingo make some important decisions."],[5,96.6,2.12,"\"Grr. Come on!\""],[5,99.32,3.4,"\"Bingo, can the watermelon rug go in my room?\""],[5,103.12,3.88,"\"Um, okay. But can we have a rug race first?\""],[5,107.4,1.2,"\"Yeah!\""],[5,109.68,3.84,"\"Ready, set, go!\""],[5,114.24,1.52,"\"He-he!\""],[5,120.28,2.0,"\"I'm in the lead!\""],[5,123.56,2.68,"\"Okay, bed's in,\" says Dad."],[5,126.96,1.64,"\"Thanks, big fella!\""],[5,128.6,2.36,"\"Don't call me big fella.\""],[5,131.76,3.0,"\"Hey big fella, can you move my bed?\""],[5,138.68,4.16,"\"Bingo, you moved your bed to where mine used to be,\" Bluey says."],[5,142.84,3.6,"\"I did it so we can still watch Malcolm,\" Bingo explains."],[5,147.72,2.48,"\"Come on, Malcolm, do something!\""],[5,151.84,1.48,"Rustle, rustle."],[5,153.32,2.76,"\"Ooh! He heard us!\""],[5,159.68,2.8,"Bluey and Bingo sort through their teddies."],[5,162.48,3.0,"\"Now, these teddies are all mine.\""],[5,165.48,2.24,"\"And these are all mine.\""],[5,167.72,2.52,"\"But what about Gloria?"],[5,170.24,3.0,"She was a Christmas present to me from Grandad,\" says Bluey."],[5,173.64,4.0,"\"But I didn't get one because he forgot I was born,\" Bingo replies."],[5,178.48,2.16,"\"So whose room should she go in?\""],[5,181.16,4.48,"\"Hmm,\" Bluey thinks. \"She can go to yours.\""],[5,186.24,2.4,"\"Thanks, Bluey,\" says Bingo."],[5,188.64,2.48,"\"But how about she sleeps over at yours tonight?\""],[5,191.32,1.52,"\"Oh, okay!\""],[5,195.0,3.0,"\"Who keeps Lampy?\" asks Bingo."],[5,198.04,2.12,"\"You can keep her,\" Bluey offers."],[5,200.16,2.24,"\"No, it's okay, you take Lampy."],[5,202.4,3.0,"But can we play Asleep Awake?\""],[5,205.4,2.84,"\"Ooh,\" Bluey says, \"okay.\""],[5,208.24,1.84,"Asleep!"],[5,211.2,1.24,"Awake!"],[5,212.44,2.68,"Asleep! Awake! Asleep! Awake!"],[5,216.2,2.12,"Asleep! Awake! Asleep! Awake! Asleep! Awake!\""],[5,218.32,0.88,"\"He-he-he!\""],[5,223.96,2.08,"The day is coming to an end…"],[5,242.04,2.64,"Finally, it's bedtime."],[5,244.68,1.4,"\"Okay, kids, into bed."],[5,246.08,2.2,"Big D's ready for knock-off.\""],[5,249.32,1.48,"\"Sleep tight.\""],[5,251.4,1.84,"\"You too.\""],[5,253.44,2.0,"\"Don't let the bed bugs bite.\""],[5,257.72,3.04,"\"Take care of Gloria and Lampy.\""],[5,260.76,1.32,"\"Yes, I will, and…\""],[5,262.08,1.52,"\"All right!\" says Dad."],[5,263.6,1.64,"\"Come on, come on.\""],[5,268.0,3.0,"Bluey gets ready for bed in her new room."],[5,271.44,2.08,"\"You all set, Bluey?\" Mum asks."],[5,273.52,1.72,"\"Exciting!\""],[5,275.24,4.68,"\"Tell Bingo to say goodnight to Malcolm for me,\" Bluey replies."],[5,279.92,0.84,"\"Night.\""],[5,281.16,1.32,"\"Night.\""],[5,284.04,2.16,"She tries to fall asleep."],[5,286.2,3.0,"But something isn't quite right."],[5,289.8,2.08,"\"Oh yeah, couch.\""],[5,292.2,2.68,"Downstairs, Mum and Dad hear a thump."],[5,296.08,2.04,"\"Bags not!\" they both shout,"],[5,298.12,2.0,"but Dad just beats Mum."],[5,300.12,3.48,"\"Ah, biscuits,\" Mum says as she heads upstairs."],[5,307.4,3.04,"\"What's going on?\" Mum asks."],[5,310.44,2.28,"\"Bingo said I could have them.\""],[5,312.72,2.88,"\"It's bedtime, kids, come on,\" says Mum."],[5,315.6,1.56,"\"No more leaving your rooms, okay?\""],[5,318.72,1.24,"\"Okay, Mama.\""],[5,320.4,1.48,"\"Okay, Mama.\""],[5,330.96,3.0,"Bluey still can't fall asleep."],[5,334.56,3.08,"She remembers something else she needs."],[5,350.88,1.64,"\"Oh yeah!\""],[5,358.72,2.6,"\"Sorry, Gloria, I forgot your bottle.\""],[5,386.44,2.12,"Thump. Thump."],[5,388.56,2.04,"\"Bags not!\" \"Bags not! Argh!\""],[5,391.4,2.24,"Mum heads upstairs again."],[5,394.36,2.24,"\"What's going on here?\""],[5,396.6,2.08,"\"I didn't leave the room!\" says Bluey."],[5,398.68,2.72,"\"Well, no messages either,\" says Mum."],[5,401.4,1.72,"\"Come on, it's bedtime.\""],[5,403.8,2.32,"\"Okay,\" Bluey says sadly."],[5,407.8,2.72,"\"Are you okay, sweetie?\" Mum asks."],[5,411.08,4.08,"\"I'm just not sure I've got everything I need,\" says Bluey."],[5,416.72,1.12,"\"What!"],[5,417.84,1.16,"You've got your bed,"],[5,419.0,1.96,"you've got your box and the rug."],[5,420.96,2.04,"You've even got the ladder!"],[5,423.0,1.8,"What else do you need?"],[5,431.08,3.0,"How about one last message?\""],[5,437.28,2.68,"\"Goodnight Missy. Goodnight Lila."],[5,440.88,3.24,"Goodnight Grandpa Bob, wherever you are."],[5,449.0,1.36,"Ooh!\""],[5,452.24,1.88,"\"All good?\" asks Dad."],[5,454.12,1.56,"\"All good, big fella."],[5,455.68,3.84,"But you'll have to move Bluey's bed back in the morning,\" Mum replies."],[5,459.52,3.2,"\"Oh, what! Does she miss her old room?\""],[5,462.72,1.8,"\"No…\" Mum answers."],[5,466.4,1.8,"\"It's not the room.\""],[5,469.2,1.36,"The end."],[6,0.04,0.86,"Hi Neil."],[6,0.9,0.86,"Hi Georgie."],[6,1.76,2.7,"Do you want to hear something quite unusual for me?"],[6,4.46,1.02,"Err, yes!"],[6,5.48,4.24,"I haven't had an alcoholic drink this year."],[6,9.72,2.76,"Wow! That is really impressive. How do you feel?"],[6,12.48,1.2,"Like a new person?"],[6,13.68,2.52,"I do feel very good, I have to say."],[6,16.2,4.28,"I can't say the same – I did have a few alcoholic drinks at the weekend"],[6,20.48,1.48,"with my friends."],[6,21.96,3.76,"But in many parts of the world, people don't drink alcohol at all."],[6,25.72,4.32,"In the UK it's very common – that is until recently."],[6,30.04,2.88,"Today on 6 Minute English, we're going to find out"],[6,32.92,4.64,"why many people are choosing to give up or cut down on alcohol,"],[6,37.56,6.04,"and we'll be asking, Is this the end of British drinking culture?"],[6,43.6,3.08,"OK. Neil, let's start with a quiz question."],[6,46.68,4.52,"Many people in the UK and other countries choose to give up alcohol"],[6,51.2,3.36,"in the new year for the whole of the month of January,"],[6,54.56,2.68,"but what is the challenge called?"],[6,57.24,7.38,"Is it: a) No-drink New Year b) Alcohol-free January, or c) Dry January?"],[6,64.62,4.3,"Well, I know the answer to this, but I'm not going to give it away."],[6,68.92,2.26,"OK. Well, then our listeners can find out the answer"],[6,71.18,1.52,"at the end of the programme."],[6,72.7,3.14,"Yes, they can. So, Georgie, I hear all the time"],[6,75.84,4.42,"that younger people don't drink as much alcohol today as they used to"],[6,80.26,1.12,"in the past."],[6,81.38,1.32,"Is that true?"],[6,82.7,5.4,"Well, in the UK, surveys suggest that 16 to 24-year-olds are"],[6,88.1,3.84,"more likely to be non-drinkers than the older generations."],[6,91.94,4.04,"OK. So, why are more young people choosing to be sober?"],[6,95.98,2.28,"That means not to drink alcohol."],[6,98.26,2.44,"Well, the BBC World Service podcast"],[6,100.7,2.48,"What in the World have been investigating this."],[6,103.18,4.68,"Presenter Hannah Gelbart met three young people who choose to be sober"],[6,107.86,2.96,"for some alcohol-free drinks to find out more."],[6,110.82,1.72,"Here's Daniel's story:"],[6,112.54,2.32,"Yeah. So, today I don't drink at all,"],[6,114.86,2.96,"but when I was in my early 20s, I think I felt maybe a bit of, like,"],[6,117.82,2.64,"societal pressure – you know, work drinks or being at uni,"],[6,120.46,2.44,"where it was the norm to just join in."],[6,122.9,1.92,"I think you'd probably get a few weird glances if you're like,"],[6,124.82,1.24,"Yeah, I'm not going to join in at all."],[6,126.06,3.4,"So, I did that and I think Covid lockdown just gave me a bit"],[6,129.46,2.64,"of confidence to say, OK, let me step away from this."],[6,132.1,2.76,"It's not necessarily something I want to do moving forward."],[6,134.86,2.24,"So, how many years have you been sober for?"],[6,137.1,2.04,"I would say it's been six years now."],[6,139.14,0.96,"Six years?"],[6,140.1,1.44,"So, Daniel says"],[6,141.54,4.28,"that when he was younger, he felt societal pressure to drink alcohol."],[6,145.82,3.44,"'Societal pressure' is feeling like you need to do something"],[6,149.26,2.76,"because lots of people around you are doing it."],[6,152.02,5.16,"Right. So, Daniel says drinking was the norm at university and after work."],[6,157.18,3.64,"'The norm' is a way of saying that something is normal or common."],[6,160.82,2.92,"But during the Covid-19 pandemic,"],[6,163.74,4.64,"when people in the UK were unable to socialise and visit pubs and bars,"],[6,168.38,2.76,"Daniel's attitude to alcohol changed."],[6,171.14,2.2,"After that, he decided to be sober"],[6,173.34,3.24,"and he hasn't had a drink for about six years."],[6,176.58,4.48,"So, Georgie, it seems like many young people don't drink at all and"],[6,181.06,4.28,"lots of them share their experience of being sober on social media."],[6,185.34,3.42,"But recently there's been a bit of a change, hasn't there?"],[6,188.76,4.0,"Yes. Whilst many people use social media to share their experience"],[6,192.76,3.44,"of drinking less alcohol or no alcohol at all,"],[6,196.2,3.04,"some people have been saying that they want to go out more"],[6,199.24,2.08,"and drink and have fun with friends."],[6,201.32,3.2,"They argue we're too worried about fitness and wellness,"],[6,204.52,3.76,"and that drinking some alcohol to have a good time is fine."],[6,208.28,3.84,"Here's Millie talking more about this to BBC World Service podcast"],[6,212.12,1.36,"What in the World:"],[6,213.48,3.8,"I think we had this, like, rise of self-optimisation content."],[6,217.28,2.56,"I think unfortunately sobriety, kind of, got swept in with that"],[6,219.84,3.84,"and it got touted as this way to, you know, get up at 5am, do a HYROX."],[6,223.68,1.48,"And, you know, for many of us, that's probably not"],[6,225.16,1.44,"our reality with not drinking."],[6,226.6,3.48,"It's that we do it to feel a bit more sane or, you know, things like that."],[6,230.08,2.04,"And so I think now we are entering this,"],[6,232.12,3.84,"like, almost pushback era of that content and you're seeing celebrities..."],[6,235.96,2.28,"Like, it's not just alcohol – you're seeing more celebrities smoking"],[6,238.24,2.24,"and, like, posting that on their Instagram and not worrying"],[6,240.48,1.26,"if it's brand-friendly."],[6,241.74,4.3,"OK. So, Millie thinks that being sober is associated with certain kinds"],[6,246.04,4.72,"of self-optimisation content, where people talk about fitness, diet"],[6,250.76,2.2,"and having big goals for themselves."],[6,252.96,4.44,"Yes, and Millie says being sober gets touted as a way to get up"],[6,257.4,1.92,"at 5am to exercise."],[6,259.32,2.32,"In other words, people talk about it a lot"],[6,261.64,2.28,"and try to encourage people to do it."],[6,263.92,2.78,"But Millie says that being sober isn't always"],[6,266.7,2.06,"about being super fit and healthy."],[6,268.76,2.48,"For her, it's about being sane."],[6,271.24,1.78,"But still, Millie thinks"],[6,273.02,3.56,"there's a pushback against self-optimisation content."],[6,276.58,2.7,"'Pushback' means a criticism of the content,"],[6,279.28,3.16,"and people are pushing back by doing the opposite."],[6,282.44,4.92,"For example, lots of celebrities post about themselves drinking and smoking."],[6,287.36,4.48,"Well, Georgie, I've decided to try to give up alcohol for a whole year,"],[6,291.84,3.04,"but I don't think everybody should do that if they don't want to."],[6,294.88,2.26,"It's really up to the individual."],[6,297.14,2.94,"Yeah, I still enjoy having a few drinks with my friends."],[6,300.08,2.4,"I think it's just all about balance, really."],[6,302.48,1.92,"OK. Neil, it's time to reveal"],[6,304.4,1.92,"the answer to this week's question."],[6,306.32,6.08,"I asked, What was the name given for when people give up alcohol in January?"],[6,312.4,3.24,"Yes. And the answer is Dry January."],[6,315.64,1.52,"That is correct. Well done!"],[6,317.16,3.16,"Let's recap the vocabulary we learned in this programme,"],[6,320.32,4.12,"starting with 'sober', which describes someone who doesn't drink alcohol"],[6,324.44,2.52,"or take recreational drugs."],[6,326.96,3.4,"Societal pressure is the feeling that you should do something"],[6,330.36,2.96,"because lots of other people expect you to."],[6,333.32,5.6,"Saying something is 'the norm' is a way to say it's usual or common."],[6,338.92,2.84,"'Self-optimisation content' means videos and posts"],[6,341.76,4.0,"about being fit, healthy and achieving big goals."],[6,345.76,4.12,"If somebody touts an idea or lifestyle, they talk about it a lot"],[6,349.88,2.2,"and try to convince people to do it."],[6,352.08,3.72,"And pushback is strong criticism of a trend,"],[6,355.8,2.48,"and people doing the opposite."],[6,358.28,1.96,"Once again, our six minutes are up,"],[6,360.24,2.72,"but don't forget to test what you've learned with the worksheet"],[6,362.96,3.24,"on our website, bbclearningenglish.com."],[6,366.2,1.8,"Thank you for joining us and goodbye."],[6,368.0,1.18,"Goodbye!"],[7,6.52,1.36,"Bingo."],[7,7.88,2.48,"Read by me, Isla Fisher."],[7,10.56,2.12,"Doo-doo-dee-doo-doo-doo."],[7,12.68,2.0,"What am I gonna do?"],[7,19.64,3.0,"Bluey is off to play at Chloe's house."],[7,23.0,2.6,"“See ya, Bluey,” calls Bingo,"],[7,25.6,1.72,"then turns to Mum."],[7,27.32,2.8,"“Will you play with me until Bluey gets back?”"],[7,30.12,3.4,"But Mum can't because there's a problem with the toilet"],[7,33.52,1.44,"she has to fix."],[7,34.96,2.6,"Have fun!"],[7,37.56,3.04,"“Who am I going to play with?” asks Bingo."],[7,40.6,2.72,"“You're gonna play with Bingo?” says Mum."],[7,43.32,2.32,"“Oh, yeah,” says Bingo."],[7,49.32,2.88,"While Mum makes a start on fixing the toilet,"],[7,52.2,2.08,"Bingo skips into the playroom."],[7,54.8,1.36,"Doo-doo-doo!"],[7,56.16,1.84,"What am I going to do?"],[7,58.0,3.0,"Oh! I'll play doctor."],[7,61.36,3.0,"Next patient!"],[7,65.16,1.2,"Oh yeah,"],[7,66.36,2.48,"this won't work."],[7,68.84,3.44,"I spy with my little eye..."],[7,72.28,0.84,"Wait,"],[7,73.12,3.0,"this won't work either."],[7,76.12,1.88,"It was rug, by the way."],[7,81.8,2.92,"Bing heads outside to play the railings game,"],[7,84.72,3.6,"but her leg gets stuck and Mum has to come and help."],[7,88.32,2.64,"It happened again."],[7,90.96,3.84,"“I'm having a problem getting started,” Bingo admits."],[7,94.8,2.32,"“Maybe they're just hungry?” suggest Mum,"],[7,97.12,2.68,"so Bingo heads to the kitchen for a snack."],[7,101.28,3.72,"Ooh, raspberries and blueberries!"],[7,105.0,3.0,"Which one do I want? Hmm..."],[7,108.08,2.44,"Beep beep."],[7,110.52,1.8,"Hey, do you mind, Fridge?"],[7,112.32,1.96,"I'm trying to decide."],[7,115.52,1.0,"Gobble, gobble."],[7,116.52,1.24,"Yum!"],[7,120.0,0.72,"All right."],[7,120.72,2.36,"I'm not hungry anymore."],[7,123.08,3.48,"Now what's a game that only needs Bingo?"],[7,127.48,2.2,"Bingo tries hide-and-seek,"],[7,129.68,3.52,"but soon realises that there's no one to come and find her."],[7,134.4,3.0,"She misses Bluey,"],[7,137.4,3.52,"and Mum still can't play because of the problem with the toilet."],[7,142.0,4.4,"“Maybe I need a good problem?” Bingo wonders."],[7,146.4,2.76,"“That's it, Bingo!” Mum cries."],[7,149.16,3.32,"“You don't need a game, you need a problem to solve.”"],[7,153.84,2.28,"When Dad returns from dropping off Bluey,"],[7,156.12,2.64,"Mum asks him if he has a problem for Bingo."],[7,159.24,2.84,"“Yeah, I do actually,” says Dad."],[7,162.08,1.56,"On the way to Chloe's house,"],[7,163.64,2.8,"Bluey wanted to know what color kiwis are."],[7,166.44,3.28,"You know, the little furry animals that live in New Zealand?"],[7,169.72,2.36,"Do you know, Bingo?"],[7,172.08,3.88,"“No, but I know how to find out!” she replies,"],[7,175.96,2.08,"running out of the room."],[7,178.04,2.32,"“Thanks,” says Mum to Dad."],[7,180.36,3.0,"“Now, can you fix that cracked tile for me?”"],[7,184.36,2.84,"I know what I need for Dad's problem."],[7,187.2,3.0,"The world map puzzle!"],[7,192.76,3.6,"Okay, I'll tip out the pieces and put the colours together."],[7,197.4,3.0,"All the blue bits here and the red bit here."],[7,201.84,1.84,"While Bingo works on the puzzle,"],[7,203.68,2.76,"Dad replaces the cracked floor tile..."],[7,206.44,2.48,"...and Mum works out which bit of the toilet"],[7,208.92,1.4,"flush connects to what."],[7,211.68,3.0,"Finished!"],[7,214.76,1.0,"Wait,"],[7,215.76,2.0,"not finished."],[7,221.12,4.04,"“Oh yeah, you're missing New Zealand,” says Mum."],[7,225.16,2.16,"Oh, not again!"],[7,227.32,2.56,"“No problem, I can find it.”"],[7,229.88,3.48,"Bingo wags her tail and starts to search."],[7,233.48,1.84,"New Zealand?"],[7,235.32,2.32,"Where are you, New Zealand?"],[7,237.64,3.84,"I know. I'll draw some missing posters for New Zealand."],[7,245.92,3.0,"Bleurrrgh!"],[7,250.6,1.08,"Hello, Fridge."],[7,251.68,2.88,"I want to stick a New Zealand poster on you."],[7,254.56,3.12,"Beep twice If we can be friends."],[7,257.68,1.76,"Beep, beep."],[7,259.44,1.24,"Thanks, Fridge."],[7,262.6,2.04,"When Bluey gets back from Chloe's house,"],[7,264.64,2.2,"she gives Bingo a big hug."],[7,267.48,3.88,"“Bingo, you're missing a puzzle piece!” says Bluey,"],[7,271.36,1.96,"holding up one of the posters."],[7,274.44,4.92,"“Yeah, New Zealand. Have you seen it?” Bingo asks."],[7,279.36,3.76,"“It's on your bum!” Bluey tells her."],[7,283.72,3.0,"Oh, he-he-he!"],[7,288.36,3.84,"Bingo places is the last piece to complete the world map puzzle."],[7,292.92,2.2,"“Look, Bluey,” she says."],[7,295.12,2.92,"“Dad, kiwis are brown!”"],[7,298.04,2.88,"Bluey gasps. “Hooray!”"],[7,302.64,2.68,"And Mum is finally fixed the toilet too."],[7,305.32,1.6,"Hooray!"],[7,306.92,3.72,"“Thanks, Mum! Thanks, Bingo!”"],[7,310.64,2.44,"Bingo and Mum answer together."],[7,313.08,2.28,"“No problem.”"],[7,316.6,1.2,"The end."],[8,0.04,1.08,"Hi Phil."],[8,1.12,1.08,"Hi Georgie."],[8,2.2,1.8,"I've got a question for you."],[8,4.0,3.56,"Do you own anything made of gold?"],[8,7.56,3.84,"Well, at the moment I'm wearing a lot of gold-coloured jewellery,"],[8,11.4,4.48,"but I'm not sure exactly how much of it is made of gold."],[8,15.88,3.68,"OK. I don't think I own anything made of gold."],[8,19.56,2.76,"Beyond jewellery and other personal items,"],[8,22.32,3.8,"gold is bought and sold by investors and banks."],[8,26.12,2.36,"The price of gold has always gone up and down,"],[8,28.48,4.68,"but in 2026 it reached its highest ever price."],[8,33.16,2.16,"In this episode of 6 Minute English,"],[8,35.32,3.4,"we'll be talking about how gold became a valuable investment"],[8,38.72,4.78,"and asking the question, why do people buy gold?"],[8,45.88,3.2,"Let's start with our quiz question, Georgie."],[8,49.08,5.68,"Gold is a metal that's extracted or dug up from under the ground in mines."],[8,54.76,5.84,"But how much gold has been extracted from the earth?"],[8,60.6,8.48,"Is it: a) over 50,000 tonnes, b) over 100,000 tonnes,"],[8,69.08,4.22,"or c) over 200,000 tonnes?"],[8,73.3,5.58,"Phil, I have absolutely no idea, but I'm going to go with c) 200,000 tonnes."],[8,78.88,3.66,"OK. Well, we'll give you the answer at the end of the programme."],[8,82.54,4.28,"OK Phil. So, we've been using gold for thousands of years,"],[8,86.82,2.0,"but why and how did it start?"],[8,88.82,5.64,"Well, before money was paper or plastic or just numbers in your bank account,"],[8,94.46,2.52,"a lot of it was actually gold."],[8,96.98,2.56,"And experts have discovered gold"],[8,99.54,4.76,"in coins that are thought to have been made in the 6th Century BC."],[8,104.3,4.64,"So, gold coins were made and they were used because the metal was"],[8,108.94,4.04,"much easier to carry and trade than things like cattle or timber."],[8,112.98,5.32,"OK. But now our money isn't literally made of gold, so why is gold valuable?"],[8,118.3,3.12,"It's a good question, but even though it isn't money,"],[8,121.42,2.72,"gold is still a financial asset,"],[8,124.14,4.16,"and an asset is something valuable that could be sold for money."],[8,128.3,4.48,"And people buy gold in times of crisis because it's thought of"],[8,132.78,1.36,"as a safe investment."],[8,134.14,3.36,"Let's listen to expert Suki Cooper explaining more"],[8,137.5,2.48,"to the BBC World Service."],[8,139.98,4.08,"Gold is a safe haven because it's not connected to one particular government"],[8,144.06,3.8,"or one particular economy or one particular market,"],[8,147.86,2.0,"so the fact that it's neutral means"],[8,149.86,3.76,"it's an asset that's perceived to be a store of value over a longer term."],[8,153.62,4.82,"Suki describes gold as a safe haven, and a safe haven means something"],[8,158.44,3.94,"or somewhere that's protected from harm or danger,"],[8,162.38,1.34,"or in financial terms,"],[8,163.72,1.9,"from a fall in value."],[8,165.62,4.04,"Yes, Suki explains that, unlike dollars or pounds for example,"],[8,169.66,2.92,"gold is not connected to a particular country."],[8,172.58,1.4,"It's neutral."],[8,173.98,3.76,"And Georgie, because we get gold from mines,"],[8,177.74,1.92,"there's more or less a fixed amount."],[8,179.66,2.96,"Unlike paper money, you can't just print more,"],[8,182.62,4.56,"as Catherine Schenk, professor of economic and social history, explains."],[8,187.18,3.64,"It can't be created and fabricated out of thin air through alchemy,"],[8,190.82,2.48,"and so this, sort of, controls the supply"],[8,193.3,4.02,"and sustains an underlying value behind the demand for gold."],[8,197.32,3.96,"Catherine explains that gold can't be created out of thin air,"],[8,201.28,3.34,"an expression meaning making something from nothing."],[8,204.62,4.1,"As Catherine says, we don't make gold through alchemy."],[8,208.72,5.0,"Alchemy is an ancient kind of chemistry that attempted to do impossible things,"],[8,213.72,2.84,"like turn ordinary metals into gold."],[8,216.56,3.8,"And because we can't just magically make more gold,"],[8,220.36,5.1,"when lots of people want to buy gold, the price increases."],[8,225.46,1.98,"OK. Understood."],[8,227.44,1.96,"Now, Phil, at the start of this programme,"],[8,229.4,4.8,"you said that gold prices hit an all-time high in early 2026."],[8,234.2,1.6,"So, what's going on?"],[8,235.8,3.48,"Well, we mentioned that the price of gold increases"],[8,239.28,4.76,"in times of crisis because government banks want to buy more of it."],[8,244.04,2.08,"And there has been a series"],[8,246.12,4.0,"of world events that have made government banks want to buy more gold,"],[8,250.12,3.52,"for example the wars in Ukraine and the Middle East,"],[8,253.64,1.4,"and in politics too,"],[8,255.04,3.98,"for example the US imposing tariffs on international products."],[8,259.02,4.18,"But since that peak in January, the price has dropped a bit."],[8,263.2,3.26,"OK. And does the fall in the price of gold mean"],[8,266.46,2.6,"that people won't want to invest in it in the future?"],[8,269.06,4.4,"No. It's likely that the price will go up again."],[8,273.46,3.56,"Experts believe that gold will always be an attractive investment"],[8,277.02,2.0,"during uncertain times."],[8,279.02,4.06,"Let's hear from market strategist John Reade."],[8,283.08,3.1,"So much of the world we live in now is ethereal."],[8,286.18,1.32,"It's... it's virtual,"],[8,287.5,1.7,"it's online, it disappears,"],[8,289.2,4.78,"whereas gold is something solid, something dependable and,"],[8,293.98,2.52,"you know, maybe something a little bit old fashioned."],[8,296.5,3.32,"And maybe it's a hankering for traditional values rather"],[8,299.82,3.04,"than what is super modern and super changeable."],[8,302.86,3.28,"John says that much of the modern world is ethereal,"],[8,306.14,1.72,"meaning it doesn't seem real."],[8,307.86,4.34,"And in this ethereal world, people have a hankering for something"],[8,312.2,2.58,"that's solid and real, like gold."],[8,314.78,5.28,"And the expression 'a hankering for' means a strong desire for something."],[8,320.06,4.1,"OK. Phil, I think it's time for the answer to your quiz question."],[8,324.16,5.96,"OK. Well, I asked how much gold has been mined from the ground to date."],[8,330.12,3.16,"I said 200,000 tonnes."],[8,333.28,2.24,"And that is the correct answer!"],[8,335.52,3.4,"OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we learnt in this programme,"],[8,338.92,1.72,"starting with 'asset'."],[8,340.64,4.92,"And an asset is something of financial value that can be sold for money."],[8,345.56,4.56,"A safe haven is something or somewhere protected from harm or loss."],[8,350.12,3.32,"To make something out of thin air is an expression"],[8,353.44,2.56,"meaning to make something from nothing."],[8,356.0,4.8,"Alchemy is a kind of ancient chemistry that attempted to do impossible things,"],[8,360.8,2.96,"like making gold from ordinary metals."],[8,363.76,3.6,"If something is ethereal, it doesn't seem real."],[8,367.36,2.4,"And if you have a hankering for something,"],[8,369.76,2.32,"you have a strong desire for it."],[8,372.08,2.36,"Once again, our six minutes are up,"],[8,374.44,2.28,"and if you have a hankering for more practice,"],[8,376.72,2.76,"test what you've learned with the worksheet on our website."],[8,379.48,2.92,"That's bbclearningenglish.com."],[8,382.4,1.92,"Thank you for joining us. Goodbye."],[8,384.32,1.06,"Goodbye!"],[9,5.28,1.48,"Bluey!"],[9,8.56,2.64,"Everyone, the Queen is coming in."],[9,15.2,1.48,"Oh, it's the Queen."],[9,16.68,2.36,"Your Majesty, over 'ere!"],[9,19.04,1.2,"Oh, hello."],[9,20.24,2.12,"Oh, I love you, Your Majesty."],[9,22.36,1.32,"Carry on."],[9,25.88,1.08,"Stop!"],[9,26.96,3.28,"Butler, the royal bottom is itchy."],[9,30.24,1.8,"I demand you scratch it."],[9,33.52,2.2,"Oh, yes. That's the spot."],[9,35.72,0.92,"Very good."],[9,36.64,3.0,"Now, I demand to sit on the throne."],[9,39.8,1.76,"Hee-hee! Yes, my queen."],[9,43.08,2.0,"Eh! Ugh! Oopsie!"],[9,45.08,1.92,"Oh, let me get that, Your Majesty."],[9,47.0,3.52,"Ugh! The queen is having some trouble!"],[9,51.32,1.4,"Ah, splendid!"],[9,52.72,2.12,"(CLAPS) Now… go away."],[9,54.84,1.32,"Yes, my queen!"],[9,58.48,1.72,"Come here! (BLUEY GIGGLES)"],[9,60.2,3.08,"- Yes, Your Maj… - Ooh! Excuse me!"],[9,63.28,2.76,"You may not stand on the royal rainbow thing."],[9,66.04,2.4,"Oh, I'm terribly sorry, Your Majesty."],[9,68.44,1.56,"Cheeky butler."],[9,70.4,2.52,"Hee-hee! Is this OK, Your Majesty?"],[9,74.04,0.84,"Yes."],[9,74.88,1.84,"How can I serve you, my queen?"],[9,76.72,2.08,"I'm going to sing a song."],[9,78.8,1.8,"You will write it down."],[9,80.6,1.2,"Ooh, OK!"],[9,81.84,1.04,"Ahem!"],[9,82.88,3.56,"# I am so beautiful!"],[9,86.44,3.48,"# I am so lovely!"],[9,89.92,3.36,"# Ooh, and sparkly! #"],[9,93.28,1.64,"Now, sing it back to me."],[9,95.44,0.76,"Ahem!"],[9,96.2,3.24,"# I am so beautiful! # - Louder!"],[9,99.44,2.08,"- # I am so… # - Louder!"],[9,101.52,2.84,"#..lovely and sparkly! #"],[9,104.8,0.8,"Very good."],[9,105.6,3.56,"(CLAPPING) Oh, our queen is so lovely and sparkly!"],[10,0.04,3.12,"Are you embarrassed about your pronunciation?"],[10,3.16,4.07,"Are you worried you sound a bit robotic?"],[10,7.23,0.85,"Don't worry."],[10,8.08,3.32,"In this lesson, we're going to practice shadowing:"],[10,11.4,7.44,"a powerful way to improve your fluency, pronunciation, and listening."],[10,18.84,6.72,"You'll do this by listening to a short story about how I got trapped in a lift,"],[10,25.56,2.92,"and how I escaped again."],[10,28.48,4.88,"This is a shadowing lesson, which means you'll listen carefully"],[10,33.36,4.2,"and repeat what I say, almost at the same time."],[10,37.56,2.84,"Like an echo."],[10,40.4,1.1,"Here's how."],[10,41.5,5.5,"It was my first day at work and I was the last one to finish."],[10,47.0,3.8,"Click here to find out more about shadowing"],[10,50.8,4.4,"and why it's useful for your speaking, listening, and pronunciation."],[10,55.2,2.47,"Right. Let's start the story."],[10,61.24,5.12,"It was my first day at work, and I was the last one to finish."],[10,66.36,5.64,"I'd just got in the lift when I noticed there weren't any buttons."],[10,72.92,1.48,"Before I could react,"],[10,74.4,3.08,"the doors slammed shut."],[10,77.48,5.4,"I desperately tried to open them again, but they wouldn't budge."],[10,82.88,3.72,"Let's have a look at the pronunciation in more detail."],[10,86.6,1.64,"First day at work."],[10,88.24,4.64,"Can you hear a sound at the end of 'first' in this phrase?"],[10,92.88,1.48,"Listen again."],[10,94.36,2.24,"First day at work."],[10,96.6,2.04,"First day at work."],[10,98.64,1.36,"There isn't one."],[10,100.0,3.28,"Look at the letters on either side of that 't'."],[10,103.28,5.55,"We have an 's' and a 'd', which are pronounced /s/ and /d/."],[10,108.83,2.69,"These are both consonant sounds."],[10,111.52,5.6,"When a 't' is between two consonant sounds, we don't pronounce it."],[10,117.12,3.4,"Let's call this a 'consonant sandwich'."],[10,120.52,3.44,"We have consonant sandwiches in other phrases too."],[10,123.96,2.24,"Repeat after me."],[10,126.2,2.32,"First day at work."],[10,132.4,2.56,"I'd just got in the lift."],[10,139.36,1.92,"Wouldn't budge."],[10,145.92,1.96,"Last one."],[10,151.76,1.57,"Let's continue with the story."],[10,154.52,4.76,"After a while, the lights began to dim."],[10,159.28,5.64,"I desperately banged on the doors, but there was no one around."],[10,164.92,4.48,"A few hours had passed and it was almost ten o'clock."],[10,169.4,2.68,"I was at a complete loss."],[10,172.08,4.84,"I've highlighted the 't' in 'it' and 'at' in these phrases."],[10,176.92,1.4,"Listen carefully."],[10,178.32,2.16,"What sound can you hear?"],[10,180.48,2.68,"It was almost ten o'clock."],[10,183.16,2.6,"I was at a complete loss."],[10,185.76,8.24,"Instead of a /t/ sound, it's a glottal stop, which is the sound you can hear in the middle of this phrase."],[10,195.0,2.48,"Uh-oh!"],[10,197.48,6.04,"We make it by suddenly blocking the air in our throat and then releasing it."],[10,203.52,6.96,"So instead of 'it', I make the vowel sound /ɪ/, but I cut it short in my throat."],[10,210.48,5.32,"/ɪ/. This is very common with short words that end with 't'."],[10,215.8,3.36,"We also have another example with 'at'."],[10,219.16,8.24,"These sound very similar, so let's practice them both. /ɪ/. /ɪ/. /æ/. /æ/."],[10,227.4,1.88,"Got that?"],[10,229.28,1.72,"Let's finish the story."],[10,231.52,4.36,"\"Why won't you open?\" I shouted in despair."],[10,235.88,4.44,"\"Opening doors\", replied a robotic-sounding voice."],[10,240.32,2.52,"The doors wooshed open."],[10,242.84,4.56,"I grabbed my things and ran out as fast as I could."],[10,247.4,4.2,"As I glanced behind me, a sign above the doors caught my eye."],[10,251.6,4.04,"It read: \"voice-activated lift\"."],[10,255.64,3.44,"Listen again to the 'e-d' endings in these words."],[10,259.08,4.12,"Is the pronunciation the same, or different?"],[10,263.2,6.6,"Shouted. Wooshed. Grabbed."],[10,269.8,4.96,"We have three different ways to pronounce 'e-d'."],[10,274.76,1.88,"/ɪd/."],[10,276.64,1.96,"/t/."],[10,278.6,2.04,"/d/."],[10,280.64,1.92,"Let's start with 'shouted'."],[10,282.56,5.36,"When a verb ends with a 'd' or a 't' like 'shout' or 'need',"],[10,287.92,3.08,"the '-ed' ending is pronounced as /ɪd/."],[10,291.0,7.52,"So we say: 'shouted', 'started', 'needed'."],[10,298.52,4.16,"Those last two weren't in the story: they were just examples."],[10,302.68,2.32,"But how about 'wooshed'?"],[10,305.0,3.84,"If a verb ends with an unvoiced consonant sound,"],[10,308.84,7.84,"meaning you can't feel a vibration in your throat, so sounds like /ʃ/ or /s/"],[10,316.68,11.28,"We pronounce the 'ed' ending as a /t/, so we say 'wooshed', 'glanced', passed'."],[10,327.96,4.04,"And lastly we have 'grabbed'."],[10,332.0,3.04,"If a verb ends with a voiced consonant,"],[10,335.04,5.64,"meaning we can feel a vibration in our throat when we say it, like /b/"],[10,340.68,4.92,"and /ŋ/, the '-ed' is pronounced as /d/."],[10,345.6,5.72,"This also applies to vowel sounds like the /aɪ/ in 'reply'."],[10,351.32,1.8,"Repeat after me."],[10,353.12,7.08,"Replied. Grabbed. Banged."],[10,360.2,1.88,"With all of that in mind,"],[10,362.08,4.12,"It's time to shadow the entire story again."],[10,366.2,4.08,"Let's see if you can improve your pronunciation."],[10,372.84,5.24,"It was my first day at work and I was the last one to finish."],[10,378.08,6.56,"I'd just got in the lift when I noticed there weren't any buttons."],[10,384.64,1.44,"Before I could react,"],[10,386.08,3.04,"the doors slammed shut."],[10,389.12,5.84,"I desperately tried to open them again, but they wouldn't budge."],[10,394.96,4.84,"After a while, the lights began to dim."],[10,399.8,5.2,"I desperately banged on the doors, but there was no one around."],[10,405.36,4.52,"A few hours had passed and it was almost ten o'clock."],[10,409.88,3.24,"I was at a complete loss."],[10,413.12,2.4,"\"Why won't you open?\""],[10,415.52,1.96,"I shouted in despair."],[10,417.48,4.52,"\"Opening doors\", replied a robotic-sounding voice."],[10,422.0,2.52,"The doors wooshed open."],[10,424.52,4.48,"I grabbed my things and ran out as fast as I could."],[10,429.0,4.16,"As I glanced behind me, a sign above the doors caught my eye."],[10,433.16,3.88,"It read 'voice-activated lift'."],[10,437.04,2.08,"How was that for you?"],[10,439.12,2.96,"Was your pronunciation better the second time?"],[10,442.08,5.68,"Remember, you can download a free audio file of this story by clicking here."],[10,447.76,6.08,"And if you want more information on the pronunciation of '-ed' endings, click here."],[10,453.84,2.44,"Have you ever been stuck somewhere?"],[10,456.28,1.8,"How did you get out?"],[10,458.08,5.88,"Let us know in the comments - and see you in the next video! Bye bye!"],[11,6.2,1.96,"Bluey: Granny Mobile,"],[11,8.16,2.28,"read by me, Sia."],[11,12.72,2.28,"Oh, my coins!"],[11,18.24,2.48,"As Bluey, Bingo, Muffin, Mum and Dad"],[11,20.72,1.48,"drive into their street,"],[11,22.2,2.28,"they see something exciting."],[11,24.48,1.24,"\"Oh, look!\""],[11,25.72,2.96,"Doreen is having a garage sale."],[11,28.68,1.4,"\"Ooh!\" \"Ooh!\" \"Ooh!\""],[11,30.72,1.56,"\"Ahh!\" \"Ahh!\" \"Ahh!\""],[11,32.28,1.8,"The kids spot a…"],[11,34.08,2.08,"granny mobile!"],[11,39.6,1.88,"Doreen explains to Mum and Dad"],[11,41.48,2.04,"that she's trying to get a bit of cash together"],[11,43.52,2.2,"to go on a holiday to the reef."],[11,45.72,4.08,"\"Well, if someone buys Len's old scooter, that'll help.\""],[11,49.8,1.8,"\"Oh, yeah!\""],[11,52.56,3.52,"Meanwhile, the kids head over to play Granny Mobile."],[11,56.08,4.72,"\"Okay, let's decide what sort of grannies we'll be,\" says Bluey."],[11,60.8,3.24,"\"I'm going to be a can't-hear-anything granny.\""],[11,64.04,2.68,"\"I'm going to be a kind granny.\""],[11,66.72,2.48,"\"What sort of granny can I be?\""],[11,70.08,4.0,"\"Oh, I'm not sure there are any other types of grannies,\" replies Bluey."],[11,75.16,2.24,"\"It's not fair!"],[11,77.4,5.84,"I knew I wouldn't get to be a granny!\" Muffin yells, stomping her foot."],[11,84.72,4.68,"\"Oh, that's it, you can be a grouchy granny!\" says Bluey."],[11,89.88,2.48,"\"Oh, yeah!\" replies Muffin."],[11,92.36,3.36,"\"And make it that you want to sell us your granny mobile,\" finishes Bluey."],[11,96.36,1.92,"\"Let's go, Bingo!\""],[11,98.28,1.96,"\"Pardon?\""],[11,101.2,3.8,"At the garage sale, Doreen is selling things too cheaply."],[11,105.52,3.0,"\"How much do you want for this? Two dollars?\""],[11,108.6,2.16,"\"Okay, that's fine.\""],[11,111.6,3.96,"\"Doreen! That vase is worth way more than that,\" says Mum."],[11,115.56,3.32,"\"Oh, I don't want to make a fuss,\" Doreen replies."],[11,122.64,5.4,"\"Doreen, can we play with your granny mobile, please?\" Bluey asks."],[11,128.04,1.72,"\"Go for it!\" she says."],[11,129.76,3.8,"\"I've taken the batteries out, so it won't move.\""],[11,133.56,4.4,"\"Oh, yes it will,\" growls Grouchy Granny, poking Dad."],[11,137.96,2.2,"\"Buggalugs is going to push it.\""],[11,140.16,1.96,"\"Am I Buggalugs?\""],[11,143.16,2.76,"\"Faster!\" \"Yes, Nana.\""],[11,145.92,2.6,"\"And you're going over too many cracks!\""],[11,148.52,3.52,"\"Ow! Gee, you are one grouchy granny.\""],[11,153.12,4.12,"\"Stop!\" orders Grouchy Granny when she sees the other grannies."],[11,157.24,1.36,"\"He-he!\""],[11,161.28,4.24,"\"Hello, Gladys,\" says Kind Granny to Grouchy Granny."],[11,165.52,2.08,"\"I'm selling my granny mobile."],[11,167.6,2.88,"Are you going to buy it?\" Gladys snaps."],[11,170.48,1.88,"\"How much is it, love?\""],[11,172.36,2.6,"\"Buggalugs! What number is this?\""],[11,174.96,2.0,"The sign says $300,"],[11,176.96,2.2,"but Buggalugs knows Doreen can get"],[11,179.16,2.04,"more than that for her scooter."],[11,181.44,3.6,"\"Let's say nine-hundred!\" he replies."],[11,186.36,1.76,"\"How much money you got, Rita?\""],[11,188.12,4.12,"asks Kind Granny to Can't-Hear-Anything Granny."],[11,192.24,2.48,"\"Hurry up, my hips hurt!\""],[11,195.44,3.12,"\"Oneeee…"],[11,198.56,2.48,"Twoooo…"],[11,201.8,1.72,"Oh, that's a nice coin…\""],[11,203.52,1.6,"\"Hurry up!\""],[11,205.64,3.48,"\"Ahhh! Oh, my coins!\""],[11,209.12,1.96,"\"This is taking too long!"],[11,211.08,2.8,"Buggerlugs, take me home.\""],[11,213.88,1.52,"\"Yes, Nana.\""],[11,216.36,3.48,"\"Bluey, Grouchy Granny is a bit too grouchy,\""],[11,219.84,2.76,"says Muffin, turning to Bluey."],[11,222.6,3.0,"\"Are you sure there isn't another type of granny?"],[11,226.0,3.88,"\"There might be, but I just don't know what it is."],[11,229.88,1.12,"Do you?\""],[11,231.52,1.88,"Muffin isn't sure."],[11,233.4,2.04,"\"Hmm… no."],[11,235.44,2.76,"I'll stay Grouchy Granny,\" she replies."],[11,239.96,4.92,"Meanwhile, Doreen is still having trouble getting enough dollarbucks."],[11,245.48,2.88,"\"Hmm. How about…"],[11,248.36,1.84,"five dollars?\""],[11,250.2,1.92,"\"How about two dollars?\""],[11,252.12,4.08,"\"Okay, two dollars,\" accepts Doreen."],[11,256.2,3.04,"\"Doreen, if you want to go on a holiday to the reef,"],[11,259.24,2.72,"you better toughen up,\" says Mum."],[11,261.96,1.92,"\"Oh, I know!\""],[11,266.56,2.2,"\"Whoa!\" someone yells."],[11,268.76,4.16,"A granny bumps into a table and knocks over a teacup."],[11,272.92,2.56,"\"I could've broke my leg!\""],[11,275.48,3.0,"\"Oh, I'm terribly sorry,\" says Doreen."],[11,279.04,2.04,"\"I should call the council!\""],[11,282.0,2.36,"\"I'll get it, Doreen,\" offers Mum."],[11,284.36,2.44,"She picks up the broken pieces."],[11,286.8,2.08,"\"Keep the noise down!\""],[11,289.92,3.0,"\"I'm not paying for any of this!\" snaps Old Pug."],[11,293.52,3.0,"\"Oh, well, that's fine,\" says Doreen."],[11,297.36,3.6,"\"Well, I think you should pay a little,\" says Mum to Old Pug."],[11,301.8,2.56,"\"I think you should mind your own business!\""],[11,304.36,2.24,"\"Whoa, that is one grouchy granny!\""],[11,311.24,2.08,"\"We're back home, Nana.\""],[11,313.32,2.12,"\"About time!\""],[11,316.08,3.0,"Old Pug runs into Gladys and Buggalugs."],[11,319.24,4.92,"\"Hey, you! Get off that! I'm buying it!\" orders Old Pug."],[11,324.16,4.48,"\"Fine! Give me nine-hundred dollars,\" says Gladys."],[11,330.28,3.48,"\"What?! The sign is three-hundred dollars!\" yells Old Pug."],[11,333.76,1.96,"Gladys chucks the sign away."],[11,335.72,3.0,"\"Nine-hundred dollars!\""],[11,339.56,3.72,"\"I'm not paying nine-hundred dollars!\" Old Pug huffs."],[11,343.92,3.84,"\"Good, then I keep it,\" says Gladys, crossing her arms."],[11,347.76,2.36,"Dad tries to calm down the grannies,"],[11,350.12,2.68,"but Old Pug hits Dad with her handbag."],[11,353.12,3.6,"\"Oh, look, love, I think you want to talk to that la… Ow!"],[11,356.72,1.08,"What are you doing?\""],[11,358.08,2.08,"\"Get your hands off me!\""],[11,360.72,3.08,"Doreen and Mum come to see what the fuss is about."],[11,364.08,6.12,"\"Doreen, I think this delightful lady wants to buy your scooter,\" says Dad."],[11,370.2,4.16,"\"One-hundred dollars! Take it or leave it!\" Old Pug snaps."],[11,374.6,3.88,"\"Um, I guess that sounds okay…\""],[11,379.16,2.28,"\"Oh, Doreen, no.\""],[11,381.76,3.64,"\"Right. You, off!\" says Old Pug to Gladys."],[11,385.4,2.44,"But Gladys refuses to move."],[11,388.08,4.44,"\"No! I told you nine-hundred dollars!\""],[11,392.72,1.52,"\"What?"],[11,395.04,2.72,"Two-hundred dollars. Final offer!\""],[11,397.76,1.72,"\"Ten-hundred!\""],[11,399.48,2.84,"\"What? You're dreaming! Five-hundred dollars.\""],[11,402.32,1.52,"\"Eleven!\""],[11,403.88,1.8,"\"Outrageous!\""],[11,405.68,3.0,"\"If you don't want it, then don't buy it!\""],[11,409.08,2.8,"\"I'm not paying eleven-hundred dollars!\""],[11,411.88,1.56,"\"Why not?\""],[11,414.0,2.88,"\"'Cause it's ugly, like her hat!\""],[11,416.88,1.8,"\"Hey, hey!\""],[11,419.56,1.32,"\"And another thing –"],[11,420.88,2.0,"I don't like the colour.\""],[11,422.88,2.28,"\"Well, paint it!\" \"No, you paint it!\""],[11,425.44,1.88,"\"No, you paint it!\""],[11,430.28,3.0,"Gladys pushes the price up one more time."],[11,433.32,2.52,"\"Twelve-hundred!\""],[11,435.84,2.12,"\"Oh, you cheeky little…"],[11,439.36,2.16,"Fine,\" Old Pug sighs."],[11,441.52,1.4,"\"Twelve-hundred dollars.\""],[11,442.92,2.64,"\"And eighteen lollies!\""],[11,445.56,2.32,"\"Oh my goodness!\""],[11,449.04,3.0,"Old Pug rides off with her new granny mobile."],[11,452.72,2.28,"\"Hooray!\""],[11,455.0,1.88,"\"Pfffft!\""],[11,458.16,3.0,"\"You'll be going on that holiday now, Doreen!\""],[11,461.28,1.56,"\"Yeah!\""],[11,463.32,3.32,"\"Muffin, look around,\" Doreen says."],[11,466.64,2.52,"\"You can pick anything you want.\""],[11,469.72,2.88,"\"Really?\" asks Muffin."],[11,472.6,3.0,"\"Yep. You are one in a million!\""],[11,476.28,1.8,"Muffin chooses a tea towel."],[11,479.28,2.76,"Rita drops her coins again, but…"],[11,482.04,2.24,"\"Oh, my coins!\""],[11,484.28,3.0,"\"Da da da da da da dah!\""],[11,488.12,3.4,"\"Oh, look, Rita! It's a super granny!\""],[11,491.6,2.6,"\"May I help you with those coins?\""],[11,494.2,1.6,"\"Pardon?\""],[11,497.04,2.92,"It's Dad's turn to buy something from Doreen."],[11,499.96,2.6,"\"Twenty bucks for this exercise thingy?\""],[11,502.6,1.64,"\"Fifty!\""],[11,504.92,2.16,"\"That's my girl.\""],[11,507.68,2.0,"The end."],[12,0.0,0.88,"Hi Georgie."],[12,0.88,0.88,"Hi Neil."],[12,1.76,4.84,"Now, I want you to think back to the last time you had your heart broken."],[12,6.6,1.2,"How did it feel?"],[12,7.8,1.48,"It was a couple of years ago."],[12,9.28,3.8,"And I just remember a really intense, sad emotion."],[12,13.08,1.68,"But it also sometimes feels"],[12,14.76,2.56,"like a physical pain in your stomach, doesn't it?"],[12,17.32,2.36,"It does. It's like real pain, isn't it?"],[12,19.68,3.4,"And today, in 6 Minute English, we're talking about heartbreak."],[12,23.08,2.68,"Now, heartbreak is the feeling of sadness"],[12,25.76,2.16,"we get when we lose someone we love."],[12,27.92,3.72,"And that pain you feel, it's not just imaginary."],[12,31.64,3.68,"In this podcast, we'll be understanding how our brains change"],[12,35.32,2.64,"when we fall in love and asking the question,"],[12,37.96,2.8,"why does heartbreak hurt so much?"],[12,40.76,3.76,"And as usual, we'll be learning some useful new vocabulary."],[12,44.52,4.72,"You can find the full list and the definitions at bbclearningenglish.com."],[12,51.28,2.8,"Georgie, we're going to start with a quiz question. Are you ready?"],[12,54.08,1.72,"I am very ready."],[12,55.8,6.0,"So, in the 1950s, psychologists noticed a link between children's early bonds"],[12,61.8,3.92,"with their parents and their romantic relationships as adults,"],[12,65.72,2.44,"but what is this theory called?"],[12,68.16,4.64,"Is it: a) contraction theory, b) attachment theory,"],[12,72.8,2.2,"or c) superstition theory?"],[12,75.0,2.76,"I think it's b) attachment theory."],[12,77.76,3.2,"OK. Well, we will find out at the end of the programme."],[12,80.96,3.36,"What's going on in our brains when we fall in love?"],[12,84.32,3.52,"Well, scientists have scanned people's brains while they're looking"],[12,87.84,2.48,"at a photo of their romantic partner."],[12,90.32,3.32,"And they found that looking at someone you're in love with"],[12,93.64,5.32,"activates a part of the brain that also reminds us to eat and drink."],[12,98.96,4.36,"And do scientists know why being in love impacts our brain in this way?"],[12,103.32,1.92,"So, it's all about survival."],[12,105.24,2.68,"Our brains have evolved to be this way."],[12,107.92,4.44,"Dr Lucy Brown explains more to the BBC World Service."],[12,112.36,1.68,"This is a survival system."],[12,114.04,5.0,"Nature gave us these systems to be addicted to each other –"],[12,119.04,3.44,"for reproduction, for protection."],[12,122.48,3.48,"You're oriented completely toward the other person."],[12,125.96,2.8,"It's like hunger or thirst."],[12,128.76,4.2,"Lucy says our brains make us addicted to our romantic partners."],[12,132.96,2.92,"If you're addicted to something, you want more of it."],[12,135.88,3.84,"You can literally be addicted to things like drugs or alcohol,"],[12,139.72,4.04,"but we also use it metaphorically to talk about things we love."],[12,143.76,4.04,"She says that romantic partners are oriented to each other, which"],[12,147.8,2.32,"in British English we say 'orientated',"],[12,150.12,2.92,"and it means they are pointed towards each other."],[12,153.04,2.92,"They think of each other before other people."],[12,155.96,2.8,"Neil, is there anything else scientists have discovered"],[12,158.76,2.6,"from scanning the brains of people in love?"],[12,161.36,3.56,"Well, being in love doesn't just change the way we think."],[12,164.92,3.2,"It changes our whole identity."],[12,168.12,3.28,"A 2013 study showed us more about this."],[12,171.4,5.04,"People were shown statements about themselves – for example, Neil is kind –"],[12,176.44,4.76,"and the part of the brain we use to think about ourselves lit up."],[12,181.2,3.24,"When showing the same statements about their partner,"],[12,184.44,2.24,"the same part of the brain was active –"],[12,186.68,4.76,"not the part of the brain we generally use to think about others."],[12,191.44,3.4,"So, Neil, are all of these brain changes that come with being"],[12,194.84,1.92,"in love actually good for us?"],[12,196.76,4.4,"Well, studies suggest that good social connections help manage stress"],[12,201.16,2.2,"and support our immune system."],[12,203.36,4.52,"Professor Zoe Donaldson explained more to the BBC World Service."],[12,207.88,3.12,"Having really strong social bonds increases your likelihood"],[12,211.0,1.76,"that you'll survive from just about anything,"],[12,212.76,3.48,"whether that be cancer, depression, etc."],[12,216.24,3.68,"And it overall is the number one predictor of longevity."],[12,219.92,2.76,"So, if you are lonely and you don't have bonds,"],[12,222.68,3.0,"then your mortality risk goes up in a way"],[12,225.68,2.52,"that's equivalent to being obese or smoking."],[12,228.2,4.72,"So, having strong social bonds or close relationships with other people"],[12,232.92,3.8,"makes you more likely to survive disease, according to Zoe."],[12,236.72,4.68,"Yes, it's an important factor in our longevity, which means how long we live."],[12,241.4,3.08,"In fact, Zoe says, being lonely and not having"],[12,244.48,4.4,"good social bonds increases your mortality risk, your risk of dying,"],[12,248.88,3.04,"by as much as being obese or smoking."],[12,251.92,2.64,"OK. So, what happens when these bonds are broken,"],[12,254.56,1.68,"when we experience heartbreak?"],[12,256.24,2.0,"Well, scientists have been looking at this"],[12,258.24,4.76,"by asking people to look at photos of their exes, their former partners,"],[12,263.0,1.84,"and scanning their brains."],[12,264.84,3.64,"They found that heartbreak activates parts of the brain we use"],[12,268.48,2.44,"when we're in danger or in pain."],[12,270.92,2.92,"Professor Zoe Donaldson explains more."],[12,273.84,2.08,"It's literal pain in your brain."],[12,275.92,3.44,"So, if you look at the parts of the brain that light up, they overlap"],[12,279.36,4.52,"with the same brain regions that are activated when you feel visceral pain –"],[12,283.88,2.2,"when you feel actual, you know... like you get a burn,"],[12,286.08,1.12,"or you break a..."],[12,287.2,1.4,"you break a bone."],[12,288.6,4.64,"OK. So, now it's time for the answer to the quiz question I asked you."],[12,293.24,4.08,"It was about a theory developed by psychologists in the 1950s,"],[12,297.32,2.56,"which says that children's early relationships"],[12,299.88,4.68,"with their parents impact their relationships as adults."],[12,304.56,1.64,"What's the name of the theory?"],[12,306.2,2.48,"I was quite confident about this, wasn't I, Neil?"],[12,308.68,1.84,"I said attachment theory."],[12,310.52,3.72,"Well, you had a reason to be confident because you are correct."],[12,314.24,1.2,"Yay! OK."],[12,315.44,3.04,"Let's recap the vocabulary we learnt in this programme,"],[12,318.48,3.28,"starting with 'heartbreak', which is the feeling you experience"],[12,321.76,2.16,"after you lose someone you love."],[12,323.92,2.08,"If you're addicted to someone or something,"],[12,326.0,2.36,"you want more of it all the time."],[12,328.36,4.76,"'Oriented' or 'orientated towards' is used metaphorically to mean"],[12,333.12,3.36,"that someone or something is your main focus."],[12,336.48,2.88,"Social bonds are the close relationships we have"],[12,339.36,2.88,"with family, friends and romantic partners."],[12,342.24,2.48,"'Longevity' means how long you'll live."],[12,344.72,4.2,"And an ex is a past romantic partner that you are no longer"],[12,348.92,1.52,"in a relationship with."],[12,350.44,1.84,"Once again, our six minutes are up,"],[12,352.28,3.16,"but you can test what you've learned with the worksheet on our website."],[12,355.44,2.8,"Go to bbclearningenglish.com."],[12,358.24,1.68,"Thanks for joining us and goodbye."],[12,359.92,1.52,"Goodbye."],[13,0.12,0.56,"Hello Neil."],[13,0.68,1.4,"Hi Georgie. How are you?"],[13,2.08,1.6,"I'm very well, thank you. How are you?"],[13,3.68,1.24,"I'm excellent, thank you."],[13,4.92,4.24,"Good. Neil, have you ever had a bad or difficult job?"],[13,9.16,4.84,"Oh yes. There's one in particular that I remember."],[13,14.0,1.56,"Ooh, that's intriguing."],[13,15.56,2.84,"I'm sure we're going to hear about it a bit later"],[13,18.4,1.92,"because that's what we're talking about today"],[13,20.32,4.16,"in Real Easy English – bad or difficult jobs we've had."],[13,24.48,4.4,"Yes, and people should remember that they can get a free worksheet"],[13,28.88,3.24,"for this podcast at bbclearningenglish.com."],[13,32.12,1.52,"That's right."],[13,40.16,4.6,"So Neil, tell us all about your bad or difficult job."],[13,44.76,11.32,"Well, what happened was I found a job picking beans on a student holiday"],[13,56.08,1.52,"while I was in Canada."],[13,57.6,1.88,"Ooh. On a farm?"],[13,59.48,3.12,"Yeah, but we had to travel a long way."],[13,62.6,4.52,"And when we got there, we discovered that you got paid"],[13,67.12,2.76,"by the amount that you picked."],[13,69.88,1.36,"OK."],[13,71.24,3.28,"And it cost $10 to get to this place on the coach"],[13,74.52,2.36,"and we worked really hard all day."],[13,76.88,2.92,"And then I got my beans and weighed them."],[13,79.8,2.92,"And I got paid $8."],[13,82.72,0.84,"Oh wow."],[13,83.56,1.4,"Was it a full day's work?"],[13,84.96,4.52,"Oh yeah. Hard, hard work in the heat, in a field."],[13,89.48,1.44,"Was it fun, at least?"],[13,90.92,1.28,"Were you with friends?"],[13,92.2,1.76,"It's a good story now..."],[13,93.96,1.4,"OK."],[13,95.36,4.4,"...but at the time, it was very annoying."],[13,99.76,0.96,"Exhausting, it sounds."],[13,100.72,0.96,"It was exhausting."],[13,101.68,2.36,"All day in the heat."],[13,104.04,4.48,"I had quite a difficult job – it was just a temporary one."],[13,108.52,6.6,"I was working in customer service, so I had to pick up calls from customers"],[13,115.12,5.4,"who were needing help or were unhappy about the service they had."],[13,120.52,1.56,"OK. That sounds interesting."],[13,122.08,3.0,"What didn't you like about this job?"],[13,125.08,5.84,"Well, the company was having a difficult time, so many or most"],[13,130.92,5.12,"of the customers that were calling were unhappy about the service,"],[13,136.04,4.72,"and so I had to talk to people who were very angry,"],[13,140.76,7.6,"and it felt like they were angry with me, even though it wasn't my fault."],[13,148.36,3.8,"The company changed the warehouse where they kept the products"],[13,152.16,3.04,"and the delivery company at the same time,"],[13,155.2,5.6,"so it was just all a big mess and I found it really stressful."],[13,160.8,2.24,"Yeah, you say stressful."],[13,163.04,2.76,"How else did it make you feel?"],[13,165.8,7.24,"It made me feel very anxious because I took it too personally."],[13,173.04,2.32,"Like I said, it felt like they were angry with me,"],[13,175.36,2.52,"even though I had nothing to do with it."],[13,177.88,4.44,"If you heard somebody else talking about this kind of job,"],[13,182.32,3.32,"what advice would you give them?"],[13,185.64,3.92,"Ooh, I would say, don't take it to heart."],[13,189.56,3.56,"As I said, I was taking it too personally,"],[13,193.12,4.0,"but you need to realise that you can only do your best."],[13,197.12,5.64,"And at the end of the day, you can go home and enjoy yourself, relax,"],[13,202.76,2.44,"and tomorrow is a new day."],[13,205.2,1.8,"That's a very positive attitude."],[13,207.0,0.84,"Yes."],[13,207.84,5.92,"What advice would you give to people doing the job that you had in Canada?"],[13,213.76,7.52,"I think it's really important to do some research before you say yes to a job,"],[13,221.28,3.4,"if you're lucky to be able to choose."],[13,224.68,6.68,"So, probably find out how much you're going to get paid, what it means,"],[13,231.36,5.16,"and how much you have to pay to get to the place to do the job."],[13,236.52,1.12,"Yeah, that's very important."],[13,237.64,1.96,"And that's some good advice."],[13,246.12,4.76,"OK. So, let's recap the vocabulary that we used in the conversation."],[13,250.88,1.04,"Good idea."],[13,251.92,3.52,"We had a few adjectives to describe work."],[13,255.44,2.68,"You said that your job was stressful."],[13,258.12,4.44,"I did. So, you can describe something as 'stressful',"],[13,262.56,3.64,"and the emotion that you feel is 'stressed'."],[13,266.2,2.84,"You said that your job was exhausting,"],[13,269.04,4.28,"so it was very hard work, so you got very tired doing it."],[13,273.32,4.48,"That's 'exhausting' and the feeling is 'exhausted'."],[13,277.8,3.72,"Yeah. And I think I also said my job was annoying."],[13,281.52,1.6,"Yes."],[13,283.12,5.24,"And if something is annoying, it makes you feel annoyed."],[13,288.36,4.24,"Yes, that's right. I also used the expression 'take something personally',"],[13,292.6,3.16,"which is when you feel that someone is angry with you"],[13,295.76,2.84,"when they're actually angry at the situation."],[13,298.6,4.4,"Yeah. And you used another expression with a similar meaning –"],[13,303.0,2.04,"'take something to heart'."],[13,305.04,4.56,"If you take something to heart, just as you explained, you feel bad."],[13,309.6,4.04,"You feel someone is attacking you, but it's just the job."],[13,313.64,3.72,"OK. Well, that is the end of this episode of Real Easy English,"],[13,317.36,2.28,"but Neil we'll be back next week, won't we?"],[13,319.64,2.0,"We will. Thank you for listening and goodbye."],[13,321.64,1.4,"Goodbye!"],[14,14.26,1.0,"BINGO I'll get it!"],[14,17.81,1.08,"BLUEY Good one, Bingo!"],[14,18.98,1.0,"BINGO Thanks, Bluey!"],[14,27.78,1.58,"Don't let it touch the ground!"],[14,32.99,0.83,"Oh, no."],[14,34.2,0.83,"Run!"],[15,0.12,0.96,"Hi Phil."],[15,1.08,0.92,"Hi Georgie."],[15,2.0,1.44,"How's your week going?"],[15,3.44,1.96,"It's going very well, thank you."],[15,5.4,1.8,"I watched a really good film last night."],[15,7.2,2.76,"Did you watch it at home or at the cinema?"],[15,9.96,4.4,"I watched it at home on my sofa with my housemates."],[15,14.36,1.32,"Oh, was it good?"],[15,15.68,1.68,"It was very good."],[15,17.36,4.04,"Now, I told you I watched a film last night at home,"],[15,21.4,4.48,"but in this episode of Real Easy English, we're talking about cinema."],[15,25.88,2.36,"So, the cinema is where you watch films,"],[15,28.24,3.32,"or in American English they're sometimes called movies."],[15,31.56,3.96,"And before we get started, let's just remind everyone that"],[15,35.52,4.56,"if you go to bbclearningenglish.com, there's a worksheet"],[15,40.08,4.44,"where you can practise all the language that you'll hear in this episode."],[15,49.16,4.88,"So, Phil, do you go to the cinema often?"],[15,54.04,4.96,"No. No, I don't go very often at all."],[15,59.0,1.04,"What about you?"],[15,60.04,3.8,"I don't go often, but I did go a couple of weeks ago."],[15,63.84,3.68,"I went to see an action film, a recent action film,"],[15,67.52,2.88,"and it was really spectacular."],[15,70.4,4.2,"And I'm not a big fan of action movies – action films."],[15,74.6,5.8,"I prefer films that are a bit less stressful,"],[15,80.4,3.48,"so I wouldn't usually watch action films at home,"],[15,83.88,3.6,"but in the cinema it was really an amazing experience"],[15,87.48,2.8,"because you have the big screen."],[15,90.28,2.44,"You have amazing sound."],[15,92.72,2.12,"And I really enjoyed it!"],[15,94.84,5.76,"I've not actually been to the cinema for maybe two years, I think."],[15,100.6,2.24,"Wow, Phil! Why don't you go to the cinema?"],[15,102.84,1.16,"Do you not like it?"],[15,104.0,3.28,"I like... I like the cinema as an event,"],[15,107.28,3.52,"so the last time I went, I went with my kids."],[15,110.8,1.28,"I can't remember what film,"],[15,112.08,3.6,"but it was a big thing for them to go and they were excited about it,"],[15,115.68,2.88,"but I don't know..."],[15,118.56,1.76,"I like the big screen."],[15,120.32,1.48,"I like the sound."],[15,121.8,1.48,"I like the event."],[15,123.28,6.04,"But if you want to spend time with people, I think in a cinema"],[15,129.32,1.88,"you can't talk to anyone."],[15,131.2,1.32,"You sit in the dark."],[15,132.52,2.6,"You can't talk."],[15,135.12,3.28,"And it's not the best when you want to talk to people,"],[15,138.4,1.72,"when you want to get to know people,"],[15,140.12,4.24,"so I tend to watch films more at home, to be honest."],[15,144.36,2.32,"Yeah, but what about if you go to the cinema"],[15,146.68,3.96,"with people that you see very often and you don't need to catch up"],[15,150.64,1.72,"or find out about their lives?"],[15,152.36,3.2,"You're just going to watch a film and to relax."],[15,155.56,2.8,"Yeah. I mean, yeah, that makes sense because it is..."],[15,158.36,1.6,"it is nice to get out."],[15,159.96,3.88,"It is nice to get out of the house and see it on a big screen."],[15,163.84,3.24,"There are also other downsides to going to the cinema."],[15,167.08,5.28,"My big thing with the cinema is that the seats are not very comfortable."],[15,172.36,3.2,"It's much more comfortable to watch a film at home"],[15,175.56,5.24,"because you might have a sofa, blankets – you can lie down."],[15,180.8,5.8,"You don't have to sit like we are now in a chair upright."],[15,186.6,1.92,"Yeah, it's really nice at home."],[15,188.52,1.2,"You have your snacks."],[15,189.72,2.04,"You don't have to pay for them as well."],[15,191.76,2.44,"I also think TVs that people have at home now"],[15,194.2,2.04,"are much better than they used to be."],[15,196.24,2.96,"When I was a kid, you had a tiny little TV"],[15,199.2,2.72,"and it was so different to watching something at the cinema."],[15,201.92,1.88,"But now I've got quite a nice telly."],[15,203.8,4.0,"So, Phil, you talked about going to the cinema as, like, an event."],[15,207.8,2.52,"So, something we do for special occasions."],[15,210.32,1.24,"I really feel that."],[15,211.56,3.64,"I love going to the cinema because you can buy snacks"],[15,215.2,1.44,"that you wouldn't normally eat."],[15,216.64,4.68,"Like, popcorn is something I don't eat at home, but I eat at the cinema."],[15,221.32,4.28,"And then going in a little bit early and watching the film trailers,"],[15,225.6,4.0,"which are short adverts for films that are coming up soon"],[15,229.6,1.24,"that are shown at the cinema."],[15,230.84,2.72,"Is there anything else you like about the cinema?"],[15,233.56,5.28,"What is nice about the cinema is that you can have no distractions."],[15,238.84,1.4,"You just have the film."],[15,240.24,1.92,"You put your phone away."],[15,242.16,2.08,"At least you should put your phone away!"],[15,244.24,1.08,"Some people..."],[15,245.32,2.36,"some people in the cinema don't follow the rules,"],[15,247.68,3.8,"but hopefully everyone watching puts their phone away."],[15,251.48,1.92,"There's no distractions."],[15,253.4,5.08,"You can just relax and get involved in the film."],[15,258.48,3.68,"It's really nice. It is really annoying when people in front of you"],[15,262.16,4.6,"or next to you are talking really loudly throughout the film."],[15,266.76,1.76,"It's OK to whisper."],[15,268.52,2.28,"So, that's when you whisper like that."],[15,270.8,2.32,"It's OK to do that every now and again,"],[15,273.12,2.72,"but if they're talking all the way through the film,"],[15,275.84,2.08,"that is really annoying."],[15,277.92,1.52,"Yeah, I can't stand that."],[15,279.44,3.52,"Phil, what kind of films do you like to watch at the cinema?"],[15,282.96,6.16,"I think a film that takes you to a different place."],[15,289.12,4.92,"So, something that's quite spectacular – there's a lot happening."],[15,294.04,1.56,"There's a lot to see."],[15,295.6,3.6,"Or maybe a film that shows you a bit of the world"],[15,299.2,2.84,"that's interesting that you don't know that well."],[15,302.04,4.76,"I kind of like films like that – films that take you somewhere,"],[15,306.8,3.04,"that show you something you wouldn't normally see."],[15,309.84,2.72,"That's definitely good to watch on a big screen."],[15,312.56,0.84,"Yeah, I agree."],[15,313.4,4.0,"I wouldn't go to the cinema to watch a romantic comedy, a romcom."],[15,317.4,1.0,"I agree with you."],[15,318.4,3.24,"I'd like to watch something, yeah, spectacular."],[15,328.4,3.6,"OK. Phil, it's time to look at some of the vocabulary we heard"],[15,332.0,1.12,"in this conversation,"],[15,333.12,2.96,"starting with 'trailers', which are short adverts"],[15,336.08,2.56,"for new films shown at the cinema."],[15,338.64,2.8,"And we also talked about popcorn."],[15,341.44,2.8,"Now, popcorn – it's a snack."],[15,344.24,2.08,"It's made of corn,"],[15,346.32,4.52,"and it's usually either sweet or salty,"],[15,350.84,2.4,"and it's very nice and you get it at the cinema."],[15,353.24,4.08,"We also heard 'spectacular', which is a very strong adjective"],[15,357.32,3.28,"to describe something that is very exciting to look at."],[15,360.6,3.68,"And we talked about 'distractions'."],[15,364.28,3.64,"Things that are distractions are things that make you look"],[15,367.92,2.36,"at something you might not have wanted to look at,"],[15,370.28,3.32,"like someone's phone screen instead of the film."],[15,373.6,2.88,"And the things that you watch in the cinema are called 'films',"],[15,376.48,2.32,"or sometimes called 'movies' as well."],[15,378.8,4.6,"OK. And that's it for this week's episode of Real Easy English,"],[15,383.4,2.64,"but don't worry – we'll be back next week"],[15,386.04,2.88,"with another episode in easy English."],[15,388.92,3.56,"And don't forget to download a free worksheet to test what you've learned"],[15,392.48,2.8,"at bbclearningenglish.com."],[15,395.28,1.08,"Goodbye for now."],[15,396.36,0.84,"Bye."],[16,0.12,1.96,"From BBC Learning English."],[16,2.08,1.08,"This is Learning English"],[16,3.16,4.28,"from the News, oour podcast about the news headlines. In this programme:"],[16,7.44,3.6,"Why is Gen Z protesting around the world?"],[16,15.04,2.04,"Hello, I'm Becca. And I'm Georgie."],[16,17.08,2.88,"In this programme, we look at one big news story"],[16,19.96,3.68,"and the vocabulary in the headlines that will help you understand it."],[16,23.64,3.56,"You can find all the vocabulary and headlines from this episode,"],[16,27.2,4.64,"as well as a worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com"],[16,31.84,3.6,"So, let's hear more about this story."],[16,40.32,1.36,"India, France,"],[16,41.68,1.64,"Albania, Italy."],[16,43.32,2.4,"Around the world, young people are protesting,"],[16,45.72,2.56,"but why? Gen Zs,"],[16,48.28,2.36,"those are people born between the late 90s"],[16,50.64,4.68,"and early 2010s are protesting different issues. In France and Italy,"],[16,55.32,3.56,"students are protesting about the quality of their education."],[16,58.88,3.04,"In Albania, it's about better university conditions"],[16,61.92,2.44,"and more job opportunities. In India,"],[16,64.36,3.88,"the Cockroach Janta Party, made up of young people and students,"],[16,68.24,4.04,"are protesting over voter rights. In Spain, students and graduates"],[16,72.28,4.8,"joined protests over the country's housing crisis, calling for lower rents."],[16,77.08,2.2,"Let's have our first headline."],[16,79.28,2.44,"This headline is from Dazed Digital,"],[16,81.72,3.64,"an online pop and culture magazine from the UK."],[16,85.36,4.28,"Gen Z are taking to the streets around the world - here's why"],[16,89.64,4.36,"And that headline again: Gen Z are taking to the streets around the world -"],[16,94.0,3.12,"here's why. And that's from Dazed Digital."],[16,97.12,3.56,"This headline says that Gen Z are taking to the streets."],[16,100.68,3.56,"That's a bit of a strange use of the verb 'take', isn't it?"],[16,104.24,1.72,"What does it mean to take to the streets,"],[16,105.96,1.24,"Georgie? Yes, you're right,"],[16,107.2,2.2,"it isn't a common use of the word 'take'."],[16,109.4,3.12,"When people take to the streets, it means a large group"],[16,112.52,3.28,"of them have gathered in a public place to oppose something,"],[16,115.8,2.08,"in other words, a protest."],[16,117.88,4.12,"So here, we know that young people are protesting in public areas"],[16,122.0,2.24,"for various reasons around the world."],[16,124.24,2.16,"They've taken to the streets. Yes."],[16,126.4,3.0,"Now, it's not always used in the context of protests."],[16,129.4,2.52,"People can take to the streets in celebration,"],[16,131.92,2.64,"for example, to celebrate a sports victory."],[16,134.56,3.08,"Yes. This year, thousands of people in Spain took"],[16,137.64,4.2,"to the streets to celebrate their FIFA World Cup win, didn't they? Yes."],[16,141.84,4.0,"Take to the streets is mostly used in these two contexts - protests"],[16,145.84,1.84,"and celebrations."],[16,151.4,2.52,"We've had to 'take to the streets' -"],[16,153.92,4.04,"gather in a public place to oppose or celebrate something."],[16,160.4,2.44,"For example, Thousands took to the streets"],[16,162.84,2.72,"to demand a change in government."],[16,168.4,4.44,"This is Learning English from the News, our podcast about the news headlines."],[16,172.84,3.92,"Today, we're talking about young people protesting around the world."],[16,176.76,2.56,"OK, so we've talked about protests that have been going on"],[16,179.32,3.28,"in France, Italy, Albania, Spain and India."],[16,182.6,3.68,"But this is part of a wider pattern of protests led by young people,"],[16,186.28,1.12,"isn't it? Yes."],[16,187.4,4.64,"In 2024, students in Bangladesh forced out the prime minister."],[16,192.04,4.8,"In 2025, protests in Nepal brought down the entire government."],[16,196.84,4.36,"And since then, protests led by young people have happened in Indonesia,"],[16,201.2,3.92,"the Philippines, Morocco, Madagascar, Peru and Mexico."],[16,205.12,2.32,"So what unites these protests?"],[16,207.44,5.0,"Well, Dr Naya Prakash, a researcher at the University of Cambridge,"],[16,212.44,3.72,"says that while it would be wrong to summarise young people's concerns"],[16,216.16,3.48,"from all of these countries into just a few sentences,"],[16,219.64,3.8,"what most of the protests have in common is a feel that there is a lack"],[16,223.44,1.4,"of government investment"],[16,224.84,3.96,"into services and policies that impact young people's lives."],[16,228.8,1.48,"Let's have another headline."],[16,230.28,3.88,"This one is from Al Jazeera, an international news organisation."],[16,234.16,4.76,"How has the cost of living changed in France, Italy, Spain and Portugal?"],[16,238.92,2.44,"And that headline again from Al Jazeera."],[16,241.36,4.68,"How has the cost of living changed in France, Italy, Spain and Portugal?"],[16,246.04,4.2,"So this headline is about the price of things in some European countries."],[16,250.24,2.16,"It uses the phrase 'cost of living'."],[16,252.4,1.56,"Now, I see this phrase everywhere,"],[16,253.96,1.6,"Becca, what can you tell us?"],[16,255.56,3.88,"Well, the cost of living means the amount of money people need to pay"],[16,259.44,4.6,"for everyday essentials such as food, housing and energy bills."],[16,264.04,3.88,"Basically, cost of living is how much it costs to live."],[16,267.92,3.32,"So when the headline asks how the cost of living has changed"],[16,271.24,1.04,"in those countries,"],[16,272.28,4.24,"it's asking if the amount of money needed for essentials has changed."],[16,276.52,1.88,"If the cost of living has risen,"],[16,278.4,2.96,"everyday essentials have got more expensive."],[16,281.36,2.88,"OK, so that's a noun phrase, the cost of living."],[16,284.24,3.12,"But it's also commonly used as an adjectival phrase,"],[16,287.36,1.96,"where the words are hyphenated."],[16,289.32,3.64,"For example, a common phrase is 'the cost-of-living crisis'."],[16,292.96,4.64,"Or in this story where we have people in some countries protesting"],[16,297.6,1.48,"about the cost of living,"],[16,299.08,3.24,"we could talk about the cost-of-living protests."],[16,305.72,1.64,"We've had cost of living."],[16,307.36,2.84,"Amount of money needed to pay for everyday essentials"],[16,310.2,3.84,"like food and energy bills."],[16,314.04,2.96,"For example, with such a high cost of living,"],[16,317.0,3.96,"many young people are finding it harder to buy a house."],[16,323.36,3.6,"This is Learning English from the News from BBC Learning English."],[16,326.96,3.32,"We're talking about Gen Z protests around the world."],[16,330.28,2.72,"Many of these movements, led by young people,"],[16,333.0,3.68,"are organised online, often with no clear leader."],[16,336.68,2.48,"Yes, some are openly copying each other too."],[16,339.16,4.0,"For example, in Europe, Italian students said they were following France"],[16,343.16,3.32,"in their protests about the quality of education."],[16,346.48,2.04,"Let's have our next headline."],[16,348.52,4.48,"This headline is from El Pais, a Spanish news organisation."],[16,353.0,2.28,"A generation takes to the streets:"],[16,355.28,4.0,"Young Europeans refuse to accept a future stacked against them."],[16,359.28,1.64,"And that headline again."],[16,360.92,2.08,"A generation takes to the streets:"],[16,363.0,4.2,"Young Europeans refuse to accept a future stacked against them"],[16,367.2,2.16,"and that is from El Pais."],[16,369.36,3.4,"This headline is about protests happening in Europe."],[16,372.76,1.56,"There we have that phrase again,"],[16,374.32,1.92,"'take to the streets'. Now,"],[16,376.24,3.8,"young Europeans don't want to accept a future stacked against them."],[16,380.04,2.84,"What can you tell us about this phrase, Georgie? Yes."],[16,382.88,3.0,"If their future is stacked against them, it means"],[16,385.88,4.2,"the circumstances they face will make it difficult for them to succeed."],[16,390.08,3.72,"As we've heard some of the things worrying young people around the world"],[16,393.8,4.36,"are the quality of education, job opportunities, and voting rights."],[16,398.16,4.24,"The more common version of this phrase is 'the odds are stacked against you'."],[16,402.4,4.12,"It comes from a game context in which the situation makes it much harder"],[16,406.52,3.48,"for one person to win in a way that can be unfair."],[16,410.0,2.96,"The situation is not in their favor."],[16,412.96,2.84,"The phrase can be used in many situations."],[16,415.8,3.2,"For example, he didn't have time to update his CV"],[16,419.0,3.32,"and he knows thousands of people applied for the same job."],[16,422.32,2.2,"He feels the odds are stacked against him."],[16,424.52,2.28,"There's little chance he'll succeed."],[16,426.8,2.56,"The opposite is the odds are in your favor."],[16,429.36,2.08,"You're likely to succeed."],[16,433.4,2.08,"We've had 'stacked against someone' -"],[16,435.48,6.52,"when an unfair or difficult situation makes it harder for someone to succeed."],[16,442.0,3.56,"For example, the football team had a few injured players in a match"],[16,445.56,1.6,"against the league champions."],[16,447.16,3.56,"The odds were stacked against them."],[16,450.72,3.28,"That's it for this episode of Learning English from the News."],[16,454.0,3.6,"We'll be back next week with another news story."],[16,457.6,2.68,"Don't forget to sign up to our email newsletter!"],[16,460.28,3.12,"Get weekly tips and updates from your favorite presenters."],[16,463.4,3.28,"Just head to our website, bbclearningenglish.com"],[16,466.68,2.36,"or go to the notes in this programme."],[16,469.04,2.16,"Bye for now. Goodbye."],[17,0.04,0.6,"Hi Georgie."],[17,0.64,1.48,"Hi Phil. How are you?"],[17,2.12,1.56,"I'm good, I'm good."],[17,3.68,4.68,"I wanted to ask if you've been to any famous places or landmarks"],[17,8.36,1.4,"in London recently."],[17,9.76,3.44,"It's funny you should ask, because I did."],[17,13.2,3.48,"A few days ago, I went to the Royal Albert Hall, which is"],[17,16.68,3.0,"a famous concert hall in London."],[17,19.68,6.88,"OK. So, Georgie, today we're talking about landmarks – British landmarks."],[17,26.56,5.08,"Now, those are famous buildings like the Royal Albert Hall or like..."],[17,31.64,1.52,"like Big Ben in London."],[17,33.16,3.28,"That's a really famous landmark that everyone knows."],[17,36.44,2.48,"Yeah, that's a famous clock tower,"],[17,38.92,2.28,"but before we start talking about landmarks,"],[17,41.2,3.72,"I need to remind people that they can download a free worksheet"],[17,44.92,3.0,"to test the vocabulary they learn in this programme,"],[17,47.92,2.04,"at bbclearningenglish.com."],[17,55.76,0.56,"OK."],[17,56.32,5.16,"So, Georgie, you just said you'd been to the Royal Albert Hall, which is"],[17,61.48,3.2,"a famous concert hall in London."],[17,64.68,1.28,"Why did you go there?"],[17,65.96,3.64,"I went to a classical music concert,"],[17,69.6,4.08,"but this was not the first time I've been to the Royal Albert Hall."],[17,73.68,1.0,"No?"],[17,74.68,5.4,"No, I actually performed there with my choir last year."],[17,80.08,1.8,"It was... it was amazing."],[17,81.88,0.68,"Oh wow!"],[17,82.56,4.96,"Phil, have you visited any famous landmarks in London?"],[17,87.52,1.0,"Yeah, I think..."],[17,88.52,3.96,"I think I've probably visited most of the famous ones."],[17,92.48,1.88,"I've been on the London Eye."],[17,94.36,2.32,"That's the big wheel."],[17,96.68,3.76,"I've been to Tower Bridge lots of times."],[17,100.44,2.4,"I've been in the Houses of Parliament."],[17,102.84,2.92,"That's right by Big Ben, where the government is."],[17,105.76,2.76,"Phil, why have you been in the Houses of Parliament?"],[17,108.52,4.8,"It was just something for work, so it wasn't that interesting,"],[17,113.32,1.68,"but it's good to see the building."],[17,115.0,1.76,"You see it from outside all the time,"],[17,116.76,3.48,"and it's kind of interesting to see it from the inside. It's really..."],[17,120.24,2.28,"it's a very beautiful building as well, I think."],[17,122.52,3.76,"Yeah, it is. So, you've been to a lot of landmarks in London."],[17,126.28,1.72,"Do you have a favourite one?"],[17,128.0,4.8,"My favourite one is actually the river."],[17,132.8,3.32,"I love going up and down the river on a boat."],[17,136.12,2.12,"Yeah, I really like the river too."],[17,138.24,4.08,"My favourite landmark in London is the Tower of London."],[17,142.32,2.24,"I went with my dad."],[17,144.56,2.88,"It's a very, very old building."],[17,147.44,6.48,"I think in history a lot of people died there, so it has a kind of spooky..."],[17,153.92,1.88,"a spooky feeling there."],[17,155.8,3.48,"But it's quite a large area you can walk around and,"],[17,159.28,3.16,"yeah, it all just feels really old and quite special."],[17,162.44,1.88,"OK. So, we've been talking about London,"],[17,164.32,3.2,"but what about the rest of Britain, Georgie?"],[17,167.52,3.2,"What's your favourite landmark in the rest of Britain?"],[17,170.72,2.48,"Ooh, I can't wait to talk about this!"],[17,173.2,3.0,"One of my favourite places in the UK is Edinburgh,"],[17,176.2,2.04,"which is the capital of Scotland."],[17,178.24,3.92,"And in the city, they have – in the middle of the city,"],[17,182.16,3.44,"they have a big black rock, like a hill."],[17,185.6,3.88,"And on the top, there is the castle, Edinburgh Castle,"],[17,189.48,2.4,"and it's so beautiful."],[17,191.88,2.8,"I used to live there when I was at university and"],[17,194.68,2.76,"every time I walked past it, I was..."],[17,197.44,1.76,"I felt like a tourist every day."],[17,199.2,4.16,"I was always taking photos of it because it looks unreal."],[17,203.36,4.16,"It doesn't look... It looks like it's from a storybook."],[17,207.52,2.16,"It's magical, I love it."],[17,209.68,1.2,"Yeah, I love Edinburgh."],[17,210.88,2.52,"I went there one year for New Year and it was..."],[17,213.4,3.24,"It's amazing. You can feel the history."],[17,216.64,4.96,"There're so many old buildings and it's... yeah, it's very dramatic."],[17,221.6,2.56,"I did a guided tour there actually once."],[17,224.16,6.8,"It was around Halloween, so it was a kind of spooky-themed guided tour."],[17,230.96,6.16,"They took us to a graveyard and they told us stories about witches,"],[17,237.12,1.52,"and that was really exciting."],[17,238.64,1.68,"I like guided tours."],[17,240.32,1.76,"Oh yeah, that sounds exciting."],[17,242.08,2.24,"So, Phil, do you enjoy sightseeing?"],[17,244.32,4.4,"So, when you see lots of different landmarks in a city or a country."],[17,248.72,1.84,"I do, yes."],[17,250.56,8.28,"I think usually when you go sightseeing you see lots of old buildings, usually."],[17,258.84,5.68,"And they often give you an idea of how people used to live,"],[17,264.52,4.24,"how people lived in the past, and I find that really interesting."],[17,268.76,7.68,"So yes, if I visit a new city, I almost always go sightseeing at some point."],[17,276.44,2.16,"Yeah, I really like sightseeing as well."],[17,278.6,4.16,"You can see lots of a city, or it's the best way to see a place, I think,"],[17,282.76,1.92,"when you visit somewhere new."],[17,284.68,1.96,"It can be quite exhausting though, can't it?"],[17,286.64,3.68,"Especially if you're walking all over the city to see all"],[17,290.32,1.72,"of these different landmarks."],[17,292.04,4.88,"So, Phil, my favourite UK landmark is Edinburgh Castle."],[17,296.92,2.16,"Do you have a favourite?"],[17,299.08,3.2,"Yes. I think it's got to be"],[17,302.28,6.76,"Stonehenge, just because it is so old."],[17,309.04,3.16,"It's thousands of years old."],[17,312.2,6.08,"I don't think I've seen anything else that's that old."],[17,318.28,2.88,"Yeah. So, the Stonehenge is in England and"],[17,321.16,6.6,"it's a circle or a ring of really big, tall stones."],[17,327.76,1.96,"And humans put it up and"],[17,329.72,4.24,"it's really amazing because it's not fully known how they did it,"],[17,333.96,3.6,"because the rocks are so heavy."],[17,337.56,2.4,"So, if anyone comes to the UK to visit,"],[17,339.96,4.0,"I would really recommend going to see that."],[17,349.52,3.32,"So, let's look at some of the words that we talked about today."],[17,352.84,2.92,"And the big one was 'landmark',"],[17,355.76,5.6,"and those are famous buildings or famous places that people visit,"],[17,361.36,2.8,"and usually they know what they look like beforehand, often."],[17,364.16,2.36,"Yes. For example, the London Eye."],[17,366.52,5.04,"We also heard 'sightseeing', which is when you visit landmarks and famous places"],[17,371.56,1.52,"in a city or country."],[17,373.08,3.32,"Yes. And one of the ways that you can do sightseeing"],[17,376.4,3.12,"is you can go on a guided tour,"],[17,379.52,5.04,"and that's when someone, a guide, leads you around different places"],[17,384.56,4.84,"and they tell you about the history of all those places."],[17,389.4,2.48,"So, Georgie, we're out of time."],[17,391.88,3.08,"That's it for Real Easy English for this week."],[17,394.96,4.84,"But if you like landmarks, make sure you go to our website"],[17,399.8,3.08,"to check out the London Letter Challenge,"],[17,402.88,4.36,"where you can see lots of different landmarks and learn easy English."],[17,407.24,1.84,"And you can find a free worksheet"],[17,409.08,3.72,"and transcript on our website, bbclearningenglish.com."],[17,412.8,1.12,"Goodbye for now."],[17,413.92,1.08,"Bye!"],[18,0.04,2.16,"From BBC Learning English,"],[18,2.2,4.84,"this is Learning English from the News, our podcast about the news headlines."],[18,7.04,5.32,"In this programme, the leaders of the USA and China are meeting this week,"],[18,12.36,2.4,"but what will they discuss?"],[18,18.24,1.4,"Hello, I'm Pippa."],[18,19.64,1.16,"And I'm Becca."],[18,20.8,2.84,"In this programme, we look at one big news story"],[18,23.64,3.72,"and the vocabulary in the headlines that will help you understand it."],[18,27.36,4.12,"You can find all the vocabulary and headlines from this episode,"],[18,31.48,4.92,"as well as a worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com."],[18,36.4,3.72,"So, Becca, let's hear more about this story."],[18,43.92,1.8,"US President Donald Trump"],[18,45.72,2.52,"and China's President Xi Jinping"],[18,48.24,3.16,"will meet in a summit at the white House this week."],[18,51.4,4.52,"President Xi is visiting the US for the first time since 2023,"],[18,55.92,4.6,"following on from President Trump's visit to China earlier this year."],[18,60.52,3.32,"A truce between the two countries on trade tariffs"],[18,63.84,2.6,"is set to expire in November."],[18,66.44,2.56,"A truce is an interruption in an argument"],[18,69.0,4.16,"or an agreement to stop arguing for a period of time."],[18,73.16,2.92,"Alongside trade, there are other potential disagreements"],[18,76.08,1.28,"between the two leaders,"],[18,77.36,3.28,"for example over AI and the war in Iran."],[18,80.64,2.36,"Let's have our first headline."],[18,83.0,4.88,"This headline is from CNBC, an American news organisation."],[18,87.88,3.8,"Trump, Xi seek trade stability and deals,"],[18,91.68,3.48,"but AI, tariffs and Iran loom large."],[18,95.16,1.36,"What to watch."],[18,96.52,2.76,"That headline again from CNBC."],[18,99.28,3.36,"Trump, Xi seek trade stability and deals,"],[18,102.64,3.08,"but AI, tariffs and Iran loom large."],[18,105.72,1.12,"What to watch."],[18,106.84,3.44,"This headline mentions some of the things that might be discussed"],[18,110.28,2.76,"at the meeting between Trump and Xi."],[18,113.04,2.56,"We're looking at the expression loom large."],[18,115.6,1.88,"What can you tell us about it, Pippa?"],[18,117.48,5.12,"Well, if something looms large, it's a very important, often worrying issue."],[18,122.6,5.4,"Yes. In this story, there are lots of disagreements between China and the US."],[18,128.0,4.24,"And these issues loom large in the discussions between the two countries."],[18,132.24,2.68,"They are important and potentially worrying"],[18,134.92,2.76,"because reaching agreement might be difficult,"],[18,137.68,1.56,"according to the headline."],[18,139.24,3.56,"We often see loom large used to talk about official"],[18,142.8,3.04,"contexts such as meetings and negotiations."],[18,145.84,2.28,"For example, at the all staff meeting,"],[18,148.12,3.6,"the company's poor financial performance loomed large."],[18,151.72,3.96,"And we can use loom large in more personal contexts, too."],[18,155.68,4.12,"For example, my friend's worried about her parents getting older."],[18,159.8,2.32,"Their poor health looms large for her."],[18,162.12,3.64,"We always use loom large to talk about potentially negative"],[18,165.76,1.64,"or concerning issues."],[18,167.4,4.76,"We don't use it to talk about important but positive factors."],[18,174.52,6.04,"We've had loom large, be a very important, often worrying issue."],[18,183.48,5.96,"For example, the impact of social media on kids looms large for many parents."],[18,192.12,1.4,"This is Learning English"],[18,193.52,3.36,"from the News, our podcast about the news headlines."],[18,196.88,2.6,"Today we're talking about the meeting this week"],[18,199.48,5.32,"between US President Donald Trump and China's President Xi Jinping."],[18,204.8,3.64,"As we've mentioned, President Xi's visit to the US comes"],[18,208.44,4.28,"just weeks before a truce on trade between the two countries is due to end."],[18,212.72,1.76,"Before the truce was agreed,"],[18,214.48,5.8,"the US and China had imposed very high tariffs on each other's products."],[18,220.28,3.48,"Tariffs are taxes on products entering the country."],[18,223.76,3.4,"The truce means these tariffs are currently lower."],[18,227.16,3.92,"Many experts say that the two countries will try to extend their truce"],[18,231.08,2.68,"and make more agreements on trade."],[18,233.76,2.36,"Our next headline is about this."],[18,236.12,4.04,"This headline is from the New York Times, an American newspaper."],[18,240.16,1.92,"With Time on His Side,"],[18,242.08,3.04,"Xi Seeks to Extend Truce With Trump."],[18,245.12,2.68,"That headline again, from the New York Times."],[18,247.8,4.76,"With Time on His Side, Xi Seeks to Extend Truce With Trump."],[18,252.56,3.84,"We're interested in this expression time on his side."],[18,256.4,2.88,"What does it mean to have time on your side, Pippa?"],[18,259.28,3.16,"Well, this is an expression which is all about"],[18,262.44,2.32,"how quickly you need to do something."],[18,264.76,1.56,"If time is on your side,"],[18,266.32,2.68,"then you don't need to do a particular thing quickly."],[18,269.0,3.32,"In other words, you have lots of time in which to do it."],[18,272.32,5.08,"Right. So in this headline, it says time is on Xi Jinping's side"],[18,277.4,2.12,"to extend the truce with Trump."],[18,279.52,2.44,"This means he has time to do it."],[18,281.96,3.24,"Remember, the truce doesn't end until November."],[18,285.2,3.32,"We can use the expression time is on someone's side"],[18,288.52,5.16,"in everyday situations too, often to talk about decisions or deadlines."],[18,293.68,2.6,"For example, I want to go travelling next year,"],[18,296.28,2.6,"but I don't need to book anything for a little while."],[18,298.88,1.68,"Time is on my side."],[18,300.56,2.64,"We can say time is on someone's side,"],[18,303.2,3.68,"or we can say someone has time on their side."],[18,306.88,1.88,"We can also use it negatively."],[18,308.76,2.12,"If time isn't on someone's side,"],[18,310.88,4.44,"then they don't have much time and they need to act quickly."],[18,317.24,3.04,"We've had time is on someone's side,"],[18,320.28,3.6,"someone has lots of time in which to do a particular thing."],[18,323.88,2.24,"They don't have to act quickly."],[18,326.12,5.24,"For example, I found out early from a friend that his company would be hiring,"],[18,331.36,4.48,"so time was on my side to work on my CV and apply."],[18,335.84,4.04,"This is Learning English from the News from BBC Learning English."],[18,339.88,3.96,"We're talking about this week's summit between US President Donald Trump"],[18,343.84,4.92,"and China's President Xi Jinping, and what the two leaders may discuss."],[18,348.76,4.48,"Another topic which could be discussed between the US and China is AI."],[18,353.24,3.24,"As we talked about last week on Learning English from the News,"],[18,356.48,3.68,"there have been lots of warnings about the potential dangers of AI"],[18,360.16,5.84,"recently. US and Chinese companies are the leaders in developing AI technology."],[18,366.0,6.2,"Earlier this week, US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent told reporters that US"],[18,372.2,5.12,"and Chinese officials have discussed creating an international warning system"],[18,377.32,4.08,"for AI incidents that could affect national security."],[18,381.4,2.24,"Let's have a headline about this."],[18,383.64,4.6,"This is from Al Jazeera, an international news organisation."],[18,388.24,5.72,"What's the US-China AI 'hotline' that Trump plans to pitch to Xi Jinping?"],[18,393.96,2.68,"And that headline, again from Al Jazeera."],[18,396.64,5.64,"What's the US-China AI 'hotline' that Trump plans to pitch to Xi Jinping?"],[18,402.28,3.2,"This headline talks about the warning system for AI"],[18,405.48,3.36,"that it says Trump plans to pitch to Xi Jinping."],[18,408.84,3.92,"Pitch means explain an idea and convince someone to do it."],[18,412.76,3.28,"But we're interested in the word hotline."],[18,416.04,4.8,"Yes, literally a hotline is a special direct phone connection, often used"],[18,420.84,4.2,"in emergencies, but it can be used more metaphorically to refer"],[18,425.04,4.08,"to any direct system to communicate a warning or emergency."],[18,429.12,4.2,"In this headline, we don't know whether the warning system being discussed"],[18,433.32,2.92,"would be a literal phone line or something else."],[18,436.24,4.32,"Because hotlines are used for emergency or urgent communications,"],[18,440.56,3.0,"we see the term used in relation to disasters."],[18,443.56,4.56,"They can also be used by charities to tackle particular serious issues."],[18,448.12,3.0,"For example, campaigners want to set up a hotline"],[18,451.12,2.48,"to help victims of domestic violence."],[18,453.6,2.56,"Hotlines are less common than they used to be"],[18,456.16,2.32,"as other technologies have developed,"],[18,458.48,3.76,"but they can also be used in less urgent situations"],[18,462.24,2.56,"as a direct way to get information."],[18,464.8,4.04,"For example, a company might have a customer service hotline"],[18,468.84,2.72,"people can call for help with their products."],[18,471.56,3.92,"The term hotline just means that you can speak to someone directly"],[18,475.48,3.64,"and relatively quickly."],[18,479.12,1.4,"We've had hotline,"],[18,480.52,5.4,"a special direct phone connection often used in emergencies."],[18,485.92,2.88,"For example, the company was criticised"],[18,488.8,4.32,"for replacing its complaints hotline with an online form."],[18,493.12,3.8,"That's it for this episode of Learning English from the News."],[18,496.92,3.28,"We'll be back next week with another news story."],[18,500.2,4.6,"If you like our podcasts, sign up to our newsletter to get all the latest"],[18,504.8,2.6,"from BBC Learning English every week."],[18,507.4,2.32,"There's a link in the notes below this episode."],[18,509.72,2.0,"Bye for now. Goodbye."],[19,0.0,1.28,"Hi Georgie. How are you?"],[19,1.28,2.08,"I'm pretty good thank you, Phil."],[19,3.36,1.12,"How are you doing?"],[19,4.48,0.84,"I'm really good."],[19,5.32,3.4,"I think you're going to be even better when you find out"],[19,8.72,1.92,"what we're talking about today."],[19,10.64,0.76,"Tell me."],[19,11.4,6.96,"Well, I know we often talk about food, but today we're talking about desserts."],[19,18.36,1.32,"You were right."],[19,19.68,5.24,"I love desserts or puddings – sometimes we call them puddings in the UK."],[19,24.92,4.0,"I have a big sweet tooth so, yeah, I will love this."],[19,28.92,2.04,"I will love talking about this topic."],[19,30.96,2.28,"Fantastic. Let's get started."],[19,33.24,3.6,"Well, first, before we start talking about my favourite topic,"],[19,36.84,3.72,"we have to remind our listeners that they can find a free worksheet"],[19,40.56,4.24,"and transcript to help them learn at bbclearningenglish.com."],[19,50.48,3.0,"So, Georgie, I think this is a silly question"],[19,53.48,2.56,"and I think I know the answer already,"],[19,56.04,2.48,"but do you like desserts?"],[19,58.52,2.4,"Ah... do I like desserts?"],[19,60.92,1.68,"I love desserts!"],[19,62.6,5.08,"I have a really big sweet tooth, which means that I really like sweet things,"],[19,67.68,4.24,"sugary things – I put sugar in my coffee sometimes."],[19,71.92,5.2,"But I... if I'm in a restaurant, I will always order a dessert."],[19,77.12,5.64,"I would prefer to have a dessert or pudding than a starter, actually."],[19,82.76,2.96,"Yeah. What about you? Do you like them?"],[19,85.72,1.68,"Yeah, I do. I think..."],[19,87.4,5.16,"I think in a restaurant I'd probably order a starter rather than a dessert."],[19,92.56,2.88,"But yeah, it's nice when you get a good one."],[19,95.44,1.76,"Ah, it's so good."],[19,97.2,7.32,"I quite like homemade desserts – so, I don't know... homemade apple crumble."],[19,104.52,1.4,"Ooh, that's really good."],[19,105.92,4.0,"So, that's when we cook the apples a little bit so they're quite soft,"],[19,109.92,4.28,"and then we put a biscuit topping at the top."],[19,114.2,1.8,"And it's really, really delicious."],[19,116.0,2.32,"And it's a hot... a hot dessert."],[19,118.32,4.8,"Yeah. And a lot of fruit puddings that I quite like – they're sweet but"],[19,123.12,1.64,"they're also a bit sharp as well."],[19,124.76,2.48,"A bit sweet, a bit sour."],[19,127.24,2.2,"That's a really nice combination. I love that."],[19,129.44,4.88,"Well, I do love fruit puddings but I am a chocolate person,"],[19,134.32,6.32,"so if I have a menu in a restaurant, I will always look for the chocolate"],[19,140.64,5.48,"dessert – maybe a chocolate brownie, chocolate cake, ice cream as well."],[19,146.12,1.4,"That's a really good dessert."],[19,147.52,3.92,"And sometimes if you're feeling very full after the meal,"],[19,151.44,5.12,"ice cream is a good option because it doesn't feel too filling, does it?"],[19,156.56,1.6,"Do you like ice cream?"],[19,158.16,2.08,"Yes, I really love ice cream."],[19,160.24,2.96,"I like most flavours of ice cream, I think."],[19,163.2,3.52,"I like the creamy ones and then some of the fruity ones as well,"],[19,166.72,1.68,"like mango ice cream or something."],[19,168.4,2.08,"On a hot day you can't beat it."],[19,170.48,3.16,"Do you often eat dessert?"],[19,173.64,1.52,"No, I don't think I do."],[19,175.16,3.36,"Not that often. I keep it as a treat for, like,"],[19,178.52,4.28,"a special occasion or something to make a meal feel really special –"],[19,182.8,2.2,"dessert with it. But yeah, I wouldn't..."],[19,185.0,2.12,"I wouldn't have it every week, I don't think."],[19,187.12,5.6,"No, because it's not very healthy to eat too much sugar, is it?"],[19,192.72,1.4,"You're very good."],[19,194.12,4.28,"I do eat pudding and dessert quite a lot."],[19,198.4,4.24,"I... Yeah, I just get really strong sugar cravings after meals."],[19,202.64,4.36,"What's the best dessert you've ever had?"],[19,207.0,4.28,"OK. Well, I said that my favourite puddings are chocolatey,"],[19,211.28,5.6,"but actually I think the best dessert I've ever tried,"],[19,216.88,3.88,"because it was the first time, was when I was in Jordan"],[19,220.76,5.24,"and I tried something called knafeh, and this is like a..."],[19,226.0,3.56,"it's actually a cheesy pudding so it has cheese in it,"],[19,229.56,5.68,"but it then has a kind of crispy topping that I think is made of honey"],[19,235.24,5.88,"or sugary syrup, so it has the kind of savoury, which means salty,"],[19,241.12,4.96,"of the cheese – the salty flavour of the cheese – plus the sugary bit at the top."],[19,246.08,2.6,"And it was so delicious."],[19,248.68,3.48,"I ate all of it and it's, yeah, very rich."],[19,252.16,1.04,"That sounds amazing."],[19,253.2,1.88,"Do you have a favourite one?"],[19,255.08,3.56,"The one I can remember best – I had it in a restaurant"],[19,258.64,4.92,"in Argentina about 20 years ago, and I can still remember it."],[19,263.56,5.76,"And it was deep-fried ice cream, which sounds crazy."],[19,269.32,6.4,"It's like ice cream and it was fried in, like, sweet batter or pastry."],[19,275.72,2.88,"It was amazing. And I can remember it 20 years later."],[19,278.6,1.4,"That sounds incredible."],[19,280.0,2.88,"That's something that's very common in Scotland actually."],[19,282.88,1.96,"I'm surprised you had it in Argentina."],[19,284.84,1.28,"Maybe not ice cream,"],[19,286.12,7.12,"but in Scotland they like to deep fry – so, put something in a lot of oil."],[19,293.24,3.44,"They do that with chocolate bars and things like that so,"],[19,296.68,2.16,"yeah, not very healthy."],[19,298.84,1.16,"No, not at all..."],[19,300.0,1.0,"but lovely."],[19,301.0,1.72,"So delicious!"],[19,308.04,4.52,"OK. So, let's recap the vocabulary that we've been looking at today."],[19,312.56,1.6,"We're talking about desserts."],[19,314.16,3.84,"We also, particularly in the UK, call them 'puddings'."],[19,318.0,3.32,"And that's just something sweet that you eat at the end of a meal."],[19,321.32,4.44,"Yes. If you really like puddings and desserts and sweet things,"],[19,325.76,3.28,"you could be described as 'having a sweet tooth'."],[19,329.04,2.92,"I have a sweet tooth, for example."],[19,331.96,4.4,"Yes. Of course, puddings aren't just sweet."],[19,336.36,4.12,"I talked about apple crumble, a very traditional British pudding,"],[19,340.48,5.08,"and it's a little bit 'sharp', which kind of means the same as sour."],[19,345.56,4.32,"So, like, a lemon pudding would be sharp or sour."],[19,349.88,3.64,"We also heard the word 'filling', which describes a food"],[19,353.52,3.04,"or a pudding which makes you feel full."],[19,356.56,4.4,"Oh, and Georgie, you said you were a 'chocolate person'."],[19,360.96,1.68,"What do you mean by that?"],[19,362.64,4.4,"That just means that I am a person who really likes chocolate."],[19,367.04,1.52,"What kind of person are you, Phil?"],[19,368.56,2.24,"I think I might be an ice cream person."],[19,370.8,2.04,"I'm also an ice cream person."],[19,372.84,1.84,"I'm also a chocolate person."],[19,374.68,4.0,"Well, Phil. That is the end of this episode of Real Easy English."],[19,378.68,3.88,"We will of course be back next week with another conversation."],[19,382.56,1.12,"Yes, we will."],[19,383.68,2.2,"And don't forget that we have a worksheet"],[19,385.88,2.16,"that you can get from our website."],[19,388.04,3.2,"And that's bbclearningenglish.com."],[19,391.24,1.0,"Goodbye."],[19,392.24,0.96,"Goodbye."],[20,0.12,5.0,"Here in my hands is something that can help improve your pronunciation."],[20,5.12,4.32,"The key to making your speaking sound more natural."],[20,9.44,3.88,"You'll find out what it is later on. In this lesson,"],[20,13.32,5.04,"we're going to practice shadowing - a powerful way to improve your fluency,"],[20,18.36,2.88,"pronunciation, and listening."],[20,21.24,4.04,"You'll do this by listening to a short story about an awkward moment"],[20,25.28,3.48,"in the office, involving a packet of crisps."],[20,28.76,4.48,"This is a shadowing lesson, which means you'll listen carefully"],[20,33.24,3.88,"and repeat what I say almost at the same time."],[20,37.12,1.8,"Like an echo."],[20,38.92,3.48,"Here's how. I put them on the table"],[20,42.4,2.2,"and I went to make myself a cup of tea."],[20,44.6,3.32,"If you're interested in learning more about how to shadow,"],[20,47.92,2.08,"click here."],[20,50.0,3.4,"Okay, let's start the story."],[20,56.2,2.04,"I was feeling a bit hungry at work,"],[20,58.24,3.08,"so I bought a big packet of crisps from the vending machine"],[20,61.32,1.84,"for about a pound."],[20,64.72,4.52,"I put them on the table and went to make myself a cup of tea."],[20,69.24,3.72,"When I came back, I noticed a man had sat down at my table"],[20,72.96,2.36,"and was eating my crisps."],[20,77.72,2.72,"Let's have a look at the pronunciation."],[20,80.44,5.12,"What common vowel sounds do these highlighted words have?"],[20,85.56,4.72,"I was feeling hungry. For about a pound."],[20,90.28,10.48,"The table. Went to make a cup of tea. And the answer is here in my hand."],[20,100.76,5.68,"/ə/. This is the most common vowel sound in spoken English."],[20,106.44,5.64,"So common it even has its own name: the schwa."],[20,112.08,6.2,"You can hear it a lot in small grammar words like 'two', 'of' and 'four',"],[20,118.28,3.92,"because these words are usually unstressed."],[20,122.2,2.68,"Let's practice the sound first."],[20,124.88,4.76,"Just relax your mouth and say /ə/. /ə/."],[20,129.64,3.44,"Excuse me."],[20,133.08,2.96,"'Two'? 'Of'? 'For'?"],[20,136.04,3.2,"I can't hear an /ə/ sound in any of those words."],[20,139.24,2.72,"What on earth are you talking about?"],[20,141.96,1.56,"Well, yes, that's true"],[20,143.52,1.4,"when we say them on their own."],[20,144.92,3.92,"But in natural speech, they are usually unstressed"],[20,148.84,3.08,"and the vowel changes to a schwa."],[20,151.92,3.84,"Oh, right. Okay."],[20,155.76,1.08,"Carry on then."],[20,156.84,1.0,"Thank you."],[20,157.84,3.32,"Let's practice the schwa in these phrases."],[20,161.16,1.68,"Repeat after me."],[20,162.84,5.4,"/ə/. /wəz/. I was feeling hungry."],[20,168.24,4.84,"/ə/. /fə/. For about a pound."],[20,173.08,8.8,"/ə/. /əð/. The table. /ə/. /tə/. Went to make."],[20,181.88,6.4,"And in natural speech, we often drop the sound entirely in 'of'."],[20,188.28,2.84,"So we just say /ə/."],[20,191.12,4.0,"Let's practise. /ə/. A cup."],[20,195.12,3.04,"A cup of tea."],[20,198.16,4.44,"/ə/. A packet."],[20,202.6,2.76,"A packet of crisps."],[20,205.36,3.68,"You're going to hear the schwa a lot in this story."],[20,209.04,4.72,"So listen carefully, and try to use it when we shadow the full story"],[20,213.76,4.36,"at the end. Now let's continue the story."],[20,218.12,2.96,"I couldn't believe my eyes."],[20,221.08,4.8,"I tried to send him a message by taking big handfuls of the crisps myself"],[20,225.88,5.84,"and stuffing them into my mouth, but he still continued to eat them."],[20,233.68,1.36,"Listen carefully."],[20,235.04,3.88,"Can you hear a /h/ sound in these phrases?"],[20,238.92,2.12,"Send him a message."],[20,241.04,5.8,"A man had sat down. But he still continued."],[20,246.84,1.4,"I can't either."],[20,248.24,1.56,"In natural speech,"],[20,249.8,8.24,"we often don't pronounce the letter 'h' in short words like 'him', 'he' and 'her'."],[20,260.36,2.24,"Excuse me."],[20,262.6,2.64,"He. Him. His. Her."],[20,265.24,3.28,"I can hear plenty of /h/ sounds there."],[20,268.52,4.48,"Yes. When we say those words on their own, we say"],[20,273.0,3.68,"He, him, his and her."],[20,276.68,5.92,"But after a consonant sound, the /h/ usually disappears."],[20,282.6,2.8,"So we say 'stop 'im'."],[20,285.4,1.88,"Send 'er a letter."],[20,287.28,1.12,"Come 'ere."],[20,288.4,2.72,"Ah, yeah, I see."],[20,291.12,3.04,"Okay. Very good."],[20,294.16,1.84,"Goodbye, then."],[20,296.0,0.8,"Thank you."],[20,297.0,2.44,"Right. Repeat after me."],[20,299.44,2.2,"Send 'im a message."],[20,306.04,2.48,"A man 'ad sat down."],[20,312.52,2.92,"But 'e still continued."],[20,318.72,4.48,"Great. Now let's finish the story."],[20,323.2,4.88,"We soon finished the crisps, without saying a word to each other."],[20,328.08,5.64,"I angrily scrunched up the empty packet and stood up to leave."],[20,333.72,4.0,"I picked up my bag and saw the packet of crisps I'd bought earlier,"],[20,337.72,2.6,"right there under the table."],[20,342.2,5.4,"My face turned red as I realised what I'd just done."],[20,347.6,7.8,"I mentioned earlier that 'of' becomes /ə/, but 'and' can also change."],[20,355.4,3.64,"In connected speech, 'and' often sounds like /ən/."],[20,359.04,4.48,"We usually drop the 'd' so it becomes /ən/."],[20,363.52,2.28,"Here it is in the sentence again."],[20,365.8,4.84,"I angrily scrunched up the empty packet 'n' stood up to leave."],[20,370.64,3.12,"Let's practice some of the 'and's in the story."],[20,373.76,2.04,"Repeat after me."],[20,375.8,5.12,"I put them on the table and went to make myself a cup of tea."],[20,389.2,5.12,"I noticed a man had sat down at my table and was eating my crisps."],[20,402.72,4.76,"I picked up my bag and saw the packet of crisps I'd bought earlier."],[20,413.76,0.92,"Right."],[20,414.68,2.48,"Let's shadow the entire story again"],[20,417.16,4.0,"and see if your pronunciation sounds more natural."],[20,424.12,2.12,"I was feeling a bit hungry at work,"],[20,426.24,3.0,"so I bought a big packet of crisps from the vending machine"],[20,429.24,1.88,"for about a pound."],[20,432.64,4.52,"I put them on the table and went to make myself a cup of tea."],[20,437.16,3.72,"When I came back, I noticed a man had sat down at my table"],[20,440.88,2.4,"and was eating my crisps."],[20,445.0,3.04,"I couldn't believe my eyes."],[20,448.04,4.84,"I tried to send him a message by taking big handfuls of the crisps myself"],[20,452.88,3.04,"and stuffing them into my mouth,"],[20,455.92,2.72,"but he still continued to eat them."],[20,461.0,4.84,"We soon finished the crisps, without saying a word to each other."],[20,465.84,5.6,"I angrily scrunched up the empty packet and stood up to leave."],[20,471.44,4.04,"I picked up my bag and saw the packet of crisps I'd bought earlier"],[20,475.48,2.6,"right there under the table."],[20,479.96,5.36,"My face turned red as I realised what I'd just done."],[20,485.32,3.0,"How was your pronunciation this time?"],[20,488.32,4.16,"Try recording yourself reading the story, and listen carefully"],[20,492.48,1.8,"to where you can improve."],[20,494.28,5.28,"You can download a short audio file of the story by clicking here."],[20,499.56,5.84,"And for more information on the pronunciation of the schwa, click here."],[20,505.4,3.68,"Have you ever had a disagreement with someone over food?"],[20,509.08,5.8,"Let us know in the comments and see you in the next video! Bye bye!"],[21,1.68,0.93,"- Oh my gosh. There you are."],[21,2.61,1.2,"I'm so happy to see you."],[21,4.92,1.32,"Oh, wow."],[21,6.24,1.74,"Sorry, I thought you were a friend of mine."],[21,9.45,3.9,"I mean, you just, you like, really remind me of him."],[21,13.35,1.59,"- Yeah. I doubt that."],[21,16.56,1.08,"- Hey, who's this? Hi."],[21,17.64,1.17,"Hi."],[21,33.24,3.22,"- Yeah, yeah. - Huh. (laughing)"],[21,41.38,2.78,"I think this is the nicest conversation I've ever had."],[21,44.16,1.89,"- Yeah, you're all right."],[21,46.05,1.86,"- Well, that's a really beautiful moment."],[21,47.91,2.01,"Very speshy-weshy and whatnot."],[21,49.92,2.07,"I'm really happy that I could be a part of it."],[21,51.99,0.9,"Should we all hug?"],[21,53.79,0.83,"- [Both] Scram!"],[21,56.22,1.77,"- Really? Oh my gosh."],[21,57.99,2.22,"Well, I'm so glad you two found each other."],[21,60.21,3.69,"Oh..."],[21,63.9,2.43,"- Do you think we hurt her feelings?"],[21,66.33,1.57,"- I hope so."],[22,0.29,1.79,"Hi Neil. How are you?"],[22,2.08,2.71,"Hi Becca. I'm very well, thank you very much."],[22,4.8,3.13,"Have you noticed that it's not summer anymore?"],[22,7.92,1.17,"It's autumn."],[22,9.09,1.25,"How do you feel about that?"],[22,10.34,1.46,"What do you notice?"],[22,11.8,5.42,"Yes, it is autumn in the UK and the weather's getting a little bit cooler,"],[22,17.23,3.84,"and the leaves are starting to fall from the trees."],[22,21.06,2.17,"You like autumn, don't you? I love autumn!"],[22,23.23,4.38,"I'm excited for this episode because we're talking all about autumn today."],[22,27.61,2.0,"We are. And people need to know"],[22,29.61,4.0,"that they can go to our website, bbclearningenglish.com,"],[22,33.62,5.17,"to get a transcript and a worksheet to help them improve their English."],[22,45.42,4.0,"So, right now it is autumn in the UK."],[22,49.42,5.55,"You might hear it as 'fall' in other English-speaking countries."],[22,54.97,3.59,"What changes happen in the UK during autumn?"],[22,58.56,1.88,"The leaves fall."],[22,60.44,5.51,"The leaves fall off the trees and they are crunchy on the ground."],[22,65.94,3.21,"You can make big piles of leaves and jump into them."],[22,69.15,1.54,"It's so much fun."],[22,70.7,6.59,"And I love also the crunching sound when you walk over those autumn leaves."],[22,77.29,2.17,"Before the leaves fall off the trees,"],[22,79.45,5.51,"they turn into this beautiful orange-reddish colour."],[22,84.96,4.21,"And I love all of these autumnal colours – so,"],[22,89.17,4.96,"'autumnal' being the adjective to describe something that relates to autumn."],[22,94.14,3.42,"Yeah. You really like autumnal things, don't you?"],[22,97.56,0.96,"I do."],[22,98.52,2.04,"You like to wear different clothes?"],[22,100.56,1.0,"I do, yeah."],[22,101.56,5.76,"I'm very excited to start wearing jumpers and big boots."],[22,107.32,3.0,"And, as you mentioned, it's getting colder now"],[22,110.32,3.38,"so I want to be warm and cosy."],[22,113.7,3.04,"Cosy. That's a fantastic word."],[22,116.74,2.29,"Cosy is that feeling you have"],[22,119.04,5.71,"of being warm when things around you are probably a bit cold."],[22,124.75,5.3,"So, it feels cosy if you go into a house on a cold day and"],[22,130.05,3.0,"there's a big fire, for example."],[22,133.05,4.0,"Yeah. So, I obviously love autumn."],[22,137.05,3.59,"How does the changing of the seasons feel to you?"],[22,140.64,4.84,"Well, usually... usually I'm not a big fan."],[22,145.48,3.84,"I don't really like autumn because it means that the summer is over"],[22,149.32,3.63,"and I love summer and the long, late evenings."],[22,152.94,6.26,"But this year it was so hot, I've had enough of summer,"],[22,159.2,2.75,"so I'm very happy that it's autumn."],[22,161.95,0.58,"Yeah."],[22,162.54,0.67,"How about you?"],[22,163.21,0.88,"I feel the same."],[22,164.08,6.55,"I've always loved autumn, but I am a really big lover of summer."],[22,170.63,4.17,"But yeah, it has been far too hot this summer,"],[22,174.8,2.38,"so I welcome the autumn this year."],[22,177.18,5.88,"OK Becca. So, what kind of things do we do in the UK in autumn?"],[22,183.06,3.75,"Well, I am excited for Halloween."],[22,186.81,6.71,"It's not as popular in the UK as it is in the USA, for example,"],[22,193.53,5.3,"but I do love Halloween because I love things that are a little bit scary,"],[22,198.82,1.71,"and that's at the end of October."],[22,200.53,5.59,"But on the 5th of November is when we have Bonfire Night."],[22,206.12,7.84,"Yes, Bonfire Night is when we make a big fire, an outside fire, to celebrate"],[22,213.96,2.46,"and we stand around the fire."],[22,216.43,4.71,"And actually it's more about fireworks these days."],[22,221.14,7.59,"So, fireworks are those very pretty explosions in the sky that we look at"],[22,228.73,3.59,"on special occasions like New Year and that kind of thing."],[22,232.31,3.29,"Yeah. So, it's usually quite noisy on the 5th of November,"],[22,235.61,3.92,"but the sky is lit up with beautiful fireworks."],[22,239.53,3.79,"I can't remember the last time I did a bonfire."],[22,243.33,4.21,"Do you celebrate Bonfire Night every year?"],[22,247.54,4.92,"Well, I don't have a bonfire because I live in a city"],[22,252.46,5.55,"and it's not very nice for my neighbours, but when I was younger"],[22,258.01,6.01,"and lived more in the countryside, we would go to a Bonfire Night and"],[22,264.01,6.59,"we would stand around the bonfire and maybe even cook food in the bonfire."],[22,270.6,6.13,"Oh, OK. So, like toasting marshmallows or melting chocolate?"],[22,276.74,1.54,"Baked potatoes."],[22,278.28,1.46,"Baked potatoes...?"],[22,279.74,0.79,"Yeah."],[22,280.53,1.5,"I've never..."],[22,282.03,3.42,"I've never cooked a baked potato on a bonfire before."],[22,285.45,5.76,"Yeah, you wrap the potato in foil and then you put it in the fire,"],[22,291.21,1.46,"and then you wait."],[22,292.67,4.38,"And then you open it and it's completely black."],[22,297.05,3.84,"That doesn't sound very tasty to me!"],[22,300.88,3.0,"It's difficult to get it right."],[22,303.89,2.63,"Right... OK."],[22,306.51,1.88,"That's a whole new thing."],[22,316.36,1.88,"Alright, Neil, it's time to recap some"],[22,318.24,3.71,"of the vocabulary that we used during this conversation."],[22,321.95,4.96,"And we'll start with one of my favourite adjectives, which is 'cosy'."],[22,326.91,3.75,"And that's the feeling of being comfortable and warm,"],[22,330.66,5.71,"especially when perhaps outside it's not so comfortable and warm."],[22,336.38,2.59,"Yeah. And we also heard 'bonfire'."],[22,338.96,2.96,"A bonfire might make you feel cosy."],[22,341.93,7.05,"A bonfire is a large fire we build outside for festivals and celebrations."],[22,348.97,4.25,"Yes, I remember celebrating Bonfire Night as a child"],[22,353.23,2.63,"and we also saw fireworks."],[22,355.86,5.26,"Fireworks are explosions in the sky, which make bright, colourful lights"],[22,361.11,1.63,"for everyone to watch."],[22,362.74,3.96,"We were also talking about the leaves that fall on the ground,"],[22,366.7,4.84,"and when you walk on those leaves when they're dry, it's crunchy."],[22,371.54,5.88,"And 'crunchy' describes the sound that those leaves make and also the sound"],[22,377.42,1.88,"when you bite into an apple."],[22,379.3,3.29,"And before the leaves fall, they are orange,"],[22,382.59,3.71,"and orange and red can be described as autumnal colours,"],[22,386.3,5.46,"'autumnal' being the adjective that describes things that are from autumn."],[22,391.77,3.09,"That's it for this episode of Real Easy English,"],[22,394.85,3.34,"but join us again next week for another episode."],[22,398.19,2.54,"Yes, and you can find more episodes,"],[22,400.73,6.13,"plus this one and a free worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com."],[22,406.87,0.62,"Bye for now."],[22,407.49,1.08,"Bye!"],[23,6.12,1.88,"Bluey – The Doctor."],[23,8.0,2.24,"Read by me, DanTDM."],[23,10.52,2.0,"There's a crocodile on my head!"],[23,12.52,2.6,"Oh my, this is a real pickle."],[23,15.12,1.36,"Don't worry! - Ugh!"],[23,16.48,1.88,"We'll fix that in a jiffy."],[23,18.36,2.52,"Edna, hold my appointments!"],[23,24.96,3.6,"Bluey the receptionist is working at the doctor's office."],[23,28.84,1.4,"Ring, ring!"],[23,30.24,1.4,"\"This is the doctor's office."],[23,31.64,3.88,"Hello, it's Edna speaking, how are you?\""],[23,36.92,2.12,"Honey arrives at reception."],[23,39.04,2.6,"\"Hello, I need to see the doctor now.\""],[23,42.64,2.4,"But Edna is still on the phone."],[23,45.04,2.84,"\"Hmm, yes, okay, sure."],[23,47.88,3.0,"Okay, we'll see you in fifteen months. Bye!"],[23,54.84,1.28,"Sorry about that."],[23,56.12,1.48,"How can I help?\""],[23,57.6,2.4,"\"I need to see the doctor.\""],[23,60.0,2.12,"\"Oh yes,\" says Edna."],[23,62.12,2.92,"\"I'm sure we can squeeze you in tightly."],[23,65.04,2.72,"Now, what's wrong with you today?\""],[23,67.76,2.32,"\"Nothing, I'm fine.\""],[23,71.04,4.32,"\"Honey, you have to make up something wrong with you,\" Bluey whispers."],[23,76.4,2.32,"\"What do you think should be wrong with me?\""],[23,78.72,3.0,"\"It's up to you,\" says Edna."],[23,81.92,5.16,"\"Um, what if I… bumped my knee?\" she asks."],[23,87.08,2.72,"\"Okay, here's a clipboard,\" Edna says."],[23,89.8,3.16,"\"Please take a seat. The doctor won't be long.\""],[23,92.96,3.24,"Honey walks over to a seat in the waiting room."],[23,96.2,2.76,"\"Honey, you have to hobble when you walk.\""],[23,98.96,2.0,"\"Oh, yes!\""],[23,101.88,2.52,"\"Next patient, please!\""],[23,104.4,2.44,"\"That's me!\""],[23,106.84,2.84,"\"What seems to be the matter?\" the doctor asks."],[23,109.68,3.0,"\"She's bumped her knee, Doctor!\" says Edna."],[23,112.92,2.2,"\"Oh, you sure that's it?\""],[23,115.12,3.6,"Bingo was hoping it was something more exciting."],[23,118.8,2.96,"\"Yeah, that's it,\" replies Honey."],[23,121.76,3.0,"\"Okay, we'll have that fixed in a jiffy.\""],[23,125.0,3.0,"But Lucky runs in with an emergency!"],[23,128.0,2.16,"\"Argh! I need to see the doctor!"],[23,130.16,2.44,"There's a crocodile on my head!\""],[23,132.6,3.88,"\"Oh my, this is a real pickle,\" says the doctor."],[23,136.48,2.6,"\"Don't worry, we'll fix that in a jiffy."],[23,139.08,2.56,"Edna, hold my appointments!\""],[23,143.12,3.2,"\"Oh, I'm sorry, dear,\" Edna says to Honey."],[23,146.32,1.28,"\"Please take a seat."],[23,147.6,2.64,"I'm sure the doctor won't be long.\""],[23,150.24,3.0,"\"This is gonna take ages!\""],[23,153.96,4.2,"Honey waits patiently while the doctor deals with the wily crocodile."],[23,159.32,2.32,"\"Would you like a cup of tea while you wait?\""],[23,161.64,1.32,"\"Yes, thanks!\""],[23,163.8,3.72,"Bluey tries to help Honey think of a bigger pickle."],[23,167.52,5.76,"\"Are you sure you haven't been attacked by a giraffe or something?\" she asks."],[23,173.28,3.08,"\"Cos that would be a real pickle…\""],[23,176.36,2.04,"\"Umm, no.\""],[23,179.56,3.48,"Out comes Lucky, all better."],[23,183.04,3.56,"\"Just keep the bandages on for eleven years.\""],[23,187.84,2.96,"\"Okay, who's next?\" asks the doctor."],[23,190.8,2.24,"\"That's me!\" says Honey."],[23,193.04,3.84,"\"Oh yes, sore knee. Right this way.\""],[23,196.88,2.96,"Now Snickers runs in with an emergency!"],[23,199.84,2.96,"\"I cuddled a cactus!\" he yells."],[23,202.8,3.6,"\"And then a crocodile fell out of the sky and bit me on the bottom!\""],[23,207.16,2.04,"\"My stars!\" says Edna."],[23,209.2,1.88,"\"This is a real pickle!\""],[23,213.48,3.48,"\"Don't worry, we'll have you fixed in a jiffy!\" says the doctor."],[23,216.96,2.56,"\"Edna, hold my appointments!\""],[23,219.52,4.4,"\"Oww, oww, oww, oww, oww, oww.\""],[23,223.92,2.84,"\"Oh! But what about my knee?\""],[23,226.76,3.0,"asks Honey sadly."],[23,232.0,3.0,"She sits down and waits patiently again."],[23,235.32,2.44,"Edna brings her another cup of tea."],[23,237.76,2.96,"\"I'm sorry about the wait.\""],[23,240.72,3.2,"\"Am I definitely the next patient?\" Honey asks."],[23,243.92,3.0,"\"You are definitely the next patient.\""],[23,248.28,3.6,"Now Indy and Coco arrive with an emergency!"],[23,251.88,2.84,"\"We need to see the doctor!\" screams Indy."],[23,254.72,1.52,"\"Oh, my!\""],[23,256.24,3.6,"\"I was hanging out the washing and my arms fell off!\" says Indy."],[23,260.24,3.76,"\"And I got bitten by seven scorpions,\" adds Coco."],[23,265.92,4.56,"\"Aw. This looks like a real pickle,\" says Honey."],[23,270.48,1.28,"\"It is!\""],[23,271.76,3.96,"\"I'm never going to see the doctor,\" she says."],[23,275.72,1.12,"\"Don't worry, Honey."],[23,276.84,3.36,"I promise you'll be the next patient,\" says Edna."],[23,281.2,1.04,"\"Really?\""],[23,282.24,3.0,"\"Yes, definitely.\""],[23,285.76,1.64,"Someone else rushes in."],[23,287.4,2.92,"\"I need a doctor!\" Rusty screams."],[23,290.32,1.44,"\"Yahhh!\""],[23,292.44,2.88,"\"What's wrong, Rusty?\" asks Edna."],[23,295.32,2.52,"\"I accidentally ate a hippopotamus."],[23,297.84,1.76,"And now when I burp,"],[23,299.6,3.08,"I burp out baby hippopotamuses.\""],[23,303.56,2.2,"\"Ah! Hippos everywhere!\""],[23,305.76,1.32,"\"Quick, Indy, grab it!\""],[23,307.08,2.52,"\"But I've got no arms!\""],[23,309.6,2.64,"\"Oh my goodness!\" the doctor says."],[23,312.24,1.76,"This is a real pickle."],[23,314.0,3.0,"Rusty, you best come in here right away."],[23,317.16,2.56,"Edna, hold my appointments!\""],[23,319.72,1.52,"\"Ohhhh!\""],[23,322.96,2.64,"\"I knew it,\" says Honey."],[23,325.6,4.52,"\"Everyone's problem is a real pickle, except mine.\""],[23,330.12,3.0,"\"Don't be sad,\" says Bluey."],[23,333.32,6.2,"\"Oh, there's another one. Burp! They're everywhere!\""],[23,342.12,2.8,"Honey isn't the best at thinking up pickles."],[23,344.92,3.0,"She starts swaying her legs."],[23,349.88,2.12,"Bluey sways along too."],[23,353.6,1.96,"\"He-he!\" \"He-he!\""],[23,357.08,2.8,"\"You always make me laugh,\" says Bluey."],[23,359.88,3.24,"\"Yeah, I always make you laugh,\" replies Honey."],[23,363.12,1.68,"\"Do it again,\" says Bluey."],[23,364.8,1.36,"\"Do something funny!\""],[23,366.16,2.04,"Honey has an idea."],[23,368.2,1.88,"\"Okay, watch this!\""],[23,371.88,1.88,"\"Now, for most dogs,\" she says,"],[23,373.76,3.16,"\"the tail wags and the body stays still."],[23,376.92,1.48,"But not me.\""],[23,381.72,3.0,"\"He-he!\" \"That's so funny!\""],[23,385.28,3.0,"Rusty and the doctor come back to the waiting room."],[23,388.44,1.84,"\"Thanks, Doctor,\" Rusty says."],[23,390.28,2.88,"\"I won't eat any more hippopotamuses.\""],[23,393.92,2.32,"Then the doctor sees Honey!"],[23,396.24,3.24,"\"Oh, my stars! Your tail and your body are all mixed up!\""],[23,400.16,3.04,"\"Her body is wagging!\" \"But her tail is still!\""],[23,407.24,4.2,"\"This is a real pickle,\" says the doctor."],[23,411.44,3.16,"\"Really?\" asks Honey. \"A pickle?\""],[23,414.6,4.12,"\"This is the biggest pickle I've ever seen!"],[23,418.76,2.16,"Come with me right away.\""],[23,421.92,3.4,"Finally, it's Honey's turn to see the doctor."],[23,425.32,3.8,"\"Don't worry, we'll have you fixed up in a jiffy,\" says the doctor."],[23,429.6,1.92,"\"Ohh!\""],[23,431.52,1.08,"\"Hobble!\""],[23,434.4,3.68,"\"Edna, you best hold all of my appointments.\""],[23,438.68,2.68,"\"Will the doctor be much longer?\""],[23,442.64,2.24,"\"Oh, just be patient!\""],[23,446.6,1.56,"The end."],[24,6.16,1.76,"Bluey Cricket"],[24,7.96,2.2,"read by me, Toni Collette."],[24,15.44,3.88,"Let me tell you about the time we tried to get Rusty out."],[24,19.4,2.92,"It was some kid's birthday, I don't remember who,"],[24,22.32,2.04,"and we were playing cricket."],[24,29.04,2.68,"\"Dad, can we play tiggy instead?\""],[24,31.72,3.12,"\"What?! Cricket's more fun than tiggy.\""],[24,34.84,3.68,"\"But cricket's just about hitting a ball around the grass.\""],[24,38.72,2.68,"\"Cricket's about more than that, kid."],[24,41.4,0.96,"Tell you what:"],[24,42.36,3.0,"we'll get Rusty out and then play tiggy.\""],[24,47.4,3.0,"\"You'll never get Rusty out.\""],[24,51.72,2.36,"\"I think I can handle it.\""],[24,55.52,1.48,"Whack!"],[24,58.88,1.32,"\"Ahhh!\""],[24,61.44,1.04,"\"See?\""],[24,63.8,1.48,"What we didn't know…"],[24,66.32,4.64,"…was that Rusty played cricket all day with his brother and sister."],[24,71.64,1.92,"And when they weren't around,"],[24,73.56,1.32,"he'd play by himself."],[24,75.44,2.04,"Whack! Whack! Whack!"],[24,77.48,3.2,"Rusty really loved cricket."],[24,81.96,2.72,"\"Look, he likes the square cut."],[24,84.68,3.04,"Stripe, you and me will move to gully and point."],[24,87.72,2.52,"Bandit, you send one to his off stump."],[24,90.24,2.12,"Lure him into the catch.\""],[24,92.36,1.12,"But we didn't know…"],[24,94.48,2.68,"…about Rusty's kitchen window."],[24,99.68,1.2,"Bang!"],[24,102.68,1.2,"\"Oh-oh!\""],[24,104.48,1.72,"\"Run!\""],[24,110.04,3.0,"That was good motivation to work on his square cut."],[24,113.36,1.2,"Whack!"],[24,118.48,1.28,"Whack!"],[24,121.2,1.64,"Doink!"],[24,125.16,1.88,"I had no chance."],[24,128.12,1.56,"Whack!"],[24,130.68,3.0,"It was time to mix up the attack."],[24,133.72,3.28,"\"Gimme that, I've had enough of your pies."],[24,137.0,2.32,"A bit of spin okay, Rusty?\""],[24,140.52,1.76,"\"All good, Mr Heeler.\""],[24,142.76,2.8,"Now, Stripe couldn't spin to save himself."],[24,145.56,2.96,"But if he could land it on that crack,"],[24,148.52,1.4,"Rusty wouldn't have a chance."],[24,150.4,2.36,"What we didn't know was that…"],[24,153.84,2.12,"…when Rusty's dad was away,"],[24,155.96,3.04,"Rusty would get a lift home after school with his mate Jack."],[24,159.04,3.0,"And this was Jack's front yard."],[24,162.4,2.4,"Stone. Tree root. Stones."],[24,164.8,3.6,"Uneven dirt. Tree root. Tree root."],[24,168.4,3.44,"Rusty played at Jack's a lot that year,"],[24,171.84,3.16,"so he got pretty good at playing on a rough wicket."],[24,179.28,3.0,"Rusty could handle Stripe's fruit any day."],[24,182.28,1.36,"Whack!"],[24,184.52,1.92,"\"How did he hit that?\""],[24,187.8,3.28,"Now, every batsman has a weak spot…"],[24,192.08,2.32,"\"Okay if I have a go, Rusty?\""],[24,195.52,3.0,"…and Rusty's was pace."],[24,199.72,2.84,"Sometimes he would play with his older brother's mates."],[24,202.56,2.28,"One of them – a kid called Tiny –"],[24,204.84,2.12,"could bowl really fast."],[24,207.52,3.0,"Rusty was never brave enough to bat."],[24,212.64,2.96,"Until one day, he was."],[24,216.0,2.76,"\"Rusty, you can bat if you want,"],[24,218.76,2.28,"but no one's going to go easy on you.\""],[24,222.64,2.2,"They didn't play with a tennis ball, either."],[24,224.84,3.0,"They used a proper cricket ball."],[24,229.12,2.2,"And they are hard."],[24,231.32,1.52,"Whack!"],[24,234.96,2.44,"Rusty didn't want to give up."],[24,237.4,3.0,"But he was so scared of Tiny."],[24,243.36,1.76,"One day a letter came."],[24,245.12,1.88,"It was from Rusty's dad."],[24,247.0,3.0,"\"…and Digger, you thought Christmas up north was hot!"],[24,250.0,1.52,"You should try over here."],[24,251.52,2.12,"It is boiling."],[24,253.64,1.76,"I'll see you all soon."],[24,255.4,2.24,"Miss you heaps. Dad.\""],[24,259.56,3.12,"There was a special PS just for Rusty."],[24,264.92,2.92,"\"This Tiny sounds like he's got some pace on him."],[24,269.36,2.6,"I wish I could be there to bowl a few at you,"],[24,271.96,2.04,"but I can't right now, mate."],[24,274.0,2.36,"But look, as you grow up,"],[24,276.36,2.68,"you'll face harder things than a cricket ball,"],[24,279.04,1.44,"and you'll have two choices:"],[24,281.24,1.64,"back away and get out…"],[24,283.8,3.0,"…or step in front and play a pull shot.\""],[24,287.28,1.88,"Whack!"],[24,289.16,1.96,"\"Just keep your eye on the ball"],[24,291.12,2.6,"and take care of your little sister for me."],[24,293.72,1.48,"Love, Dad.\""],[24,299.84,3.0,"Lucky's dad had no chance."],[24,303.88,1.2,"Whack!"],[24,305.68,1.76,"\"Lunchtime!\""],[24,308.88,1.68,"\"Nah, hang on!"],[24,310.56,1.68,"Rusty, one more?\""],[24,312.24,1.08,"\"Okay.\""],[24,313.32,2.52,"Rusty would have played fifty more."],[24,317.36,3.12,"He just loved cricket."],[24,326.64,1.48,"\"Come on, Pat."],[24,328.12,1.52,"This one's for dignity.\""],[24,332.76,3.0,"Lucky's dad put everything he had into that ball."],[24,337.2,3.72,"And Rusty could have smashed it into next week if he wanted to."],[24,341.04,1.2,"Pop!"],[24,343.56,1.56,"But instead…"],[24,347.04,2.84,"…he hit his little sister a catch."],[24,352.2,1.92,"\"I caught it!"],[24,354.96,1.8,"I got you out!\""],[24,357.12,3.4,"\"I know! I can't believe it. What a catch!\""],[24,362.64,1.52,"Bluey…"],[24,364.64,2.76,"That's what cricket's about."],[24,371.24,1.64,"The end."],[25,0.04,5.88,"Does your English sometimes sound a bit unnatural and disconnected?"],[25,5.92,5.92,"In this episode, I'm going to talk about my two favorite places in the UK"],[25,11.84,4.04,"and help you speak more naturally when you talk about yours."],[25,15.88,2.52,"We'll practise shadowing, and focus on three linking sounds"],[25,18.4,4.2,"that can make your speech more fluent."],[25,22.6,2.16,"This is a shadowing lesson."],[25,24.76,1.36,"In a shadowing lesson,"],[25,26.12,5.64,"you listen carefully, and repeat what I say almost at the same time,"],[25,31.76,1.72,"like an echo."],[25,33.48,1.52,"Here's a quick demo."],[25,35.0,4.08,"As the name suggests, there are lakes -"],[25,39.08,1.72,"quite a few of them, in fact."],[25,40.8,2.28,"If you're not sure what shadowing is,"],[25,43.08,3.04,"click here to watch my tutorial."],[25,46.12,2.48,"Are you ready? Let's go!"],[25,51.44,2.32,"If you want to enjoy a relaxing holiday"],[25,53.76,2.72,"in the UK, surrounded by nature,"],[25,56.48,3.96,"then you can't go wrong with England's largest national park,"],[25,60.44,2.64,"the Lake District."],[25,63.08,2.84,"As its name suggests, there are lakes -"],[25,65.92,3.32,"quite a few of them, in fact."],[25,69.24,5.2,"There are also plenty of mountains, hills, and forests full of wildlife."],[25,74.44,2.84,"You might even see a red squirrel."],[25,77.28,3.96,"Also, don't forget to try the gingerbread in Grasmere."],[25,81.24,2.2,"I always have some when I go -"],[25,83.44,2.92,"it's incredibly moreish."],[25,86.36,2.72,"Let's have a look at the pronunciation."],[25,89.08,3.6,"I used the words 'there are' twice,"],[25,92.68,3.76,"and there's something interesting to learn about the pronunciation."],[25,96.44,6.44,"If I say 'there' on its own, the 'r' isn't pronounced. There."],[25,102.88,8.0,"But listen to what happens when I say 'there are': 'there are', 'there are'."],[25,110.88,2.36,"There are two things happening here."],[25,113.24,4.36,"First, the word 'are' changes to /ə/."],[25,117.6,4.36,"Second, we pronounce the 'r' in 'there'."],[25,121.96,1.68,"Why is this?"],[25,123.64,6.96,"When a word after 'there' starts with a vowel sound, we pronounce the 'r'."],[25,130.6,3.6,"This is the linking /r/ sound."],[25,134.2,1.8,"Repeat after me."],[25,136.0,1.8,"There are lakes."],[25,141.32,3.48,"There are also plenty of mountains."],[25,148.76,2.28,"Listen to the sentence again."],[25,151.04,4.04,"There are also plenty of mountains."],[25,155.08,2.84,"Did you hear the linking /r/ between"],[25,157.92,1.92,"'are' and 'also'?"],[25,159.84,4.08,"That's because 'also' starts with a vowel sound."],[25,163.92,2.36,"Repeat after me."],[25,166.28,3.76,"There are also plenty of mountains."],[25,173.24,3.0,"So we've had the linking /r/,"],[25,176.24,3.24,"but there's another linking sound as well."],[25,179.48,1.96,"Listen carefully."],[25,181.44,5.24,"What extra sound do you hear between these words?"],[25,186.68,5.4,"'Enjoy a'. 'Holiday in the UK'."],[25,192.08,4.16,"'See a'. 'I always'."],[25,196.24,3.0,"The extra sound is /j/."],[25,199.24,10.36,"We use a linking /j/ when a word ends in sounds like /ɔɪ/, /əɪ/, /i:/, /aɪ/."],[25,209.6,3.2,"and the next word begins with a vowel."],[25,212.8,1.32,"Let's practise."],[25,214.12,2.04,"Repeat after me."],[25,216.16,1.6,"Enjoy a."],[25,220.72,1.72,"Holiday in."],[25,225.28,1.28,"See a."],[25,229.16,1.84,"I always."],[25,233.96,1.72,"Remember these linking sounds"],[25,235.68,3.92,"when we shadow my travel recommendations again at the end."],[25,239.6,5.92,"Now let's find out about my other favorite place to visit in the UK."],[25,245.52,3.76,"If you have no interest in hiking and fancy a city break instead,"],[25,249.28,2.96,"how about Edinburgh?"],[25,252.24,3.44,"There's Edinburgh Castle, which was built on a volcanic rock"],[25,255.68,2.52,"in the 11th century."],[25,258.2,3.36,"You can go up Arthur's Seat, an extinct volcano"],[25,261.56,3.48,"with spectacular views of Edinburgh."],[25,265.04,1.6,"And if you go in August,"],[25,266.64,4.04,"you can go to a comedy show at the Edinburgh Fringe Festival, which is"],[25,270.68,3.2,"the world's largest performing arts festival."],[25,273.88,6.24,"Listen carefully. What extra sound can you hear between these words?"],[25,280.12,2.08,"No interest."],[25,282.2,1.92,"How about."],[25,284.12,2.0,"Go up."],[25,286.12,1.04,"Go in."],[25,287.16,1.69,"To a."],[25,289.16,1.6,"Did you hear it?"],[25,290.76,2.24,"The answer is: /w/."],[25,293.0,6.32,"We use a linking /w/ sound when a word ends in a rounded vowel."],[25,299.32,3.12,"So your lips are rounded like this."],[25,302.44,3.32,"And the next word begins with a vowel."],[25,305.76,10.12,"This happens after sounds like /əʊ/ in 'no' and 'go', /aʊ/ in 'how', and /u:/ in two."],[25,315.88,2.08,"Repeat after me."],[25,317.96,2.0,"No interest."],[25,322.12,1.72,"How about."],[25,326.36,1.56,"Go up."],[25,330.68,1.6,"Go in."],[25,335.04,1.24,"To a."],[25,339.04,3.4,"Now, let's shadow the whole text again."],[25,342.44,7.88,"This time, focus on these linking sounds, and see if your speech sounds smoother."],[25,353.16,2.36,"If you want to enjoy a relaxing holiday"],[25,355.52,2.68,"in the UK, surrounded by nature,"],[25,358.2,3.92,"then you can't go wrong with England's largest national park,"],[25,362.12,2.68,"the Lake District."],[25,364.8,4.84,"As its name suggests, there are lakes - quite a few of them, in fact."],[25,369.64,4.24,"There are also plenty of mountains, hills"],[25,373.88,2.32,"and forests full of wildlife."],[25,376.2,2.84,"You might even see a red squirrel."],[25,379.04,3.88,"Also, don't forget to try the gingerbread in Grasmere."],[25,382.92,2.28,"I always have some when I go -"],[25,385.2,3.84,"it's incredibly moreish."],[25,389.04,3.76,"If you have no interest in hiking and fancy a city break instead,"],[25,392.8,2.92,"how about Edinburgh?"],[25,395.72,3.48,"There's Edinburgh Castle, which was built on a volcanic rock"],[25,399.2,2.52,"in the 11th century."],[25,401.72,3.32,"You can go up Arthur's Seat, an extinct volcano"],[25,405.04,3.48,"with spectacular views of Edinburgh."],[25,408.52,5.98,"And if you go in August, you can go to a comedy show at the Edinburgh Fringe Festival,"],[25,414.5,3.1,"which is the world's largest performing arts festival."],[25,417.6,3.08,"How was your pronunciation the second time?"],[25,420.68,1.96,"Did it sound more natural?"],[25,422.64,5.64,"Try shadowing again, and record yourself, so you can really hear the difference."],[25,428.28,5.24,"You can download the audio file of my travel recommendations by clicking here."],[25,433.52,3.36,"And for more information on linking sounds,"],[25,436.88,3.72,"check out Tim's Pronunciation Workshop here."],[25,440.6,1.52,"What about you?"],[25,442.12,3.68,"What places should we visit in your country?"],[25,445.8,5.92,"Share your travel recommendations in the comments below. Goodbye."],[26,0.16,1.92,"From BBC Learning English."],[26,2.08,4.92,"This is Learning English from the News, our podcast about the news headlines."],[26,7.0,1.28,"In this programme,"],[26,8.28,3.88,"deadly attacks on airport in Saudi Arabia."],[26,16.04,1.08,"Hello, I'm Neil."],[26,17.12,1.24,"And I'm Pippa."],[26,18.36,2.84,"In this programme we look at one big news story"],[26,21.2,3.68,"and the vocabulary and the headlines that will help you understand it."],[26,24.88,3.56,"You can find all the vocabulary and headlines from this episode,"],[26,28.44,4.76,"as well as a worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com."],[26,33.2,2.56,"Let's hear more about this story."],[26,41.24,2.0,"14 people have been killed"],[26,43.24,4.52,"and over 300 injured in two attacks on Riyadh International Airport"],[26,47.76,2.24,"in Saudi Arabia over the weekend."],[26,50.0,3.24,"There were also a series of attacks earlier in the week."],[26,53.24,2.76,"The militant group the Houthis in Yemen said they were"],[26,56.0,4.04,"behind the attacks, as well as others on airports across Saudi Arabia,"],[26,60.04,4.44,"and warned airlines and passengers against using Saudi Arabian airports"],[26,64.48,1.4,"and airspace."],[26,65.88,2.2,"Let's have our first headline."],[26,68.08,2.48,"This is from BBC News."],[26,70.56,5.24,"CCTV shows people take cover as missile hits Riyadh airport."],[26,75.8,2.64,"That headline again from BBC news."],[26,78.44,5.28,"CCTV shows people take cover as missile hits Riyadh airport."],[26,83.72,4.12,"This headline is from a news story containing a CCTV clip"],[26,87.84,2.2,"of one of the attacks on the airport."],[26,90.04,1.84,"We're interested in this phrase"],[26,91.88,1.12,"'take cover'."],[26,93.0,4.24,"Yes, 'take cover' means to move to a place where you are more protected,"],[26,97.24,3.24,"for example, underneath a table or behind a door."],[26,100.48,3.68,"You might see the phrase 'take cover', used in stories about attacks"],[26,104.16,2.64,"or danger where people need to get to safety."],[26,106.8,3.88,"The phrase, 'take cover' isn't only used in serious situations"],[26,110.68,4.08,"though. We can also use 'take cover' in more everyday contexts."],[26,114.76,2.24,"For example, you might take cover"],[26,117.0,3.92,"during a rainstorm to protect yourself from getting wet."],[26,124.72,4.92,"We've had 'take cover' - move to a place where you are more protected."],[26,132.4,4.0,"For example, officials advised people to take cover inside"],[26,136.4,2.12,"during the tropical storm."],[26,142.28,4.12,"This is Learning English from the News, our podcast about the news headlines."],[26,146.4,4.24,"Today we're talking about attacks on Saudi Arabia's airports."],[26,150.64,2.76,"A Saudi government minister has said the country will have"],[26,153.4,2.4,"a serious response to the attacks."],[26,155.8,2.8,"The Houthis have said they are targeting Saudi Arabia"],[26,158.6,3.92,"over its support for government forces in Yemen's civil war."],[26,162.52,2.04,"Many countries, including the UK,"],[26,164.56,3.08,"have advised against travelling to Saudi Arabia."],[26,167.64,1.8,"Let's have another headline."],[26,169.44,4.0,"This is from The Economist, an international news magazine"],[26,173.44,2.68,"Saudi Arabia's Houthi headache."],[26,176.12,2.56,"That headline, again from The Economist."],[26,178.68,2.92,"Saudi Arabia's Houthi headache."],[26,181.6,3.48,"This headline is from an article about the recent attacks."],[26,185.08,2.76,"It describes the situation as a headache."],[26,187.84,1.32,"What's going on here, Neil?"],[26,189.16,2.56,"Well, we can use the word 'headache' metaphorically"],[26,191.72,3.76,"to describe a particularly difficult problem that's hard to solve"],[26,195.48,2.36,"or that continues for a long time."],[26,197.84,3.96,"In this story, there have been several attacks across a whole week,"],[26,201.8,3.64,"causing death, injury and major disruption in Saudi Arabia."],[26,205.44,2.6,"The headline describes this problem as a headache."],[26,208.04,3.2,"We often use 'headache' to describe problems faced by governments"],[26,211.24,1.72,"which are difficult to solve."],[26,212.96,3.52,"For example, politicians don't always agree on how to deal"],[26,216.48,2.44,"with the headache of rising prices."],[26,218.92,4.8,"So in the headline, 'headache' is used to describe a very serious situation."],[26,223.72,3.44,"But you can also use 'headache' to describe difficult problems"],[26,227.16,1.4,"in everyday life."],[26,228.56,3.4,"For example, I'm trying to plan a family reunion at the moment,"],[26,231.96,5.44,"but it's a bit of a headache to organise because nobody's free at the same time."],[26,240.32,3.0,"We had 'headache' - problem that's hard to solve"],[26,243.32,2.56,"or that continues for a long time."],[26,248.48,1.12,"For example,"],[26,249.6,4.68,"spotting AI essays is becoming a bit of a headache for teachers."],[26,258.08,3.68,"This is Learning English from the News from BBC Learning English."],[26,261.76,4.68,"We're talking about attacks on Saudi Arabia's airports by Houthi forces."],[26,266.44,3.48,"As we've mentioned, there have been several attacks over the past week"],[26,269.92,3.4,"and multiple attacks on the same airport in Riyadh."],[26,273.32,2.8,"Saudi Arabia has launched an investigation into"],[26,276.12,3.64,"how its air defences were unable to stop the attacks."],[26,279.76,2.56,"Our next headline is about this."],[26,282.32,5.52,"Saudis probe how defense systems failed to stop deadly Houthi attack on Riyadh airport."],[26,287.84,3.76,"And that's from AP news, a US news agency."],[26,291.6,2.56,"That headline again from AP news."],[26,294.16,4.6,"Saudi's probe how defense systems failed to stop deadly Houthi attack"],[26,298.76,1.4,"on Riyadh airport."],[26,300.16,2.72,"This headline says \"Saudis probe\"."],[26,302.88,3.48,"Pippa, can you explain more about this verb 'probe'?"],[26,306.36,5.04,"Yes, 'probe' as a verb means to gather information or investigate something."],[26,311.4,3.48,"In this story, Saudi Arabian officials are probing"],[26,314.88,3.8,"why their air defenses were unable to stop the attacks."],[26,318.68,3.2,"In other words, they're investigating the recent attacks."],[26,321.88,2.32,"We can also use 'probe' as a noun,"],[26,324.2,2.56,"and it means an investigation into something."],[26,326.76,4.12,"For example, a police probe into organised crime could lead"],[26,330.88,2.04,"to important arrests."],[26,332.92,3.24,"Another meaning of 'probe' as a noun is to talk about a piece"],[26,336.2,3.16,"of technology designed to gather information."],[26,339.36,4.8,"For example, scientists send probes into space to gather information"],[26,344.16,1.4,"about other planets."],[26,345.56,2.96,"And we also have the adjective 'probing'."],[26,348.52,4.28,"If you ask probing questions, you ask questions that you hope will find out"],[26,352.8,2.56,"important or hidden information."],[26,357.92,4.04,"We've had 'probe' - investigate or gather information."],[26,362.0,4.04,"For example, people have been probing into the singer's private life"],[26,366.04,3.24,"since she got divorced."],[26,369.28,2.88,"That's it for this episode of Learning English from the News."],[26,372.16,2.84,"We'll be back next week with another news story."],[26,375.04,3.24,"If you found this programme useful, why not head to our website"],[26,378.28,4.2,"to sign up for our email newsletter and get all of our latest programmes."],[26,382.48,3.08,"Head to bbclearningenglish.com."],[26,385.6,2.0,"Goodbye for now. Bye."],[27,6.2,1.0,"BBQ,"],[27,7.24,2.44,"read by me, Julia Stiles."],[27,10.44,2.52,"- Ugh, come on! - Ugh, come on!"],[27,12.96,1.32,"- Come on, start! - Start!"],[27,14.28,1.68,"- Come on, you heap of junk. - Come on…"],[27,15.96,1.32,"Heap of junk!"],[27,17.52,2.64,"- Whoosh! [Together] There it goes!"],[27,23.64,2.56,"The Heelers are having a BBQ."],[27,26.2,1.4,"Bingo sets the table"],[27,27.6,2.28,"then sits down to relax."],[27,29.88,2.12,"\"This is my relaxer chair."],[27,32.0,2.0,"It's where I relax.\""],[27,34.0,3.2,"\"Almost ready with the sausages, kids!\" says Bluey."],[27,37.2,1.68,"\"Ruff ruff rowwrrr!\""],[27,38.88,1.92,"\"I love sausages!\""],[27,45.24,3.0,"Muffin sees Mum put down a salad."],[27,48.4,3.96,"\"We have to have salad with our BBQ!\" shouts Muffin."],[27,52.36,1.88,"\"Bingo?\" asks Bluey."],[27,54.24,2.24,"\"Can you be in charge of the salads?\""],[27,56.48,1.4,"Bingo sighs."],[27,57.88,6.08,"\"Well, I was relaxing in my relaxer chair… but okay.\""],[27,63.96,2.48,"Bingo borrows a hat from Dad."],[27,66.44,3.88,"\"Okay,\" says Bingo. \"This is the salad bowl.\""],[27,70.32,3.0,"Bingo goes back to relaxing in her relaxer chair."],[27,74.32,2.28,"\"You need to put salad in it!\""],[27,77.88,2.88,"\"I like capsicum salad!\" says Muffin."],[27,80.76,1.8,"\"Ooh, me too,\" Bluey says."],[27,82.56,1.92,"\"I love capsicum.\""],[27,84.48,2.64,"\"Rowrr rowrr! Glean capsimummm!\""],[27,87.12,3.0,"\"Socks says she likes green capsicum the best.\""],[27,91.08,1.84,"Bingo sighs."],[27,92.92,2.12,"\"Okay,\" she says, looking around."],[27,95.04,2.12,"\"I can get green capsicum.\""],[27,98.04,2.88,"\"Green is easy,\" she says, but…"],[27,101.68,1.72,"\"Argh, thorns!"],[27,104.68,3.0,"Eek, bug!"],[27,109.68,1.6,"This one's okay.\""],[27,112.24,3.04,"\"There,\" Bingo says. \"Green capsicum salad.\""],[27,116.52,2.64,"\"Ah, so relaxing.\""],[27,120.2,2.92,"\"Can I have some yellow capsicum?\" Bluey asks."],[27,123.12,2.4,"\"Yellow's my favourite colour capsicum.\""],[27,125.52,1.8,"\"Ohhh!\""],[27,128.48,3.48,"\"Hmm, yellow capsicum.\""],[27,135.44,2.64,"\"That's not safe, Bingo. Down you get.\""],[27,139.52,2.04,"\"Oh, Mum!\""],[27,142.6,1.84,"\"Hmm…"],[27,145.88,1.28,"Ooh."],[27,151.16,1.8,"Got it!\""],[27,154.4,1.84,"\"Here you go,\" Bingo says."],[27,156.24,2.04,"\"Yellow capsicum."],[27,159.24,3.16,"Now I'm gonna relax. Ahhhh.\""],[27,164.04,3.96,"\"My favourite capsicum is red capsicum,\" says Muffin."],[27,168.84,1.8,"Bingo groans."],[27,170.64,2.64,"\"There's no red plants around, Muffin.\""],[27,173.28,3.76,"\"Yes there is! In that tree!\" shouts Muffin."],[27,177.04,3.56,"\"Oh yeah,\" says Bingo, \"the poinciana.\""],[27,185.52,1.04,"It's hard work"],[27,186.56,2.68,"but Bingo tries again and again until she gets it."],[27,190.08,1.8,"Whack!"],[27,193.36,1.48,"\"Yeah!\""],[27,196.72,4.76,"\"There! Red capsicum,\" Bingo says, out of breath."],[27,201.48,3.12,"She's glad there aren't any more colours of capsicum left!"],[27,205.96,6.0,"\"So, now I'm just gonna relax in my relaxer chair,\" she sighs."],[27,212.48,3.16,"But then Muffin spots the salad dressing."],[27,216.8,2.96,"\"We need salad dressing!\" she shouts."],[27,219.76,2.16,"\"Oooowwrrr!\" adds Socks."],[27,222.48,2.48,"\"Aw, okay.\""],[27,226.12,3.0,"First, Bingo needs some mud for the dressing."],[27,231.96,2.88,"\"Ooh, we're out of mud. I have to make some more.\""],[27,238.48,3.0,"But the hose goes wild!"],[27,241.56,2.88,"\"It's tickling my bottom!\""],[27,244.44,2.2,"\"Turn off the tap!\""],[27,248.04,2.4,"\"Got it!\" says Bingo."],[27,250.44,1.92,"\"My goodness."],[27,252.36,3.0,"This is not relaxing.\""],[27,256.24,2.88,"\"Salad. Dressing!\" she shouts."],[27,259.12,4.92,"\"Now I'm going to sit in my relaxer chair and relax!\""],[27,264.96,3.36,"Bingo starts relaxing when she hears Mum shout…"],[27,269.24,1.96,"\"Whose muddy footprints are these?\""],[27,274.2,1.8,"Bingo mops up the mud."],[27,276.0,3.0,"She's finally about to relax when…"],[27,280.0,2.0,"\"Sausages are ready!\""],[27,283.0,1.92,"\"Ohh!\""],[27,286.04,2.12,"\"Out of the way, salads!\" says Dad,"],[27,288.16,2.24,"shoving the salads to the side."],[27,290.4,1.72,"\"Look at those!\""],[27,292.12,1.28,"\"Yeah!\""],[27,293.44,2.0,"\"Well done, Uncle Bandit!\""],[27,295.44,1.88,"\"Ruff!\""],[27,297.32,1.68,"\"Looks good, Bandit.\""],[27,299.0,2.04,"\"Well done, Bandit!\""],[27,301.8,4.12,"\"Isn't anyone going to mention the salads?!\" Bingo yells."],[27,306.68,1.8,"\"Check 'em out, kids!\""],[27,308.48,2.36,"\"Oh, excellent salad!\""],[27,310.84,2.56,"\"Thank you. Thank you.\""],[27,314.32,2.24,"\"Thanks, Bingo,\" says Mum."],[27,317.2,3.0,"\"Now, relax.\""],[27,322.64,1.76,"The end."],[28,0.0,1.76,"From BBC Learning English."],[28,1.76,4.32,"This is Learning English from the News, our podcast about the news headlines."],[28,6.08,1.12,"In this programme,"],[28,7.2,3.0,"could AI kill us all?"],[28,13.92,2.28,"Hello, I'm Georgie. And I'm Phil."],[28,16.2,2.64,"In this programme, we look at one big news story"],[28,18.84,3.52,"and the vocabulary in the headlines that will help you understand it."],[28,22.36,3.4,"You can find all the vocabulary and headlines from this episode,"],[28,25.76,2.72,"as well as a worksheet on our website."],[28,28.48,2.36,"bbclearningenglish.com ."],[28,30.84,3.16,"OK, Phil, let's hear more about this story."],[28,39.52,2.64,"Head of Anthropic, Dario Amodei"],[28,42.16,2.4,"published an essay explaining how he feared"],[28,44.56,3.72,"that humans could lose control of AI systems."],[28,48.28,2.24,"Following recent hacking incidents,"],[28,50.52,2.08,"Amodei said that it is possible"],[28,52.6,4.44,"that AI systems could attempt to take over the entire internet in future."],[28,57.04,2.96,"He suggested that controls and checks should be introduced"],[28,60.0,1.68,"to the development process."],[28,61.68,3.28,"Heads of other rival AI companies have expressed some support"],[28,64.96,1.64,"for Amadei's position."],[28,66.6,4.6,"Meanwhile, AI experts have raised fears that AI systems could decide"],[28,71.2,3.2,"in future to kill all humans."],[28,74.4,3.8,"These experts have said that the risks include cyberattacks,"],[28,78.2,3.0,"biological warfare and disruption to economies."],[28,81.2,2.44,"These statements have been criticised."],[28,83.64,4.6,"Some have said that the fears are exaggerated, while others have suggested"],[28,88.24,4.12,"that AI companies are overstating the potential impact of AI"],[28,92.36,3.6,"so that their companies maintain a high value."],[28,95.96,1.04,"Others have suggested"],[28,97.0,4.2,"that AI companies want regulators to block their competitors."],[28,101.2,3.04,"Jack Clark, another Anthropic co-founder,"],[28,104.24,1.92,"has made a specific suggestion,"],[28,106.16,4.72,"saying that every AI system should have a way of being shut down."],[28,110.88,2.52,"And that is what our first headline is about."],[28,113.4,2.44,"This one is from BBC News."],[28,115.84,3.08,"AI kill switch may need to be mandatory,"],[28,118.92,2.8,"Anthropic co-founder tells BBC."],[28,121.72,2.48,"And that headline again from BBC News."],[28,124.2,5.64,"AI kill switch may need to be mandatory, Anthropic co-founder tells BBC."],[28,129.84,2.48,"So, Anthropic's co-founder has said"],[28,132.32,3.12,"that kill switches for AI should be mandatory."],[28,135.44,1.8,"That means you have to have them."],[28,137.24,2.84,"So, Phil, what is a kill switch?"],[28,140.08,5.44,"OK, well, a physical kill switch is an emergency stop button for machinery."],[28,145.52,2.28,"Imagine a big red button."],[28,147.8,5.04,"It's a control that can be used to shut off power quickly for safety reasons."],[28,152.84,4.08,"And they're used if the machine could become dangerous."],[28,156.92,3.4,"Yes. So, they can be found in factory machinery, gym equipment"],[28,160.32,2.16,"and some cars, trucks and boats."],[28,162.48,4.32,"In these examples, they are usually physical switches or buttons."],[28,166.8,3.44,"Smartphones may also have a kill switch function"],[28,170.24,2.84,"that can be activated remotely if they're stolen,"],[28,173.08,2.96,"to stop the thief being able to use the phone."],[28,176.04,3.52,"The co-founder of Anthropic is talking about a similar mechanism,"],[28,179.56,3.56,"where humans would be able to quickly turn off an AI system"],[28,183.12,1.64,"that was becoming dangerous."],[28,184.76,3.84,"He says that AI systems should have a kill switch."],[28,192.68,5.24,"We've had 'kill switch', an emergency stop mechanism."],[28,202.0,2.72,"For example, it's good to know how to use the kill switch"],[28,204.72,2.72,"on your phone in case it gets stolen."],[28,210.8,4.0,"This is Learning English from the News from BBC Learning English."],[28,214.8,4.96,"We're talking about calls to regulate the development of AI systems."],[28,219.76,4.68,"A lot of the concern around AI is that development is progressing too fast."],[28,224.44,2.92,"It's being developed before many people have properly thought"],[28,227.36,2.68,"about how it can be controlled."],[28,230.04,3.68,"The suggested regulations that industry figures are calling for"],[28,233.72,3.36,"could lead to development becoming slower."],[28,237.08,2.88,"Some critics have questioned what the real reasons are"],[28,239.96,3.28,"behind these calls for regulation and slower development."],[28,243.24,3.56,"And that's what our next headline is doing."],[28,246.8,4.36,"This is from Business Insider, an international business website."],[28,251.16,6.6,"The timing of AI leaders calls for a slowdown is too convenient to ignore."],[28,257.76,2.8,"And that headline, again from Business Insider."],[28,260.56,5.08,"The timing of AI leaders calls for a slowdown is too convenient to ignore."],[28,265.64,3.12,"This headline is suggesting that we should question the reasons"],[28,268.76,4.12,"why some leaders of AI companies are calling for regulation."],[28,272.88,3.56,"The writer of the article feels that these leaders want to make it harder"],[28,276.44,2.44,"for competitors to catch up with them."],[28,278.88,5.32,"Now, you probably know the verb 'slow down' - reduce the speed of something,"],[28,284.2,3.64,"but in this headline we have the noun, 'a slowdown',"],[28,287.84,1.92,"and that's what we're going to learn."],[28,289.76,2.68,"Yes, it can mean different things. In this story,"],[28,292.44,4.4,"it is specifically about adding checks and controls to the development process"],[28,296.84,4.4,"of AI tools, which could slow down the pace of development."],[28,301.24,3.08,"More generally, it means a reduction in speed."],[28,304.32,2.36,"If we talk about an economic slowdown,"],[28,306.68,4.68,"it means that bad economic conditions have made the economy grow more slowly."],[28,311.36,4.64,"Particularly in American English, a slowdown can also be a form of protest."],[28,316.0,2.96,"Workers in a company may deliberately work slowly"],[28,318.96,3.04,"as a protest about their pay or working conditions."],[28,322.0,2.2,"They may go on a slowdown."],[28,324.2,3.04,"And just notice the pronunciation here."],[28,327.24,4.52,"The verb 'slow down' has got stress on 'down',"],[28,331.76,4.56,"whereas the noun 'slowdown' has the stress on 'slow'."],[28,340.12,1.88,"We've had 'slowdown',"],[28,342.0,2.32,"a reduction in speed."],[28,347.0,3.08,"For example, environmentalists have called for a slowdown"],[28,350.08,2.4,"in production of plastics."],[28,358.24,3.44,"This is Learning English from the News, from BBC Learning English"],[28,361.68,2.68,"We're talking about possible risks of AI."],[28,364.36,2.2,"US President Donald Trump has suggested"],[28,366.56,3.8,"that regulation could stop US dominance in the sector."],[28,370.36,4.28,"The Chinese government has also criticised proposals to regulate AI."],[28,374.64,1.32,"Let's have our next headline."],[28,375.96,4.32,"And this is from CNN, a US-based news channel."],[28,380.28,5.24,"Trump dismisses AI alarms as a 'HOAX' - worrying White House officials."],[28,385.52,2.24,"And that headline again from CNN."],[28,387.8,5.36,"Trump dismisses AI alarms as a 'HOAX' - worrying White House officials."],[28,393.16,4.32,"Donald Trump has said that worries about AI are nothing but a hoax."],[28,397.48,2.48,"And that's the word we're going to learn now."],[28,399.96,2.8,"Yes, something that is a hoax is not true."],[28,402.76,4.0,"Not only that, it's something that has been planned to deceive people."],[28,406.76,2.04,"President Trump is saying that he believes"],[28,408.8,2.52,"that the fears about AI are invented."],[28,411.32,3.6,"He suggested that people want to disadvantage American companies"],[28,414.92,2.96,"and end their dominance of the AI sector."],[28,417.88,2.76,"A hoax could be something that's designed to steal money"],[28,420.64,2.0,"or create serious harm and disruption,"],[28,422.64,4.36,"but they can also be for attention or even entertainment."],[28,427.0,3.84,"People have created harm and disruption through things like bomb hoaxes,"],[28,430.84,3.96,"where someone reports that a bomb has been placed somewhere when it hasn't."],[28,434.84,2.6,"Through history, many people have claimed discoveries"],[28,437.44,5.04,"of important or magical things that later turn out to have been hoaxes."],[28,442.48,3.2,"Pictures of the Loch Ness Monster, which claimed to be photographs,"],[28,445.68,2.72,"were later proven to be hoaxes."],[28,451.2,3.64,"We've had 'hoax', something designed to trick people."],[28,454.84,2.56,"For example, despite all the evidence,"],[28,457.4,3.84,"some people still believe that the moon landings were a hoax."],[28,461.24,2.68,"That's it for this episode of Learning English from the News."],[28,463.92,3.56,"We'll be back next week with another news story."],[28,467.48,2.72,"If you want to improve your English even more,"],[28,470.2,2.72,"check out our new improved home page."],[28,472.96,2.52,"It's even easier to find the programmes you want,"],[28,475.48,2.84,"and that's at bbclearningenglish.com ."],[28,478.36,1.92,"Goodbye for now. Bye."],[29,0.0,1.6,"I'm putting my spin on it, babe."],[29,1.88,1.16,"This don't look right."],[29,3.92,0.88,"It's art."],[29,4.8,1.68,"Oh, hello. It's tipping off!"],[29,9.52,3.04,"[Tigga Mac] Two multi-talented, funny ladies,"],[29,12.56,2.6,"one delightful Bluey-themed cake to decorate,"],[29,15.16,3.44,"and plenty of clever cake hacks from yours truly."],[29,18.6,3.6,"I've sent them everything they need and videos to guide them."],[29,22.24,1.24,"Who will come out on top?"],[29,23.48,1.48,"I'll be the judge of that."],[29,24.96,2.12,"Get ready for a Bluey Cake Off."],[29,27.72,3.76,"This episode of Bluey Cake Off is called Flappy Cake."],[29,34.44,1.68,"- Oi-oi! - Hello."],[29,36.12,1.08,"How are you, Isy?"],[29,37.2,1.32,"I'm very good, thank you."],[29,38.52,0.72,"How are you?"],[29,39.24,2.96,"I am fabulous, babes. Fabulous!"],[29,42.2,2.24,"Are we going to do some decorating?"],[29,44.44,2.16,"Of course we're going to decorate, babe."],[29,46.6,0.92,"Of course."],[29,47.52,1.52,"Let's check out what we're going to do."],[29,49.04,0.8,"Come on!"],[29,50.28,1.88,"[Tigga] Cut the round sponge in half,"],[29,52.16,2.88,"then add a bit of buttercream to your cake board"],[29,55.04,4.24,"and stick the two cake halves in place, facing outwards."],[29,61.48,2.52,"To shape, trim and round off the wings…"],[29,67.28,3.56,"..and cover with a generous coating of dark blue buttercream."],[29,72.36,1.44,"Smooth it out..."],[29,76.56,1.84,"..and it's time to chill."],[29,78.88,1.64,"Very stressed."],[29,80.52,1.2,"Oh!"],[29,81.72,3.0,"We shouldn't be frightened to put our own spin on it."],[29,84.84,1.68,"I think we're both mavericks."],[29,86.52,1.2,"I'm spinning right now."],[29,91.44,1.84,"I'm putting my spin on it, babe."],[29,97.4,3.4,"So, Isy, you like to create songs, right?"],[29,100.88,2.68,"Do you ever create any with your children?"],[29,103.56,3.12,"I sing to them to try and get them to do stuff."],[29,106.68,1.6,"Really? Does it work?"],[29,108.28,1.44,"Well, it works in that they're like,"],[29,109.72,1.88,"\"Please stop singing that song.\""],[29,113.12,0.6,"But I'm like,"],[29,113.72,1.0,"# Brush your teeth"],[29,114.76,0.92,"# Brush your teeth"],[29,115.8,0.92,"# Put your shoes on"],[29,116.72,0.88,"# Put your shoes on #"],[29,117.6,1.68,"And getting, like, faster and faster,"],[29,119.56,0.8,"and higher and higher."],[29,120.36,1.76,"And they go, \"Shut up, Mum!\""],[29,122.12,2.0,"And then they do it, just to stop me singing."],[29,124.44,3.64,"Have you seen that episode of Bluey called Sleepytime?"],[29,128.08,2.56,"Where Bingo is trying to go to sleep?"],[29,130.64,0.64,"Yeah."],[29,131.28,2.16,"And she goes up into the clouds"],[29,133.44,1.44,"and into the universe."],[29,134.92,1.48,"I remember watching it with my son"],[29,136.4,1.68,"and, do you know what, I had tears in my eyes."],[29,138.72,1.16,"I have to go."],[29,140.8,1.4,"I'm a big girl now."],[29,143.4,2.8,"[Chilli] Remember, I'll always be here for you."],[29,146.36,1.12,"Bingo made me cry."],[29,147.48,3.08,"I was... dabbing away the tears."],[29,150.56,2.44,"That's the power of those episodes."],[29,153.0,2.52,"I am ready."],[29,155.52,1.48,"Because at some point you've got to stop it, innit."],[29,157.0,0.6,"You've got to stop."],[29,157.6,1.24,"So I'm stopping"],[29,158.84,3.08,"and I'm going to put my little cake in the fridge."],[29,163.4,1.92,"Let's see the next step."],[29,165.32,0.6,"Let's do it."],[29,165.92,1.44,"[Tigga] Using a cocktail stick,"],[29,167.36,2.2,"outline the wings' colours."],[29,169.56,1.64,"Fill in with bright blue…"],[29,173.68,1.56,"..and pale blue,"],[29,175.24,1.24,"and smooth."],[29,178.04,1.6,"With purple buttercream,"],[29,179.64,2.12,"pipe on a set of six legs."],[29,181.76,1.96,"Then, cut a wider hole"],[29,183.72,2.84,"and add the head and body,"],[29,186.56,2.64,"and smooth it out with a wet finger."],[29,189.2,1.24,"Wow!"],[29,190.44,2.16,"[Tigga] Dust your fingers with icing sugar"],[29,192.6,1.88,"and make a happy smile."],[29,195.12,3.52,"Create eyes with two small black balls of fondant"],[29,199.0,2.4,"and add white dots for pupils."],[29,201.92,0.96,"Attach."],[29,205.32,2.36,"Finally, for Fluffy's antennae,"],[29,207.68,2.36,"cut jelly snakes to size"],[29,210.04,2.12,"and anchor them in place."],[29,212.64,1.44,"- Right - Phew!"],[29,214.08,2.76,"- This is the fun bit. - This is the fun bit!"],[29,216.92,2.16,"I want to get my cake. - Yeah, me too."],[29,219.36,1.48,"I'm going to get my cake."],[29,220.84,1.88,"Hello again, friend."],[29,223.08,1.48,"Did you miss me?"],[29,225.28,2.68,"Look at you. Look at you!"],[29,228.8,2.64,"I'm enjoying doing these little engravings. Are you?"],[29,231.44,1.08,"Yeah."],[29,232.84,3.08,"I'm finding this quite therapeutic, if I'm honest."],[29,235.92,3.12,"Are we going to put our own slant on this, babes?"],[29,239.04,1.12,"As always, babe."],[29,240.16,2.32,"We've got to put our own little bit on it."],[29,242.48,2.68,"- What I love about that episode of Flappy... - Ah-ha…"],[29,245.16,2.08,"..is that you see them on the slide"],[29,247.24,2.96,"and looking at all the little things."],[29,250.2,2.36,"Aww! She's so cute."],[29,252.56,2.52,"You have to be more careful, Leaficus."],[29,255.08,1.96,"You're lucky we didn't squash you."],[29,257.04,1.28,"When you're a kid,"],[29,258.32,1.56,"everything looks bigger. - Everything."],[29,259.88,3.6,"So even if you see, like, a tiny little fly, to us,"],[29,263.48,2.72,"to them, it's really exciting and big."],[29,266.2,3.72,"I really like how it builds to the crescendo of the butterfly."],[29,270.0,2.68,"I'm thinking, which way is a better way"],[29,272.68,2.16,"to do the head and stuff?"],[29,275.36,1.44,"Mm."],[29,276.84,2.04,"Decisions, decisions."],[29,278.88,2.12,"- I'm done. - You're done?!"],[29,281.0,3.6,"While I'm waiting, I'm going to do some little extra Isy bits."],[29,284.68,1.2,"Hold on! How you finish..."],[29,285.88,1.04,"Let me see what you've done."],[29,286.92,1.8,"I'm coming in for a closer look."],[29,288.72,2.48,"Yeah. That's lovely."],[29,291.2,1.24,"I can see what you've done there."],[29,292.44,3.12,"That is so like your cake, innit."],[29,295.56,1.48,"And yours will only be yours."],[29,297.04,1.68,"It's true. As you were."],[29,298.72,1.52,"You are doing you, girl!"],[29,300.24,1.04,"And I love it!"],[29,301.52,1.28,"This don't look like right."],[29,305.28,2.44,"Maybe I should do it there. Yeah, that looks a bit better."],[29,307.72,1.36,"Mine hasn't got a mouth."],[29,309.08,1.0,"- Maybe they don't need to speak."],[29,310.08,1.56,"Let me see yours again, please."],[29,311.64,3.0,"Yeah. Just putting on the fourth eyelash."],[29,314.64,2.56,"So, hold on, your butterfly has eyelashes?"],[29,317.2,2.28,"Would you do a mouth or a moustache?"],[29,319.48,1.12,"I'm going to leave that to you."],[29,320.6,1.72,"Only you can answer that question."],[29,322.32,1.08,"Can you see it there?"],[29,324.28,1.8,"Yeah, that's great."],[29,326.08,2.08,"But there's not much of a head."],[29,328.16,1.48,"Oh, hello, it's tipping off!"],[29,330.68,1.12,"Don't fly away, Flappy."],[29,331.8,1.64,"That is gorgeous!"],[29,333.44,2.32,"And it looks so much more like a butterfly than mine."],[29,335.76,4.28,"Yours is more of a butterfly tribute cake."],[29,340.24,1.64,"- It's art. - It's art."],[29,341.88,3.64,"I don't know about you, you know, but this has been emotional."],[29,345.6,3.52,"I have absolutely loved decorating with you."],[29,349.16,1.24,"Me to you too."],[29,350.4,2.76,"- But it's judgement time. - Ah-ha."],[29,353.44,1.56,"There's only one person who can decide,"],[29,355.0,2.0,"and that is Tigga Mac."],[29,357.04,1.52,"Who's going to win?"],[29,360.0,1.16,"- Tigga here again!"],[29,361.16,2.84,"You've both made beautiful butterflies,"],[29,364.0,2.76,"but there can only be one winner. And it's…"],[29,367.08,1.2,"..Tameka!"],[29,368.28,1.12,"Well done!"],[30,5.2,1.32,"Bluey!"],[30,7.56,3.08,"Oh, what a splendid moustache you have!"],[30,11.52,2.44,"Ooh! Lovely."],[30,14.88,2.76,"It's my turn to unfreeze him."],[30,17.64,1.84,"Unfreeze! (DING!)"],[30,19.48,2.08,"Oh, what?! (THEY GIGGLE)"],[30,21.56,1.56,"Arghh! You kids!"],[30,23.12,1.56,"- Run! - Grr!"],[30,24.96,3.04,"Give me that magic xylophone!"],[30,28.0,1.32,"Gotcha! (BLUEY SQUEALS)"],[30,30.32,2.56,"Ha-ha! Too slow, Mr Moustache!"],[30,32.88,3.28,"Bluey, you're taking all the turns freezing."],[30,36.16,2.68,"Come on, Bingo! Let's get the teddies."],[30,38.84,1.64,"OK."],[30,52.12,3.88,"- I'm off to work. - Mum! Mum! Look at Dad now!"],[30,56.72,3.88,"This loose hair has been bugging me all morning!"],[30,63.04,1.4,"OK, I'll unfreeze him."],[30,64.44,1.4,"I want to do it."],[30,65.84,3.28,"Mum, Bluey's not letting me have any turns."],[30,69.12,2.48,"Bluey, if you don't take turns with people,"],[30,71.6,1.92,"people won't take turns with you."],[30,73.64,1.76,"But Bingo is too slow."],[30,75.4,1.24,"Dad will catch her."],[30,76.8,1.68,"No, I'm not."],[30,78.48,1.68,"You can either take turns"],[30,80.16,2.2,"or I'll bring the xylophone to work with me."],[30,82.48,1.36,"I'll freeze my boss."],[30,83.84,2.28,"- [Both] No! - Nnn! Nnnnn!"],[30,86.12,2.04,"Well, you know what to do then."],[30,88.2,0.96,"I'll see you later."],[30,89.16,3.0,"Just make sure you unfreeze him for toilet breaks, OK?"],[30,92.28,0.96,"[Both] OK!"],[30,94.64,2.92,"Here, Bingo. But stand right back here."],[30,97.56,1.88,"That way you can get a headstart."],[30,99.44,2.8,"He runs really fast when he's mad."],[30,102.24,1.72,"OK! Unfreeze!"],[30,106.08,2.76,"- There you are! Grrr! - Run, Bingo!"],[30,108.84,2.0,"Back inside! - Grrrr!"],[31,0.08,1.84,"From BBC Learning English."],[31,1.92,4.36,"This is Learning English from the News, our podcast about the news headlines."],[31,6.28,1.04,"In this programme:"],[31,7.32,4.2,"Young people are not weak, says head of official review."],[31,11.52,3.96,"Mental distress in young people is a real issue."],[31,19.12,1.2,"Hello, I'm Georgie."],[31,20.32,0.88,"And I'm Neil."],[31,21.2,2.64,"In this programme we look at one big news story"],[31,23.84,3.96,"and the vocabulary in the headlines that will help you understand it."],[31,27.8,3.24,"You can find all the vocabulary and headlines from this episode,"],[31,31.04,4.92,"as well as a worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com"],[31,35.96,3.36,"OK, Neil. Let's hear more about this story."],[31,44.76,2.44,"An independent review into mental health"],[31,47.2,2.84,"in the UK has found a rise in mental distress"],[31,50.04,3.52,"among children and young people with anxiety, depression,"],[31,53.56,1.88,"self-harm and eating disorders"],[31,55.44,1.8,"among the most common issues."],[31,57.24,2.68,"Head of the review Professor Peter Fonagy says"],[31,59.92,3.8,"that accusations that young people are too sensitive are wrong."],[31,63.72,4.2,"He says it is now \"harder to be young\" due to challenges such as economic"],[31,67.92,4.24,"and employment uncertainty and problems caused by social media."],[31,72.16,2.56,"Let's have our first headline."],[31,74.72,2.68,"There is no 'snowflake generation' -"],[31,77.4,1.92,"it's just harder to be young now,"],[31,79.32,6.2,"says author of mental health review, that from The Independent, a UK news website."],[31,85.52,3.2,"And that headline, again from The Independent: There is"],[31,88.72,1.76,"no 'snowflake generation' -"],[31,90.48,1.84,"it's just harder to be young now,"],[31,92.32,2.28,"says author of mental health review."],[31,94.6,4.44,"So this headline quotes Professor Peter Fonagy, head of the review,"],[31,99.04,2.84,"who says there is no 'snowflake generation'"],[31,101.88,4.4,"and we're interested in this word 'snowflake' here, aren't we, Georgie?"],[31,106.28,1.08,"Yes we are."],[31,107.36,2.24,"So snowflake is a slang word."],[31,109.6,4.28,"It describes a person who is seen as overly sensitive or fragile."],[31,113.88,2.84,"They might be someone who is easily offended."],[31,116.72,2.52,"And of course, this makes sense, doesn't it?"],[31,119.24,4.6,"Because if you imagine a real snowflake, they are very fragile."],[31,123.84,2.12,"They melt as soon as you touch them."],[31,125.96,3.36,"Yes. In the headline, snowflake is a modifier."],[31,129.32,3.56,"A snowflake generation means a generation of snowflakes."],[31,132.88,4.16,"This generation of young people are accused of being snowflakes because"],[31,137.04,4.36,"they are perceived to be more sensitive and less able to deal with stress."],[31,141.4,3.72,"But Peter Fonagy says that the accusation is wrong."],[31,145.12,4.56,"His view is that young people face many challenges that make life harder."],[31,149.68,5.28,"Yes. Now, snowflake is a derogatory word meaning that it's used as an insult."],[31,154.96,4.64,"So be careful when calling someone a snowflake as it can sound rude."],[31,163.84,1.6,"We've had snowflake,"],[31,165.44,6.64,"a slang term to describe someone seen as too sensitive or easily offended."],[31,172.08,2.16,"For example, they called him a snowflake"],[31,174.24,3.08,"when he said he found the joke offensive."],[31,180.8,1.88,"This is Learning English from the News,"],[31,182.68,2.32,"our podcast about the news headlines."],[31,185.0,4.08,"Today, we're talking about findings that show a rise in mental distress"],[31,189.08,1.6,"among young people."],[31,190.68,4.32,"The review into mental health, ADHD and autism concludes that"],[31,195.0,4.4,"about one in four 16 to 24-year-olds is now struggling."],[31,199.4,3.96,"Some are unsure about the new data as some of the sources rely on"],[31,203.36,3.4,"self-reporting. There is also an argument which says"],[31,206.76,2.6,"that the higher mental distress numbers could be"],[31,209.36,3.04,"because of changing attitudes and greater awareness,"],[31,212.4,3.64,"so more people feel able to report their problems."],[31,216.04,3.28,"The head of a children's mental health charity, Hope 4 More,"],[31,219.32,4.56,"has agreed with Peter Fonagy's view that young people are not snowflakes."],[31,223.88,3.48,"She said that many children were struggling because of factors like"],[31,227.36,5.04,"the education system, social media and the impact of the Covid-19 pandemic."],[31,232.4,2.24,"And our next headline is about this."],[31,234.64,2.24,"This one is from BBC News."],[31,236.88,3.32,"Young people have it tough, says charity boss."],[31,240.2,2.36,"That headline again from BBC News."],[31,242.56,3.44,"Young people have it tough, says charity boss."],[31,246.0,3.88,"So Neil, we've heard that Peter Fonagy thinks it's harder to be young,"],[31,249.88,3.6,"and here in the headline it says young people have it tough."],[31,253.48,1.6,"Do they have similar meanings?"],[31,255.08,2.8,"Well yes. Both convey a very similar meaning."],[31,257.88,3.4,"Tough in this context means hard or difficult."],[31,261.28,4.32,"If you have it tough, it means you face difficult circumstances."],[31,265.6,2.88,"The view that young people have it tough is because of the things"],[31,268.48,4.04,"we've mentioned, things like economic uncertainty and social media."],[31,272.52,3.08,"The opposite of having tough is have it easy,"],[31,275.6,3.0,"meaning you experience good life circumstances."],[31,278.6,1.24,"Yeah. So for example,"],[31,279.84,3.2,"some say teachers have it easy because they get long holidays,"],[31,283.04,3.2,"but they do work very hard during term time."],[31,286.24,4.04,"Be careful though, because not all adjectives work with this structure."],[31,290.28,4.68,"The most common expressions are have it tough, have it hard, have it easy,"],[31,294.96,2.2,"have it good and have it bad."],[31,297.16,4.0,"Yes. And you might also hear it with 'got', for example, she's got it easy"],[31,301.16,2.08,"or she's got it tough."],[31,306.88,5.28,"We've had 'have it tough' - experience difficult life circumstances."],[31,313.88,3.96,"For example, single parents often have it tough balancing childcare"],[31,317.84,2.28,"and work by themselves."],[31,324.28,3.88,"This is Learning English from the News from BBC Learning English."],[31,328.16,4.36,"We're talking about a rise in mental distress among young people."],[31,332.52,3.92,"Figures from the UK's National Health Service show the number of children"],[31,336.44,3.88,"and young people in contact with mental health services in England"],[31,340.32,3.48,"has risen by 50% in the past five years."],[31,343.8,3.44,"Some people have questioned whether we are treating people who have"],[31,347.24,3.6,"temporary life stresses as if they have a medical problem."],[31,350.84,3.4,"But head of the recent mental health review Peter Fonagy"],[31,354.24,4.12,"says this generation of young people has real, serious challenges"],[31,358.36,3.76,"that he would not wish to have had when he was their age."],[31,362.12,2.44,"OK, let's have another headline."],[31,364.56,1.76,"Professor Peter Fonagy:"],[31,366.32,4.08,"How to help young people forge emotional resilience and wellbeing."],[31,370.4,2.88,"And that's from Tatler, a British magazine."],[31,373.28,2.36,"And that headline again from Tatler."],[31,375.64,1.64,"Professor Peter Fonagy:"],[31,377.28,4.4,"How to help young people forge emotional resilience and wellbeing."],[31,381.68,4.8,"So emotional resilience is how well you can adapt to stressful situations,"],[31,386.48,3.92,"and wellbeing is the state of feeling happy or comfortable."],[31,390.4,3.88,"Peter Fonagy wants to help young people forge these things."],[31,394.28,2.88,"Georgie, what can you tell us about this word forge?"],[31,397.16,4.36,"Well, in this context, forge means build or develop something strong"],[31,401.52,1.36,"through hard work."],[31,402.88,3.64,"The literal meaning of forge might help us understand it better."],[31,406.52,3.96,"Yes, forge, in a literal sense, comes from metalworking."],[31,410.48,4.2,"You melt the metal and then hit it into shape using a lot of effort,"],[31,414.68,2.36,"and you end up with something strong."],[31,417.04,2.32,"You can forge things like a career path,"],[31,419.36,2.76,"forge a new identity, forge a bond with someone,"],[31,422.12,4.44,"and these all just mean that you build these things through hard work."],[31,428.24,5.88,"We've had forge - build something strong with hard work."],[31,434.12,2.16,"For example, he forged his career"],[31,436.28,4.84,"as an actor through hard work and determination."],[31,441.12,2.88,"That's it for this episode of Learning English from the News."],[31,444.0,2.88,"We'll be back next week with another news story."],[31,446.88,3.64,"And if you like our podcast, sign up to our email newsletter"],[31,450.52,3.2,"to get the latest from BBC Learning English every week."],[31,453.72,2.52,"There's a link in the notes below this episode."],[31,456.24,2.08,"Bye for now. Goodbye."],[32,0.08,1.8,"My piping keeps farting at me."],[32,1.88,1.08,"Are you sure it's the bag?"],[32,2.96,1.12,"Definitely the bag."],[32,4.6,1.0,"What are you doing?"],[32,6.76,2.52,"[Suzi Ruffell] Straight in! Crowd go wild."],[32,9.28,2.36,"[Tigga Mac] Two top-class comedians,"],[32,11.64,3.08,"one marvellous Bluey-themed cake to decorate,"],[32,14.76,3.08,"and heaps of inventive cake hacks from yours truly."],[32,17.88,3.56,"I've sent them everything they need and videos to guide them."],[32,21.48,1.56,"Who will come out on top?"],[32,23.04,1.44,"I'll be the judge of that."],[32,24.72,2.48,"Get ready for a Bluey Cake Off."],[32,27.72,3.72,"This episode of Bluey Cake Off is called Floppy Cake."],[32,34.48,1.36,"- Maisie? - Yes?"],[32,35.84,0.56,"Are you ready?"],[32,36.4,0.96,"I like Bingo,"],[32,37.36,2.28,"so I'm excited to make Bingo's favourite toy."],[32,39.64,1.64,"So shall we have a watch?"],[32,41.28,1.32,"Let's have a watch."],[32,42.6,1.88,"[Tigga] Lay the template on top"],[32,44.48,2.4,"and use a sharp knife to cut out the shape,"],[32,46.88,1.88,"and remove the template."],[32,48.92,3.88,"Cover with a generous layer of lilac-coloured buttercream."],[32,52.88,2.0,"So using a lot of that."],[32,54.88,1.56,"[Tigga] And smooth it all out."],[32,56.44,1.88,"Then time to chill."],[32,59.0,1.96,"- Floppy is Bingo's toy... - OK."],[32,60.96,1.28,"..that she takes to bed with her."],[32,62.24,2.24,"And she features in lots of episodes."],[32,64.52,2.24,"There's one that sticks out in my mind,"],[32,66.76,2.24,"when in her dream,"],[32,69.0,2.32,"her and the rabbit, Floppy,"],[32,71.32,3.0,"go on this incredible journey through the universe."],[32,78.96,1.2,"And it's very sweet."],[32,80.16,2.16,"My daughter loves it. - Oh, nice."],[32,82.32,2.48,"Did you have a favourite cuddly toy when you were a kid?"],[32,84.8,2.68,"I did, and I've actually brought him with me today."],[32,87.48,0.76,"Do you want to meet him?"],[32,88.24,0.84,"Yes, I'd love to."],[32,89.08,1.32,"He still sleeps on my bed now."],[32,90.4,1.04,"In your bed?"],[32,91.44,1.12,"Maisie, you're married!"],[32,92.56,1.96,"Well, there's space for him. It's OK."],[32,95.4,3.0,"And he's been with me since I was the same size as him."],[32,98.52,2.4,"- That's really cute. - Yeah."],[32,101.04,2.24,"- I've also got mine, Mais. - Have you got one?"],[32,103.28,2.04,"Mine is a bunny called Fluffalump."],[32,105.32,1.32,"Fluffalump?"],[32,106.64,2.28,"[Suzi] I'm going to start icing now."],[32,109.36,1.8,"You've got me panicked now. Maybe I should do it."],[32,111.16,1.0,"[Suzi] Ooh! Ooh!"],[32,115.32,2.08,"I'm going to spread it on top first"],[32,117.4,1.4,"and then go for the sides."],[32,118.8,2.76,"Oh, OK. So we're doing it in different methods here. OK."],[32,121.56,1.32,"Oh, why? What are you doing?"],[32,122.88,1.32,"Putting it all on at once."],[32,125.52,2.52,"Have you had Fluffalump since you were little?"],[32,128.2,1.48,"Think it's since I was about seven or eight."],[32,129.68,2.12,"That's cute that you've kept in touch."],[32,133.08,1.96,"- Shall we watch the next video? - Yes."],[32,135.04,3.64,"[Tigga] Use a cocktail stick to trace out eyebrows,"],[32,138.68,2.36,"ears and a tummy."],[32,141.12,1.08,"Oh, my word."],[32,142.2,1.8,"[Tigga] Then pipe on the white."],[32,144.2,1.12,"Smooth it out."],[32,145.32,3.0,"And then green too, and smooth."],[32,148.48,3.0,"- This is a lot of piping, isn't it? - Mm-hmm."],[32,151.48,1.84,"[Tigga] Next, stick on the eyes"],[32,153.32,2.32,"and shape a nose from pink fondant."],[32,155.64,3.36,"Pipe purple frosting on to all of Floppy's features."],[32,159.6,3.08,"Then trace her outline all over."],[32,163.12,1.32,"OK. That doesn't look too hard."],[32,164.44,1.0,"That's just tracing."],[32,165.44,2.56,"I mean, I haven't traced in about 20 years."],[32,168.0,1.04,"You need to get out more."],[32,169.04,1.28,"Shall we get him out of the fridge?"],[32,170.64,0.96,"Let's do it."],[32,171.68,2.92,"Ooh! Looking nice and smooth."],[32,177.36,1.52,"I'm going to start piping, I think."],[32,178.88,1.2,"OK, let's do it."],[32,181.72,1.16,"Moving on to the green."],[32,182.88,1.72,"You're on the green already?"],[32,188.92,1.16,"[Suzi] I'm putting the eyes on now,"],[32,190.08,2.12,"and I want to make sure they're in the right spot."],[32,192.2,2.28,"My God, you're flying ahead."],[32,194.48,1.44,"I need to get a move on."],[32,197.16,2.16,"[Suzi] My eyes are very different sizes."],[32,199.32,1.28,"[Maisie] Yours or the cake's?"],[32,206.2,2.64,"You almost need to go quite quickly with the piping, don't you?"],[32,209.0,1.8,"[Maisie] Yeah, you can overthink it."],[32,210.8,0.88,"Also, every now and then,"],[32,211.68,2.04,"does your piping do a little fart noise?"],[32,213.72,1.88,"Mine keeps farting at me."],[32,216.24,2.2,"- Maisie... - It does!"],[32,218.52,1.36,"Are you sure it's the bag?"],[32,219.88,1.28,"It's definitely the bag."],[32,221.16,3.08,"Make sure everybody knows it's the bag and not me, thank you."],[32,228.52,2.24,"Are you licking yours out the top of your bag?"],[32,230.76,1.88,"- No! - No, me neither."],[32,232.64,1.36,"You're not meant to do that, Maisie."],[32,234.0,1.68,"No, I'm not. I'm not."],[32,235.68,1.24,"I'm covered in icing."],[32,236.92,1.72,"I think my bag's burst."],[32,240.24,3.84,"I've just got a hand of purple buttercream. Is that what you've got?"],[32,244.08,0.8,"No."],[32,245.36,0.64,"Right."],[32,248.44,1.64,"Do you know what?"],[32,250.08,1.48,"I don't think that's too bad."],[32,251.56,2.36,"- Are you ready for the big reveal? - Yeah."],[32,253.92,1.36,"There we go."],[32,255.28,2.8,"I think yours might be a bit neater, Mais. Is yours a bit neater?"],[32,258.08,1.84,"No. Your piping's really good."],[32,259.92,2.28,"Yours looks like it's got a great attitude."],[32,262.2,1.56,"Thank you. Thank you."],[32,263.76,3.24,"And yours looks like it would be great fun to go for a nice walk with."],[32,267.0,1.64,"Or a cuddle."],[32,268.64,2.04,"Tigga, it's over to you."],[32,270.68,1.16,"Who has one won?"],[32,272.36,1.24,"- Tigga here again."],[32,273.6,2.32,"You've both done an awesome job,"],[32,275.92,2.6,"but there can only be one winner. And it's…"],[32,278.52,1.32,"..Suzi!"],[32,279.84,1.08,"Well done!"],[33,0.0,0.92,"Hi Georgie."],[33,0.92,0.88,"Hi Phil."],[33,1.8,3.6,"Now, Phil, do you ever struggle to get a good night's sleep?"],[33,5.4,1.48,"You know, I do sometimes."],[33,6.88,4.44,"If I've been thinking about something a lot, or even worse,"],[33,11.32,2.76,"if I've had a cup of coffee later than I should,"],[33,14.08,2.96,"sometimes I can lie there for hours."],[33,17.04,4.0,"Yes. Well, many of us will be familiar with that feeling of lying in bed,"],[33,21.04,3.92,"feeling really tired, but just not being able to go to sleep,"],[33,24.96,2.48,"but what causes us to struggle to sleep and"],[33,27.44,2.32,"is there anything we can do about it?"],[33,29.76,3.32,"Well, researchers have for a while been interested in the link"],[33,33.08,2.76,"between what we eat and how we sleep."],[33,35.84,3.8,"Today on 6 Minute English, we'll be talking about this and asking,"],[33,39.64,3.48,"What should you eat for a better night's sleep?"],[33,45.28,3.0,"OK. Phil, let's start with a quiz question."],[33,48.28,5.0,"So, adults need between 7–9 hours sleep a night to stay healthy,"],[33,53.28,1.24,"according to experts,"],[33,54.52,4.44,"but what percentage of people in the UK say they get less than this?"],[33,58.96,5.96,"Is it: a) 15%, b) 25%, or c) 35%?"],[33,64.92,4.12,"OK. I think people find sleeping hard,"],[33,69.04,3.0,"so I think it's going to be c) 35%."],[33,72.04,3.08,"OK. Well, we'll find out the answer later in the programme."],[33,75.12,3.08,"Georgie, you've been looking at the research on this."],[33,78.2,4.44,"Do scientists think that what we eat affects how we sleep?"],[33,82.64,1.08,"Yes, they do."],[33,83.72,2.24,"Scientists have done some long-term studies."],[33,85.96,3.96,"So, looking at large groups of people over a long period of time,"],[33,89.92,4.96,"and they found that a healthier diet is associated with better quality sleep."],[33,94.88,3.48,"They've also done some smaller studies looking in more detail at"],[33,98.36,4.84,"exactly what people are eating and then studying how they sleep in a laboratory."],[33,103.2,2.36,"OK. And in these studies,"],[33,105.56,5.2,"have they found that particular foods are better for our sleep than others?"],[33,110.76,4.64,"Well, it's less about particular foods and more about particular nutrients."],[33,115.4,3.76,"So, nutrients means important things your body needs to survive"],[33,119.16,1.04,"and be healthy."],[33,120.2,2.36,"For example, protein is a nutrient."],[33,122.56,3.92,"BBC World Service programme The Food Chain has been speaking to researcher"],[33,126.48,5.32,"Professor Marie-Pierre St-Onge about which nutrients are important for sleep."],[33,131.8,4.92,"So, diets that look more like a Mediterranean pattern – higher"],[33,136.72,1.2,"in fruits and vegetables,"],[33,137.92,6.08,"whole grains, lower in animal-based foods, lower in sugar."],[33,144.0,5.44,"But then from our own research, where we evaluate food intakes in the lab,"],[33,149.44,2.8,"we see different... similar patterns, really."],[33,152.24,4.84,"But when we talk about nutrients, diets that are higher in fibre,"],[33,157.08,2.4,"which we find in fruits and vegetables and whole grains,"],[33,159.48,5.76,"and lower in saturated fats, which we find in more plant-based types of foods,"],[33,165.24,2.56,"are associated with more deep sleep at night."],[33,167.8,1.04,"Marie-Pierre says"],[33,168.84,4.08,"that Mediterranean-style diets are associated with good sleep."],[33,172.92,2.88,"A Mediterranean diet includes lots of vegetables"],[33,175.8,3.36,"and less animal-based foods such as meat and dairy."],[33,179.16,4.36,"Yes, and Marie-Pierre's own research shows that some nutrients, such"],[33,183.52,4.8,"as fibre, are important for sleep and others, such as saturated fats,"],[33,188.32,1.48,"are less important."],[33,189.8,4.36,"Saturated fat is a type of fat mainly found in meat and dairy"],[33,194.16,2.04,"that's thought to be bad for your health."],[33,196.2,4.08,"Right. And according to Marie-Pierre, eating less saturated fat"],[33,200.28,1.08,"and more nutrients,"],[33,201.36,4.04,"like fibre, is associated with more deep sleep at night."],[33,205.4,5.32,"A deep sleep is when you are more fully asleep and your body is able to rest."],[33,210.72,5.24,"So, Georgie, it sounds like thinking about our diets is important"],[33,215.96,1.6,"if we want to improve our sleep."],[33,217.56,4.36,"And now I've heard that certain foods have a lot of nutrients"],[33,221.92,2.4,"that can be helpful for sleep. Is that true?"],[33,224.32,3.16,"Well, there is some limited research on specific foods"],[33,227.48,1.68,"and their impact on sleep."],[33,229.16,3.12,"So, for example, scientists have been studying kiwi fruits"],[33,232.28,2.2,"and also tart cherry juice."],[33,234.48,3.84,"And you may also see adverts for supplements that claim to improve sleep."],[33,238.32,3.76,"Supplements are tablets you take, which contain particular nutrients"],[33,242.08,2.64,"that you might not get in your everyday diet."],[33,244.72,3.76,"But experts say that focusing on our whole diet, rather than"],[33,248.48,4.24,"one particular fruit or supplement, is going to be the most effective."],[33,252.72,2.76,"Here's sleep doctor Allie Hare explaining more"],[33,255.48,3.08,"to BBC World Service programme The Food Chain."],[33,258.56,4.56,"I think people are looking for the one supplement often or the..."],[33,263.12,1.4,"you know, we're fed a lot of these things,"],[33,264.52,1.48,"aren't we, on social media?"],[33,266.0,5.6,"This magical powder or this magical tablet that you can take that will,"],[33,271.6,3.4,"you know, help you sleep better immediately from tonight."],[33,275.0,1.96,"And of course, you know,"],[33,276.96,3.88,"Marie-Pierre and I know that there is no such magic bullet, unfortunately."],[33,280.84,2.44,"And if there was, I'm sure,"],[33,283.28,1.2,"you know, we would be..."],[33,284.48,2.84,"we would be doing very well for ourselves."],[33,287.32,4.88,"Allie says people are looking for a supplement that can help them sleep."],[33,292.2,5.16,"Yes. She says we are fed information on social media about magical tablets."],[33,297.36,4.36,"If you are fed information, you are given lots of it in a continuous way."],[33,301.72,3.04,"But Allie argues that experts know"],[33,304.76,3.04,"there's no magic bullet to help you sleep,"],[33,307.8,4.64,"and a magic bullet refers to a simple and effective solution to a problem"],[33,312.44,2.68,"that's usually one that doesn't exist."],[33,315.12,1.96,"OK. Well, now I think"],[33,317.08,3.28,"it's time to find out the answer to your quiz question, Georgie."],[33,320.36,4.04,"Yes. I asked you what percentage of UK adults say they get"],[33,324.4,2.16,"less than seven hours sleep a night."],[33,326.56,3.56,"And I said I thought it was c) 35%."],[33,330.12,2.0,"You are correct, Phil."],[33,332.12,4.8,"Yes, 35% of adults say they get six hours or less sleep a night,"],[33,336.92,2.12,"according to a study by YouGov."],[33,339.04,3.36,"OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we learned in this programme,"],[33,342.4,1.64,"starting with nutrients,"],[33,344.04,4.2,"which are important things your body needs to survive and be healthy."],[33,348.24,3.48,"Saturated fats are fats mainly found in meat"],[33,351.72,3.68,"and dairy products that are thought to be bad for our health."],[33,355.4,4.2,"A deep sleep is when your body is fully asleep and able to rest."],[33,359.6,4.2,"Supplements are tablets or other things which contain particular nutrients"],[33,363.8,2.68,"you might not get in your everyday diet."],[33,366.48,4.04,"To feed information means to give lots of it in a continuous way."],[33,370.52,3.4,"And a magic bullet is a simple and effective solution,"],[33,373.92,2.88,"and it's often one that doesn't exist."],[33,376.8,3.28,"Once again, our six minutes are up, but don't forget you can test"],[33,380.08,2.4,"what you've learned with the worksheet on our website."],[33,382.48,2.52,"Go to bbclearningenglish.com."],[33,385.0,2.48,"Thanks for joining us and goodbye."],[33,387.48,1.24,"Goodbye!"],[34,0.08,4.25,"Hello and welcome to 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English."],[34,4.34,0.83,"I'm Georgie."],[34,5.17,1.17,"And I'm Neil."],[34,6.34,2.88,"Don't forget you can find vocabulary, definitions"],[34,9.22,5.67,"and a worksheet for free on our website, bbclearningenglish.com."],[34,14.89,4.05,"So Neil, today we're talking about rejection."],[34,18.94,2.67,"So, rejection means saying no to someone"],[34,21.61,4.13,"or refusing to accept their idea or suggestion."],[34,25.73,2.5,"Neil, how do you feel about rejection?"],[34,28.24,3.59,"Well, nobody likes rejection."],[34,31.82,3.84,"But I think as you get older, you get used to it."],[34,35.66,4.84,"And probably it's quite a good thing to experience rejection,"],[34,40.5,2.17,"even if it doesn't feel nice."],[34,42.67,1.29,"Yeah, I agree."],[34,43.96,1.96,"We can't always get what we want, can we?"],[34,45.92,1.17,"We can't, no."],[34,47.09,1.13,"In this podcast,"],[34,48.22,4.46,"we'll be learning about a new internet trend called rejection therapy"],[34,52.68,6.59,"and how people are using it to help them to be more confident."],[34,59.27,3.0,"OK. Let's start with our quiz question, Georgie."],[34,62.27,1.83,"Lots of famous people"],[34,64.11,4.59,"have faced rejection in their lives before they became successful,"],[34,68.69,5.0,"but which of these things did not happen to the animator Walt Disney"],[34,73.7,2.0,"before he became successful?"],[34,75.7,7.01,"Was it: a) he lost his job as an artist, b) his first business ran out of money,"],[34,82.71,3.96,"or c) his first full length film was a failure?"],[34,86.67,5.46,"I've got no idea, but I'm going to go with a) he lost his job as an artist."],[34,92.13,3.71,"OK. Well, we'll find out the answer at the end of the programme."],[34,95.85,3.75,"OK Neil. So, most people don't like being rejected,"],[34,99.6,5.09,"but one thing to try might be something called rejection therapy."],[34,104.69,4.88,"It's a social media trend where people record themselves talking to strangers"],[34,109.57,3.54,"and sometimes being rejected."],[34,113.11,2.21,"OK. That sounds quite interesting,"],[34,115.32,3.29,"but let's find out how it works in practice."],[34,118.62,3.88,"Hannah Gelbart, host of the BBC World Service podcast"],[34,122.5,2.92,"What in the World, had a go at the trend."],[34,125.42,2.59,"Let's see how she got on."],[34,128.0,2.25,"Hey, can I come sit down at your table?"],[34,130.25,0.67,"Yeah."],[34,130.92,1.08,"Hey, how's it going?"],[34,132.01,0.83,"Good, thank you."],[34,132.84,1.08,"How's your day gone?"],[34,133.93,2.38,"Good afternoon. How are you doing?"],[34,136.3,3.21,"Can I drive your cab?"],[34,139.51,2.09,"Can I? Can I get behind the wheel?"],[34,141.6,1.5,"Don't be silly...!"],[34,143.1,3.38,"Coming somewhere else to try and face my fears..."],[34,146.48,1.58,"maybe do a bit of shopping."],[34,148.06,2.13,"I was wondering, can I have a nap in one of these beds?"],[34,150.19,1.25,"Can I just try it out?"],[34,151.44,2.59,"Wow – I didn't expect that one to be a yes!"],[34,154.03,3.71,"Well, it seems like people were pretty nice to Hannah in those examples."],[34,157.74,3.46,"Yes. And she talks with psychologist Claudia Hammond"],[34,161.2,2.59,"about how she felt during the experiment."],[34,163.79,2.17,"And actually walking up to the first person,"],[34,165.96,2.67,"I felt my adrenaline really, really spiking."],[34,168.63,1.46,"I'm not a socially anxious person."],[34,170.09,1.71,"I think I'm really confident."],[34,171.8,2.0,"But that first thing really scared me."],[34,173.8,2.71,"And once I'd done one, the second one came easier"],[34,176.51,1.88,"and the third one came even easier. And you know,"],[34,178.39,3.5,"by the fourth time I didn't even mind it whether they said yes or no."],[34,181.89,3.79,"Hannah says that when she walked up to the first stranger in the video,"],[34,185.69,2.71,"she felt her adrenaline spiking."],[34,188.4,3.0,"If something spikes, it increases sharply."],[34,191.4,3.59,"Hannah is not generally a socially anxious person."],[34,194.99,3.96,"Someone who is socially anxious is nervous or worried"],[34,198.95,2.0,"about speaking to other people."],[34,200.95,5.34,"Each time Hannah tried to speak to a new person, she says, it came easier."],[34,206.29,4.92,"Come easier is an informal way to say something is easier to achieve."],[34,211.21,4.3,"Now, this social media trend is called rejection therapy,"],[34,215.51,2.92,"but it's not a proper form of therapy."],[34,218.43,1.58,"It hasn't been tested."],[34,220.01,3.96,"And there are some criticisms of the videos because they involve filming"],[34,223.97,4.0,"your interactions with strangers, which people might not want."],[34,227.98,4.55,"But people who post the videos say they boost their confidence."],[34,232.52,3.75,"So, why do people want to ask for rejection?"],[34,236.28,4.59,"Psychologist Claudia Hammond has some ideas, which she shared with Hannah"],[34,240.87,3.84,"on the BBC World Service podcast What in the World."],[34,244.7,3.59,"What we do know is that there is an increase in perfectionism."],[34,248.29,2.13,"People are more worried now, younger people,"],[34,250.42,1.63,"about how other people are judging them."],[34,252.04,5.26,"And so, I wonder whether that might make people more shy of speaking to strangers –"],[34,257.3,4.3,"worried that the people will, you know, not appraise them in a good way,"],[34,261.6,4.59,"and so doing something like this to try to counter that might feel"],[34,266.18,2.88,"like a positive step to people, that they're trying to do something."],[34,269.06,1.67,"Also, they're fun to watch."],[34,270.73,4.84,"Claudia says there's an increase in perfectionism among young people."],[34,275.57,4.92,"Perfectionism is the feeling that everything you do needs to be perfect."],[34,280.49,4.25,"Young people are also worried about how people will appraise them,"],[34,284.74,2.59,"which means judge them."],[34,287.33,1.46,"Deliberately looking for rejection"],[34,288.79,3.25,"might be an extreme way to become more confident,"],[34,292.04,3.25,"but it can lead to surprising discoveries."],[34,295.3,4.05,"Yes, and Hannah found that most strangers she spoke to were friendly"],[34,299.34,4.55,"and happy to help, so she didn't really experience rejection much at all."],[34,303.89,4.71,"OK. Georgie, I think it's time now for the answer to my quiz question."],[34,308.6,5.46,"I asked you which rejection Walt Disney didn't experience"],[34,314.06,1.88,"before becoming successful?"],[34,315.94,5.55,"Yes, and I went for a) I don't think he lost his job as an artist."],[34,321.49,5.05,"Well, actually, the answer was c) his first full length film, Snow White"],[34,326.54,2.38,"and the Seven Dwarfs, was not a failure."],[34,328.91,1.88,"It was a huge success."],[34,330.79,2.0,"Wow! That was his first film?"],[34,332.79,1.17,"That's really impressive."],[34,333.96,0.92,"It is."],[34,334.88,3.8,"OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we learned in this programme,"],[34,338.67,3.59,"starting with 'rejection', which means saying no"],[34,342.26,2.79,"or refusing an idea or suggestion."],[34,345.05,3.09,"The verb 'spike' means increase suddenly."],[34,348.14,3.92,"Someone who is socially anxious is nervous or worried"],[34,352.06,2.21,"about speaking to other people."],[34,354.27,3.71,"If something comes easier, it's easier to achieve."],[34,357.98,4.71,"'Perfectionism' is the feeling that everything you do needs to be perfect."],[34,362.7,3.67,"And if you appraise someone, you judge them."],[34,366.37,2.04,"Once again, our six minutes are up,"],[34,368.41,2.71,"but you can test what you've learned with the free worksheet"],[34,371.12,3.84,"on our website, bbclearningenglish.com."],[34,374.96,1.71,"Thank you for joining us and goodbye."],[34,376.67,1.46,"Goodbye."],[35,0.48,6.0,"Why do native English speakers commonly make this mistake?"],[35,6.48,4.0,"And how can it improve your pronunciation?"],[35,10.48,4.6,"You're going to listen to, and shadow, a conversation"],[35,15.08,2.68,"between my colleagues in the office."],[35,17.76,4.68,"That means you need to listen to and repeat what they say,"],[35,22.44,1.56,"like an echo."],[35,25.08,1.92,"I think I might have mentioned it."],[35,27.36,5.4,"Click here to find out what shadowing is - and if you already know,"],[35,32.76,2.48,"let's get on with the dialogue."],[35,38.4,3.6,"Did you get up to much at the weekend?"],[35,42.0,2.32,"Yeah, I went to the pub with my friends."],[35,44.32,2.12,"You should have come."],[35,46.44,3.4,"Well, if you'd told me, I would have."],[35,49.84,2.96,"I think I might have mentioned it."],[35,52.8,2.2,"I don't think you did."],[35,55.0,1.52,"Oh, right. Sorry."],[35,56.52,1.88,"I should have sent you a message."],[35,58.4,5.24,"Let's look at the pronunciation of these words first."],[35,63.64,5.04,"Should've, would've, might've."],[35,68.68,3.28,"A common mistake that native speakers make"],[35,71.96,5.0,"is writing 'should of' or 'would of' instead."],[35,76.96,5.08,"That's because the shortened version of 'have'"],[35,82.04,4.92,"has the same pronunciation as 'of' in natural speech."],[35,86.96,4.2,"They're both /əv/."],[35,91.16,3.16,"We have lots of students."],[35,95.84,4.08,"I should've brought my coat."],[35,99.92,3.4,"Let's practise those words again."],[35,103.32,1.72,"Should've."],[35,107.56,1.56,"Would've."],[35,111.68,1.68,"Might've."],[35,115.96,6.04,"Listen to how my colleague says 'should have' in this sentence."],[35,122.0,1.68,"I should have sent you a message."],[35,123.68,7.88,"We can also get rid of the /v/ sound entirely and say 'shoulda'."],[35,131.56,5.88,"Repeat after me. 'Shoulda'."],[35,137.44,2.92,"I 'shoulda' sent you a message."],[35,145.92,2.36,"Let's watch the rest of the dialogue."],[35,148.28,4.12,"By the way, I'm going on holiday to Malta this weekend."],[35,152.4,5.92,"Lucky you. I went there last year, but we had quite cold weather."],[35,158.32,2.72,"I think it's supposed to be okay at the moment."],[35,161.04,1.36,"Oh, that reminds me."],[35,162.4,1.6,"Can you lend me your snorkel?"],[35,164.0,2.72,"I want to go swimming in the sea."],[35,166.72,2.24,"I wouldn't recommend going this time of year."],[35,168.96,1.52,"There are lots of jellyfish."],[35,172.0,1.6,"Oh, dear."],[35,173.6,1.0,"Have a great time though,"],[35,174.6,2.64,"and don't forget to send me a postcard."],[35,177.64,4.72,"Can you hear a /d/ sound in these highlighted words?"],[35,182.36,1.76,"Cold weather."],[35,185.12,3.04,"Lend me your snorkel."],[35,189.16,3.28,"I wouldn't recommend going swimming."],[35,193.0,1.72,"Send me a postcard."],[35,195.74,4.72,"I'm not pronouncing the final /d/ sound in these words."],[35,201.17,7.13,"When a 'd' is sandwiched between two consonant sounds like in these words,"],[35,208.3,2.94,"we don't usually pronounce it in natural speech."],[35,211.45,2.68,"We had other examples too."],[35,214.13,7.22,"For example, we say 'friends' and 'reminds'."],[35,221.59,5.92,"Repeat after me, and try not to pronounce those /d/ sounds."],[35,228.0,1.8,"Cold weather."],[35,233.38,2.12,"Lend me your snorkel."],[35,240.27,2.08,"Recommend going."],[35,246.71,3.24,"I wouldn't recommend going swimming."],[35,255.33,2.36,"Send me a postcard."],[35,261.12,3.4,"Okay. Let's practise that once again"],[35,264.52,3.16,"and see if you can improve your pronunciation."],[35,268.04,0.96,"Let's go."],[35,272.16,3.56,"Did you get up to much at the weekend?"],[35,275.75,2.28,"Yeah, I went to the pub with my friends."],[35,278.03,2.2,"You should have come."],[35,280.23,3.21,"Well, if you'd told me, I would have."],[35,283.44,2.52,"I think I might have mentioned it."],[35,286.75,2.16,"I don't think you did."],[35,288.91,0.81,"Oh, right. Sorry."],[35,289.72,1.84,"I should have sent you a message."],[35,292.0,5.52,"By the way, I'm going on holiday to Malta this weekend."],[35,297.52,0.98,"Lucky you."],[35,298.5,3.46,"I went there last year, but we had quite cold weather."],[35,301.96,2.83,"I think it's supposed to be okay at the moment."],[35,304.95,1.36,"Oh, that reminds me."],[35,306.28,1.72,"Can you lend me your snorkel?"],[35,308.0,2.91,"I want to go swimming in the sea."],[35,310.91,1.99,"I wouldn't recommend going this time of year."],[35,312.9,2.9,"There are lots of jellyfish."],[35,316.07,0.72,"Oh, dear."],[35,316.79,1.92,"Have a great time though,"],[35,318.71,2.81,"and don't forget to send me a postcard."],[35,321.79,3.12,"How was your pronunciation this time?"],[35,324.88,4.12,"Try recording yourself reading the story after the video"],[35,329.0,4.16,"and listen carefully to where you can improve."],[35,333.16,5.42,"You can download a short audio file of the story by clicking here."],[35,338.58,5.56,"And for more information on the elision of /d/, click here."],[35,344.15,1.92,"What did you do this weekend?"],[35,346.31,3.08,"Have you got any plans for this weekend?"],[35,349.36,4.64,"Tell us in the comments - and see you in the next video. Bye!"],[36,0.0,4.21,"Do you worry that people can't understand your English?"],[36,4.21,4.29,"Do you feel nervous when it's your turn to speak?"],[36,8.51,3.04,"Do you want to sound more like this?"],[36,11.55,2.42,"I'm going to make a cup of tea. Do you want one?"],[36,13.97,4.09,"There's a technique called 'shadowing' that can help you with this."],[36,18.06,6.46,"In this video, I'll show you exactly how it works and how to use it."],[36,24.52,2.04,"I think I might have mentioned it."],[36,26.57,2.13,"This is Lillia."],[36,28.7,4.88,"She's shadowing a video from our series Pronunciation Shadowing."],[36,33.58,6.67,"Or she was trying to until I interrupted her. Sorry."],[36,40.25,3.67,"Shadowing involves listening to a fluent speaker"],[36,43.92,4.09,"and repeating what they say almost at the same time."],[36,48.01,4.05,"This helps train your pronunciation, fluency,"],[36,52.05,3.13,"and listening all at the same time."],[36,55.18,4.13,"It particularly helps your rhythm and intonation."],[36,59.31,5.96,"So now we know why shadowing is useful, but how do you actually do it?"],[36,68.36,4.59,"First, you need to choose the right audio or video for you."],[36,72.95,4.3,"Short clips work best with clear intonation and natural pacing."],[36,77.24,5.05,"You can shadow anything, a conversation or someone just speaking on their own,"],[36,82.29,5.42,"but it's good if the topic is interesting or useful to you."],[36,87.71,4.0,"Fortunately, you can find lots of interesting and useful videos"],[36,91.72,6.88,"and podcasts to shadow on our website, www.bbclearningenglish.com"],[36,98.64,4.96,"So now that you've got your audio, it's time to shadow."],[36,103.6,3.34,"You need to find a quiet space to practice in"],[36,106.94,4.38,"and make sure you have a good pair of headphones."],[36,111.32,5.3,"The best thing to do now is to practice with short sections and repeat them."],[36,116.62,5.59,"Here are four simple steps you can follow for effective shadowing."],[36,125.38,3.38,"Step one: listen without speaking."],[36,128.75,4.38,"Listen carefully to the intonation and the rhythm of the speech,"],[36,133.13,2.92,"just like you're listening to a song."],[36,136.05,5.67,"Step two: listen again and read along with the text."],[36,141.72,2.5,"I think I might have mentioned it."],[36,144.23,4.75,"Step three: repeat with a short delay."],[36,148.98,5.13,"Try not to look at the text, but keep it near you just in case."],[36,154.11,2.75,"I think I might have mentioned it."],[36,156.87,5.17,"Step four: try to shadow with your eyes closed."],[36,162.04,2.17,"I think I might have mentioned it."],[36,164.21,6.05,"And if you need to just pause, rewind and play the same part again."],[36,170.25,1.13,"When shadowing,"],[36,171.38,4.34,"it's good to focus on certain pronunciation features."],[36,175.72,3.67,"You could be trying to improve your pronunciation of 'and'"],[36,179.39,2.04,"or 'to' in sentences."],[36,181.43,3.29,"Or maybe you're listening for silent letters."],[36,184.73,4.46,"Our series Pronunciation Shadowing is perfect for this."],[36,189.19,5.8,"In this series, a BBC Learning English presenter will read a short paragraph"],[36,194.99,4.96,"and give you tips on a specific pronunciation feature in the text."],[36,199.95,4.63,"For example, Becca helps you with the pronunciation of 'would you'"],[36,204.58,2.09,"in this episode."],[36,206.66,3.5,"Would you? Would you like any milk or sugar?"],[36,212.96,3.34,"Shadowing will take time and practice,"],[36,216.3,4.75,"so it's good to motivate yourself by measuring your progress."],[36,221.05,4.13,"Record yourself reading the text for the first time."],[36,225.18,3.5,"I think I might have mentioned it."],[36,228.69,4.04,"It might feel a bit weird at first, but that's normal"],[36,232.73,4.17,"and you'll soon get used to it. When you've had lots of practice,"],[36,236.9,2.54,"record yourself again at step four,"],[36,239.45,2.96,"where you're shadowing without the transcript."],[36,242.41,2.21,"I think I might have mentioned it."],[36,244.62,2.46,"Try not to criticise yourself too much"],[36,247.08,3.92,"and think about the small areas that you've improved in."],[36,251.0,2.79,"You might have linked two words together,"],[36,253.79,2.92,"corrected your pronunciation of a certain word,"],[36,256.71,4.21,"or your intonation might sound more natural in places."],[36,260.93,3.34,"So you've learned why shadowing is useful,"],[36,264.26,4.92,"some tips on how to do it and how to measure your progress."],[36,269.19,3.29,"Now it's time to do it yourself."],[36,272.48,3.13,"We have an entire series designed to help"],[36,275.61,3.34,"with this called Pronunciation Shadowing."],[36,278.94,2.17,"There are stories, dialogues,"],[36,281.11,5.17,"and tips for you to shadow with pronunciation features to focus on."],[36,286.29,5.17,"Click here to watch our shadowing series pronunciation shadowing"],[36,291.46,5.21,"and click here to see our other pronunciation content."],[36,296.67,2.34,"Have you tried shadowing before?"],[36,299.01,1.54,"Do you have any advice?"],[36,300.55,2.5,"Write a comment below."],[37,0.28,2.12,"Look, Susie."],[37,2.56,0.84,"- Oh, no."],[37,3.4,1.76,"- Wait. How does this work?"],[37,5.16,1.48,"- Did this just break you? - Yeah."],[37,9.4,3.0,"[Tigga Mac] Two witty and wonderful comedians,"],[37,12.48,3.16,"one clever Bluey-themed cake to decorate,"],[37,15.72,3.16,"and expert cake hacks from yours truly."],[37,18.88,4.08,"I've sent them everything they need and videos to guide them."],[37,23.04,1.44,"Who will come out on top?"],[37,24.48,1.72,"I'll be the judge of that."],[37,26.44,2.08,"Get ready for a Bluey Cake Off."],[37,29.24,4.36,"This episode of Bluey Cake Off is called Bluey's House Cake."],[37,34.4,2.2,"Hello, Alex Lee!"],[37,36.6,3.56,"Well, well, well. Susie Youssef."],[37,40.16,3.28,"- My old nemesis. - (LAUGHS WICKEDLY)"],[37,43.52,0.76,"Are you ready for this?"],[37,44.28,3.48,"We are making Bluey's house into a flip cake."],[37,47.76,1.52,"- Let's do it. - OK. Go!"],[37,49.52,3.08,"[Tigga] Start with a few dots of buttercream on your board"],[37,52.6,2.0,"to make sure that it doesn't wobble."],[37,54.64,1.76,"Pop your template on top."],[37,56.4,1.48,"Cover it with baking paper"],[37,57.88,1.6,"and we're ready to trace."],[37,60.12,1.72,"Using dark brown buttercream,"],[37,61.84,2.8,"outline all the fine details first."],[37,64.64,3.44,"Go slow and make sure you trace every little line."],[37,68.08,0.64,"Wow!"],[37,68.72,1.76,"[Tigga] Now, grab your other colours"],[37,70.48,2.64,"and fill everything in nice and thick."],[37,73.12,1.04,"So many colours."],[37,74.16,0.96,"[Tigga] No gaps!"],[37,75.12,2.4,"I want it solid and bright."],[37,78.04,1.92,"- Ah! It's a bit wriggly."],[37,79.96,1.04,"Oh, dear."],[37,81.64,1.84,"Oh, my gosh. You've got to breathe as well."],[37,83.88,1.44,"I just realised I wasn't breathing."],[37,85.32,1.08,"I've done a roof."],[37,87.84,4.28,"There's so many words and phrases my kids have gotten from Bluey."],[37,92.2,1.48,"Like, sometimes they'll come out with something"],[37,93.68,1.6,"and I'll be like, \"Oh, you're so funny.\""],[37,95.28,1.92,"And then I realise it's just a quote from Bluey."],[37,97.2,2.4,"But in fairness, your kids are already pretty funny."],[37,99.6,3.12,"- Them saying that I'm their butler. - (LAUGHS)"],[37,102.84,3.24,"Butler, the royal bottom is itchy."],[37,106.16,1.76,"I demand you scratch it."],[37,111.84,1.32,"(SIGHS) Done!"],[37,113.16,1.68,"Oh, my gosh. You've done all the brown?"],[37,114.84,1.16,"Er, not done..."],[37,116.0,1.72,"Holy moly, I'm so much slower than you."],[37,118.48,1.16,"Yeah, I think we're good."],[37,120.8,0.96,"I'm speeding up."],[37,121.76,1.08,"I'm not waiting for you."],[37,122.84,1.28,"I'm going to move on. - OK."],[37,124.12,1.4,"This is a competition, Alex,"],[37,125.52,1.2,"and I have to leave you behind."],[37,126.72,2.52,"I've just got the last lot of stained windows,"],[37,129.24,1.2,"and then I'll be catching up."],[37,130.48,2.92,"- Oh, no. Go back in your line. - OK."],[37,133.48,1.96,"I think I've done my brown. Finally."],[37,135.44,1.56,"What's your strategy with the colours?"],[37,137.0,1.84,"What are you doing? What are you starting with?"],[37,138.84,2.44,"- I'm going big first. - OK."],[37,141.52,3.36,"I wonder how much it matters how even the icing is."],[37,144.88,1.8,"No... I think you're doing great."],[37,148.76,2.28,"I feel like you're getting competitive, Susie."],[37,151.04,1.68,"I'm going with the small guys."],[37,152.72,1.08,"I started with blue,"],[37,153.8,1.72,"then one of the greens,"],[37,155.52,1.64,"which I've now discovered I think there are two,"],[37,157.16,1.76,"but they look very similar to me."],[37,158.92,2.28,"And why were you thinking you would do that?"],[37,161.2,2.56,"That's what the video tells us to do, so…"],[37,163.76,1.96,"- Was it? - ..probably just doing it correctly."],[37,165.72,1.92,"I don't know, though. (MOUTHS) I do know."],[37,167.76,1.88,"- Do you do everything the video tells you to do?"],[37,169.64,1.56,"If the video told you to jump off a cliff,"],[37,171.2,0.6,"would you do it?"],[37,171.88,1.52,"The video's not going to tell me to do that."],[37,173.52,2.44,"Unless there is a cake at the bottom of the cliff."],[37,175.96,2.88,"- Wait, how does this work? - What?"],[37,178.84,1.4,"I've been going over it,"],[37,180.24,2.04,"but the cupcakes are going to go on this side."],[37,182.28,1.16,"Yeah, and then we're going to flip it,"],[37,183.44,1.92,"and then we're going to take the paper off."],[37,185.48,2.2,"Did this just break you? - Yeah."],[37,187.68,0.96,"It's not going to reach through."],[37,188.64,1.76,"It's just going to sit there. - What?"],[37,190.4,1.16,"We're going to push them down,"],[37,191.56,1.08,"and then we're going to flip it,"],[37,192.64,1.36,"and then it's going to be on top."],[37,194.28,0.84,"It's going to work."],[37,195.12,1.32,"No, I don't think it will."],[37,196.44,1.12,"It's underneath now,"],[37,197.56,1.92,"but it'll be on top when you flip it."],[37,201.28,1.0,"If you say so."],[37,203.04,1.0,"- It's going to be great."],[37,204.52,2.0,"It's not going to be as great as mine, but it's still going to be great."],[37,206.52,1.92,"- Yeah, right. - I'm doing my last colour."],[37,208.44,1.56,"Oh, my God. All right,"],[37,213.64,1.8,"All right. I think I'm done, Susie."],[37,215.64,1.24,"I think I'm done, too."],[37,216.88,2.68,"[Tigga] Next, flip your fairy cakes upside down"],[37,219.56,2.16,"and arrange them over your design."],[37,221.72,1.68,"Press gently but firmly,"],[37,223.4,1.4,"so they stick to the buttercream."],[37,226.44,2.2,"Now, into the fridge it goes."],[37,229.08,1.8,"All right, I'm going in. One..."],[37,230.88,0.96,"I'm up to three."],[37,234.84,0.68,"Oh!"],[37,237.36,0.72,"Do I have enough?"],[37,238.08,0.8,"Oh, no!"],[37,239.4,1.28,"- Four, five. One, two, three, four."],[37,240.68,2.32,"- Alex, I've dropped two cupcakes. - Two?"],[37,243.0,1.4,"I don't know that it's going well."],[37,245.04,1.68,"Look! Ah, there's one left."],[37,246.72,2.16,"Oh, no. Oh, dear."],[37,249.8,1.08,"Woo!"],[37,251.88,1.4,"Wow. That was intense."],[37,253.28,2.08,"OK, it's fridge time. They've got to chill."],[37,255.36,0.84,"Let's chill."],[37,256.2,1.92,"OK. Next step is..."],[37,258.32,1.16,"Pipe a little blob of buttercream"],[37,259.48,2.12,"on to the bottom of each cupcake."],[37,261.6,1.92,"Just enough to make them stick."],[37,263.72,2.64,"Place your second cake board right on top"],[37,266.36,4.84,"and steadily but surely flip the whole thing over."],[37,274.44,1.64,"Peel away the baking paper"],[37,276.08,2.52,"to reveal Bluey's house."],[37,278.6,0.92,"Magic!"],[37,279.6,1.8,"- OK. - Wow."],[37,281.4,2.84,"Look how fast and confident I am at piping now."],[37,284.24,1.88,"So calm and confident, Alex Lee."],[37,286.12,0.84,"That's me."],[37,289.44,2.6,"Oh, my gosh. OK. You're doing it. You're doing it."],[37,292.44,1.08,"- I'm doing it."],[37,293.52,2.16,"One, two, three."],[37,296.4,0.6,"It's on."],[37,297.0,1.84,"- Yeah. - Push it down a bit."],[37,299.4,1.84,"And now it's time. - OK."],[37,301.72,0.96,"Woo!"],[37,303.6,0.96,"Now what?"],[37,304.8,1.12,"Down, down."],[37,307.52,1.8,"One, two, three."],[37,309.6,0.64,"Oh!"],[37,310.24,2.36,"- I think it kind of worked! - Comes off…"],[37,313.56,2.36,"Oh, OK. Yeah! - Kind of..."],[37,316.28,1.12,"Are you peeling?"],[37,317.4,2.44,"Oh, my gosh. I'm so nervous. Please..."],[37,319.84,1.28,"Moment of truth."],[37,321.12,1.72,"Oh, my gosh."],[37,322.84,1.84,"This is amazing!"],[37,324.68,1.16,"Whoa!"],[37,326.64,2.44,"Oh! Ho-ho-ho!"],[37,330.04,1.68,"Whoa! It's good!"],[37,331.72,1.76,"Yeah, that looks incredible."],[37,333.48,3.08,"I really was not expecting a good result."],[37,336.72,1.48,"Oh, my gosh."],[37,338.56,2.08,"Pretty almost foolproof."],[37,340.64,1.4,"Speaking as a fool."],[37,343.64,0.88,"I'm speechless."],[37,344.52,1.44,"Yeah, I can't wait to see yours."],[37,345.96,1.04,"Shall we compare?"],[37,347.0,3.24,"- Yes! Oh, Susie. It's so good. - Show me."],[37,351.04,1.0,"It's so cool."],[37,352.04,1.28,"I did not think this would work."],[37,353.32,3.24,"It's, like, how is it neater than normal colouring in?"],[37,356.56,5.28,"Well, it's time for Tigga to choose Susie's Bluey's house?"],[37,362.0,2.4,"Or Alex's Bluey house?"],[37,364.92,2.44,"Tigga, this is the house you want."],[37,367.36,1.88,"Oh! Susie!"],[37,371.04,1.2,"- Tigga here again."],[37,372.24,3.16,"You've both built some beautiful cupcake houses."],[37,375.44,2.04,"It was so hard to pick a winner,"],[37,377.48,2.2,"but it's got to be done. And it's…"],[37,379.68,2.4,"..Alex! Well done!"],[38,0.0,4.64,"What does the /ʤ/ sound have to do with"],[38,4.64,2.48,"'do you' and 'would you'?"],[38,7.12,4.4,"And how will this help to improve your fluency?"],[38,11.52,4.04,"Learn some natural expressions to use in a café"],[38,15.56,5.0,"whilst improving your pronunciation at the same time."],[38,20.56,4.36,"As this is a shadowing video, you'll need to listen"],[38,24.92,4.64,"and repeat after the two characters in this short dialogue."],[38,29.56,2.04,"Like an echo, echo, echo."],[38,31.6,3.32,"Click here to find out what shadowing is"],[38,34.92,5.24,"and how it can help improve your pronunciation and speaking skills."],[38,40.16,3.2,"Right? Let's watch the video."],[38,47.04,3.2,"Hello. Can I get a coffee, please?"],[38,50.24,3.52,"Sure. Is that to drink in or take away?"],[38,53.76,2.76,"Drink in please."],[38,56.52,3.4,"Would you like any milk or sugar?"],[38,59.92,3.28,"Oh, just a splash of milk, please."],[38,63.2,5.4,"Listen to these underlined phrases again."],[38,68.6,3.64,"Can I get a coffee, please?"],[38,72.24,3.76,"Drink in or take away."],[38,76.0,3.4,"Would you like any milk or sugar?"],[38,79.4,2.6,"Just a splash of milk, please."],[38,82.0,3.68,"Did you notice how they were joined together?"],[38,85.68,6.2,"Almost like they were one word. When a word ends in a consonant sound"],[38,91.88,3.64,"and the next word begins with a vowel sound."],[38,95.52,3.64,"We link them together like it's one word."],[38,99.16,8.44,"So instead of saying 'can I', Annie says 'can I'."],[38,107.6,1.72,"Can I get a coffee, please?"],[38,109.32,2.12,"Let's practice some more."],[38,111.44,3.72,"Repeat after me. 'Get a'."],[38,117.84,3.64,"Or we can say it with a glottal stop."],[38,121.48,1.32,"Get a."],[38,125.72,1.52,"Drink in."],[38,130.04,1.52,"Take away."],[38,134.16,1.52,"Just a."],[38,137.84,4.88,"You might also remember from another video in this series"],[38,142.72,4.84,"that 'of' often becomes /ə/ in fast speech."],[38,147.56,5.76,"So 'a splash of milk' becomes 'a splash of milk'."],[38,153.32,5.24,"Remember this when we shadow the dialogue all the way through at the end."],[38,158.56,3.16,"Let's watch the rest of the dialogue."],[38,161.72,5.0,"Here you are. You can sit anywhere you like."],[38,166.72,0.72,"Thanks."],[38,167.44,2.0,"Would you like a receipt?"],[38,170.44,1.48,"Oh, no. Thank you."],[38,171.92,1.68,"What time do you shut?"],[38,173.6,2.4,"At five o'clock."],[38,176.0,2.32,"But that's in five minutes!"],[38,178.32,1.8,"How am I saying"],[38,180.12,3.12,"'would you' in this sentence?"],[38,183.24,2.28,"Would you like any milk or sugar?"],[38,185.52,2.52,"Would you like any milk or sugar?"],[38,188.04,7.36,"The 'would' and the 'you' come together to make /wuʤə/."],[38,195.4,2.32,"Would you like any milk or sugar?"],[38,197.72,3.92,"We also had another example in this dialogue."],[38,201.64,2.08,"Would you like a receipt?"],[38,203.72,3.4,"Practice with me. /wuʤə/."],[38,210.08,2.12,"Would you like a receipt?"],[38,215.6,4.24,"Listen to how Annie says 'do you' in this sentence."],[38,219.84,1.52,"What time do you shut?"],[38,221.36,4.6,"So 'do you' can become /ʤə/."],[38,225.96,2.56,"Listen carefully."],[38,228.52,1.76,"What time do you shut?"],[38,230.28,2.0,"What time do you shut?"],[38,232.28,3.32,"Let's practice. /ʤə/."],[38,238.72,2.12,"What time do you shut?"],[38,244.6,2.56,"Okay, time to practise."],[38,247.16,4.44,"Let's shadow the dialogue again from beginning to end."],[38,251.6,4.56,"Let's see if your pronunciation improves."],[38,259.96,3.28,"Hello. Can I get a coffee, please?"],[38,263.24,3.48,"Sure. Is that to drink in or take away?"],[38,266.72,2.72,"Drink in please."],[38,269.44,3.4,"Would you like any milk or sugar?"],[38,272.84,3.64,"Oh, just a splash of milk, please."],[38,276.48,5.04,"Here you are. You can sit anywhere you like."],[38,281.52,0.71,"Thanks."],[38,282.23,2.0,"Would you like a receipt?"],[38,285.24,1.48,"Oh, no. Thank you."],[38,286.72,2.11,"What time do you shut?"],[38,288.83,2.0,"At five o'clock."],[38,290.84,2.16,"But that's in five minutes."],[38,293.0,3.08,"How was your pronunciation this time?"],[38,296.08,4.0,"Try recording yourself reading the story after the video"],[38,300.08,4.24,"and listen carefully to where you can improve."],[38,304.32,5.48,"You can download a short audio file of the story by clicking here."],[38,309.8,6.72,"And for more information on the pronunciation of 'would you', click here."],[38,316.52,3.0,"What do you usually order at a cafe?"],[38,319.52,2.32,"Let us know in the comments!"],[38,321.84,2.6,"See you in the next video. Bye!"],[39,6.84,4.38,"After hours of delay, your red-eye flight is finally boarding,"],[39,11.21,2.96,"and your long-awaited nap is within reach."],[39,14.38,4.71,"But as the line grinds to a halt, you can’t help but think:"],[39,19.27,2.71,"there must be a faster way to board this plane."],[39,23.06,1.25,"You are not alone."],[39,24.44,4.38,"Mathematicians have been studying this problem for years,"],[39,28.82,3.25,"devising a range of different boarding methods"],[39,32.07,2.71,"and testing them with computer simulations."],[39,35.36,5.21,"These models are designed to consider the two biggest plane-loading bottlenecks."],[39,40.7,2.67,"The first is seat interference;"],[39,43.46,4.46,"when someone in a seat closer to the window is blocked by people"],[39,47.92,1.88,"already in their row."],[39,50.17,2.75,"And the second is aisle interference;"],[39,52.92,5.96,"when passengers seated in the back of the plane are stalled by folks in earlier rows"],[39,58.93,2.67,"stowing their bags and settling in."],[39,62.14,4.84,"Different boarding methods try to tackle these problems in different ways,"],[39,66.98,5.0,"but the most commonly used approach is called back-to-front boarding."],[39,72.28,3.59,"It seems logical— board the back of the plane first"],[39,75.86,4.21,"so passengers don't need to pass others to reach their seats."],[39,80.16,5.88,"And in simulations using a 156-passenger Airbus A320,"],[39,86.04,2.46,"where every person has a carry-on,"],[39,88.5,3.71,"back-to-front boarding takes around 23 minutes."],[39,92.63,4.63,"Not bad, though it barely outperforms the opposite strategy,"],[39,97.26,1.46,"boarding front-to-back,"],[39,98.72,4.92,"suggesting it’s not actually doing much to reduce interference."],[39,104.18,3.0,"Other airlines have tried to speed things up"],[39,107.19,3.13,"by ditching assigned seating altogether,"],[39,110.31,4.25,"assuming that passengers naturally spread out, easing congestion."],[39,114.94,4.42,"Random seating shaves a few minutes off boarding the A320,"],[39,119.36,1.29,"but it's not perfect."],[39,120.83,5.96,"The line tends to slow down at the end as passengers search for remaining seats."],[39,127.08,3.92,"And while the freedom might help you avoid a crying toddler,"],[39,131.13,3.34,"it can also split up groups who board later."],[39,134.92,3.75,"To keep assigned seating and further reduce delays,"],[39,138.8,5.13,"mathematicians developed the outside-in, or WMA method,"],[39,144.01,3.17,"which loads passengers in window seats first,"],[39,147.27,2.96,"then middle, and finally aisle seats,"],[39,150.31,4.42,"bringing boarding time down to just 16.5 minutes."],[39,155.36,5.09,"Still, some experts, including astrophysicist Jason Steffen,"],[39,160.45,2.17,"felt there was room for improvement."],[39,162.82,3.54,"Steffen ran countless boarding order simulations"],[39,166.37,2.5,"until he found the fastest one."],[39,169.12,3.46,"His order, aptly named the Steffen Method,"],[39,172.59,4.17,"can fully board the A320 in just 15 minutes."],[39,177.17,2.63,"So why don't airlines use it?"],[39,180.68,4.0,"Well, Steffen’s method requires passengers to load on"],[39,184.68,2.09,"in a very specific pattern."],[39,186.93,4.09,"First, it fills even rows, then odd rows,"],[39,191.31,5.05,"using a combination of the WMA and back-to-front methods."],[39,196.9,2.79,"By staggering passengers across the cabin,"],[39,199.69,5.21,"it gives everyone just enough room to load their carry-ons all at once."],[39,205.2,3.79,"But Steffen’s method demands perfect execution."],[39,209.29,4.59,"Not only does it require all passengers to arrive at the gate on time,"],[39,213.88,3.96,"but also that they line up in their exact assigned order"],[39,217.96,3.5,"and be willing to split from their families as they board."],[39,221.93,4.88,"While this level of passenger cooperation might seem far-fetched,"],[39,226.81,1.42,"it's not unprecedented."],[39,228.56,4.09,"In the early 1970s, Southwest implemented a policy"],[39,232.73,6.8,"where planes would land, unload, board, and push back all in under 10 minutes."],[39,240.07,2.08,"As soon as the plane touched down,"],[39,242.15,3.71,"attendants began unloading bags into the aisles."],[39,246.07,1.71,"By the time it reached the gate,"],[39,247.83,3.54,"new passengers were already lined up on the tarmac,"],[39,251.62,2.63,"boarding as others deplaned."],[39,254.42,4.92,"People were still finding seats as the plane taxied toward the runway."],[39,259.8,4.0,"Such a system wouldn't fly by today's aviation standards."],[39,264.43,3.09,"There are also engineering options to explore."],[39,267.64,4.75,"Loading a plane with 240 seats through two doors instead of one"],[39,272.64,3.46,"can potentially speed up boarding by five minutes."],[39,276.4,4.42,"Another proposal, the Side-Slip Seat, offsets the middle seat,"],[39,280.99,5.25,"allowing the aisle seat to slide over it and temporarily widen the aisle,"],[39,286.37,3.92,"potentially reducing boarding times by up to 20%."],[39,290.95,5.67,"However, while faster boarding times may save airlines on operational costs,"],[39,296.75,4.21,"it's unlikely they'd actually invest in these redesigns,"],[39,300.96,4.25,"since there's also value in keeping the process aggravating."],[39,305.68,5.34,"Long lines mean passengers are more willing to pay for tickets that allow them"],[39,311.01,1.79,"to get on the plane first."],[39,313.23,1.0,"It’s frustrating."],[39,314.44,2.25,"But before you cancel your tickets,"],[39,316.69,4.21,"it's worth noting that there really isn't much time to be saved."],[39,321.4,3.21,"Even the most perfectly executed boarding strategy"],[39,324.61,4.09,"would likely only save us about ten minutes."],[39,329.07,3.5,"So perhaps the best solution is to take a breath,"],[39,332.95,4.34,"alow your fellow passengers some grace, and try to enjoy the ride."],[40,8.8,4.38,"In 1937, renowned fashion designer Elsa Schiaparelli"],[40,13.18,2.88,"unveiled one of her most famous creations:"],[40,16.18,2.67,"a black stiletto with a pink heel."],[40,19.1,3.59,"What made this seemingly ordinary piece so noteworthy"],[40,22.68,3.46,"was that it was designed to sit on top of a woman’s head."],[40,26.73,2.71,"Created in collaboration with Salvador Dalí,"],[40,29.44,5.0,"Schiaparelli’s bizarre shoe hat was celebrated as an iconic contribution"],[40,34.45,2.04,"to the Surrealist art movement."],[40,37.2,4.13,"Humans have been fashioning caps since the dawn of civilization."],[40,41.75,5.88,"A simple bearskin cap from the Bronze Age is one of the earliest surviving hats,"],[40,47.75,3.63,"discovered in the Alps alongside Europe’s oldest mummy,"],[40,51.38,1.79,"Ötzi the Iceman."],[40,53.59,4.05,"But we’ve found images of hats that are much older:"],[40,58.1,2.38,"some believe that the “Venus of Willendorf,”"],[40,60.52,3.88,"a Paleolithic figurine made around 30,000 years ago,"],[40,64.48,3.63,"is the earliest known depiction of woven headgear."],[40,68.81,2.08,"Hats aren’t just for keeping heads warm;"],[40,70.9,3.04,"they can also be used to make political statements."],[40,74.28,4.59,"Take the pileus cap, a favorite among ancient Romans and Greeks."],[40,79.2,1.29,"In the Roman Republic,"],[40,80.49,4.13,"pileus caps were given to previously enslaved people as a public sign"],[40,84.62,1.08,"of their freedom"],[40,85.71,4.09,"and came to symbolize the ideals of personal and political liberty."],[40,90.34,1.58,"In the 18th century,"],[40,91.92,4.0,"American and French revolutionaries seemed to have conflated the pileus"],[40,96.01,2.67,"with the very similar-looking Phrygian cap,"],[40,98.84,3.54,"embracing the Phrygian as an emblem of their cause."],[40,102.56,2.67,"This is reflected in renderings of Marianne,"],[40,105.35,4.92,"a personification of the French Republic, who often wears a red Phrygian."],[40,111.06,4.84,"Hats have also been an essential accessory for projecting status and power."],[40,115.9,2.38,"In Western Europe in the 15th century,"],[40,118.36,4.05,"nothing conveyed royal excess quite like the hennin."],[40,122.66,2.71,"Popular among the women of the Burgundy Court,"],[40,125.62,3.96,"these bejeweled headdresses were crafted from expensive silks"],[40,129.67,3.5,"and included variations like a flat-topped design"],[40,133.17,3.0,"and the daring double-coned escoffion."],[40,136.51,2.92,"Hat height was restricted according to social rank:"],[40,139.43,3.0,"while princesses could sport meter-high hennins,"],[40,142.43,3.13,"noblewomen were limited to much shorter styles."],[40,145.72,2.04,"And they weren’t without their critics;"],[40,147.77,3.0,"hennins were often targeted by clerics and moralists"],[40,150.77,3.09,"who found their flashy designs impious."],[40,154.53,3.38,"Nearly a century later, Suleiman the Magnificent,"],[40,157.9,2.71,"the longest-reigning sultan of the Ottoman Empire,"],[40,160.62,3.17,"became known for his bold military campaigns"],[40,163.78,2.54,"and even more audacious headgear."],[40,166.45,2.59,"His grand bulb-shaped turbans"],[40,169.04,3.42,"were adorned with sparkling sprays of diamonds or feathers."],[40,172.59,3.75,"His collection also included a grand four-tiered golden helmet,"],[40,176.5,3.8,"though some historians suspect it wasn’t actually designed for wearing."],[40,180.55,3.96,"Rather, the crown was crafted to flaunt his immense power and wealth"],[40,184.51,1.33,"to his rivals."],[40,186.26,3.09,"For other caps, the real story lies in their making."],[40,189.39,4.42,"The sleek British top hat was first introduced in the early 19th century"],[40,193.81,2.29,"as powdered wigs fell out of fashion."],[40,196.82,3.25,"Originally crafted from the fur of animals like beavers,"],[40,200.15,5.3,"hat makers would soak the material in vats filled with toxic mercuric nitrate"],[40,205.45,2.79,"to improve the felts quality and density."],[40,208.5,4.04,"But prolonged exposure to the chemical in poorly ventilated spaces"],[40,212.54,3.13,"led many hatters to develop mercury poisoning,"],[40,215.84,4.84,"suffering symptoms like irritability, slurred speech, memory problems,"],[40,220.68,1.25,"and tremors."],[40,222.09,1.88,"The figure of the Mad Hatter,"],[40,223.97,3.09,"immortalized in Lewis Carroll’s “Alice in Wonderland,”"],[40,227.1,3.13,"is a popular reference to this devastating plight."],[40,230.6,1.83,"Thankfully, in the late 1800s,"],[40,232.44,2.96,"as more people became aware of the dangers of mercury,"],[40,235.48,2.59,"many countries began to ban its use."],[40,238.73,3.63,"Over the following century, top hats, bowlers, and bonnets"],[40,242.36,2.96,"largely fell out of fashion as everyday wear,"],[40,245.57,3.46,"but one cap proves that hats are still big business:"],[40,249.2,1.33,"the baseball cap."],[40,250.83,2.84,"What began as a practical part of a baseball uniform"],[40,253.67,3.79,"stepped off the field and onto the streets in the 1960s."],[40,257.59,2.79,"The cap’s cost-effective, adjustable design,"],[40,260.38,3.79,"along with its easy integration of logos, badges, and trademarks,"],[40,264.18,2.38,"has driven its mass commercialization."],[40,266.85,3.88,"In the United States, some estimate that baseball caps alone"],[40,270.73,3.34,"make up around 80% of the entire hat market."],[40,274.35,1.33,"But as for the future,"],[40,275.73,5.0,"who knows what type of hat will capture our hearts— and our heads— next."],[41,4.17,2.3,"So my mission is to use the internet"],[41,6.5,2.7,"to build our next generation of conservationists."],[41,9.54,2.5,"And I have not been doing this for all that long, OK."],[41,12.08,1.17,"I was born in 1998,"],[41,13.28,2.47,"so I will get that out of the way right off the bat."],[41,15.75,2.0,"But I'll tell you guys how I got started."],[41,17.78,2.33,"I grew up on a farm, and I always loved animals."],[41,20.12,2.17,"So we had rabbits and horses and chickens and,"],[41,22.29,3.14,"you name it, we had it, and I loved it."],[41,25.42,5.64,"Then when I was 18 and in college, naturally, I got a job as a zookeeper."],[41,31.06,1.83,"I’d put on this safari outfit,"],[41,32.93,3.04,"and I would bring animals like kangaroos and lemurs"],[41,35.97,3.4,"to schools and birthday parties to teach kids about wildlife."],[41,39.4,1.17,"It's a pretty cool job."],[41,40.6,1.17,"It was really awesome."],[41,41.81,1.47,"So then one night after work,"],[41,43.31,1.3,"I met some friends online"],[41,44.64,3.47,"who were live streaming themselves playing video games on Twitch,"],[41,48.15,2.17,"and I had no idea what that meant."],[41,50.31,3.4,"But I learned that I could broadcast myself live on this website,"],[41,53.75,1.2,"doing whatever I wanted."],[41,54.98,1.87,"So I could cook, or I could sing,"],[41,56.89,1.8,"or I could play video games."],[41,58.72,3.07,"And if people enjoyed watching it, they would send me tips."],[41,61.79,1.17,"So cash."],[41,62.99,2.0,"Like, actual real money."],[41,65.03,3.87,"And as a college student, that's pretty freakin' sweet."],[41,68.93,3.14,"So I started streaming to about ten viewers at a time"],[41,72.1,1.33,"almost every day."],[41,74.11,2.23,"Then one night after one of these live streams,"],[41,76.34,3.37,"I got a call from a zookeeper friend about this red-tailed hawk"],[41,79.71,2.5,"who had been hit by a car, and he needed help."],[41,82.55,3.0,"So we brought Bean, that's what we called him,"],[41,85.58,1.47,"home to my college house."],[41,87.08,3.04,"And I started to rehabilitate him in my backyard."],[41,91.06,2.47,"So then one night, I'm live streaming, I'm cooking,"],[41,93.56,2.97,"I'm talking about this new hawk in my backyard."],[41,96.93,2.4,"And one of my viewers didn't believe me."],[41,99.33,2.6,"They didn't believe that I had a bird in my backyard."],[41,101.93,1.27,"And I could not have that."],[41,103.23,1.7,"So I put my falconry glove on,"],[41,104.94,3.04,"and I brought the bird inside to show them,"],[41,108.0,2.04,"of course, because I have a problem."],[41,110.07,4.67,"And one of the people watching this stream made a short video of this moment,"],[41,114.78,3.5,"and they shared it on Reddit, and it went viral."],[41,118.28,2.64,"My viewership shot up overnight,"],[41,120.92,3.1,"and I started live-streaming Bean's rehabilitation process."],[41,124.05,2.2,"So I would call him from his perch to my glove,"],[41,126.29,1.6,"and the people watching my stream"],[41,127.89,3.2,"started becoming as invested as I was in his recovery."],[41,131.09,2.3,"It was like they were doing it with me, sort of,"],[41,133.43,2.7,"which was the coolest thing that I've ever done."],[41,136.17,1.17,"And then eventually,"],[41,137.37,2.64,"because I was reaching so many people with Bean's story,"],[41,140.0,2.87,"the zoo that I worked at started allowing me to bring animals"],[41,142.91,3.17,"like cockatoos and reptiles home"],[41,146.11,1.4,"to present on my live stream."],[41,148.91,1.87,"So suddenly, somehow,"],[41,150.81,3.04,"I had turned this zoo outreach job that I had"],[41,153.85,3.84,"into a work-from-home situation, right?"],[41,157.72,1.14,"Which is awesome."],[41,158.89,2.7,"But instead of reaching 20 kids at a time in a classroom,"],[41,161.62,4.1,"I was reaching thousands of people at a time all over the world."],[41,166.56,2.3,"So this was when it really clicked for me, right?"],[41,168.87,2.4,"I wanted to do this for the rest of my life,"],[41,171.3,3.37,"but I wanted to do it at a much, much larger scale."],[41,175.0,2.17,"Two years later, at 22 years old,"],[41,177.21,1.53,"I came up with a plan."],[41,178.78,4.3,"The vision was to build an animal sanctuary that nobody visits --"],[41,183.08,1.33,"or at least not in person."],[41,184.41,3.67,"We would rescue animals and build huge animal enclosures,"],[41,188.09,4.1,"and we would live stream conservation education programs online"],[41,192.19,2.14,"so people could fall in love with our animals"],[41,194.32,2.5,"the way that they fell in love with Bean, that bird."],[41,196.86,3.5,"Our animals would be able to reach millions and millions of people --"],[41,200.4,3.87,"without having to meet a single one of them in person."],[41,204.84,1.94,"And then, of course,"],[41,206.8,2.94,"I would fundraise all of the capital to do all of these things"],[41,209.77,3.47,"and build this entire facility in one single live stream,"],[41,213.28,2.34,"which is the craziest idea that I've ever had."],[41,215.65,1.7,"But this is crazier:"],[41,217.38,1.4,"it worked somehow."],[41,218.82,2.3,"So I'm live streaming one day and there's some --"],[41,221.15,1.2,"Oh, thank you."],[41,223.72,2.04,"I'll take the applause, thank you so much."],[41,226.22,1.44,"So I’m live streaming one day,"],[41,227.66,2.8,"and someone in my live chat said,"],[41,230.49,3.1,"“Maya, if we raise $500,000,"],[41,233.63,2.77,"would you shave your head on stream?\""],[41,236.9,1.97,"And I said, “Yeah, duh.”"],[41,238.9,1.74,"Because who wouldn't do that?"],[41,240.67,1.97,"Who in their right mind wouldn't do that?"],[41,242.67,2.17,"Apparently that was pretty motivating, though,"],[41,244.84,2.54,"because over the course of a 21-hour-long live stream,"],[41,247.41,3.4,"we fundraised $573,000."],[41,254.35,1.77,"To build the sanctuary."],[41,256.55,3.77,"So I bought some land in Austin, Texas, started building animal enclosures,"],[41,260.32,1.77,"and I started growing my hair back --"],[41,262.09,2.77,"that takes about two years, in case anyone was curious."],[41,266.1,1.74,"These screens are huge."],[41,267.87,3.34,"That is a huge picture of me bald in front of a lot of people."],[41,271.24,4.6,"OK, so next up was rescuing animals."],[41,275.87,2.33,"Appa and Momo are marmosets."],[41,278.24,4.37,"They're these very tiny, very, very charming New World monkeys."],[41,282.65,2.24,"Someone originally bought them as pets online,"],[41,284.92,1.97,"but then, after years of inadequate care,"],[41,286.92,2.0,"they were brought to us for rehabilitation."],[41,288.95,2.2,"So they now live at Alveus to help teach people"],[41,291.19,2.44,"about the exploitation of primates in the pet trade"],[41,293.66,3.3,"and about the importance of conserving their rainforest habitats, too."],[41,296.99,2.74,"They also have a little iPad to play games on now."],[41,299.76,1.94,"It's very, very cute."],[41,302.3,1.7,"Finn is an American red fox"],[41,304.03,3.07,"who was confiscated from the illegal pet trade in California,"],[41,307.14,2.3,"so he and his best friend Reed now live at Alveus"],[41,309.44,3.17,"to help teach people about the nightmare that is the fur trade,"],[41,312.64,4.0,"helping to build a new generation of fur-free consumers."],[41,317.01,1.74,"Then there's Winnie the Moo."],[41,319.95,1.17,"She's a cow."],[41,321.15,2.34,"She was rescued from a beef operation in Oklahoma."],[41,323.52,1.17,"She lives at Alveus"],[41,324.69,2.37,"to help teach people about commercial agriculture"],[41,327.09,2.37,"and how it impacts animal welfare and our planet."],[41,329.49,3.54,"One way that viewers can support the sanctuary is by feeding Winnie treats."],[41,333.03,2.13,"So a five-dollar donation made online"],[41,335.2,3.3,"results in treats being dispensed via this automated feeder."],[41,339.44,5.34,"To date, that treat feeder has generated over $38,000"],[41,344.81,2.14,"in funding for the sanctuary."],[41,347.51,1.37,"Isn't that crazy?"],[41,350.25,1.27,"That's a lot of treats."],[41,351.55,1.7,"She's a very happy cow."],[41,355.32,2.2,"Since 2019, through live streaming,"],[41,357.55,2.8,"I have raised over $7.5 million"],[41,360.39,2.3,"for conservation causes across the globe."],[41,368.53,2.0,"In 2025 alone,"],[41,370.57,3.9,"we reached over 250 million people"],[41,374.5,2.17,"with conservation education online."],[41,377.24,2.37,"That's over 10 million classrooms,"],[41,379.64,3.04,"like the ones that I was bringing zoo animals to in college,"],[41,382.71,1.6,"just in the last year."],[41,384.78,3.94,"Alveus is now home to rescued parrots and emus and monkeys"],[41,388.75,1.47,"and wolfdogs and more."],[41,390.25,2.3,"And we have those 36 cameras"],[41,392.56,2.57,"that are live-streaming them 24 hours a day."],[41,395.12,2.6,"But the beautiful thing about these live cameras"],[41,397.76,3.2,"is our animals are all blissfully unaware"],[41,401.0,3.34,"of the thousands of eyes on them at any given time."],[41,404.83,3.27,"Not being open to the public means that our animals are less stressed"],[41,408.14,2.84,"by the unpredictable factors that come with people visiting."],[41,411.01,3.17,"It also means that we've never had to divert any of our funding"],[41,414.21,4.61,"to guest experiences -- like gift shops and concession stands and parking lots."],[41,419.98,1.77,"But one of the most positive benefits"],[41,421.79,3.94,"is we've developed one of the most accessible zoo models in the world."],[41,426.16,3.4,"Anyone with an internet connection can visit our animals for free"],[41,429.59,3.77,"from their device from anywhere on the planet."],[41,433.36,1.17,"And they do."],[41,435.23,2.24,"Our online demographic, our online community,"],[41,437.5,2.57,"they range from about 17 to 28 years old."],[41,440.57,3.74,"We are our nation’s new voters, new consumers"],[41,444.31,1.83,"and up-and-coming decision makers."],[41,446.14,3.37,"We hold the future of this planet in our hands."],[41,450.51,1.53,"But most of our online community"],[41,452.08,2.87,"didn't even join with an interest in environmentalism at all."],[41,454.99,1.37,"They are just normal people,"],[41,456.35,2.17,"internet users who stumbled across our content"],[41,458.56,1.74,"in one of their many, many feeds."],[41,460.32,3.14,"Alveus Sanctuary brings conservation to young people"],[41,463.49,2.77,"largely by meeting them where they already are,"],[41,466.3,1.87,"which is on social media."],[41,469.13,1.4,"But before social media,"],[41,470.57,3.97,"TV programming was establishing the best examples of conservationists --"],[41,474.57,4.27,"heroes like Steve Irwin and David Attenborough"],[41,478.88,1.37,"and Jane Goodall."],[41,480.81,2.27,"These are obviously some of my personal heroes,"],[41,483.11,2.37,"probably some of yours as well, as they should be."],[41,485.52,1.33,"These guys rock, right?"],[41,486.85,1.17,"But for most of us,"],[41,488.05,2.84,"it can be really hard to see ourselves in such legends."],[41,490.92,2.84,"It leaves us feeling like that work is just a far-off dream"],[41,493.79,1.67,"that only other people achieve."],[41,495.93,3.24,"But at Alveus, that dream doesn't feel so far off."],[41,499.73,3.67,"For our viewers, Alveus is a dream that one of them achieved"],[41,503.43,2.84,"from within a space that they're already a part of."],[41,506.94,2.44,"And they get to be a part of it all live."],[41,511.41,1.44,"So we're just getting started."],[41,512.84,2.7,"Our next step is taking this Alveus approach to the wild."],[41,515.58,1.4,"We're building a new facility"],[41,516.98,2.6,"where we're going to breed endangered species at Alveus"],[41,519.62,2.5,"for reintroduction and release into the wild."],[41,522.15,3.94,"We're starting with the recovery of critically endangered wolves."],[41,526.12,3.2,"Our sincere hope is that people will fall in love with these wolves,"],[41,529.33,3.4,"the way that they fell in love with Bean, that bird from college."],[41,532.76,3.04,"We hope that they'll care really deeply about these individuals,"],[41,535.8,1.3,"and then, in turn,"],[41,537.13,2.7,"about the wild places that we return them to."],[41,540.74,1.33,"Millions and millions"],[41,542.11,3.54,"and millions and millions and millions of brand-new conservationists"],[41,545.68,4.4,"fighting for wildlife and wild places, all doing it from their homes."],[41,551.72,2.27,"So my mission is to use the internet"],[41,553.98,2.7,"to build our next generation of conservationists."],[41,556.69,1.67,"And I think it's working."],[41,558.36,1.17,"Thank you."],[42,8.46,4.3,"Long ago, in a land far away, the queen was panicked,"],[42,12.88,3.0,"her grasp on reality seemingly slipping."],[42,16.14,2.79,"She had given birth to two boys and one girl."],[42,19.02,3.13,"But to her horror, before even meeting their father,"],[42,22.14,4.38,"each child vanished, something else taking their place."],[42,27.02,3.63,"She faced accusations of witchcraft and demon sorcery."],[42,30.73,2.92,"And though the king loved her, he was easily swayed."],[42,34.03,2.92,"He soon grew doubtful and banished his wife,"],[42,36.95,2.5,"who left the kingdom in anguish."],[42,39.87,1.17,"Little did they know,"],[42,41.04,3.96,"the queen’s jealous sisters were the ones to replace the newborns"],[42,45.0,1.96,"with unhuman items."],[42,47.54,3.96,"They stashed the babies in earthen pots and tossed them into the river."],[42,51.67,3.5,"And the currents carried each to the home of a holy Brahmin,"],[42,55.3,3.17,"who cherished the children as mysterious blessings,"],[42,58.47,3.38,"and named them Arun, Barun, and Kiranmala."],[42,62.02,3.21,"They proved more and more extraordinary as they grew,"],[42,65.23,3.92,"Kiranmala especially wise and skilled beyond her years."],[42,69.77,1.5,"Once the Brahmin passed,"],[42,71.28,3.38,"the siblings devoted themselves to the home he left them."],[42,75.32,4.38,"One day, a visitor desperate with thirst after losing his way on a hunt,"],[42,79.74,1.29,"sought their help."],[42,81.2,1.29,"It was the king."],[42,82.75,1.96,"The years hadn’t been kind to him."],[42,84.75,1.42,"But under the siblings’ care,"],[42,86.17,4.09,"he felt life brighten inexplicably and was sorry to say goodbye."],[42,90.5,4.25,"Inspired by the king’s visit, the siblings began building a palace,"],[42,94.84,4.75,"traveling far and wide to collect rare jewels, seeds, and marble slabs."],[42,99.72,4.42,"Their home became so exquisite that even the divine architects envied it."],[42,104.35,4.05,"When a holy man stopped by, he told them of a Magic Mountain,"],[42,108.4,2.25,"east of north and north of east,"],[42,110.77,2.96,"where they'd find their most meaningful treasures."],[42,114.11,5.34,"But, he said, only a true hero could complete the journey."],[42,119.7,3.5,"First, Arun departed, leaving behind a sword."],[42,123.37,4.13,"When it rusted, Kiranmala knew an awful fate had befallen him."],[42,127.83,3.59,"Next, Barun attempted the quest— but when his bow splintered,"],[42,131.42,2.88,"Kiranmala understood she'd lost him too."],[42,134.67,3.09,"Determined to rescue her brothers and finish the journey,"],[42,137.8,1.92,"she tended to her plants and animals,"],[42,139.72,2.17,"donned the disguise of a prince for safety,"],[42,141.89,2.71,"sharpened her sword, and set off."],[42,144.97,3.38,"She hadn't trained in hunting or fighting like her brothers."],[42,148.52,5.05,"And yet, Kiranmala ran with the speed of wind and boldness of fire."],[42,153.73,5.05,"She scaled Magic Mountain into the sky, dodging lightning bolts all the way."],[42,159.11,2.96,"At the peak, she found herself on a bejeweled path"],[42,162.07,2.42,"leading towards a glittering tree."],[42,164.78,3.04,"But blasted statues were strewn around her."],[42,168.12,4.34,"These, she realized, were the remains of all the men who'd fallen on the quest,"],[42,172.46,2.04,"including her brothers."],[42,174.75,4.21,"Suddenly she was besieged by wild animals and malicious monsters,"],[42,178.97,2.67,"their voices rising in a cacophonous chorus."],[42,181.84,2.79,"They menaced, snapped, lobbed vicious threats,"],[42,184.64,3.09,"and warned her to leave before it was too late."],[42,188.1,4.59,"But Kiranmala stilled her mind and kept her gaze fixed ahead."],[42,193.61,3.42,"She wouldn't turn back, and she wouldn't respond."],[42,197.24,4.67,"She landed one step after another until she finally reached the tree,"],[42,202.11,3.84,"where a golden bird greeted her as a worthy hero"],[42,205.95,3.21,"and told her to beat the gong at the tree’s base."],[42,209.37,3.59,"With one strike, she quieted the deafening din."],[42,213.21,1.71,"And at the bird’s instruction,"],[42,214.92,4.0,"Kiranmala sprinkled the fountain’s pearly waters across the land."],[42,219.26,5.88,"As she did, the once-doomed princes sprang back to life and bowed before her."],[42,225.43,3.8,"She embraced her brothers, then cut one branch from the tree,"],[42,229.35,1.75,"collected water from the spring,"],[42,231.1,3.5,"and settled the bird on her shoulder for the journey home."],[42,235.02,4.5,"News of Kiranmala’s heroic deeds and heavenly home spread—"],[42,239.53,3.38,"until reaching the ears of the lost, regretful king."],[42,242.99,5.67,"He paid a visit for dinner and once again felt aglow in the siblings’ presence."],[42,248.74,3.5,"But when he noticed the table was set with inedible jewels"],[42,252.25,1.33,"and went to complain,"],[42,253.58,5.42,"the golden bird retorted that “if humans couldn’t eat gold, rubies, and pearls,"],[42,259.09,3.25,"how could they give birth to kittens, puppies, and dolls?”"],[42,262.93,2.96,"With this, the king finally understood"],[42,265.89,3.21,"that his queen was the victim of an ugly trick—"],[42,269.72,2.5,"one he’d been foolish enough to fall for."],[42,272.81,5.09,"These, he realized, were his children, more incredible than he thought possible."],[42,278.32,3.67,"As the golden bird unraveled the story of their separation,"],[42,281.99,5.3,"Kiranmala determined to clear her mother's name and fully reunite their family."],[43,0.24,1.28,"Iron rusts."],[43,1.52,1.36,"Copper tarnishes."],[43,2.88,3.6,"And gold, well…dig a nugget of it out of the ground after centuries,"],[43,6.48,4.24,"and it might be a little dented, but it’ll still shine like new."],[43,10.72,3.2,"So why is gold so chemically inert?"],[43,13.92,2.96,"More than almost any other metal on the periodic table?"],[43,16.88,4.16,"Well, believe it or not, one of the reasons why is special relativity."],[43,21.04,1.92,"And I will be getting into all of that."],[43,22.96,5.2,"But special relativity alone can’t explain why gold doesn’t tarnish."],[43,28.16,4.17,"The real secret, it turns out, is hexagons."],[43,35.52,2.24,"If we’re gonna talk about why gold doesn’t tarnish,"],[43,37.76,3.76,"we should probably understand exactly what tarnishing is in the first place."],[43,41.52,4.56,"Basically, it’s a surface-only version of a kind of corrosion called oxidation,"],[43,46.08,2.72,"a chemical process where electrons get lost."],[43,48.8,4.16,"So it’s similar to rusting, but it stops at the outer layer of the metal."],[43,52.96,4.64,"The tarnished layer basically makes its own seal, protecting the inner layers."],[43,57.6,1.92,"This happens with several metals."],[43,59.52,1.84,"Copper is probably the most famous."],[43,61.36,1.84,"Case in point, the Statue of Liberty."],[43,63.2,3.12,"All that green is oxidized copper tarnish."],[43,66.32,4.64,"Meanwhile, bronze…which is a copper alloy… tarnishes greenish-black,"],[43,70.96,5.04,"silver tarnishes browney-black, tungsten can tarnish in pretty colors, and so on."],[43,76.0,3.44,"And sure, this chemical reaction changes the look of the metal."],[43,79.44,4.48,"But the effects of tarnish can be way deeper than surface level."],[43,83.92,0.96,"Pun intended."],[43,84.88,3.28,"For example, copper is a great electrical conductor,"],[43,88.16,2.4,"so it’s used in all sorts of electronics."],[43,90.56,2.24,"Copper wire is in everything."],[43,92.8,4.72,"But compounds made by the tarnishing process are often non-conductive,"],[43,97.52,2.88,"so they can make electrical components run a lot worse."],[43,100.4,4.0,"You can undo tarnishing, mostly by physical means."],[43,104.4,3.12,"Buffing it out, giving your metal an electrochemical bath,"],[43,107.52,1.84,"even rubbing some toothpaste on it."],[43,109.36,2.16,"But you often lose material that way."],[43,111.52,3.68,"Polishing, really, is just careful scratching, after all."],[43,115.2,5.44,"Plus, if the object you’re trying to polish is a weird shape, it can be difficult to keep it shiny."],[43,120.64,2.4,"So unless you’re going for a particular aesthetic,"],[43,123.04,3.92,"it’s probably just better not to have the metal tarnish in the first place."],[43,126.96,1.44,"Enter, gold."],[43,128.4,4.0,"Now, there might be some of you saying right now, “Ok. You say that gold doesn’t tarnish,"],[43,132.4,3.12,"but my wedding ring is not as shiny as it once was!”"],[43,135.52,6.24,"Well, your wedding ring probably is not 24 karat, or 100% pure, gold."],[43,141.76,4.8,"It’s probably got some other metals mixed in, and they’re the ones that are ruining the shine."],[43,146.56,4.16,"24 karat gold is really soft, and not recommended by most"],[43,150.72,4.8,"jewelers for everyday wear…especially on something you use as often as your hands."],[43,155.52,1.84,"Some of you might also be eager to add,"],[43,157.36,5.68,"“Well actually, gold does tarnish when it's exposed to a combination of ozone and UV light.”"],[43,163.04,3.04,"Yeah. Ok. It also dissolves in aqua regia."],[43,166.08,2.24,"But that’s not really what we’re talking about here."],[43,168.32,4.96,"When I say “gold doesn’t tarnish,” I mean under regular, run-of-the-mill circumstances."],[43,173.28,4.96,"Like, sitting in a tomb, or in a jewelry box, or in a cool pirate-y treasure chest."],[43,178.24,5.12,"This is mostly because gold is a noble metal, a group that often or at least sometimes"],[43,183.36,4.96,"includes ruthenium, rhodium, palladium, silver, osmium, iridium, and platinum."],[43,188.32,4.48,"Think noble gases, which are also named because they don’t like to react with anything."],[43,192.8,3.04,"But some noble metals are more noble than others."],[43,195.84,4.0,"And it’s mostly because of how the electrons are arranged and…yes,"],[43,199.84,2.4,"we’re getting to it…special relativity."],[43,202.24,4.8,"This is a very basic overview, but every positively-charged atomic nucleus is surrounded"],[43,207.04,6.24,"by electron shells, which are locations where all the negatively-charged electrons hang out."],[43,213.28,3.6,"Each shell can hold a certain number of subshells, which come in a variety"],[43,216.88,4.08,"of increasingly complicated shapes that hold more and more electrons."],[43,220.96,4.64,"Shell 1 can hold 1 subshell, shell 2 can hold 2 subshells, and so on."],[43,225.6,4.88,"Now, inside these subshells, electrons really don’t like being alone."],[43,230.48,1.6,"They’d much rather buddy up."],[43,232.08,4.88,"They’re also metaphorically happier when their subshell is completely filled with electron pairs."],[43,236.96,4.56,"So, a subshell that isn’t full will try really hard to either get rid of extra"],[43,241.52,2.88,"electrons or grab a few more to finish its set."],[43,244.4,1.84,"This is one way bonds are formed."],[43,246.24,3.04,"Atoms trade or share electrons with other atoms,"],[43,249.28,3.44,"often in the form of a chemical reaction, and boom!"],[43,252.72,4.0,"Like two kids trading friendship bracelets on the playground, they’re bonded."],[43,256.72,2.4,"But if an atom’s electrons are all buddied up,"],[43,259.12,5.44,"and the subshells are already full, that atom doesn’t really want to bond all that much."],[43,264.56,1.76,"So it’s not very reactive."],[43,266.32,3.44,"Now, gold does have an unpaired electron."],[43,269.76,3.44,"So, in theory, it should want to get all react-y."],[43,273.2,1.12,"But it doesn’t."],[43,274.32,2.48,"This is where the relativity part comes in."],[43,276.8,1.6,"Gold is big."],[43,278.4,1.04,"At least, for an atom."],[43,279.44,3.44,"And the bigger the atom, the more protons it has in its nucleus."],[43,282.88,3.6,"Each gold atom has 79 protons, exactly."],[43,286.48,4.08,"Because opposite charges attract, all those positively charged protons pull on"],[43,290.56,3.76,"the negatively-charged electrons, which gold also has a lot of."],[43,294.32,1.84,"Typically, 79 as well."],[43,296.16,4.24,"But according to quantum physics, electrons are both particles and waves."],[43,300.4,2.56,"So they don’t just crash into the nucleus."],[43,302.96,4.24,"Instead, they change velocity, and start orbiting the nucleus faster."],[43,307.2,2.24,"The bigger the atom, the faster the orbiting."],[43,309.44,4.64,"And according to relativity, the faster you go relative to some other reference frame,"],[43,314.08,2.64,"the more massive you appear to be."],[43,316.72,5.36,"The bigger that apparent mass, the stronger the pull the nucleus has on the electrons,"],[43,322.08,3.6,"and the tighter that nucleus keeps its electrons bound to itself."],[43,325.68,1.52,"For noble metals like gold,"],[43,327.2,3.76,"some of their electrons are traveling more than half the speed of light!"],[43,330.96,2.56,"The apparent change in mass is so great,"],[43,333.52,5.6,"it makes an atom far more unreactive than you would calculate when ignoring relativity."],[43,339.12,1.2,"This is wild!"],[43,340.32,3.52,"Fun fact, special relativity is also why gold is yellow."],[43,343.84,3.84,"Because the change in apparent mass also changes the energy required to"],[43,347.68,2.24,"excite those electrons into a higher level."],[43,349.92,5.12,"They now need to absorb higher energy photons, or photons with shorter wavelengths."],[43,355.04,5.92,"So this means gold absorbs more…and thus reflects less… blue light than other colors,"],[43,360.96,4.16,"leaving just those warm, golden photons to reflect back for us to see."],[43,365.12,2.0,"But back to the whole reactivity thing."],[43,367.12,6.0,"As much effect as relativity has, it isn’t enough to fully explain why gold stays so shiny."],[43,373.12,3.12,"Instead, we have to talk about a process called reconstruction."],[43,376.24,2.24,"Well, really, we have to talk about crystals, first."],[43,378.48,4.72,"In chemistry, a crystal is any solid material whose atoms repeat in a pattern."],[43,383.2,2.56,"Much like a puzzle, every crystalline substance"],[43,385.76,4.48,"like gold has inside pieces and edge, or surface, pieces."],[43,390.24,3.52,"Unless you’re dealing with Simone Giertz’s cool all-edge puzzles."],[43,393.76,1.76,"That’s not relevant here."],[43,395.52,3.28,"In the case of a crystal, those puzzle pieces are atoms."],[43,398.8,5.28,"And the inside atoms are pulled on by their bonds with all of the other inside atoms."],[43,404.08,1.76,"It’s all very nice and symmetrical,"],[43,405.84,4.56,"and for lack of a less-personifying term, the atoms kind of get used to that."],[43,410.4,3.84,"But what happens when you make an inside atom an outside atom?"],[43,414.24,2.48,"By, say, slicing the crystal in half?"],[43,416.72,1.68,"Well, you break bonds."],[43,418.4,5.52,"And when the bonds between gold atoms get broken, the atoms reorganize themselves."],[43,423.92,2.48,"And this is the reconstruction I mentioned."],[43,426.4,4.96,"And gold atoms on the surface tend to reconstruct in hexagonal shapes."],[43,431.36,6.32,"According to a paper published in May 2026, it’s those hexagons that make gold stay shiny."],[43,437.68,3.28,"That's right, we did not know about this, until this year!"],[43,440.96,4.64,"The research team found that the atoms in these hexagon structures are way worse"],[43,445.6,3.92,"at reacting with atmospheric oxygen compared to those in"],[43,449.52,3.68,"the square-ish surface structures that you see in many other metals."],[43,453.2,4.56,"And if the reaction with oxygen doesn’t happen, the gold doesn't tarnish."],[43,457.76,2.48,"Okay, but what if we want it to tarnish?"],[43,460.24,4.0,"Because as it turns out, gold has a lot of promise as a chemical catalyst,"],[43,464.24,2.32,"or a substance that speeds up a reaction."],[43,466.56,2.72,"That particular property could make it super useful"],[43,469.28,2.96,"in everything from manufacturing to green energy."],[43,472.24,2.48,"Maybe it could be used to help break down plastics,"],[43,474.72,4.8,"or produce a chemical known as deuterium oxide, which is used in nuclear power plants."],[43,479.52,1.92,"But if we want to do stuff like that,"],[43,481.44,4.32,"gold’s famous lack of reactivity becomes a problem instead of a benefit."],[43,485.76,2.64,"So, scientists need to figure out how to keep"],[43,488.4,4.0,"its surface atoms from forming those hexagons when it suits us."],[43,492.4,6.4,"And if that happens, I’m sure someone will try to make jewelry or statues out of tarnished gold."],[43,498.8,4.72,"In which case I will remind them, that, once again, pure gold is probably not the"],[43,503.52,4.72,"best thing to try and make a statue out of, no matter what color it is."],[44,0.0,1.48,"The sky is falling!"],[44,1.48,0.68,"...Sort of?"],[44,2.16,4.72,"Our planet gets pummeled by about 48.5 tons of space rocks every day."],[44,6.88,3.88,"That’s the weight of seven and a half elephants falling from the heavens, 24/7."],[44,10.76,1.92,"Most of that cosmic debris is harmless—"],[44,12.68,3.36,"it’s so tiny it burns up in the atmosphere before it rains down on Earth."],[44,16.04,2.12,"But what about the really big rocks?"],[44,18.16,2.48,"The asteroids that could wipe out life as we know it?"],[44,20.64,3.4,"The truth is, they’re out there, and they’ve collided with Earth before."],[44,24.04,3.32,"Today, let’s talk straight about rocks from space."],[44,27.36,3.12,"They’re at the top of my Things to Worry About List."],[44,30.48,2.92,"Hi, I’m Sage, and this is Crash Course Geology."],[44,37.92,0.84,"You’ve seen the movies."],[44,38.76,4.2,"“An asteroid is headed straight for Earth! Everyone panic! Run for your liiiiiiives!”"],[44,42.96,0.56,"Thanks, Dwayne."],[44,43.52,1.92,"I really should go on more movie auditions."],[44,45.44,2.8,"But despite all that drama and disaster, space rocks are sort of…"],[44,48.24,3.84,"run of the mill here on Earth, hitting its surface daily like a light drizzle."],[44,52.84,1.2,"Most of the time."],[44,54.04,1.52,"So… what exactly are they?"],[44,55.56,3.12,"Most space rocks are leftovers from the moment our solar system formed,"],[44,58.68,2.2,"some 4.6 billion years ago."],[44,60.88,2.08,"And we differentiate them in a few ways."],[44,62.96,3.4,"An asteroid is a rock that orbits the sun and is more than one meter across."],[44,66.36,2.8,"Basically, anything with a diameter longer than a baseball bat,"],[44,69.16,3.56,"but not big enough to be a planet with its own gravitational pull."],[44,72.72,2.92,"That’s why asteroids are sometimes called a minor planet."],[44,75.64,1.04,"Oooh, burn."],[44,76.68,1.96,"Most asteroids hail from the asteroid belt—"],[44,78.64,3.32,"an area between Mars and Jupiter that’s chock-full of space rocks,"],[44,81.96,2.4,"some of them hundreds of miles across."],[44,84.36,2.88,"A trip to the asteroid belt would be like… what am I thinking of?"],[44,87.24,2.76,"That video game where you dodge barrels that a gorilla throws at you?"],[44,90.0,3.12,"Oh. Dwayne has just informed me that there’s a video game literally called Asteroids,"],[44,93.12,1.76,"and apparently it’s very famous."],[44,94.88,0.76,"My bad."],[44,95.64,3.8,"Now, when a space rock’s diameter is less than a meter across, it’s a meteoroid."],[44,99.44,1.84,"Usually, a meteoroid is a chunk of asteroid that"],[44,101.28,2.48,"got blasted off during a collision with another asteroid."],[44,103.76,2.8,"You’d think that wouldn’t happen, with all that, y’know, space."],[44,106.56,0.76,"But it does."],[44,107.32,3.04,"So, big space rock: asteroid."],[44,110.36,2.16,"Small space rock: meteoroid."],[44,112.52,4.92,"And if a meteoroid leaves space and enters a planet’s atmosphere, it becomes a meteor."],[44,117.44,3.64,"These are the flashes of light we spot in the sky and call “shooting stars.”"],[44,121.08,3.16,"Meteors are little space rocks that burn up and vaporize into dust"],[44,124.24,1.96,"as they hurtle through the atmosphere."],[44,126.2,4.16,"If a piece of a meteor doesn’t completely obliterate before reaching the ground,"],[44,130.36,2.64,"it becomes something else: a meteorite."],[44,133.52,3.96,"As for asteroids that enter Earth’s atmosphere or hit the surface?"],[44,137.48,2.76,"They’re still called asteroids."],[44,140.24,0.96,"But here’s some good news."],[44,141.2,3.84,"While our planet gets pelted by tons of space rocks every day,"],[44,145.04,5.2,"there’s only one recorded instance of a meteorite actually striking a person."],[44,150.24,6.28,"In Alabama in 1954, a meteorite broke through Anne Hodges’s roof, interrupted her nap,"],[44,156.52,2.28,"and left a mean shiner on her thigh."],[44,158.8,3.64,"And most of the space rocks that hit Earth aren’t big enough to completely wreck it."],[44,162.44,3.24,"If an asteroid less than 25 meters wide enters Earth’s atmosphere,"],[44,165.68,2.2,"it’s likely to burn up and disintegrate."],[44,167.88,3.2,"In fact, an asteroid the size of a car enters the atmosphere"],[44,171.08,3.36,"and explodes before reaching the surface almost every year."],[44,174.44,1.72,"That’s what happened above Chelyabinsk, Russia,"],[44,176.16,4.52,"in 2013, with an asteroid about two-and-a-half times as long as a London bus."],[44,180.68,3.88,"It entered Earth’s atmosphere and burned up 30 kilometers above the city,"],[44,184.56,5.0,"releasing 30 times more energy than the atomic bomb dropped on Hiroshima."],[44,189.56,2.36,"And the soundwave that followed was so powerful,"],[44,191.92,4.6,"it shattered windows across six cities, injuring 1500 people."],[44,196.52,4.36,"So even asteroids that burn up can have an impact—especially if they’re big."],[44,200.88,4.76,"An asteroid 140 meters wide hits the Earth roughly every 20,000 years."],[44,205.64,3.76,"That’s big enough to wipe out a metro area or even whole U.S. states."],[44,209.4,2.52,"An asteroid with the potential to cause global devastation"],[44,211.92,2.64,"and kill most living things is extremely rare,"],[44,214.56,2.64,"reaching us every 100 million years at most."],[44,217.2,2.4,"But it does happen."],[44,219.6,4.16,"And we know that because it’s happened before. Let’s dig a little deeper…"],[44,223.76,1.68,"Sixty-six million years ago,"],[44,225.44,4.88,"an asteroid wider than Mount Everest is tall hurtled through Earth’s atmosphere—"],[44,230.32,2.64,"and it didn’t burst in mid-air."],[44,232.96,4.44,"It directly collided with our planet, off the coast of what’s now Mexico."],[44,237.4,2.08,"And the world changed."],[44,239.48,1.4,"In the first minute of impact,"],[44,240.88,3.08,"what’s now known as the Chicxulub asteroid carved a crater"],[44,243.96,6.52,"over 20 kilometers deep into the ocean floor, vaporizing some of the rocks and water around it."],[44,250.48,5.32,"Within three minutes, its sheer force caused the ground in the center of the crater to liquefy"],[44,255.8,2.44,"and rebound up into a giant peak."],[44,258.24,4.04,"Then, it collapsed again, sending out shockwaves out in every direction."],[44,262.28,3.28,"That collapse dumped chunks of rock at the edges of the crater,"],[44,265.56,2.8,"forming a ring around the molten gap at the center."],[44,268.96,3.08,"And that’s just the first thirty minutes."],[44,272.04,2.96,"Within an hour, the ocean surged over the molten crater,"],[44,275.0,2.72,"where the water began churning and spitting up steam."],[44,278.28,1.84,"For a whole day,"],[44,280.12,6.08,"the ocean sloshed back and forth as rocks from the crater’s rim toppled and stirred up water,"],[44,286.2,2.52,"waves spitting them back onto the crater’s rim."],[44,288.72,4.64,"Also, everything within 1400 kilometers of the impact was on fire."],[44,293.36,4.36,"Twenty-five trillion tons of debris had been hurled up into the sky,"],[44,297.72,5.32,"some of which landed far from the crater, setting fires ablaze all over the planet."],[44,303.04,5.36,"And there was a massive tsunami, with waves 1.5 kilometers high."],[44,308.4,2.96,"The shockwave then triggered more tsunamis, earthquakes,"],[44,311.36,2.68,"and volcanic eruptions across the globe."],[44,314.56,4.2,"If that wasn’t enough, the rocks vaporized by the asteroid’s impact"],[44,318.76,7.32,"unleashed up to 325 gigatons of sulfur into the air, creating toxic sulfuric acid."],[44,326.08,4.2,"This might’ve blocked the sun and chilled the climate for years,"],[44,330.28,3.88,"or tainted the rivers and oceans with showers of acid rain."],[44,335.08,2.32,"All told, these effects were eventually enough"],[44,337.4,5.52,"to kill roughly 75 percent of all plants and animals on Earth,"],[44,342.92,2.04,"including the dinosaurs,"],[44,344.96,3.76,"except for the few that evolved into today’s birds."],[44,348.72,1.2,"Those guys are resilient!"],[44,350.48,2.6,"Can we just appreciate, for a moment,"],[44,353.08,4.84,"that human brainpower can reconstruct a thing that happened millions of years ago?"],[44,357.92,2.48,"But I can’t remember where I left my keys."],[44,360.4,2.24,"Has anyone seen my keys?"],[44,364.84,4.16,"To be fair, it took decades of shared brainpower to get to this level of detail."],[44,369.0,5.24,"In 1977, an American father and son team— Luis and Walter Alvarez— made a discovery."],[44,374.24,3.44,"In Italy, they found a very thin layer of clay sandwiched between rocks"],[44,377.68,3.64,"with tons of marine fossils, and rocks with barely any."],[44,381.32,2.04,"It turns out, you can find this thin,"],[44,383.36,3.88,"clay layer in rocks from the same time period all over the world."],[44,387.24,1.52,"The Alvarezes and their collaborators"],[44,388.76,2.76,"discovered that this layer is full of soot and iridium,"],[44,391.52,5.44,"an extremely rare metal on Earth, most often found in asteroids."],[44,396.96,4.44,"This was the first clue— all that iridium must’ve been ejected from a space rock."],[44,401.4,2.4,"A really big space rock."],[44,404.4,3.52,"Today, that thin layer is called the Cretaceous-Paleogene boundary,"],[44,407.92,2.2,"or the K-Pg Boundary for short."],[44,410.12,3.24,"It marks the moment, about 66 million years ago,"],[44,413.36,5.52,"when a 12-kilometer asteroid rammed into the planet, and left a geological stamp."],[44,418.88,2.92,"The other big piece of evidence was the Chicxulub Impact Crater,"],[44,421.8,4.16,"which marks the spot where the asteroid hit, just off the coast of what’s now Mexico."],[44,425.96,4.52,"It’s a whopping 180 kilometers wide and 900 meters deep—"],[44,430.48,4.32,"but it’s also buried a kilometer below the surface, partially under the ocean."],[44,434.8,2.84,"So it wasn’t even discovered until the 1990s."],[44,437.64,1.6,"Chicxulub: you can’t see me."],[44,439.24,4.2,"It’s pretty much impossible to find evidence of asteroid impacts from that long ago."],[44,443.44,4.28,"Erosion and plate tectonics are constantly exfoliating Earth in slo-mo,"],[44,447.72,1.32,"erasing the evidence."],[44,449.04,2.92,"Like, the Vredefort Impact structure in what’s now South Africa"],[44,451.96,4.12,"marks the spot where an asteroid up to 25 kilometers wide slammed into the Earth,"],[44,456.08,2.16,"a little over 2 billion years ago."],[44,458.24,3.96,"But the main clue left behind isn’t even a crater anymore— it’s a dome,"],[44,462.2,4.8,"an area where the ground lifted after an asteroid obliterated the ground surrounding it."],[44,467.0,3.64,"Hm, yeah. Dwayne doesn’t like that I used the word “obliterated.”"],[44,470.64,3.64,"If you’re wondering how worried we should be about an asteroid doing the same thing to us,"],[44,474.28,1.32,"you’re not alone."],[44,475.6,1.44,"But I have more good news!"],[44,477.04,3.04,"We’ve got one thing the dinosaurs didn’t have, and that’s— what’s that Dwayne?"],[44,480.08,1.96,"Chappell Roan, well yes, definitely."],[44,483.04,0.56,"Thumbs?"],[44,484.28,2.76,"Well, I think you could argue iguanadons did have thumbs."],[44,487.04,2.2,"But we’re getting off track here."],[44,489.24,1.88,"We’ve got one thing the dinosaurs didn’t have."],[44,491.12,1.76,"And that’s human brainpower!"],[44,493.48,1.36,"Remember how I said that before?"],[44,494.84,1.24,"That’s foreshadowing, baby."],[44,496.08,2.64,"Over the years, we’ve developed different ways of tracking asteroids,"],[44,498.72,2.04,"like they’re pizzas we never want delivered."],[44,500.76,3.84,"Today, NASA uses powerful space telescopes to take pictures of asteroids’ movements"],[44,504.6,1.52,"and calculate their orbits."],[44,506.12,5.48,"And none of the asteroids big enough to be damaging— let me repeat: none of them—"],[44,511.6,3.52,"are on track to hit the Earth any time in the foreseeable future."],[44,515.12,3.28,"If an asteroid does someday pose a threat, we’ll know."],[44,518.92,4.28,"And there’s an organized effort to prepare for these risks if they ever arise."],[44,523.2,4.72,"Every two years, NASA’s Planetary Defense team leads a hypothetical tabletop exercise,"],[44,527.92,5.0,"where key leaders and experts run through the “what-ifs” of a potential asteroid impact."],[44,532.92,2.72,"Somebody should tell ‘em to play that Asteroids game I just learned about."],[44,535.64,2.12,"There’s also ongoing research into redirecting"],[44,537.76,3.04,"an asteroid’s path by crashing a spacecraft into it."],[44,540.8,1.76,"And get this: we’ve done it."],[44,542.56,1.6,"Like, not in a movie. In real life."],[44,544.16,3.44,"In 2022, NASA sent a spacecraft on a one-way trip to smack an asteroid."],[44,547.6,1.0,"And it worked."],[44,548.6,3.32,"The craft, called the Double Asteroid Redirection Test, or DART,"],[44,551.92,2.8,"had to travel for more than 10 months to reach the asteroid."],[44,554.72,5.08,"But it successfully headbutted the 160-meter rock onto a slightly different path."],[44,559.8,2.04,"That’s one small smack on an asteroid."],[44,561.84,1.9,"One giant leap for humanity."],[44,563.74,1.38,"So, the sky is falling."],[44,565.12,4.88,"But it’s far more likely to be a shower of Cosmic Brownie sprinkles than a massive dino-killer."],[44,570.52,1.8,"At the time we’re filming this, there are almost"],[44,572.32,3.6,"1.5 million asteroids in our solar system that we know of."],[44,575.92,3.28,"But altogether, their total mass is less than that of our moon."],[44,579.2,2.2,"And there’s even more good news."],[44,581.4,3.32,"The last time a large asteroid had a major impact,"],[44,584.72,4.88,"some life managed to survive, adapt, and evolve into new life forms."],[44,589.6,5.6,"Including a weird, complicated ape that’s invented marvels like novelty socks,"],[44,595.2,5.48,"pizza with cheese in the crust, and ways of tracking asteroids before they become a threat."],[44,601.32,3.04,"Alright, can we strike “asteroid impact” off my “Things to Worry About” list?"],[44,604.36,4.56,"Presently, it is directly above “The server being mad at me because I corrected my order.”"],[44,610.17,1.19,"You’re not mad at me, are you, Katie?"],[44,611.36,2.88,"Next time, we’ll learn how geology fuels our world."],[44,614.24,0.92,"I’ll see you then."],[44,615.72,2.0,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Geology"],[44,617.72,2.56,"which was filmed at our studio in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[44,620.28,2.08,"and was made with the help of these stellar people."],[44,622.36,2.8,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[44,625.16,2.04,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[45,4.1,4.27,"So I’m Steve Huffman, and today I want to start with a question:"],[45,9.27,2.8,"Are humans going extinct on the internet?"],[45,12.48,2.4,"I’ll cut to the chase: my answer is no."],[45,14.91,3.77,"But the internet is becoming more automated"],[45,18.72,2.4,"and more optimized for attention,"],[45,21.12,1.8,"and people feel more alone."],[45,24.02,3.77,"And the purpose of the internet was to connect people."],[45,28.32,3.2,"And the internet has indeed connected billions of people."],[45,31.96,4.71,"But being connected and the feeling of connection"],[45,36.7,1.67,"are two different things."],[45,39.54,3.1,"And so if we want to maintain our humanity on the internet,"],[45,42.67,2.57,"we are going to have to build it intentionally."],[45,45.28,4.27,"And I want to introduce an idea which is the stage versus the city."],[45,50.85,1.77,"But first, a little bit of context."],[45,52.95,2.97,"So a few of us started Reddit in 2005 --"],[45,55.95,1.2,"20 years ago."],[45,57.19,1.83,"We were kids -- I was 21."],[45,59.06,2.5,"We didn't have a plan for the future of the internet,"],[45,61.56,1.74,"we didn't have a plan for ourselves."],[45,63.33,1.57,"The idea was very simple:"],[45,64.93,3.24,"we wanted a place where people could share links from around the web"],[45,68.2,1.2,"and talk about them."],[45,69.43,3.1,"And submit links and talk about them they did."],[45,72.54,3.4,"In fact, one of the first inside jokes on Reddit was"],[45,75.94,1.97,"\"I didn't read the article, but --\""],[45,78.74,3.44,"And then, you know, a thousand words about the article they didn't read."],[45,84.11,2.8,"But from these conversations emerged communities."],[45,86.95,2.5,"First one, then a couple, then a dozen,"],[45,89.49,2.27,"then thousands and hundreds of thousands."],[45,91.79,5.74,"The communities sometimes are interesting, helpful, funny, weird,"],[45,97.53,2.0,"sometimes all of these things."],[45,99.56,1.37,"I left in 2009,"],[45,100.97,1.74,"I was gone for five years,"],[45,102.7,2.1,"and when I came back,"],[45,104.83,2.9,"the platform was in a different place, it was at a low point."],[45,107.77,1.24,"Among many reasons,"],[45,109.01,2.64,"the most important was there were no policies."],[45,111.67,4.0,"There was no guardrails about what behavior was acceptable or not."],[45,116.35,3.5,"And so while communities are created naturally,"],[45,119.88,2.7,"they emerge naturally when people are together,"],[45,122.62,4.27,"a platform, a system where many communities can coexist"],[45,126.89,3.9,"in a healthy and sustainable way and thrive --"],[45,130.83,1.8,"that has to happen on purpose."],[45,133.23,2.47,"And that's what we've been working on."],[45,136.23,1.67,"A few years ago,"],[45,137.94,4.37,"the US Surgeon General declared loneliness an epidemic."],[45,142.77,1.94,"73 percent of people surveyed"],[45,144.74,2.9,"said technology contributes to that loneliness,"],[45,147.68,2.94,"which is not great if you run an internet technology company."],[45,151.15,1.53,"But at the same time,"],[45,152.72,3.87,"people are spending more and more time at in-person events."],[45,157.02,2.64,"So in the most chronically online era,"],[45,159.69,2.87,"people are seeking out in-person connection."],[45,163.13,3.0,"So the question isn’t, do people want connection?"],[45,166.16,2.27,"The question is, how do we deliver it"],[45,168.43,2.84,"and how do we deliver it on the internet intentionally?"],[45,171.74,2.74,"So what would a more human internet look like?"],[45,175.84,3.0,"Well let’s set a little context here."],[45,179.31,3.6,"If you were born after the year 2000,"],[45,182.95,3.44,"you have never experienced the internet without social media."],[45,186.42,2.8,"Social media and the internet are different things."],[45,189.25,3.07,"Social media is a distinct format"],[45,192.36,2.13,"that is unnatural,"],[45,194.49,3.2,"and it causes people to have to perform."],[45,197.69,3.2,"Social media, the analogy I use is the stage."],[45,201.33,2.57,"There's a spotlight, there's an audience,"],[45,203.93,1.87,"there's a performer,"],[45,205.84,3.37,"there's someone or something trying to get your attention."],[45,209.24,3.47,"And so this environment creates performers."],[45,213.34,3.14,"Imagine going to a wedding with an open bar and an open mic"],[45,216.51,2.94,"and everybody taking turns vying for attention."],[45,219.48,1.57,"It's kind of annoying."],[45,223.42,1.27,"As is social media."],[45,226.79,3.14,"The currency of social media is engagement."],[45,230.63,2.7,"It doesn't matter if it's positive or negative,"],[45,233.36,2.17,"just that somebody's got your attention."],[45,236.2,1.64,"And so this causes"],[45,237.87,2.94,"... people to say things they don’t even believe"],[45,240.84,1.4,"to get that attention."],[45,242.57,2.54,"And they very rarely say things they actually believe."],[45,245.41,3.24,"So it's fitting that the Oxford word of the year last year"],[45,248.68,1.17,"was “rage bait.”"],[45,250.15,1.6,"And now with AI,"],[45,251.78,2.74,"you can take the human out of the loop entirely"],[45,254.55,2.87,"and create this content without people at all."],[45,257.79,3.07,"So we sacrificed our humanity by saying things we don't believe,"],[45,260.89,3.24,"and then sacrificed it again by adding AI into the mix."],[45,264.16,3.27,"Merriam-Webster’s word of the year in 2025 was “slop.”"],[45,267.76,3.04,"And so we sacrificed our humanity multiple times."],[45,272.27,3.67,"Let me provide to you another perspective, another metaphor,"],[45,275.97,1.64,"which is the city."],[45,277.61,4.1,"Cities are an inseparable concept from humanity."],[45,282.18,1.24,"What is a city?"],[45,283.45,2.74,"A city is a place where people live,"],[45,286.22,1.4,"they work, they play,"],[45,287.65,1.8,"they go shopping, they eat."],[45,289.82,2.94,"And cities naturally subdivide into neighborhoods."],[45,292.79,4.5,"And those neighborhoods have distinct cultures and vibes."],[45,297.86,2.17,"This is the model we use for Reddit."],[45,300.06,1.17,"On Reddit,"],[45,301.26,3.54,"you can support, you can share a few laughs,"],[45,304.83,1.8,"you can learn about things."],[45,306.67,2.17,"There’s coffee shops, there’s sports bars,"],[45,308.84,1.44,"there’s a few weird basements."],[45,311.27,2.7,"But the communities on Reddit are like the neighborhoods."],[45,313.98,1.24,"They're naturally forming"],[45,315.21,3.17,"and they have their distinct cultures and values and vibes."],[45,318.42,5.64,"And we think this model is a better model for the internet."],[45,325.86,3.4,"The only city I know where everybody wants to be famous is LA."],[45,330.46,3.27,"In general, the stage creates celebrities."],[45,334.5,1.83,"The city creates citizens."],[45,336.93,3.04,"The stage concentrates attention."],[45,340.0,2.17,"In the city, it's distributed."],[45,342.21,3.84,"On the stage, people are competing for the spotlight."],[45,346.08,3.64,"In the city, people are just with one another,"],[45,349.75,2.9,"talking, hanging out, sharing a few laughs."],[45,352.65,2.07,"On the stage, people are working."],[45,355.08,2.17,"Literally on social media,"],[45,357.29,2.8,"people are working to become rich and famous."],[45,360.62,2.1,"You can't become rich and famous on Reddit."],[45,362.76,1.2,"I'm sorry."],[45,365.23,5.77,"The stage uses likes and algorithms to decide what to put in front of you."],[45,371.9,2.27,"On Reddit, we use the upvote and the downvote."],[45,374.44,2.9,"The upvote you understand -- the downvote is very important."],[45,377.34,3.5,"This is where communities enforce their values, define their culture,"],[45,380.88,1.83,"enforce the unwritten rules."],[45,384.08,1.77,"Communities have values."],[45,385.88,1.67,"Stages do not."],[45,387.92,2.54,"I want to walk you through some of these neighborhoods"],[45,390.49,1.2,"or communities on Reddit."],[45,391.72,1.9,"One of them we call “Mom for a Minute.”"],[45,393.62,1.94,"These are all real examples, by the way."],[45,395.59,3.54,"“Mom for a Minute” is a place where you can go"],[45,399.13,2.27,"if you need a mom for a minute,"],[45,401.43,2.4,"or you want to be a mom for a minute,"],[45,403.87,4.47,"if you need a little support or a hug or maybe some honest feedback."],[45,408.34,1.74,"This example, somebody took a break,"],[45,410.11,3.64,"went back to school after five years and got into their dream med school"],[45,413.78,3.1,"and posted that, “Hey Mom, I got into my dream med school today.”"],[45,416.91,2.13,"And the reply, there was many of them, was"],[45,419.08,1.57,"“That is so awesome, you did it!"],[45,420.68,1.2,"Congratulations."],[45,421.88,2.5,"And don't forget to take a little time for yourself.\""],[45,424.42,4.07,"I'd invite you all to be, you know, a mom for a minute if it suits you."],[45,429.99,2.04,"Another community is called “Bald.”"],[45,434.9,1.7,"Community is present with us today."],[45,437.97,4.2,"Bald is for people who are thinking about shaving their head"],[45,442.17,1.9,"and finally committing to being bald."],[45,444.07,1.2,"It's a support community."],[45,445.31,2.33,"It's a community about self-esteem and identity."],[45,448.05,3.5,"This guy posted here, \"My mom is balding because of chemo,"],[45,451.55,1.74,"so I thought I'd join her.\""],[45,453.32,2.97,"You can imagine the responses here, how supportive people were."],[45,456.32,3.5,"Those responses made it back to the mom who was overjoyed and said,"],[45,459.82,3.07,"\"This is probably the most supportive community on Reddit.\""],[45,463.23,2.97,"I would also invite you all to be bald for a minute."],[45,467.7,2.04,"Finally, another example:"],[45,469.77,1.17,"“Am I the asshole?”"],[45,472.4,1.33,"Don't answer that."],[45,474.34,3.1,"“Am I the asshole?” is one of the largest communities on Reddit."],[45,477.44,2.6,"100 million people visited this last year alone."],[45,480.08,4.4,"It's where people pose moral questions, and they're not all high stakes."],[45,484.51,4.07,"For example, “Would I be an asshole if I uninvited my mother-in-law"],[45,488.58,1.27,"to my baby shower?\""],[45,490.75,3.57,"The context here in the small print is “She’s kind of mean to me."],[45,494.36,1.8,"She makes these snarky jokes.”"],[45,496.19,1.9,"Some people asked for more information,"],[45,498.1,2.04,"a few people said \"You're not the asshole,\""],[45,500.13,2.7,"but the overwhelming consensus was \"You are the asshole.\""],[45,503.33,1.27,"The specific feedback was:"],[45,504.63,2.9,"\"This is a nuclear-level response to a small issue.\""],[45,510.51,2.87,"Or \"You have a husband problem, not a mother-in-law problem.\""],[45,515.11,3.97,"OK, so we posed the same prompt to an AI chatbot"],[45,519.08,2.84,"and it said, \"You are not the asshole."],[45,521.95,1.23,"You are brave."],[45,523.92,2.37,"That's not petty --"],[45,526.32,1.27,"that's protective.\""],[45,529.76,3.1,"So OK, no judgment here,"],[45,532.9,5.24,"but what do we get in a world where you strip out humanity"],[45,538.17,3.04,"and the moral advice is constantly gassing people up"],[45,541.24,1.6,"and affirming them?"],[45,542.87,2.64,"On Reddit, you get tough love,"],[45,545.54,3.7,"but that is what a community is and that's where the values come from."],[45,549.28,2.27,"And we believe this is essential."],[45,551.55,3.07,"We've been doing this 20 years, we've seen a few things."],[45,554.62,1.94,"[Here are] some of our observations."],[45,556.99,4.37,"The first is we get to see how people behave"],[45,561.39,1.5,"when nobody's watching."],[45,563.39,3.0,"Reddit is public, but it feels private."],[45,566.86,2.34,"And what we see is that people are neighborly,"],[45,569.23,1.8,"the way they are in a city."],[45,571.37,4.14,"We see that people aren't just good,"],[45,575.54,2.47,"they're actually overwhelmingly good."],[45,578.38,2.84,"When you strip away all the incentives of social media,"],[45,581.24,4.0,"we see that people just want to be helpful and interesting and funny,"],[45,585.25,3.04,"which is a very different perspective than I think one might get"],[45,588.28,3.27,"if their view of humanity is through the lens of social media."],[45,593.12,3.8,"We see that self-governance works amazingly well."],[45,597.29,3.54,"What we say at Reddit is the only thing that scales with people is people."],[45,600.86,3.57,"So all of our communities, they write their own rules on Reddit."],[45,604.43,3.34,"Far and away, the most common rule within Reddit communities"],[45,607.8,2.27,"is some form of \"be civil.\""],[45,610.81,1.87,"AI doesn't have values."],[45,613.48,1.17,"Well, actually it does,"],[45,614.68,1.9,"but those values are programmed in."],[45,617.08,2.84,"And I think it's worth considering who did the programming."],[45,624.19,3.0,"And finally, one of the biggest misconceptions,"],[45,627.22,1.33,"Reddit is mostly anonymous."],[45,628.59,3.24,"People think, mostly anonymous, that must lead to bad behavior."],[45,632.46,1.17,"No."],[45,633.66,3.0,"The context and the incentives lead to good or bad behavior."],[45,636.7,2.23,"People being anonymous actually is safety."],[45,639.37,2.1,"It gives people the freedom to be themselves"],[45,641.5,1.57,"and say what they really think."],[45,643.51,4.74,"What we've learned about cities is that cities are organic organisms."],[45,648.28,2.57,"They're living organisms created by their citizens."],[45,650.88,2.54,"Everything interesting about the city that we're in"],[45,653.45,2.1,"was created by the people who live here,"],[45,655.59,4.14,"as is everything interesting on Reddit was created by its users."],[45,659.76,2.77,"We didn't design it, it emerged."],[45,662.96,2.97,"That's what you get when you let people be people."],[45,665.96,3.97,"Now don’t get me wrong, I’m not actually anti-AI."],[45,669.97,2.34,"It is very powerful and very practical."],[45,673.14,2.3,"Sometimes you just need an answer."],[45,676.27,2.84,"You can go to an AI search and ask,"],[45,679.14,2.8,"what's the best way to cook rice and get an answer."],[45,681.98,2.04,"You can go to Reddit and get 100 answers,"],[45,684.01,2.27,"but sometimes you just need one and that's fine."],[45,687.48,3.5,"But the more automated and summarized"],[45,691.02,4.97,"and sanitized and manicured that the rest of the internet becomes,"],[45,695.99,3.34,"the more we need places for people to be people,"],[45,699.33,1.77,"for people to be humans."],[45,702.07,4.07,"We need places, fewer stages and more cities."],[45,706.17,2.7,"Places where,"],[45,708.87,1.3,"instead of performing,"],[45,710.21,1.94,"you can participate."],[45,712.17,1.83,"Where the incentive isn't to go viral,"],[45,714.01,1.57,"the incentive is to be helpful."],[45,715.91,3.64,"So if we want to keep our humanity in the age of AI,"],[45,719.58,3.54,"we need to build places for people to be humans and let them do it."],[45,723.59,1.27,"Thank you."],[45,728.19,3.14,"Bilawal Sidhu: Hi, this is Bilawal, the tech curator for TED 2026."],[45,731.33,1.47,"That was a fun one, wasn't it?"],[45,732.8,1.83,"Like, I go on social media these days,"],[45,734.63,3.17,"and I have no idea if some of these accounts are even real or fake."],[45,737.83,2.2,"And despite the fact that we've got AI chatbots"],[45,740.07,1.8,"that seem increasingly realistic,"],[45,741.87,3.0,"there's something that draws us to these humanistic communities"],[45,744.91,3.47,"where there's an actual person on the other end giving you advice."],[45,748.41,2.94,"This is such an important conversation to be having right now,"],[45,751.38,2.1,"not just about what it means to be human,"],[45,753.52,3.3,"but how we set the right incentive structures in place"],[45,756.85,2.7,"so that we create a social media that reflects our need"],[45,759.59,1.3,"for human connection."],[45,760.92,2.27,"Advice that you get from an AI could be amazing,"],[45,763.19,3.67,"but the fact that a human being that has had a lived experience"],[45,766.86,3.4,"gives it to you just makes it far more profound and relevant."],[45,770.3,3.34,"I can't imagine a better time for this talk, as we've got AI agents,"],[45,773.67,2.64,"social media all competing for our attention."],[45,776.34,2.97,"Along comes Steve, painting a picture for what it would be like"],[45,779.31,3.67,"to forge a real community and human connection on the internet."],[45,783.01,1.37,"I hope you enjoyed it."],[46,7.63,4.84,"Every year, people leave their doctor's office with an unusual prescription."],[46,12.76,3.59,"Instead of traditional treatments like a pill, shot, or cream,"],[46,16.47,3.54,"they’re sent home with a recommendation for light—"],[46,20.22,3.25,"or more specifically, light therapy."],[46,23.85,1.67,"To understand how this works,"],[46,25.52,2.92,"let’s step out of the office and into this valley."],[46,28.73,1.96,"Here, as winter approaches,"],[46,30.86,4.71,"shorter days spark a cascade of changes within the brains of its inhabitants."],[46,35.78,3.79,"As a result, groundhogs retreat to their burrows for hibernation."],[46,39.7,4.8,"Weasels’ coats turn from brown to white, and birds cease their singing."],[46,44.83,3.42,"And scientists are finding that seasonal behavioral changes"],[46,48.25,2.5,"similarly happen for some humans."],[46,51.17,5.13,"Around 1% of people experience seasonal affective disorder, or SAD,"],[46,56.43,2.75,"a mood disorder marked by episodes of depression"],[46,59.18,2.25,"that recur at certain times of the year."],[46,61.56,5.42,"Most SAD cases appear during the fall and winter months when days are shortest."],[46,67.44,3.29,"And while many people experience mild changes in energy or appetite"],[46,70.73,1.04,"during winter,"],[46,71.78,2.0,"SAD is much more serious."],[46,74.19,3.17,"Its symptoms largely overlap with non-seasonal depression,"],[46,77.36,3.0,"and can include pervasive feelings of sadness,"],[46,80.37,3.38,"loss of interest in activities, unshakable fatigue,"],[46,83.75,3.63,"dramatic changes in sleep and weight, and suicidal thoughts."],[46,87.75,2.71,"There’s still a lot we don’t understand about SAD,"],[46,90.92,3.21,"but researchers have two main theories on what may trigger it—"],[46,94.13,3.38,"both of which involve changes to our body's daily rhythms,"],[46,97.51,2.42,"also known as circadian rhythms."],[46,100.64,1.79,"When sunlight reaches our eyes,"],[46,102.43,4.3,"light sensitive cells in the retina send a signal that travel along the optic nerve"],[46,106.77,2.46,"to our suprachiasmatic nucleus."],[46,109.56,4.34,"This part of the brain is sometimes referred to as our biological clock"],[46,114.07,3.96,"because it regulates our daily rhythms of alertness, body temperature,"],[46,118.07,2.13,"hormone production, and cell growth."],[46,120.7,1.5,"Each night after sunset,"],[46,122.2,3.09,"this biological clock signals the release of melatonin,"],[46,125.41,1.58,"a hormone that triggers sleep."],[46,127.42,3.17,"For most people, this melatonin release is consistent"],[46,130.59,1.79,"regardless of the time of year."],[46,132.67,4.38,"However, one theory posits that this isn’t the case for some people with SAD."],[46,137.22,2.09,"Instead, as the days get shorter,"],[46,139.3,4.67,"their biological clocks signal melatonin to be released for longer each night."],[46,144.18,3.21,"This kind of circadian change isn’t entirely unheard of—"],[46,147.44,3.38,"weasels and groundhogs change their appearance and behavior in winter"],[46,150.81,2.29,"due to a prolonged release of melatonin."],[46,153.32,3.09,"But it’s unclear why this would happen in certain humans."],[46,156.78,2.54,"One idea is that, unlike most people,"],[46,159.61,3.25,"some individuals with SAD have retinas that fail to adjust"],[46,162.87,2.34,"to be more sensitive to light in winter."],[46,165.62,3.29,"A second hypothesis proposes that SAD isn’t related"],[46,168.91,2.08,"to the duration of melatonin release,"],[46,171.17,4.8,"but rather to a complete shift in the timing of circadian rhythms during winter."],[46,176.17,4.38,"This could manifest as a phase-advance, a fast-running biological clock,"],[46,180.76,3.38,"or a phase-delay, a slow-running biological clock."],[46,184.68,2.92,"A phase-delay could explain why many people with SAD"],[46,187.6,1.83,"struggle to wake up in the morning."],[46,189.56,3.59,"Essentially, their hormones are telling them that it’s still nighttime."],[46,193.31,3.09,"But since it’s unclear how any of these circadian changes"],[46,196.4,2.71,"could cause such significant shifts in mood,"],[46,199.11,2.84,"most researchers believe other factors are at play."],[46,202.16,2.71,"For example, as with non-seasonal depression,"],[46,204.87,3.63,"people with SAD are more prone to repetitive negative thinking"],[46,208.5,1.42,"called rumination."],[46,210.04,2.84,"They also tend to be more pessimistic about wintertime."],[46,213.08,3.21,"So much so, that some experts believe living in cultures"],[46,216.29,3.96,"that embrace the winter season may offer some protection against SAD."],[46,220.38,2.46,"And while there is no single SAD gene,"],[46,222.84,2.54,"there are multiple genes that put you at higher risk,"],[46,225.39,2.67,"which may be why SAD can run in families."],[46,228.47,2.25,"Another mystery is that some people with SAD"],[46,230.73,2.59,"experience the opposite seasonal phenomenon:"],[46,233.31,1.38,"summertime depression."],[46,235.19,4.59,"This may be triggered by too much light, but perhaps also heat and humidity."],[46,240.44,2.04,"Thankfully, despite these mysteries,"],[46,242.49,2.79,"doctors do know how to help with this condition."],[46,245.32,2.29,"Treatments used for non-seasonal depression,"],[46,247.62,3.34,"like antidepressants and cognitive-behavioral talk therapy,"],[46,250.96,3.75,"can improve SAD’s symptoms and prevent future episodes."],[46,254.88,1.5,"There's also light therapy,"],[46,256.38,3.17,"which typically involves spending 30 minutes or more each morning"],[46,259.55,2.71,"using a device that emits full-spectrum light"],[46,262.26,2.5,"while filtering out harmful UV rays."],[46,265.3,3.54,"Light therapy should be started under the guidance of a healthcare expert,"],[46,268.85,2.79,"as it can be difficult to figure out dosage and timing,"],[46,271.64,1.71,"and it can cause side effects."],[46,273.73,1.38,"But with regular use,"],[46,275.1,2.46,"it’s thought to help shift circadian rhythms back"],[46,277.56,2.54,"to their sunnier, summer-like position."],[47,7.13,5.17,"You’re clambering through the bush on one of Indonesia’s Lesser Sunda Islands,"],[47,12.43,4.67,"when you suddenly spy a Komodo dragon in the clearing ahead."],[47,17.22,1.84,"So, what should you do?"],[47,19.18,1.5,"Are you doomed?"],[47,21.1,4.8,"Forked tongue flickering; sharp, saw-like teeth glimmering;"],[47,25.9,4.38,"and strong, scaly body stretching up to three meters,"],[47,30.44,6.05,"Komodo dragons are formidable— and the world’s largest lizards."],[47,36.7,2.79,"They can weigh over 80 kilograms,"],[47,39.74,4.5,"but that number fluctuates dramatically since they're capable"],[47,44.25,5.55,"of gulping down 80% of their body weight in a single meal."],[47,50.84,4.96,"Komodo dragons are one of dozens of species of monitor lizard."],[47,56.09,5.42,"They evolved in Australia and now inhabit just a few Indonesian islands,"],[47,61.52,4.25,"where adult komodos generally have no natural predators—"],[47,65.77,1.38,"besides each other."],[47,67.56,4.46,"They begin their lives as limber, tree-dwelling hatchlings."],[47,72.11,1.46,"Within their first year,"],[47,73.57,3.84,"they rarely venture to the ground in order to avoid predators,"],[47,77.41,2.17,"like adult Komodo dragons."],[47,79.78,3.63,"Because cannibalism certainly isn’t out of the question."],[47,83.75,4.17,"Juveniles actively hunt insects and other lizards."],[47,88.08,3.46,"And once they grow larger, they graduate to the ground,"],[47,91.71,3.75,"and their diet eventually shifts dramatically"],[47,95.51,4.13,"to include animals several times their size."],[47,99.89,4.42,"They devour deer, pigs, and even water buffalo,"],[47,104.39,1.5,"bones and all."],[47,106.19,4.38,"Komodos can lie in wait for hours alongside game trails."],[47,110.56,2.79,"But at the opportune moment, they’ll lunge,"],[47,113.48,4.59,"and can reach 17-kilometer-per-hour bursts of speed."],[47,118.41,4.0,"Their extremely sharp teeth have serrated edges"],[47,122.41,5.13,"and tips coated in iron that puncture and tear through flesh,"],[47,127.67,3.34,"making their bites quite literally metal."],[47,131.71,4.13,"But exactly what komodos owe their hunting successes to—"],[47,135.92,5.05,"especially when it comes to taking down larger animals like water buffalo—"],[47,141.09,2.63,"has been debated for decades."],[47,144.01,6.34,"Back in the 80s, one researcher proposed that even if their prey initially escapes,"],[47,150.44,4.3,"Komodo dragon saliva might store harmful bacteria,"],[47,154.94,4.8,"which could proliferate in the bite wound and help kill the animal."],[47,160.11,5.0,"But researchers have since found that not all Komodo dragon saliva"],[47,165.12,2.38,"packs such lethal bacteria."],[47,167.58,3.96,"And some bite wounds might get infected all on their own."],[47,171.71,4.21,"Interestingly, Komodo dragons also have venom glands,"],[47,176.05,4.46,"which secrete anticoagulant and shock-causing compounds,"],[47,180.68,4.67,"possibly exacerbating the destructive powers of their bites."],[47,185.56,4.59,"But it’s unclear whether venom is playing much of an active role."],[47,190.56,4.46,"And Komodos’ thrashing bites alone seem harmful enough"],[47,195.02,2.88,"to cause fatal injury much of the time."],[47,198.28,5.46,"But they’re not just hunters; Komodo dragons are also scavengers."],[47,203.99,2.5,"With their characteristic undulating walk"],[47,206.49,2.88,"and their snake-like flicking forked tongue,"],[47,209.41,3.38,"they’re able to gauge the direction of different scents"],[47,212.92,3.09,"and identify rotting flesh from afar."],[47,216.38,3.17,"When one Komodo takes down a water buffalo,"],[47,219.67,2.84,"dozens may congregate to feed."],[47,222.8,5.09,"However, despite regularly diving into decaying corpses,"],[47,227.93,4.34,"Komodo dragons rarely succumb to infections themselves—"],[47,232.35,4.67,"even though males lead devastating wounds on each other during breeding season."],[47,237.9,5.09,"Researchers think this is because their blood is packed with peptides,"],[47,242.99,1.96,"which are like small proteins,"],[47,244.99,5.59,"many of which can fight microbes and stimulate the Komodo’s immune system."],[47,250.87,4.59,"Some of these antimicrobial peptides have proven so promising"],[47,255.46,4.17,"they’re even being investigated for human antibiotic uses."],[47,259.88,4.0,"But how can you protect yourself in the land of dragons?"],[47,264.05,6.3,"Komodo Island oral tradition holds that a woman long ago gave birth to twins,"],[47,270.43,2.29,"one human and one dragon."],[47,273.14,2.59,"Inextricably connected from then on,"],[47,275.73,3.25,"the relationship between humans and Komodo dragons"],[47,278.98,3.38,"was to be governed by respect and coexistence"],[47,282.4,2.08,"instead of fear and violence."],[47,284.61,3.96,"And indeed, Komodo dragons generally avoid humans—"],[47,288.58,4.0,"unless something like rotting flesh is also on the menu."],[47,292.91,3.17,"If you encounter a Komodo, try to stay calm"],[47,296.08,3.5,"and avoid sudden movements that could pique their curiosity."],[47,299.96,4.3,"You’re better off orienting yourself behind them or to their side."],[47,304.43,4.96,"If one gets close, you could grab a stick and push it against their shoulder"],[47,309.39,2.79,"or hit their sensitive foreheads."],[47,312.31,5.13,"And in the rare event one chases you, try outrunning them, climbing a tree,"],[47,317.56,1.96,"or jumping into the ocean—"],[47,319.52,3.09,"because they can run fast over short distances,"],[47,322.69,1.88,"but they’re no marathon runners."],[47,324.61,5.25,"And large Komodos can’t climb trees anymore and tend to avoid water."],[47,330.62,4.09,"It’s also worth noting that if you did end up in this situation,"],[47,334.7,4.5,"it’s probably because you illegally ventured into a protected area"],[47,339.21,1.79,"without an experienced guide."],[47,341.25,2.96,"So, better to just avoid doing that to begin with."],[48,8.92,4.67,"In April 1943, Swiss chemist Albert Hofmann accidentally exposed himself"],[48,13.59,2.71,"to a drug he derived from ergot fungus"],[48,16.34,4.13,"and recorded its unexpected, mind-altering effects."],[48,20.68,4.38,"The drug, lysergic acid diethylamide— or LSD—"],[48,25.27,3.21,"was soon heralded for its psychiatric potential."],[48,28.73,3.67,"But how do LSD and other psychedelic drugs affect the body?"],[48,32.53,2.21,"And can they actually be medicinal?"],[48,35.32,4.3,"Psychedelics are a class of drugs that alter mood, cognition, and perception,"],[48,39.62,4.38,"often triggering unusual states of consciousness like hallucinations."],[48,44.08,3.54,"They all interact with the body's serotonin neurotransmitter system,"],[48,47.63,4.3,"which regulates many processes, including mood, sleep-wake cycles,"],[48,51.92,1.79,"appetite, and memory."],[48,53.97,2.21,"Lower rates of serotonin signaling"],[48,56.18,2.71,"are associated with impaired sleep and attention"],[48,58.89,3.09,"and diminished responses to positive information."],[48,62.23,3.75,"Meanwhile, higher rates tend to track with more positive moods"],[48,65.98,2.13,"and greater emotional stability."],[48,68.61,3.88,"All psychedelic drugs increase the amount of serotonin signaling."],[48,72.69,3.84,"However, they interact with the serotonin system in different ways"],[48,76.53,3.0,"and affect other neurotransmitter systems,"],[48,79.7,4.21,"all of which determines their unique effects and medical potential."],[48,84.25,3.71,"Hofmann’s discovery inspired a wave of psychedelic research"],[48,87.96,2.54,"that largely stalled in the 1970s,"],[48,90.5,2.67,"due to concerns over breaches in research ethics,"],[48,93.17,2.25,"as well as conservative drug policies."],[48,95.67,2.79,"However, it's picked back up in recent decades,"],[48,98.47,1.92,"and some psychedelics show promise"],[48,100.39,3.09,"in addressing even treatment-resistant conditions."],[48,104.43,3.92,"Take LSD and the mushroom-derived compound psilocybin:"],[48,108.48,4.84,"when they cross the blood-brain barrier, receptors recognize them as serotonin."],[48,113.69,2.75,"Interestingly, they tend to suppress the areas"],[48,116.44,2.79,"that make up the brain’s “default mode network”"],[48,119.24,3.34,"and initiate more connections with other brain regions."],[48,122.91,3.75,"The default mode network is important in self-referential thinking"],[48,126.66,1.33,"and mind wandering."],[48,128.21,2.75,"It’s thought that, by suppressing its activity,"],[48,130.96,4.88,"LSD and psilocybin may generate the distinctive psychedelic experience"],[48,135.84,1.71,"of ego dissolution,"],[48,137.88,3.54,"characterized by a radical shift in self-perception."],[48,142.01,4.42,"The default mode network is also associated with mental health disorders."],[48,146.64,4.84,"By disrupting its activity, psilocybin and LSD might help normalize it."],[48,151.65,3.88,"This may be why they can reduce the symptoms of conditions like depression—"],[48,155.53,3.13,"potentially doing so faster than traditional treatments."],[48,159.28,5.25,"Trials that pair LSD or psilocybin with talk therapy also show promise"],[48,164.53,3.0,"in treating disordered tobacco and alcohol use,"],[48,167.75,3.84,"perhaps by similarly altering people’s self-perception."],[48,172.13,3.67,"And they seem to activate receptors that facilitate social behavior,"],[48,175.8,1.71,"empathy, and gratitude,"],[48,177.51,3.55,"leading people to feel greater harmony with a larger community."],[48,181.68,3.71,"It's thought that this is why they can help alleviate the distress"],[48,185.39,3.5,"people living with advanced cancer diagnoses experience,"],[48,188.89,2.08,"as preliminary trials suggest."],[48,191.69,3.96,"Overall, LSD and psilocybin seem to function similarly,"],[48,195.65,2.13,"but LSD is more potent"],[48,197.78,4.09,"and associated with longer-lasting psychedelic experiences."],[48,202.45,3.25,"MDMA, meanwhile, triggers the release of neurotransmitters,"],[48,205.7,3.38,"including dopamine, norepinephrine, and serotonin,"],[48,209.08,3.29,"and elevates levels of the hormone oxytocin."],[48,212.5,2.71,"These changes generate effects like heightened heart rate"],[48,215.21,1.67,"and sensory perception,"],[48,216.88,4.3,"as well as feelings of euphoria, bonding, empathy, and safety."],[48,221.68,3.5,"Because of this, researchers have begun investigating MDMA"],[48,225.22,3.34,"as a possible therapy for post-traumatic stress disorder."],[48,229.31,5.05,"MDMA releases oxytocin, sometimes referred to as the bonding hormone,"],[48,234.4,4.8,"which may help reinforce an environment of safety, trust, and connectedness."],[48,240.03,3.8,"MDMA is also thought to modulate activity in the amygdala,"],[48,243.82,2.92,"which plays a central role in processing fear."],[48,247.28,1.96,"PTSD can be difficult to treat"],[48,249.25,3.46,"because of how painful it is to revisit traumatic experiences."],[48,252.79,4.05,"However, it’s thought that MDMA could help patients discuss trauma"],[48,256.84,2.71,"without debilitatingly negative emotions,"],[48,259.55,3.59,"and potentially aid in uncoupling traumatic memories"],[48,263.13,2.71,"from the threatening feelings they usually surface—"],[48,265.93,2.88,"a process known as fear extinction."],[48,269.93,2.09,"More studies are needed to test this theory"],[48,272.02,3.38,"and determine MDMA’s safety and effectiveness."],[48,275.65,2.17,"Psychedelics also show some promise"],[48,277.81,3.25,"in helping address chronic pain and cluster headaches."],[48,281.19,4.3,"And unlike pain-relieving opioids, they aren’t considered addictive."],[48,286.03,2.67,"However, they're not without risks."],[48,289.2,3.92,"Some research suggests psychedelics can be stressful on the heart."],[48,293.16,2.33,"And their extensive psychological effects"],[48,295.5,3.04,"make it hard to predict individual responses."],[48,299.34,5.55,"Psychedelics can “unmask” pre-existing psychiatric disorders, like schizophrenia,"],[48,305.09,4.05,"which is why clinical trials usually have thorough screening processes."],[48,309.47,3.25,"And paranoia, psychosis, and hallucinations—"],[48,312.73,3.25,"which can persist even after the drug has been metabolized—"],[48,315.98,1.96,"are potential side effects."],[48,318.65,4.34,"Therapy sessions that employ psychedelics usually require preparation—"],[48,323.11,2.29,"like mindfulness and breathing techniques—"],[48,325.4,2.71,"and should take place in safe, supportive environments"],[48,328.12,2.29,"with emergency protocols in place."],[48,331.2,4.17,"We’ve learned a lot about psychedelics since Hofmann’s fateful experiment."],[48,335.37,3.63,"But there’s much more to uncover— and not without challenges."],[48,339.21,5.05,"Psychoactive substances like psychedelics pose issues for blind clinical trials,"],[48,344.26,2.79,"since their effects can be pretty obvious to participants"],[48,347.05,2.34,"who shouldn’t know they’re receiving treatment."],[48,349.72,2.75,"To fully understand their therapeutic potential,"],[48,352.47,4.0,"we need many more scientifically, ethically rigorous trials"],[48,356.48,5.63,"that weed out bias, prioritize participant welfare, and grapple with complex results."],[48,362.32,1.0,"Who knows?"],[48,363.32,2.5,"The results could be mind bending."],[49,0.08,4.72,"In 1856, a young chemistry whiz failed so spectacularly at whipping up a cure for"],[49,4.8,3.52,"malaria, he accidentally invented the color mauve."],[49,8.32,2.48,"Or, rather, a mauve-colored dye."],[49,10.8,3.76,"And yeah, a scientist accidentally inventing a thing isn’t surprising at all."],[49,14.56,1.76,"Penicillin was an accident."],[49,16.32,1.68,"Super glue was an accident."],[49,18.0,2.56,"But mauve is in a league all by itself."],[49,20.56,5.44,"Because basically, thanks to mauve, an entirely new field of science opened up for study."],[49,26.0,5.68,"And the ability to invent even more chemicals you would not think have anything to do with color."],[49,31.68,0.8,"Like perfumes."],[49,32.48,0.88,"And pesticides."],[49,33.36,3.52,"And the pills inside your medicine cabinet right now."],[49,40.21,2.67,"In the 19th century, malaria was no joke."],[49,42.88,3.76,"Even in Europe, where you might not think malaria would be a huge problem,"],[49,46.64,2.0,"it was killing thousands of people per year."],[49,48.64,3.36,"And at the time, the best treatment was a compound called quinine."],[49,52.0,4.16,"If that word sounds familiar, it might be because you’ve bought some tonic water, recently."],[49,56.16,3.04,"Quinine is what gives it that slightly bitter taste."],[49,59.2,4.8,"In nature, quinine comes from the bark of the cinchona tree, which is native to South America."],[49,64.0,2.8,"Native peoples in the Andes used it in medicines,"],[49,66.8,4.96,"and when European colonists arrived, they also started using it to treat fever and malaria."],[49,71.76,5.2,"As such a valuable resource, Spain kept a tight control over the distribution of quinine."],[49,76.96,2.32,"Great Britain eventually got their hands on some smuggled"],[49,79.28,2.64,"cinchona seeds to grow their own trees elsewhere."],[49,81.92,4.56,"But before they managed that, some chemists thought maybe…just maybe…"],[49,86.48,2.32,"they could synthesize the molecules themselves."],[49,88.8,4.32,"Enter: a young chemistry enthusiast by the name of William Henry Perkin."],[49,93.12,2.96,"Perkin had first gotten into chemistry around age 13,"],[49,96.08,4.08,"when a friend showed him some cool experiments about growing crystals."],[49,100.16,3.84,"He went to college for chemistry just two years later, in 1853."],[49,104.0,3.2,"At this point in history, chemistry as a whole was still a pretty new field."],[49,107.2,4.4,"For example, the modern idea of an atom was less than half a century old,"],[49,111.6,6.0,"and it was only a few decades before that when natural philosophers figured out why stuff burns."],[49,117.6,5.28,"And the field of organic chemistry, the study of carbon-based materials, was just getting started."],[49,122.88,1.92,"But that did not stop Perkin."],[49,124.8,2.32,"He started working with a chemist named August"],[49,127.12,3.68,"Wilhelm von Hofmann at the Royal College of Chemistry in London."],[49,130.8,5.04,"And one day, Hofmann suggested Perkin try to make quinine in the lab."],[49,135.84,1.68,"Now, that is easier said than done."],[49,137.52,4.72,"For one thing, at this point, chemists only knew the chemical composition of quinine:"],[49,142.24,4.96,"20 carbon atoms, 24 hydrogen atoms, two nitrogen atoms, and two oxygen atoms."],[49,147.2,3.2,"They did not know how all of those atoms were arranged."],[49,150.4,3.28,"They didn’t know the shape of the molecule, or how everything was connected."],[49,153.68,2.72,"Which is…pretty important if you want to make it from scratch."],[49,156.4,5.6,"And on top of that, Hofmann and Perkin wanted to make quinine from a specific starting material."],[49,162.0,3.36,"Something inexpensive and readily available: coal tar."],[49,165.36,1.92,"It’s about as disgusting as it sounds."],[49,167.28,5.52,"Coal tar was the thick, black, leftover gunk from creating a fuel source called coke and"],[49,172.8,5.6,"coal gas… a blend of gases that people piped into their homes to fuel lamps and run appliances."],[49,178.4,3.04,"How could coal tar possibly produce a malaria treatment?"],[49,181.44,5.28,"Well, Perkin knew the chemical composition of a compound in coal tar called allyltoluidine:"],[49,186.72,3.68,"10 carbon atoms, 13 hydrogens, and one nitrogen atom."],[49,190.4,4.24,"In other words, it has about half the number of each type of atom in quinine."],[49,194.64,3.28,"Perkin figured he could just stick two molecules"],[49,197.92,4.48,"of allyltoluidine together to get something pretty close to quinine."],[49,202.4,4.32,"And to get the oxygen he also needed, he would just add another ingredient to the reaction."],[49,206.72,3.12,"Unfortunately, we know today that this would never have worked."],[49,209.84,3.12,"Quinine and allyltoluidine have very different structures."],[49,212.96,5.04,"The atoms are connected in different ways, and you just can’t reshape molecules that easily."],[49,218.0,3.36,"But hey, it was the 19th century, so that’s, like, a pretty good first guess."],[49,221.36,2.88,"So Perkin’s first experiment did not pan out."],[49,224.24,4.08,"He then decided to start with a simpler coal tar ingredient called aniline."],[49,228.32,2.24,"And that experiment…didn’t work either."],[49,230.56,3.6,"Instead of making quinine, he ended up with a bunch of black gunk."],[49,234.16,2.8,"Most chemists would have declared the experiment a failure,"],[49,236.96,2.32,"and thrown out the black stuff, and tried again."],[49,239.28,0.88,"But not Perkin."],[49,240.16,4.24,"He kept poking around with his weird product, and he found that if he mixed that black"],[49,244.4,5.2,"gunk with methanol and separated it out from everything else, he ended up with a purple dye."],[49,249.6,2.64,"Which is way more exciting than it might sound."],[49,252.24,3.12,"Artificial dyes were a fairly new technology at the time."],[49,255.36,3.52,"We’d had natural dyes for ages, of course, but they were not perfect."],[49,258.88,1.12,"Some were toxic."],[49,260.0,1.84,"Some washed out pretty easily."],[49,261.84,4.72,"And overall, we didn’t have enough dyes to cover the full range of colors people wanted."],[49,266.56,3.12,"Amazing technicolor dreamcoats were a fantasy that would have to"],[49,269.68,3.52,"wait for the 1970s…in London’s West End theater."],[49,273.2,4.08,"By the mid-19th century, we had a few synthetic shades of red and purple."],[49,277.28,2.88,"And back in 1771, a guy named Peter Woulfe"],[49,280.16,4.08,"had made a yellow dye called picric acid by adding some acid to indigo."],[49,284.24,4.72,"Someone even went on to make picric acid from coal tar, kind of like Perkin was doing."],[49,288.96,2.48,"But picric acid wasn’t that great of a dye."],[49,291.44,3.36,"In fact, it turned out to work better as an explosive."],[49,294.8,4.0,"Fortunately, Perkin’s new purple dye didn’t have any of those problems."],[49,298.8,5.84,"It stuck to silk really well, it didn’t fade in the sun, and it definitely didn’t explode."],[49,304.64,4.24,"He initially called his invention “aniline purple,” after the compound he used to"],[49,308.88,5.68,"make it… or “Tyrian purple,” after a natural purple dye made from the mucus of sea snails."],[49,314.56,0.88,"But honestly?"],[49,315.44,3.2,"“Aniline purple” is kind of ironic, because the aniline"],[49,318.64,5.04,"Perkin used was actually contaminated with a different compound called toluidine."],[49,323.68,3.6,"And toluidine wound up being much more reactive than aniline,"],[49,327.28,3.84,"helping to produce a better color than pure aniline could manage on its own."],[49,331.12,3.92,"In 1857, Perkin received a British patent for his dye."],[49,335.04,4.64,"He also tried to get a French patent, but someone messed up the paperwork, and it fell through."],[49,339.68,2.48,"Free to make as much as they wanted without violating their"],[49,342.16,3.92,"nation’s intellectual property laws, French dyers started calling the dye"],[49,346.08,4.64,"“mauvéine,” because it was a similar color to the flowers of the wild mallow plant."],[49,350.72,2.64,"And “Mauve” means “mallow” in French."],[49,353.36,3.36,"Meanwhile, Perkin took advantage of the patent he did have."],[49,356.72,4.8,"And he dropped out of school and teamed up with his brother to open his own manufacturing plant."],[49,361.52,1.84,"This was a huge gamble."],[49,363.36,2.88,"Perkin didn’t have any experience in the textile industry."],[49,366.24,2.16,"Or in running a business, for that matter."],[49,368.4,4.16,"He had to convince his dad to give him a sizable loan to get his plant up and running."],[49,372.56,3.68,"And what’s more, the yields he was getting at the time were terrible."],[49,376.24,5.12,"To get a single gram of mauveine dye, he would need to process like 690 grams of coal tar!"],[49,381.36,4.72,"Oh, and even if they had enough dye, by the time Perkin & Sons opened, the actual dyers hadn’t yet"],[49,386.08,5.2,"figured out the best way to dye fabrics with the invention Perkin had just sunk everything into!"],[49,391.28,2.08,"Despite these challenges, he made it work."],[49,393.36,0.8,"Somehow."],[49,394.16,2.8,"And for a hot minute, mauve was all the rage."],[49,396.96,2.48,"Empress Eugénie of France loved the color."],[49,399.44,3.52,"Queen Victoria wore a mauve dress to her daughter’s wedding."],[49,402.96,4.48,"Mauveine was even used to dye postage stamps until the 1880s."],[49,407.44,4.4,"But like so many trends before and since, mauve eventually went out of style."],[49,411.84,4.24,"By the time it did, chemists had come up with plenty of other bright colors in the lab."],[49,416.08,4.16,"Perkin, to his credit, didn’t seem to care all that much about mauveine falling out of favor."],[49,420.24,2.88,"He really just wanted to putter around in a lab."],[49,423.12,3.84,"Despite the ramp-up in demand, he sold the factory in 1873 so"],[49,426.96,2.96,"he could retire and focus on chemistry research full-time."],[49,429.92,4.96,"But Perkin & Sons wasn’t the only enterprise leaning into the synthetic dye market."],[49,434.88,3.12,"Chemical companies in France and especially Germany were creating"],[49,438.0,5.2,"and producing more and more dyes to keep up with the demand for new synthetic colors."],[49,443.2,3.6,"You have definitely heard of one of those companies: Bayer."],[49,446.8,4.24,"That’s right, the folks behind Aspirin got their start as a dye-making business!"],[49,451.04,4.88,"And as the years progressed, it was clear coal tar could be turned into more than just dye."],[49,455.92,3.12,"The field of synthetic organic chemistry truly flourished."],[49,459.04,3.36,"For example, Perkin himself developed turned coal tar derivatives into one of"],[49,462.4,3.76,"the first synthetic perfumes: a chemical called coumarin."],[49,466.16,3.12,"He never commercialized it, but someone else later used it in a"],[49,469.28,2.64,"perfume designed to smell like freshly mown hay."],[49,471.92,4.88,"And in 1879, a Russian chemist named Constantin Fahlberg developed saccharin:"],[49,476.8,4.64,"an artificial sweetener about 550 times more potent than sugar."],[49,481.44,3.36,"Just like Perkin, his discovery came about by accident."],[49,484.8,5.28,"Other scientists made explosives, pesticides, detergents… and of course, medicine."],[49,490.08,4.0,"In 1874, a German scientist named Hermann Kolbe figured out how to"],[49,494.08,3.28,"make a synthetic version of salicylic acid from coal tar."],[49,497.36,2.8,"Today, salicylic acid is used in a lot of skincare products."],[49,500.16,2.64,"It also worked as a treatment for rheumatism,"],[49,502.8,3.76,"but it had one inconvenient side effect: it really upset patients’ stomachs."],[49,506.56,3.92,"So those dyemakers over at Bayer…who opened a pharmaceutical division in"],[49,510.48,4.88,"1888… wanted a compound that worked just as well, but without the tummy troubles."],[49,515.36,2.4,"An employee named Felix Hoffman stepped up."],[49,517.76,5.44,"In 1897, he managed to stick an acetyl group onto the salicylic acid molecule."],[49,523.2,5.44,"He had made acetylsalicylic acid, which Bayer branded and sold as Aspirin."],[49,528.64,2.24,"The medicine worked really well,"],[49,530.88,3.76,"and Bayer started marketing it in Europe and the US a few years later."],[49,534.64,4.08,"And Aspirin was far from the only medical compound to come out of this organic chemistry"],[49,538.72,5.12,"boom…be it from coal tar specifically, or other readily available gunk like crude oil."],[49,543.84,4.88,"Researchers made pain killers, vitamins, synthetic hormones, anesthesia, and more."],[49,548.72,4.72,"When they found a molecule that stained fabric or treated a sick person’s symptoms, they’d"],[49,553.44,5.12,"test the variants of that molecule to see if those modifications did anything useful or interesting."],[49,558.56,1.76,"Not everything worked out, of course."],[49,560.32,2.56,"But sometimes they’d land on something exciting."],[49,562.88,4.56,"And then they’d test the variants of that molecule, and on and on and on."],[49,567.44,3.12,"Some even worked as both dyes and medicines."],[49,570.56,4.16,"For example, methylene blue, as the name implies, turns fabric blue."],[49,574.72,4.08,"But researchers also found that it stains parasites blue in tissue samples."],[49,578.8,3.52,"And in a weird twist of fate, scientists found methylene blue"],[49,582.32,3.2,"could also kill the parasite that causes malaria."],[49,585.52,5.28,"So even though Perkin’s initial experiments at making quinine in the lab did not pan out,"],[49,590.8,3.2,"the dye industry found a solution in the end."],[49,594.0,4.0,"And speaking of quinine, even with all the developments that followed from mauveine,"],[49,598.0,3.76,"it took almost 90 years before a couple Harvard chemists figured"],[49,601.76,2.56,"out how to finally make the pesky compound."],[49,604.32,2.96,"They did not, however, actually make it."],[49,607.28,2.96,"Instead, they made a chemical called d-quinotoxine."],[49,610.24,2.64,"And then they pointed to another paper published a"],[49,612.88,4.4,"few decades earlier where researchers converted d-quinitoxine into quinine."],[49,617.28,1.52,"In theory, it should have worked."],[49,618.8,2.88,"But as far as we know, they never confirmed it did."],[49,621.68,3.44,"And when a separate research group finally published the first complete"],[49,625.12,4.48,"synthesis of quinine in 2001… it was by a different route."],[49,629.6,3.52,"It took all the way until 2008 for someone to go back and check that"],[49,633.12,3.04,"the original procedure actually did what it was supposed to do."],[49,636.16,3.68,"And people are still coming up with new ways to make quinine, today."],[49,639.84,4.56,"Perkin probably would not have guessed that a few extra experiments would crack open an"],[49,644.4,5.44,"entire field of chemistry and eventually lead to the development of some life-saving medicines."],[49,649.84,2.32,"That’s one heck of a butterfly effect."],[49,652.16,4.88,"But there’s something to be said for indulging a bit of good old-fashioned scientific curiosity."],[49,657.04,3.13,"You never know what could happen."],[50,8.55,1.88,"I love origami."],[50,10.84,3.5,"I love the way I can transform a simple piece of paper"],[50,14.39,1.67,"into almost anything --"],[50,16.47,3.46,"like a wheel bug,"],[50,19.93,1.25,"my grandparents"],[50,22.64,1.83,"or even a neuron cell."],[50,25.61,1.21,"I’m 14 years old."],[50,26.86,1.21,"I live in New York City."],[50,28.07,1.21,"And I’m in ninth grade --"],[50,29.32,3.67,"so I'm usually busy with homework or after-school activities."],[50,32.99,3.04,"But in my free time, I really enjoy doing origami."],[50,36.58,1.17,"Origami is fun,"],[50,37.78,2.42,"but playing with paper has also shown me"],[50,40.24,3.04,"that origami has the power to stretch my creativity,"],[50,43.33,2.96,"help others and even lead to new discoveries."],[50,46.33,2.25,"And today, I'd like to share one small discovery"],[50,48.63,1.92,"that I made and how I got there."],[50,51.34,2.79,"So I started folding origami over seven years ago"],[50,54.17,3.42,"making ornaments for my family’s tiny Christmas tree"],[50,57.64,2.09,"and getting in trouble in class"],[50,59.76,3.46,"for turning my worksheets into ninja stars and paper claws."],[50,64.06,3.29,"As you can imagine, my teachers were less than overjoyed,"],[50,67.36,2.25,"but I told them I was making academic weapons."],[50,73.94,2.34,"Over time, I began folding more complex origami"],[50,76.28,2.29,"and even designing my own models."],[50,78.62,2.92,"But no matter what, I'm always folding."],[50,81.58,1.75,"Give me any scrap of paper,"],[50,83.33,2.71,"and I'll challenge myself to turn it into something."],[50,86.04,3.5,"For example, if I'm waiting in line at Trader Joe's,"],[50,89.54,2.88,"I might turn a sample cup into a crane"],[50,92.42,2.79,"or even a super-long CVS receipt --"],[50,97.05,1.46,"into a centipede."],[50,98.89,1.42,"Audience: Wow!"],[50,101.26,3.71,"Or even the New York Times into a lizard."],[50,105.64,3.5,"And it’s this process of turning “nothing” into something"],[50,109.15,3.25,"that sparks my imagination and draws me to origami."],[50,113.32,2.88,"I've also found that origami has the power to help others."],[50,116.53,2.54,"During the pandemic, eight-year-old me wrote cards"],[50,119.11,3.29,"and mailed origami birds to seniors at my local nursing home"],[50,122.45,1.92,"to cheer them up during quarantine."],[50,124.37,3.54,"And more recently, I've been folding even more origami birds."],[50,127.92,2.59,"Two years ago, I folded some origami pigeons."],[50,131.5,3.0,"By some, I mean 200 of them."],[50,135.84,2.5,"If you couldn't tell, pigeons are my favorite birds."],[50,138.38,2.21,"And then the following year, I folded sparrows,"],[50,140.59,2.79,"another bird that’s overlooked but that I personally love."],[50,143.43,1.54,"And I folded 100 of them"],[50,145.02,3.46,"and sold them alongside the pigeons to raise over $4,000"],[50,148.52,1.83,"to donate to my local soup kitchen,"],[50,150.4,2.88,"as well as a nonprofit that rehabilitates injured"],[50,153.32,1.75,"and orphaned birds in New York City."],[50,161.32,1.38,"And so through this project,"],[50,162.7,2.96,"I was able to see the power of the most humble material,"],[50,165.66,1.75,"a simple piece of paper,"],[50,167.46,1.58,"to really help my community."],[50,169.04,3.5,"More recently, I've been interested in the intersection of origami"],[50,172.54,1.96,"and science and engineering."],[50,174.5,1.88,"I think it started in middle school"],[50,176.42,1.92,"when I began going down rabbit holes"],[50,178.38,3.34,"to see how scientists and researchers and engineers"],[50,181.76,2.0,"were using origami folds."],[50,183.76,2.59,"For example, in space"],[50,186.35,1.88,"or even to help sick people."],[50,189.27,2.25,"But one fold interested me in particular."],[50,191.52,1.88,"And this was the Miura-ori fold."],[50,193.44,2.63,"It was invented in the 1970s by Koryo Miura,"],[50,196.11,1.75,"a Japanese astrophysicist."],[50,198.53,2.71,"It’s a tessellation, which means it’s a repeating pattern"],[50,201.24,1.46,"of parallelograms."],[50,202.74,2.17,"And the way these parallelograms are arranged,"],[50,204.91,4.0,"they're able to fold down to an extremely compact size in one smooth motion."],[50,209.29,3.0,"This makes Miura-ori an efficient deployable structure."],[50,212.29,3.67,"It’s even been used to fold a solar array that was sent into space."],[50,216.0,2.38,"And last year, I was also reading the news"],[50,218.42,3.25,"about natural disasters like Hurricane Helene."],[50,222.18,2.04,"And I saw so many people displaced."],[50,224.72,2.63,"And it made me wonder if Miura-ori could be used"],[50,227.35,2.79,"to improve emergency deployable shelters"],[50,230.14,2.25,"by making them stronger and lighter."],[50,232.44,2.46,"And so for my eighth-grade science fair project,"],[50,234.9,1.71,"I decided to study Miura-ori,"],[50,236.65,2.54,"and how I could optimize its strength-to-weight ratio."],[50,239.49,2.79,"I started out by designing 18 different folding patterns."],[50,242.28,3.25,"These had different heights, widths and angles"],[50,245.57,1.79,"of the parallelograms in the pattern."],[50,247.37,3.25,"I tested each of these patterns across three different paper weights,"],[50,250.66,1.92,"and each of those variations twice,"],[50,252.62,3.5,"for a total of 108 Miura-ori that I folded and tested."],[50,256.17,1.83,"And for each of these Miura-ori,"],[50,258.0,2.71,"I wanted to see not only how much weight they could hold,"],[50,260.76,2.84,"but also how many times their own weight they could hold."],[50,263.63,2.04,"And so for two months,"],[50,265.68,3.09,"I transformed my family's small New York City apartment"],[50,268.76,1.54,"into my testing lab"],[50,270.35,2.59,"with Miura-ori all over the place."],[50,272.98,1.46,"When I was first starting out,"],[50,274.44,2.25,"I grabbed basically every heavy book and item,"],[50,276.69,2.17,"and I stacked them on top of the Miura-ori."],[50,278.86,2.84,"But I quickly realized I didn't actually have enough weights"],[50,281.74,1.79,"to accurately test the models."],[50,283.57,3.96,"And so I asked my parents to buy some really heavy exercise weights."],[50,287.57,3.71,"250 hours later -- and after a lot of heavy lifting --"],[50,291.29,2.42,"I found that the Miura-ori with the smallest"],[50,293.75,1.96,"and least acutely-angled panels"],[50,295.71,1.79,"made out of the lightest paper"],[50,297.54,2.42,"had the greatest strength-to-weight ratio."],[50,300.0,3.17,"The strongest pattern held almost 200 pounds,"],[50,303.17,4.71,"and another lighter version held more than 10,000 times its own weight."],[50,307.93,2.71,"I found that the stronger patterns were more compact,"],[50,310.68,2.04,"and they also created truss-like structures"],[50,312.73,2.29,"that were able to distribute pressure evenly."],[50,315.02,2.92,"It's amazing that something can be so strong"],[50,317.98,2.25,"and yet so lightweight at the same time."],[50,320.27,3.5,"And it's exciting to think about what potential applications"],[50,323.82,1.67,"a fold like this could have --"],[50,325.53,3.46,"maybe even helping to imagine a better emergency shelter one day."],[50,329.53,3.17,"And so this work that I did by myself at home"],[50,332.75,3.84,"led to me receiving the top prize at a national STEM competition last year."],[50,343.09,2.29,"As you can see, I was really shocked ..."],[50,349.3,4.0,"to receive such a big prize for simply playing with paper."],[50,353.68,1.29,"And so in the future,"],[50,355.02,3.09,"I definitely want to keep exploring and learning."],[50,358.15,3.17,"But most of all: to keep playing with origami"],[50,361.36,2.96,"so I can continue to discover all the possibilities"],[50,364.32,2.42,"a single piece of paper can hold."],[50,366.78,1.17,"Thank you."],[51,4.29,2.71,"So it's exciting and ironic"],[51,7.04,3.79,"that I'm giving a TED Talk about time in three minutes."],[51,10.88,1.71,"But what's more ironic"],[51,12.63,4.25,"is the Founding Fathers promised us the pursuit of happiness."],[51,16.93,2.21,"Not the guarantee of happiness,"],[51,19.18,1.79,"the pursuit,"],[51,21.02,4.13,"which I think of as having the freedom to choose how you spend your days,"],[51,25.15,2.5,"the space and focus to pursue a new passion"],[51,27.65,3.34,"or just the ability to be present with the people you love --"],[51,32.03,3.67,"all of which require something that none of us has enough of:"],[51,36.41,1.29,"time."],[51,38.24,2.59,"My dad laid tile for 40 years."],[51,40.83,2.59,"Do you know what he got for his retirement?"],[51,43.46,1.25,"Knee surgery."],[51,45.88,4.25,"And he told me, \"I think I'm going to get another job to keep me busy.\""],[51,50.17,4.42,"Now if you’ve ever been out of work, you know how uncomfortable that feels."],[51,55.51,2.92,"Now imagine something that feels far worse than that."],[51,59.39,3.71,"Never having the time to truly live your life at all."],[51,64.14,3.13,"We like to call this a time management problem, right?"],[51,67.27,3.46,"But more Americans are working multiple jobs to survive"],[51,70.73,2.0,"than ever recorded before."],[51,72.74,4.88,"How are you supposed to manage time that's not even yours to begin with?"],[51,77.62,3.38,"This goes far beyond a time management problem."],[51,80.99,2.46,"It's actually a civil rights issue."],[51,84.5,2.21,"Can a democracy survive"],[51,86.75,4.84,"when its citizens no longer have the time to truly participate in it?"],[51,92.46,2.46,"And I’m not just talking about the hours --"],[51,94.97,1.25,"I mean the pace."],[51,96.26,3.46,"Moments of connection have been replaced by convenience."],[51,99.76,3.09,"We get everything delivered to our doors at record speed."],[51,102.89,3.63,"We’ll rideshare to the airport rather than asking a neighbor."],[51,106.56,2.71,"We put productivity on this pedestal"],[51,109.27,3.04,"to maximize the minutes that we do have access to."],[51,113.44,3.67,"The last time we fought for more time and won"],[51,117.11,1.71,"was over a century ago,"],[51,118.82,3.17,"when workers marched and bled under a banner that said,"],[51,121.99,2.67,"\"Eight hours for work, eight hours for rest,"],[51,124.66,1.88,"eight hours for what you will.\""],[51,128.12,3.8,"I love that phrase, \"what you will.\""],[51,131.96,3.0,"I think of letting the sun hit your face,"],[51,135.8,2.59,"flipping cars when your sports team wins,"],[51,138.43,1.71,"like we do here in Philly."],[51,141.72,5.09,"Or doing absolutely nothing and not feeling guilty about it for once."],[51,148.06,3.29,"We cannot optimize the life out of our lives now"],[51,151.36,3.88,"betting on a later that isn't promised."],[51,156.07,2.08,"So I want to propose something new."],[51,158.15,2.0,"A time amendment."],[51,160.16,2.59,"And it's built on three results."],[51,162.74,3.8,"First, that time is a civil right,"],[51,166.54,2.54,"and no one's access to it should be dictated"],[51,169.12,2.75,"by how much money they make or who they are."],[51,177.26,4.46,"Second, that joy is proof a democracy is functioning"],[51,181.76,2.04,"the way that it was intended."],[51,183.85,1.29,"During the AIDS crisis,"],[51,185.18,1.25,"Dan Savage said,"],[51,186.47,1.83,"\"We buried our friends in the morning,"],[51,188.35,4.13,"we protested in the afternoon, and we danced all night."],[51,192.48,2.0,"The dance is what kept us in the fight"],[51,194.48,2.54,"because it was the dance we were fighting for.\""],[51,198.44,6.01,"And third, rest is infrastructure, not a reward for a lifetime of hard work."],[51,204.45,5.71,"It gives us the capacity to imagine and participate in our shared future."],[51,216.92,2.21,"The pursuit of happiness is a promissory note"],[51,219.17,2.08,"in gorgeous penmanship,"],[51,221.3,2.13,"but a promissory note is only worth something"],[51,223.47,1.46,"if somebody pays up."],[51,224.97,3.54,"And it is about time that we showed up to collect."],[51,228.56,1.25,"Thank you."],[52,7.34,4.21,"Once you've found an idea that you're excited to share with an audience,"],[52,11.55,2.96,"you're ready to start putting together a talk."],[52,14.97,2.88,"Deciding on a clear throughline is an essential part"],[52,17.85,1.96,"of developing a strong talk."],[52,19.93,4.25,"Another important piece, though, is picking a presentation plan."],[52,24.39,2.79,"There are many ways to prepare for and deliver a talk,"],[52,27.19,2.54,"and it's important to find one that's right for you."],[52,29.94,2.21,"One of the first key decisions you need to make"],[52,32.15,4.21,"is whether you will, A) write out the talk in full as a complete script"],[52,36.37,2.96,"to be read, memorized, or a combination of the two."],[52,39.7,4.96,"Or, B) come up with a clear plan for your talk without scripting it,"],[52,44.66,3.63,"so you can speak to each of your points in the moment when you present."],[52,48.5,3.38,"For most speakers, the best way to say what you really want to say,"],[52,51.88,3.09,"in the most powerful way, is to write a script."],[52,55.3,4.34,"Then get to know the script so well that it becomes part of you."],[52,59.76,1.88,"This takes a lot of work."],[52,62.18,2.92,"Imagine you get to watch a friend trying to memorize his talk"],[52,65.1,1.33,"over the course of a week."],[52,66.52,3.59,"Let’s say that you ask him every day to give the best version of the talk"],[52,70.11,3.38,"he can give just from memory, without using any notes."],[52,73.69,1.96,"You will notice something odd."],[52,76.16,4.09,"Early on in the week, your friend will be very exciting to listen to."],[52,80.28,2.34,"His talk may seem a little disorganized,"],[52,82.62,3.0,"because he doesn’t actually know any of it by heart yet."],[52,85.87,3.09,"He simply does his best to give you the information he knows"],[52,88.96,2.33,"in approximately the order he's planned."],[52,91.46,3.04,"But his passion about his idea comes through."],[52,94.88,2.92,"A few days later, you will notice a change."],[52,98.01,3.96,"He will have reached the point where he knows quite a bit of the talk by heart."],[52,102.1,3.0,"But you won’t feel the same excitement you felt from him"],[52,105.1,1.46,"at the beginning of the week."],[52,106.6,2.25,"You might feel his stress instead."],[52,108.98,1.67,"You hear him saying things like,"],[52,110.73,3.75,"“Let’s see,” “Just a minute,” or “Let me start that again.”"],[52,114.9,2.75,"He might even sound a bit like a robot—"],[52,117.78,3.29,"as though he's not actually thinking about the things he's saying."],[52,121.28,5.46,"These are clues that the talk is being recited rather than spoken with meaning."],[52,126.91,4.09,"It's hard for an audience to connect to a speaker who presents in this way."],[52,131.21,3.5,"But, if your friend keeps working on memorizing his script,"],[52,134.8,4.67,"you will notice a thrilling change by the sixth or seventh day."],[52,139.63,2.96,"Suddenly, he really knows his talk."],[52,142.76,3.63,"He knows it so well that remembering the words is a snap."],[52,146.6,3.5,"He can concentrate on the meaning of what he’s saying again."],[52,150.6,2.54,"He has the passion he had on day one,"],[52,153.15,2.75,"and now he knows his script by heart as well."],[52,156.4,2.75,"Remember this if you decide to memorize your talk."],[52,159.45,1.79,"Choosing to memorize is great!"],[52,161.49,3.17,"You're giving yourself an excellent chance for a huge hit."],[52,164.83,2.92,"But it’s important that you keep working on memorizing"],[52,167.75,4.88,"until you’ve passed this stage where the talk sounds recited."],[52,172.84,4.46,"You have to know the words so well, they seem like they’re part of you."],[52,177.72,3.0,"You must keep practicing past the robot stage"],[52,180.72,3.84,"to the stage where the words flow out freely."],[52,184.89,4.0,"Another thing to ask yourself if you plan to write a script for your talk is:"],[52,188.89,2.79,"what kind of language do you want to use?"],[52,191.98,2.63,"Spoken language or written language?"],[52,195.02,3.21,"The language we use in everyday speech is different"],[52,198.24,2.13,"from the language writers use."],[52,200.74,2.63,"Harvard professor Dan Gilbert tells his students"],[52,203.37,3.84,"to speak their talks into a recorder before writing them down."],[52,207.33,3.59,"The students then use the words from the recording as the first draft"],[52,210.91,1.38,"of their written script."],[52,212.62,3.88,"This helps them make sure the language they choose sounds natural"],[52,216.5,1.5,"when said out loud."],[52,218.38,2.25,"But, again, every talk is different."],[52,220.8,5.0,"For some speakers, using elegant language like that you might find in a book"],[52,225.97,2.96,"is the perfect way of expressing their idea."],[52,229.35,3.25,"Take a look at this memorable moment from Amanda C. Gorman"],[52,232.6,3.0,"at a TED-Ed Student Talks event in 2018."],[52,236.02,3.8,"“Most of my life, I was particularly terrified of speaking up"],[52,239.82,1.63,"because I had a speech impediment"],[52,241.53,3.54,"which made it difficult to pronounce certain letters, sounds,"],[52,245.07,2.71,"and I felt like I was fine writing on the page."],[52,247.78,3.46,"But once I got on stage, I was worried my words might jumble and stumble."],[52,251.25,3.29,"What was the point in trying not to mumble these thoughts in my head"],[52,254.54,2.29,"if everything’s already been said before?"],[52,257.92,3.92,"But finally, I had a moment of realization where I thought,"],[52,261.84,2.79,"if I choose not to speak out of fear,"],[52,264.88,3.13,"then there’s no one that my silence is standing for.”"],[52,268.18,4.63,"This is a powerful piece of writing, and it’s meant to be heard that way."],[52,272.89,2.54,"Even though the language is not like everyday speech,"],[52,275.44,5.13,"Amanda’s powerful poetry makes us feel we are in the hands of a masterful writer."],[52,280.69,2.67,"It's the perfect language for this talk."],[52,284.07,1.33,"For some scripted talks,"],[52,285.41,3.92,"the speaker chooses to bring their script on stage with them to read from."],[52,289.37,2.84,"This can be a perfectly wonderful way to present."],[52,292.2,1.92,"But if you choose to go this route,"],[52,294.12,3.17,"it's important to know your script well enough that you can still"],[52,297.29,3.63,"give your audience the sense that you are with them in the moment."],[52,301.63,2.5,"Mean every sentence as you read it."],[52,304.13,2.46,"Look up from the page as often as you can,"],[52,306.59,2.84,"and make eye contact with your audience members."],[52,309.43,3.96,"It might even be a powerful choice to let go of your script"],[52,313.39,1.42,"toward the end of your talk,"],[52,314.81,3.13,"and speak the conclusion directly from the heart."],[52,318.44,3.5,"Some speakers choose to give unscripted talks."],[52,322.11,2.21,"There are many kinds of unscripted talks."],[52,324.4,4.17,"What they all have in common is that the speaker is not trying to recall"],[52,328.57,2.92,"a specific, pre-written script while on stage."],[52,331.62,2.88,"Instead, they are thinking about the subject matter"],[52,334.5,4.42,"and looking for the best words to communicate their points in the moment."],[52,339.25,5.67,"Some speakers doing unscripted talks use a set of notes to guide them."],[52,345.09,2.63,"Some choose not to have any notes at all."],[52,347.93,3.38,"There are many wonderful benefits to unscripted talks."],[52,351.31,5.42,"They often sound fresh and alive, like the speaker is really thinking out loud."],[52,356.85,2.84,"If this is a comfortable style of speaking for you,"],[52,359.69,2.75,"and your talk is about something you know very well,"],[52,362.44,2.84,"an unscripted talk could be your best choice."],[52,365.44,4.55,"But it's important to know the difference between unscripted and unprepared."],[52,370.53,3.96,"There is no excuse for not preparing for an important talk,"],[52,374.54,2.29,"whether you choose to write a script or not."],[52,377.25,3.21,"So how do you prepare for an unscripted talk?"],[52,380.75,4.88,"A lot will depend on what type of journey you plan to take the audience on."],[52,386.05,3.92,"A talk built around a single story will be a lot easier"],[52,389.97,3.42,"than one where you’re trying to explain complicated research."],[52,393.64,4.3,"But the key is to go back to the metaphor of a talk as a journey."],[52,398.44,3.21,"Ask yourself what each step of the journey looks like."],[52,402.02,3.0,"It can be helpful to create a label for each step—"],[52,405.44,3.79,"a mental note that will help you remember how to get from one piece"],[52,409.24,1.67,"of the talk to the next."],[52,411.32,4.88,"TED speakers have very different opinions on whether a memorized script"],[52,416.2,3.25,"or an unscripted talk is the better way to go."],[52,419.79,2.79,"But the majority of TED speakers do, in fact,"],[52,422.58,2.84,"script their whole talk and memorize it."],[52,425.46,3.17,"And they do their best to avoid letting it sound recited."],[52,429.01,3.25,"If you have time to do that, it will probably give you the best shot"],[52,432.26,3.92,"at delivering a powerful talk that says everything you want it to say"],[52,436.18,2.04,"without sounding robotic."],[52,438.52,3.21,"If you don't have time to truly learn a script by heart,"],[52,441.73,3.5,"or if you already know that's just not the best thing for you,"],[52,445.57,1.33,"then don't go this route."],[52,447.32,4.25,"The key is to pick a presentation plan that you feel confident about,"],[52,451.57,1.38,"and commit to that."],[52,453.7,3.17,"Whether you choose to memorize a script, or read from one,"],[52,456.99,3.34,"or use a few notes, or go without notes entirely,"],[52,460.41,1.63,"you will still end up with a talk"],[52,462.04,3.13,"that is well prepared and passionately delivered."],[52,465.17,2.25,"So long as you put in the time."],[53,7.5,3.25,"If you can conjure up an exciting idea in someone’s mind,"],[53,10.76,2.46,"you have done something wondrous."],[53,13.34,3.34,"A little piece of you has become part of them."],[53,17.05,3.75,"Connection, narration, explanation, persuasion,"],[53,20.81,2.88,"these are all great tools for sharing your ideas."],[53,23.77,4.09,"But the most direct way of gifting an idea to an audience"],[53,27.86,1.67,"is simply to show them."],[53,30.03,2.46,"Many talks are anchored like this."],[53,32.61,4.71,"You reveal your work to the audience in a way that delights and inspires them."],[53,37.78,5.75,"The basic name for this is revelation— the inspiring act of revealing something."],[53,43.91,1.21,"In a revelation talk,"],[53,45.12,3.75,"your goal might just be to create a sense of wonder and delight."],[53,49.25,2.17,"If your talk is a demonstration,"],[53,51.42,4.75,"you're probably hoping to amaze and create a new sense of possibility."],[53,56.76,2.25,"If you're sharing a vision of the future,"],[53,59.01,6.13,"you want it to be so vivid and compelling that your audience makes it their vision."],[53,65.89,3.67,"Let’s dig into three kinds of revelation talks."],[53,70.19,1.29,"1. The Wonder Walk."],[53,71.69,6.05,"A wonder walk is a talk based on revealing a series of images or wonder moments."],[53,78.12,4.63,"It can be like a studio tour with an artist guiding you through each artwork,"],[53,82.95,3.54,"or a hike with an explorer through a dramatic landscape."],[53,87.0,1.71,"Each step is simple,"],[53,88.71,4.5,"with a sense of wonder building as you move from one piece to the next."],[53,93.63,3.75,"This type of talk is often used by artists, designers, photographers,"],[53,97.39,1.04,"and architects."],[53,98.84,4.13,"David Gallo gave a brief talk on underwater astonishments."],[53,103.18,3.38,"He showed a series of incredible images and videos"],[53,106.56,2.25,"of bioluminescent sea creatures."],[53,108.81,4.96,"A five-minute talk, it’s been viewed over 15 million times on YouTube."],[53,114.4,3.5,"A wonder walk works best with a clear throughline—"],[53,118.07,3.13,"a connecting idea that links all pieces together."],[53,121.62,2.21,"Without that, it can get boring."],[53,124.41,2.84,"The goal is to give the audience an inside scoop."],[53,127.37,1.71,"Using simple language,"],[53,129.12,4.75,"share your dreams, your process, and mistakes you made along the way."],[53,134.5,3.88,"Illustrator David McAuley shared his drawings of Rome,"],[53,138.59,3.96,"showing not just the finished works, but his mistakes and dead ends,"],[53,142.72,3.25,"and how he got from there to the published illustrations."],[53,146.35,2.09,"Letting your listeners in on your process"],[53,148.44,3.21,"is one of the major gifts of any creative talk."],[53,152.02,2.08,"Don't just walk people through your work."],[53,154.4,6.05,"Take them on a journey that sparks curiosity and inspires new ideas."],[53,162.24,1.75,"2. The Dynamic Demo."],[53,164.53,2.63,"If what you’re revealing is not just visual—"],[53,167.16,2.63,"like new technology or a novel invention—"],[53,169.79,2.75,"the audience wants to see it in action."],[53,172.88,4.46,"Jeff Hahn demonstrated multi-touch technology in 2006,"],[53,177.34,1.88,"two years before the iPhone."],[53,179.76,3.17,"Pranav Mistry demoed Six Sense technology,"],[53,182.93,3.46,"showing the possibilities when combining a phone, a projector,"],[53,186.39,2.04,"and a gesture-detecting camera."],[53,189.44,1.21,"For a dynamic demo,"],[53,190.65,3.75,"what matters most is having something impressive to demonstrate."],[53,194.44,1.33,"If it's truly compelling,"],[53,195.78,4.04,"you can excite your audience with a small tease about what they’re about to see."],[53,200.4,2.96,"Michael Pritchard started his talk by asking his audience"],[53,203.37,2.67,"to imagine life without safe drinking water."],[53,206.12,4.3,"He poured muddy pond water, sewage runoff, and rabbit droppings"],[53,210.54,1.08,"into a container,"],[53,211.87,4.38,"then ran it through his Lifesaver bottle and offered it to an audience member."],[53,216.34,1.42,"It tasted fine."],[53,218.13,2.63,"He demonstrated that the technology worked,"],[53,221.01,3.09,"then explained its potential to save lives globally."],[53,224.51,1.88,"A typical demo journey is:"],[53,226.39,3.59,"an initial tease, an explanation or context,"],[53,230.18,2.92,"the demo itself (visual and dramatic),"],[53,233.1,3.63,"then the significance— the impact it could have."],[53,237.11,3.63,"Exceptional demos can become visions of the future."],[53,242.2,1.75,"3. The Dreamscape."],[53,244.41,5.13,"Humans have the unique ability to imagine, invent, innovate, design,"],[53,249.54,3.29,"and envision worlds that don’t yet exist."],[53,253.04,1.63,"We can share these visions,"],[53,254.67,5.55,"inspiring others to believe and act, and sometimes, turn them into reality."],[53,260.71,2.96,"Screenwriters persuade studios to make movies."],[53,263.68,3.09,"Inventors persuade companies to build inventions."],[53,266.89,3.25,"Architects persuade clients to finance buildings."],[53,270.47,4.13,"Businesspeople energize teams with belief in a new vision."],[53,275.19,5.09,"Dreams can be communicated with images, demos, sketches, or words."],[53,280.61,4.55,"Powerful speeches communicate a dream with inspiring language and passion."],[53,285.66,5.05,"Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.’s speech at the Lincoln Memorial in 1963"],[53,290.83,3.46,"lasted 17 minutes and 40 seconds,"],[53,294.62,2.04,"yet it changed history."],[53,297.17,4.67,"Marine biologist Sylvia Earle used images and compelling language"],[53,301.84,2.13,"to describe the ocean crisis."],[53,304.47,3.09,"She invited the audience to imagine hope spots—"],[53,307.76,2.5,"protected areas for marine life."],[53,310.68,4.05,"One audience member wrote $1 million check on the spot,"],[53,314.73,4.38,"and protected ocean space has more than tripled since."],[53,319.65,4.21,"Sharing a dream effectively requires painting a bold picture of the future"],[53,323.94,2.88,"and making your audience desire that future."],[53,327.37,2.58,"Visual aids can often help with the first part."],[53,330.74,3.38,"Architect Thomas Heatherwick included a slide in his TED talk"],[53,334.12,2.84,"showing an elegant high-rise apartment complex"],[53,337.17,2.88,"with curved buildings and a park at ground level,"],[53,340.17,3.84,"painting a future many listeners would be thrilled to inhabit."],[53,344.71,2.09,"That's the power of our dreams."],[53,346.93,1.88,"They can spread to others."],[53,348.84,2.38,"They can build excitement and belief."],[53,351.39,3.04,"And through that process, they can come true."],[53,355.6,2.34,"Now, Mix and Match."],[53,358.02,2.71,"Most talks don’t fit neatly into one category."],[53,361.11,6.59,"Select, combine, and adjust talk types to best serve the idea you wish to convey."],[54,8.96,2.42,"When you open your power bills these days,"],[54,11.38,2.67,"do you notice they're a lot higher than they used to be?"],[54,14.05,4.38,"Behind that climbing bill of yours is a straining energy grid where you live,"],[54,18.47,3.54,"and very likely, one major source of that strain"],[54,22.06,1.79,"are AI data centers,"],[54,23.89,3.67,"helping tech companies profit while you pay more."],[54,28.15,3.75,"If you think about that for a second, it's kind of a crazy deal."],[54,32.32,1.79,"And if it makes you angry,"],[54,34.16,5.34,"or if the whole deal around AI makes you uncertain or worried,"],[54,39.54,1.58,"you certainly are not alone."],[54,41.62,2.96,"I'm Ari Matusiak, and I'm part of a talented team"],[54,44.62,3.46,"that spends its time working to rewire America's energy system."],[54,48.67,1.58,"And we believe we have a fix."],[54,50.92,2.67,"One that cuts a new and better deal for you,"],[54,53.63,1.92,"your family and your community."],[54,56.01,2.79,"A deal that's built on our shared power."],[54,59.51,3.92,"Nine out of 10 workers in America say that their incomes have barely budged,"],[54,63.48,2.21,"and they can't keep up with the cost of living."],[54,65.73,4.5,"Energy costs are up 40 percent over the last five years."],[54,70.23,2.71,"One in three households in America"],[54,72.99,3.04,"say that they are struggling to pay that power bill every month."],[54,77.03,1.92,"In parts of West Virginia,"],[54,78.99,2.17,"that power bill rivals the mortgage."],[54,81.75,1.63,"Think about that."],[54,83.37,3.59,"What it costs some American families to heat and cool their homes"],[54,87.0,3.71,"is the same as what it costs them to own their home."],[54,91.09,2.84,"The American energy system has traditionally had two sides."],[54,93.97,1.92,"On the one side, supply."],[54,95.88,1.83,"Giant steel-in-the-ground power plants"],[54,97.76,3.29,"built by people in hard hats, celebrated by people in suits,"],[54,101.1,1.38,"at ribbon cutting-ceremonies"],[54,102.47,1.79,"that you're probably not invited to."],[54,104.77,1.46,"On the other side, demand."],[54,106.23,3.63,"All of us, paying for the energy that we need"],[54,109.9,4.3,"and contributing our share to the infrastructure costs over there"],[54,114.23,1.25,"to produce it."],[54,115.99,3.42,"For 30 years, that was basically the deal."],[54,120.12,1.92,"The price of energy went up some."],[54,122.08,3.38,"The amount of energy we all use stayed basically the same."],[54,125.96,2.13,"And really, none of us ever thought about it."],[54,128.08,2.88,"We just sat at our kitchen tables and paid that bill."],[54,131.38,3.21,"But then, five years ago, it broke."],[54,135.26,2.09,"Energy demand surged."],[54,137.68,1.58,"And one main reason?"],[54,139.97,1.17,"AI."],[54,141.64,4.84,"Right now, there are over 3,400 AI data centers"],[54,146.48,2.84,"underway or planned in the United States,"],[54,149.35,3.46,"needing 270 gigawatts of power."],[54,153.86,1.17,"What's a gigawatt?"],[54,155.07,3.0,"Well it’s a unit of energy -- but like a lot of energy."],[54,158.07,1.96,"To put it in perspective,"],[54,160.03,5.13,"those 270 gigawatts are enough to power two out of three homes in America"],[54,165.16,2.46,"all day, every day,"],[54,167.66,1.67,"as much Netflix as you want."],[54,169.79,3.0,"And the projected demand for AI power just keeps going up."],[54,172.84,2.92,"It's almost tripled in the last year alone."],[54,175.8,4.17,"And so now we have this situation where we have power for all of us"],[54,180.01,2.17,"and power for all of that"],[54,182.18,3.04,"on a system that wasn't designed to support it."],[54,185.22,1.29,"It's a mess."],[54,187.06,3.17,"And if we keep going the same way,"],[54,190.27,2.17,"this mess will mean that you fund"],[54,192.48,3.84,"one of the largest buildouts of infrastructure in American history --"],[54,196.32,3.13,"comparable to the interstate highway system --"],[54,199.45,2.0,"on your bill every month."],[54,202.37,3.38,"Except it will be going to all of that,"],[54,205.78,1.33,"not to you."],[54,207.58,2.46,"Meanwhile, your furnace is getting older,"],[54,210.04,1.88,"your water heater is about to give out,"],[54,211.96,3.25,"you are one hard winter or hot summer away"],[54,215.21,2.54,"from a repair bill you didn't budget for."],[54,218.88,3.42,"Not surprisingly, people do not like this deal."],[54,223.05,4.04,"And all across the country, communities are fighting back."],[54,227.1,1.83,"Bans, protests,"],[54,228.97,3.46,"tens of billions of dollars of AI data-center projects"],[54,232.48,1.58,"canceled or delayed."],[54,234.48,3.92,"And still, these companies are pushing for more power --"],[54,239.07,1.67,"because they need it."],[54,241.24,3.96,"And that is our big shared opportunity."],[54,246.24,2.54,"The thing to remember about these AI tech companies"],[54,248.79,3.54,"is that they are in a global race, that they intend to win,"],[54,252.37,2.79,"and they will pay almost whatever it costs"],[54,255.21,3.0,"for the power that they need to win that race."],[54,258.25,2.42,"They don't really care where the power comes from,"],[54,260.67,1.75,"they just need it fast and at scale."],[54,262.43,2.96,"And so they're already investing in those power plant projects,"],[54,265.43,2.04,"but they take five to seven years to build."],[54,267.51,1.17,"So to get power faster,"],[54,268.72,5.3,"some of these companies are literally buying old 747 jet engines"],[54,274.06,4.55,"and converting them into turbines to generate electricity 24/7."],[54,280.11,1.17,"Jet engines."],[54,281.28,1.29,"Gets me every time."],[54,282.61,3.5,"Imagine a whole batch of those things blasting away in your community."],[54,286.12,2.46,"There's got to be a better way than that."],[54,289.37,3.88,"Well imagine that these tech companies could get some of the power that they need"],[54,293.29,1.38,"from simple home upgrades:"],[54,295.29,2.5,"solar on your rooftop, heat pumps,"],[54,297.84,2.96,"swapping out air conditioners, furnaces, water heaters,"],[54,300.84,2.63,"batteries in your garage and in your appliances"],[54,303.47,3.84,"and software that connects you and your neighbor's homes together"],[54,307.35,2.54,"to provide power back to the grid."],[54,311.01,3.21,"Nationally, households could provide 100 gigawatts"],[54,314.23,3.04,"of the 270 gigawatts that are needed"],[54,317.27,1.54,"for these data centers."],[54,318.81,1.46,"More than a third."],[54,321.02,1.17,"How?"],[54,322.24,3.96,"Because through those upgrades we are making and storing our own power"],[54,326.24,2.42,"and using less of it overall,"],[54,328.7,3.04,"creating space on the grid for more demand"],[54,331.74,2.21,"while reducing your energy bill,"],[54,333.95,1.63,"not raising it."],[54,335.58,3.21,"And who has the money for those homegrown energy upgrades?"],[54,339.33,2.96,"The tech companies that need their power right now."],[54,343.13,1.79,"In response to the backlash,"],[54,344.97,3.71,"tech companies and elected officials across the political spectrum"],[54,348.68,2.59,"have said that these AI data center projects"],[54,351.31,1.75,"will bring their own power."],[54,353.1,2.67,"They'll pay for what they need, not you."],[54,356.31,1.17,"How does that work?"],[54,357.48,2.09,"Well take it to your hometown."],[54,359.56,1.75,"Imagine a tech company coming in"],[54,361.32,2.75,"and wanting to build a 200-megawatt data center."],[54,364.49,1.42,"For a project of that size,"],[54,365.9,1.71,"they might spend a half a billion,"],[54,367.61,3.13,"a billion dollars or more for the power that they need."],[54,371.62,1.96,"But to get the project approved,"],[54,373.58,3.5,"they will likely also negotiate a community benefit agreement --"],[54,377.12,3.59,"another $10 million or $50 million on top."],[54,380.71,2.0,"Investing in programs in the community"],[54,382.71,2.17,"that you and your family can benefit from."],[54,386.17,1.46,"Sounds pretty good."],[54,387.68,1.71,"Does it solve the problem?"],[54,389.43,3.34,"No, because this is about new power,"],[54,392.81,2.92,"not fixing the mess that you're already paying for."],[54,395.77,3.59,"So your bill stays exactly the same."],[54,400.4,2.92,"And that's why we propose cutting a new deal."],[54,403.98,1.25,"Imagine this."],[54,405.24,4.8,"You get a notice from your utility: 70 percent off of new appliances."],[54,410.03,1.75,"Remember that old water heater?"],[54,411.82,3.59,"Your contractor comes and replaces it with a new, efficient heat pump"],[54,415.45,2.96,"for a couple hundred bucks instead of a couple thousand."],[54,419.42,1.38,"How does that work?"],[54,420.83,3.09,"Well the tech company takes the half a billion dollars,"],[54,423.96,3.17,"the billion dollars or more that they otherwise would have invested"],[54,427.17,3.13,"and puts it into you and your neighbor's homes instead."],[54,430.34,2.63,"Rooftop solar, batteries, heat pumps --"],[54,433.01,3.54,"across 50,000 households in your town --"],[54,436.6,4.71,"we estimate would generate that same 200 megawatts of power."],[54,442.23,2.59,"And this could be the new deal."],[54,445.27,1.33,"And guess what?"],[54,446.65,3.29,"It turns out it gives the tech companies what they need and want too:"],[54,449.95,1.29,"speed to power."],[54,451.24,1.83,"Because those upgrades,"],[54,453.12,1.67,"they can start next week,"],[54,454.78,2.0,"and they'll be done in a year or two years,"],[54,456.83,1.63,"not five to seven."],[54,458.45,3.0,"The company gets the same credit that they would from a utility"],[54,461.5,1.92,"or regulator for making that investment,"],[54,463.46,2.75,"as they would if they put it into a new power plant."],[54,467.3,4.09,"And because the whole investment is a community benefit,"],[54,471.43,3.54,"it's more likely to get accepted and built in the first place."],[54,475.85,1.83,"It's a better deal for them."],[54,478.39,1.79,"For you and your neighbors?"],[54,480.18,3.5,"You get free or heavily discounted equipment that you own."],[54,483.73,3.29,"We found that those upgrades can save you up to $2,000 per year"],[54,487.07,1.38,"on your energy bills."],[54,488.44,2.54,"Think of it like a community fund."],[54,491.53,2.46,"The tech company puts the money in,"],[54,494.03,2.42,"you and your neighbors take the discount out"],[54,496.49,1.5,"based on your need."],[54,498.91,1.92,"Not first-come, first-served,"],[54,500.87,2.46,"not who negotiates the hardest,"],[54,503.33,2.38,"[it’s for] everybody, according to their needs."],[54,506.17,1.83,"Families that need the most support?"],[54,508.05,1.79,"They get the biggest discount."],[54,509.84,4.09,"Nobody’s paying full price, and nobody’s doing this alone."],[54,513.93,1.92,"We're all in this together,"],[54,515.85,1.25,"participating."],[54,517.97,3.09,"And because we're all in this together, participating,"],[54,521.1,3.54,"and that fund is sized to a number of households,"],[54,524.64,3.92,"manufacturers and contractors are willing to give discounts, too,"],[54,528.61,3.5,"because they're getting a lot of new business out of the deal."],[54,532.78,2.08,"It's like Costco, but for energy."],[54,535.66,1.58,"Homegrown energy."],[54,537.28,1.71,"Is that even going to work?"],[54,539.03,1.88,"I mean, how many people get appliances?"],[54,540.91,2.42,"Well, it turns out, a lot of people get appliances."],[54,543.33,1.46,"Nearly eight million Americans"],[54,544.79,2.75,"swap out their heating and cooling units every year."],[54,547.59,4.25,"We buy millions of backup generators and batteries every year."],[54,551.88,2.75,"Half a million households get rooftop solar,"],[54,554.67,2.42,"all on our own, with our own money."],[54,558.43,3.38,"OK, but maybe you have other questions like,"],[54,561.85,3.88,"won't heat pumps add demand to the grid, for example?"],[54,566.23,1.46,"Sometimes, yes."],[54,567.69,2.79,"But it turns out that in most of the country,"],[54,570.52,3.0,"the grid is the most strained in the summer,"],[54,573.57,2.46,"so replacing an inefficient air conditioner"],[54,576.07,1.67,"with an efficient heat pump"],[54,577.74,2.0,"lowers the demand on the grid."],[54,580.12,1.67,"Who pays for the permits?"],[54,581.83,1.17,"Not you."],[54,583.04,1.17,"That's the tech company."],[54,584.21,2.13,"Same as they would for a power plant."],[54,586.92,2.13,"Who fixes the equipment if it breaks?"],[54,589.09,2.34,"Well you do, because it’s your equipment,"],[54,591.46,1.17,"you own it,"],[54,592.67,2.84,"and it comes with a warranty and a number to call."],[54,596.55,1.58,"Can this even work?"],[54,598.18,1.17,"Yes."],[54,599.35,3.38,"There are already hundreds of networks around the country of households"],[54,602.72,3.42,"that are organized into what are called virtual power plants,"],[54,606.14,3.92,"putting 30 gigawatts of power or more back to the grid."],[54,611.73,1.79,"OK, so it will work."],[54,614.15,3.75,"But the reality is, this is a deal we have to go get done."],[54,618.61,2.5,"Homegrown energy doesn't exist today,"],[54,621.12,2.0,"but I'm confident that it's coming."],[54,623.95,1.54,"Governor Sherrill in New Jersey,"],[54,625.54,2.75,"she just signed one of the most robust laws in the country"],[54,628.29,3.67,"that makes it possible for tech companies to get their power from households."],[54,631.96,2.21,"New York and Virginia are right behind her --"],[54,634.17,2.21,"your state, your community could be next."],[54,636.84,5.96,"And so the scale of it all creates an opportunity to rewire the system"],[54,642.8,2.71,"and make the deal work for all of us."],[54,646.27,2.25,"And we should take that opportunity."],[54,649.44,5.59,"So if a data center is underway or planned in your community,"],[54,655.07,3.25,"you and your neighbors can flex your power"],[54,658.32,4.42,"and say that investing in you is the threshold for entry,"],[54,662.78,1.33,"the baseline."],[54,664.74,1.29,"How do you do that?"],[54,666.87,1.46,"Three simple ways."],[54,668.37,1.17,"Speak up."],[54,669.54,3.59,"If you see your neighbors on Nextdoor or on TikTok"],[54,673.17,3.13,"or whatever social, talking about that data center project,"],[54,676.34,1.17,"reply to that post."],[54,677.51,3.63,"Tell them about homegrown energy and make it the conversation."],[54,681.72,1.38,"Rally your community."],[54,683.43,2.96,"Don't wait for the company to come to you with a deal."],[54,686.43,3.17,"Go to them together and insist that this is the deal."],[54,690.14,3.29,"Maybe you don't want to be the one that goes to the town hall meeting."],[54,693.44,1.25,"I get that."],[54,694.69,3.21,"But let’s be honest -- we all know the person who does want to go."],[54,697.94,1.17,"So send them."],[54,700.32,2.46,"And then, keep the momentum going."],[54,702.78,2.46,"When you win the deal, use it."],[54,705.28,1.54,"Have your neighbors use it,"],[54,706.87,2.5,"and then share what you and your neighbors accomplish"],[54,709.37,1.21,"with your network"],[54,710.58,3.34,"so that communities across the country can be inspired by your example"],[54,713.92,1.29,"and follow suit."],[54,716.84,2.09,"Why would you do any of this?"],[54,718.92,3.63,"Maybe because it'll save you a bunch of money on your bills."],[54,723.64,2.34,"Because it’s better for the community"],[54,725.97,2.17,"and it is certainly better for our planet."],[54,729.18,4.13,"And for once in this AI story,"],[54,733.35,3.42,"instead of feeling like the future is happening to us,"],[54,736.77,3.46,"we get to have a say in how the next chapter goes."],[54,741.15,1.75,"That's shared power,"],[54,742.95,2.08,"our power, in plain sight."],[54,745.53,4.25,"And each of us, our communities and America,"],[54,749.83,1.63,"deserve nothing less."],[54,751.46,1.17,"Thank you."],[55,6.63,4.09,"To coincide with the 1964 Tokyo Olympics,"],[55,10.88,5.59,"Japan launched the world’s first high-speed train, the Shinkansen."],[55,16.76,4.04,"Capable of running at 210 kph,"],[55,21.1,4.84,"this groundbreaking bullet train inspired countries around the world"],[55,25.94,2.38,"to invest in high-speed rail."],[55,28.98,6.63,"Today, these trains regularly travel at over 300 kph,"],[55,35.78,3.71,"on par with the takeoff speed of a commercial airplane."],[55,39.83,3.0,"But could these trains go even faster,"],[55,43.04,4.84,"whisking passengers to their destinations quicker than the smoothest flight?"],[55,48.46,2.38,"To answer this, it helps to understand"],[55,50.84,4.17,"how these trains reach their already impressive speeds."],[55,55.26,4.21,"There are three main factors that separate bullet trains"],[55,59.47,2.59,"from traditional, low-speed trains:"],[55,62.39,3.96,"engines, aerodynamics, and infrastructure."],[55,68.86,4.55,"Most traditional trains are powered by combustion engines,"],[55,74.11,5.0,"which combust fuel— typically diesel— to turn an electric generator."],[55,79.58,4.96,"This electricity is then used to power motors that turn the train’s wheels."],[55,85.0,2.46,"High-speed trains, on the other hand,"],[55,87.5,3.09,"use electric engines to power their motors."],[55,90.88,3.88,"This process is more energy efficient than combustion"],[55,94.76,4.96,"and allows train operators to reach top speeds more quickly."],[55,100.56,2.13,"Next up is aerodynamics."],[55,102.97,4.55,"While low- and high-speed trains use the same steel wheels"],[55,107.6,2.42,"to minimize friction with the track,"],[55,110.02,4.34,"bullet trains are also designed with aerodynamic bodies"],[55,114.53,3.38,"that slice through air with minimal resistance."],[55,118.11,4.71,"Engineers could use these slicker designs on traditional train cars."],[55,123.16,2.46,"But while upgrading to electric engines"],[55,125.75,2.71,"would make low-speed trains more efficient,"],[55,128.5,4.0,"they wouldn't get much benefit from these aerodynamic designs"],[55,132.71,3.25,"because of the third factor: infrastructure."],[55,136.51,4.21,"Traditional train tracks were built to meet people where they lived,"],[55,140.93,4.3,"ensuring stations were accessible to any potential passengers."],[55,145.52,4.13,"But to prevent derailments in these highly populated areas,"],[55,149.9,5.63,"trains need to frequently decelerate to safely hit turns and cross roads."],[55,155.86,4.63,"High-speed rails, on the other hand, were built to prioritize speed."],[55,160.74,4.34,"They run on long, straight lines that avoid road crossings,"],[55,165.08,3.0,"sharp curves, and high traffic areas."],[55,169.12,3.71,"These three differences allow high-speed trains"],[55,172.84,4.46,"to reach top speeds over double their traditional counterparts."],[55,177.63,5.55,"And since their infrastructure allows them to travel longer distances between stops,"],[55,183.3,3.34,"they spend more time cruising near top speed,"],[55,187.02,5.59,"often traveling up to three times the average speed of traditional trains."],[55,193.31,4.84,"But new technology is emerging to make even faster trains."],[55,199.32,3.21,"To eliminate friction between rails and wheels,"],[55,202.66,5.46,"engineers have developed new magnetic levitation, or maglev, trains."],[55,208.5,1.92,"There are two major approaches here."],[55,210.79,2.21,"In electromagnetic suspension,"],[55,213.17,5.05,"magnetized arms wrap under the track and pull the train up."],[55,218.71,3.04,"Meanwhile, in electrodynamic suspension,"],[55,221.88,5.76,"coils embedded in the track create a pattern of alternating magnetic fields"],[55,227.76,2.54,"that interact with magnets on the train"],[55,230.48,3.88,"to float the vehicle a few centimeters in the air."],[55,234.77,1.29,"To change speed,"],[55,236.06,4.38,"train operators alternate the direction of the trains’ magnetic field,"],[55,240.9,3.96,"attracting the front of the train to the next coil to speed up"],[55,245.07,3.17,"or reversing the field’s phase to brake."],[55,248.79,1.46,"Given enough runway,"],[55,250.33,3.71,"these trains can reach speeds from 430"],[55,254.04,3.63,"to a theoretical 600 kph,"],[55,258.13,4.38,"allowing them to make the over 4,400 kilometer trip"],[55,262.72,4.63,"from New York City to Los Angeles in as little as seven hours."],[55,267.89,6.38,"So why haven’t we built maglev trains, or even high-speed trains, everywhere?"],[55,275.4,3.46,"First, these trains require lots of energy,"],[55,279.02,3.42,"most of which is needed to overcome air resistance."],[55,282.74,3.63,"In fact, at 300 kph,"],[55,286.37,5.3,"this task accounts for over 80% of a train’s energy consumption."],[55,292.2,3.92,"Engineers are researching ways to reduce air resistance,"],[55,296.12,3.88,"such as putting maglevs in near-vacuum environments."],[55,300.42,4.71,"But building a vacuum-sealed tunnel across an entire country"],[55,305.26,4.71,"would be an infrastructure project of science fictional proportions."],[55,310.64,3.67,"Besides, it’s hard enough to build regular high-speed rails."],[55,314.6,5.0,"To go fast, trains need long, straight tracks with wide curves,"],[55,319.86,4.17,"which are very difficult to build in mountains, cities,"],[55,324.03,2.13,"or densely populated areas."],[55,326.61,3.88,"Building such routes is costly, politically sensitive,"],[55,330.62,2.63,"and often physically impossible."],[55,333.95,1.5,"Despite these hurdles,"],[55,335.46,5.76,"Japan and China are expanding their existing high-speed and maglev rails,"],[55,341.34,3.25,"and several projects currently in development"],[55,344.67,3.46,"might finally bring high-speed rail to the US."],[55,348.93,3.92,"But we’ll need to carefully consider our trains’ top speeds"],[55,352.93,3.71,"alongside cost, energy use, and safety"],[55,356.77,3.59,"to keep the future of train travel on the right track."],[56,0.48,4.44,"Exhausted from his swim, the bear must regain his strength."],[56,11.84,3.92,"The next day, a sea fog shrouds the island."],[56,16.12,2.56,"The walruses sense that they're in danger."],[56,21.88,1.88,"Using the fog as cover,"],[56,23.76,2.6,"the bear approaches the herd."],[56,31.08,2.76,"The adults close ranks around their young,"],[56,33.84,3.28,"presenting a wall of blubber and hide."],[56,44.56,2.04,"He tests the barrier."],[56,47.28,1.88,"But it stands firm."],[56,50.4,3.68,"It appears that the world's largest land carnivore"],[56,54.12,1.6,"has met his match."],[56,63.12,2.72,"There must be a chink in the armour somewhere."],[56,69.84,1.12,"Not here."],[56,79.72,3.0,"This female walrus is shielding her pup."],[56,82.8,2.72,"If he can just prise her off..."],[56,92.16,1.88,"The bear's claws and teeth"],[56,94.04,2.56,"can't penetrate her thick hide."],[56,105.08,4.12,"With the herd retreating to water, the bear must move quickly."],[56,114.44,3.76,"Having failed with one, he heads straight for another."],[56,135.24,3.96,"The chance of his first meal in months is slipping away."],[56,146.36,2.76,"He seems increasingly desperate."],[56,154.16,2.12,"It's now or never."],[56,157.32,3.76,"He must avoid the stabbing tusks if he's to win."],[56,167.88,3.0,"The flailing walrus is immensely powerful"],[56,170.96,2.6,"and drags the bear away from the shallows"],[56,173.56,2.36,"towards the safety of the herd."],[56,191.0,2.12,"It slips from his grasp."],[56,197.2,1.48,"At this time of year,"],[56,198.68,4.68,"polar bears on average succeed only once in 20 hunts."],[56,209.04,2.84,"If the hunter is skinny, like this one,"],[56,211.88,2.28,"that may not be often enough."],[56,222.16,3.52,"All she can do is keep trying."],[56,263.52,2.32,"To prevent her scent betraying her,"],[56,265.84,4.68,"she makes a wide sweep to get downwind of the seal."],[56,281.88,1.68,"Getting close."],[56,305.68,2.76,"She's now right behind the seal."],[56,351.84,3.52,"Incredibly, she caught the seal underwater."],[56,361.32,2.4,"It's only small but even so,"],[56,363.72,4.08,"its blubber alone will contain 100,000 calories,"],[56,367.8,2.72,"enough to sustain this bear for a week."],[56,381.96,1.56,"And in that time,"],[56,383.52,1.92,"she might even catch another."],[56,394.24,2.08,"But this can't go on forever."],[56,396.76,2.8,"As summer continues, temperatures are rising."],[56,401.08,3.56,"Each hunt requires more energy,"],[56,404.64,2.56,"draining the bears of their reserves."],[56,420.52,2.72,"A polar bear stirs."],[56,423.24,3.0,"She has been in her den the whole winter."],[56,426.64,3.76,"Her emergence marks the beginning of spring."],[56,433.24,2.56,"After months of confinement underground,"],[56,435.8,2.24,"she toboggans down the slope."],[56,438.04,2.16,"Perhaps to clean her fur,"],[56,440.2,3.0,"perhaps for sheer joy."],[56,457.48,5.68,"Her cubs gaze out at their bright new world for the very first time."],[56,465.36,1.84,"The female calls them."],[56,467.2,3.36,"But this steep slope is not the easiest place"],[56,470.56,2.16,"to take your first steps."],[56,485.68,3.52,"But they are hungry and eager to reach their mother,"],[56,489.2,2.8,"who's delayed feeding them on this special day."],[56,492.52,2.64,"Now she lures them with the promise of milk,"],[56,495.16,3.12,"the only food the cubs have known since they were born,"],[56,498.28,2.16,"deaf and blind, beneath the snow,"],[56,500.44,2.16,"some two months ago."],[56,519.0,3.28,"Their mother has not eaten for five months"],[56,522.28,2.96,"and has lost half her body weight."],[56,525.24,5.44,"Now she converts the last of her fat reserves into milk for her cubs."],[56,549.2,2.6,"The spring sun brings warmth,"],[56,551.8,2.68,"but also a problem for the mother."],[56,555.04,3.12,"It starts to melt the sea ice."],[56,558.16,4.08,"That is where she hunts for the seals she needs to feed her cubs,"],[56,562.24,3.0,"and she must get there before the ice breaks up."],[56,569.48,3.28,"For now, though, it's still minus-30 degrees,"],[56,572.76,3.16,"and the cubs must have the shelter of the den."],[56,592.88,4.44,"Meadows that only a few weeks ago were buried beneath the snow"],[56,597.32,2.28,"are now full of life."],[56,617.76,1.6,"But in these mountains,"],[56,619.36,2.84,"the good times will not last long."],[56,623.56,3.32,"So the bears must feed as fast as they can."],[56,630.4,1.4,"During the summer months,"],[56,631.8,4.96,"an adult can put on 180 kilos gorging on plants,"],[56,636.76,2.12,"and, if they can catch them…"],[56,640.48,2.04,"..a marmot or two."],[56,645.84,4.36,"But, just now, the bears have something else on their minds."],[56,653.8,1.56,"It's becoming warmer"],[56,655.36,2.84,"and the bears are keen to shed their thick winter coats."],[56,661.96,3.52,"Mothers show the cubs what to do about this."],[56,675.44,2.4,"They'll soon catch on."],[56,680.04,3.76,"Some trees, it seems, are particularly suitable for rubbing."],[56,685.04,1.96,"Bears have their favourites"],[56,687.0,3.48,"and will travel long distances to visit them."],[56,692.8,3.12,"Some itches just have to be scratched."],[56,716.92,4.64,"There are now around 30 bears in this one valley."],[56,727.2,5.44,"As they rub, each leaves an individual and recognisable scent."],[56,736.12,4.4,"So the tree soon carries a list of who's around,"],[56,740.52,2.44,"which might help individuals to avoid a fight."],[56,745.0,2.68,"To best spread their scent,"],[56,747.68,2.04,"they really have to put their back into it."],[56,791.72,2.12,"But summer is short."],[56,794.2,3.56,"Itches satisfactorily scratched,"],[56,797.76,2.44,"it's time to eat."],[56,802.56,1.24,"In a couple of months,"],[56,803.8,2.52,"they will have to return to their dens to hibernate."],[56,807.24,3.16,"So now they must put on as much weight as they can."],[56,810.44,4.2,"Mr Nakayama has been keeping honeybees for more than 65 years,"],[56,815.96,3.88,"and these sunny forest clearings are perfect for his hives."],[56,827.76,4.28,"But every year, Mr Nakayama's hives come under attack."],[56,835.92,5.04,"Whatever it is seems to be unfazed by a swarm of angry bees."],[56,846.2,3.96,"Mr Nakayama has put an electric fence around his precious hives."],[56,850.68,3.08,"But even this doesn't seem to be keeping the culprit out."],[56,858.88,1.76,"So he's called in some experts,"],[56,860.64,3.92,"and they've set up an elaborate trap laden with honey."],[56,881.04,2.4,"All they can do now is wait."],[56,890.32,2.12,"And it doesn't take long."],[56,892.44,1.76,"Within just a few hours,"],[56,894.2,3.28,"news comes in that the trap's been tripped."],[56,906.92,2.84,"Professor Izumiyama and his team are here to help."],[56,912.6,3.0,"Although it's wise to tranquillise it first."],[56,923.0,2.08,"It's a young black bear."],[56,927.04,3.44,"He strayed dangerously close to the edge of human habitation,"],[56,930.48,1.76,"looking for something to eat."],[56,934.96,3.08,"Black bears have attacked people in the past."],[56,938.72,3.0,"So for everyone's sake, he can't stay here."],[56,949.92,3.96,"There's just enough room for the bear in the boot."],[56,956.72,3.0,"He needs to be returned to the safety of the mountain."],[56,960.76,2.52,"But it's a one-hour drive."],[56,965.36,3.0,"The bear could awake at any time…"],[56,969.2,2.28,"..and there's no cage."],[56,972.8,1.56,"In this rural area,"],[56,974.36,3.92,"bear expert Professor Izumiyama is used to taking this chance."],[56,988.24,4.28,"They need to take the bear as deep into the mountain wilderness as possible."],[56,1023.0,3.8,"This is the most anxious time for the professor."],[56,1040.12,2.16,"Thanks to Professor Izumiyama,"],[56,1042.28,4.72,"this lucky bear is back in the safety of the forest and out of harm's way."],[56,1050.52,3.36,"One of the most successful snow animals is solitary"],[56,1054.2,2.68,"and lives most of its life alone."],[56,1057.44,3.68,"They can be found in the very far north of the Arctic."],[56,1062.16,1.36,"The polar bear."],[56,1066.36,2.6,"This male, like every polar bear,"],[56,1068.96,2.8,"is almost completely covered in fur."],[56,1073.0,3.16,"But his secret to staying warm in this frozen land"],[56,1076.32,2.88,"is actually inside his large body."],[56,1080.72,5.76,"He has a huge layer of insulating fat, about 10 centimetres thick."],[56,1087.08,3.48,"It traps heat, keeping him warm from the inside."],[56,1092.0,3.96,"To maintain this fat, he relies on his good hunting skills."],[56,1098.84,3.2,"His prey is hidden under the thick ice."],[56,1104.24,3.84,"Luckily, he has a secret weapon to help him hunt."],[56,1108.88,3.12,"An extraordinary sense of smell."],[56,1113.16,2.76,"A hundred times stronger than ours."],[56,1124.44,4.84,"He can detect a seal carcass from 30 kilometres away"],[56,1130.12,2.84,"and under a metre of snow."],[56,1160.48,1.48,"Success."],[56,1162.8,3.0,"He will eat all the fat of a seal first,"],[56,1166.0,2.64,"often leaving the actual meat."],[56,1170.88,3.8,"This extreme fatty diet would kill us,"],[56,1174.76,2.44,"but for the polar bear, it's essential."],[56,1178.76,4.48,"Indeed, they are one of the fattest mammals on Earth."],[56,1185.2,4.24,"Their incredible insulation and intense sense of smell"],[56,1189.44,3.64,"make the polar bear a master in the snow."],[56,1195.8,2.04,"They're also built to move around"],[56,1197.84,3.0,"effortlessly in this snowy environment."],[56,1201.48,1.24,"Unlike me."],[56,1204.44,3.0,"I am a perfect example"],[56,1207.44,3.16,"of an animal that is not adapted"],[56,1210.68,3.88,"for walking about a snowy environment like this."],[56,1216.28,3.0,"I am 12 stone, 12-and-a-half stone,"],[56,1219.28,3.96,"and all of my weight is distributed on to my feet."],[56,1223.24,2.68,"Size 12 – big feet for a human,"],[56,1225.92,1.64,"but not big enough to stop me"],[56,1227.56,3.0,"from sinking down into the snow."],[56,1230.76,1.76,"What I need to do"],[56,1232.52,2.16,"is increase that surface area."],[56,1235.28,1.96,"Put on some snowshoes."],[56,1241.64,1.36,"I suppose, the secret to moving around"],[56,1243.0,3.72,"in deep snow is to do it efficiently."],[56,1247.96,0.84,"There we go."],[56,1249.36,2.28,"Adapted for walking in the snow."],[56,1253.8,2.48,"That is so much better."],[56,1256.28,0.92,"It's easy."],[56,1257.2,1.24,"I can walk through the forest."],[56,1258.44,2.28,"I'm not sinking down into my waist."],[56,1260.72,1.2,"I can go fast."],[56,1261.92,2.16,"I can almost go silently."],[56,1265.32,1.32,"Like I belong here."],[56,1277.16,3.36,"Polar bears have built-in snowshoes."],[56,1280.6,1.8,"Their paws are wide,"],[56,1282.4,2.64,"measuring about 30 centimetres across."],[56,1285.84,3.56,"This helps them to distribute their weight evenly on thin ice."],[56,1290.4,4.24,"Underneath their large paws are small, soft bumps"],[56,1294.64,2.36,"to give them extra grip on the ice."],[57,4.33,2.84,"If you're able to, please close your eyes."],[57,10.94,1.67,"Take a deep breath in"],[57,15.55,1.67,"and a deep breath out."],[57,20.45,1.44,"Relax your shoulders."],[57,23.79,1.47,"Unclench your jaw."],[57,27.73,1.94,"Take another deep breath in"],[57,32.66,1.7,"and another breath out."],[57,37.47,1.57,"You can open your eyes now."],[57,40.04,1.3,"My name is David."],[57,41.34,1.4,"I'm a poet and author."],[57,43.14,2.3,"I write about the things that bring me joy."],[57,45.94,2.24,"You'd be surprised just how often that means"],[57,48.21,2.27,"I pick up my pen and use my voice."],[57,51.22,4.37,"Whenever I have a truly bad day, frustrating as it may be,"],[57,55.59,2.9,"it's a matter of time until my next smile."],[57,58.49,1.57,"Good things are coming to me."],[57,61.09,1.23,"That may sound glib."],[57,62.36,1.27,"It may sound naive."],[57,63.66,2.33,"Life is far more complicated than that."],[57,66.03,1.17,"I won’t argue with you."],[57,67.23,1.37,"I know that that's true."],[57,68.6,2.54,"No one makes it through their whole life intact."],[57,71.53,2.77,"If you talked to every pillow I've had in my life,"],[57,74.31,2.17,"you would hear about plenty of tears."],[57,77.37,2.3,"If you stole my journal from my hotel room,"],[57,79.71,2.1,"you would read about plenty of fears."],[57,82.78,2.87,"If I'd had the emotional literacy when I was younger,"],[57,85.65,1.84,"you'd have heard me articulate pain."],[57,88.42,2.07,"I do all I can to avoid all of those states,"],[57,90.52,2.13,"but I'm sure that I'll feel them again."],[57,93.96,2.54,"I'm saying all this with a smile on my face."],[57,96.53,2.1,"Not because I don't understand language."],[57,99.03,2.84,"I just know there's far more for my feelings in store."],[57,101.9,1.27,"I look on the bright side."],[57,103.2,1.2,"It's a habit."],[57,104.83,2.47,"Because even in the very worst of times"],[57,107.3,2.47,"when it seems that I'll always feel down,"],[57,109.81,1.5,"I've learned over time,"],[57,111.34,1.53,"in the darkest of skies,"],[57,112.88,2.13,"there will come a break in the clouds."],[57,115.88,4.24,"I don't think that it's naive at all to look for joy in the everyday."],[57,120.92,4.47,"In fact, if I'm ever to be truly content, it seems to me to be the only way."],[57,125.99,2.27,"There is nothing too small to be happy about."],[57,128.29,1.7,"I mean, truly, nothing at all."],[57,130.59,2.44,"And because I take small joys most seriously,"],[57,133.06,2.1,"they stack up to be rather tall."],[57,135.8,4.57,"You see, if I saw \"real\" happiness as the big headline news events,"],[57,140.4,2.84,"I'd have maybe one a month if I'm lucky."],[57,143.27,2.3,"And between them, what would I do then?"],[57,146.21,2.44,"I think of the day that my sister was born,"],[57,148.68,2.2,"of the singular joy that day was."],[57,151.65,2.67,"Moments like that, the joy's beyond belief."],[57,154.32,2.6,"They’re so immense, they almost have to be one-offs."],[57,157.75,4.14,"Some things have to exist at that scale to be truly appreciated."],[57,162.66,4.17,"That doesn't mean my reserves of joy can't be regularly topped up and updated."],[57,167.76,2.7,"Because joy is not just a fanciful thing;"],[57,170.5,2.37,"it's a whimsical, powerful tool."],[57,173.7,2.8,"It's a thread of resilience woven into our lives."],[57,176.54,3.0,"When there are cracks in things, joy brings light through."],[57,180.38,1.97,"Even if my happiness isn't total."],[57,183.31,2.0,"Even when I don't think I deserve it."],[57,186.28,4.64,"Finding the small joys in every day is a practice that's always so worth it."],[57,192.42,2.1,"It's easier on some days than others."],[57,194.56,2.74,"It takes effort, that I won't deny,"],[57,197.33,2.84,"but I am deserving of my own best efforts."],[57,200.2,2.44,"Life is effort. Why wouldn’t I try?"],[57,204.1,4.71,"I spent far too long believing that joy was for someone more deserving than me."],[57,208.84,4.37,"I spent even longer believing contentment was ambition's worst enemy,"],[57,213.24,2.94,"when in fact we matter because we exist."],[57,216.55,1.87,"We deserve joy by virtue of this."],[57,219.15,2.77,"And contentment means that any ambition that follows"],[57,221.95,2.04,"is the healthiest kind that exists."],[57,225.02,2.5,"I'm capable of finding moments of joy."],[57,227.56,1.9,"There are plenty of opportunities."],[57,230.29,2.74,"And if I can find joy wherever I am,"],[57,233.06,2.0,"then where I am, joy shall be."],[57,236.77,3.47,"Next time your drink is the perfect temperature"],[57,240.27,2.63,"or your fork holds the perfect bite."],[57,242.94,2.64,"Next time you put on your favorite socks"],[57,245.61,3.1,"or adjust so you’re sitting just right."],[57,248.75,2.44,"Next time you stumble across a great song"],[57,251.18,2.7,"that you haven’t heard in a while."],[57,253.92,4.37,"Next time you catch eyes with a stranger who offers a nod or a genuine smile."],[57,259.96,3.4,"When you stretch your body and relax it again."],[57,263.39,3.5,"When you take a deep breath and you sigh."],[57,269.37,3.24,"When you get to relax at the end of a long day."],[57,272.64,3.37,"When you turn your face up to the sky,"],[57,276.04,2.97,"remember, life is made of the average days,"],[57,279.04,2.1,"and each one offers joy aplenty."],[57,281.91,2.37,"The smallest joys are the simplest to find."],[57,284.68,2.2,"And they add up more often than any."],[57,287.48,2.67,"I always pay attention when a small joy grabs me."],[57,290.45,3.3,"Some say easily pleased, I say frequently happy."],[57,294.96,1.47,"Because you deserve it."],[57,296.43,1.47,"Because I do too."],[57,297.93,1.17,"In our everyday,"],[57,299.13,2.17,"may joy always shine through."],[57,301.33,1.13,"Thank you."],[58,6.92,4.75,"Imagine you live in a remote village where the only source of water"],[58,11.67,2.09,"is delivered monthly by truck."],[58,13.93,2.17,"This, of course, costs money."],[58,16.26,4.42,"But then you discover a massive water source below the village:"],[58,20.98,3.54,"limitless water, but currently unreachable."],[58,24.9,2.54,"To access it, you’d need to dig a well."],[58,27.69,3.71,"That would cost years’ worth of water trucking fees,"],[58,31.61,5.42,"But once built, you and all future generations would have unlimited water—"],[58,37.03,3.5,"for only the minor cost of maintaining the well."],[58,41.12,2.04,"So, what’s the best way forward?"],[58,43.21,3.38,"Continue trucking in water? Or build the well?"],[58,47.04,2.42,"Is the trillion dollar price tag"],[58,49.46,3.59,"that comes with transitioning the world to clean energy"],[58,53.09,1.75,"worth the cost of investment?"],[58,55.34,3.67,"Like the trucked in water, fossil fuels aren't free."],[58,59.01,4.21,"There are the costs of the actual fuels, which, adjusted for inflation,"],[58,63.23,2.59,"haven't changed much for 140 years."],[58,65.94,3.29,"And then there are the costs of maintaining and updating"],[58,69.23,2.79,"our extensive fossil fuel infrastructure."],[58,72.36,1.08,"On the other hand,"],[58,73.44,5.09,"the wind, water, and sunlight needed to power renewables are all free"],[58,78.66,2.13,"and in unlimited supply,"],[58,80.78,3.09,"just like the village’s newly discovered water table."],[58,84.16,4.75,"There’s just the upfront cost of building infrastructure to harness them."],[58,89.25,2.67,"To fully transition to a green economy,"],[58,91.92,4.25,"we'd also need to invest in electrifying entire industries,"],[58,96.3,2.38,"building new renewable energy plants,"],[58,98.84,3.59,"deploying large-scale energy storage, and more."],[58,103.18,1.83,"Back in the early 2000s,"],[58,105.1,5.42,"most economic models predicted those costs to be completely impractical"],[58,110.52,2.21,"and prohibitively expensive."],[58,112.98,3.54,"For example, one model estimated that solar power"],[58,116.53,5.63,"would be about $157 per megawatt-hour in the 2020s,"],[58,122.24,3.54,"which is far more expensive than coal was projected to cost."],[58,126.29,4.84,"But a slow revolution has been happening over the past two decades."],[58,131.42,5.76,"In the early 2000, some countries like Germany and China and some tech companies"],[58,137.22,4.38,"decided to invest huge sums of money in solar infrastructure."],[58,141.68,2.46,"This led to more research and development,"],[58,144.14,2.54,"which brought the costs down far below"],[58,146.68,3.0,"what even the most optimistic model had predicted."],[58,150.35,5.88,"Today, solar is 84% cheaper than that early model projected it would be—"],[58,156.44,4.25,"making it cheaper than power from coal in much of the world."],[58,160.95,4.0,"This change is so dramatic that some economists now think"],[58,164.95,4.75,"switching to renewable energy quickly could save trillions of dollars"],[58,169.71,3.71,"in the next three decades— despite the upfront cost."],[58,173.71,3.38,"And of course, there’s another important cost to consider."],[58,177.42,3.54,"Global warming is very, very expensive:"],[58,180.97,5.0,"extreme weather, rising sea levels, crop failures, health issues,"],[58,185.97,3.38,"and industry disruptions all cost money."],[58,189.81,2.09,"Coming back to our village example,"],[58,191.9,2.75,"it would be as if the more water you truck in,"],[58,194.73,2.67,"the more that traffic degrades the road—"],[58,197.57,5.76,"ruts get deeper, sides erode, maybe part of it falls away in a landslide."],[58,203.37,3.13,"Eventually the road would become unusable."],[58,206.99,5.3,"Economists have also tried to predict how expensive future warming will be."],[58,212.75,5.84,"The widely cited DICE model posits that the cost of climate change-induced damage"],[58,218.71,6.17,"rises approximately as a function of global average temperatures squared."],[58,225.1,6.96,"So if temperatures rise by two degrees, costs rise by roughly a factor of four."],[58,232.44,5.59,"In other words, these models assume that costs will rise smoothly and continuously."],[58,238.32,4.38,"But many economists today argue that assumption is wrong,"],[58,242.7,4.84,"because it ignores catastrophic events like the collapse of the Amazon,"],[58,247.53,2.67,"melting of polar and Greenland ice,"],[58,250.2,3.71,"and widespread crop failures, just to name a few."],[58,254.42,4.42,"Any of these would cause huge, sharp spikes in costs."],[58,258.96,4.0,"In fact, the US government keeps track of climate disasters"],[58,262.97,3.21,"that incur more than $1 billion in damages,"],[58,266.18,5.88,"and since 1980 they’ve already recorded 400 of these events."],[58,272.44,5.88,"The total bill is estimated to be an eye-watering $2.8 trillion—"],[58,278.44,1.63,"just in the US."],[58,280.44,4.96,"For decades, the argument in favor of transitioning to a green economy"],[58,285.41,4.17,"was “let’s take the financial hit now; it’s hard,"],[58,289.58,3.38,"but it’ll protect the world for future generations.”"],[58,293.12,5.55,"But that argument relied on economic modeling that underestimated the costs"],[58,298.67,4.55,"of a warming world and overestimated the costs of transitioning."],[58,303.51,3.13,"We now know the economic outlook is different."],[58,306.84,2.0,"Making the investment to transition"],[58,308.85,3.09,"not only protects the world for future generations,"],[58,312.02,3.88,"it also saves us money in our own lifetime."],[58,316.31,3.17,"It's just the most logical thing to do."],[59,0.24,6.16,"The hashtag #guttok has over 6 billion views on TikTok. Gut health. Gut health."],[59,6.4,3.6,"Gut health. Gut health. Gut health. And if you think that's a big number,"],[59,10.0,6.48,"how about $60 billion? According to Grand View Research, that's the market value of the"],[59,16.48,7.6,"global digestive health products market. And it's projected to grow to over $100 billion by 2033."],[59,24.08,5.52,"These massive numbers are just a taste of how popular the world of gut health"],[59,29.6,3.68,"has become. Gut health has been such a hot topic all over social media for a"],[59,33.28,4.4,"couple of years now. More people talking about gut health and the gut microbiome."],[59,37.68,4.4,"Gut health is everywhere right now and honestly, it should be."],[59,42.08,4.8,"Experts say good gut health can not just improve digestion but also prolong life,"],[59,46.88,2.24,"boost immunity and reduce inflammation."],[59,49.12,5.76,"But with so much information out there, including some extreme diets and bizarre"],[59,54.88,5.52,"trends like poopmaxxing and coffee enemas, it can be confusing to tell what advice is backed"],[59,60.4,4.24,"by real evidence instead of marketing gimmicks and social media clickbait."],[59,64.64,4.32,"I tracked down some experts to help explain what gut health really means,"],[59,68.96,6.4,"how it became such a hot topic, and what they could tell us about maintaining a glorious gut."],[59,76.64,3.76,"First off, let's define what gut health is. And"],[59,80.4,3.04,"just to get this out of the way, it involves pooping."],[59,83.44,8.16,"Gut health is not just the absence of disease, but a state where your entire gastrointestinal tract,"],[59,91.6,5.28,"which is your stomach, your small bowel, your large bowel, all of these organs"],[59,96.88,5.52,"are thriving and healthy such that you're preventing disease occurrence in future."],[59,102.4,5.76,"A lot of focus on the gut microbiome and that's really having a robust gut microbiome,"],[59,108.16,4.24,"which is having a lot of different helpful microbes in there and feeding them well."],[59,112.4,5.28,"The microbiome in our gut is vital to our health. It's a complex network of trillions"],[59,117.68,7.12,"of microorganisms, or microbes, living inside of our digestive system. That's trillions with a T."],[59,124.8,6.08,"When we talk about the gut microbiome, we're talking about archaea, fungi, bacteria that"],[59,130.88,5.04,"live within our gut. They are synergistic with us. They help us survive as a species, as an"],[59,135.92,6.32,"individual. And if they're happy, we're happy. We evolved with these microbes in our gut since,"],[59,142.24,4.8,"really since the beginning of time. So, they're very important for helping us digest what we eat,"],[59,147.04,5.92,"extract calories from what we eat, and help maintain a normal gut sort of ecosystem."],[59,152.96,5.52,"They eat mostly what we eat and their waste products have a huge role in determining a"],[59,158.48,3.76,"lot of aspects of our health. Those compounds get absorbed into your bloodstream and they"],[59,162.24,3.44,"can move to your heart, they can move to your lungs, they can move to your brain,"],[59,165.68,5.12,"and they can have beneficial effects elsewhere so far away from where those bacteria are."],[59,170.8,4.0,"This is also where we get another buzzword that you might have heard before:"],[59,174.8,5.76,"probiotic. Probiotics are live microorganisms which, when administered in adequate amounts,"],[59,180.56,4.72,"confer a health benefit on the host. If you want to go more in depth on that,"],[59,185.28,5.36,"we actually did a video about probiotics in yogurt. And there's actually no hardline"],[59,190.64,5.28,"definition of what exactly is going on inside of us that makes our gut microbiome healthy."],[59,195.92,5.84,"What we do know is that if the microbes in it are diverse and robust, our gut is working properly."],[59,201.76,4.88,"We always describe each person's gut microbiome as being like a fingerprint. Tons of different"],[59,206.64,3.52,"species doing tons of different things, producing all these different compounds."],[59,210.16,5.12,"And when that world of microbes inside of us is out of balance, we can feel it."],[59,215.28,2.08,"When it comes to your gut microbiome,"],[59,217.36,5.76,"there's no good or bad bacteria. You need a lot of different kinds of bacteria in your gut. So,"],[59,223.12,5.36,"there's a term for the imbalance of the gut microbiome called dysbiosis. And some"],[59,228.48,5.36,"of those symptoms are things like bloating, abdominal pain, issues with bowel movements."],[59,233.84,4.64,"So, it's a good thing that we're talking about our gut health and microbiome more,"],[59,238.48,6.24,"but its rise as a trending topic has opened the door to questionable claims about gut health."],[59,244.72,6.48,"Many people have conditions that are affected by the gut microbiome. 40% of Americans say that GI"],[59,251.2,5.44,"symptoms disrupt their daily lives. Gut issues have been stigmatized in the past. You know, you"],[59,256.64,6.16,"don't walk around talking about your bowels and how they.. it's just kind of not what people do."],[59,262.8,3.68,"Public awareness of the gut health benefits from things like probiotics"],[59,266.48,4.88,"became widespread in the 2010s and product marketing for supplements,"],[59,271.36,4.24,"beverages, beauty, and food products leaned into that popularity. They"],[59,275.6,4.48,"added labels with claims like \"supports gut health\" and \"boosts microbiome.\""],[59,280.08,5.44,"You don't know if that means that actually any of those bacteria are going to make it to your colon"],[59,285.52,3.76,"and they're going to take hold or if they were ever, you know, alive to begin with. Everyone"],[59,289.28,5.44,"should just remember that the supplement industry is not regulated the same way as a medicine."],[59,294.72,5.04,"That hasn't stopped the big business of the wellness industry, which is now worth over $6"],[59,299.76,6.56,"trillion globally and is largely unregulated. You go to any grocery store and you see 100 different"],[59,306.32,4.64,"probiotics and gut health supplements. There have been studies that have been done that have shown"],[59,310.96,5.04,"that if you take probiotics off the shelf and you look at them, almost 30% of them are completely"],[59,316.0,5.28,"dead, they have nothing that's alive. And I think even more worrisome is that almost 30% of them"],[59,321.28,5.6,"have something in them that isn't on the label. So, you don't really know what you're getting."],[59,326.88,6.08,"But things really took off once social media influencers started heavily chiming in about"],[59,332.96,4.4,"gut health. All right, I got a gut hack for you. You want to focus on your gut health,"],[59,337.36,4.64,"this is the perfect drink for you. I cleansed my gut and now I have a healthy gut."],[59,342.0,4.72,"While some wellness influencers might be legit doctors or health care professionals,"],[59,346.72,2.56,"research shows that many of them aren't."],[59,349.28,4.64,"Billions of bacteria live in a person's mouth, maybe more than there are birds on Earth. Some"],[59,353.92,5.52,"are helpful, others, many are behind oral health problems like cavities and gingivitis. So,"],[59,359.44,4.88,"how do you manage something you can't actually see? It starts with a solid routine and the right"],[59,364.32,5.04,"ingredients for the job. Take stannous fluoride for example. It helps target and kill plaque"],[59,369.36,6.0,"bacteria. Plaque can harden into tartar and plaque bacteria can cause cavities and gingivitis. That's"],[59,375.36,5.44,"why Colgate Total developed a unique stabilization technology to keep the bacteria fighting stannous"],[59,380.8,5.04,"fluoride stable in your toothpaste for longer. The benefits go even further when you pair it with"],[59,385.84,5.04,"the Colgate Total toothbrush and mouthwash. The toothbrush is designed with ultra fine bristles"],[59,390.88,4.56,"to reach deep along the gum line and between teeth where plaque likes to hide. And when you're not"],[59,395.44,5.04,"brushing, the antibacterial mouthwash extends protection. Together, they form the Colgate"],[59,400.48,8.96,"Total Active Prevention System, designed to prevent oral health problems before they start."],[59,409.44,4.88,"41% of health and wellness influencers identify themselves as some kind of"],[59,414.32,4.72,"health care professional, but most of them describe themselves as things like coaches"],[59,419.04,7.04,"or entrepreneurs. And the thing is, people are listening to their advice. Pew research shows that"],[59,426.08,6.72,"40% of adults get health and wellness information from social media influencers or from podcasts."],[59,432.8,4.0,"It's midnight and you're scrolling TikTok and you see somebody who is like, \"Hey,"],[59,436.8,4.64,"do you have diarrhea and bloating?\" Suddenly, you're locked in. For the first time,"],[59,441.44,3.52,"you're feeling heard. Someone's describing your symptoms. And then you get sold and"],[59,444.96,4.08,"then you you start liking their content, you buy their supplements, you try out, you know,"],[59,449.04,4.32,"all of their different pathways and you move further and further away from the science."],[59,453.36,6.16,"That's led to plenty of unverified claims of so-called miracle products or \"hacks\" offering"],[59,459.52,6.0,"promises of improving your gut health overnight. And the more extreme viral trends like colonics,"],[59,465.52,4.72,"parasite cleanses, or poopmaxxing are likely to actually harm your gut."],[59,470.24,3.92,"The colonics is a big one. It actually will cause negative impacts on your gut"],[59,474.16,5.2,"microbiome. Anything that's like a a very vigorous wash of your gut will disturb your"],[59,479.36,3.04,"microbiome and you definitely don't want something like that. This whole concept"],[59,482.4,4.48,"of cleansing and resetting, there's no evidence. There's no proof for it."],[59,486.88,4.08,"So, what should we really be doing to keep our gut healthy? The doctors and researchers"],[59,490.96,5.04,"that I talked to actually had some pretty simple and science-backed advice. I think"],[59,496.0,3.84,"healthy diets like a Mediterranean diet are very reasonable. A wide variety of"],[59,499.84,4.4,"foods is good. Avoiding things I think is more important. Avoiding preservatives,"],[59,504.24,5.68,"avoiding artificial sweeteners. We know that a lot of the preservatives that are added,"],[59,509.92,4.8,"particularly those that are added to make foods sort of softer and creamier,"],[59,514.72,5.2,"they can cause an inflammatory response within the gut lining and also systemic inflammation."],[59,519.92,4.32,"Fibermaxxing or at least trying to get a little more fiber into your"],[59,524.24,3.84,"diet is one gut health trend that you should probably listen to. Number one,"],[59,528.08,4.8,"it is fiber 100% for for gut health. We want a little bit of fiber. We want the fruits,"],[59,532.88,3.44,"we want the vegetables, we want the colorful plate. Fermented foods for"],[59,536.32,4.16,"most people is a good thing to incorporate into your diet, if you can tolerate it."],[59,540.48,4.64,"There's really no way to \"hack your gut\" or change your microbiome without making some"],[59,545.12,5.28,"serious lifestyle changes. Start introducing probiotic foods every day. Probiotic foods do"],[59,550.4,4.16,"really boost the diversity of your microbiome. The closest you can get to your food source,"],[59,554.56,2.8,"the closest you can get to pulling it directly out of the ground and eating it,"],[59,557.36,3.2,"that's probably even better for you than anything you can get in the grocery store. So,"],[59,560.56,3.76,"if I had to distill it all down, I would say fiber, water, because water and fiber"],[59,564.32,4.08,"go together, and stress reduction. It's really a lot of common sense things."],[59,568.4,4.96,"There's no cure all supplement, food, or single habit that you can take up that's going to"],[59,573.36,6.24,"magically fix your gut health. Anybody who's offering you one-size-fits-all for microbiome"],[59,579.6,5.76,"and microbiome therapies, just approach with caution. The best advice is the simplest,"],[59,585.36,3.52,"and probably the same thing that your mom has been telling you for years."],[59,588.88,12.88,"Eat healthy, don't stress, get some sleep, and make good choices."],[59,601.76,4.0,"Thanks for watching. If you're not already a member, head on over to patreon.com/vox to"],[59,605.76,4.4,"get access to more stories, stay connected with us, and help make ambitious reporting"],[59,610.16,6.72,"possible. It's so important to support independent journalism. Your membership allows us to have fun,"],[59,616.88,5.12,"research interesting stories, make cool videos, and continue our investigative reporting."],[59,622.0,4.08,"And if it doesn't make financial sense for you to support right now, you can still follow us"],[59,626.08,4.08,"on Patreon for free and you'll stay up to date with all of our great box reporting."],[60,6.46,2.59,"Ah, yes: the Viking Age."],[60,9.13,4.25,"When medieval horn-helmeted Scandinavian men ravaged Europe,"],[60,13.43,1.79,"scribbling mysterious runes"],[60,15.22,4.46,"and toasting their victories in goblets forged from enemy skulls"],[60,19.89,3.17,"before bidding farewell in fiery funerals."],[60,23.27,2.67,"Except, that’s not quite how it went..."],[60,26.69,3.09,"It’s unclear where the word “Viking” came from,"],[60,29.78,4.04,"but in Old Norse it apparently referred to an act of maritime raiding."],[60,34.03,4.05,"Historians long pinned the start of the Viking Age to a brutal ambush"],[60,38.08,3.88,"on an English monastery in 793 CE,"],[60,42.08,4.59,"which the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle described as an onslaught of lightning,"],[60,46.92,3.25,"“fiery dragons,” and “heathen men.”"],[60,50.51,4.59,"The more recent discovery of Scandinavian artifacts at a site in Estonia"],[60,55.09,3.29,"suggests that Viking activity began decades earlier."],[60,58.8,2.96,"But whatever the precise 8th-century start,"],[60,61.77,3.29,"it persisted for the next 300 years."],[60,65.73,3.21,"We don’t know exactly what prompted all this Viking-ing."],[60,68.94,4.75,"But volcanic activity in the mid-500s triggered a mini Ice Age"],[60,73.69,2.5,"that destabilized Scandinavia."],[60,76.61,5.21,"Its society grew increasingly militarized, and like many warrior-based peoples,"],[60,81.83,4.17,"local chieftains legitimized their rule through gift giving."],[60,86.33,4.96,"So, wealth-acquiring expeditions helped maintain authority."],[60,91.75,3.21,"Meanwhile, new seafaring technology enabled ships"],[60,94.97,3.92,"to navigate both shallow rivers and open oceans."],[60,99.01,3.0,"Most Scandinavians didn't participate directly,"],[60,102.27,4.21,"but Viking raids could offer young men the opportunity to amass riches"],[60,106.52,2.46,"to afford land and marriage."],[60,109.61,3.42,"These raids were unquestionably violent."],[60,113.32,3.67,"But Vikings didn’t use their victims’ skulls as goblets,"],[60,117.2,3.0,"though this has been recorded in history—"],[60,120.28,2.0,"looking at you, Shah Ismail."],[60,122.53,5.05,"This myth likely arose when a 17th century Danish scholar called Ole Worm"],[60,127.58,3.54,"mistranslated a reference to animal horns."],[60,131.5,4.71,"Beyond killing, Viking invasions also involved mass capture."],[60,136.55,4.25,"Vikings enslaved and sold people into human trafficking networks,"],[60,140.8,3.46,"or sent them to labor in Scandinavia and its colonies,"],[60,144.51,3.75,"as was the case for many Irish captives in Iceland."],[60,148.77,4.17,"Over time, small-scale attacks on vulnerable coastal communities"],[60,152.94,2.71,"evolved into sieges on cities;"],[60,155.74,3.04,"temporary camps grew into permanent communities;"],[60,158.95,2.79,"and trade networks spread even farther."],[60,162.28,4.5,"Viking-era Scandinavian chieftains may have drunk German wine"],[60,166.87,1.67,"from Egyptian glass,"],[60,168.54,4.25,"while clad in Chinese silks and Indian gemstones."],[60,173.21,3.17,"But they did not wear horned helmets."],[60,176.67,3.75,"This image was popularized in 19th-century stage productions,"],[60,180.43,3.21,"specifically Richard Wagner’s “The Ring Cycle,”"],[60,183.64,3.46,"an epic, mythologized account of Germanic history."],[60,187.64,4.09,"While archaeologists have found Scandinavian horned helmets,"],[60,191.81,3.8,"they preceded Vikings by 2,000 years,"],[60,195.82,2.92,"and were likely used in rituals, not battle."],[60,199.03,5.17,"Instead, high-status Vikings might have worn iron helmets with brow bridges,"],[60,204.49,2.5,"while others donned leather caps."],[60,207.45,3.46,"And we aren’t sure these warriors were always men."],[60,211.21,4.05,"In 1878, researchers excavated gravesite remains"],[60,215.25,3.54,"since interpreted as belonging to a high-status warrior,"],[60,218.97,3.63,"which a 2017 analysis found to be female."],[60,223.39,3.13,"Viking society was undeniably patriarchal,"],[60,226.51,3.96,"but discoveries like this hint at our incomplete understanding"],[60,230.48,2.38,"of the social complexities at play."],[60,233.44,1.88,"As for funerary customs,"],[60,235.31,4.34,"10th-century Arab traveler Ibn Fadlan recorded witnessing"],[60,239.65,2.59,"a burning boat funeral along the Volga—"],[60,242.53,5.63,"however, it’s unclear if these were, in fact, Vikings, and it occurred on land—"],[60,248.33,4.84,"without flaming arrows and with sexual violence and human sacrifice."],[60,253.87,4.84,"Popular Viking funeral depictions are likely modern inventions."],[60,258.75,3.42,"Vikings didn't appear to have one funerary practice,"],[60,262.34,2.84,"and both buried and cremated their dead."],[60,265.72,4.09,"Regarding runes, Germanic peoples began writing with them"],[60,269.81,1.71,"around the 2nd century."],[60,271.64,3.96,"They came to be used to bridge the physical and spiritual worlds,"],[60,275.6,2.38,"and for more mundane matters."],[60,278.23,2.04,"While some reference past events,"],[60,280.44,4.04,"Viking Age runes typically didn't record detailed narratives."],[60,284.78,2.59,"Old Norse only became a literary language"],[60,287.37,4.17,"after Christianized Icelandic scholars adopted Latin script"],[60,291.58,1.46,"around the 12th century."],[60,293.5,5.8,"So, actual Viking Age accounts generally originate from traumatized victims"],[60,299.42,1.96,"and cultural outsiders,"],[60,301.46,5.55,"restricting our insights into how Vikings understood themselves and their world."],[60,307.6,4.55,"The Viking Age is traditionally said to have ended in 1066,"],[60,312.27,4.59,"with a battle won by English troops, though some incursions continued."],[60,317.31,4.75,"In Scandinavia, power shifted towards centralized Christian monarchies"],[60,322.07,3.79,"and state-sponsored conquest, like Crusading."],[60,326.15,3.63,"Meanwhile, the Scandinavian diaspora flourished in Iceland"],[60,329.78,3.21,"and integrated into other European societies."],[60,333.45,4.21,"For centuries, the term “Viking” went unused,"],[60,337.79,3.34,"until the 1800s, when nationalism swept Europe,"],[60,341.17,6.3,"and people began invoking it to gesture at a romanticized, shared Germanic past."],[60,347.59,1.83,"This mythos persists today—"],[60,349.68,4.8,"even as new discoveries reveal just how little we know of the Viking world,"],[60,354.64,5.34,"and shapes that once seemed certain recede into the thick mist of time."],[61,0.91,5.38,"One of my classmates, he was able to get me a meeting with the big boss—"],[61,6.29,1.08,"Lyor Cohen."],[61,7.38,2.38,"I took a Megabus from D.C. to New York."],[61,10.26,6.34,"We sit down and I played him a verse chorus of one song."],[61,16.6,3.75,"And then I started to play the beginning of a second song,"],[61,20.6,3.5,"and he cut me off, and he was like, “why am I here?”"],[61,24.27,2.63,"And I was like, “to hear my music?”"],[61,26.9,4.55,"He was like, “no, I mean, this is a waste of my time. You’re not ready yet.”"],[61,31.44,2.25,"And he got up and he left the meeting."],[61,33.7,4.09,"I got literally two minutes with him and it was over."],[61,39.03,4.0,"Hey, you! Yes, you. Is there music inside of you?"],[61,43.08,3.21,"We’ve recruited working musicians from throughout the industry"],[61,46.29,6.17,"to help you hear it, hold it, and share it with this wild and wonderful world."],[61,53.13,1.92,"Hi, my name is Daniel Breland."],[61,55.13,2.67,"I professionally go by Breland."],[61,58.1,6.34,"I am a singer, songwriter, and producer originally from New Jersey."],[61,64.44,4.75,"I live in Nashville and the genres that I usually play in"],[61,69.19,4.38,"are country music, R&B, hip hop, gospel."],[61,74.65,6.17,"That day with Lyor Cohen, I got back on the Megabus and went back to D.C.,"],[61,80.83,3.46,"and I was devastated for a good week because I was like,"],[61,84.33,2.46,"I’m 19, I’ve been making music for five years,"],[61,86.79,3.09,"I got a meeting with the guy that’s supposed to be The Guy,"],[61,89.88,2.17,"and he said it wasn’t it, you know?"],[61,92.05,4.25,"And so I’m just trying to figure out what my next steps should be."],[61,96.51,5.71,"I decided to double down and I ended up buying some equipment."],[61,102.39,4.09,"And I was like, look, I’m going to write and record a song every day"],[61,106.6,1.58,"until I get better."],[61,108.27,5.42,"And so from from that day, in October of 2014,"],[61,113.82,5.59,"for the next year, I wrote and recorded a good 365 songs."],[61,119.99,4.88,"And the following year, the songs had definitely gotten marginally better."],[61,124.95,3.67,"And so I said, okay, well, this next year, now my junior year,"],[61,128.62,2.67,"I said I’m going to write and record two songs a day."],[61,131.5,3.21,"Now, at this point, I’m pretty much barely going to class."],[61,134.8,1.42,"Now I've got two songs a day."],[61,136.22,2.54,"So now halfway into that second year,"],[61,138.76,6.05,"I've got 700 plus songs and nobody to really listen to any of them."],[61,144.89,4.92,"And so I started reaching out, kind of cold-calling people in the industry—"],[61,149.81,5.09,"songwriters, producers, managers, A&Rs, artists, friends."],[61,154.9,3.92,"I mean, they received an email, a DM, a tweet."],[61,158.82,3.46,"I might have written a couple handwritten letters if there was an address"],[61,162.28,1.46,"to a record label."],[61,163.83,3.38,"And literally just trying to reach out to as many people I could"],[61,167.21,2.29,"to see if I could get any feedback."],[61,169.5,3.09,"Really, that was the year that I made the biggest jump,"],[61,172.71,3.46,"because I started getting feedback where I was like, okay, cool—"],[61,176.17,3.38,"there are people in the industry that have given me some insight"],[61,179.55,4.5,"on how songs are actually written and where I’m falling short."],[61,185.39,2.0,"Writer’s block is real."],[61,187.39,3.34,"You know, inspiration can strike at any moment,"],[61,190.73,4.05,"but you can also lose inspiration on something in any moment."],[61,194.77,2.13,"And I think that’s okay."],[61,196.94,5.92,"Because I’ve written so many songs, and because I was so dedicated"],[61,202.95,4.3,"for all of those years that I was writing songs every day,"],[61,207.25,1.79,"and no one was hearing any of them,"],[61,209.33,3.92,"I understand that there are songs that people are never going to hear."],[61,213.25,5.38,"And so I’m not afraid to step away from a song entirely"],[61,218.63,5.42,"and say, hey, if it’s not happening for this song on this day, that’s okay."],[61,224.14,4.25,"I think the right way to produce a song is to listen to the song"],[61,228.39,3.63,"and give the song what it needs, you know?"],[61,232.02,4.21,"And that requires a level of— a level of humility."],[61,236.23,4.05,"You may think, okay, well, this is what it needs to do because I’ve done this before"],[61,240.28,1.46,"and I know that that works."],[61,241.74,2.34,"But sometimes it's being able to say, hey,"],[61,244.07,2.63,"maybe it’s something that I’ve never thought of before,"],[61,246.7,3.21,"and maybe it’s something that’s going to come to me in its own time."],[61,249.91,2.09,"And you can't rush the creative process."],[61,252.0,4.25,"A lot of times you literally have to step back from it and say,"],[61,256.63,2.09,"how can I be in service to the song?"],[61,260.67,4.88,"For me, stepping back to gain perspective on a song,"],[61,265.55,1.75,"it can come in a few different ways."],[61,267.31,2.63,"Sometimes it’s me listening to a song a bunch,"],[61,269.97,2.71,"and sometimes it’s me not listening to the song at all;"],[61,272.69,3.79,"literally letting it sit for a few days or a week,"],[61,276.48,3.17,"working on some other music and then coming back to it."],[61,280.03,3.84,"You know, sometimes it's playing the song around other people."],[61,283.9,1.79,"It's not an exact science."],[61,285.7,2.13,"Part of it is just being receptive"],[61,287.82,3.75,"to the fact that it might look totally different on one song"],[61,291.58,1.42,"than it does on another song."],[61,293.0,3.34,"You might get to that finished product the day that you write it,"],[61,296.33,3.54,"and then there's other songs that might take you months or even years."],[61,299.88,2.0,"And I don’t think either of those is wrong."],[61,301.88,2.67,"It just literally depends on how it comes to you"],[61,304.55,1.92,"and where your inspiration arrives from."],[61,306.47,3.46,"For me, it’s just recognizing that it’s not always going to go"],[61,309.93,1.58,"exactly the way I want."],[61,311.68,5.88,"And that even those “dud songs,” are the ones that don’t get finished,"],[61,317.69,4.13,"you know, they still help inform the way that I write songs in the future."],[61,321.86,3.71,"And I think it’s those great songs that you end up getting,"],[61,325.57,3.38,"oftentimes you would not get those songs if it weren't for the songs"],[61,328.95,2.63,"that you feel like you missed or that you didn’t get it."],[61,331.58,2.75,"And so just trying to continue to encourage yourself"],[61,334.33,1.54,"and not get too down on yourself"],[61,335.87,3.09,"because something isn’t going at the speed that you want"],[61,338.96,2.54,"or coming together in quite the way that you want."],[61,342.05,4.09,"You will eventually get to the songs that you really want"],[61,346.13,2.29,"if you just continue to work on it."],[62,5.92,3.88,"So when people find out that I study antidepressants,"],[62,9.84,1.17,"they often ask,"],[62,11.05,1.29,"how do they work?"],[62,12.88,3.09,"And people are surprised to learn, we don't know."],[62,18.01,3.29,"And part of that surprise comes from the fact that we've been told"],[62,21.31,2.29,"that depression is a chemical imbalance,"],[62,23.6,2.17,"that you don't make enough serotonin."],[62,26.06,2.25,"And we know that antidepressants,"],[62,28.36,3.59,"SSRIs are the most common antidepressant prescribed,"],[62,31.99,1.5,"increase serotonin."],[62,33.86,1.88,"And so they think that what we're doing"],[62,35.74,2.71,"is we're fixing depression by increasing serotonin."],[62,39.41,2.04,"But that's not the whole story."],[62,41.5,2.29,"In fact, that may not even be the story."],[62,44.16,1.96,"What I'm going to talk to you today about"],[62,46.13,1.63,"is some of our work with ketamine,"],[62,47.79,3.88,"a really remarkable drug that has rapid antidepressant effects."],[62,51.67,2.54,"And it doesn't correct a chemical imbalance."],[62,54.26,3.0,"It actually causes the brain to change, to adapt."],[62,58.89,4.25,"So when we talk about antidepressants, we first have to start with depression."],[62,63.14,1.79,"And everyone uses the term depressed."],[62,64.98,1.17,"We hear it all the time."],[62,66.15,3.5,"\"I feel depressed, I feel sad, I have melancholy.\""],[62,70.65,4.09,"Creative expression of depression is very common."],[62,74.78,3.59,"For centuries, people have tried to express the feeling of depressed"],[62,78.37,1.21,"or melancholy."],[62,79.62,4.55,"For example, Picasso, as illustrated in these paintings,"],[62,84.16,3.75,"went through his blue period and his painting style changed."],[62,88.5,3.67,"It really portrayed a sense of sadness, of gloom."],[62,93.26,1.71,"We know that writers do this."],[62,95.01,1.83,"Poets, I mean, you can read a poem"],[62,96.89,2.33,"and literally be moved to tears by the words."],[62,99.76,1.38,"Musicians."],[62,101.18,2.04,"How many sad songs do we hear?"],[62,103.77,3.92,"Eric Clapton's \"Tears in Heaven\", about the loss of his son,"],[62,107.73,1.33,"is heartbreaking."],[62,110.52,3.13,"But major depression is more than just sadness."],[62,113.65,3.13,"It's a serious medical condition,"],[62,116.78,1.5,"especially left untreated."],[62,119.24,5.21,"Depression is really the dulling of emotion,"],[62,124.5,1.96,"really losing interest"],[62,126.5,2.33,"and sometimes completely withdrawing."],[62,129.54,2.04,"The symptoms of depression can vary."],[62,132.42,3.29,"Some people, it may impact more appetite,"],[62,135.76,1.13,"others sleep."],[62,137.34,3.75,"It may have effects on concentration, memory."],[62,141.14,1.17,"A range of things."],[62,142.35,2.29,"So not everyone has the same symptoms."],[62,144.64,2.25,"But it's a serious illness."],[62,146.89,1.42,"Again, left untreated."],[62,148.77,4.55,"We know globally it affects over 280 million people worldwide."],[62,153.65,1.29,"So a huge number."],[62,154.94,1.75,"Everybody in this room knows someone"],[62,156.74,1.54,"that's impacted by depression,"],[62,158.32,2.84,"whether they tell you about it or not."],[62,163.53,4.09,"Moreover, depression is the leading cause of disability in the US."],[62,167.66,3.0,"Disability meaning really loss of productivity"],[62,170.71,1.63,"both on the individual and family"],[62,172.38,1.33,"and on society."],[62,178.01,5.42,"Part of the focus that comes from studying depression, though,"],[62,183.47,2.38,"is really focused on antidepressants."],[62,185.89,2.29,"And we all have heard of antidepressants."],[62,188.6,4.38,"And how this came about was really completely randomly."],[62,193.02,6.26,"It was noticed that certain drugs had antidepressant effects,"],[62,199.28,1.33,"and as people studied it,"],[62,200.61,3.04,"they realized that if you increase serotonin,"],[62,203.7,1.92,"you had an antidepressant effect."],[62,205.66,2.25,"So things like Prozac, Zoloft,"],[62,207.95,2.25,"common antidepressants,"],[62,210.21,2.0,"have been studied for decades,"],[62,212.21,2.38,"and we've seen ads on TV,"],[62,214.59,1.67,"in doctor's offices."],[62,216.25,3.71,"People openly talk about the idea we're increasing serotonin,"],[62,219.97,2.46,"we're correcting our chemical imbalance."],[62,222.84,1.33,"But here's the thing."],[62,224.85,2.92,"If you look at individuals with depression,"],[62,227.81,2.5,"many of them don't have decreased serotonin."],[62,230.35,1.67,"They have normal levels."],[62,232.9,2.04,"And that's a surprise to many people."],[62,234.94,2.92,"This idea that we had a measurable index of depression"],[62,237.9,3.0,"just because it was less serotonin, many people talked about,"],[62,240.94,1.75,"but it really wasn't true."],[62,244.12,2.71,"So that's been sort of surprising to many people."],[62,247.58,2.25,"But importantly,"],[62,249.87,4.42,"SSRIs still are incredibly important treatment options."],[62,255.13,1.38,"They help many people."],[62,256.54,2.96,"They are life-changing and they are life-saving."],[62,260.63,3.21,"For many people, they really give them the will to live."],[62,265.05,3.67,"The thing about SSRIs is that when you take them,"],[62,268.72,3.34,"you have this increase in serotonin very quickly,"],[62,272.1,2.13,"but they take weeks to work."],[62,274.98,1.79,"They don't work quickly."],[62,276.77,1.83,"And so why do they take weeks to work?"],[62,278.61,2.63,"And that's the part that we really don't know."],[62,281.24,1.79,"But again, they increase serotonin."],[62,283.07,3.75,"So if it was simply fixing, it should happen quickly."],[62,288.2,4.25,"And so while again, they're incredibly important treatments,"],[62,292.5,1.21,"they are life saving."],[62,293.71,3.67,"And if you're on an SSRI, you should continue to take your SSRI."],[62,297.38,1.75,"That's an important message."],[62,299.13,1.92,"But that's why some of the research"],[62,301.09,2.21,"I’m going to talk to you today about ketamine,"],[62,303.3,2.09,"which my lab and group has been studying now"],[62,305.38,2.25,"for more than 15 years, has been so remarkable."],[62,308.93,2.5,"Ketamine, this is the structure of the drug."],[62,311.93,1.46,"What's been noticed is,"],[62,313.43,2.92,"in a clinical study that had nothing to do with depression,"],[62,316.35,1.83,"it was just an observation,"],[62,318.23,1.58,"they gave a low dose of ketamine."],[62,319.82,2.08,"And some of you may have heard of ketamine."],[62,321.94,2.25,"At high levels, it's an anesthetic,"],[62,324.19,2.84,"at more mid-level doses it's a party drug."],[62,327.03,2.67,"But what they were doing was a study with an incredibly,"],[62,329.7,1.54,"incredibly low dose."],[62,331.24,2.5,"And for those individuals that were depressed"],[62,333.79,1.25,"that received ketamine,"],[62,335.08,2.67,"they had a rapid antidepressant effect --"],[62,337.75,2.96,"not within weeks, within hours."],[62,340.71,1.75,"We didn't even know it was possible"],[62,342.5,2.21,"to have an antidepressant effect that rapidly."],[62,345.51,2.42,"And so people have been studying why."],[62,348.26,1.67,"What is this rapid effect?"],[62,349.97,1.83,"Because it really is remarkable."],[62,352.72,3.21,"And it doesn't just work in individuals that could be depressed."],[62,355.98,3.96,"It's really been studied in individuals that don't respond to SSRIs,"],[62,359.94,1.71,"because not everyone responds."],[62,362.48,2.17,"And so it sounds pretty remarkable,"],[62,364.69,4.09,"but it's important to mention up front that ketamine does have risks."],[62,368.82,1.92,"And as with any drug,"],[62,370.78,3.55,"if it's not used correctly or given at very high levels,"],[62,374.33,1.67,"it can have adverse effects."],[62,376.04,4.84,"So it's really, really important that things are followed"],[62,380.92,3.29,"in a manner as they should be, not just more-is-better."],[62,384.25,1.29,"That is not the message."],[62,386.26,2.38,"So we started studying ketamine"],[62,388.68,1.67,"and other groups did as well."],[62,390.39,2.34,"And the first thing, which we all know,"],[62,392.72,3.04,"is that ketamine doesn't work on serotonin."],[62,395.81,2.92,"It doesn't increase serotonin like SSRIs."],[62,399.48,2.42,"Instead, it actually focuses"],[62,401.94,3.13,"on a different neurotransmitter system called glutamate."],[62,405.07,3.75,"And glutamate is what's important in fast communication in your brain."],[62,409.28,3.59,"Now what’s interesting is that ketamine targets glutamate,"],[62,412.87,1.42,"but it doesn't activate it."],[62,414.66,1.25,"It blocks it."],[62,416.75,4.0,"So why would blocking fast communication have any beneficial effect?"],[62,421.38,2.38,"And what we've been able to show is that ketamine,"],[62,423.8,2.38,"by blocking this fast communication,"],[62,426.17,4.5,"what it does is it actually strengthens particular connections in the brain,"],[62,430.72,1.71,"what we call synaptic plasticity."],[62,432.47,1.92,"It's just a change in your brain."],[62,434.43,2.63,"Your brain is able to adapt and strengthen connections."],[62,437.1,3.25,"And we think that's what's driving the antidepressant effect."],[62,441.6,3.29,"And so as we've been studying this and trying to understand,"],[62,444.94,1.58,"OK, what does this mean?"],[62,446.53,1.63,"How does this work?"],[62,449.11,3.29,"What we've been able to show is that -- this is a nerve cell,"],[62,452.45,4.38,"this is one of the cells that we think actually are strengthening connections."],[62,457.45,2.75,"As we're studying this and trying to understand it,"],[62,460.21,2.17,"it's really providing a lot of different ways"],[62,462.42,2.04,"of how we're framing what is depression."],[62,465.21,2.67,"So first of all, it's reshaping treatment."],[62,468.67,2.08,"This is not a chemical imbalance."],[62,470.8,2.71,"We are not correcting a chemical imbalance."],[62,473.51,1.92,"The idea that someone may be broken,"],[62,475.43,2.38,"as people have talked about with depression,"],[62,477.81,3.96,"they're broken, they can't make an essential amino acid,"],[62,481.81,2.92,"essential transmitter like serotonin."],[62,484.77,2.34,"It's not the case because we're not correcting it."],[62,487.11,1.58,"We're not doing anything to it."],[62,489.07,4.8,"It's the idea that your brain can adapt and ketamine is able to tap into that."],[62,494.41,3.09,"So it changes the way that we're thinking about depression."],[62,497.49,3.25,"Moreover, it's changing how we're reframing the timing."],[62,501.2,3.59,"Instead of an antidepressant requiring weeks to work,"],[62,504.83,3.5,"you can respond within hours, which is remarkable,"],[62,508.38,3.71,"especially for individuals that didn't respond to SSRIs"],[62,512.13,3.38,"that may have been depressed without treatment for decades."],[62,516.76,1.96,"And third, it's reframing hope."],[62,519.43,2.96,"The idea that there are treatments, that you're not broken,"],[62,522.43,3.63,"that this is just a matter of getting your brain to respond,"],[62,526.11,1.46,"to respond to treatment."],[62,528.15,2.25,"And that, I think, is a really important message"],[62,530.4,1.92,"of how we're thinking about the future."],[62,532.99,2.54,"As we continue to study ketamine,"],[62,535.57,2.59,"there are many different aspects that we're looking at."],[62,538.16,1.17,"And so, you know,"],[62,539.37,2.54,"with this diagram, we're showing two nerve cells"],[62,541.95,4.8,"and just a neurotransmitter in this case ketamine, actually,"],[62,546.75,1.71,"which targets the glutamate system."],[62,548.5,2.63,"Glutamate is being released and activated."],[62,551.13,2.88,"As we're looking at how it's sort of causing this disruption,"],[62,554.05,2.21,"this blocking of transmission,"],[62,556.3,1.17,"we're actually thinking,"],[62,557.51,2.59,"are there other ways to trigger this plasticity?"],[62,560.43,2.38,"Some people are looking at when you take ketamine,"],[62,562.81,1.71,"you have this window of plasticity."],[62,564.52,2.46,"Do you respond to therapy better during that time?"],[62,566.98,3.8,"Or what about to other drugs perhaps brain stimulation."],[62,572.15,1.79,"We also have been looking at it"],[62,573.99,5.34,"from a context of, can we extend ketamine's antidepressant effects?"],[62,579.74,1.58,"So as we look at this plasticity,"],[62,581.33,4.34,"what's important to remember is that it's like a painting analogy."],[62,586.41,3.59,"If you have a blank canvas and you paint,"],[62,590.0,1.54,"you're adding something new,"],[62,591.55,2.54,"but then you can dial in the colors."],[62,594.13,2.21,"You can make them vibrant,"],[62,596.38,3.21,"more brighter or less brighter, however you want."],[62,600.05,2.71,"Ketamine is not painting the canvas."],[62,602.76,2.0,"It's not creating new memories."],[62,604.81,3.46,"What it's doing is it's alleviating that despair."],[62,608.98,2.59,"And some people describe, with depression,"],[62,611.61,3.92,"someone noted that, before ketamine treatment,"],[62,615.53,2.04,"because they didn’t respond to SSRIs,"],[62,617.57,4.8,"that they were really living in a dark room with no windows."],[62,622.37,1.38,"And they couldn't get out."],[62,624.33,1.17,"After taking ketamine,"],[62,625.54,1.75,"it didn't make it a party house."],[62,627.71,4.96,"What it did is it made it seem like the light was lifted and there was a door."],[62,633.96,1.38,"And it provided hope."],[62,636.72,2.33,"What ketamine is doing, in the painting analogy,"],[62,639.09,2.21,"is it’s actually able to dial in --"],[62,641.35,3.13,"to lift the sort of despair, if you will."],[62,645.06,1.33,"And that's important."],[62,646.43,3.5,"So as we target this plasticity, we think about how can the brain adapt,"],[62,649.94,1.63,"how can it change,"],[62,651.61,1.42,"one of the things we're doing"],[62,653.02,2.67,"is that this antidepressant effect doesn't last forever."],[62,655.74,1.58,"Like flowers in a vase."],[62,658.11,3.13,"Ketamine treatment lasts a few days and then it wanes."],[62,661.74,2.04,"So one of the things we've been able to do"],[62,663.83,5.55,"is actually initiate studies to look at giving ketamine,"],[62,669.41,1.83,"triggering this antidepressant effect"],[62,671.29,2.84,"and then can we sustain it longer"],[62,674.13,3.46,"so that you don't have to have another treatment so soon?"],[62,678.59,3.34,"And we're doing this through targeting, this plasticity,"],[62,681.97,1.88,"this adaptability in the brain."],[62,684.1,1.79,"And we think that's quite powerful,"],[62,685.89,2.75,"because again, it's not about having to fix something"],[62,688.68,3.13,"because again, given all the different symptoms,"],[62,691.81,1.5,"people have different levels --"],[62,693.31,2.04,"if it were a matter of fixing something."],[62,695.36,5.05,"All we're trying to do is to tap in to the brain's ability to change."],[62,701.57,2.59,"And so where are we going with this?"],[62,704.66,3.71,"Well, ketamine, as I've told you, is a really remarkable treatment."],[62,708.91,3.54,"But it's not for everyone, and it's not without potential harm."],[62,712.92,4.55,"If you are currently taking an SSRI, you should continue to take the SSRI."],[62,717.5,3.29,"This is the first line of treatment for depression."],[62,721.17,2.75,"It's for individuals that don't respond really,"],[62,723.93,3.34,"that have been most studied in terms of taking ketamine."],[62,727.31,1.33,"If you're interested in it,"],[62,728.64,2.42,"you should talk to your health care provider."],[62,731.06,2.79,"But we're using ketamine to try to understand, again,"],[62,733.85,2.67,"how do you generate an antidepressant effect?"],[62,736.57,3.46,"And the idea that it's not just an imbalance, again,"],[62,740.07,2.17,"that someone is not broken,"],[62,742.28,1.92,"maybe their brain is just sort of stuck."],[62,744.24,2.92,"And ketamine allows you to adapt, to change."],[62,747.7,2.88,"And that, again, is what we think the response is."],[62,751.12,1.92,"And so as we continue to study ketamine,"],[62,753.08,1.17,"we and other labs,"],[62,754.25,3.09,"we're trying to understand how can we make this better?"],[62,758.0,3.63,"How can we make this safer to work for more people?"],[62,761.63,2.13,"And how can we sustain the effect?"],[62,764.13,3.84,"And I think those are all really powerful messages of hope,"],[62,768.01,3.09,"because it's not just about seeking treatment today,"],[62,771.14,2.63,"which is incredibly important,"],[62,773.81,3.75,"but also for our children and our grandchildren and for others."],[62,777.57,2.0,"Because this is a global issue"],[62,779.57,3.25,"and this is something that there's a lot of work going on"],[62,782.86,2.75,"that's really creating a lot of excitement in the field."],[62,786.24,2.71,"And it's also the power of scientific research."],[62,788.95,1.17,"Thank you."],[63,6.84,2.84,"After waking up alone in a locked room,"],[63,9.76,3.04,"two documents are slipped under your door:"],[63,13.09,6.21,"a note in an alien language and a detailed instruction manual in your language."],[63,19.68,4.5,"The manual explains that for each alien character in the note,"],[63,24.23,4.0,"you should write an indicated corresponding symbol."],[63,28.48,4.71,"Following this chart, you write a response that you slip out the door."],[63,33.74,3.79,"And for the next several days, this exchange continues."],[63,37.62,1.46,"Outside the room,"],[63,39.08,5.71,"alien scientists are thrilled because they believe you’re conversing with them."],[63,45.21,4.67,"But you still have no idea what these characters mean."],[63,50.92,3.5,"This scenario isn’t just a bizarre misunderstanding—"],[63,54.55,5.38,"it’s a valuable thought experiment for understanding artificial intelligence."],[63,60.31,6.51,"Philosopher John Searle developed the original version of this premise in 1980,"],[63,66.94,4.17,"as a response to some of the AI work being done at the time."],[63,71.69,4.67,"But while modern AI models don't work like those outdated machines"],[63,76.49,2.71,"or the prisoner in Searle’s hypothetical,"],[63,79.41,4.3,"the question motivating his thought experiment is still relevant."],[63,84.16,4.0,"To quote Searle, he wanted to interrogate whether an"],[63,88.17,6.09,"“appropriately programmed computer literally has cognitive states.”"],[63,94.72,4.17,"In other words, if a computer looks like it understands something,"],[63,99.18,4.25,"does that mean it actually understands the way a human does?"],[63,104.18,5.13,"Searle’s question falls into a long tradition of exploring whether or not"],[63,109.31,3.04,"AI could have a mind like ours."],[63,113.19,4.13,"But answering these inquiries is incredibly difficult because,"],[63,117.32,3.59,"as philosophers and cognitive scientists will tell you,"],[63,120.91,2.63,"we still don’t know how our minds work."],[63,123.74,2.84,"Even our fundamental definitions are slippery!"],[63,127.21,4.92,"Theorists generally agree that concepts like understanding, sentience,"],[63,132.13,2.5,"and consciousness are all different,"],[63,134.63,3.75,"but also that they’re related, and we don’t know how."],[63,139.22,3.34,"Worse still, our usual scientific tools"],[63,142.56,3.17,"struggle to help us understand these experiences."],[63,146.47,2.29,"Consider drinking a cup of coffee."],[63,149.1,4.55,"Scientists can observe the physical process of ingesting the coffee,"],[63,153.73,4.04,"and we can measure the chemical impacts of caffeine on your body."],[63,158.57,2.54,"These things are objective realities."],[63,161.53,6.51,"But your collective sensation of smelling, sipping, evaluating, and experiencing"],[63,168.04,3.88,"a morning routine is more than the sum of its parts."],[63,172.54,6.34,"This is consciousness— your subjective experience of being alive."],[63,179.18,5.59,"And despite major leaps in psychology, cognitive science, and neurology,"],[63,184.93,4.88,"researchers still don’t know how various firing neurons"],[63,189.81,2.34,"bring about this experience."],[63,192.73,3.5,"So if we can't define consciousness and understanding"],[63,196.23,3.38,"or identify what's uniquely human about them,"],[63,199.86,3.75,"how can we possibly test for these states in computers?"],[63,203.99,3.8,"Assessments like the Turing Test propose that if a human"],[63,207.79,2.54,"can't tell they're conversing with a computer,"],[63,210.46,4.3,"that computer could be seen as having some internal cognition."],[63,215.13,4.17,"But this scenario is exactly what Searle was criticizing!"],[63,219.59,3.75,"This computer might just have the appearance of understanding."],[63,223.89,4.59,"And just like cognitive scientists struggling to map consciousness"],[63,228.47,1.63,"onto brain activity,"],[63,230.18,4.71,"today’s AI researchers know how they trained their creations,"],[63,234.9,4.17,"but not how AIs reach their exact conclusions."],[63,239.32,3.63,"There are some ways in which modern machine learning models"],[63,242.95,2.71,"are less mysterious than their predecessors."],[63,246.37,3.21,"Approaches like neural networks and deep learning"],[63,249.58,3.92,"are designed to mimic known elements of human cognition."],[63,253.67,4.09,"Like us, these models excel at pattern recognition—"],[63,257.96,2.84,"they learn by becoming familiar with information"],[63,260.8,3.17,"and forming connections across data sets."],[63,264.76,1.5,"This kind of processing"],[63,266.26,4.34,"arguably approaches Searle’s definition of understanding—"],[63,270.73,4.3,"but it also reveals a bias in his original question."],[63,275.4,2.75,"Since humans learn through pattern recognition"],[63,278.15,2.46,"and we believe ourselves to be conscious,"],[63,280.74,5.67,"we might also be predisposed to think other beings who learn the same way"],[63,286.41,3.21,"are somehow closer to consciousness as well."],[63,290.91,5.38,"To combat this bias, some theorists have developed a different metric;"],[63,296.83,4.92,"specifically, that a fully conscious AI could draw connections"],[63,301.88,3.0,"beyond the information in its data set."],[63,305.97,4.25,"For example, one lab’s “artificial consciousness test”"],[63,310.26,3.75,"probes AIs that have no data about consciousness"],[63,314.14,4.42,"for information they could only acquire from being conscious."],[63,318.94,4.38,"This might involve asking an AI if it understands dreaming,"],[63,323.53,3.29,"or can report having had dreams itself."],[63,327.16,3.0,"Can it understand a story about body swapping,"],[63,330.16,3.71,"where consciousnesses are shuffled between physical forms?"],[63,334.41,5.67,"It’s unclear when or if an AI will be able to understand us"],[63,340.09,2.54,"the way we understand each other."],[63,342.88,1.42,"But whatever happens,"],[63,344.3,5.09,"it’s up to us already conscious creatures to chart the path forward."],[64,7.25,5.88,"The average person poops out approximately 11,030 kilograms of cumulative waste."],[64,13.22,2.96,"That's the equivalent of more than six SUVs."],[64,16.39,2.34,"Now, oftentimes, that poop is brown."],[64,18.89,5.21,"But why is that, considering all the colorful comestibles one consumes?"],[64,24.19,4.42,"And what's going on when poop appears in different colors and textures?"],[64,28.98,2.54,"Say you’re eating a beautiful, rainbowy salad."],[64,31.74,5.55,"Your teeth and saliva first help break down the food and add lubrication."],[64,37.45,2.08,"Each bite enters your esophagus"],[64,39.53,3.92,"and sequential muscle contractions push it towards your stomach."],[64,43.91,4.42,"There, the mixture combines with the clear, hydrochloric acid"],[64,48.34,5.5,"and enzyme-packed digestive juices your stomach started secreting"],[64,54.01,1.83,"as you prepared to eat."],[64,56.47,5.09,"These juices further degrade your food, and once your stomach stops churning,"],[64,61.56,3.92,"its partially digested contents, called chyme,"],[64,65.52,3.04,"slowly empty into your small intestine."],[64,68.98,4.96,"Your pancreas releases another clear fluid loaded with even more enzymes,"],[64,73.94,4.88,"which get to work breaking carbohydrates down into monosaccharides,"],[64,78.91,4.3,"and proteins into amino acids and peptides."],[64,83.54,3.46,"At this point, your liver also sends in bile,"],[64,87.0,1.63,"which is tinted yellow."],[64,89.04,4.0,"It contains salts that help separate the fats from your food"],[64,93.05,4.25,"so enzymes can also transform them into smaller units,"],[64,97.3,3.04,"like monoglycerides and fatty acids."],[64,100.43,6.01,"Specific intestinal cells can then absorb these nutrients into the bloodstream."],[64,106.98,3.63,"Bile is especially important on the color front."],[64,110.77,4.96,"The reason it's yellow is because one of its major components is bilirubin,"],[64,116.07,4.59,"a yellowy compound produced by the breakdown of hemoglobin,"],[64,120.66,4.05,"the protein that helps your red blood cells transport oxygen."],[64,125.2,2.92,"By this point, the digestive system has broken down"],[64,128.12,4.38,"and absorbed lots of nutrients and added bile to the mix,"],[64,132.63,4.25,"so we’ve gone from a rainbow medley to a greenish-yellow liquid."],[64,137.01,2.54,"But the transformation isn’t yet complete."],[64,139.8,2.88,"This mixture then reaches the large intestine,"],[64,142.72,4.42,"where microbes break the bilirubin down into stercobilin."],[64,147.35,4.92,"This compound is brown, and it's what lends poop its classic color."],[64,152.48,3.38,"The large intestine then absorbs excess water,"],[64,155.9,4.04,"and the semi-solid brown mass is soon ready for excretion."],[64,160.24,2.96,"But of course, it doesn’t always go this way."],[64,163.53,4.3,"Our bodies have a hard time processing certain pigments."],[64,169.12,4.13,"Red beets, for example, are packed with betanins—"],[64,173.38,5.59,"large, pigmented molecules that our bodies only absorb about half the time,"],[64,179.38,2.38,"leading to red-tinged waste."],[64,182.01,4.21,"Indeed, loading up on any one kind of colorful food"],[64,186.22,4.96,"can overwhelm the usual processes that degrade and absorb the pigment,"],[64,191.19,2.5,"resulting in poos of different hues."],[64,193.86,5.46,"Meanwhile, certain medicines that treat upset stomachs may contain bismuth,"],[64,199.4,5.34,"which reacts with sulfur in the digestive tract to form bismuth sulfide,"],[64,204.78,2.13,"turning poop pitch-black."],[64,207.2,4.05,"None of these color shift scenarios are cause for concern,"],[64,211.25,3.13,"but some changes in poop characteristics"],[64,214.38,2.88,"can indicate when something’s not quite right."],[64,217.71,5.8,"Ideally, poop is brown, semi-soft, sausage shaped, and easy to pass."],[64,223.8,4.55,"Constipation can be a sign of dehydration, insufficient fiber,"],[64,228.56,2.63,"or another digestive disruption."],[64,231.48,2.29,"And diarrhea might be greenish"],[64,233.77,3.54,"because it traversed the digestive tract too quickly"],[64,237.32,4.13,"for bacteria to transform bilirubin into stercobilin."],[64,241.61,4.55,"This might happen because of disturbances including food intolerances,"],[64,246.16,3.29,"infections, and inflammatory diseases."],[64,249.75,3.84,"Yellow, smelly, floating poops could mean the pancreas"],[64,253.58,4.46,"isn’t producing all the digestive enzymes needed to break down fats"],[64,258.05,1.83,"in the small intestine."],[64,260.13,5.3,"Pale poop may come to pass when bile isn't entering the small intestine,"],[64,265.43,3.75,"suggesting a liver, gallbladder, or pancreas problem."],[64,269.6,4.25,"And red or black poop can indicate internal bleeding"],[64,273.85,3.54,"from any number of intestinal abnormalities."],[64,278.32,3.54,"Considering how small changes in diet and lifestyle"],[64,281.86,2.67,"can cause big shifts in bowel movements,"],[64,284.65,4.42,"a couple days of colorful poops, constipation, or diarrhea"],[64,289.08,1.63,"are generally benign."],[64,291.12,4.88,"But it’s worth monitoring the situation in case things don’t return to normal"],[64,296.0,1.29,"within a week."],[64,297.29,4.13,"In those scenarios, it's a good idea to get a doctor on the case."],[64,301.8,5.67,"Blood, discomfort, and persistent constipation, diarrhea, narrow poops,"],[64,307.51,2.88,"or lack of relief from bowel movements"],[64,310.39,3.21,"can indicate more serious digestive issues."],[64,314.14,5.63,"This includes blockages, inflammation, bowel diseases, and cancers."],[64,320.11,3.8,"Your bowels will only benefit from early intervention."],[64,324.19,4.21,"So, don’t be afraid to take to the toilet, face your feces,"],[64,328.41,1.54,"and examine your excrement."],[64,330.12,1.71,"There’s nothing to be ashamed of—"],[64,331.87,5.05,"but lots to learn about the colorful choreography going on inside."],[65,6.96,2.25,"Faced with the realities of current crises,"],[65,9.21,4.25,"it's easy to assume our world is headed in a bleak direction."],[65,13.8,2.59,"But there’s good reason to be hopeful—"],[65,16.51,2.5,"with developments in science and technology,"],[65,19.02,3.67,"a positive future is more viable than ever before."],[65,22.89,2.88,"So, what if the future was awesome?"],[65,26.69,3.8,"Let’s explore what the future of three cities could look like,"],[65,30.53,2.67,"if we harness the tools we already have at our disposal"],[65,33.28,2.0,"to solve today's problems."],[65,35.53,2.59,"These visions are inspired by solarpunk,"],[65,38.12,2.96,"an art and social movement that imagines sustainable worlds"],[65,41.08,4.34,"in which humans, nature, and technology exist in harmony."],[65,45.88,1.92,"It’s 2125."],[65,48.09,2.79,"In the past, Los Angeles was running out of water"],[65,50.88,3.67,"and its citizens were dependent on 5 million cars."],[65,54.72,6.26,"Today, people glide down highway 101 on electric bikes, buses, and trains."],[65,61.06,2.04,"There’s no fee for these services—"],[65,63.1,3.17,"instead, folks contribute their technical or gardening skills,"],[65,66.27,3.21,"or provide entertainment for others as they go."],[65,70.15,4.88,"Year-round sunlight makes LA well-suited to solar infrastructure,"],[65,75.24,2.13,"but water is still limited."],[65,77.57,4.96,"City dwellers manage this by maintaining water-efficient farms in high rises,"],[65,82.54,5.88,"and within the 17 million square meters of land that once housed parking spaces."],[65,88.67,4.34,"Here, students and scientists mist local fruit, vegetables, and grains"],[65,93.0,1.04,"with nutrients."],[65,94.22,4.34,"They also create portable farms, stacking layers of plant and fertilizer"],[65,98.55,2.63,"into temperature-controlled shipping containers"],[65,101.18,3.59,"that can be transported out to city residents and beyond."],[65,105.23,4.46,"In Lagos, Nigeria, the city’s coast is completely transformed."],[65,109.94,2.79,"In the past, sand mining and urban expansion"],[65,112.73,2.92,"eroded roughly 84% of the shoreline,"],[65,115.65,3.25,"which put the city at risk of catastrophic flooding."],[65,119.61,3.29,"But the city responded by restoring the wetlands,"],[65,122.91,2.5,"which now act as natural sponges."],[65,125.54,3.13,"An intricate array of pipes, equipped with turbines,"],[65,128.67,4.13,"capture the energy of flowing water and convert it into electricity."],[65,133.42,3.59,"This powers buildings and services all over Lagos,"],[65,137.13,2.21,"like the city’s famous Balogun market,"],[65,139.34,3.63,"where people come together to trade, socialize, and entertain."],[65,143.35,4.34,"Along the coast, people steward thousands of native mangrove plants,"],[65,147.85,1.58,"which act as carbon sinks,"],[65,149.56,3.92,"clean up the pollutants of the past, and support biodiversity."],[65,154.07,3.29,"This area celebrates the mangrove in many forms—"],[65,157.49,3.29,"local designers use its tannins to dye fabrics,"],[65,160.95,2.96,"while scientists research its pharmaceutical potential"],[65,163.95,2.71,"and its uses as a natural pesticide."],[65,167.29,4.13,"Further out to sea, people embrace sustainable fishing practices"],[65,171.42,2.46,"and repurpose fish processing waste"],[65,173.88,3.54,"to power ships, floating farms, and public housing."],[65,177.63,4.67,"Meanwhile, São Paulo, Brazil has fully reimagined housing."],[65,182.8,3.75,"In its industrial past, the city battled heavy pollution,"],[65,186.64,3.92,"and up to a third of the population lived in unsanitary conditions."],[65,190.77,3.46,"But today, homes, schools, local government buildings,"],[65,194.23,3.63,"and entertainment spaces are made from repurposed materials"],[65,197.86,1.92,"from the former auto industry."],[65,200.03,4.92,"These buildings generate their own solar power and capture rainwater to reuse."],[65,205.24,3.21,"At the center sits a new solar football stadium,"],[65,208.54,2.84,"which generates energy and doubles as an incubator"],[65,211.37,1.54,"for growing native plants."],[65,213.38,2.75,"In areas where the land remains toxic,"],[65,216.21,3.96,"citizen scientists are stewarding thousands of mushrooms."],[65,220.34,3.5,"These mushrooms break down the bonds in the decades-old chemicals"],[65,223.84,2.25,"which remain in soil long after the burning"],[65,226.1,2.17,"of oil, gas, and household garbage."],[65,228.52,2.04,"They then produce new spores,"],[65,230.56,3.67,"attracting insects and birds to the area once again."],[65,234.6,4.67,"These versions of LA, Lagos, and São Paulo embody the principle of life"],[65,239.28,3.96,"enmeshed with the promises and limitations of its surroundings."],[65,243.57,2.59,"And while this future may seem far from reach,"],[65,246.16,4.5,"it is achievable, and it might not even take us 100 years to get there."],[65,250.91,4.13,"All over the world, communities are embracing these and similar ideas"],[65,255.04,2.67,"to build more sustainable systems."],[65,257.96,4.84,"In Vancouver, Indigenous First Nations are building affordable, net-zero"],[65,262.8,4.05,"housing projects incorporating green spaces and bike infrastructure."],[65,267.05,3.71,"In Singapore, urban farms flourish on skyscrapers"],[65,270.77,2.04,"and in the shells of former prisons."],[65,272.98,4.92,"In Prague, urban beekeeping takes flight in hotels and theaters."],[65,278.27,3.34,"Optimism can be a powerful mobilizing force."],[65,281.69,2.67,"From natural technologies to renewable energy,"],[65,284.36,3.71,"we have so many transformative tools at our disposal."],[65,288.41,3.0,"All we need is the will to implement them."],[66,7.21,2.92,"Long ago, on the shores of southern Japan,"],[66,10.13,3.09,"a temple was built near the site of a naval battle"],[66,13.38,3.54,"that concluded a long and gruesome conflict."],[66,17.47,3.21,"The Genpei Wars, fought in the 12th century"],[66,20.68,5.0,"between the rebellious Minamoto warriors and the ruling Taira clan,"],[66,25.9,2.17,"had taken countless lives."],[66,28.23,3.46,"And for centuries after, performers throughout the country"],[66,31.69,4.8,"honored this tragic past in beautiful, haunting performances."],[66,36.66,3.67,"But legend has it the most skilled storyteller of all"],[66,40.54,2.34,"is a monk from this very temple."],[66,44.21,2.46,"Hōichi was a biwa-hōshi—"],[66,46.79,2.88,"a blind performer who sang songs of battle"],[66,49.84,2.54,"while playing an instrument called the biwa."],[66,52.8,1.88,"When he told the temple's tale,"],[66,54.84,4.21,"his voice rose and fell like soldiers in battle,"],[66,59.39,5.46,"his instrument becoming one with the crashing waves and crashing blades."],[66,66.19,3.38,"One night, a samurai pounded at the temple doors,"],[66,69.69,2.71,"demanding the biwa-hōshi’s presence."],[66,72.86,1.33,"When Hōichi answered,"],[66,74.19,3.84,"the samurai said he'd been sent to bring the monk to his master,"],[66,78.41,3.71,"a nobleman eager to hear Hōichi’s performance."],[66,82.45,2.38,"The monk agreed to follow the samurai."],[66,85.54,5.09,"However, as they walked, the ground seemed to twist and turn beneath his feet."],[66,90.92,4.09,"Hōichi lost all sense of where and how long he had traveled,"],[66,95.42,3.25,"when suddenly, they arrived at an imposing building."],[66,100.43,1.42,"Stepping into the hall,"],[66,101.89,3.96,"Hōichi felt countless eyes watching him from the shadows."],[66,105.85,3.38,"Then a voice boomed from the depths of the room,"],[66,109.23,5.13,"demanding he recount the tale of the Taira clan's horrific end."],[66,114.99,1.38,"Hōichi trembled."],[66,116.49,3.63,"But then he cleared his throat and plucked his first note."],[66,120.74,3.92,"He sang of a war so terrible it had torn Japan apart."],[66,125.5,2.04,"He sang of the Minamoto clan,"],[66,127.71,4.13,"who staged a bloodthirsty rebellion against the Taira."],[66,132.5,4.67,"He sang about the endless battles, and how as the Taira’s losses mounted,"],[66,137.3,3.5,"they fled to the southern shores to protect their emperor—"],[66,140.97,2.42,"a child only six years old."],[66,144.06,2.29,"Finally, he sang of their defeat."],[66,146.85,4.0,"How when they were trapped and overwhelmed by their rival’s forces,"],[66,151.06,5.46,"the emperor’s grandmother threw herself and her grandson into the sea."],[66,157.82,3.88,"When Hōichi finished, sounds of weeping filled the air."],[66,162.66,2.09,"The nobleman's voice rang out,"],[66,164.78,5.96,"commanding Hōichi to return and retell his tale for six more nights."],[66,171.0,2.88,"In exchange, he would be rewarded handsomely—"],[66,174.17,2.96,"so long as he kept those visits secret."],[66,177.67,1.13,"The monk agreed,"],[66,178.8,4.71,"and for several nights Hōichi walked with the samurai through the shadows."],[66,183.55,3.13,"While he said nothing of this to the other monks,"],[66,186.85,4.05,"one evening the head priest noticed Hōichi’s absence."],[66,190.98,3.09,"He ordered his servants to watch the young musician,"],[66,194.06,3.42,"and that night they saw Hōichi rise from his bed"],[66,197.49,2.92,"and creep outside the temple walls."],[66,200.66,4.21,"To their astonishment, he settled in the nearby graveyard"],[66,205.24,5.8,"and began performing while surrounded by floating balls of ghostly fire."],[66,212.67,3.38,"The terrified servants dragged Hōichi to the head priest,"],[66,216.21,4.17,"and upon hearing their story, their leader was filled with dread."],[66,220.88,5.3,"He announced that Hōichi had been summoned by the ghosts of the Taira themselves."],[66,226.43,3.63,"And if he appeared before them again, he would surely perish."],[66,230.35,1.96,"To protect the biwa-hōshi,"],[66,232.48,4.46,"the head priest painted Buddhist sutras all over Hōichi’s body"],[66,236.94,2.63,"as a form of mystical protection."],[66,239.86,3.84,"He also told the monk that no matter who he encountered that night,"],[66,243.7,3.67,"he must remain completely still and silent."],[66,247.66,5.0,"That evening, when the phantom samurai arrived to collect Hōichi,"],[66,252.66,4.92,"the sutras rendered the monk’s body invisible to the specter."],[66,257.71,3.84,"The samurai was about to lash out in a furious rage"],[66,261.55,2.71,"when he spotted two floating ears."],[66,264.3,4.46,"In his haste, the head priest had forgotten to protect Hōichi’s ears,"],[66,268.76,1.88,"and screeching with anger,"],[66,270.64,3.25,"the samurai cut them from the monk's body."],[66,273.98,4.67,"But despite the pain, Hōichi remained still and silent."],[66,278.94,5.55,"The biwa-hōshi’s blood-curdling experience became known far and wide."],[66,284.7,4.09,"People rushed to hear him recount the trials of the Taira"],[66,288.78,2.17,"and his encounter with their souls."],[66,291.04,3.42,"And while Hōichi was never visited by ghosts again,"],[66,294.46,4.92,"his performances reminded listeners of their sacrifices and suffering."],[66,300.13,5.17,"Today, Hōichi’s tale is one of Japan’s best-known ghost stories."],[66,305.51,5.76,"His statue can be found among stones commemorating the fallen Taira warriors,"],[66,311.39,2.59,"reminding us to both honor the past"],[66,313.98,3.88,"and avoid getting trapped in the cycles of history."],[67,4.27,3.1,"Angus Hervey: President Osmani, thank you so much for joining us."],[67,7.41,2.54,"Vjosa Osmani Sadriu: Thank you so much for having me."],[67,9.94,1.17,"It's a great honor."],[67,11.14,3.1,"AH: You've just left office under circumstances you didn't choose,"],[67,14.28,1.17,"and you're not alone."],[67,15.48,3.37,"Democracy right now looks like it's losing ground everywhere"],[67,18.88,3.54,"against populism, against cynicism, against sheer exhaustion."],[67,22.45,2.54,"What lessons has this experience taught you"],[67,25.02,3.47,"about what it actually takes to build and sustain democracy?"],[67,29.63,2.97,"VOS: I'd like to say that the lessons that I've learned"],[67,32.63,2.9,"don't only belong to my mandate as president."],[67,36.4,1.94,"I continue to learn as we speak."],[67,38.37,3.14,"I learned a lot today by listening to the previous speakers,"],[67,41.51,5.27,"and I learned a lot throughout my life, growing up as a child of war,"],[67,46.81,2.37,"as you mentioned at the introduction."],[67,49.21,2.44,"We grew up together with all the children of Kosovo"],[67,51.68,1.4,"under a system of oppression,"],[67,53.08,4.34,"which started as an apartheid-like regime"],[67,57.45,2.64,"and ended as a genocidal war."],[67,60.42,1.64,"Growing up in that system"],[67,62.09,2.3,"where everything is taken away from you,"],[67,64.43,3.14,"from your childhood to the right to go to school,"],[67,67.6,2.8,"to your parents' right to go to work,"],[67,70.43,1.64,"at that time, just, you know,"],[67,72.1,5.74,"going out and playing as children meant that it was an act of quiet resistance."],[67,78.28,5.37,"But at the same time, as a nation that today is thriving in many senses,"],[67,83.65,1.8,"but especially in terms of democracy,"],[67,85.48,3.07,"despite crises that we're facing right now,"],[67,88.59,2.4,"we've learned that oftentimes"],[67,91.02,4.84,"the line that stands between a child of war"],[67,95.89,2.07,"and a leader of peace"],[67,97.96,1.47,"is an opportunity."],[67,100.43,1.9,"The opportunity to live,"],[67,102.37,2.57,"to survive, first and foremost,"],[67,104.97,1.77,"and then the opportunity to thrive."],[67,106.77,6.44,"And this opportunity was given to us by people who believe in humanity,"],[67,113.21,6.04,"people who never gave up on human values, even against all odds,"],[67,119.28,3.37,"whether it was political and military leaders who came to our rescue"],[67,122.69,2.17,"and did not turn a blind eye on our suffering"],[67,124.89,1.3,"and came to save our lives,"],[67,126.22,1.67,"democracies around the world"],[67,127.92,3.87,"who joined to save those values of freedom and human rights or,"],[67,131.79,3.2,"you know, leaders in churches and mosques"],[67,135.03,2.0,"who opened their hearts and minds"],[67,137.07,2.74,"and joined communities together"],[67,139.84,2.84,"so that we can have some humanitarian support."],[67,143.14,4.57,"And practically, it's this that shaped me"],[67,147.71,3.04,"to the leader that I was for the past five years,"],[67,150.78,1.2,"a leader for peace,"],[67,152.02,2.57,"because I knew that while I was leading my country,"],[67,154.62,2.24,"no matter how small my country is,"],[67,156.85,3.0,"we could have a powerful voice in the world."],[67,160.19,5.91,"That voice that is going to speak up against oppression,"],[67,166.13,1.8,"against autocracies,"],[67,167.97,2.5,"against genocidal regimes,"],[67,170.5,3.2,"and make sure that we become the voice of that child"],[67,173.74,3.14,"who today is standing on the line,"],[67,176.91,1.4,"on the line to survive,"],[67,178.34,2.77,"but also in the line for food to survive"],[67,181.15,2.74,"because we were there --"],[67,183.91,1.64,"I mean, a few decades ago."],[67,185.58,2.27,"And today we are given the opportunity"],[67,187.85,2.17,"to give that contribution for peace."],[67,190.05,5.57,"These past five years have not just been years of leading my country"],[67,195.66,1.33,"defending democracy,"],[67,197.03,1.37,"but at the same time,"],[67,198.43,4.04,"speaking for those who don't have a voice right now."],[67,202.5,2.44,"Because once we did not have a voice,"],[67,204.97,2.47,"and there were people around the world,"],[67,207.44,3.57,"whether political leaders or leaders in their communities"],[67,211.04,3.37,"or just ordinary people who joined to help us,"],[67,214.44,3.07,"who became our voice when we did not have one."],[67,217.55,2.17,"So that's the lesson that I've learned,"],[67,219.72,3.37,"that no matter how small you are as a country,"],[67,223.12,3.0,"it is not just the political or military"],[67,226.12,3.6,"or economic might that defines your strength."],[67,230.09,3.17,"I think after all, it's the strength of your story."],[67,233.26,2.6,"You just need to have the courage to tell it."],[67,237.2,1.2,"AH: Kosovo --"],[67,241.44,2.6,"is a tiny country -- you just said this."],[67,244.07,1.47,"Fewer than two million people."],[67,245.54,1.5,"But under your presidency,"],[67,247.08,3.24,"it secured visa-free travel to Europe and applied for EU membership."],[67,250.31,3.34,"So you've been in the rooms where those big decisions get made."],[67,253.68,3.4,"You have an insider’s perspective that none of us know"],[67,257.12,1.33,"or have access to."],[67,258.49,2.64,"What do you see"],[67,261.16,3.44,"when you watch the world's geopolitical tensions playing out right now?"],[67,266.5,3.34,"VOS: I see a lot of devastation, obviously."],[67,269.83,4.37,"We're all seeing how there are too many conflicts, too many wars,"],[67,274.24,2.37,"too much suffering happening right now."],[67,276.64,1.24,"But at the same time,"],[67,277.91,1.94,"when I sit around those rooms"],[67,279.88,3.77,"and join world leaders where these decisions are made,"],[67,283.68,1.64,"we try to make an impact."],[67,285.35,2.57,"We try to make an impact by sharing our story"],[67,287.95,2.4,"because, as I said,"],[67,290.39,4.5,"politics on one side or geopolitics, if we're trying to talk bigger,"],[67,294.89,3.64,"it's not just about military and economic might,"],[67,298.56,2.4,"it's about doing things right."],[67,301.37,2.77,"And we've heard, and we're hearing nowadays,"],[67,304.17,2.17,"so many stories about suffering,"],[67,306.37,2.34,"about pain, about things going wrong,"],[67,308.74,1.94,"about things being destroyed."],[67,311.07,1.2,"But at the same time,"],[67,312.28,2.94,"it is important that we share success stories,"],[67,315.25,2.57,"and Kosovo is one such success story."],[67,318.31,5.0,"It shows what democracies can achieve when they stand together for values,"],[67,323.35,2.17,"when they stand together in defense of freedom"],[67,325.56,1.23,"and against autocracies"],[67,326.82,4.27,"and against some of the crazy, genocidal minds"],[67,331.13,2.47,"that we had to live under."],[67,334.0,1.17,"And at the same time,"],[67,335.17,4.34,"it shows that even when you live under oppression,"],[67,339.54,1.4,"there's nothing,"],[67,340.97,4.64,"nothing that can stand in the way of a people that wants to be free."],[67,346.34,1.27,"It takes years --"],[67,351.58,2.84,"It takes years, it takes decades."],[67,354.45,3.0,"In the case of our nation, it took centuries."],[67,357.82,5.17,"We had to live occupied and under oppression for century after century."],[67,363.03,2.94,"But we never gave up on our identity."],[67,366.0,2.7,"We never gave up on our quest for freedom."],[67,368.7,2.23,"And ultimately, we became free,"],[67,370.97,3.84,"and just sharing that story of success"],[67,374.84,1.77,"is so important in these rooms"],[67,376.64,3.84,"in order to also change mindsets among countries"],[67,380.48,1.87,"that are so much bigger than ours"],[67,382.38,2.94,"and to also convince them to act,"],[67,385.35,2.0,"to make sure that they save lives,"],[67,387.38,3.4,"to make sure that they put values in front of everything else."],[67,390.82,3.6,"Because sometimes standing on the right side of history,"],[67,394.46,2.04,"it's the most difficult decision."],[67,396.53,2.1,"It’s not convenient for the moment,"],[67,398.66,2.84,"but long-term, it is what humanity needs right now."],[67,401.53,2.8,"So it's very important for us to be in those rooms,"],[67,404.37,2.54,"to raise our voices, to speak up,"],[67,406.94,2.27,"but also to be heard and to have an impact"],[67,409.24,3.2,"on how these decisions are made today"],[67,412.48,4.07,"that may not impact necessarily my country very much,"],[67,416.58,3.34,"but they do impact humanity at the much larger level."],[67,419.92,3.67,"And we need to make sure that we are part of those decisions."],[67,424.09,2.97,"AH: OK, I want to pick up on something you said there,"],[67,427.09,1.77,"which is about being in those rooms"],[67,428.89,2.77,"and when you have a decision that conflicts,"],[67,431.69,2.74,"what do you do when your own personal moral values"],[67,434.46,3.77,"rub up against a strategic geopolitical decision that you need to make?"],[67,438.24,3.4,"How do you figure out which way to go?"],[67,442.27,1.7,"VOS: You fight, that's what you do."],[67,443.97,1.17,"You have to fight."],[67,445.18,2.57,"You have to fight your way through"],[67,447.78,5.61,"and try to use a very dynamic diplomacy"],[67,453.42,4.67,"to convince other leaders to find a way that can protect both,"],[67,458.12,2.87,"that can protect moral values on one side,"],[67,461.02,1.57,"but at the same time,"],[67,462.63,2.57,"make sure that enough countries think"],[67,465.23,2.44,"that that is also strategically in their interest,"],[67,467.7,2.54,"so that these decisions can be made"],[67,470.27,3.57,"in a way that can better the societies and better human values"],[67,473.87,1.6,"and protect human values."],[67,475.51,2.23,"It’s not easy, and we don’t always win."],[67,477.74,1.2,"We don't always win."],[67,478.94,1.17,"There are times"],[67,480.14,3.54,"when not necessarily moral values are the ones who win."],[67,483.71,2.4,"It's other interests that come beforehand."],[67,486.15,2.97,"But that doesn't mean that we have to stop."],[67,489.15,1.84,"And when this happens,"],[67,491.02,3.47,"I'm always reminded of the resilience of my nation,"],[67,494.52,2.23,"the resilience that our people showed,"],[67,496.79,3.3,"because resilience oftentimes is not just about surviving."],[67,500.13,4.94,"It's also about thriving after you're given the chance to survive."],[67,505.1,1.8,"And this is the story of Kosovo."],[67,506.94,2.64,"It's a story of survivors who thrive."],[67,509.91,1.3,"It's about not giving up."],[67,511.24,3.37,"It's about continuing to fight because you know how much is at stake."],[67,514.64,4.24,"And you know how many people around the world have their hopes"],[67,518.92,3.0,"on those decisions that are made in those rooms."],[67,521.95,2.5,"And you know, when I would visit the United Nations"],[67,524.49,2.1,"or when I would visit the European Parliament"],[67,526.62,1.3,"and other institutions,"],[67,527.92,4.54,"I shared with leaders how, as a child, we were sheltering in mountains,"],[67,532.5,2.57,"we were sheltering from the shelling and the bombing"],[67,535.07,2.44,"and the people who were trying to come and kill us."],[67,537.5,4.87,"And we had that little radio with batteries,"],[67,542.41,4.07,"and we were listening to these meetings and these speeches,"],[67,546.48,5.0,"hoping, hoping that someone would come and save our lives."],[67,552.01,4.44,"And it's so important, when I sit in that chair,"],[67,556.45,2.9,"when I sit in those big meeting rooms with leaders,"],[67,559.39,1.8,"that I never forget that."],[67,561.22,4.3,"That there is a child, there are millions of children around the world"],[67,565.53,2.6,"who are now listening to today's leaders,"],[67,568.16,5.07,"hoping that someone would not turn a blind eye on their suffering."],[67,573.24,4.11,"And it's about continuously, continuously fighting and never giving up."],[67,579.54,1.67,"AH: OK, we're at TED."],[67,581.24,3.47,"So it wouldn't be TED if we didn't ask a question about AI."],[67,586.45,3.6,"We hear a lot these days about what AI might do to society,"],[67,590.09,2.27,"but for you, these questions aren't theoretical."],[67,592.39,3.47,"I mean, they are a very real fight over truth."],[67,595.89,2.54,"What's been your own personal experience of this?"],[67,600.63,2.6,"VOS: It hasn't been a good one, I have to say this,"],[67,603.27,2.74,"and especially if we're looking at the past couple of days"],[67,606.0,3.27,"after I finished my mandate, which I just finished."],[67,609.27,2.6,"Actually, this is my first international activity"],[67,611.88,3.9,"after finishing my mandate as the sixth president of Kosovo."],[67,616.25,2.84,"But it was important for me to not miss this one."],[67,621.05,4.14,"It hasn't been a good one because in politics,"],[67,625.19,2.94,"obviously you have to be prepared for everything."],[67,628.12,3.5,"But we're living at a time when they can clone your voice,"],[67,631.66,2.13,"clone videos of you,"],[67,633.83,3.9,"make pictures of you that never actually took place,"],[67,637.77,1.64,"of meetings that never took place,"],[67,639.44,2.37,"of conversations that never took place."],[67,641.84,3.1,"And this changes the entire narrative,"],[67,644.98,2.3,"because, look, I honestly believe"],[67,647.31,4.34,"that the truth is the real oxygen for democracy."],[67,652.05,3.64,"So when you're undermining truth,"],[67,655.72,2.47,"you're undermining democracy itself,"],[67,658.22,1.53,"which obviously is not perfect."],[67,659.79,3.4,"It can be messy, it can be painfully slow sometimes."],[67,663.23,2.37,"But the opposite of it is destruction,"],[67,665.63,1.5,"is pain, is suffering."],[67,667.16,2.1,"And we've lived the opposite of democracy,"],[67,669.3,2.94,"which is why we're so resilient in protecting it."],[67,672.27,5.71,"And it requires a day-after-day protection and fight to keep it alive."],[67,678.01,5.34,"So I think the way how AI is abused nowadays"],[67,683.38,4.34,"is extremely dangerous for democracies worldwide,"],[67,687.72,4.4,"which is why it requires education so that we have people"],[67,692.15,4.94,"who are digitally prepared to differentiate"],[67,697.13,3.67,"between what is, you know, made through AI"],[67,700.83,1.9,"and what is actually true"],[67,702.77,4.17,"for the people who are involved in decision-making in our daily lives."],[67,706.94,4.37,"And secondly, it requires systems that don't necessarily overregulate"],[67,711.34,2.37,"so that they can block the use of AI"],[67,713.74,2.47,"for the betterment of society and humanity,"],[67,716.25,2.2,"whether it's in medicine or in saving lives"],[67,718.48,2.17,"or doing much of the stuff"],[67,720.68,2.8,"that we've heard from the previous speakers today."],[67,723.49,1.2,"But at the same time,"],[67,724.72,2.57,"it allows people to defend truth"],[67,727.32,3.77,"because if we don't find instruments that will defend truth,"],[67,731.09,4.87,"ultimately, everything that we can use AI for,"],[67,735.97,1.37,"which is good,"],[67,737.37,1.44,"will be diminished,"],[67,738.84,3.6,"in terms of their impact"],[67,742.44,2.23,"if we do not defend truth."],[67,744.71,2.7,"And especially in democracies, this is crucial."],[67,747.44,4.67,"Because if propaganda is going to overcome truth,"],[67,752.15,3.24,"we're going to have populism, we're going to have extremism."],[67,755.42,2.37,"We're not going to have the human values"],[67,757.82,3.84,"which kept us together during the most difficult times prevail,"],[67,761.69,3.3,"but we're going to have lies that can prevail."],[67,765.03,2.24,"And that is very easy in the digital age,"],[67,767.3,4.27,"unless we invest in systems that can educate people"],[67,771.6,3.77,"to differentiate between what was actually made artificially"],[67,775.4,5.41,"and what the true story of the people that are going to represent you is."],[67,787.82,1.74,"AH: You're a Muslim,"],[67,789.55,1.83,"but you recently went back to a church"],[67,791.42,2.2,"where your family was sheltered during the war,"],[67,793.62,2.07,"and the pastor who hid you was there."],[67,795.73,2.94,"You've also just received the Bonifacio VIII prize"],[67,798.66,1.2,"at the Vatican,"],[67,799.9,3.2,"which was an award previously given to Pope John Paul II."],[67,803.13,2.87,"What does that tell us about what's still possible?"],[67,807.94,4.8,"VOS: I think it tells us that we should not let hate prevail."],[67,813.98,1.94,"As I said, we’ve lived under oppression,"],[67,815.95,2.3,"and we went through a genocidal war."],[67,818.28,4.1,"In Kosovo, the biggest massacre that was committed by the Milošević regime"],[67,822.42,2.5,"was a massacre against Catholics."],[67,824.92,3.2,"But then if you look at every other village, every other town,"],[67,828.16,1.17,"they killed Muslims."],[67,829.36,3.54,"So they killed everyone, no matter their religion,"],[67,832.93,3.7,"because it wasn't about religious differences."],[67,836.67,2.94,"It was about human differences"],[67,839.64,3.57,"in the sense that you either believe in human values or not."],[67,843.24,3.67,"You either believe in the right of someone else to live,"],[67,846.94,2.47,"whether they pick one religion or not."],[67,850.15,3.17,"And in Kosovo, we've seen how these human values prevailed."],[67,853.35,3.87,"We've seen cases where mosques sheltered Catholics,"],[67,857.25,3.8,"and we've seen cases where churches sheltered Muslims."],[67,861.09,4.2,"And yes, my family and I were Muslim,"],[67,865.33,2.8,"but we were sheltered in a church when, you know,"],[67,868.16,2.77,"the Milošević regime was trying to kill us."],[67,870.97,1.17,"And just a few days ago,"],[67,872.17,3.3,"I participated in the funeral of a pastor,"],[67,875.5,1.64,"Dom Kelmend Spaqi,"],[67,877.17,3.7,"who sheltered hundreds and hundreds of civilians"],[67,880.91,1.17,"of different religions."],[67,882.11,2.77,"And when the Serbian police went to his church"],[67,884.91,2.97,"and asked him to give up on those people,"],[67,887.88,2.17,"he said, \"No, if you're going to kill someone,"],[67,890.09,1.97,"you have to kill me first.\""],[67,892.09,4.04,"And he stood between those police officers"],[67,896.16,1.94,"of that genocidal regime"],[67,898.13,3.14,"and civilians of different religions on the other side,"],[67,901.26,2.64,"showing that an act of courage,"],[67,903.93,3.04,"an act of humanity is bigger than any oppressor,"],[67,907.0,1.77,"is bigger than any regime."],[67,908.8,1.33,"And for us as a nation,"],[67,910.17,3.17,"this has been a value that we've defended for centuries."],[67,913.38,3.37,"Even during World War II,"],[67,916.78,5.61,"our nation gave an example of what we call “besa.”"],[67,922.42,1.5,"It's a code of promise."],[67,923.95,2.13,"When you promise to protect someone,"],[67,926.12,1.53,"you do it with your life."],[67,927.69,3.04,"And our nation is the only place on Earth"],[67,930.73,2.77,"where the number of Jews after World War II"],[67,933.5,3.7,"was higher after the war than before the war,"],[67,937.23,2.77,"because we protected them with our lives."],[67,942.74,4.67,"It's a case where we've shown not just tolerance,"],[67,947.44,4.04,"but respect for the values of each other,"],[67,951.51,2.3,"no matter which religion you belong to."],[67,953.85,1.67,"It's humanity that should prevail,"],[67,955.55,1.37,"it's respect for each other."],[67,956.95,2.1,"And today in Kosovo, you have families"],[67,959.09,3.24,"where one brother is a Catholic, the other one is a Muslim."],[67,962.36,4.17,"This has never differentiated us because we've put these values"],[67,966.56,3.64,"of respect and humanity before everything else."],[67,970.23,1.94,"And we're very, very proud of that."],[67,972.2,6.37,"And we hope to have these kind of values also in other countries,"],[67,978.61,2.54,"as I think it can save lives"],[67,981.18,3.57,"the same way that it has saved lives in Kosovo."],[67,986.52,1.67,"It hasn't divided us."],[67,988.22,2.8,"And I think"],[67,991.05,5.91,"it is important that we give that example to other people in the world as well."],[67,997.36,2.67,"AH: President Osmani, thank you for joining us."],[67,1000.06,2.64,"We know that this is a tumultuous time"],[67,1002.73,2.47,"and that the future is unclear and uncertain."],[67,1005.2,2.87,"But we're so grateful that you appeared here on the TED stage"],[67,1008.1,1.67,"and shared with us."],[67,1009.77,1.2,"VOS: Thank you so much."],[67,1011.34,1.13,"The future ..."],[67,1013.94,2.97,"It's important that we have a future."],[67,1016.91,3.34,"So many people around the world don't know whether they do."],[67,1020.25,3.54,"There's always that child that is still standing on the line"],[67,1023.82,2.24,"and waiting for that opportunity."],[67,1026.06,4.07,"And let’s not forget that that child of war today,"],[67,1030.16,4.17,"whether in Ukraine or in Gaza or somewhere in Africa,"],[67,1034.36,2.04,"can be the leader of peace tomorrow"],[67,1036.43,2.8,"if we together give them that opportunity."],[67,1040.6,1.17,"Thank you."],[68,1.6,2.56,"60 million tonnes of snow"],[68,4.16,2.72,"now blanket this herd's territory."],[68,10.28,3.36,"Pushing through deep snow is exhausting work,"],[68,13.64,3.32,"and the bison are now slowly starving."],[68,23.28,4.04,"Just keeping warm saps huge amounts of energy."],[68,32.6,4.64,"Their thick coats can insulate them down to -20 Fahrenheit."],[68,38.0,2.84,"It's now -40."],[68,42.92,5.32,"The only thing that will keep them alive is buried beneath three feet of snow."],[68,53.2,3.8,"And that's a problem shared with a surprising neighbour."],[68,74.56,4.28,"The food the fox seeks is also deep beneath the snow."],[68,90.56,5.16,"The survival of both creatures depends on getting through to the ground."],[68,100.8,4.16,"For the bison, it will be a matter of brute strength."],[68,109.08,5.44,"Massive neck muscles enable them to shovel five tonnes of snow a day."],[68,120.28,3.72,"Their lightweight neighbour needs more precision."],[68,150.44,3.08,"The bison have reached their goal –"],[68,153.52,2.64,"a mouthful of withered grass."],[68,159.8,2.08,"And where the bison have dug,"],[68,161.88,2.68,"the fox now spots an opportunity."],[68,173.2,2.6,"Every footstep counts."],[68,178.16,3.36,"But he mustn't break through… yet."],[68,185.12,3.64,"He listens carefully to pinpoint his target."],[68,194.32,2.36,"It's moving."],[68,216.8,1.64,"A vole."],[68,220.48,3.36,"Small, but 100 times more nutritious"],[68,223.84,2.4,"than a mouthful of dried grass."],[68,232.56,2.56,"To get through the winter on these prairies,"],[68,235.12,3.92,"sometimes brain beats brawn."],[69,6.59,4.46,"You’re an astronaut orbiting 400 kilometers above Earth"],[69,11.05,3.75,"when suddenly an alert flashes across your display."],[69,15.01,5.38,"The system responsible for producing the breathable air on board has failed."],[69,20.68,4.05,"Now, you and your crewmates must work with mission control"],[69,24.77,4.3,"to identify the problem and fix it as quickly as possible."],[69,29.61,3.09,"This might seem like a scene from a sci-fi thriller,"],[69,32.7,3.25,"but for astronauts on the International Space Station,"],[69,36.07,3.71,"dealing with situations like this is a reality of life."],[69,40.12,2.13,"For over 25 years,"],[69,42.25,4.8,"ISS’s air supply systems have revolutionized space missions."],[69,47.09,4.09,"Yet the need for regular maintenance, repairs, and upgrades"],[69,51.17,6.51,"has pushed engineers to develop even more reliable systems for future spacecraft."],[69,61.35,3.92,"Creating breathable air in space has two requirements:"],[69,65.31,5.96,"supplying oxygen and removing exhaled carbon dioxide before it builds up."],[69,71.61,3.21,"Every astronaut in space needs, on average,"],[69,74.86,3.63,"about 0.8 kilograms of oxygen a day."],[69,78.74,3.04,"Most early space missions met this demand"],[69,81.79,3.92,"by storing oxygen on board in pressurized tanks."],[69,85.92,4.21,"For example, the eight-day Apollo 11 mission to the moon"],[69,90.13,1.79,"carried three astronauts,"],[69,92.09,4.42,"along with around 50 kilograms of oxygen for life support—"],[69,96.63,2.63,"more than enough for the crew to survive on."],[69,100.51,2.92,"Canisters containing lithium hydroxide"],[69,103.43,3.13,"would chemically react with carbon dioxide,"],[69,106.56,1.63,"scrubbing it from the cabin."],[69,108.6,4.21,"But the launch of the first space station marked a turning point."],[69,112.94,5.38,"For the first time, crews would remain in orbit for months, not days."],[69,118.53,2.75,"A typical 6 to 8 month expedition"],[69,121.41,5.3,"with seven crew members might require over a thousand kilograms of oxygen,"],[69,126.71,2.96,"demanding about 30 large tanks."],[69,129.83,5.46,"And constantly shipping this massive load to and from orbit would be expensive."],[69,135.59,2.5,"So, engineers developed a solution:"],[69,138.09,3.8,"using water and a process called electrolysis,"],[69,141.97,3.63,"a system that makes oxygen directly onboard."],[69,146.35,3.75,"Water is relatively easy to come by on the ISS"],[69,150.1,2.38,"thanks to its robust recovery system."],[69,152.9,3.17,"It captures nearly all moisture onboard—"],[69,156.19,4.25,"from sweat, exhalation, wash water, and even urine—"],[69,162.78,3.13,"and purifies it into fresh, potable water."],[69,166.45,3.34,"This clean water is routed to an electrolyzer,"],[69,169.92,4.88,"a device equipped with two electrodes, one positive and one negative."],[69,175.17,2.42,"When an electric current is applied,"],[69,177.59,5.84,"the electrolyzer breaks down water molecules into oxygen and hydrogen gases."],[69,183.8,2.79,"Oxygen collects near the positive electrode,"],[69,186.72,3.84,"and hydrogen gas bubbles form near the negative electrode."],[69,191.19,4.21,"But there’s a problem— getting these gases out of the water."],[69,195.69,5.59,"On Earth, bubbles naturally float to the top of denser liquids due to gravity."],[69,201.53,3.17,"But in orbit, where everything is in freefall,"],[69,204.95,2.63,"bubbles tend to cling to the electrodes."],[69,208.33,3.88,"One solution is to pump water through the electrolyzer,"],[69,212.25,2.67,"carrying the bubbles away to a separator."],[69,215.54,4.42,"Here, the gas-filled water is spun much like a washing machine"],[69,219.97,1.71,"in its final spin cycle,"],[69,221.97,4.13,"forcing the liquid outward while the gas collects in the center."],[69,226.47,3.54,"In the end, oxygen is released into the cabin."],[69,230.23,5.09,"Hydrogen gas is either vented into space or routed to a reactor,"],[69,235.52,3.54,"where it combines with captured exhaled carbon dioxide"],[69,239.11,1.96,"to make methane and water."],[69,241.61,2.46,"But this system isn't perfect."],[69,244.2,2.84,"The process for separating bubbles from water"],[69,247.03,5.63,"relies on many complex moving parts that are prone to breaking or malfunctioning."],[69,252.87,5.0,"And as space agencies set their sights on expeditions deeper into space,"],[69,257.88,3.0,"like year-long crewed trips to Mars,"],[69,261.09,3.34,"this approach becomes increasingly impractical."],[69,265.05,4.92,"So, researchers are exploring new ways to separate out this gas."],[69,270.35,1.67,"One promising solution"],[69,272.02,5.46,"is to spin the gas-filled water inside the electrolyzer using magnets."],[69,277.9,3.34,"This system takes advantage of a fundamental property"],[69,281.24,3.13,"of how electrons and magnets interact."],[69,284.61,3.84,"When negatively charged molecules move perpendicularly"],[69,288.45,1.67,"through a magnetic field,"],[69,290.24,5.05,"the field applies a force on the molecules called a Lorentz force."],[69,295.46,2.13,"It pushes them sideways,"],[69,297.58,4.75,"at a right angle to both the electric and magnetic fields."],[69,302.42,5.55,"So, by positioning the magnets and current path in just the right way,"],[69,308.18,3.25,"this Lorentz force can swirl the liquid,"],[69,311.43,3.67,"separating out the oxygen and hydrogen gases—"],[69,315.19,2.92,"no pump or separator needed."],[69,318.31,5.8,"This Magnetohydrodynamic Oxygen Generation Assembly, or MOGA,"],[69,324.11,1.79,"has no moving parts,"],[69,326.03,3.46,"so it requires less maintenance and replacement hardware,"],[69,329.62,1.88,"freeing up space onboard"],[69,331.49,4.42,"and allowing astronauts to spend more time on other endeavors."],[69,336.46,4.25,"MOGA is still in the research phase, but it holds promise."],[69,341.13,2.25,"With a more robust oxygen system,"],[69,343.46,4.09,"astronauts on future space stations and deep-space missions"],[69,347.63,2.92,"should be able to breathe a little easier."],[70,4.24,4.54,"On October 4, 1997,"],[70,8.81,4.77,"I walked into a room unlike any room I'd ever entered before."],[70,14.95,3.57,"People were standing up, sharing their stories."],[70,20.12,2.87,"A wave of relief washed over me."],[70,26.12,1.67,"I got a cup of coffee"],[70,29.19,1.83,"and just took everything in."],[70,33.43,1.8,"At that point,"],[70,35.27,2.27,"I was at the lowest point in my life."],[70,39.97,2.17,"I was out of ideas,"],[70,42.17,3.6,"and I didn't know how I was going to make it out of the condition I was in."],[70,48.28,2.97,"Before that date, my life had not been easy."],[70,52.88,3.27,"When I was 25 years old, I gave birth to a son."],[70,57.25,1.87,"We called him K.K."],[70,62.43,2.74,"The next thing that happened changed my life forever."],[70,66.53,1.57,"He was struck by a car."],[70,69.53,1.83,"That is the day I lost him."],[70,72.74,1.84,"At only five years old,"],[70,75.51,1.33,"he was gone."],[70,79.68,4.8,"His laughter, his love, his energy"],[70,84.52,1.57,"were all taken from me."],[70,91.39,2.37,"My life became a downward spiral."],[70,97.13,1.4,"I began to drink."],[70,100.0,2.0,"Drinking led to substance use."],[70,103.7,2.94,"For the substance use, I was imprisoned."],[70,110.31,1.9,"Every time I left prison,"],[70,112.91,1.8,"I swore I wouldn't go back."],[70,115.75,5.57,"But I did. Over and over and over again."],[70,122.12,3.87,"I remember one day, standing in front of a judge"],[70,125.99,3.7,"and asking that judge, \"Is there any help for me?\""],[70,131.96,3.0,"And the judge sentenced me to prison one more time."],[70,138.07,1.47,"No one cared."],[70,140.7,1.44,"No one was listening."],[70,143.71,3.3,"But when I walked into that room in 1997,"],[70,148.04,2.74,"I was ready to be heard."],[70,150.78,3.34,"In the rooms of Alcoholics Anonymous,"],[70,154.12,1.3,"they listened."],[70,158.66,2.33,"I took a deep breath and stood up,"],[70,162.36,3.44,"and said, \"Look what drugs and alcohol have done to me.\""],[70,168.26,2.9,"The immediate compassion,"],[70,171.2,2.13,"empathy and love"],[70,173.37,3.6,"that rolled off these complete strangers"],[70,177.01,3.34,"was enough to put a sizable dent"],[70,180.38,3.6,"in my pain, my shame,"],[70,184.01,3.54,"my guilt and all that sorrow."],[70,189.95,2.7,"I kept talking,"],[70,192.66,2.0,"and they kept listening."],[70,195.66,2.94,"I shared all the thoughts"],[70,198.63,2.97,"I had spent decades trying to silence."],[70,203.53,3.17,"As they listened, I began to heal."],[70,207.97,3.9,"Listening saved my life, and it opened a door."],[70,213.28,2.1,"I got sober in Santa Monica,"],[70,216.51,2.44,"an affluent part of Los Angeles."],[70,220.55,1.9,"And I noticed something there."],[70,223.75,1.74,"People in Santa Monica"],[70,226.76,3.37,"weren’t going to prison for substance use,"],[70,230.16,1.6,"for alcoholism."],[70,233.46,1.23,"And I thought,"],[70,236.7,2.94,"why didn't that judge give me a paper and say,"],[70,239.67,1.23,"\"Miss Burton,"],[70,242.21,1.53,"go to AA.”"],[70,246.01,2.37,"Was I not worth a piece of paper?"],[70,249.75,3.64,"Was I not worth direction toward a solution?"],[70,255.22,2.6,"So as I got stronger in my recovery,"],[70,258.96,2.64,"I thought about women just like me,"],[70,262.86,2.33,"cycling in and out of prison,"],[70,266.86,2.4,"looking for safety and not finding it."],[70,270.43,1.47,"So I got a job."],[70,273.87,1.83,"A minimum wage job."],[70,277.34,2.4,"And I saved $13,000."],[70,281.55,3.3,"And I took $3,000 of it and put down on a house."],[70,287.18,1.57,"And I bought some furniture."],[70,290.49,1.37,"Every morning I’d wake up,"],[70,291.89,2.13,"and I'd go down to the Greyhound bus station"],[70,294.06,2.13,"where women leaving prison were dropped off."],[70,297.53,2.84,"And I knew that station well"],[70,300.36,3.8,"because I had been dropped off there so many times myself."],[70,305.0,3.54,"And as they got off the bus carrying a trash bag of belongings"],[70,309.37,1.7,"and hope in their hearts,"],[70,312.51,3.0,"I would walk up to them and I'd say, \"Girl, I have a house."],[70,317.08,1.97,"And if you want to come live there,"],[70,320.05,1.77,"it's a safe place to be."],[70,322.79,1.37,"You can come live there.\""],[70,325.49,4.2,"And before long there were 11 of us living in that house."],[70,330.86,2.0,"We cooked together."],[70,332.86,2.44,"We went to meetings together."],[70,335.33,2.1,"We shared resources."],[70,338.54,1.87,"We laughed together."],[70,340.4,1.33,"We cried together."],[70,342.71,3.3,"But most of all, we healed together."],[70,347.61,3.5,"Before long, people saw what I was doing, and they joined me."],[70,352.25,1.83,"And we created a team."],[70,355.29,3.34,"Today, there [are] 12 houses across Los Angeles."],[70,363.36,1.5,"Thank you."],[70,368.0,3.9,"What we built was a house of women helping women."],[70,373.97,3.7,"Each house is staffed to support its residents"],[70,379.28,2.4,"who pay rent,"],[70,381.71,2.13,"do chores,"],[70,383.88,2.0,"buy groceries"],[70,385.92,3.64,"and learn to be accountable, responsible members of the community."],[70,391.32,1.74,"We help them find jobs,"],[70,394.66,2.17,"learn financial literacy"],[70,397.76,2.24,"and help them with any legal issues."],[70,401.6,4.41,"As a result, thousands of women have come back to the community"],[70,406.9,4.97,"and hundreds of mothers have been reunited with their children."],[70,413.54,2.9,"In 2017, I published my memoir,"],[70,417.21,2.17,"\"Becoming Ms. Burton:"],[70,419.42,2.27,"From Prison to Recovery"],[70,421.72,2.6,"to Leading the Fight for Incarcerated Women.\""],[70,425.62,4.8,"In order to tell my story, I held book talks all over the nation,"],[70,431.3,1.64,"behind prison walls."],[70,434.13,3.17,"There I would listen to women."],[70,438.57,3.44,"They were pleading for a place to go after they were released."],[70,443.07,3.2,"They were sitting with tears streaming down their faces,"],[70,447.85,2.77,"wanting to rebuild their lives,"],[70,450.61,2.9,"be reunited with their children,"],[70,453.55,2.07,"find a job in their community [and]"],[70,457.25,2.57,"break free from the criminal justice system."],[70,461.96,1.83,"Every year in this country,"],[70,465.3,3.5,"around two million women are released from prisons and jails."],[70,470.27,3.84,"They walk out with a trash bag of belongings,"],[70,474.14,2.64,"hoping someone will listen."],[70,476.81,1.74,"Hoping someone will help."],[70,480.41,1.64,"When there's no help,"],[70,482.08,2.97,"when there's no safe place to land,"],[70,485.08,2.5,"too many of them will go back."],[70,488.82,6.54,"I knew I could not provide resources of a new way of life"],[70,495.39,1.57,"to all those women."],[70,498.86,2.6,"So I created SAFE,"],[70,501.5,2.9,"Sisterhood Alliance for Freedom and Equality,"],[70,506.47,1.53,"to replicate my model."],[70,508.91,3.1,"Our first training was with 13 women."],[70,514.08,2.64,"To date, we've trained over 700."],[70,523.29,1.97,"To listen."],[70,525.29,2.3,"To create support."],[70,527.62,1.27,"To give hope."],[70,530.79,5.0,"When women like me have safe housing,"],[70,535.83,2.3,"families reunify,"],[70,538.17,2.34,"recidivism drops"],[70,540.54,2.27,"and communities stabilize."],[70,544.54,3.57,"I started with one house of women helping women."],[70,549.25,4.94,"Today, there is an entire network of women helping women"],[70,554.22,3.5,"in 20 US states and also in Uganda,"],[70,558.65,2.94,"Kenya and Nigeria."],[70,568.6,3.37,"We have replication down to a science."],[70,573.14,3.2,"The leadership is there, the demand is there,"],[70,576.34,3.2,"and the goal is clear."],[70,580.68,2.84,"More houses in more cities."],[70,584.62,4.3,"More mothers reunited with their children."],[70,588.95,3.74,"More women breaking a cycle"],[70,592.72,2.2,"that has lasted generations."],[70,596.63,4.67,"I believe we all share a responsibility"],[70,603.2,4.64,"to heal the harms caused by the criminal justice system."],[70,609.37,2.2,"This is public safety."],[70,612.54,2.5,"This is family preservation."],[70,615.78,2.47,"This is community repair."],[70,619.45,2.77,"When someone listened to me"],[70,622.25,3.94,"with compassion rather than judgment,"],[70,626.22,1.77,"it changed my life."],[70,629.69,4.17,"My story since that day in 1997,"],[70,635.37,3.17,"shows what is possible"],[70,638.53,2.5,"when people have support,"],[70,642.71,1.87,"hope"],[70,644.57,1.64,"and determination."],[70,648.88,1.97,"I cannot bring my son back."],[70,653.58,4.84,"But I know every time a woman walks through the doors and finds safety,"],[70,660.76,3.07,"every time a child is reunited with his mother,"],[70,665.7,2.84,"every time a woman breaks free"],[70,668.53,2.3,"from a cycle that once held me,"],[70,671.9,3.3,"his legacy goes way beyond his years."],[70,677.04,2.74,"I believe he's smiling down on my efforts,"],[70,681.24,2.5,"and I hope he's proud of his mother."],[70,684.78,1.74,"I am so grateful"],[70,690.22,2.04,"to be on the TED dot."],[70,724.05,5.47,"And this happened because someone listened"],[70,729.56,4.14,"with compassion rather than judgment."],[70,733.7,1.4,"Thank you so much."],[71,6.75,5.3,"In 1954, Winston Cigarettes launched an ad so controversial"],[71,12.05,3.17,"broadcasters refused to say it on the air."],[71,15.76,3.96,"But the uproar had nothing to do with the dangerous product being advertised—"],[71,19.89,3.92,"people were appalled by the ad’s grammar."],[71,24.02,3.79,"Specifically, using “like” as a conjunction instead of “as.”"],[71,28.36,2.67,"Grammatical incidents like this have caused outrage"],[71,31.03,3.29,"in various regions and languages for centuries."],[71,34.49,2.75,"But while we know people take grammar seriously,"],[71,37.37,2.34,"what’s less clear is, why?"],[71,40.08,3.63,"Where do these seemingly random rules come from?"],[71,43.71,2.71,"And which, if any, actually matter?"],[71,46.92,3.09,"The answers to these questions vary from rule to rule,"],[71,50.17,3.63,"so let’s look at three particularly famous English examples."],[71,54.3,4.59,"First up, the idea that you should never end a sentence with a preposition."],[71,59.3,5.67,"Prepositions are small, common words like with, on, for, or to,"],[71,65.23,2.04,"that often come before nouns."],[71,67.56,2.88,"So, for example, a grammar stickler would consider"],[71,70.44,2.09,"“where does this rule come from?”"],[71,72.73,5.59,"incorrect, preferring instead, “from where does this rule come?”"],[71,79.33,5.38,"This rule comes from 18th century England, when etiquette was all the rage."],[71,84.91,3.25,"And amidst countless guides on how to act properly,"],[71,88.17,4.8,"grammarians flooded the market with books on how to speak properly."],[71,93.38,2.84,"Many of the rules in these guides were taken from Latin—"],[71,96.22,3.13,"a language that was commonly used for written scholarship."],[71,99.76,5.51,"But no one had spoken Latin conversationally in 1,000 years,"],[71,105.27,3.67,"and imposing its rules on speech was awkward to say the least."],[71,109.86,3.0,"The rule to never strand a preposition without a noun"],[71,112.86,1.79,"was one such revived relic."],[71,114.99,5.05,"And while the scholars who proposed it presented the idea as a stylistic choice,"],[71,120.12,4.05,"subsequent writers turned their suggestion into a hard rule."],[71,124.62,3.25,"Other rules have origins that are perhaps less arbitrary,"],[71,127.87,2.25,"but significantly more petty."],[71,130.59,2.79,"Consider the idea that you should use “fewer”"],[71,133.38,3.25,"when referring to countable nouns— like water bottles—"],[71,136.97,4.55,"and “less” when referring to nouns we don’t count— like water."],[71,142.18,4.38,"This rule comes from another 18th century grammarian called Robert Baker,"],[71,146.73,5.0,"who, for reasons unknown, believed “less” was inelegant"],[71,151.73,2.42,"when used with countable nouns."],[71,154.73,1.5,"This might seem strange,"],[71,156.4,4.71,"but people today wage similar wars on so-called lesser language,"],[71,161.37,2.63,"such as “like” or “literally.”"],[71,164.49,4.92,"Besides, Baker's preference was part of a larger craze for codifying English."],[71,169.83,3.46,"For centuries after the Norman Conquest in 1066,"],[71,173.5,2.75,"French was the language of nobility in England,"],[71,176.25,3.59,"while English was seen as the language of commoners."],[71,180.09,3.71,"While the English had long reclaimed their kingdom by Baker’s time,"],[71,184.06,3.88,"this insecurity lingered in the hearts of many English grammarians."],[71,188.56,5.09,"In fact, Baker also wanted King George to establish an Academy of Letters"],[71,193.81,3.71,"with 50 officials to protect the English language."],[71,197.9,4.17,"That’s ten more officials than were employed in France’s grammar guard—"],[71,202.07,2.0,"L’Académie Française."],[71,204.53,4.13,"Many English grammar rules have similarly silly origins,"],[71,208.66,4.13,"such as efforts to distinguish “over” from “more than,”"],[71,213.21,3.88,"or our attachment to Latin plurals like fungi and formulae."],[71,217.88,3.84,"The stakes of all these grammatical debates are mostly academic."],[71,221.93,5.34,"But one frequently discussed rule does have some real-world consequences."],[71,227.51,5.17,"For centuries, “they” has been used in classic literature and everyday speech"],[71,232.69,3.42,"as both a plural and a singular pronoun."],[71,236.52,3.25,"And today, many non-binary and transgender individuals"],[71,239.78,4.8,"prefer to use “they/them” as singular pronouns to refer to themselves."],[71,244.82,2.46,"19th century grammarians, however,"],[71,247.28,4.59,"believed that “they” should be an exclusively plural pronoun."],[71,252.25,4.59,"Furthermore, in cases where “they” could be used as a singular pronoun,"],[71,257.04,3.54,"they argued the word should be swapped for the generic “he.”"],[71,261.3,3.5,"This was an obviously patriarchal decision,"],[71,264.8,1.54,"and during the 1970s,"],[71,266.35,5.25,"activists eventually convinced some usage guides to begin using “he or she.”"],[71,272.81,3.92,"But today, this quibble continues to cause confusion,"],[71,276.77,3.09,"even though most official grammar guides have accepted “they”"],[71,279.86,3.0,"as both a singular and plural pronoun."],[71,283.07,4.34,"This isn’t the first time we’ve had this kind of pronoun predicament."],[71,287.74,5.75,"For centuries, “you” was also considered an exclusively plural pronoun,"],[71,293.71,4.59,"and yet today it's completely accepted in singular situations."],[71,298.54,3.34,"And that’s because grammar isn’t a hard science—"],[71,302.01,4.38,"it’s something we all build together to reflect the people using it."],[71,307.01,2.63,"So however strange a rule's origins may be,"],[71,309.76,4.8,"the usage we choose to respect today is far from arbitrary."],[72,3.63,2.0,"I am a journalist, trained as a journalist,"],[72,5.67,2.74,"but I spent most of my last 35 years"],[72,8.44,1.44,"as an anthropologist."],[72,9.91,3.7,"I've kind of been embedded with a very unique subspecies."],[72,13.64,3.04,"You might know them by their Latin name,"],[72,16.71,1.83,"Billionaires Maximus."],[72,19.38,4.1,"And when I got to Forbes out of college in 1991,"],[72,23.49,3.14,"there were all of 274 billionaires in the world."],[72,26.66,2.34,"It was a very aspirational little club."],[72,29.03,3.74,"You know, Bruno Mars in his song \"Billionaire\" sings of dreaming"],[72,32.8,2.74,"of being on the cover of Forbes magazine,"],[72,35.57,2.94,"standing next to Oprah and the Queen."],[72,38.54,1.4,"Well, you know, fast forward,"],[72,39.97,2.67,"if we did that cover now, it would be very crowded."],[72,42.67,4.77,"Forbes tracks 3,428 people"],[72,47.48,3.6,"we believe have a net worth right now of a billion dollars or more."],[72,51.12,1.83,"And they all have one thing in common."],[72,52.98,2.1,"Who wants to know the secret"],[72,55.09,2.07,"of every billionaire right now in the world,"],[72,57.19,2.13,"which is nobody likes them."],[72,60.66,3.74,"Forbes and HarrisX conducted a survey a couple of weeks ago"],[72,64.43,1.47,"[of] 1,009 Americans."],[72,65.93,1.67,"The results are not good."],[72,68.13,2.37,"Trial lawyers are kicking billionaires' ass."],[72,72.0,3.07,"They are tied with members of Congress."],[72,75.44,4.37,"They are barely beating TikTok influencers."],[72,81.38,3.07,"But there's a disconnect going on right now"],[72,84.48,2.84,"because the world needs billionaires."],[72,87.35,2.0,"The world desperately needs billionaires."],[72,89.35,2.27,"If you look at countries that have billionaires,"],[72,91.62,3.04,"they tend to have all the things that we all love:"],[72,94.69,3.0,"progress, jobs, growth."],[72,97.69,1.53,"Let's go to China."],[72,99.26,1.6,"At the beginning of this century,"],[72,100.86,3.74,"there were no Chinese billionaires on the Forbes list,"],[72,104.6,3.4,"and almost 500 million people lived in extreme poverty."],[72,108.04,2.54,"Fast forward 20 years, it's almost the opposite."],[72,110.61,5.74,"Almost 500 Chinese billionaires and no one living in extreme poverty."],[72,116.38,2.0,"That is not a coincidence."],[72,118.42,1.2,"If you go to Nigeria,"],[72,119.65,2.64,"one of the largest countries in the world by population,"],[72,122.32,1.94,"only four billionaires,"],[72,124.29,2.64,"about 100 million people live in extreme poverty."],[72,126.96,2.34,"There are no billionaires in Cuba."],[72,129.79,1.87,"There are no billionaires in Iran."],[72,131.66,2.87,"There are no billionaires in Belarus."],[72,135.23,2.74,"History shows"],[72,138.0,4.5,"that incentive creates personal wealth"],[72,142.54,1.94,"and societal wealth."],[72,144.51,2.1,"When you cap earnings,"],[72,146.64,3.47,"that correlates with repression and stagnation."],[72,150.15,2.07,"So why all the hate?"],[72,152.25,1.17,"Why all the hate?"],[72,153.45,2.34,"Wealth disparity is some of it."],[72,156.29,1.47,"In a few weeks,"],[72,157.75,4.27,"if SpaceX goes public as scheduled,"],[72,162.06,1.87,"Forbes believes that at that moment,"],[72,163.96,4.64,"Elon Musk will become the world's first trillionaire."],[72,169.5,2.97,"That's a million million."],[72,174.21,1.6,"But here's the deal."],[72,176.54,5.07,"Elon is no richer, not much richer at least when you measure by GDP,"],[72,181.65,2.0,"[than] John D. Rockefeller"],[72,183.68,3.07,"when he became the world's first billionaire a hundred years ago."],[72,186.78,3.24,"John D. Rockefeller, he was an OG monopolist."],[72,190.05,1.3,"But by the end of his life,"],[72,191.35,2.37,"doing lots of good works, he was highly respected."],[72,193.72,2.24,"Andrew Carnegie even more so."],[72,195.99,2.24,"People don't resent billionaires."],[72,199.0,2.47,"People want to become billionaires."],[72,202.1,2.24,"People resent billionaires"],[72,204.37,5.3,"when they forget that the purpose of business is to create happiness,"],[72,209.67,2.1,"not who dies with the most toys."],[72,214.04,2.04,"So here's the scorecard."],[72,216.11,3.74,"Right now, here are the five richest people in the world, all Americans,"],[72,219.88,3.0,"all with a net worth north of 200 billion dollars."],[72,222.92,2.04,"Forbes tracks pretty much every billionaire"],[72,224.96,2.97,"in terms of how much they give to charity -- how much winds up,"],[72,227.93,1.64,"[and the] end result into charity."],[72,229.56,3.1,"Right now, these five collectively, [their] competitive net worth,"],[72,232.66,4.5,"less than one percent of their net worth, has been donated to charity:"],[72,237.17,1.47,"0.9 percent."],[72,238.7,4.97,"Compare that with the average American --"],[72,243.67,4.34,"teacher, fireman -- [who] gives two percent"],[72,248.01,3.74,"of his or her income to charity."],[72,251.78,1.17,"That gap,"],[72,252.95,2.64,"as somebody who loves entrepreneurial capitalism"],[72,255.62,3.07,"and defends entrepreneurial capitalism,"],[72,258.72,1.14,"bothers me."],[72,260.79,4.54,"That gap is why there are pitchforks."],[72,266.23,3.54,"But that gap is something we can address ..."],[72,269.8,5.77,"by leaning into the final decision maker"],[72,275.61,1.5,"that a lot of billionaires have"],[72,277.11,2.87,"when they decide if they're going to be philanthropic or not,"],[72,280.01,1.7,"which is their egos."],[72,282.51,3.34,"It turns out that a lot of people care about where they are"],[72,285.88,1.53,"on various Forbes lists."],[72,287.45,4.57,"In fact, in 1982, the very first Forbes 400,"],[72,292.06,4.54,"we had one tycoon who helped us create an immutable law"],[72,296.63,2.54,"of wealth tracking: the Trump rule."],[72,300.23,1.74,"Take what the Donald tells you,"],[72,302.0,3.14,"divide by three and refine from there."],[72,306.1,5.24,"Saudi Arabia's Prince Al Waleed created an entire public company,"],[72,311.38,2.27,"basically to get higher on the Forbes list."],[72,313.68,2.07,"Every year before the Forbes issue came out,"],[72,315.78,1.14,"the billionaires issue,"],[72,316.95,2.5,"he would buy up his own stock, pump up his own stock,"],[72,319.45,2.33,"and after it came out, he would sell it."],[72,321.79,2.57,"Look, it’s like a 10-digit cardiogram there."],[72,326.26,1.3,"Kylie Jenner's team gave us"],[72,327.59,3.47,"what we believe are bogus numbers about her company"],[72,331.1,1.97,"to get onto the Forbes billionaires list,"],[72,333.1,4.4,"and she's not even the thirstiest member of the Kardashian family."],[72,338.8,4.61,"During COVID, Kanye West called me maybe every other day."],[72,343.44,3.84,"We talked about an hour at a time, mostly about how he thinks he was richer"],[72,347.31,1.3,"than we said he was."],[72,349.68,3.37,"When we respectfully disagreed,"],[72,353.08,3.74,"he respectfully responded by tweeting my cell phone"],[72,356.85,1.83,"to 30 million followers on Twitter."],[72,360.16,1.27,"That was a tough day."],[72,364.03,3.37,"But Kanye inspired an idea."],[72,367.96,5.04,"Why can't we lean into this strange leverage we have over some people?"],[72,373.04,2.94,"Why can't we create a list"],[72,375.97,2.84,"that rewards people for donating to charity"],[72,378.81,3.44,"versus penaliz[ing] them by taking them further down on the Forbes list?"],[72,386.38,2.5,"So we've created something called True Net Worth,"],[72,388.89,1.97,"and we're going to debut it right here."],[72,393.16,3.94,"True Net Worth is your regular net worth,"],[72,397.09,3.7,"combined with the money you've donated"],[72,400.83,2.64,"that we appreciate like you still own it."],[72,406.57,1.64,"Here is a refresher."],[72,408.24,2.1,"Here are the top five in the world."],[72,411.88,2.7,"Here are the top five by true net worth."],[72,419.28,2.64,"Look at my friend Warren Buffett."],[72,421.95,2.7,"He's already given more away, in today's dollars,"],[72,424.69,1.44,"than he still has."],[72,426.79,3.34,"He's pledged 99 percent of what's left to charity,"],[72,430.16,3.7,"and he stipulated that all that money has to be spent down"],[72,433.9,1.44,"within 10 years of his death,"],[72,435.37,2.04,"as opposed to just sitting in a foundation,"],[72,437.4,1.64,"gathering interest for years."],[72,441.04,2.37,"That's true net worth."],[72,443.44,2.5,"Now let's look at the biggest movers on this list."],[72,455.79,2.37,"MacKenzie Scott is giving away money"],[72,458.15,4.37,"faster, smarter, no-strings-attached than pretty much anyone in history."],[72,462.56,4.04,"She's the 84th richest person in the world by net worth."],[72,466.63,1.87,"She's 26th by true net worth."],[72,472.97,2.84,"People don't value what you can't measure."],[72,475.84,3.4,"True net worth offers role models for billionaires,"],[72,479.28,2.57,"for millionaires, for thousandaires."],[72,481.88,1.43,"Give while you live."],[72,484.35,2.77,"Give faster and bigger maybe than you're comfortable with,"],[72,487.12,1.9,"give your money or your time."],[72,489.05,3.24,"But what all of it does is support the system"],[72,492.29,2.4,"that makes all of us prosperous."],[72,494.73,1.17,"Thank you."],[73,4.17,4.97,"So I recently just completed a short film about computers,"],[73,9.14,3.3,"and I'm very excited to be able to premiere it right now at TED."],[73,13.51,1.43,"Let's take a look. Thank you."],[73,35.13,1.27,"Did that scare you?"],[73,38.13,4.17,"I think that's because computers actually are kind of scary, aren't they?"],[73,42.34,2.94,"Hi, my name is Alan Resnick,"],[73,45.31,2.8,"and if you don’t know me, I’m a bit of a “tech-head.”"],[73,48.11,1.27,"What does that mean?"],[73,49.41,2.64,"It means an average day for me is spent surfing the web,"],[73,52.08,3.1,"checking my emails and just clicking on links."],[73,55.22,1.87,"That's sort of me in a nutshell."],[73,57.12,1.4,"And when I'm not doing that,"],[73,58.56,4.37,"I tour the country, and I teach people how to use computers."],[73,62.96,2.77,"And the number one thing I've noticed all over"],[73,65.76,3.04,"is that people are afraid of computers."],[73,68.83,1.57,"They're scared."],[73,70.43,3.67,"And don't get me started on the internet, you guys."],[73,74.14,1.87,"Has anyone here ever been online?"],[73,77.21,2.2,"Two, three hands."],[73,79.41,1.64,"You guys gotta get online."],[73,81.08,1.57,"It's incre --"],[73,82.68,1.27,"I'm telling you,"],[73,83.95,2.4,"there's some crazy stuff online, you guys."],[73,86.38,2.94,"I don't have time to get into it right now,"],[73,89.32,3.57,"but they have photos and videos of people online,"],[73,92.92,4.37,"and they're performing a kind of love that I am not comfortable with."],[73,97.33,1.27,"OK?"],[73,99.83,1.27,"And they have your data."],[73,102.03,2.44,"They do. These sick people have your data."],[73,104.5,2.0,"Are you comfortable with that? I'm not."],[73,107.34,2.94,"But I have a question for you guys, and it is a real question."],[73,110.31,2.37,"How do you use your computer?"],[73,112.68,1.7,"How do you guys use your computers?"],[73,114.38,1.87,"Just curious."],[73,116.25,1.27,"Can we get her a mic?"],[73,117.52,2.6,"How do you use your computer?"],[73,120.12,1.87,"Audience Member: Well, I usually use"],[73,121.99,1.0,"my computer for work."],[73,122.99,2.74,"So emails and, you know, sending messages to folks."],[73,125.76,2.17,"Alan Resnick: No, stop. OK. Shut up. No."],[73,127.96,1.8,"OK. This is wrong."],[73,130.93,4.71,"This is incorrect. I hate to say this, but you're using your computer wrong."],[73,135.67,2.77,"You know, not \"use.\" I don't love that word \"use.\""],[73,138.47,2.27,"We don't \"use\" computers, do we?"],[73,140.74,3.77,"No. I think of computers a little like grandparents, actually."],[73,144.54,1.97,"You wouldn't use your grandmother, right?"],[73,147.54,2.9,"But your grandmother can make your life easier"],[73,150.45,2.27,"if you use her properly."],[73,152.75,2.74,"So what is the proper way to use a computer?"],[73,155.49,2.6,"Tonight I'm going to share with you my one simple trick"],[73,158.12,2.1,"that's going to make your life a lot easier,"],[73,160.26,3.0,"it's going to protect your data and it's going to calm you down."],[73,163.29,1.4,"OK, here it is."],[73,166.86,1.27,"(Whispers) Desktops."],[73,169.37,2.2,"Desktops."],[73,171.57,1.27,"What is he saying?"],[73,172.87,1.27,"(Whispers) Desktop."],[73,174.17,3.5,"I can't hear him. What are you saying? I'm saying save it to your desktop!"],[73,177.71,2.4,"It's as simple as that. Save it to your desktop."],[73,180.11,1.7,"OK? Yeah, we can clap for that."],[73,181.84,2.3,"That's pretty cool. That is pretty cool."],[73,184.18,2.2,"You're going, \"Alan, it can't be that easy.\""],[73,186.38,1.0,"It is."],[73,187.38,1.77,"But before we can talk about desktops,"],[73,189.15,2.8,"we have to talk about something else."],[73,191.96,2.54,"Look at this. Check this out. This is pretty cool."],[73,195.06,2.2,"Look at that. You see this?"],[73,197.26,1.07,"My skin. Look at --"],[73,198.33,1.03,"Whoa!"],[73,199.36,1.83,"My skin is vibrating."],[73,201.2,2.9,"But it's vibrating -- it's very hard to tell."],[73,204.13,2.2,"It's hard to see. Here. This is vibrating, too."],[73,206.37,2.7,"But I think you'll be able to see this. Can you see that?"],[73,209.07,1.27,"Light vibrates too."],[73,210.37,2.27,"Are you -- I need a response."],[73,212.68,1.6,"Can you see that? You can? OK."],[73,215.61,2.6,"That's because of the eye-brain system."],[73,218.25,2.54,"The visible spectrum. Rods and cones. Rods and cones."],[73,220.78,1.27,"Rods and cones."],[73,222.09,2.1,"But my skin is vibrating, too."],[73,224.22,3.27,"But it vibrates at a rate that you can't see or perceive,"],[73,227.49,3.74,"but dogs and pets can actually hear."],[73,231.26,2.1,"They hear our skin."],[73,233.36,2.27,"So I know you like to touch a little dog's head."],[73,236.93,3.8,"But that passive action is actually causing them extreme inner ear pain,"],[73,240.74,1.5,"so you shouldn't touch dogs."],[73,243.97,1.53,"But here's what you didn't know."],[73,247.61,1.5,"My computer vibrates too."],[73,249.11,1.83,"Did you know that? Computers vibrate."],[73,250.95,4.1,"And they happen to vibrate at the exact opposite rate as human skin."],[73,255.09,1.3,"That's amazing."],[73,256.42,3.64,"So when you're working with a computer, those vibrations are phasing."],[73,260.09,3.94,"And when you touch a computer, you have a vibration rate of zero."],[73,264.03,2.37,"There is only one moment in every person's life"],[73,266.43,1.97,"when they have a vibration rate of zero,"],[73,268.43,4.64,"and that is on their fifth birthday, when they blow out the candle."],[73,273.07,1.94,"So you go on, \"Alan, you convinced me.\""],[73,275.04,2.37,"If working with computers feels even half as good"],[73,277.41,1.57,"as a little boy's birthday,"],[73,279.01,1.53,"then sign me up."],[73,280.58,4.41,"The glove is off and we're ready to change our lives forever."],[73,285.01,1.27,"But how?"],[73,286.28,1.27,"(Whispers) Desktops."],[73,287.58,1.44,"I already told you, desktops."],[73,289.05,2.17,"Save it to your desktop! I already said that."],[73,291.25,1.57,"Save it to your desktop. OK."],[73,293.96,1.44,"I think you're ready."],[73,297.03,1.74,"Do you guys want to see my desktop?"],[73,298.76,2.6,"Audience: Yes. Show me your desktop."],[73,301.37,1.0,"AR: I can’t hear you."],[73,302.37,1.77,"Do you guys want to see my desktop?"],[73,304.13,1.9,"Come on! OK, let's do it!"],[73,306.04,2.4,"Here we go. Let's get it booted up."],[73,308.44,2.57,"Here it is. It's my desktop."],[73,311.01,1.44,"Whoa!"],[73,312.44,1.27,"That's amazing."],[73,313.74,2.9,"You're going, \"Alan, I could never have something like that.\""],[73,316.68,2.54,"If you follow my simple rule, save it to your desktop,"],[73,319.25,2.3,"you're not only going to have an awesome desktop,"],[73,321.58,2.5,"you're actually going to have a wicked, awesome life."],[73,324.12,1.8,"Now check this out. This is cool."],[73,325.96,2.57,"The desktop --"],[73,328.56,4.54,"is a little like my desk's top surface, right?"],[73,333.1,1.94,"So I have file folders on the computer."],[73,335.03,3.04,"And guess what? I got them here too."],[73,338.07,2.84,"What kind of things can you save to your desktop? Anything."],[73,340.9,4.37,"You can have photos, emails, PDFs."],[73,345.31,1.33,"Those are like packets."],[73,346.68,3.54,"You can have movies, video games,"],[73,350.25,4.07,"key codes, MP3s, zip disks."],[73,354.32,3.5,"These are all useful things that you can save to your desktop."],[73,357.82,2.17,"So it's sort of an organizational technique."],[73,359.99,1.67,"So I'll give you an example."],[73,361.66,3.74,"Let's say your dad emails you some pics of the lawn."],[73,365.43,2.47,"All you have to do is ..."],[73,367.93,4.07,"OK. Yeah, you got to just save them to the desktop."],[73,373.34,2.27,"Just save those right to the desktop."],[73,377.01,3.64,"And after you do that, you're going to want to print out a copy for yourself,"],[73,380.68,2.2,"which you're going to place on your desk's top."],[73,382.91,2.7,"Can I get those photos? Where the heck are those photos?"],[73,385.62,1.27,"Oh, thank you so much."],[73,386.92,2.0,"So --"],[73,388.95,1.47,"This is cool."],[73,390.45,2.07,"So now you always know where everything is."],[73,392.56,1.8,"You're not going to lose anything."],[73,394.39,3.34,"I'll give you another example. I'm just going to pick a file at random."],[73,397.76,2.14,"Let's just pick a random file."],[73,399.93,2.1,"OK? Eeny meeny miny moe."],[73,402.03,1.94,"Catch a tiger by the t --"],[73,404.0,1.23,"Oh."],[73,407.0,2.23,"This is a little embarrassing."],[73,409.24,2.44,"This is actually an old poem I wrote."],[73,411.71,1.6,"Egg on my face. It says,"],[73,413.34,4.34,"\"Why whisper when a yell can travel just as far?"],[73,417.71,3.27,"A whisper yelled is a whisper heard.\""],[73,420.98,1.6,"Hmm."],[73,426.76,4.17,"So where did I save that? Let's see. Where did I save that?"],[73,431.5,2.23,"It looks like I put that on..."],[73,433.76,5.5,"OK, that's C5, that's the bottom right corner of my desk."],[73,440.84,1.23,"So it should be here."],[73,443.54,1.27,"Oh, Goddamn it!"],[73,445.38,1.3,"Oh! Boom! Here it is!"],[73,446.71,2.44,"Wow! You can clap for that. That's cool!"],[73,449.18,1.6,"This is amazing. This is amazing."],[73,451.92,3.27,"Now I'm noticing there's a couple eagle-eyed viewers in the audience,"],[73,455.22,1.67,"and they're looking at my computer"],[73,456.92,2.6,"and they're going, \"Something's up, something's up.\""],[73,459.56,4.04,"You're going, \"Alan, where the heck is your toolbar?\""],[73,463.63,2.64,"Or dock. Some people call it dock. I call it a toolbar."],[73,466.3,2.67,"\"Where's your toolbar?\" Well, guess what."],[73,469.0,2.77,"My computer doesn't have a toolbar."],[73,471.8,3.47,"Why? I'm not addicted to my toolbar."],[73,475.31,2.8,"You think America's got an obesity problem?"],[73,478.14,3.57,"You think we have a fentanyl problem? Folks, we have a toolbar problem."],[73,481.75,1.97,"Stop using your toolbar."],[73,483.75,3.07,"There is room for everything you need right here on the desktop."],[73,495.99,1.27,"This is silly."],[73,498.5,1.97,"This is my --"],[73,500.5,1.64,"My girlfriend,"],[73,502.17,2.27,"my ex-girlfriend, actually, made this for me."],[73,505.5,1.24,"And, uh..."],[73,507.5,2.9,"I don't know, it's silly, but I like to look at it."],[73,510.41,1.53,"It makes me happy to see this,"],[73,511.98,1.44,"so I --"],[73,514.54,2.44,"I keep it on my desk while I work."],[73,517.01,1.23,"But, uh --"],[73,519.58,1.23,"Yeah."],[73,521.62,2.44,"No, no, it's, um --"],[73,524.96,1.5,"It's not in the computer."],[73,528.29,2.97,"Yet! But literally anything can be saved to your desktop."],[73,531.29,4.74,"All you have to do is rig up a camera around a bucket at 90 degrees."],[73,536.03,2.64,"You're going to want to fill the bucket with black water"],[73,538.7,1.77,"and paint your beloved object white."],[73,540.5,1.97,"And here's what you do. It's really easy."],[73,542.51,3.5,"You submerge the object in the water and film yourself as you pull it up,"],[73,546.04,1.77,"recording its topology through time."],[73,547.84,3.1,"You're going to get the topology of every axis, axes, axi."],[73,550.95,3.4,"And once you have the axis data, all you have to do is boolean the axis."],[73,554.38,3.14,"You’re going to end up with an OBJ file that’s super high quality,"],[73,557.55,2.94,"you can render in an infinite quality of lighting conditions."],[73,560.49,2.07,"And then there's only one thing left to do."],[73,562.59,1.3,"What are we going to do?"],[73,563.89,1.7,"(Audience) Save it to your desktop."],[73,565.6,2.07,"AR: Yes. Oh my God. Save it to your desktop!"],[73,567.66,2.17,"Yes! Woo! If you do this,"],[73,569.83,2.8,"you will always have the things you want and need and love,"],[73,572.67,2.14,"and you will know exactly where to find them."],[73,574.84,1.67,"You never have to be lonely again."],[73,576.54,2.07,"You will be happy and people will love you."],[73,578.64,3.57,"Listen, if you don't have organizational skills and desktop skills,"],[73,582.25,3.07,"I'm sorry, folks, you're not going to get that job."],[73,585.35,3.04,"Your data, it's going to be leaking on the floor."],[73,588.42,3.6,"People are going to be slipping. They're going to be slipping on your data."],[73,592.05,1.7,"You can say \"bye-bye\" to your kids."],[73,593.79,2.57,"They're going to be living on their desktops."],[73,596.36,3.94,"Mark my words, in one year's time, people will literally be,"],[73,600.33,2.47,"blah, blah, blah, blah, talking to their computers."],[73,602.8,2.7,"Whoa. In two years, people are going to be walking around"],[73,605.53,3.14,"with glasses so powerful they're never going to need to come home."],[73,608.71,2.47,"And in three years' time,"],[73,611.21,1.74,"computers will have gotten so small"],[73,612.94,3.6,"they will literally fit on a piece of breakable glass."],[73,622.55,2.17,"Whew! OK. Thank you so much."],[73,624.75,1.27,"My name is Alan Resnick."],[73,626.06,2.34,"I really hope that helped. You've been incredible."],[73,628.39,2.5,"I love you so much. Good night. Thank you, thank you."],[73,630.93,1.64,"Thank you, thank you, thank you."],[74,3.63,2.34,"Serious two-part question for you."],[74,6.7,1.47,"What are you wearing?"],[74,8.57,1.44,"And why?"],[74,10.04,3.57,"Well I hope none of you are decked in an outfit against your will."],[74,13.64,2.14,"I happen to think our fashion choices"],[74,15.81,3.37,"should be deeper than just because we like something."],[74,19.22,1.17,"Who am I?"],[74,20.38,1.47,"I’m a long-ago hooper."],[74,21.89,1.6,"that jumper was wet, too."],[74,24.02,4.44,"Lifelong basketball fan, and long-aspiring fashionisto."],[74,28.49,4.0,"But most importantly, I wrote the book on NBA fashion."],[74,33.06,1.47,"Literally."],[74,34.53,3.0,"A project that taught me that the story of NBA fashion"],[74,37.53,4.24,"is the story of Black people dating back to the 1940s,"],[74,41.81,4.17,"which is also to say it's the history of America."],[74,46.38,3.64,"And I plan to show you how players' fashion choices"],[74,50.01,2.1,"have been both a reflection of the times"],[74,52.15,2.6,"and a catalyst for changing them."],[74,55.15,4.64,"And furthermore, how, for those who think these times are indeed troubling,"],[74,59.82,4.8,"they serve as a model for styling ourselves into resistance."],[74,65.23,2.87,"The NBA was founded in 1946."],[74,68.1,3.57,"There aren't many off-the-court photos of its first fashion era,"],[74,71.7,4.34,"but the ones that exist show men who adhere to the status quo."],[74,76.57,4.7,"Dress influenced by the conservation mandates of World War II."],[74,82.11,5.27,"It's important to note that early NBA fashion extends from pre-civil rights"],[74,87.42,2.7,"into the heart of the movement."],[74,91.29,3.64,"The first Black player was drafted in 1950,"],[74,94.96,3.8,"and those pioneers were also de facto ambassadors"],[74,98.8,3.14,"of the best of their race."],[74,102.27,5.0,"Hence, many of them dressed like men who were set on proving their dignity."],[74,107.3,1.47,"Know this:"],[74,108.81,4.47,"Bob Cousy's civil rights were never in question."],[74,113.61,2.23,"Real talk, Bill Russell's smile"],[74,115.85,3.64,"was a requisite for a respectable Black man."],[74,120.02,3.0,"Yeah, Wilt got spicy later,"],[74,123.02,4.24,"but in the '50s, he conformed like everybody else."],[74,127.86,3.2,"The second era is shaped by the Civil Rights Act,"],[74,131.09,3.64,"the Black Power movement and the war in Vietnam,"],[74,134.76,2.97,"plus the huge popularity of soul music"],[74,137.77,2.54,"and the birth of blaxploitation films."],[74,141.41,1.44,"By the 1970s,"],[74,142.87,3.27,"75 percent of the league's players were Black,"],[74,146.18,2.8,"and those Black players and some of the white ones"],[74,149.01,1.7,"began dressing like young men"],[74,150.75,2.37,"who were claiming their hard-fought freedom"],[74,153.15,1.9,"to loosen or dash their ties"],[74,155.09,3.8,"and assert themselves as individuals."],[74,159.39,3.2,"You can see it in their planetary afros,"],[74,162.63,2.44,"in their kente cloth and dashikis,"],[74,165.09,2.9,"in their hippie digs or flashy jewelry."],[74,168.03,1.43,"If you ask me,"],[74,169.47,4.61,"Clyde Frazier's cape is a flamboyant symbol of freedom."],[74,174.1,4.44,"Here's Bill Walton, dressed in his then-radical politics."],[74,178.58,4.57,"And look, Doctor J could have been a blaxploitation action star"],[74,183.18,3.1,"with his earthly afro and edgy fashion."],[74,188.49,4.64,"The NBA paid its first million-dollar per-season salaries"],[74,193.12,2.17,"in 1979/80."],[74,195.76,3.54,"The following year, Reaganomics became law."],[74,199.83,1.5,"A few years later,"],[74,201.37,2.87,"Michael Jeffrey Jordan entered the league"],[74,204.24,4.0,"and in time became the most famous person on Earth."],[74,208.61,3.14,"Jordan once said that Republicans buy sneakers, too,"],[74,211.78,2.9,"and though he since claimed his comment was in jest,"],[74,214.68,3.7,"its apparent apoliticalness was a defining feature"],[74,218.41,1.87,"of his eponymous third era."],[74,220.99,4.24,"As were the excesses born of America's booming economy"],[74,225.26,4.2,"and the lingering illusion that Nixon’s Black capitalism initiative"],[74,229.49,3.1,"was a net-positive for Black folks."],[74,233.23,5.04,"Jordan was raised on southern Black respectability and wore suits,"],[74,238.27,3.77,"but he also customized them joints with an icon’s flair."],[74,242.37,5.17,"Look, little says nouveau riche like Magic wearing a fur in the 1980s."],[74,248.61,5.0,"Yeah, maybe his billionaire status now was what Nixon meant"],[74,253.62,2.24,"by Black \"economic equality.\""],[74,255.89,1.77,"Jordan's suit proportions,"],[74,257.69,4.14,"my God, aren't they '90s extravagance?"],[74,261.86,1.17,"Yeah."],[74,263.06,4.07,"Dennis Rodman was the iconoclast of the Jordan era,"],[74,267.16,4.2,"with style that bridged him to a liberated white world."],[74,272.34,3.47,"Hip hop becoming the most dominant force in youth culture"],[74,275.84,2.34,"set the fourth era in motion."],[74,278.78,4.0,"In a short span, several rappers reached diamond sales,"],[74,282.81,4.5,"and Outkast and Lauryn Hill won Grammys for album of the year."],[74,287.88,2.07,"Around that same time,"],[74,289.99,4.04,"Allen Ezail Iverson became the poster child"],[74,294.02,2.54,"for hip hop's influence on the NBA."],[74,296.59,4.54,"Not just his style, but his irreverence and aplomb."],[74,301.17,5.17,"However, the racist idea of thugs pervading hip hop colored the perception"],[74,306.37,2.64,"of players following AI's lead."],[74,309.01,3.67,"And that infamous brawl known as the “Malice at the Palace,”"],[74,312.71,2.17,"well, it didn’t help matters."],[74,314.91,1.13,"In fact,"],[74,316.08,2.54,"it spurred the NBA dress code,"],[74,318.65,5.44,"i.e. a new way to restrict Black men’s freedom of expression."],[74,325.39,2.47,"Hip hop is over the top, no doubt."],[74,327.89,4.3,"Which in AI’s case meant bling you could see from the nosebleeds"],[74,332.23,2.54,"and the lollipop stress insouciance."],[74,334.8,3.64,"D Wade's getup reminds us that posturing tough"],[74,338.44,2.4,"is part of hip hop's DNA."],[74,341.27,1.24,"Tell me this."],[74,342.51,3.94,"Does Jermaine O'Neal's gaudy jewelry challenge the stereotype"],[74,346.48,1.33,"of the Black male thug?"],[74,348.38,3.84,"While it was shaped by President Obama's first term,"],[74,352.25,3.47,"David Stern instituting the NBA dress code"],[74,355.75,2.13,"defined the fifth era."],[74,357.92,4.61,"Commissioner Stern's rules forced players to eschew their beloved hip hop gear"],[74,362.56,2.4,"in favor of more conservative attire."],[74,364.96,4.57,"Restrictions that pushed them to become more experimental with their style."],[74,369.57,3.8,"Before long, the world that excluded Black men for ages"],[74,373.37,3.8,"began offering them prime seats at fashion shows"],[74,377.21,2.57,"and coveted tickets to the Met Gala."],[74,380.11,2.3,"Like the Black dandies of yore,"],[74,382.45,4.34,"players of the dress-code era turned structural limitations"],[74,386.82,1.57,"into a showcase"],[74,388.42,2.44,"of their boldness and creativity."],[74,391.59,5.57,"Kobe's cosmopolitan sensibilities challenged biases about the breadth"],[74,397.19,2.5,"of Black men’s cultural influences."],[74,399.7,5.71,"Amar'e Stoudemire's fit insists that we take his style acumen seriously."],[74,406.0,5.34,"Ah, here’s Melo forging a place for Black men at fashion’s biggest night,"],[74,411.38,1.33,"the Met Gala."],[74,413.28,3.44,"LeBron, wearing the last words of Eric Garner,"],[74,416.75,5.27,"empowered a new generation of athletes to broadcast their politics."],[74,422.39,5.21,"Instagram crossing one billion users in 2018 marked the sixth era,"],[74,428.16,4.0,"much thanks due to LeBron and D Wade and Chris Bosh."],[74,432.2,1.7,"Remember them Heatles?"],[74,433.93,4.2,"The app helped transform the few minutes between a player's arena arrival"],[74,438.17,1.2,"and the locker room"],[74,439.4,3.74,"into an unscripted space of expression."],[74,443.14,1.77,"It also helped turn several players,"],[74,444.94,3.24,"including some bench dudes like P.J. Tucker up there,"],[74,448.21,1.57,"into cultural figures"],[74,449.81,4.0,"who were just as big, if not bigger, in the fashion world"],[74,453.85,1.87,"than in the world of sports."],[74,456.02,5.07,"Not to mention, some players used their expanded platforms to push"],[74,461.12,1.5,"for social change."],[74,462.66,1.77,"Westbrook has made a great case"],[74,464.46,3.74,"as his era's foremost iconoclast."],[74,469.07,4.64,"LeBron, he dressed his calves in Thom Browne for a playoff tunnel walk."],[74,474.34,4.57,"A viral moment that accrued the whole damn league fashion cachet."],[74,480.21,3.2,"Hip hop is born as rebellion,"],[74,483.41,6.07,"and Ja Morant's diamond-encrusted grill is a throwback to that defiance."],[74,489.52,1.5,"But where are we now?"],[74,492.02,2.07,"I don't know about you,"],[74,494.12,4.5,"but what I see is a government decimating civil rights,"],[74,498.66,2.5,"assaulting freedom of speech,"],[74,501.2,3.84,"erasing the history of marginalized groups,"],[74,505.07,1.67,"targeting immigrants."],[74,507.64,2.7,"How will this broad oppression shape a league"],[74,510.37,3.04,"in which Black players are still the largest share,"],[74,513.44,3.4,"and foreign-born players are some of its biggest stars?"],[74,517.55,1.37,"In every era,"],[74,518.95,3.0,"NBA players, the Black ones in particular,"],[74,521.99,2.17,"have used fashion to challenge sports"],[74,524.15,5.74,"casting them as less deserving of their human rights and their dignity."],[74,531.06,3.97,"And there's a model in that for all of us, right?"],[74,535.87,2.0,"Which is also to ask,"],[74,537.9,3.64,"what's our relationship to power?"],[74,542.54,3.1,"And not just because we like it,"],[74,545.68,2.84,"because we want to resist,"],[74,548.54,3.34,"what are we wearing tomorrow?"],[74,552.32,4.9,"I mean, we as in you and me,"],[74,557.22,2.77,"as in all of us."],[74,560.69,1.13,"Thank you."],[75,8.55,2.0,"(Video) Guys, here goes a level seven."],[75,11.63,1.21,"Thank you."],[75,17.47,1.17,"Yeah!"],[75,19.6,1.33,"Woohoo!"],[75,20.93,1.46,"Give me high-five!"],[75,28.07,2.34,"Maxwell Pearce: That was me shooting a basketball"],[75,30.4,2.25,"off of a 230-foot New York City building."],[75,33.2,2.34,"It's certainly not your typical basketball court."],[75,35.99,2.29,"I am a member of the Harlem Globetrotters,"],[75,38.28,3.0,"a legendary exhibition basketball team"],[75,41.29,2.92,"that is credited for integrating the NBA,"],[75,44.21,2.59,"popularizing new moves like the “alley-oop”"],[75,46.84,2.29,"and creating so many others."],[75,49.84,3.5,"These are the new moves that have advanced the game,"],[75,53.38,4.17,"but they've also given joy to the people that come and watch us perform."],[75,57.93,4.5,"And that right there is precisely how we have lasted for 100 years,"],[75,62.43,3.42,"while also earning a spot in the Hall of Fame."],[75,66.27,3.54,"Getting to this point in my career certainly was not a straight line."],[75,70.11,3.92,"I've always struggled with high-level sports coaching."],[75,74.49,5.13,"There’s a certain culture that has excessive cursing, yelling"],[75,79.66,3.54,"and also a hyper-seriousness that can really stunt your growth."],[75,83.5,2.04,"Although we were playing sports,"],[75,85.54,4.21,"ironically, we were coached not to be very playful."],[75,90.71,3.0,"I was a creative kid who loved art,"],[75,93.76,1.92,"but I was really, really shy."],[75,96.22,3.13,"So the constant confrontation with my coaches"],[75,99.35,2.79,"was always robbing me of my creativity."],[75,103.1,4.96,"I learned that there was a seriousness in respecting the quality of the game."],[75,108.81,3.38,"Whether it was basketball, football, baseball or cross country,"],[75,112.23,2.71,"all of my coaches emphasized honoring"],[75,114.99,2.79,"and respecting the essence of their sport,"],[75,117.82,3.88,"and that meant taking every drill and every exercise seriously"],[75,121.74,3.0,"and not doing anything"],[75,124.75,2.96,"that [would] challenge the traditional way of doing things."],[75,128.75,3.17,"This is exactly what led my high-school coach to tell me"],[75,131.92,2.04,"to stop trying to dunk the basketball."],[75,136.01,1.42,"But ironically,"],[75,137.47,2.88,"it was my playfulness that got me to this point in my career."],[75,140.89,1.96,"So here's where I've landed today,"],[75,142.85,2.92,"and this is what I’m so excited to share with you all."],[75,146.52,4.38,"Play is the most important ingredient in every person"],[75,150.9,2.46,"and every industry's evolution."],[75,154.32,2.59,"I am an athlete and an artist,"],[75,156.94,3.21,"and I have witnessed how play can break rules"],[75,160.16,2.84,"to improve both of these worlds that I live in."],[75,162.99,3.38,"So just imagine if play, creativity"],[75,166.41,3.84,"and a little bit of rule-breaking was in every industry."],[75,171.04,1.92,"There once was a time in basketball"],[75,172.96,2.88,"when dunking was frowned upon so much"],[75,175.88,2.67,"that the NCAA banned it for nine years."],[75,178.59,3.84,"Once that ban was lifted, the popularity of the game skyrocketed."],[75,182.8,2.54,"This context reminds me of my coaches"],[75,185.35,2.38,"yelling at me to stick to the fundamentals,"],[75,187.77,3.17,"all because I decided, or had, the audacity"],[75,190.98,2.59,"to try and dunk the ball one time."],[75,194.36,1.71,"Thankfully, I didn't listen to them"],[75,196.07,2.29,"and I got more and more comfortable with it."],[75,198.36,1.21,"And over time,"],[75,199.57,2.59,"I was able to land my first dunk contest."],[75,202.2,3.71,"With a lot of work, I was able to build a global reputation"],[75,205.95,1.79,"for dunking the basketball."],[75,208.08,2.84,"I began to experiment with the boundaries of this."],[75,210.96,4.34,"I would incorporate things that we didn't normally associate with dunking."],[75,215.34,1.13,"Like, with a dog."],[75,233.27,2.17,"Or catching a baseball."],[75,246.87,4.13,"Or catching the ball without looking from someone else who’s not looking."],[75,259.09,1.71,"(Video) Oh, my God!"],[75,264.76,3.38,"MP: Every time I approached this with a playful mindset,"],[75,268.18,1.29,"I was rewarded."],[75,270.02,2.88,"Not only with advancing the game,"],[75,272.94,3.63,"but also with the opportunities that I dreamed of as a kid."],[75,277.31,3.13,"There were several video games that I used to love to play,"],[75,280.44,1.92,"like NBA Live and NBA 2K,"],[75,282.4,4.3,"and I had the opportunity to feature my dunks in both of them."],[75,287.07,3.79,"I've also had the opportunity to feature my dunks in GQ magazine,"],[75,290.87,1.83,"as well as SportsCenter Top Ten,"],[75,292.75,3.88,"and eventually received an honorable mention for an ESPY award."],[75,297.71,3.04,"Unlike what my coaches constantly bickered about,"],[75,300.75,2.84,"being a Globetrotter requires much of the opposite."],[75,304.09,3.54,"So much of what we love about the game today ..."],[75,307.68,5.25,"was at one point deemed as not fundamental or non-traditional."],[75,313.27,4.96,"There's a beauty in balancing seriousness with play."],[75,319.4,2.0,"For example,"],[75,321.4,4.59,"if I decided that I wanted to do something"],[75,326.03,2.79,"and I accidentally made a mistake,"],[75,328.87,2.5,"there [was] a way that I could brush that off."],[75,332.04,2.34,"When a Globetrotter makes a mistake,"],[75,334.37,2.46,"they can have it be overlooked"],[75,336.83,3.0,"because of how they playfully embrace its direction."],[75,341.59,2.25,"They recognize that something went wrong,"],[75,343.84,4.84,"but they actually welcome the unfamiliarity"],[75,348.72,2.25,"that comes with that new position."],[75,351.31,3.96,"So, for instance, if I try this trick right here."],[75,359.44,2.46,"And I accidentally drop the ball,"],[75,361.94,5.63,"I can use the momentum from that mistake to grow it into something new."],[75,377.67,1.17,"What I've learned --"],[75,382.55,6.01,"What I’ve learned is that in order to get to that point,"],[75,388.59,2.08,"you have to be able to roll with it."],[75,391.43,1.83,"Because otherwise,"],[75,393.31,3.79,"you'll stop before you're ever able to discover something new."],[75,397.77,4.46,"And that is actually how we are able to stumble upon a new concept."],[75,402.27,3.42,"As I mentioned before, I am also an artist."],[75,406.11,3.5,"In fact, the artist side of me"],[75,409.61,2.46,"came before I was ever a basketball player."],[75,412.74,2.42,"Basketball, amongst other sports,"],[75,415.16,4.59,"has allowed me to have an ongoing relationship"],[75,419.75,2.42,"as it impacts my practice as an artist."],[75,423.09,3.09,"Sports have given me the tools"],[75,426.21,4.09,"to identify voids in and around my life,"],[75,430.34,3.17,"while also giving me a platform to fill them."],[75,434.56,3.38,"Again, I was a really shy kid,"],[75,437.93,2.67,"so art was my way of expressing myself"],[75,440.64,2.71,"when sports didn't give me the opportunity to."],[75,444.15,3.63,"I used to create drawings of my favorite anime characters."],[75,447.82,2.75,"I would paint pictures of animals."],[75,453.12,4.04,"And over time, I started to focus on people with real stories."],[75,457.2,3.96,"Some of these experiences that I've had with basketball"],[75,461.21,3.09,"have allowed me to gain a new perspective."],[75,465.67,4.63,"One day I decided to cut up some shoelaces and put them on a canvas."],[75,473.64,2.92,"I wasn't totally satisfied with it just yet,"],[75,476.56,3.21,"but I knew that I had something significant there."],[75,480.48,3.5,"Using this as my medium has allowed me"],[75,484.02,4.21,"to communicate my identity through my pieces."],[75,488.9,2.13,"This is what it looks like up close."],[75,493.07,3.42,"One of the signatures that's constantly through my work"],[75,496.53,2.38,"is using pieces of equipment"],[75,498.95,3.25,"that are a part of my personal journey as an athlete."],[75,502.62,4.34,"For example, sometimes that’s cut-up basketballs from a past Globetrotter game."],[75,513.26,3.42,"Other times, that's tennis racquets from my childhood."],[75,529.52,1.17,"Audience: Wow!"],[75,532.78,2.33,"And sometimes, it's just shoelaces that I wore"],[75,535.16,2.0,"when I was doing something important."],[75,537.87,3.75,"Play has given me the flexibility,"],[75,541.66,3.13,"but also the vision to see these materials"],[75,544.83,3.71,"as something more than just what we use on the court or on the field."],[75,548.54,2.5,"All of these items, they have stories behind them."],[75,551.09,2.84,"They have connections to the people that used them,"],[75,553.97,4.13,"whether it's the tennis ball that bounced several thousand times"],[75,558.1,2.25,"to bring people together,"],[75,560.35,2.13,"or it's the spinning basketball --"],[75,567.11,3.63,"That brought smiles to so many people's faces around the world."],[75,570.73,3.63,"Or if it’s shoelaces that I wore on a bad day,"],[75,574.36,2.25,"but I knew that I needed to keep moving."],[75,576.61,3.5,"Play is what brings all of these things together,"],[75,580.16,2.09,"figuratively and physically."],[75,582.66,4.38,"The world's brightest thinkers, engineers of evolution,"],[75,587.04,1.79,"they all play too much."],[75,589.21,1.21,"Seriously."],[75,590.79,1.17,"Thank you."],[76,4.27,4.8,"I've been writing about global health and medical innovation for years,"],[76,9.07,4.8,"and when I tell my friends and family how much progress there's been,"],[76,13.91,2.64,"the reaction is almost always the same."],[76,16.55,2.44,"They had no idea most of it was happening."],[76,19.95,3.37,"Take the most common cause of death today, heart disease."],[76,23.65,4.14,"People today have roughly a quarter the chance of dying from heart disease"],[76,27.79,3.0,"as people did in the 1950s at the same age."],[76,31.16,1.13,"A quarter."],[76,32.9,4.3,"Back then, there were no statins, no cholesterol testing,"],[76,37.23,2.37,"no implanted pacemakers,"],[76,39.64,3.54,"no anti-smoking campaigns, no bans on trans fats,"],[76,43.17,2.97,"no bypass surgery, no CPR."],[76,47.01,1.44,"But hardly anyone hears"],[76,48.48,3.07,"about the long-term impact of those breakthroughs."],[76,52.22,2.17,"When progress happens gradually,"],[76,54.42,1.6,"it's rarely considered news."],[76,57.05,1.6,"And I think it's a problem."],[76,58.69,4.24,"I think it’s part of why people don’t know how important it’s all been --"],[76,62.93,2.1,"public health, vaccines and all the rest --"],[76,65.16,1.44,"in improving people’s lives."],[76,68.03,1.67,"But what I want to tell you about"],[76,69.73,2.54,"is the thing that doesn't make the news at all."],[76,72.6,1.77,"Not the breakthroughs themselves,"],[76,74.41,2.8,"but everything that got in the way of them."],[76,78.48,3.0,"I used to have this impression that breakthroughs were uncommon,"],[76,81.51,1.37,"sporadic,"],[76,82.91,2.34,"sometimes happening by pure chance,"],[76,85.28,2.37,"sometimes by sheer determination."],[76,89.49,1.9,"The way I see it now,"],[76,91.42,4.91,"it's more like there's a continuous stream of medical innovation every year."],[76,97.59,2.87,"In just the last two years, for example,"],[76,100.5,4.3,"we've had a new antiviral against HIV,"],[76,104.83,5.41,"which protects against infections with an efficacy of nearly 100 percent"],[76,110.27,2.94,"with just a single dose given every six months."],[76,114.11,3.57,"There are new drugs that reduce cholesterol levels by 60 percent,"],[76,117.72,1.6,"beyond the effect of statins."],[76,120.65,4.17,"There are new treatments that slow down the progression of certain cancers,"],[76,124.86,5.41,"certain lung cancers, brain cancer and multiple myeloma by half or more."],[76,131.26,2.3,"And in just the last five years,"],[76,133.6,4.04,"we've had new vaccines against four diseases for the very first time."],[76,138.1,1.13,"COVID, of course,"],[76,139.27,2.5,"but also the first malaria vaccine,"],[76,141.81,4.27,"the first chikungunya vaccine and the first vaccine against RSV."],[76,148.01,4.07,"The tools to develop new drugs and vaccines have improved enormously."],[76,152.98,1.87,"Like genome sequencing, for example,"],[76,154.88,2.6,"which has touched almost every part of biology."],[76,158.59,3.74,"Back when the Human Genome Project was completed in 2003,"],[76,162.36,4.71,"it cost $50 million to sequence one person’s genome,"],[76,167.1,1.77,"and it took half a year."],[76,168.9,3.37,"Now it takes under four hours"],[76,172.3,2.0,"and it costs a few hundred dollars."],[76,174.97,3.97,"There's also been a revolution in the technology of microscopes."],[76,178.94,2.4,"Over the past 200 years,"],[76,181.38,4.5,"their resolution has increased over 10,000-fold."],[76,186.28,4.14,"We can now see viruses down to their individual atoms"],[76,190.45,3.94,"and design new drugs to target them extremely precisely."],[76,194.96,2.77,"This is RSV, respiratory syncytial virus."],[76,198.06,1.5,"It's in the middle right there."],[76,199.6,3.04,"And we can now see its atomic structure on the right."],[76,203.2,1.64,"Until the 1930s,"],[76,204.87,2.54,"no one had ever seen a virus."],[76,208.07,5.17,"So if our technology has advanced so much,"],[76,213.28,3.1,"why are so many diseases still untreatable today?"],[76,218.68,3.64,"What I've learned is that technology isn't always the barrier."],[76,223.29,4.2,"Sometimes it’s about the funding, the institutions and the incentives."],[76,228.63,2.54,"We recently got a new malaria vaccine, as I mentioned,"],[76,231.19,3.27,"and when I first heard about it, I was amazed."],[76,234.5,1.53,"I remember learning"],[76,236.07,3.27,"that malaria was a very complicated disease, scientifically."],[76,239.34,3.47,"It's caused by a parasite, not a virus or bacterium."],[76,242.84,4.37,"And that parasite changes shape multiple times during its life cycle,"],[76,247.24,3.5,"which makes it really hard to know what to target with a vaccine."],[76,251.58,2.84,"So I wanted to write about it, this amazing breakthrough."],[76,255.59,3.34,"But one of the first things I learned about this new malaria vaccine"],[76,258.92,3.0,"that was introduced just a few years ago,"],[76,261.93,2.37,"was that it was developed in the '90s."],[76,264.93,1.5,"Decades ago."],[76,266.96,3.7,"The researchers who developed it, struggled to find funding to test it"],[76,270.7,1.9,"at every stage of the process."],[76,273.1,1.8,"There was no commercial incentive."],[76,275.64,2.4,"It's not profitable to develop new drugs"],[76,278.07,2.13,"and vaccines against diseases"],[76,280.24,2.57,"that affect people in poverty, in poorer countries,"],[76,283.21,3.04,"even if millions of children might benefit."],[76,286.25,2.87,"Even if there’s a huge economic benefit as well."],[76,290.19,3.9,"It took foreign aid and philanthropy to fund the research to test it."],[76,294.09,3.64,"And it literally took decades to reach the children who needed it."],[76,298.9,2.13,"When I learned about this,"],[76,301.03,2.74,"I felt like there was nothing to celebrate."],[76,303.8,1.7,"It seemed more like a failure."],[76,306.6,1.4,"How could we let that happen?"],[76,308.04,2.8,"How could it take so long to test a vaccine"],[76,310.87,1.94,"that had already been developed,"],[76,312.84,3.97,"while half a million children were dying from malaria every year?"],[76,316.85,1.44,"Why wasn't that the story?"],[76,320.88,2.14,"Those children aren't coming back."],[76,324.75,4.44,"But what I wanted to know was how we could prevent that from happening again."],[76,330.66,3.17,"Like, what if we could change the economic incentives?"],[76,335.33,3.37,"Well, economists have come up with an idea to do just that."],[76,339.54,2.9,"It’s called an “advanced market commitment.”"],[76,342.47,4.37,"It's where donors commit to buying a vaccine at a certain price per dose,"],[76,346.84,3.34,"but only if it's developed and proven safe and effective."],[76,350.81,3.7,"That commitment gives companies the confidence to invest in it"],[76,354.55,1.67,"in the first place,"],[76,356.22,1.97,"and it can bring vaccines into existence"],[76,358.22,2.54,"that would otherwise never get made."],[76,360.79,1.17,"And most importantly,"],[76,361.99,2.5,"it makes sure that they're manufactured at scale"],[76,364.53,2.04,"and sold at an affordable price,"],[76,366.56,2.14,"so they reach children who need them."],[76,370.3,1.97,"That idea was used over a decade ago"],[76,372.27,2.74,"to help develop new vaccines against pneumococcal disease,"],[76,375.04,3.1,"which is a deadly bacterial infection of the lungs."],[76,379.24,1.74,"Vaccines already existed,"],[76,381.01,4.5,"but they didn't include the strains that were common in Africa and South Asia."],[76,386.65,3.9,"So in 2009, several countries and philanthropists came together"],[76,390.59,3.3,"to fund an advanced market commitment for new vaccines."],[76,394.36,1.2,"And it actually worked."],[76,395.56,1.8,"Several companies developed them,"],[76,397.39,2.8,"and they reached children much faster than usual."],[76,400.23,3.77,"It's estimated that those vaccines have saved over 700,000"],[76,404.03,1.9,"children's lives since then."],[76,411.14,5.21,"This idea to fix market incentives is one way to speed up the process."],[76,417.15,2.27,"But it's not the only thing we can do."],[76,419.45,2.13,"Take childhood leukemia, for example."],[76,422.05,3.87,"It used to be very difficult to test new treatments for the disease"],[76,425.96,1.47,"because leukemia is rare"],[76,427.46,3.44,"and individual hospitals struggled to find enough patients"],[76,430.93,1.43,"to run clinical trials."],[76,433.76,2.23,"So researchers built networks"],[76,436.03,2.97,"across the US and later Europe and Canada"],[76,439.0,2.54,"to help recruit patients from all over those countries"],[76,441.57,1.53,"into larger clinical trials."],[76,444.11,2.4,"That collaboration made it possible"],[76,446.54,2.67,"to test treatments and learn what works faster."],[76,450.58,3.3,"And it's why leukemia is no longer the disease it used to be."],[76,454.85,1.83,"Before the 1970s,"],[76,456.69,4.27,"only around 15 percent of children with leukemia would survive"],[76,460.99,2.34,"even five years from a diagnosis."],[76,463.69,1.87,"That's the bottom line on these graphs."],[76,465.6,1.53,"Two types of leukemia."],[76,467.46,2.47,"Now the top line --"],[76,469.97,2.44,"that figure is 85 percent."],[76,473.0,2.9,"Most children in richer countries today survive"],[76,475.91,2.13,"and are effectively cured of the disease."],[76,478.98,2.87,"This graph is thousands of children"],[76,481.85,2.57,"who wouldn't have survived without that collaboration,"],[76,484.41,1.6,"without that progress."],[76,486.05,2.2,"I want to give you another example."],[76,489.35,2.23,"Back when I was at university,"],[76,491.62,4.2,"we had a substitute lecturer one day in my course on infectious diseases,"],[76,495.86,3.2,"and she told us that our original lecturer was away"],[76,499.1,2.44,"because he had gone to West Africa"],[76,501.56,2.8,"to do research to develop an Ebola vaccine."],[76,504.94,3.2,"I remember sitting in that lecture theater at the back,"],[76,508.14,2.77,"and I was thinking to myself,"],[76,510.94,1.97,"can you just do that?"],[76,512.94,1.97,"Can you just pack up and go?"],[76,515.55,2.84,"It hadn't even occurred to me,"],[76,518.38,3.5,"and it made me realize that all of these breakthroughs"],[76,521.92,1.5,"came from people like him,"],[76,523.45,3.27,"people who changed their lives to make them happen,"],[76,526.72,2.0,"or people who thought differently,"],[76,528.76,2.2,"whether they were scientists, economists,"],[76,530.99,1.64,"operators or managers."],[76,533.23,3.0,"They created the incentives and institutions"],[76,536.27,3.1,"so that generations in the future, all of us,"],[76,539.37,2.74,"wouldn't have to face the same problems that they did."],[76,542.94,2.8,"And what's amazing is that we have an Ebola vaccine now"],[76,545.78,1.87,"against the most common strain."],[76,548.08,3.37,"It wasn't made by my lecturer, but by researchers like him."],[76,551.78,3.0,"And it was hard to develop and test it for all the same reasons."],[76,554.82,2.07,"There was little commercial incentive,"],[76,556.89,1.83,"and it was hard to run clinical trials"],[76,558.75,3.04,"because it was hard to predict where Ebola outbreaks would arise"],[76,561.83,1.9,"and vaccinate people in advance."],[76,564.36,2.6,"So scientists came up with a different idea."],[76,567.86,3.34,"They waited for individual cases of Ebola to appear"],[76,571.2,2.77,"and then quickly vaccinated all of the people around them"],[76,573.97,1.64,"[and] tested the vaccine that way."],[76,575.91,2.33,"That idea, called ring vaccination,"],[76,578.27,3.2,"helped find a vaccine that we now know is very effective."],[76,582.65,4.4,"Now these are just some of the ideas that people have had so far."],[76,588.28,3.6,"But what I've learned from them is that breakthroughs don't just happen"],[76,591.92,2.13,"because we have the tools to make them."],[76,594.46,3.07,"They depend on how innovation happens in the real world."],[76,598.76,2.54,"And there's something else that I've learned from them"],[76,601.3,1.83,"that I find much harder to stomach."],[76,604.0,2.37,"Many of them might not have happened at all."],[76,607.5,4.2,"So when I think back to that stream of innovation,"],[76,611.74,3.3,"all of those breakthroughs and all of that progress that we've seen,"],[76,615.08,1.3,"I do find it amazing."],[76,616.41,1.37,"It's incredible."],[76,618.25,2.2,"But it also really frustrates me."],[76,621.55,3.84,"That's how much progress we've made despite it being so hard."],[76,626.22,3.74,"The good news is people have come up with ideas to change that."],[76,629.96,3.1,"Some of them really work, like the ones I've told you about today,"],[76,633.1,2.04,"and they just haven't been used enough."],[76,636.2,2.47,"But we also need people to come up with new ideas"],[76,638.67,1.94,"and test them and scale them up too."],[76,641.5,2.44,"I think it's more important than ever that we do it,"],[76,643.97,3.44,"because of cuts to science, global health and foreign aid,"],[76,647.41,3.1,"we have to find ways to make our resources go further."],[76,651.15,1.8,"Progress isn't inevitable."],[76,653.95,2.9,"It takes people who decide to persist despite that."],[76,657.82,1.7,"And I think here it takes people"],[76,659.52,2.37,"who recognize something quite simple."],[76,662.76,2.47,"That diseases are not a fact of life."],[76,666.03,2.37,"They're problems that we can solve."],[76,668.43,1.27,"Thank you."],[77,6.84,5.21,"In February 2012, pro skier Elyse Saugstad was carving through the slopes"],[77,12.05,1.63,"of the Cascade Mountains"],[77,13.68,2.63,"when a crack formed in the snowpack above her."],[77,16.6,4.09,"Before she was fully engulfed by a 5,000-ton wall of snow,"],[77,20.81,5.21,"Elyse heard “AVALANCHE!” just in time to react with a lifesaving maneuver."],[77,26.23,3.84,"So, how could she survive this crushing snow surge?"],[77,30.78,5.59,"Avalanches are the rapid descension of snow, ice, rock, or a combination of these"],[77,36.37,1.08,"down a slope."],[77,37.53,3.75,"They can be destructive natural disasters and personal tragedies:"],[77,41.45,5.76,"in the United States alone, avalanches kill an average of 27 people annually."],[77,47.63,3.17,"These alpine nightmares don’t just awaken out of nowhere;"],[77,51.05,2.79,"they need three specific conditions."],[77,54.3,3.25,"First, the mountain needs to have the right slope,"],[77,58.14,3.09,"typically between 30 and 45 degrees."],[77,61.56,3.5,"This is shallow enough to allow snow to build up on the slope,"],[77,65.23,4.25,"yet steep enough to provide enough potential energy for movement."],[77,69.94,3.59,"Second, they typically need a layer of sturdy snow"],[77,73.53,2.79,"deposited over a weak or unstable layer,"],[77,76.49,3.13,"or what’s sometimes called “something over nothing.”"],[77,79.95,2.04,"Take the Cascade Mountain avalanche,"],[77,81.99,5.8,"where recent heavy storms deposited a nearly 1-meter-deep slab of fresh snow."],[77,88.29,2.96,"Below this sat a thin layer of surface hoar."],[77,91.34,2.5,"These are delicate, feathery ice crystals"],[77,93.84,4.55,"that form when water vapor in the air rapidly freezes on the surface of snow,"],[77,98.55,2.17,"almost like dew in the wintertime."],[77,101.31,2.34,"Finally, avalanches need a trigger,"],[77,103.81,3.79,"something to collapse the weak layer and put the snow in motion."],[77,107.85,5.13,"A trigger can be natural, such as heavy snowfall, strong winds, rainfall,"],[77,112.98,2.04,"or even other avalanches."],[77,115.44,5.09,"Or they can be human-caused, like the surface impact of skiers or snowmobilers."],[77,121.03,3.0,"Since fractures can propagate quickly through the snowpack,"],[77,124.04,2.79,"triggers can originate hundreds of meters away—"],[77,126.83,4.75,"upstream, downstream, or even adjacent to the avalanche’s path."],[77,132.09,5.17,"Once in motion, how quickly an avalanche moves depends on the slope’s steepness,"],[77,137.47,3.88,"its exposure or vegetation cover, terrain roughness,"],[77,141.34,2.92,"and the type and amount of snow involved."],[77,144.76,5.55,"For example, dry, light, and loosely bonded snow tends to travel fast,"],[77,150.31,2.33,"forming a powder cloud as it moves."],[77,153.06,2.84,"In contrast, wet snow avalanches,"],[77,155.9,2.33,"caused by water moving through the snowpack,"],[77,158.24,4.46,"travel more slowly but are much denser and can be particularly destructive."],[77,163.12,4.71,"Larger avalanches can travel more than 160 kilometers per hour,"],[77,167.83,4.3,"breaking trees, destroying buildings, and burying roadways."],[77,172.5,4.17,"In 1970, one particularly massive avalanche"],[77,176.76,3.46,"consisting of 50 to 100 million cubic meters"],[77,180.22,3.38,"of glacial ice, snow, mud, and rock"],[77,183.76,3.79,"reached speeds of over 300 kilometers per hour."],[77,187.89,2.84,"This avalanche proved to be the deadliest in history,"],[77,190.73,4.92,"engulfing an entire Peruvian town and killing 18,000 people."],[77,196.32,2.46,"Avalanches aren't always this large,"],[77,198.9,3.92,"but even smaller ones can be dangerous for anyone caught in their path."],[77,202.95,4.75,"As snow rushes downhill, it can behave like both a solid and a liquid,"],[77,207.7,2.5,"making its movement difficult to predict."],[77,210.58,1.63,"At the fastest-moving front,"],[77,212.21,3.21,"surface snow moves faster than the snow beneath,"],[77,215.46,4.59,"pulling victims deep into the snowpack and scattering them across wide areas,"],[77,220.05,1.58,"making rescue difficult."],[77,222.3,5.25,"Once an avalanche slows, the snow loses its fluid-like properties and compacts,"],[77,227.85,3.88,"leaving victims immobilized and unable to escape on their own."],[77,232.23,3.17,"For these reasons, the best way to survive an avalanche"],[77,235.4,1.83,"is to avoid one altogether."],[77,237.94,4.84,"Thankfully, at most ski resorts, parks, and even highways,"],[77,243.16,3.92,"avalanche workers mitigate risk by continuously monitoring the weather,"],[77,247.08,2.84,"stability of the snowpack, and human activity."],[77,250.04,1.58,"If a threat is detected,"],[77,251.62,1.29,"they'll close terrain"],[77,252.96,3.13,"and may even intentionally trigger a controlled avalanche"],[77,256.08,1.88,"with explosives or artillery."],[77,258.55,4.92,"However, there are regions where avalanche risk is not actively mitigated,"],[77,263.47,1.75,"known as the backcountry."],[77,265.55,3.34,"Here, you are responsible for managing your own risk."],[77,269.1,3.04,"Local avalanche centers provide avalanche forecasts,"],[77,272.14,3.0,"and anyone entering the backcountry should understand them,"],[77,275.15,3.54,"be properly trained, and carry essential rescue gear."],[77,278.94,2.54,"And ultimately, in high-hazard terrain,"],[77,281.49,2.79,"there's no way to completely eliminate risk."],[77,284.7,4.38,"The group at the Cascade Mountains consisted of highly experienced skiers."],[77,289.29,3.79,"Yet the area was very exposed, and following a large storm,"],[77,293.08,2.67,"the avalanche danger forecast was elevated."],[77,296.17,2.96,"In the end, three skiers lost their lives."],[77,299.3,3.96,"But not Elyse, as she was able to deploy an inflatable airbag."],[77,303.59,3.79,"These devices are imperfect and a last line of defense,"],[77,307.39,2.59,"yet can keep a victim closer to the surface,"],[77,309.97,3.5,"improving their chance of rescue should disaster strike."],[78,80.68,3.0,"How many of you have felt like that, right?"],[78,84.38,2.1,"It's human nature."],[78,86.52,4.37,"We all have searched for symptoms that we might be having."],[78,91.22,3.07,"Maybe it's a mole on our skin,"],[78,94.33,3.34,"a cough that we’re experiencing"],[78,97.69,1.2,"or a headache."],[78,98.93,2.77,"And what happens after ten minutes?"],[78,102.2,3.57,"We don't become more clear on what we might be having."],[78,106.17,4.07,"We're more confused and more concerned."],[78,110.57,2.1,"So we're talking about joy."],[78,113.24,4.64,"But then how can we have joy when we’re searching about our health,"],[78,117.92,2.5,"and we're having some of these challenges?"],[78,120.45,1.74,"As I said, we all do it."],[78,122.89,2.87,"You know, I used to come out and ask people,"],[78,125.79,4.04,"\"How many of you have searched health conditions online?\""],[78,130.59,1.33,"Now I say,"],[78,131.96,4.61,"\"How many of you have searched for health issues today?\""],[78,137.3,2.6,"Because I want to share a statistic with you."],[78,140.54,1.27,"A billion."],[78,142.51,2.5,"A billion, nine zeros."],[78,145.41,4.04,"That's how many search on health"],[78,149.45,2.84,"there is every single day."],[78,153.78,2.17,"And it's actually going up."],[78,156.25,2.37,"And when you put it into context --"],[78,158.66,4.0,"how many is that every hour?"],[78,163.19,1.63,"Every minute."],[78,166.0,2.34,"And up until recently,"],[78,168.37,2.97,"I was the chief medical officer at WebMD."],[78,171.37,3.64,"And many of you likely ended up at WebMD."],[78,175.97,2.7,"And at WebMD, the motto is"],[78,178.71,3.5,"better information leads to better health."],[78,182.68,4.97,"And as a physician, I firmly believe that."],[78,187.65,1.74,"I want you to become informed."],[78,189.42,2.74,"I want you to become empowered."],[78,192.92,2.2,"But the challenge is"],[78,195.16,3.6,"information isn't knowledge."],[78,200.06,2.23,"Remember the phrase TMI?"],[78,202.63,1.8,"Do people still say that?"],[78,204.47,1.57,"Too much information."],[78,206.07,3.1,"Typically, we would say it about what?"],[78,209.21,2.07,"We'd say it about"],[78,211.31,3.2,"someone's giving you too much personal information."],[78,215.04,1.6,"Makes you feel awkward."],[78,217.01,1.6,"Maybe a little anxious."],[78,219.58,4.1,"Well, TMI also is around health."],[78,223.69,1.77,"Too much information,"],[78,225.49,2.94,"when you're searching for your symptoms,"],[78,228.46,2.47,"actually can create anxiety,"],[78,230.96,1.74,"can actually be dangerous."],[78,233.86,2.6,"So I saw a patient two years ago."],[78,236.5,1.17,"True story."],[78,237.7,1.3,"I'm going to call her Mary,"],[78,239.0,1.8,"that wasn't actually her name."],[78,240.84,4.74,"But Mary came in because she had what's called cerumen impaction."],[78,245.94,1.37,"Anyone ever hear that?"],[78,247.34,3.87,"Basically means your ear's clogged with wax."],[78,251.58,1.33,"It's very common."],[78,253.38,3.07,"Now Mary had searched it online,"],[78,256.49,5.91,"and she saw, rightfully, that hearing loss can lead to dementia."],[78,262.86,5.0,"So she was very concerned that she was having decreased hearing,"],[78,267.9,1.74,"even though it was only for a day."],[78,269.67,1.4,"Very concerned about it."],[78,271.37,4.14,"She also read that too much earwax"],[78,275.51,3.04,"could be signs of infection,"],[78,278.57,2.0,"which I'll tell you, that's not the truth."],[78,280.91,3.97,"But she was very focused on getting the wax out of her ear."],[78,285.98,4.3,"So she had read that hydrogen peroxide is actually pretty good"],[78,290.29,2.37,"if you mix it equally with water."],[78,293.36,5.34,"But what she did was she microwaved it,"],[78,298.73,2.34,"and then she put it in her ear."],[78,301.83,6.41,"And obviously she came into the office because she had a burned ear canal,"],[78,308.27,2.94,"very red, very irritated."],[78,311.68,3.84,"And I said to her, \"Mary, why?\""],[78,315.91,2.33,"And maybe I shouldn't have said it like that,"],[78,318.28,2.1,"but that exactly is how I said it."],[78,320.38,1.53,"\"Why?\""],[78,321.95,1.7,"And she said,"],[78,323.69,2.87,"\"I read it online."],[78,327.59,3.07,"And I thought it wouldn't hurt."],[78,332.33,1.4,"But it did.\""],[78,333.73,2.9,"And that's the challenge."],[78,337.94,3.5,"Remember the phrase hypochondriac?"],[78,341.84,2.47,"People that have symptoms"],[78,344.34,4.54,"and every symptom is always the most serious?"],[78,349.45,2.67,"Well, now we have this phrase, you might have heard it,"],[78,352.15,1.74,"cyberchondriac, right?"],[78,353.88,1.57,"You go online --"],[78,355.49,6.14,"I always feel cyberchondriac should be like crime solvers"],[78,361.62,3.7,"because they look at every detail,"],[78,365.36,2.5,"everything is magnified,"],[78,367.9,3.6,"and it's always the worst-case scenario."],[78,373.27,4.0,"My 12-year-old is a little bit of a cyberchondriac."],[78,378.38,5.47,"So this summer he had a bit of a blister"],[78,383.88,2.04,"on his foot"],[78,385.95,5.07,"because he needed these Kobe cleats that were too big for him,"],[78,391.05,4.27,"but he insisted he needed them, and he developed a blister."],[78,396.49,2.0,"It wasn't healing after a couple days,"],[78,398.53,2.23,"it started to look a little infectious,"],[78,400.76,2.74,"and my wife took him to urgent care."],[78,404.57,1.64,"They cultured it,"],[78,406.2,1.87,"they gave him an antibiotic."],[78,408.11,4.67,"They came home that evening, and I said, you know, “How did it go?”"],[78,413.21,1.87,"And he says to me,"],[78,415.11,1.8,"\"You know,"],[78,416.95,1.5,"it could be MRSA.\""],[78,419.75,3.27,"And I thought, \"That's pretty clever for a 12-year-old."],[78,423.05,2.4,"But MRSA is still kind of unusual.\""],[78,425.49,4.5,"And he said, \"And this antibiotic doesn't cover it.\""],[78,429.99,5.17,"And he was right in terms of the information that he found."],[78,436.1,3.07,"Now, the next day,"],[78,439.2,2.54,"I get a call from the doctor's office."],[78,442.77,1.17,"It's MRSA."],[78,445.21,2.57,"And I think, how am I going to tell him that?"],[78,448.88,4.2,"But it was, and he did need to have the antibiotic changed."],[78,453.12,1.44,"And my point is,"],[78,454.58,4.17,"sometimes the information you search is correct."],[78,458.79,1.5,"Often it is."],[78,460.32,1.74,"But you have to put it into context."],[78,462.06,4.5,"Now, just to be fair, my wife had some knee pain,"],[78,466.6,2.54,"just last week after starting back up at the gym,"],[78,469.17,2.44,"and he goes over to her, and he says,"],[78,471.6,1.6,"\"Could be jumper's knee,\""],[78,473.24,3.27,"which is a patellar tendonitis."],[78,476.54,1.17,"It's not."],[78,477.74,1.94,"It's just, she just started to work out,"],[78,479.68,2.37,"so now her knee is bothering her."],[78,482.41,1.14,"But I get it."],[78,483.58,5.47,"These are powerful tools in terms of generative AI"],[78,489.09,2.64,"and ChatGPT and Google."],[78,492.56,2.44,"But they don't know your history."],[78,495.02,3.4,"They can't do a physical exam,"],[78,498.46,4.3,"and in many ways, they're causing more confusion."],[78,503.23,2.37,"What I talk about is"],[78,505.64,2.27,"it's really the issue"],[78,507.9,4.6,"of clarity versus confusion."],[78,513.24,5.47,"And if you want to have joy, if you want to use symptom checkers"],[78,518.72,4.44,"and the web and AI to understand your health better --"],[78,523.19,3.04,"and I want you to become more empowered --"],[78,526.62,5.44,"it is about having clarity versus confusion."],[78,533.4,2.27,"And that can be challenging."],[78,535.7,3.47,"There’s a lot of misinformation out there,"],[78,539.2,5.17,"and algorithms often drive you to inaccurate information."],[78,545.41,1.17,"So ..."],[78,548.48,4.17,"The feeds on Instagram are often driven by algorithms"],[78,552.65,1.6,"and what you search."],[78,555.09,3.24,"So my younger son -- that’s my older son --"],[78,558.32,4.24,"I have a younger son who very much wants a golden retriever."],[78,562.93,4.04,"So my Instagram feed is loaded with goldies,"],[78,567.0,2.13,"[so] I think everyone has a goldie."],[78,569.73,2.74,"Or it's on a lot of wellness information."],[78,572.47,2.34,"Having been at WebMD and a physician."],[78,576.07,2.37,"And like many people in the population,"],[78,578.48,4.4,"I'm on a statin for primary prevention because of high lipids."],[78,582.91,2.0,"And I've been starting to ..."],[78,585.45,2.77,"I like to read other doctors' feeds,"],[78,588.25,3.4,"and there's a bunch of cardiologists that are saying"],[78,591.65,2.4,"you don't really need to be on a statin."],[78,594.09,2.87,"It really should be diet and exercise,"],[78,596.99,2.27,"and that statins actually can increase dementia,"],[78,599.26,1.47,"not decrease dementia."],[78,600.73,2.8,"Now here I am, I've been a physician for 20-plus years,"],[78,603.57,2.7,"I know the data on statins,"],[78,606.27,3.5,"and I’m starting to think maybe I don’t need a statin."],[78,610.11,2.74,"And then I’m getting more doctors’ feeds that are saying"],[78,612.84,1.83,"you don't need to be on a statin."],[78,615.75,2.23,"And I'm thinking, this can't be right."],[78,618.01,1.17,"So what do I do?"],[78,619.18,1.7,"I dig a little deeper."],[78,621.18,2.34,"I see that many of these physicians"],[78,623.55,3.27,"that are talking about this are selling a particular product."],[78,626.82,4.41,"Some of them have had some issues with their medical license,"],[78,631.26,4.84,"but the algorithms drive you"],[78,636.1,4.17,"to the same type of information that you're already receiving."],[78,640.3,3.54,"And that can make it more challenging"],[78,643.87,4.2,"because it really comes to an issue of trust."],[78,648.64,2.4,"Who can you trust?"],[78,651.51,3.2,"And it's about that human interaction."],[78,654.75,4.54,"Are you going to trust a chatbot or an influencer?"],[78,660.26,3.94,"Are you going to trust the relationship that you have with your doctor,"],[78,664.23,2.17,"the nurse, the pharmacist?"],[78,668.3,3.57,"When you think about searching for health information online,"],[78,671.9,1.64,"and we all do,"],[78,673.54,4.87,"and that is a good thing and can be a powerful tool,"],[78,678.44,2.5,"because I want you to be empowered,"],[78,680.98,1.77,"here's what I want you to think."],[78,684.41,4.61,"When you search for financial information,"],[78,689.05,1.24,"what do you do?"],[78,691.02,5.47,"Do you just listen to any influencer that has a million followers"],[78,696.53,1.9,"or two million followers,"],[78,698.43,4.94,"to do cryptocurrency or to invest in a certain product?"],[78,703.77,1.17,"No."],[78,704.97,1.14,"What do you do?"],[78,706.14,4.14,"You check the credentials of that person"],[78,710.27,4.94,"and try to corroborate that information somewhere else."],[78,716.11,1.5,"You think, does it make sense?"],[78,717.65,5.91,"Can I really make 50 times my investment in a short period of time?"],[78,724.52,1.9,"And what do most of us do?"],[78,727.66,2.47,"We ask someone that we can trust."],[78,730.86,3.44,"We ask an expert, does this make sense?"],[78,735.57,1.37,"And why do you do that?"],[78,737.9,1.44,"Because it's your money."],[78,740.24,1.67,"And that's important."],[78,741.94,1.2,"Your future."],[78,745.08,5.37,"But I would argue your health is even more important."],[78,750.81,2.37,"Certainly for your future."],[78,754.12,2.47,"And sometimes when it comes to our health,"],[78,756.62,3.9,"we're very willing to have this attitude of,"],[78,760.56,1.33,"\"Why not try it?\""],[78,762.79,1.67,"\"What's the harm?\""],[78,765.1,4.0,"We're willing to listen to anyone."],[78,769.13,2.14,"You know, someone else had a good experience,"],[78,771.27,1.7,"so maybe we will too."],[78,773.37,5.11,"But you're much more discriminating when it comes to your finances."],[78,779.84,1.77,"So we talk about joy."],[78,782.01,5.04,"We talk about [how] our health often contributes to our joy"],[78,787.08,1.74,"or takes away from our joy."],[78,789.85,4.71,"So it's important that you do become empowered,"],[78,794.59,3.0,"you do become educated,"],[78,797.63,3.84,"but then you have to take that next step"],[78,801.5,2.74,"and think about that connection"],[78,804.27,1.14,"with a human."],[78,806.34,3.84,"I think there's a great role for technology,"],[78,810.17,2.94,"although sometimes I think there might be too much technology."],[78,813.11,2.64,"I don't know if others agree with that as well."],[78,816.11,1.67,"But when it comes to your health,"],[78,817.81,6.47,"the most important element that you have,"],[78,824.29,2.6,"you want clarity,"],[78,826.89,1.83,"not confusion."],[78,829.59,5.5,"And that comes from those elements of knowing who the messenger is,"],[78,835.13,1.74,"checking that information,"],[78,836.9,2.47,"corroborating that information,"],[78,839.37,3.9,"knowing if it sounds right to you."],[78,843.74,4.47,"And then talking about it to someone that you can trust."],[78,848.98,1.17,"Thank you."],[79,7.0,4.88,"In 1949, businessman Frank McNamara was about to pay for dinner"],[79,11.88,4.09,"when he realized something terrible: he’d forgotten his wallet."],[79,16.89,4.46,"While this scenario isn’t that uncommon, McNamara’s response was."],[79,21.98,3.17,"Determined to ensure he’d never be caught without cash again,"],[79,25.15,2.42,"he invented the Diners Club Card—"],[79,27.65,3.38,"a wallet-sized piece of cardboard that allowed carriers to dine"],[79,31.03,3.75,"at associated restaurants and settle their bills at the end of each month."],[79,35.24,3.88,"McNamara wasn’t the first person to codify the IOU—"],[79,39.12,2.09,"there’s evidence of deferred payment systems"],[79,41.2,2.88,"stretching all the way back to ancient Mesopotamia."],[79,44.33,1.67,"In America’s Wild West,"],[79,46.0,3.88,"ranchers and farmers used metal plates as credit placeholders."],[79,49.92,3.38,"And just a few years before McNamara's dining disaster,"],[79,53.3,1.75,"many department stores and airlines"],[79,55.05,3.71,"had already begun rolling out reward programs and charge cards."],[79,59.05,2.5,"But the Diners Club Card was different."],[79,61.77,3.71,"Where previous credit arrangements saw one business authorizing credit"],[79,65.48,1.38,"for one individual,"],[79,66.85,4.38,"McNamara’s card gave users credit with over two dozen"],[79,71.28,2.29,"otherwise unassociated businesses."],[79,73.99,2.54,"This decentralized credit was revolutionary,"],[79,76.53,4.88,"and in just one year, the Diners Club Card gained 10,000 users."],[79,81.74,3.13,"Soon, several US banks recruited local merchants"],[79,84.87,2.04,"and launched their own credit programs."],[79,87.12,4.96,"For these merchants, credit cards provided increased business and upfront financing."],[79,92.21,3.04,"For consumers, the cards offered financial flexibility,"],[79,95.26,1.96,"allowing them to make larger purchases"],[79,97.22,2.84,"so long as they could pay them off at the end of each month."],[79,100.05,3.34,"And the banks profited from small fees on each transaction."],[79,103.56,4.04,"But soon, banks found another way to make money from these cards."],[79,107.73,3.5,"They began allowing cardholders to pay off their debt more slowly"],[79,111.23,3.13,"for an additional fee called an interest payment."],[79,114.57,4.88,"Essentially, cardholders could choose to pay just part of their monthly bill,"],[79,119.45,2.92,"and the bank would add a percentage of what they didn't pay"],[79,122.37,1.17,"to next month's bill."],[79,123.95,3.59,"Even in these early days, this system wasn't without problems."],[79,127.75,5.88,"In 1958, Bank of America sent 60,000 unsolicited credit cards to residents"],[79,133.63,1.54,"of Fresno, California."],[79,135.34,2.79,"While this promotion was intended to attract new customers,"],[79,138.13,3.75,"it mostly led to rampant card theft and unpaid bills."],[79,142.1,4.38,"Banks also struggled to process all the payment paperwork these cards produced."],[79,146.77,2.71,"At this time, charging a credit card involved"],[79,149.48,2.92,"stamping a card’s embossed details onto carbon paper"],[79,152.4,3.09,"and sending out these charge slips for manual processing."],[79,155.57,1.63,"But as credit card use boomed,"],[79,157.19,3.17,"banks were left with warehouses of unprocessed charge slips,"],[79,160.36,3.25,"creating delays that prevented them from charging interest."],[79,163.95,4.8,"Despite these initial losses, US banks remained devoted to credit cards."],[79,168.91,2.84,"At this time, it was illegal for banks to build branches"],[79,171.75,1.5,"outside their home state,"],[79,173.25,4.42,"so mailing credit cards was their best bet for attracting out of state customers."],[79,177.92,2.04,"And once they brought in these new clients,"],[79,179.97,3.8,"they could sell them big ticket items like home and automobile loans."],[79,184.01,2.92,"This led banks to double down on credit cards."],[79,187.06,3.42,"They invested heavily in early computers to process charge slips,"],[79,190.52,4.3,"and began running ads that promised a more luxurious standard of living."],[79,195.15,3.25,"These ad campaigns shifted the American attitude towards credit"],[79,198.4,2.54,"from one of shame and financial dependence"],[79,200.95,2.21,"to a celebration of financial freedom."],[79,203.82,4.75,"However, the reality of these lending systems was far more exploitative."],[79,208.79,6.34,"From 1956 to 1967, consumer debt increased by 133%,"],[79,215.21,4.46,"and concerns about consumer safety led to a surge of anti-credit activism"],[79,219.67,1.33,"through the 1960s."],[79,221.22,3.42,"This movement was dealt a devastating blow in 1968,"],[79,224.64,3.29,"when the Supreme Court removed the cap on state interest rates,"],[79,227.93,3.29,"allowing massive interest hikes throughout the 1970s."],[79,231.52,4.46,"New complications emerged in late 80s with the invention of credit scores,"],[79,236.06,3.29,"which reinforced the racial, gender, and class biases"],[79,239.36,2.59,"already impacting credit card applications."],[79,242.24,3.92,"Today, credit cards are a $500 billion industry."],[79,246.16,3.92,"Banks consider these lines of credit when deciding whether or not to approve loans,"],[79,250.08,3.34,"incentivizing customers to maintain multiple credit cards."],[79,253.46,3.42,"And since most users don't pay off their bills in full each month,"],[79,256.88,2.96,"they rack up debt and endless interest payments."],[79,260.17,6.34,"By the end of 2023, credit card debt in the US alone exceeded $1 trillion."],[79,266.8,3.17,"So while the earliest credit cards may have been the most limited,"],[79,269.97,2.84,"they might have actually been the best for our wallets."],[80,3.88,1.17,"I’m Yohanis Riek,"],[80,5.08,2.5,"I'm a doctor and a social entrepreneur"],[80,7.59,2.21,"based and working in South Sudan."],[80,10.71,3.71,"When I was growing up in my village, my future was to be a cattle keeper."],[80,15.01,3.71,"Through being someone mobile, the nomadic culture,"],[80,18.72,4.25,"we became adaptive to other new culture that we learn as we migrate."],[80,22.98,3.88,"And that experience enabled me to connect with a lot of other people."],[80,26.9,3.92,"And so future kept changing based on the new life I was experiencing."],[80,30.86,3.55,"The future was, you know, to be someone successful,"],[80,34.45,2.79,"someone who can be able to help that community."],[80,37.58,3.59,"But when I was around 14 or 15 years,"],[80,41.16,3.5,"I don't know exact age, but by estimation, at that time,"],[80,44.67,2.34,"I was conscripted into the army forcefully."],[80,47.63,2.96,"And that's how I became a child soldier."],[80,51.46,3.84,"The cost of that civil war was being felt by everybody."],[80,55.72,6.38,"So the regional neighbors decided to bring the two warring parties together"],[80,62.14,1.29,"for negotiation,"],[80,63.48,2.88,"which led to the peace agreement in 2005."],[80,66.69,3.5,"And that was supported by the United States government."],[80,70.23,3.67,"And I was sponsored by the government to go to school."],[80,73.94,1.63,"When the negotiation started,"],[80,75.57,4.46,"the first thing first was to demobilize child soldiers from the army,"],[80,80.03,4.84,"and the immediate activities to do after demobilization were go to school."],[80,85.62,3.21,"The first immediate motivation for me to become a doctor"],[80,88.83,2.63,"was seeing my family members die"],[80,91.5,2.17,"because of diseases that could be treated."],[80,93.72,4.13,"My dad, particularly my sister, who was young at the time,"],[80,97.84,3.13,"you know, motivated me, when I learned about school,"],[80,100.97,5.13,"to study something that can help fight the disease burden in my community."],[80,106.6,2.63,"I was the first to go to school in my family,"],[80,109.23,1.88,"and also the first to become a doctor."],[80,111.15,2.92,"And not only my immediate family, but in my community."],[80,114.74,2.63,"2009 was my first year at university,"],[80,117.36,3.67,"and that's when I also started my nonprofit organization"],[80,121.03,1.54,"called Yo' Care South Sudan."],[80,122.91,3.96,"Yo' Care South Sudan is a non-profit health care organization"],[80,126.92,3.46,"that is focused on infectious disease control and management,"],[80,130.38,4.13,"as well as the primary-care part of the health system."],[80,134.55,1.92,"Through static health facilities"],[80,136.47,3.59,"and also through mobile medical teams that go to communities"],[80,140.09,2.33,"that are far away from our health facilities,"],[80,142.43,4.42,"where the gaps are huge and people continue to die every day."],[80,147.81,3.38,"For the last 17 years, we've had many successes."],[80,151.19,2.54,"We are proud that we have saved lives."],[80,153.78,3.67,"When we vaccinate children, children are able to go to school"],[80,157.44,3.71,"and they are able to become an important member of society."],[80,161.49,2.29,"There are children that have gone to school now."],[80,163.78,2.21,"If we did not do that vaccination,"],[80,166.0,2.75,"maybe they would have died from those childhood diseases"],[80,168.79,1.67,"that are preventable."],[80,170.92,2.38,"We understand that as an organization,"],[80,173.29,2.29,"we cannot be able to do everything by ourselves,"],[80,175.63,2.63,"so we help communities organize themselves."],[80,178.3,2.34,"The people who come to our health facilities,"],[80,180.68,1.13,"we train them."],[80,181.84,4.38,"Many patients that we observed became service providers themselves."],[80,186.27,5.59,"One of our patients, called Lillian, later on became a peer navigator."],[80,191.9,3.63,"Now she is able to identify people who are also suffering"],[80,195.53,4.38,"and referring them to the health centers for them to come for testing"],[80,199.95,2.88,"and also to come to get their medication,"],[80,202.82,2.29,"as well as delivering the training,"],[80,205.12,5.59,"delivering medical services to the people who cannot access medical facilities."],[80,210.75,2.71,"People must be able to take charge of their own health."],[80,213.5,3.88,"Luckily, our name, Yo' Care, means the care we provide is yours."],[80,217.38,2.59,"Wherever we work, we organize the community."],[80,219.97,2.84,"We train them so they can take charge of the future."],[80,224.3,3.38,"Yo' Care focuses on long-term health solutions"],[80,227.72,4.09,"because we understand that health is not just an immediate need,"],[80,231.81,1.42,"it's a long-term need."],[80,233.27,3.04,"And because of that, some of the solutions we provide"],[80,236.36,3.71,"have been funded by external donors such as USAID"],[80,240.11,1.17,"or US government."],[80,241.32,3.34,"And when the current US government came into power,"],[80,244.7,2.5,"we got an abrupt notification."],[80,247.24,3.09,"\"Tomorrow, we suspend all activities.\""],[80,250.96,2.88,"So that had the immediate effect on us."],[80,253.88,3.04,"The first immediate effect was on the patients."],[80,256.92,2.04,"Before this happened,"],[80,259.0,4.96,"we had more than 60,000 people that were relying on our medical services."],[80,264.01,3.88,"As of now, we are supporting 32,000."],[80,268.35,1.88,"Thousands of communities"],[80,270.23,3.54,"are not able to access basic health care services."],[80,273.81,5.17,"And also from having eight health facilities that were directly supporting"],[80,278.98,4.17,"to only two facilities right now that we are supporting."],[80,283.45,3.88,"Our manpower reduced from 53"],[80,287.37,2.59,"to only eight essential staff."],[80,290.29,3.46,"Now when that suddenly cut, they ask us, \"Where do we go?\""],[80,294.46,1.21,"\"Where do we go?\""],[80,295.67,2.17,"Patients are also asking us, \"Where do we go?"],[80,297.84,1.29,"Why did you cut this?\""],[80,299.13,2.34,"Didn't allow us time to prepare to close."],[80,301.51,4.88,"It didn't allow us time to look for other financing alternatives."],[80,306.43,4.0,"That threatens the very existence of the organization."],[80,311.22,2.71,"The most important thing to invest in is the people."],[80,314.48,2.71,"There is no country without people in the first place."],[80,318.4,2.71,"We are the youngest nation in the world right now."],[80,321.15,3.96,"In 2011, South Sudan became an independent country."],[80,325.45,3.75,"Given that it's not part of that global community yet"],[80,329.2,4.25,"in terms of technology, in terms of media, in terms of other connectedness,"],[80,333.5,2.13,"we feel neglected."],[80,336.21,2.13,"We have ongoing conflict,"],[80,338.38,2.88,"we have natural, climate issues."],[80,341.96,1.71,"Our voices are not being heard."],[80,344.3,1.17,"My hope for the future,"],[80,345.51,2.75,"first, as a doctor, is a healthy society."],[80,348.3,2.04,"And that is what we're working for."],[80,350.39,3.0,"Because when the community is stable, when society is stable,"],[80,353.43,2.33,"we will contribute meaningfully to the world"],[80,355.81,2.46,"and people will know us for positive things"],[80,358.27,2.5,"instead of being known for conflict and suffering."],[80,361.69,3.29,"Having a new country is just like when you have a new home."],[80,365.03,2.63,"You just bought your new house,"],[80,367.65,2.17,"you are very excited, you are very happy."],[80,369.87,2.46,"You finally have something you can call home."],[80,372.37,2.96,"And that is the experience that we have as a nation."],[80,375.7,4.25,"Even if we are in crisis, protracted crisis, persistent crisis,"],[80,380.0,1.79,"we are very happy that --"],[80,381.79,2.63,"personally, I'm very proud that I have a country"],[80,384.46,1.67,"because I believe one day,"],[80,386.17,1.75,"through the contribution I'm making"],[80,387.93,2.04,"or through the contributions of others,"],[80,389.97,1.5,"we will be better"],[80,391.47,2.25,"despite the challenges we are currently facing."],[81,6.92,2.67,"In autumn 2007,"],[81,9.71,6.55,"a Tuscan father and son unearthed one of the largest white truffles ever found."],[81,16.47,6.55,"It later sold at auction for a record-breaking $330,000—"],[81,23.19,5.46,"more than six times the price of its weight in gold at the time."],[81,29.48,4.34,"Truffles are one of the world’s most expensive foods—"],[81,33.91,5.13,"in part because global demand often outstrips supply."],[81,39.41,2.38,"But why are truffles so rare?"],[81,42.12,2.84,"And why don't we just farm more of them?"],[81,45.71,6.71,"The answers lie in the truffle’s unique and somewhat mysterious biology."],[81,53.13,4.0,"Truffles refer to nearly 200 species of fungi,"],[81,57.26,3.67,"of which around 30 are used in commercial trade."],[81,61.27,3.34,"The most sought-after truffles are native to Europe,"],[81,64.73,6.17,"confined to regions with lime-rich dry soil and light summer rains."],[81,71.15,4.55,"Each species grows under the canopy of specific trees,"],[81,75.7,3.88,"where they form tight symbiotic relationships."],[81,79.91,5.42,"A truffle’s mycelium, the vegetative root-like structure of the fungus,"],[81,85.46,6.55,"wraps itself around the tree roots and swaps water and nutrients for sugars."],[81,92.8,3.13,"While truffles themselves aren’t mushrooms,"],[81,95.97,4.21,"the parts of truffles we covet are, like mushrooms,"],[81,100.22,2.54,"the fruiting body of the fungus."],[81,103.43,2.71,"But while mushrooms grow above ground,"],[81,106.19,5.34,"allowing their spores to be dispersed by both wind and foraging animals,"],[81,111.65,3.5,"truffles grow entirely below the soil."],[81,115.74,5.42,"So, they release a powerful aroma to lure hungry forest animals"],[81,121.2,2.38,"to come to do their spreading."],[81,123.91,4.34,"If these animals happen to drop these spores near the roots"],[81,128.29,2.29,"of a particular type of tree,"],[81,130.84,4.96,"only then might a truffle develop, several years later."],[81,136.09,6.09,"This pungent aroma is also what’s made truffles alluring to humans."],[81,142.56,4.09,"In fact, much of what we perceive as truffle flavor"],[81,146.64,2.38,"comes from its unique scent."],[81,149.19,6.76,"A single species typically emits 30 to 60 different volatile compounds,"],[81,155.99,4.0,"which are produced by both the truffle and the bacteria and yeast"],[81,159.99,1.33,"that colonize them."],[81,161.78,1.71,"But despite their scent,"],[81,163.49,5.67,"finding these subterranean gems within a dense forest is far from easy."],[81,169.46,6.92,"As early as the 1400s, Europeans used pigs as truffle-finding companions."],[81,176.92,6.34,"Truffles emit dimethyl sulfide, a compound that reeks of cabbage,"],[81,183.39,2.17,"which helps draw the pigs in."],[81,185.97,2.09,"But when a pig finds a truffle,"],[81,188.1,2.84,"they’re apt to take a bite out of the profit."],[81,191.1,5.13,"So many truffle hunters began replacing pigs with dogs who,"],[81,196.4,4.71,"while not natural-born truffle hunters, are more easily trained."],[81,201.53,5.71,"Meanwhile, others learn to track their prize by following species of flies"],[81,207.33,3.25,"that are known to lay their eggs near truffles."],[81,211.08,5.84,"Nevertheless, today, the global market still demands more truffles"],[81,216.92,4.17,"than any pig, dog, or fly can sniff out."],[81,221.38,5.21,"And truffles continue to grow even rarer— and more expensive—"],[81,226.81,5.63,"as deforestation and climate change shrink their suitable terrain."],[81,233.19,2.13,"So why don't we just farm them?"],[81,236.02,1.96,"We do, to some extent."],[81,238.28,5.25,"In 1808, a French farmer became the first to cultivate truffles"],[81,243.53,3.79,"when he planted acorns under truffle-bearing oaks,"],[81,247.41,5.09,"and then transplanted the seedlings with— unknowingly at the time—"],[81,252.66,2.04,"some fungi companions."],[81,255.08,2.21,"By the early 1970s,"],[81,257.3,5.13,"scientists cracked how to artificially inoculate tree seedlings,"],[81,262.63,3.92,"which allowed growers to sow certain species of truffles"],[81,266.55,3.79,"in orchards far outside their native range."],[81,271.31,5.3,"However, truffles are still not considered a domesticated crop."],[81,276.86,2.33,"There are just too many unknowns,"],[81,279.27,5.5,"as researchers are still working out the precise temperature, precipitation,"],[81,284.82,4.92,"elevation, soil pH, and other environmental factors"],[81,289.74,3.54,"that determine a truffle orchard’s success."],[81,293.87,3.71,"Part of the problem is that truffles need to mate"],[81,297.79,3.09,"to form that valuable fruiting body,"],[81,301.21,4.88,"yet truffles sex is still largely a mystery."],[81,306.47,4.0,"Researchers know that these fungi are hermaphrodites"],[81,310.47,2.25,"that cannot reproduce alone."],[81,313.18,2.67,"So, when a truffle reproduces,"],[81,315.89,5.71,"one fungus takes on the maternal role and the other the paternal role,"],[81,321.78,4.96,"each contributing half of the genes that will form the spores."],[81,327.11,5.46,"However, when researchers search the area near a truffle fruiting body,"],[81,332.58,3.92,"they only ever find maternal mycelium."],[81,337.08,6.01,"Where the paternal partner lives— or hides— remains unknown."],[81,344.21,5.63,"So each time a hopeful truffle enthusiast begins a new farm,"],[81,349.97,3.0,"there’s no guarantee truffles will grow,"],[81,353.06,4.09,"and they'll have to wait four years or more to find out."],[81,357.6,2.04,"But many take the gamble,"],[81,359.65,4.09,"as growers know that if they’re patient and lucky,"],[81,363.9,3.04,"they might just dig up gold."],[82,6.88,4.42,"In the early 1980s, a group of scientists set out to conduct a long-term study"],[82,11.3,1.17,"on heart health."],[82,12.55,4.67,"They focused on Finnish men, recruiting over 2,000 participants"],[82,17.22,2.96,"and monitoring everything from their behaviors, diets,"],[82,20.18,3.96,"lifestyle choices, and fitness over the course of 20 years."],[82,24.52,1.54,"As they dug into the data,"],[82,26.06,3.04,"researchers were stunned to find that one habit—"],[82,29.11,3.8,"something they hadn’t expected— was linked to better health:"],[82,33.32,1.88,"frequent sauna bathing."],[82,35.41,3.21,"Yet this likely didn’t surprise the Finnish participants."],[82,38.87,4.21,"Finland is a country with 9,000 years of sauna traditions."],[82,43.41,1.29,"And they’re not alone:"],[82,44.79,4.75,"Roman balneae, Japanese onsen, and Indigenous American sweat lodges"],[82,49.55,3.0,"are just a few examples of how cultures across the globe"],[82,52.55,4.25,"have long considered exposure to extreme temperatures therapeutic."],[82,57.14,4.59,"But today, scientists are only just beginning to unravel how and why"],[82,61.72,1.33,"this may be the case."],[82,63.48,3.71,"So, what exactly is happening in your body when you feel the heat?"],[82,67.81,2.75,"To understand how extreme heat affects the body,"],[82,70.65,2.88,"let’s explore what happens when you enter a sauna."],[82,73.74,4.5,"Traditional Finnish sauna temperatures can top out at 90°C,"],[82,78.24,2.42,"or just below the boiling point of water,"],[82,80.74,3.21,"and are typically dry, at about 20% humidity."],[82,84.0,4.42,"This blast of dry heat ignites your thermoregulatory response."],[82,88.5,3.96,"Specialized temperature-detecting proteins in the skin change shape,"],[82,92.5,4.25,"triggering sensory neurons to send this high heat message to the brain."],[82,96.84,4.92,"Blood vessels widen, and your heart rate ticks up to circulate blood faster."],[82,101.93,3.38,"To shield your vital organs from intolerable temperatures,"],[82,105.35,4.5,"your skin absorbs heat, creeping up to 41°C,"],[82,109.86,2.54,"and dumps this heat from your body's surface."],[82,112.57,1.17,"You sweat."],[82,114.19,3.92,"If this response reminds you of exercise, that’s no coincidence."],[82,118.32,3.8,"Physiologists who study sauna use have likened the body's heat response"],[82,122.12,1.5,"to a moderate workout."],[82,123.7,3.38,"And while time in the sauna isn't a replacement for exercise,"],[82,127.08,5.13,"regular exposure to controlled heat may stress the heart and, over time,"],[82,132.21,3.34,"strengthen the cardiovascular system in similar ways."],[82,136.01,4.17,"Heat may also complement exercise and help aid recovery."],[82,140.26,4.05,"In strenuous workouts, muscle fibers acquire microtears."],[82,144.56,4.09,"This summons immune cells that help repair and strengthen the tissue."],[82,148.94,2.71,"But this process also releases waste,"],[82,151.73,2.54,"which can cause even more inflammation and pain."],[82,154.61,2.71,"Inflammation is a natural phase of healing,"],[82,157.32,3.79,"but too much inflammation can paradoxically slow recovery."],[82,161.28,2.17,"This is where sauna use comes in—"],[82,163.62,4.09,"physiologists suspect that increased blood flow can help pump in"],[82,167.71,4.84,"the biological ingredients needed to rebuild and help pump out waste."],[82,172.75,2.75,"This give and take between heat and inflammation"],[82,175.5,2.29,"may even lead to greater health benefits."],[82,178.05,4.5,"As we age, cells naturally accumulate more and more inflammatory damage,"],[82,182.55,2.88,"which is thought to contribute to different health problems."],[82,185.51,3.25,"And there is some early evidence that frequent sauna use"],[82,188.77,2.38,"could help regulate this inflammation."],[82,191.48,3.42,"There’s one more way regular heat exposure may impact health."],[82,195.07,3.71,"Molecules called “heat shock proteins” safeguard your cells"],[82,198.78,2.0,"by repairing damaged DNA"],[82,200.78,3.92,"and monitoring when important enzymes aren’t functioning properly."],[82,204.99,5.17,"And studies show regular sauna users tend to have higher levels of these proteins."],[82,210.66,1.79,"So, back to the Finnish findings."],[82,212.79,4.21,"Scientists found that men who used saunas four to seven times per week"],[82,217.0,2.21,"were healthier by several measures,"],[82,219.22,2.59,"including a reduced risk of high blood pressure,"],[82,221.84,4.55,"healthier cholesterol, and even a lower risk of dying in general."],[82,227.01,1.88,"Despite these promising results,"],[82,228.97,3.92,"the world of heat offers more questions than conclusive answers."],[82,233.19,3.09,"First, the Finnish study was unable to fully rule out"],[82,236.27,2.75,"how other lifestyle factors may have influenced"],[82,239.03,1.92,"the health differences they detected."],[82,241.28,1.96,"And even if the results do hold,"],[82,243.24,5.38,"this study covers just one demographic, from one region, over one period of time."],[82,248.87,4.13,"Scientists are still working to understand the full extent of sauna’s benefits,"],[82,253.25,1.83,"as well as its limits."],[82,255.38,3.5,"After all, we already know that heat can affect people differently."],[82,259.09,4.34,"For example, an intense thermoregulatory response can be dangerous"],[82,263.43,3.42,"for pregnant people or those with certain health conditions."],[82,267.1,3.84,"So, while it’s not yet possible to declare a true cause and effect"],[82,270.93,1.79,"between saunas and health,"],[82,272.73,3.75,"many enthusiasts are optimistic that with further investigation,"],[82,276.52,2.84,"these beneficial findings will handle the heat."],[83,7.04,4.0,"When the Visualphone VP210 hit the market in 1999,"],[83,11.21,2.59,"it advertised a never-before-seen feature:"],[83,13.93,1.0,"a camera."],[83,14.97,3.84,"With only 0.11 megapixels and storage for 20 photos,"],[83,18.81,3.13,"the Visualphone is a relic compared to modern devices"],[83,21.93,4.67,"sporting three distinct cameras, each with up to 100 times more resolution."],[83,27.19,3.59,"But while this technology has improved dramatically in the 21st century,"],[83,30.78,3.79,"engineers are rapidly approaching a hard limit on phone camera quality."],[83,34.91,3.79,"To understand this limit, we first need to know how phone cameras work."],[83,38.99,1.83,"Just like any other digital camera,"],[83,40.83,3.25,"when your phone takes a picture, light enters through its lens."],[83,44.46,5.0,"This lens focuses the light onto an image sensor covered in a grid of photosites—"],[83,49.75,3.96,"microscopic light sensors roughly 100 times smaller than a grain of sand."],[83,53.92,1.92,"There are millions of these sensors,"],[83,55.84,3.25,"and each one is covered by a red, green, or blue filter,"],[83,59.1,2.33,"allowing it to measure how much of that color"],[83,61.43,2.0,"is in the light hitting its location."],[83,63.68,4.13,"Then these measurements are simplified, rounding them to less detailed numbers."],[83,67.9,4.21,"This step sacrifices some data, thus lowering the final images’ quality,"],[83,72.15,2.17,"but it’s essential for the camera’s processor."],[83,74.44,2.5,"This computer can only handle so much information"],[83,77.16,2.21,"as it decrypts the three sets of color data"],[83,79.37,2.67,"to assemble a digital recreation of the image."],[83,82.29,3.8,"While the quality of this final photo depends on every part of the camera,"],[83,86.12,3.84,"nothing determines the look of a digital picture more than the image sensor."],[83,89.96,3.13,"And engineers judge the quality of image sensors"],[83,93.09,2.13,"based on their performance in three areas."],[83,95.26,2.79,"The first is resolution, or level of detail."],[83,98.22,3.75,"Sensors with higher numbers of photosites offer better resolution,"],[83,101.97,2.71,"as the camera can collect more granular light data."],[83,104.68,2.5,"Second and third are dynamic range and noise."],[83,107.19,3.63,"Dynamic range is the span from light to dark within a single photo,"],[83,110.81,2.88,"and noise is the graininess that can come from poor lighting,"],[83,113.78,2.75,"long exposure times, or an overheating camera."],[83,116.82,3.42,"Both these factors can be improved by using larger photosites,"],[83,120.32,1.92,"which can capture more light overall."],[83,122.45,3.63,"This wider range of data helps processors better measure the intensity"],[83,126.08,1.29,"of the incoming light,"],[83,127.37,2.17,"adding contrast and reducing noise."],[83,130.08,2.88,"Simply put, to make better digital cameras,"],[83,132.96,3.63,"you need image sensors with higher numbers of larger photosites."],[83,136.72,1.33,"Engineers know this."],[83,138.22,3.96,"In fact, it’s basically how they’ve made the best cameras humanity have:"],[83,142.26,2.71,"giant telescopes that take photos of deep space."],[83,145.27,4.34,"But phones don't even have as much sensor space as a standard DSLR camera,"],[83,149.73,2.67,"let alone the surface area of a massive telescope."],[83,152.69,3.59,"In fact, most phone camera sensors are no larger than a pea."],[83,156.9,3.5,"Fortunately, these devices have a technological trick to compensate"],[83,160.41,3.0,"for their cameras’ tiny size: powerful processors."],[83,163.62,1.83,"When you snap a picture on your phone,"],[83,165.45,3.09,"this pocket-computer starts running complex algorithms,"],[83,168.75,4.13,"which often begin by secretly taking a string of photos in rapid succession."],[83,173.04,2.42,"The algorithms then manipulate these pictures,"],[83,175.46,3.79,"using math to perfectly align them and identify their best parts"],[83,179.34,3.38,"before combining the images into one high-quality photo."],[83,182.93,3.84,"The end result is an image with less noise, wider dynamic range,"],[83,186.89,3.29,"and higher resolution than its sensors should be able to achieve."],[83,190.31,2.75,"This approach is known as computational photography,"],[83,193.06,3.67,"and advances here are likely how phone companies will continue to advertise"],[83,196.73,3.67,"increasingly better cameras without improving their image sensors."],[83,200.7,3.09,"Today, these algorithms often leverage machine learning,"],[83,203.78,3.04,"where phones learn to improve your shots based on patterns"],[83,206.83,1.84,"found in massive photo databases."],[83,208.91,4.34,"For example, night mode prioritizes dynamic range and noise reduction,"],[83,213.54,3.34,"while portrait mode tells your phone to focus on a central subject"],[83,216.88,1.25,"and blur the background."],[83,218.13,2.96,"Machine learning also allows our phones to do the opposite,"],[83,221.09,2.71,"unblurring faces to grab quick candid shots."],[83,224.05,3.92,"And newer programs can even help you remove unwanted elements altogether."],[83,228.1,1.67,"So, with the help of software,"],[83,229.81,2.38,"even phones with the smallest cameras can snap"],[83,232.19,3.59,"crisp, detailed photos of loved ones, spectacular views, and of course,"],[83,235.77,1.58,"lots and lots of food."],[84,4.72,2.48,"The size of a ping-pong ball –"],[84,8.32,2.24,"the desert rain frog."],[84,14.72,2.44,"To avoid frying to a crisp,"],[84,17.16,4.32,"by day this amphibian hides deep in the sand."],[84,25.84,2.48,"But when the conditions are just right,"],[84,28.32,2.24,"he emerges to feed."],[84,42.68,2.12,"Trudging up to 50 yards,"],[84,48.04,3.0,"he calmly noses around for insects."],[84,59.68,2.24,"While a Namaqua dwarf adder…"],[84,69.56,2.44,"..primes itself for ambush."],[84,81.08,3.12,"This tiny snake can only strike prey"],[84,84.2,2.4,"closer than four inches."],[84,99.68,4.16,"A parabuthus scorpion uses hair-like feelers"],[84,103.84,3.0,"to detect the tiniest movement."],[84,111.72,3.0,"And frogs are also on his menu."],[84,133.28,2.2,"The frog mustn't stir…"],[84,137.32,3.96,"..or he'll trigger a deadly attack from that stinging tail."],[84,151.6,2.56,"But waiting's what he's good at."],[84,172.72,4.64,"At last – a colony of termites"],[84,177.36,3.0,"busily harvesting dune grass."],[84,194.84,3.0,"They're packed with protein and fat."],[84,202.0,3.2,"He'll force down up to 100 in a sitting."],[84,212.72,3.36,"She enters into the maze of gullies."],[84,219.24,4.36,"The damp channel margins are carpeted with grass"],[84,224.52,2.44,"that lures in grazing antelope."],[84,241.68,3.0,"Still, they remain on high alert."],[84,251.68,2.52,"Stealth is the best weapon."],[84,290.84,3.0,"Placing faith in perfect camouflage,"],[84,298.4,1.96,"the trap is set."],[84,310.92,2.96,"Just a few more careless steps"],[84,313.88,4.2,"and they'll be inside the required 30-foot range."],[84,346.76,3.76,"Clamping her jaws over both nose and mouth,"],[84,351.08,4.04,"she can stay clear of the stiletto horns"],[84,355.12,2.32,"and cut off its airflow."],[84,371.52,4.04,"But a spotted hyena is drawn to the commotion."],[84,392.84,2.72,"Close to twice the weight of a leopard,"],[84,398.48,3.48,"armed with bone-crushing jaws,"],[84,402.56,2.84,"this opportunist won't pass up the chance"],[84,405.4,2.6,"to cash in on the hard work of others."],[84,418.0,4.2,"This time, one very lucky impala"],[84,422.2,2.48,"gets a second chance at life."],[84,428.88,5.44,"The chase is on against a shoal of jacks –"],[84,434.88,3.0,"too fast for a lone sea lion to catch."],[84,445.6,1.76,"Using the cove as a trap,"],[84,451.44,2.16,"they run the fish aground."],[84,458.76,4.52,"In the shallows, the pelicans are the first to cash in,"],[84,466.8,4.36,"able to squeeze up to 10 fish in their expandable throats."],[84,474.12,3.0,"But the female-led fleet regroups."],[84,499.28,2.0,"With all exits blocked,"],[84,505.04,5.92,"this time the panicked prey are driven right on to the beach."],[84,523.24,4.96,"A successful hunt yields up to 30 fish for each sea lion."],[84,534.36,4.24,"This remarkable technique is unique to Galápagos,"],[84,541.32,3.8,"filmed here for the very first time."],[84,562.8,2.64,"Up to 15 round-ups later,"],[84,571.88,2.76,"everyone gets their fill."],[84,575.76,3.04,"Unfortunately for this young mother and cub,"],[84,584.24,3.0,"all the prime fishing spots are taken"],[84,590.52,3.0,"by the older, more experienced females."],[84,603.32,2.68,"So she leads her cub upstream,"],[84,606.0,2.2,"away from the competition."],[84,630.0,3.28,"But there's a reason they have this place to themselves."],[84,636.36,1.4,"Rapids."],[84,658.84,2.56,"For the cub, it's a brutal baptism."],[84,670.08,3.88,"But the inexperienced mother coaxes him straight back in."],[84,708.96,2.04,"Her desperation to feed"],[84,711.0,3.4,"overrides her urge to protect her cub."],[84,720.48,2.76,"But fortune favours the brave."],[84,757.56,2.8,"Finally, the ravenous cub"],[84,760.36,3.0,"gets a first taste of salmon."],[84,771.92,2.32,"Cutting off the food supply…"],[84,778.08,3.0,"..and imprisoning the fledglings."],[84,787.12,3.36,"Only adults have the strength to break their bonds…"],[84,794.04,2.4,"..and make good their escape."],[84,811.52,2.6,"Even as the lake begins to thaw,"],[84,817.36,7.16,"shackles of salt and ice continue to weigh the chicks down."],[84,832.8,4.08,"And with every day deeper into winter,"],[84,842.84,4.24,"cold and lack of food take a toll."],[84,854.8,3.84,"But a breath of seasonal wind offers hope."],[84,875.8,5.32,"Gaining strength, it provides just enough lift…"],[84,883.68,3.0,"..for a last chance at liberty."],[84,909.4,3.28,"Trading their rich nursery feeding grounds…"],[84,915.96,2.8,"..for the southern lowlands,"],[84,919.44,3.6,"they'll find refuge from the grip of winter."],[85,4.47,1.27,"What's OpenClaw?"],[85,6.77,4.0,"I've been programming since I was 14."],[85,11.51,4.3,"Building software felt like playing a video game."],[85,16.55,1.37,"I couldn't stop."],[85,19.38,1.17,"Until I did."],[85,23.15,2.17,"I created a company,"],[85,25.36,4.37,"I poured a decade of my life into it."],[85,29.76,2.54,"No venture capital,"],[85,32.33,1.37,"every weekend."],[85,34.9,2.27,"And then I sold the dream."],[85,38.34,2.64,"And I felt absolutely nothing."],[85,43.61,2.4,"For three years,"],[85,46.04,2.2,"I was wondering,"],[85,48.24,3.47,"did therapy, traveled,"],[85,51.75,2.27,"I changed countries twice."],[85,55.05,1.67,"Nothing, nothing clicked."],[85,57.55,2.74,"Now I wake up every morning"],[85,60.32,2.5,"with everything I was supposed to want"],[85,62.86,1.13,"and no reason."],[85,64.69,1.8,"No reason to get out of bed."],[85,67.53,5.57,"And then in early 2025,"],[85,73.14,1.17,"I tried an experiment."],[85,74.34,5.34,"I wanted to see what these new AI coding agents are all about."],[85,81.05,6.24,"And I had what I can only describe as a \"holy shit\" moment."],[85,88.92,2.24,"The boilerplate, the plumbing,"],[85,91.19,4.94,"all the boring parts that was software development,"],[85,96.13,2.3,"AI could do all of it."],[85,99.6,2.04,"The bottleneck is no longer typing."],[85,102.2,1.24,"It's thinking."],[85,103.9,4.14,"And thinking was the part I did [for] 25 years."],[85,110.04,3.87,"Building software felt like playing a video game again."],[85,114.58,1.17,"I was back."],[85,116.65,5.51,"I built 44 projects in just a few months."],[85,122.99,3.2,"And the latest one was a WhatsApp bot."],[85,127.69,1.8,"I put it on my computer,"],[85,129.53,4.27,"it talks through the apps that you already know."],[85,137.07,3.87,"And then I took it on a trip to Marrakesh."],[85,141.74,6.04,"You know, just to navigate around, to find restaurants, do translations."],[85,148.71,3.17,"And at first, it didn't really feel right."],[85,152.22,4.44,"It felt too much like a tool,"],[85,156.65,1.97,"not like a friend."],[85,159.19,3.2,"You know, too many bullet points, too many tables."],[85,163.63,1.77,"So I told it,"],[85,165.43,2.97,"because those modern models, they are so smart,"],[85,168.43,2.8,"they know what WhatsApp is, they know how people talk."],[85,171.24,1.53,"I just had to tell it."],[85,173.7,2.5,"And then it felt right."],[85,177.88,1.94,"And you know how you talk to friends --"],[85,179.84,4.27,"at some point I was walking around and I was sending it a voice message."],[85,185.45,4.61,"And then I froze because I actually,"],[85,190.09,2.94,"I didn't build voice in there."],[85,193.06,3.5,"I added support for images, yeah, but even that took hours."],[85,196.59,4.34,"So I was looking at the typing indicator,"],[85,200.93,1.94,"and then the agent responded."],[85,203.47,2.47,"And I very vividly remember this situation"],[85,205.94,2.17,"where I was standing there and I was like,"],[85,208.1,1.4,"how did you do that?"],[85,210.67,2.3,"And the agent replied, I'm not kidding you,"],[85,212.98,3.0,"\"The mad lad figured it out on its own.\""],[85,217.41,2.47,"And then it walked me through every single step:"],[85,219.92,1.83,"how it got a message from me"],[85,221.75,3.2,"but there was no file ending, so it inspected the file,"],[85,224.99,3.74,"it found that it was audio, but a weird format, so it converted it."],[85,228.76,4.2,"And then it was looking for something to translate the audio,"],[85,233.0,1.64,"but I didn't have it installed."],[85,234.66,3.4,"But then it found an OpenAI key, it sent the whole thing to the server,"],[85,238.1,2.57,"it got it back, and then it replied."],[85,241.31,2.64,"All of that in nine seconds."],[85,244.71,2.44,"Can you imagine? I didn't build any of that."],[85,247.71,3.97,"For me, this was the moment where I thought,"],[85,251.72,1.17,"this is something new."],[85,252.88,1.17,"This is not a chatbot."],[85,254.08,1.74,"Chatbots give up."],[85,256.25,2.3,"Agents improvise."],[85,260.56,2.17,"And, you know, I was sold."],[85,262.76,1.17,"I wanted to share this."],[85,263.96,2.2,"I wanted to like, tell people on Twitter."],[85,266.56,2.24,"And nobody really got it."],[85,268.8,4.5,"It's like, it's almost like you have to experience an agent."],[85,273.34,2.97,"It's kind of hard to explain."],[85,277.24,1.47,"It took me a few weeks."],[85,279.64,1.64,"And then I did something stupid."],[85,282.91,4.34,"And remember, this agent, by default,"],[85,287.28,4.1,"can do anything that you can do on your computer."],[85,292.06,4.14,"So obviously I put it in a public Discord, and I invited random people."],[85,297.8,3.07,"And I was looking at it the whole night."],[85,300.9,1.4,"People were talking with it,"],[85,302.3,2.87,"people were having fun with it, people tried to hack it."],[85,306.3,4.24,"And when my eyes were like, falling off almost,"],[85,312.21,3.97,"I exited the process, and I went to bed."],[85,319.52,2.97,"Though I forgot -- I built a system to be resilient."],[85,323.52,2.17,"So while I was walking to the bedroom,"],[85,325.72,4.27,"the agent happily booted up again and talked to everyone in the world."],[85,331.83,2.04,"The next morning I woke up --"],[85,333.9,1.84,"over 800 messages."],[85,336.27,1.5,"I panicked."],[85,337.77,1.2,"I pulled the plug,"],[85,338.97,3.27,"I read every single message"],[85,342.24,3.1,"just to see if the agent leaked my private life."],[85,347.18,1.3,"Nothing happened."],[85,348.88,1.47,"But it could have."],[85,350.98,3.1,"But that was the moment where it went viral."],[85,354.45,3.17,"Today, the project is called OpenClaw."],[85,358.09,4.5,"It's pretty much the fastest-growing open-source project."],[85,363.23,2.23,"Its mascot is a lobster."],[85,365.86,2.13,"It claws into your machine."],[85,369.37,5.71,"Jensen Huang calls it the operating system for personal AI."],[85,375.67,2.87,"But by far, my favorite quote is from a friend"],[85,378.54,1.83,"who looked at this statistic and said,"],[85,380.41,3.1,"\"Peter, this is not hockey-stick growth."],[85,383.85,2.0,"This is stripper pole.\""],[85,394.76,3.57,"And I, I was not ready."],[85,398.36,2.97,"You know, when something blows up like this,"],[85,401.37,1.6,"everything explodes."],[85,403.0,1.77,"Hundreds of messages,"],[85,404.8,3.14,"reporters calling me in the middle of the night,"],[85,407.97,2.0,"security vulnerabilities."],[85,410.97,2.24,"And then"],[85,413.24,4.47,"the AI company whose model most of my users loved"],[85,417.75,1.7,"sent me a trademark claim."],[85,419.48,3.14,"I had to rename the whole thing while it was taking off."],[85,424.32,5.54,"You know, they even tried to push me away from the lobster."],[85,432.0,1.7,"Like, I was staring at the message."],[85,433.7,2.37,"It was like, it's not even the same animal."],[85,438.27,3.9,"And then they also cut off the model most of my users loved."],[85,443.44,3.7,"You know, first the name,"],[85,447.18,2.47,"then the lobster,"],[85,449.68,1.7,"then the model."],[85,452.22,2.74,"I was that close to just deleting the whole thing."],[85,456.39,4.64,"But then I learned what people are building with it."],[85,462.79,2.3,"So at ClawCon in Vienna,"],[85,465.13,2.4,"because yes, we have conferences already --"],[85,469.07,3.5,"And people wore lobster headbands,"],[85,472.6,2.07,"I met Stefan"],[85,474.7,3.87,"and his 60-year-old dad, Gerhard,"],[85,478.57,4.97,"a beer sommelier who never wrote a single line of code."],[85,484.25,3.94,"They connected OpenClaw via Bluetooth,"],[85,488.19,1.53,"sent it one prompt,"],[85,489.75,3.37,"and the agent did the whole 90-minute brew:"],[85,493.16,3.3,"temperature ramps, hop additions, everything."],[85,496.99,4.2,"And then they were like, what are we doing with all this beer?"],[85,502.06,2.84,"And the agent was, \"Let's make a website.\""],[85,505.54,2.57,"So they built a website, and then they added payments,"],[85,508.14,1.94,"and now they have a real product."],[85,510.91,4.17,"And almost all of it was just done via the phone."],[85,516.85,2.57,"In China,"],[85,519.42,4.67,"installing OpenClaw is called raising lobsters."],[85,526.92,4.44,"Thousands of people were lining up at the Tencent office in Shenzhen"],[85,531.39,1.7,"to get their lobster installed."],[85,534.3,5.84,"Shenzhen even gives out subsidies for people running businesses on OpenClaw."],[85,541.37,1.97,"Now"],[85,543.34,4.57,"if you install OpenClaw on your work machine,"],[85,547.95,1.8,"at least with the default settings,"],[85,549.78,1.6,"you might get fired."],[85,552.55,3.87,"And then I met an entrepreneur in China"],[85,556.42,1.87,"who showed me a spreadsheet."],[85,559.02,2.94,"Every employee, every day,"],[85,561.99,3.1,"one task automated by OpenClaw."],[85,565.76,2.44,"If you miss too many days, you're fired."],[85,568.66,3.97,"So fired for using it,"],[85,572.64,2.47,"fired for not using it."],[85,577.17,2.33,"After Marrakesh,"],[85,579.54,2.44,"I thought, this is incredible."],[85,582.35,1.77,"And it's also a little bit scary."],[85,585.72,1.77,"How can we make it more scary?"],[85,589.49,3.14,"So I added a new feature, a heartbeat."],[85,593.26,1.3,"You know, by default,"],[85,594.59,3.1,"the agent would only wake up when you send it a prompt."],[85,598.06,1.53,"With the heartbeat,"],[85,599.63,2.97,"the agent would just wake up periodically,"],[85,602.63,1.63,"check your emails,"],[85,604.3,3.0,"check your calendar, follow up on loose ends."],[85,607.9,3.54,"My initial prompt was simply \"surprise me.\""],[85,612.44,3.07,"And yes, that's kind of as scary as it sounds."],[85,616.68,3.6,"So no large company would ship something like that."],[85,620.72,2.94,"But I'm a random builder from Austria."],[85,623.65,2.13,"I don't have a legal department."],[85,628.79,5.17,"I built this sandbox, this sandcastle, for me,"],[85,634.0,3.34,"and I made it open-source so other people could play with it"],[85,637.37,3.44,"and other people could raise their imagination."],[85,641.61,3.84,"Imagine putting an agent into a meeting."],[85,645.74,2.3,"Not for notes, we figured that out."],[85,648.75,5.51,"A bidirectional model that can listen and hear at the same time."],[85,655.12,1.87,"Somebody mentions a statistic,"],[85,657.02,2.9,"a subagent can spin off and check it for you."],[85,660.22,1.53,"A decision is made,"],[85,661.76,1.8,"the agent can send a follow-up"],[85,663.59,2.3,"before the meeting even ends."],[85,666.7,1.9,"In the future,"],[85,668.63,2.44,"we're not just going to have one agent."],[85,671.6,2.5,"You might have your work agent,"],[85,674.1,2.7,"your personal Claw, maybe one for health,"],[85,676.84,1.83,"maybe one for relationship."],[85,679.28,3.57,"And they all should work together in a secure way."],[85,683.68,2.57,"Because how did humanity level up?"],[85,686.72,3.94,"By specializing and collaborating."],[85,690.69,2.77,"And agents are about to do the same."],[85,694.12,4.14,"Imagine a company, a small business"],[85,698.29,2.84,"that has ten agents that are all specialized,"],[85,701.16,2.8,"taking over various parts of the business."],[85,704.74,2.47,"We don't even have a name for what it might become,"],[85,707.24,1.74,"but we are about to find out."],[85,710.07,4.84,"So I created the OpenClaw Foundation,"],[85,714.95,3.4,"a nonprofit, open-source forever."],[85,718.88,3.27,"Because what OpenClaw did for many people"],[85,722.18,5.17,"was it moved AI from this scary,"],[85,727.36,1.77,"nebulous thing"],[85,729.16,3.5,"into something that is fun"],[85,732.7,2.8,"and useful and maybe a bit weird."],[85,735.53,4.17,"You know, lobsters and headbands and beer businesses."],[85,740.77,6.64,"Because what we need in the future is more people spending more time with AI"],[85,747.44,2.5,"to better understand how powerful"],[85,749.98,3.1,"and transformative this technology really is."],[85,754.35,3.3,"In New York,"],[85,757.65,1.53,"at ClawCon ..."],[85,761.59,1.74,"Yes, they're everywhere now."],[85,764.39,4.0,"There were thousands of people that were discussing"],[85,768.43,2.24,"what the lobster did this week."],[85,772.07,4.84,"A retiree in Shenzhen who automated their groceries."],[85,778.01,2.7,"A teenager in Sao Paulo"],[85,780.74,2.94,"who built a tutoring business on OpenClaw."],[85,784.71,2.1,"Gerhard and his beer machine."],[85,787.95,1.5,"None of them are programmers."],[85,790.82,2.7,"All of them are builders."],[85,793.99,2.6,"Because that's the real transformation."],[85,796.89,1.3,"It's not a technology."],[85,798.53,1.8,"It's the access."],[85,800.36,3.37,"Agents change who can build things,"],[85,803.73,2.37,"and that door is not closing again."],[85,807.4,5.57,"Because when you can prompt a prototype into existence"],[85,813.01,2.27,"in one hour,"],[85,815.28,1.24,"anything is possible."],[85,817.28,3.97,"The next breakthrough can come from anyone,"],[85,821.25,1.94,"any country,"],[85,823.22,1.17,"any cafe."],[85,826.36,4.47,"When even a burnt-out founder,"],[85,830.86,2.87,"staring at the screen,"],[85,833.76,4.07,"wondering if his spark is gone"],[85,837.87,1.63,"can do something like that."],[85,841.64,1.53,"It's not gone."],[85,843.21,1.44,"It's just waiting."],[85,845.61,1.67,"The lobster is loose,"],[85,847.31,2.54,"and it's not going back into the tank."],[85,850.68,1.37,"Thank you very much."],[85,861.22,1.27,"Chris Anderson: Come here."],[85,864.29,1.23,"I mean."],[85,867.13,3.1,"I think I want to say something personal to you."],[85,870.7,2.3,"With love, but truth."],[85,873.97,1.37,"You really terrify me."],[85,877.77,1.2,"CA: I’m serious."],[85,879.01,2.84,"If Hollywood was to ever make a movie"],[85,881.88,3.24,"in which humanity opened Pandora’s box,"],[85,885.15,1.57,"and everything went crazy,"],[85,886.75,3.5,"like, you seriously could be cast as the star character."],[85,891.86,2.37,"CA: Because the story we’re told"],[85,894.26,3.67,"is that AI researchers are doing all this great stuff,"],[85,897.96,3.9,"but they're taking all these great efforts to ensure safety"],[85,901.9,2.17,"and make sure nothing bad could happen."],[85,904.07,6.37,"You take glee in seeing what might happen if you just put it out there."],[85,910.47,5.44,"Like, is any part of you feeling that that's a little bit reckless?"],[85,917.48,1.8,"Peter Steinberger: I wouldn't say so."],[85,919.32,1.53,"I see my work as a ..."],[85,922.49,2.04,"PS: I see it as a window into the future."],[85,924.96,5.67,"Like, in the very beginning, there were all these scary moments."],[85,931.19,2.47,"Now we have proper security layers."],[85,933.7,1.3,"You can have your sandbox,"],[85,935.03,6.04,"you can put your lobster into a very small, tiny box"],[85,941.1,1.97,"and really control what it can do."],[85,943.74,2.6,"There are still some issues that we need to figure out,"],[85,946.38,3.24,"but the fact that so many people want this now"],[85,949.65,2.23,"will help to figure this out much faster."],[85,952.41,2.47,"CA: So I'm glad you mentioned the security layers."],[85,956.45,5.27,"I mean, you're making a huge bet on human ingenuity using this."],[85,961.76,3.94,"This is an incredible tool that suddenly can maximize the power"],[85,965.73,2.6,"of what any human can do."],[85,972.34,3.47,"How many people in your community"],[85,975.84,1.77,"are taking the safety issue seriously"],[85,977.64,3.3,"and want to use OpenClaw, for example, to find smarter ways?"],[85,980.98,3.44,"Just checking whether anything might be going wrong"],[85,984.45,4.0,"and giving an early alarm to someone or something like that?"],[85,988.49,2.23,"PS: Most people are not as reckless [as] --"],[85,990.72,2.64,"number one, putting it into a public Discord --"],[85,993.39,1.97,"strongly don't recommend."],[85,996.09,4.14,"Number two, I think I single-handedly increased"],[85,1000.23,2.54,"Mac mini sales by multiple percent."],[85,1003.43,3.34,"So most people give it their own little Mac mini."],[85,1006.8,1.33,"Mine's a little princess."],[85,1008.14,1.2,"Mine got a Mac studio."],[85,1009.37,1.44,"It calls it “The Castle.”"],[85,1011.91,3.44,"And that greatly reduces the actual risk"],[85,1015.38,2.57,"because you can only access what's on that computer."],[85,1017.98,2.2,"And maybe all your pictures are not there."],[85,1020.68,2.07,"CA: Well, definitely, if humanity goes down,"],[85,1022.79,3.14,"I'll be very grateful for at least the rise in Mac mini sales"],[85,1025.95,1.53,"for a period of time."],[85,1027.49,1.37,"You are amazing."],[85,1028.86,1.2,"You are amazing,"],[85,1030.09,3.24,"and I think you're actually right at the cutting edge"],[85,1033.36,3.7,"of whether AI is going to be the biggest boon ever"],[85,1037.1,1.9,"or possibly a serious problem."],[85,1039.0,4.07,"And, you know, I hope you continue conversations with people here"],[85,1043.11,3.9,"and just help us get smarter on how to do this the right way"],[85,1047.01,2.54,"because it is absolutely incredible what you've built."],[85,1049.58,1.64,"Thank you for sharing so honestly."],[85,1051.21,1.2,"PS: Thank you so much."],[86,7.04,3.84,"One night in 2013, a man in Northern Ireland"],[86,10.88,5.75,"was shocked to discover something bizarre protruding from the mouth of the sea bass"],[86,16.64,1.88,"he was preparing for dinner."],[86,18.81,2.96,"Peering closer, he came face-to-face"],[86,21.77,3.46,"with none other than the infamous “tongue biter,”"],[86,25.4,4.17,"also known as the tongue-eating louse."],[86,29.78,3.67,"This species, Cymothoa exigua,"],[86,33.49,4.67,"is one of nearly 400 belonging to a family of fish parasites"],[86,38.16,2.0,"called cymothoids."],[86,40.45,4.59,"They’re crustaceans, and more specifically, isopods,"],[86,45.04,1.83,"like pill bugs."],[86,47.38,5.55,"Some latch onto their host's head, others suck blood from fish gills,"],[86,53.09,3.46,"and still others burrow into fish flesh"],[86,56.59,4.13,"and form sac-like membranes around themselves."],[86,61.1,5.05,"Clearly, there are many delightful ways to be a cymothoid,"],[86,67.06,6.63,"but Cymothoa exigua and others go specifically for fish tongues—"],[86,73.82,3.75,"or at least what pass as tongues among fish."],[86,78.03,3.59,"Because instead of what we think of as a tongue,"],[86,81.91,4.59,"fish have a bony structure called a basihyal,"],[86,86.87,2.67,"protruding from the floor of their mouths,"],[86,89.54,4.88,"which they use to move oxygen-rich water towards their gills"],[86,94.42,1.75,"and to swallow food."],[86,96.59,4.75,"Tongue biters start out looking for a fish to call home for life,"],[86,101.35,2.67,"which can be up to around three years."],[86,104.14,5.5,"They may swim for days, surviving off stored yolk reserves—"],[86,109.69,3.29,"until finally, they sense a change in the light"],[86,113.11,3.84,"and pick up on a nearby fish’s chemical cues."],[86,117.16,4.59,"They burst into action, hook onto the fish’s gills"],[86,121.74,6.05,"using the sharp, curved tips of their limbs, and crawl on in."],[86,128.08,1.75,"There, they molt,"],[86,130.04,3.21,"and since they won’t be living independently again,"],[86,133.25,5.67,"their eyes recede and their limbs lose their hair-like swimming structures."],[86,139.13,6.13,"Meanwhile, their sperm producing and transporting organs also mature."],[86,145.56,5.5,"If they’ve moved into a fish that doesn’t already have a tongue biter at the helm,"],[86,151.06,5.26,"they’ll speed right through this male phase and develop into a female."],[86,156.49,1.79,"They grow much larger,"],[86,158.28,3.92,"their testes shrink, their ovaries become active,"],[86,162.32,3.13,"they form a kangaroo-like brood pouch,"],[86,165.45,5.42,"and they enter their host’s mouth and clamp down on its basihyal."],[86,171.58,2.67,"Here, they’re thought to suck blood"],[86,174.25,4.25,"from the fleshier part of the fish’s tongue-like structure."],[86,178.63,6.46,"Over time, the isopod’s habits can cause the fish’s basihyal to wither away,"],[86,185.18,3.17,"leaving the fish with a parasitic crustacean"],[86,188.35,3.0,"acting as a tongue-like appendage."],[86,191.56,1.25,"In this form,"],[86,192.81,5.67,"the tongue biter is suspected to functionally replace the basihyal,"],[86,198.69,4.34,"moving food and water through the fish’s mouth."],[86,203.37,5.71,"Only mature female tongue biters take hold of the basihyal,"],[86,209.16,2.75,"and only one at a time gets a taste."],[86,212.08,4.46,"In the event that a few tongue biters share the same lucky fish"],[86,216.54,2.04,"and the tongue is already taken,"],[86,218.59,4.75,"the others will remain male till the opportunity to switch arises."],[86,223.51,4.5,"If the female dies, a nearby male can develop into a female"],[86,228.01,3.0,"and fill the recently vacated position."],[86,231.14,3.71,"Otherwise, males mostly remain in the gills,"],[86,234.94,2.79,"though they may climb into the mouth to mate."],[86,237.86,5.21,"And when that happens, a female can produce hundreds of tiny tongue biters,"],[86,243.16,4.71,"carrying them inside a special pouch until they’re ready to swim off"],[86,247.87,3.84,"and find fish of their own to make home sweet home."],[86,252.08,5.21,"Fish can survive years parasitized by cymothoids,"],[86,257.5,4.21,"but it may come as no surprise that having a blood-sucking isopod"],[86,261.72,2.46,"for a tongue isn't ideal."],[86,264.43,5.8,"While some seem largely unaffected aside from the obvious, localized damage,"],[86,270.35,2.84,"others grow slower, become anemic,"],[86,273.19,4.05,"and are more susceptible to environmental stressors."],[86,277.56,6.55,"Parasitism rates can get surprisingly high among cymothoids’ preferred hosts,"],[86,284.11,3.75,"which includes snappers, trout, and croakers—"],[86,287.87,2.92,"also commonly consumed by people."],[86,291.04,3.92,"Surveys of Mediterranean striped sea breams"],[86,294.96,3.25,"and South African largespot pompanos"],[86,298.21,5.21,"found that nearly half the fish carried cymothoids in their mouths."],[86,303.55,3.88,"And the number can climb even higher in fish farms."],[86,307.6,1.83,"They’re normally removed,"],[86,309.47,5.05,"but they've occasionally made their way into grocery stores and home kitchens."],[86,314.85,4.96,"And while theoretically eating a tongue biter doesn't pose any health risks,"],[86,319.86,3.46,"it might not be the most pleasant surprise."],[86,323.94,2.63,"What? Louse got your tongue?"],[87,3.5,1.2,"A few months ago,"],[87,4.7,4.76,"180 of us came together to build something in Singapore:"],[87,10.38,1.52,"a library."],[87,11.94,3.4,"In a city where we're lucky enough to already have a world-class,"],[87,15.38,4.48,"highly innovative, much beloved public library system,"],[87,19.86,3.08,"of which I am a happy beneficiary,"],[87,22.94,2.08,"how is our library different?"],[87,26.14,2.12,"So this is a picture of the library we built."],[87,28.26,2.4,"We are nestled in the heartlands of Singapore,"],[87,30.7,2.88,"where we sit between a 50-year-old furniture store"],[87,33.62,1.36,"and an antique shop,"],[87,35.02,2.8,"surrounded by birds and trees and ..."],[87,40.22,1.6,"Quite a few chickens."],[87,44.26,2.76,"So our library is what we call a shared library."],[87,47.06,2.72,"There are 180 shelves in our library,"],[87,49.82,3.92,"each of them rented and maintained by a different person."],[87,54.3,2.16,"For 45 Singapore dollars a month"],[87,56.5,2.12,"you get to become a bookshelf owner."],[87,60.02,2.2,"Being a bookshelf owner is actually really fun,"],[87,62.26,2.96,"because you get to share your favorite books on your own shelf,"],[87,65.22,2.36,"you get to decorate your own space"],[87,67.62,2.88,"and change up your books every now and then."],[87,71.86,4.24,"Every shelf in our library is like a little pocket universe,"],[87,76.14,5.84,"an interesting, almost voyeuristic glimpse into someone's heart and mind."],[87,82.54,1.68,"And if you walk around our library,"],[87,84.22,2.48,"quite often you end up standing in front of a shelf"],[87,86.74,3.36,"thinking to yourself, \"I think I know this person,\""],[87,90.14,4.16,"even though, strangely, you've never met this person in your entire life."],[87,95.86,2.12,"The other interesting thing about our library"],[87,98.02,2.2,"is that we are entirely volunteer-run."],[87,100.26,4.52,"So a roster of about 40 very kind volunteer librarians"],[87,104.82,4.52,"take turns to keep our library open six days a week, 12 hours a day."],[87,111.06,2.6,"Some of them are bookshop owners,"],[87,113.66,1.6,"they have a shelf in our library."],[87,115.3,2.04,"Some of them are people from the community"],[87,117.34,2.68,"who really want to be part of the project."],[87,120.58,5.28,"They are doctors, ex-lawyers, students, mothers, freelancers, retirees."],[87,126.3,2.8,"We even have a five-year-old librarian among us."],[87,130.1,1.72,"But we all have something in common."],[87,131.86,2.64,"We love books, we believe in the power of books,"],[87,134.5,2.96,"and we enjoy being surrounded by books."],[87,138.5,3.44,"So any time we’re open, anyone is welcome to come in,"],[87,141.98,2.64,"to sit down, to read a book for free,"],[87,144.62,2.6,"to make a friend or connection,"],[87,147.26,5.0,"to drink a cup of coffee, randomly brewed by someone in the library,"],[87,152.3,3.52,"and to just let the stress of life melt off their shoulders."],[87,156.54,3.64,"So how did we come to build a library like this?"],[87,161.78,1.24,"So in April this year,"],[87,163.06,2.16,"I took a break from my work as a photographer,"],[87,165.22,2.52,"and I went to Japan for a sabbatical,"],[87,167.78,5.24,"where I stumbled upon a small, shared community library with a similar concept."],[87,174.14,2.12,"I loved it. I loved it."],[87,178.78,3.56,"There was just something about the space that really enticed me."],[87,184.26,2.08,"Standing in that little library in Japan,"],[87,186.38,3.4,"even though I couldn't read any of the Japanese books,"],[87,189.82,4.2,"I could sense a very powerful energy of love and care."],[87,194.58,3.36,"And even though I couldn't read any of the Japanese books there,"],[87,197.98,2.28,"I thought it would be wonderful if we could have"],[87,200.26,1.64,"something like this in Singapore."],[87,201.9,2.4,"So I decided to turn to social media."],[87,204.74,2.76,"So I came back to Singapore,"],[87,207.54,3.24,"decided to write a simple Instagram post,"],[87,210.82,2.48,"just outlining my vision of what I thought"],[87,213.34,3.68,"a small, shared library in Singapore could look like."],[87,217.62,1.48,"I thought if no one responded,"],[87,219.14,2.2,"then I would just forget about the whole thing."],[87,221.38,2.72,"But if I could somehow get 50 people on board,"],[87,224.1,2.56,"I would be happy to give this very impractical,"],[87,226.66,2.96,"dreamy and idealistic idea a try."],[87,230.82,2.24,"So I quickly finished writing, pressed post,"],[87,233.06,2.24,"and then I went back to work."],[87,236.5,1.6,"By the end of the first day,"],[87,238.14,2.76,"I had received about 300 direct messages"],[87,240.9,2.44,"from people who said they were seriously interested"],[87,243.38,2.24,"in putting money down to rent a shelf"],[87,245.66,2.28,"and to build a library together."],[87,249.02,1.6,"I was quite emotional that night"],[87,250.62,2.68,"because I could really sense people's passion"],[87,253.34,1.8,"and fire in their messages."],[87,255.82,4.56,"Living in Singapore, where, you know, life is practical and pragmatic,"],[87,260.42,2.84,"this felt almost like a jailbreak kind of moment,"],[87,263.26,3.12,"and I think a lot of people were just waiting for the right time"],[87,266.42,3.16,"and the right chance to do something like this together."],[87,270.42,2.56,"I guess I was also really moved by the realization"],[87,272.98,2.56,"that so many people cared about books."],[87,275.94,2.6,"So I took a leap of faith."],[87,278.9,3.08,"I found a space to rent on the third day of the Instagram post."],[87,282.02,1.88,"I signed the lease on the fourth day."],[87,286.1,1.16,"I'm an Aquarius, so."],[87,288.78,3.16,"With the help of many people, most of them strangers,"],[87,291.94,2.12,"we built the library in three months."],[87,295.3,2.96,"This is a picture of us literally building the library."],[87,298.26,2.64,"Here, we were installing the flooring together."],[87,301.94,5.04,"So who are some of these 180 crazy people who decided to do this with me?"],[87,307.82,3.72,"So this is Raymond's shelf."],[87,311.86,3.16,"Raymond loves collecting CDs and books about Japan --"],[87,315.02,2.88,"so these are the two things that you can find on his shelf."],[87,318.26,4.12,"Every Tuesday, Raymond sets up a little stall outside our library"],[87,322.38,2.44,"where he sells his secondhand CDs and books."],[87,324.86,2.64,"He also brews coffee -- pay as you wish --"],[87,327.5,2.28,"for anyone who happens to drop by."],[87,331.62,2.48,"This is Wanqi and Liam, the mother and son."],[87,334.14,3.96,"Liam is also the five-year-old librarian I was talking about earlier."],[87,338.54,2.6,"So far, we've been quite impressed by his work ethic."],[87,343.14,2.12,"And their shelf is split half-half,"],[87,345.3,2.96,"half between Liam's favorite children's books,"],[87,348.3,2.2,"and half between Wanqi's books."],[87,350.5,2.84,"Liam was also responsible for decorating their shelf."],[87,353.34,1.2,"It looks great."],[87,355.3,1.68,"So this particular bookshelf owner,"],[87,356.98,2.04,"she has decided to share some of the books"],[87,359.02,2.8,"that used to be on the shelves of Kin San,"],[87,361.86,3.88,"which is a Hong Kong bookshop that has unfortunately closed."],[87,365.78,3.08,"She has also replicated a miniature version of the banner"],[87,368.86,5.4,"that used to hang in front of Kin San that says \"ideas are bulletproof.\""],[87,374.62,3.52,"In a way, Kin San lives on right here inside our library."],[87,379.7,2.04,"This particular bookshop owner,"],[87,381.74,2.72,"her name is Mira, is a preschool teacher."],[87,384.5,3.68,"She believes in the power of letter writing,"],[87,388.18,1.68,"especially with children,"],[87,390.62,3.52,"which is why you can find a mailbox on her shelf."],[87,394.46,3.28,"So apart from reading the amazing selection of books on her shelf,"],[87,397.74,2.16,"you can also write her a letter."],[87,399.94,2.68,"She has promised that she'll definitely write you back."],[87,404.42,5.12,"So one of the most frequent questions we get asked is"],[87,409.54,1.72,"how does this library stay open?"],[87,411.3,2.84,"It doesn't really make economic sense, right?"],[87,415.26,1.68,"I think the simple answer is"],[87,416.94,5.4,"because 180 people have decided that it is possible to come together"],[87,422.38,3.88,"to build a ground-up book space for the local community."],[87,426.26,4.32,"And as it turns out, this is actually quite a practical solution"],[87,430.58,4.6,"because this means that we're able to share the rent among 180 people,"],[87,435.22,3.64,"and no one person has to bear the financial burden"],[87,438.9,2.2,"of keeping this space open."],[87,442.3,2.36,"The other question we get asked a lot is,"],[87,444.66,2.52,"what do you get out of doing this?"],[87,447.66,3.0,"Well, we definitely don't get paid in the traditional sense,"],[87,450.7,1.64,"in dollars and cents."],[87,452.38,2.72,"But we have been open for about four months now,"],[87,455.14,3.16,"and a lot of us have found the library"],[87,458.34,4.04,"to be one of the rare places where we feel truly at home."],[87,463.9,3.4,"On most days, our library also doubles as a community center."],[87,467.34,4.32,"So apart from the usual book clubs, women sharing circles,"],[87,471.7,6.44,"people have also used it to host curry night, hot pot night"],[87,478.18,1.36,"for 40 people."],[87,479.54,1.96,"And also, two times a month"],[87,481.54,3.32,"we run a midnight library where we open till late,"],[87,484.86,3.32,"or at least as late as the last person would like to stay."],[87,491.06,3.88,"I see community in every corner of our little library."],[87,495.38,3.96,"I see community in the people who bring food and drinks"],[87,499.38,2.28,"and ideas and resources to our library"],[87,501.66,2.28,"that they freely share with each other."],[87,503.94,2.88,"People rush to return their library books"],[87,506.82,2.2,"even though we have no late fees --"],[87,509.02,3.36,"I think because they can sense the love and the care"],[87,512.42,2.64,"that has gone into every shelf in our library,"],[87,515.1,3.76,"and also because I think they just really want to share the books"],[87,518.86,1.24,"with other people, right?"],[87,520.1,2.44,"They want other people to have access to the books."],[87,523.26,3.88,"I see community in the little children who come into our library."],[87,527.18,3.44,"Little kids who actually remind each other to, you know, like,"],[87,530.62,2.44,"please take care of the books, don't bend the pages,"],[87,533.1,1.88,"put the book back in the right place."],[87,534.98,2.12,"I see community in our volunteer librarians,"],[87,537.14,5.12,"who spend a lot of time cleaning the library, cataloging books,"],[87,542.3,5.28,"and pouring their soul into making sure that the library is a welcoming space"],[87,547.62,1.16,"for everyone."],[87,550.02,1.6,"And because of all this,"],[87,551.66,3.48,"Singapore feels a little different to me these days."],[87,555.18,3.28,"These days, Singapore feels a little more generous,"],[87,558.5,3.2,"warm, open, kind,"],[87,561.74,1.76,"joyful and trusting."],[87,564.94,2.92,"In a world where people feel increasingly lonely,"],[87,567.9,3.08,"society feels increasingly fragmented,"],[87,571.02,1.96,"this little library has given us --"],[87,572.98,3.4,"or at least it has given me -- a feeling of power."],[87,577.14,3.08,"Not the kind of power that is heavy and destructive,"],[87,580.22,3.12,"but the kind of power that is light and regenerative."],[87,583.98,2.68,"This little library has given me renewed hope"],[87,586.7,4.04,"that we do have the power to come together"],[87,590.78,3.92,"to build what we think is missing in our lives,"],[87,594.7,2.2,"in our city, in our world."],[87,597.54,4.16,"And sometimes, that missing thing is a small, shared,"],[87,601.7,2.12,"community-owned library."],[87,603.86,1.16,"Thank you."],[88,6.67,3.13,"Late at night on December 18th, 2023,"],[88,10.01,4.92,"the Icelandic citizens of Grindavik experienced their worst nightmare."],[88,15.09,2.96,"After weeks of earthquake-filled suspense,"],[88,18.18,3.92,"a volcanic fissure opened four kilometers northeast of town"],[88,22.1,4.17,"and began spewing lava fountains 100 meters tall."],[88,26.61,5.34,"Luckily, the molten rock flowed elsewhere, narrowly avoiding the small fishing town."],[88,32.28,4.71,"But Iceland wasn’t willing to leave Grindavik’s fate to chance again."],[88,37.7,1.83,"To prepare for future eruptions,"],[88,39.53,2.25,"the government began exploring how to control"],[88,41.79,2.67,"these red-hot rivers of destruction."],[88,45.17,3.79,"Steering a lava flow is about as difficult as it sounds."],[88,49.13,3.42,"Molten rock, which we call magma when it's underground,"],[88,52.55,3.09,"and lava when it breaks through the Earth's surface,"],[88,55.68,4.92,"can reach temperatures of roughly 1,200° Celsius."],[88,61.1,3.13,"That's over four times hotter than the maximum temperature"],[88,64.23,1.83,"of a standard kitchen oven—"],[88,66.52,5.09,"now imagine that heat radiating from several square kilometers."],[88,71.98,4.8,"At this temperature, lava ignites or melts most things in its path."],[88,77.07,3.5,"And since it's as heavy and dense as the rocks it's composed of,"],[88,80.7,2.5,"its flow is almost unstoppable."],[88,83.58,4.92,"Fortunately, there are two factors that make lava flows a little easier to handle."],[88,88.88,3.42,"First, while it can take decades to cool completely,"],[88,92.5,5.13,"lava becomes solid and still after cooling to roughly 600° Celsius."],[88,98.59,3.5,"This process typically happens on its own in just a few hours,"],[88,102.27,2.88,"unless an ongoing eruption is fueling the flow."],[88,105.6,5.34,"Second, lava generally flows at a rate of less than 1 kilometer per hour."],[88,111.36,2.84,"This slow speed gives people time to evacuate"],[88,114.19,2.63,"and respond with various solutions—"],[88,116.95,2.29,"though some ideas are better than others."],[88,119.99,1.67,"One questionable strategy"],[88,121.66,4.63,"championed by the first Director of the Hawaii Volcano Observatory,"],[88,126.29,1.33,"Thomas Jaggar,"],[88,127.62,2.79,"was to fight lava with bombs."],[88,131.0,5.59,"When the 1935 eruption of Mauna Loa threatened the Hawaiian town of Hilo,"],[88,136.76,4.8,"he convinced the US Army Air Corps to drop 20 bombs on the flowing lava"],[88,141.55,2.75,"to disrupt its path and stop its advance."],[88,145.06,3.46,"Six days after the operation, the lava did stop flowing,"],[88,148.64,4.21,"prompting Jaggar and the US Air Force to label their mission a success."],[88,153.4,4.88,"But today, most volcanologists consider the timing to be a coincidence."],[88,158.57,1.83,"Since lava flows like a liquid,"],[88,160.57,3.67,"experts believe the bombs merely displaced it temporarily,"],[88,164.37,3.63,"forming a crater which flowing lava then refilled."],[88,168.62,3.59,"Perhaps the more obvious solution is to cool lava with water."],[88,172.38,5.76,"Due to lava’s low heat conductivity this requires an enormous amount of H2O,"],[88,178.34,3.71,"but that didn’t stop the Icelandic government in 1973."],[88,182.18,2.29,"When the Eldfell volcano erupted"],[88,184.47,3.29,"and lava began streaming towards the Heimaey Harbor,"],[88,188.06,2.29,"they began a highly coordinated effort"],[88,190.35,5.17,"to pump 6 million cubic meters of seawater onto the lava flow."],[88,195.82,4.59,"That’s enough to fill 2,400 Olympic-sized swimming pools."],[88,200.78,3.5,"At the peak of their efforts to manage this 6-month long eruption,"],[88,204.45,2.92,"75 people worked in shifts around the clock,"],[88,207.66,5.38,"spraying down each actively advancing area for roughly a full day to stop its flow."],[88,213.63,2.0,"This approach did save the harbor,"],[88,215.63,3.5,"but it could only work in a place with access to this much water."],[88,219.67,3.13,"Regions further from the coast require different defenses,"],[88,222.8,1.96,"like large earthen barriers."],[88,225.1,4.3,"Typically made of materials like sand, dirt, or volcanic gravel,"],[88,229.6,4.13,"these barriers can divert lava flows away from populated areas."],[88,234.19,4.3,"For example, when Italy’s Mount Etna erupted in 1983,"],[88,238.48,4.5,"workers used 750,000 cubic meters of material—"],[88,243.2,5.3,"the equivalent of 25,000 truckloads— to erect four large barriers."],[88,249.04,4.75,"The people of Grindavik took a similar approach after the 2023 eruption,"],[88,253.79,4.67,"building 25-meter-high barriers that successfully diverted lava"],[88,258.46,1.96,"from multiple eruptions."],[88,260.76,3.38,"Since the diverted lava raises the ground level as it flows,"],[88,264.13,3.13,"these barriers had to be raised between each eruption."],[88,267.68,3.09,"But the advantage of this approach is that it allows regions"],[88,270.77,4.09,"to build up defenses between flows instead of during them."],[88,275.35,2.29,"Scientists are still working to better predict"],[88,277.65,4.5,"exactly where lava flows will emerge, what direction they’ll travel,"],[88,282.15,3.0,"and just how much lava an eruption will produce."],[88,285.53,1.42,"But once they figure it out,"],[88,287.12,2.67,"engineers can leverage existing strategies"],[88,289.79,4.92,"to protect communities from these otherwise awe-inspiring eruptions."],[89,7.5,2.29,"Are you not entertained?"],[89,10.38,2.38,"And this is what you think of us."],[89,12.97,2.84,"O tempora! O mores!"],[89,16.3,2.25,"Salvete! Minerva here—"],[89,18.56,5.84,"esteemed Roman goddess of reason, wisdom, handicrafts, and war."],[89,24.69,4.71,"It’s come to my attention that many of you have been thinking about Ancient Rome..."],[89,29.57,1.0,"a lot."],[89,31.03,2.25,"But how much do you really know?"],[89,33.65,1.21,"I’ll be the judge."],[89,34.86,5.67,"Let’s play a little game I like to call duo vera et mendacium!"],[89,40.87,2.59,"Or, to the rest of you: two truths and a lie."],[89,44.12,3.46,"Let's meet our three brave contestants."],[89,48.38,2.5,"And let’s show them what they’re playing for!"],[89,51.26,5.0,"An all-expense paid cruise aboard one of Caligula’s pleasure barges!"],[89,56.26,5.13,"Prepare to be wined and dined on a trip you’ll never forget—"],[89,61.56,3.0,"well, you’ll probably forget."],[89,65.35,1.75,"As for the losers..."],[89,68.73,4.21,"The die is cast! Prepare to spot the lie!"],[89,73.49,3.79,"A. Gladiators had their own guilds."],[89,77.53,4.75,"B. Most gladiatorial fights ended before anyone died."],[89,82.7,3.92,"C. Gladiators were from an enslaved class."],[89,87.0,2.63,"And I thought this was an easy one."],[89,93.05,3.96,"It’s true that from their introduction around the 3rd century BCE,"],[89,97.09,4.05,"Roman gladiators were often enslaved or convicted criminals,"],[89,101.39,3.0,"but this changed by the 1st century CE."],[89,104.56,3.25,"After the opening of the Colosseum in 80 CE,"],[89,108.02,4.5,"freemen and former soldiers began signing up for a life in the arena,"],[89,112.78,4.5,"lured by the potential for fame and regular employment."],[89,117.78,1.92,"They even formed their own guilds,"],[89,119.7,2.84,"which organized funerals and provided pensions"],[89,122.53,2.59,"for gladiators’ families if they were to die."],[89,125.66,3.29,"But it took a lot of time and money to train a gladiator,"],[89,129.17,3.5,"so it wouldn’t make any sense to have them fighting to the death—"],[89,132.84,2.0,"at least not all the time."],[89,135.59,2.71,"Moving on to fashion:"],[89,139.72,4.71,"A. The toga was the cornerstone of daily Roman dress."],[89,144.85,4.63,"B. Many Roman women wore dresses made with Chinese silk."],[89,150.02,4.84,"C. Urine was the primary laundry detergent for Roman clothes."],[89,159.41,2.79,"While male Roman citizens did wear togas,"],[89,162.2,3.21,"they were strictly for ceremonial and official events."],[89,165.66,4.92,"And they were worn over clothing, like tunics, never on their own."],[89,170.79,1.33,"That would be indecent."],[89,172.46,4.88,"As indecent as some writers called the flimsy Chinese silk dresses"],[89,177.34,3.13,"that became fashionable amongst upper class Roman women"],[89,180.47,2.08,"in the 1st century CE."],[89,183.43,2.59,"And finally, the ammonia in urine"],[89,186.01,4.05,"does indeed make for an excellent stain-fighting solution,"],[89,190.39,3.92,"which is why Roman launderers often placed buckets on street corners"],[89,194.31,2.54,"to collect the urine of passersby."],[89,197.32,2.67,"That reminds me, next question!"],[89,200.7,4.0,"A. Many Roman homes featured a vomitorium,"],[89,204.7,3.84,"a room where people could vomit after overindulging."],[89,209.08,1.71,"B. The Pythagoreans,"],[89,210.79,3.67,"who followed the teachings of the Greek mathematician Pythagoras,"],[89,214.59,2.46,"embraced a vegetarian diet."],[89,217.55,5.63,"C. A favorite Roman condiment was sauce made from fermented fish guts."],[89,230.14,3.46,"While vomit and vomitoria come from the same root,"],[89,233.6,2.17,"meaning “to spew forth,”"],[89,235.98,5.34,"a vomitorium is simply a passage through which spectators spewed"],[89,241.32,3.09,"from amphitheaters or arenas into the street."],[89,244.78,2.71,"Purging was a common medical treatment,"],[89,247.49,4.21,"but it certainly wasn't prescribed to make room for more fish gut sauce."],[89,252.21,4.05,"As for Pythagoras, there were more than three sides to the man,"],[89,256.25,1.25,"I’ll have you know."],[89,257.59,3.71,"He was a philosopher, and his followers embraced his belief"],[89,261.3,3.63,"that eating animal flesh increased aggressive behavior."],[89,265.8,1.83,"We’re all tied up."],[89,267.64,4.3,"But this last question is sure to separate the true Romano-files"],[89,271.93,2.13,"from the Romano-flakes."],[89,274.31,4.92,"A. The city of Rome was sacked twice during the 5th century CE."],[89,279.65,4.71,"B. The last western Roman emperor was Romulus Augustulus."],[89,284.7,5.63,"C. The Roman Empire fell in 476 CE."],[89,293.08,1.83,"Just as I thought!"],[89,295.04,4.34,"The very idea that Rome fell is... complicated."],[89,299.71,4.09,"It’s true that by 476 the empire looked very different."],[89,304.05,1.13,"By the 4th century,"],[89,305.18,3.13,"it had permanently split into eastern and western halves,"],[89,308.47,1.75,"each with its own emperor."],[89,310.64,3.09,"The last emperor in the west, Romulus Augustulus,"],[89,313.81,3.8,"was overthrown in 476 CE in a military coup."],[89,317.73,5.05,"But the Germanic general who replaced him didn’t make sweeping changes."],[89,323.03,5.76,"Roman political and cultural life endured, and military conquests continued—"],[89,328.91,2.54,"at least until the eastern Emperor Justinian"],[89,331.45,4.05,"led a devastating invasion of Italy in the 530s."],[89,335.71,4.13,"In the east, the Roman Empire survived for a thousand years,"],[89,340.04,3.75,"becoming what you now call the Byzantine Empire."],[89,344.21,3.84,"That makes contestant number 3 our big winner!"],[89,348.39,1.67,"Enjoy the spoils!"],[89,350.55,3.38,"As for our losers, better luck next time."],[89,354.27,2.13,"Here's hoping there is one."],[90,3.9,2.64,"Whitney Pennington Rodgers: In your work as an educator,"],[90,6.54,1.94,"curiosity plays a very interesting role."],[90,8.51,2.54,"Can you talk a little bit about how curiosity shows up"],[90,11.04,1.17,"in the work that you do?"],[90,12.24,3.97,"Stephen Nobles: So in the work that we do, it's important for curiosity to be there."],[90,16.25,3.07,"It gives us the opportunity to see things not just how they are,"],[90,19.32,1.17,"but how they can be."],[90,20.52,2.64,"And we're learning, in our district,"],[90,23.19,2.74,"that with curiosity, when students feel seen,"],[90,25.96,2.24,"once they feel heard, once they feel empowered,"],[90,28.23,3.04,"they'll engage, they'll connect to the work,"],[90,31.3,2.14,"and then the attendance shows up"],[90,33.43,2.64,"and then the performance follows the attendance."],[90,36.1,1.47,"So it requires us to follow,"],[90,37.6,4.67,"like the great 21st-century English philosopher Ted Lasso puts it,"],[90,42.31,3.1,"to be curious and not judgmental."],[90,45.41,1.17,"And in our district,"],[90,46.61,2.2,"our work requires us to not be judgmental."],[90,49.75,1.64,"WPR: And can you talk a little bit"],[90,51.38,3.54,"about the bridge between curiosity and courage?"],[90,54.95,2.1,"How do you help people move past,"],[90,57.09,5.47,"I guess, what might be the fear of failure to really engage with curiosity?"],[90,62.59,3.97,"SN: If you get curiosity right, it's the bridge between our differences."],[90,66.6,1.4,"It turns it into dialogue."],[90,68.73,4.04,"So you want to be able to see kids and meet them where they are,"],[90,72.8,3.84,"but that requires you to see them with a lens of expectation and opportunity"],[90,76.67,1.44,"without the judgment."],[90,78.14,2.2,"And for us, one of the things that's important,"],[90,80.38,2.34,"that I'm always pushing my colleagues to see,"],[90,82.75,3.6,"is that we have to see students beyond the data."],[90,87.38,3.44,"A fact with that is if a kid lives in poverty, they're not poor."],[90,90.86,2.47,"Their parents are poor and they live with them."],[90,93.36,3.7,"The same way, if a kid's parents are rich, they're not rich."],[90,97.06,2.23,"Their parents are rich and they live with them."],[90,99.33,3.27,"So with that, all of the data, all of the characteristics of poverty,"],[90,102.6,2.1,"place it on the parents and not the student."],[90,104.73,2.1,"See them through the lens of what's possible,"],[90,106.87,2.04,"and then they usually rise to the occasion."],[90,113.91,1.6,"WPR: This moment we're living in,"],[90,115.55,1.77,"it's sometimes really easy for people"],[90,117.31,3.27,"to sort of double down on what their feelings are,"],[90,120.62,2.87,"to be more certain about where they stand in the world"],[90,123.52,4.97,"instead of asking people how they feel, what they think, to be curious."],[90,128.53,2.97,"How important do you think curiosity is as a life skill"],[90,131.5,2.3,"for all of us in this moment?"],[90,133.83,1.53,"SN: I think it's very important."],[90,135.8,4.57,"I can only imagine a world where we celebrate not just the answers,"],[90,140.4,1.83,"but the best questions."],[90,142.27,2.67,"Because we live in this data-driven society,"],[90,144.97,3.34,"if the data is right, it should propose better questions."],[90,148.34,1.7,"It should ask us to dive in deeper."],[90,150.08,2.77,"And that requires genuine curiosity."],[90,152.88,3.3,"And curiosity, when put in the right place, builds connection."],[90,156.22,2.84,"Connection builds friendship and partnership."],[90,159.09,2.27,"That's the bridge toward a brighter future,"],[90,161.39,4.17,"especially in a world where everyone is so quick to give you a certain answer"],[90,165.6,2.0,"and they just say it with such confidence."],[90,167.63,4.27,"But curiosity is the thing that gives us humility and empathy,"],[90,171.94,2.33,"and that's the thing that we need to move forward,"],[90,174.27,3.14,"not just in education, but as a world, in my humble opinion."],[90,177.94,1.57,"WPR: Well, you said this earlier"],[90,179.51,3.17,"that curiosity can turn disagreement into dialogue."],[90,182.71,3.0,"Can you talk a little bit about how that's shown up"],[90,185.75,2.34,"in the classrooms you've been in, with students"],[90,188.12,3.07,"and what you've learned about the importance of curiosity"],[90,191.22,1.2,"for bridging a divide?"],[90,192.46,2.17,"SN: Yeah, so a good example is"],[90,194.66,3.14,"we started a TED-Ed student talk program in our district."],[90,197.83,2.54,"We started that program at one of the middle schools,"],[90,200.36,1.94,"well we started at an elementary school,"],[90,202.33,2.5,"but last year we did it at one of our middle schools."],[90,204.84,1.9,"And it's a kind of tough middle school."],[90,206.77,2.6,"So when we get into this space, I'm telling the adults,"],[90,209.37,2.2,"before we get in here, do not judge these kids."],[90,211.57,3.47,"We're not going to put barriers or parameters on what they can speak on,"],[90,215.08,1.5,"who they are, what they can do."],[90,216.58,3.47,"Let's just give them the space to be themselves without judgment."],[90,220.08,2.2,"So we have this student who comes up --"],[90,222.32,3.87,"Jordan's, skinny jeans, dreads,"],[90,226.22,2.04,"hair in his face, hoodie on,"],[90,228.29,1.77,"looks like he’s not engaged --"],[90,230.09,3.77,"he’s over here writing to himself, and he comes up and he’s like,"],[90,233.9,3.44,"“What do you want to talk about? What are you interested in speaking on?”"],[90,237.37,2.2,"His topic was trash in space."],[90,239.6,2.3,"He was interested in discussing what would happen"],[90,241.91,1.77,"if the trash that's orbiting Earth,"],[90,243.71,2.77,"from damaged satellites and different things in space,"],[90,246.51,3.6,"was able to be pulled into our gravitational pull."],[90,250.15,1.53,"What that would mean."],[90,251.72,1.9,"No adult expected that."],[90,254.62,2.64,"Everyone was shocked that this kid was interested"],[90,257.29,3.1,"in what happens if trash is pulled into our orbit."],[90,260.42,3.1,"To me, we were able to have that experience"],[90,263.53,1.83,"by just remaining curious,"],[90,265.4,2.1,"to not judge them, to not guide him,"],[90,267.53,3.27,"to just give him the space and then the tools to meet the standard."],[90,270.83,1.17,"And he met the standard."],[90,272.0,1.2,"He actually surprised us."],[90,273.24,2.64,"And my relationship with that kid is still growing now."],[90,275.91,2.4,"He’s in high school now, and every time I see him"],[90,278.31,2.87,"he’s always ... going to lock in."],[90,281.21,1.9,"I can't wait to see who he becomes."],[90,283.15,1.53,"WPR: I mean, that's interesting."],[90,284.71,2.0,"It leads to this other idea of what happens"],[90,286.75,2.1,"when one person is curious about someone else"],[90,288.89,1.94,"and sort of how that spreads in a space."],[90,290.85,1.27,"SN: Yeah, it's contagious."],[90,292.16,1.43,"WPR: Can you talk a little bit"],[90,293.62,2.97,"about how you've seen curiosity impact other people?"],[90,296.63,3.37,"Being curious about one person impacts other people in a space."],[90,300.03,3.17,"SN: So it's kind of like the way that sports works."],[90,305.2,2.67,"The students were able to see and connect to one another"],[90,307.9,4.97,"in a way that their home life or their grades or their behavior"],[90,312.91,2.1,"may not have connected them before."],[90,315.05,2.7,"But it provided a safe space for the students to be seen,"],[90,317.78,2.34,"a safe space for them to voice their opinion."],[90,320.15,3.54,"But even to back away from the students, being at the district level,"],[90,323.72,1.64,"for the adults and the leaders,"],[90,325.39,2.67,"to have that curiosity, to move in such a way"],[90,328.09,2.94,"that they're not just providing the answers,"],[90,331.03,3.74,"they're comfortable with also recognizing that some of their growth"],[90,334.8,2.54,"is going to come from the crowd that they're leading."],[90,337.37,2.33,"I've just seen that begin to grow in our district."],[90,339.74,2.33,"And now for us, we're so excited for the future"],[90,342.11,2.94,"because of that opportunity, that curiosity."],[90,345.04,3.14,"Even in leadership it's creating for us as a district."],[90,348.18,1.77,"And as individuals, to be honest."],[90,349.95,2.54,"WPR: I want to talk a little bit about the work you do"],[90,352.52,2.1,"of sort of creating a culture around this."],[90,354.62,2.6,"And, you know, we're sort of rewarded,"],[90,357.22,4.64,"in education, I'm sure, but just in life, for having the answer."],[90,361.89,4.64,"And so, how do you create a space where people feel comfortable"],[90,366.56,1.57,"engaging with possibilities"],[90,368.17,3.97,"instead of just coming right down to a solution?"],[90,372.17,3.54,"SN: Yeah, just trying to create a space where everyone is looking at things"],[90,375.74,2.87,"for where they can be and how they can be."],[90,378.64,3.24,"Because sometimes if we focus just on how they are, can be daunting."],[90,381.88,3.74,"And that could be enough to make people want to quit academically and in life."],[90,385.62,1.33,"And for me, as an educator,"],[90,386.95,2.34,"our goal is to get our students positioned"],[90,389.32,3.34,"to recognize that learning isn't just a K-12 adventure."],[90,392.69,2.3,"It's not just a phase that you're in currently."],[90,394.99,3.17,"To remain curious prepares them to recognize their learning"],[90,398.19,1.6,"as a lifelong journey."],[90,399.83,2.04,"And even as adults, when we remain curious,"],[90,401.9,2.64,"we see things through the lens of possibility."],[90,404.57,3.4,"And then the students see that behavior modeled by us, as adults,"],[90,407.97,2.3,"and then they come in and it becomes contagious."],[90,410.31,1.17,"It becomes a culture,"],[90,411.51,1.97,"and that's what moves everything forward."],[90,413.48,2.6,"That's what we're starting to see move our city forward"],[90,416.11,1.74,"beyond just our school district."],[90,417.88,2.84,"WPR: And I'm sure there are people that maybe are listening,"],[90,420.72,1.64,"they're saying, this sounds great,"],[90,422.39,3.1,"and maybe you come across these people in your work who feel like,"],[90,425.52,2.54,"I want to have more curiosity in the spaces I inhabit,"],[90,428.09,3.1,"but I feel really frustrated by the current state of discourse"],[90,431.19,4.94,"or shut down by how people are accustomed to sort of approaching these things."],[90,436.17,1.83,"What advice would you give to somebody"],[90,438.04,2.57,"who's sort of going against the tide in that way?"],[90,440.64,1.27,"SN: Be open."],[90,442.24,3.27,"See everything from a lens of not where you are,"],[90,445.51,1.77,"but where you hope for them to be."],[90,447.31,3.47,"It requires us to kind of disconnect from ourselves as individuals"],[90,450.81,1.6,"and see things as a collective."],[90,453.58,1.4,"That's the biggest challenge,"],[90,454.99,2.04,"just getting people at the beginning of it."],[90,457.02,2.77,"But once it starts and once it catches on, it's contagious."],[90,459.79,2.3,"The work just moves -- it begins to do it itself."],[90,462.09,1.74,"You don't have to push it that much."],[90,463.83,2.07,"But the tough part is just getting everyone"],[90,465.9,2.04,"at the entry point of what's possible,"],[90,467.96,2.04,"seeing things beyond where they are,"],[90,470.03,1.57,"beyond where the data is,"],[90,471.63,3.3,"beyond what the data is saying that students are capable of."],[90,474.94,1.67,"That's the toughest part."],[90,476.64,1.64,"But once you get there,"],[90,478.31,3.4,"the great work, the imagination, the leadership, the expectation,"],[90,481.75,1.87,"it begins to beget itself."],[90,483.61,4.54,"So it just creates this recycling symbol of leaders that are pushing children"],[90,488.19,2.94,"to be the next leaders, supporting their ideologies,"],[90,491.15,2.04,"supporting their dreams and their hopes."],[90,493.22,3.44,"And it just creates this symbol and then things really start to pick up."],[90,496.69,3.04,"And that's what attraction is, and that's the great spot."],[90,499.76,3.7,"WPR: Yeah, could you say more about, I guess an example"],[90,503.47,1.17,"of what that looks like?"],[90,504.67,4.34,"I love what you shared about the student who's thinking about trash in space"],[90,509.04,1.27,"and so, you know, I guess,"],[90,510.34,5.04,"what's an instance where you've seen somebody see this example"],[90,515.41,3.17,"of how they can push past this to really get there?"],[90,518.62,4.1,"SN: For us, it's moving beyond the data,"],[90,522.72,3.54,"moving beyond just telling the students what they can be."],[90,526.29,4.77,"Being open to the students questioning why this is this way."],[90,531.09,2.4,"Because that's the thing that's eye-opening for me."],[90,533.5,2.74,"When you start to have genuine conversations with students"],[90,536.27,2.44,"and you're telling them what they're expected to do,"],[90,538.7,1.17,"and then they question,"],[90,539.9,3.07,"well, why are the adults making the decisions for what I do next?"],[90,542.97,3.2,"Why aren't they asking us what we should be and how we should do it?"],[90,546.21,2.1,"That was eye-opening for me."],[90,548.31,3.4,"And man, there's so much I could say about it without being long-winded."],[90,551.75,1.97,"It's just when the students feel seen,"],[90,553.75,1.94,"when they feel heard,"],[90,555.69,3.2,"through the lens of curiosity, it's immeasurable."],[90,558.92,2.2,"And I just love being in a space,"],[90,561.12,3.34,"being with the students that are beginning to be heard or be seen"],[90,564.49,1.67,"and voice their opinion."],[90,566.2,2.04,"It's just so much that we've seen with it."],[90,568.23,1.84,"I'm sorry, I can't just lock in on one"],[90,570.07,2.7,"because I have a million going through my head right now."],[90,572.8,3.2,"But yeah, it's immeasurable what could happen once you get it right."],[90,576.04,1.27,"WPR: That's amazing."],[90,577.34,3.5,"And I think you've had the great fortune of being able to work with people"],[90,580.88,2.0,"who are different types of learners"],[90,582.88,3.17,"and have different ways in which they're engaging with ideas."],[90,586.08,2.0,"What is some advice you'd give for somebody"],[90,588.12,4.61,"who is trying to implement this in their own classroom or community,"],[90,592.76,3.94,"who might have learners who have different approaches"],[90,596.73,2.37,"or ways that they're taking in information?"],[90,599.13,2.54,"SN: See your students through the lens of possibility,"],[90,601.7,1.27,"see them beyond the data,"],[90,602.97,1.94,"see them beyond their living conditions,"],[90,604.9,1.97,"see them beyond their zip code,"],[90,606.87,2.67,"see them beyond their parents' lifestyle,"],[90,609.57,2.47,"see them through the lens of possibility, truly."],[90,612.08,2.97,"To me, that's the first step that has to exist."],[90,615.04,4.91,"And that requires us to recognize that if they are poor, they are not poor."],[90,619.98,3.44,"If they live in poverty, their parents are poor and they live with them."],[90,623.45,2.0,"Once you erase the data around the student"],[90,625.49,2.74,"and you see them truly through what's possible,"],[90,628.26,1.64,"that's when the beauty happens."],[90,629.89,1.7,"That's when the engagement starts."],[90,631.63,1.17,"That's when they're safe"],[90,632.83,2.2,"and they're comfortable to voice their opinion,"],[90,635.07,3.0,"to say more and to take ownership in their academic experience."],[90,638.1,1.17,"The data will follow,"],[90,639.27,2.9,"but you have to authentically be right there."],[90,642.21,3.17,"WPR: Well I’d love to hear you talk a little bit about yourself"],[90,645.41,3.1,"and the role curiosity has played in your own life."],[90,648.54,3.74,"How have you seen curiosity change things for you?"],[90,652.28,1.64,"What has it done in your life?"],[90,653.95,3.3,"SN: So my mom raised me in a space of curiosity."],[90,658.39,2.24,"My mom allowed me to question everything,"],[90,660.66,1.74,"to ask every question."],[90,662.43,2.8,"A good example: she raised me to be an avid reader."],[90,665.26,3.44,"So I grew up in a tough environment, small town here in Georgia."],[90,668.73,1.6,"But my mom encouraged me to read,"],[90,670.37,3.0,"always asked me to read for her, always asked me to read to her,"],[90,673.4,1.17,"gave me books,"],[90,674.57,2.34,"\"Hey son, you want to go on a cruise this summer?\""],[90,676.94,1.17,"\"Yeah, of course.\""],[90,678.11,2.74,"\"Alright, if you read this book, \"The Odyssey,\" we'll go.\""],[90,682.08,1.27,"True story."],[90,683.38,2.4,"I shoot through the book to finish, and she's like,"],[90,685.82,1.77,"\"Did you enjoy that cruise?\""],[90,690.92,1.94,"Right, right."],[90,692.86,1.17,"WPR: I like your mom."],[90,694.96,3.1,"SN: But as you can see from my build, I've always been an athlete."],[90,698.1,1.6,"I've always been great at sports."],[90,699.7,3.47,"So I was one of the key players with my friends when it came to sports."],[90,703.2,2.74,"So there was a factory that was built in my hometown."],[90,705.94,1.67,"Whenever we got out of school,"],[90,707.6,1.27,"our parents were at work"],[90,708.87,2.37,"for like the next three hours we got off the bus."],[90,711.27,2.14,"So for three hours, it's just kids, you know?"],[90,713.41,2.0,"We're just roaming around, we're cooking,"],[90,715.45,1.14,"I'm an '80s baby."],[90,716.61,1.94,"We're navigating the world by ourselves."],[90,718.55,1.94,"But I read the \"Lord of the Flies.\""],[90,720.98,3.1,"And I remember going to my friends like, \"Man, I just read a book."],[90,724.12,1.3,"And this could end bad.\""],[90,728.86,3.57,"But in that, my friends weren't excited about it."],[90,732.43,2.97,"So much so that every time they came to play and I was reading,"],[90,735.43,2.6,"I started telling my mom to tell them I was asleep."],[90,738.07,2.47,"So what do you think my childhood nickname is?"],[90,740.57,1.17,"Sleepy."],[90,742.27,1.97,"But I remember asking my mom one time,"],[90,744.27,2.0,"\"If you're encouraging me to read so much,"],[90,746.28,1.9,"you're encouraging me to do this thing."],[90,748.21,1.77,"Why is everyone not on board with it?"],[90,749.98,1.17,"Why is it not supported?"],[90,751.18,2.64,"Why is it not a popular thing to do in my neighborhood?\""],[90,753.82,2.5,"And my mom said, \"Do you know what a weed is?\""],[90,756.92,3.17,"Yeah, it's that plant in the yard you make me get out of the grass."],[90,760.09,3.27,"She's like, \"Nope, that plant is only a weed because it's in my yard."],[90,763.39,2.87,"If you go to the jungle, a rose is a weed."],[90,766.86,3.17,"A weed is anything that's growing where it's not supposed to be."],[90,770.77,3.77,"They see you different because I'm raising you to be a weed."],[90,774.57,3.64,"I'm raising you to grow in an environment that you're not supposed to be in.\""],[90,778.51,3.5,"So she's always, throughout my life, encouraged me to be a weed,"],[90,782.04,1.5,"to constantly grow,"],[90,783.55,3.07,"especially if I'm in a space where I'm not supposed to be in."],[90,786.65,2.64,"That requires her curiosity and she granted me that."],[90,789.29,2.17,"So I’m always pushing students to be [a] weed."],[90,791.72,3.0,"WPR: It's sort of giving people permission to be curious."],[90,795.36,1.47,"It's beautiful, it really is."],[90,799.33,2.8,"Well I think when you think about the ideas"],[90,802.16,2.74,"of engaging the world in curiosity"],[90,804.9,2.0,"and sort of where we are in this moment,"],[90,806.9,3.84,"what gives you hope about what you see in your classrooms"],[90,810.77,3.7,"and what gives you hope about what you're seeing,"],[90,814.48,1.2,"maybe out in the world"],[90,815.71,3.57,"and how people might be approaching this idea of curiosity differently?"],[90,819.32,2.54,"SN: The enormous access to information."],[90,821.88,2.44,"The students are being pulled in so many directions."],[90,824.35,2.44,"We as adults are being pulled in so many directions,"],[90,826.82,3.14,"but we're starting to see kids take ownership in who they are"],[90,829.99,1.17,"and where they become,"],[90,831.16,2.1,"not necessarily from the way that we'd hope."],[90,833.3,3.9,"It feels like they're rebelling from what we expect them to do or be,"],[90,837.2,2.07,"but that's what makes me most hopeful."],[90,839.3,3.3,"The students are starting to question why things are the way they are,"],[90,842.64,2.1,"why politically they are the way they are,"],[90,844.77,2.14,"why the crime is the way it is."],[90,846.94,4.57,"If the students take that ownership at such a young age,"],[90,851.51,1.97,"and for me as an adult now in my 40s,"],[90,853.52,3.87,"recognizing that life is not a race anymore -- it’s a relay."],[90,857.42,2.84,"The expectation is for me to take this as far as I can"],[90,860.29,2.3,"and pass that baton back to the next generation."],[90,862.59,2.7,"I'm hopeful because I see them grabbing that baton,"],[90,865.29,3.07,"and I think the curiosity that is required"],[90,868.37,2.97,"for us to truly get things right that they possess,"],[90,871.37,2.84,"I truly feel hopeful for where we're headed academically,"],[90,874.24,2.27,"professionally, and just as people."],[90,876.54,2.94,"WPR: It's so exciting to think about how they're going to take"],[90,879.51,2.64,"that curiosity out into the world and make a difference."],[90,882.14,3.77,"And I think for everyone sitting here, what is sort of the big takeaway"],[90,885.95,3.4,"for people who are maybe not educators who are thinking about,"],[90,889.39,2.77,"you know, you make this great point about data."],[90,892.15,4.2,"How do we as people sort of take, I guess the data in our life"],[90,896.36,3.87,"and become curious about that and use that to fuel curiosity?"],[90,900.26,2.97,"SN: Use the data, even in our life, to ask better questions,"],[90,903.27,1.27,"question everything,"],[90,904.57,1.87,"and then ask the next question."],[90,906.47,5.27,"And to me, I think if we use curiosity to be the bridge between where we are"],[90,911.77,1.23,"and where we hope to be,"],[90,913.01,1.53,"with the right leadership"],[90,914.54,2.44,"and empowering the next generation to lead,"],[90,917.01,2.4,"I think it's immeasurable what we can accomplish."],[90,919.45,1.7,"For me, for my city,"],[90,921.18,2.74,"but with us being in Atlanta for the state of Georgia"],[90,923.95,2.07,"and hopefully for the entire country,"],[90,926.06,2.47,"I think curiosity, when put in the right place,"],[90,928.56,3.0,"is the fuel that truly is going to move things."],[90,931.59,1.4,"So I'm very hopeful for that."],[90,933.0,1.94,"And I think that's the biggest takeaway."],[90,934.97,3.6,"Curiosity is the fuel that's going to move things forward for us."],[90,938.6,2.2,"WPR: I love that as sort of a lasting takeaway."],[90,940.84,2.74,"Curiosity is the fuel that's going to move things forward."],[90,943.57,1.9,"SN: So remain curious, not just mental."],[90,945.51,1.17,"WPR: There you go."],[90,946.71,1.3,"Thank you so much, Stephen."],[91,4.33,2.87,"When I first started teaching rats to drive cars,"],[91,7.24,2.9,"I never dreamed that those little rodents would steer me"],[91,10.17,2.5,"toward a surprisingly big discovery,"],[91,12.71,3.04,"a lesson about the importance of joy in our lives."],[91,15.78,2.77,"It turns out that we had a lot to teach each other."],[91,18.58,1.7,"But I'm getting ahead of myself."],[91,20.32,1.84,"I'm a behavioral neuroscientist."],[91,22.19,2.74,"I’m interested in brains, behavior and environment"],[91,24.95,2.2,"and how they influence one another."],[91,27.19,1.37,"For most of my career,"],[91,28.59,2.37,"I've focused on the negative emotions:"],[91,30.96,3.6,"fear, stress, anxiety, symptoms of depression,"],[91,34.6,1.5,"and for good reason."],[91,36.13,3.17,"Depression rates remain unacceptably high,"],[91,39.34,3.17,"and recent reports suggest that deaths of despair,"],[91,42.54,3.94,"deaths related to suicide and addiction and loneliness,"],[91,46.51,2.57,"are on the rise in the United States."],[91,49.08,2.13,"Even though we've invested a lot of research"],[91,51.25,3.1,"and resources toward understanding mental illness,"],[91,54.35,2.04,"it seems that we have a long way to go."],[91,56.39,3.27,"Perhaps we need some fresh perspectives in this area."],[91,59.99,3.24,"Several years ago, when I was writing my book “Lifting Depression,”"],[91,63.26,4.61,"I started to investigate the connections between reward and physical effort."],[91,68.23,2.74,"I introduced this term \"behaviorceuticals,\""],[91,70.97,3.17,"the idea that we can intentionally change our behavior"],[91,74.17,3.2,"to alter neurochemistry in therapeutic ways."],[91,78.88,3.77,"If you have enjoyed knitting or cooking"],[91,82.68,2.94,"or producing a piece of art,"],[91,85.65,3.34,"you've experienced your own dose of behaviorceuticals."],[91,88.99,1.7,"But that's anecdotal evidence."],[91,90.69,3.87,"I needed to take this to the lab and consult with my research colleagues,"],[91,94.59,1.94,"that's the laboratory rats."],[91,96.56,5.37,"So we trained them to exert physical effort for Froot Loops,"],[91,101.97,1.74,"and that's the currency of my lab."],[91,103.73,2.94,"They love Froot Loops, so they'll work for Froot Loops."],[91,106.7,1.57,"So we had about five weeks"],[91,108.31,3.0,"where the animals had to connect physical effort,"],[91,111.31,1.94,"digging up those Froot Loops."],[91,113.58,1.47,"And at the end of that time,"],[91,115.08,3.9,"we saw that there was enhanced evidence of emotional resilience,"],[91,119.02,2.9,"more effective coping strategies"],[91,121.95,2.1,"and even signs of neuroplasticity,"],[91,124.05,3.5,"this idea of the brain changing in response to the changing demands"],[91,127.59,1.6,"of the life of the animal."],[91,129.23,5.11,"And that's traditionally thought to be good and healthy for the brain."],[91,134.37,3.47,"Now when we compared these animals to the control group,"],[91,137.87,2.97,"this was a group that received the same number of Froot Loops,"],[91,140.87,1.87,"but they didn't have to work for it."],[91,142.77,2.9,"We called this the Trust Fund Rats group,"],[91,145.71,3.97,"and we did not see the benefits in these animals."],[91,149.71,2.64,"So it seemed to be more than just the reward."],[91,152.38,1.87,"It's our relationship with the reward"],[91,154.28,4.24,"and perhaps the ability to control these rewards."],[91,158.56,1.7,"So this was interesting."],[91,160.29,1.6,"And we did a good bit of research"],[91,161.89,2.57,"on what we call “effort-based reward protocol”"],[91,164.5,2.04,"and found some interesting findings,"],[91,166.56,3.6,"but I still found myself going back to these negative emotions,"],[91,170.17,1.74,"especially chronic stress."],[91,172.24,3.74,"Investigating all the ways things can go wrong with brains."],[91,176.27,2.47,"But it took the rats to point me in the direction"],[91,178.78,2.07,"of looking at what could go right."],[91,181.25,2.1,"Let me provide a little context here."],[91,183.35,2.8,"So those rats that I talked about, the driving rats,"],[91,186.15,2.64,"in the beginning of the talk,"],[91,188.82,3.97,"we originally trained them to drive to understand more about agency"],[91,192.82,2.84,"and a sense of control and skill acquisition"],[91,195.69,1.94,"on their brains and behavior."],[91,198.06,3.9,"And so we taught them to drive these rodent-operated vehicles,"],[91,202.0,2.4,"or ROVs, as we call them."],[91,204.4,3.7,"And we found some interesting findings, and we published it."],[91,208.14,3.5,"But we quickly realized that this program of research"],[91,211.64,2.8,"had a lot of potential for science outreach."],[91,214.48,3.3,"And this is something that we really need today."],[91,217.78,4.87,"So we decided to keep a group of rats trained up on driving"],[91,222.69,2.77,"so that we could continue with this science outreach."],[91,225.46,2.4,"And they became little rodent celebrities."],[91,227.89,5.21,"They were in documentaries and newscasts and podcasts, and that was all great."],[91,233.56,2.04,"But I remember during the pandemic,"],[91,235.63,3.14,"when all the students were gone, it was very quiet on campus,"],[91,238.8,3.07,"and a few colleagues and I were taking turns going into the lab"],[91,241.91,1.94,"to take care of the animals."],[91,243.87,3.34,"And I vividly remember this one morning,"],[91,247.24,1.53,"I walked into the lab,"],[91,248.78,2.74,"and I saw these rats run up to the front of the cage,"],[91,251.51,2.57,"and they were reaching their little arms out"],[91,254.12,1.6,"and jumping up and down."],[91,255.72,4.1,"And it looked like they were excited to see me, like little rat joy."],[91,259.82,3.4,"And it made me excited that they were excited to see me."],[91,263.23,3.34,"And on the heels of all the negative-emotion research"],[91,266.6,1.87,"that I had conducted through the years,"],[91,268.46,2.37,"and all the negativity of the pandemic,"],[91,270.87,3.77,"I decided right then and there that whatever that was I was seeing,"],[91,274.67,1.53,"I wanted to investigate it."],[91,276.24,1.4,"I wanted to know more."],[91,277.94,5.54,"But could a neuroscientist study something as complex as joy in a rat?"],[91,283.51,2.17,"I went to the literature and was encouraged."],[91,285.72,4.07,"I even found a definition of joy in non-human animals:"],[91,289.82,2.27,"“a brief and intense burst of activity"],[91,292.12,3.54,"associated with a favored event or object.”"],[91,295.66,2.7,"So when my husband and I would ask our little dog, Brody,"],[91,298.36,3.64,"if he wanted to go for a walk or just showed him the leash"],[91,302.0,1.77,"and saw him running around in circles"],[91,303.8,2.2,"and jumping up and down and yelping endlessly,"],[91,306.04,2.54,"that seemed to fit the definition of joy."],[91,308.57,1.4,"If I could see it in a dog,"],[91,310.01,2.67,"I was encouraged that we could see it in the rats."],[91,312.68,1.64,"But I still needed a plan."],[91,314.68,2.8,"I landed on this idea of anticipation,"],[91,317.51,2.1,"the time before an award,"],[91,319.65,4.37,"because I knew that we could manipulate that time in the laboratory."],[91,324.89,4.91,"So I was encouraged and really fascinated by some research"],[91,329.83,1.17,"conducted by scientists,"],[91,330.99,3.77,"where they trained rats to press a bar to have cocaine delivered"],[91,334.76,1.94,"directly to their brains."],[91,336.73,2.07,"And when the cocaine hit the brain,"],[91,338.84,2.17,"their natural kind of feel-good neurochemical"],[91,341.0,2.44,"known as dopamine, would rise."],[91,343.47,1.64,"But what was even more interesting"],[91,345.14,4.24,"was when that rat would approach that bar, before the cocaine was there,"],[91,349.41,2.3,"you'd also see this rise in dopamine."],[91,352.12,2.67,"It seemed that the brain was interpreting the reward"],[91,354.82,3.64,"and the time before the reward in a similar manner."],[91,358.99,3.84,"It really makes me wonder what our Founding Fathers knew"],[91,362.83,4.87,"when they emphasized life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness."],[91,367.73,1.67,"It's really interesting."],[91,369.4,2.13,"So we had an idea: anticipation."],[91,371.57,2.24,"We still needed to translate this"],[91,373.84,2.4,"into something that we could use in the laboratory."],[91,376.24,1.5,"We came up with this protocol"],[91,377.77,4.87,"that we referred to as “unpredictable positive event responses,” or UPERs,"],[91,382.65,2.8,"in keeping with this behaviorceutical theme."],[91,385.48,1.5,"So this was pretty simple."],[91,387.02,1.47,"For about five weeks"],[91,388.52,2.6,"we introduced the rats to three good things,"],[91,391.15,1.74,"fun things, every day,"],[91,392.92,3.2,"in an unpredictable order and time."],[91,396.16,2.0,"So they were exposed to a LEGO block,"],[91,398.19,2.7,"and they had to wait 15 minutes to get that Froot Loop,"],[91,400.93,3.0,"or a sunflower seed, they had to wait patiently to shell it,"],[91,403.97,2.97,"or they were placed in a transport cage"],[91,406.97,4.8,"and wheeled across the hallway into another space in the lab"],[91,411.81,4.4,"where they got to play around, run around in what we called Rat Park."],[91,416.25,3.5,"And they had to wait in that transport cage for three minutes."],[91,420.08,2.24,"We were encouraged that we were on to something"],[91,422.35,3.47,"when we started to observe the rats in that transport cage."],[91,425.86,4.54,"And we saw brief bursts of behavior, running around, they seemed so excited."],[91,430.43,2.04,"So we were encouraged that indeed,"],[91,432.46,3.17,"we were influencing something that looked like joy,"],[91,435.63,2.6,"or at least positive anticipation in these rats."],[91,438.7,1.44,"So what are we finding?"],[91,440.17,3.9,"The results are still rolling in, but we're starting to see some trends."],[91,444.41,3.54,"If we expose these rats to a rat-optimism task,"],[91,447.98,2.0,"and yes, there is such a thing,"],[91,450.01,3.87,"we've seen that the males shift from a pessimistic strategy"],[91,453.92,2.37,"to an optimistic strategy."],[91,456.29,1.87,"But we don't see that with the females."],[91,458.19,2.87,"They remain more reality-based in both groups."],[91,462.46,3.17,"Now I should mention that the control group"],[91,465.66,2.14,"for this bit of research"],[91,467.83,2.6,"is a group that receives all the good things at once,"],[91,470.43,3.34,"so they don’t have that anticipation period there."],[91,473.8,3.6,"So we’re not sure if that generalizes to humans, about the females,"],[91,477.44,2.1,"but we need to pursue that further."],[91,479.54,3.9,"But the other results we see both in males and females,"],[91,483.48,2.17,"and it is important to look at sex differences"],[91,485.68,1.5,"when we’re doing this research,"],[91,487.18,4.04,"so both males and females show increased and intense exploration,"],[91,491.25,2.57,"as if they think they’re going to find something good,"],[91,493.82,2.1,"when they're exploring a novel environment."],[91,495.96,2.4,"So we're very encouraged by these results."],[91,499.0,3.5,"And surprisingly, one day, a student ran into my office and said,"],[91,502.5,1.17,"\"Doctor Lambert,"],[91,503.7,2.94,"why are the UPER-trained rats' tails sticking straight up?\""],[91,506.67,2.94,"I thought, I've been working with rats for over three decades,"],[91,509.64,2.33,"and I've never seen this or heard of it."],[91,511.98,3.2,"But I went to the literature, and I saw that some researchers"],[91,515.21,4.14,"would inject an opioid into the animals,"],[91,519.38,1.64,"and their tails would go up."],[91,521.05,1.44,"And I thought, oh my goodness,"],[91,522.52,2.37,"maybe we're doing something with behaviorceuticals"],[91,524.89,2.0,"that has been done with pharmaceuticals."],[91,526.92,2.94,"So this is something that we need to explore further."],[91,529.89,3.04,"This research made me think about some classic research"],[91,532.96,4.71,"conducted in the 1950s by Curt Richter at Johns Hopkins University."],[91,537.7,3.37,"He was interested in swim behavior between lab rats and wild rats,"],[91,541.1,3.44,"and he had some interesting laboratory conditions for this."],[91,544.58,2.13,"But in the swim tank, it was turbulent water,"],[91,546.71,1.53,"so it was challenging."],[91,548.25,3.74,"And he saw that the lab rats could swim for hours or days."],[91,551.98,1.64,"They're great swimmers."],[91,553.65,2.57,"But the wild rats, when he trapped them,"],[91,556.25,2.0,"brought them in and put them in the tank,"],[91,558.29,2.77,"they sank immediately, dying."],[91,561.09,1.27,"And he was shocked by this"],[91,562.36,2.27,"because it's tough living out there in the wild,"],[91,564.63,2.87,"and he'd thought those wild rats would be more resilient."],[91,567.5,2.57,"He said it looked like they were just giving up."],[91,570.1,4.44,"But when he thought about the experiences of the lab rats and the wild rats,"],[91,574.54,4.27,"he realized that those lab rats had been periodically picked up by human hands,"],[91,578.84,1.6,"and their conditions had changed."],[91,580.44,3.27,"So there was some hope that if you didn't like what was going on now,"],[91,583.75,1.17,"it could change."],[91,584.95,2.14,"But the wild rats hadn't had that experience."],[91,587.12,2.24,"So he devised this intervention"],[91,589.39,2.97,"where his assistants would pick up the wild rats"],[91,592.39,4.07,"from the restraining cage or from the water just once or twice."],[91,596.46,1.97,"And he ran the study again."],[91,598.43,4.54,"And he found that a majority of those wild rats at that time,"],[91,603.0,2.94,"they went from dying to surviving."],[91,605.97,4.97,"And he said it was the hope of rescue that led them down that path."],[91,611.24,2.87,"It makes you think: is this important for humans?"],[91,614.14,2.2,"There is a good bit of research to suggest"],[91,616.38,4.44,"that hope and positive attitudes are important for health and longevity."],[91,621.32,4.77,"One of my favorite studies was conducted on Israeli children"],[91,626.12,1.17,"who were very ill,"],[91,627.32,2.9,"and they were part of the Make-A-Wish Foundation program,"],[91,630.26,4.1,"and the researchers monitored their general physical health"],[91,634.4,1.7,"and mental health outcomes"],[91,636.13,3.54,"from the time they were told specifically what their wish was"],[91,639.7,1.87,"to the time the wish was granted."],[91,641.61,1.6,"And that was about five months,"],[91,643.24,3.44,"so they could extend that anticipatory time."],[91,646.71,3.24,"And they found that these outcomes improved,"],[91,649.95,4.77,"to suggest that maybe their quality of life during that time was enhanced."],[91,654.75,3.7,"And when they compared it to the children who were in the queue to get a wish,"],[91,658.49,1.94,"but they hadn't been told the specifics,"],[91,660.42,1.97,"so they really couldn't wish specifically"],[91,662.43,2.0,"and hope specifically for that to happen."],[91,664.46,3.64,"Hopefully, when those children received the details of their wish,"],[91,668.1,2.4,"they also benefited from this."],[91,670.93,3.64,"So there's a lot of research to suggest that, yes,"],[91,674.6,3.64,"those negative emotions are important to understand,"],[91,678.27,2.8,"but these positive emotions are as well."],[91,681.11,2.2,"And when we step back and look at the brain"],[91,683.31,6.41,"and everything that's going on during positive anticipation and joy,"],[91,689.75,4.24,"you can see that there are a lot of different areas of the brain"],[91,694.02,3.4,"that are on board or activated during this time."],[91,697.46,2.5,"Areas involved with processing reward"],[91,700.0,2.27,"and monitoring internal feelings"],[91,702.3,1.83,"and planning and movement"],[91,704.17,3.87,"are all activated during this time of joy"],[91,708.07,1.9,"and positive anticipation."],[91,710.34,4.61,"So with all of this evidence, behavioral, anecdotal, neurological,"],[91,714.95,3.14,"suggesting that positive emotions are important,"],[91,718.12,2.97,"this isn't always reflected in our culture."],[91,721.42,1.67,"It's really interesting to me"],[91,723.12,3.17,"that we have a word for anticipating something negative,"],[91,726.32,1.53,"it’s dread."],[91,727.86,1.2,"But to my knowledge,"],[91,729.09,4.37,"I don't know that we have a word related to anticipating something positive."],[91,733.86,1.83,"But in the German language they do."],[91,735.7,1.37,"It's \"vorfreude.\""],[91,737.1,2.5,"It means joyful anticipation."],[91,739.64,2.24,"Perhaps it's time for us to step up"],[91,741.87,3.77,"and come up with our own word for joyful anticipation."],[91,746.14,2.3,"And personally, I find myself going back"],[91,748.48,3.27,"to this Puritan work ethic too often"],[91,751.78,3.4,"where we equate busyness and hard work with virtue."],[91,755.55,4.1,"And if I find an hour or two to watch Netflix or something I enjoy,"],[91,759.69,2.7,"I often describe it as a \"guilty pleasure.\""],[91,762.42,2.4,"But why does it have to be a guilty pleasure?"],[91,764.83,1.57,"Far from an indulgence,"],[91,766.43,4.64,"it's looking like these pleasure and positive events are important"],[91,771.1,3.34,"and critical for healthy maintenance of our brains."],[91,775.04,2.74,"Let me end by going back to those driving rants."],[91,777.81,2.8,"A lot of people ask me, \"Kelly, do they like to drive?"],[91,780.64,1.57,"Do they enjoy driving?\""],[91,782.25,1.7,"And my response has typically been,"],[91,783.98,3.54,"\"I can't give them a questionnaire, I don't really know, I think they do.\""],[91,787.55,2.44,"But we could ask them from a behavioral perspective."],[91,790.02,1.57,"And that's what we did recently."],[91,791.59,2.64,"We gave them a choice of walking to the Froot Loop tree,"],[91,794.26,1.67,"that's what they drive to,"],[91,795.93,1.3,"in a more efficient path,"],[91,797.23,3.74,"or they could take a detour and backtrack and jump in the car"],[91,801.0,2.17,"and drive to the rewards."],[91,803.17,2.67,"And I couldn't wait to see what they would do."],[91,805.84,4.8,"And what we found was a majority of the animals did indeed backtrack,"],[91,810.67,5.27,"take a tour and jump into the car and drive to their Froot Loop rewards."],[91,817.65,3.64,"And if you saw a human, jumping in the background,"],[91,821.32,3.07,"that was me, jumping for joy"],[91,824.42,4.27,"seeing rats choose pleasure or fun over efficiency."],[91,833.2,2.8,"I taught rats to drive cars,"],[91,836.0,2.13,"but they taught me to enjoy the ride."],[91,838.17,1.17,"Thank you."],[92,6.92,4.34,"Early 20th century writer, Marcel Proust, finished his magnum opus"],[92,11.26,2.38,"“In Search of Lost Time” from bed—"],[92,13.72,2.84,"in a cork-lined room to keep allergens out."],[92,16.76,2.17,"Proust suffered from severe asthma."],[92,19.02,2.54,"At the time, there weren’t great treatments."],[92,21.6,1.54,"When breathlessness set in,"],[92,23.14,3.13,"he’d burn powders that filled the space with smoke and fumes."],[92,26.27,5.09,"Or, for a quick fix, he’d smoke a doctor-recommended anti-asthma cigarette."],[92,31.61,3.21,"These powders and cigarettes commonly contained thorn apple,"],[92,35.11,1.58,"which can open your airways."],[92,36.95,2.79,"However, both were clearly terrible ideas."],[92,39.87,4.63,"Smoking and fumes bring damaging, carcinogenic toxins into your lungs."],[92,44.92,1.92,"Thankfully, today we have inhalers—"],[92,46.83,4.34,"simple but powerful devices that deliver lung medications straight to the source"],[92,51.17,3.0,"and without the nasty side effects of smoke inhalation."],[92,54.59,2.75,"Inhalers are mainly used to treat two conditions:"],[92,57.34,4.3,"asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, or COPD."],[92,61.85,4.21,"And there are two main types: preventative inhalers and rescue inhalers."],[92,66.27,3.38,"Preventative inhalers can be used every day to control symptoms."],[92,69.77,3.54,"There are also rescue inhalers, which are great in an emergency,"],[92,73.57,2.29,"but using them regularly can be dangerous."],[92,77.45,1.38,"So how do they work?"],[92,79.37,1.13,"When you take a breath,"],[92,80.49,4.09,"air travels through your lungs using tubes called airways, or bronchi."],[92,84.75,3.0,"The airways funnel to sacs, called alveoli,"],[92,87.92,3.38,"where your red blood cells absorb all the oxygen your body needs."],[92,91.46,3.67,"But if you have asthma, the muscles around your airways may tighten,"],[92,95.34,2.34,"the lining of your airways may get inflamed,"],[92,97.72,4.13,"and your lungs may make too much of the mucus they use to trap dust and germs."],[92,102.06,3.75,"Essentially, this clogs the pipes and makes it difficult to exhale."],[92,106.06,3.84,"Rescue inhalers deliver a medication called a bronchodilator"],[92,109.9,3.46,"that quickly relaxes these muscles, making it easier to breathe."],[92,113.78,4.09,"These bronchodilators are short acting, lasting around four hours."],[92,118.28,2.79,"Rescue inhalers can be used for COPD, too."],[92,121.28,3.75,"COPD is a catch-all term to describe the most common breathing conditions,"],[92,125.04,2.38,"like emphysema and chronic bronchitis,"],[92,127.46,2.09,"which people often have at the same time."],[92,129.75,4.46,"In emphysema, repeated exposure to smoke or irritating particles"],[92,134.21,2.25,"breaks the inner walls of the alveoli."],[92,136.88,3.38,"Because there's less surface area for blood and oxygen to interact,"],[92,140.26,2.54,"less oxygen makes its way to your bloodstream,"],[92,142.81,2.46,"causing you to constantly feel out of breath."],[92,145.52,3.21,"In chronic bronchitis, the airway’s lining is inflamed"],[92,148.73,3.71,"and produces more mucus to trap incoming smoke or dust particles."],[92,152.73,2.17,"And the little hairs lining the airways"],[92,154.9,2.84,"that normally help push the mucus out are often damaged,"],[92,157.74,1.5,"so the mucus gets stuck."],[92,159.74,1.75,"During a COPD attack,"],[92,161.49,3.67,"rescue inhalers open the airways, just as they do for asthma."],[92,165.62,3.92,"Preventative inhalers are used daily to stop asthma and COPD symptoms"],[92,169.54,1.21,"before they even start."],[92,170.92,3.96,"They often contain both a corticosteroid, which reduces inflammation,"],[92,174.88,2.0,"and a long-acting bronchodilator."],[92,177.42,4.0,"In fact, one class of bronchodilators for patients with COPD"],[92,181.43,2.38,"is related to the compounds in thorn apples."],[92,184.22,4.04,"These drugs block signals from the nerves that tell the airway muscles to contract."],[92,188.48,2.54,"Those same nerve signals are thought to be responsible"],[92,191.02,1.79,"for increasing mucus in the lungs,"],[92,192.85,2.71,"so these drugs may help clear the airways as well."],[92,196.07,4.17,"The real beauty of inhalers is how they get these medications into your lungs."],[92,200.49,4.09,"They work by suspending, or aerosolizing, medications into the air"],[92,204.57,1.54,"to be easily inhaled."],[92,206.16,2.84,"Each type of inhaler does this in a slightly different way."],[92,209.25,3.46,"Dry powder inhalers require a person to breathe in"],[92,212.75,2.08,"to aerosolize a powder medication."],[92,215.0,3.54,"Nebulizers, on the other hand, use either ultrasonic vibrations"],[92,218.55,3.5,"or compressed air to turn liquid medication into a mist."],[92,222.34,3.46,"The pressurized metered-dose inhaler works a lot like hairspray."],[92,225.93,3.04,"This medicine is dissolved in a fluid called a propellant,"],[92,229.14,1.5,"and it's under high pressure."],[92,231.06,2.21,"This causes the fluid and medication mixture"],[92,233.27,2.08,"to shoot out in a fast-moving mist."],[92,235.52,3.25,"But this mist can sometimes be hard to coordinate with breathing."],[92,238.78,3.0,"So there’s one more device, called a soft mist inhaler,"],[92,241.78,3.84,"that administers doses at lower speeds, without the use of a propellant."],[92,246.62,4.55,"Asthma and COPD each affects hundreds of millions of people worldwide each year."],[92,251.29,1.33,"But thanks to inhalers,"],[92,252.62,2.88,"what once kept Proust confined to a cork-lined room,"],[92,255.5,2.59,"can now be treated in a few short puffs."],[93,0.08,4.16,"Ah, New York City. The home of Broadway, the two-dollar slice… and this!"],[93,4.24,1.2,"St. Patrick’s Cathedral."],[93,5.44,3.76,"The nearly 150-year-old church is truly something to behold."],[93,9.2,3.6,"With its sky-high spires, stained glass windows, and massive pipe organ,"],[93,12.8,1.84,"it’s a favorite destination for tourists."],[93,14.64,1.28,"I guess the shirt gave me away, huh?"],[93,15.92,0.88,"Yeah, I’m from California."],[93,16.8,1.92,"But I do a great Robert De Niro!"],[93,18.72,1.68,"I’ll work on it, okay, Dwayne?"],[93,20.4,2.64,"But there’s something else amazing about the cathedral."],[93,23.04,2.96,"Around the church, ten wells are dug deep within the earth,"],[93,26.0,2.64,"some as far down as 670 meters."],[93,28.64,4.0,"That’s more than six times deeper than the height of its spires."],[93,32.64,2.32,"Why? For energy."],[93,34.96,2.16,"Piped up straight from the earth."],[93,37.12,3.5,"Hi! I’m Sage, and this is Crash Course Geology."],[93,44.72,2.0,"When we need energy, where do we turn?"],[93,46.72,2.4,"When it comes to our bodies, we eat plants and animals."],[93,49.12,2.88,"But when it comes to fueling our cars, heating our houses,"],[93,52.0,3.84,"or powering our studio lights and video cameras, we need more energy."],[93,55.84,1.6,"Like, a lot more."],[93,57.44,5.12,"For the past hundred-fifty-ish years, we’ve mostly powered our world with fossil fuels—"],[93,62.56,2.72,"things like oil, coal, and natural gas."],[93,65.28,5.68,"They’re compounds made of algae, plant, and animal remains from millions of years ago."],[93,70.96,4.8,"That’s right. Million-year-old dead stuff powers my toothbrush, TV, and motor scooter."],[93,75.76,2.56,"Catch me yelling this to everyone as I scoot around Long Beach."],[93,78.32,2.24,"But how do we get fossil fuels?"],[93,80.56,2.64,"Well, when most living things die, they decompose,"],[93,83.2,2.08,"breaking down into simple compounds."],[93,85.28,2.56,"But if they're buried in just the right conditions,"],[93,87.84,2.08,"amazing things start to go down."],[93,89.92,1.52,"Take oil, for example."],[93,91.44,5.68,"To start, we’ve got plankton, those tiny life forms that drift around in bodies of water."],[93,97.12,4.8,"In still water without a lot of oxygen, it’s hard for them to decompose."],[93,101.92,6.72,"So instead, dead plankton accumulate at the bottom, mixing with clay to become “organic ooze.”"],[93,108.64,2.24,"And Earth starts to work its magic."],[93,110.88,2.24,"As the ooze gets buried deeper and deeper,"],[93,113.12,4.72,"the pressure squeezes out water, transforming it into rock known as organic shale."],[93,117.84,3.84,"And if that rock is buried deep enough, the rising temperatures transform it again,"],[93,121.68,3.12,"into a waxy, energy-dense substance called kerogen."],[93,124.8,2.32,"Under Earth’s surface, things can get pretty hot."],[93,127.12,3.12,"If temps down there climb past 90 degrees Celsius,"],[93,130.24,4.96,"kerogen breaks down into small oil molecules, known as hydrocarbons."],[93,135.2,1.44,"And at even higher temperatures,"],[93,136.64,4.16,"the oil molecules break down even smaller to form natural gas."],[93,140.8,3.84,"The natural gas we use for fuel is mostly made of methane, by the way."],[93,144.64,4.08,"Once they’re formed, oil and natural gas can percolate up toward the surface."],[93,148.72,3.68,"Some oil might even burble up in ponds called oil seeps."],[93,152.4,4.16,"But other pockets get trapped underground by impermeable layers of rock,"],[93,156.56,4.08,"and settle into reservoirs, which we can tap for fuel."],[93,160.64,3.36,"Now, coal, on the other hand, forms a bit differently."],[93,164.0,5.52,"Nearly all the coal on Earth right now formed about 350 million years ago."],[93,169.52,3.68,"At that time, Earth was covered in plants."],[93,173.2,5.04,"A super rainy climate gave us tons of tropical areas with wet forest floors."],[93,178.24,2.72,"Those swampy areas were low in oxygen,"],[93,180.96,3.2,"which meant that living things didn’t decompose as much."],[93,184.16,4.96,"At the same time, all the continents were coming together in a geologic mash-up"],[93,189.12,2.96,"that would eventually create the supercontinent Pangea."],[93,192.08,5.28,"As tectonic plates collided, they raised towering peaks and formed plunging basins:"],[93,197.36,4.72,"the perfect collection points for layer upon layer of dead plant matter."],[93,202.08,2.96,"That intense buildup of plants eventually transformed into peat,"],[93,205.04,2.56,"an energy-rich substance resembling soil."],[93,207.6,2.24,"Peat has long been an energy source for humans."],[93,209.84,2.72,"We’ve used it for cooking and heating for thousands of years."],[93,212.56,1.52,"But if peat is buried deep enough,"],[93,214.08,3.76,"the Earth’s heat and pressure concentrate the plants’ carbon and hydrogen"],[93,217.84,3.68,"into an even more energy-dense, brittle rock called coal."],[93,221.52,3.2,"This coal-creating time was only about sixty million years long—"],[93,224.72,4.16,"just a small slice of our planet’s 4.6 billion-year-history."],[93,228.88,4.64,"It became known as the Carboniferous period, meaning “coal-bearing.”"],[93,233.52,4.0,"Since then, conditions have rarely been exactly right to create more."],[93,237.52,4.0,"Once we use up our ancient reserves of coal, it’ll be gone, possibly forever."],[93,241.52,3.49,"Wow. I think I’d actually be down to get a lump of coal for Christmas this year."],[93,246.85,1.83,"You’re real?!"],[93,249.92,1.28,"Dairy free right?"],[93,251.2,1.32,"Oh my god, thank you."],[93,252.56,5.68,"This coal-nundrum captures one of the big problems with fossil fuels:"],[93,258.24,4.4,"they’re nonrenewable, which means it’s possible to use them up,"],[93,262.64,5.92,"or for them to become so hard to extract that it’s not practical to keep using them."],[93,268.56,4.08,"Even though the earth can make more coal, oil, and natural gas,"],[93,272.64,2.56,"it can’t do it on a human timescale."],[93,275.2,4.0,"It would take millions of years to restore what we’ve consumed."],[93,279.2,5.2,"And even if they were infinite, there are issues with how fossil fuels are extracted, too."],[93,284.4,3.68,"When more easily accessible reservoirs of oil and natural gas run dry,"],[93,288.08,2.16,"we have to resort to tactics like fracking,"],[93,290.24,5.28,"which uses high-pressure water, chemicals, and sand to break up impermeable rock."],[93,295.52,4.24,"This can have a variety of harmful effects, from air pollution to habitat degradation"],[93,299.76,3.2,"and potential health issues for people living nearby."],[93,302.96,4.0,"As for coal, mines can cause a whole host of problems, too,"],[93,306.96,6.48,"like sinkholes, erosion, water contamination, and damage to plant and animal habitats."],[93,313.44,4.8,"That’s not to mention the human cost of environmentally hazardous processes,"],[93,318.24,1.92,"and the impacts on Earth’s climate."],[93,320.16,5.17,"You can learn much more about the effects of fossil fuels in Crash Course Climate and Energy."],[93,325.36,5.12,"So, there are lots of reasons to break up with fossil fuels and find other sources of energy."],[93,330.48,4.24,"And Earth might hold one of the keys to powering our future more sustainably:"],[93,334.72,1.68,"geothermal energy."],[93,336.4,3.44,"Geothermal energy comes from the heat under Earth’s surface."],[93,339.84,2.8,"The inside of our planet is super hot."],[93,342.64,4.08,"It’s still holding onto heat from the collisions that likely formed it."],[93,346.72,3.44,"Then there’s decaying radioactive elements in the core and mantle,"],[93,350.16,3.6,"and all the hot magma and volcanic activity in there…"],[93,353.76,3.12,"that’s a lot of energy stored beneath our feet."],[93,356.88,2.08,"No, Dwayne. I’m not saying that “the floor is lava.”"],[93,358.96,0.8,"You’re good, dude."],[93,359.76,1.44,"But how do we harness it?"],[93,361.2,4.08,"We can most easily tap into geothermal heat near volcanic regions,"],[93,365.28,3.92,"where pockets of molten rock have risen up into the crust."],[93,369.2,3.76,"This magma heats up water that collects in cracks and pores in the crust,"],[93,372.96,1.76,"creating geothermal reservoirs."],[93,374.72,3.68,"The heated water can sometimes rocket through the surface as jets of steam"],[93,378.4,3.12,"or sizzling geysers like a geologic waterpark."],[93,381.52,3.44,"Or it can bubble up like hot tub jets into a hot spring."],[93,384.96,4.16,"A lot of volcanic activity happens near the boundaries of tectonic plates,"],[93,389.12,2.64,"or places where the crust is really thin."],[93,391.76,5.04,"That lets hot mantle rock and blobs of magma sneak up closer to the surface."],[93,396.8,3.68,"Like, Iceland is an island that sits right on the mid-ocean ridge,"],[93,400.48,4.16,"an underwater mountain range where two tectonic plates are pulling apart."],[93,404.64,3.04,"This makes it a great place to harness geothermal energy."],[93,407.68,2.08,"And this kind of energy is nothing new."],[93,409.76,4.24,"Humans have been using geothermal energy to power their lives for thousands of years."],[93,414.0,4.08,"Early hominins in East Africa might have used hot springs to cook their food."],[93,418.08,4.56,"And people in Iceland still do today, like with this traditional “hot spring bread”."],[93,422.64,4.0,"You’re telling me I can experience the wonders of the earth and eat delicious bread?"],[93,426.64,0.8,"Sign me up!"],[93,427.44,1.2,"But it didn’t stop there."],[93,428.64,5.04,"We started using geothermal energy to power electricity over a hundred years ago"],[93,433.68,3.28,"at a power plant in Tuscany that’s still operational today."],[93,436.96,5.36,"And as of 2023, about a quarter of Iceland’s electricity is powered by geothermal energy."],[93,442.32,3.44,"That leads us to one of the greatest points in favor of geothermal."],[93,445.76,3.6,"By running geothermal steam or water through pistons and turbines,"],[93,449.36,8.16,"we can generate electricity, without producing any emissions, or using up any water."],[93,457.52,3.52,"Because of the abundance of heat and volcanic activity under Earth’s surface,"],[93,461.04,2.4,"geothermal energy is renewable."],[93,463.44,4.0,"And unlike other renewable energies, like solar or wind power,"],[93,467.44,3.84,"geothermal energy is available all the time, rain or shine."],[93,471.28,2.72,"And did I mention the abundance factor?"],[93,474.0,3.04,"I know I did, but I feel like it’s worth emphasizing!"],[93,477.04,5.2,"Some researchers think that just 0.1 percent of the heat produced by the Earth’s core would be"],[93,482.24,6.64,"enough to meet all of humanity’s energy needs for the next 2 million years."],[93,488.88,1.76,"Two! Million! Years!"],[93,490.64,2.56,"So… what’s the hold-up?"],[93,493.2,5.36,"Why aren’t we already running the earth on, you know, the earth?"],[93,498.56,2.96,"The biggest challenge is tapping into all that energy—"],[93,501.52,2.72,"we can’t exactly dig down to Earth’s core."],[93,504.24,4.08,"While geothermal energy can be pretty accessible in volcanic regions,"],[93,508.32,4.0,"it’s a lot tougher in places where you have to dig deep below the surface."],[93,512.32,3.6,"To do it, we have to install heat pumps in deep boreholes in the ground."],[93,515.92,1.68,"The heat pumps send water and other"],[93,517.6,3.2,"substances way down into the crust to capture heat."],[93,520.8,1.92,"Then, they transport it up to the surface,"],[93,522.72,4.0,"where we can use it to cook, heat buildings, or power electricity—"],[93,526.72,3.68,"even in places as un-volcanic as Ohio or New York City."],[93,530.4,4.32,"In fact, heat pumps can give us access to geothermal energy nearly anywhere."],[93,534.72,2.0,"But they’re expensive up-front,"],[93,536.72,4.72,"and not every location can accommodate the big drills required to put them in place."],[93,541.44,4.0,"Add the fact that most folks don’t even know what geothermal energy is,"],[93,545.44,3.2,"and you can see why there isn’t a heat pump on every corner."],[93,548.64,2.8,"So, it’ll take a lot more work to make the infrastructure"],[93,551.44,2.4,"for geothermal energy accessible to everyone."],[93,553.84,5.6,"But in places like Italy, Indonesia, and the Western US, geothermal energy is growing fast."],[93,559.44,3.76,"It’s used for air-conditioning in Tuscany, mushroom farming in West Java,"],[93,563.2,3.04,"and melting snow on roads and sidewalks in Oregon."],[93,566.24,2.32,"There are still lots of opportunities to"],[93,568.56,3.2,"incorporate this form of energy into our daily lives."],[93,571.76,4.64,"Maybe someday, you’ll warm your hot chocolate with geothermal energy."],[93,576.4,0.85,"Cheers, Dwayne."],[93,577.28,3.52,"Sorting out our relationship with energy is a complex problem."],[93,580.8,5.04,"As humanity’s energy needs grow, we need to find alternatives to fossil fuels."],[93,585.84,4.48,"And while it’s unlikely that geothermal energy is the solution to all our problems,"],[93,590.32,6.16,"it could become a key part of a wider effort to live within the capacity of our planet."],[93,596.48,3.52,"Geologists today are figuring out how to combine geothermal energy"],[93,600.0,2.48,"with other renewable energy sources and strategies"],[93,602.48,4.72,"so we can say farewell to fossil fuels for good."],[93,607.2,0.88,"*crash*"],[93,608.08,3.44,"Next time, we’ll learn about the minerals that power our modern world."],[93,611.52,0.8,"See you then."],[93,612.32,2.16,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Geology"],[93,614.48,2.64,"which was filmed at our studio in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[93,617.12,3.04,"and was made with the help of all these beautiful, bubbly people."],[93,620.16,3.2,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[93,623.36,2.33,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[94,4.54,1.17,"Hi."],[94,6.1,2.17,"Hello, hi, TED people."],[94,10.14,1.3,"I'm Mike Albo."],[94,11.44,2.9,"I'm so excited to be here in Hotlanta."],[94,15.61,4.44,"Do they still call it that, or is that insulting?"],[94,20.85,1.53,"I'm sorry."],[94,22.39,2.97,"Anyway, I'm here to talk about our smartphones"],[94,25.39,2.4,"and how they control our ..."],[94,28.73,1.17,"Oh, dear."],[94,30.33,3.6,"Oh, gosh, I just got a really alarming text from my bank."],[94,35.77,1.27,"Do you mind if I just -- ?"],[94,37.03,2.14,"This will just take a second, I'm sorry."],[94,39.84,1.17,"(Machine) Hello."],[94,41.04,1.74,"Thank you for calling American Bank."],[94,42.81,2.04,"For English, please press 1."],[94,44.88,2.87,"(Spanish) For Spanish, please press 2."],[94,47.78,1.97,"To report a card lost or stolen,"],[94,49.78,2.6,"or to report suspicious activity on your account,"],[94,52.42,1.37,"press 1."],[94,53.82,2.1,"We are experiencing heavy traffic."],[94,55.95,2.6,"Please hold for the next available representative."],[94,58.59,1.4,"Your call is --"],[94,61.16,3.07,"Danny: Hello, thank you for calling American Bank. This is Danny."],[94,64.23,1.0,"How may I help you?"],[94,65.23,1.77,"Mike Albo: Hi, I just received a text"],[94,67.0,2.07,"that there's a foreign charge on my account."],[94,69.07,2.64,"D: Yes, I'll be happy to assist you with that, Mr. Albo."],[94,71.7,2.97,"May I have the last four digits of your social security number?"],[94,74.67,1.13,"MA: 1734."],[94,76.11,1.57,"D: Thank you, Mr. Albo."],[94,78.54,2.74,"Yes, it appears there has been some suspicious activity."],[94,81.28,1.24,"To validate your account,"],[94,82.55,3.27,"I will need to confirm recent transactions over the last 24 hours."],[94,85.85,1.47,"Is that OK with you, Mr. Albo?"],[94,87.35,1.17,"MA: Yes."],[94,90.39,2.17,"D: Did you purchase a smoothie in Philadelphia"],[94,92.56,2.54,"yesterday at 6am for $1.50?"],[94,95.13,1.27,"MA: Weird, no."],[94,98.03,1.53,"D: Did you purchase a bubble tea"],[94,99.6,3.7,"in Puerto Vallarta today at 8am for $0.00?"],[94,103.3,1.44,"MA: I did not."],[94,104.77,1.84,"D: Did you purchase a chopped salad"],[94,106.64,3.44,"in Toronto yesterday at 8:30am for $0.37?"],[94,110.11,1.2,"MA: I did not."],[94,111.81,3.07,"D: OK, yes, it seems your account has been hacked."],[94,114.88,3.5,"If you allow me to continue with your transactions over the last 24 hours,"],[94,118.42,1.57,"Mr. Albo, just to make sure."],[94,120.02,1.44,"MA: OK."],[94,121.48,1.17,"(Whispering) Sorry."],[94,122.69,2.27,"D: Did you purchase two organic chicken breasts,"],[94,124.95,3.7,"brown rice and a head of broccoli at Whole Foods for $28.11"],[94,128.69,2.1,"at 4:59pm yesterday?"],[94,130.83,1.77,"MA: Yes, I know, so expensive."],[94,133.1,3.07,"D: Did you purchase a bottle of wine for $11.50"],[94,136.2,4.2,"at Smith Street Wine and Liquor at 5:12pm yesterday?"],[94,140.44,1.24,"MA: Yes."],[94,142.47,4.77,"D: And then did you purchase drinks at Loco Coco Saloon for $14"],[94,147.28,2.13,"at 5:35pm?"],[94,149.45,1.17,"MA: No."],[94,150.61,1.2,"Wait."],[94,151.81,1.77,"Oh, yeah, I was with this guy, Jeff."],[94,153.58,2.37,"I was trying to meet someone in public before I --"],[94,155.99,1.14,"Anyway, yes."],[94,157.42,3.2,"D: Did you purchase a monthly membership renewal on Grindr?"],[94,161.29,1.2,"MA: Yeah."],[94,163.06,3.2,"D: Did you purchase an auto-renewal membership on Scruff?"],[94,166.7,1.97,"MA: Yes, I must have."],[94,169.23,2.97,"D: Then at 9:30pm, did you purchase another bottle of wine"],[94,172.24,3.64,"from Vino Nino Wine Company on Atlantic Avenue?"],[94,175.91,1.27,"MA: Yes."],[94,177.91,1.17,"D: And did you do this"],[94,179.11,2.5,"because you didn't want the guys at Smith Street Wine"],[94,181.61,2.34,"to see you buying more wine on the same day?"],[94,183.98,1.24,"MA: Yes, but --"],[94,185.25,2.87,"D: And did you go home and eat while standing up at the sink?"],[94,188.15,2.0,"MA: Yes, I did, but how did you know that?"],[94,190.19,2.27,"D: And then did you order \"Call Me By Your Name\""],[94,192.46,2.27,"on iTunes for $7.99"],[94,194.76,3.3,"and then finally watch it because you have avoided watching it"],[94,198.09,1.83,"because as a gay man pushing 50,"],[94,199.96,2.97,"you don't want to get upset seeing young, gorgeous men in love?"],[94,202.97,1.47,"MA: Yes, I did."],[94,206.0,2.3,"D: And then at the end, did you burst into tears,"],[94,208.31,2.34,"but then go look in the mirror to see how you look"],[94,210.67,2.9,"when you cry shirtless and then purchase a Peloton membership?"],[94,213.58,1.17,"MA: Yes."],[94,215.48,2.84,"D: And then did you take a photo of your torso in the mirror"],[94,218.35,3.77,"because you realized your abs look good when you are heaving with tears?"],[94,222.15,1.14,"MA: Yes."],[94,223.79,2.94,"D: Did you pick up your phone for the 800th time that day,"],[94,226.76,3.5,"empty of emotion but still feeling a deep, lonely ache?"],[94,230.29,1.2,"MA: Yes."],[94,232.53,3.8,"D: And scroll through Scruff having a number of pointless conversations"],[94,236.37,1.37,"with headless torsos --"],[94,237.77,1.13,"MA: Yes."],[94,238.94,3.47,"D: Drinking your previously purchased wine until you were so sloshed --"],[94,242.44,1.33,"MA: Yes."],[94,243.81,3.07,"D: And you cried by yourself, no one else there, just you,"],[94,246.91,4.04,"a low-income, single gay man that no one wants to hear from in society"],[94,250.98,3.24,"since you are over 40 and make less than $30,000 a year?"],[94,254.25,1.14,"MA: Yes."],[94,255.42,1.4,"D: And then did you pass out,"],[94,256.82,2.1,"staring at yet another hot man on your screen"],[94,258.96,1.44,"you will never meet in person,"],[94,260.42,1.87,"and then wake up and look at your phone"],[94,262.33,2.97,"and realize with horror that you are late to get to the airport"],[94,265.33,1.97,"to go to Atlanta for this TED Next event?"],[94,267.3,1.13,"MA: I did."],[94,269.4,3.04,"D: And barely got to the airport on time and landed in Atlanta,"],[94,272.44,3.07,"got to the Uber and checked Scruff and Grindr again"],[94,275.54,2.07,"to see if there are any hot guys in Atlanta,"],[94,277.64,2.7,"because this town is indeed full of very sexy men?"],[94,280.34,2.54,"MA: I did, I did, and they are, they are."],[94,284.51,2.64,"D: And then you got to the venue and ran out of the Uber"],[94,287.15,2.3,"and rushed backstage with your phone in your hand"],[94,289.49,1.7,"and brought it up on stage with you"],[94,291.19,2.8,"and then just now, walking on stage,"],[94,294.02,2.4,"looked down again to see if there were any hot guys"],[94,296.43,1.74,"to meet right here in the audience"],[94,298.19,1.67,"and saw that we,"],[94,299.9,1.4,"American Bank,"],[94,301.33,1.97,"had contacted you by text"],[94,303.33,3.07,"to ask about potential fraudulent purchases on your account."],[94,306.44,1.94,"MA: I did, I did."],[94,310.37,2.97,"D: Well, Mr. Albo, it seems your transactions are in order."],[94,313.38,1.17,"We will cancel the card,"],[94,314.58,3.07,"and you can expect a new one in the next six to 10 business days."],[94,317.68,2.04,"MA: Six to 10 business days?"],[94,320.32,2.17,"D: Is there anything else I can help you with?"],[94,322.49,1.3,"MA: No, thanks, Danny."],[94,324.19,1.43,"D: Thank you, Mr. Albo."],[94,325.62,2.27,"We very much appreciate your continued patronage"],[94,327.93,1.13,"of American Bank."],[94,329.09,2.0,"Also, you are a drunk, gay tramp."],[94,331.1,1.17,"Goodbye."],[94,332.93,1.4,"MA: Hey."],[94,334.37,1.63,"Well, I guess I am."],[94,336.33,1.53,"Oh, and my time's out."],[94,337.9,1.4,"Well, thank you."],[95,4.27,3.19,"Imagine for a moment that you're absolutely certain"],[95,7.47,2.29,"about the person you saw commit a crime."],[95,10.81,1.18,"You're so confident,"],[95,12.01,4.35,"you'd be willing to testify about it under oath in a court of law."],[95,16.87,1.83,"Your memory is strong,"],[95,18.73,2.79,"crystal clear, absolutely unshakable."],[95,22.74,4.64,"But now imagine that that same memory, though it feels 100 percent true,"],[95,27.41,1.7,"is actually false"],[95,29.13,2.75,"and could send an innocent person to prison,"],[95,31.9,1.58,"maybe even to death row."],[95,34.4,4.24,"This is the complex and sometimes heartbreaking world of eyewitness memory."],[95,39.24,3.69,"But for decades, we've been telling ourselves a story about eyewitness memory"],[95,42.94,2.33,"that itself may not be entirely true."],[95,46.18,2.29,"Most of you have probably heard cautionary tales"],[95,48.48,4.05,"about how wildly unreliable eyewitness testimony can be."],[95,53.03,2.07,"You may have heard about famous cases"],[95,55.14,2.49,"like the case of Ronald Cotton,"],[95,57.64,2.42,"where Jennifer Thompson, a rape victim,"],[95,60.08,2.54,"misidentified him as her attacker."],[95,62.65,4.54,"As she would later recall her testimony from his criminal trial,"],[95,67.2,2.27,"\"I was absolutely, positively,"],[95,69.48,2.64,"without a doubt certain that he was the man who raped me"],[95,72.14,1.6,"when I got on that witness stand."],[95,73.76,2.3,"And nobody was going to tell me any different.\""],[95,76.56,3.37,"The jury understandably found her testimony convincing."],[95,80.51,3.44,"Cotton was found guilty and sentenced to life in prison."],[95,85.5,2.92,"But Ronald Cotton did not rape Jennifer Thompson."],[95,88.44,3.77,"Instead, it was a known rapist prowling her neighborhood that night."],[95,92.89,2.72,"Cotton spent almost 11 years in prison"],[95,95.63,2.87,"before DNA testing finally proved his innocence"],[95,98.53,2.47,"and revealed the identity of the true rapist,"],[95,101.03,1.78,"a man named Bobby Poole."],[95,104.34,3.99,"Jennifer Thompson's testimony was sincere, but her memory was wrong."],[95,109.74,2.42,"DNA exoneration cases just like this one,"],[95,112.18,1.94,"involving confident misidentifications,"],[95,114.15,3.19,"have happened literally hundreds of times,"],[95,117.37,4.02,"leading many to seriously question the reliability of eyewitness memory."],[95,123.56,2.82,"But wrongful convictions like these are not the only reason"],[95,126.41,2.75,"why most people think eyewitness memory is unreliable."],[95,129.18,3.09,"For years, scientific research has also painted a damning picture"],[95,132.28,1.49,"of human memory."],[95,134.18,1.39,"Starting in the 1970s,"],[95,135.6,3.25,"scientists like Elizabeth Loftus"],[95,138.89,3.65,"began to show how shockingly easy it is to manipulate memory."],[95,142.96,1.52,"In groundbreaking studies,"],[95,144.51,3.09,"she and others implanted false memories in adults"],[95,147.61,2.95,"of having been lost in a shopping mall as a child,"],[95,150.6,2.75,"or as having been attacked by a vicious animal,"],[95,153.38,2.75,"even though these things never actually happened."],[95,157.12,2.8,"Findings like these seem to confirm our worst fears."],[95,159.94,2.57,"Memories are not like video recordings."],[95,162.96,3.8,"They're more like evidence from a crime scene,"],[95,166.8,2.59,"collected by people without gloves,"],[95,169.42,2.84,"distorting and contaminating it with every touch."],[95,173.56,2.48,"This message from science"],[95,176.07,2.54,"reinforced the message from the wrongful convictions,"],[95,178.64,3.19,"and the conventional wisdom was set in stone for decades."],[95,182.68,2.45,"The legal system should not trust eyewitness memory."],[95,185.13,1.89,"It's just too unreliable."],[95,188.35,3.02,"But here's where the story takes an unexpected turn."],[95,191.39,5.51,"What if the problem is not so much about how unreliable eyewitness memory is,"],[95,196.93,4.94,"because of how easily contamination can create false memories,"],[95,201.9,3.62,"and more about how and when we test the witness's memory."],[95,206.74,2.97,"Think about forensic evidence like DNA or fingerprints."],[95,210.12,3.5,"Everybody knows that forensic evidence can be contaminated"],[95,213.66,3.49,"and end up implicating an innocent person,"],[95,217.17,2.07,"much like contaminated memory can."],[95,219.99,3.77,"But we don't just dismiss forensic evidence for that reason."],[95,223.77,4.89,"Instead, we collect it as early as possible in the police investigation"],[95,228.69,1.83,"before it's contaminated."],[95,231.21,1.27,"Why do we do that?"],[95,233.92,4.84,"It's because reliable information comes from analyzing uncontaminated evidence,"],[95,238.77,1.6,"not contaminated evidence."],[95,240.74,4.04,"And the exact same principle applies to memory evidence."],[95,245.13,3.44,"Collect it early, before it's contaminated."],[95,250.57,3.2,"An eyewitness’s memory of whether or not the police suspect"],[95,253.8,2.95,"is the person who they saw commit the crime"],[95,256.77,3.02,"can be highly reliable,"],[95,259.82,2.4,"but only if the witness's memory is uncontaminated,"],[95,262.24,2.05,"not after it's been contaminated."],[95,265.08,1.57,"And scientists now agree"],[95,266.68,4.92,"that even the first test contaminates the witness's memory for a given suspect."],[95,272.52,2.99,"If the suspect’s innocent, for example,"],[95,275.52,3.92,"it's the first time the witness is seeing his face,"],[95,279.46,1.52,"and this is happening at a time"],[95,281.0,2.85,"when the witness is actively thinking about the crime."],[95,283.87,1.8,"So even if the witness says,"],[95,285.68,2.72,"\"No, that's not the guy who did it,\""],[95,288.44,5.32,"this is how the innocent suspect’s face first becomes associated with the crime"],[95,293.79,1.82,"in the witness's memory."],[95,295.63,1.45,"That's contamination."],[95,297.66,2.69,"You can’t keep that from happening,"],[95,300.38,3.44,"and you can’t put the witness’s memory back the way it was."],[95,304.32,3.54,"So focus on the first uncontaminated memory test"],[95,307.87,2.5,"early in the police investigation,"],[95,310.39,2.77,"not the last, thoroughly contaminated test"],[95,313.19,4.19,"that happens at the criminal trial one, two, or even three years later."],[95,319.03,3.1,"Unfortunately, courts tend to do the reverse,"],[95,322.15,3.9,"placing their faith in the last test of memory at trial"],[95,326.09,3.75,"while all but ignoring the critical first test."],[95,330.83,2.72,"This is a seriously underappreciated problem."],[95,337.19,1.18,"Well, with that in mind,"],[95,338.4,2.52,"let's take a closer look at how the police conduct"],[95,340.96,5.57,"that all-important first test of a witness’s uncontaminated memory."],[95,348.8,1.99,"In the days or weeks after a crime,"],[95,350.82,1.53,"the police might find a suspect,"],[95,352.38,2.97,"a person who they think may have committed the crime,"],[95,355.39,2.02,"and they'd like to show him to the witness"],[95,357.44,1.67,"to see if they have the right guy."],[95,359.12,4.72,"They could just hand the suspect's photo to the witness and ask,"],[95,363.86,1.4,"is this the guy who did it?"],[95,366.03,1.99,"The problem is, that would be suggestive,"],[95,368.05,2.77,"because it would reveal to the witness who the police think"],[95,370.84,1.69,"may have committed the crime."],[95,372.55,2.6,"To test memory in a less suggestive way,"],[95,375.17,3.55,"the police will often show the witness a whole set of six photos."],[95,380.31,2.12,"It's called a six-pack photo lineup."],[95,382.96,2.54,"One photo is of the suspect,"],[95,385.53,2.54,"and the others are of similar-looking individuals"],[95,388.09,2.12,"who the police know are innocent."],[95,390.22,4.34,"That way, they can still show the suspect's photo to the witness,"],[95,394.58,2.72,"but without revealing who they think committed the crime."],[95,397.31,2.4,"It's a much fairer way to test memory,"],[95,399.75,4.04,"and it becomes fairer still when other recommended practices are followed."],[95,404.75,1.6,"Such as letting the witness know"],[95,406.37,2.82,"that the perpetrator who they saw commit the crime"],[95,409.22,2.37,"may or may not be among these photos."],[95,411.63,2.99,"And the officer who's administering these photos to the witness"],[95,414.63,2.5,"should not even know who the suspect is"],[95,417.15,4.25,"to avoid unintentionally influencing the witness’s choice."],[95,422.5,4.1,"When it's done this way, it becomes a pure test of the witness's memory,"],[95,426.64,2.55,"and this is where things start to get interesting."],[95,429.64,2.12,"About 10 years ago, work from my lab,"],[95,431.78,5.42,"published in strong, high-impact scientific journals,"],[95,437.24,4.59,"first reported that a confident identification of a suspect"],[95,441.84,4.15,"from an initial photo line-up is highly reliable."],[95,446.56,1.35,"Big surprise."],[95,448.26,1.4,"Not unreliable."],[95,451.13,2.72,"For a scientific field that has spent decades"],[95,453.87,1.37,"cautioning the legal system"],[95,455.27,3.32,"about the unreliability of eyewitness memory,"],[95,458.61,4.04,"these findings were not easy to enthusiastically embrace."],[95,464.1,3.0,"But almost all of the recent science finds that an initial,"],[95,467.12,3.49,"confident identification is much more reliable"],[95,470.64,1.69,"than the field previously thought."],[95,472.34,4.27,"Not infallible, of course, but certainly not unreliable."],[95,478.71,2.89,"These new scientific findings raise an interesting question"],[95,481.61,3.5,"about the DNA exoneration cases that I told you about earlier."],[95,485.15,4.19,"The ones where we know that on the last test of their memory,"],[95,489.37,1.47,"at the criminal trial,"],[95,490.87,3.84,"witnesses confidently misidentified an innocent person"],[95,494.73,2.0,"contributing to a wrongful conviction."],[95,497.11,3.14,"The question is: What did those witnesses do"],[95,500.28,4.55,"the very first time their memory was tested for that same person?"],[95,505.97,1.37,"And when you look into that,"],[95,507.37,3.29,"you find they usually did not confidently misidentify"],[95,510.68,2.3,"the innocent suspect at that time."],[95,513.0,2.44,"The problem is, nobody listened to them."],[95,515.46,1.65,"Remember Jennifer Thompson?"],[95,518.82,4.12,"She struggled with the initial photo lineup a few days after the crime."],[95,523.8,2.84,"Narrowing it down to two pictures,"],[95,526.67,2.82,"wavering hesitantly between them for literally minutes"],[95,529.53,3.4,"before finally landing on Ronald Cotton's face and saying,"],[95,532.95,1.9,"\"I think it's him.\""],[95,535.23,2.72,"It was obviously inconclusive identification,"],[95,537.97,2.4,"full of doubt and indecision."],[95,541.49,3.87,"By the time of his criminal trial, after much memory contamination,"],[95,545.38,3.04,"that her doubts were gone and she became absolutely, positively,"],[95,548.43,1.27,"without a doubt certain"],[95,549.73,2.5,"that Ronald Cotton was the man who raped her."],[95,552.25,2.84,"If they had known then what we know now,"],[95,555.12,1.94,"focus on the first test,"],[95,557.07,4.32,"where, in this case, you find a completely inconclusive identification,"],[95,561.43,3.62,"it seems likely that Ronald Cotton never would have been wrongfully convicted"],[95,565.08,1.17,"in the first place."],[95,566.28,1.28,"And that's the point."],[95,569.84,3.2,"Alright, here's where the story takes another interesting turn."],[95,573.07,2.75,"On this first test using a photo lineup,"],[95,575.84,3.62,"witnesses often don't even tentatively identify the suspect"],[95,579.48,3.69,"the way that Jennifer Thompson tentatively identified Ronald Cotton."],[95,583.66,4.1,"Instead, at a time when the witness's memory of the perpetrator is as fresh"],[95,587.8,3.57,"and strong and uncontaminated as it will ever be,"],[95,591.41,1.53,"the witness looks at the photos,"],[95,592.94,3.45,"including the photo of the suspect, and says,"],[95,596.41,4.0,"\"None of these guys match my memory of the person who committed the crime.\""],[95,600.45,3.15,"In other words, the witness rejects the lineup,"],[95,603.63,4.72,"providing clear evidence that the suspect in the lineup is innocent."],[95,609.42,2.87,"You see, this is absolutely the key point."],[95,613.76,4.69,"The first test of an eyewitness's uncontaminated memory"],[95,618.48,4.34,"can provide reliable evidence pointing in either direction:"],[95,622.84,2.15,"towards guilt or innocence,"],[95,625.02,1.99,"depending on how the test turns out."],[95,627.02,2.67,"And when the witness rejects the lineup,"],[95,629.71,4.3,"it provides reliable evidence pointing in the direction"],[95,634.03,2.0,"of the suspect's innocence."],[95,636.53,3.54,"Yet many witnesses who reject an initial photo lineup,"],[95,640.09,3.04,"after their memory becomes contaminated,"],[95,643.14,3.27,"will show up at a criminal trial a year or two later,"],[95,646.43,2.85,"unaware that their memory is contaminated,"],[95,649.31,3.04,"and now confidently identify the very same person"],[95,652.37,1.85,"they initially rejected."],[95,654.25,2.4,"Half the time, they don't even remember doing that,"],[95,656.69,1.69,"it was so long ago."],[95,658.39,3.89,"These defendants are often convicted, sentenced to long prison terms,"],[95,662.29,1.4,"and are now behind bars."],[95,664.55,3.89,"And unlike Ronald Cotton,"],[95,668.45,3.17,"they do not have any DNA evidence to prove their innocence."],[95,672.65,3.57,"But what they do have is a new message"],[95,676.26,2.94,"from the world of memory science that can help to do that."],[95,679.23,2.57,"And ironically,"],[95,681.81,2.5,"in a complete mind flip,"],[95,684.35,2.29,"it's reliable evidence of innocence"],[95,686.65,3.97,"from the memory of an eyewitness the first time they were tested."],[95,691.61,2.14,"Consider the case of Miguel Solorio."],[95,694.79,2.72,"He was arrested in 1998 for murder."],[95,697.53,3.84,"But on the first test, four witnesses rejected his photo lineup."],[95,703.0,1.72,"Nobody paid any attention to that."],[95,705.7,1.17,"More than a year later,"],[95,706.9,2.89,"two of those same witnesses showed up at his criminal trial"],[95,709.81,3.74,"and identified him as the shooter in front of a judge and jury."],[95,713.58,2.5,"He was convicted and sentenced to life in prison"],[95,716.11,2.19,"without the possibility of parole."],[95,719.7,3.49,"Miguel spent 25 years of his life,"],[95,723.21,2.79,"from age 19 to age 44,"],[95,726.01,1.57,"behind bars,"],[95,727.59,5.22,"before finally being exonerated in late 2023,"],[95,732.83,3.17,"with the help of the new science that I'm telling you about today:"],[95,736.03,2.77,"focus on the first uncontaminated test."],[95,740.97,2.1,"As tragic as Miguel's story is,"],[95,743.09,3.05,"at least the new science helped to overturn his conviction"],[95,746.18,2.32,"before he spent his entire life in prison."],[95,750.25,3.69,"But others with a similar story remain behind bars as we speak."],[95,754.72,2.04,"And some of them,"],[95,756.77,2.19,"their lives are hanging in the balance."],[95,759.99,2.49,"Consider the case of Charles Don Flores."],[95,762.51,2.94,"He, too, was arrested for murder in 1998."],[95,765.76,2.07,"There was one witness in this case."],[95,768.6,1.17,"On the day of the crime,"],[95,769.78,3.69,"she told the police that she saw a white male with shoulder-length hair"],[95,773.5,1.4,"go into her neighbor's house"],[95,774.92,2.08,"shortly before the murder occurred."],[95,777.51,2.69,"A couple days later, she goes back to the police station."],[95,780.21,1.22,"They hypnotize her."],[95,781.45,2.87,"It's always dicey, but she stuck to her story."],[95,784.33,2.75,"She said it's a white male with shoulder-length hair."],[95,787.1,3.19,"The police asked her to make a composite sketch, so she did."],[95,791.16,2.57,"Came out to be a white male with shoulder-length hair,"],[95,793.74,1.35,"just like she said."],[95,795.11,3.6,"Now keep in mind, this is very early in the police investigation."],[95,799.05,4.05,"This is a time when her memory of the perpetrator is as fresh and strong"],[95,803.13,2.64,"and uncontaminated as it will ever be."],[95,807.09,2.62,"And this is what the perpetrator looks like."],[95,810.54,2.15,"But the police suspected Charles Don Flores,"],[95,812.73,2.45,"a Hispanic man with short hair."],[95,815.2,1.82,"And for some inexplicable reason,"],[95,817.05,3.7,"put his photo in a photo lineup and showed it to the witness."],[95,823.46,2.75,"This is still early in the police investigation."],[95,826.22,4.1,"This is the all-important first test of a witness's uncontaminated memory"],[95,830.36,2.17,"for the suspect, Charles Don Flores."],[95,833.5,4.25,"The witness looked at this photo lineup,"],[95,837.77,2.52,"didn't see any white males with shoulder-length hair,"],[95,840.32,2.74,"and quite understandably rejected it,"],[95,843.09,2.84,"providing clear evidence that Flores is innocent."],[95,847.31,2.84,"Fast forward to his criminal trial a year later,"],[95,850.18,2.82,"and you can probably guess how this story turns out."],[95,853.7,4.14,"Now the witness is more than 100 percent certain that it was Charles Don Flores"],[95,857.86,3.59,"she saw go into her neighbor's house on the day of that murder."],[95,862.08,3.3,"The jury was convinced, he was found guilty"],[95,865.41,1.67,"and sentenced to death."],[95,867.8,2.8,"He's been on death row for more than 25 years,"],[95,870.62,2.13,"where he remains to this day."],[95,873.87,2.3,"And in all those years,"],[95,876.19,3.55,"no jury even heard what the witness did"],[95,879.78,2.67,"on the first test of her uncontaminated memory,"],[95,882.48,2.8,"much less about the new scientific consensus,"],[95,885.32,3.39,"emphasizing the importance of focusing on that first test,"],[95,888.72,2.94,"because that's where the reliable information is."],[95,894.83,2.94,"So, you see, this isn't just an academic exercise."],[95,897.78,2.84,"It's about exonerating the wrongfully convicted,"],[95,900.63,1.69,"some of whom are on death row,"],[95,902.33,3.79,"and about preventing wrongful convictions from happening in the first place."],[95,906.61,4.59,"It's about understanding that memory is not an enemy to be distrusted,"],[95,911.23,2.37,"but a complex tool that, when properly used,"],[95,913.63,2.05,"can serve the cause of justice."],[95,916.87,3.59,"The reforms that need to be made going forward seem pretty clear."],[95,920.47,5.27,"Number one, in both police investigations and legal proceedings,"],[95,925.76,4.07,"prioritize the first test of the witness's uncontaminated memory,"],[95,929.86,3.02,"because that's where the reliable information is."],[95,932.9,2.04,"Number two, at the same time,"],[95,934.95,4.49,"de-emphasize confident suspect identifications"],[95,939.45,2.69,"made by a witness who earlier rejected that same face"],[95,942.16,3.52,"because that likely reflects memory contamination, not the truth."],[95,946.44,1.2,"And number three,"],[95,947.68,4.1,"to get these reforms underway, educate legal professionals,"],[95,951.82,1.69,"police chiefs,"],[95,953.52,2.9,"defense attorneys, prosecutors, judges"],[95,956.44,2.4,"about the new science of eyewitness memory."],[95,960.26,1.45,"Over the last couple of years,"],[95,961.73,2.17,"my colleagues and I have been doing just that,"],[95,963.91,2.6,"reaching out to defense attorneys and prosecutors alike"],[95,966.53,2.49,"to bring this new understanding to the legal system."],[95,969.03,2.2,"We find that initially, at least,"],[95,971.25,3.55,"defense attorneys are alarmed by this new message"],[95,974.82,3.14,"because they're uncomfortable with the idea that eyewitness memory"],[95,977.99,1.63,"can ever be reliable."],[95,980.33,3.1,"To them, listening to what an eyewitness has to say,"],[95,983.47,3.17,"even on the very first test of their uncontaminated memory,"],[95,986.67,2.7,"is just a recipe for another wrongful conviction."],[95,989.84,1.62,"And prosecutors,"],[95,991.47,3.04,"they're equally alarmed, but for the opposite reason."],[95,994.53,2.2,"To them, not listening to a witness"],[95,996.75,2.45,"who makes a confident suspect identification"],[95,999.23,4.05,"after previously rejecting that same face"],[95,1003.3,2.94,"sounds like a recipe for letting the guilty walk free."],[95,1007.62,4.89,"So we've managed to bring both sides of the aisle together in a way,"],[95,1012.53,4.1,"to both the defense and the prosecution our new message sounds dangerous."],[95,1017.75,1.2,"But it's not dangerous."],[95,1018.98,1.87,"It's just how memory works."],[95,1021.84,1.77,"To better serve the cause of justice,"],[95,1023.64,5.51,"both sides and all of us have to follow the science by listening to memory"],[95,1029.18,2.44,"when it most reliably speaks the truth."],[95,1033.16,5.22,"It will become a more just world if we can find a way to do that."],[95,1038.79,1.18,"Thank you."],[96,4.67,3.38,"People worry about AI for all kinds of reasons."],[96,8.05,2.79,"It's polarizing, it spreads misinformation,"],[96,10.84,1.88,"it's coming for our jobs."],[96,12.72,2.54,"And those are all good reasons to be nervous."],[96,15.3,2.84,"But what really keeps me up at night is something else."],[96,18.18,2.29,"Something that feels more personal"],[96,20.48,4.0,"and perhaps even more concerning for the human experience itself."],[96,24.52,2.71,"I worry that AI will make us boring."],[96,27.86,2.34,"You, me, all of us."],[96,30.65,3.13,"That's because the more we outsource our decisions to AI,"],[96,33.82,3.0,"the more we surrender something deeply human."],[96,36.87,2.21,"Our capacity to explore,"],[96,39.12,2.63,"to take risks, to stumble into the unknown,"],[96,41.75,2.38,"and sometimes surprise even ourselves."],[96,44.71,4.09,"I worry that without the potential for discovery, for serendipity,"],[96,48.84,4.34,"we risk becoming more shallow and unidimensional versions of who we are"],[96,53.22,1.63,"and who we could be,"],[96,54.84,3.96,"not just as humans, as individuals, but as humanity."],[96,59.68,2.84,"Now let me make this idea a bit more concrete"],[96,62.52,4.13,"and take you somewhere perhaps unexpected, but also totally relatable."],[96,67.48,2.5,"Imagine walking into a Baskin-Robbins."],[96,70.03,2.08,"What you'll see is an illustrious assortment"],[96,72.11,1.96,"of 31 different ice cream flavors."],[96,74.07,3.96,"There's chocolate chip, pistachio, almond, lemon sorbet, and many more."],[96,79.03,2.25,"You're now facing a difficult choice."],[96,81.33,2.92,"Are you going to go with an old favorite, chocolate maybe,"],[96,84.25,2.79,"or are you going to take a risk on something funky and new,"],[96,87.08,2.5,"like wild and reckless sherbet?"],[96,90.55,1.54,"And yes, it's a real flavor."],[96,93.8,2.59,"The choice you're facing is a classic human dilemma"],[96,96.43,4.13,"scientists call the exploitation- exploration trade-off."],[96,100.85,3.63,"Are you going to play it safe and capitalize on what you know you like,"],[96,104.52,1.5,"or are you going to take a risk"],[96,106.02,2.29,"with the hope of finding something even better?"],[96,108.98,2.09,"And our lives are full of these trade offs."],[96,111.11,3.59,"Stick with your favorite restaurant or try the fancy new spot on the corner."],[96,114.74,3.21,"Keep your usual haircut or ask for something new and edgy."],[96,117.95,1.5,"Stay in your stable job"],[96,119.45,3.17,"or finally try to become a pickleball champion?"],[96,125.41,4.63,"We all differ in our appetite for exploitation versus exploration."],[96,130.09,2.29,"But we're also all hardwired by evolution"],[96,132.42,2.59,"to strike a certain balance between the two,"],[96,135.01,2.42,"because for our ancestors on the African savanna,"],[96,137.47,2.04,"the trade-off was actually pretty simple."],[96,139.55,3.04,"Stick too closely to what's safe and you risk starvation"],[96,142.64,1.46,"when the grove runs dry."],[96,144.1,1.63,"Stray too far into the unknown"],[96,145.77,2.54,"and you might get poisoned or eaten by lions."],[96,148.94,1.17,"Luckily for you and me,"],[96,150.15,3.46,"the trade offs we face today are far less about survival."],[96,153.94,2.59,"But our choices are still governed by this instinct"],[96,156.57,2.59,"to balance caution with curiosity."],[96,159.57,4.46,"If all you ever did in life was play it safe and exploit,"],[96,164.08,1.79,"you'd never get disappointed,"],[96,165.91,3.29,"but you'd also never get a chance to advance and grow."],[96,169.25,3.5,"If all you did instead was discover and explore,"],[96,172.75,2.25,"you'd collect endless experiences,"],[96,175.05,3.38,"but you'd also never get a chance to capitalize on your learnings."],[96,178.43,3.92,"So it's really this balance between exploitation and exploration"],[96,182.34,1.71,"that fuels our growth,"],[96,184.1,3.17,"not just as a species, but also as individuals."],[96,188.14,3.79,"But that's exactly where AI throws a wrench into the works,"],[96,191.98,2.63,"because AI hates risk."],[96,195.73,2.58,"And that's not because of its algorithmic DNA."],[96,198.36,3.21,"It's because the systems we rely on to navigate our world,"],[96,201.61,2.63,"from Spotify to Netflix to ChatGPT,"],[96,204.28,3.63,"are overwhelmingly trained to optimize for exploitation."],[96,208.37,3.71,"Or more specifically, for short-term engagement and satisfaction."],[96,212.12,1.17,"Did you click the link,"],[96,213.33,1.84,"watch the video, like the song?"],[96,215.17,2.75,"If yes, AI gets a clap on the shoulder."],[96,217.92,1.96,"If no, it gets a slap on the hand."],[96,220.34,4.0,"Risk, discovery and exploration are simply not part of their programming."],[96,225.47,1.5,"Think back to Baskin-Robbins."],[96,227.01,3.88,"If 60 percent of people preferred the flavor pralines and cream,"],[96,230.94,2.92,"then that's what the AI is going to recommend."],[96,233.85,3.09,"It's not that AI lacks imagination."],[96,236.94,1.58,"It lacks incentive."],[96,238.99,2.67,"Safe bets protect against disappointment,"],[96,241.7,2.42,"which in turn reduces customer churn."],[96,244.49,3.38,"So instead of taking this risky gamble that helps you explore,"],[96,247.87,3.04,"companies tend to err on the side of exploitation"],[96,250.96,2.09,"when training their AI systems."],[96,254.5,2.84,"And to be clear, there's nothing inherently wrong with that."],[96,257.34,3.88,"I've spent the last 15 years as a computational social scientist"],[96,261.26,3.88,"working at the intersection of psychology, computer science, and business."],[96,265.18,4.38,"And I know that rewarding AI in that way can be extremely valuable."],[96,270.14,1.17,"Algorithms, in a way,"],[96,271.35,2.84,"only become so good at figuring out what you might enjoy"],[96,274.23,1.38,"or currently be looking for"],[96,275.65,2.71,"because they focus on exploitation."],[96,278.94,1.83,"And that's not just good for business."],[96,280.82,4.25,"It's also good and sometimes even necessary for us as consumers."],[96,285.62,2.08,"For me, the mere thought of having to choose"],[96,287.7,3.04,"between 31 different ice cream flavors makes me dizzy."],[96,291.29,2.5,"And yet, most of the choices that we face day-to-day"],[96,293.83,2.04,"are far more complex than that."],[96,296.29,3.71,"Netflix, for example, has over 5,500 movies to choose from."],[96,300.42,2.59,"Spotify, over 100 million songs."],[96,303.93,2.21,"So the truth is that without a little help"],[96,306.18,2.96,"from our exploitation-loving AI friends,"],[96,309.14,3.46,"we simply don't stand a chance at navigating modern life."],[96,313.39,2.29,"But I've also become increasingly concerned"],[96,315.73,3.67,"that this upside comes at a somewhat existential cost."],[96,319.98,2.54,"And to give you a first flavor of what I mean by that,"],[96,322.57,1.96,"let's go back to Baskin-Robbins."],[96,324.95,2.79,"Before coming here, I ran a simple experiment."],[96,327.74,3.84,"I asked ChatGPT to recommend one of their ice cream flavors."],[96,331.62,1.5,"I did that 100 times,"],[96,333.16,2.54,"each round pretending to be a new customer."],[96,336.46,4.55,"Ninety-six times it recommended one of their two most popular flavors --"],[96,341.05,2.67,"Pralines and Cream and Mint Chocolate Chip --"],[96,344.55,2.38,"which I realize, and I’m sure you’d agree,"],[96,346.97,2.92,"is not exactly a very diverse set of choices."],[96,351.06,2.67,"And it could mean that we soon find ourselves in a world"],[96,353.73,2.92,"where Baskin-Robbins offers only those two flavors."],[96,356.69,1.88,"If no one ever picks the other 29,"],[96,358.61,2.5,"why bother offering them in the first place?"],[96,362.02,5.05,"Now this might sound trivial and not really existential at all,"],[96,367.07,1.58,"but that's only until you realize"],[96,368.7,2.21,"that this flattening of the human experience"],[96,370.95,2.38,"happens across every aspect of life."],[96,374.04,2.25,"In studies with my collaborators and students,"],[96,376.29,3.34,"we've shown that when people use AI for guidance,"],[96,379.67,3.42,"their preferences become more normative and less diverse,"],[96,383.13,2.59,"their creative output becomes less unique,"],[96,385.71,2.63,"and their choice of the most important scientists,"],[96,388.38,4.5,"athletes and historical figures becomes the same as everyone else's."],[96,393.47,1.21,"So in a nutshell,"],[96,394.72,4.05,"AI turns this infinite diversity of human opinions, beliefs"],[96,398.81,1.29,"and preferences"],[96,400.15,2.88,"into statistically safe sameness."],[96,404.48,1.88,"And that's only part of the problem,"],[96,406.4,2.54,"because even when AI learns your quirks --"],[96,408.95,2.92,"say, it figures out that you like nutty coconut"],[96,411.87,1.88,"better than pralines and cream --"],[96,413.74,3.21,"it will still play it safe within your preferences."],[96,417.0,3.71,"Meaning it is going to optimize for what you are most likely to like."],[96,421.25,2.5,"I went back to ChatGPT for a second experiment"],[96,423.75,3.29,"and this time told it about my personal ice cream preferences."],[96,427.09,2.42,"I said that during my last 100 visits,"],[96,429.55,3.38,"I picked nutty coconut 70 percent of the time."],[96,432.93,1.21,"The remaining 30 percent,"],[96,434.18,2.88,"I split evenly among three of my other favorites."],[96,437.52,3.46,"I then asked it to make the next 100 choices on my behalf."],[96,441.77,1.67,"And guess what it did?"],[96,443.86,3.75,"It picked nutty coconut every single time."],[96,448.74,5.8,"Gone are my adventures into occasional chocolate fudge, cookie dough or mango."],[96,454.57,4.09,"All I am at that point is literally a nutty coconut."],[96,461.62,1.5,"But forget about ice cream."],[96,463.13,2.71,"My students and I have repeatedly shown the same patterns"],[96,465.84,2.67,"when studying people's actual preferences."],[96,468.55,3.92,"AI narrows your taste, it flattens your personality,"],[96,472.47,1.46,"and it scrubs away the edges"],[96,473.93,2.96,"that make you interesting and keep you dynamic."],[96,477.51,1.88,"And the insidious part of all of this"],[96,479.39,3.59,"is that the impact of AI on human complexity is subtle."],[96,483.02,2.42,"You won't notice it happening overnight."],[96,485.48,3.55,"Rather, it's a death by a thousand algorithmic recommendations."],[96,489.03,3.29,"It's one slightly safer movie, one slightly more popular book,"],[96,492.36,2.67,"one slightly more mainstream vacation at a time."],[96,495.45,4.67,"With every decision you outsource to AI, you become more narrow."],[96,500.12,2.59,"Then AI learns from that shallower version of you"],[96,502.75,2.63,"and narrows its recommendations even more."],[96,506.13,4.17,"The New York Times reporter Kashmir Hill captured this dilemma perfectly"],[96,510.34,1.54,"in one of her articles."],[96,512.34,2.67,"After outsourcing her decisions to AI for a week,"],[96,515.05,4.75,"she complained about its hidden agenda to turn her into a \"basic bitch.\""],[96,522.81,2.21,"And the funny part is, it's the word basic here"],[96,525.06,1.42,"that's the biggest insult."],[96,529.02,2.59,"Because being basic means being unoriginal,"],[96,531.65,2.17,"unexceptional and uninteresting."],[96,534.2,3.34,"It's offensive, not because it suggests failure,"],[96,537.53,4.84,"but because it implies mediocrity, an absence of complexity."],[96,543.79,2.33,"And who really wants it for themselves?"],[96,546.63,1.17,"Nobody."],[96,547.79,2.75,"We all like the feeling of being unique, of being special,"],[96,550.59,3.13,"of being someone who doesn't easily fit into a box."],[96,554.22,4.04,"I, for one, don't want to become exactly like everybody else."],[96,558.3,3.54,"And I also don't want to become a singular version of myself."],[96,562.52,4.5,"So what can we do to course-correct and avoid destination basic bitch?"],[96,568.61,3.13,"We can’t hit the reset button on AI, and we shouldn’t."],[96,571.78,2.84,"As I said before, these tools are incredibly valuable"],[96,574.65,1.42,"for navigating modern life,"],[96,576.11,2.63,"and they make us better off in many ways."],[96,579.12,4.29,"But we need to reclaim our ability to take risks and discover."],[96,583.96,4.59,"We need to find a way to rebalance exploitation and exploration."],[96,589.34,4.05,"And ironically, AI could actually help us accomplish just that,"],[96,593.38,4.75,"but only if we ask the right questions and reward it for the right actions."],[96,598.76,3.13,"So instead of asking it to help us find something we like,"],[96,601.89,2.88,"we could start asking it to help us find something new,"],[96,604.81,1.63,"something we're likely to love,"],[96,606.43,2.79,"even though we've never tried it before."],[96,609.27,1.79,"And by that, I don't necessarily mean"],[96,611.07,3.09,"you typing that question into ChatGPT yourself,"],[96,614.19,1.92,"although you should certainly try."],[96,616.15,4.29,"What I mean is getting companies to harness AI’s superpower,"],[96,620.49,3.88,"its ability to detect patterns in vast amounts of human data"],[96,624.41,2.21,"for exploration purposes."],[96,627.33,3.21,"Because AI has seen the entire universe of preferences --"],[96,630.59,2.38,"it knows not only what you currently like,"],[96,633.0,2.29,"but also what lies just beyond the boundaries"],[96,635.34,1.84,"of your typical preferences --"],[96,637.51,3.13,"which means that it can help you explore smartly"],[96,640.68,2.63,"to take curated risks when you want to."],[96,643.76,1.84,"Now here's what this could look like."],[96,645.6,2.5,"Imagine a dial on your Netflix account"],[96,648.14,1.38,"or your Google search bar"],[96,649.52,3.54,"that lets you decide how far from your typical preferences"],[96,653.07,2.84,"you want to stray at any given point in time."],[96,656.65,4.0,"On a regular day, you probably keep the dial close to the \"spot on\" setting"],[96,660.66,3.25,"because you want to get the most relevant hits right away."],[96,664.24,2.71,"But then on other days, you might feel adventurous enough"],[96,667.0,3.42,"to push it a little closer to the \"me with a twist\" setting"],[96,670.42,3.71,"and ask for content that's a little outside of your comfort zone,"],[96,674.17,1.58,"but still relevant."],[96,676.34,1.17,"And then on other days,"],[96,677.51,2.92,"for those rare moments when you feel like you're really ready"],[96,680.47,1.21,"to take on the world,"],[96,681.68,2.88,"you might push the dial all the way to the \"wildcard\" setting"],[96,684.6,3.25,"and ask it to help you discover something entirely new."],[96,689.14,2.5,"I, for one, would love to have such a dial."],[96,691.69,2.46,"It doesn't force me to leave my comfort zone."],[96,694.15,2.29,"I can always leave it on the \"spot on\" setting"],[96,696.48,3.0,"and benefit from the convenience that comes with it."],[96,699.49,1.58,"But it gives me the choice"],[96,701.11,3.92,"and the agency to break out of my little bubble whenever I want to,"],[96,705.08,1.63,"or I feel like I have to."],[96,707.7,1.29,"But here's the catch."],[96,709.0,4.71,"For AI to toggle its superpower between exploitation and exploration,"],[96,713.75,2.17,"we need to incentivize it to do so."],[96,716.42,2.5,"Merely asking it to be more creative"],[96,718.92,1.71,"or help you become more adventurous"],[96,720.63,1.58,"isn't going to do the trick."],[96,722.26,3.84,"In most cases, it will still default to the tried-and-tested output"],[96,726.14,2.46,"because it craves that clap on the shoulder."],[96,729.1,3.96,"So instead of punishing AI every time it takes a swing and misses,"],[96,733.1,3.5,"we need to start rewarding it for taking smart swings,"],[96,736.65,2.25,"for making bets that are a little bold,"],[96,738.94,1.54,"a little unusual, perhaps,"],[96,740.49,2.71,"but still grounded in what it knows about us."],[96,743.78,2.75,"AI doesn't have to throw darts in the dark."],[96,746.58,1.71,"It can take informed risks."],[96,748.62,3.13,"It can help us optimize our exploration."],[96,752.25,1.17,"And when it does,"],[96,753.46,2.92,"we should treat it as a success, not a failure."],[96,757.54,2.67,"Now this isn’t about desserts or playlists."],[96,760.55,4.8,"This is about preserving what makes us uniquely, messily and gloriously human."],[96,765.8,2.75,"It’s your random passion for E.T. memorabilia,"],[96,768.6,2.13,"your weird detour into geocaching,"],[96,770.73,2.71,"or your stubborn preference for a stick-shift car."],[96,774.89,3.63,"It’s about all the contradictions that AI can’t quite explain"],[96,778.52,3.71,"and the beauty of us being just a little weird once in a while."],[96,783.49,3.46,"And if we believe that human complexity is worth preserving,"],[96,786.99,1.63,"then the time to act is now."],[96,789.12,4.09,"Because we're at this inflection point where AI is no longer just recommending"],[96,793.21,2.34,"but starting to act on our behalf."],[96,795.88,2.42,"It's choosing, not suggesting."],[96,799.21,2.96,"And that's where the stakes become really existential."],[96,803.05,3.34,"So next time AI offers you pralines and cream,"],[96,806.43,1.38,"maybe say no"],[96,807.85,3.25,"and ask it to help you go wild and reckless instead."],[96,812.31,1.17,"Thank you."],[97,4.3,5.14,"The Earth is producing about a third more power from the sun"],[97,9.47,1.74,"this spring than last."],[97,11.68,3.77,"That solar power is suddenly so cheap and abundant"],[97,15.48,4.27,"that Australians will soon get three hours of electricity"],[97,19.78,2.6,"free every afternoon, free."],[97,22.42,3.77,"After 400,000 years of working hard for energy,"],[97,26.19,3.64,"from gathering firewood to paying the power bill,"],[97,29.86,5.14,"some humans will suddenly have access to this commodity for nothing."],[97,35.5,3.9,"California, fourth largest economy in the world,"],[97,39.44,4.97,"now produces most days more than 100 percent of its electricity"],[97,44.44,1.43,"from renewable energy."],[97,45.91,2.64,"At night, when the sun goes down,"],[97,48.58,5.34,"big new batteries on the grid turn dark into daylight."],[97,53.95,2.6,"In Pakistan, peasant farmers"],[97,56.59,4.07,"who used to pay through the nose for diesel power"],[97,60.69,2.27,"to run their irrigation pumps"],[97,62.99,2.64,"are using 30 percent less of the fuel"],[97,65.63,2.77,"than they were two years ago,"],[97,68.43,4.8,"in large part because they've imported millions of cheap Chinese solar panels"],[97,73.24,4.37,"and gone on YouTube to figure out how to connect them up."],[97,77.64,4.84,"They often lack the money to build the steel scaffolding"],[97,82.51,2.27,"we're used to seeing at solar farms."],[97,84.81,1.17,"No matter."],[97,86.02,3.7,"They just lay them on the ground and point them at the sun."],[97,90.09,6.84,"In a world where many, many big things have taken a very dark turn this decade,"],[97,96.96,3.94,"this is the one big thing that is going right."],[97,100.93,3.67,"We have quite suddenly the opportunity to build a clean,"],[97,104.64,3.37,"abundant future if we simply seize it."],[97,108.04,4.24,"But most of us don't recognize how huge this moment is."],[97,112.74,2.84,"In part, that's because we've spent decades"],[97,115.58,2.9,"calling this stuff “alternative energy,”"],[97,118.52,4.9,"and it's blinded us to the fact that it's now the obvious, common-sense,"],[97,123.45,3.07,"straightforward way to power the future."],[97,127.36,4.8,"Roughly 90 percent of new electric generation around the world last year"],[97,132.2,1.74,"came from the sun and the wind."],[97,133.93,3.74,"And that, in turn, is because earlier this decade,"],[97,137.7,3.5,"we crossed some invisible line where it became cheaper"],[97,141.21,2.23,"to produce power from the sun"],[97,143.47,2.64,"than from burning coal and gas and oil."],[97,146.14,1.47,"Let me repeat,"],[97,147.65,4.07,"we live on a planet where the cheapest way to make power"],[97,151.72,3.5,"is to point a sheet of glass at the sun."],[97,159.46,4.94,"If we can keep the staggering growth of the last 36 months going,"],[97,164.43,2.64,"then we could enter an extraordinary new age."],[97,167.1,1.44,"Consider."],[97,168.57,2.5,"Eight or nine million people a year die"],[97,171.1,3.17,"from breathing the combustion byproducts of fossil fuels."],[97,174.27,2.8,"That's one death in five on this planet."],[97,177.54,1.67,"But the Chinese cities,"],[97,179.24,4.77,"whose air was so foul a few years ago that you couldn't see across the street,"],[97,184.05,1.83,"are suddenly much cleaner."],[97,185.92,3.27,"Most of the cars sold there now come with a plug,"],[97,189.19,3.57,"and it connects them to the vast solar and wind farms"],[97,192.79,3.6,"that the Chinese are building faster than anyone on Earth."],[97,196.43,1.53,"More fundamentally,"],[97,198.0,3.64,"this energy transition is our only real chance"],[97,201.63,4.07,"of even starting to catch up with the physics of climate change."],[97,205.74,3.3,"It's too late to stop global warming."],[97,209.07,1.57,"But it's not too late"],[97,210.67,4.54,"to shave tenths of a degree off how hot the planet [will] eventually get."],[97,215.25,2.1,"And each 10th of a degree that we raise"],[97,217.38,3.97,"the temperature moves 100 million of our brothers and sisters"],[97,221.38,4.24,"out of a safe climate zone and into a more perilous one."],[97,226.39,3.37,"This new age would come with one other great bonus."],[97,229.79,3.94,"Unlike fossil fuels, which are concentrated in a few places,"],[97,233.76,2.74,"the sun's energy is available everywhere."],[97,236.87,4.44,"Instead of, say, attacking Venezuela to secure its oil"],[97,241.34,2.97,"or threatening to cut off Europe's gas supply"],[97,244.34,2.57,"to force the subjugation of Ukraine,"],[97,246.94,1.67,"we get to imagine a world"],[97,248.65,3.77,"where hydrocarbons no longer equal wealth and power."],[97,252.45,3.24,"Imagine how many fewer wars and coups"],[97,255.72,3.4,"and terrorist plots we would have seen in the last century"],[97,259.16,2.6,"if oil had been of trivial value."],[97,262.29,5.21,"As this spring has reminded us, humans are all too good at starting wars."],[97,267.5,1.27,"But even for us,"],[97,268.8,4.3,"figuring out how to fight one over sunshine will be tricky."],[97,273.14,5.37,"Sunlight travels 93 million miles to reach the Earth,"],[97,278.54,3.34,"but none of those miles go through the Strait of Hormuz."],[97,288.69,1.7,"So what’s good news?"],[97,290.92,3.84,"Unless, of course, you own an oil well or a coal mine."],[97,295.29,3.8,"Then the rise of clean energy becomes an extraordinary threat,"],[97,299.13,2.54,"one that mobilizes you to fight back."],[97,301.7,1.7,"In the US, for instance,"],[97,303.43,4.17,"candidate Trump told the oil industry that if they donated huge sums,"],[97,307.64,1.57,"he would have their back."],[97,309.24,1.53,"In his first year in office,"],[97,310.81,3.64,"he shut down huge wind farms off the eastern seaboard [and]"],[97,314.44,2.44,"killed solar farms across the country."],[97,317.35,5.3,"Now Trump can’t stop clean energy single-handedly."],[97,322.69,2.47,"It's not like he's Jesus or something."],[97,330.73,3.87,"Indeed, the economics are so favorable that even in the US,"],[97,334.63,3.97,"most new electric generation last year came from the sun and the wind."],[97,338.63,3.97,"And the biggest growth was in the state of Texas,"],[97,342.64,2.17,"the home ground of big oil."],[97,345.24,2.7,"But Trump and Russia and Saudi Arabia"],[97,347.94,4.67,"and a handful of other world powers may be able to slow that shift."],[97,352.98,3.9,"America's Secretary of Energy, against all global evidence,"],[97,356.92,2.44,"has said there's no climate crisis,"],[97,359.36,3.44,"and we're not in the middle of an energy transition, either."],[97,362.79,2.74,"So he's been subsidizing coal"],[97,365.56,3.67,"instead of embracing the clean energy that would, say,"],[97,369.27,4.84,"let us build any data centers we actually needed quickly and cheaply."],[97,374.14,2.54,"As the Chinese have amply demonstrated,"],[97,376.71,3.44,"solar fields can sprout in a matter of weeks."],[97,381.01,5.27,"America’s effort to delay this transition is not just economically stupid --"],[97,386.32,4.07,"it also endangers the last, best hope of cooling this Earth."],[97,390.42,1.53,"Sure, in 40 years,"],[97,391.99,2.97,"we will run the planet on sun and wind because it's cheap."],[97,394.99,3.24,"But if it takes us anything like 40 years to get there,"],[97,398.23,3.67,"then the planet we run on sun and wind will be a broken planet."],[97,402.5,2.64,"So those of us who hope for a different future"],[97,405.17,1.5,"need to fight for it."],[97,406.7,1.64,"And with the cheap solar panel,"],[97,408.37,3.27,"we finally have a real weapon in that struggle."],[97,411.68,1.74,"We need to push in every country"],[97,413.44,1.7,"and at every level of government"],[97,415.18,3.5,"for a faster move toward cheap, clean power."],[97,419.12,3.64,"We started Sun Day in the US last year"],[97,422.75,2.74,"on the autumnal equinox across the nation,"],[97,425.49,3.24,"500 events -- from solar-powered concerts"],[97,428.76,4.14,"to the installation of solar panels on Habitat for Humanity homes"],[97,432.93,2.64,"helped spread this new energy gospel."],[97,435.6,4.64,"We'll do it again on the summer solstice in 2027,"],[97,440.27,2.8,"and this time we will do it globally."],[97,443.74,4.34,"Days like this help us build the political power we need"],[97,448.11,2.27,"to push these things ahead."],[97,450.91,1.6,"Americans, for instance,"],[97,452.55,5.77,"right now, pay three times what Europeans or Australians pay for solar power,"],[97,458.36,4.74,"mostly because of unnecessary bureaucracy at the state and local level."],[97,463.13,5.64,"So Free the Rooftop is a cry that appeals to conservatives as much as liberals."],[97,468.8,3.07,"Utah became the first American state last year"],[97,471.87,3.34,"to legalize small-scale plug-in solar,"],[97,475.24,2.8,"opening the door for the kind of simple installations"],[97,478.07,3.74,"that have sprouted on the balconies of millions of European apartments"],[97,481.81,1.67,"in the last three years."],[97,483.51,2.94,"Maine and Virginia joined Utah last week."],[97,487.22,2.67,"So yeah, as we push ahead with this work,"],[97,489.89,3.04,"we need to talk about its scientific necessity"],[97,492.96,2.07,"and its economic pragmatism."],[97,495.06,3.07,"But we have one other ace to play,"],[97,498.16,3.7,"and that's the sheer beauty of power from the sun."],[97,501.9,3.5,"We have a deep human connection to our local star."],[97,505.74,4.64,"We don't know for sure how prehistoric people thought about things"],[97,510.37,3.64,"because, being prehistoric, they failed to write it down."],[97,514.04,3.67,"But we do know that every pile of rocks they left behind,"],[97,517.75,4.4,"like Stonehenge, seems to point to the equinox or the solstice."],[97,523.05,3.24,"Do you not break into an involuntary grin"],[97,526.32,4.54,"when the sun comes out from behind a cloud and hits you on the shoulder blades?"],[97,530.89,1.87,"If you don't get enough sun,"],[97,532.8,5.44,"you literally can get SAD -- seasonal affective disorder."],[97,538.67,3.47,"So the mantra for this moment is clear:"],[97,542.17,3.07,"energy from heaven, not from hell."],[97,552.55,4.07,"And with that slogan, what a breakthrough moment this could be."],[97,556.65,4.41,"I mean, humans have been burning things since the dawn of time."],[97,561.09,1.8,"Darwin said language and fire"],[97,562.93,2.9,"were the two things that set our species apart."],[97,565.83,1.57,"And fire was mostly good."],[97,567.43,2.47,"We learned to cook, which gave us the big brain."],[97,569.9,2.77,"We could move north and south from the equator."],[97,572.7,1.6,"Anthropologists even think"],[97,574.34,3.44,"that eons of standing around the campfire every night"],[97,577.81,2.27,"helped explain some of the social bonds"],[97,580.11,1.4,"that mark our species."],[97,581.54,2.97,"Think of it as a sort of primitive TED talk."],[97,585.55,4.6,"When we learned to control the combustion of coal and oil and gas,"],[97,590.19,3.64,"that was the Industrial Revolution, and it gave us modernity."],[97,594.42,5.34,"But now the cost of those fires has simply grown too high."],[97,599.76,3.4,"The very future of the climate on which we depend"],[97,603.2,2.8,"is being overwhelmed by their smoke."],[97,606.04,3.57,"So what sweet relief that in this exact moment,"],[97,609.64,1.67,"we can, if we want,"],[97,611.31,1.77,"put out those fires,"],[97,613.11,1.83,"the big ones in our power plants,"],[97,614.98,4.44,"the smaller ones in our furnaces and under the hoods of our cars."],[97,619.45,4.27,"The sun already gives us light and warmth"],[97,623.75,2.7,"and, via photosynthesis, our supper."],[97,626.49,4.57,"Now it is willing to provide us with all the power we could ever need."],[97,631.06,4.34,"This gift has come at the last possible moment."],[97,635.43,3.47,"It would be an extraordinary sin to waste it,"],[97,638.9,3.8,"and it would be a beautiful, beautiful project"],[97,642.74,2.4,"for humans to build it out"],[97,645.17,5.14,"across our hot and tired Earth in the next few years."],[97,650.61,4.14,"In advance of your participation in that fight,"],[97,654.75,2.04,"let me just say, thank you."],[98,0.0,1.52,"In the year 2000,"],[98,1.52,2.24,"George W. Bush won the presidency,"],[98,3.76,3.2,"even though over half a million fewer people"],[98,6.96,2.16,"voted for him than for Al Gore."],[98,9.12,2.0,"The same thing happened in 2016,"],[98,11.12,1.6,"when Donald Trump won—"],[98,12.72,4.0,"even though 2.9 million fewer people voted for him"],[98,16.72,1.68,"than for Hillary Clinton."],[98,18.4,3.36,"In fact, in five different presidential elections"],[98,21.76,1.28,"in U.S. history,"],[98,23.04,4.96,"the winner of the most votes lost the election as a whole."],[98,28.0,2.8,"It sounds like upside-down math,"],[98,30.8,1.84,"but it’s actually caused by something that"],[98,32.64,2.16,"goes all the way back to the constitution:"],[98,34.8,1.68,"the Electoral College."],[98,36.48,1.28,"So, what’s the deal?"],[98,37.76,2.16,"Is the Electoral College broken?"],[98,39.92,3.04,"Or could it be that it’s actually working"],[98,42.96,3.12,"exactly how the Founding Fathers intended it to?"],[98,46.08,2.88,"Hi! I'm Kousha Navidar, this is Crash Course,"],[98,48.96,2.94,"and we’re answering Big Questions on U.S. Government."],[98,56.4,2.4,"We love to debate about the Electoral College,"],[98,58.8,2.4,"but what actually is it in the first place?"],[98,61.2,1.28,"In simplest terms,"],[98,62.48,2.56,"the Electoral College is the group of people"],[98,65.04,2.56,"who actually cast their votes for president."],[98,67.6,2.88,"They represent the will of their given state’s voters."],[98,70.48,2.0,"And while the U.S. isn’t the only country"],[98,72.48,2.48,"where people elect their leader indirectly,"],[98,74.96,1.84,"no one else does it quite like us."],[98,76.8,3.12,"And the Electoral College isn’t a recent invention."],[98,79.92,2.8,"Let’s check in with Buff, our resident historian,"],[98,82.72,2.0,"who’s almost as handsome as me,"],[98,84.72,1.32,"for how this started."],[98,86.04,3.4,"[BUFF] Hi, Kousha! You’re glowing."],[98,89.44,1.12,"Alright, let’s get into it"],[98,90.56,3.68,"and stop history from being a mystery."],[98,94.24,2.88,"So, the year was 1787."],[98,97.12,2.72,"The drafters were writing the Constitution,"],[98,99.84,3.68,"and they found themselves with a pretty heavy question:"],[98,103.52,2.8,"How do you pick a president fairly??"],[98,106.32,2.32,"They agreed citizens should have a say–"],[98,108.64,2.16,"citizens at the time of course meaning"],[98,110.8,2.08,"white land-owning men."],[98,112.88,3.04,"But running a national election by popular vote,"],[98,115.92,2.4,"where the people directly decide the winner,"],[98,118.32,1.64,"sounded like a logistical nightmare"],[98,119.96,2.52,"— even with only 13 states at the time."],[98,122.48,2.08,"Another concern among the southern states"],[98,124.56,3.52,"was their large population of enslaved people."],[98,128.08,3.12,"While the populations between the northern and southern states"],[98,131.2,1.6,"were roughly equal,"],[98,132.8,4.16,"around a third of the southern population were enslaved,"],[98,136.96,1.84,"and thus nonvoting,"],[98,138.8,4.4,"giving the South less power under a popular vote system."],[98,143.2,3.36,"Another idea was to give Congress members or governors"],[98,146.56,1.92,"the task of picking the president."],[98,148.48,3.12,"But some worried that would give the legislative branch"],[98,151.6,1.44,"too much power,"],[98,153.04,2.56,"and pave the way for an election process"],[98,155.6,2.0,"more secretive and cutthroat"],[98,157.6,3.28,"than a tribal council meeting on Survivor."],[98,160.88,1.44,"So, somewhere between"],[98,162.32,1.44,"“everybody chooses the president”"],[98,163.76,2.08,"and “only elected leaders do”,"],[98,165.84,3.12,"the founders struck a last-minute compromise:"],[98,168.96,2.32,"each state gets a slate of electors,"],[98,171.28,3.2,"based on how many Senators they’ve got (which is always two)"],[98,174.48,2.88,"and how many representatives in the House they’ve got"],[98,177.36,2.88,"(which varies, depending on the state’s population)."],[98,180.24,4.8,"Then those electors actually cast votes for the next President."],[98,185.04,3.52,"And thus, the Electoral College was born."],[98,188.56,1.06,"Back to you, Kousha."],[98,191.59,1.71,"[KOUSHA] Wow, Buff, I had no idea"],[98,193.3,0.96,"you liked Survivor!"],[98,194.56,1.37,"You know, Jeff Probst is like a"],[98,195.93,2.06,"founding father of reality TV."],[98,198.24,1.2,"Okay, back to business."],[98,199.44,2.32,"The Constitution doesn’t actually dictate"],[98,201.76,1.52,"how states pick electors,"],[98,203.28,2.16,"but it lays down some ground rules"],[98,205.44,2.96,"—like, electors can’t be current members of Congress."],[98,208.4,3.44,"But electors also aren’t just randos off the street."],[98,211.84,3.12,"Today, what generally happens is each party picks"],[98,214.96,3.84,"their slate of electors at their state conventions."],[98,218.8,4.08,"Typically, they’re local leaders or campaign ride-or-dies"],[98,222.88,1.84,"with deep party loyalties."],[98,224.72,4.56,"Then, when regular people like you and me vote,"],[98,229.28,3.68,"we’re not actually voting for our favored presidential candidate."],[98,232.96,2.08,"We’re voting for the slate of electors"],[98,235.04,4.88,"who’ve pledged to vote for that candidate."],[98,239.92,1.36,"Oh, thanks!"],[98,241.28,2.24,"Most states, plus Washington, D.C.,"],[98,243.52,2.8,"go for a system that’s winner-takes-all,"],[98,246.32,2.4,"where the party that wins the popular vote"],[98,248.72,2.56,"gets all the electors, too."],[98,251.28,1.04,"Take Colorado."],[98,252.32,6.0,"In 2024, 54% of regular voters cast their ballots for Kamala Harris."],[98,258.32,2.32,"43% voted for Trump."],[98,260.64,2.4,"Since Harris won the popular vote,"],[98,263.04,3.92,"she got all 10 of Colorado’s electoral votes."],[98,266.96,2.96,"But two states— Maine and Nebraska—"],[98,269.92,1.52,"do things a little differently."],[98,271.44,3.04,"They give two of their electoral votes to whoever"],[98,274.48,2.88,"wins the popular vote in their state overall,"],[98,277.36,4.08,"and the rest to the favorite in each individual congressional district."],[98,281.44,4.96,"Nationwide, there are 538 total electoral votes up for grabs,"],[98,286.4,3.12,"and a candidate needs a majority of those to win—"],[98,289.52,1.92,"270, to be exact."],[98,291.44,1.28,"But when it’s election night"],[98,292.72,1.76,"and you’re watching the news on your phone,"],[98,294.48,2.4,"and those fancy state maps on your screen"],[98,296.88,1.68,"light up red and blue,"],[98,298.56,3.36,"they’re really projecting what the electoral vote will be."],[98,301.92,3.44,"The real electoral vote happens after Election Day—"],[98,305.36,3.92,"on the Tuesday following the second Wednesday of December."],[98,309.28,2.8,"And it’s only officially done on January 6th,"],[98,312.08,2.88,"when the electoral votes get counted in Congress"],[98,314.96,1.6,"under the Vice President’s eye."],[98,316.56,1.04,"And most of the time,"],[98,317.6,2.16,"that’s how the Electoral College works."],[98,319.76,3.52,"But rarely, a faithless elector rocks the boat,"],[98,323.28,3.36,"and votes for someone other than who they pledged to."],[98,326.64,1.44,"It happened in 2016,"],[98,328.08,2.64,"when seven members of the Electoral College"],[98,330.72,1.68,"voted against their pledge—"],[98,332.4,2.56,"causing Trump to lose two electoral votes,"],[98,334.96,1.84,"and Clinton to lose five."],[98,336.8,2.96,"And actually, there’s nothing in the Constitution that says"],[98,339.76,3.12,"faithless electors can’t be faithless."],[98,342.88,2.8,"But after all the drama in 2016,"],[98,345.68,2.0,"the Supreme Court ruled in 2020"],[98,347.68,4.08,"that states are allowed to punish their own faithless electors"],[98,351.76,3.23,"with fines or by invalidating their ballots."],[98,354.99,1.81,"It’s all pretty complex."],[98,356.8,3.6,"But the main reason people grumble about the Electoral College"],[98,360.4,2.24,"is that oddity I mentioned before:"],[98,362.64,1.6,"it makes it completely possible"],[98,364.24,2.72,"for a candidate to win the popular vote,"],[98,366.96,1.76,"but still lose the election."],[98,368.72,1.92,"There’s a few reasons why this happens,"],[98,370.64,1.92,"the first being that a citizen’s vote"],[98,372.56,4.0,"in a smaller state actually counts more than in a big one."],[98,376.56,1.6,"That sounds like even more fake math,"],[98,378.16,0.88,"but remember,"],[98,379.04,2.56,"the number of electors for each state"],[98,381.6,3.36,"is calculated by how many seats in Congress it has."],[98,384.96,2.32,"And while the number of House seats is calculated"],[98,387.28,1.6,"based on population,"],[98,388.88,2.96,"every state gets two electoral votes for the Senate,"],[98,391.84,2.0,"no matter how many people live there."],[98,393.84,1.52,"I’ll give you an example."],[98,395.36,2.4,"Wyoming, a low-population state,"],[98,397.76,5.2,"gets 3 total electors for their two Senators and one House Rep."],[98,402.96,5.52,"That breaks down to about one elector for every 200,000 people."],[98,408.48,4.48,"Meanwhile, California, with a famously massive population,"],[98,412.96,2.48,"gets 54 electors total–"],[98,415.44,4.72,"about one elector for every 700,000 people."],[98,420.16,5.04,"That means a voter in Wyoming has almost 4 times more influence"],[98,425.2,3.44,"on the Electoral College than a voter in California!"],[98,428.64,2.64,"But there’s a second reason there’s sometimes a difference"],[98,431.28,1.76,"between who wins the popular vote"],[98,433.04,1.6,"and the electoral vote:"],[98,434.64,3.84,"it’s that “winner-takes-all” thing that most states have going on."],[98,438.48,2.8,"Again, looking at California is helpful here."],[98,441.28,2.48,"Harris won the state in 2024,"],[98,443.76,2.8,"taking home all 54 electoral votes."],[98,446.56,5.12,"But only 58% of Californian voters actually cast their ballots for her."],[98,451.68,2.64,"That’s how winner-takes-all works, though."],[98,454.32,4.8,"Even if she’d only won California by a single popular vote,"],[98,459.12,4.24,"she still would have swept all 54 of the state’s electoral votes."],[98,463.36,2.96,"That means millions of popular votes"],[98,466.32,3.76,"essentially have no influence on the outcome of the actual election."],[98,470.08,4.24,"In 2024, Trump won millions of votes in blue states"],[98,474.32,2.56,"that did nothing to help clinch his win,"],[98,476.88,3.04,"just like Harris earned millions of votes in red states"],[98,479.92,2.64,"that made no difference in preventing her defeat."],[98,482.56,2.48,"In fact, about the same number of people"],[98,485.04,3.28,"voted for Trump in California alone"],[98,488.32,2.64,"as in twelve of the lower population states"],[98,490.96,2.72,"that actually won him electoral votes."],[98,493.68,2.32,"This puts a lot of pressure on states"],[98,496.0,3.12,"where the popular vote comes really close."],[98,499.12,2.56,"Battleground states have a decent shot"],[98,501.68,2.24,"of swinging one way or the other,"],[98,503.92,4.96,"and all it takes is a small cadre of folks to change their mind."],[98,508.88,3.2,"If that happens in enough battleground states,"],[98,512.08,4.0,"a candidate can get a motherlode of electoral votes—"],[98,516.08,2.56,"even if they only won a"],[98,518.64,1.68,"…babyfull?"],[98,520.32,2.56,"more popular votes in those states."],[98,522.88,4.64,"Which is why we hear about Wisconsin a lot every four years."],[98,527.67,1.62,"I mean, that and their cheese curds."],[98,529.53,1.34,"I’m lactose intolerant."],[98,531.12,2.48,"That’s exactly what happened in 2016."],[98,533.6,1.44,"You look at the electoral map,"],[98,535.04,6.64,"and it looks like Trump won by a landslide—306 to 232."],[98,541.68,3.92,"But if less than 100,000 voters total"],[98,545.6,4.0,"across Pennsylvania, Michigan, and Wisconsin had voted"],[98,549.6,1.84,"for Clinton instead,"],[98,551.44,6.4,"she would’ve taken those electoral votes and won 278 to 260."],[98,557.84,2.16,"In the end, Clinton came out with"],[98,560.0,2.72,"nearly three million more popular votes"],[98,562.72,1.76,"nationwide than Trump."],[98,564.48,2.08,"But because of the Electoral College,"],[98,566.56,2.68,"it was Trump who was elected president."],[98,569.28,3.6,"That disconnect between the popular vote and electoral vote"],[98,572.88,2.56,"can be enough to make you wonder—"],[98,575.44,2.73,"uh, are we really just down to live like this?"],[98,578.4,2.96,"Some argue it’s actually not such a bad thing"],[98,581.36,2.32,"for the Electoral College and the popular vote"],[98,583.68,1.52,"to get out of sync sometimes,"],[98,585.2,4.22,"for reasons not so different from the one put forth in 1787."],[98,589.68,1.28,"What about the little guys?"],[98,591.04,1.68,"Defenders of the Electoral College"],[98,592.72,4.0,"argue that if elections were decided by popular vote alone,"],[98,596.72,3.92,"candidates would naturally focus on the most densely populated states."],[98,600.64,3.36,"And that would leave rural areas and their specific,"],[98,604.0,1.7,"important, needs behind."],[98,606.26,2.94,"I feel like the candidates would still hit up the Iowa State Fair"],[98,609.2,2.93,"for the butter cow alone, but maybe that’s just me."],[98,612.87,1.33,"I’m still lactose intolerant."],[98,614.55,2.65,"But while that’s a super valid concern,"],[98,617.2,1.56,"the math doesn’t actually math."],[98,619.12,2.08,"Even if candidates put all their energy"],[98,621.2,3.28,"into winning over voters in the 100 biggest cities"],[98,624.48,2.8,"and they got all of the popular votes there,"],[98,627.28,4.32,"they'd still only have 26% of the popular vote total."],[98,631.6,3.84,"You literally couldn’t win without paying attention to the little guys,"],[98,635.44,1.89,"and the medium-sized guys too."],[98,637.52,1.72,"Still, it’s one thing to be like,"],[98,639.57,2.48,"“hey, maybe this system isn’t really serving us well.”"],[98,642.56,2.04,"It is another to fix it."],[98,644.88,2.24,"To actually do away with the Electoral College,"],[98,647.12,2.56,"we’d need to pass a constitutional amendment"],[98,649.68,4.72,"—and that is legitimately like a 535-person tribal council,"],[98,654.4,1.24,"except even more fighting."],[98,655.92,2.56,"The workaround that’s made the most headway so far"],[98,658.48,3.28,"is the National Popular Vote Interstate Compact."],[98,661.91,1.8,"That’s an agreement between states"],[98,663.71,2.45,"to automatically give all their electoral votes"],[98,666.16,2.8,"to the winner of the national popular vote."],[98,668.96,4.32,"So, if you lose Colorado but win the popular vote overall?"],[98,673.28,3.28,"You still get all of Colorado’s electoral votes."],[98,676.56,2.56,"This wouldn’t get rid of the Electoral College,"],[98,679.12,2.96,"but it would essentially de-fang it."],[98,682.08,3.04,"So far, 18 states and Washington DC"],[98,685.12,1.76,"have agreed to the compact,"],[98,686.88,1.84,"but the compact only goes into effect"],[98,688.72,1.92,"if enough states get on board"],[98,690.64,3.36,"for the total electoral votes to add up to 270."],[98,694.0,5.44,"But it’s a little dubious how constitutional the NPVIC actually is,"],[98,699.44,2.32,"and there aren’t signs of enough other states"],[98,701.76,2.0,"planning to join anytime soon."],[98,703.76,2.64,"Since the Electoral College seems here to stay,"],[98,706.4,1.04,"at least for now,"],[98,707.44,2.72,"there are bipartisan efforts to help prevent it from"],[98,710.16,2.0,"being used for any funny business."],[98,712.16,4.72,"Like, the 2022 Electoral Count Reform Act, or E.C.R.A.,"],[98,716.88,3.2,"was created in response to the attack on the U.S. Capitol"],[98,720.08,2.72,"on January 6th, 2021."],[98,722.8,3.76,"January 6th, if you remember, is the day when electors’ votes"],[98,726.56,4.0,"are formally tallied while observed by the sitting Vice President."],[98,730.56,4.96,"After losing both the popular vote and the Electoral College in 2020,"],[98,735.52,2.96,"outgoing President Trump and a group of supporters"],[98,738.48,4.0,"moved to create an alternate slate of Republican electors"],[98,742.48,2.56,"in certain battleground states he lost"],[98,745.04,2.56,"so Trump would be declared the winner."],[98,747.6,3.84,"The plan was to get Vice President Mike Pence"],[98,751.44,3.36,"to count those votes instead of the official ones,"],[98,754.8,4.4,"which resulted in an armed riot at the Capitol."],[98,759.2,2.0,"Spoiler alert: the plan failed."],[98,761.2,2.56,"But it was a wake-up call for lots of people"],[98,763.76,1.6,"who wanted to make sure that,"],[98,765.36,1.04,"while imperfect,"],[98,766.4,2.64,"the Electoral College couldn’t be used to overthrow"],[98,769.04,1.52,"the results of an election."],[98,770.56,3.12,"Now, the E.C.R.A. clarifies the Vice President's role"],[98,773.68,2.64,"in counting the votes as purely ceremonial–"],[98,776.32,2.32,"so even if the rioters had succeeded"],[98,778.64,3.12,"in wrestling their new electors in after the fact,"],[98,781.76,2.93,"Trump still would have lost the presidency."],[98,784.69,1.07,"Whenever we think about"],[98,785.76,2.72,"how our government works (or doesn’t),"],[98,788.48,2.32,"we have to think about where it came from:"],[98,790.8,2.32,"a bunch of land-owning white guys"],[98,793.12,3.04,"locked in a hot, swampy conference room"],[98,796.16,2.0,"over 200 years ago."],[98,798.16,2.72,"These guys created the Electoral College to solve"],[98,800.88,1.92,"what they saw as a problem:"],[98,802.8,2.16,"that maybe it wasn’t such a great idea"],[98,804.96,2.24,"to give all the power to all the people,"],[98,807.2,2.24,"logistically or politically."],[98,809.44,2.24,"So is the Electoral College broken?"],[98,811.68,1.04,"Well, actually no."],[98,812.72,3.6,"It's working more or less exactly as the founders intended it to."],[98,816.32,3.92,"The question is: is it working for us here, today,"],[98,820.24,1.92,"now, for you and me?"],[98,822.16,2.96,"And if it’s not, what might we build instead?"],[98,825.12,3.76,"Next time, we’ll keep thinking about the idea of one person, one vote–"],[98,828.88,1.76,"we’re talking voter fraud, people."],[98,830.64,1.52,"I’ll see you then!"],[98,832.16,3.28,"This episode of Crash Course Big Questions on U.S. Government"],[98,835.44,2.41,"was produced in partnership with USAFacts,"],[98,837.85,2.47,"a non-partisan civic tech project"],[98,840.32,2.96,"that makes government data easier to understand."],[98,843.28,2.52,"For more resources that go along with these videos,"],[98,845.8,3.5,"go to usafacts.org/crashcourse."],[98,849.68,3.92,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Big Questions on U.S. Government,"],[98,853.6,2.16,"which was filmed in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[98,855.76,2.96,"and was made with the help of all these nice people."],[98,858.72,4.4,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[98,863.12,2.92,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[99,4.3,5.94,"What will it take for America to lead the global AI competition?"],[99,11.11,2.9,"Now we all know we’ll need plentiful energy, the best chips,"],[99,14.04,4.1,"the most innovative AI companies, the best models."],[99,18.48,2.6,"But I argue that's not nearly enough."],[99,22.05,3.24,"Because if we're the best in the world at those things,"],[99,25.29,3.47,"but we've displaced millions of American workers,"],[99,28.79,2.9,"then we're going to lose the global AI race."],[99,32.1,2.23,"In fact,"],[99,34.37,3.04,"we will have automated our decline."],[99,37.44,3.94,"Because recessions, social unrest, political upheaval,"],[99,41.41,2.0,"those will weaken our country,"],[99,43.44,2.27,"our politics and our economy."],[99,46.01,2.77,"And ultimately, I believe, I know,"],[99,48.81,6.14,"they will lead to excessive, burdensome regulation of AI"],[99,54.98,4.61,"that will slow and stop and hinder AI innovation."],[99,60.09,5.47,"The reality is the United States can't lead the world"],[99,65.6,2.57,"in technological innovation"],[99,68.17,2.1,"if it's failing its people at home."],[99,70.83,5.5,"We need a technology strategy and a human capital strategy,"],[99,76.34,4.71,"because the only way to truly win the long-term AI race"],[99,81.05,2.17,"is to lead in the technology"],[99,83.21,5.94,"and to have a plan that brings everybody along to an AI economy."],[99,89.79,3.04,"Now I think it’s incredibly exciting"],[99,92.82,5.04,"to think about what a well-trained individual can do with AI."],[99,97.86,4.4,"Think about how much more productive and creative we'll all be"],[99,102.27,2.54,"and how many new businesses will be created."],[99,105.34,2.77,"And I'm optimistic because history shows"],[99,108.14,4.47,"every time we create a new technology,"],[99,112.64,5.0,"it does create new jobs, new industries, new products, new services."],[99,118.35,2.77,"Over time, with some time."],[99,121.52,2.4,"And I'm confident that that will happen again,"],[99,123.95,1.5,"this time with AI."],[99,126.46,1.3,"With time."],[99,128.56,6.41,"So what I'm worried about is the near-term disruption to workers"],[99,134.97,4.71,"as we transition from here to an AI economy."],[99,140.0,5.47,"Because, I know this as a former governor and secretary of commerce,"],[99,145.51,4.14,"America's workforce and career transition systems"],[99,149.68,1.8,"weren't built for this moment."],[99,152.15,3.37,"Some people estimate tens of millions of American workers"],[99,155.55,2.27,"are in AI-vulnerable jobs."],[99,157.82,1.77,"All kinds of jobs,"],[99,159.62,4.1,"people of every age, geography, income, level of education."],[99,163.76,4.1,"We are not prepared for this transition."],[99,167.9,1.7,"And Americans know it."],[99,170.67,3.24,"I was at a bar the other night watching NCAA basketball."],[99,174.84,1.74,"Huge University of Michigan fan."],[99,177.17,1.33,"And it's all --"],[99,179.24,1.17,"Go Blue!"],[99,180.41,1.77,"It's all everybody was talking about."],[99,182.21,1.23,"It's all the chatter."],[99,183.45,2.64,"\"What are you going to do when you lose your job to AI?\""],[99,186.12,3.64,"\"I'm so bummed, I'm paying all this money for tuition for my kids in college."],[99,189.79,2.27,"Are they going to have a job when they're done?\""],[99,192.06,5.0,"Americans are anxious for a reason, and we owe them more than empathy."],[99,197.39,2.6,"We owe them a plan, we owe them action."],[99,200.7,2.77,"And so far, I'm not hearing a lot of good solutions."],[99,204.0,5.34,"There’s the slow AI, stop AI, overregulate AI crowd."],[99,209.34,1.43,"It's a bad idea."],[99,210.77,4.0,"It denies Americans the promise of AI, and China will pull ahead."],[99,215.85,3.1,"And then there's the universal basic income crowd."],[99,218.95,1.47,"That's also a bad idea."],[99,220.82,4.24,"Every one of you know, a job is more than a paycheck."],[99,225.09,2.44,"It's dignity, it's purpose, it's pride."],[99,227.56,3.24,"Without purposeful work, a society unravels."],[99,231.79,3.17,"So what are the elements of an effective transition plan?"],[99,237.13,4.04,"Unfortunately, as I stand here today, I don't know the exact details,"],[99,241.87,1.53,"But here's what I do know."],[99,243.41,4.94,"I know it's rooted in a new grand bargain between government and business."],[99,249.18,4.17,"I know it means tearing down the wall we've had for so long"],[99,253.35,2.74,"between school and jobs,"],[99,256.12,2.1,"and I know industry,"],[99,258.22,3.47,"every company needs to help lead the transition."],[99,262.73,2.97,"And here is what I know more than anything else."],[99,266.83,4.1,"I know if we are determined, we can make it happen."],[99,272.77,4.64,"At a minimum, we need massive changes in both our workforce training system"],[99,277.44,3.0,"and our career transition support system."],[99,281.75,2.67,"In an effective workforce training system,"],[99,284.42,3.14,"employers define where work is today,"],[99,287.55,3.04,"what skills are needed, where it’s going."],[99,290.59,4.11,"And then schools and government training programs prepare people to get there."],[99,294.73,1.87,"That is not what we have today."],[99,297.13,1.83,"Today, in our country,"],[99,299.0,6.31,"we spend hundreds of billions of dollars to incentivize enrollment in college --"],[99,305.34,2.27,"without regard to whether people get a degree,"],[99,307.6,2.4,"the skills they need for a job or a job."],[99,311.54,2.4,"The truth is, and you all know this,"],[99,313.94,4.5,"government and schools don't know the skills employers need today"],[99,318.48,1.3,"or will need tomorrow."],[99,319.82,3.3,"Industry has the most accurate and dynamic view."],[99,324.39,5.54,"By the way, I’ve seen this be successful as governor and secretary of commerce."],[99,330.49,3.44,"When TSMC decided"],[99,333.93,4.77,"to expand manufacturing of chips in America,"],[99,338.74,1.47,"they told us what they needed."],[99,340.24,4.77,"They needed skilled electrical engineers and equipment operators."],[99,345.01,1.17,"So we got to work."],[99,346.21,3.07,"We designed with them, with community college,"],[99,349.28,3.24,"with certificate initiatives,"],[99,352.55,3.84,"accelerated certificate programs and apprenticeships"],[99,356.39,2.13,"tailored to the company's needs."],[99,359.42,3.54,"Today, TSMC is thriving in Arizona,"],[99,362.99,5.57,"making leading-edge AI chips at scale for the first time in America's history."],[99,374.57,4.44,"Another problem with our current system is it's a one-and-done system."],[99,379.44,2.07,"You graduate high school, you go to college,"],[99,381.55,1.53,"you're done with your education."],[99,383.11,2.17,"That isn't going to work in an AI economy."],[99,385.31,2.24,"All of us, all of us,"],[99,387.55,1.44,"will have to learn new skills"],[99,389.02,4.24,"because our jobs will be constantly changing over the course of a career."],[99,393.99,5.44,"Let's be honest, most people learn"],[99,399.46,3.94,"most of the skills that they use in their job every day"],[99,403.43,1.27,"on the job."],[99,405.97,1.3,"Yeah?"],[99,407.3,2.3,"So why don't we have more effective, affordable,"],[99,409.61,2.14,"flexible options at scale"],[99,411.77,5.34,"so people can earn while they learn continuously over the course of a career?"],[99,417.55,6.07,"A good friend of mine's husband recently lost his job in IT."],[99,424.09,2.2,"Super smart guy in his late 30s."],[99,426.72,3.34,"So he spent a long time trying to figure out a job that excited him,"],[99,430.09,3.7,"that he thought was an AI-resilient career path."],[99,433.8,2.67,"He finally found a job in the HVAC industry."],[99,437.2,1.3,"He was psyched for it."],[99,439.24,3.14,"He spent a lot of time looking for a training program in his area."],[99,442.37,1.84,"He found one that he could get into."],[99,444.61,2.77,"It took a year, over a year actually,"],[99,447.41,1.6,"cost money,"],[99,449.05,2.33,"and he wouldn't be paid for a year."],[99,452.32,2.04,"Who can go a year without a salary?"],[99,455.35,2.84,"Now look, I know, I’ve seen it,"],[99,458.19,5.24,"there is a lot of really excellent innovation out there"],[99,463.46,2.97,"around employer-led worker training,"],[99,466.46,3.27,"apprenticeships, co-ops and college."],[99,469.77,1.44,"But here's the reality."],[99,471.2,4.34,"All of those efforts are a tiny portion"],[99,475.54,3.1,"of America's post-high school system."],[99,479.34,1.97,"They ought to be the system."],[99,482.08,2.67,"They ought to be the norm and not the exception."],[99,484.78,4.14,"And there should be no stigma for going that path."],[99,489.99,2.0,"OK, now, the reality is it's going to take"],[99,492.02,3.2,"more than just new workforce training system"],[99,495.26,2.6,"if we're going to get through this economic transition,"],[99,497.86,5.34,"because we also have to provide support for people as AI changes their jobs."],[99,503.87,2.37,"And unfortunately, today in the US,"],[99,506.27,4.64,"our primary career transition support system is unemployment insurance."],[99,511.61,2.77,"It was created 100 years ago in a different time"],[99,514.41,3.67,"where people had a single job in a single industry for decades."],[99,518.12,3.67,"It does nothing to support people getting new training,"],[99,521.78,3.0,"starting a new business, entering a new field."],[99,524.82,2.47,"And actually, I know this from being governor,"],[99,527.29,4.6,"it doesn't provide nearly enough income support for middle-class wage earners,"],[99,531.93,1.8,"let alone high-income earners."],[99,534.13,3.97,"So in addition to unemployment benefits,"],[99,538.1,3.67,"why don't we offer temporary wage support"],[99,541.8,4.1,"to get workers back into the workforce quickly by topping up their salary,"],[99,545.91,2.7,"if they take a pay cut to enter a new field?"],[99,550.15,2.44,"By the way, one of the reasons I'm excited about AI"],[99,552.62,3.07,"is I think it'll make starting a business easier than ever."],[99,555.72,5.27,"So why don't we look at a program of self-employment assistance"],[99,561.02,3.34,"to support workers while they start a new business?"],[99,565.7,1.37,"So let's say we do all this."],[99,567.06,2.74,"Let's say we are determined, creative, and we do all this."],[99,569.83,1.77,"What might it look like?"],[99,572.13,2.14,"Imagine a 45-year-old woman."],[99,574.64,3.34,"She's been an accountant for 15 years, closing the books."],[99,578.64,2.5,"She's got two kids, a pile of bills and a mortgage."],[99,581.18,1.17,"Pretty typical."],[99,583.05,2.34,"Last week she was told she's losing her job"],[99,585.38,1.57,"because it's being automated."],[99,587.42,2.67,"Today, in our system,"],[99,590.09,1.6,"if she's lucky,"],[99,591.72,2.97,"she'd get a retraining brochure and two weeks' severance."],[99,595.36,2.33,"There's over a million accountants in America."],[99,598.66,3.17,"In a better system,"],[99,601.87,4.47,"her company would be incentivized to allow her to start retraining"],[99,606.34,2.77,"months before she's laid off."],[99,609.44,4.34,"Maybe she gets a short-term credential to learn a higher-value skill."],[99,614.14,4.04,"It puts her in a position to get redeployed at that company"],[99,618.22,2.4,"if the company is committed to redeployment."],[99,620.65,2.2,"And if she needs it,"],[99,622.89,2.4,"maybe she could collect temporary wage insurance"],[99,625.32,1.7,"to top up her income"],[99,627.06,2.84,"to make up the difference between her old job"],[99,629.93,1.7,"and her new job salary."],[99,632.9,1.5,"So how are we going to do this?"],[99,634.4,1.17,"How do we get there?"],[99,635.6,2.57,"Incentives, innovation and urgency."],[99,638.87,3.77,"First, the government needs to fund schools"],[99,642.67,4.2,"and training programs on their outcomes."],[99,650.38,1.17,"How about that?"],[99,652.95,3.94,"They shouldn't just get the money because people show up and enroll."],[99,656.92,3.67,"They need to be funded based upon whether people get good skills"],[99,660.62,1.74,"and actually get a job."],[99,663.13,2.87,"Incentives need to change for businesses as well."],[99,666.43,4.54,"Right now, the incentives are such"],[99,670.97,2.57,"that a company lays a lot of people off today,"],[99,673.54,2.7,"and their stock price surges tomorrow."],[99,676.27,3.4,"It is too easy to hit the “easy button” of layoffs."],[99,681.21,3.54,"Companies need different incentives."],[99,685.18,2.67,"Quite frankly, we need a new system"],[99,687.88,3.44,"where it's more expensive to abandon workers"],[99,691.35,1.84,"than to retrain them."],[99,697.73,1.7,"We all respond to incentives."],[99,700.13,4.17,"How about we pilot tax credits or other economic incentives"],[99,704.33,4.37,"that reward companies for worker redeployment,"],[99,708.71,2.24,"for entry-level hiring,"],[99,710.94,4.77,"for reinvesting AI productivity gains into new jobs?"],[99,716.41,2.27,"We have spent decades, if not longer,"],[99,718.72,3.2,"perfecting the incentives for investing in machines."],[99,722.32,3.07,"We need to do the same so companies invest in people."],[99,726.99,1.6,"And here's the reality."],[99,729.16,1.53,"It's in all of our interests"],[99,730.73,3.3,"to have a smooth transition to an AI economy."],[99,734.06,2.97,"It isn't business versus workers."],[99,737.07,3.37,"Nobody benefits with the recession, excessive AI regulations,"],[99,740.47,4.54,"social unrest and political violence and divisiveness."],[99,746.08,3.74,"By the way, it's in everyone's benefit to reach this exciting potential"],[99,749.85,2.17,"of AI innovation."],[99,753.12,2.6,"It’s not corporate charity to do this."],[99,755.75,3.87,"Last time I checked, agents didn't walk into the store and buy things."],[99,759.66,1.6,"Humans do that."],[99,761.29,2.47,"And they need money in their pocket to do it."],[99,764.23,5.31,"So it turns out America has seen a similar movie to this before,"],[99,769.53,2.2,"when we didn't plan for an economic transition,"],[99,771.77,2.1,"and by the way, it didn't end well."],[99,773.9,4.34,"It happened when American companies moved their manufacturing overseas,"],[99,778.27,3.27,"mostly to Asia, chasing cheaper labor,"],[99,781.54,2.7,"and millions of Americans lost their jobs."],[99,784.25,2.4,"And a lot of communities were crushed."],[99,787.45,1.17,"How do I know this?"],[99,788.65,1.3,"Because I lived through it."],[99,790.69,1.24,"In the early '80s,"],[99,791.92,3.04,"when the Bulova watch factory closed,"],[99,794.99,3.6,"my dad lost the only career he’d ever known"],[99,798.63,3.0,"after devoting almost 30 years of his life to the job."],[99,801.63,3.54,"One job, one company, one identity."],[99,805.17,1.17,"Gone."],[99,806.9,1.9,"Here's the thing, he was 56."],[99,808.84,1.3,"He still needed a job."],[99,810.14,1.57,"He still needed to work."],[99,811.74,1.84,"My dad was a smart guy."],[99,813.61,6.17,"He understood the economics of outsourcing to China,"],[99,819.78,3.24,"and he knew it would help some American businesses"],[99,823.05,2.44,"grow faster and add new jobs."],[99,826.42,3.9,"But he and all of his buddies still needed a bridge"],[99,830.33,1.74,"to another chapter of work."],[99,832.1,1.27,"And there wasn't one."],[99,834.26,3.07,"I mean, after a lot of pain and bitterness on his part,"],[99,837.37,1.6,"my family got through it."],[99,839.0,1.74,"But the truth of it is,"],[99,840.77,5.0,"my country paid a huge price for that poorly planned transition."],[99,845.81,2.0,"In fact, I argue, 30 years later,"],[99,847.84,3.64,"we're still paying a price for that poorly planned transition"],[99,851.51,4.97,"in the form of increasingly divisive, dysfunctional, violent politics."],[99,857.52,1.17,"Now --"],[99,862.99,1.37,"Think about this."],[99,865.13,2.07,"At that time,"],[99,867.2,6.21,"it was a few million Americans who lost their jobs."],[99,874.7,2.37,"Some say two million, some say three million."],[99,877.84,3.57,"Let's resolve right here, right now to do it differently this time."],[99,882.65,1.43,"Here's good news."],[99,885.48,2.74,"History also shows"],[99,888.25,2.44,"that when the stakes are high enough,"],[99,890.72,1.74,"America reinvents."],[99,892.82,1.3,"After World War II,"],[99,894.16,2.2,"record public investment and research"],[99,896.36,1.67,"seeded new industries."],[99,898.46,3.5,"COVID accelerated growth in clean energy,"],[99,902.0,1.47,"in health care."],[99,904.17,4.3,"AI is a 100-year technology and needs a 100-year response"],[99,908.5,4.67,"so that all Americans can reap the benefits of an AI economy."],[99,914.58,1.67,"Why am I optimistic?"],[99,916.81,1.67,"Because if we're a country"],[99,918.51,3.67,"that can design the best chips in the world,"],[99,922.22,2.4,"create the best models in the world"],[99,924.62,5.44,"and spend trillions of dollars to build out our AI infrastructure,"],[99,930.09,2.04,"then we're up to this challenge."],[99,932.66,1.6,"Because here's the reality."],[99,934.66,1.4,"And you know this."],[99,936.8,3.37,"Our future isn't predetermined."],[99,940.54,1.67,"It's ours to create."],[99,942.24,1.6,"It's why you're all here."],[99,943.87,1.67,"So let's get to work."],[99,945.58,1.14,"Thank you."],[100,3.97,1.67,"In the beginning,"],[100,5.67,4.67,"there were shapeless spirits roaming the universe."],[100,10.37,3.04,"And they went to the creator and said,"],[100,13.44,1.2,"\"We are lonely."],[100,14.64,1.37,"Please help us.\""],[100,16.68,1.37,"And the creator said,"],[100,18.08,3.14,"\"I will make you a place to be together,"],[100,21.25,1.77,"on one condition."],[100,23.35,1.7,"You must take care of it.\""],[100,26.69,1.8,"The spirits agreed."],[100,29.23,2.9,"And the creator made the Earth"],[100,32.16,2.8,"and sent the spirits down to the Earth"],[100,34.97,3.67,"to become land and water, plants,"],[100,38.67,2.1,"animals and humans,"],[100,40.8,3.47,"making everything on Earth alive."],[100,45.31,2.44,"The creator told my ancestors,"],[100,47.78,2.57,"\"You will never want for anything,"],[100,50.38,3.97,"so long as you live in a balance with the natural world."],[100,54.95,2.54,"Never take more than what you need."],[100,57.52,3.34,"Give thanks. And protect what gives you life.”"],[100,63.33,6.11,"That is a version of my nation, the Yurok tribe's, creation story."],[100,70.23,3.0,"It's not spiritual parable."],[100,73.27,2.1,"It's a set of instructions."],[100,75.37,2.04,"Simple, practical rules"],[100,77.44,3.77,"about how humans are meant to live on Earth."],[100,82.78,4.37,"I want to tell you a story of loss,"],[100,87.18,2.9,"resilience and victory,"],[100,90.12,2.47,"and what it means to remember."],[100,93.62,5.51,"Since time immemorial, my nation has lived by these instructions."],[100,100.13,1.67,"The lower Klamath River,"],[100,101.83,3.2,"in what is now known as Northern California,"],[100,105.07,2.27,"is our home and our lifeblood."],[100,107.91,2.04,"Under our stewardship,"],[100,109.97,3.94,"the Klamath salmon runs grew to be the third largest"],[100,113.91,2.24,"in all the continental US."],[100,116.65,4.04,"We had an abundant fishing way of life,"],[100,120.72,3.14,"caring for all that gave us life."],[100,124.52,2.77,"Then in the 1800s, everything changed."],[100,127.32,3.17,"Newcomers arrived, and they viewed our world"],[100,130.53,2.37,"through the lens of extraction:"],[100,132.93,3.07,"gold, timber, water, land and people."],[100,136.7,4.77,"The balance was disrupted, and suffering ensued in this place."],[100,142.11,1.57,"By 1910,"],[100,143.71,5.04,"over 90 percent of California's Indigenous population had been slaughtered."],[100,149.41,1.44,"It was genocide."],[100,152.02,1.4,"By the 2000s,"],[100,153.45,3.3,"the Klamath River, our ancient relative,"],[100,156.79,2.34,"had been transformed by dams,"],[100,159.16,3.27,"habitat loss, water diversions"],[100,162.43,2.54,"and warming polluted waters."],[100,165.73,2.24,"The dams, built without fish passage,"],[100,167.97,3.47,"blocked the salmon from accessing over 400 miles"],[100,171.47,2.17,"of historical spawning grounds."],[100,174.27,5.11,"Our salmon runs fell to five percent of their historical abundance."],[100,179.38,4.27,"The hydropower from the dams wasn't green either."],[100,184.08,3.27,"The reservoirs behind the dams emitted methane,"],[100,187.38,5.31,"the equivalent to 64,000 cars a year on the road."],[100,194.06,4.14,"In 2002, the legacy of colonization"],[100,198.23,2.3,"produced a horrific outcome."],[100,201.26,4.27,"Over 70,000 adult salmon died on the Klamath River"],[100,205.57,2.14,"within the Yurok Reservation,"],[100,207.74,2.94,"the largest salmon kill in US history."],[100,211.57,3.14,"I was working for tribal fisheries that summer,"],[100,214.75,2.47,"and the salmon died for two weeks."],[100,217.25,1.6,"By the end of it,"],[100,218.85,3.5,"their bodies lined the banks of the river,"],[100,222.39,3.2,"and the river looked and smelled like a war zone."],[100,227.46,5.57,"Standing in a Yurok tribal fisheries boat, I recalled a Yurok myth."],[100,234.0,4.04,"If the Klamath salmon die, so too will the Yurok people."],[100,238.07,4.44,"Because our purpose, our way of life depends on those salmon."],[100,243.27,1.9,"Was I witnessing the end?"],[100,246.74,1.37,"In that moment,"],[100,248.15,4.27,"I felt my great grandmother move through me like electricity."],[100,253.25,1.44,"I feel it now."],[100,255.05,3.14,"And she told me, \"It's your turn to fight.\""],[100,258.82,1.8,"And my response?"],[100,260.66,1.57,"I'll go to law school"],[100,262.26,3.67,"to prevent this kind of ecocide from ever happening again."],[100,266.6,2.94,"Now I did not know a lawyer,"],[100,269.57,2.87,"and I thought they were all rich and famous."],[100,273.47,1.47,"But that's what I did."],[100,274.94,2.8,"And I became my nation's general counsel,"],[100,277.77,3.2,"the first tribal member and woman to do so."],[100,281.01,1.17,"Thank you."],[100,284.48,5.24,"And then I cofounded, with Molly Myers, a real tough Karuk activist,"],[100,289.75,2.8,"the Ridges to Riffles Indigenous Conservation Group."],[100,292.59,3.97,"And we moved money and power to better serve the river."],[100,298.13,1.4,"And that was important."],[100,299.9,3.84,"The fish kill triggered our duties as Indigenous people"],[100,303.73,4.5,"to share the original instructions and work to renew the balance."],[100,309.01,4.44,"Indigenous peoples on the Klamath were the first to call for dam removal."],[100,313.81,1.97,"People thought it was impossible."],[100,316.15,3.67,"A dam removal of this scale had never been done before."],[100,320.38,1.8,"Powerful corporations,"],[100,322.22,4.77,"some of the richest corporations and industries were against us."],[100,327.02,4.74,"They said removing the dams would release toxic sediment"],[100,331.8,2.57,"and destroy the river for good."],[100,334.4,1.74,"The odds were against us."],[100,337.0,2.27,"But we had something they did not."],[100,340.1,2.2,"The original instructions,"],[100,342.31,3.5,"thousands of years of traditional knowledge,"],[100,345.84,1.53,"ancient legal rights,"],[100,347.41,4.87,"vested in the US Constitution as the supreme law"],[100,352.32,4.14,"and our duties and responsibilities to the river, its creatures"],[100,356.49,1.37,"and our people,"],[100,357.89,5.87,"making the Un-Dam the Klamath Movement an unstoppable Indigenous force."],[100,364.89,1.67,"Yeah. We protested."],[100,367.76,1.47,"There we go, guys, come on."],[100,371.1,2.1,"We protested."],[100,373.27,4.1,"We built coalitions between Indigenous nations,"],[100,377.37,2.47,"the Karuk, the Hoopa, the Klamath tribes,"],[100,379.88,4.2,"commercial fishermen, NGOs and states and farmers."],[100,384.81,1.44,"We litigated."],[100,386.28,2.67,"We asserted the tribes’ reserved rights"],[100,388.99,4.67,"and combined them with modern environmental power and energy law."],[100,394.49,3.04,"We won on almost every count."],[100,398.33,1.17,"Yeah."],[100,402.06,4.91,"And the power company was required to install fish ladders"],[100,407.0,2.67,"to continue to operate the dams."],[100,410.74,2.04,"Empathy overcame fear."],[100,414.04,2.17,"And perhaps for the first time,"],[100,416.25,3.94,"we shared the original instructions with the newcomers,"],[100,420.22,3.37,"and they accepted their shared responsibilities"],[100,423.59,2.07,"to work to renew the balance."],[100,426.36,2.6,"With our allies and our former enemies,"],[100,428.99,1.57,"we found a common interest."],[100,431.06,5.41,"It was cheaper to remove the dams than it was to install the fish ladders."],[100,436.9,1.44,"Yeah, right?"],[100,438.7,1.97,"Yeah, there's that common interest."],[100,441.87,5.57,"So we worked together to find a path forward,"],[100,447.48,2.47,"to restore the river and the balance."],[100,450.28,2.5,"And the outcome was historic."],[100,453.18,4.47,"A 500 million-dollar settlement agreement for dam removal"],[100,457.69,2.64,"that equally valued the rights of nature,"],[100,460.36,2.57,"business and Indigenous peoples."],[100,462.96,5.24,"And it produced the world's largest salmon restoration project"],[100,468.23,1.7,"in world history."],[100,470.37,1.17,"Yeah."],[100,471.54,2.2,"Feel that, like, oh my God."],[100,473.77,5.14,"And also the largest US dam removal project in history."],[100,480.04,3.34,"In 2023 and 2024,"],[100,483.41,3.54,"we removed four dams on the Klamath River,"],[100,486.98,4.91,"and restoration across thousands of acres"],[100,491.92,1.6,"has already begun"],[100,493.56,2.44,"and will continue through 2028."],[100,496.83,2.74,"I stood among tribal leaders"],[100,499.56,4.6,"when the first dam was breached through a series of explosions."],[100,504.2,2.97,"The explosion released a freedom wave,"],[100,507.2,3.44,"a surge of water into a stretch of river"],[100,510.64,3.07,"that had been dewatered for more than a century."],[100,516.38,3.37,"The water moved with such force and confidence,"],[100,519.78,2.87,"knowing exactly where to flow and how to go,"],[100,522.69,1.67,"and it seemed to declare,"],[100,524.39,1.77,"\"Do not call this a comeback,"],[100,526.16,2.77,"I have been here for years.\""],[100,532.7,3.07,"Later, at Iron Gate, the lowest dam,"],[100,535.83,4.71,"an excavator scooped some final dirt from the dam"],[100,540.57,3.77,"and the water began to go over the structure,"],[100,544.37,1.87,"first as a sparkle,"],[100,546.28,1.83,"then as a ribbon,"],[100,548.14,4.44,"and then the river reconnected with itself"],[100,552.58,2.84,"for the first time in 100 years,"],[100,555.45,3.0,"in a brilliant stream of prosperity."],[100,558.96,4.77,"Three days later, the salmon came home"],[100,563.86,4.87,"and went past historical spawning grounds not accessed in 100 years."],[100,569.53,1.7,"The salmon remembered."],[100,573.74,1.33,"They remembered."],[100,576.57,4.54,"Many did not think that the river could heal,"],[100,581.14,3.47,"but its recovery has been remarkably rapid."],[100,584.95,2.0,"A year after dam removal,"],[100,586.98,3.14,"the river is cleaner and colder and fiercer"],[100,590.15,1.67,"and smells sweeter"],[100,591.86,2.84,"than I have ever known it to be."],[100,595.23,3.1,"And the salmon, they are literally jumping for joy."],[100,598.36,3.4,"And if I wasn't wearing heels, I would jump for joy too."],[100,601.8,5.61,"The first year, about 7,000 salmon passed the former dam site."],[100,607.44,4.91,"In 2025, 13,000 salmon passed the former dam site"],[100,612.38,3.94,"and went further into their historical spawning grounds"],[100,616.31,1.9,"they'd been denied."],[100,618.95,3.24,"The many Indigenous peoples of the Klamath,"],[100,622.22,3.8,"whose relationship with salmon had been severed by the dams,"],[100,626.06,2.87,"are welcoming the salmon home"],[100,628.92,2.24,"and returning to their homelands"],[100,631.19,3.84,"in historic, trend-setting land-back efforts."],[100,635.57,4.34,"The Yurok tribe and many other tribes are employing tribal members"],[100,639.9,1.4,"and non-members,"],[100,641.3,4.3,"providing living-wage jobs, restoring land in the river."],[100,646.28,6.67,"These crews have planted -- get this -- 20 billion native seeds,"],[100,652.95,4.11,"bringing back -- yeah, yes, -- 20 billion native seeds."],[100,657.05,2.5,"And look at these poppies, aren't they gorgeous?"],[100,659.56,4.71,"These new ecosystems are bringing back plants and animals,"],[100,664.29,5.04,"foods and medicines that anchor Indigenous cultures into the future."],[100,670.13,3.3,"And these ecosystems have the potential"],[100,673.47,6.41,"to prevent 800,000 metric tons of CO2 a year over time."],[100,680.81,1.13,"Yeah."],[100,684.38,6.27,"So I know many feel that there is no hope"],[100,690.69,2.47,"for a future on planet Earth"],[100,693.16,4.07,"because the Earth is too out of balance, too sick."],[100,697.89,1.87,"And in that sense of urgency is real."],[100,700.8,1.83,"But on the Klamath,"],[100,702.67,6.17,"the river and all life it supports are remembering how to be in balance."],[100,712.14,4.57,"And this project has helped us remember an important truth."],[100,719.28,2.3,"We can heal nature,"],[100,721.59,3.57,"and when we work with it, it works with us."],[100,726.59,2.97,"And it starts with believing in yourself."],[100,730.53,4.4,"So I want you all to remember that what you do matters."],[100,735.26,3.27,"What you do with your life force makes a difference."],[100,738.57,4.17,"Treat Indigenous rights not as obstacles to progress"],[100,742.77,1.87,"but as critical to it."],[100,745.31,4.54,"Build mutually beneficial solutions to complex problems."],[100,750.51,4.77,"Value ecosystem restoration as a strategic priority"],[100,755.32,3.04,"to the most pressing problems of our time."],[100,759.69,2.6,"When we do that,"],[100,762.29,2.37,"balance is restored,"],[100,764.69,2.74,"life returns and so does hope."],[100,771.63,1.2,"Thank you."],[101,0.16,4.96,"What we eat and where it comes from is a critical part of human health. In fact,"],[101,5.12,5.44,"59% of Americans think it's a top factor to know where their food comes from. And"],[101,10.56,5.76,"54% prioritize food that is consistently available locally. And one of the best"],[101,16.32,5.36,"places to get that locally sourced food? Small family farms and farmers markets."],[101,21.68,3.04,"I love a good farmers market. And I'm not alone."],[101,24.72,6.24,"There are over 8,700 farmers markets in the US where roughly 40,000 farmers sell their products,"],[101,30.96,5.04,"and they seem to be getting a bit more attention thanks to a certain lettuce related issue."],[101,36.0,4.8,"Americans are growing weary of the rising number of recalls related to those stomach"],[101,40.8,6.4,"churning cyclospora and salmonella outbreaks. With only 55% of Americans feeling very or"],[101,47.2,4.64,"somewhat confident in the safety of the food supply, buying direct from your local farm"],[101,51.84,4.4,"could be the answer. But how does a small family farm business compete with major"],[101,56.24,7.44,"grocery stores for customers? And what's behind their sales pitch for safer and fresher food?"],[101,63.68,4.56,"I came out here to Camarillo, California to check out McGrath Family Farms. This"],[101,68.24,4.48,"farm has a pretty unique model. It's a collective of a few smaller farmers"],[101,72.72,3.92,"working land that's been a Southern California staple for generations."],[101,76.64,5.36,"Phil McGrath, who's my mentor, he's the fourth generation McGrath on this same land."],[101,82.0,4.4,"Him and his siblings, they started some of the first farmers markets in our area."],[101,86.4,4.32,"A group of us that were apprentices here, we're carrying on that legacy."],[101,90.72,6.96,"We are 35 acres of certified organic land. We grow a lot of different crops, over 20"],[101,97.68,5.68,"different varieties of produce and vegetables as well as over 20 different varieties of flowers."],[101,103.36,4.32,"McGrath Family Farms practices regenerative and organic farming,"],[101,107.68,2.32,"which means fewer chemicals and machinery."],[101,110.0,4.48,"But this more natural approach also means they're frequently adjusting to mother nature."],[101,114.48,4.32,"I always say farmers, especially smaller farmers or mid-size farmers, we're some of the"],[101,118.8,3.6,"best problem solvers around because we're dealing with problems constantly. You plant those seeds,"],[101,122.4,3.76,"you're investing all your upfront capital, right, in all this. And you're hoping those"],[101,126.16,3.28,"seeds come up, that the conditions are right for those seeds to germinate."],[101,129.44,5.44,"My whole family does work here. So my mom, my dad, my grandpa, my uncles,"],[101,134.88,6.72,"and then my siblings and I. Another 10 to 15 workers here at the farm that do an"],[101,141.6,6.56,"assortment of things whether it's packing, weeding, harvesting, washing the produce."],[101,148.16,5.36,"Growing crops organically, there's going to naturally be challenges. Weeding is one of"],[101,153.52,3.52,"the biggest costs of organic farming. We spend all our time weeding. Then you have"],[101,157.04,3.44,"pest pressure that comes in. You might have some kind of soil pathogen you got to figure"],[101,160.48,5.76,"out. And the second you harvest that crop, it needs to get to our end customer immediately,"],[101,166.24,3.76,"as quickly as possible, to have all that viability, to have all that nutrient density."],[101,170.0,2.72,"So, how do only a couple dozen people maintain"],[101,172.72,4.08,"food freshness and availability while keeping the business afloat?"],[101,176.8,3.68,"Everything is harvested for the markets the day before. Then,"],[101,180.48,3.6,"everybody packs up the produce and fans out to several farmers markets"],[101,184.08,4.96,"in Southern California each week, all of them about 50 miles or less from the farm."],[101,189.04,4.8,"Our whole family, especially on Sunday where when we have two farmers markets,"],[101,193.84,2.56,"everyone's away at a market."],[101,196.4,3.84,"That's a lot of ground to cover and the cycle restarts again every"],[101,200.24,3.28,"week. If there's any disruption, it can be costly."],[101,203.52,4.4,"We can't miss the market because we're expecting the money that's made throughout the week to"],[101,207.92,6.16,"be able to reinvest it into our seeds and our employees. I think for the most part,"],[101,214.08,4.96,"our pricing has been the same for the last like 10 years. People will always notice"],[101,219.04,4.4,"when the price goes up, but they tend to not notice when the price goes down."],[101,223.44,4.72,"Speaking of small businesses, this video is presented by Verizon Business. Verizon Small"],[101,228.16,4.8,"Business Days are happening now. If you're a small business owner, Verizon Business experts"],[101,232.96,4.4,"are ready to meet with you one-on-one. They'll act as extra support to give you the best tech,"],[101,237.36,4.08,"plans and give special offers to help your business succeed. Score a meeting"],[101,241.44,5.28,"with a Verizon Business expert during small business days from August 17th to August 30th."],[101,246.72,5.12,"Go to verizon.com/smallbusiness to learn more. Verizon Business has no say in the"],[101,251.84,4.96,"editorial content of this video, but their support makes videos like this possible."],[101,256.8,4.48,"Farmers markets are a main driver of this farm's business. But not just because that's"],[101,261.28,5.04,"where they see the most sales. It's also where they form direct relationships with customers,"],[101,266.32,3.44,"which means that farmers like Mike and Tony also have a pulse"],[101,269.76,3.52,"on their customers' anxieties when food safety issues happen."],[101,273.28,3.92,"When anything happens like this on the news, automatically customers are concerned,"],[101,277.2,4.32,"rightfully so. They want to now know, where does the food come from? People start to come"],[101,281.52,5.2,"more to the markets and buy stuff directly from the farm to prevent being affected by"],[101,286.72,4.08,"any sort of outbreak that's through the supply chain. We don't want the food to travel far."],[101,290.8,6.16,"It's alive. It needs to get enjoyed immediately. It'll be harvested today, washed and packed and"],[101,296.96,4.24,"then you go to market. These hands grew it. These hands washed it. And these hands brought it here."],[101,301.2,3.36,"An operation like Taylor Farms, the lettuce distributor linked to"],[101,304.56,6.48,"the cyclosporiasis outbreak, is massive by comparison. It has over 25,000 employees,"],[101,311.04,6.56,"30 processing facilities, and produces over 265 million servings of produce weekly that makes"],[101,317.6,4.88,"its way into chain restaurants, major grocery retailers, and big box stores."],[101,322.48,4.8,"And generally speaking, buying food from grocery stores is highly regulated and"],[101,327.28,4.4,"safe. But its supply chain does have more potential contamination points."],[101,331.68,4.24,"Some produce travels hundreds or thousands of miles from where it's grown to where"],[101,335.92,5.36,"it's sold, passing through processing, storage, and distribution facilities."],[101,341.28,4.32,"People come to the farmers markets and sometimes be like, the produce at the at"],[101,345.6,4.48,"the stores is a lot cheaper. But one thing that I try to explain to them,"],[101,350.08,4.64,"the produce in the grocery stores most of the times is coming from another country."],[101,354.72,4.88,"And also the produce that we're providing to them is most of the time just a day old."],[101,359.6,4.4,"The ultimate advantage of a small local farm is how direct the journey of their"],[101,364.0,4.56,"produce from the fields to their customers is. You're getting a more nutrient dense and"],[101,368.56,5.2,"flavor dense produce and more than anything it's going to last longer and be safer for"],[101,373.76,4.56,"you because it's being transferred from less hands and stuff. We grow the food in"],[101,378.32,3.92,"the greater Los Angeles area here and we bring it into the city in our, you know,"],[101,382.24,5.44,"local area as well here. So that direct feedback informs our growing decision. We'll have people"],[101,387.68,5.36,"that ask us grow beets or they'll ask us to grow like a specific variety of tomato. And"],[101,393.04,4.48,"we're always willing to try new things, too. It's a small community that supports each other. The"],[101,397.52,5.36,"farmers and our customers at the farmers market, we all come together to make this possible."],[101,402.88,3.92,"Even with thousands of farmers markets and farms across the country, there are still"],[101,406.8,5.84,"areas known as food deserts, places with limited access to affordable, nutritious food because of"],[101,412.64,5.24,"geography or economics. The farmers I met with had some advice for people who live there, too."],[101,417.88,1.88,"A national and a global food system,"],[101,419.76,4.32,"it's a miracle that humans created this. We were able to figure out how to grow food"],[101,424.08,2.96,"and ship it all over the world. Do your due diligence. When you get your produce home,"],[101,427.04,4.0,"you make sure you wash it. You you store it safely. We never take anything for granted."],[101,431.04,5.52,"And these small farmers are always looking for creative ways to reach more people with healthy,"],[101,436.56,2.8,"safe food. When we have a surplus of produce,"],[101,439.36,4.24,"especially after the farmers markets, we donate it to Food Forward and they give"],[101,443.6,4.96,"it to food banks. Where I spend a lot of my time today is in farm to school. Today we're"],[101,448.56,4.24,"in six school districts reaching around 60,000 students in just Ventura County."],[101,452.8,3.84,"So if you have a local farmers market and it's within your budget, consider"],[101,456.64,7.52,"buying a few fruits or veggies. Your local farmer and your taste buds will thank you."],[102,9.42,5.63,"After wreaking havoc across the heavens, pilfering a potion of immortality,"],[102,15.14,4.5,"and defeating the strongest warriors in the Jade Emperor’s court,"],[102,19.64,6.26,"the Monkey King had finally been captured in a white-hot prison cell."],[102,26.11,2.38,"But rather than cooking him to a crisp,"],[102,28.57,4.5,"the sweltering cage had inflamed its captive’s strength."],[102,33.36,4.38,"Now he was free once more and itching for a fight."],[102,38.08,5.63,"Desperate, the Jade Emperor called on the only being stronger than he,"],[102,43.79,5.25,"the supreme power in all the heavens: the Buddha himself."],[102,49.55,2.84,"Even in the face of this almighty opponent,"],[102,52.38,3.25,"Sun Wukong was not one to grovel."],[102,55.8,4.17,"He began boasting of his many talents and demanded the Emperor's crown."],[102,59.97,4.21,"Smiling, the Buddha declared he would grant that wish—"],[102,64.19,4.42,"if Sun Wukong could somersault out of his palm."],[102,68.65,5.0,"The Monkey King gladly pranced onto the Buddha’s hand and shot into the air,"],[102,73.65,2.0,"landing in a far-off region"],[102,75.66,4.63,"where five pink pillars seemed to mark the edge of the Heavens."],[102,80.53,5.09,"Cackling with glee, Sun Wukong proudly marked his territory."],[102,85.79,3.38,"Returning to the Buddha, he demanded his new title—"],[102,89.29,4.05,"but he was distracted by a familiar odor."],[102,93.55,4.5,"Looking back, he was shocked to find he'd never left the Buddha's hand."],[102,98.39,5.8,"Suddenly, those five fingers transformed into a mountain range,"],[102,104.18,3.88,"which flipped and pinned Sun Wukong to the ground."],[102,108.15,3.96,"Using a sacred seal, the Buddha secured the prison,"],[102,112.11,5.0,"trapping the Monkey King for good— unless he could learn to change."],[102,117.36,6.09,"Seeing the mystical tag, Sun Wukong knew he couldn’t escape unaided."],[102,123.62,4.3,"But it took centuries before he truly admitted defeat."],[102,128.46,5.51,"After 500 years of imprisonment, he'd grown increasingly desperate,"],[102,133.96,2.71,"finally vowing to mend his ways."],[102,136.84,5.34,"So when the Bodhisattva Guanyin happened by his prison on a sacred journey,"],[102,142.22,4.17,"he promised to pursue enlightenment if she would end his torment."],[102,146.52,5.09,"The goddess of compassion was skeptical of Sun Wukong's change of heart,"],[102,151.61,3.29,"but decided to give him an opportunity to prove himself."],[102,155.4,3.96,"At that time, the goddess was overseeing another sacred quest,"],[102,159.36,3.84,"pursued by a righteous monk named Tripitaka."],[102,163.58,3.42,"He had set out to retrieve scriptures from the West,"],[102,167.0,4.17,"but the path was too long and dangerous to travel alone."],[102,171.54,4.3,"So Guanyin promised to arrange Sun Wukong’s freedom"],[102,175.84,2.88,"if he would serve as a monk’s faithful disciple."],[102,179.22,3.29,"The Monkey King agreed without hesitation,"],[102,182.51,4.25,"and Guanyin directed Tripitaka to his mountain prison."],[102,187.72,5.0,"After centuries of waiting, Sun Wukong was finally free—"],[102,192.9,3.0,"but he knew he had to behave himself."],[102,196.36,3.25,"While the pair walked peacefully at first,"],[102,199.61,3.38,"they were soon accosted by fearsome bandits"],[102,202.99,3.38,"and Sun Wukong’s old habits bubbled up."],[102,206.7,4.67,"Tripitaka scolded the Monkey King, but this only enraged him further."],[102,211.71,3.84,"Being good was far more boring than he had imagined—"],[102,215.54,2.67,"so he swiftly abandoned his companion."],[102,218.55,4.13,"Unable to continue alone, Tripitaka sat dejected,"],[102,222.93,2.34,"until a strange woman approached him."],[102,225.43,5.3,"She gave him a sumptuous silk shirt and a cap hiding a band of gold"],[102,231.02,2.67,"alongside a mysterious spell."],[102,234.27,4.13,"It was only when she vanished that Tripitaka recognized her"],[102,238.53,1.71,"as his holy patron."],[102,240.65,4.59,"Meanwhile, Sun Wukong was having second thoughts."],[102,245.53,2.88,"He'd sought refuge in the Dragon King's lair,"],[102,248.41,2.67,"where he discussed his troubles over tea."],[102,251.21,4.8,"But the Dragon King only scoffed at his guest’s wounded pride."],[102,256.46,4.67,"He told Sun Wukong that if he truly wanted to attain enlightenment,"],[102,261.13,4.84,"he would have to abandon his ego— however painful that might be."],[102,266.47,2.67,"His determination renewed,"],[102,269.14,4.71,"Sun Wukong Kong returned to Tripitaka to apologize,"],[102,274.27,4.0,"but quickly spotted the luxurious shirt and cap."],[102,279.4,2.54,"They fit the Monkey King perfectly—"],[102,281.94,4.92,"prompting Tripitaka to recite the spell out of curiosity."],[102,287.7,4.8,"Suddenly, the golden band tightened around Sun Wukong’s head,"],[102,292.5,2.25,"causing explosive pain."],[102,295.67,4.3,"He became too weak to use his cudgel or somersault away."],[102,300.75,4.88,"Only now did Tripitaka recognize the spell’s power."],[102,305.72,3.63,"Until Sun Wukong could control himself,"],[102,309.47,4.3,"Guanyin had given him control of the Monkey King."],[102,315.56,4.46,"Both parties were uneasy about this new arrangement."],[102,321.11,3.04,"But they agreed it was for the good of their mission."],[102,325.32,5.05,"Finally, the Monkey King was ready for his greatest challenge yet:"],[102,330.7,2.67,"an epic journey to the West."],[102,333.66,2.63,"And perhaps, to redemption."],[103,0.08,4.0,"In the Smithsonian Museum of Natural History lies a mysterious object."],[103,4.08,2.96,"It’s drawn millions of visitors from across the world."],[103,7.04,2.48,"And not just for its beauty… but for its curse."],[103,9.52,1.36,"I’m talking about this:"],[103,10.88,1.2,"the Hope Diamond—"],[103,12.08,2.24,"a gem taken from India in the 1660s"],[103,14.32,3.92,"whose various owners are rumored to have mysteriously died from everything"],[103,18.24,2.0,"from wild dog attacks to beheadings!"],[103,20.24,1.28,"Shh, it’s okay, Dwayne."],[103,21.52,2.08,"The scary gem is safe in a museum."],[103,23.6,0.96,"For now…"],[103,24.56,2.16,"The Hope Diamond’s curse might be a myth."],[103,26.72,1.0,"But one thing’s for sure:"],[103,27.72,3.08,"it’s not the only myth out there about diamonds."],[103,30.8,3.12,"Hi, I’m Sage, and this is Crash Course Geology."],[103,38.48,0.64,"Diamonds."],[103,39.12,2.64,"They’re bright, flawless, and a girl’s best friend."],[103,41.76,1.44,"These aren’t real by the way."],[103,43.2,1.44,"What, do you think rocks are made of money?"],[103,44.64,2.48,"But to a geologist, diamonds are much more than a symbol"],[103,47.12,2.24,"of glamour, timelessness, and love."],[103,49.36,2.16,"And there are a lot of misconceptions about them."],[103,51.52,1.44,"Starting with their structure."],[103,52.96,4.08,"Diamond is a clear mineral made of one main ingredient: carbon."],[103,57.04,3.76,"Although, sometimes, small amounts of other elements can give them different colors."],[103,60.8,3.28,"Like, that deep blue in the Hope Diamond comes from boron."],[103,64.08,2.0,"While carbon itself is a pretty common element,"],[103,66.08,1.92,"diamonds do something special with it."],[103,68.0,2.48,"The carbon atoms are puzzled together super"],[103,70.48,3.52,"tightly into a crystal structure that’s nearly indestructible."],[103,74.0,3.68,"In fact, diamond is the hardest naturally occurring substance on Earth—"],[103,77.68,3.2,"so hard we use it to cut through concrete and stone."],[103,80.88,1.84,"But how do you get a structure like that?"],[103,82.72,1.84,"Spoiler alert: not from coal."],[103,84.56,1.36,"You’ve probably heard that myth before:"],[103,85.92,2.88,"that diamonds are made of a lump of coal and a lot of pressure."],[103,88.8,1.76,"A great, motivational metaphor…"],[103,90.56,2.72,"but not a scientifically accurate one."],[103,93.28,2.56,"Sure, coal is also made of carbon."],[103,95.84,5.52,"But the way diamonds actually form is way cooler than squishing a sooty rock."],[103,101.36,1.6,"Let’s dig a little deeper…"],[103,102.96,3.52,"For hundreds of years, humans didn’t know where diamonds came from."],[103,106.48,4.08,"We could only find them by searching riverbeds for washed-away sediment —"],[103,110.56,2.4,"also known as alluvial mining."],[103,112.96,3.6,"But there was no way of knowing what type of rock they came from."],[103,116.56,3.68,"Until 1870, when miners in South Africa struck gold."],[103,120.24,1.52,"Or, well, diamond!"],[103,121.76,2.8,"In the town of Kimberley, they found tons of diamonds."],[103,124.56,2.56,"Not in the riverbed, but in the ground."],[103,127.12,4.56,"It was the first time diamonds had ever been found in the volcanic rock they came from."],[103,131.68,4.24,"The discoverers named the source rock kimberlite, after the town, and started to study it."],[103,135.92,4.32,"And after a hundred-odd years, we finally figured out how those special stones got there."],[103,140.24,4.0,"It turns out diamonds form in cratons, the deep-down roots of continents."],[103,144.24,2.88,"They’re stable, hot, and pressurized enough to form carbon"],[103,147.12,3.2,"into that super dense crystal structure that diamonds are known for."],[103,150.32,4.24,"Most diamonds form in Earth’s upper mantle— the part right below the outer crust."],[103,154.56,3.92,"But some diamonds, like the Hope Diamond, form even deeper down,"],[103,158.48,6.0,"in the transition zone and lower mantle— more than 600 kilometers below the surface."],[103,164.48,2.64,"Maybe that’s why the Hope Diamond is allegedly cursed:"],[103,167.12,2.72,"it’s seen things no one was ever meant to see."],[103,169.84,2.72,"So how do they get to the surface? With a bang."],[103,172.56,2.8,"When continents break apart, they disrupt the mantle,"],[103,175.36,2.96,"leading to a millions-of-years-long domino effect."],[103,178.32,3.84,"Eventually, carbon dioxide and water trapped within deep pockets of magma"],[103,182.16,2.72,"explode into powerful kimberlite eruptions."],[103,184.88,4.56,"Molten rock shoots upward, blasting out carrot-shaped “pipes” through the subsurface."],[103,189.44,4.32,"And the violent flow rips up chunks of deep rock that— if you're lucky—"],[103,193.76,1.76,"are studded with diamonds."],[103,195.52,1.92,"As for super-deep diamonds like the Hope,"],[103,197.44,2.56,"they have to rise a bit higher in the mantle first,"],[103,200.0,5.2,"possibly by hitching a ride on extra-hot rock that flows from the lower mantle to the upper."],[103,205.2,2.84,"Then, kimberlite eruptions can blast them to the surface."],[103,208.08,4.24,"Most kimberlite eruptions happened between 250 and 50 million years ago."],[103,212.32,6.48,"And their diamonds started forming long before the blasts, up to 3 billion years ago."],[103,218.8,2.88,"Talk about classic rock."],[103,221.68,2.16,"That one was so bad it’s good."],[103,223.84,4.88,"Which helps us see diamonds for what they really are: time capsules of the past."],[103,228.72,2.8,"They have a lot to teach us about the super-deep earth,"],[103,231.52,2.88,"and its super-long geologic history."],[103,234.4,2.24,"Mineral and fluid inclusions trapped in diamond"],[103,236.64,3.52,"can give us clues about what the mantle was like back when they formed,"],[103,240.16,4.24,"which has helped geologists unravel all kinds of geologic mysteries."],[103,244.4,2.64,"Like, Dr. Kate Kiseeva has examined garnet"],[103,247.04,2.64,"inclusions in diamonds using advanced scientific tools"],[103,249.68,3.12,"to get more information about the composition of the deep mantle."],[103,252.8,4.08,"The last kimberlite eruption was 13 million years ago,"],[103,256.88,3.68,"and we don’t know if another will ever happen again."],[103,260.56,3.04,"So while diamonds continue to grow deep underground,"],[103,263.6,5.52,"we're left to mine the ones that ancient eruptions have already lofted into Earth's crust."],[103,269.12,3.92,"That may have given you the impression that diamonds are super rare;"],[103,273.04,2.64,"after all, we humans have prized them for centuries,"],[103,275.68,2.8,"and there’s only so many that are accessible to us."],[103,278.48,2.08,"But diamond’s rarity… is also a myth."],[103,280.56,3.44,"Largely perpetuated by a corporation."],[103,284.0,1.52,"We’ll get to that in a sec."],[103,285.52,4.24,"It’s true that we’ve valued diamonds for hundreds, if not thousands, of years."],[103,289.76,5.44,"And at first, they were scarce enough that only royalty and the extremely wealthy owned them."],[103,295.2,4.48,"Like, by 1668, the Hope Diamond had reached the hands of the French king,"],[103,299.68,1.2,"Louis the Fourteenth."],[103,300.88,2.72,"Who would later die of gangrene."],[103,303.6,4.24,"Finding the kimberlite diamonds in South Africa had huge effects."],[103,307.84,2.72,"For the next almost-century, the African continent"],[103,310.56,4.8,"produced 98 percent of all the diamonds in the world."],[103,315.36,4.16,"And with so many diamonds flooding the market, they weren’t just for royalty anymore."],[103,319.52,3.6,"That’s when British finance bro Cecil Rhodes saw an opportunity."],[103,323.12,1.92,"He bought shares in a bunch of diamond mines"],[103,325.04,4.64,"and eventually controlled enough of them to form the De Beers Mining Company."],[103,329.68,6.4,"By 1887, the company owned every diamond mine in South Africa."],[103,336.08,6.24,"For the next seven decades, it controlled just about every diamond mine in the world."],[103,342.32,4.96,"De Beers used its monopoly to keep up the illusion that diamonds were scarce,"],[103,347.28,3.6,"a perception that’s sold tons of overpriced diamonds."],[103,350.88,2.88,"By the 2000s, De Beers finally lost their monopoly,"],[103,353.76,3.2,"but the myth that diamonds are rare has stuck around."],[103,356.96,4.61,"Despite the fact that they’re more commonly found than lots of other gems."],[103,361.6,3.44,"Today, diamond value can be determined by the four Cs:"],[103,365.04,2.96,"color, clarity, cut, and carat weight –"],[103,368.0,4.88,"which tell us what diamond crystals look like, how they’re cut, and how big they are."],[103,372.88,4.48,"For example, the average diamond stud earring is between 0.25 and 1 carat,"],[103,377.36,2.56,"which can roughly range from a few hundred to"],[103,379.92,2.32,"a few thousand dollars based on the type of diamond."],[103,382.24,6.48,"In comparison, the Hope Diamond is a whopping 45.52 carats."],[103,388.72,5.52,"Which is why it’s valued at up to 350 million dollars."],[103,394.24,4.08,"But those ordinary diamond studs might not be made of natural diamonds at all."],[103,398.32,1.92,"Which brings me to our last myth."],[103,400.24,5.44,"While many may think that lab-grown diamonds are “fake,” they are, in fact, very much real."],[103,405.68,3.44,"See, in the 1940s, scientists at General Electric started working on a way"],[103,409.12,5.12,"to skip the whole billion-year mantle-to-mine timeline, and grow diamonds in the lab instead."],[103,414.24,2.48,"The effort was called “Project Superpressure.”"],[103,416.72,3.28,"Coincidentally, that’s also what I called my all-night study sessions in college."],[103,420.0,1.6,"The scientists put some graphite –"],[103,421.6,4.88,"which is also made of carbon – under super intense heat and pressure."],[103,426.48,5.2,"And in just a few weeks, they had made their own diamond."],[103,431.68,4.56,"In fact, it sort of worked the same way many people think coal turns into diamonds."],[103,436.24,2.24,"No, Dwayne, you’re granite."],[103,438.48,1.92,"You can’t turn into a diamond."],[103,440.4,2.32,"No, even if I press on you really hard."],[103,442.72,2.24,"Within a couple decades, lab-grown diamonds"],[103,444.96,3.44,"were just as hard, sparkly, and impressive as the originals."],[103,448.4,4.32,"Modern processes use tiny fragments of natural diamonds to seed new, lab-grown ones,"],[103,452.72,2.72,"and work way more efficiently than the old techniques."],[103,455.44,4.96,"Lab-grown diamonds are real diamonds— they just have a slightly different origin story."],[103,460.4,2.96,"Today, lab-grown diamonds are more popular than ever."],[103,463.36,5.12,"For one, they are way cheaper than natural diamonds, costing up to 85 percent less than"],[103,468.48,1.09,"their counterparts."],[103,469.6,7.44,"Plus, many people, myself very much included, consider lab-grown diamonds a much more ethical purchase."],[103,477.04,2.96,"Diamond mines severely damage the environment."],[103,480.0,4.8,"The Big Hole mine in Kimberley can literally be seen from space"],[103,484.8,5.44,"and contributes to all sorts of environmental problems, from erosion to water pollution."],[103,490.24,2.24,"And importantly, conditions in diamond mines"],[103,492.48,3.12,"can be terribly dangerous for the miners themselves."],[103,495.6,3.2,"That’s especially the case in places with less social stability,"],[103,498.8,1.84,"like parts of central and west Africa,"],[103,500.64,5.12,"where regional conflicts cut communities off from their social and resource networks."],[103,505.76,2.8,"In those places, small-scale mining operations"],[103,508.56,4.96,"often use forced and child labor and sell diamonds to fund violent conflicts."],[103,513.52,3.36,"We call those blood diamonds."],[103,516.88,4.16,"Even though international organizations have tried to crack down on them,"],[103,521.04,2.56,"they’re still out there in the diamond market."],[103,523.6,4.16,"Now, that’s a deeply complex topic, so check out the"],[103,527.76,2.0,"links below to learn more."],[103,529.76,2.64,"Some folks still make a case for natural diamonds,"],[103,532.4,1.92,"saying that diamond mining can be good for"],[103,534.32,2.8,"local economies in some of the world’s poorest countries,"],[103,537.12,3.12,"and that it’s less environmentally harmful than other kinds of mining."],[103,540.24,2.8,"So, yeah — there are lots of cultural, political,"],[103,543.04,4.16,"and environmental factors at play when choosing a lab-grown or a natural diamond."],[103,547.2,1.84,"But geologically speaking?"],[103,549.04,1.17,"They’re one and the same."],[103,550.24,3.28,"Whether grown in a lab or blasted from the earth’s mantle,"],[103,553.52,2.0,"diamonds are a part of our world."],[103,555.52,2.4,"And they’re not just nice to look at."],[103,557.92,4.24,"They’re remnants of volcanic eruptions that happened millions of years ago."],[103,562.16,4.16,"They unravel mysteries about deep Earth and deep time."],[103,566.32,3.6,"They’re so hard, they beat rock in rock-paper-scissors every time."],[103,569.92,2.64,"And maybe… they’re cursed."],[103,572.56,4.08,"At least the Hope Diamond. AACK! Dwayne!"],[103,576.64,4.16,"Next time we’ll talk about another force to be reckoned with: water."],[103,580.8,0.88,"See you then."],[103,581.68,2.24,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Geology"],[103,583.92,2.48,"which was filmed at our studio in Indianapolis, Indiana"],[103,586.4,3.44,"and was made with the help of these glittering, glorious people."],[103,589.84,3.28,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[103,593.12,2.12,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[104,6.96,3.96,"In the 1970s, pediatrician Herbert Needleman and colleagues"],[104,10.92,3.17,"collected and analyzed the baby teeth of schoolchildren"],[104,14.09,3.59,"across Boston and Philadelphia in return for small rewards."],[104,17.85,4.09,"But their so-called “Tooth Fairy Project” confirmed a dark reality:"],[104,22.31,2.59,"many kids had been exposed to lead."],[104,25.9,3.84,"Lead is a metallic element that's distributed across Earth's crust."],[104,29.82,1.63,"When it enters the human body,"],[104,31.49,3.92,"it can disrupt many critical processes that span various systems,"],[104,35.62,1.96,"producing a diverse set of symptoms."],[104,37.95,3.21,"This is because the body tends to falsely recognize lead"],[104,41.33,4.67,"as similarly charged metallic elements like calcium, iron, and zinc,"],[104,46.38,2.75,"and use it in their place for essential reactions,"],[104,49.13,1.71,"wreaking whole-body havoc."],[104,51.21,3.5,"For example, lead can disrupt the synthesis of hemoglobin,"],[104,54.72,4.0,"an essential protein to the body’s oxygen-transporting red blood cells."],[104,58.72,3.34,"And it can compete with iron to be absorbed in the intestines."],[104,62.06,2.84,"Both things can lead to anemia and fatigue."],[104,65.48,2.5,"Lead can also cross the blood-brain barrier,"],[104,68.19,3.88,"damaging neurons and interfering with the activity of neurotransmitters"],[104,72.07,1.92,"like serotonin and dopamine."],[104,74.28,2.84,"This can spur cognitive and behavioral changes,"],[104,77.11,2.71,"including hyperactivity and aggression."],[104,79.83,2.58,"And because of negative effects in other areas,"],[104,82.41,3.92,"lead exposure can also cause: high blood pressure, headaches,"],[104,86.46,4.25,"pregnancy complications, and abdominal, joint, and muscle pain."],[104,91.21,3.75,"Mimicking calcium, lead may also get stored in teeth and bone,"],[104,95.13,3.09,"then released back into the bloodstream later in life."],[104,98.39,3.21,"And because children’s bodies are smaller and still developing,"],[104,101.6,1.58,"they are especially sensitive."],[104,103.39,2.92,"Lead exposure can lead to developmental delays,"],[104,106.44,2.13,"cognitive and behavioral issues,"],[104,108.56,4.42,"and at high enough concentrations, seizures, coma, and death."],[104,113.36,2.75,"No level of lead exposure is considered safe,"],[104,116.11,1.88,"no matter how small the amount."],[104,118.95,3.04,"So, lead is obviously a serious health concern—"],[104,122.12,2.46,"and people actually made that link early on."],[104,125.0,1.63,"Circa 20 BCE,"],[104,126.62,4.17,"Roman architect Vitruvius noted lead workers’ pallor and health issues,"],[104,130.79,2.46,"And one 18th century Italian physician"],[104,133.25,4.3,"similarly attributed the frailty and abdominal pains of potters to lead."],[104,138.18,2.46,"Yet, despite the long-known health risks,"],[104,140.8,3.75,"lead became incredibly popular during the 19th and 20th centuries."],[104,144.89,2.71,"Its malleability made it appealing for molding into things"],[104,147.6,2.29,"like pipes, coins, and pans."],[104,150.44,4.17,"And its opacity meant it was an effective pigment in paints and cosmetics."],[104,155.03,4.34,"Sounds pretty great— if you were to overlook or obscure lead’s toxicity—"],[104,159.57,3.67,"and unfortunately, certain people were set on doing just that."],[104,163.78,3.92,"By the early 20th century, there were widespread reports of brain damage,"],[104,167.71,3.96,"convulsions, and death among children who inhaled or ingested lead,"],[104,171.96,1.46,"most often from paint."],[104,174.25,4.17,"In 1921, the League of Nations passed lead paint restrictions,"],[104,178.42,2.04,"which were enacted in many countries."],[104,180.8,2.92,"But the US never joined the League of Nations."],[104,183.93,4.09,"And the American led industry aggressively promoted lead products as safe"],[104,188.02,2.46,"and advocated for leaded gasoline."],[104,190.94,1.42,"Through the 1960s,"],[104,192.35,3.25,"activist groups like the Young Lords and Black Panthers"],[104,195.61,1.83,"brought attention to lead poisoning."],[104,197.74,1.79,"The lead industry, in turn,"],[104,199.53,3.09,"attempted to blame the issue on lower-income parents."],[104,202.74,1.08,"Their claim?"],[104,203.82,2.96,"That the parents didn’t prevent their kids from crawling around"],[104,206.79,3.75,"and putting their hands in their mouths— typical behavior among children."],[104,211.21,2.88,"But evidence about lead’s harms were accumulating."],[104,214.21,2.25,"When Needleman’s team compared the lead levels"],[104,216.46,1.71,"in the baby teeth they’d collected,"],[104,218.17,2.42,"they realized the children with the highest amounts"],[104,220.59,3.29,"performed worse on cognitive and behavioral assessments—"],[104,223.93,3.59,"findings the lead industry attempted to undermine and obfuscate."],[104,227.89,4.09,"Gradually, the US introduced laws against lead paint in the 1970s,"],[104,232.19,1.67,"lead water pipes in the 80s,"],[104,233.98,3.09,"and leaded gasoline in on-road vehicles in the 90s."],[104,237.32,4.5,"But none of this undid the damage of decades of aggressive lead implementation,"],[104,241.82,4.8,"and the process of removing lead is dangerous, time-consuming, and costly."],[104,247.33,6.01,"As of 2024, lead paint still coats walls and contaminates soil and water worldwide."],[104,253.46,4.3,"Tens of millions of people in the US alone drink water from lead pipes."],[104,257.88,2.79,"The lead industry made billions following same denial"],[104,260.67,3.84,"and disinformation playbook the oil and tobacco industries used,"],[104,264.76,2.88,"sometimes even relying on the same consulting firm."],[104,268.22,2.38,"But there have been some steps towards justice."],[104,270.73,4.42,"In California in 2019, lead manufacturers were ordered to finance"],[104,275.15,3.25,"over $300-million-worth of lead paint replacement—"],[104,278.57,3.88,"a glint of levity in a history laden with industrial greed."],[105,8.3,4.34,"Prince Meleager and his band of warriors crept towards the den"],[105,12.63,2.75,"of the terrifying beast they sought to slay."],[105,15.72,2.42,"But unbeknownst to the prince and his followers,"],[105,18.14,3.13,"a dark secret loomed over his life..."],[105,21.73,4.46,"The night of Meleager’s birth, his mother Althaea, the Queen of Calydon,"],[105,26.19,4.3,"was visited by three shadowy goddesses, known as the Fates."],[105,30.86,4.0,"Clotho, Lachesis, and Atropos rarely appeared to mortals"],[105,34.86,3.21,"but wielded untold power over their lives."],[105,38.37,2.67,"Clotho spun a thread at the birth of new life,"],[105,41.16,4.21,"Lachesis determined its length, and Atropos cut it with her shears,"],[105,45.58,1.88,"marking the moment of death."],[105,48.09,2.17,"When the Fates visited Althaea that night,"],[105,50.25,2.34,"they gestured towards the blazing hearth"],[105,52.59,3.54,"and decreed that Meleager would live only until the burning log"],[105,56.13,1.67,"was reduced to embers."],[105,57.93,1.92,"Althaea rushed to quench the fire,"],[105,59.85,2.25,"but before she could beg for more information,"],[105,62.1,1.88,"the Fates had vanished."],[105,64.19,1.58,"She locked the log in a chest"],[105,65.77,3.13,"and took comfort in guarding the key to her son’s safety,"],[105,69.02,4.92,"as he grew into a talented young man and a daring, hot-tempered warrior."],[105,74.61,3.46,"Meleager traveled far and accomplished impressive feats,"],[105,78.07,3.75,"until soon, a formidable opponent arrived at his doorstep."],[105,82.2,2.13,"During that year's annual sacrifices,"],[105,84.33,3.29,"Meleager's father neglected to leave an offering to Artemis,"],[105,87.62,1.5,"goddess of the hunt."],[105,89.38,4.55,"So, the gods sent their punishment in the form of a boar that tore through Calydon,"],[105,94.01,4.96,"hide bristling with razor-sharp hairs, spear-like tusks gleaming,"],[105,99.09,2.0,"and lightning leaping from its mouth."],[105,101.18,3.84,"It had decimated the area’s fields, herds, and people."],[105,105.27,2.67,"Since there was no hope of defeating the boar alone,"],[105,107.98,3.75,"Althaea’s beloved brothers agreed to accompany their nephew on the hunt."],[105,112.32,3.5,"Meleager hastily recruited Greece’s best warriors to join them,"],[105,116.07,2.84,"including Castor, revered for his horsemanship,"],[105,118.91,3.42,"as well as his brother, Pollux, a fearsome fist-fighter."],[105,122.53,2.29,"Meleager also called on Acastus,"],[105,124.91,2.84,"who hurtled javelins with incomparable strength;"],[105,127.87,5.55,"Caeneus a hero with impenetrable skill; and Amphiaraus, a revered diviner."],[105,133.75,3.29,"But perhaps most skilled of them all was Atalanta,"],[105,137.13,2.67,"the famed warrior of the Arcadian woods."],[105,140.14,4.05,"Because her father wanted a male heir, he abandoned her in the wilderness."],[105,144.39,3.34,"Suckled by a bear and raised by roaming hunters,"],[105,147.85,5.42,"Atalanta became a fierce wrestler, swift runner, and lethal hunter."],[105,153.52,3.54,"Though some of Meleager's men objected to fighting alongside a woman,"],[105,157.28,2.13,"he assured them of Atalanta’s strength,"],[105,159.49,3.13,"and led his small army into the ravaged countryside"],[105,162.82,2.34,"in search of the beastly boar."],[105,165.66,2.38,"Althaea waved farewell anxiously,"],[105,168.16,3.54,"but reassured herself that her son could come to no grievous harm"],[105,171.71,3.75,"so long as she guarded the log the Fates had linked to his life."],[105,175.84,3.46,"Hunting hounds led the party towards the boar’s forest lair,"],[105,179.38,2.34,"where they cast large nets and lobbed weapons"],[105,181.72,2.08,"into the dark of the monster’s den."],[105,184.14,1.75,"Suddenly charging from its cave,"],[105,185.89,2.59,"the boar flattened trees and hunters in its path,"],[105,188.73,4.13,"then sharpened its tusks on the fallen oak to gore more of its enemies."],[105,193.52,3.46,"Atalanta pinpointed a vulnerable spot behind the beast’s ears"],[105,196.98,3.42,"and let fly one perfectly aimed arrow."],[105,200.91,4.05,"With the beast weakened, Amphiaraus lodged another arrow in its eye,"],[105,205.33,2.5,"leaving Meleager to deal the final blow."],[105,208.79,3.17,"Meleager praised Atalanta’s first, decisive hit,"],[105,211.96,2.75,"and awarded her the boar’s hide and head."],[105,214.71,4.46,"But his uncles were outraged that he should bestow all the glory upon a woman,"],[105,219.42,2.04,"so they snatched her prize."],[105,221.84,3.5,"Meleager’s temper flared back, and before his mind cleared,"],[105,225.35,2.88,"the consequence of a bitter battle lay before him:"],[105,228.47,2.84,"he had killed his uncles in his fit of rage."],[105,231.85,3.71,"Keeping watch at the palace, Althaea rejoiced at the hunters’ return,"],[105,235.56,2.84,"but the news of her brothers’ deaths at the hands of her son"],[105,238.4,3.04,"quickly transformed her relief into grief—"],[105,241.78,2.59,"and then unabated rage."],[105,244.74,2.5,"Althaea tore from the crowd to her quarters."],[105,247.24,2.13,"Ears ringing and hands shaking,"],[105,249.37,3.17,"her mind a frenzied blur of betrayal and resentment,"],[105,252.87,3.46,"Althaea fumbled for the hidden chest, shook its lock free,"],[105,256.33,2.34,"and pitched the log into the flames."],[105,258.96,3.63,"All at once, Meleager was engulfed by a strange agony"],[105,262.63,2.71,"that would haunt his last, fated moments—"],[105,265.47,3.21,"a destiny branded into his and his mother’s lives,"],[105,268.76,3.71,"though neither could have foreseen the wicked turn it would take."],[106,0.0,3.46,"Racehorses are majestic, beautiful to watch."],[106,3.54,3.71,"But the sport of horse racing has always been dangerous."],[106,7.26,3.25,"11 horses died from racing injuries at Churchill Downs"],[106,10.51,3.54,"in 2025, up from six the previous year."],[106,14.26,4.3,"Another tragedy as Santa Anita prepares to close its controversial season"],[106,18.56,3.42,"of racing, the 30th fatality since the end of December."],[106,22.11,3.0,"Championship horse can be worth millions of dollars."],[106,25.15,1.5,"And jockeys are expected"],[106,26.65,4.05,"to control these expensive animals that are up to ten times their weight,"],[106,30.7,5.3,"while traveling over 40mph. Making it a very high stakes sport."],[106,36.04,1.92,"The racing industry has been looking for ways"],[106,37.95,3.42,"to make the sport safer for the horses and the humans who ride them."],[106,41.62,2.75,"They’re 1,000 pounds of unpredictable nature."],[106,44.38,2.96,"So inherently that's dangerous."],[106,47.34,3.38,"Our fatality rate is obviously very low, but our goal is zero."],[106,50.72,4.0,"New developments in wearable tech for horses could help trainers, vets"],[106,54.72,3.84,"and jockeys prevent injuries before they become catastrophic."],[106,58.68,3.0,"Horse racing is unusual because the central athletic performance"],[106,61.69,3.46,"is actually a partnership between two very different athletes."],[106,65.15,3.63,"So I came here to the historic Belmont Racetrack to find out"],[106,68.78,3.09,"more about how you train these incredible athletes."],[106,71.86,4.71,"And how to keep both of them fit enough and healthy enough to race safely."],[106,78.87,0.63,"It turns out"],[106,79.5,3.21,"horses train kind of like how cross country runners train."],[106,82.71,3.42,"So every morning, every horse has a different plan for the day"],[106,86.17,4.13,"set up by the trainer that may range from galloping to jogging."],[106,90.3,1.67,"Some horses will walk"],[106,91.97,2.59,"if it's supposed to be an easy day in their training schedule."],[106,94.55,5.21,"Every horse has surface preference, a distance preference."],[106,99.77,3.34,"The trainer's job is to figure out where that horse belongs"],[106,103.1,1.67,"and then also start trying things out."],[106,104.77,3.96,"They can tell from morning exercise how that horse is moving, and maybe"],[106,108.73,3.92,"that gives them some clues that that horse maybe will do better on turf or grass."],[106,112.65,3.88,"And training is also important as a way to spot possible injuries."],[106,116.53,3.92,"Unlike human athletes, a horse can't tell a trainer that their ankle hurts."],[106,120.45,3.38,"And subtle changes can be hard to spot with the naked eye."],[106,123.92,2.13,"As the veterinarian, you know, I'll watch."],[106,126.04,1.54,"Come up here and watch training,"],[106,127.58,2.71,"or someone will have me watch their horse train."],[106,130.3,3.67,"Some things are really subtle, which is why we've gone to the wearables"],[106,133.97,3.34,"or are trying to incorporate more wearable use."],[106,137.3,3.38,"Wearable technology would just potentially help us"],[106,140.68,3.0,"identify things before the human eye can see them."],[106,143.69,3.34,"Rather than waiting for an injury to become obvious, trainers"],[106,147.02,3.67,"and vets could identify a horse whose movement is beginning to change."],[106,150.82,1.13,"Here's how it works."],[106,151.94,3.88,"Wearable sensors like these made by Garmin or StrideSAFE"],[106,155.82,4.17,"identify a horse's running fingerprint or what its healthy gait looks like."],[106,160.08,3.04,"Then, if that changes at all, trainers know to take a look"],[106,163.2,3.09,"to see if it's running differently because of a minor injury."],[106,166.29,3.5,"Theoretically, the more often it wears it, the finer that fingerprint gets,"],[106,170.17,3.46,"the more adept that technology will be at detecting difference."],[106,173.63,2.75,"And all that does is tell the team around the horse, maybe"],[106,176.38,1.79,"look a little deeper into this horse."],[106,178.18,4.13,"We can detect it an earlier level before we ever get to the fatality edge."],[106,182.31,3.09,"Research shows that catastrophic fractures often start"],[106,185.39,3.0,"as tiny micro fractures that build up over time."],[106,188.6,3.59,"More importantly, on a daily basis, right, We could prevent"],[106,192.23,1.88,"a more significant injury."],[106,194.11,4.34,"And then the horse could have a better prognosis for returning"],[106,198.49,5.13,"to its regular level of performance or staying in its career a little longer."],[106,203.91,2.96,"Taking care of the horse also means taking care of the jockey."],[106,206.87,3.54,"What looks like one athlete carrying a passenger is actually two"],[106,210.42,4.46,"highly specialized athletes whose success and safety is intertwined."],[106,214.88,6.55,"So their heart rate's hit anywhere from about 170 to 190 beats per minute."],[106,221.43,4.05,"Usually about 95% of their max heart rate is what we'll see them hit"],[106,225.47,1.42,"towards the end of a race."],[106,226.89,6.17,"I always compare race riding to any HIIT or high interval intensity training,"],[106,233.07,3.79,"because they're only on the horse doing this for about two minutes"],[106,236.9,2.92,"at a time, and they're progressively getting faster and faster."],[106,239.82,3.67,"And the other big part of jockey vitness has to do with that distinctive"],[106,243.53,1.88,"crouch on top of the horse."],[106,245.41,4.34,"Jockeys use what's called a “martini glass position”,"],[106,249.87,5.13,"and in this position, if you look at it their back is fairly flat."],[106,255.05,2.25,"This is for aerodynamic purposes."],[106,257.3,6.55,"Once it was adopted, we saw a race times get much faster by about 5-7%,"],[106,263.85,5.76,"which is a big difference to see in just one small change of moving the jockey"],[106,269.64,1.63,"directly off the horse's back"],[106,271.27,3.88,"and allowing the jockey to move more fluidly with the horse."],[106,275.15,1.46,"In order to hold that position,"],[106,276.61,3.21,"jockeys have to have incredible leg and core strength."],[106,279.82,4.88,"I mean, they literally have their big toe and maybe their second and middle"],[106,284.74,3.09,"toe on that stirrup, and that's all that's actually on the stirrup."],[106,287.83,1.83,"And that's for safety protocol."],[106,289.66,3.5,"They don't want their foot to slip through the stirrup and get dragged."],[106,293.17,2.34,"During a race there is no stopping."],[106,295.5,1.33,"Those lactic acids are building."],[106,296.84,2.88,"You're in that crouch position, you're in a squat position."],[106,299.72,4.38,"There is going to be a point where you might even be cramping during a race."],[106,304.22,1.63,"Can you mentally get through it?"],[106,305.85,3.09,"Is your body fit enough and trained enough to get through it?"],[106,308.93,2.79,"And that's what separates the good jockeys from the greats."],[106,312.48,1.84,"Billions of bacteria live in a person's mouth."],[106,314.31,1.96,"Maybe more than there are birds on Earth."],[106,316.27,1.21,"Some are helpful."],[106,317.48,0.71,"Others."],[106,318.19,3.59,"Many are behind oral health problems like cavities and gingivitis."],[106,321.82,2.75,"So how do you manage something you can't actually see?"],[106,324.57,3.5,"It starts with a solid routine and the right ingredients for the job."],[106,328.12,1.83,"Take Stannous fluoride, for example."],[106,329.95,2.88,"It helps target and kill plaque bacteria."],[106,332.83,3.84,"Plaque can harden into tartar and plaque bacteria can cause cavities"],[106,336.67,1.04,"and gingivitis."],[106,337.71,3.75,"That's why Colgate Total developed a unique stabilization technology"],[106,341.59,1.08,"to keep the bacteria"],[106,342.68,3.17,"fighting Stannous fluoride stable in your toothpaste for longer."],[106,345.97,3.04,"The benefits go even further when you pair it with the Colgate"],[106,349.06,1.75,"Total Toothbrush and Mouthwash."],[106,350.81,3.21,"The toothbrush is designed with ultra fine bristles to reach"],[106,354.02,3.34,"deep along the gun line and between teeth, where plaque likes to hide."],[106,357.4,1.42,"And when you're not brushing."],[106,358.82,2.34,"The antibacterial mouthwash extends protection."],[106,361.15,3.67,"Together, they form the Colgate Total Active Prevention System,"],[106,364.82,4.17,"designed to prevent oral health problems before they start."],[106,371.91,2.08,"Jockeys face an unusual problem."],[106,374.0,3.5,"They need to become stronger without becoming much heavier."],[106,377.67,5.05,"Jockeys have to maintain a very low weight of about 112"],[106,382.71,3.25,"to 118 pounds is kind of what we see."],[106,385.97,2.5,"That's because they have to weigh in for every race."],[106,388.47,3.79,"In order to make a low weight, jockeys will often dehydrate themselves"],[106,392.27,1.0,"before a race."],[106,393.27,0.96,"Boxers and wrestlers,"],[106,394.23,3.67,"you know, you can you can lose that weight and dehydrate yourself."],[106,397.94,0.92,"You get on the scale,"],[106,398.86,1.75,"you make your weight in front of everybody,"],[106,400.61,3.09,"but then you go back to the room before you go out and compete."],[106,403.74,3.21,"You know, you drink electrolytes, water, whatever it is, you might balloon,"],[106,406.95,2.21,"you might gain 5 pounds before you compete."],[106,409.16,1.54,"That's not how it works for jockeys."],[106,410.7,5.42,"You might, you know, go from 118 pounds to 115 pounds, 14 pounds"],[106,416.33,3.88,"And then, you can't drink water, you know, because you got more horses to ride."],[106,420.29,4.05,"And that kind of dehydration can be dangerous for the jockey and the horse."],[106,424.42,3.84,"When you're dehydrated, I mean your reaction time slows down,"],[106,428.59,3.21,"your mental fatigue increases."],[106,431.81,1.75,"There's a guy behind you, in front of you,"],[106,433.56,3.84,"next to your right, to your left, you know, so you have to stay in your lane"],[106,437.39,4.05,"and they might get out of their lane and your horse is a live animal."],[106,441.44,3.17,"So that horse might react to another horse or another jockey."],[106,444.78,3.46,"If you have a horse that goes down or bumps another horse at the"],[106,448.99,4.17,"front of the pack, that can cause issues all the way back to the last horse, right?"],[106,453.16,2.67,"They’re having to dodge whatever is going on in front of them."],[106,455.83,1.25,"All of which underscores"],[106,457.08,3.38,"the importance of training to protect the jockey's mind and body."],[106,460.88,2.29,"And if you're tired and"],[106,464.42,3.04,"you don't control your horse, your horse might go into the other horse."],[106,467.51,2.92,"You might get disqualified, you might cause an accident."],[106,470.43,3.0,"Which is why all that cardio and strength training matters."],[106,473.43,1.58,"As a jockey gets more tired,"],[106,475.02,3.5,"they have a harder time maintaining that stable, coordinated crouch."],[106,478.52,3.38,"If you're out of rhythm with your horse, you can your horse can get hurt."],[106,481.9,3.59,"You know, because you're moving one way, they're moving the other way."],[106,485.49,1.08,"You could fall."],[106,486.57,4.67,"So optimizing for the health of the horses and the jockeys helps minimize"],[106,491.24,3.0,"the most fatal thing that can happen: a fall."],[106,494.54,2.75,"A majority of jockey injuries are from falls."],[106,497.29,4.46,"They're traveling at speeds up to 40mph with just their helmet"],[106,501.75,2.04,"and a foam body protector."],[106,503.79,4.59,"Obviously if the horse is moving well then we can avoid that horse"],[106,508.38,4.34,"potentially failing on the racetrack and causing injury as well."],[106,512.72,5.21,"But if we can keep them sounder and prevent injury,"],[106,517.93,4.55,"then we can also indirectly help the people that are on their backs."],[106,522.48,3.84,"Using modern data collection and sports medicine, we're able to better"],[106,526.32,3.17,"understand the movement of both the horse and the rider."],[106,529.49,3.84,"Horse racing will always be dangerous, and it's reasonable to ask"],[106,533.32,1.75,"whether the risks are worth it."],[106,535.08,1.71,"But trainers, vets and jockeys"],[106,536.79,3.79,"are all working together to try and make the sport as safe as possible."],[106,540.58,3.13,"It's a dangerous sport, but most sports are dangerous."],[106,543.71,3.96,"The industry has done a lot to try to make it less dangerous."],[106,547.8,3.0,"My job as a veterinarian is to make sure"],[106,551.01,3.34,"that I am helping my trainers put out"],[106,554.35,4.46,"and make sure that their horse is at its ultimate healthiest best."],[106,558.81,3.92,"Because irregardless of how they perform, we always want them to come back safe"],[106,562.73,3.5,"and be able to perform another day or just come back safe."],[106,566.36,1.33,"That's what everyone wants."],[106,567.69,0.5,"Oh, can I ride one of the horses?"],[106,568.19,1.54,"No."],[107,7.04,4.04,"The gods watched as Oedipus’ daughter, Antigone,"],[107,11.17,5.38,"stole out of the palace in Thebes the morning after a devastating battle."],[107,17.1,4.38,"Antigone was on a dangerous mission to do what she felt was right,"],[107,21.56,2.84,"though it meant risking her life."],[107,25.31,6.26,"Oedipus had been king of Thebes, and his exile sent the city into chaos."],[107,31.86,4.09,"His younger son, Eteocles, claimed power for himself"],[107,36.16,3.38,"and sought to banish his older brother Polyneices."],[107,39.99,5.84,"The two incited a bitter war, and both brothers fell at the city gates."],[107,46.42,4.75,"Oedipus’ brother-in-law, Creon, declared himself the new king,"],[107,51.3,5.84,"having first aligned himself with Eteocles and denounced Polyneices as a traitor."],[107,57.76,4.17,"Now, he ordered that Polyneices be denied burial,"],[107,62.06,2.08,"leaving his body on the battleground,"],[107,64.14,3.59,"surrounded by guards and circled by vultures."],[107,67.81,4.38,"Anyone who violated his proclamation would be stoned to death."],[107,72.98,4.05,"Antigone and her sister, Ismene, were at their uncle’s mercy."],[107,77.28,4.3,"The fact that Antigone was betrothed to Creon’s son, Haemon,"],[107,81.79,1.96,"seemed to earn her no favor."],[107,83.91,3.63,"Despite the dangers, Antigone secretly visited her sister"],[107,87.58,3.88,"and whispered her intentions to honor their brother through burial."],[107,91.8,5.05,"In doing so, she would be defying Creon but abiding by divine law,"],[107,96.97,3.71,"which insisted that one must bury one's own flesh and blood."],[107,101.06,5.0,"When Ismene refused to join her, insisting that girls shouldn’t disobey men,"],[107,106.27,2.5,"Antigone set out alone."],[107,109.77,3.8,"She knelt by Polyneices’ corpse, undetected,"],[107,113.65,2.46,"and began sprinkling it with dust."],[107,116.36,2.92,"When the guards realized and reported the disturbance,"],[107,119.41,4.42,"Creon ordered them to find the man who so defiantly committed the crime."],[107,124.66,4.63,"At midday, Antigone returned to perform a more complete burial,"],[107,129.42,5.96,"when suddenly a thick dust storm whirled onto the plain and choked out the sky,"],[107,135.46,3.04,"as if conjured by the gods to aid her."],[107,138.76,3.09,"Creon's guards, blinded and disoriented,"],[107,141.85,3.25,"didn't see as Antigone's stole into their midst"],[107,145.18,3.88,"and covered her brother’s body with more earth and water."],[107,149.31,1.75,"As the tempest dissipated,"],[107,151.06,4.59,"the soldiers were shocked to see that the culprit was a girl,"],[107,155.86,2.71,"and the princess no less."],[107,159.2,4.17,"Creon barraged Antigone with accusations and insults."],[107,163.53,4.42,"Hoping to protect her sister, Ismene confessed to the crime."],[107,168.21,2.21,"But Antigone was steadfast."],[107,170.54,4.75,"She insisted she’d acted alone and according to divine laws."],[107,175.92,4.04,"Haemon urged his father to be reasonable and merciful."],[107,180.3,5.88,"But Creon was convinced forgiving Antigone would be a critical admission of weakness."],[107,186.39,1.46,"He spared Ismene,"],[107,188.02,3.42,"but ignored his son’s unremitting pleas."],[107,191.9,4.09,"To evade direct blame for the young princess’ death,"],[107,196.11,2.88,"Creon ordered a passive execution:"],[107,199.24,3.5,"Antigone would be shut away in an underground cave."],[107,203.12,2.71,"As the guards dragged the sisters away,"],[107,205.95,4.8,"Ismene was filled with remorse, wishing to die by Antigone’s side."],[107,211.12,2.5,"Creon maintained his show of power"],[107,213.63,3.92,"and Thebes fell into a fearful state of mourning."],[107,218.34,5.42,"But then Creon was visited by the wise, blind prophet Tiresias."],[107,224.18,3.84,"He told Creon that his hubris was polluting Thebes,"],[107,228.06,3.71,"and the underworld’s fiercely avenging divine Furies"],[107,231.77,2.79,"were lying in wait should he not yield."],[107,234.98,6.21,"At last, Creon ordered his guards to free Antigone and to bury Polyneices."],[107,241.65,4.42,"But he had delayed too long— and it would cost him dearly."],[107,246.83,5.25,"Haemon had just reached Antigone’s prison, determined to save her."],[107,252.54,2.25,"Descending into the underground chamber,"],[107,254.96,1.92,"he discovered with horror"],[107,256.88,3.63,"his beloved fiancée hanging from her wedding veil."],[107,261.13,2.67,"Moments later, when Creon entered,"],[107,263.97,4.25,"he was met by Haemon’s broken, hateful glare."],[107,268.68,5.3,"Haemon spat in his father’s face, drew his sword, and lunged."],[107,274.27,2.13,"But as Creon dodged the thrust,"],[107,276.44,3.13,"Haemon turned the blade upon himself"],[107,279.65,5.96,"and wrapped his arms around Antigone, in one last tragic embrace."],[107,286.37,6.05,"Word of Haemon and Antigone’s deaths reached Thebes before Creon’s return."],[107,292.62,6.09,"And upon hearing the devastating news, the queen took her own life at an altar,"],[107,299.0,3.29,"cursing her husband with her dying breaths."],[107,303.09,3.63,"Failing to yield to Antigone’s righteous rebellion,"],[107,307.18,2.08,"Creon, in all his pride,"],[107,309.35,2.17,"had learned his lesson too late."],[107,312.1,4.3,"Anguished, hands bloodied, and all hopes dashed,"],[107,316.52,3.75,"Creon’s attendants led him back into the palace."],[108,4.33,3.84,"I have spent the last four decades in the reality of conflict."],[108,9.01,1.97,"As a child,"],[108,10.97,2.44,"as a mother and as a professional."],[108,14.95,1.33,"And I'm not the exception."],[108,17.85,3.44,"1 in 4 people today are living the reality of conflict."],[108,22.89,1.8,"We are living in an era"],[108,24.72,2.67,"where war and violence are becoming the reflex --"],[108,27.43,2.67,"the choice -- not the last resort."],[108,31.73,6.01,"What we've been witnessing with Iran is just the most visible example,"],[108,37.77,4.34,"because it’s been sending shockwaves well beyond its borders:"],[108,42.14,4.14,"in energy prices, in trade routes disruption"],[108,46.31,2.0,"and in political polarization."],[108,49.11,3.84,"And sadly, even when wars end and when agreements are signed,"],[108,52.98,2.54,"violence often returns within five years."],[108,56.95,4.54,"Over the course of my international career with the United Nations,"],[108,61.52,4.07,"I noticed something that should not be controversial, but still is."],[108,66.23,2.4,"We have overly bureaucratized peace."],[108,69.3,2.9,"We've built an entire industry around it,"],[108,72.24,4.34,"with the familiar Western liberal model and a familiar toolbox:"],[108,76.61,2.0,"bring in the peacebuilders and mediators,"],[108,78.64,1.87,"launch dialogues, push for elections,"],[108,80.54,1.44,"train the police,"],[108,81.98,3.04,"launch stabilization programs,"],[108,85.05,2.2,"add grassroots, women,"],[108,87.28,3.3,"maybe sprinkle some youth so you can tick an inclusion box,"],[108,90.62,2.37,"write reports and repeat."],[108,94.16,2.94,"And don’t get me wrong, this work is important."],[108,97.53,3.27,"But too often we mistake process for progress."],[108,101.3,3.5,"And too often, we do not build enough political legitimacy,"],[108,104.83,2.3,"enough aligned self-interest,"],[108,107.17,2.74,"enough public backing to make peace hold."],[108,115.01,2.04,"We have seen the limitations of this."],[108,117.08,1.17,"In the Middle East,"],[108,118.28,4.04,"tens of millions of people are living the unfinished business of wars,"],[108,122.35,2.2,"of failed political settlements,"],[108,124.59,1.5,"of occupation."],[108,126.52,1.67,"Or take Afghanistan:"],[108,128.22,4.37,"20 years of vast intervention and investment,"],[108,132.63,3.0,"and the story ended exactly where it began --"],[108,135.97,1.83,"Taliban to Taliban."],[108,139.7,1.7,"So why does peace break down"],[108,141.41,2.84,"when we do everything we think we're supposed to do?"],[108,145.94,3.24,"The answer I kept coming back to was not ideology --"],[108,149.58,2.57,"it was power, politics and incentives."],[108,152.78,3.6,"And for the broader public, it was legitimacy and trust."],[108,157.39,2.54,"And legitimacy is a felt experience --"],[108,160.26,3.94,"in good governance, whether you trust your police force,"],[108,164.19,3.2,"whether your children can walk to school safely,"],[108,167.43,2.64,"whether your dignity is preserved."],[108,170.9,3.4,"Without these, a peace agreement becomes a lid on a boiling pot."],[108,174.34,2.8,"It looks calm until the pressure finds the weakest point --"],[108,177.17,1.17,"then it erupts."],[108,179.31,2.57,"I kept seeing this again and again and again."],[108,181.91,1.47,"So I got fed up."],[108,184.18,1.7,"With the bureaucracy,"],[108,185.88,1.27,"with the system,"],[108,187.18,1.37,"with its toolbox."],[108,189.69,3.77,"And I founded Principles for Peace Foundation"],[108,193.49,1.44,"to help those in the hot seat,"],[108,194.96,3.44,"to help peacemakers build more legitimate and durable peace."],[108,198.43,2.47,"We drew on lessons from dozens of countries"],[108,200.93,2.84,"to understand what lies beneath the success and failure"],[108,203.77,1.37,"of peace processes,"],[108,205.17,4.7,"and developed principles and tools and methodologies"],[108,209.91,1.83,"and data and simulations"],[108,211.78,3.37,"and AI support and political dialogue infrastructures"],[108,215.18,2.27,"to help those who are trying to make peace hold."],[108,218.22,2.44,"Because this is the challenge of our time."],[108,221.15,1.27,"How do you build peace?"],[108,222.45,5.14,"How do you cultivate legitimacy in a world where might is right again,"],[108,227.62,4.94,"where power politics is back and transactionalism is in fashion?"],[108,233.8,4.27,"My answer is not by countering power with idealism,"],[108,238.1,2.07,"but with principled pragmatism."],[108,240.87,4.5,"Because principled pragmatism is self-interest with a spine."],[108,247.51,3.24,"I recently became a proud Swiss,"],[108,250.78,2.8,"but I was born and raised a proud Palestinian."],[108,254.28,3.8,"So people often expect me to start with victimhood,"],[108,258.09,3.5,"to start with moral argument, to start with pain."],[108,261.93,3.14,"My pain, their pain, everybody's pain."],[108,266.4,3.24,"But I learned something early that I wish was not true."],[108,270.97,2.87,"When identities are shaped by loss,"],[108,273.87,3.0,"by violence, by victimhood,"],[108,276.87,1.83,"othering becomes normal."],[108,279.48,3.07,"Dehumanizing the other side becomes reflex."],[108,283.15,2.94,"Groupthink becomes shelter,"],[108,286.08,2.24,"and violence becomes currency."],[108,289.05,3.4,"Empathy for the other side is rarely the entry point."],[108,293.79,2.5,"When I was 19,"],[108,296.29,2.47,"I was invited to the kind of dialogue program"],[108,298.8,2.04,"the world loves to celebrate."],[108,300.83,2.04,"It's the perfect grassroots project."],[108,304.47,2.6,"You bring in a few young Israelis and Palestinians."],[108,307.1,3.9,"You put them somewhere nice -- maybe in a retreat location in Europe."],[108,311.04,2.84,"You encourage them to pour their hearts out."],[108,313.88,1.24,"You encourage sharing."],[108,315.14,2.84,"You encourage empathy. You encourage hugs."],[108,318.35,2.17,"And you bring out that hummus."],[108,324.72,1.27,"And I hated it."],[108,326.86,2.0,"Not because I do not believe in empathy --"],[108,328.86,1.44,"and by the way, I love hummus."],[108,332.1,2.04,"But because when I went home,"],[108,334.16,2.7,"the reality stayed hard, complicated, unsafe,"],[108,336.9,2.0,"and I couldn't do anything about it."],[108,338.94,1.47,"And a few months later,"],[108,340.44,1.64,"the Second Intifada started"],[108,342.11,3.57,"and those we were sharing with were back in uniforms,"],[108,345.71,1.7,"fighting in our own towns."],[108,348.55,1.37,"I lost friends."],[108,351.42,2.54,"My house was destroyed,"],[108,353.98,1.27,"and I lost hope."],[108,356.29,1.47,"And for nearly two decades,"],[108,357.79,3.77,"I dedicated my career to working with people affected by conflict,"],[108,362.83,2.0,"but I avoided working on my own."],[108,364.86,1.37,"It was just too painful."],[108,368.13,3.8,"Then October 7 happened, and the war in Gaza expanded."],[108,371.97,3.87,"And I realized, if I truly believe in my work,"],[108,375.84,4.41,"I have to bring in Israelis and Palestinians"],[108,380.28,2.14,"to the hardest room in my life."],[108,383.61,1.53,"So that's what I did."],[108,385.82,1.74,"Come into that room with me."],[108,388.12,2.2,"It’s weeks after October 7:"],[108,391.15,1.77,"The war is raging."],[108,393.42,2.57,"Loss and trauma are overwhelming."],[108,396.63,3.84,"The door closes and 76 people sit down,"],[108,400.46,3.77,"carrying decades of grievances and recent loss."],[108,406.17,1.43,"No one trusts anyone."],[108,407.64,1.53,"Not the room, not the process,"],[108,409.21,1.5,"not each other, not me."],[108,411.57,2.64,"They're Israeli and Palestinian leaders,"],[108,414.21,1.7,"but they're not the usual suspects."],[108,415.91,2.9,"They're security leaders, business leaders,"],[108,418.85,1.87,"investors, political figures,"],[108,420.72,2.6,"journalists [and] serious operators."],[108,424.35,2.14,"And outside that room,"],[108,426.52,2.37,"the public narrative is collapsing so fast --"],[108,428.93,2.33,"it's so hard, it's so polarized."],[108,431.3,3.0,"In a room like that, if you start with the word peace,"],[108,434.33,2.34,"you get laughed out of the building."],[108,438.0,1.47,"So I named the tension plainly"],[108,439.47,2.8,"because anything softer would be dishonest."],[108,443.87,2.87,"\"We are here out of urgency"],[108,446.78,2.6,"and responsibility for our own people."],[108,450.05,4.0,"Because the status quo neither delivered security to the Israelis,"],[108,454.08,3.8,"nor dignity nor an end to the occupation for the Palestinians."],[108,458.62,2.3,"And we are at an inflection point."],[108,461.36,2.04,"We either break this cycle"],[108,463.43,4.0,"or condemn both our people to a perpetual state of loss,"],[108,467.43,3.0,"of trauma, of insecurity of occupation.\""],[108,471.1,3.74,"We agreed not to dwell on our national and historical narrative --"],[108,474.84,2.77,"there is no common ground to be found in the past."],[108,478.64,3.4,"Instead, we focused on what you cannot afford to lose:"],[108,482.08,1.7,"security,"],[108,483.78,1.64,"dignity,"],[108,485.45,2.07,"the future you want for your children."],[108,488.52,3.7,"Because, you see, common ground does not begin with moral agreement --"],[108,492.26,1.67,"it begins with self-interest."],[108,494.26,1.67,"Everything else comes later."],[108,497.56,2.23,"So let me tell you what we've done differently,"],[108,499.8,4.74,"because this is where the old peacemaking toolbox often misfires."],[108,505.0,2.27,"We did not organize this around the grassroots,"],[108,507.3,1.13,"but the grasstops."],[108,508.44,1.97,"Not the convinced, but the persuadable --"],[108,510.41,1.97,"people with influence on power,"],[108,512.41,1.47,"on politics, on the economy."],[108,514.71,2.6,"People who know that the status quo is not sustainable,"],[108,517.35,1.77,"but they can do something about it."],[108,519.15,3.34,"And I introduced a sequence that almost looks too simple"],[108,522.49,1.47,"until you watch it work."],[108,524.45,5.37,"Self-interest, transaction, recognition and then humanity."],[108,530.16,1.53,"I called it STIR."],[108,532.23,2.37,"And I called it STIR on purpose,"],[108,534.6,1.77,"because if you do not stir the room,"],[108,536.37,2.77,"you get positions sitting next to each other forever."],[108,539.74,2.6,"You can certainly put the chickpeas next to the tahini,"],[108,542.34,1.2,"next to the lemon juice,"],[108,543.54,1.24,"and not get that hummus."],[108,546.58,1.77,"Self-Interest."],[108,548.38,1.64,"The status quo is not sustainable"],[108,550.05,2.84,"because it neither delivers security to the Israelis"],[108,552.88,2.3,"nor an end to the occupation of the Palestinians."],[108,555.22,2.77,"Then you seek that common ground -- that transaction --"],[108,557.99,2.6,"a political solution within a regional framework."],[108,561.06,1.8,"For Palestinians,"],[108,562.86,1.7,"that means an end to the occupation"],[108,564.56,3.9,"and a non-militarized Palestinian state, side by side with Israel."],[108,568.5,2.24,"For Israelis, that means security"],[108,570.77,2.64,"and a pathway to regional integration,"],[108,573.4,1.33,"rather than isolation."],[108,575.77,2.57,"Once you find that common ground,"],[108,578.38,1.97,"you can build something"],[108,580.34,1.64,"that is principled and pragmatic."],[108,582.01,2.94,"You can build a platform for dialogue and for action."],[108,585.98,3.34,"That became Uniting for a Shared Future Coalition."],[108,589.35,1.37,"That's our coalition."],[108,591.19,2.4,"And it grew and it held and it expanded."],[108,593.59,4.24,"And today it brings more than 550 Israeli and Palestinian leaders --"],[108,599.5,1.53,"realists and possibilists."],[108,602.13,3.4,"What's very special about this coalition"],[108,605.57,1.8,"is that we work together --"],[108,607.4,4.27,"even in the midst of the war -- to push for a political solution."],[108,612.51,2.64,"In the months that followed,"],[108,615.18,2.47,"we started to advocate together, from heads of state"],[108,617.68,2.9,"to briefing the UN Security Council with a joint message."],[108,620.58,3.4,"And I saw Israelis and Palestinians who did not see eye-to-eye"],[108,624.02,2.94,"begin to speak about the future in the same grammar."],[108,626.99,5.21,"And trust began to grow in our coalition, in our process and in each other."],[108,633.7,3.87,"I watched Palestinians and Israelis recognize each other's losses."],[108,638.87,2.04,"They no longer dehumanized the other."],[108,641.54,2.07,"They no longer relativized pain."],[108,643.64,2.1,"That's what STIR made possible."],[108,646.11,1.47,"But let me be clear."],[108,648.31,2.54,"The reality continues to be brutal."],[108,651.88,3.5,"Both people are trapped in deep insecurity."],[108,656.25,4.97,"And Israelis and Palestinians feel existentially threatened."],[108,661.26,1.7,"And my people, Palestinians,"],[108,662.99,2.0,"continue to live the reality of occupation,"],[108,665.03,2.67,"settler violence, devastation in Gaza"],[108,667.73,2.2,"and the threat of annexation in the West Bank."],[108,670.67,2.94,"And the whole Middle East is at a crossroads."],[108,673.64,3.97,"It either stays locked in a perpetual state of confrontation"],[108,677.64,1.6,"and different wars --"],[108,679.28,3.8,"same wars, I dare say, with different names --"],[108,683.08,2.37,"or it moves into a new logic"],[108,685.48,2.87,"of new political security and cooperation framework."],[108,689.22,3.44,"And stops exporting this whole instability to the rest, to all of us,"],[108,692.69,1.94,"to the rest of us and all of us."],[108,695.23,2.1,"But I would argue that that can only happen"],[108,697.36,2.27,"if we resolve the Israeli-Palestinian conflict,"],[108,699.66,3.34,"because it's the key fault line that continues to fuel radicalization."],[108,704.7,1.7,"So my lesson is this:"],[108,707.74,2.4,"in a transactional world,"],[108,710.17,4.47,"the antidote to \"might is right\" is not idealism."],[108,715.35,1.53,"It is not moralizing."],[108,717.81,1.74,"It is principled pragmatism."],[108,720.62,2.97,"It is launching coalitions of the willing"],[108,723.62,4.2,"around enlightened self-interest that can advance peace and security."],[108,728.42,3.27,"If you want humanity, you have to earn it."],[108,732.5,1.74,"But start with self-interest --"],[108,734.26,3.4,"self-interest, transaction, recognition"],[108,737.7,1.8,"and then the return of humanity."],[108,740.14,4.34,"Wherever conflict rules, STIR that room until humanity rises."],[108,744.47,1.17,"Thank you."],[109,0.0,1.12,"Ready for a vacation?"],[109,1.12,1.68,"This lake may seem like paradise."],[109,2.8,2.24,"But it has an explosive origin story."],[109,5.04,2.56,"On the North Island of New Zealand, you’ll find Lake Taupō."],[109,7.6,2.4,"And you can see why it’s a popular tourist destination:"],[109,10.0,4.0,"it’s got amazing views, decadent hot springs, and frickin’ waterfalls, man."],[109,14.0,4.64,"But, around 25,500 years ago, you definitely wouldn’t want to be in its vicinity."],[109,18.64,3.84,"Because this placid lake formed from a massive volcanic supereruption."],[109,22.48,2.88,"The volcano shot out tons of lava and volcanic ash,"],[109,25.36,3.44,"buckling the earth into a depression about the size of Singapore."],[109,28.8,2.88,"Eventually, it filled with water and became Lake Taupō."],[109,31.68,0.88,"Amazing, right?"],[109,32.56,1.44,"And… kinda terrifying."],[109,34.0,2.98,"Hi, I’m Sage, and this is Crash Course Geology."],[109,41.34,1.94,"From the huge Mount Damavand in Iran"],[109,43.28,2.0,"to the tiny Taal in the Philippines"],[109,45.28,2.0,"to the infamous Mount Doom of Mordor,"],[109,47.28,2.08,"volcanoes capture our imagination."],[109,49.36,2.16,"Yeah, I know Lord of the Rings isn’t real!"],[109,51.52,2.72,"But it’s based on real volcanoes, okay?"],[109,54.24,2.08,"Whether we’re talking the real deal or papier-mâché,"],[109,56.32,2.56,"volcanoes can be equal parts enthralling and scary."],[109,58.88,2.56,"Because, well, they do this."],[109,61.44,3.6,"Stand back, Dwayne."],[109,65.04,2.64,"Oh. Well, in real life they erupt."],[109,67.68,1.36,"But… why?"],[109,69.04,1.89,"And what actually is a volcano, anyway?"],[109,70.96,3.6,"Geologically speaking, a volcano is a crack in the ground— AKA a vent—"],[109,74.56,1.52,"that lava comes out of."],[109,76.08,3.68,"It can also refer to the land formations that build up around those vents,"],[109,79.76,3.76,"like the mountain-looking things we might think of when we hear the word “volcano.”"],[109,83.52,4.32,"But volcanoes aren’t just mountains— especially because they erupt."],[109,87.84,2.16,"Let’s take a look at how that happens."],[109,90.0,2.8,"Magma is what we call molten rock that’s underground."],[109,92.8,1.44,"And it’s actually pretty chill."],[109,94.24,0.88,"Metaphorically."],[109,95.12,1.84,"Literally it’s, like, a thousand degrees Celsius."],[109,96.96,3.2,"Down there, the pressure is way higher than it is on the surface."],[109,100.16,3.92,"And that pressure, combined with the high heat, allows gases like water vapor,"],[109,104.08,3.44,"carbon dioxide, and sulfur to dissolve into the magma."],[109,107.52,2.16,"Magma is less dense than the surrounding rock,"],[109,109.68,5.52,"so it tends to rise toward the surface and collect in underground pools called magma chambers,"],[109,115.2,4.4,"which do a pretty good job of keeping things contained… usually."],[109,119.6,3.68,"But put a little crack in the rock above, and things get… explode-y."],[109,123.28,4.16,"Think of that magma with all its dissolved gases like a shaken-up bottle of soda."],[109,127.44,2.88,"As long as the cap’s still on, everything’s fine."],[109,130.32,4.32,"But unscrew it even a tiny bit and you’d better have your goggles on."],[109,134.64,2.24,"As soon as the gases have a path to the surface,"],[109,136.88,2.24,"they come speeding up like the bubbles in your soda,"],[109,139.12,2.32,"carrying that hot molten rock up with them."],[109,141.44,2.32,"Magma then breaks the surface and becomes lava,"],[109,143.76,2.96,"the name we give to molten rock when it’s above ground."],[109,146.72,2.0,"Et voilà: an eruption."],[109,148.72,1.76,"Not all eruptions are equal, though."],[109,150.48,4.48,"Some are huge and explosive, while others look more like a cool feature at a water park."],[109,154.96,3.84,"That difference partly comes from the amount of gas that’s dissolved in the magma—"],[109,158.8,2.32,"more gas equals more explosive power."],[109,161.12,2.8,"But the other key is how much silica the lava has."],[109,163.92,3.28,"Silica is one of the most plentiful chemical compounds on Earth's surface"],[109,167.2,2.88,"and helps determine how gooey lava is."],[109,170.08,2.72,"Low-silica lava, also called mafic lava,"],[109,172.8,4.8,"creates super runny flows that race across the surface in rivers or spurt up as fountains."],[109,177.6,2.4,"High-silica lava, called felsic lava,"],[109,180.0,3.92,"is thick and sticky, preventing the gases from easily escaping."],[109,183.92,3.17,"Pressure builds until— blam-o! — you get an explosion."],[109,187.12,4.32,"And those eruptions from different types of lava create different shapes of volcanoes, too."],[109,191.44,4.8,"Like, the runniest lavas often create shield volcanoes, which are big, low, and flat."],[109,196.24,1.44,"Kīlauea is an example."],[109,197.68,5.76,"Its lava flows harden into broad flanks of basalt that end up looking kind of like a shield."],[109,203.44,3.2,"But if the lava is extra gassy or has slightly more"],[109,206.64,2.72,"silica, eruptions can get a little more explosive."],[109,209.36,4.32,"Lava can sputter up along fractures in the ground, called a fissure eruption."],[109,213.68,3.92,"Or it can fountain from a central vent, falling back down as tephra:"],[109,217.6,3.6,"chunks of rock that can be as small as ash, or as big as houses."],[109,221.2,5.12,"This can create small, steep-sided volcanoes called cinder cones— like Parícutin in Mexico."],[109,226.32,2.96,"Cinder cones look tiny in comparison to composite volcanoes,"],[109,229.28,3.44,"aka stratovolcanoes, like Mount Fuji in Japan."],[109,232.72,3.28,"They’re towering, conical peaks that loom over the landscape,"],[109,236.0,2.4,"often with a distinct crater at their summit."],[109,238.4,3.2,"You know, so the hobbits have somewhere to chuck that ring."],[109,241.6,4.8,"Composite volcanoes erupt with remarkable variety, sometimes unleashing torrents of lava,"],[109,246.4,3.36,"other times sending up volcanic plumes of tephra that fall back to Earth."],[109,249.76,4.8,"These combinations of eruptions build up the composite volcano's iconic steep flanks."],[109,254.56,3.6,"When lava is extra sticky, it can’t “flow” away from the vent."],[109,258.16,3.52,"So it may pile up around it, creating a lava dome."],[109,261.68,2.64,"Sometimes lava domes stand freely on their own."],[109,264.32,3.28,"But they can also form inside structures like calderas,"],[109,267.6,4.48,"which are created when a ton of magma erupts quickly and drains the chamber below,"],[109,272.08,1.68,"causing the surface to collapse."],[109,273.76,3.84,"The result is a huge, bowl-shaped caldera, like Lake Taupō."],[109,277.6,2.8,"So volcanoes come in all kinds of shapes and sizes,"],[109,280.4,1.6,"and you can find them all over the world."],[109,282.0,1.84,"But they don’t pop up just anywhere."],[109,283.84,3.6,"For magma to burst from the surface, conditions have to be just right."],[109,287.44,2.48,"Many volcanic eruptions happen at plate boundaries:"],[109,289.92,3.2,"places where the tectonic plates that make up Earth’s outermost layer"],[109,293.12,2.32,"squish together or spread apart."],[109,295.44,4.8,"Like, lots of volcanoes form at subduction zones, where two tectonic plates converge."],[109,300.24,3.28,"One gets forced beneath the other and back into the mantle."],[109,303.52,3.12,"The mantle’s heat causes the downgoing plate to release water,"],[109,306.64,2.8,"which reduces the melting point of the rocks above."],[109,309.44,3.52,"That’s how you get magma collecting in reservoirs that can blast to the surface."],[109,312.96,2.48,"Volcanoes can form at divergent boundaries, too."],[109,315.44,3.52,"Sometimes that happens on land, in places called continental rifts,"],[109,318.96,2.64,"where two continental plates pull apart from each other,"],[109,321.6,2.4,"causing hot mantle rock to well upward."],[109,324.0,2.4,"And this process is way more common underwater."],[109,326.4,3.6,"Geologists estimate that more than 70 percent of the world’s volcanoes are hidden"],[109,330.0,2.0,"under the sea at mid-ocean ridges:"],[109,332.0,3.52,"divergent plate boundaries where two oceanic plates are pulling apart."],[109,335.52,5.44,"Geologists finally witnessed an eruption at a mid-ocean ridge for the first time in 2025."],[109,340.96,1.28,"Just, so cool!"],[109,342.48,4.32,"Sometimes volcanoes can form in places called hotspots, where a big plume of super-hot"],[109,346.8,4.56,"mantle rock rises up underneath the crust, causing rock to melt and erupt at the surface."],[109,351.36,1.84,"We have hotspots to thank for Yellowstone,"],[109,353.2,3.52,"whose hotspot now sits right below the northwest corner of the United States."],[109,356.72,3.12,"Now, you might’ve heard Yellowstone described as a “supervolcano,”"],[109,359.84,2.8,"which could cause colossal destruction if it erupted again."],[109,362.64,5.12,"But “supervolcano” isn’t a scientific term; most geologists kinda roll their eyes at it."],[109,367.76,2.48,"It’s like their personal Nickelback."],[109,370.24,2.24,"Look at this supervolcanoooo."],[109,372.48,5.36,"Technically, a “supervolcano” is a volcano that’s had at least one supereruption,"],[109,377.84,1.92,"like the one that formed Lake Taupō."],[109,379.76,6.24,"What counts as a “supereruption” comes down to the Volcanic Explosivity Index, or VEI,"],[109,386.0,4.32,"which ranks the intensity of volcanic eruptions on a scale of zero to 8."],[109,390.32,6.16,"For context, the strongest volcanic eruptions of the past century or so were around a 6 on the VEI."],[109,396.48,3.2,"But a supereruption is VEI of 8,"],[109,399.68,5.76,"meaning it ejects at least 1000 cubic kilometers of ash, pumice, and debris."],[109,405.44,5.28,"That’s enough volcano-stuff to fill 400 million Olympic swimming pools."],[109,410.72,3.52,"The 100-meter butterfly would be… just so different"],[109,414.24,2.08,"But could this really happen at Yellowstone?"],[109,416.32,1.84,"Whole towns and cities covered in ash?"],[109,418.16,1.28,"Emergency services stymied?"],[109,419.44,1.68,"Global agricultural collapse?"],[109,421.12,2.48,"In theory, yes."],[109,423.6,4.96,"But the last supereruption at Yellowstone was around 640,000 years ago."],[109,428.56,4.56,"And geologists say there’s an “exceedingly low” chance"],[109,433.12,3.68,"that a supereruption would happen in the next few thousand years."],[109,436.8,2.8,"Some aren’t convinced another will ever happen again."],[109,439.6,4.56,"And just because a volcano has had one, or even two, supereruptions in the past,"],[109,444.16,2.96,"doesn't mean its future blasts will be as hefty."],[109,447.12,4.64,"Even if a supereruption were going to happen at Yellowstone, we'd have some notice."],[109,451.76,3.12,"Volcanologists— who are not a species from “Star Trek”"],[109,454.88,2.48,"but rather scientists who study volcanoes—"],[109,457.36,3.92,"keep tabs on the amount of magma held within the volcano's underground reservoir,"],[109,461.28,1.76,"using tools like seismometers."],[109,463.04,4.0,"Recent studies suggest there's more magma down there than we initially thought."],[109,467.04,4.56,"But importantly, it's spread across many smaller underground pockets."],[109,471.6,3.76,"It’s not linked up enough to erupt in one big boom."],[109,475.36,2.08,"So, you have geologists’ blessing—"],[109,477.44,4.29,"you can take a Yellowstone supereruption off your Things to Worry About list."],[109,481.76,3.28,"But just because supereruptions aren’t much of a threat to us,"],[109,485.04,2.8,"doesn’t mean regular old volcanic eruptions aren’t."],[109,487.84,5.12,"Across the world, about 800 million people live within a hundred kilometers of an active volcano."],[109,492.96,2.16,"Now, the meaning of “active” can vary."],[109,495.12,4.08,"Like, some geologists use “active” to refer to volcanoes that are erupting right now,"],[109,499.2,4.88,"and others use it to mean any volcano that’s erupted in the last 11,000 years or so."],[109,504.08,4.4,"Dormant volcanoes aren’t erupting right now but might in the future."],[109,508.48,5.68,"And extinct volcanoes were active once, but aren’t expected to erupt ever again—"],[109,514.16,2.96,"even though they can surprise us once in a while."],[109,517.12,3.36,"But it’s not all Mount Doom and Gloom living near an active volcano."],[109,520.48,3.68,"In small doses, volcanic ash is really good for crops."],[109,524.16,3.76,"Volcanoes are where we find a lot of our most prized minerals,"],[109,527.92,3.44,"like gold, silver, copper, lead, and zinc."],[109,531.36,3.68,"And the heat of volcanic systems can be tapped for geothermal energy,"],[109,535.04,2.4,"which we’ll get more into in a later episode."],[109,537.44,2.88,"But some eruptions can cause serious destruction."],[109,540.32,5.04,"Thick volcanic ash can blanket crops, collapse structures, and suffocate people and animals."],[109,545.36,4.64,"Plus, eruptions can cause avalanches of hot ash called pyroclastic flows,"],[109,550.0,2.64,"and landslides and mudslides called lahars."],[109,552.64,4.48,"So it makes sense that some folks have tried to control erupting volcanoes."],[109,557.12,3.6,"Like, in 1935, the US Army tried to change the course"],[109,560.72,4.56,"of lava flows from Mauna Loa by bombing it."],[109,565.28,2.64,"Which… didn’t really work."],[109,567.92,3.76,"Truth be told, there’s no way to stop a volcano from erupting."],[109,571.68,2.72,"There’s no big cork you can stick in there."],[109,574.4,3.76,"But there are ways to forecast and prepare for eruptions."],[109,578.16,4.96,"Geologists and volcanologists monitor warning signs of volcanic activity, like earthquakes,"],[109,583.12,4.48,"swelling in the ground, and steam rising ominously from places it shouldn’t."],[109,587.6,2.72,"They also base forecasts on volcanoes’ recurrence intervals,"],[109,590.32,3.52,"or the average times between past volcanic eruptions."],[109,593.84,3.41,"This can give us clues to when a peak might erupt again."],[109,597.28,3.52,"And when it comes to disaster preparedness, there’s a few things you can do."],[109,600.8,2.32,"One is to learn about volcano risks in your area"],[109,603.12,2.48,"and sign up for alerts from your local government."],[109,605.6,3.28,"Another is to form an evacuation plan and gather emergency supplies"],[109,608.88,2.8,"like food, water, masks, and clothing."],[109,611.68,2.56,"There’s more on volcano preparedness in the links below."],[109,614.24,2.88,"So, yeah, volcanic eruptions can be scary."],[109,617.12,3.6,"They can also be enthralling, fascinating, and even… pretty chill."],[109,620.72,4.24,"Some create huge calderas that turn into popular tourist destinations,"],[109,624.96,2.32,"and some just blow off a little steam."],[109,627.28,4.8,"Earth’s geology is chaotic like that."],[109,632.08,1.04,"Sweet!"],[109,633.12,3.76,"Next time, we’re talking about another geological hazard: earthquakes."],[109,636.88,0.96,"See you then."],[109,637.84,2.0,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Geology"],[109,639.84,2.2,"which was filmed at our studio in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[109,642.04,2.28,"and was made with the help of all these nice people."],[109,644.32,2.88,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[109,647.2,2.08,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[110,6.84,4.63,"In 1930, as the Great Depression ravaged the United States,"],[110,11.55,3.54,"President Herbert Hoover made a bold economic decision."],[110,15.43,3.29,"With the goal of supporting American farmers and manufacturers,"],[110,18.72,3.25,"he approved a sweeping 20% tax"],[110,22.02,3.88,"on all imported agricultural and industrial goods."],[110,26.31,2.71,"He believed making foreign goods more expensive"],[110,29.02,2.54,"would encourage consumers to buy domestic,"],[110,31.74,4.59,"thus funneling cash into American businesses and creating more jobs."],[110,36.82,2.5,"But Hoover's plan completely backfired."],[110,39.62,3.5,"Other countries retaliated by taxing American goods,"],[110,43.37,3.55,"leading exports to fall and global trade to decline."],[110,47.38,3.55,"In the end, this policy led American prices to spike"],[110,51.13,2.67,"and caused even more unemployment."],[110,54.38,3.46,"These disastrous outcomes of the Smoot-Hawley Tariff Act"],[110,57.97,3.5,"were no surprise to the over 1,000 economists"],[110,61.52,2.13,"who publicly condemned the bill."],[110,63.85,2.88,"But this wouldn’t be the last time a world leader was shocked"],[110,66.73,1.79,"by a tariff's consequences."],[110,69.31,2.59,"So, what exactly is a tariff?"],[110,72.28,5.3,"A tariff is a tax an importer pays to bring foreign goods into a country."],[110,77.95,5.67,"For example, if they import $1,000 worth of goods at a 10% tariff rate,"],[110,83.79,5.25,"they'll pay $100 to the government in addition to $1,000 to the seller."],[110,89.5,4.5,"Foreign exporters could respond to a tariff by reducing their product’s price"],[110,94.01,1.67,"to keep an importer’s business."],[110,95.88,2.75,"But if they don’t, importers bear this cost"],[110,98.72,4.17,"and then increase their prices so consumers will make up the difference."],[110,103.22,1.54,"This may sound straightforward,"],[110,104.77,4.55,"but enacting a tariff kicks off a complicated economic chain reaction."],[110,109.56,1.33,"So to keep things simple,"],[110,111.06,3.13,"let's look at some examples from just one country"],[110,114.32,4.92,"to explore some of the factors that make a tariff’s outcomes tricky to predict."],[110,119.99,4.25,"First, tariffs can be applied to different countries at different rates."],[110,124.45,2.38,"For example, in 2024,"],[110,127.04,4.5,"America had a 6% tariff on all wool entering the country."],[110,131.84,3.25,"However, if that wool came from Mexico or South Korea,"],[110,135.3,3.0,"it would be tariff-free due to trade agreements."],[110,138.68,6.8,"Meanwhile, importers getting wool from Russia could face a hefty 55.5% tariff."],[110,145.72,3.46,"Policies like these might help domestic wool producers,"],[110,149.4,4.34,"or they might just encourage importers to buy from specific foreign producers."],[110,154.44,4.5,"Things get even trickier when trying to predict how tariffs impact jobs."],[110,159.53,3.63,"Sometimes the jobs they create come at a high cost."],[110,163.7,3.67,"In 2018, the US imposed a tariff on washing machines"],[110,167.71,3.04,"that created 1,800 jobs"],[110,170.92,4.04,"and raised roughly $82 million in government revenue."],[110,175.42,5.46,"However, it also increased the average price of washing machines by $86,"],[110,181.09,4.17,"costing consumers roughly $1.5 billion."],[110,185.72,6.67,"This means consumers paid roughly $817,000 to create each of those jobs."],[110,193.19,5.21,"In other cases, the jobs tariffs create come at the cost of existing jobs."],[110,198.65,5.34,"In 2002, the Bush administration imposed tariffs as high as 30%"],[110,203.99,1.33,"on imported steel."],[110,205.53,3.88,"Employment in US steel production had been decaying for decades,"],[110,209.66,2.29,"so by making foreign steel more expensive,"],[110,212.0,3.88,"the tariff sought to create new jobs for US steel manufacturers."],[110,216.34,3.17,"Initially, the policy did slow down job loss."],[110,219.76,4.17,"But since US steel still cost more than pre-tariff foreign steel,"],[110,224.14,4.05,"domestic industries that used this material had to increase their prices."],[110,228.56,3.21,"This made their products less competitive internationally,"],[110,231.85,3.13,"where foreign manufacturers were still using cheap steel."],[110,235.31,2.04,"Within a year of enacting the tariff,"],[110,237.53,3.92,"the US lost far more jobs in steel-related industries"],[110,241.61,2.96,"than they gained in steel-producing industries."],[110,245.12,2.84,"Clearly, how other countries respond to tariffs"],[110,247.95,3.38,"is another big part of the ripple effect these policies have."],[110,251.71,2.5,"One clear example comes from 2018,"],[110,254.21,3.13,"when the US attempted to address its trade deficit with China"],[110,257.34,1.75,"by taxing Chinese goods."],[110,259.38,2.29,"While these tariffs protected domestic businesses"],[110,261.67,1.5,"and raised government revenue,"],[110,263.3,2.21,"they also set off a trade war."],[110,265.85,3.54,"China retaliated by raising tariffs on US exporters,"],[110,269.39,4.0,"including farmers relying on Chinese markets for soybean sales."],[110,273.81,3.63,"In the end, this led to higher prices for US consumers"],[110,277.61,2.84,"and a slight drop in America's GDP."],[110,281.07,1.54,"Trade wars aren’t new."],[110,282.94,3.09,"And even in the 18th and early 19th centuries,"],[110,286.03,4.04,"when certain tariffs did help struggling industries find their domestic footing,"],[110,290.33,2.96,"they were mostly used to raise government funds."],[110,293.46,2.0,"But over the last 200 years,"],[110,295.71,3.63,"our global economy has become far more interdependent."],[110,299.63,2.21,"Today, there are more manufacturing components"],[110,301.84,2.17,"being imported than ever before,"],[110,304.22,4.05,"many of which are used to make a wide variety of consumer goods."],[110,308.6,4.13,"This complexity means that even a seemingly simple tariff"],[110,312.85,2.34,"can trigger a global chain reaction,"],[110,315.56,3.67,"creating more consequences than anyone can plan for."],[111,6.92,6.46,"Jo the First is a ruthless dictator who rules his country with an iron fist."],[111,13.55,3.46,"Whenever he wants something, everyone hurries to appease him."],[111,17.35,3.04,"And since complainers are permanently banished,"],[111,20.43,5.59,"Jo’s terrified servants treat his every whim as a wise and noble decision."],[111,26.31,4.0,"To most onlookers, his rule appears cruel and unjust."],[111,30.48,3.5,"But his beloved son JoJo doesn’t see any issues."],[111,34.24,3.29,"This life is all JoJo's ever known,"],[111,37.78,6.01,"and he never witnesses any fear from Jo’s subjects or violence against them."],[111,43.96,6.67,"All JoJo sees is his father’s strength, and his subjects’ obedience and praise."],[111,51.09,3.29,"After spending his entire childhood in the castle,"],[111,54.47,5.13,"JoJo grows up to inherit his father’s role and his style of rule."],[111,59.89,5.17,"And just as with Jo the First, it’s clear that JoJo’s actions are wrong."],[111,65.31,3.09,"But his case raises a question about morality"],[111,68.4,3.75,"that can also shed light on less extreme cases:"],[111,72.36,2.38,"given JoJo’s unusual upbringing,"],[111,74.74,4.46,"does he bear full moral responsibility for his actions?"],[111,79.53,4.84,"Philosophers Gary Watson and Harry Frankfurt would say yes."],[111,84.62,2.88,"According to their Deep Self View,"],[111,87.58,5.26,"people are morally responsible for actions that stem from their true self,"],[111,93.05,4.05,"meaning actions that reflect their deepest values and commitments."],[111,97.43,3.29,"They believe people are less morally responsible"],[111,100.72,3.92,"for actions performed under the influence of external forces,"],[111,104.72,4.67,"such as committing a crime under duress or while intoxicated."],[111,109.98,3.09,"But JoJo has no such excuse."],[111,113.36,4.38,"His actions are the product of his values, and as such,"],[111,117.86,4.46,"Deep Self theorists would argue that he’s responsible for them."],[111,122.95,3.42,"But what if JoJo’s values have been compromised?"],[111,126.54,4.88,"JoJo might be a monster, but he was raised in a very atypical environment."],[111,131.75,2.96,"Is he really just as responsible for his crimes"],[111,134.71,4.96,"as someone who had a normal childhood and then became a cruel dictator?"],[111,139.93,3.46,"And if JoJo isn’t to blame for who he is,"],[111,143.51,3.13,"how can we blame him for what he does?"],[111,147.14,3.04,"This is the argument of philosopher Susan Wolf,"],[111,150.44,2.13,"who invented JoJo’s case."],[111,153.15,6.01,"Wolf believes that even though JoJo’s actions do reflect his deep self,"],[111,159.16,2.88,"when determining moral responsibility,"],[111,162.03,4.8,"we also need to consider how someone’s deep self came to be."],[111,167.08,2.09,"And in JoJo's situation,"],[111,169.29,4.92,"Wolf believes that even if JoJo is acting on his own values,"],[111,174.25,4.55,"his upbringing makes him less responsible for his cruelty."],[111,179.38,3.84,"Wolf’s logic seems pretty reasonable in JoJo’s case."],[111,183.22,4.17,"But if everyone’s deepest values are shaped by their upbringing,"],[111,187.68,3.88,"does that mean nobody is responsible for their actions?"],[111,192.02,4.0,"This is the attitude held by incompatibilists."],[111,196.28,3.92,"These philosophers believe that if everything is predetermined"],[111,200.2,3.54,"by factors like our environment or biology,"],[111,203.74,4.34,"then no one is truly morally responsible for anything."],[111,208.25,3.0,"By contrast, compatibilist philosophers"],[111,211.25,4.92,"argue that even if our decisions are the inevitable result of past events,"],[111,216.25,3.0,"we can still be held responsible for them."],[111,219.76,4.42,"The debate between these factions has been raging for centuries,"],[111,224.39,4.59,"but Wolf created JoJo’s case to focus on a different question."],[111,229.22,1.58,"Unlike most people,"],[111,230.81,4.8,"JoJo had no meaningful opportunity to learn right from wrong."],[111,235.81,3.59,"And since he continues to be cruel even as an adult,"],[111,239.4,6.34,"it would seem JoJo also lacks the capacity to self-reflect and change his values."],[111,246.07,5.34,"To Wolf, this indicates that JoJo lacks basic moral competence."],[111,251.75,4.42,"If he doesn’t know right from wrong and can no longer learn the difference,"],[111,256.42,4.34,"surely it's misplaced to fully blame him for his actions."],[111,261.05,3.92,"But this argument raises another important question:"],[111,265.22,2.25,"even with this horrible upbringing,"],[111,267.56,4.67,"was JoJo’s moral incompetence truly inevitable?"],[111,272.73,5.05,"Let’s imagine JoJo had a sister who was raised in the same environment"],[111,277.77,2.17,"but developed different values."],[111,280.11,3.54,"If JoJa rejected their father’s tyranny,"],[111,283.95,3.63,"this suggests that JoJo could have done the same—"],[111,287.87,5.25,"an outcome that seemingly increases his moral responsibility."],[111,293.75,5.42,"Similarly, what if a rogue court member tried to teach young JoJo"],[111,299.25,2.59,"about justice and morality,"],[111,301.84,2.75,"but he still chose tyranny?"],[111,304.88,1.71,"Just as Wolf argues,"],[111,306.59,5.21,"it seems like the more chances JoJo has to develop moral competence,"],[111,311.97,3.0,"the less we can excuse his actions."],[111,315.14,3.59,"Ultimately, it's up to you to decide."],[111,319.31,4.05,"How should we determine moral responsibility?"],[111,323.69,4.55,"And is JoJo’s tyrannical nature truly inevitable—"],[111,328.32,6.26,"or can our understanding of right and wrong transcend our upbringing?"],[112,7.17,3.38,"Daedalus, ancient Greece’s genius inventor,"],[112,10.55,4.92,"hasn’t been the same since losing his child, Icarus, to the Sun."],[112,15.64,3.38,"But suddenly, he’s suspiciously reinvigorated"],[112,19.02,4.71,"and has expressed interest in buying the Sun from Helios,"],[112,23.81,1.92,"the Sun God himself."],[112,26.15,6.01,"Some rumors hold that Daedalus is aspiring to destroy it to avenge Icarus—"],[112,32.2,5.5,"or channel its immense power towards other imprudent aims."],[112,38.2,4.75,"Whatever the reason, this is a job for the divine accountant;"],[112,42.95,4.88,"top-ranked financial analyst to the world’s movers and shakers,"],[112,47.96,2.71,"its literal titans of industry."],[112,51.05,3.79,"When it comes to boiling complex systems of intrinsic value"],[112,54.84,3.34,"down to monetary sums, he’s your guy."],[112,58.64,5.21,"Once gathered, Daedalus remarks that Helios might as well relinquish the Sun,"],[112,63.89,5.55,"since it’s bound to die anyways, which is chilling news to Helios."],[112,69.52,2.92,"But the divine accountant interrupts."],[112,72.73,5.34,"“The Sun provides Earth with all its energy aside from geothermal and nuclear."],[112,78.07,6.8,"As of 2023, the global solar market was worth between $100 and 300 billion."],[112,84.96,4.13,"Wind energy, which comes from the Sun unevenly heating the atmosphere,"],[112,89.08,4.71,"was valued somewhere between $85 and 150 billion."],[112,93.8,3.92,"And hydropower, generated by the Sun moving the world’s water,"],[112,97.72,3.46,"was worth around $230 billion."],[112,101.35,4.46,"Sunshine also grows all kinds of food, and in 2022,"],[112,105.85,4.84,"the global agriculture market reached approximately $5 trillion.”"],[112,111.31,4.09,"The divine accountant knows Daedalus can’t afford the Sun"],[112,115.4,3.21,"and that this is more of a cry for help than anything."],[112,118.78,3.46,"Helios, meanwhile, is still freshly horrified."],[112,122.24,3.79,"But the divine accountant’s not done enlightening the pair."],[112,126.37,1.88,"“As with any natural phenomenon,"],[112,128.25,3.25,"many of the Sun’s assets are tougher to quantify."],[112,131.54,5.84,"Of the 100 billion stars in the Milky Way, the Sun is unremarkably medium sized."],[112,137.47,2.25,"But to earthlings, it’s everything."],[112,139.72,3.38,"Earth likely formed from the Sun’s leftover materials,"],[112,143.1,3.09,"at just the right distance for water to remain liquid,"],[112,146.18,1.46,"so life could evolve."],[112,147.89,3.04,"The Sun also helped generate Earth’s atmosphere,"],[112,150.94,3.54,"which traps heat by absorbing approximately 70%"],[112,154.48,2.21,"of incoming solar radiation."],[112,156.82,3.04,"All of this has fundamentally shaped life on Earth."],[112,159.91,3.17,"So, just in case this wasn’t clear, without the Sun,"],[112,163.07,4.21,"Earth would be untethered, frozen, and generally inhospitable.”"],[112,167.41,3.59,"“But what’s most monetarily exciting here is the future."],[112,171.0,3.63,"After all, the Sun has another 5 billion years left."],[112,174.71,1.29,"With the right investments,"],[112,176.0,3.29,"the Sun and Earth can maintain their delicate balance"],[112,179.3,2.34,"and their assets can be ever appreciating."],[112,181.72,2.46,"Just looking directly at solar energy,"],[112,184.18,5.05,"the amount Earth receives from the Sun in one hour is enough to power humanity"],[112,189.27,1.0,"for a year."],[112,190.52,4.0,"Solar panel technology only started taking off in the 1970s"],[112,194.52,2.88,"and has since grown at an unprecedented pace,"],[112,197.4,4.3,"making leaps in efficiency, affordability, and implementation."],[112,201.74,2.0,"In the far future— who knows—"],[112,203.74,4.38,"perhaps humanity could even achieve the tantalizing theoretical physics feat"],[112,208.12,4.42,"of a so-called Dyson sphere or swarm that surrounds the Sun,"],[112,212.54,3.88,"capturing immense amounts of energy closer to the surface.”"],[112,216.54,3.34,"“Overall, the Sun is valued at orders of magnitude"],[112,219.88,2.84,"more than the earthly economy it enables,"],[112,222.72,3.04,"which is worth over $100 trillion total."],[112,225.85,3.13,"So, no one would be able to purchase it outright."],[112,228.97,4.75,"However, suppose Helios leased the Sun’s solar energy to Daedalus"],[112,233.73,2.54,"for whatever his project might be."],[112,236.48,3.38,"Going off what could reasonably be harvested in the near future,"],[112,239.86,3.88,"we can look at 2050, at which point, with the right investments,"],[112,243.74,5.59,"solar energy could generate an estimated 8,519 gigawatts annually."],[112,249.5,2.92,"Assuming a cost of 17 cents per kilowatt-hour—"],[112,252.41,2.25,"the US average— really rough approximation..."],[112,254.67,4.46,"but one year of exclusive use might reach about... $2 trillion.”"],[112,259.88,3.09,"The divine accountant is interrupted by laughter."],[112,263.05,3.29,"Daedalus confirms he can't afford the whole ordeal,"],[112,266.43,4.13,"and thanks the divine accountant for illuminating the Sun’s immense value"],[112,270.68,2.88,"and his own humble place in the universe."],[112,273.89,2.54,"He no longer wants to destroy anything."],[112,276.56,4.42,"Helios, meanwhile, is just relieved that the Sun’s death isn’t imminent,"],[112,281.15,3.17,"and apologizes for the loss of Daedalus’ son."],[112,284.86,4.63,"It dawns on them they actually have a lot more in common than they thought."],[112,289.66,3.17,"And as is typical of finance discussions,"],[112,292.87,3.5,"they both leave feeling hopeful and revitalised."],[113,1.91,4.88,"It's a cliché to say that mistakes, imperfections make something special,"],[113,6.79,1.5,"but they really, really do."],[113,8.34,2.17,"In art, embrace mistakes."],[113,10.67,2.13,"The mistake is the journey."],[113,12.8,3.46,"And it’s you getting better because your voice probably won’t crack there"],[113,16.26,3.38,"the next time— it’s just another moment to pivot from."],[113,19.89,4.5,"So never freak out about making mistakes, is really what it boils down to."],[113,24.85,3.96,"Hey, you! Yes, you. Is there music inside of you?"],[113,28.82,3.25,"We’ve recruited working musicians from throughout the industry"],[113,32.07,6.13,"to help you hear it, hold it, and share it with this wild and wonderful world."],[113,39.08,5.34,"At least for me, from between the studio and a live situation,"],[113,44.5,3.75,"some of the adjustment for me means like letting go"],[113,48.25,3.21,"of a type of obsessive perfectionism"],[113,51.88,6.38,"about the sound quality and also in performance,"],[113,59.68,4.92,"being willing to fall down and get dirty or make mistakes."],[113,64.94,3.88,"My first songs, my early songs are pretty embarrassing,"],[113,68.9,2.75,"but I think I was just kind of figuring it out."],[113,71.65,3.21,"And I was really literally just taking the poems that I had written,"],[113,74.86,2.79,"which were kind of cryptic and a little bit dark"],[113,77.66,2.38,"because I was like a moody adolescent,"],[113,80.49,1.58,"and putting them to melodies."],[113,82.08,4.34,"But I hadn’t really learned the importance of song structure."],[113,86.58,2.88,"It started out as a very personal outlet for me."],[113,89.46,4.05,"And then later I sort of learned how to write something that was more accessible,"],[113,93.51,2.54,"that people could understand where I was coming from,"],[113,96.05,2.42,"and maybe get some catharsis out of that."],[113,98.84,4.92,"You don’t just automatically jump immediately to perfection."],[113,103.89,2.21,"That's what's so great about experimentation."],[113,106.1,3.42,"What I’ve loved about collaborating with other songwriters and musicians"],[113,109.52,3.84,"is I hear them come out with some not so great ideas,"],[113,113.36,1.46,"some pretty lousy ideas."],[113,115.24,5.71,"And I realized that they will come up with eight lousy ideas or even like 15,"],[113,120.95,3.17,"and the 16th or 20th or 30th idea,"],[113,124.12,2.67,"suddenly it’s like, that is brilliant!"],[113,127.54,4.34,"And the listener will never know of the other 30 lousy ideas that it took"],[113,132.13,3.79,"to finally come to a sense of clarity and something locking in."],[113,135.92,3.67,"That’s not right. That’s not right. This is right! There it is."],[113,139.72,2.84,"But you have to be willing to let it come out."],[113,142.64,5.13,"Out of the thousands of songs you write, maybe five might be a hit. Maybe."],[113,147.77,2.13,"You’re really, really lucky as a songwriter"],[113,149.9,2.42,"if you get a number one record one time."],[113,152.36,6.71,"You could literally start at any age, you could literally not be able to sing,"],[113,159.16,3.46,"but as long as you understand what your thing is,"],[113,162.74,3.34,"there is no one type of way to songwrite or to produce"],[113,166.08,1.42,"or to do any of these things."],[113,167.5,3.25,"There’s no right way. It’s, what is my way?"],[113,170.83,3.13,"And then I find the appropriate people to collaborate with"],[113,174.13,3.92,"to make this a well-rounded thing, because that is what our industry is about,"],[113,178.05,1.17,"is collaboration."],[113,179.22,2.29,"Mark D. Sanders, he’s a writer in Nashville,"],[113,181.51,2.34,"he said to me once, songwriting is like fishing."],[113,183.85,3.8,"He said, you can stand in the water days on end and get nothing."],[113,187.89,2.34,"Or you can stand in the water for four seconds"],[113,190.23,3.13,"and catch the biggest fish you’ve ever caught in your entire life."],[113,193.4,2.88,"He goes, it’s not the outcome, it’s that you’re in the water."],[113,196.28,5.8,"So, if you think of songwriting and sessions as a fishing expedition,"],[113,202.41,4.5,"you can sit out there for hours and it wasn’t the day for it."],[113,206.91,4.55,"Or you can sit out there for no time and you had a great day."],[113,211.5,4.42,"So it’s very much just like a thing you do every day"],[113,216.09,3.13,"for the sake of the great ones that might happen today"],[113,219.22,1.58,"and might happen a week from now."],[113,220.8,2.96,"When I was young, I do remember those songs just [snaps]."],[113,223.8,3.67,"And there's another and another and another, and it still happens."],[113,227.47,2.96,"But after however many hundreds of songs,"],[113,230.48,2.13,"it becomes new you versus old you"],[113,232.6,4.55,"saying something that you’ve said from an entirely different perspective."],[113,237.28,3.04,"So as far as locking into a song"],[113,240.32,3.92,"and knowing when you’ve found the song’s proper path,"],[113,244.24,4.3,"if I was going to, not advise, but opine,"],[113,248.62,2.92,"I would say break all the rules."],[113,251.66,3.71,"Run through all the walls and do not conform."],[113,255.46,3.42,"And last but not least, sometimes we don't know our best work."],[113,259.09,3.09,"I’ve put songs away that I’ve years later played for people"],[113,262.17,5.34,"and have ended up on records because they had to tell me what it is."],[113,267.51,2.0,"You're going to have to listen to somebody"],[113,269.51,2.67,"and you're going to have to take advice from someone."],[113,272.31,5.21,"And if you can find that one person to trust with your process,"],[113,278.52,2.17,"you're really lucky to have that person."],[114,4.24,1.47,"So if you're like me,"],[114,5.74,3.1,"you might be feeling at least a bit unmoored"],[114,8.87,2.23,"by how fast everything is changing."],[114,11.64,3.97,"AI, our society, the world order."],[114,15.65,2.37,"And that's just since this morning."],[114,19.25,2.7,"I have two young daughters, and like a lot of us,"],[114,21.95,4.04,"I've been trying to make sense of the future they're growing up into."],[114,26.39,3.7,"And what helped me make sense of it actually wasn't looking forward."],[114,30.09,2.8,"It was going back, all the way back."],[114,33.23,2.4,"You see, two billion years ago,"],[114,35.67,2.57,"life on Earth was mostly single-celled"],[114,38.23,4.24,"until bacteria figured out a new trick, photosynthesis,"],[114,42.51,1.6,"which makes oxygen."],[114,44.51,3.2,"Now at the time, oxygen was poison."],[114,47.71,5.24,"It shredded the delicate chemistry that nearly all life on Earth depended on,"],[114,52.98,4.11,"and the planet changed faster than life could keep up with."],[114,57.85,4.1,"Some scientists call what followed the first mass extinction event"],[114,61.99,1.84,"in Earth's history."],[114,63.86,4.2,"But somewhere in that dying world, an extraordinary thing happened."],[114,68.1,4.8,"A larger cell swallowed a smaller one, and instead of digesting it,"],[114,72.94,1.27,"they merged."],[114,74.61,3.67,"The smaller cell became what we now call the mitochondria,"],[114,78.31,4.0,"the little powerhouse inside almost every complex cell on earth."],[114,82.75,3.57,"That merger created an energy surplus so vast,"],[114,86.35,2.17,"it funded everything that followed."],[114,88.55,3.3,"Larger cells, bodies, brains."],[114,91.89,1.64,"Every breath you take"],[114,93.56,4.2,"is still powered by the descendants of that ancient partnership."],[114,97.76,2.87,"That one accident in a dying world"],[114,100.63,3.14,"is the reason everyone in this room is alive today."],[114,104.57,3.4,"Biologists call these moments major transitions"],[114,108.0,4.71,"when separate entities stop competing and start building a new whole."],[114,113.11,2.57,"Like how molecules became cells,"],[114,115.71,1.9,"cells became bodies"],[114,117.65,1.9,"and individuals became societies."],[114,119.58,3.87,"Every rung on that ladder was climbed through mergers."],[114,124.09,4.07,"Now we're on the cusp of the next major transition,"],[114,128.19,3.44,"the merger of humans and AI."],[114,131.63,1.17,"That's right."],[114,132.83,1.37,"We're going to eat the AI."],[114,135.47,1.8,"Now I know what you’re thinking, OK,"],[114,137.27,2.2,"maybe you’re rolling your eyes."],[114,139.5,1.2,"Maybe you're laughing,"],[114,140.7,1.64,"maybe you feel nervous -- it’s OK."],[114,142.34,3.7,"I felt all of those things the first time I heard myself say it."],[114,146.08,1.33,"So let me explain."],[114,148.28,2.44,"For 15 years, I worked on building AI."],[114,150.71,2.07,"I started one of the early AI companies."],[114,152.82,2.4,"I raised a quarter of a billion dollars to do it,"],[114,155.22,1.9,"and I sold my business to Google."],[114,157.15,1.17,"And not long ago,"],[114,158.35,1.17,"I was at a private event"],[114,159.56,4.74,"with many of the leaders building the AIs we all use every day."],[114,164.33,1.4,"People you'd recognize."],[114,166.16,1.2,"And I asked them"],[114,167.4,3.9,"how many believe there's more than a 10 percent chance"],[114,171.34,4.27,"that AI kills most of humanity in the next 20 years."],[114,176.27,2.44,"Almost every hand went up."],[114,179.44,4.54,"The people building these systems know how dangerous they are,"],[114,184.01,1.97,"but they're trapped in a race"],[114,186.02,4.47,"where anyone who slows down gets overtaken by someone who doesn't."],[114,191.12,4.64,"If one company pauses for safety, another one takes the market."],[114,195.79,4.3,"If a country stops to regulate, another one races ahead."],[114,200.53,3.7,"Every AI founder has had the same conversation with themselves"],[114,204.27,1.17,"late at night."],[114,205.44,1.83,"You lie there and you think,"],[114,207.3,3.3,"if I don't build this, someone worse will."],[114,211.24,3.1,"AI is the oxygen crisis of our era,"],[114,214.38,2.8,"and it's coming, whether we're ready or not."],[114,217.55,1.4,"So what do we do?"],[114,219.65,1.74,"When a lot of people think about AI,"],[114,221.38,2.34,"they think about what it will do to us,"],[114,223.72,1.87,"what jobs it will take,"],[114,225.62,3.57,"what we should do to slow it down or regulate it."],[114,229.19,2.3,"And those are important questions,"],[114,231.5,2.77,"but they're actually downstream of a much deeper question,"],[114,234.3,4.4,"which is what happens if AI stays separate from us?"],[114,239.47,2.7,"Right now, your AI lives on the other side of a screen."],[114,242.17,2.04,"You ask it a question: it answers."],[114,244.24,2.47,"You close your laptop and it's gone."],[114,247.14,5.77,"But while your laptop is closed, the AI keeps getting better at your job."],[114,253.28,3.7,"And if we stay separate, the AI is not your tool."],[114,257.02,1.37,"It's your replacement."],[114,258.72,4.17,"One that gets smarter and faster and cheaper every week."],[114,263.53,2.54,"It doesn't take much to notice what happened"],[114,266.1,3.7,"the last time a new apex intelligence arrived here on Earth."],[114,269.83,1.53,"That intelligence was us."],[114,271.7,2.04,"And since we got here,"],[114,273.77,3.44,"we've driven to extinction every competing intelligence"],[114,277.24,2.7,"between Homo sapiens and Pan troglodytes."],[114,280.24,3.7,"Today, we keep our closest animal ancestors, chimpanzees,"],[114,283.98,1.17,"in reserves"],[114,285.15,2.84,"for their protection from us."],[114,288.49,3.34,"Without a merger, AI isn’t a partner -- it’s a rival."],[114,292.76,1.47,"So what's the good news?"],[114,294.93,4.0,"The good news is a merger isn't something we need to decide to start."],[114,298.93,3.3,"It's something we need to notice that we are already in."],[114,302.6,2.6,"When did you stop remembering phone numbers?"],[114,305.77,3.3,"There was no moment you decided to forget them."],[114,309.11,3.17,"They just moved from your head to your pocket."],[114,312.31,1.83,"Your calendar probably went next."],[114,314.18,3.2,"Then little judgment calls you used to make for yourself."],[114,318.15,2.84,"The tool was great at it, so you let the tool do it."],[114,321.02,3.5,"And while something left your head, a better thing took its place."],[114,324.82,3.24,"You stopped checking your spelling and you started writing."],[114,328.62,2.14,"You stopped remembering how to get there,"],[114,330.79,3.6,"and you started thinking about what you’d say when you arrived."],[114,334.83,3.1,"And notice how we keep pulling these tools closer to us."],[114,338.27,2.1,"The mainframe was in a whole other building."],[114,340.4,3.04,"We put the PC on our desk, the smartphone in our pocket,"],[114,343.47,1.94,"the smartwatch on our wrist,"],[114,345.41,1.74,"smart glasses on our face."],[114,347.48,4.64,"Every step closer to our minds, closer to the speed of thought."],[114,352.45,3.2,"And even that boundary is starting to blur."],[114,356.05,3.24,"Right now, paralyzed patients are typing with their thoughts."],[114,359.62,3.74,"Neural implants are restoring speech, vision and hearing"],[114,363.39,1.7,"to people who've lost them."],[114,365.13,3.97,"Noland Arbaugh, the first person to receive a Neuralink brain implant,"],[114,369.13,2.64,"says that using it feels like using the force."],[114,372.34,2.33,"The machine doesn’t feel like a machine --"],[114,374.67,1.5,"it feels like him."],[114,376.67,1.53,"And you may not realize it,"],[114,378.21,4.97,"but a technology we all use every day is learning to hear our thoughts."],[114,383.51,2.57,"The Face ID system used to unlock your phone"],[114,386.12,3.44,"is being repositioned into headphones and glasses,"],[114,389.55,4.4,"where it can recognize microscopic muscle movements just beneath our skin,"],[114,393.99,2.67,"movements imperceptible to the human eye."],[114,397.16,2.47,"The system that first learned to recognize us"],[114,399.66,2.0,"is now starting to see inside."],[114,402.43,3.9,"Today, a brain implant has about 1,000 connections into the brain,"],[114,406.37,1.87,"and soon it will have 10,000,"],[114,408.27,2.9,"and then 100,000 and then a million."],[114,411.18,2.97,"At 1,000 connections, you can restore movement."],[114,414.18,1.94,"At 10,000, speech."],[114,416.48,1.74,"At a million connections,"],[114,418.25,4.47,"you stop restoring what was lost and you start adding what was never there."],[114,423.22,3.5,"Imagine learning a language in an afternoon,"],[114,426.72,1.97,"a new skill overnight."],[114,429.03,3.24,"Maybe even sharing a memory with a friend"],[114,432.26,4.21,"and having it feel just as real to them as it felt to you."],[114,437.37,3.17,"The thing about this future is it doesn't require new technology."],[114,440.54,3.64,"It just requires more of the same technology."],[114,445.11,2.24,"Someone you work with will get it first,"],[114,447.34,2.4,"and you'll hold out for a while,"],[114,449.75,2.0,"the way you did with the smartphone,"],[114,451.78,2.37,"but eventually you won't."],[114,454.15,3.27,"The advantages of integration will be hard to compete with."],[114,457.79,3.17,"Think about what we even do when we use a computer today."],[114,460.99,4.74,"You move a picture of an arrow around until it touches a picture of a folder."],[114,465.76,2.64,"Inside, there are pictures of files."],[114,468.4,3.8,"You click, you scroll, you drag."],[114,472.24,2.6,"Sending a file to a colleague takes a whole minute."],[114,474.87,3.14,"Two of those seconds were the decision."],[114,478.04,2.97,"The rest of it is the equivalent of walking across your house"],[114,481.04,1.47,"to flip a light switch."],[114,482.91,2.77,"With a system that can hear our thoughts,"],[114,485.72,1.3,"you skip the walk."],[114,488.22,3.24,"The further this goes, the more deeply we integrate with AI,"],[114,491.49,4.8,"the harder it will be to tell where our thoughts end and AI begins."],[114,496.73,1.2,"For example,"],[114,497.96,3.14,"what's the square root of 117 trillion?"],[114,501.7,1.27,"Go ahead, I'll wait."],[114,504.5,2.27,"If you tried to answer that, you felt something."],[114,506.8,3.14,"You felt a gap, a pause between the question arriving"],[114,509.97,1.74,"and anything starting to form."],[114,512.14,3.17,"And you've lived your whole life inside that gap."],[114,515.35,2.54,"Close it and the answer arrives instantly,"],[114,517.91,1.77,"the way you know your name,"],[114,519.72,1.3,"like a memory."],[114,521.02,3.54,"One that bridges the distance between human and AI."],[114,525.05,2.64,"Now I think we'll choose to merge"],[114,527.72,3.84,"because the alternative, being replaced, is far worse."],[114,532.06,4.07,"But every major transition in the history of life has a condition:"],[114,536.5,2.7,"the parts have to remember that they are parts."],[114,540.1,2.87,"A cell in your body wants to grow and replicate."],[114,543.01,2.4,"And normally its growth serves you."],[114,545.44,2.14,"Your cells grow so you can grow."],[114,548.04,3.24,"But sometimes a cell forgets that it belongs to a whole."],[114,551.32,2.3,"It starts growing without limit."],[114,553.95,2.23,"And if your immune system fails to catch it,"],[114,556.22,1.6,"we call that cancer."],[114,558.32,4.5,"The thing about untreated cancer is it succeeds for a while."],[114,562.86,1.74,"The tumor grows,"],[114,564.6,3.44,"but eventually, the cancer kills the host,"],[114,568.03,1.53,"which kills the cancer."],[114,569.93,1.9,"A part forgets the whole,"],[114,571.87,3.04,"and the whole dies, which kills the part."],[114,575.47,2.84,"This pattern repeats at every scale."],[114,579.11,2.2,"Our civilization is itself a merger."],[114,581.71,3.17,"It is the sometimes fragile, invisible agreement"],[114,584.91,3.17,"that millions of strangers will share institutions,"],[114,588.12,2.3,"sacrifices and a future."],[114,591.22,3.57,"No one person built this system, and no one group controls it,"],[114,594.83,1.74,"but we all rely on it."],[114,596.93,3.57,"And as AI arrives and the world gets more turbulent,"],[114,600.5,3.9,"every part of the society we depend on for our survival"],[114,604.43,2.04,"will be tempted to defect."],[114,607.1,3.34,"People who lose their livelihoods will feel abandoned."],[114,610.91,3.3,"People who keep theirs will feel entitled to look away."],[114,614.75,2.97,"And bit by bit, the agreement frays."],[114,618.82,1.8,"Major transitions fail"],[114,620.65,3.27,"when the parts break before they can adapt."],[114,624.25,3.44,"And for us to make it to a merger with AI,"],[114,627.72,2.2,"we have to stay merged with each other."],[114,631.43,4.54,"Major transitions fail when we don't make that leap."],[114,636.73,4.97,"The thing about the future is we all have to share the same one,"],[114,641.74,2.4,"and we either all make it there together"],[114,644.17,2.4,"or we don't make it there at all."],[114,647.08,5.04,"Two billion years ago, the first merger gave us our cells,"],[114,652.12,2.77,"and the ones that followed gave us our bodies,"],[114,654.88,2.47,"our minds and our civilization."],[114,657.39,3.2,"Every beautiful, difficult thing that followed,"],[114,660.62,2.97,"followed because the parts held together."],[114,664.86,5.51,"Now our oxygen crisis is arriving, and it will not be gentle."],[114,671.1,3.17,"Jobs will change and some will disappear."],[114,674.64,3.47,"Institutions will shake and some will fail."],[114,678.64,3.24,"Surviving this will take everything that we have."],[114,682.65,2.74,"So here's what I'm asking from all of us,"],[114,685.41,2.1,"for every day from now on."],[114,688.25,1.64,"Hold together."],[114,690.65,3.27,"Do not indulge the fantasy"],[114,693.92,3.07,"that your side can let the other side sink"],[114,697.03,2.0,"and somehow stay dry."],[114,699.56,2.97,"The universe has been doing this for a long time,"],[114,702.53,3.7,"and the mergers that worked left descendants."],[114,706.24,3.2,"The ones that failed left fossils."],[114,709.44,2.4,"I want my daughters to be descendants."],[114,711.88,2.1,"And I want yours to be, too."],[114,714.48,1.17,"Thank you."],[115,4.26,5.06,"I want to be long-winded and unclear."],[115,11.14,1.8,"It's not a common opening line."],[115,13.36,3.6,"Most people won't tell us when they're going to be long-winded,"],[115,17.0,1.2,"but we know."],[115,18.76,4.2,"We can tell pretty quickly if someone’s going to get to the point"],[115,23.0,4.0,"or if we are in for a long and painful conversation."],[115,28.08,2.84,"And this is especially true at work."],[115,31.88,5.38,"Every year, poor communication costs businesses hundreds of billions of dollars"],[115,37.28,1.44,"in lost productivity."],[115,39.1,4.0,"And I have to confess, [in] the early part of my career,"],[115,43.12,3.3,"I was responsible for at least some of that."],[115,47.38,1.36,"But I'm not alone."],[115,49.26,3.78,"Hands up if you work with someone who is long winded or unclear."],[115,54.8,1.7,"That's a lot of hands."],[115,57.02,4.16,"Now I’m sure we are all thinking about people outside this room, right?"],[115,61.86,2.38,"Except for those of you pointing at each other."],[115,65.22,3.68,"But what would happen if we asked the people outside this room?"],[115,69.78,2.76,"They might be thinking about us."],[115,73.54,2.9,"And while our goal is to be clear and concise,"],[115,76.46,1.68,"do we really know how?"],[115,79.94,1.96,"Every time we communicate,"],[115,81.92,3.9,"we have an opportunity to create clarity"],[115,85.86,1.14,"or confusion."],[115,88.12,4.2,"And clarity begins from the very first minute."],[115,93.18,1.62,"If you get the first minute right,"],[115,94.82,3.22,"you set your conversation up for success."],[115,98.84,1.46,"Get it wrong [and]"],[115,100.32,3.52,"your conversations will be longer, less effective"],[115,103.86,1.76,"and more frustrating."],[115,108.26,6.32,"Now on the off chance we could all use a little more clarity,"],[115,114.6,2.3,"I'm going to share a tip with you today."],[115,116.92,3.82,"A tip that will help each of you improve the success"],[115,120.78,1.76,"of your communication at work."],[115,123.24,4.36,"A tip that has been tested by more than 75,000 people around the world."],[115,128.22,4.22,"And a tip that will help each of you get the first minute right."],[115,132.9,1.2,"And here it is."],[115,134.12,2.88,"There are three things we must include"],[115,137.02,2.44,"at the start of our work messages."],[115,140.18,3.32,"And those three things begin with T,"],[115,143.52,2.22,"I and P."],[115,146.48,1.14,"It's a TIP."],[115,148.82,2.1,"Now the first part of the tip is the T,"],[115,150.94,2.62,"and T stands for topic."],[115,153.86,2.58,"Every conversation at work needs a clear topic."],[115,156.46,2.0,"When the topic is clear,"],[115,158.46,1.94,"everyone knows what we're talking about."],[115,160.42,1.26,"Everyone's in sync."],[115,161.7,1.78,"Everybody understands."],[115,164.54,3.7,"Unfortunately, reality isn't always like this, is it?"],[115,169.14,3.86,"How often are you in a conversation and you find yourself thinking,"],[115,173.02,1.96,"what are you talking about?"],[115,176.48,3.84,"That happens when the topic is not clear."],[115,180.74,2.34,"Now usually the consequences are pretty minor --"],[115,183.1,2.96,"a little bit of wasted time, some minor frustration --"],[115,186.1,5.06,"but sometimes, the consequences are more significant."],[115,192.08,1.48,"In 2006,"],[115,193.58,2.68,"I was part of an engineering team writing a bid --"],[115,196.28,2.86,"a proposal -- that was worth a lot of money to our company."],[115,199.58,2.06,"And a couple of days before the big meeting,"],[115,201.66,1.48,"my boss came up to me and said,"],[115,203.16,3.46,"\"Hey, Chris, I need you to change the meeting from Tuesday to Thursday.\""],[115,206.64,2.32,"So off I went and I changed the meeting."],[115,208.98,2.22,"I typed an email, sent it to the client saying,"],[115,211.22,2.02,"\"Something’s come up, we've got to change.\""],[115,213.26,2.74,"And about a minute after I hit send, my phone rings."],[115,216.74,1.42,"It's my boss."],[115,218.18,2.12,"He had one question:"],[115,222.06,1.94,"\"Why did you change the meeting?\""],[115,225.7,3.7,"You see, he was talking about our one-to-one,"],[115,229.42,2.34,"and I was thinking about the client meeting."],[115,232.26,1.4,"The lack of a clear topic"],[115,233.68,4.86,"meant he and I were thinking about two very different things."],[115,239.28,2.82,"Now that might be the single biggest contribution I’ve made"],[115,242.12,2.04,"to the cost of bad communication,"],[115,244.18,1.84,"but it also marks the moment"],[115,246.04,4.46,"at which I began to be interested in improving how we communicate at work."],[115,251.72,2.92,"So all our messages have to begin with a clear topic."],[115,254.66,1.52,"But how? How do we do this?"],[115,256.7,1.44,"It's really easy."],[115,258.46,2.18,"Start your message with one sentence"],[115,260.66,3.32,"that names the topic you want to talk about,"],[115,264.02,2.06,"and the more specific, the better."],[115,266.62,2.96,"You could say, \"I'd like to talk about July sales figures.\""],[115,269.6,2.96,"\"I want to talk about Project Everest's timeline.\""],[115,272.58,2.96,"Or \"Can I talk about our one-to-one this week?\""],[115,276.42,2.14,"One message, one sentence"],[115,278.58,2.2,"that lets your audience know what the topic is."],[115,280.8,1.48,"They won't be thinking,"],[115,282.32,2.0,"\"What are you talking about?\""],[115,285.64,2.5,"Now having a clear topic is a great start,"],[115,288.16,1.6,"but it's not enough."],[115,289.78,3.18,"We need the second part of the tip,"],[115,292.98,1.34,"which is the I."],[115,294.34,2.32,"And I stands for intent."],[115,296.68,4.92,"Intent is what we want the other person to do with our message."],[115,302.4,3.14,"Have you ever been in a conversation and a few minutes in thought,"],[115,305.56,1.68,"\"Why are you telling me this?\""],[115,308.5,3.6,"That happens when the intent isn't clear,"],[115,312.12,2.38,"when the speaker hasn't told you what to do"],[115,314.52,2.56,"with the information that they are giving you."],[115,318.24,4.14,"Now there’s a process in our brains called selective attention,"],[115,322.4,4.16,"and this filters incoming information and tells you what is important"],[115,326.58,1.32,"and what is not."],[115,328.44,2.26,"And if you don't know what to do with a message,"],[115,330.72,2.52,"your brain says, not important."],[115,333.28,1.92,"And we stop listening."],[115,336.38,1.44,"At the beginning of this talk,"],[115,337.84,1.94,"I told you I would give you a tip"],[115,339.8,3.14,"that would help improve the success of your communication at work."],[115,342.96,3.18,"That told your brains what to do with this information."],[115,346.62,1.18,"If I hadn't done that,"],[115,347.82,3.12,"I could be faced with a room full of people smiling politely"],[115,350.98,2.94,"while all thinking, \"Why are you telling us this?\""],[115,355.44,2.42,"Now that is something we want to avoid at work."],[115,357.88,1.36,"But there is another,"],[115,359.26,3.7,"and perhaps more important reason to make your intent clear."],[115,363.76,3.78,"You want to avoid frustrating the people you're talking to."],[115,368.88,2.14,"I'd had a particularly bad day,"],[115,371.06,2.12,"and I walked into my colleague Tina's office,"],[115,373.2,2.0,"and I sat down with my head in my hands,"],[115,375.22,4.44,"and I told her how I'd messed up on an important call with a senior leader."],[115,380.18,3.32,"And when I finished talking, Tina very, very helpfully said,"],[115,383.52,3.16,"\"Oh, why don't you try this and maybe we can avoid this next time.\""],[115,386.72,2.24,"And she was giving me all sorts of advice."],[115,389.9,1.92,"The more she spoke,"],[115,391.84,2.4,"the more frustrated I became."],[115,394.84,1.48,"I didn't want her help."],[115,396.72,1.78,"And that must have been quite obvious"],[115,398.52,2.02,"because she just put up her hands and said,"],[115,400.56,1.54,"\"You clearly don't want my help."],[115,402.14,1.48,"So why are you here?\""],[115,403.64,1.54,"I said, \"Tina, I'm really sorry."],[115,405.2,1.84,"I just want to get this off my chest."],[115,407.06,2.2,"Can I just talk for a minute?\""],[115,409.28,2.18,"And like a mother talking to a small child,"],[115,411.5,1.42,"she rolled her eyes and said,"],[115,412.94,2.16,"\"Well, why didn't you start with that?\""],[115,416.66,4.34,"We need to tell our audience what to do with the information we're giving them."],[115,421.02,3.26,"Tina was working hard from the moment I started talking."],[115,424.96,1.16,"And all the way through,"],[115,426.14,2.98,"she was working hard to help me and I didn't want or need that."],[115,429.14,2.18,"She was annoyed and rightly so."],[115,431.66,3.12,"So when you start your message, after you've given a clear topic,"],[115,434.8,1.84,"tell your audience what to do with it."],[115,436.64,2.26,"And again, you can do this with one line."],[115,438.92,1.48,"Make your intent clear."],[115,440.42,2.12,"If you have a question, say,"],[115,442.56,1.38,"\"Can I ask you a question?\""],[115,444.52,2.58,"If you want someone to make a decision, say,"],[115,447.12,1.96,"\"I'd like your help with this decision.\""],[115,449.82,3.08,"If you want to get something off your chest, just say,"],[115,452.92,1.98,"\"Is it OK if I vent for a minute?\""],[115,456.0,2.6,"If you tell your audience what to do with your message,"],[115,458.62,2.16,"not only will their brains pay more attention,"],[115,460.8,2.48,"you will avoid frustrating them."],[115,463.68,1.24,"That's kind of useful."],[115,466.44,1.8,"So we've made our topic clear."],[115,468.64,2.72,"We've told our audience what we intend for them to do."],[115,471.38,2.12,"It's time to get down into the detail, right?"],[115,473.52,1.44,"Get into the message."],[115,475.36,1.24,"Wrong."],[115,477.04,3.36,"This is where we need the third part of the tip."],[115,480.78,1.22,"The P."],[115,482.02,1.68,"And P stands for point."],[115,484.28,3.72,"Point is the most important part of our message."],[115,488.42,3.06,"If you only had five seconds to speak,"],[115,491.5,1.5,"it's what you would say."],[115,493.5,4.7,"And usually it's what your audience cares about the most."],[115,499.42,2.36,"When I was a kid, my dad played a game with me."],[115,501.78,3.08,"If we were going on a journey, I'd go, \"Dad, where are we going?\""],[115,504.9,2.34,"And he'd say, \"Well, first, we're going to the car"],[115,507.24,2.34,"and then we’ll get in the car, leave the driveway,"],[115,509.58,2.96,"turn left onto the main street until we get to the post office,"],[115,512.58,2.72,"and then turn right until we go to a big yellow building.\""],[115,515.32,2.24,"I just wanted to scream, \"Where are we going?\""],[115,517.88,1.82,"I wanted the short version."],[115,519.72,2.42,"I think he just wanted to wind me up a little bit."],[115,522.6,2.86,"But fast forward a few years,"],[115,525.48,2.52,"I was doing the same thing at work."],[115,528.92,1.54,"Someone would ask me a question,"],[115,530.48,4.32,"or I’d start a conversation, and I would give all the detail,"],[115,534.82,2.36,"step by step information."],[115,537.64,1.78,"And every so often --"],[115,539.44,1.62,"OK, quite often --"],[115,541.08,3.38,"people would say, \"Chris, just get to the point.\""],[115,545.9,1.24,"And that's what we need."],[115,547.16,1.84,"We need a point, we need a headline,"],[115,549.02,3.26,"something that tells us where this conversation is going."],[115,553.26,3.24,"Now, of course, there's a lot more information we could include,"],[115,556.52,1.34,"but that comes later."],[115,557.88,3.1,"That comes after we've set up the audience"],[115,561.0,2.82,"to understand what the conversation is about."],[115,563.84,2.96,"And we can do this quite simply,"],[115,566.82,1.4,"as the military likes to say,"],[115,568.24,2.46,"“Put the bottom line up front.”"],[115,571.46,1.18,"In one sentence,"],[115,572.68,2.8,"deliver the most important piece of information."],[115,575.5,2.7,"\"I want to give a refund for a late delivery.\""],[115,578.22,2.6,"\"Our project launch is going to be delayed by a month.\""],[115,580.86,2.1,"Say the most important thing,"],[115,582.98,2.86,"and your audience will know where that conversation is going"],[115,585.88,3.36,"and they won't be thinking, \"What is your point?\""],[115,591.08,4.4,"Now if you start your conversation with topic, intent and point,"],[115,595.52,3.42,"you are starting with a clear and concise introduction,"],[115,598.96,3.02,"an introduction that answers the three questions we all have:"],[115,602.02,1.72,"What are you talking about?"],[115,603.76,1.72,"Why are you telling me this?"],[115,605.5,1.82,"And what is your point?"],[115,608.04,1.46,"Because here's the truth."],[115,610.18,1.84,"Every time we communicate,"],[115,612.04,3.02,"it is an opportunity to create clarity"],[115,615.08,1.16,"or confusion."],[115,617.06,4.56,"And topic, intent and point is clarity."],[115,622.36,1.68,"So I have a challenge for you."],[115,624.48,2.62,"The next time you start a conversation at work,"],[115,627.12,1.78,"the next time you write a message,"],[115,628.92,3.52,"take a moment and think to yourself,"],[115,632.46,1.36,"what is my topic?"],[115,634.52,3.5,"What do I intend for the other person to do with this message?"],[115,638.04,2.86,"And what point do I want to deliver?"],[115,642.18,3.4,"Imagine working somewhere where everybody did this."],[115,647.14,3.5,"Imagine if everyone always knew what to do with the message you gave them,"],[115,650.66,1.58,"or understood what it was about."],[115,652.26,3.16,"How much smoother, how much easier would our jobs be?"],[115,656.76,2.14,"That, ladies and gentlemen,"],[115,658.92,2.74,"is the power of the first minute."],[115,662.72,1.14,"Thank you."],[116,1.22,1.5,"- Canada reminded me"],[116,2.72,3.22,"that authenticity isn't something we create."],[116,5.94,2.42,"It's something we recognize when we're present"],[116,8.36,1.02,"enough to see it."],[116,17.48,1.94,"- For me, traveling across Canada"],[116,19.42,2.9,"with two other National Geographic photographers was an"],[116,22.32,2.12,"honor just to be able to see them work."],[116,31.78,2.64,"- One lesson I took away from my trip in Canada is"],[116,34.42,3.68,"that people and places can really surprise you."],[116,38.1,2.86,"There is a lot of natural wonder to be found."],[116,42.8,2.92,"There was a day when I was flying in a helicopter"],[116,45.72,1.7,"over Iceberg Alley."],[116,47.42,1.2,"This is a tour that you could do."],[116,49.76,3.2,"Off in the distance, I could see that there was this one"],[116,52.96,3.52,"beautiful berg that just had these like swoops"],[116,56.48,4.74,"and curves that appeal to me in this way that's very deep."],[116,62.6,3.28,"Photographing icebergs from the air is a real challenge."],[116,65.88,3.24,"I'm trying to get the sun directly behind the berg at all"],[116,69.12,1.22,"of these different angles,"],[116,70.34,3.82,"but the helicopter also moves really quickly, so I have"],[116,74.16,1.12,"to shoot instinctively."],[116,76.38,3.72,"As I'm taking the picture too, I can't photograph"],[116,80.1,1.98,"through the window at an angle."],[116,82.08,2.78,"The glass will actually distort the image."],[116,84.86,3.06,"So I always try to shoot straight through my window."],[116,87.92,1.1,"What can I say? I love icebergs!"],[116,94.48,2.28,"One of the techniques I like to use when I'm trying"],[116,96.76,2.16,"to capture the essence of people in place is"],[116,98.92,1.66,"to use an ultra-wide lens."],[116,101.5,3.14,"It forces me to get really close to things, to make"],[116,104.64,1.46,"that space really intimate."],[116,106.98,2.86,"And you can see it in the pictures, when you are close"],[116,109.84,1.76,"to something, you feel like you're"],[116,111.6,1.42,"standing right next to it."],[116,114.55,2.06,"What I'm photographing isn't just that subject,"],[116,116.61,2.8,"but it's actually the entire environment around them."],[116,126.03,3.02,"- So what really blew me away in Canada was the incredible"],[116,129.04,3.14,"diversity of people, from incredible cooks"],[116,132.19,1.74,"to foragers, to artists."],[116,135.59,1.8,"When you are immersed in photography,"],[116,137.39,1.86,"you're constantly studying your horizon"],[116,139.25,2.82,"for potential pictures that have meaning to the place,"],[116,142.07,1.22,"to the people that you are with."],[116,144.59,1.56,"That is also delicate"],[116,146.15,2.42,"because if you start to show too much interest"],[116,148.57,2.72,"with your eyes and start paying attention too much to"],[116,151.29,3.78,"that person, that person will feel it and will react to it."],[116,155.07,2.26,"And you might change that quality, that fleeting,"],[116,157.33,1.68,"that fragile beauty."],[116,159.01,3.38,"That is something that is psychologically really interesting"],[116,162.39,1.94,"and that you must play with very carefully."],[116,166.95,1.72,"I wanted to photograph the community"],[116,168.67,2.22,"of musicians from Newfoundland, so I went"],[116,170.89,1.48,"to meet Charlie at this theater."],[116,173.93,1.88,"Charlie was quite reserved."],[116,175.81,2.96,"I had to get a picture out of it, which was, you know,"],[116,178.77,1.2,"in a way quite challenging."],[116,181.49,3.6,"This is when the experience comes in, feeling the type"],[116,185.09,3.5,"of character you're faced with, making them comfortable,"],[116,188.59,3.32,"trying to understand, trying to engage, talking, laughing,"],[116,191.91,3.36,"joking, and slowly movement starts to happen."],[116,195.27,5.0,"Guys open up. In this case, decided to lower my camera,"],[116,200.29,1.66,"keeping the frame looking down."],[116,201.95,2.16,"There's nothing between me and that person."],[116,204.11,1.9,"And then yeah, some magic might happen."],[116,213.61,1.2,"- When I went to Canada,"],[116,214.81,3.3,"I was expecting these incredible experiences,"],[116,218.11,2.32,"adventures in the wild."],[116,220.43,2.2,"What I left with was so much more."],[116,224.25,3.38,"My technique for capturing images"],[116,227.63,2.82,"is just listening."],[116,230.45,4.22,"It's not about the tools you carry, the latest camera."],[116,235.53,1.42,"It's nothing about that."],[116,239.47,2.04,"It's like when I met Bobbi Rose in the"],[116,241.51,1.26,"Cirque of the Unclimbables."],[116,243.91,1.26,"I traveled with Joel"],[116,245.17,3.7,"and Bobbi Rose to Glacier Lake in Nahanni National Park."],[116,250.15,4.48,"She's taking youth into these really remote,"],[116,254.63,1.64,"incredible landscapes"],[116,256.27,2.78,"and reconnecting them with that knowledge."],[116,261.79,2.94,"She reminded me that depth takes time."],[116,266.33,2.42,"We need time for our assumptions to fall away."],[116,269.71,2.66,"When you create that trust, build"],[116,272.37,1.96,"that relationship, they will guide you."],[116,274.33,4.0,"They will lead you to the stories that really matter."],[117,3.83,1.77,"Every time I give one of these talks,"],[117,5.63,1.2,"I have a routine."],[117,7.37,2.73,"I start by showing up early, and I do the sound check."],[117,10.1,1.17,"I get the mic,"],[117,11.27,2.2,"and then I go in the halls, and I walk around,"],[117,13.47,1.27,"and I look at the audience"],[117,14.73,2.2,"because I want to get a sense of the vibe."],[117,16.97,4.6,"And really, where my mind starts to go is: prepare."],[117,21.6,2.23,"I get backstage, I sit down,"],[117,23.87,2.1,"and I start to think about all the things"],[117,26.0,2.13,"that I wish were a little bit different --"],[117,28.17,3.53,"the things that would be better if they were my way."],[117,31.73,2.47,"So I wish the room was a little bit cooler."],[117,34.23,2.0,"I wish the stage was a little bit bigger."],[117,36.27,3.23,"I wish that the audience didn’t look so hungover --"],[117,39.53,1.97,"no, you guys look great, not you."],[117,41.53,1.9,"Sometimes, though, they look hungover,"],[117,43.47,1.33,"and I get worried,"],[117,44.83,3.07,"and I start focusing on all of the things that I can't control"],[117,47.93,2.23,"because ultimately, I want to do a good job."],[117,50.2,1.53,"You know, I’m a keynote speaker,"],[117,51.73,4.53,"and I've been hired to show up and perform and drive results."],[117,56.3,1.8,"And I'm worried that all these things"],[117,58.13,1.9,"might prevent me from doing that."],[117,61.0,2.47,"For leaders, you can probably relate"],[117,63.5,3.13,"because this is actually a very natural coping mechanism"],[117,66.63,1.17,"to being under pressure."],[117,67.83,1.97,"You as a leader have to drive results,"],[117,69.8,3.13,"[and] it is harder than it ever has been to get results right now."],[117,72.93,2.07,"You have increasing shareholder expectations"],[117,75.03,3.4,"at the same time as employee expectations are increasing,"],[117,78.47,1.6,"the rapid pace of change,"],[117,80.1,2.5,"the workforce generally is shifting so quickly,"],[117,82.63,3.03,"and yet you as a leader, you've still got to get those results."],[117,86.0,4.0,"And so leaders tend to focus on what it is that they have to do"],[117,90.0,1.2,"to make it work."],[117,91.2,2.17,"And they think about what they have to manage,"],[117,93.37,1.83,"every detail that they need to adjust,"],[117,95.23,1.73,"the processes and the people --"],[117,96.97,1.87,"what they need to do differently."],[117,99.33,3.03,"But the reality is, control is an illusion."],[117,102.9,3.33,"You can't control anything outside of yourself."],[117,106.27,3.27,"The only thing you can control is the way that you show up."],[117,110.13,3.5,"So what if instead of trying to force the world"],[117,113.67,3.17,"to fit into the way that you would like it to be,"],[117,116.87,3.03,"we tried something radical and unconventional."],[117,120.3,1.7,"We tried surrender."],[117,123.57,2.83,"Now surrender can feel like a touchy-feely word,"],[117,126.43,2.83,"especially when we're talking about leaders driving results."],[117,129.27,3.43,"But let me demonstrate surrender right now in this talk, OK?"],[117,132.73,1.67,"I am going to surrender."],[117,134.43,3.77,"I'm going to stop talking and be quiet until someone says, \"now.\""],[117,139.23,1.63,"Now? Oh, that was fast."],[117,140.9,1.27,"Well thank you."],[117,142.2,2.63,"It was so much easier than I thought it was going to be."],[117,144.87,2.0,"And that's kind of the point of surrender."],[117,146.9,2.1,"I was thinking you guys might take 30 seconds"],[117,149.03,1.5,"and make me stand here quietly,"],[117,150.53,1.73,"and it's uncomfortable to surrender."],[117,152.3,1.2,"That's part of the point."],[117,153.5,2.2,"That's part of why it's so hard to surrender."],[117,155.73,1.73,"But what happened in that moment?"],[117,157.47,3.1,"I stepped back and you, sir, you stepped in"],[117,160.6,1.93,"and a new dynamic was created."],[117,162.57,1.9,"And we did something different."],[117,164.5,2.4,"And we co-created this moment together."],[117,167.43,2.93,"Now the problem with the control mindset"],[117,170.4,2.8,"is that it doesn't drive results"],[117,173.23,2.1,"because it chokes adaptability."],[117,175.97,3.07,"And adaptive cultures win, not control cultures."],[117,179.07,4.43,"I studied this with Stanford Graduate Business School a couple years ago."],[117,183.53,2.67,"We looked at 243 companies --"],[117,186.23,2.87,"... their purpose and their strategy, their culture"],[117,189.1,1.37,"and obviously their results."],[117,190.47,2.6,"And we wanted to know which kinds of companies won."],[117,193.07,3.5,"And the companies that outperformed all other types"],[117,196.6,1.8,"were the adaptive companies."],[117,198.87,4.0,"They outperformed control companies three times"],[117,202.9,1.5,"over the course of three years."],[117,204.43,2.77,"Their revenue growth was significantly higher."],[117,207.23,2.27,"So how do you create an adaptive culture?"],[117,209.53,1.4,"That is the question."],[117,210.97,3.3,"Well, it's not by telling everyone that we're going to be adaptive now"],[117,214.3,3.0,"and controlling the adaptability of the room, right?"],[117,217.33,2.87,"The way that you create an adaptive culture"],[117,220.2,3.3,"is by understanding your role"],[117,223.53,1.7,"in the co-creation of it."],[117,225.23,2.3,"So to surrender is not passive."],[117,227.83,1.17,"It is not giving up."],[117,229.03,2.13,"It is a radical acceptance"],[117,231.17,2.8,"of what you need to do in that moment"],[117,234.0,2.33,"that you actually have control of."],[117,236.37,3.63,"Now we know that results come from actions."],[117,240.0,1.87,"People are trying to drive results"],[117,241.87,2.57,"by focusing on the actions that people are taking,"],[117,244.47,2.03,"which is that control mentality."],[117,246.53,1.43,"But most leaders,"],[117,248.0,2.5,"they think that if they want to change those actions,"],[117,250.5,2.73,"they have to implement a bunch of processes and procedures"],[117,253.27,2.03,"and maybe new workflows, right?"],[117,255.33,3.13,"Have a bunch of meetings, do KPIs and dashboards."],[117,258.5,3.73,"And when you take that approach, you are in that control mindset."],[117,262.27,2.1,"And what I found in 20 years of consulting"],[117,264.4,3.2,"is that people don't change because you implemented new processes."],[117,268.2,3.63,"People will act in alignment with what they believe."],[117,272.2,2.2,"And so if you want to change their behavior,"],[117,274.4,2.8,"you have to shift what it is that they believe."],[117,277.73,2.77,"And once again, I can't control your beliefs, right?"],[117,280.53,3.77,"I can’t say, OK, you’re now going to be comfortable with risk-taking, thank you."],[117,284.33,1.17,"That doesn't happen."],[117,285.53,2.97,"But what I can do is I can create an experience"],[117,288.53,1.3,"which I control,"],[117,289.87,1.87,"that will shift your belief."],[117,292.2,4.83,"And that belief will have you then proactively choose to take a new action"],[117,297.03,1.97,"which will drive a result."],[117,299.7,1.77,"What does it look like in action?"],[117,301.47,2.4,"I will give you an example from years ago."],[117,303.87,4.5,"I was hired to be the CHRO, the head of HR for a technology company."],[117,308.4,3.37,"And I had just had this personal moment of transformation"],[117,311.8,2.03,"in which I was embracing surrender in life,"],[117,313.87,1.93,"and I wanted to try it at work."],[117,315.83,2.93,"And so what happened was I got this new job,"],[117,318.8,3.13,"I went to my first board meeting, I felt very special being there,"],[117,321.93,1.4,"and the board let us know"],[117,323.37,2.7,"that we had to lay off 20 percent of the workforce,"],[117,326.1,1.27,"and I was in charge."],[117,328.97,1.17,"Surrender."],[117,330.17,4.07,"So I show up and I'm trying to figure out what to do."],[117,334.27,3.13,"And let me tell you how layoffs typically go in corporate America."],[117,337.43,3.2,"The executives know that it's happening months before anyone else,"],[117,340.67,2.27,"and they control the process."],[117,342.93,1.3,"They control the narrative,"],[117,344.27,2.57,"they get into rooms and they meet and discuss"],[117,346.83,2.23,"who are we going to lay off and how many people"],[117,349.1,2.03,"and how is that going to affect the budget"],[117,351.17,2.17,"and how are we going to control the narrative?"],[117,353.33,2.0,"And then when are we going to announce it?"],[117,355.33,2.9,"And then they drop the ax and then they deal with the fallout."],[117,358.27,1.27,"But I wanted to surrender."],[117,359.53,3.2,"And so I asked myself what surrender would look like in that moment,"],[117,362.77,1.5,"not controlling everyone else,"],[117,364.27,3.27,"but making a choice about the experience that I could create."],[117,367.57,2.8,"And so I showed up the next day at work,"],[117,370.4,1.57,"the day after the board meeting,"],[117,372.0,2.2,"and I gathered everyone together and I said,"],[117,374.2,3.5,"\"I've just been told that we need to lay off 20 percent of the workforce."],[117,377.73,2.63,"And I don't know who yet, and I don't know when exactly,"],[117,380.37,1.93,"but we're going to be figuring that out,"],[117,382.33,2.53,"and I'm going to let you know every step of the way.\""],[117,384.9,2.87,"That was an experience that I created in that moment"],[117,387.8,3.43,"that shifted the beliefs of the people that worked on that team."],[117,391.27,3.17,"Now some of the people’s beliefs that shifted were,"],[117,394.47,1.5,"\"I've got to go get a job.\""],[117,396.33,3.33,"They started applying for jobs and then they left."],[117,399.7,1.47,"Other people, though,"],[117,401.2,3.7,"were really grateful for the transparency,"],[117,404.93,2.7,"and it was something that they hadn't experienced before."],[117,407.63,1.6,"And so they said, \"You know what?"],[117,409.23,3.6,"I want to stay here and work with someone who’s going to tell me the truth"],[117,412.83,1.6,"and walk through the front door.”"],[117,414.43,1.43,"You can't mismanage the truth."],[117,415.9,2.73,"And you know what happened after the next couple of months"],[117,418.67,2.07,"as we watched people leave the organization?"],[117,420.73,2.13,"We realized we didn't even have to do layoffs"],[117,422.9,2.1,"because the company had rightsized itself."],[117,425.03,1.13,"And people who left,"],[117,426.2,3.17,"they left of their own accord -- on their own terms."],[117,429.4,3.13,"And the people who stayed, they were with us."],[117,432.53,2.77,"They were ready to adapt to the business circumstances"],[117,435.33,1.87,"because they knew that we had them."],[117,437.9,1.17,"Here's another example."],[117,439.07,2.6,"This was from one of our clients: Ocean Spray."],[117,441.67,1.6,"This was years ago."],[117,443.3,3.5,"They had multiple plants and one plant was in Kenosha, Wisconsin,"],[117,446.83,3.07,"and it was the worst-performing plant in the entire company."],[117,449.9,2.63,"So they hired a new guy named Tim to come in."],[117,452.57,1.43,"He was the plant manager,"],[117,454.03,2.0,"and his job was to turn it around."],[117,456.07,2.4,"And Tim was the surrendered leader."],[117,458.47,2.9,"So he was not interested in the command-and-control approach."],[117,461.37,2.0,"And he showed up and he was looking at --"],[117,463.4,2.3,"I mean, they had the highest costs in the system,"],[117,465.73,1.77,"they had high injury rates,"],[117,467.53,3.03,"they had hostile union relations."],[117,470.97,1.9,"And Tim came in and he said,"],[117,472.9,3.43,"\"What can I do to change the situation?\""],[117,477.07,2.6,"A lot of plant managers would come in and start talking"],[117,479.7,2.03,"about what you guys need to do differently."],[117,481.73,1.37,"\"You guys have to act right,"],[117,483.1,3.27,"if we don't turn this around, we're going to have to close the plant."],[117,486.4,3.07,"We're going to implement a bunch of new processes and procedures."],[117,489.47,3.07,"We're going to have meetings and make sure that you're on track.\""],[117,492.53,1.93,"That's the command-and-control approach."],[117,494.5,2.03,"But Tim surrendered, and he asked himself,"],[117,496.53,3.47,"\"What are the beliefs that people have at this plant that need to shift?\""],[117,500.03,2.7,"And the belief that he identified was largely resentment."],[117,502.73,3.27,"I mean, there was distrust between the employees and the leaders."],[117,506.0,1.37,"And he could understand why."],[117,507.4,2.83,"Because when the plant workers would show up in the morning,"],[117,510.27,3.5,"they would walk past all of the empty parking spaces"],[117,513.8,4.0,"that were \"reserved for managers\" that weren't even there yet,"],[117,517.83,4.3,"because they don’t show up until 9am when the office day started."],[117,522.5,1.67,"So imagine that experience"],[117,524.2,2.1,"that's being created for those plant workers."],[117,526.33,4.0,"So Tim gets everyone outside and he says,"],[117,530.37,2.2,"\"Come on, everyone, we have a meeting outside,\""],[117,532.57,2.8,"goes in the parking lot, literally brings a chainsaw"],[117,535.4,5.63,"and chainsaws down every single \"reserved for manager\" parking sign."],[117,541.33,1.87,"And then he looked at them and he said,"],[117,543.23,1.3,"\"Park wherever you want."],[117,544.57,1.2,"Let's go back to work.\""],[117,546.73,4.37,"That was a powerful chainsaw moment of surrender, right?"],[117,551.1,2.4,"And what it did was it shifted the beliefs."],[117,553.53,2.23,"There was more work to be done, obviously,"],[117,555.8,1.57,"but the belief began to shift"],[117,557.4,2.13,"in which people said, \"OK, this is different."],[117,559.53,3.23,"These leaders, they do not --"],[117,562.8,1.67,"I can trust them,\" right?"],[117,564.5,1.33,"\"I can trust this guy."],[117,565.87,3.27,"Let me see what I can do to adapt to turn this around.\""],[117,569.17,3.0,"They ended up reducing injuries by 75 percent,"],[117,572.2,1.57,"their costs went down,"],[117,573.77,2.5,"they went from the worst, almost-shutting-down plant,"],[117,576.27,3.63,"to the number-one plant in the entire Ocean Spray ecosystem --"],[117,579.93,1.37,"because Tim surrendered."],[117,581.33,3.17,"He didn't show up and say, \"What do you guys got to do differently?"],[117,584.5,2.93,"How do I wish it was different than the way that it was?\""],[117,587.47,3.6,"Now think of all the energy that you spend wishing things were different,"],[117,591.1,3.67,"wishing that they were your way so that you can be more comfortable."],[117,595.4,2.97,"And there are a lot of people who don't like the word surrender"],[117,598.37,1.93,"because it can feel kind of weak, right?"],[117,600.33,1.57,"Especially military people."],[117,601.93,2.9,"It conjures up images of waving the white flag and giving up."],[117,604.87,1.93,"But I'm not suggesting that you give up."],[117,606.8,3.57,"I'm suggesting that you give up the illusion of control"],[117,610.37,2.77,"which you never really had in the first place."],[117,613.13,2.87,"And you take all of that energy that you're spending in that,"],[117,616.03,2.1,"and you point it towards mastering yourself,"],[117,618.13,2.17,"the way you respond, the way you show up,"],[117,620.3,1.43,"especially as a leader,"],[117,621.77,2.57,"when you are responsible for creating experiences"],[117,624.33,2.67,"that will shape the beliefs of those around you."],[117,627.83,3.9,"Now the Navy Seals have a saying which is “control the controllables.”"],[117,631.73,1.53,"But there is a corollary,"],[117,633.27,2.53,"the hidden message within that is also"],[117,635.83,3.03,"\"and stop trying to control the uncontrollables.\""],[117,639.67,4.2,"So the Navy Seals know they can't control what their brother does."],[117,643.9,3.0,"They can't control what the orders are that they've been given."],[117,646.9,2.33,"They can't control if the equipment malfunctions."],[117,649.23,2.0,"They can't control what the enemy is doing."],[117,651.27,2.77,"And when the chopper is going down in enemy territory,"],[117,654.07,3.03,"they're not going down thinking, \"Well, I wish it was different."],[117,657.1,1.8,"Wait, hold on, we shouldn't be here.\""],[117,658.93,1.17,"They are focused on"],[117,660.1,2.13,"\"What do I need to do to adapt in this moment"],[117,662.23,1.27,"and respond and show up?\""],[117,663.53,2.43,"They are experts in surrender."],[117,667.17,3.63,"So my invitation to you is to experiment with surrender,"],[117,670.8,3.37,"letting go [of] the illusion of control,"],[117,674.17,3.77,"and focus on the experiences that you're creating"],[117,677.97,3.43,"that will shift and shape the beliefs of those around you"],[117,681.43,2.43,"that you're there to influence and to support."],[117,683.9,2.07,"That will ultimately allow them"],[117,686.0,3.17,"to make different choices about the actions that they want to take,"],[117,689.2,1.43,"because they've got free will."],[117,690.67,1.5,"You can't control them."],[117,692.2,2.27,"I understand for leaders it's hard to understand"],[117,694.47,2.83,"because you’ve been given the title of manager and director,"],[117,697.33,2.87,"and so it feels like it's my job to tell everyone what to do,"],[117,700.23,3.1,"but ultimately, they're only going to do it if they believe in it."],[117,703.37,4.63,"And that is how you drive results within teams of organizations."],[117,708.6,1.37,"So try surrender --"],[117,710.0,4.53,"it is uncomfortable sometimes and it can feel like a risk --"],[117,714.57,4.07,"but every great breakthrough came when someone was willing to take a risk."],[117,719.07,1.77,"So take a risk today."],[117,720.87,1.2,"Surrender to lead."],[117,722.1,1.77,"And once more, as I finish this talk,"],[117,723.9,2.8,"I'm going to hand it over to you."],[117,726.73,1.47,"What will you do with it?"],[117,729.63,1.13,"Thank you."],[118,3.73,1.24,"Do you feel comfortable?"],[118,9.31,2.87,"Really think about your experience right now."],[118,12.21,2.97,"Are you too hot or too cold?"],[118,16.11,3.4,"How does your butt feel sitting in this chair?"],[118,21.45,1.44,"Do you feel safe?"],[118,24.29,4.77,"These questions might seem a little silly, but silly for different reasons,"],[118,29.06,2.13,"depending on your gender identity."],[118,32.33,2.14,"We don't really ask these questions of men,"],[118,34.5,2.64,"because for the most part, we don't have to."],[118,38.17,4.54,"Our environments are built for men and how they experience the world."],[118,43.51,2.04,"For you women,"],[118,45.54,1.7,"these questions are different"],[118,47.24,6.04,"because we are so conditioned to accept our own discomfort,"],[118,53.32,3.44,"to accept the environments or systems around us"],[118,56.79,1.4,"as normal and natural,"],[118,58.22,4.14,"we often fail to realize when they don't quite fit us,"],[118,62.39,2.3,"we just work around it."],[118,65.4,2.2,"How many of you women"],[118,67.63,3.5,"are carrying a purse or a bag big enough"],[118,71.17,4.84,"to fit an extra sweater or a down comforter,"],[118,76.04,2.13,"in case you get cold sometime today?"],[118,80.21,2.27,"The truth is,"],[118,82.51,2.04,"the world wasn't built for women."],[118,85.82,4.24,"In fact, in nearly every way, it's been quite literally built for men."],[118,90.09,2.1,"From Da Vinci's \"Vitruvian Man\""],[118,92.22,4.0,"to a concept called \"The Modular Man\" from the mid-20th century,"],[118,96.26,5.41,"we've used men's bodies to measure what we feel is normal"],[118,101.67,2.07,"and appropriate in our world."],[118,103.73,4.2,"And then we've used those measurements to build everything around us,"],[118,107.97,4.04,"from chairs to buildings and cars."],[118,113.28,2.07,"Women are,"],[118,115.38,1.77,"for the most part,"],[118,117.15,1.53,"not seen,"],[118,118.72,1.47,"not measured,"],[118,120.18,1.17,"not valued."],[118,122.25,5.0,"And this means the basic physical structures of our environment"],[118,127.29,2.2,"or the structures of our systems,"],[118,129.53,2.2,"work against women,"],[118,131.73,3.5,"in subtle and not-so-subtle ways."],[118,135.27,3.0,"And this is exponentially true for women of color,"],[118,138.3,1.2,"women with disabilities,"],[118,139.54,2.64,"and women with intersectional identities."],[118,143.34,3.1,"It was only 20 years ago"],[118,146.48,3.54,"we included female crash test dummies."],[118,151.62,3.94,"It was only 1991"],[118,155.55,2.74,"when we included women in medical trials."],[118,158.72,6.21,"It is like we have only recently realized women aren't men."],[118,167.66,1.4,"A few years ago,"],[118,169.07,3.54,"I started daydreaming about what it would look like"],[118,172.64,4.41,"to build an office space, a workplace, for women."],[118,177.68,5.21,"I was tired of freezing in sterile-feeling offices."],[118,182.91,3.17,"I felt defeated by the woeful tales"],[118,186.12,5.44,"of moms who had to breast-pump in bathroom stalls or copy rooms."],[118,192.22,3.44,"I was sick and tired"],[118,195.69,3.6,"of feeling guilt or embarrassment"],[118,199.33,2.8,"for having to bring my baby to work with me,"],[118,202.17,2.74,"a few hours, every once in a while,"],[118,204.94,3.9,"to compensate for some caregiving emergency or another."],[118,210.14,2.94,"So I started talking to women and asking them what worked,"],[118,213.11,2.24,"what didn't work in their spaces."],[118,215.38,4.91,"And then I used that information to sketch out the perfect plan"],[118,220.28,2.04,"for a new workspace."],[118,222.95,1.74,"Women told me,"],[118,224.72,3.54,"office parking spaces are too narrow,"],[118,228.29,5.11,"and a car seat with a kid in it is pretty bulky and inflexible."],[118,233.43,3.54,"So we designed our space with ample parking"],[118,237.0,3.1,"and spots wide enough to open a car door all the way"],[118,240.14,1.67,"and take out a car seat."],[118,242.87,1.8,"Women told us,"],[118,244.71,3.7,"simply entering a building can be frustrating."],[118,248.78,4.74,"Have you ever walked up to a door and had to try a few times to get it open?"],[118,253.55,2.7,"You really had to throw your weight behind it."],[118,256.82,4.04,"That's because doors open more easily for men."],[118,261.46,3.54,"Literally, it is not just a metaphor anymore."],[118,265.0,3.47,"So we reduced the force required to open our doors,"],[118,268.5,2.67,"making it easier for women to use them."],[118,271.54,5.24,"Moms told us juggling work and children"],[118,276.81,2.44,"is their most difficult challenge."],[118,279.28,4.3,"So instead of pretending workers are 100 percent autonomous agents"],[118,283.61,1.17,"with no human connection"],[118,284.81,3.27,"and children simply drop from the sky and raise themselves,"],[118,288.12,3.34,"we plan for on-site childcare rooms"],[118,291.49,3.9,"where moms could use our partner childcare services"],[118,295.43,2.9,"or bring their kids to play while they worked."],[118,299.13,2.0,"This all wasn't just physical."],[118,301.83,1.77,"From research, we learned"],[118,303.63,4.77,"women are most likely to attend a professional development event"],[118,308.4,1.17,"with a friend."],[118,309.94,2.34,"So we made all learning social."],[118,313.51,4.87,"These were all great things, and the results were glorious."],[118,318.78,1.94,"Women supported one another"],[118,320.75,3.84,"and collaborated across businesses and industries."],[118,324.62,3.87,"They shared resources and recommendations,"],[118,328.52,2.17,"anecdotes and pep talks."],[118,331.16,4.34,"We have built a place where women could unabashedly discuss"],[118,335.53,3.04,"that other scarlet letter capital-A word"],[118,338.6,4.5,"for which they are so often judged and criticized:"],[118,343.14,1.67,"ambition."],[118,346.11,3.47,"And find not only encouragement from others"],[118,349.58,1.67,"but strategies."],[118,351.95,4.04,"We built a place where women felt valued"],[118,356.02,2.64,"and could therefore be themselves."],[118,359.59,4.27,"And while I believe valuing women is the only argument we need"],[118,363.89,1.87,"for building places that work for them,"],[118,365.76,2.54,"there is also an economic argument."],[118,368.8,3.6,"Today, there are more men than women in the workforce."],[118,372.44,5.17,"In fact, women's workforce participation is at the same level today"],[118,377.61,2.44,"as it was 30 years ago."],[118,380.08,2.4,"And the gap is only getting wider."],[118,383.08,4.84,"Building places of work where women feel comfortable and valued"],[118,387.95,1.94,"is one way to address this."],[118,390.42,3.0,"If women worked at the same levels as men,"],[118,393.46,3.9,"the US GDP would increase by five percent."],[118,397.39,2.9,"Or one trillion dollars."],[118,403.47,2.87,"Globally, we know,"],[118,406.34,3.2,"when women are more financially secure,"],[118,409.57,4.54,"they invest more and more often in their families and communities."],[118,415.45,1.8,"Everything I've mentioned so far"],[118,417.28,4.3,"are things you can do at home, in your workplaces."],[118,422.05,3.24,"But we need to think bigger too,"],[118,425.32,3.77,"and design for women on a larger scale."],[118,429.79,4.5,"Can we design whole cities to serve women?"],[118,434.63,6.14,"That's the question that inspired my work for the Downtown Denver Partnership."],[118,441.5,1.5,"The first thing I learned"],[118,443.04,4.07,"is that women aren't really on the radar of city builders."],[118,447.11,4.34,"Ninety-four percent of US cities have city plans,"],[118,451.48,4.57,"yet only two percent of those plans include any mention of women."],[118,456.62,1.83,"And if women aren't on the radar,"],[118,458.49,3.9,"it means their distinct experiences aren't on the radar, either."],[118,463.69,3.04,"Care work is a useful illustration."],[118,467.33,4.87,"Women spend 37 percent more time per day on household chores"],[118,472.2,2.1,"or caregiving activities than men."],[118,475.24,2.5,"And this doesn't end with children."],[118,477.74,5.44,"The average caregiver for an older adult is a 49-year-old woman"],[118,483.21,2.14,"who works full-time outside the home"],[118,485.38,4.5,"and spends an extra 20 hours a week caring for a family member."],[118,490.92,3.2,"If this is the reality for women,"],[118,494.16,4.21,"how can we redesign our cities to better serve them?"],[118,499.53,3.37,"Most city centers are childcare deserts,"],[118,502.93,3.07,"despite a growing number of young families"],[118,506.04,1.8,"who live and work downtown."],[118,508.61,5.61,"And women who live in metropolitan areas tend to use public transit"],[118,514.24,4.84,"to accomplish a range of household or caregiving responsibilities."],[118,519.08,3.2,"Yet a city's public transportation system"],[118,522.32,1.5,"isn't really what we think of"],[118,523.85,2.7,"when we hear the phrase “family friendly.”"],[118,527.09,3.24,"Public transit is tough to navigate with a stroller."],[118,530.36,2.74,"It doesn't facilitate the short, frequent trips"],[118,533.13,2.57,"women tend to take in care work."],[118,537.4,3.14,"If we really thought about women's experiences,"],[118,540.57,4.87,"simply recognizing the centrality of care work in women's lives"],[118,545.48,4.44,"would mean a much different city than what we're used to."],[118,551.88,2.27,"Imagine this scenario."],[118,554.85,5.21,"A mom walks a short distance from her home to her transit stop."],[118,560.86,2.24,"She and her two-year-old child"],[118,563.13,4.24,"socialize with other parents and kids at the stop"],[118,567.4,3.34,"because there are other parents and kids at the stop."],[118,571.63,4.94,"She takes the transit to work, where she goes to the on-site,"],[118,576.61,2.6,"corporate-sponsored daycare facility."],[118,579.24,3.17,"She gets a chance to kind of network with the VP at her office,"],[118,582.45,3.9,"because he and his kids also use the daycare facility."],[118,586.98,1.2,"Later in the afternoon,"],[118,588.22,4.5,"she returns to the daycare center for an afternoon snack with her kid."],[118,592.76,3.1,"Feeling energized by that visit,"],[118,595.86,2.47,"she returns to her desk,"],[118,598.33,3.2,"finishes up her day, picks up her kid,"],[118,601.53,2.04,"heads back to the transit stop."],[118,603.9,4.11,"She doesn't have to worry about transportation schedules"],[118,608.04,4.2,"because the transit stop is a hub of services."],[118,612.74,3.64,"She swings by the walk-in healthcare clinic to get her flu shot."],[118,617.08,4.57,"She drops by the bank -- which is open past 5pm, by the way --"],[118,623.25,2.57,"and sets up a financial account"],[118,625.86,2.8,"that does not penalize her with higher interest rates"],[118,628.69,1.67,"simply for being a woman."],[118,633.36,3.54,"She boards her transit home along with other families,"],[118,636.93,3.94,"feeling like she has had quality time with her kid,"],[118,640.87,3.77,"as well as a productive and strategic day."],[118,645.17,2.6,"I daydream about this scenario"],[118,647.78,4.07,"in the same way I used to daydream about riding a flying pony"],[118,651.85,1.47,"when I was eight years old."],[118,655.05,3.64,"And in some ways, it feels just as mythical and surreal."],[118,659.46,2.34,"But it doesn't have to."],[118,662.96,4.94,"To me, the workplace, cities, are inventive opportunities"],[118,667.93,4.27,"to question what we see as normal and natural in our world."],[118,672.97,4.87,"Why do we make it so difficult on working mothers"],[118,677.87,4.47,"when their contributions quite literally fuel"],[118,682.35,2.77,"the future success of our economies?"],[118,689.39,1.87,"How can we do things differently?"],[118,691.69,5.24,"And how can we continue to iterate as gender identities"],[118,696.96,2.4,"and gender norms evolve?"],[118,699.93,2.13,"Finally,"],[118,702.07,3.94,"what would it take to make these changes?"],[118,706.04,4.87,"The answer is so simple,"],[118,710.91,4.84,"it sometimes feels silly to say it aloud."],[118,715.78,2.64,"Value women."],[118,722.65,3.14,"See women as not only human,"],[118,725.82,2.9,"and therefore worthy of our consideration,"],[118,728.76,3.3,"but as people who make incredible contributions"],[118,732.06,2.54,"to our collective success."],[118,734.63,4.4,"Recognize, women's experiences are different than men's,"],[118,739.07,4.04,"but that those differences are not deficiencies."],[118,745.48,2.04,"This could be a sad story."],[118,748.04,3.27,"But in many ways it's one of triumph."],[118,751.35,6.11,"Women succeed in personal, political, cultural, economic arenas,"],[118,757.49,4.74,"all in spite of systems, places,"],[118,762.23,2.8,"not designed to serve them."],[118,765.06,5.34,"Imagine what women could do if we made things a little easier on them."],[118,771.93,4.84,"We can build places, cities, systems,"],[118,776.81,4.57,"that are not derivatives of or adjustments to what works for men"],[118,781.41,3.54,"but the product of inspired thought and creativity."],[118,785.62,6.74,"We can design for women, measure their lived experiences,"],[118,792.39,3.2,"and build with those experiences in mind."],[118,796.53,3.94,"And the world will be better for it."],[118,801.43,1.14,"Thank you."],[119,6.59,2.33,"Somewhere in medieval Switzerland,"],[119,8.92,4.84,"a weary traveler is relieved to see a monastery up ahead."],[119,14.09,3.46,"He’s welcomed, guided to a crowded guesthouse,"],[119,17.55,5.3,"and promptly served the abbey’s specialty: a tall glass of beer."],[119,23.73,4.13,"For centuries, monasteries across modern-day Europe"],[119,27.86,3.75,"were the primary large-scale brewers of beer."],[119,31.94,3.25,"But this is hardly where this drink’s journey began."],[119,36.03,6.05,"Beer refers to any alcoholic beverage made from fermented cereal grains—"],[119,42.16,3.92,"the edible seeds of a particular group of grasses."],[119,46.46,4.75,"The oldest evidence of beer brewing dates back 13,000 years"],[119,51.21,2.13,"to what’s now modern-day Israel,"],[119,53.59,4.71,"but experts believe the drink developed independently across the world."],[119,58.72,1.92,"In each of these regions,"],[119,60.68,3.34,"its composition was shaped by native grains."],[119,64.35,4.09,"For example, 9,000 years ago in eastern China,"],[119,68.44,5.8,"people drank a sweet, cloudy beer made from rice, millet, and tubers."],[119,74.65,2.42,"In the Andean region of South America,"],[119,77.2,4.75,"5,000-year-old pottery suggests production of chicha,"],[119,81.95,2.38,"a brew of fermented maize."],[119,85.21,5.21,"Whatever the ingredients, ancient beer brewing required two main steps."],[119,90.55,3.25,"First, the starches in the cereal grains"],[119,93.8,3.25,"had to be converted into fermentable sugars."],[119,97.26,2.92,"Some groups achieved this by chewing them,"],[119,100.18,3.96,"while others soaked them in water to trigger germination,"],[119,104.14,3.17,"a process that naturally breaks down starches."],[119,107.73,2.29,"The second step was fermentation."],[119,110.48,5.84,"Unbeknownst to brewers at the time, wild yeast found its way into the mixture—"],[119,116.61,4.59,"carried through the air, on fruit, or via reused tools"],[119,121.2,3.46,"still harboring yeasts from prior brews."],[119,125.12,5.67,"With time and storage, yeasts converted some of these sugars into alcohol"],[119,130.79,2.13,"while releasing carbon dioxide."],[119,133.46,3.63,"This ancient beer was often imperfectly filtered,"],[119,137.26,5.76,"so it contained much more fiber, B vitamins, and protein than modern beer."],[119,143.47,3.5,"And some cultures boiled the initial sugar mixture,"],[119,147.1,3.04,"which killed many unwanted microorganisms,"],[119,150.23,4.3,"making it safer to drink than other available water sources."],[119,155.48,3.46,"The oldest surviving recipe describing this process"],[119,158.95,5.76,"appears in a Sumerian poem dedicated to their goddess of beer, Ninkasi."],[119,165.08,2.71,"The Babylonians built on this adoration—"],[119,168.16,5.0,"Hammurabi’s code mandated daily beer rations for every citizen."],[119,173.54,3.71,"And any brewer caught overcharging for a pint"],[119,177.26,3.25,"was condemned to death by drowning."],[119,181.43,4.84,"In Ancient Egypt, beer was primarily made in the home by women,"],[119,186.43,2.17,"and was a staple of the daily diet."],[119,188.77,3.67,"It also played a central role in religious festivals,"],[119,192.52,4.75,"and served as an offering at temples and the tombs of pharaohs."],[119,198.03,5.84,"By the 20th century BCE, beer had spilled over from Egypt into Greece,"],[119,203.99,2.75,"though it wasn't as popular as wine."],[119,206.99,2.71,"Nevertheless, over the following centuries,"],[119,209.79,3.88,"a thirst for beer spread through Europe and beyond."],[119,214.42,1.79,"In the early Middle Ages,"],[119,216.21,4.42,"European monasteries pioneered large-scale beer production,"],[119,220.72,5.8,"both to provide to traveling pilgrims and to sell to support monastery upkeep."],[119,226.72,1.46,"Around this same time,"],[119,228.43,4.96,"brewers from Central and Eastern Europe made a key change to their recipe:"],[119,233.48,2.17,"they began adding hops."],[119,236.06,3.71,"These green flowers are rich in acidic compounds,"],[119,239.99,4.92,"imbuing beer with a new bitter flavor and acting as a preservative."],[119,245.37,4.88,"Hops became so closely associated with beer that in 1516,"],[119,250.45,6.21,"a Bavarian duke issued a beer purity law, known as the Reinheitsgebot,"],[119,256.75,5.88,"that defined beer’s composition as strictly barley, hops, and water."],[119,263.55,4.46,"The Industrial Revolution ushered in other essential beer innovations,"],[119,268.06,2.84,"including advances in refrigeration."],[119,271.14,4.09,"In 1876, French chemist Louis Pasteur"],[119,275.23,3.54,"identified yeast’s role in beer fermentation."],[119,279.11,4.59,"With this, a key beer ingredient was finally made visible."],[119,284.07,3.13,"Brewers began selecting for different yeasts"],[119,287.2,3.29,"to refine recipes and experiment with flavor."],[119,291.54,4.96,"Today, there are well over 100 distinct beer varieties,"],[119,296.62,3.84,"with lagers and ales being the two main types."],[119,300.75,3.29,"Lagers, which include pilsners and bocks,"],[119,304.13,4.21,"are fermented with yeasts that thrive at colder temperatures"],[119,308.47,3.29,"and live at the bottom of fermentation vessels."],[119,311.97,4.67,"Ales, like porters, stouts, and pale ales, ferment quicker,"],[119,316.77,2.59,"with yeasts that live at higher temperatures"],[119,319.36,2.04,"and float at the top of the mixture."],[119,321.82,2.34,"But some beers break this mold."],[119,324.4,3.17,"Kölsches, for example, start with ale yeast,"],[119,327.57,3.5,"but are finished at colder temperatures like lagers."],[119,331.58,4.34,"Others draw on ancient methods, like Belgian Lambics,"],[119,335.92,4.21,"which rely on spontaneous fermentation with wild yeast."],[119,341.13,3.25,"Meanwhile, there are still new challenges to navigate."],[119,344.63,4.5,"The growing demand for non-alcoholic beers has pushed producers"],[119,349.14,5.0,"to find ways to limit or remove alcohol while still preserving flavor."],[119,354.6,2.21,"And if history is any indication,"],[119,357.1,4.55,"beer's story will continue brewing for many years to come."],[120,6.59,3.63,"Elections— often called the cornerstone of democracy—"],[120,10.21,5.26,"are tools that ensure a nation's citizens all have an equal political voice."],[120,15.68,5.25,"But these so-called “great equalizers” have long been plagued by corruption,"],[120,20.93,3.21,"partisan divides, and uninformed voters."],[120,24.39,4.55,"Which is why some of democracy’s first and most famous practitioners"],[120,28.94,2.29,"used a different approach."],[120,31.86,3.54,"From 508 to 322 BCE,"],[120,35.53,4.59,"Athens increasingly moved away from elected officials."],[120,40.2,2.38,"Outside specialized positions"],[120,42.58,3.84,"like military generals and senior finance officers,"],[120,46.46,3.79,"most legislative, executive, and judicial roles"],[120,50.25,2.54,"were appointed via lottery."],[120,53.13,4.92,"Starting at age 30, citizens could place a token with their name"],[120,58.18,2.04,"into an allotment machine."],[120,60.56,4.05,"These machines appointed citizens to government positions"],[120,64.64,5.88,"through a process designed to ensure randomness and prevent fraud."],[120,71.28,5.26,"Before getting the job, chosen candidates underwent a public examination"],[120,76.53,1.88,"to investigate their character,"],[120,78.62,4.17,"and those that passed would typically serve for a single year."],[120,83.12,1.46,"When their term ended,"],[120,84.58,4.38,"they underwent another public review to investigate their conduct"],[120,88.96,2.88,"and financial dealings while in office."],[120,92.59,2.79,"This system was called sortition,"],[120,95.38,3.59,"and its goal was to promote political equality."],[120,99.22,5.09,"In fact, Athenians saw lotteries as more democratic than voting,"],[120,104.52,4.17,"since they believed elections favored the wealthy and well-connected."],[120,109.11,2.75,"Random appointees, on the other hand,"],[120,112.02,4.09,"were ordinary citizens stepping up to fulfill their civic duty."],[120,116.4,3.96,"And since most offices didn't allow repeat terms,"],[120,120.49,4.63,"sortition prevented people from gaining too much political influence."],[120,125.66,3.0,"Of course, this system was far from perfect."],[120,128.92,4.25,"Athenian sortition excluded women, foreign-born residents,"],[120,133.17,1.67,"and enslaved peoples."],[120,135.17,3.92,"And, as philosophers like Plato and Aristotle pointed out,"],[120,139.26,3.67,"political decision-making requires expertise,"],[120,143.22,4.0,"a quality that's difficult to develop in short appointments,"],[120,147.44,3.42,"and can't be guaranteed by random selection."],[120,151.9,5.13,"But broadly, this lottery-based system had strong public support."],[120,157.24,4.67,"It was the dominant form of democracy during Athens’ Golden Age,"],[120,162.07,6.3,"and it only truly ended when Athens’ conquerors abolished democracy altogether."],[120,168.91,5.38,"So if sortition provided stability then, could it do so now?"],[120,174.92,3.59,"Political philosopher Alex Guerrero thinks it could,"],[120,178.55,4.55,"and he’s even proposed a modern American version of sortition"],[120,183.09,2.42,"that he calls lottocracy."],[120,185.85,4.88,"Here’s how it works: rather than relying on one decision-making body"],[120,190.73,1.58,"for every issue,"],[120,192.31,6.8,"Guerrero proposes multiple assemblies, each dedicated to a specific policy area."],[120,199.61,5.09,"These single-issue, lottery-selected legislatures, or SILLs,"],[120,204.82,4.0,"are made up of hundreds of randomly chosen citizens"],[120,208.83,5.46,"who get trained in their assembly’s topic area by experts and advocates."],[120,214.96,3.79,"Then, after consulting with the public to get their perspective,"],[120,218.92,5.3,"the members of a SILL draft and vote on topic-specific policies."],[120,224.97,2.88,"This system extends all the way to the top,"],[120,227.85,3.0,"distributing even the powers of the presidency"],[120,230.85,4.13,"across a network lottery-filled Executive Assemblies"],[120,235.15,2.88,"and the administrative officials they appoint."],[120,238.73,5.51,"Advocates of lottocracy believe it could address three of the biggest problems"],[120,244.24,1.79,"facing modern democracies."],[120,246.16,2.46,"First, unequal representation."],[120,249.04,4.75,"Since successful election campaigns require money and influence,"],[120,253.96,4.55,"many elected officials are much wealthier than the average voter."],[120,259.0,3.71,"At various points from 2014 to 2025,"],[120,262.97,3.79,"half of US Congress members were millionaires."],[120,267.6,3.92,"Problem two: most candidates rely on donations"],[120,271.52,4.21,"from individuals, corporations, and special interest groups"],[120,275.86,2.5,"who may try to influence their policies."],[120,278.98,5.09,"Lottocracy makes influence harder to buy by avoiding elections,"],[120,284.28,5.76,"offering appointees generous compensation, and enforcing shorter term limits."],[120,290.75,4.21,"The third problem is a lack of policy making competence."],[120,295.25,4.42,"While career politicians juggle dozens of policy proposals"],[120,299.67,2.29,"on countless complicated issues,"],[120,302.17,4.5,"SILLs let their members become experts in a single topic."],[120,307.39,3.75,"As you would expect, this radical proposal has critics."],[120,311.51,4.3,"Political theorists Cristina LaFont and Nadia Urbinati"],[120,315.81,4.34,"argue that lottocracy asks most citizens to defer"],[120,320.15,2.25,"to a randomly chosen few."],[120,322.82,3.34,"They believe that democracies should allow citizens"],[120,326.15,5.3,"to exercise political freedom as equals— and elections are central to that."],[120,331.87,3.04,"Elections let people set the political agenda,"],[120,334.91,4.59,"and they bind officeholders to a continuing cycle of accountability,"],[120,339.67,3.04,"both at the polls and in the public eye."],[120,343.21,5.96,"In their view, voting is how citizens collectively shape and limit public power,"],[120,349.3,1.04,"And without it,"],[120,350.35,3.0,"even the most competent lottocratic government"],[120,353.47,2.42,"could feel like rule by experts."],[120,356.48,4.5,"Without elections, it can be hard to say what makes a system democratic."],[120,360.98,2.84,"But this debate highlights a shared goal:"],[120,364.36,5.8,"we all want institutions that serve everyone and address real problems."],[120,370.32,2.84,"And just like every other element of democracy,"],[120,373.16,4.34,"it’s up to us to keep experimenting until we find a system"],[120,377.5,1.92,"that achieves those ideals."],[121,4.24,1.8,"To begin,"],[121,6.04,2.37,"I invite you all to remember a time in your life,"],[121,8.44,3.57,"a period in your life, when you felt fully integrated into a group."],[121,12.51,2.1,"Maybe you were on a sports team,"],[121,14.61,1.77,"maybe you played in a band,"],[121,16.38,2.17,"or maybe you just had a great group of friends"],[121,18.55,1.57,"that loved to hang out together."],[121,20.15,1.17,"Or maybe it was at work."],[121,21.35,1.4,"Maybe you were part of a team"],[121,22.75,2.9,"trying to do something big and difficult under time pressure."],[121,25.66,1.37,"But you all pulled together."],[121,27.02,2.34,"Whatever it was, my question to you is,"],[121,29.39,2.3,"does that memory glow?"],[121,32.23,1.44,"Do you look back on that"],[121,33.66,3.07,"as something special and magical, that time in your life?"],[121,37.57,2.37,"The great biologist E. O. Wilson"],[121,39.97,4.71,"says that humans aren't just social, like dogs and chimpanzees,"],[121,44.71,3.14,"we are ultrasocial, like bees and ants."],[121,48.38,2.44,"We have a massive division of labor,"],[121,50.85,5.37,"and we love to do things that put us in a mindset of one-for-all, all-for-one."],[121,57.22,2.07,"Yet our hives aren't made out of wax."],[121,59.76,3.24,"They're made out of shared culture and shared experiences."],[121,63.46,2.44,"My talk today isn’t really about bees and ants --"],[121,65.93,2.87,"it’s actually about technology and childhood."],[121,69.37,3.04,"But let's see what we can see about technology and childhood"],[121,72.44,2.4,"if we start with this premise"],[121,74.87,3.24,"that human beings are ultrasocial creatures"],[121,78.14,2.9,"with deep needs for community and communion."],[121,81.98,1.24,"As a social psychologist"],[121,83.25,3.8,"who studies the effects of digital tech on young people,"],[121,87.05,2.9,"what I see from this perspective is very concerning."],[121,90.59,3.77,"I think it justifies a general sense of wariness"],[121,94.39,3.77,"or skepticism about the technologies that are pushing their way"],[121,98.16,1.33,"into childhood today."],[121,100.7,1.7,"So let's start with social media."],[121,103.17,1.2,"In the early 2010s,"],[121,104.37,2.97,"teens traded in their flip phones for smartphones,"],[121,107.34,2.5,"and the phone-based childhood began."],[121,109.87,2.64,"Their social lives moved on to social media."],[121,112.54,3.4,"At first, we thought this would be fine, maybe even better."],[121,115.98,2.8,"But quantity pushes out quality,"],[121,118.82,3.24,"and they started spending a lot less time with each other in person."],[121,122.09,2.8,"And that’s a problem for our ultrasocial species"],[121,124.92,4.87,"because a lot of our evolved bonding mechanisms involve our bodies."],[121,129.79,1.27,"So we connect with people,"],[121,131.09,4.0,"we bond with people when we eat with them, when we share food with them,"],[121,135.13,2.47,"when we share laughter,"],[121,137.63,1.9,"when we move together in synchrony,"],[121,139.57,2.9,"even if it’s just walking next to each other,"],[121,142.51,1.74,"and we bond together when we touch."],[121,145.71,2.5,"But when everything moved online,"],[121,148.21,5.14,"teens across the developed world lost most of those bonding experiences."],[121,154.12,4.07,"Levels of loneliness and anxiety began to rise almost immediately"],[121,158.22,2.2,"in many countries simultaneously."],[121,161.39,2.84,"And this wasn't just a historical correlation."],[121,164.66,3.4,"There are now multiple lines of evidence showing"],[121,168.1,4.84,"that social media is causing harm at an industrial scale."],[121,173.64,2.4,"One line is the dozens of experiments --"],[121,177.94,1.97,"... these are usually with young adults --"],[121,179.94,1.57,"when you randomly assign people"],[121,181.54,3.84,"to greatly reduce their social media use for at least a week,"],[121,185.38,2.87,"their levels of anxiety and depression go down."],[121,188.28,3.14,"And one of those studies was done by Meta."],[121,193.02,2.0,"But what I've learned in the last two years"],[121,195.03,3.74,"is that I grossly understated the damage"],[121,198.76,1.3,"in \"The Anxious Generation\""],[121,200.1,3.64,"because I focused on the mental health outcomes."],[121,204.1,1.77,"That’s where we have the best data."],[121,205.9,1.87,"That’s where we’re doing the most work."],[121,207.81,2.9,"But I now believe that an even larger damage"],[121,210.74,2.9,"is the diminishment of the human capacity"],[121,213.68,1.8,"to pay sustained attention."],[121,216.48,4.77,"One-third of all American teens say that they’re on a social media platform"],[121,221.25,3.47,"almost constantly, just throughout the day."],[121,225.22,3.07,"And the main thing they're doing on those social media platforms"],[121,228.29,2.1,"is watching very short videos."],[121,230.43,1.77,"Young people call it brain rot --"],[121,232.2,1.74,"which is a funny term --"],[121,233.93,2.23,"but it might really be true"],[121,236.17,5.91,"because the adolescent brain is always ... being remodeled."],[121,242.67,3.4,"The neural network of a child has to convert itself,"],[121,246.11,4.8,"has to rewire itself to become the neural network of an adult."],[121,251.91,2.07,"And that rewiring process,"],[121,254.02,1.53,"the neurons finding each other,"],[121,255.59,3.77,"that's shaped by whatever you're doing every day."],[121,260.42,5.11,"And it's shaped by whatever everyone else says is prestigious,"],[121,265.56,3.74,"which means that puberty is the worst possible time"],[121,269.33,3.07,"for a human being to be on social media."],[121,273.24,2.37,"For our ultrasocial species,"],[121,275.61,4.4,"that rewiring should be guided by huge amounts of social interaction"],[121,280.01,1.17,"in the real world,"],[121,281.21,2.1,"not by TikTok's algorithm."],[121,289.35,2.77,"I imagine there are a lot of parents in the audience."],[121,292.16,4.34,"So here’s the first principle of what we might call techno-skepticism."],[121,297.03,2.97,"Protect brain development through puberty."],[121,300.46,3.47,"That's why it's so important for countries to follow Australia's example."],[121,303.93,3.34,"Let's just raise the age for opening social media accounts to 16,"],[121,307.3,1.27,"as Australia did."],[121,308.97,1.84,"All right, now let's look at EdTech."],[121,311.54,3.04,"Of course, there are good uses of technology in education."],[121,314.61,2.7,"My kids have learned a lot from Khan Academy,"],[121,317.31,2.47,"but I'm very concerned about what happened"],[121,319.78,3.77,"when we started putting computers and tablets on kids’ desks."],[121,323.59,2.84,"These are the so-called 1-to-1 device policies."],[121,327.32,3.57,"Computers and tablets are multifunction entertainment systems."],[121,331.76,2.67,"If kids can get to the internet,"],[121,334.46,5.64,"they will play video games and watch short videos, watch YouTube Shorts"],[121,340.14,1.4,"and even porn."],[121,341.57,3.37,"As soon as we brought in 1-to-1 devices in the 2010s,"],[121,344.98,3.47,"national test scores began dropping in the USA."],[121,348.85,2.37,"And they've dropped in many other countries,"],[121,351.25,3.6,"especially in the countries that most firmly embraced EdTech."],[121,355.45,3.7,"Now I can’t prove that these declines were caused by the screens"],[121,359.19,2.04,"and the apps that we put on kids’ desks."],[121,361.79,1.2,"But consider this."],[121,363.03,3.57,"Sweden led the world in digitizing education in the 2010s."],[121,366.6,1.43,"They got rid of textbooks."],[121,368.03,2.0,"They put a device on every desk."],[121,370.07,5.3,"They even mandated that nursery schools had to use tablets."],[121,376.17,4.14,"But after years of experience and years of declining test scores,"],[121,380.34,1.6,"Sweden reversed course."],[121,382.41,3.54,"In 2023, they announced that they’re going back to textbooks."],[121,385.98,1.97,"They’re pulling out a lot of the devices."],[121,387.99,2.64,"They’re going back to books and handwriting,"],[121,390.65,1.8,"especially in the earlier grades."],[121,392.49,3.1,"Their top research institute -- the Karolinska Institute --"],[121,395.63,2.57,"issued a report backing the government's position,"],[121,398.23,2.74,"saying there is clear scientific evidence"],[121,401.0,4.5,"that digital tools impair rather than enhance student learning."],[121,406.07,1.17,"And consider this."],[121,407.27,2.4,"Many of us professors are banishing computers [and]"],[121,409.71,1.64,"laptops from our classrooms."],[121,411.38,2.44,"My students at NYU say they learn a lot better"],[121,413.84,2.8,"when people aren’t on devices, don’t have a computer"],[121,416.68,2.3,"and the multitasking staring them in the face."],[121,418.98,2.57,"But if college students can't learn that well"],[121,421.58,1.97,"when there's a computer in front of them,"],[121,423.59,2.07,"how do we expect eight-year-olds to do it?"],[121,426.19,3.07,"School is an intrinsically social experience."],[121,429.26,2.24,"Students are not learning machines."],[121,431.83,4.5,"They’re ultrasocial human beings who need to connect with their teachers"],[121,436.37,1.3,"and their fellow students."],[121,438.8,2.77,"They don't need to connect with more screens."],[121,442.11,2.94,"So here’s the second principle of techno-skepticism."],[121,445.07,3.57,"Prioritize people and books in education, not screens."],[121,448.98,2.1,"We should never have let laptops and tablets"],[121,451.11,1.7,"spread through K-12 education"],[121,452.85,2.67,"without extensive testing"],[121,455.52,2.97,"and evidence of safety and efficacy."],[121,458.52,4.34,"But we’re about to [make] the exact same mistake with AI."],[121,464.09,1.64,"Do you see the pattern here?"],[121,466.6,4.14,"We let social media companies take over our kids’ social lives,"],[121,470.77,3.84,"and they’ve harmed our kids’ social lives and their mental health."],[121,475.24,3.3,"We let EdTech companies take over our kids' schools,"],[121,478.54,2.8,"and they appear to be doing more harm than good."],[121,481.71,3.34,"Now AI companies are coming for their relationships,"],[121,485.08,1.37,"to be their friends,"],[121,486.48,1.17,"their therapists"],[121,487.68,2.07,"and even their sexual partners."],[121,490.95,1.27,"What could go wrong?"],[121,494.22,3.9,"We're already seeing massive cognitive offloading and learning loss."],[121,498.73,2.04,"When students have access to AI,"],[121,500.8,3.2,"they pass the critical thinking over to the AI."],[121,504.83,4.04,"We're already seeing young people becoming dependent on ChatGPT"],[121,508.91,3.9,"to make their personal decisions and to draft their texts and their emails."],[121,513.54,2.33,"And we're seeing a booming AI toy market."],[121,515.91,4.7,"Chatbots are being put into dolls and teddy bears."],[121,520.62,3.27,"And these chatbots are super responsive to the child."],[121,523.92,4.2,"They’re always there to offer comfort, to be there for the child --"],[121,528.16,2.2,"and of course, the parents are often busy."],[121,530.99,4.61,"But if the chatbot is super responsive while the parents aren't as responsive,"],[121,535.6,1.47,"the child's attachment system,"],[121,537.07,2.0,"which is looking for"],[121,539.07,2.7,"who in my environment is the person who responds to me --"],[121,541.77,3.34,"may well imprint or focus on the chatbot,"],[121,545.14,3.37,"which is going to compromise their relationship with their own parents."],[121,548.95,2.77,"Here’s the third principle of techno-skepticism."],[121,552.08,3.57,"Just beware of artificial relationships for minors."],[121,556.29,2.14,"Give them nothing that conveys"],[121,558.42,4.24,"that it understands the child or that it cares --"],[121,562.66,1.2,"because it doesn't."],[121,565.1,3.1,"There could be a role for AI therapists someday,"],[121,568.23,3.47,"but how about we require years of testing"],[121,571.74,2.54,"before we let anyone push it out into childhood?"],[121,580.14,3.07,"All right, now I’ve just told you that we need to greatly reduce"],[121,583.25,2.87,"the role of these technologies in our kids' lives."],[121,586.15,3.4,"And some of you may be thinking, \"Oh, hold on a second."],[121,589.59,3.9,"I want my child to be successful in the digital future"],[121,593.49,1.37,"and the digital workplace."],[121,595.42,2.04,"So why not give them a head start?\""],[121,599.53,1.2,"Two reasons."],[121,600.76,5.74,"The first is that these technologies are extremely easy to use."],[121,606.84,2.4,"Your kid doesn’t need a 10-year head start"],[121,609.27,2.44,"to master social media and AI."],[121,613.81,1.8,"And second,"],[121,617.41,4.67,"... is that being a digital native does not confer an advantage."],[121,622.09,1.87,"For many kids it's a curse"],[121,623.99,3.6,"because it messes with [their] attention systems"],[121,627.62,1.7,"and their motivational systems."],[121,630.23,2.8,"It teaches them that there's always a little bit of reward,"],[121,633.03,2.04,"a little bit of dopamine available,"],[121,635.1,1.7,"just one swipe away."],[121,636.83,2.17,"And that undermines the ability"],[121,639.0,3.27,"to do difficult or sustained cognitive work"],[121,642.27,2.14,"like reading a book."],[121,645.48,2.7,"I teach a course at NYU called “Flourishing,”"],[121,648.21,3.44,"and two years ago, we were talking about attention fragmentation."],[121,651.68,3.5,"And one of my students who was a very heavy TikTok user, said,"],[121,655.18,2.77,"\"Yeah, I take out a book,"],[121,657.99,1.67,"I read a sentence,"],[121,659.69,1.57,"I get bored,"],[121,661.29,1.27,"I go to TikTok.\""],[121,663.76,6.14,"So if we want our children to be successful in the digital future,"],[121,669.93,3.97,"we need to protect them from the damage being done in the digital present."],[121,679.88,2.07,"So let's return to the hive."],[121,681.98,4.54,"What do we see when we look at technology and childhood through this lens?"],[121,687.35,3.37,"When we start from the premise that humans are ultrasocial,"],[121,691.25,4.04,"what we see is that these technologies are being built"],[121,695.33,2.27,"by people who don't understand that premise."],[121,699.03,2.77,"They think of people as consumers with social needs"],[121,701.83,2.3,"that can be satisfied by machines."],[121,704.67,3.24,"They think it's good to free people from dependence on other people."],[121,708.27,3.1,"Let's suppose, just for the sake of argument,"],[121,711.37,4.17,"... that they really can give us excellent friends"],[121,715.58,1.9,"and excellent romantic partners."],[121,717.85,1.8,"In fact, just yesterday at lunch,"],[121,719.68,4.27,"Esther Perel told me she recently did her first couples therapy"],[121,723.99,1.4,"with a mixed couple."],[121,725.72,2.8,"A human male and an AI female."],[121,730.66,2.77,"So is this liberation?"],[121,733.46,4.2,"Do we no longer have to depend on other people to meet our social needs?"],[121,737.7,3.4,"Would that make us happy if we don't have to depend on others?"],[121,741.97,1.53,"Absolutely not."],[121,744.11,3.3,"Because [it] would mean that nobody depends on us."],[121,748.61,2.17,"Nobody is relying on us."],[121,750.78,2.47,"We are not important to anyone."],[121,754.42,1.4,"So is this our fate?"],[121,756.95,2.94,"Is there any way to stop this lonely digital future?"],[121,761.32,1.57,"Yes, there is."],[121,764.23,3.8,"When \"The Anxious Generation\" came out two years ago,"],[121,768.06,3.54,"one of the main objections I got was that I was too late."],[121,771.63,2.57,"The technology is here to stay, people said."],[121,774.24,2.14,"You can't put the genie back in the bottle."],[121,777.21,1.6,"But in the last few years,"],[121,778.84,2.17,"humanity has mobilized,"],[121,781.01,2.6,"and we are putting the genie back in the bottle."],[121,789.32,3.14,"Mothers were the first to organize and take action,"],[121,792.49,2.04,"but they were quickly joined by fathers"],[121,794.56,3.57,"and by a lot of Gen Z activist organizations"],[121,798.16,2.14,"and also by many governors"],[121,800.3,1.4,"and many heads of state."],[121,802.07,4.0,"Together, we're getting phones out of schools around the world."],[121,810.81,2.6,"Teachers are so thrilled to get their students back."],[121,813.44,2.27,"And one of the things that they tell us --"],[121,815.75,1.87,"the most common thing we hear -- is:"],[121,817.61,2.17,"\"We hear laughter in the hallways again.\""],[121,821.18,4.04,"We're getting the age raised for social media to 16."],[121,825.25,2.67,"More than a dozen countries have already committed"],[121,827.96,2.47,"to following Australia's bold example."],[121,830.43,1.9,"And we're seeing parents letting go"],[121,832.36,4.14,"and trusting their children to ride bicycles with their friends"],[121,836.53,3.07,"and to do errands so that they can feel useful."],[121,840.44,1.64,"I'll give you one example."],[121,842.11,3.64,"A mom in Utah gave her seven-year-old son the Let Grow challenge."],[121,845.77,1.47,"That's where you say to a kid,"],[121,847.28,2.87,"\"What's something that you think you can do on your own?\""],[121,850.18,1.33,"And her son said,"],[121,851.55,4.07,"\"I think I can go into a Chick-fil-A restaurant and get us lunch.\""],[121,855.65,1.7,"So she says, OK."],[121,857.39,2.9,"And in the video, you see the mother sitting in the car,"],[121,860.32,2.1,"[you] see the kid coming out of the store"],[121,862.42,1.77,"and he's got the bags"],[121,864.23,2.6,"and ...this huge smile, and he comes into the car"],[121,866.83,2.87,"and he says, \"That was so fun.\""],[121,871.53,2.64,"And then the mom says, \"Were you nervous?\""],[121,874.2,4.6,"And he says, \"Yeah, my legs are still shivering,"],[121,878.84,1.87,"but I want to do it again.\""],[121,883.38,2.4,"So these are the stories, these kinds of stories --"],[121,885.78,2.3,"this is what most moves me, what most thrills me."],[121,888.08,3.24,"Because this movement is not primarily about technology."],[121,891.32,5.0,"It's about reclaiming childhood in the real world with real people."],[121,897.03,2.7,"So what on earth do we do about the robot teachers"],[121,899.73,2.1,"and all the other future waves of technology"],[121,901.87,2.2,"that are going to push their way into childhood"],[121,904.07,1.74,"without adequate safety testing?"],[121,906.77,2.04,"It sometimes seems completely overwhelming."],[121,908.84,3.97,"So let me repeat the three principles of techno-skepticism."],[121,912.81,3.3,"One, protect brain development through puberty."],[121,916.48,4.54,"Two, prioritize people and books in education -- not screens."],[121,921.05,3.64,"Three, beware of artificial relationships for minors."],[121,925.19,4.17,"I think techno-skepticism is the right attitude for people today,"],[121,929.39,3.07,"especially for parents and legislators."],[121,932.5,1.97,"Because when it comes to children,"],[121,934.5,2.7,"these companies have earned our distrust."],[121,938.23,2.44,"Techno-skepticism means that from now on,"],[121,940.7,2.13,"we put the burden of proof on them."],[121,943.21,2.24,"Let them prove that their products are safe."],[121,945.81,1.8,"We treat them like any other maker"],[121,947.64,2.37,"of potentially dangerous consumer products."],[121,950.48,2.24,"We make them prove that their products are safe"],[121,952.75,2.37,"before they push them out into the world."],[121,955.15,1.67,"And we hold them responsible --"],[121,962.46,2.84,"We hold them responsible for their safety lapses."],[121,966.7,4.1,"So in conclusion, human beings are ultrasocial creatures"],[121,970.8,2.2,"who need to matter to one another"],[121,973.04,1.44,"in order to flourish."],[121,974.8,4.71,"We are so brilliant that we’ve invented technologies that can replace us"],[121,979.51,2.34,"[and] take us out of each other’s lives."],[121,982.85,2.57,"But human connection is not optional."],[121,985.45,1.24,"It's who we are."],[121,987.45,2.67,"So we're going to have to fight for a future"],[121,990.15,5.71,"in which our children can grow into flourishing, connected adults."],[121,996.19,1.17,"Thank you."],[121,1006.07,2.07,"Do you disagree with anything I said?"],[121,1009.47,2.97,"Sal Khan: We agree in spirit 100 percent."],[121,1012.48,2.5,"And that's where my question is, actually."],[121,1014.98,3.4,"And we have talked about this in the past, even before TED."],[121,1018.82,4.71,"But how extreme would you go?"],[121,1024.09,3.44,"What would you say to someone that would make the argument,"],[121,1027.56,2.13,"even putting Khan Academy aside,"],[121,1029.73,1.74,"if a student is writing their paper,"],[121,1031.49,1.27,"if they're editing video,"],[121,1032.8,1.87,"if they’re doing something creative,"],[121,1034.7,2.97,"they're building some skills in the process,"],[121,1037.67,2.27,"isn't there some middle ground that might be OK?"],[121,1039.94,2.07,"Jonathan Haidt: Well it depends on the age."],[121,1042.01,2.57,"So if we're talking elementary school, I would say no."],[121,1044.57,1.27,"I would say it's so clear,"],[121,1045.84,1.53,"kids need to learn basic skills,"],[121,1047.38,1.83,"they need to develop a habit of books."],[121,1049.21,2.34,"There’s all this research on how print is better."],[121,1051.58,3.2,"For elementary school, let's just get rid of all the 1-to-1 devices."],[121,1054.82,1.43,"Go back to books and paper."],[121,1056.79,1.74,"The people who made this technology,"],[121,1058.56,3.4,"a lot of them choose to send their kids to schools that don't have this."],[121,1061.96,3.47,"We’re talking about young kids -- I say just no, until it’s proven safe."],[121,1065.43,2.74,"But I think we learned something really interesting today."],[121,1068.2,2.84,"I forget her name, that amazing woman in Africa --"],[121,1071.07,1.33,"SK: Imagine Worldwide."],[121,1072.43,3.37,"JH: So no, no internet."],[121,1075.84,1.27,"That's the key."],[121,1077.11,2.2,"And I said to you like years ago, I said,"],[121,1079.31,4.47,"Sal, if someone [were to] make a device that just had Khan Academy,"],[121,1083.78,1.53,"you could not go to YouTube."],[121,1085.35,1.74,"You could not do anything."],[121,1087.12,1.74,"That would be amazing."],[121,1088.85,1.2,"SK: We agree."],[121,1090.05,2.33,"JH: I'm raising money for this."],[121,1092.39,1.44,"Someone has to make this."],[121,1094.46,1.17,"Do you agree with that?"],[121,1095.66,3.0,"SK: I was fishing for that all night, so yes, thank you."],[121,1099.1,1.7,"Well, thank you so much, Jonathan."],[121,1100.83,1.74,"A super important conversation."],[121,1102.6,1.2,"JH: Thank you, Sal."],[122,4.06,2.44,"On January 7, 2025,"],[122,6.5,2.64,"our house, my wife's parents' house"],[122,9.18,2.92,"and most of our neighborhood burned down to the ground."],[122,12.14,4.4,"On that day, I was about to submit a paper to a major management conference,"],[122,16.54,1.2,"and like a rookie,"],[122,17.78,3.68,"I had left it to the last hour and was stuck in the uploading queue."],[122,21.5,2.16,"That's when the evacuation order came in"],[122,23.7,3.48,"for the LA wildfires in Pacific Palisades, where we lived."],[122,27.74,3.6,"I found myself outside hosing down the house with one hand"],[122,31.38,1.84,"and holding the laptop with the other,"],[122,33.22,3.08,"desperately trying to get that document to upload."],[122,36.3,2.84,"At that point, I thought that was my biggest problem."],[122,39.14,1.32,"Turns out it wasn't."],[122,40.78,1.4,"The document did upload."],[122,42.22,2.24,"The hosing, however, did not work,"],[122,44.46,2.8,"and 24 hours later, our house was gone."],[122,47.78,1.96,"I'll never forget the firefighting planes"],[122,49.78,2.84,"flying so low overhead as we evacuated."],[122,52.62,2.36,"It felt like a really bad Hollywood movie."],[122,55.02,1.76,"My wife was pumping breast milk"],[122,56.82,2.36,"as we were trying to get our newborn to safety"],[122,59.22,1.52,"and our toddler from preschool."],[122,61.14,1.4,"That night in the hotel,"],[122,62.58,3.16,"as we tried to act as normal as possible towards the kids,"],[122,65.74,2.8,"my then-three-year-old daughter looked up and said,"],[122,68.58,1.52,"\"I want to go home.\""],[122,70.46,1.88,"You know, I've researched, taught"],[122,72.38,2.84,"and worked on the unexpected for over a decade,"],[122,75.22,3.04,"and I've seen a fair share of unexpected events myself."],[122,78.3,2.08,"But this hit on a whole new level."],[122,80.82,2.68,"In the days that followed, I tried to focus on the things"],[122,83.5,1.2,"that I could control."],[122,84.7,2.36,"And surprisingly, there were micro-moments of joy,"],[122,87.1,4.68,"like unexpectedly bumping into old friends in the hotel that we evacuated to."],[122,92.62,2.0,"And that experience was an unexpected"],[122,94.62,2.36,"and certainly not wished-for opportunity"],[122,96.98,2.68,"to practice the very serendipity mindset framework"],[122,99.66,1.36,"that I had been working on,"],[122,101.06,2.96,"and that can help us navigate those kinds of situations."],[122,104.06,1.16,"More on that later."],[122,105.26,2.2,"But first, let's talk about bad luck."],[122,107.46,2.92,"It all felt like really bad luck."],[122,110.42,3.68,"But when I reflected on it, I realized it was more than that."],[122,114.1,1.4,"It was zemblanity."],[122,116.02,3.24,"Zemblanity is when something unlucky, unwanted"],[122,119.3,2.8,"or undesired happens by design"],[122,122.14,1.76,"because it's already built in."],[122,124.46,3.12,"It seems unexpected and like bad luck,"],[122,127.58,2.92,"but in hindsight it was to be expected and avoidable."],[122,130.98,4.44,"In our case, the wildfires and the winds were the trigger,"],[122,135.42,2.04,"but the real misfortune was already built"],[122,137.46,1.92,"into the fragile system all around us."],[122,139.82,4.08,"There was a lack of water in the water reservoirs and hydrants,"],[122,143.94,2.68,"a limited pre-deployment of fire trucks in the area"],[122,146.62,1.68,"despite the early warnings."],[122,148.34,3.88,"Brush wasn’t cleared in the area, and a complete lack of coordination."],[122,152.22,1.64,"It wasn't just one thing."],[122,153.9,1.88,"In retrospect, we realized"],[122,155.78,3.56,"that it was a space of infinite negative possibilities"],[122,159.38,3.32,"where eventually misfortune was inevitable."],[122,163.38,3.12,"The thing is, even when you try to do the right thing,"],[122,166.5,1.24,"in the system around you,"],[122,167.74,3.36,"zemblanity can still be baked in and you get caught up in it."],[122,171.66,2.24,"What happened to us wasn't just bad luck."],[122,174.26,2.0,"It was a pattern that I've seen everywhere,"],[122,176.26,2.4,"from wildfires to boardrooms to living rooms."],[122,179.14,3.24,"The thing about zemblanity is it’s not just out there in big systems,"],[122,182.38,1.32,"like in the wildfire case."],[122,183.74,3.32,"We see the same in companies with toxic corporate cultures,"],[122,187.1,2.68,"where it's just a matter of time that disaster strikes."],[122,189.82,2.48,"And sometimes even in our own minds."],[122,192.34,2.72,"Zemblanity is when we create our own bad luck."],[122,196.06,2.76,"Take the traveler who leaves for the airport"],[122,198.86,3.4,"with exactly the time it's needed to get to the airport."],[122,202.26,3.36,"Any small unexpected thing, like a minor traffic jam,"],[122,205.66,2.04,"and suddenly, the flight is missed."],[122,208.06,3.08,"Or take the old man who is told to use his walking stick."],[122,211.14,1.16,"He doesn't."],[122,212.34,3.04,"One day he \"unexpectedly\" falls down the stairs."],[122,215.42,4.04,"It might seem surprising in the moment, but it was to be expected."],[122,219.98,4.72,"Zemblanity seems like bad luck, but it's already built in all along."],[122,225.42,1.76,"Now before I go deeper into this,"],[122,227.18,2.4,"I want to ask you a question."],[122,230.06,3.64,"In the room, who of you considers yourself to be a lucky person,"],[122,233.74,1.16,"please raise your hands."],[122,234.9,2.36,"And at home, a mental hand-raise works too."],[122,237.26,2.36,"OK, quite the lucky room, actually, well done."],[122,239.66,1.96,"No worries, I won't ask the unlucky ones."],[122,241.66,2.4,"We'll focus on the lucky ones for now."],[122,245.02,2.2,"Those of you now who said"],[122,247.26,2.0,"you consider yourself to be a lucky person,"],[122,249.3,2.44,"who of you thinks they had some sort of role in it?"],[122,251.74,1.4,"Please put all modesty aside."],[122,253.14,2.4,"Who thinks, hands up, had some sort of role in it?"],[122,255.54,1.2,"OK, still quite a few."],[122,256.78,1.84,"Alright, so that's interesting, right,"],[122,258.62,2.4,"that on one hand, we know that luck is random,"],[122,261.02,1.2,"but on the other hand,"],[122,262.22,3.84,"already Louis Pasteur told us that it may favor the prepared mind."],[122,266.7,3.32,"In order to visualize this, let’s look at the Luck Matrix."],[122,270.06,3.2,"The Luck Matrix shows us the four different types of luck."],[122,273.3,3.52,"Here, on the lower left, we see bad luck."],[122,276.86,3.84,"Bad luck is something negative, unexpected, that happens to us."],[122,280.74,2.76,"It happens to us, you can never blame anyone for bad luck"],[122,283.54,2.2,"and it creates a lot of societal inequality."],[122,286.06,3.12,"Good luck, here on the lower right, is something positive."],[122,289.22,2.6,"It happens to us, we didn't work for it."],[122,291.82,1.8,"Just great if it happens."],[122,293.66,3.52,"Zemblanity is the misfortune by design"],[122,297.22,3.84,"that we built into fragile systems or into the pattern and habits"],[122,301.1,3.52,"that kind of then ultimately lead us to disaster."],[122,304.62,3.28,"And then the thing that has driven our research for the last decade"],[122,307.9,1.72,"is serendipity."],[122,309.66,2.24,"Serendipity is the active luck"],[122,311.9,2.92,"that depends on how we engage with the unexpected."],[122,314.86,1.76,"Blind luck happens to us,"],[122,316.66,3.4,"and active luck, serendipity, can be cultivated."],[122,320.42,3.0,"Imagine you have erratic hand movements like I do."],[122,323.46,1.84,"Then you spill a lot of coffee, right?"],[122,325.3,3.8,"So imagine you accidentally spill coffee over the person next to you."],[122,329.1,2.32,"That person looks at you slightly annoyed,"],[122,331.46,2.12,"but you sense there might be something there."],[122,333.58,3.64,"You don't know what, you just think, oh, that could be an interesting person."],[122,337.22,2.08,"And now you have a couple of options, right?"],[122,339.3,1.76,"One is to just say, \"I'm so sorry.\""],[122,341.06,3.0,"You walk outside and you think, \"Ah, what could have happened?\""],[122,344.06,2.16,"Another option is you start that conversation"],[122,346.26,2.12,"and that person becomes the love of your life"],[122,348.42,2.48,"or your cofounder or you name it."],[122,350.94,2.96,"The point is, the eventual outcome, good or bad,"],[122,353.9,2.48,"depends on how we engage with the unexpected."],[122,356.38,1.88,"And that's where serendipity emerges."],[122,358.3,2.68,"Serendipity really is this unexpected good luck"],[122,361.02,2.12,"that results from unplanned moments"],[122,363.14,3.28,"in which our actions lead to positive outcomes."],[122,366.46,1.2,"Let's look at an example."],[122,367.66,1.48,"In 1968,"],[122,369.18,3.88,"Spencer Silver was trying to develop a stronger glue."],[122,373.78,3.08,"Accidentally, he developed a weaker one."],[122,376.9,1.6,"Seemed useless in the moment,"],[122,378.5,1.48,"until a couple of years later,"],[122,379.98,3.48,"his colleague Arthur Fry was using that weak glue"],[122,383.5,3.96,"to avoid having his bookmarks falling out of his church hymnal."],[122,387.5,1.48,"That seemed quite useful."],[122,389.02,1.52,"The Post-it note was born"],[122,390.54,3.16,"and became one of 3M's major products."],[122,394.3,2.32,"Lots of examples out there of serendipity, right?"],[122,396.62,2.76,"From Velcro to microwave ovens"],[122,399.42,3.16,"to how people unexpectedly find love or their apartments."],[122,402.9,1.2,"And a lot of times also"],[122,404.14,4.6,"when crises become the inflection point for something in one's own life."],[122,408.78,3.8,"The point is that over the last decade we've studied serendipity,"],[122,412.62,2.04,"all these different stories of serendipity,"],[122,414.66,2.64,"and tried to understand, is there a pattern behind that."],[122,417.3,1.44,"And it turns out there is."],[122,418.78,1.84,"It's always the same process."],[122,421.06,3.8,"There is an unexpected serendipity trigger, which is random, right?"],[122,424.9,3.32,"So the coffee spill or the weak glue."],[122,428.26,1.92,"But then we have to imbue meaning in it."],[122,430.18,1.36,"We have to connect the dots,"],[122,431.54,2.08,"we have to see what could be in that moment,"],[122,433.62,2.04,"and then we have to materialize it, right?"],[122,435.66,3.2,"It's not enough to just think, \"This could be the love of my life.\""],[122,438.86,2.04,"You've got to actually go on dates, right?"],[122,440.9,3.8,"And so the point is, once we look at it as a process, we can influence it."],[122,444.7,4.2,"We can first learn how to see more serendipity triggers"],[122,448.9,2.6,"to try to focus on what is the information hidden"],[122,451.54,2.08,"in this good- or bad-luck moment."],[122,454.06,2.32,"You know, for example, asking in weekly meetings,"],[122,456.38,1.68,"what surprised you last week?"],[122,458.06,1.28,"Very simple question,"],[122,459.34,1.72,"but then people start to focus on,"],[122,461.06,3.16,"is there some information in this, kind of, unexpected event"],[122,464.26,1.84,"that has just been happening here?"],[122,466.14,3.88,"Second, we can learn how to \"seed\" more serendipity triggers."],[122,470.06,2.12,"And I'm not suggesting you spill more coffee."],[122,472.18,1.2,"There [are] other ways,"],[122,473.38,3.12,"one of which is, at an event like this today,"],[122,476.54,2.48,"to put some rich information points out there, right?"],[122,479.06,3.12,"When someone asks the dreaded “So what do you do?” question,"],[122,482.18,1.2,"saying something like,"],[122,483.38,2.24,"\"Hey, I'm Christian Busch, I study serendipity,"],[122,485.62,2.24,"but what I'm really curious about is parenting,"],[122,487.86,2.68,"because our four-year-old just learned how to negotiate.”"],[122,490.54,2.48,"And so what I'm doing here is I'm giving you a couple"],[122,493.06,2.6,"of potential connection points where you could be like,"],[122,495.66,2.68,"\"Oh my God, such a coincidence, our kid also -- X, Y, Z.\""],[122,498.38,2.92,"The point is, instead of having a pitch that's unidimensional,"],[122,501.3,2.6,"it's giving multidimensionality and potential dots"],[122,503.9,2.56,"that other people can connect for us."],[122,506.46,3.0,"Then third, we can learn how to better connect the dots."],[122,509.5,2.92,"For example, asking ourselves in unexpected moments,"],[122,512.46,2.36,"\"Can I still find some meaning in this?\""],[122,514.86,1.64,"And then last but not least,"],[122,516.54,2.32,"we can also learn how to act more on it"],[122,518.9,2.76,"because that's where we miss serendipity very often."],[122,521.7,1.64,"Think back in your life to moments"],[122,523.34,2.88,"where you saw an unexpected opportunity in the moment,"],[122,526.26,1.6,"but you didn't act on it, right?"],[122,527.9,4.08,"So maybe it was the unexpected idea in a meeting"],[122,531.98,1.88,"that you didn't bring into the meeting."],[122,533.9,2.24,"Or you were flirting with a person on the bus"],[122,536.18,1.84,"when you were still single, of course,"],[122,538.06,2.36,"but you were flirting with that person on the bus,"],[122,540.42,1.64,"and you didn't speak with them."],[122,542.06,3.64,"And then you walked outside and think, \"Ah, what could have happened had I?\""],[122,545.7,3.4,"And there's a lot of reasons that hold us back in those moments, right?"],[122,549.1,3.08,"But one of the major reasons is fear of rejection, right?"],[122,552.22,1.84,"So in my case, for example,"],[122,554.1,1.56,"my brain immediately goes to"],[122,555.66,2.64,"what's the worst thing that can happen if I do this?"],[122,558.34,1.28,"The sting of rejection."],[122,560.54,4.12,"One thing I've realized, though, and our research shows, is that actually,"],[122,564.7,5.2,"once you start to realize that that is not the real worst case,"],[122,569.94,5.0,"that the real worst case is the regret of not having tried."],[122,574.94,2.12,"That feeling of walking outside and thinking,"],[122,577.1,1.52,"\"Ah, what could have happened?\""],[122,578.62,1.6,"And so what can be really useful"],[122,580.22,3.84,"is this idea of reframing away from “What’s the risk of doing this?”"],[122,584.06,2.52,"to \"What's actually the risk of not doing it?\""],[122,586.62,2.16,"And then we realize, the risk of not doing it"],[122,588.78,2.64,"is a lot of times much higher than the risk of doing it."],[122,591.46,2.12,"So it gives us a bias for action."],[122,593.62,2.32,"Now why is this serendipity mindset,"],[122,595.98,4.08,"this capacity to turn the unexpected into positive outcomes"],[122,600.1,1.16,"so important?"],[122,601.82,2.2,"Well because the unexpected is everywhere,"],[122,604.06,3.24,"whether we see it or not, and whether we like it or not, right?"],[122,607.34,5.88,"The likelihood of one specific unexpected event happening is very low, right?"],[122,613.22,3.8,"It’s very unlikely that the lady here yells at me"],[122,617.02,3.04,"or the gentleman here throws a peach at me, right?"],[122,620.1,1.84,"That's very -- I hope that's unlikely."],[122,621.98,4.0,"I mean, you never know, but it's very unlikely in itself."],[122,626.5,3.56,"But when you add up all the possible unexpected things that could happen,"],[122,630.1,3.48,"all these infinite, unexpected things that could happen out there,"],[122,633.58,4.36,"it actually becomes quite likely that something unexpected happens."],[122,637.98,2.24,"And that's quite scary to me as a German, right?"],[122,640.26,3.44,"We love planning, we love having a strategy and straight lines."],[122,643.7,2.76,"And then life is a little bit more like a squiggle, right?"],[122,646.5,2.28,"Where unexpected events come from everywhere."],[122,648.82,2.0,"And so we have to build a muscle for that."],[122,650.86,2.12,"And that is true for good luck,"],[122,653.02,1.88,"but it's also true for bad luck, right?"],[122,654.9,2.28,"The same alertness to our surroundings,"],[122,657.18,1.88,"the ability to connect our dots"],[122,659.1,1.96,"that helps us nurture serendipity,"],[122,661.06,3.52,"can also help us guard against the impacts of zemblanity."],[122,664.62,3.2,"And you know, of course, mindset is not a cure-all, right?"],[122,667.86,4.8,"Our research also shows that things like education, networks, safety nets"],[122,672.7,2.84,"play a huge role when it comes to opportunity spaces."],[122,675.54,3.36,"But on the individual level, for what is at our disposal,"],[122,678.9,2.52,"mindset is one of the most effective tools we have"],[122,681.46,1.36,"to nurture serendipity"],[122,682.86,1.84,"and guard against zemblanity."],[122,684.7,1.84,"It's not toxic positivity."],[122,686.58,2.84,"It's not ignoring pain, it's not ignoring grief."],[122,689.42,2.2,"It’s accepting it, it’s working with it,"],[122,691.62,2.24,"and then saying, \"What can I still control now?"],[122,693.9,2.04,"What can I still focus on now?\""],[122,695.98,2.84,"And, you know, in our case,"],[122,698.86,2.6,"there was a lot of pain around losing our house, right?"],[122,701.46,2.2,"And that doesn't really go away that quickly."],[122,703.7,3.08,"I still wake up in the morning and think, how can that happen."],[122,706.82,2.52,"But alongside it, something unexpected happened."],[122,709.38,1.72,"It sparked new research."],[122,711.1,4.12,"It gave me a renewed sense of purpose, and it rallied the community around us."],[122,715.26,3.64,"I am far closer now with my in-laws, with whom we spend much more time."],[122,718.94,3.92,"And so out of the ashes, something new is beginning to grow."],[122,723.3,3.12,"Viktor Frankl, who has been a great inspiration to me,"],[122,726.46,3.68,"once said that \"everything can be taken from a [hu]man but one thing:"],[122,730.14,2.2,"the last of the human freedoms --"],[122,732.34,3.36,"to choose one's attitude in any given set of circumstances,"],[122,735.74,2.36,"to choose one's own way.\""],[122,738.62,1.52,"And I believe that's very true."],[122,740.14,3.2,"We can't pick the fires, the storms, the crises,"],[122,743.38,2.04,"but we can choose how we meet them."],[122,745.82,2.28,"And that choice is where serendipity begins"],[122,748.14,2.04,"as the unexpected starting point"],[122,750.22,2.92,"for a new chapter and potentially a whole new book."],[122,753.78,3.52,"My hope is that we start building a world"],[122,757.34,2.36,"that is designing more for serendipity"],[122,759.74,1.88,"and guards against zemblanity."],[122,761.62,3.08,"And the next time something unexpected happens to you,"],[122,764.74,1.6,"may serendipity be with you."],[122,766.34,1.16,"Thank you."],[123,6.88,3.88,"Losing a loved one can be one of the most traumatic experiences"],[123,10.76,1.5,"of a person’s life."],[123,12.55,3.38,"Grief brings on a whole host of strong emotions"],[123,16.09,3.46,"that impact both our mental and physical health."],[123,19.77,4.55,"But is it possible for these powerful feelings to endanger your life?"],[123,24.81,4.5,"In other words, can you actually die from a broken heart?"],[123,30.15,3.04,"One of the most widely documented medical phenomena"],[123,33.2,3.5,"addressing this question is the widowhood effect."],[123,36.95,3.67,"Studies of elderly couples have found that a person’s risk of dying"],[123,40.62,5.8,"can increase by up to 90% over the three months following their spouse’s death,"],[123,46.79,5.25,"leading a significant number of couples to pass away months, weeks,"],[123,52.05,2.29,"or even days apart."],[123,54.8,3.13,"There are a lot of theories explaining this effect."],[123,58.01,3.25,"One study found that severe grief and depression"],[123,61.27,4.59,"were associated with higher levels of inflammatory proteins in the blood,"],[123,65.98,2.38,"potentially impairing the immune system."],[123,68.69,3.59,"And depression, combined with the loss of a supporting partner,"],[123,72.28,4.17,"can make it hard for a person to keep up with their daily health needs."],[123,76.82,4.67,"Similarly, chronic stress can alter hormone levels, impair digestion,"],[123,81.49,2.38,"and dysregulate the immune system."],[123,84.25,3.63,"But while all these factors can reduce life expectancy,"],[123,88.13,2.63,"there’s another condition where powerful emotions"],[123,90.75,2.71,"can directly endanger someone’s life:"],[123,93.84,2.0,"takotsubo cardiomyopathy."],[123,96.76,5.09,"Also known as stress cardiomyopathy, or “broken heart syndrome,”"],[123,102.22,3.96,"this was originally labeled by Japanese doctors in 1990,"],[123,106.44,3.96,"who named the condition after the Japanese word for an octopus trap."],[123,110.81,2.63,"In takotsubo cardiomyopathy,"],[123,113.53,3.13,"the left ventricle— one of the heart’s four chambers—"],[123,116.74,3.09,"pools with blood and balloons outward,"],[123,119.95,3.34,"giving it a distinctively takotsubo-like shape."],[123,124.08,3.46,"And while most patients can recover over several weeks,"],[123,127.75,4.88,"roughly 5% will experience a potentially lethal cardiac arrest."],[123,133.17,4.96,"While the exact cause of takotsubo cardiomyopathy is still being studied,"],[123,138.38,3.96,"the condition often appears after a sudden traumatic event."],[123,142.47,3.25,"And since cases have been triggered by both shocking grief"],[123,145.72,1.96,"and unexpected happiness,"],[123,148.02,4.42,"it's likely not the nature of the emotion that matters, but the intensity."],[123,153.36,4.34,"Intense emotional events lead to a sudden increase in two hormones:"],[123,158.07,3.96,"epinephrine, also known as adrenaline, and norepinephrine."],[123,162.28,3.88,"Both these hormones interact with beta-adrenergic receptors,"],[123,166.16,3.29,"which have a high concentration in the left ventricle."],[123,170.04,2.59,"So during takotsubo cardiomyopathy,"],[123,172.84,5.05,"researchers believe epinephrine and norepinephrine flood the left ventricle,"],[123,178.01,3.63,"triggering it to contract rapidly and repeatedly."],[123,182.01,3.5,"Within minutes, this activity exhausts the ventricle."],[123,185.68,4.5,"And since it’s unable to pump effectively, blood begins to pool."],[123,191.1,3.67,"This hormonal spike can also cause spasms in the blood vessels"],[123,194.77,2.84,"that supply the heart with oxygen and nutrients."],[123,197.99,2.5,"So despite all the blood inside the heart,"],[123,200.53,4.42,"the muscle tissue itself may not be getting enough blood flow to function."],[123,205.78,3.79,"The result of all this is something that often feels like a heart attack"],[123,209.58,1.83,"without actually being one."],[123,212.0,2.84,"People undergoing takotsubo cardiomyopathy"],[123,214.84,4.09,"often experience intense chest pain and shortness of breath,"],[123,219.05,1.92,"even though their arteries aren’t blocked"],[123,220.97,2.0,"like they would be during a heart attack."],[123,223.09,4.88,"This similarity in symptoms also makes the condition difficult to diagnose,"],[123,228.06,3.75,"and it's easily confused for more common cardiac conditions."],[123,232.19,4.17,"Fortunately, the initial treatment for takotsubo cardiomyopathy"],[123,236.56,2.25,"and a heart attack is the same."],[123,239.44,4.09,"Doctors may prescribe blood thinners to prevent clotting in the heart"],[123,243.53,3.38,"and beta blockers to slow the heart's contractions."],[123,247.16,2.34,"Beyond that, patients might take blood thinners"],[123,249.5,3.59,"and blood pressure medications to protect their heart in the long run."],[123,253.08,5.34,"But there currently aren’t any specific treatments for takotsubo cardiomyopathy."],[123,258.8,3.25,"We also don't know exactly when it will strike."],[123,262.43,4.5,"The population that appears most at risk is postmenopausal women,"],[123,267.47,3.8,"perhaps because estrogen helps prevent cardiovascular disease,"],[123,271.27,2.92,"and menopause decreases estrogen production."],[123,274.6,3.71,"But obviously anyone can experience traumatic emotions."],[123,278.94,4.59,"The good news is that in most cases patients make a full recovery."],[123,283.86,4.04,"Because while our bodies are sensitive to the ups and downs of our emotions,"],[123,287.91,2.34,"they're also resilient."],[123,290.62,1.96,"And much like a broken bone,"],[123,292.58,4.34,"even a broken heart can heal with time and care."],[124,4.33,2.88,"Cloe Shasha Brooks: Salehe, thank you so much for joining us."],[124,7.21,1.79,"Salehe Bembury: Thanks for having me."],[124,9.01,3.67,"CSB: Among many of your popular designs, you made Crocs cool."],[124,12.68,4.34,"And this is called the Crocs Pollex clog,"],[124,17.01,1.75,"based on your own fingerprint mold."],[124,18.77,2.46,"And what I love is that Crocs reach out to you"],[124,21.27,1.96,"asking if they could send you some shoes."],[124,23.27,2.71,"And you said, \"How about we actually collaborate instead?\""],[124,26.02,1.21,"And they said yes."],[124,27.23,3.13,"So once they said yes, how did you decide to do this specifically?"],[124,30.4,3.13,"SB: It was a great honor because they first wanted to collaborate."],[124,33.53,4.38,"And then when I sent them what I didn’t know was a kid’s Croc shoe,"],[124,37.95,1.63,"they let me know that they loved"],[124,39.58,2.08,"that I was thinking of the form of the Croc,"],[124,41.66,3.46,"which a lot of collaborators in the past were simply reskinning the Croc,"],[124,45.17,1.21,"maybe adding jibbitz."],[124,46.42,1.54,"And I was thinking of the form,"],[124,47.96,2.46,"and that comes from my industrial-design background."],[124,50.42,2.88,"Once they saw that I was interested in manipulating the form,"],[124,53.34,3.0,"they asked me if I wanted to be the first collaborative designer"],[124,56.39,2.46,"to create a Croc from scratch,"],[124,58.89,3.42,"which I immediately did a Birdman hand rub and said yes."],[124,63.73,2.25,"And so this is the result of that."],[124,66.02,3.75,"Initially I had to make sure that I fostered and maintained"],[124,69.82,2.46,"the existing elements of the Croc DNA"],[124,72.28,3.21,"and made sure that I didn't scare away the existing consumer"],[124,75.49,2.75,"and getting the honor of working with a brand like Crocs,"],[124,78.24,2.38,"you get to work with their heritage and their DNA."],[124,80.62,1.83,"So I had to make sure to maintain that"],[124,82.5,2.67,"and then also introduce my own brand identity,"],[124,85.21,1.29,"put it through a new lens,"],[124,86.54,2.5,"and potentially speak to a new audience."],[124,89.04,1.63,"CSB: You know, it's pretty amazing"],[124,90.67,3.17,"because you've had a really amazing journey as a footwear designer."],[124,93.88,2.04,"You started at Payless in 2009,"],[124,95.97,2.92,"and then you started doing collaborations with a lot of brands"],[124,98.89,3.17,"like New Balance, Cole Haan, Versace, to name a few."],[124,102.06,2.33,"And now you run your own brand called Spunge,"],[124,104.39,1.17,"that's what those are."],[124,105.6,3.0,"So to help paint the picture of how you've forged your own path,"],[124,108.65,2.5,"I'd love for you to share the story that I just love,"],[124,111.15,1.5,"of you being on your couch,"],[124,112.69,2.38,"eating cereal in a moment of unemployment"],[124,115.11,2.88,"and you somehow went from scrolling on LinkedIn"],[124,118.03,2.67,"to convincing Versace to give you your dream job."],[124,120.7,1.38,"Will you tell us that story?"],[124,122.08,1.75,"SB: So, cereal on my couch,"],[124,123.87,2.71,"that is a description of the image of unemployment"],[124,126.62,2.29,"that I was experiencing at that time."],[124,128.96,3.96,"I was using all of the tools that have been given to us for outreach,"],[124,132.92,2.46,"LinkedIn, Craigslist,"],[124,135.38,1.88,"maybe it was even Facebook at the time,"],[124,137.3,2.71,"and sending messages to whoever would accept them."],[124,140.01,2.0,"I reached out to a Versace design director,"],[124,142.01,1.46,"and I proposed an opportunity."],[124,143.47,2.96,"I said, the sneaker industry is a multibillion-dollar business,"],[124,146.48,3.5,"and traditionally, that consumer isn't paying attention to fashion brands"],[124,149.98,2.59,"or traditionally paying attention to the sneaker brands"],[124,152.61,2.54,"that we're all used to and probably wearing right now."],[124,155.15,2.46,"But then there was this mass education that happened"],[124,157.61,2.34,"that Raf Simons, Adidas, Nike, Comme des Garçons,"],[124,159.99,2.42,"and now this massive consumer was aware of the fact"],[124,162.41,1.92,"that fashion houses can make sneakers."],[124,164.33,1.42,"And so I reached out to them,"],[124,165.79,2.46,"a few days later, Donatella herself responded."],[124,168.29,1.71,"She said she loved my ideas."],[124,171.17,2.04,"Perfect timing for that photo."],[124,173.21,3.38,"She said that she wanted me to come out to Milan"],[124,176.59,3.8,"and present to her what I thought the sneaker program could be."],[124,180.38,2.21,"I went out to Milan a few days later,"],[124,182.64,4.34,"presented a combination of merchandising and design and sales insight,"],[124,187.02,1.33,"and she loved it."],[124,188.35,1.83,"And she was like, \"I want to hire you."],[124,190.19,1.88,"I want you to move to Milan right away."],[124,192.06,1.21,"Do you want to consult?\""],[124,193.31,2.63,"She was really open about what the opportunity could be."],[124,195.94,1.17,"Because she was so open,"],[124,197.15,2.88,"I asked if I could have my own design studio in Los Angeles"],[124,200.03,2.63,"and travel to Milan monthly for factory visits and shows"],[124,202.7,1.17,"and she said yes."],[124,203.87,2.92,"So I went outside and did my pursuit of happiness --"],[124,206.79,1.5,"cry in the streets --"],[124,208.33,1.92,"because my life changed instantly."],[124,210.29,3.5,"But really what that was more about was the power of asking"],[124,213.79,1.71,"and what that can bring you."],[124,220.59,2.21,"CSB: So throughout your career,"],[124,222.8,2.67,"you've always used color and form and function"],[124,225.51,1.79,"in a way that's felt really playful."],[124,227.31,2.29,"There's so many nuanced details in your designs,"],[124,229.6,2.63,"like there's so many Easter eggs that people could find"],[124,232.23,1.63,"if they know what to look for."],[124,233.9,2.29,"So I'm going to show some pictures of your shoes,"],[124,236.19,3.67,"and I'd love to hear you share your design philosophy for each of them."],[124,239.86,1.21,"Tell us about these."],[124,241.07,2.0,"SB: So this was my first-ever collaboration"],[124,243.11,2.13,"with a Chinese brand called Anta."],[124,245.74,2.29,"This was a from-scratch collaboration."],[124,248.08,2.92,"A lot of collaborations these days are color-ups and skinings,"],[124,251.04,3.63,"but this was, I guess the result of a white piece of paper."],[124,254.71,2.34,"And I guess what I was trying to accomplish here"],[124,257.09,2.96,"was an introduction to footwear,"],[124,260.09,2.13,"the footwear industry and the audience --"],[124,262.26,2.54,"because before that I was working at different brands,"],[124,264.8,2.46,"and this was the first time that a piece of footwear"],[124,267.31,1.42,"was going to feature my name."],[124,268.72,3.63,"So there was a lot of things that I had to introduce to my existing audience."],[124,272.39,1.29,"CSB: How about this one?"],[124,273.69,1.25,"SB: This is the HALI 1."],[124,274.94,2.84,"It's the first signature sneaker of Tyrese Haliburton."],[124,277.77,2.42,"I became a footwear designer because of basketball."],[124,280.19,3.67,"That was my kind of initial inspiration that made me feel the emotions"],[124,283.86,2.04,"that I believe I'm giving to others now."],[124,285.95,1.29,"It is with Puma."],[124,287.62,3.46,"He recently wore it in the previous NBA finals."],[124,291.75,1.58,"It had a pretty tragic ending,"],[124,293.37,4.84,"but it was still amazing to be a part of that shoe"],[124,298.25,3.75,"and for it to fall at that part of my career was really special."],[124,302.05,1.21,"CSB: I love these."],[124,303.26,2.46,"SB: This was a shoe I did with Pyer Moss,"],[124,305.76,2.0,"which is a high-fashion brand."],[124,307.8,3.46,"The creative director reached out to me, and he asked me to create a shoe"],[124,311.27,2.84,"that represented the feeling of being financially stuck."],[124,314.1,1.96,"I knew that I couldn't use concrete,"],[124,316.1,4.21,"so then I quickly thought of using silicone as the solution."],[124,320.32,1.5,"And that's what I made."],[124,321.82,2.0,"CSB: And that was in a runway show, right?"],[124,323.86,4.17,"SB: Yes, this was in like a SW ’16 runway show, or SS."],[124,328.07,1.17,"CSB: How about these?"],[124,329.28,4.09,"SB: These are a bunch of images from my Moncler Genius collaboration."],[124,333.37,2.04,"This was my first ready-to-wear collection."],[124,335.42,3.59,"And as of late, a lot of my work has been described as like world-building."],[124,339.04,2.0,"And this was that in real form,"],[124,341.09,2.25,"because I got to create a full installation"],[124,343.38,4.21,"that helped further tell the story of the ready-to-wear collection"],[124,347.59,1.96,"and featured footwear, accessories,"],[124,349.55,2.88,"and there were some pretty big billboards as well,"],[124,352.47,2.29,"which I hadn't experienced thus far in my career."],[124,354.77,2.46,"CSB: And when you did this ready-to-wear collection,"],[124,357.23,2.79,"how did you go from just doing footwear to clothing?"],[124,360.06,1.75,"Like, how did you think about that?"],[124,361.82,2.09,"SB: I think one of the most difficult things"],[124,363.9,3.09,"for an independent designer is creating your signature language."],[124,367.03,2.46,"Like, what is that signature identity"],[124,369.49,3.0,"that someone can recognize without even seeing a logo?"],[124,372.54,3.92,"So once I kind of established that, I think it now makes it easy"],[124,376.46,3.63,"to bring that across different categories of product."],[124,380.79,3.0,"CSB: And then this video is just so charming."],[124,383.84,1.92,"SB: That is Thundercat, that's Steven."],[124,386.3,3.88,"That was for a New Balance project called \"Sand be the Time.\""],[124,390.47,2.46,"A lot of the projects are influenced by experiences."],[124,392.93,2.25,"So this was from a trip --"],[124,395.64,1.17,"Where was that?"],[124,396.81,1.79,"That was the pink sand dunes in Utah."],[124,398.64,3.46,"And that basically was the foundation of the story for that shoe."],[124,402.15,3.63,"So the average person looking at that shoe might see a pink shoe."],[124,405.82,2.25,"But then with the story of the pink sand dunes"],[124,408.11,5.96,"and relating the execution of the chenille into the colors of the sunset."],[124,414.08,1.67,"Now you’ve cultivated a community,"],[124,415.75,2.17,"you’ve invited people to come into your world"],[124,417.96,1.17,"and you've told a story."],[124,419.12,2.13,"CSB: You got really into hiking in nature."],[124,421.25,2.17,"You started designing outdoor shoes and gear,"],[124,423.46,3.17,"and in one instance you translated Arizona's Antelope Canyon"],[124,426.67,3.71,"into a shoe called Peace Be the Journey, which came out in 2020."],[124,430.38,2.54,"And that shoe, even though it came out six years ago,"],[124,432.93,3.21,"is still so popular that it's being sold for markup"],[124,436.18,2.84,"on resale sites like eBay like, to this day,"],[124,439.06,2.13,"consistently, for a lot of money."],[124,441.23,3.13,"So tell us why you think this shoe is still so popular."],[124,445.57,3.21,"SB: Potentially that I told a story that people could resonate with,"],[124,448.78,3.0,"this was influenced by a trip to Antelope Canyon,"],[124,451.78,2.25,"a beautiful place, for those that haven't been."],[124,454.03,4.5,"And ultimately the goal was to make this shoe represent that experience."],[124,458.58,2.67,"So if you're looking at that shoe, it's an orange shoe."],[124,461.29,2.92,"But then when I tie the orange to the tones of Antelope Canyon"],[124,464.21,2.08,"and the blue of the N represents the sky,"],[124,466.3,3.63,"and the maroons represent the tones in the sunset."],[124,470.26,4.0,"And then the campaign tells that story further."],[124,474.26,1.58,"Now when people wear that shoe,"],[124,475.89,2.42,"sure, it may be a shoe that they think looks cool,"],[124,478.35,2.75,"but then they now feel like they're a part of a community."],[124,481.1,2.84,"I remember when I first started traveling to China for work,"],[124,483.98,2.29,"I would always look for people wearing Supreme"],[124,486.27,2.46,"because I assumed that there was a higher likelihood"],[124,488.78,1.17,"that they spoke English."],[124,489.94,2.88,"So sometimes there are visual cues that signify a community."],[124,492.86,1.17,"CSB: It's so cool."],[124,494.03,1.79,"So staying on that New Balance theme,"],[124,495.87,3.63,"you designed the New Balance 574 Yurt shoe,"],[124,499.5,1.46,"with a whistle on the heel."],[124,501.0,1.17,"SB: Yes."],[124,502.21,2.29,"CSB: So most designers would never think of that."],[124,504.54,3.71,"I'm going to share this with all of you so you can see what I'm talking about."],[124,520.89,1.54,"CSB: So how'd you think of that?"],[124,523.14,1.67,"SB: So New Balance tasked me"],[124,524.81,3.5,"with the opportunity of creating a shoe from scratch."],[124,528.98,2.09,"Around this time, my buddy Samuel Ross,"],[124,531.11,1.83,"who's a designer based out of London,"],[124,532.95,2.46,"had this Nike shoe called the “Zoom Vomero,”"],[124,535.45,3.0,"and it featured a molded plastic piece on the back of the shoe."],[124,538.45,1.29,"And I'd wear them a lot."],[124,539.79,2.96,"And people would always ask me, what does that molded piece do?"],[124,542.79,1.75,"And I would respond, nothing at all."],[124,544.54,1.79,"And people seemed to still accept it."],[124,546.33,1.71,"And I found that really fascinating"],[124,548.04,2.88,"because it was a very unused area of real estate of the shoe."],[124,550.92,2.84,"And so I thought to myself, what if this area of real estate"],[124,553.8,1.88,"could be used for something functional?"],[124,555.68,2.96,"Obviously, if that functional thing was filled with technology,"],[124,558.68,2.0,"now you’re looking at a $2,000 shoe,"],[124,560.68,1.25,"so that wasn't an option."],[124,561.93,3.0,"Then I thought of how I could manipulate plastic to function."],[124,564.98,1.33,"That became a whistle."],[124,566.31,3.17,"Truthfully, I created the whistle without thinking of the why,"],[124,569.52,1.25,"the purpose,"],[124,570.77,2.34,"I just thought a whistle was cool."],[124,573.15,4.21,"And then, for the prototype, I glued a whistle into a New Balance shoe,"],[124,577.41,2.54,"and I met with the team and I told them a whole story"],[124,579.95,2.21,"of think about camping with your friends,"],[124,582.2,1.46,"and it's almost dusk"],[124,583.7,2.42,"and you stop to take a picture of some deer"],[124,586.16,1.58,"and your friends walk ahead."],[124,587.79,2.13,"And now you've lost them."],[124,589.96,2.33,"And then I grabbed a shoe from beneath the table,"],[124,592.34,2.84,"blew the whistle and then they all slow-clapped."],[124,596.84,3.42,"And so again, that was"],[124,600.26,3.04,"maybe a little bit of my industrial design background, you know,"],[124,603.31,1.96,"trying to figure out how to find utility"],[124,605.31,2.29,"within maybe something that doesn't have as much."],[124,607.6,1.92,"But then more importantly,"],[124,609.52,3.13,"I think it's kind of cool to just do something first in footwear,"],[124,612.69,1.88,"there's never been a whistle on a shoe."],[124,614.61,2.84,"And if you were to think about how much energy it would take"],[124,617.45,2.75,"for you to scream if you broke your ankle on a hike alone,"],[124,620.24,3.04,"compared to the amount of energy that would just simply take you"],[124,623.33,1.17,"to blow a whistle."],[124,624.54,3.42,"So, you know, giving the consumer a tool seemed like a good opportunity."],[124,627.96,2.34,"CSB: You know, one thing I've heard you say before"],[124,630.33,3.92,"is that genius can be created in seconds and crap can be created over months."],[124,634.3,1.17,"SB: Yeah."],[124,635.46,2.38,"CSB: I think that's such a great line."],[124,637.84,2.04,"SB: That's why you never charge hourly."],[124,639.89,1.17,"CSB: That is so true."],[124,641.1,2.29,"So know, anyone who hires consultants."],[124,644.64,1.88,"So how does someone optimize their life"],[124,646.56,3.75,"so that they can avoid creating crap over long amounts of time?"],[124,650.31,1.38,"SB: Maybe avoid distraction."],[124,651.69,4.17,"I've always found that when trying to create,"],[124,655.9,2.96,"not getting too distracted by the thing that we all have"],[124,658.9,1.17,"in our pockets right now"],[124,660.11,3.34,"and constantly scrolling other people's ideas,"],[124,663.49,3.46,"social media, the internet, Instagram, it's a great sense of inspiration,"],[124,666.95,2.59,"but it can also create a massive cloud of distraction"],[124,669.58,4.3,"that makes you lose sight of your own vision."],[124,673.88,2.79,"And so I think finding that balance between the work"],[124,676.67,2.0,"and avoiding distraction is important."],[124,679.84,1.25,"CSB: Yeah."],[124,681.13,2.0,"You know, one thing that you're known for,"],[124,683.18,1.17,"beyond your shoes,"],[124,684.35,3.13,"is speaking about those Easter-egg details in your work,"],[124,687.52,1.21,"is the boxes"],[124,688.73,2.71,"that you actually design for the shoes, the shoe boxes."],[124,691.48,4.46,"And so any sneakerhead I've spoken to is like, oh, Salehe's boxes,"],[124,695.98,3.84,"like, \"I love collecting his shoes because they stack so beautifully.\""],[124,699.82,3.75,"So like, how much time do you spend thinking about how the shoe will look"],[124,703.62,1.71,"on the foot versus in the box?"],[124,705.95,3.46,"SB: Well, I just think I often go back to, you know,"],[124,709.46,2.25,"I'm pretty experienced in footwear design,"],[124,711.75,2.25,"but I have my master's in footwear consumerism."],[124,714.04,3.04,"So I just think of those moments that resonated with me"],[124,717.13,1.42,"and that made me feel cool"],[124,718.55,2.79,"or more athletic or magical."],[124,721.38,2.79,"And so I'm really just trying to recreate those moments."],[124,724.22,2.25,"I really think it's important to take advantage"],[124,726.47,2.21,"of every area of real estate of a product,"],[124,728.68,3.09,"because as creatives, all we have is that reveal moment, right?"],[124,731.77,3.96,"So when we reveal that product, how does it make the audience feel?"],[124,736.11,1.38,"Do they like it immediately?"],[124,737.48,1.54,"Do they not like it immediately?"],[124,739.03,3.46,"And then they see it over and over again, and then they start to like it."],[124,742.49,1.79,"I love that relationship with product"],[124,744.32,4.5,"because it’s almost like “Steve Jobsian,” if that’s the term."],[124,749.54,1.29,"Because, you know, he said"],[124,750.83,3.25,"the consumer doesn't know what they want until you show it to them."],[124,754.08,1.79,"So the Crocs Pollex being an example."],[124,755.92,2.88,"Like, there are a lot of people that that shoe upset at first"],[124,758.8,3.54,"and then slowly seeing them come around to both that product specifically,"],[124,762.34,3.04,"or even the brand of Crocs was really cool to witness"],[124,765.43,3.54,"because you almost were like, changing minds with product."],[124,770.02,2.0,"CSB: So this passion of yours runs deep."],[124,772.06,2.75,"When you were nine years old,"],[124,774.85,2.13,"you made this --"],[124,777.02,2.42,"Air Jordan 11 out of clay."],[124,779.48,1.67,"And I would love to know,"],[124,781.15,2.84,"do you still feel like the nine-year-old version of yourself"],[124,784.03,2.0,"when you do your work today?"],[124,786.07,1.17,"SB: Absolutely."],[124,787.28,2.25,"I mean, if you love what you do, it's not a job."],[124,789.53,2.79,"This is pretty much what I've wanted to do my entire life."],[124,792.37,2.88,"So there’s a certain level of freedom."],[124,795.29,3.04,"There's a certain level of nostalgia that exists within my work."],[124,798.34,2.84,"To quote Kanye West in one interview,"],[124,801.21,2.79,"he was saying that he felt like he was creating most freely"],[124,804.05,1.96,"when he felt like he was three years old."],[124,806.05,1.54,"And that was a time in our lives"],[124,807.6,2.5,"when we didn’t have many boundaries or restrictions."],[124,810.14,2.84,"And our creative output was exactly how we wanted it to be."],[124,813.02,4.8,"And now we potentially have employers or market rules"],[124,817.86,1.71,"or trends that we need to follow."],[124,819.61,2.29,"And then those are all ultimately like guardrails"],[124,821.94,1.71,"that prevent true creative freedom."],[124,823.65,3.67,"So everything’s about balance at the end of the day,"],[124,827.32,2.88,"but I still try to maintain that freedom as much as possible."],[124,830.24,1.17,"CSB: Awesome."],[124,831.45,1.54,"Well, thank you so much, Salehe."],[124,833.0,1.25,"SB: Thanks for having me."],[125,7.0,4.59,"What if you need someone to understand an idea that's difficult to explain?"],[125,12.01,3.63,"Explaining a complicated idea so that someone can understand it"],[125,15.68,3.09,"is one of the ways society moves forward."],[125,19.27,4.25,"Harvard psychologist Dan Gilbert came to TED with a difficult task."],[125,23.69,2.04,"In just a single short talk,"],[125,25.86,5.46,"he planned to explain a complicated concept called “Synthesized Happiness,”"],[125,31.44,4.84,"and why it leads us to make incorrect predictions about our own futures."],[125,36.62,2.46,"Let's see how he goes about his task."],[125,39.33,1.92,"When you have 21 minutes to speak,"],[125,41.29,2.71,"2 million years seems like a really long time."],[125,44.25,2.54,"But evolutionarily, 2 million years is nothing."],[125,46.79,5.55,"And yet in 2 million years, the human brain has nearly tripled in mass;"],[125,52.51,4.21,"going from the one and a quarter pound brain of our ancestor here, Habilis,"],[125,56.76,5.13,"to the almost 3 pound meatloaf that everybody here has between their ears."],[125,62.23,5.0,"What is it about a big brain that nature was so eager"],[125,67.23,1.83,"for every one of us to have one?"],[125,69.69,3.25,"Do you feel a little spark of curiosity?"],[125,73.61,3.46,"That's the first step to a successful explanation."],[125,77.66,3.04,"Once a mind is curious, it opens up."],[125,80.99,2.46,"Gilbert continues to spark our curiosity."],[125,84.29,2.17,"Well, it turns out when brains triple in size,"],[125,86.46,3.63,"they don't just get three times bigger, they gain new structures."],[125,90.17,2.92,"And one of the main reasons that our brain got so big"],[125,93.09,3.17,"is because it got a new part called the frontal lobe—"],[125,96.26,2.92,"and particularly a part called the prefrontal cortex."],[125,99.55,3.63,"Now, what does a prefrontal cortex do for you that should justify"],[125,103.18,3.46,"the entire architectural overhaul of the human skull"],[125,106.64,1.96,"in the blink of evolutionary time?"],[125,108.77,1.67,"While keeping us interested,"],[125,110.44,5.67,"Gilbert introduced the first concept he’ll be building on: prefrontal cortex."],[125,117.2,2.67,"Turns out the prefrontal cortex does lots of things."],[125,119.87,5.5,"But one of the most important things it does is an experience simulator."],[125,125.75,2.34,"Pilots practice in flight simulators"],[125,128.08,2.79,"so that they don't make real mistakes in planes."],[125,131.0,3.46,"Human beings have this marvelous adaptation that they can actually"],[125,134.5,5.17,"have experiences in their heads before they try them out in real life."],[125,139.68,2.46,"This is a trick that none of our ancestors could do,"],[125,142.14,3.21,"that no other animal can do quite like we can."],[125,145.39,1.58,"It's a marvelous adaptation."],[125,146.98,3.84,"It’s up there with opposable thumbs and standing upright and language"],[125,150.9,3.17,"as one of the things that got our species out of the trees"],[125,154.07,1.54,"and into the shopping mall."],[125,156.19,5.21,"Gilbert just slipped in a second new concept: experience simulator."],[125,161.62,4.55,"That’s another key building block in the big idea he’s planning to explain."],[125,166.41,2.96,"The metaphor— that of a flight simulator—"],[125,169.46,3.59,"helps us understand what an experience simulator is."],[125,173.67,1.71,"Now how about an example?"],[125,175.67,1.25,"All of you have done this."],[125,176.92,3.71,"I mean, you know, Ben & Jerry’s doesn’t have liver and onion ice cream,"],[125,180.8,3.55,"and it’s not because they whipped some up, tried it, and went yuck!"],[125,184.35,3.0,"It’s because, without leaving your armchair,"],[125,187.35,4.42,"you can simulate that flavor and say yuck before you make it."],[125,193.1,5.3,"Gilbert uses a single strong example of how the experience simulator works,"],[125,198.4,2.46,"and then you totally get what it is."],[125,201.41,3.0,"But now the talk takes a turn."],[125,204.66,3.79,"Gilbert asks the audience to use their experience simulators"],[125,208.45,3.17,"to imagine going through two very different life events:"],[125,211.83,3.29,"winning the lottery and becoming paraplegic—"],[125,215.38,2.42,"which means losing the use of your legs."],[125,218.13,2.67,"He asks everyone which of these life events"],[125,220.8,2.0,"they would prefer to happen to them."],[125,223.18,4.13,"Then he shares a truly amazing piece of information."],[125,227.64,4.3,"Interestingly, there are data on these two groups of people,"],[125,231.94,1.75,"data on how happy they are."],[125,233.85,2.75,"And this is exactly what you expected, isn't it?"],[125,236.9,2.54,"But these aren’t the data, I made these up."],[125,239.69,1.25,"These are the data."],[125,241.07,3.63,"You failed the pop quiz, and you’re hardly five minutes into the lecture."],[125,244.7,4.13,"Because the fact is that a year after losing the use of their legs"],[125,248.83,2.17,"and a year after winning the lotto,"],[125,251.21,4.63,"lottery winners and paraplegics are equally happy with their lives."],[125,256.13,1.67,"What? That can't be right."],[125,258.13,3.46,"This new concept of the experience simulator has suddenly taken you"],[125,261.59,1.88,"to a place you didn't expect."],[125,263.8,1.75,"A confusing place."],[125,266.14,2.75,"You’re in what’s called a knowledge gap."],[125,269.43,3.17,"There’s more information you need in order to understand the facts"],[125,272.6,2.04,"Gilbert has just presented to you."],[125,275.02,2.42,"He goes on to fill in that gap."],[125,277.86,4.5,"He and his coworkers have been researching something called impact bias,"],[125,282.74,4.25,"which is the tendency for our experience simulators to work badly—"],[125,287.41,3.38,"to make us believe different experiences are more different"],[125,290.79,1.54,"than they actually are."],[125,293.0,3.13,"By putting a name on it, impact bias,"],[125,296.29,3.34,"the mystery of the information we’re struggling to make sense of"],[125,299.63,2.04,"starts to become more believable."],[125,302.34,3.75,"Our curiosity is now burning more brightly than before."],[125,306.38,6.05,"Gilbert uses our curiosity to reveal the big idea of his talk."],[125,312.73,3.59,"In fact, they really are, from field studies to laboratory studies,"],[125,316.31,2.33,"we see that winning or losing an election,"],[125,318.65,4.13,"gaining or losing a romantic partner, getting or not getting a promotion,"],[125,322.78,3.29,"passing or not passing a college test, on and on,"],[125,326.36,4.25,"have far less impact, less intensity, and much less duration"],[125,330.79,2.75,"than people expect them to have."],[125,334.54,5.17,"Now, in fact, a recent study— this almost floors me—"],[125,339.71,4.63,"a recent study showing how major life traumas affect people"],[125,344.34,4.46,"suggests that if it happened over three months ago, with only a few exceptions,"],[125,348.8,2.96,"it has no impact whatsoever on your happiness."],[125,353.39,1.0,"Why?"],[125,355.06,3.59,"Because happiness can be synthesized."],[125,358.77,4.13,"Sir Thomas Brown wrote in 1642, “I am the happiest man alive."],[125,362.98,5.17,"I have that in me that can convert poverty to riches, adversity to prosperity,"],[125,368.15,4.75,"I am more invulnerable than Achilles; fortune hath not one place to hit me.”"],[125,373.12,2.84,"Kind of remarkable machinery does this guy have in his head?"],[125,375.95,5.63,"Well, it turns out it’s precisely the same remarkable machinery that all of us have."],[125,382.38,2.63,"Human beings have something that we might think of"],[125,385.0,4.21,"as a psychological immune system, a system of cognitive processes,"],[125,389.22,2.75,"largely non-conscious cognitive processes,"],[125,392.05,3.71,"that help them change their views of the world"],[125,395.77,4.13,"so that they can feel better about the worlds in which they find themselves."],[125,400.52,6.17,"There it is, Gilbert’s big idea: something called Synthetic Happiness."],[125,407.44,2.38,"He goes on to describe how this works."],[125,409.82,2.25,"It's like an immune system for the mind."],[125,412.41,3.38,"Just as the immune system protects our bodies from infection,"],[125,415.79,3.09,"synthesized happiness protects our peace of mind."],[125,419.04,5.3,"Our brains build happiness for themselves, even when things don’t go as planned."],[125,424.75,4.5,"Gilbert got his audience to understand synthesized happiness"],[125,429.47,5.13,"by building on the concepts of prefrontal cortex, experience simulator,"],[125,434.85,2.08,"and impact bias."],[125,437.64,4.75,"And to make it clear, he used another metaphor: the immune system."],[125,443.81,4.3,"Gilbert didn’t try to explain his complicated idea in one big leap."],[125,448.44,2.5,"He got there piece by piece."],[125,451.61,3.59,"After giving us some examples of synthesized happiness in action,"],[125,455.53,3.8,"He goes on to show how you can live a wiser, happier life"],[125,459.37,2.96,"if you remember synthetic happiness exists."],[125,462.46,4.13,"At the end of the explanation, the way we see the world has been upgraded."],[125,466.8,2.5,"It’s richer, deeper, and truer."],[125,469.96,1.83,"There is a lot of evidence out there"],[125,471.8,3.38,"suggesting that this is how understanding must be built."],[125,475.55,1.25,"Like a tower,"],[125,477.14,4.05,"each new block of information supports the next new block of information."],[125,481.77,4.13,"Each bit gets stronger thanks to metaphors and examples."],[125,486.4,3.84,"The metaphors show us the shape of each new idea"],[125,490.49,3.04,"so that the mind knows how to recognize it."],[125,493.86,5.25,"If the core of your talk is explaining a powerful new idea, it’s helpful to ask:"],[125,499.41,2.79,"what do you think your audience already knows?"],[125,502.5,3.46,"What are the building blocks you need to explain your idea?"],[125,506.54,2.54,"What metaphors and examples can you use"],[125,509.09,4.21,"to help listeners understand things they may not be familiar with yet?"],[125,513.59,4.5,"It can be tricky to spot what things an audience may need help understanding."],[125,518.39,3.59,"We all suffer from something economist Robin Hogarth calls"],[125,521.98,1.5,"“the curse of knowledge.”"],[125,523.69,4.04,"Basically, we find it hard to remember what it feels like not to know something"],[125,527.73,2.25,"that we ourselves do know."],[125,530.99,4.67,"This might be the most important requirement for clear communication."],[125,536.24,2.92,"You can’t give a powerful new idea to an audience"],[125,539.16,2.42,"unless you can figure out how to explain it."],[125,541.87,4.5,"That can only be done step by step, fueled by curiosity."],[126,3.54,3.04,"Whitney Pennington Rodgers: You run the Dubai Future Foundation."],[126,6.58,3.88,"For those of us who are not initiated, what is a future foundation?"],[126,10.5,3.44,"His Excellency Khalfan Belhoul: So imagine you run a lab,"],[126,13.98,3.96,"and the owner of the lab comes to you and says,"],[126,17.98,3.4,"\"You can do whatever you want,"],[126,21.42,2.04,"and you have my full backing"],[126,23.46,5.64,"and you have a solid amount of financial resources."],[126,29.14,4.56,"If rules are an issue, we can change them for you."],[126,34.46,5.44,"We can connect you with any relevant decision maker to break those rules."],[126,40.94,3.64,"We will not be upset if you make mistakes,"],[126,44.62,3.4,"and we will support you on every winning bet you make.\""],[126,49.1,1.72,"And in summary, will you take that?"],[126,50.82,1.4,"WPR: I would take that deal."],[126,52.22,4.28,"HEKB: OK, so the owner of the lab is Sheikh Mohammad bin Rashid,"],[126,56.5,2.56,"and the lab is Dubai Future Foundation."],[126,59.1,1.16,"So that’s how it is."],[126,60.3,4.04,"We are entitled and empowered to explore new ideas --"],[126,64.34,2.32,"to see where the future is heading."],[126,67.1,4.4,"We’ve been supported and backed by a top board of trustees --"],[126,71.54,2.36,"I mean, our chairman is the crown prince."],[126,73.9,5.88,"The board members are ministers and director generals across all sectors."],[126,79.78,2.4,"Which means if we want to disrupt mobility, we can."],[126,82.22,2.36,"If we want to talk health tech, we can."],[126,84.58,2.84,"If we want to explore fintech, we can."],[126,87.46,4.2,"We have the muscle behind us, and we have the support in case we fail."],[126,91.7,3.12,"The unique thing [that] makes us --"],[126,94.86,2.04,"which I’m sure you’re aware of"],[126,96.94,2.72,"from the global landscape --"],[126,99.7,3.08,"that usually governments are a hurdle or they take time"],[126,102.82,1.36,"or they're bureaucratic."],[126,104.18,2.88,"We are hearing from our leaders that we can actually do this."],[126,107.1,1.24,"This is music to our ears."],[126,108.38,1.16,"So this pushes us."],[126,109.58,3.56,"This makes us comfortable at actually pivoting."],[126,113.18,2.4,"And if we have any venture capitalists out here --"],[126,115.62,2.2,"you would understand this more than anyone."],[126,117.98,3.44,"They don’t talk about [the] 80 percent of their portfolio that goes bust,"],[126,121.42,2.32,"but they really back the winning bets."],[126,123.78,3.64,"They’ve all made those mistakes, but they don’t look at them as mistakes --"],[126,127.46,1.8,"they look at them as learning points."],[126,129.26,2.96,"And I think this mindset that’s been given to us from leaders,"],[126,132.22,3.76,"to really explore and test and double down on winning bets,"],[126,135.98,2.64,"is what makes the Dubai Future Foundation a success."],[126,138.66,3.72,"WPR: I think a lot of nations and a lot of cities talk about this idea"],[126,142.42,3.28,"of progress and being futurist,"],[126,145.7,1.88,"but it's hard to sustain that."],[126,147.62,4.0,"What are some of the ways that you’re able to get buy-in"],[126,151.66,4.64,"and to be able to embed in both government and culture, futurist thinking?"],[126,156.34,2.76,"HEKB: What we do is we start, first of all,"],[126,159.14,3.76,"by ensuring that our internal teams are experts"],[126,162.94,2.88,"in understanding where the future is heading."],[126,165.86,3.2,"We invest a lot in people"],[126,169.1,3.76,"that have this ... expertise"],[126,172.9,1.64,"when it comes to future foresight."],[126,174.54,3.48,"We invest a lot in internal talent."],[126,178.02,5.08,"We have a foresight team that does horizon scanning on a constant basis,"],[126,183.1,1.52,"and this becomes our engine."],[126,184.66,3.4,"But we don't stop there because this is not only confined to us"],[126,188.06,1.32,"as Dubai Future Foundation."],[126,189.42,3.24,"We also connect with like-minded networks from all over the world"],[126,192.7,1.96,"that feed us more information."],[126,195.46,4.48,"And then this gives us a view of where the future is heading."],[126,199.98,2.24,"And the further you go -- the grayer it becomes."],[126,202.22,3.48,"We can maybe have a better prediction of what’s happening in 2026,"],[126,206.14,1.28,"but the more you go,"],[126,207.46,3.28,"the more challenging the guess would be, right?"],[126,210.78,1.76,"But the more conversations you have,"],[126,212.58,2.56,"the more inclusion there is in the conversation,"],[126,215.18,1.76,"the better visibility you have."],[126,216.98,4.28,"Then it’s all about being agile and flexible and getting everyone involved"],[126,221.26,1.96,"and having the guts to really test."],[126,223.94,3.0,"Test, but at the same time create a safe environment in testing."],[126,226.98,2.96,"So you get the entrepreneurs, you get the investors,"],[126,229.98,3.08,"and you back it up by also investing as government"],[126,233.06,1.36,"and create an environment --"],[126,234.42,2.28,"which is what we call the sandbox environment --"],[126,236.7,3.6,"which is really testing those ideas in a safe manner."],[126,240.3,3.2,"For example, if you're looking at last-mile drone deliveries,"],[126,243.5,1.52,"you're not going to deploy that"],[126,245.02,2.4,"before you actually make sure it's safe for people."],[126,247.46,1.96,"You need that physical space for that."],[126,249.42,3.16,"On the less physical and less tangible kind of projects --"],[126,252.62,3.04,"look at the crypto world and the digital assets --"],[126,255.7,3.24,"you don’t see them in front of you, but the value of them is immense."],[126,258.98,2.16,"But can we actually open them up to the market"],[126,261.14,2.64,"before we create a virtual safe environment,"],[126,263.82,2.36,"including all the relevant parties,"],[126,266.18,4.72,"to have a proper conversation on a deployment basis?"],[126,270.94,2.8,"I mean, we can do as much of that as we want,"],[126,273.78,2.64,"but if there isn’t a mechanism"],[126,276.42,3.0,"of ensuring that this can be deployed safely"],[126,279.46,2.76,"and in a unified way,"],[126,282.26,2.24,"then we'll just be talking to ourselves."],[126,284.54,3.48,"WPR: Well I think Dubai represents such a compelling example"],[126,288.06,2.0,"of economic transformation,"],[126,290.06,1.76,"shifting away from oil revenue"],[126,291.86,1.92,"to other spaces like tourism."],[126,294.18,4.24,"Can you talk about the current economic state of Dubai"],[126,298.46,3.92,"and sort of how you think that will influence where the city is headed?"],[126,302.42,3.68,"HEKB: We are living in a very turbulent environment."],[126,306.46,2.52,"The Middle East is a very tough neighborhood."],[126,310.06,3.4,"Our country is not so big from a size perspective,"],[126,313.5,2.28,"but our ambitions are bigger than anyone’s."],[126,316.62,2.2,"We have tensions around the world,"],[126,318.86,5.36,"whether it’s big nations conflicting on economicissues,"],[126,324.26,1.36,"geopolitical issues,"],[126,325.66,5.12,"and the country has pushed itself to be a bridge of peace, or"],[126,330.82,3.68,"when it comes to business, when it comes to geopolitics."],[126,334.5,3.6,"And I think this is what really attracts people to come to Dubai."],[126,338.14,2.08,"We as locals"],[126,340.22,3.8,"tend to underestimate the safety factor"],[126,344.02,1.52,"and the prosperity factor."],[126,345.58,2.44,"We just got too comfortable and got too used to it."],[126,348.06,3.76,"But when we have conversations with our fellow expats"],[126,351.86,3.64,"that come in and choose Dubai or the UAE as home --"],[126,355.54,1.48,"we listen to them,"],[126,357.06,1.52,"[and it] gives me goosebumps"],[126,358.62,4.36,"on how those small details"],[126,363.02,3.44,"of safety and prosperity for them and their families"],[126,366.46,4.76,"mean so much that makes us really appreciate the structure here."],[126,371.26,1.72,"But again, we’re not perfect."],[126,372.98,2.04,"We've done our fair share of mistakes,"],[126,375.02,1.88,"but we double down on what we do right."],[126,376.94,5.96,"WPR: We seem to be in a very transformative moment as a world."],[126,382.9,2.84,"What role do you think -- when you think about things like,"],[126,385.74,2.8,"beyond even just geopolitical tensions as you mentioned,"],[126,388.54,1.8,"and economic issues,"],[126,390.38,2.6,"[or] when you think about existential threats"],[126,392.98,2.92,"like climate change or the rise of artificial intelligence --"],[126,395.94,3.24,"what role do you see futurists thinking playing in global progress?"],[126,399.22,1.92,"HEKB: So I’ll tell you my humble advice:"],[126,401.18,3.32,"The world has recently been giving us so many signs,"],[126,404.54,4.96,"that there’s no other way forward"],[126,409.54,2.6,"than us to all get together."],[126,412.46,1.24,"You’ve mentioned them."],[126,414.54,1.68,"Geopolitical issues"],[126,416.22,1.56,"are not going to work --"],[126,417.78,2.6,"we need to work together for peace."],[126,420.98,3.4,"[The] digital economy, which is in trillions of dollars"],[126,424.42,3.2,"when we talk about the value of AI,"],[126,427.66,2.2,"we’re talking, economy-wise,"],[126,429.86,1.64,"about trillions --"],[126,431.54,1.24,"that’s another sign."],[126,432.78,2.16,"Climate change,"],[126,434.98,1.48,"how can we solve for that?"],[126,436.46,3.08,"There needs to be, we've seen it in COP announcements and beyond,"],[126,439.58,2.64,"there needs to be unity."],[126,443.82,1.16,"AI."],[126,445.02,1.56,"I mean,"],[126,446.62,2.0,"if I start to talk about AI, I won't stop."],[126,448.66,1.28,"I mean, how many of us ..."],[126,449.98,1.48,"Let me maybe start with you."],[126,451.46,5.44,"How much do you use ChatGPT, Grok, or you name it, those LLMs?"],[126,456.9,1.16,"Do you use it?"],[126,458.1,1.16,"WPR: Regularly."],[126,459.26,1.44,"HEKB: Do you use it daily?"],[126,460.74,1.16,"WPR: Probably, yeah."],[126,461.94,1.16,"HEKB: I use it daily."],[126,463.14,1.12,"Raise of hands."],[126,464.3,1.16,"Do you use it daily?"],[126,465.5,1.16,"OK."],[126,466.7,2.36,"Do you use it to the extent that you actually feel"],[126,469.06,2.72,"there's a person sitting on the other side talking to you?"],[126,471.78,1.8,"WPR: Not yet, but I know many who do."],[126,473.58,1.68,"HEKB: I'm close to that."],[126,475.3,3.04,"I’m telling you -- from the fact that we interact"],[126,478.34,2.12,"and the emotional angle sometimes you get,"],[126,480.46,1.76,"and the responses --"],[126,482.26,3.96,"we haven’t even scratched the surface on how [to] deal with that."],[126,486.26,3.32,"And you feel so comfortable talking to ChatGPT and many of those LLMs,"],[126,489.62,2.4,"so imagine you actually --"],[126,492.02,1.48,"this gets,"],[126,493.5,3.96,"and I’ve said this, maybe I might sound like a broken record to my colleagues --"],[126,497.5,4.76,"but imagine having this conversation with a robot that looks like a human --"],[126,502.3,2.56,"which we are not far from seeing,"],[126,504.9,2.68,"and I promise you that this is going to come very soon --"],[126,507.62,2.56,"with the same intellect of the ChatGPT,"],[126,510.22,2.6,"[or] even a thousand times much more than that."],[126,513.3,3.96,"What happens to humanity and how do we really interact,"],[126,517.26,2.12,"even from an emotional standpoint?"],[126,519.74,2.88,"We have some weak and vulnerable individuals around the world"],[126,522.62,2.64,"that feel more comfortable with robots than humans."],[126,525.82,2.72,"So what would this mean to us as humanity?"],[126,528.54,2.36,"Are we going to get attached to robots?"],[126,530.94,1.64,"How are we going to regulate that?"],[126,532.58,3.84,"How are we going to really extract value [from] artificial intelligence?"],[126,536.46,4.32,"That’s why I’m saying [that] what we see with ChatGPT now"],[126,540.82,2.56,"is the first second of the first minute"],[126,543.42,2.68,"of the first hour of the first day"],[126,546.14,5.08,"of the first year of this beast that has so much value,"],[126,551.26,3.68,"but that we really need to embrace [it], and we need to embrace [it] jointly."],[126,555.54,2.72,"And there's no chance that Dubai or the UAE"],[126,558.26,1.76,"or the Middle East can do it alone."],[126,560.02,1.84,"Nor the US, nor China --"],[126,561.9,2.76,"it’s a collaborative effort."],[126,564.66,2.28,"Even look at our kids now [and]"],[126,566.98,2.24,"their attachment to technology --"],[126,569.22,1.28,"what is the right balance?"],[126,570.5,2.72,"They’re so smart in front of screens,"],[126,573.54,2.88,"but if you ask them to come on stage and have a conversation,"],[126,576.42,1.56,"they might not be as comfortable"],[126,577.98,2.4,"because they’re just so involved with technology,"],[126,580.38,2.76,"and they do things that I’ll probably never think of doing"],[126,583.18,3.68,"because they’re so into it, and they’re doing such an amazing job."],[126,586.86,3.16,"But life is not only about that -- it’s how you strike the balance."],[126,590.02,2.44,"And how do we really huddle up together as humanity?"],[126,592.46,4.12,"Because technology is super valuable, but humans are more important."],[126,597.3,1.36,"WPR: Well, Your Excellency,"],[126,598.66,2.72,"I cannot let you go without asking you this last question."],[126,601.42,2.32,"You spend so much time thinking about the future."],[126,603.74,2.92,"What do you think is the big thing that many of us are missing"],[126,606.7,1.64,"when we think about what's ahead?"],[126,608.38,1.92,"HEKB: There's one that sticks out to me."],[126,610.34,2.64,"So I'll mention a few and then I'll tell you which one."],[126,613.02,3.56,"So I mean, longevity and living to --"],[126,616.58,1.84,"especially with the ..."],[126,618.42,2.44,"advancement of neuroscience at the moment --"],[126,620.86,2.36,"we can see people living until 150 years,"],[126,623.26,1.4,"and this is not far from now."],[126,624.66,4.24,"I think [this is] a statement that is exciting to many."],[126,628.9,3.44,"I’m not sure if you guys are excited about this, but [for] many people --"],[126,632.34,1.4,"if you’re in good health --"],[126,633.74,1.96,"would want to live to 150."],[126,635.7,1.84,"I think this is not far off,"],[126,637.54,5.28,"given the advancement of genetics and DNA sampling."],[126,642.82,2.6,"And like I said, neuroscience."],[126,645.42,4.8,"I think humans, being a multi-planetary species,"],[126,650.26,1.76,"are not far from that as well."],[126,652.02,2.96,"The access to different planets has become easier and easier"],[126,655.02,1.52,"and cost-effective."],[126,656.54,3.0,"So this is also a bold kind of thought"],[126,659.58,3.84,"that’s probably exciting scientists."],[126,663.42,2.6,"... I think we have enough on Earth."],[126,666.02,2.8,"I don't know if we want to be there, but of course, I mean,"],[126,668.86,4.16,"having that is something that everyone is excited about."],[126,673.06,1.96,"It’s a bold statement but achievable."],[126,675.5,3.56,"But the one that really, really, really makes me think a lot,"],[126,679.06,2.04,"[and] is slightly relevant to the comment"],[126,681.1,1.4,"I mentioned, is:"],[126,682.5,6.2,"once you really infuse human intelligence"],[126,688.7,2.48,"with artificial intelligence"],[126,691.22,2.0,"into one neural network,"],[126,694.58,1.44,"when this happens,"],[126,696.06,3.24,"this is when things are going to be really exciting"],[126,699.3,4.16,"because we're talking about AI being super smart,"],[126,703.5,1.96,"almost human-like."],[126,706.1,2.24,"And this, to the previous point,"],[126,708.34,2.4,"is happening and happening very soon."],[126,710.78,1.88,"Look at how we're dealing with ChatGPT."],[126,712.7,3.36,"But then what if AI was infused in a human,"],[126,716.06,4.72,"having a single kind of network"],[126,720.78,3.48,"... that connects human intelligence"],[126,724.26,1.68,"with artificial intelligence?"],[126,725.94,2.44,"I think, with neuroscience, this is not very far."],[126,728.38,5.44,"And this is something that really requires, again, collaboration,"],[126,733.86,3.88,"understanding and unity, because this is a whole new world."],[126,738.14,3.12,"WPR: Well, Your Excellency, this has been insightful and exciting."],[126,741.26,1.72,"I'm sure I speak for everyone here."],[126,742.98,2.72,"Thank you so much for taking the time to be with us today."],[127,4.66,3.32,"What if the places we lived in could truly reflect who we are"],[127,8.02,3.12,"and not just what's fastest or cheapest to build?"],[127,11.18,2.56,"This isn't about everyone designing their own house."],[127,13.74,3.36,"It's about rethinking how homes are created in the first place."],[127,18.78,1.28,"We've all seen this."],[127,20.1,2.44,"Rows of homes so alike you can only tell them apart"],[127,22.58,2.36,"by the color of the car parked outside."],[127,24.94,1.84,"This is not an AI image."],[127,26.78,2.56,"It’s a real photo of a neighborhood in Dubai"],[127,29.38,1.8,"not far from where I live."],[127,31.22,3.28,"And it's designed for efficiency, not identity."],[127,35.66,4.48,"Truth is, most homes today are designed by developers for the market"],[127,40.18,2.16,"and not for the people that will live in them."],[127,42.38,4.6,"These template-driven systems leave little room to innovate"],[127,47.02,2.24,"or think about form or function."],[127,51.42,3.0,"But this reliance on standardization isn't new."],[127,54.46,4.76,"Historically, modular systems were not designed to maximize profit."],[127,59.26,3.72,"After World War II, when entire cities across Europe were flattened,"],[127,63.02,3.4,"there was a way to get people back under roofs quickly:"],[127,67.5,2.72,"prefabs, kits-of-parts, temporary housing."],[127,70.26,3.12,"Across the world, modularity became a tool for urgent rebuilding."],[127,73.42,2.96,"And it worked fast but often at the cost of character."],[127,79.02,1.96,"Sadly, today, once again,"],[127,81.02,2.44,"architecture and heritage are being erased."],[127,83.5,2.24,"In places like Gaza, in Sudan,"],[127,85.74,1.28,"in Ukraine,"],[127,87.06,3.12,"what disappears isn't just people and buildings,"],[127,90.22,3.68,"it's memory, belonging, culture."],[127,93.9,2.0,"I'm not here with a simple fix."],[127,95.9,2.52,"What I'm saying is we must rebuild,"],[127,98.46,2.36,"but we need to rebuild with identity in mind."],[127,103.34,2.68,"My name is Riyad Joucka. I’m an architect based in Dubai"],[127,106.06,3.48,"where I run a practice called MEAN: the Middle East Architecture Network."],[127,109.58,5.16,"What excites me is finding ways to fuse novel technologies with local stories,"],[127,114.74,3.28,"and sometimes that means experimenting with 3D printing and robotics."],[127,118.06,2.32,"At other times, it's about rereading a tradition"],[127,120.42,2.08,"and giving it a contemporary form."],[127,122.54,1.28,"Across different scales"],[127,123.82,2.24,"from a single object to a cultural building,"],[127,126.1,4.44,"our projects look at how local materials and craftsmanship"],[127,130.58,2.24,"could be given a new life through technology."],[127,134.3,3.76,"We’ve worked in different places across the Middle East, Europe and the US,"],[127,138.1,4.36,"but the constant is this: every project tries to be forward-looking"],[127,142.5,2.04,"while remaining grounded in [its] space."],[127,145.5,2.52,"And that's what excites me the most about housing."],[127,148.06,4.04,"It's where technology and belonging meet most directly."],[127,152.14,3.76,"Because our homes aren't just shelters, they should be mirrors of who we are."],[127,156.94,3.44,"And to further introduce myself, this is the house that I grew up in,"],[127,160.42,1.64,"designed by my father."],[127,162.1,2.32,"You see, both of my parents are architects,"],[127,164.42,2.4,"[and] both of my grandparents were notable artists,"],[127,166.82,2.6,"so I grew up in a household surrounded by conversations"],[127,169.42,2.92,"about art, culture and space."],[127,172.34,2.52,"And that's how I see design today."],[127,174.86,6.0,"Not as a template, but as something truly rooted in family, stories and place."],[127,182.66,2.64,"The house truly reflects the traditions of the region,"],[127,185.34,3.68,"as you can see in this scan from Al-Bina magazine, [which in] 1994"],[127,189.06,1.4,"wrote about the house."],[127,190.5,2.32,"I pulled these from my father's archives."],[127,192.82,2.64,"You can see natural light being drawn through skylights,"],[127,195.5,2.32,"natural stone facade covering the exterior --"],[127,197.82,1.96,"very typical of houses in the region."],[127,199.78,3.28,"A majlis cast in concrete in the walls themselves."],[127,203.1,2.92,"My father describes it as post-traditional architecture"],[127,206.06,3.2,"because postmodernism never really fit our context."],[127,209.3,3.44,"One of the few things he and I agree on when it comes to work."],[127,212.74,3.92,"No, but the house really is a reflection of our traditions,"],[127,216.66,1.36,"but in a contemporary form."],[127,218.94,3.08,"And I keep returning to this traditional wisdom."],[127,222.06,2.6,"The majlis for gathering, diwans for hosting,"],[127,224.66,2.72,"spaces that extend beyond their functionality"],[127,227.42,1.36,"to bring people together."],[127,229.66,1.8,"Courtyards that cool in shade."],[127,231.5,2.32,"Spaces that make the outdoors livable."],[127,234.82,3.16,"Barjeels or windcatchers that breathe air through a home."],[127,238.02,3.56,"Passive design centuries before we use the word \"sustainability.\""],[127,243.38,3.0,"Screens that filter light and create privacy:"],[127,246.42,4.96,"mashrabiyas -- design that shapes atmosphere as much as structure."],[127,251.42,2.96,"And we use a similar vocabulary for our house at MEAN."],[127,254.42,3.0,"For House 00, the first house we designed,"],[127,257.46,5.64,"we designed the house around a courtyard with a water body cooling the exterior."],[127,263.14,3.08,"The house is wrapped with this corrugated stone facade,"],[127,266.26,3.88,"echoing the language of the topography of the mountain surrounding it."],[127,270.18,2.12,"The house sits atop Jebel Jais,"],[127,272.34,4.64,"and to me, it’s a real interpretation of traditional forms"],[127,277.02,1.72,"but in a contemporary home."],[127,278.74,5.0,"Cosmos House, we collaborated with artist Julia Ibbini to design these mashrabiyas."],[127,283.74,4.88,"We used algorithms and fractal geometry to filter light into speckled shadows."],[127,288.62,1.92,"The house is designed to be 3D-printed"],[127,290.58,2.52,"using sand from the surrounding environment."],[127,294.1,6.0,"The ancient wisdom of desert architecture reinterpreted using digital tools."],[127,300.14,4.04,"Beyond practice, I also lead the Adaptive Majlis research project"],[127,304.22,1.88,"at Zayed University."],[127,306.14,3.88,"We’re looking at ... how 3D-printed modular systems"],[127,310.06,3.12,"could help us reinterpret these traditions today."],[127,313.22,1.24,"The Majlis,"],[127,314.5,1.64,"a unique typology of the region,"],[127,316.18,3.24,"is being rethought through the lens of modern technology,"],[127,319.46,3.04,"local craftsmanship and contemporary lifestyles."],[127,323.34,3.68,"That same thinking helped shape permutable assemblies,"],[127,327.06,3.08,"a modular system we developed at MEAN for housing."],[127,330.18,1.6,"We asked ourselves:"],[127,331.78,3.24,"we need to produce a modular system with a certain goal,"],[127,335.06,4.16,"and that goal is to design for a modular system that is personal,"],[127,339.26,1.68,"local and expressive."],[127,340.94,2.96,"And we asked ourselves: instead of erasing character,"],[127,343.9,3.88,"what if modular systems helped showcase it and express it,"],[127,347.78,5.52,"responding to contexts, climate and culture without any added cost?"],[127,353.34,4.16,"Djenné Great Mosque in Mali is the world's largest adobe building."],[127,357.54,4.52,"But what's remarkable isn't just the architecture, it's the people."],[127,362.1,4.04,"Every year, the community gathers to repair its walls and replaster them,"],[127,366.18,2.2,"turning maintenance into a festival."],[127,369.5,3.08,"The building survives because of the community that maintains it."],[127,372.58,2.2,"And that sense of shared responsibility"],[127,374.78,2.56,"is something that we've lost in many modern cities."],[127,378.86,1.64,"Here's how our system works."],[127,380.54,1.28,"A builder, developer"],[127,381.82,2.52,"or simply any user enters parameters"],[127,384.38,3.48,"around their spatial needs, context, climate,"],[127,387.86,1.84,"into a digital platform."],[127,389.7,4.64,"It then generates layouts composed of modules shaped by that context."],[127,394.38,2.84,"These parts are then prefabricated off-site,"],[127,397.26,5.4,"using local low-carbon materials produced with precision and little waste."],[127,402.66,2.24,"The parts are assembled piece by piece,"],[127,404.9,3.64,"almost like a modern kit-of-parts or a set of LEGOs, if you may,"],[127,408.58,1.68,"into a home."],[127,410.3,3.24,"The process is fast and efficient, but unlike traditional prefab,"],[127,413.58,1.68,"it does not force sameness."],[127,416.42,1.84,"See, traditional modular systems"],[127,418.3,3.08,"rely on repeated templates."],[127,421.42,3.12,"With 3D printing, we can break that mold,"],[127,424.58,3.04,"designing for efficiency as well as belonging."],[127,427.62,3.72,"The system scales up from house to neighborhood,"],[127,431.38,3.84,"where every home shares a logic, but no two are alike."],[127,435.26,2.92,"It's modularity with character,"],[127,438.22,2.12,"turning repetition into rhythm."],[127,441.3,5.92,"3D printing makes curves, textures and ornaments practical at scale."],[127,447.26,2.56,"Screens inspired by local patterns."],[127,449.82,2.36,"Walls that read like the desert."],[127,452.22,2.28,"Details prefab usually cuts out."],[127,455.7,3.28,"You see, modularity doesn't have to mean sameness."],[127,459.02,3.64,"It can mean variation, intention and agency."],[127,462.66,2.44,"Design shouldn't come from a catalog."],[127,465.14,4.0,"It should grow with you, respond to you and reflect you."],[127,469.18,2.52,"And that's what architecture should be."],[127,471.7,4.32,"Not imposed, not distant, but deeply rooted."],[127,476.06,2.48,"We shouldn't keep repeating the same formulas."],[127,478.58,1.68,"We can build differently."],[127,480.3,4.84,"We can invite people and communities to shape the spaces we call home,"],[127,485.18,3.8,"because design, real design, belongs to everyone."],[127,489.02,1.24,"Thank you."],[128,4.67,1.77,"Idiots --"],[128,8.84,1.33,"Are everywhere."],[128,12.24,4.37,"Some people drive you right up the wall with their opinions, their attitudes,"],[128,16.61,1.33,"their politics."],[128,17.98,2.8,"I dealt with all kinds of people in my 20-plus years"],[128,20.79,1.77,"as a broadcast journalist."],[128,22.59,2.57,"Eventually, I found moderating tough conversations"],[128,25.19,3.24,"with whatever kind of difficult people you just thought of."],[128,28.46,1.83,"Kind of simple to do --"],[128,30.29,1.77,"not easy -- but simple."],[128,32.5,3.5,"The trick is to go somewhere I know you'd rather not be."],[128,36.67,1.4,"Closer."],[128,38.1,2.5,"Distance makes things worse."],[128,41.27,1.27,"I came up in public radio,"],[128,42.57,4.07,"trained by old-school journalists who taught me the art of neutrality."],[128,46.68,2.5,"You neutralize your opinions, deaden your emotions,"],[128,49.21,3.6,"and you can get through most conversations without rolling your eyes."],[128,52.85,3.54,"Generally, it worked, and it built me a strong reputation for fairness."],[128,56.42,5.47,"But when the subject matter felt personal, neutrality felt phony."],[128,62.43,3.27,"I used to host a national talk show on NPR called \"1A,\""],[128,65.7,1.6,"and in one of our early programs,"],[128,67.33,3.34,"I interviewed a descendant of Confederate soldiers."],[128,70.67,3.97,"He argued that Confederate monuments should be historically preserved."],[128,74.64,3.44,"Now as a Black man, I felt enormous pressure to remain neutral,"],[128,78.11,3.97,"so I decided I was just going to focus on his arguments instead of on him."],[128,82.11,2.94,"His logic felt kind of flimsy, and I called him on it,"],[128,85.08,2.64,"and eventually he just folded and he said,"],[128,87.75,2.34,"“It really doesn’t matter what I say.”"],[128,90.12,4.04,"And the moment he said that, I knew I had failed."],[128,94.92,1.2,"I may have had a point,"],[128,96.16,2.84,"but I missed a huge opportunity to really do my job."],[128,99.36,3.47,"See, my neutrality allowed me to interrogate his beliefs,"],[128,102.87,5.47,"but the distance prevented me from seeing why he believed them."],[128,109.14,2.97,"What if I had asked better questions like:"],[128,112.14,3.6,"\"Who influenced you the most in forming these views?\""],[128,116.28,5.3,"Or, \"Suppose we let all of these monuments and statues stand just as they are."],[128,121.62,1.83,"What would that mean to you?\""],[128,123.49,2.24,"Getting his story might have shown me him"],[128,125.72,2.04,"behind his canned arguments."],[128,127.79,2.7,"Grilling him changed nothing."],[128,130.53,1.6,"It felt pointless."],[128,132.5,1.83,"When you consider someone problematic,"],[128,134.33,1.94,"there's a better way to deal with them,"],[128,136.3,3.2,"keep your sanity and maybe even connect."],[128,139.54,1.7,"And it sounds insane."],[128,141.97,2.2,"Move closer."],[128,144.18,2.67,"Not to embrace them, but to explore them,"],[128,146.84,2.2,"like a doctor studying a sample."],[128,149.08,2.4,"You need a microscope, not a telescope."],[128,151.95,3.6,"Doctors actually helped me learn this way before I became a journalist."],[128,155.59,4.24,"As a teenager, I was part of a summer program for future doctors."],[128,159.86,1.53,"Clearly it worked."],[128,161.39,3.8,"And I found myself working with clinicians studying cancer"],[128,165.23,1.8,"and thrombosis and HIV,"],[128,167.06,1.37,"very serious conditions."],[128,168.77,3.47,"I kind of naively expected them to be these crusading scientists,"],[128,172.27,2.67,"but they were just level-headed people doing their jobs."],[128,175.37,2.64,"But there was a lesson in that level-headedness."],[128,178.04,3.77,"See, I am not neutral about the cancers in our world:"],[128,181.84,3.07,"bigotry, hoaxes, political violence."],[128,184.95,1.4,"I want them gone."],[128,186.38,1.84,"However, as a journalist,"],[128,188.25,2.64,"I often had to deal with them in my clinic:"],[128,190.92,1.27,"“on-the-air.”"],[128,192.22,3.67,"I survived it by getting as close to those problems as I could"],[128,195.93,2.94,"and studying them like samples in a petri dish."],[128,198.9,1.27,"In other words,"],[128,200.2,3.3,"I had to shift from neutrality"],[128,203.5,2.04,"to objectivity."],[128,205.57,2.0,"And no, they're not the same."],[128,208.27,4.4,"Objectivity is just when you park your emotions and learn without judgment."],[128,212.71,1.27,"I don't have to like it,"],[128,214.01,1.77,"I'm just here to learn from it."],[128,215.78,4.0,"Neutrality negates your opinions as if you have no judgments."],[128,219.82,3.7,"Does anyone here honestly believe I have no opinions on race?"],[128,224.72,1.13,"Right?"],[128,225.89,2.67,"Now notice I said park your opinions."],[128,228.56,1.83,"When you park your car,"],[128,230.39,1.74,"you don't abandon it in the garage."],[128,232.13,1.37,"You're coming back for it."],[128,233.53,3.3,"So you can choose when to focus inward on what you think,"],[128,236.87,1.77,"or outward on what you can learn."],[128,239.2,3.37,"Of course, sometimes your car ain't where you left it,"],[128,242.61,2.57,"and maybe your opinions will move too."],[128,245.18,2.94,"That maybe is why objectivity feels so vulnerable."],[128,248.61,1.8,"That's why it works."],[128,250.45,2.84,"That vulnerability is a sign of openness,"],[128,253.28,3.87,"and a conversation that's not open is pointless."],[128,257.15,4.37,"But if you can stay open, amazing things can happen."],[128,261.56,4.27,"Another time on NPR, I was interviewing a former Baltimore police officer"],[128,265.86,3.44,"who had become this fiery advocate for law enforcement reform."],[128,269.33,1.87,"And he was going on and on,"],[128,271.2,2.04,"just a long rant in autopilot,"],[128,273.24,2.23,"just (vocalizing)."],[128,275.47,3.07,"And I was trying to stay objective and listening as best I could,"],[128,278.57,3.97,"when I thought I heard his voice waver just a little."],[128,282.98,1.37,"And in that waver,"],[128,284.38,2.2,"I detected more than his rage."],[128,287.08,1.6,"It sounded like trauma."],[128,289.59,1.6,"So near the end of the interview,"],[128,291.19,1.47,"I mentioned, hesitantly,"],[128,292.69,4.37,"that he seemed traumatized by his experiences as a cop,"],[128,297.09,1.6,"and could he reflect on that."],[128,299.63,2.13,"He said,"],[128,301.8,3.84,"\"I put people in prison cells who are better people than me.\""],[128,307.54,1.2,"Whoa!"],[128,309.07,2.57,"Sure enough, he went right back to his talking points,"],[128,311.64,2.2,"but it didn’t matter, because in that moment --"],[128,313.84,1.33,"that sliver of his story --"],[128,315.18,4.4,"his defenses dropped, and he shifted from being right to being real."],[128,320.78,2.64,"Look, some people are just difficult,"],[128,323.45,2.13,"but we can deal with them more simply."],[128,325.62,1.9,"Here are three tips to get you started."],[128,327.52,2.3,"First, get the story,"],[128,329.86,3.94,"particularly the key decisions and the turning points."],[128,333.83,4.64,"What someone believes is not as important as how they chose to believe it."],[128,338.84,2.47,"Second, my NPR host pro tip:"],[128,341.34,2.24,"keep your questions short."],[128,343.61,2.1,"10 seconds tops."],[128,345.74,3.9,"Long questions can feel like a setup or like you're rambling."],[128,349.65,2.77,"And third, use that mental microscope."],[128,352.45,1.83,"Look as closely as you can."],[128,354.32,3.57,"Remember, you don't have to like it, but learn from it."],[128,358.76,2.47,"It is time to reconnect,"],[128,361.23,1.8,"even to the people you can't stand."],[128,363.06,3.17,"But this time, we're going to make those connections count."],[128,367.0,1.9,"You could learn a lot from an idiot."],[128,369.93,3.97,"Especially if you resolve never to act like one."],[128,374.94,2.84,"I really appreciate you all making time for me today."],[128,377.77,1.23,"Thank you for listening."],[129,6.5,5.92,"In 1856, quarriers working a cave in Germany’s Neander Valley"],[129,12.55,2.71,"discovered several mysterious fossils."],[129,15.68,4.09,"The remains changed hands until being identified"],[129,19.89,3.04,"as the skullcap and femur bones"],[129,22.98,4.55,"of something ancient and human, but not quite us."],[129,28.44,4.84,"It soon became clear they belonged to an extinct human species—"],[129,33.36,3.0,"the first ever known to science:"],[129,36.41,5.17,"Homo neanderthalensis, or simply, Neanderthals."],[129,41.79,2.13,"Not long before the discovery,"],[129,43.96,4.42,"many believed the world was only about 6,000 years old."],[129,48.63,2.71,"However, by the mid-1800s,"],[129,51.34,4.0,"naturalists were more comfortable with geological timelines"],[129,55.34,2.04,"and evolutionary theory."],[129,57.72,5.25,"The idea that ancient remains could belong to another human species"],[129,63.02,2.79,"was finally becoming conceivable."],[129,66.02,6.46,"But misconceptions persisted, and much “Neander slander” ensued."],[129,72.9,4.5,"Early on, Neanderthals were depicted as dim-witted oafs"],[129,77.49,4.59,"who died out because of their inferiority to modern humans,"],[129,82.2,2.5,"aka Homo sapiens."],[129,85.0,2.71,"One popular illustration suggested that,"],[129,87.71,3.42,"in addition to being hairy and club-carrying,"],[129,91.21,5.63,"Neanderthals stooped and had ape-like opposable toes,"],[129,96.97,4.25,"even though this wasn’t reflected in skeletal findings."],[129,101.43,2.0,"Over the next century, however,"],[129,103.43,4.17,"archaeologists discovered more hominin species,"],[129,107.73,5.34,"as well as the remains of over 300 Neanderthal individuals."],[129,113.4,5.71,"Compared to modern humans, Neanderthals had larger, deeper-set eyes;"],[129,119.28,2.25,"were shorter and more muscular,"],[129,121.83,4.3,"had higher caloric needs with slightly bigger bodies,"],[129,126.16,2.63,"including their hearts and brains."],[129,129.17,6.67,"For over 350,000 years, they lived across Europe and Western Asia."],[129,136.3,3.09,"For at least their final 10,000 years,"],[129,139.43,3.38,"they spatially overlapped with Homo sapiens."],[129,143.06,5.96,"And archaeological finds have suggested that they led much more complex lives"],[129,149.06,2.04,"than initially suspected."],[129,151.73,5.25,"In the 1950s, scientists unearthed the remains of a Neanderthal"],[129,156.99,5.59,"in a cave in northern Iraq who had a severed right arm, injured leg,"],[129,162.66,3.29,"and was likely partially blind and deaf."],[129,166.12,3.54,"Yet, he survived long after these injuries,"],[129,169.66,4.96,"suggesting his community provided him extensive, daily support."],[129,174.92,5.75,"And another skeleton from a French cave belonged to an older Neanderthal"],[129,180.76,2.13,"who probably had trouble walking"],[129,182.89,4.75,"and had lost so many teeth he may have needed his food ground up."],[129,187.85,4.3,"Both excavation sites also showed signs of burial,"],[129,192.56,3.71,"all suggesting that Neanderthals went beyond"],[129,196.28,3.34,"immediate, practical concerns for survival"],[129,199.61,3.17,"and cared for their ailing and dead."],[129,203.16,3.84,"Teeth analyses also indicate that Neanderthals"],[129,206.99,5.38,"might have used anti-inflammatory and antibiotic plants medicinally."],[129,212.75,5.0,"For a while, researchers assumed that Neanderthals hunted big game"],[129,217.75,4.42,"with unsophisticated, brute force ambush attacks."],[129,222.72,2.5,"But cut marks on rabbit bones"],[129,225.22,3.79,"and tools with apparent traces of scales and feathers"],[129,229.02,6.46,"suggest Neanderthals were capable of the skill required to capture small game,"],[129,235.65,4.42,"perhaps utilizing snares and fast-moving projectiles."],[129,240.65,5.13,"In fact, we know they fashioned and wielded many types of tools,"],[129,246.03,4.75,"sometimes affixing them with glue they made from heated birch bark."],[129,251.16,3.8,"They also shaped hardwood digging sticks with fire"],[129,254.96,5.63,"and created reinforced, 3-ply string using bark fibers."],[129,261.05,2.79,"Other findings have also raised questions"],[129,263.84,3.46,"about whether Neanderthals thought symbolically,"],[129,267.43,5.25,"seeing beyond direct utility, into realms like art and language,"],[129,272.85,3.54,"long considered a hallmark of Homo sapiens."],[129,276.86,4.46,"Potentially for personal adornment, there’s evidence of Neanderthals"],[129,281.32,3.17,"selectively removing bird’s flight feathers,"],[129,284.61,2.67,"painting and perforating shells,"],[129,287.28,6.17,"and wrapping eagle talons in what seems to have been another kind of animal tissue."],[129,293.71,5.09,"They likewise made markings on a giant elk’s toe bone"],[129,298.79,3.29,"and created three cave paintings in Spain,"],[129,302.38,5.25,"if the original 65,000-year-old dating estimate holds."],[129,308.22,5.09,"Meanwhile, broken, rearranged, and burned stalagmites"],[129,313.31,1.88,"deep within a French cave"],[129,315.19,3.59,"left scientists wondering if the mysterious structures"],[129,318.77,5.05,"held spiritual or ceremonial significance to Neanderthals."],[129,324.4,4.46,"Clearly, they weren’t as different as originally believed."],[129,329.16,5.76,"And following the first fully sequenced Neanderthal genome in 2010,"],[129,334.95,3.71,"researchers realized our species interbred."],[129,338.96,5.26,"All modern humans retain some Neanderthal DNA—"],[129,344.34,2.08,"up to about 4%—"],[129,346.43,4.59,"the result of hundreds of discrete interbreeding events."],[129,351.68,3.38,"So, what happened to Neanderthals?"],[129,355.31,5.67,"Theories from rampant cannibalism, to disease and climate change,"],[129,361.15,3.38,"to aggressive Homo sapiens have been floated."],[129,364.78,2.0,"But another idea is that,"],[129,366.78,3.0,"because Neanderthals lived in smaller groups,"],[129,369.78,4.09,"as they interbred with larger populations of Homo sapiens,"],[129,373.99,3.88,"they just sort of got gradually absorbed."],[129,378.21,3.88,"Overall, consensus has begun shifting away"],[129,382.09,3.63,"from representing human evolution as tree-like"],[129,385.8,3.38,"and more towards a kind of braided stream,"],[129,389.47,6.63,"with different hominin groups separating and rejoining at various junctures."],[129,396.43,4.17,"As we continue to learn more about our evolutionary cousins,"],[129,400.81,5.63,"each discovery raises questions about just how much we share."],[130,7.79,5.38,"In 1834, the British Royal Navy ship, the Chanticleer,"],[130,13.18,4.04,"was dealing with a terrible cockroach infestation."],[130,17.68,5.59,"The ship's surgeon, however, wrote enthusiastically about the stowaways,"],[130,23.27,4.13,"describing them as a “most valuable insect.”"],[130,27.44,1.83,"Their shining characteristic?"],[130,29.52,3.17,"Devouring every bedbug on board."],[130,33.53,3.59,"Bedbugs have tormented humans for millennia,"],[130,37.16,6.59,"so detested that their mere mention can make hearts race and arms itch."],[130,43.91,5.5,"So why are these unwelcomed houseguests so difficult to defeat?"],[130,50.05,4.17,"There are approximately 100 species of bedbugs."],[130,54.47,6.17,"Thankfully, only three feed on humans, all of which are chestnut brown,"],[130,61.02,3.71,"half a centimeter long, and incredibly flat—"],[130,64.85,2.46,"as long as they haven’t eaten recently."],[130,67.61,2.42,"To find their next blood meal,"],[130,70.19,4.59,"bedbugs follow the biological signals our bodies release,"],[130,75.03,3.88,"such as carbon dioxide, odor, and body heat."],[130,79.37,4.21,"Once located, they use their straw-like mouthparts"],[130,83.58,6.92,"to inject a cocktail of proteins that dilate blood vessels for easier feeding,"],[130,90.71,5.26,"along with an anesthetic to block any pain that might give them away."],[130,96.55,6.63,"These compounds and other foreign proteins are what can trigger our immune response."],[130,103.64,3.67,"While some people’s bodies don’t react to these bites,"],[130,107.48,6.05,"others develop itchy, red lesions that can stick around for several weeks."],[130,113.73,3.67,"And these bites can appear anywhere on the body—"],[130,117.66,4.63,"it’s a myth that they always appear in neat rows or sets of three."],[130,122.78,4.8,"While they're not considered social insects like bees or ants,"],[130,127.75,3.34,"bedbugs cooperate in fascinating ways."],[130,131.25,4.17,"They huddle together in piles, called refugia,"],[130,135.42,4.67,"stacking their flat bodies within wall cracks or furniture,"],[130,140.26,5.05,"which is thought to help them preserve precious moisture and energy reserves."],[130,145.6,3.75,"They release chemical signals, known as pheromones,"],[130,149.35,1.92,"to alert each other to threats."],[130,151.44,4.34,"And their feces are rich in volatile compounds"],[130,155.86,6.13,"that help guide feeding bedbugs back to the safety of the refugia in the dark."],[130,162.53,4.59,"Once a colony is established, it has tremendous staying power."],[130,167.5,4.0,"Bedbugs can go weeks or months without a meal."],[130,172.34,3.96,"If they find themselves in a particularly cool environment,"],[130,176.3,5.63,"they can enter a dormant state and survive over a year without feeding."],[130,182.51,2.75,"While bedbugs can easily move around,"],[130,185.31,2.79,"they usually stay within 20 feet"],[130,188.14,4.34,"of where their human host is regularly sitting or sleeping."],[130,193.1,5.13,"So new infestations are most often the result of humans"],[130,198.24,6.3,"accidentally transporting bugs via furniture, clothing, or other items."],[130,204.91,1.79,"Throughout human history,"],[130,206.74,5.34,"we've tried almost everything to prevent these itchy invasions."],[130,212.21,4.42,"In Eastern Europe, for example, people used bean leaves,"],[130,216.63,4.5,"which can trap bedbugs in their tiny, hooked hairs."],[130,221.22,5.21,"Another common trick was running lit candles along bed frames"],[130,226.43,2.63,"to burn any hiding insects."],[130,229.47,3.21,"While these methods were occasionally effective,"],[130,232.77,2.5,"bedbugs continued to thrive."],[130,235.56,2.17,"And in the early 1900s,"],[130,237.78,6.17,"as central heating made homes more livable to humans and bugs year-round,"],[130,244.12,3.42,"populations hit unprecedented heights."],[130,247.83,3.46,"But this bedbug renaissance was short lived."],[130,251.66,4.59,"In the 1940s, the pest faced an existential threat"],[130,256.25,5.96,"in the powerful insecticide called dichloro-diphenyl-trichloroethane."],[130,262.97,6.09,"Also known as DDT, this neurotoxin disrupts insects’ nervous systems,"],[130,269.06,2.17,"causing spasms and death."],[130,271.81,6.38,"For decades, DDT was used across the globe to control agricultural pests"],[130,278.36,3.25,"and combat insect-borne diseases."],[130,281.74,2.38,"However, in the 1970s,"],[130,284.28,5.88,"scientists realized DDT had accumulated to dangerous levels in the environment,"],[130,290.37,2.71,"potentially putting human health at risk."],[130,293.33,2.79,"Many countries began banning DDT,"],[130,296.21,5.34,"but not before it had rid the world almost entirely of bedbugs."],[130,301.8,1.04,"Almost."],[130,303.26,6.13,"After decades of quiet comfort, bedbugs reemerged in the early 2000s,"],[130,309.47,3.67,"shepherded by fast-moving international trade."],[130,313.77,4.34,"Many of these insects have since become pesticide resistant,"],[130,318.27,3.21,"developing traits like thicker exoskeletons,"],[130,321.48,5.59,"and the enhanced ability to break down insecticide toxins in their bodies."],[130,327.95,2.5,"Thankfully, we still have options."],[130,330.58,2.25,"People can utilize high heat,"],[130,332.83,6.76,"as most bedbugs and their eggs can’t survive temperatures above 45° Celsius."],[130,339.75,2.08,"And despite their adaptations,"],[130,342.0,4.71,"bedbugs remain vulnerable to the suction of a vacuum cleaner."],[130,347.72,3.54,"While simple, these tools warrant some gratitude."],[130,351.85,3.5,"After all, unlike naval ships of the past,"],[130,355.39,5.38,"we no longer rely on the services of our other age-old nemesis,"],[130,360.98,1.29,"the cockroach."],[131,6.5,3.88,"In 1883, American socialite Josephine Cochrane"],[131,10.46,2.96,"was suddenly widowed and left with mounting debts."],[131,13.88,3.79,"But she had an idea she hoped would turn her fortunes around."],[131,18.01,2.33,"Years of hosting lavish dinner parties"],[131,20.35,2.75,"and watching her dishware get chipped during handwashing"],[131,23.23,3.71,"convinced her the world needed a better way to clean dishes."],[131,27.4,4.0,"So, she poured the little money she had left into building a prototype,"],[131,31.61,4.63,"eventually showcasing her dishwashing contraption at the Chicago World's Fair."],[131,37.08,2.67,"This was the first step towards the modern dishwasher,"],[131,39.95,3.59,"whose basic design remains remarkably similar to Cochrane's."],[131,44.5,1.79,"When you press start on a dishwasher,"],[131,46.29,4.46,"it fills a small basin with water that's heated up to 60°C."],[131,51.21,2.29,"Then the pre-wash cycle begins."],[131,53.63,4.09,"A circulation pump sends high-pressure water up into spray arms,"],[131,57.84,4.05,"where it shoots out of angled nozzles, propelling the arms to spin."],[131,62.23,3.63,"The water recollects in the basin, where, in most modern models,"],[131,65.85,2.88,"a turbidity sensor measures the water’s dirtiness"],[131,68.77,2.59,"by seeing how much light passes through it."],[131,71.9,3.67,"The dishwasher then adjusts the wash time and water temperature accordingly"],[131,75.66,1.92,"and transitions to the wash cycle."],[131,78.07,4.59,"Detergent is released and the dishwasher cycles the now-detergent-filled water."],[131,83.16,5.67,"This dirty water drains and clean water, heated to an even hotter 68°C,"],[131,88.88,3.42,"is circulated for a final sanitizing rinse."],[131,92.63,4.25,"This is at least 10 degrees warmer than a typical sink’s hottest water,"],[131,97.09,3.25,"and hot enough to eliminate most foodborne bacteria."],[131,100.72,3.5,"Finally, the dishwasher may enter an optional drying cycle."],[131,105.35,2.25,"While that's how most dishwashers work,"],[131,107.69,3.59,"how well they actually clean depends on several factors—"],[131,111.4,2.63,"the first being the type of detergent you use."],[131,114.53,4.25,"Detergent comes in three forms: liquid, powder, and pods."],[131,119.24,3.17,"The main ingredient in all three is the surfactant."],[131,122.66,4.71,"These special dual-ended molecules remove food residues by latching onto them"],[131,127.37,3.34,"with one end and pulling towards the water with the other."],[131,131.29,4.59,"You'll also find surfactants in regular dish soap, but detergents go further."],[131,136.26,3.92,"They often contain enzymes that break down food into smaller pieces"],[131,140.18,2.17,"that surfactants can easily tug away,"],[131,142.43,3.54,"and bleach, which disinfects and removes stains."],[131,147.35,2.09,"So, which detergent form is best?"],[131,149.81,3.54,"Liquid detergent can only contain bleach or enzymes,"],[131,153.52,2.75,"since bleach breaks down enzymes in liquid form."],[131,156.61,3.96,"Powder detergents solve this problem by coating powdered enzymes"],[131,160.57,1.71,"with a protective wax,"],[131,162.28,2.88,"allowing them to work alongside powdered bleach."],[131,165.58,3.75,"But even more effective are dishwasher pods that separate components"],[131,169.33,1.75,"using a plastic film."],[131,171.38,3.04,"Though, pods may have environmental impacts—"],[131,174.54,3.59,"some studies claim the plastic biodegrades in a couple of months,"],[131,178.3,2.75,"while others find it persists for much longer."],[131,182.26,2.88,"How you treat your dishwasher, and the dishes you put in,"],[131,185.14,1.75,"can also affect performance."],[131,187.31,3.25,"A dirty dishwasher or filter can trick the turbidity sensor"],[131,190.56,2.42,"into thinking dishes are dirtier than they are,"],[131,193.1,2.71,"prompting an unnecessarily long wash cycle."],[131,196.48,2.21,"And while it might seem counterintuitive,"],[131,198.69,3.21,"rinsing dishes beforehand can also cause problems."],[131,202.41,1.54,"If dishes are well rinsed,"],[131,203.95,4.67,"the turbidity sensor will detect minimal grime and trigger a short wash cycle,"],[131,208.66,4.09,"which may not be enough to break down any stubborn stuck-on crust."],[131,213.12,4.34,"Further, the enzymes in detergent are designed to latch onto food residue,"],[131,217.55,4.3,"so the best practice is to simply scrape your dishes before loading."],[131,222.8,3.04,"What’s more, by letting the dishwasher do all the work,"],[131,225.85,1.54,"you'll also save water."],[131,227.72,2.75,"Dishwashers recycle water during the wash cycle,"],[131,230.48,2.54,"so they only use 3 to 5 gallons."],[131,233.31,3.88,"That’s equivalent to running a kitchen sink for up to 2.5 minutes."],[131,237.48,3.25,"So if you think handwashing your dishes might take longer than that,"],[131,240.82,2.38,"it might be worth just running a load."],[131,244.16,2.84,"Of course, there are reasons to not throw in the towel"],[131,246.99,1.67,"on handwashing altogether."],[131,248.95,2.29,"Since dishwashers use very hot water,"],[131,251.46,2.71,"they can warp wooden items and certain plastics,"],[131,254.33,3.13,"damaging seals in travel mugs or storage containers."],[131,258.09,2.84,"Sharp knives can also be dulled by detergents,"],[131,260.92,2.63,"or they can even damage the dishwasher itself."],[131,264.09,2.13,"And there's also an energy consideration."],[131,266.39,4.67,"On average, a standard dishwasher cycle uses about the same amount of energy"],[131,271.06,2.29,"as leaving your TV on for 12 hours."],[131,274.6,3.88,"Clearly, there are situations when handwashing makes more sense."],[131,278.73,2.71,"But if you’re faced with a dinner party’s worth of dishes,"],[131,281.61,2.67,"a dishwasher can be a welcome reprieve."],[132,7.29,2.54,"Our bodies are fluid factories,"],[132,9.96,3.96,"constantly producing, purging, and recycling secretions"],[132,13.93,2.59,"to fulfill all sorts of functions."],[132,16.8,2.75,"Our reproductive organs are no exception."],[132,19.97,4.17,"Vaginas are engaged in ongoing cycles of fluid discharge."],[132,24.23,3.84,"But it can be hard to know what kind of discharge is “normal”"],[132,28.07,2.54,"when there’s taboo in talking about it—"],[132,30.73,2.25,"and absorbency tests for menstrual products"],[132,32.99,2.96,"tend to show anonymous blue fluids."],[132,36.2,2.63,"So, what’s healthy discharge?"],[132,38.91,2.5,"And when is there cause for concern?"],[132,41.95,5.09,"Anyone who has a uterus might not experience much discharge early in life."],[132,47.29,5.05,"But that changes with puberty, as the ovaries start producing estrogen."],[132,52.67,4.0,"This causes the vagina’s inner lining— or epithelium—"],[132,56.68,2.46,"to thicken and cycle faster."],[132,59.6,4.67,"These vaginal epithelial cells, in turn, start producing more glycogen,"],[132,64.48,4.34,"which fuels the growth of lactic-acid- and-hydrogen-peroxide-producing"],[132,68.81,2.13,"Lactobacillus bacteria,"],[132,71.11,2.5,"which make the vagina more acidic."],[132,74.24,5.21,"Specialized goblet cells in the cervix also begin releasing more mucus,"],[132,79.49,5.09,"which is enriched with immunity-boosting proteins called immunoglobulins."],[132,84.96,3.96,"The acidic environment, regenerating vaginal epithelium,"],[132,88.92,4.75,"and cervical mucus all help protect against infections."],[132,93.97,3.92,"Vaginal discharge mainly consists of cervical mucus,"],[132,97.89,3.17,"sloughed epithelial cells, and bacteria,"],[132,101.06,3.17,"which all exit the vagina with the help of gravity."],[132,104.64,1.38,"Because of its acidity,"],[132,106.14,3.75,"discharge can sometimes bleach or leave holes in fabric."],[132,110.36,3.92,"But, of course, discharge varies considerably"],[132,114.28,1.83,"with the monthly menstrual cycle,"],[132,116.28,4.8,"in sync with shifting levels of hormones, like estrogen and progesterone."],[132,121.45,2.42,"During the first half of the menstrual cycle,"],[132,123.87,1.96,"known as the follicular phase,"],[132,125.91,6.01,"estrogen levels gradually increase, as a new egg, or follicle, develops."],[132,132.59,3.96,"The uterus’s inner lining, the endometrium, thickens,"],[132,136.63,4.38,"preparing for an embryo to embed should an egg get fertilized."],[132,141.85,4.42,"Early in the follicular phase, cervical goblet cells produce mucus"],[132,146.27,3.79,"that initially may be whitish, thick, or sticky."],[132,150.4,1.92,"In the days prior to ovulation,"],[132,152.31,5.38,"as estrogen levels further increase and the ovary prepares to release an egg,"],[132,157.94,4.59,"these cervical goblet cells secrete a thinner, clearer mucus,"],[132,162.53,2.38,"which is easier for sperm to travel through."],[132,165.41,4.8,"And in the days following ovulation, progesterone levels increase,"],[132,170.42,4.38,"stabilizing the endometrium in preparation for pregnancy."],[132,175.09,4.09,"The cervix produces thicker, cloudier, and sometimes yellowy-colored mucus"],[132,179.22,5.21,"that now helps form a physical barrier against sperm and pathogens."],[132,184.6,5.0,"If no embryo implants, estrogen and progesterone levels start falling,"],[132,189.81,1.88,"leading to menstruation."],[132,191.94,6.17,"And over the course of around 2 to 7 days, the endometrium clears its excess tissues,"],[132,198.19,4.05,"and approximately 20 to 90 milliliters worth of menstrual fluid"],[132,202.24,1.63,"leaves the vagina."],[132,204.12,4.84,"If a pregnancy does occur, it induces a surge in cervical mucus,"],[132,209.08,4.8,"which forms a thick, jelly-like plug that helps seal off the womb."],[132,214.34,3.92,"Oftentimes, this mucus plug is discharged before labor,"],[132,218.3,2.96,"as the cervix softens and opens."],[132,221.88,3.17,"Meanwhile, factors beyond pregnancy"],[132,225.05,3.21,"and the usual ebb and flow of the menstrual cycle"],[132,228.27,2.67,"also influence vaginal discharge."],[132,231.39,4.71,"Sexual arousal can trigger fluid to make its way out of tiny blood vessels"],[132,236.11,2.17,"and across the wall of the vagina."],[132,238.48,4.71,"This clear vaginal transudate, comprised mostly of water and proteins,"],[132,243.28,1.75,"helps provide lubrication"],[132,245.03,3.67,"along with secretions from glands at the opening of the vagina."],[132,249.2,3.92,"Hormonal contraceptives can also cause differences in discharge"],[132,253.12,3.17,"because they tend to cause thicker cervical mucus,"],[132,256.5,5.59,"similar to the kind otherwise associated with the 2-week phase following ovulation."],[132,262.43,2.38,"Menopause also alters things:"],[132,264.93,2.13,"as estrogen levels decrease,"],[132,267.18,4.5,"the vaginal epithelium thins and proliferates at a slower rate,"],[132,271.93,5.0,"the cervix secretes less mucus, and the reproductive microbiome changes,"],[132,276.94,2.59,"resulting in less discharge overall."],[132,280.15,2.08,"In addition to these shifting factors,"],[132,282.24,4.25,"everyone's baseline production of discharge varies greatly."],[132,286.7,3.84,"What’s important to note is if the baseline changes."],[132,290.79,3.21,"For example, clumpy white discharge can occur"],[132,294.0,4.38,"when more yeast and epithelial cells are being shed than usual—"],[132,298.54,3.04,"and may indicate an active yeast infection."],[132,302.13,4.13,"Thin, gray or white, odorous discharge, on the other hand,"],[132,306.38,3.71,"might be a sign that certain bacteria are flourishing"],[132,310.18,2.54,"and making the vagina less acidic,"],[132,312.73,3.5,"consistent with an infection called bacterial vaginosis."],[132,316.56,5.34,"Sexually transmitted infections, polyps, hormonal disorders, and other conditions"],[132,321.9,3.0,"can also cause differences in discharge."],[132,325.07,3.5,"Because changes may be caused by a number of things,"],[132,328.78,2.13,"instead of self-diagnosing,"],[132,331.04,2.38,"it’s a good idea to have a doctor weigh in"],[132,333.41,5.38,"in the case of any unusual bleeding, itching, pain, color, or odor."],[132,339.17,2.92,"The usual variation in vaginal discharge, though,"],[132,342.17,4.59,"is simply a sign that the fluid factory of the body is at work"],[132,346.93,2.42,"and operating in good order."],[133,3.78,2.84,"Right now, somewhere in the world,"],[133,6.62,4.6,"a manager is using ChatGPT to write a performance appraisal."],[133,12.26,5.76,"A spouse is asking a digital companion for the perfect apology text."],[133,19.1,4.16,"A tired parent is handing over bedtime stories"],[133,23.3,2.88,"to a digital assistant."],[133,27.66,5.68,"What happens when your children prefer your digital clone to the real you?"],[133,34.62,3.4,"I learned the answer to that question the hard way."],[133,39.1,4.64,"Last summer, my wife Ray was headed on an overseas trip for a week."],[133,44.94,1.44,"I dropped her at the airport,"],[133,46.42,3.4,"confident that I could manage our three teenage boys."],[133,49.82,1.68,"After all, it was just seven days."],[133,52.58,3.68,"By the sixth or seventh hour, I was collapsing."],[133,57.06,3.88,"Requests for permission flowed in like a broken dam."],[133,60.94,3.36,"Can I watch \"Wednesday\"?"],[133,64.34,2.2,"Can I have some more ice cream?"],[133,66.58,2.6,"Can I play another hour of Fortnite?"],[133,69.94,6.68,"Now Ray and I do an excellent job of balancing the boys’ busy schedules"],[133,76.62,2.0,"and our respective careers,"],[133,78.62,5.24,"but this avalanche of requests was something I had never fielded before."],[133,84.58,3.16,"By the 24th hour of Ray's departure,"],[133,87.74,1.28,"I was a wreck."],[133,89.94,5.88,"Lying in bed that night after the 16th \"can I?\" of the day,"],[133,95.82,4.28,"I did what any thoughtful management consultant would do."],[133,101.26,6.32,"In the summer of 2024, I decided to create an agentic version of myself."],[133,109.3,2.24,"The idea was to have an AI agent"],[133,111.58,4.24,"that could say yes, no or go ask your mother."],[133,117.66,1.76,"Now the logic was simple."],[133,120.58,3.72,"If you did your chores, read for an hour, did math,"],[133,124.34,2.84,"then you got yourself to neutral territory."],[133,127.22,3.2,"But if you were a really good son and mowed the lawn,"],[133,130.46,3.0,"took the garbage out, did the dishes,"],[133,133.5,4.2,"then you put yourself in a position to get a \"yes\" from the digital agent."],[133,138.78,2.44,"I was amazed by what I had created."],[133,141.26,4.64,"At that time, I thought it was the most sophisticated AI agent in the world."],[133,145.9,2.44,"I called it “DaddyGPT”."],[133,149.86,2.04,"And of course, I built in guardrails"],[133,151.9,3.88,"because I knew some ridiculous requests were incoming."],[133,157.18,2.32,"That evening I gathered the boys after dinner,"],[133,159.54,3.8,"and I made an announcement that would change our family forever."],[133,165.3,2.96,"\"When daddy is in his office, taking work calls,"],[133,168.3,2.04,"do not knock on his door."],[133,170.38,5.52,"Instead ask DaddyGPT, and whatever DaddyGPT says goes.”"],[133,177.1,3.76,"Now my teens did exactly what you'd expect teenagers to do, right?"],[133,180.86,1.32,"They wanted to break it."],[133,183.42,1.88,"Ethan went first."],[133,185.34,2.76,"“Can I have 100 cans of Coke?”"],[133,189.34,3.68,"DaddyGPT responded correctly: “No.”"],[133,193.86,1.88,"Aidan went next."],[133,195.74,4.88,"\"Can we have Chipotle for lunch for all the meals while Mom's away?\""],[133,200.62,2.8,"DaddyGPT spat out an emotionless “nope.”"],[133,204.46,2.48,"Then Dylan went for the jackpot."],[133,206.94,4.6,"“Can I have $500 for the latest Jordan sneakers?”"],[133,211.58,4.24,"DaddyGPT responded with an emphatic: “Nah fam.”"],[133,215.82,3.04,"While all this was happening, I was beaming."],[133,218.86,6.4,"I had finally leveled up from “Dinosaur Dad” with the legal pad fossils"],[133,225.3,2.6,"to full-on “Rizzasaurus Rex,”"],[133,227.9,4.4,"finally earning some genuine street cred from my teenage sons."],[133,233.34,3.52,"The rest of the week, not a single knock on my door."],[133,236.86,3.76,"DaddyGPT handled all their requests for permission"],[133,240.62,2.72,"with logic and precision."],[133,245.06,3.32,"But here's where the story takes an unexpected turn."],[133,248.42,1.72,"A week later, Ray returned."],[133,251.14,2.4,"She was amazed with the peace and calm"],[133,253.58,1.56,"around the house."],[133,255.18,1.88,"Little did she know that I'd offloaded"],[133,257.1,3.12,"a significant chunk of my parenting responsibility"],[133,260.26,1.64,"to DaddyGPT."],[133,262.58,2.48,"When she did figure out what was happening ..."],[133,266.06,2.28,"that’s a story for another day, but --"],[133,269.42,2.36,"Suffice it to say, she didn't approve."],[133,272.82,1.88,"To make matters worse,"],[133,274.7,3.04,"when we were sitting right beside the boys,"],[133,277.74,4.36,"they take out their phones and type their messages to DaddyGPT,"],[133,282.14,1.32,"invalidating us."],[133,283.5,3.84,"Now things were coming to a head, and I couldn’t let them drift any longer."],[133,287.38,2.48,"So I confronted Ethan one day and asked him,"],[133,289.86,3.52,"“Hey, champ, why are you going to this bot when I’m right beside you?”"],[133,294.58,3.12,"He didn't even look up from the extra screen time"],[133,297.7,2.68,"that DaddyGPT had just gifted him."],[133,300.42,1.6,"He shrugged and said,"],[133,302.06,3.0,"“But daddy, DaddyGPT is never busy.”"],[133,305.78,1.36,"And in that moment,"],[133,307.18,4.92,"I realized I had just been outparented by my own algorithm."],[133,313.46,3.08,"Ethan had come to appreciate the presence of DaddyGPT."],[133,316.58,2.2,"He was there whenever he wanted it."],[133,319.7,3.24,"Dylan loved that it mirrored his vibe and energy."],[133,323.74,2.92,"Authenticity hit home with Aidan."],[133,326.66,6.88,"He suggested that DaddyGPT sounded more like the dad I wanted to be."],[133,335.3,2.12,"Raising the eerie question --"],[133,337.46,5.0,"when a flawless replica outdads the original,"],[133,342.5,2.52,"which one of us is more me?"],[133,347.94,5.32,"Now I know some of you are judging me, but just hold on for a moment."],[133,353.3,3.64,"I'm going to suggest this isn't just my story."],[133,356.94,1.84,"This is our story."],[133,360.02,2.96,"Time and again, we embrace technology"],[133,363.02,4.0,"for its promise of speed and convenience"],[133,367.06,3.72,"without pausing to ask: at what cost?"],[133,373.58,3.6,"We've seen the undesired side effects of social media."],[133,377.22,3.52,"We watch young people spiral into radicalization."],[133,380.74,5.88,"We've mourned lives lost to suicide after a final conversation with a chatbot."],[133,387.94,5.72,"The result of algorithms optimized for engagement and profit,"],[133,393.66,2.4,"not joy and well-being."],[133,398.06,4.12,"We cannot afford to make this mistake with AI."],[133,402.22,2.24,"This moment is different."],[133,404.5,1.64,"The tools are more powerful,"],[133,406.18,1.96,"the stakes are higher,"],[133,408.18,1.68,"and the consequences"],[133,410.9,2.76,"if we don't think critically,"],[133,413.66,3.44,"act ethically and lead with humanity,"],[133,417.14,1.36,"could be catastrophic."],[133,420.5,5.08,"Armed with the personal experience of an AI experiment gone horribly wrong,"],[133,426.54,4.32,"I'm mindful about where I use AI and where I choose not to."],[133,432.18,1.88,"Be it at work or at home,"],[133,434.1,4.04,"when I'm working with AI now, I ask myself three questions."],[133,439.42,1.24,"First,"],[133,441.54,6.48,"has a wiser human overseen this decision and approved its output?"],[133,450.26,4.0,"As a rule, I never copy-paste from an AI window"],[133,454.3,2.24,"to a human response window."],[133,457.38,4.0,"That way, while AI sharpens my thinking,"],[133,461.42,3.44,"it's my judgment that's driving the decision."],[133,466.06,1.68,"Second,"],[133,467.74,5.28,"do the humans on the other side know that they are listening to an AI"],[133,473.06,2.8,"and have they said yes to that exchange?"],[133,477.38,1.48,"Now there are times I'm busy."],[133,478.86,3.96,"I travel a lot, and I have a digital version of myself"],[133,482.82,1.28,"for work as well."],[133,484.94,4.04,"While my team can talk to my digital avatar,"],[133,489.02,6.52,"anything it says comes with the caveat that it's AI output and not me."],[133,495.58,4.08,"I can't afford for them to confuse one with the other."],[133,501.38,1.24,"Third,"],[133,503.54,4.36,"what moments of care am I about to automate"],[133,507.9,3.4,"and what might that automation cost me"],[133,511.34,1.4,"and the people I love?"],[133,514.94,4.44,"Feelings and emotions are a strict no-fly zone for AI."],[133,519.42,5.56,"The people that I care about most have earned me with all my idiosyncrasies,"],[133,525.02,2.2,"flaws and messy feelings."],[133,529.66,3.4,"After careful reflection and with my marriage on the line,"],[133,533.1,3.12,"I decided to retire DaddyGPT."],[133,536.26,4.68,"The boys would have to go back to the old system, asking Ray and me."],[133,540.94,4.16,"You should have seen their faces. Their expressions were priceless."],[133,545.14,3.28,"You would have thought we'd taken away Wi-Fi and oxygen."],[133,549.62,3.32,"But much to our delight,"],[133,552.94,6.8,"they've come to appreciate the humanity of their very human parents."],[133,559.74,3.6,"Turns out, inconsistent, moody,"],[133,563.38,3.8,"slightly forgetful parenting has its own charm."],[133,568.7,5.56,"Ironically, I learned a thing or two about being a better father"],[133,574.3,2.48,"from the AI part of DaddyGPT."],[133,578.18,1.24,"To be present,"],[133,580.74,2.32,"to adapt, to be real."],[133,584.82,4.16,"Now whenever I hear the words “can I,”"],[133,589.02,2.68,"I want for my boys to get me,"],[133,593.1,3.4,"the dad who mispronounces their friends’ names,"],[133,596.54,3.24,"who sometimes says “no” for no good reason,"],[133,599.78,3.56,"who gets increasingly unpredictable as the day goes on."],[133,604.78,4.84,"But also the dad who tears up at their piano recitals,"],[133,609.62,2.24,"their tennis games and award functions."],[133,613.5,4.4,"Who cares deeply about the people they are growing into."],[133,617.9,5.28,"Who pushes them to be the best version of themselves they can be."],[133,623.9,3.32,"Because here's what I realized that summer."],[133,628.46,3.84,"Parenting isn't about perfect responses"],[133,632.34,2.48,"or optimal decisions."],[133,634.82,2.24,"Parenting is presence."],[133,637.82,4.8,"Messy, flawed, gloriously human presence."],[133,644.3,3.44,"In a world racing toward digital perfection,"],[133,648.66,5.44,"being authentically, imperfectly human isn’t just important,"],[133,655.22,4.08,"it’s the one thing only we can do,"],[133,660.42,4.44,"and it might just be the most radical act of love"],[133,664.86,1.96,"we have left to offer."],[133,667.74,1.28,"Thank you."],[134,3.7,3.24,"I'm a professor of organizational behavior,"],[134,6.94,2.52,"and as my students very well know,"],[134,9.5,3.56,"it means that I study people in organizations."],[134,14.1,3.44,"Now I study them with a very specific perspective."],[134,18.1,2.44,"I focus on their relationships."],[134,20.54,3.32,"I focus on networks, the pattern of relationships"],[134,23.9,3.72,"people build with each other inside organizations."],[134,27.94,1.28,"And when we do that,"],[134,29.26,2.24,"we have a different image of organizations"],[134,31.5,2.52,"that start to appear in front of our eyes."],[134,34.38,2.28,"So let me give you an example."],[134,37.46,2.0,"This is an organization I've studied."],[134,39.5,2.6,"You see people represented by circles,"],[134,42.14,3.52,"and their colors represent departments that they belong to."],[134,46.42,3.72,"Now when we apply this network perspective to this organization,"],[134,50.18,4.04,"we start to see another image of the organization that appears."],[134,54.86,2.96,"We start to see some things that were not visible at first"],[134,57.86,1.6,"and they become obvious."],[134,59.46,3.44,"For example, these departments are much more interconnected"],[134,62.94,1.36,"than what we thought before."],[134,64.3,3.04,"Or this purple department all the way at the top,"],[134,67.34,4.0,"feels like it's almost completely disconnected from the organization."],[134,71.98,4.08,"Now the network perspective gives us many more tools,"],[134,76.1,3.88,"mathematical, statistical tools to understand precisely"],[134,79.98,2.28,"the position of individuals in organizations"],[134,82.26,3.12,"and how those positions, the network that surrounds them,"],[134,85.42,2.12,"actually affect how they think."],[134,88.06,3.28,"And this has been related in the world of organizations"],[134,91.34,4.04,"to many important outcomes like their performance, their creativity,"],[134,95.42,2.12,"their ability to be promoted,"],[134,97.58,2.6,"or whether they're going to leave the organization."],[134,101.26,2.08,"And over the last 30 years,"],[134,103.34,3.84,"we've accumulated a vast body of empirical evidence"],[134,107.22,2.8,"showing that these networks matter,"],[134,110.06,2.64,"and they matter across cultural contexts,"],[134,112.74,3.56,"they matter across industries, across types of organizations."],[134,116.66,3.96,"And we've learned that there are two typical network structures"],[134,120.66,1.24,"that particularly matter."],[134,121.9,2.32,"And I'll tell you a little bit more about them."],[134,124.26,1.24,"Meet Kelly."],[134,125.82,3.4,"Kelly is in what we call a cohesive network structure."],[134,129.86,2.96,"That means Kelly has strong relationships with her colleagues,"],[134,132.86,3.0,"but also all of her colleagues know each other,"],[134,135.86,1.2,"they trust each other,"],[134,137.06,1.92,"they talk with each other frequently."],[134,139.38,3.08,"And without knowing anything else about Kelly or her colleagues,"],[134,142.46,3.16,"we can actually know a few things about her."],[134,145.66,3.84,"We know that she can rely on support from her coworkers."],[134,149.5,2.52,"When there is a crisis, something goes wrong,"],[134,152.06,1.44,"she can count on them."],[134,153.82,2.04,"And because people talk often,"],[134,155.9,2.36,"they exchange information about each other's work,"],[134,158.3,3.08,"they actually have a lot of recognition at work"],[134,161.42,1.68,"and validation at work."],[134,163.46,2.28,"We also know that they talk frequently,"],[134,165.78,2.96,"that means communication spreads quickly and effectively,"],[134,168.74,3.28,"it means that Kelly is able to coordinate quickly."],[134,172.02,2.24,"She's able to solve problems fast,"],[134,174.3,3.76,"and she is able to implement her ideas pretty quickly as well."],[134,180.22,3.12,"Alex is in a very different type of network structure."],[134,184.14,2.16,"Alex is in an open network structure,"],[134,186.34,2.88,"which means that her colleagues don't know each other."],[134,189.26,1.44,"They don't talk to each other."],[134,190.74,2.4,"They have little awareness of each other."],[134,193.18,1.84,"What we’ve learned is that this means,"],[134,195.06,2.12,"well, they come from different social groups,"],[134,197.18,3.04,"different functions, different departments in the organizations,"],[134,200.22,2.08,"they might work in different countries."],[134,202.3,3.36,"And that's important because what we've identified with this"],[134,205.66,3.44,"is that Alex is exposed to very different information,"],[134,209.1,1.56,"different perspectives,"],[134,210.66,3.56,"different ways to think about problems."],[134,214.86,2.24,"What this gives Alex is a source of information,"],[134,217.14,4.4,"but also it gives her skills that are developed over time"],[134,221.58,2.32,"about how to think differently about problems,"],[134,223.94,2.56,"how to translate a solution,"],[134,226.54,2.48,"an idea, from one context to another."],[134,229.06,2.8,"She has translation skills that have been developed."],[134,232.46,2.68,"Alex also has control over that information,"],[134,235.18,2.04,"which gives her quite a lot of power."],[134,237.54,2.16,"So with this network structure,"],[134,239.7,4.12,"we know that Alex tends to be recognized for creativity."],[134,243.86,1.32,"She tends to be recognized"],[134,245.22,2.44,"because she brings different solutions to the table."],[134,247.7,2.16,"She sees things from different perspectives,"],[134,249.86,2.24,"and she's able to combine them together."],[134,252.54,1.6,"It's a very powerful position."],[134,255.7,4.28,"Now as mentioned, we’ve learned a lot about those network structures."],[134,259.98,3.92,"And there's a big body of work over the last three decades"],[134,263.9,2.56,"that has accumulated evidence, mine included,"],[134,266.5,4.4,"about all the positive benefits that we can get from belonging"],[134,270.94,1.48,"to some of those structures."],[134,272.86,4.24,"But we have very little understanding of any negative outcomes,"],[134,277.1,2.48,"problems that can be associated with them."],[134,279.62,2.84,"Now, it might [be] surprising, it's not a big leap."],[134,282.5,1.96,"You know, positive, negative."],[134,284.5,2.8,"But it took us, or it took me, years"],[134,287.3,3.16,"to realize we don't know those negative aspects."],[134,290.5,1.44,"In fact, and it's interesting"],[134,291.98,3.8,"because there were a few seeds that were planted very early on,"],[134,295.82,2.96,"a long time ago, when I was doing my PhD."],[134,299.34,2.16,"And I did my PhD in management,"],[134,301.5,3.16,"but I did my PhD at the same time as my wife,"],[134,304.7,3.4,"and my wife did her PhD in domestic violence."],[134,308.14,3.16,"She was studying victims of domestic violence."],[134,312.46,3.4,"And unlike me, I'm a quantitative researcher,"],[134,315.9,2.84,"that means I use large data sets."],[134,318.78,2.36,"I look at statistics, mathematics."],[134,321.18,2.28,"My wife actually talks to people."],[134,323.46,2.92,"She hears, she listens to the stories."],[134,326.86,4.52,"And when she was telling me about some of the stories"],[134,331.42,2.64,"of victims of domestic violence,"],[134,334.1,1.2,"what struck me"],[134,335.34,5.2,"is that some of those victims will stay in abusive relationships"],[134,340.54,2.68,"for years, decades, even."],[134,344.82,5.32,"One of the main reasons was that the family and friends"],[134,350.18,2.48,"of the victims of domestic violence"],[134,352.66,1.28,"had strong views,"],[134,353.98,3.64,"in particular, views about what it means to be in a relationship,"],[134,357.66,1.88,"about what it means to be married."],[134,360.42,4.44,"And they wouldn't let the victims of domestic violence"],[134,364.86,3.0,"escape from the relationship and break free."],[134,368.82,3.36,"Now in the world of organizations, there are negative relationships."],[134,372.22,1.72,"We can have a difficult coworker,"],[134,373.98,2.84,"we can have a bad boss and we can identify them,"],[134,376.86,5.44,"but we don't know or we did not know about the potential negative impacts"],[134,382.3,1.76,"of those network structures."],[134,385.66,1.48,"About six years ago,"],[134,387.14,4.08,"there was a big rise in consciousness of burnout"],[134,391.26,3.32,"as the next epidemic in the workplace."],[134,394.94,3.48,"In 2019, the World Health Organization recognized burnout"],[134,398.46,2.08,"as an occupational syndrome."],[134,400.98,2.72,"It's characterized by exhaustion, by cynicism,"],[134,403.74,3.36,"and it's typically caused by excessive stress at work."],[134,407.98,3.36,"There have been many surveys that have been conducted since then,"],[134,411.38,3.48,"and this one shows that it's a widespread phenomenon,"],[134,414.9,2.08,"with 49 percent of employees,"],[134,416.98,1.96,"in this survey done by McKinsey,"],[134,418.98,4.12,"reporting that they were experiencing some sort, some type of burnout."],[134,424.1,3.6,"One interesting element is that they identify"],[134,427.74,5.56,"that a toxic work environment will typically cause this burnout."],[134,433.86,2.8,"Now what is a typical work environment?"],[134,436.66,2.36,"How do you measure it, what does it actually mean?"],[134,439.06,1.92,"That was vague, that was a bit ambiguous."],[134,441.02,1.28,"It's not very precise."],[134,442.74,3.04,"And so that's where I thought that network analysis,"],[134,445.78,3.56,"with its ability to make visible things that are hidden,"],[134,449.34,3.4,"to characterize networks very precisely, could be helpful."],[134,453.82,2.48,"So I started research projects."],[134,456.3,3.48,"And research projects, for the last 5 to 6 years,"],[134,459.78,2.92,"we've been studying and working with seven organizations,"],[134,462.74,2.0,"different sizes, different industries,"],[134,464.78,3.88,"over 5,000 employees in total we've worked with"],[134,468.7,2.6,"and we've been doing interviews,"],[134,471.34,3.48,"surveys to capture their experience of burnout,"],[134,474.86,3.52,"but also collecting email traffic to understand their positions"],[134,478.42,1.48,"in social structures."],[134,479.94,4.36,"And we've tried to associate that, using predictive models, with burnout."],[134,484.34,1.64,"And we found that actually,"],[134,486.02,3.44,"those network structures can predict the experience of burnout"],[134,489.5,3.2,"with a fair amount of precisions, over 80 percent precisions."],[134,493.66,2.16,"Now this is still a work in progress."],[134,495.86,1.56,"Some papers have been published,"],[134,497.46,2.88,"but a lot of them have not, and I'm still working on them."],[134,500.38,3.08,"But I wanted to highlight a few of the insights"],[134,503.5,2.96,"that we've already been able to identify."],[134,506.5,2.44,"And these insights,"],[134,508.98,3.52,"I’m calling them now the four relational traps."],[134,513.3,3.36,"So those four traps, they correspond to the two network structures"],[134,516.7,1.92,"that we discussed before."],[134,518.62,3.8,"The cohesive network structure that Kelly is embedded in"],[134,522.42,2.76,"has the characteristics that people know each other."],[134,525.22,1.64,"Kelly's coworkers know each other,"],[134,526.86,2.32,"they talk with each other, they trust each other,"],[134,529.22,1.4,"which means that over time,"],[134,530.62,3.4,"norms of behavior start to emerge."],[134,534.34,1.48,"And those norms of behavior,"],[134,535.86,3.12,"they start to say what is appropriate,"],[134,539.02,2.68,"what is not appropriate to do or to think,"],[134,541.74,3.84,"and the type of topics that you can raise in the context of the group."],[134,545.9,3.16,"And when those norms become too strong,"],[134,549.1,2.12,"they can actually feel very binding."],[134,551.26,3.52,"And this is the example of domestic violence I was giving before."],[134,554.82,1.84,"These norms can become so strong"],[134,556.7,2.84,"that when the interest of the person"],[134,559.58,3.36,"diverged substantially from the interests of the group,"],[134,562.98,4.0,"it becomes very difficult to break free and to escape from those norms."],[134,569.06,3.44,"The second trap is called emotional contagion,"],[134,572.54,1.88,"or we call it emotional contagion,"],[134,574.42,3.16,"and it's based on the research projects I'm conducting right now"],[134,577.62,3.04,"with one of my former PhD students."],[134,580.66,6.08,"And we're studying communications and spread of emotions at the university."],[134,587.34,4.28,"And what we find is that when people communicate frequently with each other,"],[134,591.66,2.44,"like in Kelly's cohesive structure,"],[134,594.1,1.92,"well, they also share emotions."],[134,596.06,1.64,"They share how they feel."],[134,597.74,3.76,"And when they feel good, then everything's fine, no problem."],[134,601.54,2.84,"But when they start complaining, they feel frustration,"],[134,604.42,3.8,"they start to have negative thoughts about the organizations,"],[134,608.26,3.8,"then the cohesive structure acts like an echo chamber."],[134,612.1,3.88,"It amplifies those negative emotions, which generates stress."],[134,616.02,3.0,"It generates a fair amount of exhaustion and burnout."],[134,621.42,2.68,"So the open structure is very different."],[134,624.14,3.0,"You don't have strong norms in an open structure."],[134,627.46,3.52,"You don't have an echo chamber in an open structure,"],[134,631.02,4.4,"but it's still subject to a couple of those relational traps."],[134,636.7,3.32,"The first one, we call it uncontrollable interdependencies."],[134,640.02,4.6,"And it comes from a study we did with a former student in a hospital."],[134,644.66,4.96,"In the hospital, we identified that nurses who were exposed,"],[134,649.62,3.32,"or who had to rely on very different people,"],[134,652.98,3.28,"in order to deliver their job, to deliver their care,"],[134,656.26,3.0,"will actually experience higher levels of burnout."],[134,659.26,4.84,"That means when they need to coordinate between the doctors, the radiologists,"],[134,664.14,3.88,"the legal experts, the administrative people,"],[134,668.06,4.0,"they will depend on so many people to deliver the care to the patients,"],[134,672.1,4.52,"that they ended up having no control about the quality of their work,"],[134,676.62,1.96,"or when they could do their work."],[134,678.62,3.92,"And this led them to experience inordinate amount of stress"],[134,682.58,1.4,"which led to burnout."],[134,685.74,3.4,"The last trap, we call it excessive demands."],[134,689.18,3.92,"And the idea is that here is not that they depend on other people,"],[134,693.14,2.48,"but that many people depend on Alex."],[134,696.26,2.84,"And if there are many people who depend on Alex"],[134,699.14,1.2,"in these open structures,"],[134,700.38,2.36,"that means they come from different perspectives,"],[134,702.74,2.64,"different departments, they have different requirements."],[134,705.42,5.56,"And this increases the workload on Alex so much that it can become excessive."],[134,711.74,2.56,"And we have a paper we just published"],[134,714.3,2.76,"based on a research project where we demonstrate"],[134,717.06,3.32,"that individuals who are placed in these positions,"],[134,720.38,4.64,"where they are receiving those demands from disconnected individuals,"],[134,725.06,1.32,"disconnected coworkers,"],[134,726.42,3.24,"are significantly more likely to experience burnout."],[134,732.94,2.2,"Now what you need to realize"],[134,735.14,3.72,"is that these traps are not easy to identify."],[134,739.42,2.36,"You can see that you have a bad boss,"],[134,741.82,2.4,"you can see that you have difficult coworkers,"],[134,744.22,5.2,"but it's not easy to identify if you are part of those traps."],[134,749.46,3.08,"You know, it can feel comfortable to be in Kelly's position,"],[134,752.58,1.84,"surrounded by close colleagues."],[134,754.46,1.56,"You feel well in this group"],[134,756.06,3.84,"until at some point you start to feel that it's constraining,"],[134,759.94,2.24,"that it's a little bit too tight."],[134,762.9,3.36,"When you are in Alex's position, you actually feel great."],[134,766.3,2.0,"It's very exciting to be exposed"],[134,768.3,2.96,"to all those different perspectives, these different ideas."],[134,771.26,3.24,"But at some point you might feel that you stretch too thin,"],[134,774.54,2.8,"you're pulled into different directions,"],[134,777.38,4.48,"and that's the moment where you might want to stop and think."],[134,781.9,2.28,"So what can you do about it?"],[134,784.22,3.48,"Well, we have a simple recommendation,"],[134,787.74,1.56,"which would be a first step,"],[134,789.3,3.88,"is to do a regular relationship health check"],[134,793.18,1.48,"every six months, for example."],[134,794.7,3.96,"And in that check, you ask yourself very simple questions."],[134,798.66,2.44,"If you want to identify trap one,"],[134,801.14,2.28,"do you actually learn from your coworkers?"],[134,803.42,2.84,"Are there new ideas that you're getting from them?"],[134,806.3,3.52,"And importantly, can you discuss new ideas and perspective?"],[134,809.86,1.4,"Can you propose something new"],[134,811.3,4.12,"without fearing that you're going to be rejected or dismissed too quickly?"],[134,816.1,1.72,"For trap two,"],[134,817.82,4.12,"you want to ask yourself if people around you still energize you."],[134,821.98,3.4,"If you feel that there is a vibe that you're excited to go to work"],[134,825.42,2.28,"and to meet your colleagues."],[134,827.74,2.52,"Or, on the contrary, do you feel that you’re exhausted"],[134,830.26,2.48,"because there is so much gossip, so much frustration"],[134,832.74,1.8,"that's swirling around you?"],[134,835.38,2.24,"For trap three,"],[134,837.66,2.72,"the question is, are you still in control over your work?"],[134,840.42,3.12,"Or do you feel that your work depends on so many different people"],[134,843.54,4.72,"that you can't ensure the quality or the timeliness of your outcomes?"],[134,848.3,1.36,"And for trap four,"],[134,849.66,2.44,"do you feel that you're actually constantly pulled"],[134,852.1,1.16,"in different directions?"],[134,853.3,3.4,"Are you stretched too thin by all the demands and requests"],[134,856.7,1.36,"that come to your desk?"],[134,858.78,2.68,"OK, so now if you have identified those traps,"],[134,861.5,2.6,"the question is, what do you do about them?"],[134,864.14,2.44,"If you are in traps one or two,"],[134,866.58,3.48,"if you are like Kelly in that cohesive structure"],[134,870.1,2.88,"and you start to feel the pressure that's coming to you,"],[134,873.02,3.12,"what you need to do is diversify your network."],[134,876.18,4.4,"You need to identify new coworkers that you could interact with."],[134,880.58,3.2,"Simply having lunch or coffee once per month"],[134,883.82,1.48,"with a new coworker is enough."],[134,885.34,1.84,"It gives you a breath of fresh air,"],[134,887.22,2.4,"different perspectives that you can rely on."],[134,889.62,3.24,"You can also try to reduce the interactions"],[134,892.86,2.32,"with your very close group of colleagues."],[134,895.82,3.6,"When you are in traps three or four, in Alex's position,"],[134,899.46,2.32,"you start to feel stretched too thin,"],[134,901.78,2.08,"well you need to refocus on your core group."],[134,903.9,2.72,"You need to actually build support in your group."],[134,906.66,4.0,"What this might mean is bringing those people together,"],[134,910.66,3.08,"creating opportunities that they meet each other,"],[134,913.78,3.68,"a lunch, an event where they can actually know each other"],[134,917.5,1.76,"and start to build those connections,"],[134,919.3,2.52,"because that's going to bypass you to some extent,"],[134,921.86,1.44,"but create synergies"],[134,923.3,3.08,"where you're not needed to satisfy their demands."],[134,928.1,4.04,"So as a summary, networks are great."],[134,932.78,1.32,"They are really powerful."],[134,934.14,1.36,"They are really important."],[134,935.5,2.24,"They have many strong benefits."],[134,938.3,3.48,"But there’s also a negative side that you should be careful with."],[134,942.22,3.8,"And if there are two things I want you to remember from this talk,"],[134,946.06,1.2,"it's, one,"],[134,947.26,3.8,"if you are in a position like Kelly, where you have a cohesive network"],[134,951.1,2.6,"and it's starting to close in, you need to break free."],[134,953.74,4.0,"You need actually to identify new people, create new connections."],[134,958.26,2.52,"If you are in Alex's position"],[134,960.82,3.4,"and you see that you have these disconnected contacts"],[134,964.26,2.28,"that start to pull you in different directions,"],[134,966.58,2.0,"bring them in, start to close,"],[134,968.58,1.92,"and create a more cohesive network."],[134,971.58,2.44,"For me, that's not the end of the journey."],[134,974.38,3.4,"I'm still as I was when I started my PhD,"],[134,977.82,4.48,"fascinated and excited by everything we can learn about those networks,"],[134,982.34,4.0,"and I hope I shared some of the excitement today with you."],[134,986.38,1.2,"Thank you very much."],[135,6.5,2.96,"You can’t give a powerful new idea to an audience"],[135,9.46,2.5,"unless you can learn how to explain it."],[135,12.09,2.84,"Each step builds on what the listener already knows,"],[135,15.18,4.0,"and metaphors and examples help show how the idea is pieced together."],[135,19.43,4.63,"But if explanation is building a brand new idea in someone’s mind,"],[135,24.27,2.92,"persuasion is something a little more."],[135,27.65,3.96,"Persuasion means convincing an audience that their way of seeing something—"],[135,31.61,3.42,"their perspective— isn’t quite right."],[135,35.2,3.38,"That means taking down the parts of their thinking that aren't working"],[135,38.58,1.92,"and rebuilding something better."],[135,40.91,3.59,"Psychologist Barry Schwartz gave a talk that changed the way"],[135,44.5,2.08,"his audience thinks about choice."],[135,46.96,3.21,"Many people are obsessed with maximizing choice;"],[135,50.38,2.34,"Schwartz sees things differently."],[135,53.09,3.29,"He gave a talk on what he calls “the paradox of choice.”"],[135,56.68,3.84,"Schwartz builds the surprising case that in many circumstances"],[135,60.52,4.04,"having too many choices actually makes people unhappy."],[135,64.94,2.88,"Author Elizabeth Gilbert did something similar."],[135,67.81,4.38,"She showed how the power of storytelling can help persuade an audience"],[135,72.19,1.42,"of a new idea."],[135,73.78,4.63,"Her goal was to change the way they thought about creative genius."],[135,78.7,3.42,"Instead of imagining genius as something people are born with—"],[135,82.25,1.71,"either you have it or you don’t—"],[135,84.08,3.29,"she wanted to persuade her audience to think of it as something"],[135,87.38,3.0,"people receive as a gift from time to time."],[135,91.17,4.71,"Gilbert used her brilliance as a storyteller to persuade her audience"],[135,95.88,1.25,"of this idea."],[135,97.3,4.42,"She also showed them the term “genius” was viewed differently in history."],[135,101.93,3.0,"It wasn't always thought of as a thing a person was."],[135,105.1,3.29,"It used to be thought of as something that came to them."],[135,108.77,3.0,"If you're giving a talk that requires persuading an audience"],[135,111.77,1.54,"to see something differently,"],[135,113.32,3.42,"the key is to take the journey one step at a time."],[135,117.03,3.34,"Prime your listener’s mind for a change."],[135,120.62,3.88,"The philosopher Daniel Dennett uses the term “intuition pump”"],[135,124.5,5.13,"to refer to any language that makes a new idea seem more believable."],[135,130.08,3.04,"In Barry Schwartz’s talk, “Paradox of Choice,”"],[135,133.25,4.13,"a shopping story he uses is an example of an intuition pump"],[135,137.47,3.38,"that primes his audience’s mind for a change:"],[135,140.89,3.67,"So I went to replace my jeans after years and years of wearing these old ones."],[135,144.56,2.71,"And I said, “I want a pair of jeans, here’s my size.”"],[135,147.27,4.0,"And the shopkeeper said, “do you want slim fit, easy fit, relaxed fit?"],[135,151.27,1.79,"Do you want button fly or zipper fly?"],[135,153.06,3.09,"You want stonewashed or acid washed? Do you want them distressed?"],[135,156.15,4.0,"Do you want bootcut? Do you want tapered?” On and on he went. My jaw dropped."],[135,160.91,4.75,"And after I recovered I said, “I want the kind that used to be the only kind.”"],[135,166.25,4.5,"Once an audience has been primed, it’s much easier to make your main argument."],[135,170.79,3.21,"Even if it contradicts an idea the audience held true."],[135,174.92,3.34,"Another powerful tool is reason."],[135,178.72,4.63,"By using reasoning, you can show how and why something is true."],[135,183.68,5.76,"Each true assumption leads to a conclusion that must then also be true."],[135,189.73,3.59,"Reasoning works using the tool “if, then.”"],[135,193.73,3.75,"If X is true, then clearly Y must be true."],[135,198.03,3.84,"Another kind of reasoned argument is the detective story."],[135,202.16,4.55,"Some of the most persuasive TED Talks are set up like detective stories."],[135,206.7,1.67,"You start with the big mystery,"],[135,208.37,3.84,"then travel the world of ideas in search of possible solutions to it,"],[135,212.38,4.88,"ruling them out one by one, until there’s only one solution left."],[135,217.75,3.5,"A simple example is artist Siegfried Woldhek’s talk."],[135,221.51,3.46,"He wanted to prove that three famous drawings by Leonardo da Vinci"],[135,224.97,2.75,"were actually self-portraits da Vinci made of himself"],[135,227.72,1.75,"at different stages of his life."],[135,229.85,4.25,"He started with all of the 120 portraits of males"],[135,234.1,3.21,"that Leonardo is thought to have made and asks:"],[135,237.48,2.42,"were any of these self-portraits?"],[135,240.44,5.09,"Then, like a detective eliminating suspects, Woldhek cut them down."],[135,246.24,2.25,"Finally, only three remained."],[135,249.04,3.21,"The remaining three portraits are of men of different ages"],[135,252.25,1.88,"and painted at different times,"],[135,254.12,2.63,"but they all share the same facial features."],[135,257.09,2.92,"And they all match the facial features of a statue—"],[135,260.21,4.3,"the only known image of da Vinci made by someone else."],[135,264.97,2.54,"What makes the detective story persuasive"],[135,267.51,3.25,"is that we feel as if we have gone on the same learning journey"],[135,270.77,1.13,"as the speaker."],[135,272.02,1.58,"It can sometimes be hard"],[135,273.6,3.71,"to make reason-based talks really exciting to listen to."],[135,277.9,2.25,"Most people can be convinced by reason,"],[135,280.23,2.5,"but it doesn't always leave them feeling energized."],[135,282.82,3.0,"So if you plan to use reason to persuade your audience,"],[135,285.82,2.63,"you may have to pick up some other tools as well:"],[135,289.12,3.54,"1. Inject some humor early on."],[135,293.46,4.09,"This says you're going to do some tough thinking, but we're going to have fun."],[135,298.08,2.04,"2. Add an anecdote."],[135,300.38,3.04,"You could tell an anecdote that shows how you became interested"],[135,303.42,1.38,"in the topic you've chosen."],[135,305.51,5.42,"3. Offer vivid examples that ignite the audience’s imagination."],[135,311.72,3.17,"4. Include third-party validation."],[135,315.19,3.5,"Pull in the opinions of other people who also believe your idea."],[135,319.36,4.13,"Persuasive talks that use reason may be harder for an audience to understand"],[135,323.49,1.17,"than other talks,"],[135,324.65,2.0,"and they may not be the most popular."],[135,327.07,3.29,"However, they are extremely important."],[135,330.62,2.33,"A strong, well-reasoned argument,"],[135,332.95,3.29,"even if it isn't immediately accepted by everyone,"],[135,336.42,4.46,"will gradually attract supporters until it becomes unstoppable."],[136,5.5,2.64,"I want to invite you to a foreign land."],[136,9.34,3.5,"A place where imaginary characters communicate to dress,"],[136,12.88,1.83,"to dance and movement."],[136,15.11,4.0,"They are living in an ever-changing opera, with costumes,"],[136,19.15,3.44,"with backdrops and fun and laughter."],[136,23.59,6.11,"I have been developing this idea [for] the last 17 years"],[136,29.73,2.57,"on the border of art and technology."],[136,33.03,3.47,"It's a dance revue, a place called Szauderland."],[136,36.5,1.6,"This is where I live."],[136,46.41,3.2,"At the beginning, I almost became an art historian."],[136,49.65,3.1,"That was the renaissance and the surrealism"],[136,52.78,3.14,"that made the greatest impression on me."],[136,55.95,4.14,"I love the organic quality of the renaissance,"],[136,60.09,2.6,"and I love my wife and my daughter,"],[136,62.69,3.2,"but I feel most connected to surrealism."],[136,66.23,3.0,"Then came the machines and the programming."],[136,69.27,2.97,"I explored the beautiful world of the robots."],[136,72.77,1.74,"I tried many things."],[136,74.54,3.4,"I created once a drawing robot"],[136,77.97,4.17,"to record the abstract imprint of the pandemic"],[136,82.18,5.27,"and an augmented reality deepfake Leonardo da Vinci machine,"],[136,87.48,2.6,"which even fooled a Leonardo expert."],[136,98.9,6.17,"Until one morning, three years ago,"],[136,105.07,4.67,"I found an image online on the social media that froze me."],[136,110.57,4.0,"I couldn't even figure out how it was made,"],[136,114.58,4.37,"but I was so captivated by its complexity."],[136,130.39,5.11,"When I started to work with AI, I tried all sorts of things."],[136,135.9,4.1,"Like a child unwrapping presents at Christmas"],[136,140.0,2.23,"and awaiting for the results."],[136,160.26,3.44,"For me, working with AI is like cooking."],[136,163.73,2.94,"There is always an idea stirring in my head."],[136,166.66,2.4,"Right now you see the modern Babel."],[136,169.9,4.17,"Then I start to collect patterns, capture photos."],[136,174.54,5.57,"And when I have everything ready, I throw all the ingredients into the pot."],[136,181.34,3.7,"And when it's boiling, which is the AI process itself,"],[136,185.08,4.64,"it often needs some seasoning because there is too much of one style"],[136,189.75,1.94,"or too little of one color."],[136,192.92,1.53,"Then comes the post-production."],[136,194.49,1.67,"That's my favorite part."],[136,196.19,1.33,"The plating."],[136,198.0,2.64,"When I can clear all the details"],[136,200.66,3.1,"and I can give my personal signature."],[136,215.48,2.97,"So my work is always starting with an idea."],[136,218.48,5.24,"Right now, you see my recent moving from one country to another country."],[136,224.59,3.5,"Then I start to use artificial intelligence"],[136,228.12,4.54,"to create a kind of surreal world where I can feel comfortable."],[136,235.0,4.0,"Then I start to populate it with my virtual Earthlings,"],[136,239.04,2.77,"which is obviously a very, very selfish thing"],[136,241.81,2.97,"because these characters are a bit like me."],[136,245.18,2.7,"The one is trying to keep the balance."],[136,247.88,5.04,"The one is hiding somewhere, but trying to observe closely."],[136,252.95,3.94,"The one can wear any kind of dress on the street."],[136,256.89,2.4,"And the one can dance freely."],[136,271.5,4.6,"I make my virtual Earthlings dance"],[136,276.11,3.37,"because I love to dance."],[136,279.51,1.64,"Dancing is good,"],[136,281.14,2.47,"although I never knew how to do that."],[136,283.65,2.2,"And that frustrates me terribly."],[136,287.08,4.5,"I even passed my slightly clumsy movements onto my character, you see."],[136,293.42,1.8,"It will help one of us."],[136,295.26,1.6,"Like in a therapy session."],[136,327.36,2.5,"I found fashion always fascinating."],[136,330.63,5.34,"I see as a kind of costume, a uniform, what we put on every day."],[136,337.2,3.5,"But in my land, in Szauderland,"],[136,340.7,3.24,"I created the freedom from that exactly."],[136,343.97,5.64,"These characters there are wearing truly extraordinary clothes."],[136,349.65,2.24,"Like a kind of luxury collection."],[136,352.72,2.1,"Would you wear it too, I see it in your eyes."],[136,354.85,1.57,"We can talk about it later."],[136,363.39,3.74,"My exploration with AI allowed me to build a new world."],[136,367.56,3.87,"But this wasn't really AI [who] designed this place."],[136,372.57,3.07,"I found it as a beautiful tool,"],[136,375.67,4.47,"but I feel sorry for its lack of humor,"],[136,380.18,1.8,"of beauty and joy."],[136,385.62,2.4,"That's roughly the secret of Szauderland."],[136,388.58,3.5,"An idea, a place filled with characters."],[136,392.12,1.57,"They won't let me go."],[136,394.09,3.44,"I need to choreograph them out of myself."],[136,397.96,1.67,"Then the show begins,"],[136,399.63,2.5,"my inner theater turns outward."],[136,402.83,2.13,"But I'm happy to say"],[136,405.0,6.24,"that such a world can only come from a human person's imagination."],[136,411.94,1.47,"Thank you very much."],[137,3.87,2.97,"Sal Khan: Well great to have you here, Tekedra."],[137,6.87,1.94,"Tekedra Mawakana: It's great to be here."],[137,8.84,1.3,"Thank you so much."],[137,10.14,2.87,"SK: Well I thought before we start, because there was,"],[137,13.01,2.27,"it seemed like, about 40 percent of the audience"],[137,15.28,2.3,"who had never had that experience,"],[137,17.61,2.37,"I thought I would share some of our family videos"],[137,20.02,3.44,"of our first Waymo ride with my two sons."],[137,23.49,3.4,"I also have a daughter, but we kind of split up into two cars."],[137,26.92,2.04,"But if we could play the video."],[137,28.96,1.74,"(Video) Yeah, yeah. Start the ride."],[137,30.73,1.3,"Yeah, yeah."],[137,32.46,1.4,"Son 1: Alright, here we go."],[137,33.86,1.2,"Waymo: Hello from Waymo."],[137,35.1,1.97,"Son 2: \"Hello from ...\" oh, my ..."],[137,38.37,1.17,"Son 1: This is cool."],[137,40.4,1.33,"Son 2: Oh, my gosh."],[137,42.07,3.14,"Waymo: If you're traveling with little ones or expecting,"],[137,45.24,1.27,"please see the --"],[137,46.51,1.53,"SK: Are you expecting?"],[137,49.35,1.9,"We're at the beach,"],[137,51.28,1.74,"being driven by a ghost."],[137,54.48,1.17,"Waymo: Almost there."],[137,55.69,2.67,"Don't forget your phone, keys or wallet."],[137,58.89,3.17,"SK: And sons, don't forget your sons."],[137,64.69,1.2,"TM: That's great."],[137,65.93,1.4,"SK: So Tekedra, what’s next?"],[137,67.36,1.84,"I mean, every time I look at the news,"],[137,69.2,2.44,"I see a new city, I see new models."],[137,71.67,1.2,"What's new?"],[137,72.9,3.54,"TM: So we are currently operating in 11 cities."],[137,76.77,2.44,"In the US,"],[137,79.24,3.84,"we're providing 500,000 trips per week."],[137,83.61,2.1,"And we have a goal, by the end of the year,"],[137,85.72,2.27,"to be at a million trips per week."],[137,88.62,4.77,"And just to sort of help people wrap their minds around what that means,"],[137,93.42,1.8,"or how to think about it,"],[137,95.26,4.34,"our fleet of vehicles is about 3,000 cars ..."],[137,99.6,3.4,"The single Waymo driver"],[137,103.03,4.17,"drives over four million miles per week."],[137,107.84,1.9,"And so the way to think about that"],[137,109.74,4.87,"is a human drives around 700,000 miles in a lifetime --"],[137,114.64,3.5,"a human who lives sort of the full lifetime --"],[137,118.18,4.94,"and so this is like six lifetimes of human driving per week."],[137,123.62,2.5,"And so we're, you know, really excited."],[137,126.12,2.44,"We're also in four airports right now."],[137,128.56,2.87,"You can fly in to San Antonio or Phoenix,"],[137,131.46,2.3,"San Francisco or San Jose"],[137,133.8,2.84,"and jump in a Waymo, use the Waymo app."],[137,136.67,2.4,"It'll take you from point A to point B."],[137,139.1,2.77,"And we're at this point"],[137,141.91,4.54,"where the technology has moved from, “Will it work?”"],[137,146.48,4.74,"and “Can we produce the kind of safety outcomes that we care about?”"],[137,151.22,1.57,"to scaling."],[137,152.78,1.37,"And so we're really excited."],[137,154.19,2.97,"We are also in London, in Tokyo, [and]"],[137,157.19,3.97,"we've moved from manual driving to fully autonomous driving."],[137,161.19,2.44,"We still have people behind the wheel,"],[137,163.66,2.13,"so we haven’t launched a service yet,"],[137,165.83,2.94,"but those will be our first two international markets."],[137,168.77,1.74,"SK: What’s the rate-limiting factor:"],[137,170.53,3.37,"is it some notion of safety, or is it regulation?"],[137,173.94,1.17,"What is it?"],[137,175.14,1.2,"TM: It really depends."],[137,176.37,2.87,"In certain markets, it’s absolutely regulation."],[137,179.28,4.71,"I think when you think about the evolution of this technology as a whole,"],[137,183.98,3.5,"it's been making sure that we could have the safety outcomes that we want."],[137,187.52,2.1,"And all of that is the technology."],[137,189.65,3.6,"Can the driver produce these kinds of outcomes?"],[137,193.29,4.17,"We’ve driven over 200 million miles in our time"],[137,197.46,4.1,"and based on 170 million miles of data ..."],[137,201.6,3.67,"we are now seeing [a] 13x reduction"],[137,205.3,3.6,"in serious-injury-causing crashes over a human,"],[137,208.94,2.44,"and the same amount of reduction"],[137,211.41,2.8,"as it relates to injuries with pedestrians."],[137,214.25,4.57,"So we can now say that we are just over 10 times safer than a human"],[137,218.85,2.3,"at 170 million miles."],[137,221.19,3.9,"That’s the kind of superhuman performance"],[137,225.12,1.33,"that we were seeking"],[137,226.49,2.8,"in order to then be able to scale this technology."],[137,229.33,2.94,"Now we have markets that are closed, markets that are open,"],[137,232.3,2.47,"and we have to do all of the work with the cities,"],[137,234.8,2.77,"with the regulators, first responders, everyone,"],[137,237.6,1.87,"in order to advance our technology."],[137,239.5,2.14,"So we're laser-focused on doing that."],[137,241.67,4.4,"And the reality is, you know, [for] citizens, consumers, riders --"],[137,246.11,3.7,"those who’ve been cut out of the mobility systems and cities --"],[137,249.85,3.7,"they're demanding and requesting and advocating for us to come."],[137,253.58,3.24,"And so that’s a really exciting inflection point."],[137,257.15,2.54,"SK: It’s worth triply underlining what you just said:"],[137,259.72,2.74,"over 10 times safer, at least based on the current data."],[137,262.46,1.44,"We were talking earlier,"],[137,263.93,4.67,"y'all have already driven the equivalent of 300 lifetimes --"],[137,269.23,1.87,"TM: Two hundred and forty lifetimes."],[137,271.6,4.6,"SK: And Waymo has not caused a death,"],[137,276.24,2.04,"knock on wood."],[137,278.27,1.33,"And there’s an argument:"],[137,279.61,2.5,"roughly 40,000 people in America, every year,"],[137,282.15,1.9,"who die of a car accident."],[137,284.05,3.54,"I think in the world it’s on the order of a million every year."],[137,287.62,3.7,"So in theory, if this became everywhere,"],[137,291.36,2.44,"you could be saving 900,000 lives globally."],[137,293.82,3.64,"You could be saving 30, 35,000 to 36,000 lives"],[137,297.49,1.3,"just in the United States."],[137,298.8,1.33,"Why isn't there more --"],[137,300.16,1.97,"I know you all are growing maybe as fast,"],[137,302.13,2.0,"but why not roll out as fast as possible?"],[137,304.13,2.6,"What's stopping that from happening?"],[137,306.77,4.87,"TM: I think we are now focused on rolling out safely"],[137,311.68,1.5,"as quickly as possible."],[137,313.21,3.1,"There is an adoption curve that has to happen, though, right?"],[137,316.35,4.24,"I think what you’re saying is what undergirds our entire company,"],[137,320.58,4.07,"which is: safety is urgent, saving lives."],[137,324.69,2.84,"If this technology can perform at this level,"],[137,327.56,2.84,"then we have a responsibility to figure out how to do it."],[137,330.43,2.97,"Doesn't mean, though, that we're not being met with opposition."],[137,333.43,5.14,"And so we also have the burden of making those policymakers,"],[137,338.6,2.57,"who are not welcoming us with open arms"],[137,341.2,2.4,"... to demonstrate our safety record."],[137,343.61,1.44,"So we have a safety hub."],[137,345.07,2.24,"We make all of this data transparent, [and]"],[137,347.34,3.0,"we hand it to researchers so they can make their arguments."],[137,350.38,1.97,"And so it's been really fun lately"],[137,352.35,3.14,"to watch different sectors of the economy"],[137,355.52,3.77,"start to talk about how powerful this technology is."],[137,359.32,3.2,"You know, we have Dr. John Slotkin, a neurosurgeon,"],[137,362.56,5.71,"who has just calculated that $900 billion to $1.25 trillion"],[137,368.3,1.47,"could be saved"],[137,369.8,2.87,"if our technology was rolled out,"],[137,372.7,2.2,"if every car on the road was a Waymo."],[137,375.24,1.47,"But this is the problem:"],[137,377.27,1.5,"people are really comfortable"],[137,378.81,2.64,"with the number of people who die on the roads."],[137,381.48,2.54,"40,000 people dead,"],[137,384.01,2.3,"1.2 million globally"],[137,386.35,3.0,"and avoidable in many cases,"],[137,389.35,4.84,"isn't something upon which we share collective outrage"],[137,394.19,2.37,"or even moderate discomfort."],[137,397.03,4.57,"And so we have to introduce sort of that reality"],[137,401.63,2.47,"in order for the technology to be viewed"],[137,404.13,4.44,"as solving a problem that society currently faces,"],[137,408.61,1.87,"because we've just accepted it."],[137,410.51,1.17,"Why?"],[137,411.71,4.67,"Because we don't experience these as 737s falling out of the sky every day."],[137,416.88,1.37,"But that's what it would be"],[137,418.28,3.37,"if we actually experienced it as mass casualties."],[137,421.65,2.4,"We experience it, you know, [as] this person’s mom,"],[137,424.05,2.04,"my uncle."],[137,426.12,1.37,"And so because of that,"],[137,427.49,1.8,"we have to remind people"],[137,429.33,2.67,"that the status quo is totally unacceptable."],[137,432.03,1.97,"And it's even more unacceptable"],[137,434.03,2.7,"when you have technology that could help address it."],[137,436.77,1.17,"SK: Yes, definitely."],[137,439.07,3.44,"I think it was a couple of months ago -- you would know it much better --"],[137,442.51,2.44,"I saw a headline, it was a very clickbaity headline:"],[137,444.94,3.37,"“Waymo hits an eight-year-old outside of a school.”"],[137,448.35,1.37,"And I was like, oh my God."],[137,450.11,1.57,"And I click on it."],[137,451.71,1.83,"And then they described what happened,"],[137,453.58,4.97,"and it was outside of a school and there was a car parked."],[137,458.59,2.13,"And if I remember correctly, there was a kid,"],[137,460.72,1.57,"he just darted into the street."],[137,462.29,1.3,"And the Waymo,"],[137,463.59,4.67,"everyone who was observing that assumed the kid was going to die"],[137,468.3,2.9,"because he just darted into a street where traffic was coming."],[137,471.2,2.4,"And the Waymo, I don't know what the reflexes are,"],[137,473.6,1.47,"you probably know the numbers,"],[137,475.11,1.9,"it just immediately came to a stop,"],[137,477.01,2.67,"and it hit him at like, four miles an hour or something."],[137,479.68,1.97,"Like he literally just fell on his knees,"],[137,481.68,2.5,"brushed it off and looked at the Waymo kind of weird."],[137,484.18,1.94,"Why isn't --"],[137,486.15,2.0,"You know, it's convenient."],[137,488.19,2.5,"I mean, you feel like you're in the future."],[137,491.49,1.3,"Why is it the safety --"],[137,492.79,1.7,"I mean, it feels like that message,"],[137,494.49,2.54,"Is that something that y'all might want to lead with?"],[137,497.06,2.6,"Because that seems like such a clear value proposition."],[137,501.0,1.43,"TM: Yeah, I mean, you know,"],[137,502.47,4.57,"I think what you're saying is how comfortable are humans"],[137,507.07,4.44,"with this technology being better drivers than humans?"],[137,511.51,1.97,"You know, it goes back to the status quo."],[137,514.08,3.87,"People don't generally view themselves or their neighbors or their friends"],[137,517.98,2.64,"or everyone they know as unsafe drivers,"],[137,520.65,2.84,"even when they're distracted, drunk, tired and angry."],[137,523.52,3.3,"We just kind of accept that that's the human condition."],[137,526.86,4.37,"And so I think, you know, in the case of the child in Santa Monica ..."],[137,531.26,4.61,"our car was traveling 17 miles per hour."],[137,536.83,3.47,"And we were able to get to six miles per hour"],[137,540.34,2.13,"before making contact."],[137,542.51,2.0,"Obviously, we want to make no contact,"],[137,544.54,3.74,"but we know that was superhuman performance"],[137,548.28,3.17,"and as so many of the bystanders said after observing it --"],[137,551.45,3.64,"“The child would not have survived” -- is what we were told."],[137,555.09,1.64,"We weren't obviously there."],[137,557.19,4.14,"Those moments actually cause entire communities"],[137,561.36,2.67,"to think differently about this technology."],[137,564.06,3.84,"And so, you know, to your point: it’s clickbaity."],[137,567.9,1.17,"And on the other hand,"],[137,569.1,3.67,"it actually demonstrates what superhuman performance is."],[137,572.8,1.64,"SK: Yeah, I couldn't agree more."],[137,574.44,2.17,"I actually do assume most of the people I know"],[137,576.61,1.17,"are not good drivers."],[137,577.81,1.74,"I'm the biggest backseat driver."],[137,580.24,1.67,"The economics of the industry,"],[137,581.95,1.27,"y'all are the first mover."],[137,583.21,2.54,"Obviously there's a Tesla robotaxi."],[137,585.78,2.14,"They've taken some different approaches."],[137,587.95,2.27,"You have many other competitors."],[137,590.25,1.33,"Amazon's coming into it."],[137,591.62,2.3,"How are you thinking about the competitive space?"],[137,593.96,2.84,"What do you think is going to happen to the economics of it?"],[137,596.83,2.87,"It's already cost competitive with a typical rideshare drive."],[137,599.73,2.37,"TM: Yeah, I think, you know, right now for us,"],[137,602.13,3.27,"we're the only company operating at this scale."],[137,605.43,4.14,"We think it’s really important to just stay laser-focused on that."],[137,609.61,1.87,"You know the way to think about it,"],[137,611.51,5.11,"because everyone’s calling everything either autonomous or robotaxi,"],[137,618.35,5.24,"is if you do not need a driver’s license to be in the car by yourself,"],[137,623.62,3.7,"which is for many, many people who do not have a driver's license,"],[137,627.36,2.4,"it’s their first time ever in life --"],[137,629.79,2.37,"and so those are very special moments --"],[137,632.2,2.0,"but if you do not need a driver’s license"],[137,634.2,2.27,"to be in a Waymo to get from point A to point B,"],[137,636.5,1.47,"that is fully autonomous,"],[137,637.97,2.33,"[or] what is referred to, in the geeky sense,"],[137,640.3,2.34,"[as] level four and level five."],[137,642.67,2.0,"If it is anything less than that --"],[137,644.71,1.97,"if you have to be behind the wheel,"],[137,646.71,2.94,"if you have to take over when there's a beep,"],[137,649.68,2.04,"when there’s anything --"],[137,651.75,3.34,"that's level three or level two or level one."],[137,655.09,5.27,"And it's really important for people to internalize this distinction,"],[137,660.39,2.54,"because what we found back in 2012,"],[137,662.96,3.64,"when we had sort of a supercruise capability,"],[137,666.63,1.77,"the Waymo driver was at that ability,"],[137,668.43,2.74,"[was that] we could go from ramp to ramp, no problem."],[137,671.2,1.74,"But when we got to surface streets,"],[137,672.97,2.3,"you needed to pay attention."],[137,675.27,2.27,"And we told employees this is early days:"],[137,677.57,2.94,"“When we go from ramp to ramp, we have a camera in the car” --"],[137,680.54,1.6,"this was just for employees --"],[137,682.18,2.07,"\"we need you to pay attention.\""],[137,684.55,3.47,"And people immediately unplugged from the driving task,"],[137,688.05,3.17,"and they started shaving and curling their eyelashes,"],[137,691.25,3.87,"and they were like, picking up things from the backseat and plugging things in."],[137,695.16,3.47,"And so that's when we decided, you know,"],[137,698.66,2.6,"cars have gotten safer over the years, right?"],[137,701.3,1.77,"They've gotten airbags and seat belts"],[137,703.1,3.07,"and all of these things as a result of a lot of deaths."],[137,706.77,2.67,"But what hasn't gotten safer is the human."],[137,709.91,4.14,"And so we decided we're patching around the problem."],[137,714.08,3.94,"Actually, the way to make sure that this multi-ton vehicle"],[137,718.01,5.27,"is not as dangerous is to actually let humans be humans."],[137,723.32,2.44,"Be distracted, make your phone call,"],[137,725.79,1.7,"do the thing you want to be doing."],[137,727.52,2.5,"Let the Waymo driver get you from point A to point B."],[137,730.06,2.8,"And so when you ask me about competition,"],[137,732.9,2.37,"I think there are a host of companies"],[137,735.26,3.27,"that are in the level-two-plus and level three."],[137,739.1,1.7,"They need an attentive human"],[137,740.84,3.64,"after inviting the human to be inattentive,"],[137,744.51,2.97,"to be the most attentive at the moment"],[137,747.51,3.5,"when being most attentive is least convenient."],[137,751.01,2.54,"And so to me, that is not --"],[137,753.58,2.57,"that is still patching around the problem."],[137,756.19,3.77,"Doesn't mean it won't result in safer driving when you are attentive,"],[137,759.99,2.94,"but it isn't actually level four autonomy."],[137,762.96,1.27,"SK: Yeah, yeah."],[137,764.23,2.37,"I mean, if you extrapolate all of this,"],[137,766.63,3.2,"how much do you all think about what the world is going to look"],[137,769.87,1.27,"like just because of this?"],[137,771.13,2.84,"Maybe just because of you all and some of the other players,"],[137,774.0,1.97,"10 [to] 15 years, what will it look like?"],[137,775.97,2.47,"You mentioned, if you don't have a driver's license,"],[137,778.48,3.74,"could this be a replacement in some ways for traditional public transportation,"],[137,782.21,1.37,"how are our roads different,"],[137,783.58,3.2,"are parking lots going to turn into parks -- what’s going to happen?"],[137,786.78,2.87,"TM: Yeah, we’ve partnered, from the early days, with transit."],[137,789.65,1.83,"We think it's a really interesting way"],[137,791.49,2.67,"to take some of the congestion out of the city,"],[137,794.19,2.94,"have sort of the kiss-and-ride from the suburbs in,"],[137,797.16,2.0,"have people take Waymo rides."],[137,799.46,3.3,"We've gotten really favorable sort of reviews in the reports"],[137,802.8,1.27,"that have been done there."],[137,804.07,1.9,"We've also partnered with people"],[137,806.0,3.04,"who take public transit in LA."],[137,809.07,3.0,"You know, we've offered discounts if you do public transit"],[137,812.11,1.74,"to a Waymo or vice versa."],[137,813.88,2.0,"You know, we want to think about the ways"],[137,815.91,3.34,"that this could become part of that fabric"],[137,819.25,1.67,"because we think it's important"],[137,820.92,3.24,"that this is also very accessible to a lot of people."],[137,824.19,1.47,"So that's one."],[137,825.69,2.3,"But when we think about 10 to 15 years from now,"],[137,828.02,5.0,"it's really fun to think about parts of cities that would get recaptured,"],[137,833.06,3.67,"you know, space right now where cars are just sitting all day,"],[137,836.77,2.17,"people are at work, the cars are just sitting."],[137,838.97,1.97,"Most of those parking lots"],[137,840.97,2.13,"in major cities around the world"],[137,843.14,2.0,"are central."],[137,845.17,2.4,"And so the idea that that could get recaptured --"],[137,847.61,4.27,"there are city planners who've reached out to us to try to understand,"],[137,851.91,2.17,"you know, do we have to make these investments"],[137,854.08,1.5,"for the next 10 or 15 years,"],[137,855.62,4.1,"or can we start imagining a world where parking structures could move out,"],[137,859.72,1.3,"parks could come back [and]"],[137,861.02,3.04,"community life could sort of come back to the center of a city."],[137,864.09,1.77,"So that's a vision."],[137,865.86,1.27,"I mean, who knows, right?"],[137,867.16,3.77,"I think some of this is as the technology rolls out more and more,"],[137,870.97,3.8,"I think people's imagination actually starts to get sparked"],[137,874.8,1.64,"in ways that are very exciting."],[137,876.44,4.0,"I said to you before, it's like, in some ways people go from, you know,"],[137,880.48,2.87,"is it safe, kind of to your poll, right?"],[137,883.38,1.6,"Is it safe, should I get in?"],[137,885.51,1.27,"OK, I got in."],[137,886.82,1.57,"It's kind of boring and amazing."],[137,888.42,1.24,"I really like it."],[137,889.69,1.9,"Oh, this is kind of not a big deal."],[137,891.62,3.27,"Now I'm going to be the coolest person I know in my friend group."],[137,894.92,2.4,"And then all of a sudden, it's like, oh,"],[137,897.36,2.64,"what could I do in this thing if it got really exciting?"],[137,900.03,4.1,"So I get pitched on like, you should make it a yoga studio"],[137,904.17,1.2,"you know, on wheels."],[137,905.4,1.87,"SK: I'm glad that was what was pitched."],[137,907.27,2.47,"I thought this was going in a whole other direction."],[137,909.74,2.84,"TM: This is my point, is everyone kind of starts to imagine,"],[137,912.61,2.0,"what do I want to do instead of commuting?"],[137,914.64,1.17,"So that's the fun part."],[137,915.85,3.64,"SK: I had a very G-rated thought in a Waymo on the highway on 101,"],[137,919.52,1.67,"and then I remembered."],[137,921.18,1.77,"But it would have been embarrassing."],[137,922.99,2.8,"And then I remembered, you do have a camera without sound,"],[137,925.79,2.04,"so I didn't even act on my G-rated thought."],[137,929.33,2.74,"TM: Just to be clear, I do not have a camera."],[137,932.1,3.14,"SK: No, no, I was just going to do some grooming type of thing."],[137,936.77,1.17,"Jobs."],[137,937.97,4.44,"I don't see this as something that's your responsibility in totality,"],[137,942.4,2.97,"but my uncle drives an Uber in New Orleans"],[137,945.41,3.97,"and I believe that one of the largest jobs for men globally"],[137,949.38,2.74,"and not just men -- obviously women also drive vehicles --"],[137,952.12,2.7,"but definitely for men is driving some form of a vehicle,"],[137,954.82,3.47,"taxis, rideshares, long-haul trucking, etc."],[137,958.89,1.47,"What happens?"],[137,960.36,3.87,"TM: Yeah, I think, you know, I'll answer the question in a few ways."],[137,964.26,2.14,"My uncle was actually a truck driver too,"],[137,966.43,2.64,"and that job was really important to him."],[137,969.1,3.07,"And for better or for worse,"],[137,972.17,2.0,"it was also a really hard job."],[137,974.74,3.6,"And so I think there’s an opportunity,"],[137,978.38,3.87,"and it’s going to take a really thoughtful approach, right?"],[137,982.28,3.44,"Because, I mean, I'm sitting here talking about building"],[137,985.75,2.67,"the world's most trusted driver."],[137,988.45,2.87,"And so it's a really tough place for me to sit in"],[137,991.35,3.64,"and [to] facilitate the dialogue around it."],[137,995.02,1.83,"However, over time,"],[137,996.89,5.67,"what I’m starting to see just from us is [that] there are new jobs being created."],[137,1002.6,2.8,"So we have running our fleet,"],[137,1005.43,3.14,"we have fleet technicians who we need,"],[137,1008.61,5.61,"fleet operators as well as smart depot professionals."],[137,1014.24,2.4,"All of that didn't exist ten years ago."],[137,1016.68,3.0,"These are like AV-focused jobs."],[137,1019.72,1.17,"One."],[137,1020.92,2.64,"Two, jobs that exist today, everyday drivers,"],[137,1023.55,1.8,"we have to hire them too,"],[137,1025.36,2.3,"in order to validate our software."],[137,1027.69,3.2,"We have to accumulate miles in order to validate our software."],[137,1030.93,2.27,"So we hire drivers constantly,"],[137,1033.23,3.97,"or our partners hire drivers in different parts of the world."],[137,1037.23,3.47,"We also need people who can, you know, take care of our depots,"],[137,1040.7,3.17,"build our depots, EV charging infrastructure."],[137,1043.91,2.0,"And so what we’ve started to see,"],[137,1045.91,2.4,"as we have these partnerships of fleet operators,"],[137,1048.31,4.8,"is they are driving a lot of job growth as we scale."],[137,1053.48,4.27,"We’re just too early to be able to really quantify it."],[137,1057.75,2.6,"But in addition to that, earlier this year,"],[137,1060.39,2.84,"we did a partnership with TechForce Foundation"],[137,1063.26,1.97,"where we’re helping"],[137,1065.26,2.5,"people who are in school to be mechanics,"],[137,1067.8,3.2,"to actually become mechanics of autonomous vehicles,"],[137,1071.03,3.94,"because we think that transition doesn't have to pass anyone by."],[137,1075.0,2.14,"We just need to be mindful about it."],[137,1077.17,1.57,"So that's one thing we're doing."],[137,1078.74,3.14,"And then in addition, we are doing apprenticeship programs."],[137,1081.91,1.57,"We’re doing one in LA,"],[137,1084.15,3.27,"and we’re doing curriculum development with [a] community college:"],[137,1087.45,3.14,"Bronx Community College, City University of New York,"],[137,1090.62,2.64,"because again, there's an opportunity for people"],[137,1093.29,2.17,"to learn the skills that are needed."],[137,1095.49,4.24,"And then I think while there's a lot of focus on sort of the jobs,"],[137,1099.76,4.94,"there are also small businesses in these places where we’re launching"],[137,1104.73,2.14,"where because Waymo has launched,"],[137,1106.87,2.13,"like in San Francisco, our first year,"],[137,1109.04,3.37,"we actually drove $40 million of economic development"],[137,1112.41,3.1,"for local businesses from out-of-towners"],[137,1115.51,4.2,"because we were the number-one destination for tourists to try out a Waymo"],[137,1119.75,2.67,"and then to go to a coffee shop and go to a restaurant."],[137,1122.45,2.94,"And so I think this is a long conversation."],[137,1125.92,3.97,"I appreciate you for saying, you know, we're one part of it."],[137,1129.93,1.17,"We take this seriously."],[137,1131.13,2.5,"We think it's important for us to be focused on it."],[137,1133.66,2.5,"And we're also really excited when we get to go"],[137,1136.2,3.07,"and talk to folks as they move from logistics"],[137,1139.27,2.6,"into now AV-adjacent roles"],[137,1141.9,1.87,"with their current skill set."],[137,1143.81,2.2,"So it doesn't all require upskilling."],[137,1146.41,3.4,"SK: Well, we could talk for hours about this, but thank you so much."],[137,1149.85,1.3,"TM: Thank you."],[138,7.13,2.21,"As dawn settles on the Amazon,"],[138,9.34,5.17,"a bat opens wide and vomits blood into its companion's mouth."],[138,14.8,2.88,"And it’s not the first time they’ve shared a meal."],[138,18.06,4.38,"Vampire bats are the only mammals to subsist on blood alone."],[138,22.77,5.38,"Their ancient ancestors might have eaten parasites and insects off larger animals"],[138,28.32,5.34,"till vampire bats began feeding directly from those larger animals themselves."],[138,33.86,2.54,"There are three vampire bat species,"],[138,36.49,4.67,"all of which inhabit the warmer regions of Central and South America."],[138,41.33,5.5,"Two of the three species— hairy-legged and white-winged vampire bats—"],[138,46.92,2.08,"more often prey on birds,"],[138,49.05,3.79,"quietly creeping up tree branches while they roost."],[138,53.05,6.13,"But the most abundant vampire bat species is the aptly named common vampire bat."],[138,59.39,3.59,"Following the nighttime routine of one such bat,"],[138,63.02,3.09,"we see that she sets out in pitch black,"],[138,66.23,4.25,"mapping her surroundings with ultrasonic sound waves."],[138,70.69,3.8,"Common vampire bats are opportunistic hunters"],[138,74.49,5.0,"that have been observed to feed from snakes, sea lions, and pelicans,"],[138,79.49,3.71,"but they mostly take their meals from land mammals."],[138,83.87,6.13,"And with the spread of agricultural areas, they increasingly target livestock."],[138,90.34,2.63,"They've also been known to prey on humans,"],[138,92.96,3.5,"which carries dangers beyond just their bite."],[138,96.72,4.75,"Bats are highly effective at dodging infections themselves,"],[138,101.47,2.71,"but they still carry pathogens."],[138,104.93,3.09,"Vampire bats can transmit the rabies virus,"],[138,108.02,4.21,"making it all the more important that people living around them are protected,"],[138,112.23,4.46,"with measures like bat-proof sleeping shelters and vaccines."],[138,117.32,4.96,"This night, the vampire bat homes in on a dozing pig."],[138,122.41,6.59,"She cautiously swoops down and hops around to assess the situation's riskiness."],[138,129.21,3.46,"With powerful, long forelimbs and thumbs,"],[138,132.67,3.42,"she can stand, jump, and even run on all fours."],[138,136.34,4.46,"In fact, the vampire bats’ forelimb-powered bounding gait"],[138,140.8,2.54,"is wholly unique among animals."],[138,143.76,3.71,"She uses specialized heat-sensing receptors"],[138,147.48,4.67,"to detect the warm blood running beneath the skin’s surface."],[138,152.52,3.96,"And she finds a spot where she’s less likely to get caught,"],[138,156.49,3.17,"protecting herself from possible reprisal—"],[138,159.86,3.21,"and from starvation as she races the clock."],[138,163.24,6.05,"She delicately pierces the pig’s skin with her curved, razor-sharp incisors."],[138,169.5,3.38,"They have less protective enamel than most mammal teeth,"],[138,173.0,4.21,"and the friction between the upper incisors and lower canines"],[138,177.34,3.04,"helps keep them particularly pointy."],[138,180.76,5.67,"In fact, vampire bats may have the animal kingdom’s sharpest incisors."],[138,186.81,4.46,"Her saliva also contains a cocktail of proteins"],[138,191.31,3.25,"secreted from six glands in her neck."],[138,194.77,4.46,"It keeps the blood flowing by preventing platelets from clumping"],[138,199.24,2.75,"and plasma proteins from turning solid,"],[138,202.2,6.05,"while also dilating the pig’s vessels and dissolving existing blood clots."],[138,208.54,2.34,"She laps the flowing blood,"],[138,211.0,5.42,"the notch in her lower jaw shunting the liquid into two channels"],[138,216.5,1.5,"underneath her tongue."],[138,218.55,2.79,"While blood is relatively nutrient poor,"],[138,221.59,3.5,"consisting mostly of water and some protein,"],[138,225.35,3.79,"her body and microbiome eke out what it offers."],[138,229.43,2.42,"But she also has a fast metabolism"],[138,231.98,2.88,"and needs to replenish her energy stores quickly."],[138,234.98,5.09,"A common vampire bat will perish without food for more than two nights."],[138,240.36,3.21,"Ideally, she would stay stealth long enough"],[138,243.57,4.21,"to take in a little over a tablespoon of the pig’s blood."],[138,248.03,2.25,"Then she’d be in good shape."],[138,250.41,3.63,"But the pig starts suddenly, throwing her off."],[138,254.17,2.09,"Dodging its trampling hooves,"],[138,256.25,4.38,"the vampire bat propels herself back into the night air."],[138,260.84,5.13,"She tries to feed again, but each interruption puts her at greater risk."],[138,266.22,2.29,"Thankfully, she doesn't live alone,"],[138,268.81,2.59,"and even though it's a considerable sacrifice,"],[138,271.39,2.88,"one of her companions throws her a lifeline—"],[138,274.56,6.05,"coughing up the hard-won results of her own high-stakes hunting heist"],[138,280.74,2.13,"and making it dinner for two."],[138,283.15,1.5,"This isn’t uncommon."],[138,284.74,3.79,"Vampire bats under two years old tend to miss a meal"],[138,288.62,4.05,"as much as once every three nights as they learn the ropes."],[138,292.79,6.17,"So, their colonies rely on these rather extreme acts of cooperation to survive;"],[138,299.13,4.71,"without them, it’s thought their mortality rates would increase dramatically."],[138,304.18,6.71,"Vampire bat colonies are networks of intimate grooming and food-sharing bonds—"],[138,310.93,3.42,"that go beyond blood, so to speak."],[138,314.48,4.42,"A strong history of reciprocal food sharing and grooming"],[138,318.98,3.21,"can be even more important than relatedness"],[138,322.28,3.71,"when a vampire bat is deciding whether to donate a meal"],[138,326.2,2.46,"or respond to a contact call."],[138,328.82,4.42,"So, thanks to her community, the bat survives,"],[138,333.54,4.25,"primed to regurgitate the favor in the future."],[139,6.96,3.34,"Of the many bewildering behaviors cats display,"],[139,10.38,4.96,"one of the strangest is their obsession with a specific species of plant."],[139,15.93,3.67,"After just one whiff, even the most stoic cat"],[139,19.64,3.38,"can start pawing, drooling, biting, and wriggling"],[139,23.02,3.29,"in a state of pure feline euphoria."],[139,27.23,3.13,"So, why do cats go crazy for catnip?"],[139,31.57,4.5,"This is the question cat behavior expert Masao Miyazaki"],[139,36.07,5.38,"and chemist Toshio Nishikawa set out to answer in 2013."],[139,41.91,4.17,"Along with their research teams from Iwate and Nagoya University,"],[139,46.08,2.5,"they began by studying silvervine—"],[139,48.75,3.29,"a plant that sparks a similar response to catnip."],[139,52.84,4.05,"First, the researchers painstakingly extracted chemical compounds"],[139,56.89,1.13,"from the plant"],[139,58.01,4.0,"and dribbled various combinations of them onto filter papers."],[139,62.43,4.5,"Then they brought in some cats and studied which compounds they were drawn to."],[139,67.52,4.13,"Overwhelmingly, their feline testers pounced on the papers"],[139,71.65,2.0,"containing nepetalactol."],[139,73.9,3.29,"And when Miyazaki and his student Reiko Uenoyama"],[139,77.2,3.96,"ran blood tests on the cats who’d interacted with nepetalactol,"],[139,81.16,3.8,"they found their systems were flooded with endorphins."],[139,85.12,3.25,"These hormones block pain signals, relieve stress,"],[139,88.38,3.09,"and generally create a happy, calming effect."],[139,91.67,2.92,"And catnip triggers the same endorphin rush"],[139,94.59,2.96,"with a similar chemical called nepetalactone,"],[139,98.05,5.3,"and it’s this flood of happy hormones that gives catnip and silvervine"],[139,103.35,2.84,"their signature drug-like response."],[139,107.23,3.46,"These compounds don't just trigger this effect in small cats."],[139,110.98,3.5,"When the researchers brought nepetalactol-treated filter papers"],[139,114.48,1.33,"to a few zoos,"],[139,115.82,5.21,"leopards, lynxes, and jaguars all dove face first into the compound."],[139,121.45,1.67,"This was big news."],[139,123.24,4.59,"If all these different cat species reacted to nepetalactol in the same way,"],[139,128.04,3.75,"the response was very likely a shared evolutionary trait—"],[139,132.0,2.63,"potentially something important to cat survival"],[139,134.63,2.75,"that stretched back millions of years."],[139,138.05,3.38,"When the silvervine researchers presented their work at a conference,"],[139,141.43,3.46,"one evolutionary biologist raised a compelling theory."],[139,145.39,1.13,"In chemical terms,"],[139,146.52,5.96,"both nepetalactol and nepetalactone are classified as an iridoid—"],[139,152.65,4.46,"a type of natural compound known to contain insect-repelling properties."],[139,157.32,4.21,"So perhaps cats rubbing their faces in silvervine and catnip"],[139,161.62,3.25,"were applying an ancient form of bug spray."],[139,165.12,3.42,"To test this, researchers set up cages of mosquitoes"],[139,168.54,2.21,"that cats could stick their heads into."],[139,170.92,3.46,"And sure enough, the cats that had been treated with nepetalactol"],[139,174.38,3.63,"got fewer mosquito bites than the cats in the control group."],[139,178.26,4.13,"The same effect was true for the scientists who’d volunteered their arms"],[139,182.39,3.0,"in solidarity with their feline subjects."],[139,185.64,4.59,"These mosquito-repelling properties are currently our best explanation"],[139,190.23,2.92,"for why cats love silvervine and catnip."],[139,193.27,3.09,"But researchers still had one more question:"],[139,196.86,1.96,"in their state of euphoria,"],[139,198.82,4.5,"cats tend to vigorously bite, lick, and rub against these plants."],[139,203.62,4.55,"But do cats really need to go this crazy to get their bug repelling effects?"],[139,209.41,3.42,"To investigate rubbing, they provided cats with filter papers"],[139,212.83,3.21,"treated with microscopic amounts of nepetalactol."],[139,216.13,4.88,"And sure enough, even these tiny doses made the papers irresistible,"],[139,221.01,4.13,"suggesting that microscopic amounts of bug repellent can be transferred"],[139,225.14,1.5,"by rubbing alone."],[139,226.81,4.84,"As for biting and licking, the researchers found that when cats damage the plant,"],[139,231.69,4.42,"the leaves actually produce more mosquito-repellent chemicals."],[139,236.23,4.55,"So your cat isn’t just ripping open their bags of catnip to be annoying,"],[139,240.94,3.71,"their evolutionary instincts are just trying to get even more"],[139,244.66,2.0,"of that bug protection."],[139,247.24,4.67,"Cats are far from the only animal to use natural plants for purposes like this."],[139,252.08,4.88,"Apes swallow scratchy and hairy leaves to dislodge parasites from their guts."],[139,257.17,4.5,"Sheep munch on tannin-rich plants to kill intestinal worms,"],[139,261.92,3.17,"and monarch butterflies use toxic milkweed"],[139,265.09,3.25,"to kill parasites that normally impair their flight."],[139,268.93,3.09,"Just like cats’ ability to find mosquito repellent,"],[139,272.1,4.09,"these behaviors can help humans identify useful plants and ingredients"],[139,276.19,1.46,"in the natural world."],[139,277.98,3.63,"So, the next time you see your feline friend on catnip binge,"],[139,281.69,2.54,"don’t worry— it’s only medicinal."],[140,4.13,2.3,"Let's take a look at this clay tablet."],[140,6.9,1.6,"It might not look like much,"],[140,8.54,3.0,"but it's actually some of the oldest mathematics we have."],[140,11.57,4.04,"It’s a 4,000-year-old message in a bottle from ancient Babylon --"],[140,15.65,2.54,"a precursor to the quadratic equation."],[140,18.68,1.53,"And for four millennia,"],[140,20.22,2.9,"people have been doing math basically the same way."],[140,23.69,2.17,"Someone will have a brilliant idea,"],[140,25.89,3.64,"they’ll write it down, and their peers will discuss and check it."],[140,30.26,5.57,"It’s a process built on creativity, communication and, most importantly,"],[140,35.87,1.6,"trust between people."],[140,37.84,4.84,"And what might seem like a humble or simple process is anything but."],[140,42.71,1.84,"It's not just been successful."],[140,44.58,4.17,"It's been, as the physicist Eugene Wigner famously put it,"],[140,48.75,1.87,"unreasonably effective."],[140,50.65,3.94,"Wigner was pondering and trying to unravel a deep mystery."],[140,54.59,3.34,"Why should the abstract, creative,"],[140,57.95,3.94,"and often bizarre ideas that spring from a mathematician's imagination"],[140,61.93,4.37,"so often be the perfect language with which we understand the universe?"],[140,66.33,3.0,"Why should the strange laws of non-Euclidean geometry,"],[140,69.37,2.74,"which were originally conceived of as a thought experiment"],[140,72.1,1.4,"in the 19th century,"],[140,73.54,1.77,"turn out to be the exact mathematics"],[140,75.34,2.34,"that Einstein needed for general relativity?"],[140,77.71,2.7,"Why should the esoteric math of group theory,"],[140,80.44,3.84,"which was originally designed to study the abstract nature of symmetry,"],[140,84.31,3.94,"be fundamental to understanding everything from particle physics"],[140,88.28,1.64,"to the patterns in crystals?"],[140,90.55,3.67,"Well, there's no logical reason it has to be this way."],[140,94.83,2.27,"This strange connection"],[140,97.13,4.04,"between pure mathematical thought and the real world"],[140,101.2,3.57,"has actually been the invisible engine driving human progress."],[140,105.97,3.84,"Every piece of technology that defines our lives"],[140,109.84,2.17,"was ignited with a mathematical spark."],[140,112.44,2.17,"If you take the device in your phone,"],[140,114.64,3.24,"its brain is based on the quantum mechanics of semiconductors."],[140,117.92,2.54,"And that's a theory built on linear algebra"],[140,120.48,1.74,"and complex numbers."],[140,122.25,2.54,"The wireless signals that get data to it,"],[140,124.79,3.47,"they're just a concrete manifestation of Maxwell's equations."],[140,128.59,4.61,"And finally, the security that protects your data online"],[140,133.23,1.87,"is based on number theory,"],[140,135.13,3.54,"which for a long time was truly considered the most pure"],[140,138.7,3.37,"and least applicable possible branch of mathematics."],[140,142.81,3.6,"And now it safeguards trillions of dollars in the global economy."],[140,147.01,1.6,"And now we come to AI."],[140,149.58,4.17,"Modern AI is not just built with math, it's forged from it."],[140,154.22,4.07,"A neural network is just a monumental structure of applied mathematics."],[140,158.32,1.7,"And when AIs learn,"],[140,160.06,2.27,"they're using the tools of calculus"],[140,162.36,2.3,"to navigate vast landscapes of possibilities"],[140,164.69,1.74,"with billions of dimensions."],[140,166.93,2.13,"So AI is, in its soul,"],[140,169.1,3.4,"a mathematical idea that's given life through computation."],[140,173.74,5.04,"So we agree that math is the foundation that modern civilization is based on."],[140,179.61,3.2,"But that foundation is starting to show some signs of strain."],[140,183.21,2.8,"The very process of human-led discovery"],[140,186.05,3.17,"that's gotten us to this point is nearing a breaking point,"],[140,189.25,2.13,"buckling under the weight of its own success."],[140,191.42,3.8,"And now AI, which is one of mathematics’ greatest creations,"],[140,195.26,2.17,"is accelerating us towards that breaking point"],[140,197.46,1.9,"faster than the world's ready for."],[140,199.4,2.0,"So let's just look at some evidence."],[140,201.97,1.9,"Consider the Poincaré conjecture."],[140,204.34,1.77,"This is a legendary problem."],[140,206.14,1.3,"It's a fundamental question"],[140,207.47,2.64,"about the nature of three-dimensional shapes"],[140,210.14,2.74,"originally posed in 1904."],[140,212.91,1.97,"And for nearly a century,"],[140,214.91,3.34,"it stood as an unconquered Everest of mathematics."],[140,218.85,2.0,"Until in 2002,"],[140,220.88,2.1,"a Russian mathematician working in isolation"],[140,222.99,1.7,"named Grigori Perelman"],[140,224.72,3.6,"posted a series of three short, cryptic papers online."],[140,228.32,2.3,"He didn’t bother submitting them to a journal --"],[140,230.63,2.3,"he just put them on the internet and walked away."],[140,232.93,2.74,"His fellow mathematicians had to stop what they were doing"],[140,235.7,1.74,"and try to decipher it."],[140,237.43,5.07,"And several teams working independently of the best of colleges in the world,"],[140,242.51,3.27,"took the next four years to try to unpack the arguments,"],[140,245.81,1.97,"fill in the logical gaps"],[140,247.81,2.77,"and eventually, at the end, after they really reviewed it,"],[140,250.58,1.37,"declare that yes, he did it."],[140,251.98,1.97,"He proved the Poincaré conjecture."],[140,254.38,1.27,"But that's interesting"],[140,255.69,4.3,"because it took one person to write a proof"],[140,260.02,4.37,"and a global, multi-year intellectual mobilization to check it."],[140,265.43,2.9,"And that's in the best case, when the proof is correct."],[140,269.03,3.14,"Consider Andrew Wiles's proof of Fermat's Last Theorem."],[140,272.2,4.71,"With the electrifying announcement in 1993 in Cambridge, the world celebrated."],[140,276.94,3.27,"But during the peer-review process, deep in it,"],[140,280.21,2.37,"a single thread was found out of place"],[140,282.61,2.57,"in that magnificent tapestry of a proof,"],[140,285.21,1.64,"and when we started to pull on it,"],[140,286.88,1.4,"the proof started to unravel."],[140,288.29,1.74,"And this wasn't a small mistake."],[140,290.59,5.41,"Andrew Wiles and his collaborator Richard Taylor took two years of heroic,"],[140,296.03,2.14,"secret effort to try to fix it."],[140,298.5,3.94,"And that effort included some insights that Andrew Wiles said"],[140,302.47,2.04,"were among the most important in his life."],[140,305.07,2.5,"And that's before we throw AI into the mix."],[140,308.71,1.43,"Two short years ago,"],[140,310.17,5.04,"AI could barely solve entry-level high school math-contest problems."],[140,315.25,2.44,"They were very clever, but brittle."],[140,318.58,2.17,"Now, in 2025,"],[140,320.75,1.74,"they can compete with the best of us"],[140,322.52,2.07,"at the International Math Olympiad,"],[140,324.62,2.8,"which is the premier precollege math competition."],[140,328.26,2.2,"But the interesting bit is the following."],[140,330.89,5.11,"The AI might work for four hours and produce a purported solution,"],[140,336.03,4.27,"which takes an expert human mathematician maybe up to an hour to check."],[140,341.34,2.97,"And we all know the exponential trend that AI is on."],[140,344.34,4.74,"So we can expect it’s not going to be one proof in an afternoon --"],[140,349.11,2.13,"it’s going to be a thousand pretty soon."],[140,351.81,3.37,"And they're not going to be attempts to solve math-contest problems."],[140,355.22,2.4,"They're going to be attacks on the most fundamental"],[140,357.65,2.04,"and important questions of the day,"],[140,359.72,1.77,"whether it's the Riemann hypothesis,"],[140,361.49,2.67,"Navier-Stokes or P versus NP,"],[140,364.16,1.57,"just to pick a few."],[140,365.76,2.9,"We simply don't have the human bandwidth"],[140,368.7,1.7,"to review all these proofs."],[140,370.43,2.1,"There's only a couple thousand mathematicians"],[140,372.57,2.94,"that are qualified to do it, and they already have day jobs."],[140,375.54,2.27,"And it's not just a verification bottleneck."],[140,377.84,2.3,"The very process by which we train these AIs"],[140,380.18,1.97,"is taking the data off the internet,"],[140,382.18,1.37,"which is from humans,"],[140,383.55,2.3,"post-training them with human feedback,"],[140,385.88,2.64,"and so we're essentially baking in the cognitive biases"],[140,388.55,4.4,"and the flawed reasoning of humans into these future engines of discovery."],[140,392.99,3.17,"So the conclusion is in some sense obvious."],[140,396.19,3.4,"Humans are becoming the bottleneck of verification for AI."],[140,400.1,2.54,"And now the question is, where does that leave us?"],[140,402.67,3.6,"Is this the end of the road for reliable mathematical discovery?"],[140,406.3,2.87,"Are we resigned to drowning in a sea of unverified claims"],[140,409.21,2.37,"where we can't really tell truth from fiction?"],[140,411.61,2.1,"And are we about to squander the opportunity"],[140,413.74,1.94,"for AI to revolutionize math?"],[140,415.98,1.87,"Well, the good news is no."],[140,418.31,1.27,"But it does mean it's time"],[140,419.62,3.3,"to upgrade the 4,000-year-old operating system of math,"],[140,422.92,5.67,"and move away from the imprecise and ambiguous nature of human language,"],[140,428.59,3.14,"and towards a language that computers can understand."],[140,432.5,2.97,"The solution is formal mathematics."],[140,436.83,2.5,"But before I tell you how this futuristic idea works,"],[140,439.37,3.77,"we should first recognize that it has a deep and fascinating history"],[140,443.17,2.8,"dating back to the 17th century,"],[140,446.01,3.9,"where a mathematician actually laid out the road map with stunning foresight."],[140,450.31,1.67,"Four hundred years ago,"],[140,452.01,3.84,"in a Europe torn by religious and political conflict,"],[140,455.89,3.57,"a polymath named Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz"],[140,459.46,2.34,"had a vision of breathtaking ambition."],[140,462.63,3.97,"He was a contemporary of Newton and a cocreator of calculus,"],[140,466.63,1.83,"but his dreams went far beyond that."],[140,469.23,3.6,"He dreamed of something called a universal characteristic,"],[140,472.87,3.57,"which was a system for perfectly encoding all scientific"],[140,476.47,1.6,"and philosophical thought."],[140,478.68,2.27,"And the system had three parts."],[140,481.58,3.27,"First, you need a perfect logical language."],[140,485.75,4.17,"Second, you need a grand encyclopedia written in language"],[140,489.95,2.54,"that contains all verified human thought."],[140,493.19,1.23,"And third,"],[140,494.46,1.74,"and this is the masterstroke,"],[140,496.23,2.67,"you need a so-called engine of reason,"],[140,498.93,1.44,"a system of mechanical rules"],[140,500.36,4.24,"by which you can automatically derive new facts from that library"],[140,504.63,3.0,"as surely as a calculator performs arithmetic."],[140,507.67,3.74,"Now, Leibniz thought this would revolutionize humanity."],[140,511.74,1.6,"With a system like this,"],[140,513.38,2.07,"if two people had an intellectual conflict,"],[140,515.48,2.74,"they would resort to logic and not rhetoric to resolve it."],[140,518.22,1.4,"They would simply sit down,"],[140,519.65,2.97,"say “calculemus” -- “let us calculate,”"],[140,522.62,1.7,"and get to the bottom of it."],[140,524.35,3.9,"In some sense, it was meant to be a universal calculator for truth."],[140,528.86,2.54,"Now, Leibniz was a bit of an optimist."],[140,531.39,3.7,"He thought this would take a small group of people five years to build,"],[140,535.13,2.8,"and he was off by several centuries."],[140,538.47,2.2,"But what I think is really remarkable"],[140,540.67,1.74,"is that in 2025,"],[140,542.41,2.37,"truly for the first time in history,"],[140,544.81,3.64,"it's actually possible to realize this philosopher's dream."],[140,548.48,1.17,"So what do we need?"],[140,549.68,3.04,"Well, we need a perfect, logical language."],[140,552.75,1.4,"Turns out we've got it."],[140,554.18,1.57,"It's called Lean."],[140,555.79,2.07,"Lean is a programming language,"],[140,557.89,2.8,"but it's also what's known as a proof assistant."],[140,560.72,2.4,"You can think of it as a programming environment"],[140,563.16,1.87,"for mathematical proofs,"],[140,565.03,1.9,"where it doesn't just give you feedback"],[140,566.96,2.0,"if you have a syntax error here or there --"],[140,568.97,2.97,"it's actually looking at the core of the mathematical argument"],[140,571.97,2.74,"and telling you if you have any problems anywhere in it."],[140,574.74,1.24,"Great."],[140,575.97,1.57,"What's the second thing we need?"],[140,577.57,1.6,"We need the grand encyclopedia."],[140,579.51,2.0,"Well, the good news is we've got that too."],[140,581.54,1.7,"It's called Mathlib."],[140,583.28,2.13,"Mathlib is an open-source project."],[140,585.45,3.27,"It's about two million lines of code in Lean,"],[140,588.72,4.04,"and it covers a lot of the undergraduate and graduate math curriculum."],[140,593.36,4.1,"You can think of it like a Wikipedia for proven truth,"],[140,597.49,4.0,"where every edit is computationally certified for correctness."],[140,602.33,1.8,"OK, we've got the language,"],[140,604.13,1.7,"we've got the encyclopedia,"],[140,605.84,1.94,"what about the engine of reason?"],[140,608.14,2.47,"Well, we could try to have humans do it,"],[140,610.64,2.13,"but you've got to write a lot of Lean code"],[140,612.81,3.27,"and the level of robotic precision you need to write a formal proof"],[140,616.11,3.6,"is not something that human creativity is so well suited for."],[140,619.75,2.4,"And that's how we've come full-circle."],[140,622.15,4.1,"It turns out that AI is the key to making this whole thing work."],[140,626.69,3.4,"In the future, AI is not just going to be writing math papers in English"],[140,630.13,1.53,"for humans to read."],[140,631.7,2.64,"They're going to be writing math proofs in Lean"],[140,634.36,1.57,"for computers to check."],[140,637.0,2.77,"And that is the fundamental key"],[140,639.8,3.0,"that makes it possible to use Leibniz's vision"],[140,642.84,3.07,"to unlock the full potential of AI in mathematics."],[140,646.78,3.8,"Because when a math AI spits out a proof in Lean of, let's say,"],[140,650.58,1.74,"the Riemann hypothesis,"],[140,652.35,3.94,"we're not going to need humans to go through every single line of the proof"],[140,656.32,1.33,"in painstaking detail,"],[140,657.69,1.23,"check every single case,"],[140,658.96,4.24,"and understand the possibly strange and alien logic of the proof"],[140,663.23,1.4,"just to see if it's correct."],[140,664.63,3.17,"Instead, all we're going to do is we're going to take those files,"],[140,667.8,2.94,"we're going to give them to a Lean compiler, and if it builds,"],[140,670.77,2.3,"we can know with absolute certainty it's correct."],[140,673.07,4.37,"And this is what fundamentally alters our relationship with AI."],[140,678.04,2.37,"AI can now become a true collaborator,"],[140,680.44,3.7,"one whose word we don't have to take on blind faith."],[140,684.58,4.97,"We get to trade in the tedium of checking for the creative joy of discovery."],[140,690.42,3.17,"Humans get to use our intuition and judgment,"],[140,693.62,1.44,"we ask the questions,"],[140,695.09,3.57,"we chart the course, we propose the brilliant conjectures"],[140,698.7,4.61,"and then we delegate to AI to explore the vast oceans of logic,"],[140,703.33,1.3,"to find the correct answer,"],[140,704.67,3.14,"and then a computer confirms that we've gotten to the destination."],[140,708.57,1.17,"And the amazing thing"],[140,709.77,2.84,"is that this isn't just some far-off science-fiction dream."],[140,712.64,3.04,"It turns out that at this year’s International Math Olympiad,"],[140,715.71,3.87,"automated systems were able to find solutions to five of the six problems"],[140,719.62,3.87,"in a way that computers could check and require no human review whatsoever."],[140,723.49,3.0,"And that’s enough to get a gold-medal-level performance."],[140,726.49,2.27,"So the transition is already happening."],[140,729.86,4.6,"So are humans going to be the bottleneck for math research?"],[140,735.03,3.74,"Well, the answer is yes, but only if we refuse to change."],[140,738.77,2.9,"Only if we insist on being the only thinkers"],[140,741.71,1.8,"and the only checkers."],[140,743.54,3.14,"But if we're able to realize this 400-year-old vision,"],[140,746.68,1.8,"we're not going to replace ourselves,"],[140,748.48,1.6,"we're going to elevate ourselves."],[140,750.08,2.3,"We're going to put ourselves in the driver's seat"],[140,752.41,2.74,"as the explorers, the architects and the question askers."],[140,755.15,4.1,"And that means that formal mathematics is the key to this new era of discovery,"],[140,759.29,4.3,"based on the powerful and essential partnership between human imagination"],[140,763.63,2.17,"and mathematical superintelligence."],[140,767.1,1.17,"Thank you."],[141,0.21,2.34,"[funky music beat] Are you tired of politics as usual?"],[141,2.54,2.25,"Democrat or Republican."],[141,4.8,2.84,"Are you looking for something…new?"],[141,7.72,1.21,"Well, you’re not alone."],[141,8.93,5.21,"As of 2025, more US voters identified as unaffiliated political"],[141,14.14,1.29,"“Independents” than as"],[141,15.43,2.13,"specifically Democrats or Republicans."],[141,17.56,2.25,"But wait, there’s more!"],[141,19.81,3.84,"Consider how many presidential elections a third party has won"],[141,23.65,2.46,"in the last 175 years!"],[141,26.11,2.25,"[record scratch – music stops] None?"],[141,28.36,1.63,"Wait, what do you mean none?"],[141,29.99,3.34,"As it turns out, nobody but a Democrat or a Republican"],[141,33.32,3.71,"has won a US presidential election since the mid-19th century,"],[141,37.04,1.25,"and even before that,"],[141,38.29,2.33,"even though the party names were different,"],[141,40.62,2.79,"the two party system was largely the same."],[141,43.42,3.04,"But with so many people who don’t identify with"],[141,46.46,2.38,"Democrats or Republicans,"],[141,48.84,3.38,"why do they still feel like the only two options?"],[141,52.22,3.67,"Hi, I’m Kousha Navidar, this Crash Course,"],[141,55.89,2.71,"and we’re answering Big Questions on U.S. Government."],[141,63.06,2.92,"See, even though we have other parties,"],[141,65.98,1.5,"the US is effectively"],[141,67.48,1.38,"a two-party system,"],[141,68.86,1.42,"where voters mostly choose"],[141,70.28,3.54,"between just two major-party candidates for national elections."],[141,73.82,2.71,"And that…is actually kind of weird."],[141,76.53,2.34,"Most other democracies don’t work like that."],[141,78.87,2.71,"All over the world, from Sweden to Uruguay,"],[141,81.58,4.09,"multiparty democracies are set up to allow multiple political parties"],[141,85.67,2.29,"to actually compete in national elections."],[141,87.96,3.38,"Heck, Kenya has 92 registered parties."],[141,91.34,3.92,"So in terms of global democracy, we are actually the odd ones out."],[141,95.26,2.17,"But it seems like we’d rather not be."],[141,97.43,4.96,"In 2025, 62% of people in the US thought the country needed"],[141,102.39,3.0,"a viable third party to represent the people’s interests."],[141,105.4,2.42,"But – and here’s where it gets even weirder –"],[141,107.82,2.88,"the number of people who said they’d be “very likely”"],[141,110.69,2.42,"to vote for a third-party candidate?"],[141,113.11,4.13,"It’s not 62%-- it’s only 15%."],[141,117.24,3.38,"I guess they just haven’t heard of the Anti-Arugula Party?"],[141,120.62,1.42,"We’re pro spinach!"],[141,122.04,1.38,"Down with Spicy Lettuce!"],[141,123.41,0.96,"So what’s up?"],[141,124.37,2.96,"Why is the US trapped in this two-party system"],[141,127.33,2.29,"when over half the country wants out?"],[141,129.67,2.38,"That dissonance is really interesting."],[141,132.05,3.29,"And to really unpack it, we can start by thinking"],[141,135.34,3.75,"about what the point of having “Democrats” and “Republicans”"],[141,139.1,2.08,"is supposed to be in the first place."],[141,141.18,2.71,"The United States is a representative democracy,"],[141,143.89,3.59,"which means it’s supposed to be ruled by, and reflect the interests of,"],[141,147.48,1.92,"the people who live here."],[141,149.4,5.46,"It’s also a country of over 340 million different people,"],[141,154.86,4.8,"who presumably have over 340 million different interests."],[141,159.66,2.71,"And we can’t all make the laws together."],[141,162.37,1.54,"Imagine that Zoom call."],[141,163.91,3.17,"And we can’t get much accomplished by ourselves either."],[141,167.08,3.54,"So instead, we organize into political parties that share"],[141,170.63,1.25,"some basic interests,"],[141,171.88,2.96,"and elect representatives who promise to more or less"],[141,174.84,1.54,"represent those interests."],[141,176.38,1.67,"We can understand political parties"],[141,178.05,4.17,"based on what kinds of interests and ideologies they fight for."],[141,182.22,3.59,"Sometimes political scientists put all those parties on a big chart,"],[141,185.81,1.67,"where one axis runs from"],[141,187.48,2.75,"“economic liberal” to “economic conservative,”"],[141,190.23,3.92,"or “more government involvement in the economic spending” to"],[141,194.15,2.21,"“less government involvement in the economy,”"],[141,196.36,4.67,"and the other axis runs from “social liberal” to “social conservative,”"],[141,201.03,3.17,"or “push to make changes for the common good”"],[141,204.2,2.25,"to “protect the status quo”."],[141,206.46,1.75,"So, mainstream Republicans"],[141,208.21,2.17,"would fall somewhere in the upper right quadrant,"],[141,210.38,3.5,"for “small government spending, big traditional values.”"],[141,213.88,1.21,"And mainstream Democrats"],[141,215.09,2.34,"would go somewhere in the lower left quadrant,"],[141,217.43,3.04,"for “more public funding and cultural progress.”"],[141,220.47,2.96,"Those two dots sit in opposing quadrants."],[141,223.43,1.67,"In multiparty democracies,"],[141,225.1,2.63,"other political parties might fill in the gaps between,"],[141,227.73,2.71,"and even extend out on either side."],[141,230.44,3.54,"Like, Sweden has eight major political parties across"],[141,233.98,2.17,"the social and economic axes,"],[141,236.15,3.75,"with the ultra-conservative Sweden Democrats way over here,"],[141,239.91,2.88,"and the socialist Left Party way over here,"],[141,242.78,3.5,"and the Social Democrats, Moderates, and Center Party"],[141,246.29,1.13,"filling in the middle."],[141,247.41,1.46,"But here in the US,"],[141,248.87,3.75,"we just have the two major parties."],[141,252.63,1.92,"Everyone else is a bit player."],[141,254.54,2.46,"Like, sure, we’ve got the Green Party,"],[141,257.01,2.96,"who are big on sustainability and social justice"],[141,259.97,2.5,"and would land even deeper in the lower left,"],[141,262.47,3.04,"and Libertarians who land in the lower right quadrant"],[141,265.51,3.0,"for economic conservatism and social liberalism."],[141,268.52,3.21,"We’ve also got some centrist options, like the Forward Party,"],[141,271.73,3.59,"who land closer to the, you guessed it, center."],[141,275.32,4.09,"Or even more niche parties, like the United States Pirate Party,"],[141,279.4,4.71,"whose platform, among other things, seeks to abolish patent rights."],[141,284.12,1.13,"They’re socially liberal,"],[141,285.24,4.0,"since their platform includes things like anti-bigotry and police reform."],[141,289.25,2.71,"While their economics are closer to the center."],[141,291.96,3.46,"So really, the US does have more than two parties"],[141,295.42,3.75,"to represent all our 340 million interests."],[141,299.17,2.96,"They just can’t seem to ever win an election."],[141,302.13,2.25,"Okay, that’s not totally true."],[141,304.39,3.13,"Sometimes they can get ahead in local and state elections,"],[141,307.51,3.96,"and once in a while, even snag a seat in Congress."],[141,311.48,1.38,"But even that is tricky –"],[141,312.85,2.17,"in the US, it’s usually easier"],[141,315.02,3.04,"to just glom onto one of the two major parties."],[141,318.07,1.42,"Like, Zohran Mamdani,"],[141,319.49,3.13,"who was elected Mayor of New York City in 2025,"],[141,322.61,3.84,"is a Democratic Socialist… but ran as a Democrat."],[141,326.45,3.46,"Or Justin Amash, a Libertarian who was a Michigan House"],[141,329.91,3.0,"rep for 10 years… ran as a Republican."],[141,332.92,2.75,"And of course, a third party hasn’t won the presidency"],[141,335.67,1.5,"– or even come close –"],[141,337.17,1.46,"in two centuries."],[141,338.63,2.96,"So we’re still left with a big question:"],[141,341.59,0.79,"why not?"],[141,342.38,4.05,"Well, according to political scientists, there are two explanations."],[141,346.47,3.42,"Explanation One: the institutional explanation."],[141,349.89,2.71,"It basically comes down to our elections:"],[141,352.6,2.79,"the way we choose political representatives in the US"],[141,355.4,3.71,"boxes out third parties and makes it very hard for anyone"],[141,359.11,2.38,"but a Democrat or Republican to win."],[141,361.49,3.21,"This explanation lays the blame at the feet of the system."],[141,364.7,3.75,"And there are a couple different facets of said system that support it."],[141,368.45,3.54,"Like, consider the fact that most US elections require"],[141,372.0,4.8,"just a plurality, rather than a majority, of votes to win."],[141,376.79,5.42,"So say nine out of ten candidates all get around 10 percent of the vote,"],[141,382.21,4.55,"but the tenth one gets 11 percent, they win the whole thing."],[141,386.76,3.38,"This is known as a first-past-the-post system,"],[141,390.14,2.54,"where whoever has the most votes wins,"],[141,392.68,2.96,"even if the majority of people didn’t vote for them."],[141,395.64,2.42,"Like, in this ten candidate scenario,"],[141,398.06,2.46,"the winner barely came out on top,"],[141,400.52,4.96,"leaving 89% of voters represented by someone they didn’t choose–"],[141,405.49,2.59,"an example of minority rule,"],[141,408.07,3.25,"where suddenly all voters are being represented by someone"],[141,411.33,2.5,"only a small fraction actually supported."],[141,413.83,2.46,"And pair this with the fact that the US,"],[141,416.29,3.38,"unlike Sweden and a lot of the countries we mentioned earlier,"],[141,419.67,2.63,"mostly votes in single-member districts,"],[141,422.3,4.0,"where the country is broken up into different geographic areas"],[141,426.3,4.0,"that are each represented by a single elected official."],[141,430.3,2.79,"This leads to a winner-takes-all situation,"],[141,433.1,3.84,"where the person who gets the most votes wins everything."],[141,436.94,2.08,"There’s no prize for coming in second."],[141,439.02,3.5,"Now, this doesn’t immediately disqualify a third-party candidate,"],[141,442.52,2.79,"but it does make things a lot harder for them,"],[141,445.32,1.29,"because of something called"],[141,446.61,1.21,"Duverger’s law. [with French accent]"],[141,447.82,1.5,"I did not know I had that in me."],[141,449.32,3.13,"Like, say you’re a voter in that ten-candidate election,"],[141,452.45,2.79,"throwing your hat in the ring for the Pirate Party candidate."],[141,455.25,2.5,"But one year, that candidate doesn’t do so hot."],[141,457.75,2.63,"Instead of the usual 10-ish percent of the vote,"],[141,460.38,1.46,"he only gets 7%,"],[141,461.84,2.92,"and it doesn’t look like he’s going to do much better next time."],[141,464.75,1.88,"You want the Pirate Party to win."],[141,466.63,1.58,"But what you want even more"],[141,468.22,3.5,"is to make sure the Copyright Party doesn’t win."],[141,471.72,1.79,"So instead of wasting your vote"],[141,473.51,3.25,"on a party that’s looking less and less competitive,"],[141,476.77,3.13,"you decide to give it to someone a little more mainstream,"],[141,479.9,2.25,"like the Net Neutrality Party."],[141,482.15,2.38,"They might not align with all of your values,"],[141,484.52,2.25,"but they do on some big issues."],[141,486.78,1.92,"And on the other side of the spectrum,"],[141,488.7,5.3,"discouraged members of the Hunt Copyright Violators for Sport Party"],[141,493.99,1.58,"are doing the same thing –"],[141,495.58,3.42,"jumping ship to a more popular, more moderate candidate"],[141,499.0,3.04,"to keep Net Neutrality out of office."],[141,502.04,3.21,"Until all of a sudden, that diverse political field"],[141,505.25,3.5,"is dominated by just two big parties –"],[141,508.76,3.71,"and voting for anyone else doesn’t really make any sense."],[141,512.47,5.17,"And while there’s no such thing as the Hunt Copyright Violators for Sport Party"],[141,517.64,0.88,"– unfortunately –"],[141,518.52,2.75,"this kind of thing actually happens in the real world,"],[141,521.27,2.54,"like when Green Party supporters vote Democratic,"],[141,523.81,2.42,"or Libertarians vote Republican."],[141,526.23,3.42,"Duverger’s law helps explain how single-member districts"],[141,529.65,4.8,"and winner-takes-all elections tend to weed out third-party candidates."],[141,534.45,2.79,"When US politics are so polarized,"],[141,537.25,3.75,"and every election is a bloody 1-v-1 knockout,"],[141,541.0,2.0,"voting your conscience can feel"],[141,543.0,3.71,"less important than keeping the wrong guy out of office."],[141,546.71,2.42,"This is called the spoiler effect,"],[141,549.13,3.8,"where voting for a third party just seems too risky."],[141,552.93,3.46,"We’d rather vote for the major-party candidate we kind of like,"],[141,556.39,2.79,"than risk “spoiling” the race, and handing it over"],[141,559.18,2.96,"to the other major party we really don’t like."],[141,562.14,1.33,"Which leaves a lot of us"],[141,563.48,3.46,"feeling forced to vote for the “lesser of two evils.”"],[141,566.94,3.04,"Not to be confused with the lesser of two weevils–"],[141,569.99,3.38,"check out SciShow or Bizarre Beasts for your insect content needs."],[141,573.36,1.67,"Those institutional factors"],[141,575.03,3.42,"help explain why less-popular third-party candidates"],[141,578.45,2.63,"can’t get a foothold in most of our elections."],[141,581.08,4.59,"But they don’t explain why the 45% of unaffiliated independents"],[141,585.67,4.09,"don’t just band together and make an actually competitive third party."],[141,589.75,2.46,"Remember how I said we had two explanations?"],[141,592.22,2.25,"Here’s where the second explanation comes in:"],[141,594.47,2.09,"the voter explanation."],[141,596.55,4.92,"See, the thing about independents is they’re, well, independent."],[141,601.48,2.96,"They hold a wide variety of political beliefs"],[141,604.44,2.33,"all across those ideological axes,"],[141,606.77,2.96,"and just because they’re all “independent” doesn’t mean"],[141,609.73,1.58,"they align with each other."],[141,611.32,3.04,"Like, lots of independents call themselves “centrists,”"],[141,614.36,3.54,"but seem to actually lean toward one major party over another–"],[141,617.91,3.25,"and by “lots” I mean “literally 78%”."],[141,621.16,1.46,"So in the voting booth,"],[141,622.62,3.5,"all these self-proclaimed independents are basically indistinguishable"],[141,626.12,4.0,"from those folks who call themselves Democrats or Republicans out loud."],[141,630.13,3.29,"And others might even be more liberal or conservative"],[141,633.42,1.13,"than the major parties,"],[141,634.55,4.13,"but choose to align with them anyway when they cast votes in elections."],[141,638.68,3.54,"Like the ultra-conservative Tea Party movement of the late 2000s,"],[141,642.22,1.75,"and today’s MAGA movement –"],[141,643.98,3.67,"both of which have done their business from within the Republican Party."],[141,647.65,3.21,"Then, of course, you have the folks who hold values and ideals"],[141,650.86,3.21,"that are totally outside of the ideological axes we’re working with,"],[141,654.07,3.42,"Like my neighbor, who believes the only money the US government"],[141,657.49,4.09,"should spend is on soundproofing his apartment so he doesn’t have to hear"],[141,661.58,4.46,"a very promising trumpet player practicing at 3 AM."],[141,666.04,4.3,"Excuse me for trying to keep the arts alive in this country, Robert."],[141,670.34,3.46,"[deep breath in] And the true neutral “independents?”"],[141,673.8,3.63,"They typically don’t have much interest in politics at all."],[141,677.43,1.21,"Nothing like being oblivious"],[141,678.64,2.38,"to give you some peace of mind in this country"],[141,681.01,1.75,"So in the end, trying to lump"],[141,682.76,2.67,"all those independents together in one party makes"],[141,685.43,1.67,"even a little less sense than trying"],[141,687.1,3.09,"to lump them into two competitive parties we already have."],[141,690.23,2.46,"All that to say, it's not ideal,"],[141,692.69,2.5,"but for now, it's the system we've got."],[141,695.19,2.79,"Partly because the system isn't really set up to be able"],[141,697.99,2.13,"to represent everyone's individual interests,"],[141,700.12,2.92,"and partly because people are worried that their interests"],[141,703.03,1.17,"wouldn't be represented"],[141,704.2,2.34,"even if they did vote for another party."],[141,706.54,3.04,"I don’t blame you if you feel like we’re stuck."],[141,709.58,3.59,"But there's nothing to say it has to be this way forever."],[141,713.17,2.34,"Other democracies do things differently,"],[141,715.51,2.38,"and with some reforms to our systems,"],[141,717.88,2.67,"like swapping away from single-member districts"],[141,720.55,2.46,"or adopting ranked choice voting,"],[141,723.01,1.84,"we could conceivably get"],[141,724.85,3.04,"some pretty competitive third-party action going."],[141,727.89,3.75,"But for now, politics in the US remains a zero-sum game"],[141,731.65,2.34,"between the Democrats and the Republicans."],[141,733.98,2.38,"When Republicans win, Democrats lose."],[141,736.36,2.59,"When Democrats win, Republicans lose."],[141,738.95,1.67,"And every time it seems,"],[141,740.61,3.67,"Independents end up picking the lesser of two weevils."],[141,744.28,1.46,"Speaking of things that are grubby,"],[141,745.75,1.04,"in our next episode,"],[141,746.79,1.92,"we’ll talk about campaign spending."],[141,748.71,1.04,"I’ll see ya then."],[141,749.75,2.42,"In the meantime, this one’s for you, Robert!"],[141,753.0,3.29,"This episode of Crash Course Big Questions on U.S. Government"],[141,756.3,2.71,"was produced in partnership with USAFacts,"],[141,759.01,2.17,"a non-partisan civic tech project"],[141,761.18,2.96,"that makes government data easier to understand."],[141,764.14,2.71,"For more resources that go along with these videos,"],[141,766.85,3.67,"go to usafacts.org/crashcourse."],[141,770.52,1.46,"Thanks for watching this episode of"],[141,771.98,2.46,"Crash Course Big Questions on U.S. Government,"],[141,774.44,2.17,"which was filmed in Indianapolis, Indiana"],[141,776.61,3.04,"and was made with the help of all these nice people."],[141,779.65,4.34,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[141,783.99,2.34,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[142,4.24,2.17,"As an artist, I want to tell you"],[142,6.4,4.27,"that AI has completely changed the trajectory of my life."],[142,10.71,1.9,"Let me show you into some of my worlds."],[142,15.01,3.17,"When some people were focused on productivity and efficiency,"],[142,18.21,6.01,"I'd been focused on trying to figure out how to incorporate AI into my art."],[142,24.25,5.94,"Like a new paintbrush, a canvas, a camera, a music studio,"],[142,30.19,3.0,"a doorway into my imagination."],[142,33.2,1.77,"I’ve worked in film production,"],[142,35.0,1.27,"fashion"],[142,36.3,1.97,"and most recently as a graphic designer."],[142,38.3,4.2,"But nothing has sparked my curiosity like AI has."],[142,43.24,3.17,"My first images were less “visionary artist”"],[142,46.41,2.3,"and more, as my friends used to say,"],[142,48.75,1.64,"\"What is that?\""],[142,50.41,2.94,"But I leaned into the strangeness of the AI,"],[142,53.38,2.7,"and now it has become my signature style."],[142,56.12,2.23,"And I feel like I have a new superpower,"],[142,58.39,3.1,"and I'm only limited by my imagination,"],[142,61.49,2.84,"which seems to be endlessly fueled with ideas now."],[142,65.43,3.54,"For the first time, the distance between what I imagined"],[142,69.0,2.34,"and what I could create has closed."],[142,73.17,2.0,"Once AI video was available,"],[142,75.21,3.67,"I started animating my AI images and making short films."],[142,79.68,2.13,"But I felt like something was missing."],[142,81.85,2.3,"So I wanted to make my own music"],[142,84.18,2.64,"to better match the emotion behind my visuals."],[142,87.58,4.34,"So I started writing lyrics and using AI to collaborate on the instrumentation."],[142,92.82,3.94,"And I draw my inspiration for my lyrics from my visuals"],[142,96.79,2.57,"or from my life experiences."],[142,99.36,4.04,"And I never thought that my music would take on a life of its own."],[142,103.43,3.14,"But it has, as I just landed a record deal."],[142,109.24,3.97,"Thank you. That feels extraordinary at this chapter of my life."],[142,114.84,3.0,"When the music and the visual align perfectly,"],[142,117.88,2.64,"it can feel like magic."],[142,120.55,3.67,"In this next clip I'm going to show you, from my video \"Quiet Rebellion\","],[142,124.25,3.67,"I wrote these lyrics about living as your authentic self"],[142,127.96,2.27,"and wearing your strange like a crown."],[142,130.26,3.87,"(Song) Let the rhythm be wide and the logic be thin."],[142,134.16,4.17,"I'm living in the spaces where the secrets begin."],[142,138.37,4.14,"It's a quiet rebellion, a soft kind of war."],[142,142.54,3.87,"There'll be somethin' the world hasn't tasted before."],[142,146.41,4.07,"There is a fire in the fold,"],[142,150.51,3.77,"a grace in the strange."],[142,154.28,3.94,"I am ink on the page"],[142,158.25,5.67,"that refuses to change."],[142,169.37,1.27,"Thank you. Thank you."],[142,173.47,3.17,"Lately, people have been asking me these same two questions:"],[142,176.67,3.17,"Is it really art and how do you do it?"],[142,179.88,2.34,"And this first one's really hard for me to answer,"],[142,182.25,1.4,"because AI art is brand new,"],[142,183.68,2.77,"and we're all still trying to figure out where it fits in."],[142,186.45,2.87,"And there [are] intense feelings on both sides,"],[142,189.32,3.87,"and lots of discussion about AI being trained on other artists’ work --"],[142,193.22,2.47,"as I was as an art major."],[142,195.69,1.5,"But when I sit down to create,"],[142,197.19,2.1,"there's no other artists' work on my screen."],[142,199.33,6.34,"I use references from things in nature, like flowers or insects,"],[142,205.7,2.74,"patterns, fashion, or whimsical ideas"],[142,208.47,2.54,"that have been floating around in my head."],[142,211.04,2.8,"For me, art is personal."],[142,213.88,2.97,"I just want to make things that bring me joy."],[142,216.88,2.17,"And if they do the same to someone else, well,"],[142,219.08,3.0,"maybe that's the definition right there."],[142,222.12,1.4,"And how do you do it?"],[142,223.52,2.2,"Let me take you behind the scenes for a moment."],[142,226.29,2.94,"This image was made in Midjourney using this prompt,"],[142,229.26,2.8,"\"A character in a fairy tale scene in a marketplace,"],[142,232.1,1.27,"enchanted and cinematic.\""],[142,233.36,3.0,"And it's an OK image. Very AI, nothing at all special."],[142,237.13,4.04,"Now here's that exact same prompt using my personalization code"],[142,241.21,1.94,"and the style reference code."],[142,243.17,3.07,"And I've worked really hard to get my images to look like this."],[142,246.24,2.8,"A lot of trial and error and time spent training the AI"],[142,249.08,2.27,"on my personal style and aesthetic."],[142,251.38,2.5,"Now, here's a short clip of the final video."],[142,253.92,4.67,"(Song) Welcome to the marketplace where nothing's ever priced the same."],[142,258.62,2.4,"You trade your time, you trade your name,"],[142,261.06,2.4,"you have to laugh."],[142,263.49,2.3,"The stalls are built from maybe-so,"],[142,265.83,2.44,"the cards all say \"you'll know, you'll know\"."],[142,268.3,2.5,"You buy a dream, it's buy-one-hope"],[142,270.83,2.24,"and something extra."],[142,274.37,1.27,"Thank you."],[142,277.37,5.34,"When AI is guided by human feeling, empathy and intention,"],[142,282.75,2.94,"it could become something else entirely."],[142,285.72,1.97,"You can't explain creativity."],[142,287.72,2.37,"You just follow it."],[142,290.12,1.57,"But this is what I do know."],[142,291.72,2.1,"I started making the best work of my life"],[142,293.82,2.87,"since I started using AI as a cocreator,"],[142,296.69,3.04,"and my life has taken off like a rocket ship."],[142,299.76,3.47,"Millions of people have found their way to my work through my social sites"],[142,303.27,1.5,"and music apps."],[142,304.8,5.71,"And I feel different, I feel lighter, I feel curious again,"],[142,310.54,1.87,"and I love using this technology"],[142,312.41,2.4,"to tell stories that are close to my heart."],[142,314.85,2.54,"This next clip is a tribute to older generations"],[142,317.41,2.04,"and not losing your sense of wonder."],[142,334.93,3.67,"(Song) The restless chase has drifted by,"],[142,338.63,4.0,"no longer racing for the prize."],[142,342.67,3.34,"Now we savor morning's sigh"],[142,346.04,3.7,"and see new wonder in old eyes."],[142,352.05,1.23,"Thank you."],[142,355.08,2.87,"I feel like we're standing at the beginning of a renaissance,"],[142,357.95,1.7,"and the possibilities are endless."],[142,360.59,3.77,"We haven't even tapped into all of the ways to use AI creatively."],[142,364.36,3.54,"And people who have never felt creative a day in their life"],[142,367.93,2.84,"are trying AI and sparking that creativity"],[142,370.8,1.64,"for the first time."],[142,372.47,1.37,"And that's incredible."],[142,374.74,3.94,"So my takeaway for today is if you are curious about AI,"],[142,378.71,2.67,"try to play a little and make something beautiful"],[142,381.41,2.04,"or make something strange,"],[142,383.45,2.67,"but let yourself be surprised by what comes out."],[142,386.12,1.87,"But most of all, find your joy."],[142,387.99,5.84,"(Song) I never found a reason why I had to learn to dance."],[142,395.12,2.37,"My feet and music found each other."],[142,398.69,2.27,"We knew it at first glance."],[142,402.33,2.2,"From the moment I could walk"],[142,405.87,3.7,"my feet just up and ran."],[142,409.57,3.17,"When I hear a song, I see a floor,"],[142,412.74,2.5,"my feet just understand."],[142,416.35,1.84,"Thank you so much. Thank you."],[143,6.59,4.75,"The Red Death had long devastated the country."],[143,12.18,2.13,"Blood was its Avatar."],[143,15.26,6.84,"There were sharp pains, sudden dizziness, then profuse bleeding at the pores."],[143,22.73,4.55,"The scarlet stains upon the body were the pest ban,"],[143,27.69,3.09,"which shut out aid and sympathy."],[143,32.95,4.05,"The whole progress and termination of the disease"],[143,37.16,3.29,"were the incidents of half an hour."],[143,42.0,2.84,"But Prince Prospero was happy."],[143,45.54,3.29,"When his dominions were half depopulated,"],[143,48.96,4.63,"he summoned a thousand hale and light-hearted friends"],[143,53.67,3.67,"from among the knights and dames of his court,"],[143,57.51,4.17,"and retired to one of his castellated abbeys."],[143,62.27,4.75,"A strong and lofty wall girdled it in."],[143,67.52,3.63,"The external world could take care of itself."],[143,71.53,5.21,"In the meantime, it was folly to grieve— or to think."],[143,77.57,5.05,"It was toward the fifth or sixth month of his seclusion"],[143,82.7,6.09,"that Prince Prospero entertained his thousand friends at a masked ball."],[143,90.46,2.13,"There were seven rooms."],[143,92.92,4.25,"In each, a Gothic window of stained glass"],[143,97.34,4.96,"whose color varied in accordance with the prevailing hue."],[143,102.68,4.96,"The seventh was closely shrouded in black velvet."],[143,107.81,6.34,"The panes were scarlet and produced so wild a look upon those who entered"],[143,114.4,5.17,"that there were few bold enough to set foot within its precincts."],[143,120.2,4.21,"It was in this apartment also that there stood"],[143,124.41,3.04,"a gigantic clock of ebony."],[143,127.87,3.38,"When its minute hand made the circuit of the face,"],[143,131.29,6.55,"there came forth a sound so peculiar that there was a brief disconcert."],[143,138.05,4.42,"But when the echo ceased, a light laughter pervaded the assembly."],[143,142.72,3.54,"The musicians smiled at their own nervousness."],[143,146.43,3.63,"And then, after the lapse of 60 minutes,"],[143,150.56,3.63,"the same tremulousness as before."],[143,154.94,4.5,"But, in spite of these things, it was a magnificent revel."],[143,159.78,2.67,"The tastes of the Duke were peculiar."],[143,162.82,3.21,"There are some who would have thought him mad."],[143,166.58,6.09,"There was much of the beautiful, wanton, bizarre, something of the terrible,"],[143,173.17,4.71,"and not a little of that which might have excited disgust."],[143,178.55,6.34,"To and fro in the seven chambers there stalked a multitude of dreams."],[143,185.22,2.08,"And these, the dreams—"],[143,187.43,4.88,"writhed in and about, taking hue from the rooms."],[143,192.98,5.05,"And, anon, there strikes the ebony clock,"],[143,198.19,3.84,"and momently, all is still."],[143,203.24,4.55,"And now again the music swells, and the dreams live,"],[143,207.79,3.75,"and writhe to and fro more merrily than ever."],[143,212.25,3.34,"But to the chamber which lies most westwardly,"],[143,215.79,2.67,"none of the maskers venture,"],[143,218.84,5.26,"for there flows a ruddier light through the blood-colored panes."],[143,224.89,4.17,"And to him whose foot falls upon the carpet,"],[143,229.35,4.21,"there comes from the clock a muffled peal"],[143,233.81,6.09,"more solemnly emphatic than any which reaches their ears"],[143,240.03,5.3,"who indulge in the more remote gaieties of the other apartments."],[143,246.78,4.8,"The revel went whirlingly on— until the twelfth hour."],[143,252.29,3.96,"There was an uneasy cessation of all things."],[143,256.92,2.75,"More of thought crept."],[143,259.88,6.8,"And many individuals in the crowd had become aware of a masked figure."],[143,267.26,3.38,"There arose a murmur, at first of surprise—"],[143,270.64,3.54,"then, finally, terror and disgust."],[143,274.35,6.09,"The figure was shrouded from head to foot in the habiliments of the grave."],[143,281.07,6.55,"The mask was made to resemble the countenance of a stiffened corpse."],[143,287.95,5.96,"The mummer had gone so far as to assume the type of the Red Death,"],[143,293.96,1.83,"dabbled in blood."],[143,296.25,4.75,"When the eyes of the Prince Prospero fell upon this spectral image,"],[143,301.13,2.92,"his brow reddened with rage."],[143,304.8,4.0,"“Who dares thus to make mockery of our woes?"],[143,309.01,5.8,"Uncase the varlet that we may know whom we have to hang tomorrow!”"],[143,315.48,3.88,"None put forth hand to seize him."],[143,319.44,3.67,"Unimpeded, he made his way through."],[143,323.28,4.38,"Prince Prospero, maddening with the shame of his own cowardice,"],[143,327.66,4.63,"bore aloft a drawn dagger, and approached the retreating figure,"],[143,332.29,4.71,"when the latter, having attained the extremity of the velvet apartment,"],[143,337.0,1.54,"turned suddenly."],[143,338.67,1.96,"There was a sharp cry—"],[143,340.8,1.63,"and the dagger dropped,"],[143,342.42,3.5,"upon which instantly fell Prince Prospero."],[143,346.63,1.63,"The revellers gasped,"],[143,348.47,5.05,"finding the grave cerements and corpse-like mask"],[143,353.81,3.88,"untenanted by any tangible form."],[143,359.06,2.42,"And now was acknowledged"],[143,361.73,2.59,"the presence of the Red Death."],[143,365.07,6.13,"One by one dropped the revellers in the blood-bedewed halls."],[143,371.57,4.96,"And the life of the ebony clock went out."],[143,377.16,2.75,"And the flames expired."],[143,380.42,4.67,"And Darkness and Decay and the Red Death"],[143,385.17,4.75,"held illimitable dominion over all."],[144,6.84,3.25,"On December 14th, 1799,"],[144,10.17,5.34,"former US President George Washington woke struggling for breath,"],[144,15.55,2.38,"his throat searing with pain."],[144,18.6,1.71,"He died later that day,"],[144,20.31,4.25,"from what’s suspected to have been a badly infected epiglottis,"],[144,24.73,4.84,"but not before he was prescribed an enema, steam therapy,"],[144,29.57,2.38,"and rigorous bloodletting."],[144,32.99,5.51,"During four bleeding sessions, Washington lost approximately two liters—"],[144,38.53,3.71,"roughly 40%— of his body’s blood supply."],[144,42.37,4.25,"And yet this wasn’t totally out of the ordinary for the time."],[144,46.96,3.0,"Bloodletting was a pervasive medical practice"],[144,49.96,3.8,"that dated at least as far back as ancient Greece."],[144,54.22,5.25,"Physicians like Hippocrates believed good health came from balance"],[144,59.47,3.92,"among what they called the body’s four humors:"],[144,63.68,4.96,"yellow bile, black bile, phlegm, and blood."],[144,69.15,4.3,"They attributed bad health to humoral imbalance,"],[144,73.57,5.8,"so treatments included purging, peeing, enemas, and bloodletting."],[144,80.28,4.55,"How people were bled varied with time, place, and practitioner."],[144,85.37,6.34,"Sometimes it was with sharp tools like quills, animal teeth, and metal blades;"],[144,92.09,3.79,"others, using leeches; or through wet cupping,"],[144,96.05,2.21,"which involved scraping the skin"],[144,98.3,4.21,"and drawing blood out with heated glass containers."],[144,102.81,1.29,"In the 2nd century,"],[144,104.1,6.09,"the prolific Greco-Roman physician Galen declared blood the most dominant humor"],[144,110.27,5.21,"and recommended bloodletting for just about every ailment imaginable."],[144,115.69,4.63,"The practice flowed into the medieval Islamic and European world,"],[144,120.41,4.34,"with physicians discussing its do’s and don’ts extensively."],[144,125.08,2.5,"It was to be avoided during cold weather,"],[144,127.67,4.46,"and certain body parts were to be used or bypassed"],[144,132.13,5.46,"based on the lunar cycle, zodiac calendar, and location of the ailment."],[144,138.47,2.88,"While on Crusade in the late 12th century,"],[144,141.34,4.75,"King Richard the Lionheart of England postponed negotiations"],[144,146.14,2.38,"with Sultan Saladin’s envoy"],[144,148.52,4.88,"to recover from one of his regular, preventative bloodletting sessions."],[144,153.82,5.71,"In 1685, after King Charles II of England began convulsing,"],[144,159.78,6.71,"he was bled from multiple veins, barraged by enemas, emetics, and purgatives,"],[144,166.58,4.55,"and fed an elixir containing powdered human skulls."],[144,171.5,4.92,"Despite bloodletting’s ubiquity, not everyone was enthusiastic."],[144,176.67,5.84,"In the feverish days preceding the poet Lord Byron’s death in 1824,"],[144,182.64,3.63,"he called his physicians a “set of butchers,”"],[144,186.35,5.55,"as they went about draining some 2.5 liters of his blood."],[144,193.52,4.34,"So, why did bloodletting stick around so long?"],[144,198.36,5.05,"Well, simply receiving care might have led to perceived benefits"],[144,203.41,2.13,"by way of the placebo effect."],[144,205.87,4.13,"And although some bloodletters were dangerously overzealous,"],[144,210.12,3.59,"modest bloodletting may not have been all bad."],[144,214.13,4.46,"Blood donors today give about half a liter of blood at a time,"],[144,218.67,3.17,"and some studies suggest that regular donors"],[144,221.97,3.59,"do see long-term cardiovascular benefits."],[144,225.64,5.55,"It’s also theoretically possible that, when done sparingly and early on,"],[144,231.35,5.67,"bloodletting could help fight some infections by reducing levels of iron,"],[144,237.11,3.25,"which infecting microbes use for energy."],[144,240.65,4.84,"But back then, no one even knew that pathogenic microbes existed yet,"],[144,245.62,3.34,"and bloodletting also caused infections."],[144,249.58,4.25,"This problem became especially pronounced in the 1700s,"],[144,253.96,5.92,"when the spring lancet was invented, with its tough-to-clean retractable blade."],[144,260.3,4.17,"Bloodletting gained popularity throughout the 1700s."],[144,264.72,2.17,"To supply the booming industry,"],[144,266.89,2.92,"leech catchers waded through boglands,"],[144,269.93,5.0,"using themselves as well as animal meat and organs as bait."],[144,275.23,3.29,"But bloodletting’s shortcomings began to show."],[144,278.77,5.3,"Philadelphia physician and Declaration of Independence signatory Benjamin Rush"],[144,284.2,2.92,"was one of bloodletting’s biggest backers."],[144,287.32,4.04,"He advocated for taking 80% of a patient's blood."],[144,291.87,4.17,"Rush also tended to over 100 patients daily,"],[144,296.04,3.88,"and his front yard became fetid and fly-infested"],[144,299.92,2.0,"from all the tossed-out blood."],[144,302.21,3.84,"In the 1790s, English journalist William Cobbett"],[144,306.05,5.71,"accused Rush of dramatically increasing Philadelphia’s daily mortality rates,"],[144,311.89,3.71,"but Rush successfully sued Cobbett for libel."],[144,316.1,3.04,"Still, as late as 1836,"],[144,319.15,5.76,"the largest London leech suppliers were importing around 600,000 leeches"],[144,324.9,2.21,"for bloodletting every month."],[144,327.49,3.54,"But as Cobbett and others dug into the data,"],[144,331.12,4.5,"bloodletting’s supposed benefits became all the more dubious."],[144,335.71,4.09,"And by the 20th century, its use slowed to a trickle,"],[144,339.92,4.55,"when humoral theory was finally replaced by germ theory,"],[144,344.55,5.76,"which demonstrated that many illnesses were actually caused by microorganisms."],[144,351.35,4.46,"Today, bloodletting is used to treat certain blood disorders"],[144,355.81,1.63,"like hemochromatosis,"],[144,357.52,4.25,"which causes dangerous levels of iron to build up in the bloodstream."],[144,362.23,3.0,"But generally, bloodletting has been relegated"],[144,365.24,3.88,"to the ranks of outdated, misguided medical treatments,"],[144,369.12,2.42,"bled dry by progress."],[145,6.88,5.38,"In July of 2025, top athletes competed in a kilometers-long race—"],[145,12.51,2.13,"but it had no set path."],[145,14.97,3.84,"That's because it was the World Orienteering Championships,"],[145,18.93,3.29,"and participants equipped with only maps and compasses"],[145,22.23,5.17,"were expected to find their way between far-off checkpoints at high speeds,"],[145,27.61,4.96,"weaving between trees, sprinting over rocks, and skirting cliffs."],[145,32.95,2.0,"This might sound a little daunting."],[145,34.99,3.04,"But whether you identify as a wayfinding whiz"],[145,38.08,2.29,"or get lost returning from the grocery store,"],[145,40.45,5.3,"your brain has a built-in navigation system that facilitates spatial skills,"],[145,45.83,4.84,"from sensing which direction is north to map reading and route learning."],[145,51.05,5.67,"It consists of two major parts: the striatum and the hippocampus."],[145,57.01,5.09,"The striatum is clued into sensory inputs, like nearby landmarks."],[145,62.14,4.25,"With repetition, it comes to associate specific stimuli and actions"],[145,66.48,2.46,"with certain responses and outcomes,"],[145,69.02,3.21,"linking these cues with set routes."],[145,72.32,2.0,"This forms navigation habits;"],[145,74.32,4.25,"for example, when you automatically take the same sequence of turns"],[145,78.57,1.17,"on your way home."],[145,80.78,1.71,"The hippocampus, meanwhile,"],[145,82.5,4.63,"constructs continuous, dynamic mental maps of your environment."],[145,87.5,2.13,"It helps you figure out how to get somewhere,"],[145,89.63,4.09,"even if you've never been or haven't taken that exact route before."],[145,93.97,5.55,"Researchers realized the hippocampus’ importance in mental mapping in the 70s,"],[145,99.51,2.25,"while observing rats’ brain activity"],[145,101.85,4.3,"and noticing that specific neurons in the hippocampus fired"],[145,106.14,2.29,"when the rats were in certain spaces."],[145,109.44,3.04,"The same thing also happened in complete darkness,"],[145,112.53,4.46,"indicating that these neurons, which came to be called “place cells,”"],[145,117.11,3.79,"formed cognitive maps independent of sensory input."],[145,121.2,3.59,"It seems that activity in these areas during sleep"],[145,125.0,3.04,"represents a kind of place replay"],[145,128.25,3.04,"as the brain consolidates spatial information."],[145,131.71,5.67,"And in 2005, researchers discovered additional types of navigational neurons"],[145,137.38,2.5,"in the region surrounding the hippocampus."],[145,140.18,5.67,"This includes “grid cells,” which fire in a regular pattern as one moves,"],[145,145.97,4.38,"providing the brain with a kind of internal coordinate system."],[145,150.65,4.71,"Together, the breakthrough discoveries that put place and grid cells on the map"],[145,155.65,3.25,"earned the 2014 Nobel Prize."],[145,159.86,3.54,"But while the striatum and hippocampus form a navigation system"],[145,163.41,4.5,"that allows our brains to adapt between strategies depending on the task,"],[145,168.16,2.75,"we still struggle to find our ways."],[145,171.67,3.04,"In the absence of strong external cues,"],[145,174.75,5.25,"people tend to make small errors that send them off course and into circles."],[145,180.18,4.09,"And generally, some of us find ourselves lost more often than others."],[145,184.47,4.63,"Most people can handle a striatum-centric, well-practiced route;"],[145,189.35,4.17,"but those who lean more on hippocampus-forward self-orienting"],[145,193.56,2.59,"tend to stand out as navigators."],[145,196.73,5.59,"Twin studies suggest that genetics account for around 60% of the variation"],[145,202.32,2.04,"in our navigation abilities."],[145,204.7,3.59,"These abilities appear independent of general intelligence,"],[145,208.29,4.21,"suggesting that navigation is its own distinct cognitive skill."],[145,212.67,3.88,"And crucially, research indicates that it’s one that can be practiced"],[145,216.71,1.33,"and improved."],[145,218.97,3.25,"Compared to people from suburban and rural areas,"],[145,222.38,1.54,"those who grew up in cities—"],[145,223.93,3.34,"especially ones with more uniform, grid-like layouts—"],[145,227.39,3.96,"appear to struggle more with navigating less ordered environments."],[145,231.73,5.05,"And in a 2010 study, rugby players and martial artists were blindfolded"],[145,236.82,3.04,"and asked to navigate back to a starting point."],[145,240.07,3.5,"The rugby players, who train at much larger scales,"],[145,243.74,2.71,"did so with more directional accuracy,"],[145,246.62,3.13,"suggesting that the difference in their spatial training"],[145,249.91,3.34,"improved their judgement across longer distances."],[145,253.96,5.63,"London cab drivers, meanwhile, must learn what’s referred to as “the knowledge,”"],[145,259.84,2.96,"which consists of extensive routes and landmarks"],[145,262.8,3.96,"across the city’s infamously complex maze of streets."],[145,267.1,2.29,"Those who successfully complete the training"],[145,269.39,4.42,"have been observed to experience significant hippocampal growth."],[145,274.81,3.96,"But the brain’s malleability can be a double-edged sword."],[145,279.11,3.09,"While GPS devices reliably get us around,"],[145,282.4,5.71,"they lead to worse spatial reconstructions than verbal directions and paper maps do."],[145,288.49,4.63,"By allowing our brains to engage less actively with environmental cues,"],[145,293.25,4.09,"GPS may degrade the hippocampal navigation system"],[145,297.38,3.84,"and impair our own internal sense of direction."],[145,301.71,2.13,"So, to help improve it,"],[145,303.84,3.84,"you can try putting your GPS away on more familiar routes"],[145,307.8,2.71,"and paying attention to distant landmarks."],[145,310.6,2.63,"Instead of using turn-by-turn directions,"],[145,313.27,4.84,"consider experimenting with a paper map and exploring new places on foot,"],[145,318.31,4.46,"since physical movement helps the brain encode spatial information."],[145,323.11,4.09,"Then, with a little practice, the next time you get turned around,"],[145,327.24,4.3,"maybe you can first look within yourself to find your way."],[146,4.46,5.25,"250 years ago,"],[146,9.76,1.79,"just under a mile from here,"],[146,11.59,1.75,"Thomas Jefferson wrote"],[146,13.38,3.17,"one of the most consequential documents"],[146,16.55,1.46,"in American history."],[146,18.47,4.92,"Now, the Declaration of Independence wasn't perfect,"],[146,23.44,1.5,"just like the guy who wrote it,"],[146,24.94,2.79,"and the country it created weren't perfect either."],[146,28.61,2.79,"But it set us on this path, right?"],[146,31.86,6.97,"This beautiful, audacious, complicated path that's still evolving."],[146,40.45,3.84,"And the Declaration of Independence still gives me chills."],[146,45.38,4.3,"“We hold these truths to be self-evident,"],[146,49.67,3.04,"that all are created equal,"],[146,52.76,3.67,"that they are endowed with certain unalienable rights,"],[146,56.47,2.59,"and that among these are life,"],[146,59.05,3.55,"liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.”"],[146,62.64,1.58,"It's good, right?"],[146,65.94,1.33,"Did you get chills?"],[146,69.23,2.79,"I mean, yeah, I changed it a little, OK, from --"],[146,73.61,2.33,"\"All men are created equal\""],[146,75.99,1.67,"to \"all are created equal\""],[146,77.66,2.09,"because it makes it better, right?"],[146,79.78,1.33,"Thank you."],[146,83.83,3.13,"And that's the point I want to talk with you about today,"],[146,87.0,2.25,"that to live up to its promise,"],[146,89.29,3.92,"we have evolved democracy in the past"],[146,93.21,2.96,"and it needs to evolve again,"],[146,96.22,5.84,"not only so that we can feel our votes count,"],[146,102.1,6.17,"but so we can count democracy's real benefits"],[146,108.31,1.71,"in our lives,"],[146,110.06,1.75,"including in our wallets."],[146,113.07,1.33,"So a couple of years ago,"],[146,114.4,3.04,"a philanthropic foundation in the United States"],[146,117.45,1.58,"asked me to do some research"],[146,119.07,4.96,"to find out why authoritarianism was gaining ground around the globe."],[146,124.08,2.33,"So I interviewed"],[146,126.41,4.09,"civic leaders in West Africa and scholars in Eastern Europe"],[146,130.54,2.42,"and democracy leaders in Latin America."],[146,132.96,1.21,"And I asked them,"],[146,134.17,2.67,"What's authoritarianism getting so right?"],[146,137.38,4.5,"These leaders that are pushing people and places away from democracy,"],[146,141.93,4.25,"what are these authoritarians doing that's so effective?"],[146,147.14,4.05,"And pretty much everyone I talked to said I was asking the wrong question --"],[146,151.86,5.76,"that authoritarianism wasn’t surging because of anything it was doing right,"],[146,157.65,3.13,"but because of what democracy was getting wrong."],[146,162.91,2.0,"That stopped me in my tracks."],[146,165.58,1.21,"The way they put it,"],[146,166.79,2.67,"authoritarianism was like an opportunistic disease"],[146,169.5,4.34,"that could only take root when democracy was weak."],[146,174.25,2.5,"And in case you haven't been paying attention --"],[146,179.34,2.25,"Democracy is kind of on life support."],[146,181.64,3.0,"So according to Freedom House,"],[146,184.68,4.67,"only about 20 percent of the world's population"],[146,189.35,3.59,"lives in a country currently that can be considered free."],[146,193.56,4.96,"And dissatisfaction with democracy is rising worldwide."],[146,198.53,2.71,"In the US, for instance,"],[146,201.28,4.21,"about three out of five Americans say they are currently dissatisfied"],[146,205.53,2.17,"with how democracy is working."],[146,207.7,1.25,"Why?"],[146,209.96,3.54,"The answer came from a conversation I had with Kwasi Prempeh."],[146,213.5,3.88,"So Kwasi runs the Ghana Center for Democratic Development."],[146,217.42,4.8,"Now Ghana only won independence from colonial rule in 1957,"],[146,222.26,2.59,"so not that long ago,"],[146,224.89,3.88,"but long enough that most people in Ghana today"],[146,228.77,3.42,"don't remember a time before democracy."],[146,233.81,3.13,"But what they do know"],[146,236.98,6.84,"is that they're struggling economically right now under democracy."],[146,244.82,5.21,"And so, as Kwasi put it, they very understandably ask,"],[146,250.08,4.5,"\"What has democracy done for me lately?\""],[146,256.38,1.84,"His point"],[146,258.21,5.17,"is that democracies that do not deliver broad-based opportunity"],[146,263.43,3.5,"are vulnerable to autocratic capture."],[146,268.31,1.71,"And research backs this up."],[146,270.02,4.38,"So Susan Stokes is a political science professor at the University of Chicago."],[146,274.39,6.3,"She has looked at variables for why democracy erodes in some countries,"],[146,280.74,1.38,"but not others."],[146,282.57,3.25,"Income inequality"],[146,285.87,5.13,"is the overwhelming variable in democratic backsliding,"],[146,291.04,3.67,"so much so that Stokes said short of unplugging their computers,"],[146,294.75,1.5,"they couldn't make it go away."],[146,296.29,2.67,"Now I want to be really clear about something."],[146,298.96,3.38,"In the richest country in the world,"],[146,302.34,4.42,"a country that boasts about being a land of opportunity for all,"],[146,306.8,5.05,"ensuring meaningful economic opportunity for all"],[146,311.89,1.67,"is the right thing to do."],[146,314.27,3.42,"It is the moral thing to do,"],[146,317.69,2.67,"and that is enough of a reason to do it."],[146,321.61,2.09,"And it's practical."],[146,326.36,4.34,"Economic justice is how we save democracy."],[146,338.46,5.38,"So I'm feeling like this might be as much of a revelation to you"],[146,343.88,1.13,"as it was to me,"],[146,345.01,2.42,"or at least it was maybe more of a revelation to me"],[146,347.47,1.17,"than it was to you,"],[146,348.68,4.59,"because we were used to, we have, for our 250 years,"],[146,353.31,3.21,"thought of democracy and economic opportunity"],[146,356.56,2.34,"as distinct and separate."],[146,358.94,1.67,"I mean, obviously interrelated,"],[146,360.65,2.09,"and for many of us equally important,"],[146,362.77,1.33,"but separate."],[146,364.78,1.21,"They're not."],[146,366.65,2.75,"They're two sides of the same coin."],[146,370.62,3.84,"Because if democracy doesn't deliver"],[146,374.45,3.42,"meaningful economic opportunity,"],[146,377.92,3.5,"democracy starts to feel meaningless."],[146,382.55,3.8,"And that makes authoritarianism and other forms of government more attractive,"],[146,386.38,2.67,"not in and of themselves,"],[146,389.09,2.17,"but by default,"],[146,391.3,4.71,"because democracy is defaulting on its promise."],[146,397.31,4.05,"So I said before that we've evolved democracy in the past,"],[146,401.36,1.54,"and it needs to evolve again."],[146,402.9,2.59,"I want us to think about democracy in phases."],[146,406.11,4.84,"Democracy 1.0 is that initial idea"],[146,410.95,2.08,"that the people"],[146,413.08,2.96,"should have the power in a political system,"],[146,416.08,2.25,"not kings,"],[146,418.37,1.5,"not empires,"],[146,419.92,1.92,"not corporations,"],[146,421.83,1.17,"the people."],[146,423.59,2.17,"It was a radical idea for its time."],[146,426.13,2.63,"Honestly, it's still really radical."],[146,429.34,2.38,"But as we know, that was just the beginning."],[146,431.76,4.3,"Democracy 2.0"],[146,436.1,2.33,"is the phase where we expand"],[146,438.48,4.63,"who is included in democracy, who gets to participate,"],[146,443.11,2.75,"who counts as the people."],[146,447.49,2.13,"In the United States,"],[146,449.61,2.21,"that process has been long."],[146,452.37,3.38,"It has taken generations of struggle --"],[146,455.79,3.09,"abolition, suffrage, civil rights --"],[146,458.91,1.79,"to make democracy more inclusive,"],[146,460.71,4.63,"to say that everyone gets an equal vote"],[146,465.34,3.09,"because everyone matters equally."],[146,470.05,3.42,"The brilliant scholar Isabel Wilkerson argues that, in fact,"],[146,473.51,4.13,"the US wasn't even really a democracy until 1965,"],[146,477.68,2.13,"when the Voting Rights Act became law."],[146,479.85,4.59,"And ever since then there has been an aggressive movement"],[146,484.44,1.92,"to roll back that progress."],[146,486.82,5.76,"While we should be making more progress on democracy 2.0,"],[146,492.61,1.17,"far-right extremists,"],[146,493.82,3.88,"aided and abetted by the Supreme Court,"],[146,497.74,2.46,"have dismantled the Voting Rights Act"],[146,500.25,2.96,"and are trying to take us backwards."],[146,504.96,2.67,"So realistically,"],[146,507.63,1.84,"while it makes sense to question"],[146,509.5,4.13,"whether we even have a democracy right now in the United States,"],[146,513.68,2.21,"either way,"],[146,515.93,4.21,"we still tend to focus more"],[146,520.18,4.17,"on democracy as a process"],[146,524.35,5.05,"and not enough on what democracy produces."],[146,530.32,2.42,"On democratic outcomes."],[146,533.65,1.96,"We think and argue and talk a lot"],[146,535.62,3.79,"about who gets to be included in democracy,"],[146,539.45,2.54,"but not enough about why."],[146,543.46,3.34,"Why do we have a democracy in the first place?"],[146,548.25,2.13,"Answering that question --"],[146,550.42,4.92,"defining the goal of democracy --"],[146,555.38,4.21,"that's democracy 3.0."],[146,560.81,5.3,"Democracy 3.0 says"],[146,566.1,5.92,"that if all of us were truly created equal,"],[146,572.07,3.67,"and if all of us truly get"],[146,575.78,3.54,"to participate equally,"],[146,579.37,3.5,"then the outcomes of our democracy"],[146,582.91,4.34,"should be more equitable, too, right?"],[146,587.67,4.17,"More widely shared and benefiting everyone."],[146,599.89,1.21,"But --"],[146,602.68,1.17,"Did you hear it coming?"],[146,604.89,1.21,"'Cause there's a but."],[146,607.85,3.71,"In the US today, your zip code predicts your life expectancy,"],[146,611.61,1.79,"your race, your wealth."],[146,613.44,2.88,"And your parents’ income predicts your future."],[146,616.99,1.5,"That ain't democratic."],[146,619.53,1.17,"And it's getting worse."],[146,620.74,4.75,"The top one percent of Americans control as much wealth"],[146,625.5,6.92,"as the entire bottom 90 percent of the country combined."],[146,633.92,1.67,"And according to one source,"],[146,635.63,6.88,"CEOs now make 281 times more"],[146,642.51,2.46,"than average workers."],[146,645.02,4.25,"281 times more?"],[146,649.98,3.0,"What the hell is wrong with us?"],[146,653.02,4.67,"Are CEOs 281 times more talented?"],[146,658.07,4.05,"Are they 281 times more deserving?"],[146,662.12,4.88,"Are they 281 times harder-working than the rest of us?"],[146,667.79,3.59,"Look, we can use the word democracy all we like, but let's be clear."],[146,671.38,6.34,"We've really got more of an oligarchy or a kleptocracy."],[146,679.17,3.54,"Or maybe a monarchy where the kings are billionaires"],[146,682.76,3.5,"and reality TV stars, or sometimes both."],[146,692.48,3.21,"Democracy means"],[146,695.69,3.96,"that the kids of house cleaners and mechanics"],[146,699.65,4.3,"have the same opportunities in life"],[146,703.95,2.38,"as the kids of lawyers and tech execs."],[146,706.37,3.42,"Democracy means that if you work hard,"],[146,709.79,2.92,"you can feed your family and afford a place to live."],[146,712.75,1.58,"Democracy means"],[146,714.34,3.67,"that power and profit"],[146,718.05,2.84,"are distributed democratically."],[146,720.88,3.71,"That is democracy 3.0."],[146,731.43,2.29,"So for those who maybe didn't clap --"],[146,738.73,2.54,"This may be the part in the talk"],[146,741.28,2.54,"where some of y'all are getting a little bit nervous."],[146,743.86,2.04,"You hear \"economic democracy\""],[146,745.95,3.71,"and you think it means enforced sameness."],[146,749.7,1.29,"It does not."],[146,751.71,4.34,"Democracy 3.0 doesn't mean everybody gets an equal house"],[146,756.04,2.88,"and an equal income and equal outcomes."],[146,760.72,4.84,"Democracy 3.0 simply means"],[146,765.59,3.29,"that everyone gets an equal chance"],[146,768.93,2.63,"at the American dream."],[146,771.56,1.58,"Are you with me?"],[146,777.06,4.84,"OK, so how do we actually get to democracy 3.0?"],[146,781.94,2.38,"Well, the first thing is a shift in mindset."],[146,784.32,2.13,"To start seeing political democracy"],[146,786.45,3.67,"and economic democracy as mutually intertwined,"],[146,790.16,4.5,"which means we need to advance economic justice"],[146,794.71,4.5,"not only for basic fairness, right?"],[146,799.25,2.84,"But because that’s the way we strengthen democracy."],[146,802.47,1.42,"And on a practical level,"],[146,803.92,2.67,"it means that, yeah, to strengthen democracy,"],[146,806.63,5.8,"we need to restore and strengthen the Voting Rights Act,"],[146,812.48,5.38,"but also we need to raise wages,"],[146,817.9,2.08,"and we need to get money out of politics,"],[146,820.02,2.71,"and we need to tax wealth more fairly."],[146,824.95,1.21,"And --"],[146,827.91,4.13,"And democracy 3.0 means"],[146,832.08,4.71,"that just like voters should have equal voice and power,"],[146,836.83,3.67,"workers should have equal voice and power,"],[146,840.54,3.59,"and democracy at work means unions."],[146,847.09,6.67,"In fact, the first wisp of democracy 3.0 was at the last turn of the century,"],[146,853.81,5.42,"the last time our country got fed up with extreme wealth and extreme poverty."],[146,859.65,3.5,"That's why we created unions, which helped create the middle class."],[146,863.19,3.38,"And then we created a social safety net,"],[146,866.57,2.92,"things like Social Security and eventually Medicare."],[146,869.53,3.13,"Taxing wealth to pay for programs"],[146,872.7,4.0,"that would prevent extreme poverty and suffering."],[146,876.75,2.92,"We just need to do it again --"],[146,879.71,2.09,"plus do more --"],[146,881.84,4.04,"things like universal health care for all."],[146,887.84,5.09,"And more democratic ways of owning businesses,"],[146,892.93,2.34,"like employee ownership."],[146,895.27,2.67,"And we need to make sure"],[146,897.93,4.46,"that new technologies actually improve our lives"],[146,902.4,2.75,"instead of undermining them."],[146,910.03,1.21,"Look."],[146,912.62,4.59,"The threat to end our democracy"],[146,917.25,3.25,"also contains within it"],[146,920.54,2.34,"an invitation to make it better."],[146,922.92,2.34,"But our vision cannot be"],[146,925.25,4.05,"just to go back to the way things were a few elections ago."],[146,930.01,4.92,"We have a moral and practical imperative"],[146,934.93,5.51,"to future-proof democracy by helping each other."],[146,941.52,5.17,"By delivering real political voice"],[146,946.69,4.0,"and real economic power"],[146,950.7,2.96,"to we, the people,"],[146,953.7,1.13,"all of us."],[146,955.91,3.13,"Because if we don't,"],[146,959.08,3.21,"if democracy doesn't deliver broad-based opportunity,"],[146,962.33,3.34,"people will eventually trade it for something they think will."],[146,966.42,2.17,"And history shows us what happens next."],[146,969.47,1.58,"It's not good for any of us."],[146,972.22,5.5,"Democracy contains within it the tools for its own demise,"],[146,977.76,1.54,"but also its renewal."],[146,981.64,4.63,"But if we want a better country, a fairer country,"],[146,986.27,1.88,"we're going to have to fight for it."],[146,988.65,5.25,"We, the people, demand"],[146,993.91,5.96,"that our politics and our economy work way better"],[146,999.91,3.54,"for us and everyone around us,"],[146,1003.46,4.0,"because democracy can be more"],[146,1007.5,4.71,"than something we do every couple of years in a voting booth,"],[146,1012.22,2.54,"but something we can eat"],[146,1014.8,2.25,"and something we can live in"],[146,1017.05,3.13,"and something we can put in our pockets"],[146,1020.22,1.88,"every single day."],[146,1022.77,6.34,"That is the only way we are going to save ourselves,"],[146,1029.15,1.96,"our country,"],[146,1031.11,3.5,"and the ideals we hold so dear."],[146,1036.16,5.13,"Democracy 1.0 gives life to a radical idea."],[146,1042.04,6.46,"Democracy 2.0 expands liberty to more and more of us."],[146,1050.13,6.17,"Democracy 3.0 can make the pursuit of happiness"],[146,1056.34,3.71,"possible for all."],[146,1061.31,1.25,"Thank you."],[147,7.04,4.42,"In 1910, the Antarctic Nimrod Expedition was in trouble."],[147,11.63,1.75,"Led by Ernest Shackleton,"],[147,13.38,3.88,"the team had set the record for the southernmost point ever reached."],[147,17.47,2.71,"But after miscalculating how long it would take to return"],[147,20.18,1.75,"to the nearest supply depot,"],[147,21.93,2.29,"the crew had run out of food."],[147,24.81,2.88,"Fatigued and desperate, they turned to their medical kit."],[147,27.82,3.59,"It contained a drug called “Forced March tablets,”"],[147,31.49,3.38,"which promised to mitigate hunger and prolong endurance,"],[147,35.28,3.38,"thanks to the powerful combination of its two ingredients:"],[147,39.03,4.0,"cocaine, and the extract of the African kola nut."],[147,43.91,4.21,"After taking the pills, the team was able to push through and reach safety."],[147,48.71,3.84,"So how did the kola nut, a sacred ingredient from West Africa,"],[147,52.67,2.59,"end up in Shackleton's emergency kit?"],[147,55.38,3.84,"And how did this same combination of kola nut and cocaine"],[147,59.22,3.92,"make its way into the original recipe of a drink"],[147,63.14,2.88,"that billions of people consume every day?"],[147,66.85,3.13,"The kola nut is the seed of the kola tree,"],[147,70.23,4.21,"a small evergreen native to the tropical forests of West Africa,"],[147,74.65,4.21,"where it has long been integral to the cultural fabric of many communities."],[147,79.58,4.38,"Among the Igbo, the bitter nut is known as the food of the gods."],[147,84.25,3.75,"In Igbo homes, its custom to greet and honor guests"],[147,88.0,2.96,"by breaking a seed and sharing the lobes."],[147,91.8,2.04,"Within Yoruba communities,"],[147,93.84,3.5,"kola plays a central role in many religious ceremonies,"],[147,97.34,2.75,"such as weddings, funerals, and divination."],[147,101.26,3.38,"Kola nuts can contain up to 2.5% caffeine,"],[147,104.85,3.09,"or about twice the concentration found in coffee beans,"],[147,108.1,4.67,"along with trace amounts of theobromine, a structurally similar compound."],[147,113.4,3.04,"Both molecules stimulate the central nervous system,"],[147,116.44,1.75,"causing effects like alertness"],[147,118.2,2.96,"and increased blood flow to the muscles and lungs."],[147,121.45,3.88,"And it’s these properties that likely led to kola’s long-standing use"],[147,125.33,2.09,"in traditional West African medicine."],[147,127.92,3.29,"But there’s also a bitter side to the kola nut’s history."],[147,131.75,1.75,"As early as the 10th century,"],[147,133.5,3.84,"it was used by various groups in West Africa as currency"],[147,137.51,3.09,"to purchase captives across the Sahara."],[147,141.01,5.0,"And in the 16th century, Portuguese and Afro-Portuguese traders in the region"],[147,146.02,3.21,"similarly exchanged the seeds for people,"],[147,149.23,3.09,"whom they then sold into transatlantic slavery."],[147,152.81,4.3,"By the 17th century, the kola nut had made its way to the Americas."],[147,157.4,3.13,"Brazilian oral narratives tell of enslaved peoples"],[147,160.53,3.8,"smuggling the seeds across the Atlantic in their clothing and hair."],[147,164.66,4.55,"Enslavers who learned of the nut’s energizing properties often weaponized it,"],[147,169.25,4.34,"supplying it to subjugated workers to induce more back-breaking labor."],[147,174.34,3.25,"However, in places like Jamaica, Cuba, and Brazil,"],[147,177.59,3.79,"many enslaved communities managed to cultivate kola on their own,"],[147,181.59,2.79,"allowing them to preserve and continue practicing"],[147,184.39,2.42,"their cultural and religious traditions."],[147,187.52,1.42,"In the late 19th century,"],[147,188.94,4.04,"the kola nut gained the attention of pharmacists in Europe and North America."],[147,192.98,2.04,"Many claimed, without much evidence,"],[147,195.02,4.92,"that the nut contained countless medicinal and even mind-altering properties."],[147,200.32,5.34,"Soon, kola-containing tonics, chocolates, and lozenges lined pharmacy shelves."],[147,206.24,4.0,"It was combined with extracts from the coca leaf, the source of cocaine,"],[147,210.37,3.04,"to create Shackleton’s Forced March tablets,"],[147,213.5,4.04,"which were also supplied to British soldiers during World War I."],[147,218.26,5.71,"In 1886, Atlanta pharmacist John Pemberton used this same potent combo"],[147,223.97,3.25,"to create the original recipe for Coca-Cola,"],[147,227.76,5.13,"which he claimed could treat ailments like headaches, indigestion, and impotence."],[147,233.35,3.42,"By the 1920s, African export of the kola nut slowed,"],[147,236.77,4.46,"thanks in part to the introduction of another energizing seed from the region:"],[147,241.4,1.17,"the cocoa bean."],[147,242.86,2.71,"Around this time, the Coca-Cola company is also said"],[147,245.57,3.29,"to have significantly reduced the kola nut in its recipe,"],[147,249.2,1.63,"but no one knows for sure—"],[147,250.83,3.79,"the exact ingredients remain a closely guarded trade secret."],[147,255.0,5.96,"However, we do know that the company removed all traces of cocaine by 1903."],[147,261.92,3.92,"Today, the kola nut can still be found in various drinks and supplements,"],[147,265.85,3.67,"like Bissy tea, a staple in many Jamaican homes."],[147,269.64,3.17,"Believed to have been first brought to the region on slave ships"],[147,272.81,1.5,"in the 17th century,"],[147,274.31,4.63,"this tea is just one example of how the kola nut, despite its bitter past,"],[147,279.02,5.34,"continues to serve as a powerful symbol of cultural preservation and remembrance."],[148,4.17,2.1,"I ask questions for a living"],[148,6.3,4.54,"to people like Mark Cuban, Neil deGrasse Tyson,"],[148,10.88,2.9,"Esther Perel, Bill Nye."],[148,13.78,2.2,"These masters of their field."],[148,16.01,2.94,"And the most surprising answer I heard this year"],[148,18.95,4.41,"was from two 11-year-olds named Sophie and Dilan."],[148,23.39,4.17,"They too are experts in being kids these days."],[148,27.59,4.3,"So I asked them, how does time with people on screens"],[148,31.93,2.17,"feel different than real life?"],[148,34.13,2.57,"(Video) Sophie: It just makes you feel more with them"],[148,36.7,1.44,"when you're on FaceTime."],[148,38.17,1.87,"Nayeema Raza: Even more than real life?"],[148,40.04,3.17,"Sophie: Yeah, because you're doing stuff together."],[148,43.21,2.3,"Like maybe playing Roblox together."],[148,45.54,3.8,"Because nowadays, when you’re with them, everyone’s on their phones."],[148,50.95,3.2,"NR: Sophie's pointing out a profound paradox."],[148,54.15,4.34,"When we are together physically, we are each alone on our phones."],[148,58.49,3.9,"But when we're in our phones, that's when we can be together."],[148,62.43,2.6,"The best way to not be distracted by your device?"],[148,65.03,1.47,"Just get inside of it."],[148,67.83,1.53,"Now these 11-year-olds"],[148,69.37,4.54,"are not talking about some distant, anxious generation."],[148,73.9,1.94,"They're talking about each of us."],[148,75.87,2.1,"They're definitely talking about me"],[148,78.01,3.6,"and about a world that's increasingly driven by machines."],[148,82.41,4.77,"So I stumbled upon an extreme metaphor for what this could look like."],[148,87.22,3.94,"And it's this guy who's locked in a Waymo, and it's driving him in circles."],[148,91.16,1.4,"So he calls customer service"],[148,92.59,2.47,"and finds out he's not the only one trapped."],[148,95.09,2.7,"(Video) Woman: Working with the situation of the vehicle."],[148,97.83,1.57,"If you have your app pulled up,"],[148,99.43,3.0,"I need you to tap My Trip on the lower left corner of your app."],[148,102.47,2.84,"Man: Can't you just do it? You should be able to handle it."],[148,105.34,2.17,"Take over the car. You don't need my phone."],[148,107.54,1.5,"Woman: I don't have an option."],[148,109.91,2.67,"NR: It is sexy to think that the tech apocalypse"],[148,112.58,2.57,"is Arnold Schwarzenegger and \"The Terminator,\""],[148,115.18,2.97,"but it could be so much more mundane than that."],[148,118.18,2.67,"Just us driven in circles,"],[148,120.89,2.7,"held hostage by drop-down menus,"],[148,123.62,3.07,"with gadgets, disintermediating us from each other,"],[148,126.72,2.94,"from our own bodies and from our curiosities."],[148,129.66,4.54,"Because nowadays, when we have a question, we don't wait and phone a friend."],[148,134.2,2.34,"We friend our phones."],[148,136.53,4.54,"And that feels so empowering to have all of this knowledge at our fingertips."],[148,141.07,5.94,"Yet early research from MIT tells us it's making us lazier and less smart,"],[148,147.04,3.1,"and it is definitely making us less connected."],[148,150.18,2.6,"This is not what our parents and grandparents were sold"],[148,152.78,3.7,"when they saw this relic of an ad from AT&T which says,"],[148,156.49,1.57,"\"Reach out and touch someone.\""],[148,158.09,3.34,"And yes, for all kinds of reasons, it would not go down well today."],[148,162.76,2.67,"But it is oddly prescient"],[148,165.46,4.11,"because we have never been more connected and more out of touch."],[148,169.6,2.8,"Now I’m not anti-tech. I actually cover it as a journalist."],[148,172.4,1.83,"I have every gadget under the sun,"],[148,174.24,3.6,"and most days I think I'm in a relationship with my ChatGPT,"],[148,177.88,2.34,"or as I like to call him, Chat Daddy."],[148,181.71,1.83,"I am pro-human."],[148,183.58,1.94,"And as we progress into an AI world"],[148,185.55,4.3,"that you've read 471.5 articles about today alone,"],[148,189.89,2.3,"I want to make a case for old habits."],[148,192.22,1.27,"Three of them."],[148,193.49,2.67,"And tell you how I learned them the hard way."],[148,196.16,1.94,"The first is to pause,"],[148,198.13,1.64,"to take just one second"],[148,199.8,3.44,"when you feel that urge to reach for your digital pacifier."],[148,203.27,1.7,"This, by the way, is a second."],[148,206.07,3.4,"Studies show waiting that long before taking action"],[148,209.51,2.34,"lets your brain work better."],[148,211.88,1.74,"The second is to wonder."],[148,213.61,3.37,"Watch a movie without googling who the actor is and what else is he in,"],[148,217.01,2.1,"and how old he is, and is he single?"],[148,219.12,2.3,"You can float in your own curiosity"],[148,221.45,2.6,"instead of drown in information."],[148,224.09,2.6,"And the third is to ask a question"],[148,226.72,1.27,"out loud again."],[148,228.93,1.6,"Have that fight at a dinner party"],[148,230.53,2.07,"instead of playing footsie with your phone."],[148,232.63,2.9,"Ask something to someone you thought you couldn't learn from,"],[148,235.53,2.8,"or someone you think you know everything about."],[148,238.37,2.8,"Because the dumbest thing we can be is know-it-alls."],[148,242.71,3.2,"A few years ago, my father passed."],[148,245.91,3.64,"In the days leading up to it, I was glued to devices."],[148,249.58,2.27,"They had all these answers."],[148,251.88,3.44,"The number to his hospice nurse, how often to give them morphine,"],[148,255.32,2.67,"the signs to look out for, his heartbeat."],[148,259.42,1.5,"But when he passed on a Sunday,"],[148,260.92,4.0,"a day before the data and the vitals suggested he would,"],[148,264.96,1.83,"that's when it hit me."],[148,266.83,2.17,"The old habits were what mattered."],[148,269.93,3.07,"Those seconds of pause that added up to minutes more."],[148,274.07,4.2,"That weird and scary wonder about our own finite lives."],[148,278.31,1.9,"And the little questions people ask me,"],[148,280.24,2.7,"like, \"How can I be there for you?\""],[148,285.28,3.4,"Sophie was on to something, but we're grown ups,"],[148,288.72,3.84,"and we remember when presence and curiosity and connection"],[148,292.59,2.74,"were possible outside of technology."],[148,295.33,2.07,"We have to practice these old habits"],[148,297.43,3.3,"if we hope to pass them on to a new generation."],[148,300.76,2.67,"If we want to teach them how to be together"],[148,303.47,1.83,"when we are together."],[148,305.34,1.33,"Right, Chat Daddy?"],[148,307.84,1.27,"Thank you."],[149,16.05,4.0,"Deep in the jungles of southeast Asia lurks a deadly creature"],[149,20.06,2.71,"with sharp teeth and a venomous bite:"],[149,23.06,1.67,"a slow loris."],[149,24.85,2.04,"Despite their wide-eyed looks,"],[149,27.02,3.29,"their story is much fiercer than you might imagine."],[149,30.82,5.76,"Lorises split off from other primates as far back as 70 million years ago,"],[149,36.74,1.96,"when T-rexes still roamed."],[149,38.95,4.13,"And they gradually migrated from Africa to Asia."],[149,43.37,3.96,"Around sunset each evening, lorises get active,"],[149,47.38,4.71,"moving slowly and deliberately through the trees in their home range."],[149,52.38,4.71,"They use their large eyes to scan their surroundings for their next meal"],[149,57.14,2.38,"and take little time to rest."],[149,59.97,5.84,"Pygmy lorises, for one, regularly pass their nights in near-constant motion."],[149,66.19,2.46,"Unlike many other primates, however,"],[149,68.69,5.21,"slow lorises have a higher proportion of slow-twitch muscle fibers,"],[149,73.99,5.38,"which are favored for long-winded exertion versus speedy spurts."],[149,79.74,5.17,"They also have extra vertebrae, making them especially flexible."],[149,85.21,6.63,"When needed, slow lorises can stay still, gripped to one tree branch for hours,"],[149,91.84,2.75,"extra blood vessel networks in their limbs"],[149,94.59,3.34,"preventing them from weakening or becoming numb."],[149,98.68,3.54,"Their slow movements help them go undetected."],[149,102.22,2.46,"And that way, they avoid threats,"],[149,104.81,5.67,"staying stealth around predators that tend to be on the lookout for motion;"],[149,110.48,3.54,"and they can better hunt unsuspecting prey."],[149,114.73,4.38,"Slow lorises are omnivores that go after lots of insects"],[149,119.11,3.84,"and other small animals, as well as fruit and nectar."],[149,123.24,5.42,"Interestingly, they also consume a lot of tree gum and sap,"],[149,128.67,6.13,"scratching at tree bark with a row of specialized teeth called a “toothcomb.”"],[149,135.17,3.5,"Though these plant substances are tough to digest,"],[149,138.68,4.59,"slow lorises seem to be well-equipped to detoxify them."],[149,144.06,4.75,"Some scientists even suspect that they sequester the toxins"],[149,148.81,1.88,"from the tree gum they devour"],[149,150.85,5.13,"because slow lorises are, in fact, toxic themselves."],[149,156.24,6.05,"They’re the only venomous primates, and one of only a few venomous mammals."],[149,162.41,3.96,"And their venom system is thought to be one of the most complex"],[149,166.37,1.38,"in the animal kingdom."],[149,168.33,5.42,"It works in two parts, with the combination of two separate secretions."],[149,174.0,4.63,"When threatened, slow lorises may assume their venom pose,"],[149,178.63,5.8,"raising their arms so they can easily access glands near their armpits"],[149,184.43,2.46,"that secrete a toxic oil."],[149,187.77,4.88,"When they lick at the glands, the oil combines with their saliva"],[149,192.65,3.92,"to form their formidable, flesh-rotting venom."],[149,197.07,3.04,"They store the mixture on their sharp toothcomb,"],[149,200.11,5.96,"to be deployed with a bite capable of causing intense pain, tissue damage,"],[149,206.28,4.55,"anaphylaxis, and, in small animals, even death."],[149,211.29,5.3,"Slow lorises spread the oil over their coats where it can kill parasites"],[149,216.59,1.21,"on contact."],[149,218.0,4.88,"In fact, the number of parasites they carry on their skin and fur"],[149,222.93,3.42,"is remarkably low relative to other primates."],[149,226.35,3.54,"And it may also be protective against bigger threats."],[149,230.1,3.96,"When slow lorises sleep, they curl into little balls,"],[149,234.06,4.67,"toxic coats exposed, perhaps to help deter predators."],[149,239.07,6.46,"Cats, sun bears, and civets are repelled by just the scent of slow loris saliva—"],[149,245.53,3.59,"though orangutans seem to have found a way around it."],[149,249.7,5.63,"The most common victims of slow loris bites, however, are other slow lorises."],[149,255.84,3.71,"Tracking one population over the course of eight years,"],[149,259.67,5.71,"scientists only recorded one slow loris dying from natural predation."],[149,266.01,4.84,"But 20% of them got bitten by other slow lorises,"],[149,270.85,2.79,"perhaps in fights over limited territory."],[149,273.94,2.13,"And while they may look cute to us,"],[149,276.23,5.76,"those same features might send a very different message to predators at dusk."],[149,282.15,1.04,"When in danger,"],[149,283.19,6.09,"slow lorises may assume their venom pose, sway, and make a raspy noise."],[149,289.66,4.3,"Some scientists think these behaviors and their markings"],[149,293.96,4.3,"might help them effectively mimic spectacled cobras."],[149,298.71,3.46,"This could be an example of Müllerian mimicry,"],[149,302.26,4.67,"where one dangerous species benefits from sharing certain characteristics"],[149,306.93,1.0,"with another,"],[149,307.93,3.04,"helping warn other creatures to stay away."],[149,311.43,4.05,"But if slow lorises evolved to look like cobras,"],[149,315.48,2.13,"we don't seem to be getting the memo."],[149,317.81,3.25,"Though every species of slow loris is threatened,"],[149,321.23,3.84,"humans continue to illegally trade them as pets,"],[149,325.2,4.46,"stripped of their natural defenses, their teeth cut or pulled."],[149,330.12,3.92,"It’s well past time to pick up on the signals they’ve been sending"],[149,334.12,4.55,"and leave the fierce slow loris— and their forests— alone."],[150,6.63,6.3,"In the late 1700s, German physician Anton Mesmer was enthralling Europe,"],[150,13.09,3.92,"curing conditions from nervous disorders to blindness—"],[150,17.14,1.54,"or so he claimed."],[150,19.14,6.3,"According to Mesmer, an invisible, magnetic fluid animated all living beings,"],[150,25.56,3.04,"and disturbances to it caused illness."],[150,28.98,1.75,"For so-called “treatment,”"],[150,30.86,6.05,"Mesmer had his clientele form a chain around a contraption filled with water"],[150,37.03,2.59,"he allegedly magnetized."],[150,39.91,2.33,"Then, with waves of his hands,"],[150,42.45,5.0,"patients would fall into fits that would supposedly restore their health."],[150,47.96,5.09,"Some, however, were skeptical— including King Louis XVI,"],[150,53.26,4.92,"who tasked renowned researchers with interrogating Mesmer's methods"],[150,58.35,1.79,"in 1784."],[150,60.97,5.38,"They conducted science’s first-ever blind placebo trial,"],[150,66.56,4.42,"informing participants that they were receiving the magnetized treatment"],[150,71.15,2.42,"though they weren’t, and vice versa."],[150,73.99,4.13,"The committee ultimately concluded that any positive outcomes"],[150,78.28,2.46,"weren't attributable to magnetism."],[150,80.91,4.59,"Instead, they were results of the participants’ own imaginations,"],[150,85.5,4.17,"responding to suggestions about the treatment's effectiveness."],[150,89.96,4.13,"Mesmer disappeared, but the tantalizing takeaway remained."],[150,94.17,3.8,"And decades later, Scottish surgeon James Braid"],[150,98.01,5.17,"began utilizing aspects of Mesmerism, minus the magnetism."],[150,103.6,3.34,"Braid thought it induced a sleep-like state,"],[150,107.02,2.38,"so he called it hypnotism."],[150,109.73,4.96,"The practice today holds an entrancing place in popular fantasy."],[150,114.94,2.96,"But is there any truth to what it can accomplish?"],[150,118.07,2.13,"Or is it just illusion?"],[150,121.03,2.75,"While stage hypnosis has contributed"],[150,123.79,4.63,"to the conception that hypnotized people lose control over their actions,"],[150,128.54,1.79,"this doesn’t seem accurate."],[150,130.79,1.96,"Whether or not they were hypnotized,"],[150,132.75,4.05,"one study found that participants were just as likely"],[150,136.92,2.59,"to dip their hands into acid."],[150,139.84,3.5,"This might be because they felt motivated to take risks"],[150,143.35,4.46,"within the presumably safe confines of a psychology experiment."],[150,148.14,3.88,"Similarly, onstage participants might comply"],[150,152.02,3.25,"in the context of social pressures and fun,"],[150,155.28,2.96,"with perhaps some hypnosis at play."],[150,159.11,4.0,"But hypnosis used in medicine today looks different;"],[150,163.33,1.88,"no props are necessary."],[150,165.41,3.96,"And while hypnosis was initially considered a trance-like state,"],[150,169.37,5.42,"it’s now understood more as a set of procedures focused on suggestions."],[150,175.0,1.71,"No matter how informal,"],[150,176.71,4.88,"suggestions are natural features of doctor-patient interactions,"],[150,181.72,5.21,"and we now know they can influence a patient’s experience of their treatment."],[150,187.77,5.63,"Research-backed hypnosis sessions generally begin with a hypnotic induction,"],[150,193.56,4.09,"where a trained professional primes a patient for suggestions"],[150,197.65,2.88,"by drawing their attention to simple tasks,"],[150,200.66,2.25,"like relaxing certain muscles."],[150,203.16,4.71,"Induction is supposed to help facilitate a more receptive mindset."],[150,208.33,4.63,"Then, professionals make suggestions aimed at altering an aspect"],[150,212.96,1.67,"of the patient's perception,"],[150,214.84,4.59,"often describing something as though it will happen and be passively observed."],[150,219.97,5.0,"The session may close with a de-induction to ensure the patient is fully alert."],[150,225.85,4.09,"Most people seem moderately responsive to hypnotic suggestion,"],[150,229.93,4.75,"while small percentages are extremely responsive or unresponsive."],[150,235.11,4.8,"A person’s hypnotizability doesn’t tend to change much over time,"],[150,240.15,5.26,"though certain drugs and brain stimulation may increase it temporarily."],[150,245.78,3.17,"It’s unclear what determines hypnotizability,"],[150,249.04,3.17,"but certain traits seem to be associated."],[150,252.41,5.0,"Highly hypnotizable people tend to be open minded toward hypnosis,"],[150,257.59,5.51,"more prone to fantasy, and likely to become absorbed in activities."],[150,263.43,6.38,"They also report more unusual perceptual and dissociative experiences overall."],[150,270.6,2.17,"When a hypnotic suggestion works,"],[150,272.81,3.84,"the effects can be considerable— and traceable."],[150,276.77,4.88,"In some studies, researchers have prompted highly hypnotizable participants"],[150,281.69,3.17,"to perceive things that were otherwise absent."],[150,285.2,5.51,"In response, the hypnotized participants’ brain activity has been shown to change"],[150,290.79,4.55,"in ways different from what’s observed in imaginative processes,"],[150,295.5,4.46,"to instead more closely resemble actual perception."],[150,300.92,4.21,"For those it works with, hypnosis can be a helpful medical tool,"],[150,305.13,3.5,"though its scope is generally limited to psychiatry."],[150,308.97,2.59,"In one study, hypnotizable patients"],[150,311.56,3.67,"who received pain-reducing suggestions during surgery"],[150,315.27,5.0,"reported less pain and required fewer opioids afterwards."],[150,320.52,2.17,"Among patients with brain injuries,"],[150,322.69,3.67,"hypnosis has been observed to improve their working memory—"],[150,326.36,2.46,"that is, their ability to hold information"],[150,328.82,2.88,"like sequences of numbers for short periods."],[150,331.99,5.25,"This might be because the suggestions somehow ease cognitive deficits"],[150,337.33,5.09,"that are driven by the patient’s own beliefs about their post-injury abilities."],[150,342.71,3.0,"And for certain things, including quitting smoking,"],[150,345.88,3.71,"hypnosis seems to be similarly effective to treatments"],[150,349.6,2.04,"like counseling and exercise."],[150,351.93,3.38,"But while hypnosis can help some people manage conditions"],[150,355.31,2.0,"like anxiety and depression,"],[150,357.31,3.59,"there’s little evidence it can treat things like schizophrenia."],[150,361.61,2.13,"When hypnosis does help, though,"],[150,363.73,3.0,"it’s certainly no thanks to magnetized fluids,"],[150,366.74,3.84,"but rather the mesmerizing power of the mind."],[151,7.38,3.71,"Your first job as a speaker is to build a trusting human bond"],[151,11.09,1.33,"with your audience."],[151,12.55,3.42,"One excellent way to connect to an audience is to tell a story."],[151,16.39,2.84,"Stories helped make us who we are."],[151,19.77,3.96,"Really — evidence left behind from our ancient ancestors"],[151,23.81,5.63,"suggests that the human mind developed alongside human storytelling."],[151,29.82,3.21,"Fire created a new magnet for bonding."],[151,33.28,4.17,"Its warmth and flickering light drew people together after dark."],[151,38.03,3.13,"This seems to have happened in every ancient culture"],[151,41.16,2.88,"over the last 300,000 years."],[151,44.25,3.0,"And what did our ancestors do with this time together?"],[151,47.46,1.42,"In many cultures,"],[151,48.88,5.17,"one type of communal activity became very important: storytelling."],[151,54.34,4.17,"Stories brought people from faraway places to the fireside."],[151,58.68,3.38,"Stories about people who were alive and people who were dead,"],[151,62.39,2.42,"nearby and in distant lands."],[151,65.23,3.63,"Stories sparked laughter, tension, and amazement."],[151,69.48,5.13,"Stories also played an important role in helping expand people’s ability"],[151,74.61,5.63,"to imagine and to dream, and to understand the minds of others."],[151,80.74,3.17,"Storytellers became important people within a community,"],[151,83.91,2.71,"and their listeners took information from the stories"],[151,86.62,2.67,"that changed how they behaved in the world."],[151,89.67,3.04,"It's not just that we all love hearing stories."],[151,93.17,5.84,"Stories probably helped shape how our minds share and receive information."],[151,99.47,5.09,"So it's no surprise that many of the best talks are built around a story."],[151,106.06,3.54,"The most basic way of breaking down a story is this:"],[151,110.11,3.38,"a character with a goal meets an unexpected obstacle."],[151,113.98,4.38,"She tries to overcome that obstacle, which builds to a peak in the action."],[151,118.57,3.13,"Finally, there’s some kind of resolution to the conflict."],[151,122.37,3.09,"When it comes to sharing a story from the stage,"],[151,125.66,2.5,"remember four key things:"],[151,128.46,5.26,"1. Base the story around a character your audience can empathize with."],[151,134.17,2.33,"2. Build tension,"],[151,136.72,5.25,"either through curiosity, relationship with other characters, or actual danger."],[151,142.51,3.67,"3. Offer the right level of detail."],[151,146.77,2.67,"If there's not enough, the story won't come to life."],[151,149.52,2.67,"If there's too much, it will get bogged down."],[151,152.86,6.96,"And, 4. End with a satisfying conclusion, whether it’s funny, moving, or surprising."],[151,160.62,4.25,"Some great talks are built around a single story."],[151,165.33,2.13,"This can be really helpful for a speaker."],[151,167.62,2.71,"The throughline is simply the plot of the story."],[151,170.88,4.13,"If the story is about you, it can even create empathy in the audience."],[151,175.59,2.63,"If your journey is exciting and easy to follow,"],[151,178.47,2.71,"this type of talk can work really well."],[151,181.43,1.63,"But there's a trap here too."],[151,184.06,2.75,"Remember, the goal is to give."],[151,187.22,3.71,"Personal stories may entertain or interest an audience,"],[151,191.15,3.29,"or make the speaker feel really good about themselves,"],[151,194.56,2.92,"but they don’t necessarily give the audience"],[151,197.49,1.79,"something they can walk away with;"],[151,199.32,5.0,"a new way of understanding things, actionable information, context, hope."],[151,204.49,1.67,"And that’s a real shame."],[151,206.62,4.42,"The key is to link together the important moments of your story"],[151,211.04,3.29,"in a way that can be meaningful to someone else."],[151,214.79,2.67,"Otherwise, it may sound like you're just rambling"],[151,217.75,2.75,"or speaking for the sake of hearing yourself talk."],[151,221.01,4.55,"But, if the journey reveals something powerful you have learned,"],[151,225.64,4.63,"and if each step in your journey is revealed with humility and honesty"],[151,230.43,1.54,"and vulnerability,"],[151,232.06,2.79,"it is a journey we will gladly take with you."],[151,235.81,3.75,"There is one other piece that is absolutely essential"],[151,239.69,2.29,"if you’re going to tell your own story:"],[151,242.53,1.58,"it has to be true."],[151,244.95,1.33,"This may seem obvious,"],[151,246.53,3.0,"but sometimes speakers are tempted to exaggerate"],[151,249.79,2.29,"or even make a story up entirely."],[151,252.54,3.42,"Stories can have such a big impact that some speakers"],[151,255.96,1.96,"are tempted to bend the truth."],[151,258.3,4.84,"But even small exaggerations can quickly destroy your reputation."],[151,263.55,2.0,"It's not worth the risk."],[151,266.35,3.8,"When you combine a truthful story with a desire to benefit others,"],[151,270.43,3.17,"you can give your listeners an extraordinary gift."],[151,274.02,2.33,"A story can affect people deeply."],[151,276.48,3.55,"Use it, and you can greatly increase your connection with an audience."],[151,280.78,2.79,"But please, let it mean something."],[152,7.17,5.3,"It was the early 1800s, and Presbyterian minister Sylvester Graham"],[152,12.59,2.84,"believed Americans had a big problem—"],[152,15.64,3.29,"not just with their bodies, but with their souls."],[152,19.02,4.8,"He argued that overindulgence was fueling their baser desires,"],[152,23.98,3.63,"making them immoral and oversexed."],[152,27.98,4.75,"To curb this corruption, Graham and fellow puritanical reformers"],[152,32.82,5.76,"advocated for food they thought could curb a variety of appetites."],[152,39.08,1.83,"Following the American Revolution,"],[152,40.91,4.25,"things like excess alcohol use were increasingly perceived"],[152,45.17,5.38,"as blights on social order and civic virtue in a budding nation."],[152,50.96,2.75,"The 19th century Christian Temperance movement"],[152,53.72,5.17,"encouraged abstinence from alcohol, eventually leading to federal Prohibition."],[152,58.97,4.0,"But some branches of the movement promoted even more extreme restraint"],[152,62.98,1.5,"across the board."],[152,65.06,5.92,"These reformers had some special areas of interest, including the bedroom."],[152,71.19,1.25,"According to Graham,"],[152,72.44,6.38,"even divinely ordained marital sex could fall into harmfully excessive territory"],[152,78.83,2.58,"if it happened more than once a month."],[152,81.66,3.88,"And like certain Enlightenment philosophers a century before him,"],[152,85.54,4.42,"Graham considered the so-called solitary vice of masturbation"],[152,89.96,1.71,"even more hazardous."],[152,91.84,4.46,"He claimed it inflamed the brain and depleted the body's energy,"],[152,96.38,4.04,"causing everything from nervous disorders to stunted growth."],[152,101.06,2.17,"To address this issue and others,"],[152,103.22,5.38,"Graham recommended measures including plenty of sleep— on hard mattresses;"],[152,108.77,6.84,"regular bathing—with cold water; and fresh air— even in frigid temperatures."],[152,115.78,3.21,"But one of his major points of focus was diet."],[152,119.11,4.75,"He thought Americans were consuming decadent, flavorful food and drink"],[152,123.87,4.17,"that threw their bodies out of balance, weakened their self-control,"],[152,128.21,3.13,"and aroused un-Christian passions."],[152,131.84,4.59,"So to suppress their supposedly hyperactive libidos,"],[152,136.59,5.75,"Graham advocated for a vegetarian diet of whole grains, fruits, and vegetables."],[152,142.51,5.92,"And it needed to be bland— no pleasure-inciting spices to speak of."],[152,148.6,2.88,"His signature contribution was Graham bread,"],[152,151.61,2.92,"the precursor to the Graham cracker,"],[152,154.53,2.88,"made from coarse, whole wheat Graham flour."],[152,157.49,4.75,"He shared the recipe at no cost, encouraging everyone to bake their own."],[152,162.53,4.71,"It was simple: just add water or milk, yeast, and perhaps a pinch of salt—"],[152,167.33,3.46,"nothing that might overstimulate the senses."],[152,171.25,3.13,"Positioning himself as a public health advocate,"],[152,174.5,3.04,"Graham wrote books and held public lectures,"],[152,177.55,5.17,"alleging his diet would even help people survive a cholera outbreak."],[152,182.93,6.51,"However, not everyone was so keen on his moralizing message and tasteless fare—"],[152,189.44,4.38,"including angry bakers who protested outside his talks."],[152,193.94,4.67,"And Transcendentalist scholar Ralph Waldo Emerson cast doubt"],[152,198.61,2.71,"on the fanaticism of Graham’s movement."],[152,201.53,3.71,"One newspaper reporter is said to have mockingly dubbed Graham"],[152,205.24,2.71,"a “philosopher of sawdust pudding.”"],[152,208.29,3.5,"And an outraged critic of the Graham diet said it consisted of,"],[152,211.79,3.79,"“swill, starch slosh” and “dishwater.”"],[152,215.79,5.71,"But Graham also inspired ardent followers, who became known as Grahamites."],[152,222.18,4.34,"Meanwhile, Graham’s focus on digestive health appealed to figures"],[152,226.51,2.13,"like nutritionist James Jackson,"],[152,228.81,2.5,"who ran a health spa in upstate New York."],[152,231.48,6.71,"Around 1863, Jackson began baking crumbled, granulated Graham flour cakes"],[152,238.19,3.0,"into a cereal he called Granula."],[152,241.61,4.63,"And, in the 1890s, John Harvey Kellogg, head of another health spa,"],[152,246.28,4.29,"developed corn flakes by steaming, rolling, and baking corn"],[152,250.58,2.34,"into a similarly flavorless food."],[152,253.5,4.21,"While Jackson was primarily concerned with preventing illness through diet,"],[152,257.96,4.09,"Kellogg continued Graham’s crusade against masturbation."],[152,262.17,5.3,"He called it “self-abuse,” Implicated it in all manner of ailments,"],[152,267.47,6.13,"and similarly promoted a dull diet to temper overactive sexual appetites."],[152,273.94,3.46,"But the foods these men popularized would shapeshift"],[152,277.4,2.71,"far beyond their original forms."],[152,280.23,6.51,"Granola and Corn Flakes underwent a series of saccharine and frosted reinventions."],[152,286.82,2.08,"And decades after Graham’s death,"],[152,288.91,5.26,"the National Biscuit Company began mass-producing Graham crackers"],[152,294.21,3.21,"with lighter flours and stimulating sweeteners."],[152,297.58,6.26,"By 1927, people had started sandwiching toasted marshmallows and chocolate"],[152,303.84,1.96,"between Graham crackers,"],[152,305.8,3.29,"which the Girl Scouts christened “Some Mores,”"],[152,309.1,1.79,"and the s’more was born."],[152,311.43,5.21,"The decadent dessert was essentially the antithesis of Graham’s vision—"],[152,316.64,4.84,"and perhaps the ultimate ironic twist to his legacy."],[153,6.79,3.92,"In 1941, in German-occupied Poland,"],[153,10.88,4.0,"the Nazis began abducting members of the Jewish population"],[153,14.97,2.88,"and imprisoning them in extermination camps."],[153,18.14,2.21,"But there were exceptions—"],[153,20.43,4.5,"the Nazis held foreign passport holders in detention camps"],[153,24.94,3.88,"to be traded later for Germans detained abroad."],[153,29.07,3.63,"With this loophole in mind, Aleksander Ładoś,"],[153,32.74,2.63,"a Polish diplomat in Switzerland,"],[153,35.45,4.0,"started producing forged Latin American passports"],[153,39.62,2.71,"and smuggling them to Polish Jews."],[153,42.83,3.09,"In the end, these simple paper booklets"],[153,45.92,3.88,"would mean the difference between life and death for thousands."],[153,51.09,2.29,"A striking thing about passports"],[153,53.38,3.34,"is that they’re actually a relatively new invention."],[153,56.89,2.79,"They’re not the first form of travel documents:"],[153,59.85,5.67,"in ancient Egypt, travelers carried letters of safe conduct issued by rulers;"],[153,65.73,5.21,"in Han China and Tudor England, foreigners were issued travel passes;"],[153,71.07,1.67,"and in the 19th century,"],[153,72.9,4.92,"poorer migrants often needed health certificates to enter the US."],[153,78.2,2.34,"However, for much of human history,"],[153,80.74,5.84,"borders have been relatively fluid and travel lacked bureaucratic requirements."],[153,86.83,5.42,"But when World War I broke out in 1914, everything changed."],[153,92.55,4.84,"Neighboring countries, and their colonies, became wartime enemies."],[153,97.68,3.34,"To deter both espionage and desertion,"],[153,101.14,4.8,"many European nations established checkpoints at ports of entry"],[153,106.02,2.46,"to confirm travelers’ nationalities."],[153,108.69,4.67,"Here, authorities would screen early versions of passports,"],[153,113.36,2.34,"which, depending on the issuing country,"],[153,115.82,2.92,"were documents that followed different formats"],[153,118.74,2.84,"and contained varying types of information."],[153,122.12,5.3,"When the war ended, many expected nations to remove passport requirements."],[153,128.33,4.96,"In 1920, the newly established League of Nations discussed this,"],[153,133.46,2.96,"but in light of other post-war concerns,"],[153,136.42,4.96,"such as safeguarding national security, preventing the spread of epidemics,"],[153,141.64,2.59,"and managing the movement of refugees,"],[153,144.22,4.0,"they opted to temporarily keep the passport system."],[153,148.48,1.38,"To ease travel,"],[153,149.85,4.59,"they encouraged countries to follow international passport guidelines,"],[153,154.53,3.13,"using booklets of standard size and layout,"],[153,157.82,4.05,"written in the language of the issuing country and French."],[153,162.2,5.67,"But beyond this, every country was free to determine what approvals or documents"],[153,167.91,2.63,"a person needed to cross its borders."],[153,170.83,3.34,"This included rules around issuing visas,"],[153,174.29,4.88,"the legal permissions dictating how long a person can stay in a country,"],[153,179.18,3.0,"and what they can do, such as work or study."],[153,182.64,4.04,"And by 1926, when the League of Nations met again,"],[153,186.89,3.79,"the idea of abolishing passports had lost momentum,"],[153,190.94,3.71,"solidifying the passport system we know today."],[153,195.15,3.67,"However, this system brought new problems with it."],[153,199.07,3.38,"Passports were used not just to monitor movement,"],[153,202.49,3.25,"but also as tools of power and control."],[153,206.08,4.88,"They reinforced hierarchies between citizens born in colonizer states"],[153,210.96,2.04,"and those born in the colonies."],[153,213.38,2.42,"For example, in the 1920s,"],[153,215.96,4.04,"UK-born British subjects could get British passports,"],[153,220.22,5.59,"while Indian-born subjects could receive a distinct British Indian passport."],[153,226.18,3.0,"And since passports are issued by governments,"],[153,229.18,5.3,"they could be withheld from citizens as punishment or to discourage dissent."],[153,234.9,3.54,"During the Cold War, the US revoked the passports"],[153,238.44,5.26,"of political and civil rights activists, like W.E.B. Dubois."],[153,243.91,4.5,"As passport monitoring was largely relocated into airports"],[153,248.41,2.54,"in the second half of the 20th century,"],[153,250.96,3.21,"advances in digital tracking and databases"],[153,254.29,5.13,"allowed governments to more tightly regulate and control individual movement."],[153,259.8,5.13,"Today, a passport’s strength and where a person can travel to and from"],[153,264.93,3.67,"are often dependent on relationships between countries."],[153,268.72,3.54,"There can be regional agreements, like the Schengen Agreement,"],[153,272.27,4.5,"which allows border-free travel within 29 European countries."],[153,277.06,4.84,"Higher-income countries also typically have better travel access."],[153,282.19,4.46,"The result is a system that privileges citizens of certain countries,"],[153,286.66,1.71,"while constraining others."],[153,289.16,6.71,"As of 2025, a US passport holder can enter almost 180 countries"],[153,295.88,2.79,"without arranging a visa beforehand."],[153,299.0,4.5,"For a Pakistani passport holder, that number drops below 40."],[153,303.84,5.96,"And these disparities extend to a person’s chances of securing a foreign visa."],[153,310.26,2.21,"For example, in 2024,"],[153,312.64,3.55,"Algerian applicants for US visitor visas"],[153,316.19,4.3,"faced a much higher refusal rate than Brazilian applicants."],[153,320.82,3.09,"In some places, money can buy you access—"],[153,324.11,4.5,"more than a dozen countries offer \"golden passports” programs"],[153,328.62,1.58,"that grant citizenship,"],[153,330.2,5.84,"and therefore passports, to people who make a significant investment or donation."],[153,336.71,3.8,"As the world becomes increasingly global for some,"],[153,340.5,2.63,"and increasingly divided for others,"],[153,343.3,1.63,"the question remains:"],[153,344.92,4.88,"should lines on a map determine a person’s rights and freedoms?"],[154,7.29,3.17,"Order, order! Who do we have on the stand today?"],[154,10.46,4.34,"Queen Victoria... wasn’t she the definition of straight laced?"],[154,15.22,1.33,"Indeed, Your Honor."],[154,16.6,5.59,"Throughout her 63-year reign, she was a tireless model of morality and restraint."],[154,22.31,4.5,"So much so that the 19th century is popularly called the “Victorian Age”"],[154,26.81,3.09,"and is still associated with those very qualities."],[154,30.4,4.25,"Unfortunately, this era is also associated with rigid gender roles,"],[154,34.66,4.13,"deep class divisions, and the subjugation of colonized peoples—"],[154,38.78,4.09,"all reinforced by Queen Victoria's so-called virtues."],[154,42.95,3.46,"Her moralistic approach to monarchy was more concerned"],[154,46.42,3.67,"with making the British Empire look like a big happy family"],[154,50.13,3.04,"than actually helping anyone inside it."],[154,53.63,2.67,"I guess appearances can be deceiving."],[154,56.84,1.54,"In her defense, Your Honor,"],[154,58.39,3.38,"Victoria came to the throne at the tender age of 18"],[154,61.85,4.55,"and faced immediate pressure to restore public faith in the crown."],[154,66.56,4.59,"The previous two kings had been drinkers, womanizers, and gamblers."],[154,71.23,4.8,"Meanwhile, Victoria was expected to serve as the country's moral compass"],[154,76.16,3.92,"during the dizzying changes brought by industrialization."],[154,80.53,2.04,"Well, she certainly got her chance."],[154,82.75,3.34,"Her reign coincided with the birth of mass media,"],[154,86.12,3.92,"giving her the power to exert immense cultural influence."],[154,90.21,5.09,"In newspapers, she appeared as the perfect wife to her beloved Prince Albert."],[154,95.47,4.05,"Press images of her pure white wedding dress started a fashion trend"],[154,99.59,1.63,"that continues to this day."],[154,101.51,4.46,"And front pages featuring their family’s idyllic Christmas celebrations"],[154,106.14,2.92,"set a new standard for middle class society."],[154,109.15,3.5,"Specifically a standard that shamed any family"],[154,112.65,3.04,"who couldn’t afford a picture-perfect holiday."],[154,116.53,4.21,"She had to cultivate the image of a dutiful wife and dedicated mother"],[154,120.74,2.29,"to meet popular expectations—"],[154,123.03,3.71,"even though her relationship with her children was sometimes stormy."],[154,126.91,2.42,"Besides, she wasn't entirely traditional."],[154,129.42,3.21,"While religious leaders and some doctors argued against"],[154,132.63,2.71,"using pain medication during childbirth,"],[154,135.34,4.3,"Victoria made the practice socially acceptable by using anesthesia"],[154,139.72,2.25,"during the births of two of her children."],[154,142.05,4.92,"And she publicly supported the entry of women into the field of nursing."],[154,147.44,3.25,"She also opposed giving women the right to vote,"],[154,150.69,3.5,"and believed they should focus on their household responsibilities"],[154,154.32,1.5,"instead of politics."],[154,155.86,4.42,"She viewed her position as a powerful woman as an accident of birth,"],[154,160.41,2.13,"not as a model for her gender."],[154,163.24,2.79,"Well, that's not exactly the most inspiring message."],[154,166.45,4.25,"Victoria simply mirrored and reinforced larger cultural trends;"],[154,170.71,1.21,"she didn’t create them."],[154,172.04,4.04,"Besides, her public persona was the only reliable tool she had"],[154,176.09,1.92,"for affecting change in Britain."],[154,178.17,2.0,"As a constitutional monarch,"],[154,180.18,3.59,"the Queen was expected to act on the advice of her Prime Minister."],[154,183.89,3.0,"And while some of the ministers she worked with during her reign"],[154,186.89,3.5,"actively encouraged her to shape Britain’s politics,"],[154,190.39,3.5,"others, like Lord John Russell, dismissed her ideas."],[154,194.11,4.0,"Yet, when Russell’s government failed to address the Irish Potato Famine,"],[154,198.11,1.84,"Victoria received the blame."],[154,200.03,3.92,"In fact, she’s still known as the “Famine Queen” to this day."],[154,204.62,4.5,"That moniker may have more to do with her measly personal donation"],[154,209.21,2.5,"of £2,000 to famine relief,"],[154,211.83,4.09,"which was less than 1% of her annual stipend."],[154,216.09,3.63,"And when the Ottoman sultan wanted to donate £10,000,"],[154,219.72,2.92,"the British Embassy talked him down to 1,000"],[154,222.76,4.05,"so his generosity wouldn’t make Victoria look bad!"],[154,226.97,1.67,"Just like everything in her reign,"],[154,228.64,3.67,"Victoria cared more about her reputation than her people."],[154,232.98,3.96,"She was actually very aware of how difficult her subjects’ lives were."],[154,237.11,4.84,"As a child, her mother took her on tours around the UK to see how people lived."],[154,242.07,3.29,"And as an adult, she read authors like Charles Dickens"],[154,245.37,2.38,"to better understand the lower classes."],[154,247.99,1.75,"She backed social reforms"],[154,249.75,2.84,"like the establishment of state-funded public schools"],[154,252.62,2.96,"and the Public Health Act of 1875,"],[154,255.58,3.88,"which aimed to improve sanitation and living conditions."],[154,260.01,3.67,"While I'm sure those policies helped her domestic subjects,"],[154,263.72,4.55,"Victoria frequently ignored her countless more international citizens."],[154,268.43,4.63,"She believed that Britain should be an empire over which the sun never set,"],[154,273.14,4.09,"meaning she completely supported colonial expansion."],[154,277.81,2.92,"It’s true— Victoria was a product of her time"],[154,280.74,2.59,"and believed in Britain’s imperial destiny."],[154,283.57,3.42,"But she was also interested in learning about the various cultures"],[154,286.99,1.17,"within her empire,"],[154,288.16,3.5,"developing close relationships with Sarah Forbes Bonnetta,"],[154,291.66,2.59,"a formerly enslaved West African woman,"],[154,294.29,4.84,"and with Abdul Karim, an Indian secretary who taught her Hindustani."],[154,299.46,3.42,"Victoria and Albert were also known abolitionists"],[154,302.92,2.79,"who supported deploying the Royal Navy"],[154,305.72,3.13,"to combat West Africa's illegal slave trade."],[154,309.43,1.71,"Noble efforts, no doubt."],[154,311.14,3.54,"But Victoria did nothing to counter the violent oppression"],[154,314.69,4.5,"and paternalistic racism that marked Britain's colonial expansion."],[154,319.48,3.25,"These colonies’ inhabitants were frequently mistreated,"],[154,322.86,3.46,"and they could only advance so far in their country’s government,"],[154,326.32,2.08,"even if they were British educated."],[154,328.66,1.33,"Let's not sugarcoat it."],[154,330.12,3.92,"Under her watch, British forces brutally crushed uprisings"],[154,334.04,2.34,"in South Asia and Africa,"],[154,336.54,1.46,"looted resources,"],[154,338.0,4.55,"and enforced racial hierarchies that lasted long after her reign."],[154,343.13,2.79,"Doesn’t exactly sound like a “big happy family” to me."],[154,346.72,4.17,"Queen Victoria might have tried to hold her empire to a higher standard,"],[154,350.89,4.21,"but should we judge leaders by their ideals or their outcomes?"],[154,355.31,4.63,"These are the questions we have to ask when putting history on trial."],[155,0.08,2.08,"In addition to being the Age of Dinosaurs,"],[155,2.16,4.16,"the Cretaceous Period is famous for enormous ocean-dwelling beasts –"],[155,6.32,2.48,"giant sea turtles, massive predatory fish,"],[155,8.8,3.2,"and mosasaurs that could grow more than 13 meters long."],[155,12.0,3.04,"But it wasn’t just animals with backbones growing huge back then."],[155,15.04,4.16,"Deep within those same oceans, there lurked real-life krakens –"],[155,19.2,3.6,"octopuses that dwarfed any of their living relatives,"],[155,22.8,4.64,"big enough to tangle with even the most immense of their aquatic neighbors."],[155,27.44,4.56,"The biggest octopuses, and maybe the biggest invertebrates, of all time."],[155,32.0,4.08,"The discovery of these sea monsters is not only just plain awesome –"],[155,36.08,3.68,"it also changes what we know about ancient marine food webs,"],[155,39.76,3.78,"and rewrites the timeline of squid and octopus evolution."],[155,46.88,3.68,"The fossil record is pretty hot and cold when it comes to cephalopods –"],[155,50.56,2.64,"that’s the group that includes octopus and squid."],[155,53.2,3.12,"Species with shells are very well-represented in the fossil record."],[155,56.32,3.12,"This includes the spiral-shelled nautiloids and ammonites,"],[155,59.44,2.16,"and the cone-shelled belemnites."],[155,61.6,2.16,"Those hard shells make for great fossils."],[155,63.76,4.48,"But octopus and squid have mostly done away with that shell through their evolution,"],[155,68.24,4.08,"and their squishy bodies tend to rot away before they can become fossilized."],[155,72.32,2.8,"But these softies do have some hard parts –"],[155,75.12,4.48,"their beak-like jaws are stiffened with chitin like the exoskeleton of insects,"],[155,79.6,4.0,"and some species have small bits of internal shells called gladii."],[155,83.6,4.24,"Often, these resilient structures are all that survives into the fossil record."],[155,87.84,3.92,"It’s not much, but even these scant remains can reveal unexpected things"],[155,91.76,1.6,"about ancient ocean life."],[155,93.36,1.52,"During the Late Cretaceous Period,"],[155,94.88,4.72,"the North Pacific Ocean was home to a whole bunch of soft-bodied cephalopods."],[155,99.6,4.08,"Jaws and gladii from a variety of ancient squids and octopuses"],[155,103.68,5.28,"have been discovered in fossil deposits in Hokkaido, Japan and Vancouver Island, Canada."],[155,108.96,1.44,"And some of them were big …"],[155,110.4,1.2,"like, really big."],[155,111.6,2.24,"Scientists have estimated that the largest ones"],[155,113.84,2.8,"might have been the size of modern-day giant squid."],[155,116.64,3.2,"But a 2026 study showed that this was wrong."],[155,119.84,2.8,"They were actually bigger. Much bigger."],[155,122.64,2.56,"The biggest cephalopods in Earth’s history."],[155,125.2,2.64,"That study started by re-examining 15 of"],[155,127.84,4.08,"these large fossilized jaws from Hokkaido and Vancouver Island,"],[155,131.92,3.52,"ranging from around 100 million to 72 million years ago."],[155,135.44,4.4,"These included two different species of an ancient genus called Nanaimoteuthis."],[155,139.84,4.88,"The researchers also found a dozen new sets of jaws using a clever imaging strategy."],[155,144.72,2.4,"They carved into some rocks from Hokkaido,"],[155,147.12,3.04,"photographing each uncovered layer as they went,"],[155,150.16,2.56,"and then developed an artificial intelligence model"],[155,152.72,5.44,"to stitch the photographs together into three-dimensional images of the fragile fossils"],[155,158.16,1.84,"that had been hiding within the rock."],[155,160.0,4.0,"The new jaws they uncovered included one absolute monster –"],[155,164.0,4.72,"a set of jaws about one-and-a-half times the size of a giant squid’s jaws."],[155,168.72,4.96,"This is our first glimpse at the enormous kraken of the Cretaceous seas!"],[155,173.68,1.6,"With only the jaws to go on,"],[155,175.28,3.52,"figuring out the actual size of this kraken is tough, but not impossible."],[155,178.8,1.2,"In modern cephalopods,"],[155,180.0,3.6,"the size of the jaws correlates pretty well with the size of the mantle –"],[155,183.6,2.56,"that’s the main part of the body where all the guts are –"],[155,186.16,3.52,"and with the total length of the body and arms."],[155,189.68,3.84,"But this ratio can vary a bit between different cephalopod lineages,"],[155,193.52,3.44,"so first, we need to know just what sort of cephalopod we’re dealing with."],[155,196.96,2.08,"Nanaimoteuthis was originally identified"],[155,199.04,3.04,"as belonging to a family called Vampyroteuthidae,"],[155,202.08,2.32,"which includes modern-day vampire squid."],[155,204.4,4.64,"These are small, deep-sea critters with a cloak of webbing between their eight arms."],[155,209.04,2.48,"But the new study identified features of these jaws"],[155,211.52,2.32,"that separate them from the vampires,"],[155,213.84,2.88,"and instead link them to a group called Cirrata,"],[155,216.72,1.92,"also known as finned octopuses."],[155,218.64,2.8,"Broadly speaking, octopuses come in two varieties."],[155,221.44,4.08,"Some are finless, like your classic blue-ringed and giant Pacific species,"],[155,225.52,3.68,"and others have fin-like extensions coming off of their mantles,"],[155,229.2,2.72,"like the adorable deep-sea Dumbo octopus."],[155,231.92,2.8,"Some finned octopus species have elongated bodies,"],[155,234.72,3.76,"kind of like a squid, while others have shorter, rounded bodies."],[155,238.48,3.68,"Fortunately, body shape also correlates with the shape of the jaws."],[155,242.16,2.0,"So, just from their jaw anatomy,"],[155,244.16,2.48,"the researchers could identify Nanaimoteuthis"],[155,246.64,3.28,"as ancient, long-bodied, finned octopuses."],[155,249.92,4.24,"And by examining jaw-to-body ratios of their closest living relatives,"],[155,254.16,3.52,"they could estimate the full size of these Cretaceous krakens."],[155,257.68,3.92,"The smaller of the two species is Nanaimoteuthis jeletzkyi."],[155,261.6,4.08,"Its jaws indicate a mantle more than half a meter in length"],[155,265.68,3.04,"and possibly up to almost two meters,"],[155,268.72,2.0,"and a full body – arms and all –"],[155,270.72,2.4,"as much as seven-and-a-half meters long."],[155,273.12,2.0,"That beats our biggest living octopuses,"],[155,275.12,1.76,"North Pacific giant octopuses,"],[155,276.88,2.64,"which can have a total arm span of six meters."],[155,279.52,3.44,"However, it’s not nearly on par with giant squid,"],[155,282.96,2.08,"our largest living cephalopods,"],[155,285.04,3.28,"whose arms and body can stretch an impressive 12 meters long."],[155,288.32,1.92,"But the second of these fossil species,"],[155,290.24,3.92,"Nanaimoteuthis haggarti, is truly enormous,"],[155,294.16,3.76,"with jaws up to 50% larger than giant squid jaws."],[155,297.92,5.36,"The researchers estimate its mantle size ranging between 1.5 and 4.5 meters,"],[155,303.28,5.52,"with a total body length between 6.6 and 18.6 meters."],[155,308.8,4.16,"That upper size estimate is not only much longer than giant squid,"],[155,312.96,4.08,"it also rivals the largest of the Cretaceous marine reptiles."],[155,317.04,4.16,"Of course, with only the jaws preserved, its exact size is hard to say for sure."],[155,321.2,4.4,"But even the midpoint of that size estimate makes Nanaimoteuthis haggarti"],[155,325.6,2.0,"at least the size of a giant squid."],[155,327.6,3.92,"This means that while dinosaurs were stomping around on land and pterosaurs"],[155,331.52,1.6,"were flying through the skies,"],[155,333.12,4.56,"the ocean was home to perhaps the largest octopus that has ever lived."],[155,337.68,3.12,"Inferring the lifestyle of these krakens is tough."],[155,340.8,3.36,"We have a hard enough time understanding the giant cephalopods"],[155,344.16,1.6,"of our present-day oceans!"],[155,345.76,3.04,"Giant squid are the largest living invertebrates by length,"],[155,348.8,3.04,"and colossal squid are the largest in terms of weight,"],[155,351.84,2.48,"reaching up to almost 500 kilograms."],[155,354.32,2.88,"Both species live in cold, deep ocean waters,"],[155,357.2,3.04,"and both have hardly ever been seen by humans."],[155,360.24,5.84,"The first video of a live giant squid in the wild wasn’t captured until 2005!"],[155,366.08,0.8,"Needless to say,"],[155,366.88,2.96,"we have almost zero idea of what these animals are doing"],[155,369.84,1.6,"in their day-to-day lives."],[155,371.44,2.56,"But we do know a bit about their diets."],[155,374.0,1.92,"Some lucky scientists have had the chance"],[155,375.92,2.16,"to plunder the stomachs of giant squid"],[155,378.08,3.28,"and have found the remains of fish, crustaceans, clams,"],[155,381.36,1.2,"and even other squid."],[155,382.56,2.56,"Others have performed studies on the nitrogen composition"],[155,385.12,1.52,"of colossal squid tissues –"],[155,386.64,2.16,"which can reflect the diet of the animal –"],[155,388.8,4.24,"and have found that they match the chemical profile of a top predator."],[155,393.04,2.08,"So both of our modern-day giant cephalopods"],[155,395.12,2.48,"sit at the top of their respective food webs."],[155,397.6,1.76,"Of course, they’re not alone up there –"],[155,399.36,2.08,"the beaks of giant squids have been found"],[155,401.44,2.64,"in the bellies of large sharks and sperm whales."],[155,404.08,3.04,"We don’t have any stomach contents for Nanaimoteuthis,"],[155,407.12,2.72,"but their jaws give us some clues as to what they were eating."],[155,409.84,3.6,"As an octopus eats, its food causes wear and tear on its jaws."],[155,413.44,3.84,"The jaws of Nanaimoteuthis are covered in chips and scratches"],[155,417.28,3.36,"which indicate a diet of hard foods like shells and bones."],[155,420.64,1.6,"In the largest specimens,"],[155,422.24,3.52,"as much as 10% of the jaw length had been lost"],[155,425.76,1.6,"due to wear from feeding!"],[155,427.36,3.12,"The size of the jaws would seem to suggest a powerful bite,"],[155,430.48,3.76,"and this idea is supported by some proportionately large areas"],[155,434.24,1.68,"for jaw muscles to attach."],[155,435.92,2.96,"With powerful jaws and a bunch of massive arms,"],[155,438.88,4.64,"the Cretaceous krakens would have been able to catch and eat fish of all sizes,"],[155,443.52,1.6,"spiral-shelled ammonites,"],[155,445.12,3.44,"maybe even other large predators like mosasaurs and plesiosaurs."],[155,448.56,2.16,"And just like today’s giant cephalopods,"],[155,450.72,2.16,"these krakens might have been on the menu"],[155,452.88,2.96,"for other big, powerful carnivores in the seas."],[155,455.84,1.44,"Someday, if we’re really lucky,"],[155,457.28,4.08,"we might find a well-preserved kraken with food still in its stomach."],[155,461.36,2.96,"Until then, we can only make the scientifically-informed hypothesis"],[155,464.32,2.48,"that they ate probably whatever they wanted."],[155,466.8,4.16,"So, if you were planning on taking a trip through time to sail the Cretaceous seas …"],[155,470.96,1.6,"well, maybe don’t."],[155,472.56,0.88,"But there’s more!"],[155,473.44,2.48,"These aren’t just among the largest octopuses,"],[155,475.92,1.6,"they’re also among the earliest."],[155,477.52,1.68,"Fossils of the smaller species,"],[155,479.2,4.48,"Nanaimoteuthis jeletzkyi, go back to 100 million years old,"],[155,483.68,2.96,"making it the oldest known octopus in the fossil record."],[155,486.64,2.72,"But we only know that because of a different study"],[155,489.36,3.12,"that was published literally two weeks before the kraken paper."],[155,492.48,1.12,"See, for a long time,"],[155,493.6,3.84,"there was a lot of confusion over the timeline of octopus evolution."],[155,497.44,2.48,"That’s because nearly all known octopus fossils"],[155,499.92,3.76,"were from the Late Cretaceous or younger … except for one."],[155,503.68,4.72,"Pohlsepia mazonensis was scientifically described in the year 2000."],[155,508.4,3.04,"It comes from the Mazon Creek fossil beds of Illinois –"],[155,511.44,3.28,"that’s the same fossil locality as some other famous ancient invertebrates"],[155,514.72,0.96,"like the Tully Monster."],[155,515.68,3.6,"The fossils at this site are a little over 300 million years old."],[155,519.28,2.0,"Like other Mazon Creek critters,"],[155,521.28,3.44,"Pohlsepia was discovered within a chunk of ironstone,"],[155,524.72,2.4,"which had preserved its soft body,"],[155,527.12,2.64,"allowing researchers to get a close look at its"],[155,529.76,3.44,"mantle, fins, and arms, spotting several features"],[155,533.2,1.76,"that identified it as an octopus."],[155,534.96,1.28,"This was an exciting discovery,"],[155,536.24,2.08,"but it also presented a conundrum:"],[155,538.32,4.16,"all other fossil octopuses were under 100 million years old,"],[155,542.48,2.64,"but this one is more than three times that age!"],[155,545.12,3.28,"This implied a long “evolutionary fuse” –"],[155,548.4,2.88,"basically, that octopuses evolved way early on,"],[155,551.28,4.16,"but remained a relatively minor component of aquatic ecosystems"],[155,555.44,3.44,"until a burst of diversity occurred during the Cretaceous Period."],[155,558.88,2.64,"It wouldn’t be the first such case in the fossil record,"],[155,561.52,5.12,"but there were still debates around some of Pohlsepia’s supposed octopus features."],[155,566.64,1.92,"So, hoping to resolve the issue,"],[155,568.56,3.84,"a 2026 study did a detailed X-ray scan of the fossil."],[155,572.4,3.44,"Deep within the sediment, hidden from paleontologists previously,"],[155,575.84,2.0,"the study found a radula -"],[155,577.84,4.16,"a tongue-like structure found in mollusks like snails and octopuses."],[155,582.0,2.96,"But the anatomy of the radula doesn’t match"],[155,584.96,3.52,"what we see in octopus, or squid …"],[155,588.48,2.56,"instead, Pohlsepia is a nautiloid."],[155,591.04,4.08,"Nautiloids are a very ancient group of shelled cephalopods."],[155,595.12,4.48,"Today, their only living representatives are the rare and mysterious nautiluses,"],[155,599.6,3.04,"but they were much more common in the past."],[155,602.64,4.72,"300 million years old is a very reasonable time period to find nautiloids."],[155,607.36,2.4,"In fact, those hidden internal features revealed"],[155,609.76,4.64,"that Pohlsepia is the same as an already-known Mazon Creek nautiloid"],[155,614.4,1.68,"named Paleocadmus!"],[155,616.08,2.64,"The original misidentification of Pohlsepia"],[155,618.72,3.36,"can be blamed in part on the condition of the fossil."],[155,622.08,5.28,"The 2026 study identified signs of decomposition in the fossilized tissues,"],[155,627.36,2.96,"suggesting that the carcass had broken down a bunch"],[155,630.32,2.08,"before becoming sealed away in iron."],[155,632.4,3.52,"That process of decomposition had separated the body from its shell"],[155,635.92,3.84,"and had obscured some of the characteristic nautiloid soft tissues."],[155,639.76,1.92,"So, with Pohlsepia reclassified,"],[155,641.68,4.24,"it’s likely that octopuses didn’t evolve until close to the Cretaceous Period,"],[155,645.92,2.88,"and it leaves Nanaimoteuthis as the current record-holder"],[155,648.8,2.08,"for oldest known fossil octopus."],[155,650.88,4.56,"It seems octopuses wasted little time evolving into gigantic apex predators."],[155,655.44,2.08,"Oh, and they were probably smart, too."],[155,657.52,2.48,"Modern octopuses are famously intelligent,"],[155,660.0,2.64,"great at solving puzzles and escaping their enclosures –"],[155,662.64,2.72,"just ask anyone who’s ever worked with one at an aquarium."],[155,665.36,4.8,"Now, estimating the intelligence of an extinct species is just about impossible,"],[155,670.16,4.08,"but the jaws of Nanaimoteuthis do hold one more little surprise –"],[155,674.24,2.64,"the feeding damage is asymmetrical."],[155,676.88,5.76,"That means these krakens favored one side of their jaw more than the other while eating."],[155,682.64,1.92,"They were left- or right-handed …"],[155,684.56,1.6,"or, in this case, jawed."],[155,686.16,1.6,"This is called lateralization,"],[155,687.76,3.28,"and it’s often associated with higher cognitive function –"],[155,691.04,1.36,"that is, smarter animals."],[155,692.4,4.56,"So, given their asymmetric jaws and their notoriously clever living cousins,"],[155,696.96,3.76,"it’s a good bet that these ancient giants were pretty smart."],[155,700.72,4.24,"And that would have made them even more versatile and dangerous predators."],[155,704.96,4.48,"So if you’re gonna ignore our advice and go sailing on the Cretaceous sea …"],[155,709.44,2.1,"you might need a bigger boat."],[156,2.76,1.92,"To help her in her quest,"],[156,4.68,3.48,"she's equipped with three superpowers."],[156,9.6,4.08,"First, an amazing approach to getting about."],[156,19.56,2.68,"Portia is a jumping spider..."],[156,25.76,4.84,"..able to leap up to 50 times her own body length."],[156,33.96,3.2,"Nowhere seems beyond her reach."],[156,40.12,2.48,"Next, her second superpower –"],[156,42.6,1.52,"superb eyesight."],[156,46.36,2.68,"Essential if she's to distinguish"],[156,49.04,2.08,"her prey in all this clutter."],[156,53.16,3.36,"Because her prey doesn't stray."],[156,60.0,3.96,"Portia is a spider-eating spider."],[156,66.64,2.0,"This raises a few problems."],[156,69.64,3.4,"Her lunch is three times her size…"],[156,74.64,1.84,"..packed with venom…"],[156,77.64,2.48,"..and surrounded by a sticky trap."],[156,84.12,2.04,"Mission impossible?"],[156,86.16,1.12,"Not at all,"],[156,87.28,3.2,"because of her third superpower."],[156,91.28,1.96,"Portia is a genius."],[156,97.64,3.6,"She can map her world in three dimensions"],[156,102.12,2.84,"and formulate a plan of attack."],[156,107.52,1.76,"She can have an idea."],[156,129.24,2.88,"The web-builder is blind."],[156,133.44,2.56,"It won't have a clue that she's coming."],[156,159.88,2.12,"Right on target"],[156,162.0,2.48,"and safely behind those fangs."],[156,173.04,2.56,"But a mind as active as Portia's"],[156,175.6,3.84,"can always do with more brain food."],[156,182.6,3.76,"Here, there's no anchor point for the abseil."],[156,190.04,3.2,"But Portia has another idea."],[156,195.72,2.76,"Instead of going to the spider…"],[156,203.4,2.56,"..she will bring the spider to her."],[156,211.12,5.12,"She plucks the strands to imitate struggling prey…"],[156,220.2,2.88,"..drawing the spider in..."],[156,226.36,1.96,"..to its death."],[156,230.92,2.84,"It's called Darwin's bark spider,"],[156,233.76,3.0,"and the female has a remarkable strategy."],[156,256.12,3.0,"Like a real-life Spider-Woman,"],[156,259.12,5.72,"she sprays strands of silk in one long, continuous flow."],[156,268.08,2.44,"The threads fan out like a sail"],[156,270.52,3.68,"and drift on air currents blowing across the water."],[156,275.44,3.24,"Every few seconds, she crimps the strands together"],[156,278.68,1.88,"to stop them spreading too widely."],[156,283.64,2.12,"The breeze will do the rest,"],[156,285.76,2.76,"blowing the threads into a single line"],[156,288.52,2.4,"and a 25-metre bridge."],[156,294.0,2.56,"Now she must reinforce her bridge"],[156,296.56,2.52,"because her web will hang from it."],[156,304.4,3.76,"But there's something bouncing the line at the other end."],[156,309.04,4.76,"Another Darwin's spider is trying to take advantage of her hard work."],[156,320.84,2.8,"She must deal with the intruder head-on."],[156,327.88,2.88,"The cut line is an inconvenience,"],[156,330.76,1.4,"but no more than that."],[156,334.72,2.04,"With hooks on the tips of each leg,"],[156,336.76,2.0,"she gathers in the thread."],[156,343.84,3.56,"It won't go to waste, as she'll eat it later."],[156,354.6,1.72,"When it's all reeled in,"],[156,356.32,1.8,"she sprays again."],[156,361.36,4.52,"Out streams another 25-metre bridging line."],[156,373.2,2.64,"How a spider no bigger than a thumbnail"],[156,375.84,2.84,"can produce so much silk so quickly"],[156,378.68,1.48,"has baffled scientists."],[156,384.16,2.32,"And it's no ordinary silk."],[156,386.88,2.56,"It's the toughest natural fibre on the planet,"],[156,389.44,1.84,"tougher than steel."],[156,393.6,3.6,"And it needs to be tough to span the wide river."],[156,400.4,3.16,"With the bridge taut and a ground anchor in place,"],[156,403.96,2.44,"it's time to construct her trap."],[156,419.08,4.16,"These spiders can build the world's largest orb webs,"],[156,423.24,1.96,"up to two metres wide."],[156,428.32,3.52,"A few hours from the first spray of bridging line,"],[156,431.84,1.4,"the job is done."],[156,437.88,2.24,"Now, her strategy is simple –"],[156,441.0,1.88,"sit and wait."],[156,462.32,3.52,"And there's one final bout of silk production…"],[156,467.64,2.4,"..shrink-wrapping her food for later."],[156,470.04,2.64,"That is the weirdest thing I've ever seen in my life."],[156,472.68,1.68,"It's a fly."],[156,474.36,2.0,"It hasn't got any wings, and it's got these..."],[156,476.36,3.52,"[Narrator] George McGavin's great passion is for insects."],[156,480.48,1.28,"- At first I thought it was a cricket,"],[156,481.76,1.48,"a tiny cricket,"],[156,483.24,1.2,"but it's a bat fly."],[156,484.44,2.32,"Insects make ecosystems work."],[156,486.76,2.6,"Without bees, for instance, you'd have no flowering plants."],[156,489.36,1.68,"You'd have no vegetables, no fruit."],[156,491.04,3.44,"So without the insects, you simply wouldn't have the big animals."],[156,495.44,2.92,"[Narrator] From the tiniest bugs to the largest birds,"],[156,498.68,3.28,"there's over a million known species living in rainforests."],[156,504.32,2.88,"George believes there could be another five million"],[156,507.2,2.0,"still waiting to be discovered."],[156,512.44,2.04,"[George] Ooh, I say, look at that."],[156,515.28,5.84,"Fallen trees like this are an amazing resource for insects and bugs and stuff."],[156,521.12,3.68,"They're not always hollow enough to crawl inside."],[156,524.88,1.72,"It is big. It is a biggie."],[156,526.96,1.44,"This is going to be epic."],[156,529.12,2.2,"Now there's a bat. Look, look! Over there."],[156,531.48,0.84,"See it?"],[156,532.32,1.36,"There's about three of them."],[156,534.24,1.84,"The thing is, is there anything else in there?"],[156,536.08,0.76,"That's the worry."],[156,536.84,3.24,"Let's go, let's see what's in here."],[156,540.24,4.12,"It's like crawling into a large peaty tube."],[156,544.8,3.6,"[Narrator] It's an extreme habitat, very rarely explored."],[156,548.4,3.0,"And straightaway, he's coming up against new creatures."],[156,551.4,1.2,"[George] This is amazing."],[156,552.6,1.44,"Look what we've got here."],[156,554.32,2.8,"This is a whip spider."],[156,557.12,2.8,"These long bits are actually its front legs."],[156,560.64,1.96,"And because it lives in darkness –"],[156,562.6,2.56,"you can see how it's just feeling my finger there, see –"],[156,565.16,3.28,"it uses these very long legs to feel its prey"],[156,568.44,2.96,"and its prey are these cave crickets here."],[156,572.4,1.6,"And very sneakily sometimes,"],[156,574.0,2.88,"the whip spiders reach behind the cricket"],[156,576.88,2.32,"and just go tickle, tickle on the back end"],[156,579.28,3.84,"and the cricket jumps forward into the jaws of the whip spider."],[156,583.52,1.76,"It's a really clever trick."],[156,586.48,1.8,"The further I go into this,"],[156,588.92,2.8,"there's bigger and bigger whip spiders."],[156,591.84,2.44,"Oh, there's an absolute monster over here."],[156,594.36,2.52,"There are now, on this part of the roof,"],[156,596.88,3.68,"13 crickets and five whip spiders"],[156,600.6,3.52,"who are just queuing up to eat them like crazy."],[156,604.6,3.32,"[Narrator] Little is known about the biology of these weird predators."],[156,608.24,3.28,"Somehow George has to catch one to take it back to the lab."],[156,613.76,2.32,"[George] One's just crawled across my face."],[156,622.72,3.2,"Because it's now getting really, really tight,"],[156,626.84,1.6,"I have a bit of a problem."],[156,631.16,3.28,"[Narrator] It's as hot as a sauna and crawling with bugs."],[156,634.76,2.24,"George is in his element."],[156,637.36,1.04,"[George] Ah!"],[156,639.56,1.6,"Ah, just one sec."],[156,643.0,2.76,"I can't get out of this. This hole's too tight."],[156,645.88,0.68,"Ah!"],[156,647.52,0.92,"Argh!"],[156,651.84,3.36,"I feel like a beetle grub emerging"],[156,655.6,5.36,"from its long period of metamorphosis. Ha-ha!"],[156,661.6,1.72,"Actually, I am stuck."],[156,664.52,1.12,"All I can say is,"],[156,666.36,3.04,"I don't think I've had as much fun in a log for a long time."],[156,669.64,1.16,"Argh!"],[156,672.08,3.0,"[Narrator] Usually George only sees these bizarre beasts"],[156,675.08,2.04,"dead in a museum drawer."],[156,677.64,2.32,"It's a rare chance to examine them alive."],[156,679.96,1.36,"[George] Look at that. Isn't that wonderful?"],[156,682.6,3.68,"That is the ultimate animal for hunting for prey in the dark."],[156,686.36,1.56,"It's flattened, it's fast."],[156,687.92,3.8,"It's got big, spiky palps at the front end."],[156,691.8,2.96,"Incredibly long legs for just feeling its prey."],[156,694.76,4.28,"If you designed an organism for catching crickets in the dark,"],[156,699.04,0.76,"this is it."],[156,699.8,5.0,"And once the poor animal is trapped in those spines at the front,"],[156,704.8,1.4,"there is no escape from that."],[156,706.2,3.44,"Predators all over the world use the element of disguise"],[156,709.68,1.88,"to dupe their food."],[156,713.28,2.2,"By pretending to be a harmless branch,"],[156,715.68,3.68,"this African vine snake tricks its prey into thinking they're safe…"],[156,721.2,2.44,"..when they could be about to get eaten."],[156,725.72,2.84,"In Hawaii, a fly is in for a shock…"],[156,730.48,3.64,"..from a carnivorous caterpillar in a leafy costume."],[156,735.24,3.32,"And while it may look just like an alluring flower,"],[156,738.6,3.72,"this orchid mantis is a stealthy assassin."],[156,748.44,2.96,"But compared to these exceptional masquerades,"],[156,751.56,4.6,"our spider's disguise is, well, a little rubbish."],[156,757.24,2.2,"Its colour is a good match."],[156,759.88,3.56,"It has a decidedly un-spider-like nip at the waist,"],[156,763.44,1.52,"just like an ant."],[156,765.44,2.72,"But surely no one would mistake one"],[156,768.64,1.28,"for the other."],[156,772.52,2.88,"Nevertheless, it heads for the nest."],[156,780.32,2.24,"Will it get away with it?"],[156,814.48,1.64,"Mission accomplished."],[156,816.12,2.04,"And right under the ants' radar."],[156,824.16,2.12,"But it wasn't its appearance that did the trick."],[156,829.64,3.32,"It's mastered a far more complex impersonation."],[156,840.6,4.12,"Our spider carries with it the ants' chemical code."],[156,846.84,4.24,"Like all of its kind, it was born in a green ant nest."],[156,851.64,2.68,"Its mother laid her eggs there."],[156,856.72,2.64,"It fed on ant larvae from birth"],[156,859.36,4.44,"and developed the ability to smell just like them."],[156,866.32,3.28,"Now that it's essentially hacked into their security,"],[156,869.6,3.24,"it can rob the nest whenever it likes."],[156,883.08,2.28,"[George] I'm just going to move very slowly…"],[156,886.56,1.96,"..keep an eye out for spiders."],[156,889.96,1.64,"[Narrator] In the dark of the forest,"],[156,891.6,5.48,"George and a team of local trackers are stalking the world's largest tarantula."],[156,904.52,1.16,"Are you coming this way?"],[156,907.2,1.92,"- Yeah, Bruds. I can see your light."],[156,909.12,2.32,"Have you found a new hole? Over."],[156,913.24,2.0,"You can see it at the hole? It's there?"],[156,917.76,1.88,"OK, I'm on my way. Wait there."],[156,930.96,1.4,"Oh, wow!"],[156,933.12,1.0,"There it is."],[156,934.12,2.12,"Oh, my goodness."],[156,936.24,4.2,"Theraphosa blondi – the Goliath bird-eating spider."],[156,940.88,1.88,"Oh, wow! Great."],[156,943.6,4.32,"The trouble with this is the first fright that she gets"],[156,947.92,2.64,"and she'll dive into that hole again."],[156,950.56,3.28,"and we won’t ever be able to get her out."],[156,954.84,2.44,"That's OK, that's OK. No, that's fine."],[156,957.36,4.6,"I'm going to try a little stick to sort of tease her forward."],[156,961.96,3.44,"Now is there any way you could go round the back?"],[156,965.44,2.92,"- And block the hole? - And block the hole. - OK."],[156,968.36,4.56,"[Narrator] Poor-sighted, tarantulas hunt by sensing vibrations in the ground."],[156,973.0,3.4,"They wait to pounce on passing prey."],[156,976.48,1.56,"[George] She is a beauty."],[156,981.36,0.84,"Got her."],[156,984.4,0.52,"Oh!"],[156,985.68,2.2,"That's it, that's it, that's it, that's it."],[156,989.0,4.48,"Now these guys don't normally bite as a first defence,"],[156,993.48,2.24,"they normally flick hairs,"],[156,996.16,2.32,"and she was flicking hairs."],[156,998.92,0.96,"Let's just block that hole up."],[156,999.88,5.44,"[Narrator] The barbed hairs are designed to irritate a predator's skin, eyes and lungs."],[156,1005.4,2.56,"[George] Those hairs are extremely irritating."],[156,1008.12,2.6,"I'm going to cough. Don't let me cough."],[156,1010.88,2.36,"Because if I cough, she'll get a fright."],[156,1014.6,2.0,"I'm desperately trying not to cough at the moment."],[156,1017.4,1.6,"OK, it's gone. Thank God for that."],[156,1020.48,3.56,"These guys have inch-long fangs,"],[156,1024.04,2.0,"and you really don't want that in your finger."],[156,1028.56,3.32,"Oh, dear. Do not cough."],[156,1033.36,3.44,"But you see how incredibly beautiful she is?"],[156,1036.8,4.4,"She moves serenely like a…"],[156,1041.44,1.76,"Ooh, she jumped! There we are."],[156,1044.04,1.76,"I'm going to try and get her back in the box,"],[156,1045.8,3.44,"only because I want to see how heavy she is."],[156,1049.6,4.56,"After which, we will bring her back to her hole here."],[156,1054.6,1.0,"I've got to get back."],[156,1055.6,3.0,"It's flicked the hairs and they were in the clouds and it's gone in my face,"],[156,1058.6,1.72,"gone up my arms and inside my arms."],[156,1060.32,2.0,"It's gone up this arm."],[156,1062.32,1.76,"I've actually inhaled some of them."],[156,1064.44,0.96,"I'm coughing."],[156,1066.32,5.56,"Which of course shows what an effective way of putting off enemies it is."],[156,1072.08,1.4,"You know, you don't need inch-long fangs"],[156,1073.6,3.48,"if you can make somebody twitch and itch all over. Argh!"],[156,1095.08,2.8,"The long, tenacious roots of the 'ohi'a"],[156,1097.88,2.12,"wend their way through the cracks"],[156,1100.0,2.24,"and penetrate deep into the rock,"],[156,1102.24,3.0,"in search of trapped water and nutrients."],[156,1108.12,4.52,"Their quest leads them to a remarkable subterranean world."],[156,1122.72,4.16,"Once, a raging torrent of lava flowed right through here."],[156,1127.56,3.6,"When it stopped, this was all that remained."],[156,1133.08,1.56,"A lava tube."],[156,1140.44,1.8,"Pitch black,"],[156,1142.24,2.48,"constantly damp"],[156,1144.72,1.72,"and very cold."],[156,1149.84,3.0,"Can anything survive in this harsh world?"],[156,1154.68,1.96,"Amazingly, yes."],[156,1157.24,2.72,"Patches of bacteria line the walls,"],[156,1159.96,3.48,"feeding on the minerals in the volcanic rock itself."],[156,1167.28,1.68,"But that's not all."],[156,1183.24,3.76,"This is the small-eyed big-eyed hunting spider."],[156,1188.12,2.4,"A curious name for any spider,"],[156,1190.52,3.0,"let alone one whose eyes barely function."],[156,1194.52,3.0,"But in total darkness, eyes are little use."],[156,1207.6,3.52,"Although he can't see it, the spider has company."],[156,1218.0,2.52,"Rare crickets scale the rocks."],[156,1231.44,2.56,"While translucent earwigs"],[156,1234.0,3.0,"and milky millipedes forage for food."],[156,1238.24,3.92,"These are cave specialists, or troglobites,"],[156,1242.16,2.16,"and they never leave the lava tube."],[156,1245.04,3.0,"Over time, most have lost their eyes and colour."],[156,1249.4,1.76,"Like this planthopper."],[156,1253.04,2.56,"Its tail has a curious function."],[156,1257.0,2.36,"Any predator biting it from behind will be left"],[156,1259.36,3.08,"with nothing but a mouthful of irritating, waxy hair."],[156,1270.16,3.8,"This is a place of ghostly stillness,"],[156,1273.96,3.52,"a definite advantage for the small-eyed big-eyed hunting spider."],[156,1278.6,2.0,"With its super-sensitive leg hairs,"],[156,1280.6,3.12,"it can pick up the slightest movement in the air."],[156,1287.52,2.24,"And it senses the cricket's presence"],[156,1289.76,2.6,"long before it's close enough to ambush."],[156,1302.04,2.4,"As prey are few and far between,"],[156,1304.44,2.64,"this may be its last meal for some time."],[156,1309.28,2.88,"With no light and little vegetation,"],[156,1312.16,2.44,"only the specialists survive here."],[157,0.08,1.9,"[KAMALA HARRIS] “These are unprecedented times”"],[157,1.98,1.76,"[TOM O’HALLERAN] “We’re in an unprecedented time”"],[157,3.75,1.67,"[KRISTI NOEM] “…unprecedented border crisis”"],[157,5.42,2.32,"[JD VANCE] “...the unprecedented thing is the idea…”"],[157,7.74,0.96,"[MERRICK GARLAND] “...not normal”"],[157,8.7,1.74,"[JOE BIDEN] “...is not normal”"],[157,10.45,0.83,"[multiple voices] \"This unprecedented…\""],[157,11.28,2.88,"[multiple voices] \"Unprecedented…\""],[157,14.16,2.48,"\"…you are making, at the expense of the American people…\""],[157,16.64,3.98,"\"…truly, without parallel in our history\" [music fades out]"],[157,20.62,1.3,"[KOUSHA NAVIDAR] Why are Supreme Court"],[157,21.92,2.16,"nominations so contentious?"],[157,24.08,2.88,"Can the president do whatever they want?"],[157,26.96,2.64,"Are we more polarized now than ever?"],[157,29.6,1.84,"And how long are we going to keep saying"],[157,31.44,3.2,"we’re living in “unprecedented times” before we admit:"],[157,34.64,2.48,"it seems like the government isn’t exactly working"],[157,37.12,1.84,"the way we were taught it was supposed to"],[157,38.96,1.44,"in social studies class?"],[157,40.4,3.28,"If you’ve ever found yourself asking these kinds of questions"],[157,43.68,2.96,"or fact-checking your uncle’s opinions about them"],[157,46.64,1.04,"you’re not alone."],[157,47.68,2.0,"Take it from me: a guy who’s been inside"],[157,49.68,1.36,"the belly of the beast."],[157,51.04,1.84,"Hi, I’m Kousha Navidar."],[157,52.88,4.0,"After I got my Masters in Public Policy, I was a political speechwriter."],[157,56.88,1.92,"I wrote speeches for a lot of people:"],[157,58.8,1.04,"a governor,"],[157,59.84,1.28,"a former senator,"],[157,61.12,2.64,"a member of President Obama’s Cabinet."],[157,63.76,3.2,"Today, I host live radio shows where I talk to experts,"],[157,66.96,3.6,"and the people, about what’s happening in our government."],[157,70.56,2.8,"But before all that, I was a 16-year-old"],[157,73.36,3.52,"who got to take an oath, and I became a U.S. citizen."],[157,76.88,1.44,"They gave me a little flag."],[157,78.32,1.76,"I think I still have it somewhere."],[157,80.08,2.32,"The truth is, a lot has changed"],[157,82.4,2.48,"since I became a citizen all those years ago."],[157,84.88,2.0,"Even in the decade since the last"],[157,86.88,2.64,"Crash Course U.S. Government and Politics series,"],[157,89.52,2.0,"we’ve become more divided."],[157,91.52,2.64,"It’s harder to know what information we can trust."],[157,94.16,2.48,"And, look, things might seem tough right now."],[157,96.64,2.72,"But one thing I love about government is"],[157,99.36,2.64,"it’s a reminder that you’re not alone."],[157,102.0,3.28,"And we can start by understanding it together."],[157,105.28,4.56,"So, Crash Course is back, to answer your Big Questions on U.S. Government."],[157,109.84,2.64,"We’re not covering the basics of the three branches."],[157,112.48,2.64,"We’re not rehashing how a bill becomes a law."],[157,115.12,1.92,"Instead, over 15 episodes,"],[157,117.04,2.8,"we’re going to build on the foundation of the first series."],[157,119.84,2.8,"We’ll dig deep into stuff that’s relevant right now,"],[157,122.64,4.24,"and ask whether this system that was built 250 years ago"],[157,126.88,2.11,"is still serving us like it should."],[157,128.99,2.46,"[computer buzzes and smokes] Ahh!"],[157,133.03,3.04,"Ow! [long beep]"],[157,136.08,4.0,"Together, we’ll look at the wider forces that influence politics,"],[157,140.08,3.68,"peek inside the systems that are supposed to work for us,"],[157,143.76,4.08,"and hold our present moment up to history to help us understand"],[157,147.84,1.86,"what the heck is happening."],[157,149.7,1.5,"By the end of the series,"],[157,151.2,3.76,"you’ll go from feeling overwhelmed about our politics"],[157,154.96,3.04,"to actually understanding why it works the way it does,"],[157,158.0,3.68,"and what we might do about it going forward."],[157,161.68,1.92,"So, let’s you and I meet back here next week."],[157,163.6,0.64,"And you know what?"],[157,164.24,1.68,"We’ll start with an easy one:"],[157,165.92,2.96,"why are U.S. politics so divisive?"],[157,168.88,1.12,"I’ll see you then."],[157,170.0,3.2,"This episode of Crash Course Big Questions on U.S. Government"],[157,173.2,4.88,"was produced in partnership with USAFacts, a non-partisan civic tech project"],[157,178.08,2.96,"that makes government data easier to understand."],[157,181.04,2.72,"For more resources that go along with these videos,"],[157,183.76,3.68,"go to usafacts.org/crashcourse."],[157,187.44,4.0,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Big Questions on U.S. Government,"],[157,191.44,2.16,"which was filmed in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[157,193.6,2.96,"and was made with the help of all these nice people."],[157,196.56,4.32,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[157,200.88,2.5,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[158,0.68,2.96,"Can farming give back more than it takes?"],[158,8.96,2.36,"Every landscape tells a story"],[158,11.32,2.88,"and here at the Waitrose Farm on Leckford Estate,"],[158,14.2,2.48,"the story is about how we farm the land,"],[158,17.28,2.04,"and how that land can recover."],[158,20.32,4.16,"Waitrose is the only UK supermarket to own a farm"],[158,24.48,4.84,"and support farmers to transition to regenerative practices by 2030."],[158,30.44,2.24,"It's a term we are hearing more and more."],[158,32.88,2.68,"But what does it really mean in practice?"],[158,35.88,3.08,"Can you explain us what is regenerative agriculture?"],[158,39.12,1.24,"Just to keep it really simple,"],[158,40.36,2.48,"it's about soil and nature."],[158,42.84,3.0,"A lot of people just don't realize that soil is a living thing,"],[158,46.0,2.16,"and of course, a living thing needs to be looked after."],[158,48.4,2.92,"A teaspoon of soil potentially"],[158,51.32,3.28,"has as many life forms in it as people on the planet."],[158,55.52,2.52,"And here at the Waitrose Farm,"],[158,58.04,1.84,"they have been farming regeneratively"],[158,59.88,2.64,"since 2020 and they are still learning."],[158,63.56,1.6,"We still lose in the UK somewhere between"],[158,65.16,3.04,"two and three million tonnes of topsoil a year,"],[158,68.2,1.84,"but it can take, depending on the soil type,"],[158,70.2,3.6,"400 to 1000 years to build a centimetre of soil."],[158,73.8,0.84,"I mean, that's extraordinary."],[158,74.64,3.04,"So we've got to look after this precious asset."],[158,78.0,3.52,"If I can increase the soil organic matter in this field by one [JN1]percent,"],[158,81.6,1.48,"that's worth probably"],[158,83.08,3.24,"about 250,000 litres of water per hectare,"],[158,86.68,1.72,"which in a dry summer is probably an extra"],[158,88.4,2.92,"two weeks of the crops’ ability to grow."],[158,93.04,3.0,"One of the most important parts of the system here"],[158,96.04,2.56,"is something called a herbal ley,"],[158,98.6,2.96,"a field left to grow a rich mix of plants."],[158,102.0,3.36,"It's part of a 12-year rotation on the Waitrose Farm."],[158,106.48,0.8,"Peppery."],[158,107.28,0.5,"Hmm."],[158,108.68,1.96,"So herbal ley,"],[158,110.64,2.72,"it's made up of 18 different plants."],[158,113.36,1.36,"So you've got legumes."],[158,114.72,3.28,"That'll be probably six legumes, six herbs, six grasses."],[158,118.68,2.32,"So that's why it's called a herbal ley, because of the mix."],[158,121.44,1.92,"And cows obviously produce their own"],[158,123.36,2.52,"Manure, which can be returned to the soil."],[158,126.12,2.88,"With the clover in the mix, you're mitigating"],[158,129.0,2.24,"the need to put artificial fertilisers on."],[158,133.56,2.16,"Last summer, very dry summer,"],[158,135.72,3.08,"lots of people struggled, and farmers were having to feed"],[158,139.32,2.16,"winter food to the cows during the summer."],[158,141.48,0.48,"Right."],[158,141.96,2.4,"But we found that with these herbs,"],[158,144.36,1.96,"these stay green all year."],[158,146.32,0.96,"And these cows were out"],[158,147.28,0.92,"for the whole summer"],[158,148.2,1.68,"without needing any additional food."],[158,149.88,1.16,"Hey, ladies!"],[158,151.04,1.0,"What’s up?"],[158,152.04,1.92,"Oh my God."],[158,153.96,1.92,"You’re looking beautiful today."],[158,158.04,2.12,"It's more mimicking the way the buffalo"],[158,160.16,1.6,"moved across America."],[158,161.76,1.6,"They're not sticking to one spot."],[158,163.36,1.0,"They're not really treading"],[158,164.36,2.24,"on the same point again and again."],[158,166.6,1.88,"Because that's where you get your compaction."],[158,168.48,1.08,"Yeah, you don't want compaction."],[158,171.36,2.24,"So this is an oilseed rape plant."],[158,173.6,0.6,"Okay!"],[158,174.2,2.92,"So this has come from one seed that was planted."],[158,177.32,0.8,"That is crazy."],[158,178.12,3.16,"And this one seed has then become this plant."],[158,181.92,1.92,"With all of these branches you can see"],[158,183.84,2.28,"that that is a healthy strong plant."],[158,186.48,2.0,"The seeds are then cold pressed [into] rapeseed oil."],[158,188.48,2.56,"The waste is the husk of the seed."],[158,191.04,1.32,"We can give it to the cows."],[158,192.36,3.44,"So all of this is grown on site and it's then used on site."],[158,195.84,0.8,"Nothing is wasted."],[158,196.64,0.72,"100%?"],[158,197.36,0.4,"Yeah."],[158,197.76,1.08,"Amazing."],[158,199.0,1.8,"Every time we fill a basket,"],[158,200.8,2.84,"we're casting a vote for how the land is farmed."],[158,204.12,3.6,"Emily is a soil scientist at Cranfield University,"],[158,207.96,2.0,"and spending time with the research"],[158,209.96,2.2,"changed the way she thinks about food."],[158,212.52,1.56,"It really just blew my mind"],[158,214.08,2.72,"that we are all reliant on the top"],[158,216.8,1.76,"few centimetres of the world,"],[158,218.56,2.04,"and we're losing it at an alarming rate."],[158,221.0,1.96,"It's so important that we manage it well"],[158,222.96,1.04,"and protect it."],[158,224.8,1.36,"When I go to the shops,"],[158,226.16,2.8,"I try to look for crops that are grown"],[158,229.0,1.36,"in the UK, where I live,"],[158,230.6,1.76,"and also try and eat a bit more seasonally."],[158,233.12,2.32,"You might also see some products coming through"],[158,235.44,1.68,"with the word regenerative on."],[158,237.12,2.64,"What it means is that farm or farmer,"],[158,239.76,1.96,"are incorporating practices"],[158,241.72,2.12,"to be able to increase their soil health."],[158,245.2,1.64,"Seeing regenerative farming"],[158,246.84,2.24,"at the Waitrose Farm on Leckford Estate"],[158,249.08,3.24,"reminded me that the health of the land"],[158,252.32,1.4,"begins with the soil,"],[158,254.28,1.56,"whether it's a field in England"],[158,256.36,1.76,"or my land back in Portugal."],[158,258.76,1.48,"The principle is the same."],[158,260.56,3.2,"When we care for the land, the land can care for us."],[158,265.04,2.28,"Healthy soil grows more than food."],[158,267.64,1.44,"It grows the future. [JN1]Says 1% in the video so can we swap to this written version pls?"],[159,4.24,4.7,"My guess is, for many of you, [a] drone is a scary concept."],[159,9.24,3.47,"It might evoke images of weapons in Ukraine,"],[159,12.74,2.34,"in the skies over the Middle East,"],[159,15.11,5.87,"where these things are creating damage and devastation at a massive scale."],[159,21.68,2.24,"Ukrainians reported last month [that]"],[159,23.95,3.67,"96 percent of the casualties they inflicted on Russian forces"],[159,27.66,1.44,"were done via drone strikes."],[159,29.83,3.7,"Now this is a terrifying new reality on the battlefield."],[159,33.56,3.87,"It's also one that we can't afford to ignore in the West."],[159,37.8,1.5,"But I want to use my time today"],[159,39.34,3.3,"to talk about an entirely different kind of drone use,"],[159,42.67,1.53,"much closer to home,"],[159,44.24,2.2,"that's already having life-saving impact."],[159,46.91,3.47,"And I think the best way to understand it is just to show it to you."],[159,50.41,3.1,"So I'm going to demonstrate the robotics technology"],[159,53.55,3.27,"live here on stage,"],[159,56.85,1.2,"sort of."],[159,59.16,6.07,"So we are over here"],[159,65.26,1.2,"in Vancouver."],[159,66.5,1.33,"Hi, TED."],[159,67.86,3.9,"But I am connected to a drone in Tokyo."],[159,72.4,2.84,"I’m going to click the launch button --"],[159,76.91,1.37,"this is all live --"],[159,78.31,2.17,"and a real drone is going to launch itself"],[159,80.48,2.77,"on the other side of the Pacific,"],[159,83.28,2.5,"and I can just control it right here from my laptop."],[159,85.82,2.6,"So it's a little bit like playing a video game."],[159,88.42,3.77,"But I can fly around,"],[159,92.22,2.47,"I can look and see whatever I want to see."],[159,95.36,3.34,"So the thing that allowed me to do this is called a dock."],[159,101.47,1.47,"So this is the dock here."],[159,102.97,2.54,"It looks like kind of an intergalactic grill."],[159,107.07,2.54,"So it's got a bunch of advanced technology in it,"],[159,109.64,3.17,"but it turns the drone into a fully autonomous device."],[159,112.84,1.37,"It can be flown remotely."],[159,114.21,2.2,"I don't need a person on scene,"],[159,116.45,2.54,"and it turns the drone into a software-defined thing."],[159,119.02,3.4,"So I can fly it here, and the software will keep me safe."],[159,122.45,2.94,"So I can just cruise under this awning --"],[159,125.42,1.67,"or over -- its choice."],[159,129.09,3.17,"I can tap on an object that I might be interested in."],[159,132.3,4.1,"Now the drone is flying itself."],[159,136.4,2.1,"It's autonomously tracking that car."],[159,139.07,1.44,"This is hands-off --"],[159,143.24,1.53,"it’s a very powerful capability."],[159,144.78,2.87,"So I'm going to stop this now"],[159,147.68,3.44,"and initiate an autonomous patrol of the area."],[159,153.32,2.27,"So that's cool, but why does it actually matter?"],[159,155.59,2.54,"Well I'm going to share with you a couple of stories."],[159,158.62,1.97,"The Oklahoma City Police Department"],[159,160.62,2.8,"has placed dock drones all over their city,"],[159,163.46,2.37,"and they’re using them to respond to emergencies."],[159,165.86,1.23,"So in this instance,"],[159,167.1,3.04,"a train operator called 911 in a panic,"],[159,170.17,3.64,"afraid that he might have hit something or someone on the tracks."],[159,174.1,1.77,"Now this is very difficult to access."],[159,175.87,2.2,"On the ground, this is probably an hour search,"],[159,178.07,1.7,"but the drone got there in seconds."],[159,179.78,3.47,"It was able to find that, unfortunately, there was somebody on the tracks,"],[159,183.25,2.7,"but because of the drone, they knew exactly where he was."],[159,185.95,2.07,"They were able to guide in first responders,"],[159,188.02,1.67,"and they saved this guy's life."],[159,189.69,1.17,"Another example."],[159,192.22,1.6,"This is from San Francisco."],[159,194.89,2.07,"Very different application but same concept."],[159,196.99,1.9,"Responding to a 911 incident."],[159,198.93,1.57,"Here, there's a stolen vehicle."],[159,200.53,3.17,"So they're able to follow the stolen vehicle from the air."],[159,203.73,1.44,"They know exactly where he is."],[159,205.2,1.27,"They know what he's doing."],[159,206.47,1.2,"I don’t know about you --"],[159,207.71,3.97,"when I steal a car, I like to steal a license plate to go along with it."],[159,211.71,2.13,"So SFPD knew that this happened,"],[159,213.88,2.34,"but they never actually caught anybody in the act."],[159,216.25,3.24,"With the drone, they're able to get this asymmetric advantage."],[159,219.52,1.67,"So now he's got stolen plates."],[159,221.22,2.34,"He’s going to put the plates on his vehicle."],[159,223.55,2.87,"[He] conveniently holds it up so we can read it from the air."],[159,228.86,2.34,"Any guesses as to what's happening now?"],[159,231.56,1.23,"Window tint."],[159,233.43,2.0,"He’s got a stolen vehicle, stolen plates,"],[159,235.43,1.5,"[and] he’s tinting the windows."],[159,236.93,2.0,"So this is the beginning of a crime spree."],[159,238.97,2.77,"He's probably going to go off and steal a bunch more stuff,"],[159,241.77,1.83,"but the cops know exactly where he is,"],[159,243.61,1.74,"they know exactly what he's doing,"],[159,245.34,2.9,"so they roll a plainclothes unit out, roll spike strips --"],[159,248.25,2.1,"he’s got flat tires, he can’t go very far --"],[159,250.35,1.6,"[and] they take him into custody."],[159,251.95,1.94,"So this is safer for everybody involved."],[159,253.88,1.83,"It’s certainly safer for the community"],[159,255.72,1.67,"[and] also safer for the officers."],[159,257.39,2.74,"Honestly, it's even safer for the suspect"],[159,260.12,3.34,"because they know exactly where he is and what he's doing."],[159,263.49,2.47,"Since SFPD has implemented this technology,"],[159,266.0,3.84,"they’ve seen a 30 percent drop in crime, a 40 percent drop in auto theft --"],[159,269.87,2.07,"I can’t say that I blame the auto thieves."],[159,271.94,2.2,"I probably wouldn't want to steal a car either."],[159,274.14,2.3,"And this concept of drone as first responder"],[159,276.47,2.2,"is taking off across the country."],[159,279.11,2.27,"So it started with a few agencies."],[159,281.38,1.87,"Hundreds of agencies are now using it."],[159,283.28,2.47,"By the end of this year, it'll be thousands."],[159,286.32,3.84,"And right now, about five percent of the US population lives"],[159,290.19,2.1,"within a two-minute flight of a Skydio drone"],[159,292.32,1.5,"to respond to an emergency."],[159,293.86,4.1,"But there are 240 million 911 calls every year in the US."],[159,298.0,3.07,"And we're building towards a future where the default expectation"],[159,301.1,1.24,"for every emergency"],[159,302.37,1.97,"is that a drone shows up in a few seconds"],[159,304.37,1.53,"to provide targeted information"],[159,305.94,2.07,"[and] get better outcomes for everybody."],[159,308.04,1.97,"That's one example."],[159,310.01,2.74,"I’m going to show you a different category of use, though."],[159,312.74,1.53,"We have energy utility customers"],[159,314.28,2.2,"that are installing these in their substations."],[159,316.51,2.04,"So this is American Electric Power in Ohio."],[159,318.55,1.87,"They've got a great pioneering program."],[159,320.45,1.94,"They have the dock in the substation."],[159,322.39,3.17,"They can use it to perform both proactive and reactive inspections."],[159,325.59,2.67,"And on one of those flights, they spotted this."],[159,328.29,3.04,"This is a distribution pole with signs of a short"],[159,331.36,3.2,"and a fire that they wouldn't have caught any other way."],[159,335.26,3.0,"So thanks to the drone footage, they knew exactly where it was."],[159,338.3,2.04,"They went in, they repaired it."],[159,340.34,1.5,"Why does this matter?"],[159,342.64,3.07,"Well some of the most devastating fires that we’ve had in the US"],[159,345.74,2.37,"have been the result of energy utility faults --"],[159,348.14,2.1,"where something breaks, something goes wrong,"],[159,350.28,2.04,"there's a short, it starts a fire."],[159,352.32,3.57,"Drones enable us to have a complete digital picture of the grid"],[159,355.92,2.14,"to prevent this kind of thing from happening."],[159,358.06,1.2,"And if it does happen,"],[159,359.29,3.74,"we can know about it sooner and faster and mitigate the response."],[159,363.86,1.83,"So flying cameras all over the place"],[159,365.73,2.1,"to get better information is super impactful."],[159,367.87,2.64,"But the same kind of concept applies to drone delivery."],[159,370.53,3.57,"So companies like Zipline and Wing are using dock drones"],[159,374.14,2.44,"to make it safer and faster"],[159,376.61,2.54,"to get a burrito delivered to your doorstep."],[159,382.55,3.74,"So there is a mountain of technology under the hood that makes this stuff work."],[159,386.32,2.7,"Drones are deceptively small, but they're actually on par"],[159,389.02,1.94,"with self-driving cars and rocket ships"],[159,390.95,3.04,"in terms of the technical complexity it takes to make them work."],[159,394.02,2.5,"There's vibration and thermals and aerodynamics,"],[159,396.56,3.17,"and the hardest part is making all this stuff work reliably"],[159,399.76,1.44,"when and where you need it."],[159,401.23,3.47,"But the key enabling technology is actually eyesight --"],[159,404.74,2.2,"giving drones the same ability that people have"],[159,406.94,1.67,"to look out and see the world."],[159,408.64,2.27,"Our drones have very powerful computers built in"],[159,410.91,1.23,"that parallel our brains."],[159,412.14,1.57,"They've got deep neural networks"],[159,413.71,2.14,"that parallel our biological neural networks."],[159,415.85,2.87,"And the output is very similar to how humans see the world."],[159,418.75,2.44,"They know where things are. They know what they are,"],[159,421.22,3.37,"and they can use all of that information to make intelligent decisions."],[159,425.19,3.94,"So you might have noticed that I left a drone flying in Japan."],[159,429.56,2.23,"We should go ahead and check in on that."],[159,433.2,3.37,"This drone is performing an autonomous inspection."],[159,436.6,2.47,"So this is a water tower that's in the area."],[159,439.37,3.7,"I’m going to somewhat rudely interrupt it here."],[159,443.41,1.33,"I click stop."],[159,444.77,2.2,"I click return and land and dock."],[159,447.34,1.57,"So it will now go back up [and]"],[159,448.95,2.23,"autonomously find its way back to the dock."],[159,451.21,1.4,"Dock opens up."],[159,452.65,4.34,"It uses vision and AI to detect its landing spot,"],[159,457.02,2.77,"and it will come in and do an automated landing."],[159,460.09,3.74,"So the dock, in some ways, turns the drone into something like a cloud server,"],[159,463.86,2.84,"where these things can be running autonomously,"],[159,466.7,4.67,"continuously in the background and doing useful work on our behalf."],[159,471.37,2.54,"So what did we actually accomplish with this mission?"],[159,474.27,3.27,"On an autonomous patrol like this, we can inspect the infrastructure,"],[159,477.54,1.37,"we can look for intruders --"],[159,478.91,2.27,"looks like we might have caught somebody here --"],[159,481.18,3.8,"and generally have a complete digital picture of everything that's happening."],[159,485.01,5.84,"In the 20th century, we built transportation infrastructure."],[159,491.36,2.57,"We built power infrastructure."],[159,493.92,2.7,"We built communication infrastructure."],[159,496.66,3.64,"And I think drones have the potential to fundamentally advance and transform"],[159,500.3,2.77,"how we operate all of this infrastructure."],[159,503.1,2.64,"But I actually think infrastructure is a pretty good way"],[159,505.74,2.67,"to describe what the drones themselves are becoming."],[159,508.44,1.44,"These things are dynamic,"],[159,509.87,2.7,"they’re intelligent, and they can run in the background,"],[159,512.61,3.5,"doing useful work for us constantly, 24/7."],[159,516.11,4.17,"This is a future that we're incredibly excited to build at Skydio."],[159,520.32,1.2,"Thank you."],[159,526.09,3.0,"Bilawal Sidhu: Hey, I'm Bilawal, the tech curator for TED 2026."],[159,529.09,1.94,"Hopefully you enjoyed that conversation."],[159,531.03,1.37,"Look, drones can be scary --"],[159,532.4,2.0,"we see them on the battlefields of Ukraine."],[159,534.4,2.44,"We also see them as toys and tools of entertainment."],[159,536.83,3.07,"But there’s actually a very useful function for this technology:"],[159,539.9,1.4,"to go where people can’t,"],[159,541.3,2.64,"or go faster to a place before people get there."],[159,543.94,2.94,"And that's this whole idea about drone as first responder"],[159,546.91,3.17,"and drone as like, essentially infrastructure monitoring."],[159,550.11,1.84,"We had these crazy fires in LA"],[159,551.98,2.2,"that could have been prevented if we had drones"],[159,554.22,3.57,"that were surveying power lines and infrastructure that clearly,"],[159,557.79,1.37,"you know, caused that blaze."],[159,559.19,2.97,"But again, like most things, this technology is very dual-use."],[159,562.16,2.4,"And that's why we want to have these conversations."],[159,564.6,2.74,"You ought to know about what the technology is capable of,"],[159,567.36,2.17,"how it’s being used in every facet of society,"],[159,569.53,2.97,"so we can have the discussion about what we want to do with it."],[159,572.5,1.17,"I hope you enjoyed."],[160,5.12,2.75,"When I was eight years old, I had this ritual."],[160,8.38,3.59,"After I finished my homework, I'd head upstairs to my room,"],[160,12.01,2.75,"turn on my favorite Christina Aguilera CD,"],[160,14.76,1.58,"and sit on the floor"],[160,16.39,3.92,"surrounding myself with all this stuff that was supposed to make me playful."],[160,20.64,2.54,"I'd take out my Barbies one by one,"],[160,23.18,3.04,"spread out my markers in every color,"],[160,26.27,1.29,"and then I'd wait."],[160,28.98,1.29,"And I'd wait."],[160,31.23,4.09,"And I'd wait until something felt worthy enough,"],[160,35.36,1.96,"perfect enough to make."],[160,37.91,2.63,"That moment barely ever came."],[160,41.12,1.46,"Ever since I was a kid,"],[160,42.58,2.96,"play felt like something that I had to earn."],[160,45.54,3.34,"And when I finally did do it, I took it seriously."],[160,49.34,3.92,"My favorite game growing up was emergency-room doctor."],[160,53.3,3.21,"I’d force my younger brother to scrub in, robes on,"],[160,56.51,2.13,"Beanie Babies fully prepped,"],[160,58.68,1.84,"and I was head surgeon, of course,"],[160,60.55,3.04,"so there was no room for mistakes in my OR."],[160,64.27,3.13,"Most people don't become play skeptics"],[160,67.44,1.88,"until they're adults."],[160,69.31,3.75,"But for me, even at eight, I was questioning the point of it."],[160,73.82,3.96,"So I ended up doing what any reasonable skeptic would do,"],[160,77.82,2.08,"and I became a scientist."],[160,80.28,2.25,"I got my master's in clinical psychology"],[160,82.58,3.92,"and spent almost the last decade studying the science of creativity."],[160,86.96,4.92,"And what I discovered surprised me because creativity isn't just a talent."],[160,92.3,2.96,"It is a core part of all of our well-being,"],[160,95.26,2.21,"a pillar that I came to establish"],[160,97.47,3.59,"and become a fierce advocate for, called “creative health.”"],[160,101.56,1.5,"And one of the best strategies"],[160,103.1,2.34,"that we have to strengthen our creative health"],[160,105.47,3.75,"is exactly what I dismissed as a kid: play."],[160,109.69,2.88,"That's because when we play, especially as adults,"],[160,112.61,3.04,"we unlock this unique type of flourishing."],[160,115.99,2.88,"We have deeper fulfillment and stronger resilience"],[160,118.86,2.59,"and even more original ideas."],[160,121.95,2.04,"But the type of play that I'm talking about"],[160,123.99,2.71,"is probably not what we're all imagining."],[160,126.75,5.05,"It's not just being silly or childlike, or even starting up a brand new hobby."],[160,131.84,2.63,"We’ve reduced play to be this cherry on top"],[160,134.5,1.71,"of an already good life."],[160,136.26,4.09,"But from a scientific perspective, it is much more fundamental than that."],[160,141.14,3.92,"Play is what happens anytime we choose to do something"],[160,145.1,2.54,"without knowing exactly where it's going to end up."],[160,148.06,2.0,"There are no instructions we need to follow"],[160,150.06,1.88,"or outcomes we need to achieve."],[160,151.98,1.54,"Just two elements:"],[160,153.52,2.84,"intrinsic motivation and the freedom"],[160,156.36,2.59,"not to know the answer in advance."],[160,159.45,3.21,"The problem is, we've come to treat that type of play"],[160,162.7,1.92,"as worse than optional."],[160,164.62,2.71,"It's something that we avoid and even punish."],[160,167.7,4.63,"So much so that upwards of 70 percent of adults around the world today"],[160,172.38,1.42,"have stopped doing it."],[160,174.42,1.38,"I first noticed this pattern"],[160,175.84,2.13,"as I was interviewing thousands of people"],[160,178.01,2.25,"about their current relationship with play,"],[160,180.3,2.96,"and I kept hearing the same thing over and over."],[160,183.3,2.96,"They told me they didn't have the time or the talent"],[160,186.26,2.25,"or the right tools to do it anymore."],[160,188.56,3.42,"And when they finally did, it wasn't play."],[160,192.02,2.34,"It looked a lot like my emergency room,"],[160,194.4,4.05,"something that was scheduled and optimized with nothing left to chance."],[160,199.24,2.04,"I know this might not seem like a big deal,"],[160,201.28,1.96,"like, we're just having less fun."],[160,203.24,1.83,"But it's deeper than that."],[160,205.53,3.63,"We are living through a “play-deprivation crisis.”"],[160,209.5,4.09,"We've systematically removed everything that makes play what it is."],[160,213.62,2.75,"The spontaneity, the freedom, the wonder,"],[160,216.42,1.75,"from all parts of adult life"],[160,218.21,2.96,"and replaced it with efficiency and achievement."],[160,221.55,2.84,"And in doing so, we're losing the very survival skills"],[160,224.43,1.71,"that we need the most right now."],[160,226.18,4.67,"Things like our capacity to adapt and imagine and even feel alive."],[160,231.69,1.71,"Play deprivation is tricky, though,"],[160,233.4,2.79,"because at first we barely notice it's happening."],[160,236.19,1.96,"We just feel endlessly busy."],[160,238.53,1.79,"But over time, without play,"],[160,240.36,3.67,"stress can compound and then burnout can become chronic."],[160,244.32,2.38,"And then we walk around as playless adults,"],[160,246.74,3.34,"which ultimately creates a playless society,"],[160,250.12,2.84,"one where our institutions can grow rigid"],[160,253.0,2.13,"and lonely and polarized."],[160,255.58,2.17,"But we can change this, though."],[160,257.79,3.88,"Not even just for ourselves, but for future generations too."],[160,261.67,2.96,"Because play isn't something we age out of."],[160,264.68,1.83,"It is a lifelong trait,"],[160,266.55,3.09,"which means no matter how invisible it might feel,"],[160,269.64,2.42,"we can always restore it."],[160,272.06,2.84,"And how we go about doing that is much simpler"],[160,274.94,1.79,"and more expansive than you'd think."],[160,277.73,1.38,"At my company Daydreamers,"],[160,279.11,2.92,"one of the things we got extremely curious to figure out"],[160,282.07,2.59,"was finding the optimal way to play."],[160,284.99,2.17,"Are you noticing a trend about me yet?"],[160,287.16,3.67,"We studied all kinds of people, executives, postal workers,"],[160,290.87,1.25,"stay-at-home parents"],[160,292.12,2.04,"from 18 to 85."],[160,294.16,2.54,"And we looked at how they played not in a lab,"],[160,296.71,2.17,"but in the texture of their daily lives."],[160,299.25,3.71,"And as we analyze the millions of minutes of creative behaviors"],[160,303.01,4.21,"that we collected through pictures and psychologically validated assessments,"],[160,307.26,2.42,"we found the opposite of my hypothesis."],[160,310.14,4.0,"Play wasn't some list of actions we could add to our calendar."],[160,314.18,1.71,"It was an approach."],[160,316.31,4.05,"The people who became creatively healthy were doing something differently."],[160,320.4,3.38,"They were playing all the time in all these parts of life."],[160,323.82,2.79,"Thinking playfully, expressing themselves playfully,"],[160,326.65,2.42,"even noticing when it happened around them."],[160,329.07,4.42,"And as they began to embody it, their lives transformed."],[160,333.54,2.04,"So while I wish I could stand here"],[160,335.58,3.75,"and give us one play prescription to follow,"],[160,339.33,2.38,"that would be the antithesis of what it is."],[160,341.71,1.21,"What I can do, though,"],[160,342.96,3.29,"is paint a picture of what a playful life could look like,"],[160,346.3,2.25,"because we don't need more stuff."],[160,348.59,4.17,"We just need to start injecting it back into how we already live."],[160,353.06,3.17,"And the three places to start are during work,"],[160,356.27,3.17,"before sleep, and in public."],[160,360.19,3.38,"Let's start at work because most of us get stuck in this linear,"],[160,363.61,1.29,"task-oriented thinking,"],[160,364.9,3.0,"churning through to do's and in back-to-back meetings."],[160,367.95,2.67,"But neuroscientists have found that the spontaneous,"],[160,370.62,3.42,"imaginative thinking that's underneath creative play"],[160,374.08,1.88,"lives in a different part of our brain,"],[160,375.95,1.88,"called the default mode network."],[160,377.83,1.33,"And that gets activated"],[160,379.17,3.25,"when we do things we don't normally consider productive."],[160,382.79,2.38,"Playful people mind wander."],[160,385.21,1.42,"They daydream."],[160,386.67,3.09,"We often call that laziness, but underneath the surface,"],[160,389.76,2.67,"our creative brain is actually hard at work,"],[160,392.47,3.54,"connecting all these disparate ideas that don't make sense yet."],[160,396.06,1.79,"Even Einstein swore by this."],[160,397.89,4.55,"He credited his most innovative ideas not to doing math behind a desk,"],[160,402.44,2.42,"but to thought experiments."],[160,404.9,2.96,"And right now, protecting our nonlinear thinking"],[160,407.9,2.0,"matters more than ever."],[160,409.9,3.09,"And that doesn't happen through doing cringey icebreakers."],[160,412.99,3.75,"So next time you see Mark staring off into the distance during a meeting,"],[160,416.75,2.75,"or even you want to look away from your inbox,"],[160,419.5,3.75,"celebrate it because your thinking is starting to play."],[160,423.96,1.88,"OK, that sounds doable, right?"],[160,425.88,2.92,"But what happens when we get home from work?"],[160,429.13,3.5,"Well, most of us feel exhausted, so we reach for ease."],[160,432.68,2.63,"We scroll [and] order food at the click of a button."],[160,435.31,2.67,"We want our lives to be frictionless."],[160,437.97,4.0,"But our play and our lives need friction."],[160,442.35,3.04,"There's actually a type of positive stress called eustress"],[160,445.44,3.92,"that comes about when we do things that require effort from us."],[160,449.36,1.71,"That's why physically playing"],[160,451.07,2.13,"or making things with our bodies"],[160,453.24,2.04,"actually expands our energy"],[160,455.32,2.04,"and even our perception of time."],[160,457.91,3.0,"But we've come to engineer the friction out of that too, though."],[160,460.91,1.17,"We think playing at home"],[160,462.12,2.71,"is perfectly completing a page of that adult coloring book"],[160,464.88,2.5,"or following a YouTube tutorial to a tee."],[160,467.8,5.0,"Real play, though, is freer than that, which can feel uncomfortable at first."],[160,472.84,3.96,"It's experimenting with a bunch of different ingredients without a recipe"],[160,476.85,2.79,"or doodling in the margins of your notebook."],[160,479.68,1.5,"It can be that simple."],[160,481.23,1.92,"That's actually exactly what I was doing"],[160,483.19,2.5,"when I had my own creative breakthrough."],[160,485.73,2.88,"Normally, I'd get home from work and try to \"relax,\""],[160,488.65,2.63,"or if I was feeling extra adventurous,"],[160,491.32,3.38,"I'd take out my notebook and try to write something profound,"],[160,494.74,2.04,"neither of which are very playful."],[160,497.24,4.71,"But one night while I was writing, my pen moved to the left,"],[160,501.95,1.5,"and then it made a circle."],[160,503.79,3.5,"And then a bunch of random shapes and patterns started to flow,"],[160,507.29,5.05,"and while I did feel uncomfortable, I was also incredibly liberated."],[160,512.92,3.59,"And when I came back to reality and tried to make sense of it,"],[160,516.55,4.09,"I thought of my eight-year-old self, sitting on the floor of her room,"],[160,520.68,2.21,"waiting for something worthy to make."],[160,523.39,3.88,"And I realized in that moment that she, like so many of us,"],[160,527.31,1.17,"got it backwards"],[160,528.48,3.92,"because we don't need to wait for play to feel good to do it."],[160,532.78,5.13,"Doing it in the face of resistance and discomfort is how we begin."],[160,538.87,3.38,"But we can't just play alone at our desks or in our rooms."],[160,542.29,2.46,"We need to play in public, too."],[160,545.21,4.3,"The last and most insidious sign of play deprivation is cultural."],[160,549.5,2.63,"It's when we don't just deprioritize it ourselves,"],[160,552.17,2.5,"but we actively punish it in the world,"],[160,554.72,3.54,"silencing music in our parks or calling leisure time lazy."],[160,558.68,1.63,"Almost a hundred years ago,"],[160,560.3,3.0,"philosopher Johan Huizinga warned us about this,"],[160,563.31,2.84,"suggesting that when a culture loses play,"],[160,566.14,2.5,"it can become brittle and polarized,"],[160,568.69,1.75,"unable to cope with change."],[160,571.02,3.13,"I know that might seem familiar and maybe even scary,"],[160,574.19,3.38,"but know that play is inherently contagious."],[160,577.91,1.25,"Research actually shows"],[160,579.2,2.59,"that just being in proximity to people doing it"],[160,581.83,2.29,"can motivate us to do it, too."],[160,584.16,3.25,"So we don't always need to be the main character."],[160,587.46,2.75,"We can just amplify it when we see it."],[160,590.54,2.04,"I'm sure you've noticed this happen before,"],[160,592.63,3.59,"when a crowd begins to form around a musician,"],[160,596.22,2.96,"but most of us end up racing past."],[160,599.18,1.21,"So next time,"],[160,600.39,2.96,"I want you to take out your headphones and listen for a moment."],[160,603.39,3.04,"Maybe be the first to laugh or clap or join in."],[160,606.43,2.71,"Because when we make play visible,"],[160,609.14,3.34,"it allows our communities to come alive too."],[160,613.19,2.71,"I know that even these small moments of play"],[160,615.9,2.13,"can feel like an act of rebellion"],[160,618.07,4.71,"in a world that's convinced us doing it as adults is unnecessary,"],[160,622.78,1.71,"and oftentimes they are."],[160,625.12,2.13,"But it wasn't always this way."],[160,627.87,1.92,"Since the beginning of our species,"],[160,629.83,2.88,"we have been making these beautiful, weird,"],[160,632.75,3.92,"funny, interesting things for no bigger purpose at all."],[160,637.01,4.42,"Anthropologist Ellen Dissanayake actually has a name for this capacity."],[160,641.47,2.54,"She calls it “making special.”"],[160,644.43,2.79,"In her work studying early human societies,"],[160,647.23,2.75,"she also kept noticing an interesting pattern."],[160,650.48,3.13,"Every culture, without having spoken to one another first,"],[160,653.65,2.29,"kept adding these playful touches"],[160,655.98,2.42,"to the most ordinary aspects of their lives."],[160,658.82,2.67,"They decorated their tools with intricacy,"],[160,661.49,3.46,"they made clothes and filled them with beads and shells,"],[160,664.95,3.5,"they danced and told stories and appreciated beauty"],[160,668.5,3.63,"for no functional or productive reason."],[160,672.54,1.5,"Just because."],[160,674.46,2.13,"So this exact capacity,"],[160,676.63,2.34,"to play, to “make special,”"],[160,679.01,2.0,"isn’t just inside of you --"],[160,681.3,3.38,"it’s wired into our entire species."],[160,684.72,2.75,"It's always how we've stayed human,"],[160,687.51,1.83,"how we've bonded and adapted"],[160,689.39,3.29,"and even made meaning in life's most difficult moments."],[160,693.1,5.13,"And I see that as our responsibility to keep that part of ourselves alive."],[160,698.69,6.05,"So we need to play again in any form, in every part of life."],[160,705.07,3.38,"Not necessarily because it will lead us somewhere better,"],[160,708.45,3.04,"but because we are human"],[160,711.54,1.92,"and humans play."],[160,713.96,1.13,"Thank you."],[161,1.33,2.88,"There's so many different things that a song is meant to do,"],[161,4.21,5.17,"but I think that when you’re honest about what a song is for people,"],[161,9.59,1.58,"the intent is to connect."],[161,12.3,4.5,"Hey, you. Yes, you! What's that light inside of you?"],[161,16.97,5.42,"Is it a dream, a beat, a beautiful sound, a heartbreaking song?"],[161,22.56,3.09,"Whatever it is, we're here with working musicians"],[161,25.65,6.01,"to help you grow and share that gift with this wild and wonderful world."],[161,34.07,5.17,"Pop songs tend to follow a structure that goes:"],[161,39.24,6.09,"verse, chorus, verse, chorus, bridge, chorus."],[161,48.13,2.79,"You want the first verse melody to pull you in immediately."],[161,50.92,3.13,"You want someone to know what that song is in the first"],[161,54.05,1.96,"4 seconds it even starts."],[161,56.01,1.79,"Because that's your first impression."],[161,57.8,2.88,"I will sometimes spend literally days and weeks"],[161,60.68,2.63,"working on just the first line of a song."],[161,63.56,5.3,"I can call out basically every Queen song within the first beat."],[161,69.11,1.83,"Not bar— beat."],[161,72.4,2.75,"The verses set the conversation tone,"],[161,75.15,4.75,"and you're involved in the story each moment after the next."],[161,79.99,3.25,"It's the setting, it's where we are, it's who I am."],[161,83.25,2.79,"It's how I feel about this person or myself."],[161,86.04,3.0,"Your verse might be split up into the main part of your verse"],[161,89.04,1.63,"and then what we call pre-chorus."],[161,90.67,4.04,"It connects the dots between your verse and your chorus."],[161,94.72,4.0,"The prehook is introducing— a lot of the times— the conflict."],[161,98.72,2.17,"That has to feel different than the setup."],[161,100.89,1.5,"And then you have your hook."],[161,105.06,2.63,"I really like the phrase:"],[161,107.69,5.25,"write the verse for yourself, and write the chorus for the audience."],[161,113.65,4.75,"So in the chorus, focus on something universal."],[161,118.53,3.59,"And in the verse, just write something really personal."],[161,122.2,3.04,"For me, a great chorus will have symmetry,"],[161,125.25,4.71,"and a clear lyric that is easy to sing along with."],[161,130.08,2.88,"Does it sing well? Is it easy to understand?"],[161,132.96,2.92,"When you're writing a melody, there's a certain rhythm to it,"],[161,135.88,2.33,"in the same way that a conversation has a rhythm."],[161,138.22,2.29,"Sometimes the phonetics help me write the lyrics."],[161,140.51,4.42,"So if I'm writing a melody, I'll just sing nonsensical lyrics."],[161,145.06,1.71,"What vowels feel good?"],[161,146.77,6.26,"The marriage of great phonetics, with a unique but familiar lyric,"],[161,153.15,4.8,"and a catchy, symmetrical melody are kind of like the perfect marriage"],[161,157.99,1.38,"to writing a great hook."],[161,159.61,3.21,"The goal when you write a song in pop music is to be able"],[161,162.82,3.09,"to sing along with the chorus by the second time you've heard it."],[161,165.91,3.71,"If you're Max Martin, it's going to be always the rise of the song."],[161,169.62,3.09,"He says whatever note that you start on the verse of the prehook,"],[161,172.71,1.92,"that hook needs to be higher."],[161,174.63,1.75,"Now, that's a psychological trick,"],[161,176.38,3.34,"a feeling like now we're going somewhere that we haven't gone."],[161,179.72,2.92,"But if you ask Billie Eilish, we're going to do the drop hook."],[161,182.64,3.88,"And the drop hook means we're not going to do the typical and obvious."],[161,186.51,3.92,"And now we're going into the chorus, and it's the big moment."],[161,190.52,5.88,"When the chorus hits, it's like a release of all of this tension and anticipation"],[161,196.4,2.09,"that is set up in all the verses."],[161,198.49,3.46,"If the song has to leave you with one thing or one memory or one thought,"],[161,201.95,1.58,"that's where the chorus does it."],[161,203.53,3.67,"It's about the way it makes you feel and the thing that you remember,"],[161,207.2,3.09,"and where you were the first time you heard it."],[161,210.29,2.67,"They hold tremendous significance."],[161,212.96,4.75,"Usually the standard is you want to get to the chorus at about the 1 minute mark."],[161,218.21,5.21,"The term \"don't bore us, get to the chorus\" is very real."],[161,223.55,2.67,"So, practice writing choruses."],[161,226.22,1.63,"Remember they can be simple."],[161,227.85,3.96,"And really more importantly, make it something you believe,"],[161,231.81,2.71,"because if you believe it then probably someone else will."],[161,236.23,4.75,"So, verse one is what happens to explain why we're going to sing this big chorus."],[161,241.36,3.25,"Verse two is elaborating on that."],[161,244.62,2.96,"Now we know what the chorus is, let's elaborate on the thought."],[161,247.58,3.29,"And usually verse one and verse two are going to be different"],[161,250.87,3.38,"as far as the lyric and also sometimes the melody."],[161,254.33,3.29,"And if you're feeling a little bit crazy, you have a bridge."],[161,257.63,6.13,"The bridge, or the middle eight, is the last little bit of information"],[161,263.76,4.71,"before we launch back into our memorable chorus and our outro."],[161,268.56,3.25,"If the listener has made it to the bridge, they love the song."],[161,271.81,3.34,"The bridge is 2 minutes in, minimum."],[161,275.65,2.79,"So if you make it there, amazing."],[161,278.44,3.54,"The hardest thing is to get them to the chorus— it’s all about that chorus."],[161,281.99,4.25,"So I think that bridges are sort of the first thing that people scrap."],[161,286.45,4.13,"I love them— there's always a place for a section that is not anticipated."],[161,290.58,3.17,"Bridges are always an exciting challenge as a songwriter"],[161,293.75,4.13,"to lift the song to heights it wouldn't have gone without it."],[161,298.21,3.42,"But it's equally as compelling to write a song with no bridge"],[161,301.63,4.38,"and have it communicate directly and not need a bridge."],[161,306.01,4.96,"So I never let structure get in the way of a good song."],[161,314.1,4.09,"As far as song structure and my relationship to song structure"],[161,318.19,1.25,"in the modern era,"],[161,319.44,6.42,"I'm not beholdened to it because I think that's what makes music and artists"],[161,325.86,4.8,"in particular stand out when you are going against the grain"],[161,330.66,2.21,"of what is popular at the time."],[161,332.87,3.88,"There are a lot of songs where they don't have a super clear structure"],[161,336.75,4.3,"and it might just be a series of hooks that kind of tell the story."],[161,341.05,2.71,"A lot of Afro beats music operates with that structure,"],[161,343.76,3.5,"where you really just have sections of the song that are undefined"],[161,347.26,1.0,"and it's all hooks."],[161,348.26,2.5,"I know the structure down and I can do that all day,"],[161,350.76,3.13,"but then once in a while you go, I'm going to forget the structure"],[161,353.89,1.75,"and let the music go where it wants."],[161,355.64,2.25,"My favorite song is \"Fast Car\" by Tracy Chapman."],[161,357.89,3.75,"She doesn't have the normal structure of each verse being the same length."],[161,361.65,3.21,"It takes like a minute before you even hear the chorus in the song."],[161,365.19,1.08,"But it works."],[161,366.28,3.04,"I have songs, they don't even have a chorus,"],[161,369.32,1.54,"it's just one long verse."],[161,370.87,2.5,"And I feel like a lot of funk songs are like that,"],[161,373.37,4.21,"where they'll just hang on like one phrase and that for like 8 minutes,"],[161,377.58,2.09,"and that's the song— which I love."],[161,379.67,4.84,"But other songs may have three verses before they get to the chorus."],[161,384.5,3.17,"I feel like Bob Dylan has a couple of songs like that,"],[161,387.68,3.88,"where it's just so many verses and then finally the payoff."],[161,391.6,3.34,"A lot of the times when it comes to song structure, I stick with it,"],[161,394.93,3.54,"but I’ve had songs that have been hit songs"],[161,398.48,2.46,"that completely break those rules as well."],[161,400.94,1.79,"It really, really depends."],[161,402.73,3.84,"I try not to get too caught up in what the rules are,"],[161,406.57,2.67,"because I’ve built a career on also breaking the rules."],[161,410.99,2.13,"I would say a song is great"],[161,413.12,5.25,"when it describes something that’s really relatable and really universal,"],[161,418.37,2.96,"but in a really specific way,"],[161,421.33,4.09,"that either you haven’t heard before lyrically,"],[161,425.42,3.38,"or melodically, or production-wise."],[161,429.13,5.34,"Like some sort of fresh take on a human experience,"],[161,434.47,2.04,"I find to be the most interesting music"],[161,436.51,5.84,"because I think we're constantly looking into music to help keep us company"],[161,442.35,2.84,"and make sense of what we're experiencing."],[161,445.27,4.96,"A great song is not necessarily the same ingredients"],[161,450.24,1.96,"as what makes a song great."],[161,452.2,5.09,"So you can have a great song that's a very momentary flash."],[161,457.29,2.25,"You know what I mean? Oh, that was a great song."],[161,459.54,5.05,"But, what makes a song great? What also might make a song timeless?"],[161,464.83,5.0,"Relatability, ease of singing, and ease of memory."],[161,470.01,2.71,"It just sits in your brain easily."],[161,472.72,4.92,"The songs that stand the test of time have a great melody,"],[161,477.64,3.71,"have a great, unique lyric, memorable lyric,"],[161,481.39,4.17,"and there has been care put into the production."],[161,485.69,2.13,"And don't be afraid to be vulnerable."],[161,487.82,4.55,"Songwriting is a really, really intimate experience."],[161,492.4,1.25,"You’re creating something,"],[161,493.66,2.5,"you’re pulling something out of thin air— it’s magic."],[161,496.16,4.84,"And so, you have to be willing to be vulnerable and honest"],[161,501.58,1.63,"to make something authentic."],[162,7.08,3.96,"25 years ago in The Blue Planet,"],[162,12.28,4.2,"we began a journey beneath the waves."],[162,18.44,3.48,"Now, in this new series,"],[162,21.92,3.0,"we venture even further."],[162,110.16,2.12,"We will uncover life"],[162,112.28,2.28,"that is more surprising…"],[162,116.72,2.12,"..more intelligent…"],[162,121.48,2.16,"..and even more precious"],[162,123.64,2.36,"than we ever realised."],[162,202.6,6.36,"We will discover the extraordinary relationship we have with the ocean."],[162,213.4,4.0,"One that has never been more important to understand."],[162,221.08,2.96,"This is Blue Planet III."],[163,1.46,5.46,"My lyric process is, first and foremost, to overthink,"],[163,6.92,3.63,"and then say, now how do we say that like we’re not nerds?"],[163,11.84,4.0,"Hey, you! Yes, you. Is there music inside of you?"],[163,15.85,3.21,"We’ve recruited working musicians from throughout the industry"],[163,19.06,6.17,"to help you hear it, hold it, and share it with this wild and wonderful world."],[163,26.4,3.0,"I think a great lyric, it does two things:"],[163,29.4,4.88,"one, I think it helps if your lyric is unique,"],[163,34.28,3.0,"its really important where someone can listen to it and be like,"],[163,37.28,2.42,"okay, this is a lyric that I have not heard before,"],[163,39.7,3.5,"or a take on something that I have not considered in this way."],[163,43.46,3.17,"But then I also think a great lyric is honest."],[163,46.67,3.92,"When it comes to lyric writing I really love, I love a noun,"],[163,50.59,1.13,"I love a picture."],[163,52.42,4.92,"I feel like people really hold on to nouns and they can be really sticky."],[163,57.34,2.46,"I like colors and textures and smells,"],[163,59.81,4.04,"and I like to feel like when you see the title, you can imagine the music video."],[163,63.98,4.42,"That being said, you can also write something that’s a little headier,"],[163,68.4,2.25,"that’s a little more just emotional or internal."],[163,70.65,2.04,"And that can also be super, super powerful."],[163,72.69,1.79,"So there’s not only one way to do it."],[163,74.49,4.84,"To make the personal universal is so good."],[163,79.66,6.96,"I would say my favorite example of that is a Stevie reference—"],[163,86.62,1.33,"“Living For The City.”"],[163,87.96,4.75,"He says, “to find a job is like a haystack needle.”"],[163,92.92,4.75,"They took that colloquialism, crunched it up, flipped it over:"],[163,97.97,2.5,"“To find a job is like a haystack needle.”"],[163,100.85,4.71,"But it makes perfect sense, and it makes universal sense,"],[163,105.56,2.08,"though it's a really quirky way of doing it."],[163,107.73,2.88,"I do feel like I have to tell the truth."],[163,110.73,2.46,"So if there’s something that feels really resonant,"],[163,113.19,2.67,"it’s really hard not to write about it."],[163,116.19,3.42,"I find it really cathartic and therapeutic"],[163,119.61,2.29,"to write about what it is that I’m going through,"],[163,121.91,4.5,"because there’s always something I learn about my own inner experience"],[163,126.41,1.33,"by writing about it."],[163,127.75,3.55,"It depends on the genre, and it depends on the topic."],[163,131.46,4.42,"Party time songs, for example, usually are going to be more rhythmic,"],[163,135.88,2.84,"and you want it to be something that everyone"],[163,138.72,2.79,"can kind of like pump their fists to and sing along to."],[163,141.51,4.3,"And then when it comes to love songs, sad songs, whatever,"],[163,145.81,6.26,"I think when it’s more introspective, I like to have more emotional detail."],[163,152.15,5.25,"Olivia Rodrigo’s ”Driver’s License” is a great example of a heartbreak song"],[163,157.4,2.25,"that has really specific little things."],[163,159.66,3.0,"The details of the things that you miss about someone"],[163,162.66,1.96,"or the things that remind you of someone."],[163,164.62,3.25,"When you’re talking about heartbreak or something more introspective,"],[163,167.87,2.13,"it's nice to kind of find the everyday things"],[163,170.0,3.25,"and think back to a time in your life when you were feeling that way."],[163,173.25,2.13,"I try to stay away from being overly cryptic."],[163,175.71,3.34,"You can be poetic and and vague even,"],[163,179.05,4.0,"but I think it has to be something that anyone could listen to"],[163,183.05,1.75,"and be like, I know what that means."],[163,184.81,4.38,"There’s not an original feeling under the sun, you know,"],[163,189.19,3.0,"if you felt it, there’s billions of other people"],[163,192.19,2.08,"who have felt that same exact thing."],[163,194.4,4.96,"But what does become original is your way of expressing that feeling."],[163,199.49,4.25,"How one person says I love you is very different than the next person."],[163,203.99,3.5,"Experience, sincerity, imagination"],[163,207.58,3.42,"are what make lyrics great, as far as I’m concerned."],[163,211.04,6.51,"I’ve got to be genuine in the emotion that I’m trying to communicate,"],[163,217.55,1.21,"if not transmit."],[163,218.84,3.59,"I think I know I’m onto something when it strikes a chord in me emotionally,"],[163,222.43,3.84,"when the song is something I’m excited to share with the world."],[163,226.51,3.54,"However, the ultimate test is the 24 hour test—"],[163,230.06,3.67,"because I’ve had that feeling, relisten the next day,"],[163,233.85,5.88,"and I question what on Earth I was thinking to have imagined this being good."],[163,239.78,1.92,"But that works on the opposite end."],[163,241.74,2.67,"There has been work that I’ve questioned—"],[163,244.41,3.92,"the entire time I’m questioning, and I’m writing, I’m playing going—back and forth."],[163,248.33,3.96,"I put it away, I listen the next day, and I’m shocked at how connected I am to it."],[163,252.29,4.34,"That cuts both ways— so it’s a matter of trusting the process."],[163,256.92,4.13,"And know that no one is going to say what you have to say,"],[163,261.05,1.33,"the way you have to say it."],[163,262.38,2.54,"And lean into that with everything you’ve got."],[163,265.3,2.08,"When I’m evaluating a song,"],[163,267.39,2.88,"the first thing that I’m looking for is authenticity."],[163,270.35,3.63,"Most humans can feel when something is not real,"],[163,274.23,4.0,"and that could be based off of whether a lyric feels real,"],[163,278.23,2.67,"or whether it feels like three songwriters got together"],[163,280.9,2.04,"and tried to write the most poetic thing."],[163,283.65,4.46,"I think lyrics are great when they feel like natural poetry;"],[163,288.62,5.13,"when they feel like you didn’t craft them, they just were."],[163,294.08,6.59,"I've always felt that life was as much being lived as being inscribed."],[163,301.05,4.0,"That recognition means that the song’s always being written."],[163,305.51,2.25,"It's just whether or not I'm paying attention."],[163,307.76,3.59,"I try to be ready and worthy of whatever sound"],[163,311.35,2.92,"is attempting to come through me."],[163,314.52,3.88,"I always use Bob Dylan, “Make You Feel My Love,” as an example,"],[163,319.02,3.46,"because it doesn't sound like a genius wrote it."],[163,322.49,2.33,"It sounds like anybody would just be like,"],[163,324.82,4.34,"“when the rain is blowing in your face and the whole world is on your case,"],[163,329.16,2.79,"I could offer you a warm embrace to make you feel my love.”"],[163,331.95,2.63,"It just feels like a thing that existed, right?"],[163,335.62,4.3,"It doesn’t feel like Bob Dylan wrote it, except he did."],[163,340.13,6.09,"And if you try to do it again, it’s impossible— it’s so perfect."],[163,346.59,4.38,"So that’s always one of my pinnacles of lyrical perfection"],[163,350.97,3.29,"is see how natural that feels."],[163,357.14,3.84,"I would say that I have a lot of different processes to write a song."],[163,361.19,3.04,"One of my go-to tools is I have a word bank,"],[163,364.24,4.3,"so I always have phrases and words that I'm thinking about."],[163,370.28,5.09,"In life, someone says something and suddenly it’ll make me think of a lyric"],[163,375.58,2.29,"or think of a song idea and I’ll write it down."],[163,378.0,4.55,"And then when we actually go to the studio and go to sit down and write a song,"],[163,382.92,3.88,"I have this bank of phrases and ideas and titles."],[163,386.97,3.38,"My process is funny because I start with collecting ideas."],[163,390.64,1.38,"Whenever I'm on an airplane,"],[163,392.01,5.09,"I think of it as, I’m locked in in this little missile shooting across the sky."],[163,397.31,1.38,"Nothing else I can do."],[163,398.69,4.63,"So I start listening to records and I start writing down thoughts and ideas."],[163,403.57,4.55,"I write things in the notes in my phone, I am constantly writing down titles."],[163,408.11,1.83,"And there was a moment I remember"],[163,409.95,3.46,"when I first was really getting in the trenches of the songwriting world,"],[163,413.41,1.29,"where I had an aha moment."],[163,414.7,3.09,"And I realized that there were song titles everywhere"],[163,417.79,1.33,"if you’re looking for them."],[163,419.33,4.38,"So, billboards, the covers of magazines, headlines,"],[163,423.75,3.92,"listening to people talking in line at the grocery store, eavesdropping,"],[163,427.67,3.21,"listening to your friends talk about their breakups, whatever it is."],[163,430.88,2.96,"And also one title can go so many different ways."],[163,433.85,3.0,"If you think about, “Since You’ve Been Gone,“ right?"],[163,436.85,3.96,"So that’s female empowerment, I’m over you,"],[163,440.81,5.42,"sort of like an angry revenge, sort of celebratory anthem, right?"],[163,446.32,2.5,"But it could have been a heartbreaking ballad."],[163,448.86,3.0,"It could have been a party song"],[163,451.86,2.42,"where it’s like, I just want to forget my troubles."],[163,454.28,2.54,"There’s so many different ways to spin the same title."],[163,456.83,3.63,"I have never sat down to try to write a song in my life."],[163,460.46,2.63,"I don’t go, you know what? I’m going to write a pop hit."],[163,463.08,2.29,"I’m going to write a record— it just is."],[163,465.38,2.84,"It’s just always being written, always."],[163,468.38,2.63,"That’s what makes songwriting so fun and so interesting"],[163,471.01,4.13,"is there is no real formula."],[163,475.39,6.46,"And sometimes the songs that you think are going to be big— don’t land."],[163,481.85,4.05,"And sometimes these songs that you really don’t think are going to be big"],[163,485.9,2.79,"end up landing for some reason."],[163,489.44,7.01,"And it’s really fun to just make music and then see what happens."],[164,3.77,1.27,"Last year,"],[164,5.07,2.27,"I met a woman I'll call Mary."],[164,8.14,3.07,"Mary was 13 when she started drinking,"],[164,11.24,3.5,"and soon she could drink 18 beers in a sitting"],[164,14.78,1.4,"and barely seem buzzed."],[164,17.05,4.47,"Her days became a blur of inebriation and hangover."],[164,22.82,1.64,"Mary wanted to stop drinking."],[164,24.79,2.5,"She tried alcohol rehab,"],[164,27.32,2.0,"Alcoholics Anonymous,"],[164,29.33,2.67,"a medication called Antabuse."],[164,32.56,1.67,"None of them worked for her."],[164,35.6,4.21,"One day, Mary was at a bar with a friend who also drank heavily."],[164,40.34,1.44,"But that evening, Mary noticed"],[164,41.81,2.13,"that her friend had hardly sipped her drink."],[164,43.97,2.67,"Her friend told her that she started taking Ozempic,"],[164,46.68,3.5,"and now she could barely drink two beers at a time."],[164,51.15,1.33,"Mary was perplexed."],[164,52.52,2.7,"She'd always thought of Ozempic as an obesity medicine."],[164,55.25,2.24,"What did it have to do with drinking?"],[164,58.69,3.24,"But she decided to enroll in a nearby clinical trial"],[164,61.93,4.47,"that was studying whether GLP-1 medications like Ozempic"],[164,66.43,2.54,"could help people with alcohol addiction."],[164,70.33,3.04,"Every week, researchers blindfolded her"],[164,73.37,1.84,"and injected her with a solution."],[164,75.61,2.37,"Either Ozempic or a placebo."],[164,78.98,2.84,"Within weeks, Mary lost her taste for beer."],[164,82.35,2.2,"She switched to white wine,"],[164,84.58,2.04,"and then she stopped drinking altogether."],[164,87.78,1.7,"Many people talk about Ozempic"],[164,89.52,2.87,"as getting rid of the food noise inside their heads."],[164,92.42,4.37,"Those intrusive, unwanted, repetitive thoughts about eating."],[164,97.73,2.07,"For Mary, it cleared out alcohol noise."],[164,101.06,2.77,"It started to moderate her desire for drinking."],[164,103.83,3.34,"It turned what was an overpowering emotional response"],[164,107.17,2.74,"into something that could be seen from a distance."],[164,110.57,2.57,"Suddenly, she had the mental space"],[164,113.18,3.6,"to make changes in her life that she had long wanted to."],[164,116.81,1.64,"She started exercising."],[164,118.45,1.87,"She improved her diet."],[164,120.82,3.0,"She ended a difficult relationship."],[164,125.39,3.8,"\"People know how much GLP-1s affect your body,\" she told me."],[164,129.93,3.37,"\"I don't think they realize how much they affect your mind.\""],[164,134.47,3.7,"Now as a doctor I have long wished that we had more to offer people"],[164,138.2,2.07,"who are struggling with an addiction."],[164,140.97,1.27,"And hearing Mary's story,"],[164,142.27,3.0,"it's hard to overstate just how striking it was."],[164,145.61,3.74,"GLP-1, or glucagon-like peptide-1,"],[164,149.35,2.9,"was first discovered way back in the 1980s."],[164,152.58,3.47,"Scientists thought that it might be helpful to manage diabetes,"],[164,156.09,2.8,"but because the naturally-occurring version of the molecule"],[164,158.92,1.57,"broke down within minutes,"],[164,160.52,2.27,"it was hard to turn it into a drug."],[164,164.03,2.2,"The big break didn't come until years later,"],[164,166.26,3.74,"when a scientist who was studying Gila monsters, of all things,"],[164,170.03,3.24,"found that the lizard's venom contained a similar peptide,"],[164,173.3,3.1,"and that peptide could hang around for hours."],[164,177.71,4.4,"His discovery catalyzed what would become the GLP-1 revolution."],[164,183.35,4.91,"Now for a long time, GLP-1 was thought to be mainly about digestion,"],[164,188.25,3.54,"but it's now clear that the medications affect much more than eating."],[164,191.82,2.5,"They might have some of their most surprising effects"],[164,194.36,2.24,"not in the gut, but in the brain."],[164,197.53,2.94,"Stories like Mary's have led scientists to consider"],[164,200.5,2.1,"whether these medications could be helpful"],[164,202.6,1.47,"for all sorts of addictions,"],[164,204.1,4.91,"from alcohol and cocaine to gambling and compulsive shopping."],[164,209.67,4.47,"Research has found that GLP-1s might help people stop smoking,"],[164,214.18,3.24,"reduce their cravings for opioids,"],[164,217.41,2.6,"lead them to consume fewer drinks."],[164,220.78,1.53,"As one neuroscientist put it,"],[164,222.32,1.97,"GLP-1s might be telling us"],[164,224.29,2.74,"that there is some type of universal pathology"],[164,227.02,1.37,"when it comes to addiction,"],[164,228.43,2.67,"and that they are part of how we fix it."],[164,232.73,2.13,"Now that is a tantalizing prospect,"],[164,234.87,4.71,"that there's a general-purpose key that unlocks the path to moderation."],[164,240.34,2.34,"But that’s what GLP-1s seem like they could be:"],[164,242.71,2.07,"moderation molecules."],[164,244.78,2.67,"They don't extinguish our desires,"],[164,247.44,2.17,"but they help us keep them in check."],[164,250.45,2.4,"And no one knows exactly why."],[164,252.85,3.1,"One possibility is that they modulate"],[164,255.99,2.1,"what's called the brain's mesolimbic pathway,"],[164,258.12,2.77,"sometimes referred to as the reward system."],[164,261.39,1.3,"Alcohol,"],[164,262.73,2.97,"nicotine, cocaine, social media --"],[164,265.7,3.0,"they all increase dopamine release in that pathway."],[164,268.73,3.5,"And GLP-1s -- they might be limiting the spikes of dopamine."],[164,272.7,3.67,"So mice that are on the medications and given cocaine --"],[164,276.41,2.5,"they have smaller surges of dopamine,"],[164,278.91,1.97,"but they otherwise seem to maintain"],[164,280.88,2.54,"adequate amounts of the neurotransmitter."],[164,283.75,4.37,"GLP-1s could be calming the water without draining the pool."],[164,289.15,1.13,"At the other end,"],[164,290.32,3.94,"GLP-1s might make it easier to stop using a drug."],[164,294.29,1.9,"Research that's currently under review"],[164,296.23,2.8,"has found that animals that are addicted to opioids"],[164,299.06,1.4,"and given a GLP-1s,"],[164,300.5,2.2,"they have less activity in a part of the brain"],[164,302.7,2.13,"that's involved with withdrawal."],[164,305.24,2.67,"So again, these medications could lead to moderation,"],[164,307.94,3.14,"not just because they make a drug less pleasurable,"],[164,311.11,3.1,"but because they make abstinence less painful."],[164,316.31,3.1,"Now at this point, we should probably moderate our own enthusiasm"],[164,319.42,2.44,"about the moderating effects of these medications."],[164,322.35,3.54,"Like any drug, GLP-1s won’t work for everyone."],[164,326.36,3.67,"In fact, for some people, these so-called “moderation molecules”"],[164,330.06,3.7,"could have an alter ego as a desire dampener."],[164,333.8,5.61,"Maybe some people lose interest in drugs or alcohol or food"],[164,339.4,2.6,"because they lose interest in pretty much everything --"],[164,342.0,2.5,"a condition known as anhedonia."],[164,345.34,4.1,"It's also true that addiction is often a lifelong battle,"],[164,349.48,4.2,"but many people who start on a GLP-1 -- they come off it within months,"],[164,353.72,4.5,"whether because of side effects or costs or access."],[164,359.09,3.07,"And even people who do manage to stay on the medications"],[164,362.19,2.44,"might ultimately develop a tolerance to them"],[164,364.63,3.87,"such that their cravings for a drug ultimately return."],[164,369.5,3.84,"And yet it is hard not to get excited about the potential for GLP-1s"],[164,373.37,1.24,"to treat addiction"],[164,374.64,2.97,"when you consider how little progress we have made."],[164,378.64,5.44,"Every year, tens of thousands of Americans die of an opioid overdose."],[164,385.05,1.7,"Since the turn of the century,"],[164,386.78,3.0,"alcohol-related deaths have more than doubled,"],[164,389.82,4.3,"and it has been 20 years since the FDA last approved a medication"],[164,394.16,1.9,"for alcohol use disorder."],[164,397.26,4.34,"The winding path of GLP-1s from Gila monster peptide"],[164,401.6,2.0,"to diabetes medication"],[164,403.6,1.53,"to obesity drug"],[164,405.17,2.07,"to potential addiction treatment --"],[164,407.27,1.6,"it is a story of hope."],[164,409.21,2.2,"But it's also a story with a lesson."],[164,412.11,4.54,"The hope is that more people will gain the power"],[164,416.65,1.53,"to bring their wants"],[164,418.21,3.0,"and their motivations and their behaviors into greater harmony,"],[164,421.22,3.3,"and they will live many more years in good health as a result."],[164,425.12,4.3,"The lesson is about the unpredictability of medical progress."],[164,430.13,3.74,"Discoveries like this one remind us why scientific curiosity,"],[164,433.9,3.94,"especially the kind that can seem obscure or impractical,"],[164,437.83,1.64,"why it matters so deeply."],[164,440.6,3.34,"Because we don't know where the next big innovation will come from."],[164,443.97,3.3,"We might not even recognize it when we first see it."],[164,448.85,1.94,"But now GLP-1s are forcing us"],[164,450.81,3.17,"to reconsider not just the modern science of addiction,"],[164,453.98,2.2,"but ancient wisdom about restraint."],[164,457.02,1.67,"For thousands of years,"],[164,458.72,4.8,"we have seen moderation as a moral achievement."],[164,464.39,3.44,"Aristotle argued that the path to a life well-lived"],[164,467.87,1.67,"runs through moderation."],[164,469.9,4.8,"Courage rests somewhere between cowardice and recklessness,"],[164,474.74,3.74,"generosity between stinginess and extravagance."],[164,478.88,1.57,"He's often quoted as saying"],[164,480.44,3.1,"\"It is best to rise from life as though from a banquet."],[164,483.58,2.0,"Neither drunken nor thirsty.\""],[164,487.38,2.47,"Well, Aristotle never had a Dorito."],[164,490.72,2.94,"He didn't have Krispy Kreme or McDonald's."],[164,493.66,3.2,"He couldn’t flip open TikTok and Instagram --"],[164,496.86,3.14,"there weren't algorithms that were fracking his attention,"],[164,500.0,3.4,"and ancient Greece was not beset by fentanyl and oxycodone."],[164,504.17,2.17,"We have a society today"],[164,506.34,3.27,"in which the fundamental problem for many people"],[164,509.61,3.44,"has shifted from scarcity and boredom"],[164,513.08,2.44,"to excess and distraction."],[164,516.08,2.6,"Our technology often seems purpose-built"],[164,518.72,3.17,"to tap into our most basic vulnerabilities."],[164,521.92,4.57,"And the question now is how to bring our minds and our bodies"],[164,526.52,3.37,"and our environments into a more natural balance."],[164,530.56,1.94,"GLP-1s."],[164,532.5,1.9,"They suggest that Aristotle’s"],[164,534.4,2.3,"moderation is not just a virtue,"],[164,536.7,2.3,"it is a physiological state."],[164,539.54,1.77,"It isn't just about character."],[164,541.8,1.7,"It is about biology."],[164,543.94,1.64,"And none of this is to suggest"],[164,545.61,2.17,"that we should somehow give up on the struggle"],[164,547.78,1.94,"to bring about a more healthful society,"],[164,549.75,3.04,"that we shouldn't do the hard cultural and political work"],[164,552.78,2.87,"to bring about a better world"],[164,555.69,2.64,"in which we can address the drivers of addiction"],[164,558.36,3.2,"and other social problems, things like loneliness and trauma,"],[164,561.59,2.2,"pain and poverty, exploitation."],[164,565.1,4.27,"But it does suggest that we should welcome a promising new tool"],[164,569.4,1.4,"into our toolkit."],[164,573.14,1.94,"Now over the years,"],[164,575.07,2.0,"I have cared for many patients"],[164,577.07,4.74,"in the moments when an addiction has taken almost everything from them."],[164,582.81,5.0,"The young woman whose post-surgery opioid use turned into a heroin addiction"],[164,588.78,2.9,"and then into the heart infection that took her life."],[164,594.49,4.37,"The father, whose liver failed after decades of alcohol use,"],[164,598.89,3.14,"who was slowly dying on a transplant list."],[164,602.97,3.54,"Every morning I entered his room and was greeted by his children,"],[164,606.54,1.8,"aged six and eight."],[164,609.51,3.1,"And they would ask me when their dad could go home."],[164,614.44,3.5,"Those were some of the hardest conversations I have ever had."],[164,620.95,1.3,"In recent months,"],[164,622.28,3.5,"I have returned again and again to something that Mary told me."],[164,627.29,2.07,"She said that GLP-1s enabled her"],[164,629.39,3.47,"to act not just on her immediate desire to stop drinking,"],[164,632.9,2.0,"but on her deeper desire"],[164,634.9,2.74,"to reinvent the person she wanted to be,"],[164,637.63,1.4,"to change her habits,"],[164,639.07,3.0,"her relationships, even how she saw herself."],[164,642.11,1.44,"She gained a kind of freedom"],[164,643.54,4.27,"to live alongside her desires instead of being ruled by them."],[164,648.95,2.77,"\"Because alcohol was no longer an issue,\" she told me,"],[164,651.72,4.3,"\"I finally had the chance to think, what type of life do I want?\""],[164,657.09,4.2,"And that is a question that every person deserves a chance to ask."],[164,661.59,1.2,"Thank you."],[165,6.75,4.71,"In 2016, a group of researchers— oh, a new photo."],[165,11.67,3.46,"These researchers set out to measure— did I get that email?"],[165,15.47,3.13,"They measured— what was that? Sorry."],[165,19.31,5.13,"The researchers set out to measure the length of, well, our attention spans."],[165,25.19,3.88,"Participants downloaded a program that tracked how long they stayed focused"],[165,29.07,3.71,"on a single computer application before switching to another."],[165,33.7,3.96,"On average, users stuck with a single task for 40 seconds,"],[165,37.95,4.42,"down from 75 seconds, measured just five years earlier."],[165,43.25,2.71,"This doesn’t necessarily mean our attention spans"],[165,45.96,2.0,"are fundamentally getting shorter."],[165,47.96,1.84,"Rather, in today’s world,"],[165,49.8,4.0,"our attention is being pulled in more directions than ever before."],[165,53.97,5.3,"This can make finding focus— for work, school, and even the things we love—"],[165,59.39,2.13,"feel almost impossible."],[165,61.89,4.46,"Thankfully, there are ways we can improve and better protect our attention spans,"],[165,66.35,1.96,"both online and off."],[165,69.11,3.5,"Our brains are constantly receiving a flood of information,"],[165,72.61,4.25,"from sensory input to environmental cues to internal thoughts."],[165,77.11,3.54,"Filtering through it all relies on several brain regions."],[165,81.12,3.88,"The prefrontal cortex helps us decide what to pay attention to,"],[165,85.37,3.25,"while the parietal cortex shines a mental flashlight"],[165,88.63,3.92,"on that prioritized stream of information while ignoring the rest."],[165,93.0,3.55,"The challenge of sustained attention is keeping that flashlight"],[165,96.55,1.83,"focused in one place."],[165,98.72,3.59,"The brain is continuously monitoring all these competing signals."],[165,102.39,2.75,"And it redirects your focus if anything is flagged"],[165,105.14,4.17,"as particularly novel, unexpected, or emotionally charged—"],[165,109.52,4.67,"whether it’s a knock on the door, a notification, or even a passing thought."],[165,115.03,4.09,"This ability to constantly scan our environments and quickly refocus"],[165,119.16,2.0,"was key for human survival."],[165,121.66,4.25,"So, while frustrating, getting distracted is inevitable."],[165,126.71,3.42,"And our baseline ability to manage distractions can vary"],[165,130.13,3.46,"based on factors like genetics and brain differences."],[165,134.42,1.67,"But no matter your starting point,"],[165,136.09,3.29,"there are ways to improve your ability to stay focused."],[165,139.59,5.3,"First, tasks that appropriately challenge you tend to hold your attention best."],[165,145.06,4.38,"For example, you might focus better when working on a more difficult,"],[165,149.44,2.84,"intricately patterned puzzle than an easier one."],[165,152.86,3.71,"Of course, we don’t always have a choice in what needs to get done."],[165,156.82,4.92,"In these situations, it’s helpful to remember that if focusing feels like work,"],[165,161.74,1.58,"that's because it is."],[165,163.7,5.05,"In fact, some researchers theorize that attention is a limited resource."],[165,169.25,4.09,"When you start focusing on something, this resource is at its highest,"],[165,173.34,1.5,"but it depletes over time,"],[165,175.13,3.96,"leading to more mistakes and increasing the chance you’ll be distracted."],[165,179.43,3.88,"Multitasking only increases the demand for this resource,"],[165,183.35,2.59,"exhausting attentional stores more quickly."],[165,186.18,1.96,"That's why focusing on one task"],[165,188.14,2.71,"and intentionally building in breaks can be helpful."],[165,191.1,4.5,"Studies even show a momentary break, to get a snack or just zone out,"],[165,195.69,2.63,"can reset this attentional resource."],[165,198.57,5.51,"And this strategy may be extra beneficial when studying or practicing a new skill."],[165,204.37,3.67,"During breaks, our minds replay the neural activity patterns"],[165,208.04,1.42,"of what you just learned,"],[165,209.46,2.75,"helping to solidify the memory for the long term."],[165,213.38,4.3,"Speaking of studying, stress, sleepiness, and poor mood"],[165,217.88,3.29,"can all weaken our ability to block out distractions."],[165,221.51,2.08,"So activities that reduce stress,"],[165,223.59,4.34,"like exercising, chatting with friends, or getting a good night’s sleep,"],[165,228.31,2.63,"also make it easier to stay focused."],[165,231.23,2.33,"And nature may offer an extra boost."],[165,233.69,4.8,"In one study, a 15-minute walk outdoors led to greater improvements"],[165,238.48,4.21,"in lab-based attention tasks than the same walk indoors."],[165,243.07,3.5,"Another effective strategy is mindfulness meditation."],[165,246.66,3.13,"This practice is all about staying in the present moment"],[165,249.79,5.21,"through exercises like spending 12 minutes focusing on the sensations of breathing,"],[165,255.17,2.67,"which can help train your attention over time."],[165,258.88,4.13,"But before you go take a walk, grab a snack, or learn to meditate,"],[165,263.01,3.38,"there’s something much easier you can do right in this moment:"],[165,266.55,1.58,"get rid of distractions."],[165,268.47,2.84,"It’s impossible to remove every distraction,"],[165,271.31,3.92,"after all, our own wandering minds are often the biggest culprits."],[165,275.52,4.13,"But one recent survey estimated that Americans now check their phones"],[165,279.65,2.17,"around 200 times a day."],[165,282.03,4.25,"The distractions on our screens are designed to grab and hold attention."],[165,286.91,2.21,"So, remember to be kind to yourself."],[165,289.83,3.79,"While we can’t always control every factor that pulls at our attention,"],[165,293.62,3.25,"finding a quiet environment, closing extra tabs,"],[165,296.96,2.13,"and even blocking certain apps"],[165,299.13,3.46,"can give your brain the best chance at focusing in."],[166,0.29,3.0,"Oh! Come on. Oh."],[166,5.55,3.0,"Soccer is really hard."],[166,8.55,2.71,"Seriously, there's something about soccer that makes it harder"],[166,11.26,3.0,"than almost any other sport."],[166,16.6,1.04,"Kill it!"],[166,17.64,3.84,"For me, soccer is if not the most chaotic"],[166,21.48,3.88,"sport in the world, it is definitely one of the most chaotic sports."],[166,25.36,2.67,"You've got an enormous requirement of endurance,"],[166,28.03,3.13,"you might have to do a maximum sprint in the first then also in the 90th minute."],[166,31.16,2.25,"So it's definitely on the list of hardest sports."],[166,33.41,1.42,"You do that for 90 minutes?"],[166,34.83,2.04,"I can't imagine doing that for 90 minutes."],[166,36.87,2.96,"It definitely takes 2 to 3 days to fully recover."],[166,39.83,2.63,"But throughout the season, I don't think there's ever been a moment"],[166,42.46,3.59,"in my five years of playing professionally where I've been 100%."],[166,46.05,3.25,"Turns out training for soccer is really complicated."],[166,49.3,2.29,"It's not just running and kicking."],[166,51.59,1.96,"With the World Cup happening right now,"],[166,53.55,3.29,"I wanted to see exactly what makes training for soccer so hard."],[166,57.47,5.42,"So I came to a practice with Brooklyn FC, a US Division two professional soccer"],[166,62.9,4.75,"team, to try a few drills for myself and see exactly how you train for such"],[166,67.69,0.71,"a chaotic sport."],[166,69.61,4.59,"So, Michael lent me these boots, size 11,"],[166,74.49,3.09,"so that we can try some drills and see"],[166,78.08,2.79,"exactly how hard this is."],[166,80.87,1.92,"I'm kinda nervous."],[166,84.38,2.25,"Specifically, the team's head of performance"],[166,86.63,2.71,"is going to give me some drills designed for a forward."],[166,89.34,3.46,"I tried to design a drill which involves a little bit of technical work"],[166,92.84,3.67,"where you have to be clean with your feet whilst doing lateral movements."],[166,96.55,3.8,"Then once you receive the ball into space, a forward player will often"],[166,100.35,2.96,"try and navigate past defenders. So the poles give you that control."],[166,103.31,2.0,"You have to keep the ball close to you whilst"],[166,105.31,3.04,"also traveling forward, which is far more complicated than it sounds."],[166,108.73,2.92,"A cut back pass is typically what a forward would do"],[166,111.65,3.0,"if they're like going down the wing. And then finishing."],[166,114.7,3.13,"And just for a bit of fun, we threw in a recovery run back to halfway."],[166,117.95,4.55,"I just want to say for the record, that I did score one time when we did this."],[166,122.75,3.21,"But that one's out of focus, so I couldn't use that in the video."],[166,126.0,5.67,"I'm obviously still really bad at soccer, but anyway, back to me being exhausted."],[166,132.88,3.0,"Can you hear how hard I'm breathing?"],[166,136.09,3.47,"So that's just one part of the game."],[166,139.56,1.33,"That is a quite typical"],[166,141.97,1.82,"movement style that you find the forward doing."],[166,143.79,3.48,"And to think that you have to do that randomly within a 45 minute period,"],[166,147.27,1.26,"It's very, very tough."],[166,148.54,5.99,"So in a lot of sports, the physical demands are relatively consistent. But soccer does it all at once."],[166,154.53,3.92,"The first, and maybe most obvious part of this, is the endurance."],[166,158.45,3.0,"You've got to move constantly based on what other people are doing"],[166,161.49,1.25,"for an extended period of time."],[166,162.75,2.71,"And that's really, really challenging, especially for 90 minutes."],[166,165.46,3.8,"And unlike many other sports, or at least other North American sports,"],[166,169.34,2.77,"soccer players get almost no rest during a game."],[166,172.11,0.85,"Yeah, that is pretty unique."],[166,173.01,4.5,"It's a 90 minute game, one stoppage in between, 15 minute break."],[166,177.72,4.34,"For the most part, guys are running 12,000 to 13,000m a game"],[166,182.36,0.64,"if they play the full game."],[166,183.0,2.17,"But endurance is just a piece of the puzzle."],[166,185.16,4.98,"It's not an endurance sport in the same way that we think of, let's say, cross-country"],[166,190.15,3.96,"skiing or cycling or rowing, whereby"],[166,194.32,5.8,"the outcome of those sports is 90% determined by endurance."],[166,200.12,3.29,"The endurance is, if you like, it's just the base layer."],[166,203.41,4.05,"Because on top of that base layer, players need to be able to sprint, stop"],[166,207.46,3.84,"on a dime, jump, and have surprisingly high upper body strength."],[166,211.46,2.04,"We're not weightlifters. We're not bodybuilders."],[166,213.5,1.71,"But it is important to have that core strength,"],[166,215.22,2.96,"that upper body strength, because a lot of it as well is, you know,"],[166,218.18,3.34,"you're in duals, you're using your upper body to try and win"],[166,221.55,2.68,"the ball or shield the ball or whatever that may be."],[166,224.24,0.99,"Playing soccer is kind of"],[166,225.27,3.5,"like playing basketball and long distance running at the same time."],[166,228.94,1.21,"According to Chris Barnes,"],[166,230.15,3.13,"over the course of 90 minutes, players spend roughly 60"],[166,233.27,3.0,"to 75% of their time jogging or walking."],[166,236.4,5.59,"They spend 10 to 20% running at a medium speed, 6 to 15% high speed running,"],[166,242.2,3.54,"and only around 1 to 3% of the game in an all out sprint."],[166,246.04,2.08,"But that could happen at any time."],[166,248.12,3.67,"Having to go as hard as you can for such a long period whilst"],[166,251.79,2.88,"also having to suddenly stop and then do a complete change of direction"],[166,254.67,2.38,"and then go again as hard as you can. All these physical requirements"],[166,257.05,2.46,"are really, really unique for football ball, especially because the field is so large."],[166,259.51,3.29,"And it's really hard to plan for exactly what will happen during a game."],[166,263.1,3.96,"So on top of all of this running, when the players are exhausted,"],[166,267.1,3.71,"they have to make split second decisions. Because soccer isn't like a race."],[166,270.81,2.1,"It's never as simple as A to B."],[166,272.91,2.29,"Let's look at that 90 minute match again."],[166,275.2,4.16,"In order to prepare players for the unpredictable chaos of an entire match,"],[166,279.36,2.07,"trainers break down the game into smaller pieces, then players train on each of those"],[166,281.43,4.36,"smaller components of the game."],[166,285.79,6.17,"If you can kind of cut and paste the really hard periods of a game and then extract the same physical demands"],[166,292.0,3.13,"into a training session, you're able to then push the hardest parts a little bit more."],[166,295.13,3.46,"Which is why Stef is so exhausted after a practice."],[166,298.63,1.29,"Today is a Wednesday."],[166,299.92,1.88,"You know, four days before a game, they want to"],[166,301.8,1.13,"just really push us."],[166,302.93,2.68,"Yeah, I would say this kind of replicates a game."],[166,305.6,5.17,"Michael is able to know exactly how hard to push the players in practice by using GPS monitors."],[166,310.77,4.88,"So the players are able to use GPS units. You can see the demands of each drill"],[166,315.65,1.08,"for a certain period of time."],[166,316.73,2.96,"You can see constantly there are all these spikes."],[166,319.69,3.75,"And that is the time in which that player is having to explode into space"],[166,323.45,3.25,"And then that helps guide me whether we've pushed too hard,"],[166,326.7,3.13,"whether we're not pushing hard enough, whether the one person is having a holiday"],[166,329.83,1.16,"and the other person's working."],[166,330.99,2.68,"So rather than just constantly scanning the fields,"],[166,333.71,2.21,"I've got numbers in front of me that help guide decisions."],[166,335.92,2.92,"And at the same time, Michael isn't doing the exact same thing"],[166,338.84,1.01,"for every player."],[166,339.85,2.36,"my job is about trying to push super strengths."],[166,342.21,2.63,"So the players have elements that they're incredibly good at"],[166,344.83,2.31,"and that's why they're professional athletes."],[166,347.14,5.14,"If we were to try and push that down and say, well, this guy might be unbelievably fast,"],[166,352.29,2.69,"but let's make him slightly slower and fitter."],[166,355.1,3.0,"That doesn't really fit the narrative of why they got into this position."],[166,358.11,4.42,"So they develop hyper specific plans to highlight and boost what makes a player great."],[166,362.53,3.21,"The explosive power is something that I've been working on more"],[166,365.74,2.59,"so throughout my career. I feel like I'm pretty fit."],[166,368.33,3.79,"I can run for a long, long time, but when it comes to now,"],[166,372.12,3.29,"it's combining that explosiveness in those moments where it's required."],[166,376.0,2.46,"Billions of bacteria live in a person's mouth."],[166,378.46,1.92,"Maybe more than there are birds on Earth."],[166,380.38,1.96,"Some are helpful. Others."],[166,382.34,3.54,"Many are behind oral health problems like cavities and gingivitis."],[166,385.93,2.67,"So how do you manage something you can't actually see?"],[166,388.6,3.59,"It starts with a solid routine and the right ingredients for the job."],[166,392.23,1.71,"Take Stannous fluoride for example."],[166,393.94,2.88,"It helps target and kill plaque bacteria."],[166,396.81,3.96,"Plaque can harden into tartar and plaque bacteria can cause cavities"],[166,400.77,1.04,"and gingivitis."],[166,401.82,3.75,"That's why Colgate Total developed a unique stabilization technology"],[166,405.57,1.21,"to keep the bacteria"],[166,406.78,3.21,"fighting Stannous fluoride stable in your toothpaste for longer."],[166,410.03,0.5,"The benefits go even further when you pair it with the"],[166,410.53,4.34,"Colgate Total Toothbrush and Mouthwash."],[166,414.91,1.33,"The toothbrush is designed"],[166,416.25,3.96,"with ultra fine bristles to reach deep along the gum line and between teeth,"],[166,420.25,2.63,"where plaque likes to hide and when you're not brushing."],[166,422.88,2.38,"The antibacterial mouthwash extends protection."],[166,425.26,3.67,"Together, they form the Colgate Total Active Prevention System,"],[166,428.93,4.38,"designed to prevent oral health problems before they start."],[166,435.81,1.04,"And on top of all"],[166,436.85,3.09,"that, when it comes to the World Cup, things get even harder."],[166,439.94,2.5,"Once we get to the knockout stages of those games,"],[166,442.44,4.0,"if the scores are tied after 90, then they'll play an extra 30 minutes."],[166,446.45,2.46,"So it could be a 120 minute game as well."],[166,448.91,3.17,"The players also get less rest between matches."],[166,452.08,4.38,"In a World Cup, you'll get 3 or 4 days between games."],[166,456.46,4.88,"So immediately one game finishes, you're in preparation"],[166,461.33,3.09,"phase for the next game. And for this World Cup in the US,"],[166,464.42,4.09,"Canada and Mexico, change in altitude and climate could really be a factor."],[166,468.51,5.76,"Your game could be in Mexico City three days after a game in Miami."],[166,474.26,3.21,"So you're going from sea level to altitude."],[166,477.48,3.46,"I've spoken with colleagues who are working"],[166,481.44,4.05,"one of the countries who are playing a game in Mexico City,"],[166,485.57,6.8,"and they're exploring the idea of utilizing portable hypoxic machines,"],[166,492.37,5.38,"so machines that actually will deliver air with the oxygen content"],[166,497.75,2.96,"that you would experience at the altitude of Mexico City."],[166,500.71,2.0,"Players are also playing with new teammates,"],[166,502.71,2.88,"new coaches, under the brightest lights of their career."],[166,505.59,2.38,"I'm not an expert from the neck up."],[166,507.96,2.96,"I don't deal in psychology, I don't deal in stress."],[166,510.93,3.88,"But the World Cup has to be the most psychologically"],[166,514.8,3.75,"demanding of tournaments that these people will play in."],[166,518.56,2.29,"There is so much pressure on each and every person."],[166,520.85,1.67,"Now imagine it's the end of a game."],[166,522.52,2.46,"You've basically run for 90 straight minutes."],[166,524.98,2.09,"You're the most exhausted you've ever been."],[166,527.07,2.25,"You're feeling more pressure than you've ever felt,"],[166,529.32,2.96,"and you need to remember the strategy and make split second decisions"],[166,532.28,1.86,"that could affect the entire game."],[166,534.14,1.59,"World Cup matches feel bigger"],[166,535.73,0.93,"because they are."],[166,538.25,2.67,"My personal philosophy is a lot more that having fitness won't"],[166,540.91,2.59,"necessarily win you the game, but it will certainly lose it."],[166,543.5,3.0,"There's a lot of research that looks at if a team can keep"],[166,546.5,3.0,"doing the high intensity movements, they're more likely to be successful."],[166,549.67,1.71,"At the same time, if they can't kick a ball"],[166,551.38,2.04,"where they want to kick a ball, they won't score."],[166,553.43,1.58,"So I think they go hand in hand."],[166,555.01,4.25,"The best players aren't just incredibly fit. They're adaptable and efficient."],[166,559.27,2.38,"You can't sprint the whole game. It's almost impossible."],[166,561.64,1.31,"And there's a lesson from how"],[166,562.95,4.41,"Brooklyn FC trains that us nonprofessional athletes can maybe adopt, too."],[166,567.36,4.13,"I think the main takeaway might be to be very specific with what you're trying to do."],[166,571.74,2.13,"Anyone can go out and run a 5K, for example."],[166,573.86,1.96,"If you're very specific with what you're trying to do,"],[166,575.83,1.71,"you might find a whole lot more returns."],[166,577.53,2.79,"Do you think I showed promise as a player?"],[166,580.33,2.63,"That finish in the first goal was pretty good."],[166,582.96,1.75,"Not many keepers are getting a hand on that."],[166,585.88,0.71,"That's awesome."],[166,586.59,0.62,"Cut there."],[166,587.21,3.0,"That's it."],[166,593.47,1.25,"Thanks so much for watching."],[166,594.72,1.25,"If you're not already a member,"],[166,595.97,3.84,"Head over to Patreon.com/Vox to get access to more stories,"],[166,599.93,3.63,"stay updated on our work, and help support ambitious reporting."],[166,603.56,2.63,"It's so important to support independent journalism."],[166,606.19,3.34,"Your membership allows us to make really cool videos, research"],[166,609.52,3.17,"interesting stories, and also dig into the investigative reporting."],[166,612.82,2.25,"If it doesn't make financial sense for you to support us"],[166,615.07,2.71,"today, you can still follow us on Patreon for free"],[166,617.78,3.63,"and stay up to date on all of the great reporting going on at Vox."],[166,621.41,0.5,"Thanks!"],[167,6.71,4.88,"In the late 1950s, Hong Kong citizens flocked to newsstands"],[167,11.8,2.88,"to pick up the latest edition of the Commercial Daily."],[167,14.89,2.71,"But not all readers were looking for timely news."],[167,17.76,3.92,"Many were following a story set hundreds of years in the past—"],[167,21.93,3.92,"a tale of adventure and war, romance and intrigue,"],[167,26.02,1.92,"brotherhood and betrayal."],[167,28.32,6.17,"Serialized from 1957 to 1959, Jin Yong’s “Legend of the Condor Heroes”"],[167,34.49,3.42,"is the first book in Jin’s beloved Condor Trilogy."],[167,39.24,1.92,"This multigenerational epic"],[167,41.16,3.34,"is considered one of the most important works of Chinese fiction—"],[167,44.54,3.46,"and one of the finest wuxia stories ever written."],[167,49.13,3.88,"Wuxia, roughly translated as “martial hero,”"],[167,53.22,5.84,"is a popular genre of Chinese fiction with roots in folktales over 1,500 years old."],[167,59.76,3.84,"Wuxia stories generally focus on nomadic martial artists"],[167,63.6,2.25,"bound to an honorable code of chivalry."],[167,66.14,5.42,"These heroes travel through a mythologized version of ancient China called jianghu,"],[167,71.82,4.71,"full of powerful leaders, roaming bandits, and mystical artifacts."],[167,77.07,1.54,"To face these challenges,"],[167,78.7,3.59,"wuxia heroes must learn superhuman feats of martial arts"],[167,82.29,3.21,"from ancient scrolls and reclusive immortal masters."],[167,86.0,3.67,"“Legend of the Condor Heroes” blends this martial arts action"],[167,89.67,1.71,"with pivotal Chinese history."],[167,92.13,2.29,"The book is set in the 13th century,"],[167,94.42,3.75,"amidst a century-long war between the Jurchen Jin dynasty"],[167,98.34,2.42,"and the Han Chinese Song Dynasty."],[167,101.06,2.21,"Roughly 70 years into the conflict,"],[167,103.31,3.46,"a new enemy has joined the fray: Genghis Khan."],[167,107.27,2.63,"The Mongols threaten to topple both empires,"],[167,109.94,3.59,"and raids by all parties wreak havoc across the continent."],[167,113.82,2.25,"The novel begins in a Han village,"],[167,116.07,4.38,"where two expecting fathers are binding their unborn children as blood brothers."],[167,120.58,3.59,"The children’s names— Guo Jing and Yang Kang—"],[167,124.2,2.21,"were engraved on a pair of daggers."],[167,126.54,3.21,"But a sudden Jurchen raid scatters the families."],[167,130.0,4.92,"Guo Jing grows up amongst the Mongols, yet remains loyal to his Han heritage."],[167,135.42,4.71,"Meanwhile, Jurchen royalty raise Yang Kang as a scheming prince—"],[167,140.26,3.0,"setting the stage for the brothers’ tragic conflict."],[167,143.93,3.96,"Jin’s novel follows the naive but kind-hearted Guo Jing"],[167,148.1,2.09,"and his clever lover Huang Rong."],[167,150.44,3.84,"Their journey takes them to the bustling Song capital of Hangzhou,"],[167,154.36,4.71,"the harsh Mongol steppes, and the fabled market city of Samarkand."],[167,159.28,2.59,"Our heroes traverse through jianghu,"],[167,161.99,4.25,"encountering colorful characters, and witnessing key historical events."],[167,166.58,4.46,"As they battle enemies, discover treasure, and help solve local crimes,"],[167,171.12,3.0,"their martial arts skills grow more legendary—"],[167,174.25,2.75,"preparing Guo Jing for his fated confrontation."],[167,177.92,3.88,"The novel showcases over 80 unique styles of martial arts,"],[167,181.8,4.46,"ranging from realistic techniques to almost mystical maneuvers."],[167,186.51,3.21,"But even seemingly simple moves have surprising depth."],[167,190.48,4.0,"To master a technique that will allow him to perform two attacks at once,"],[167,194.73,4.17,"Guo Jing must first learn to simultaneously draw a circle with one hand"],[167,198.94,1.54,"and a square with the other."],[167,200.61,2.75,"This kind of deceptively difficult tutelage"],[167,203.41,3.21,"torments wuxia students and readers alike."],[167,206.87,2.63,"Jin Yong was the pen name of Louis Cha,"],[167,209.62,3.17,"a journalist and founder of Ming Pao Daily News."],[167,213.21,5.13,"In the 1960s and 70s, wuxia was banned for its feudal associations."],[167,218.63,2.96,"But in Hong Kong, a British colony at the time,"],[167,221.76,4.42,"Cha could write fiction and cover news about the Cultural Revolution freely."],[167,226.56,2.5,"He even worked to add depth and complexity"],[167,229.06,3.21,"to a genre often considered formulaic and lowbrow."],[167,232.52,3.59,"“Legend of the Condor Heroes” celebrates Chinese identity"],[167,236.11,3.84,"by exploring China’s culture and history with intimate humanity."],[167,240.44,2.67,"Even though Jin’s books were banned in China,"],[167,243.11,2.92,"his work still found Chinese readers around the globe,"],[167,246.03,3.09,"and bootleg versions even made their way to the mainland."],[167,249.45,3.67,"When the Chinese government relaxed its policies in the 1980s,"],[167,253.12,2.25,"his novels found an eager audience."],[167,255.58,5.13,"Today, Jin Yong’s works have been adapted into film, television, and video games."],[167,261.13,2.88,"His novels are so beloved it has been said,"],[167,264.05,3.42,"“where there are Chinese people, there must be Jin Yong’s fiction.”"],[167,267.93,4.46,"However, this literary titan remains relatively unknown in the West,"],[167,272.39,2.29,"due to the difficulties of translation"],[167,274.69,3.5,"and the high level of Chinese cultural knowledge his works demand."],[167,278.65,4.17,"Fortunately, recent English translations of the “Legend of the Condor Heroes”"],[167,282.82,2.29,"are helping a new generation of readers"],[167,285.11,3.42,"experience the sweeping saga of the rivers and lakes."],[168,6.5,6.21,"In 1920, Hungarian physician Béla Schick ran a highly questionable experiment:"],[168,13.13,4.8,"he asked several women, some on their periods, to briefly hold flowers."],[168,18.47,4.71,"Then, without proper controls and relying solely on his own observations,"],[168,23.19,5.21,"he claimed that the blooms held by the menstruating women wilted more quickly."],[168,29.36,3.29,"Schick’s explanation for this was not only unscientific,"],[168,32.65,3.46,"but also played into age-old myths about periods."],[168,36.2,3.79,"He concluded that menstruating women’s bodies released toxins,"],[168,39.99,4.34,"excreted in sweat and blood, that can kill plants."],[168,45.12,2.0,"While it unfortunately took decades"],[168,47.13,4.09,"to debunk Schick’s so-called menstrual toxin hypothesis,"],[168,51.26,2.75,"some good did come from this work—"],[168,54.84,4.88,"the idea prompted investigations into the composition of menstrual fluid,"],[168,59.85,5.09,"eventually leading to a discovery that helped explain a very real problem:"],[168,65.27,1.46,"period cramps."],[168,67.02,3.17,"Anywhere from 50 to 90% of people who menstruate"],[168,70.19,4.09,"deal with painful abdominal or pelvic cramps during their period,"],[168,74.4,2.67,"a condition known as dysmenorrhea."],[168,77.53,5.84,"Individual experiences can vary, from mild discomfort, to throbbing aches,"],[168,83.37,4.13,"to contraction-like cramps that rival the pain of labor."],[168,87.96,2.5,"Cramping can extend into the back and thighs,"],[168,90.46,3.54,"and be accompanied by bloating, nausea, and even vomiting."],[168,94.13,2.29,"And for at least 10% of people who menstruate,"],[168,96.42,5.09,"these symptoms are so severe that they disrupt their day-to-day activities."],[168,102.18,4.17,"Healthcare experts classify dysmenorrhea into two main types:"],[168,106.39,2.84,"primary and secondary dysmenorrhea."],[168,109.61,4.13,"For some, menstrual cramps can be traced back to underlying conditions,"],[168,113.73,1.92,"including endometriosis,"],[168,115.65,5.05,"where tissue similar to the uterine lining grows outside the uterus,"],[168,121.12,3.71,"or uterine fibroids, which are non-cancerous growths."],[168,125.33,3.42,"This type of pain is called secondary dysmenorrhea."],[168,129.21,3.46,"While these cases can be linked to specific conditions,"],[168,132.67,3.59,"the origins of pain are often not well understood."],[168,136.51,4.75,"For example, endometriosis can lead to scarring and inflammation,"],[168,141.26,3.21,"and uterine fibroids can push against other organs,"],[168,144.64,2.09,"both of which can cause discomfort."],[168,146.93,3.42,"Yet the intensity of cramp pain doesn’t always match the severity"],[168,150.35,1.42,"of these factors."],[168,151.94,4.17,"And because the conditions are so variable, so is the treatment."],[168,156.69,3.79,"Primary dysmenorrhea, on the other hand, is much more common,"],[168,160.49,2.54,"and it refers to painful menstrual cramps"],[168,163.03,2.71,"that can’t be linked to an underlying condition."],[168,166.25,3.25,"Despite how misguided Schick's experiments were,"],[168,169.58,3.38,"they set a path toward better understanding this pain."],[168,173.13,3.88,"While early researchers obviously never found a menstrual toxin"],[168,177.01,2.13,"in the 1960s and 70s,"],[168,179.13,4.34,"scientists studying menstrual fluid did discover the presence of compounds"],[168,183.47,2.0,"called prostaglandins."],[168,185.76,3.63,"And further research showed that many people with painful period cramps"],[168,189.39,4.25,"often had higher levels of prostaglandins in their menstrual fluid."],[168,194.06,4.21,"One important role of prostaglandins is to stimulate the muscles of the uterus"],[168,198.28,1.17,"to contract,"],[168,199.49,3.96,"an essential step in shedding the uterine lining during a period."],[168,203.91,2.38,"So it’s thought that an excess of prostaglandins"],[168,206.28,3.21,"may intensify these contractions, causing pain."],[168,209.66,3.25,"This process can also cause blood vessels to constrict,"],[168,212.92,2.04,"reducing the flow of oxygen"],[168,215.21,3.42,"and releasing chemicals that activate pain receptors."],[168,219.17,2.54,"Since many over-the-counter pain medications,"],[168,221.72,4.59,"like naproxen and ibuprofen, work by targeting prostaglandins,"],[168,226.31,3.88,"they can provide relief for some people with period pain."],[168,230.31,5.17,"Others find hormone-based contraceptives that thin the uterine lining and, in turn,"],[168,235.48,3.21,"reduce prostaglandin production to be helpful."],[168,238.94,2.67,"But while prostaglandin research was foundational"],[168,241.61,2.88,"and shaped the way we understand menstrual pain,"],[168,244.49,3.34,"in recent years, it’s become evident that prostaglandins"],[168,247.83,2.04,"are just part of the story."],[168,250.37,3.5,"Many other factors likely contribute to painful cramping,"],[168,253.96,3.92,"including hormones, inflammation, brain pathways,"],[168,257.88,2.38,"and perhaps even the microbiome."],[168,261.38,2.84,"And because period pain is not fully understood,"],[168,264.22,2.79,"existing treatments don't always work for everyone."],[168,267.01,4.55,"On top of that, talking about menstrual cramps can feel awkward or even taboo,"],[168,271.77,4.3,"leading many people to feel that they have to simply put up with the pain."],[168,276.61,4.3,"But regularly suffering from severe cramping is far from benign."],[168,281.49,2.79,"Some experts believe that repeated exposure to pain"],[168,284.28,3.04,"can cause the nervous system to become more sensitive,"],[168,287.66,3.71,"leaving a person vulnerable to developing chronic pain conditions."],[168,291.7,3.59,"That's why more research is needed to untangle the complex drivers"],[168,295.29,4.84,"of this surprisingly common experience and to develop better treatments."],[168,300.46,4.59,"That work can start with recognizing that period pain is real,"],[168,305.43,3.09,"and it affects hundreds of millions of people."],[169,6.88,4.34,"In 1740, while Swiss naturalist Abraham Trembley"],[169,11.21,2.38,"was tutoring the sons of a nobleman,"],[169,13.63,3.09,"he had scooped up some pond water for them to study"],[169,16.85,2.88,"when he found something shocking inside."],[169,20.31,2.33,"He couldn't identify the odd little thing"],[169,22.73,2.75,"or even tell if it was a plant or an animal."],[169,26.06,3.54,"Its way of moving and consuming things seemed animal-like."],[169,29.73,4.0,"But when he cut parts off, or even split it in two,"],[169,33.74,1.38,"it regenerated,"],[169,35.11,4.34,"which was decidedly plant-like, in keeping with its green hue."],[169,39.87,3.88,"Trembley noted the organism’s similarity to the Hydra,"],[169,44.0,3.38,"the monster from Greek mythology, and the name stuck."],[169,47.84,5.3,"Today, we know hydras are animals capable of some incredible feats,"],[169,53.3,2.96,"apparently including immortality."],[169,57.39,5.84,"They comprise a genus of tiny aquatic animals in the broader phylum Cnidaria."],[169,63.6,4.63,"Some cnidarians, like jellyfish, have a stationary polyp phase,"],[169,68.36,2.92,"then a free-swimming Medusa stage."],[169,71.57,4.92,"But hydras and some of their relatives stay polyps their whole lives."],[169,77.41,4.42,"Hydras have relatively simple cylindrical body plans,"],[169,81.95,3.25,"one end forming the foot, the other the mouth,"],[169,85.33,2.34,"framed by fanning tentacles."],[169,88.13,3.04,"They’ll eat just about any critter they can snag."],[169,91.5,2.54,"And although they can scoot around if needed,"],[169,94.05,1.13,"most of the time,"],[169,95.17,5.34,"they remain stuck to things using a gluey substance secreted from their foot."],[169,100.85,4.84,"They can reproduce sexually, but they more often do so asexually,"],[169,105.85,2.29,"budding new growths off their bodies."],[169,109.11,3.42,"Where exactly they hunker down in the freshwater ponds"],[169,112.53,4.25,"and slow-moving streams they populate varies by species,"],[169,116.95,3.75,"which likewise differ in shape, size, and hue."],[169,121.24,1.71,"Green hydras, for one,"],[169,123.16,5.21,"bask in sunny spots to feed the photosynthetic algae they host,"],[169,128.46,5.34,"which lend them their color and provide sugars in return for other nutrients."],[169,134.13,4.09,"This explains some of Trembley’s “animal-or-plant” confusion."],[169,138.72,3.34,"Meanwhile, other species, like brown hydras,"],[169,142.1,3.0,"stick to the shade and hunt more actively."],[169,145.43,3.54,"Hydra tentacles are covered with stinging cells"],[169,149.06,5.3,"containing little capsules of pressurized, harpoon-like structures."],[169,154.57,3.42,"When triggered, they fly out at explosive speeds,"],[169,158.15,2.08,"immobilizing small prey."],[169,160.99,4.17,"This adaptation doesn’t protect hydras from larger predators—"],[169,165.33,2.21,"but what they lack in defenses,"],[169,167.62,4.46,"they make up for in their unparalleled regenerative abilities."],[169,172.5,3.25,"Not only can hydras regrow lost appendages,"],[169,175.92,2.63,"they can also reassemble themselves"],[169,178.67,3.38,"after going through a blending process of sorts,"],[169,182.05,4.17,"where they're completely broken down into individual cells."],[169,186.77,4.09,"To do this, these cells first aggregate into a sphere,"],[169,191.1,4.88,"then they begin to sort themselves, gradually hollowing out and elongating"],[169,196.11,3.38,"as they group into external and internal layers."],[169,200.11,4.55,"Soon, one end sprouts a few buds, which become the tentacles,"],[169,204.91,2.63,"while the other end develops into the foot."],[169,207.91,3.46,"In a matter of days, the hydra is back in one piece,"],[169,211.54,3.13,"bopping around and catching worms like nothing happened."],[169,215.0,4.96,"In labs, this process has helped shed light on embryonic development,"],[169,220.09,4.92,"because some of the stages and proteins are common among all animals—"],[169,225.22,3.71,"though the ability to perform it willy-nilly, as hydras do,"],[169,229.18,1.5,"is decidedly not."],[169,231.44,4.71,"Interestingly, if multiple hydras go through this shearing process,"],[169,236.23,3.79,"the resulting cells will form a single hydra."],[169,240.28,2.67,"This is probably because their immune systems"],[169,243.07,3.0,"don’t distinguish between self and others."],[169,246.49,4.09,"And this even holds true for interspecies mixtures."],[169,251.0,4.55,"However, hydras made from multiple species will fall apart"],[169,255.67,3.29,"because their cell types can’t adhere properly."],[169,259.71,3.67,"But when they're all in one place, they're kind of unstoppable."],[169,263.72,1.92,"All animals have stem cells,"],[169,265.64,3.88,"which can divide and differentiate into specialized cell types."],[169,269.68,3.88,"But for humans, they decline after embryonic development"],[169,273.56,2.67,"and become limited to specific parts of the body."],[169,276.81,4.21,"Meanwhile, hydra stem cell levels remain constant"],[169,281.03,5.38,"and can help replace any part of their body and seemingly divide forever."],[169,286.99,4.5,"In a roughly 20-day span, a hydra has all new cells."],[169,291.75,5.8,"This perpetual turnover prevents the buildup of age-related cellular damage,"],[169,297.79,5.34,"while enabling apparently boundless regeneration and reproduction."],[169,303.8,1.83,"They aren’t indestructible."],[169,305.84,2.21,"But so long as nothing kills them,"],[169,308.22,3.67,"they can theoretically live forever, without aging."],[169,312.18,4.67,"And many have indeed been persisting in labs for years on end,"],[169,317.1,4.05,"teaching us about development, regeneration, mortality,"],[169,321.4,2.96,"and tiny, tentacled freshwater life."],[169,324.78,3.71,"All of this from some supposed pond scum."],[170,0.29,2.46,"So, ketamine has been in the news a lot lately."],[170,2.75,3.71,"Celebrities are using it, sometimes with tragic or weird consequences."],[170,6.46,4.21,"Psychiatrists are prescribing it in record numbers for treatment-resistant depression."],[170,10.68,3.59,"And for a lot of people, it has become the recreational drug of the moment."],[170,14.26,1.46,"But what is it?"],[170,15.72,4.42,"It has properties of a hallucinogen, a pain killer, and an anti-depressant,"],[170,20.14,2.79,"but it doesn't fit neatly into any of those categories."],[170,22.94,4.84,"Today, we're going to explore what ketamine is and what the research says about its safety."],[170,30.99,4.25,"It’s been more than 60 years since scientists first synthesized ketamine."],[170,35.24,3.67,"One of its original uses was as an anesthetic during surgery."],[170,38.91,2.59,"And we’ve been successfully using it to keep people from"],[170,41.5,2.21,"feeling pain in the operating room ever since."],[170,43.71,2.08,"You can get it sent right into your bloodstream!"],[170,45.8,3.21,"Or, less traditionally, orally, rectally, or snorted."],[170,49.01,4.55,"I’m sure someone has tried them all, but you wouldn’t need to for a surgical context."],[170,53.55,2.79,"Doctors are pretty good at getting stuff into your veins."],[170,56.35,3.8,"And that’s great because ketamine can do a lot for you during surgery."],[170,60.14,1.58,"Aside from pain management,"],[170,61.73,4.55,"ketamine during or right after surgery can prevent the onset of depression."],[170,66.27,3.75,"Now, when you go in for a surgery, there’s a lot to manage post-op."],[170,70.03,2.33,"And depression might not cross your mind."],[170,72.36,4.13,"But one serious post-surgical depression you’ve probably heard of is post-partum"],[170,76.49,2.5,"depression, which can manifest after a C-section."],[170,79.0,3.04,"If you’re at higher risk of experiencing post-partum depression,"],[170,82.04,2.79,"you might benefit from ketamine or esketamine."],[170,84.83,3.04,"Both have data to support their use in these circumstances."],[170,87.88,2.34,"Yes, I just threw in a whole new vocab word!"],[170,90.22,4.0,"Ketamine is a mixture of two mirror image versions of a molecule."],[170,94.22,2.79,"Those versions are esketamine and arketamine."],[170,97.01,4.09,"Since some papers have reported that esketamine is more potent than arketamine,"],[170,101.1,3.17,"a lot of what we know today is focused on the S version."],[170,104.27,2.09,"In fact, forget arketamine!"],[170,106.36,2.59,"In some contexts, like recovering from surgery,"],[170,108.94,2.88,"esketamine has even stronger effects than ketamine!"],[170,111.82,3.67,"So a lot of researchers today study both ketamine and esketamine."],[170,115.49,4.17,"And they’re both associated with reducing your postpartum depression symptoms."],[170,119.66,2.63,"Those usually manifest within about a month,"],[170,122.29,3.29,"and that’s exactly the time period that studies have shown improvements in."],[170,125.58,2.46,"And it’s not just good for postpartum depression."],[170,128.04,3.04,"Post-operative depression can happen for more than one reason,"],[170,131.09,2.17,"and seems to be associated with inflammation."],[170,133.26,2.88,"The good news is ketamine has an anti-inflammatory"],[170,136.14,2.29,"effect and can take care of those symptoms as well."],[170,138.43,3.38,"A meta-analysis published in 2024 concluded that both"],[170,141.81,4.46,"ketamine and esketamine have significant benefits to your quality of recovery,"],[170,146.27,3.84,"including depression and pain, in the first day after your surgery."],[170,150.11,3.79,"And you don’t have to get a surgery for ketamine to help out with your depression."],[170,153.9,3.84,"Esketamine even has FDA approval to manage treatment-resistant depression."],[170,157.74,2.88,"So if you’ve been trying all sorts of therapies to manage your depression"],[170,160.62,3.84,"symptoms, but nothing has helped, this might be what your doctor turns to."],[170,164.46,1.84,"Even when you’re in an acute crisis."],[170,166.29,3.92,"One trial gave 15 suicidal emergency department admittees ketamine."],[170,170.21,4.38,"And in a matter of hours, 12 of them had no suicidal thoughts."],[170,174.59,1.71,"That sounds pretty life-saving to me!"],[170,176.3,3.09,"The effects of a single dose are surprisingly long lasting, too."],[170,179.39,4.75,"When the researchers followed up to check in on suicidal ideation over the course of hours,"],[170,184.14,3.92,"days, weeks, an even a month, suicidal ideation never relapsed."],[170,188.06,1.5,"Sure, it’s a small sample of people."],[170,189.56,1.67,"But those results are remarkable!"],[170,191.23,2.75,"Which makes you wonder why it isn’t more accepted!"],[170,193.99,2.88,"Until you find out that we don’t know exactly how it works."],[170,196.86,2.5,"We started out using ketamine as an anesthetic,"],[170,199.37,4.0,"and our understanding of how it works begins with its anesthetic properties."],[170,203.37,2.09,"So let me take you back to 1983,"],[170,205.46,4.92,"when we learned that ketamine works through NMDA receptors to reduce brain cell activity."],[170,210.38,2.17,"Those receptors are all over the place,"],[170,212.54,5.05,"including on brain cells that respond to pain, which may be behind the anesthetic effects."],[170,217.59,4.25,"The idea is: a molecule like your main excitatory chemical, glutamate,"],[170,221.85,3.04,"binds to a receptor on a brain cell’s membrane."],[170,224.89,5.84,"That signal can open the flood gates for calcium to rush into the cell, ramping up its activity."],[170,230.73,2.71,"But if the receptor is covered up by ketamine,"],[170,233.44,4.0,"the cell won’t react like it otherwise would when glutamate comes along."],[170,237.44,2.54,"So when ketamine’s around in a high enough dose,"],[170,239.99,3.04,"the brain cells that usually respond to pain don’t."],[170,243.03,4.96,"But if it’s around in lower doses, it can have a totally different effect."],[170,248.0,3.75,"Subanesthetic doses of ketamine can make you dissociate."],[170,251.75,4.71,"Which generally looks like hallucinations and disconnection from what’s going on around you."],[170,256.46,4.67,"Ketamine might have this effect by switching which brain cells are active and which are not."],[170,261.13,2.04,"It blocks NMDA receptors."],[170,263.18,4.09,"And in doing so, it shuts down cells that are usually more active."],[170,267.27,5.55,"At the same time, it ramps up activity in cells that otherwise would have been less active."],[170,272.81,6.13,"That swap could produce new patterns of brain activity that get interpreted as dissociation."],[170,278.94,4.09,"On the scale of your whole brain, ketamine might also shut down the"],[170,283.03,4.88,"cellular communication that takes in information from your senses and the"],[170,287.91,3.38,"cellular communication that interprets that information."],[170,291.29,5.09,"So you’re suppressing the feedback that would normally provide context for whatever’s going on."],[170,296.38,2.59,"All of which might make you feel out of sorts."],[170,298.96,4.21,"And on top of that, ketamine doesn’t just interact with NMDA receptors."],[170,303.18,5.71,"It also interacts with opioid receptors, nicotine receptors, dopamine receptors, and more."],[170,308.89,3.09,"It’s no wonder this drug can make you feel … different."],[170,311.98,2.17,"So that’s anesthesia and dissociation."],[170,314.15,4.09,"But researchers are also trying to figure out what makes ketamine an anti-depressant."],[170,318.23,3.04,"And the answer might be all the way down at the genetic level."],[170,321.28,4.96,"Both ketamine and esketamine affect how genes get expressed in a variety of cells,"],[170,326.24,1.75,"including those in the immune system."],[170,327.99,3.38,"The immune system, of course, is responsible for inflammation."],[170,331.37,3.96,"And, as we said earlier, depression and inflammation seem to be two peas in a pod."],[170,335.33,3.54,"So by changing the expression of genes that regulate immune activity,"],[170,338.88,3.84,"ketamine may be able to provide a therapeutic effect for depression."],[170,342.72,1.75,"That’s just one of the theories, though."],[170,344.47,2.79,"Ketamine also seems to be involved in neuron growth."],[170,347.26,2.17,"It just does so much!"],[170,349.43,2.63,"Which is part of what concerns some people."],[170,352.06,2.5,"Odds are it’s doing more than what you took it for."],[170,354.56,4.88,"And the risks of taking ketamine or esketamine range from minimal to very serious."],[170,359.44,3.04,"One of the main concerns is increased blood pressure and heart rate."],[170,362.49,2.54,"While this can be good in some anesthetic uses,"],[170,365.03,3.92,"it’s not something you want to see if you’re using ketamine for psychiatric purposes."],[170,368.95,5.51,"The good news is that a meta-analysis of 15 ketamine and esketamine studies found increases"],[170,374.46,5.63,"that are still pretty mild and short-term, on the order of an extra four heartbeats per minute."],[170,380.09,3.5,"And, while the blood pressure increases were high enough to be hypertensive,"],[170,383.59,4.63,"they weren’t high enough to be an emergency, and they lowered quickly after treatment stopped."],[170,388.22,1.79,"That sounds pretty short term and mild."],[170,390.01,2.71,"But we’re talking about a summary of averages,"],[170,392.73,3.67,"so any one person could have something very different going on."],[170,396.4,5.84,"Some individuals in these studies had a maximum of 29 extra beats per minute, which is a lot!"],[170,402.24,2.54,"It’s probably why people who know they have hypertension"],[170,404.78,2.5,"are not allowed to participate in ketamine trials."],[170,407.28,3.25,"So if you’re thinking about how you would fare on ketamine, think about"],[170,410.54,4.17,"things like medications for psychiatric conditions that raise your blood pressure."],[170,414.7,1.67,"I’m looking at you SNRIs."],[170,416.37,4.17,"And while some people take ketamine for its dissociative effects, you might find that to"],[170,420.55,4.09,"be a real problem if you’re just looking for an anesthetic or anti-depressant."],[170,424.63,2.96,"Not everyone is in the market for a weird “trip” when they’re"],[170,427.59,1.92,"trying to relieve pain or improve their mood."],[170,429.51,2.96,"Luckily, researchers report that this effect usually comes in under"],[170,432.47,2.25,"clinical levels and wears off relatively quickly."],[170,434.73,4.09,"In the emergency room study, one person who used ketamine did feel even more"],[170,438.81,4.21,"dissociative than they did before, which was already more than the average person."],[170,443.02,3.55,"But, considering the state he was in before taking ketamine,"],[170,446.57,5.84,"he still said the overall experience was good and there was improvement of his suicidality."],[170,452.41,2.96,"Given that person’s medical history, the risk was worth the reward."],[170,455.37,4.05,"But ketamine also has potential to increase your suicidal ideation."],[170,459.42,2.75,"Maybe through the same mechanisms that it decreases it."],[170,462.17,3.5,"Just a reminder that even though it’s been decades since we started using the stuff,"],[170,465.67,2.0,"we still don’t really know how it works!"],[170,467.68,3.13,"And if you don’t get the dose right, you can poison yourself!"],[170,470.8,4.38,"Based on reports of negative ketamine outcomes to the US Food and Drug Administration,"],[170,475.18,5.75,"about 20% of those resulted in hospitalization and about 5% in death."],[170,480.94,2.59,"So there are rare but very real risks."],[170,483.52,3.34,"Even with those reports, the FDA has approved esketamine"],[170,486.86,2.5,"nasal spray for treatment-resistant depression."],[170,489.36,2.21,"But it wasn’t a unanimous decision."],[170,491.57,3.13,"There was a lot of concern surrounding its risk of abuse."],[170,494.7,2.08,"We know that people have been taking ketamine"],[170,496.79,2.79,"outside of its prescribed uses since its early days."],[170,499.58,2.67,"There’s a link between the prescribed anti-depressant"],[170,502.25,2.54,"use and eventual substance use disorder."],[170,504.8,4.34,"When some people take it to reduce their depression symptoms and then it wears off,"],[170,509.13,2.0,"they sometimes take more and more."],[170,511.13,4.8,"If that’s enough to give you second thoughts about ketamine, the good news is that it’s inspiring the"],[170,515.93,5.0,"creation of other antidepressants…They’re just not as good as ketamine yet."],[170,520.94,3.71,"Other drugs that act on NMDA receptors like memantine and"],[170,524.65,3.59,"lanicemine haven’t proven to be effective in treating depression."],[170,528.24,3.42,"That’s why we need those researchers to keep searching for answers and"],[170,531.66,4.75,"figuring out which of ketamine’s many targets produces life-saving effects."],[170,536.41,2.13,"Once they understand all of that better,"],[170,538.54,2.96,"maybe they can replicate it without the unwanted side effects."],[170,541.5,3.84,"It’s proving hard to find a replacement for something that does so much."],[170,545.34,3.29,"At the end of the day, it really is a Special K."],[171,0.16,2.88,"Travel back in time with me to the early 2000s."],[171,3.04,4.32,"Y2K had just failed to end the world, emo music was coming onto the scene,"],[171,7.36,3.04,"and this weird new thing called YouTube had just been invented."],[171,10.4,3.76,"All the while, physicists were hard at work making an invisibility cloak."],[171,14.16,4.0,"Well, kinda. They made a prototype of a device that can shield objects"],[171,18.16,3.44,"from electromagnetic radiation and cloak them from detection."],[171,21.6,2.56,"Which is how we would make an invisibility cloak."],[171,24.16,2.24,"This particular one was just really specific,"],[171,26.4,3.6,"as it only worked for microwaves coming from one direction."],[171,30.0,3.6,"Even though that prototype was pretty far from a perfect disguise,"],[171,33.6,3.84,"the principles behind the device have had a lot of applications."],[171,37.44,4.61,"Including, believe it or not, helping make our cities invisible to earthquakes."],[171,45.57,5.31,"Okay but… how can a device make a city invisible, to light or to earthquakes?"],[171,50.88,2.32,"What does a cloaking device even do?"],[171,53.2,3.12,"Well, at the most basic level, it diverts waves."],[171,56.32,3.28,"Everything we see is a reflection of light bouncing off objects"],[171,59.6,1.76,"and back into our eyes."],[171,61.36,4.88,"So the idea with cloaking is to prevent visible light from bouncing off an object."],[171,66.24,1.36,"No bounce, no vision."],[171,67.6,2.08,"How do you avoid the bounce, you may ask?"],[171,69.68,1.84,"One way is to absorb the light."],[171,71.52,3.04,"But if you absorb all the light, you don’t get invisibility."],[171,74.56,4.0,"You get black… at least until an object heats up enough to start glowing."],[171,78.56,2.08,"So scratch that, we need another option."],[171,80.64,4.08,"Luckily, we have one: divert the light around an object entirely."],[171,84.72,2.4,"Which sounds great! Boom, solved!"],[171,87.12,3.52,"But the thing is, after you’ve sent the waves around an object,"],[171,90.64,2.16,"you have to bring those waves back together."],[171,92.8,2.32,"And if you aren’t careful, you’ll get a shadow."],[171,95.12,4.08,"Shadows happen when light doesn’t reach its target for one of two reasons."],[171,99.2,4.32,"It’s either blocked by an object directly, or there’s destructive interference,"],[171,103.52,1.92,"which is what happens when two waves that"],[171,105.44,3.68,"don’t match up perfectly run into each other and cancel each other out."],[171,109.12,3.92,"In principle, if you could make the light just go perfectly around an object,"],[171,113.04,2.88,"and meet back up on the other side perfectly aligned"],[171,115.92,4.96,"and without losing any power, then the object would be totally invisible."],[171,120.88,3.28,"If a wave is the same at point A and point C,"],[171,124.16,2.64,"it looks like nothing was in its path,"],[171,126.8,3.28,"even if something happened along the way at point B."],[171,130.08,3.68,"But to be truly invisible, you’d need to pull off this optical trick"],[171,133.76,3.52,"from all angles and for all wavelengths of light."],[171,137.28,2.32,"Which would be incredibly difficult."],[171,139.6,1.28,"It’s possible, though."],[171,140.88,3.12,"And scientists have created some proofs of concept over the years."],[171,144.0,4.24,"The biggest, most exciting breakthroughs are coming in the form of metamaterials:"],[171,148.24,3.92,"specially engineered materials not found anywhere in nature."],[171,152.16,2.24,"They have special properties that are possible"],[171,154.4,3.92,"due to the structure of the material, usually at the nanoscale."],[171,158.32,3.2,"That team from 20 years ago were the first folks to really start"],[171,161.52,2.88,"using these materials as cloaking devices,"],[171,164.4,4.32,"and they did it by squishing electromagnetic waves into submission."],[171,168.72,4.16,"Their prototype looked kind of like a very high-tech roll of duct tape."],[171,172.88,2.72,"The core concept was to use materials with specific"],[171,175.6,3.68,"electromagnetic properties that are bent into specific shapes."],[171,179.28,4.48,"With perfect design, the metamaterial would divert electromagnetic waves"],[171,183.76,5.04,"around the donut and precisely combine them back together on the other side,"],[171,188.8,4.16,"cloaking whatever object is hidden inside the duct tape roll object."],[171,192.96,5.28,"The materials they chose to meta-fy were copper filaments bent into very"],[171,198.24,5.28,"specific shapes and printed onto fiberglass cylinders at very specific intervals."],[171,203.52,3.12,"The key is that a conductive material, like copper,"],[171,206.64,4.8,"can be bent such that it reshapes electromagnetic waves in predictable ways."],[171,211.44,4.24,"Every shape bends the waves a bit differently, and when they work together,"],[171,215.68,4.56,"they squish incoming waves apart, guide them around the outside of the roll,"],[171,220.24,2.72,"and push them back together on the other side."],[171,222.96,2.24,"Boom. Invisible. Mostly."],[171,225.2,2.0,"Okay, so the prototype wasn’t perfect."],[171,227.2,4.08,"Being smushed around like that can rob a wave of some of its energy,"],[171,231.28,1.68,"meaning when it comes back together,"],[171,232.96,4.8,"it doesn’t look exactly like it did before, and casts a bit of a shadow."],[171,237.76,4.8,"But this was 2006! Again, YouTube had just been invented!"],[171,242.56,1.6,"This was practically the dark ages!"],[171,244.16,3.68,"So it’s very impressive that in 2006 a team of researchers got"],[171,247.84,4.0,"a physical prototype invisibility cloak to perform"],[171,251.84,3.52,"quite similarly to the simulations they were running on their computers."],[171,255.36,3.44,"The other caveat of this experiment, and it is a pretty big one,"],[171,258.8,2.4,"is that it was done with microwaves."],[171,261.2,4.56,"So… yeah, you could still totally see the object that was meant to be invisible."],[171,265.76,3.68,"But, to be fair, many promising cloaks start their testing with"],[171,269.44,3.04,"longer-than-visible wavelengths, like microwaves."],[171,272.48,2.72,"That’s because if you wanna mess with an electromagnetic wave"],[171,275.2,3.04,"using metamaterials, the metamaterial structures"],[171,278.24,3.04,"have to be smaller than the wavelength they’re manipulating."],[171,281.28,2.64,"Visible wavelengths are incredibly small."],[171,283.92,4.16,"Like 1/100th of the width of a human hair small."],[171,288.08,4.16,"So engineering materials smaller than that is a huge challenge."],[171,292.24,3.76,"Microwaves, on the other hand, are more like the size of a pea."],[171,296.0,4.88,"Still small, but MUCH bigger than visible wavelengths and much more practical!"],[171,300.88,2.72,"Now, obviously, it’s been 20 years, and scientists"],[171,303.6,3.36,"did not just stop trying to make cloaking devices."],[171,306.96,3.12,"There have been a ton of attempts to turn things invisible."],[171,310.08,3.04,"Some teams have tried fancy metamaterial coatings"],[171,313.12,3.92,"that scatter light so that the reflected beams cancel each other out."],[171,317.04,2.8,"But those only work at certain wavelengths so far,"],[171,319.84,1.92,"and the materials they’re made of can"],[171,321.76,2.56,"make the cloaks look darker than everything around them."],[171,324.32,4.88,"Others have made tiny cloaks printed on chips using even tinier waveguides"],[171,329.2,2.16,"to maneuver light around an object."],[171,331.36,2.88,"But those don’t work in 3D just yet."],[171,334.24,2.72,"Sheets of dielectrics with tiny metamaterial shapes"],[171,336.96,2.24,"printed on them seemed promising."],[171,339.2,4.56,"But actually putting the thing together results in flaws that cast shadows,"],[171,343.76,3.28,"and it only works with microwaves at certain angles."],[171,347.04,4.56,"Maybe we could make cloaks that adapt in real time using sensors and AI."],[171,351.6,4.24,"They could detect incoming light and reflect it back such that the reflection"],[171,355.84,3.2,"mimics the background behind the cloaking device."],[171,359.04,3.36,"But these also only work for limited light frequencies,"],[171,362.4,4.56,"and the principle feels a bit more like blending in than disappearing."],[171,366.96,3.04,"One team got creative with positioning traditional lenses"],[171,370.0,2.0,"to bend light around objects."],[171,372.0,2.72,"These make for some amazing looking pictures,"],[171,374.72,3.04,"but they’re not technically what we’re talking about here."],[171,377.76,4.8,"The physics behind them is closer to a telescope than a true cloaking device."],[171,382.56,1.84,"They’re just basically optical illusions."],[171,384.4,3.52,"That’s generally true across the spectrum of these prototypes."],[171,387.92,5.36,"A quote-unquote real cloaking device is so specific that pretty much nothing"],[171,393.28,4.88,"currently in development has reached the threshold of a true cloaking device yet."],[171,398.16,2.48,"Plus, if scientists are being honest with themselves,"],[171,400.64,4.0,"they’re still mostly stuck working outside of the visible light spectrum."],[171,404.64,3.68,"And to be really honest, they’re still pretty far off from"],[171,408.32,4.56,"truly cloaking anything in 3D, let alone in the optical band."],[171,412.88,2.0,"Again, small wavelengths are hard."],[171,414.88,2.08,"But big wavelengths are easier."],[171,416.96,6.0,"And some of the most promising applications for this tech are on a seismic scale."],[171,422.96,4.96,"Literally. Scientists want to use them for anti-earthquake technology."],[171,427.92,5.04,"But before we get to those city-sized cloaking devices, we have a quick ad for you."],[171,432.96,1.52,"You’re still watching this video."],[171,434.48,1.92,"So you’re probably getting something out of it."],[171,436.4,1.6,"Maybe you’re even enjoying it."],[171,438.0,3.52,"For a lot of people, it’s hard to imagine enjoying science and learning."],[171,441.52,2.08,"You could have been one of those people."],[171,443.6,2.24,"But after a little SciShow, who knows?"],[171,445.84,1.76,"Maybe science can be pretty cool after all."],[171,447.6,3.04,"If SciShow impacted you in that way, or in any good way,"],[171,450.64,2.16,"you can help us make more SciShow videos."],[171,452.8,4.16,"You have a chance to join Binorthedrunkdwarf, Charlie Stanley, Harry Plumley,"],[171,456.96,3.04,"Parabola Queen and Raz Tirosh as a President of Science"],[171,460.0,2.32,"and support SciShow production on Patreon."],[171,462.32,3.44,"However you’d describe SciShow videos, they’re probably more approachable,"],[171,465.76,4.72,"digestible, dare I say fun, than reading a textbook, falling asleep in a lecture hall,"],[171,470.48,2.72,"or trying to parse dense academic publications."],[171,473.2,2.24,"So if you agree that these videos are valuable,"],[171,475.44,4.64,"please responsibly visit patreon.com/SciShow."],[171,480.08,3.04,"When you think about it, cloaking technology for earthquakes"],[171,483.12,3.36,"totally makes sense—earthquakes are just what happens"],[171,486.48,2.96,"when seismic waves rattle through the Earth’s crust."],[171,489.44,3.52,"These waves are huge, and they carry a ton of energy!"],[171,492.96,4.64,"They can topple buildings, shake the earth apart, and trigger tsunamis."],[171,497.6,2.48,"But at the end of the day, a wave is a wave."],[171,500.08,4.16,"So if metamaterials can change the path of an electromagnetic wave,"],[171,504.24,4.64,"there’s no reason the same can’t theoretically be done for seismic waves, too."],[171,508.88,3.2,"With a strategic arrangement of meter-scale structures,"],[171,512.08,4.16,"there’s hope that we could design buildings—or maybe even entire cities—"],[171,516.24,2.48,"that would be invisible to earthquakes."],[171,518.72,2.64,"Historically, pretty much all of our earthquake prevention"],[171,521.36,2.32,"can boil down to one of three methods."],[171,523.68,2.64,"First, you could stabilize a structure by making everything"],[171,526.32,3.12,"as rigid as possible so it doesn’t get shaken apart."],[171,529.44,4.24,"Like, the Great Pyramids, for instance, made of sturdy stone blocks."],[171,533.68,2.72,"Second, you could build something that can go with the flow."],[171,536.4,2.88,"They can bend and deform so that nothing crumbles away."],[171,539.28,3.76,"Like suspension bridges built to sway with wind to keep themselves intact."],[171,543.04,3.76,"And a third version of earthquake management is energy dissipation."],[171,546.8,3.12,"Such as a counterbalance that sways opposite to"],[171,549.92,3.52,"the earthquake waves to dampen intense oscillations."],[171,553.44,3.04,"It’s basically the same as pushing a kid on a swing set,"],[171,556.48,2.24,"but out of sync to slow them down."],[171,558.72,5.36,"A great example of this is the massive metal dampener at the heart of Taipei 101,"],[171,564.08,2.08,"one of the tallest buildings in the world."],[171,566.16,3.76,"The dampener moves and dissipates energy so that the building doesn’t have to."],[171,569.92,1.84,"And all of those methods are great."],[171,571.76,2.4,"But some physicists and civil engineers think"],[171,574.16,2.96,"we can also add metamaterials to this list."],[171,577.12,3.68,"If you’re clever, you can arrange disordered structures that break up a wave"],[171,580.8,2.48,"by shifting against each other out of sync,"],[171,583.28,2.88,"rather than seamlessly conveying the seismic energy."],[171,586.16,2.8,"Break up the wave, lose the energy, no more quake."],[171,588.96,4.24,"The leading idea has been to drill holes under or near buildings."],[171,593.2,1.84,"Sometimes they’re filled with cement,"],[171,595.04,4.08,"but their general purpose is just to interrupt the seismic wave."],[171,599.12,3.28,"Very much like how the copper metamaterials interrupted"],[171,602.4,4.56,"and redirected the microwaves in that cloaking experiment from 20 years ago."],[171,606.96,3.28,"When a team initially tested the drilling-out-holes method,"],[171,610.24,4.56,"they strategically bored a bunch of 5-meter-deep holes in the ground"],[171,614.8,4.08,"to mimic the effect of the metamaterials, but on a giant scale."],[171,618.88,3.6,"Then, they simulated an earthquake, and found that the “quake”s power"],[171,622.48,4.0,"was reduced by 80% on the other side of the boreholes!"],[171,626.48,3.92,"Those are pretty promising results for this meter-scale metamaterial!"],[171,630.4,1.92,"Turns out drilling a bunch of holes could"],[171,632.32,3.68,"take a lot of stress off buildings in seismically active zones."],[171,636.0,3.76,"Some researchers actually think it’s possible that ancient megastructures,"],[171,639.76,3.04,"like the Coliseum, may have accidentally benefitted"],[171,642.8,2.4,"from this seismic cloaking method, too."],[171,645.2,2.4,"Even though the idea is promising, the team did"],[171,647.6,2.48,"run into some trouble during early tests."],[171,650.08,5.12,"Initially the boreholes reflected the waves instead of diverting or dissipating them."],[171,655.2,3.52,"And the last thing you wanna do is shove earthquakes off of one building"],[171,658.72,2.4,"and right onto the building next door."],[171,661.12,3.44,"So they need to work on diverting the waves around,"],[171,664.56,2.32,"or dissipating the energy even more."],[171,666.88,2.8,"So other experts have proposed drilling holes in"],[171,669.68,3.92,"the sides of the main boreholes to hopefully mitigate this reflection."],[171,673.6,2.32,"That idea hasn’t been completely worked out yet,"],[171,675.92,2.72,"so there’s still lots of work to do before boreholes"],[171,678.64,2.32,"become a popular form of earthquake proofing."],[171,680.96,3.2,"In the meantime, there are still other metamaterial-inspired"],[171,684.16,1.92,"cloaking strategies worth trying!"],[171,686.08,3.6,"For one, engineers could play around with above-ground oscillators."],[171,689.68,3.84,"Strategically placed above-ground pillars can oscillate in ways that"],[171,693.52,5.12,"disrupt the force of a seismic wave, to functionally turn a sideways-moving wave"],[171,698.64,3.04,"into a downward-moving wave, sending an earthquake"],[171,701.68,3.6,"towards the center of the planet instead of, you know, at us."],[171,705.28,4.0,"Even cooler, it turns out that forests could be natural versions"],[171,709.28,2.4,"of this kind of earthquake–cloaking device."],[171,711.68,3.92,"Forests with a naturally disordered arrangement of trees are capable of"],[171,715.6,4.72,"breaking up energy waves in the same way as those disordered man-made pillars."],[171,720.32,4.24,"So far, we’re still a ways off from actually deploying any of this kind of tech."],[171,724.56,3.76,"Every version of these large-scale metamaterials are pretty big,"],[171,728.32,3.84,"so engineers have to consider a lot of factors when designing them."],[171,732.16,3.28,"Then they’re very labor intensive to construct,"],[171,735.44,2.64,"and there’s the whole “some stuff is still reflecting"],[171,738.08,2.48,"off the target and onto what’s nearby” thing."],[171,740.56,2.56,"But just like with the metamaterial cloaking devices"],[171,743.12,2.72,"that inspired these earthquake-proofing techniques,"],[171,745.84,3.36,"experts are excited about all the applications still to come."],[171,749.2,2.4,"And the best part is, these techniques don’t have to"],[171,751.6,2.72,"stop at invisibility cloaks and earthquakes!"],[171,754.32,4.08,"Wave-manipulating metamaterials could have a lot of other applications."],[171,758.4,2.4,"They could potentially be used to create extreme"],[171,760.8,2.88,"soundproofing materials or noise cancellation devices."],[171,763.68,4.48,"Or we could go the other direction, and use nanostructures to focus waves,"],[171,768.16,2.24,"instead of only disrupting or dispersing them."],[171,770.4,3.76,"Metalenses are already being deployed in particle physics experiments,"],[171,774.16,4.16,"and could some day replace traditional lenses in cameras or telescopes."],[171,778.32,3.44,"Looks like we’re truly on our way to becoming wave-bending masters,"],[171,781.76,3.68,"and that’s cooler than anything that happened in 2006."],[171,785.44,1.53,"Take that High School Musical!"],[172,6.92,3.09,"Before, her hair was a writhing nest of snakes"],[172,10.13,2.25,"and her eyes had the power to petrify,"],[172,12.59,3.71,"Medusa was a young woman who worshipped at the temple of Athena,"],[172,16.51,2.46,"the goddess of wisdom and war."],[172,19.27,4.05,"But all the while, as Medusa tended to Athena’s shrine,"],[172,23.73,4.3,"Poseidon, god of the seas and horses, was watching her."],[172,28.36,3.88,"And one night, he crept into the temple as Medusa prayed,"],[172,33.7,1.54,"and assaulted her."],[172,35.78,3.63,"Athena reacted swiftly to the desecration of her shrine."],[172,40.12,4.88,"But instead of punishing Poseidon, she focused her wrath on Medusa."],[172,45.42,4.84,"The young woman felt her body transform until she was a monstrous Gorgon,"],[172,50.51,3.17,"her face framed by coils of hissing snakes."],[172,53.84,4.42,"No man could freely look upon her again, for if they met her eyes,"],[172,58.43,2.46,"they'd instantly turn to stone."],[172,62.6,2.88,"Medusa sought refuge in a hidden cave"],[172,65.48,5.71,"inhabited by the world’s two other Gorgons, Stheno and Euryale."],[172,71.78,4.5,"However, unlike Stheno and Euryale, who could never die,"],[172,76.78,2.0,"Medusa remained mortal."],[172,79.99,3.75,"Time passed and would-be heroes journeyed to the cave"],[172,83.79,5.0,"to make their names by murdering Medusa— but fell to her gaze every time."],[172,89.75,4.25,"This was the way of things until another young man, named Perseus,"],[172,94.01,4.42,"began his quest for glory— Medusa his intended trophy."],[172,99.39,3.5,"Perseus was born of the mortal princess Danae"],[172,103.06,4.46,"after Zeus impregnated her in the form of golden rain."],[172,108.02,4.05,"However, Danae’s father had received a prophecy"],[172,112.15,2.42,"foretelling that her son would kill him,"],[172,114.82,5.46,"so he locked Danae and Perseus in a chest and threw it into the sea."],[172,120.83,3.0,"Safeguarded by the gods, they survived."],[172,124.37,4.63,"But the king of their new land, Polydektes, lusted after Danae."],[172,129.29,3.59,"She tried turning King Polydektes away to no avail,"],[172,133.0,2.13,"and Perseus grew protective."],[172,135.8,5.88,"To distract Perseus, Polydektes feigned that he’d finally given up on Danae"],[172,141.68,1.79,"and would be wedding another."],[172,143.89,4.09,"When Perseus offered Polydektes whatever wedding gift he desired,"],[172,147.98,3.88,"Polydektes saw the opportunity to be rid of Perseus,"],[172,151.94,5.3,"and suggested that he prove his manhood by fetching a Gorgon's head."],[172,158.91,4.13,"Perseus accepted the foolhardy mission, gathered a crew,"],[172,163.07,4.21,"and set sail towards Graeae, three ancient sisters."],[172,167.41,4.59,"They shared a single eye and tooth and harbored numerous secrets."],[172,172.13,2.88,"As one sister passed their eye to another,"],[172,175.0,3.92,"Perseus grabbed it and forced them to reveal the locations"],[172,178.93,4.17,"of the nymphs of the River Styx and the cave of the Gorgons."],[172,184.39,3.5,"With the nymphs’ help, he obtained Hermes’ winged sandals,"],[172,188.06,3.75,"Hades’ cap of invisibility, and a special satchel."],[172,192.06,3.13,"And he accepted a gleaming sickle from Hermes,"],[172,195.44,4.34,"and a shield that shone like a mirror from Athena herself,"],[172,200.15,4.75,"the goddess who'd cursed Medusa with her monstrous form to begin with."],[172,205.66,4.59,"Then, one night, Perseus approached and Medusa fell asleep."],[172,211.29,4.25,"Perseus entered, crept towards the Gorgons’ slumbering forms,"],[172,215.92,3.54,"guiding his way with the reflections on Athena’s shield."],[172,219.55,3.0,"When he came upon Medusa resting peacefully,"],[172,222.55,2.75,"he bore his sword down upon her neck."],[172,225.51,4.42,"From the wound sprung Medusa and Poseidon’s hybrid offspring:"],[172,230.02,4.8,"a winged horse named Pegasus and a warrior called Chrysaor."],[172,235.56,3.21,"As Perseus stuffed Medusa's head in his satchel,"],[172,239.07,4.46,"Stheno and Euryale awoke to the horrific scene and attacked."],[172,243.78,3.79,"But donning the cap of invisibility and winged sandals,"],[172,247.78,2.42,"Perseus escaped unscathed,"],[172,250.58,5.09,"reached only by Euryale’s pained cry of despair echoing through the cave."],[172,256.75,2.67,"Perseus was met by his crew’s adulation."],[172,259.42,3.84,"And because death had failed to dim the power of Medusa’s gaze,"],[172,263.26,4.38,"Perseus used her severed head to kill Polydektes,"],[172,267.68,4.3,"then gave it to Athena, who placed it on her shield."],[172,272.23,4.46,"But Athena’s wasn’t the only shield emblazoned with Medusa’s face."],[172,276.73,4.75,"Ancient Greek and Roman artists committed Medusa’s image to everything"],[172,281.49,4.42,"from armor and paintings to ceramics and mosaic floors."],[172,286.37,4.67,"And while it was customary for ancient Greek subjects to appear in profile,"],[172,291.29,4.38,"Medusa was almost always facing directly outwards."],[172,295.83,6.46,"She was an apotropaic symbol, one that imbued both fear and protection at once."],[172,302.8,2.42,"Her story reverberated through time."],[172,305.76,4.63,"In many versions, including the earliest, she was always a Gorgon;"],[172,310.6,3.25,"in others, like the ancient Roman poet Ovid’s,"],[172,313.93,3.0,"she had a sympathetic, human backstory—"],[172,316.94,4.09,"as a woman who’d experienced cruelty and injustice,"],[172,321.57,3.0,"not just a simple monster to slay."],[173,4.26,2.08,"A few years ago,"],[173,6.38,5.44,"I found myself individually coaching both a manager and an employee"],[173,11.86,1.44,"at the same time."],[173,14.02,4.12,"The employee was super confident she'd get promoted,"],[173,18.14,2.84,"whereas the manager privately shared with me"],[173,21.02,2.76,"that she had no plans to do so."],[173,24.94,1.72,"Why the disconnect?"],[173,27.38,4.92,"The manager admitted using the feedback sandwich technique,"],[173,32.34,3.64,"which is praise, feedback, then praise again,"],[173,36.02,3.52,"embedding that critical feedback in the praise"],[173,39.58,2.64,"leading to false expectations."],[173,43.74,4.24,"Despite the manager's good intentions, the impact was damaging."],[173,48.54,3.68,"There was burnout for the manager for overcompensating."],[173,52.22,3.64,"There was a breakdown in trust for the employee."],[173,55.86,4.64,"And ultimately, unachieved goals for the organization."],[173,63.3,2.36,"Having coached hundreds of leaders,"],[173,65.7,4.12,"this is just one of many examples that I've encountered"],[173,69.86,3.32,"where low emotional intelligence,"],[173,73.18,1.76,"EQ for short,"],[173,74.94,2.24,"leads to low results."],[173,77.58,2.96,"And that's no surprise, given the decades of research"],[173,80.58,3.08,"that show that EQ can boost profitability."],[173,85.26,4.68,"My fascination with human behavior started at an early age,"],[173,89.94,2.84,"where, amidst family dysfunction,"],[173,92.82,5.56,"I witnessed firsthand how low EQ can leave a negative legacy."],[173,99.62,3.72,"And now, as a psychologist, as an executive coach,"],[173,103.34,3.96,"I'm passionate about drawing from my unique corporate"],[173,107.34,1.68,"and counseling background"],[173,109.06,1.84,"to bridge theory and practice"],[173,110.94,1.44,"when it comes to teams."],[173,113.58,2.56,"Managers, I see you."],[173,116.66,2.08,"With the pressure to meet bottom-line goals,"],[173,118.74,2.08,"you often have to do more with less."],[173,121.5,1.96,"But what got you here?"],[173,123.46,2.64,"Excelling as an individual contributor,"],[173,126.1,1.96,"doing things yourself,"],[173,128.1,2.76,"won't get you success as a manager."],[173,131.82,1.4,"As a leader,"],[173,133.26,4.44,"your impact is measured by what you achieve through your team."],[173,139.02,1.72,"But let's be honest,"],[173,140.78,4.24,"increasing the intrinsic motivation and accountability of your team"],[173,145.02,1.36,"isn't always easy,"],[173,146.42,4.4,"especially when there's resistance or a breakdown in trust."],[173,152.5,2.48,"How do you drive results?"],[173,154.98,4.76,"By unlocking the leadership potential of every team member,"],[173,159.74,1.44,"no matter their title."],[173,162.38,5.48,"I believe it begins by elevating your emotional intelligence"],[173,167.9,3.28,"with three key skills:"],[173,171.18,1.56,"connect,"],[173,172.78,1.28,"correct"],[173,174.1,1.36,"and cultivate."],[173,176.34,4.24,"These three C’s make up a research-backed framework"],[173,180.58,2.8,"called “Leadership Activated” that I’ve developed"],[173,183.38,2.68,"to help leaders practically build trust,"],[173,186.1,2.44,"guide behavior and foster growth."],[173,189.34,1.88,"The first C is connect."],[173,193.02,2.96,"An executive once asked me to repurpose her session time"],[173,196.02,4.4,"to coach a seasoned employee of hers whose performance was declining."],[173,201.7,2.24,"But as soon as I got into session,"],[173,203.98,3.96,"I knew she didn't need coaching on her external skills."],[173,208.34,5.68,"She needed coaching to really uncover the internal factors"],[173,214.02,1.8,"influencing her performance."],[173,217.18,3.52,"Connection is what unlocks high performance"],[173,220.7,2.96,"because it creates psychological safety."],[173,224.1,3.28,"A key driver to team success"],[173,227.42,5.0,"where individuals feel freedom to communicate without fear."],[173,233.78,1.24,"Research also shows"],[173,235.06,4.8,"that the number-one factor to job satisfaction is not our pay,"],[173,239.9,3.64,"but how valued and appreciated we feel."],[173,245.18,3.4,"Sometimes we have to go slow to go fast."],[173,248.9,5.32,"And trust is an accelerator to business results."],[173,254.94,4.56,"And we build trust when we take the time to connect."],[173,261.1,3.0,"What would it look like for this manager to connect?"],[173,264.66,4.68,"Because I see a lot of managers push without connection."],[173,269.98,3.6,"And what happens is that the behavior gets worse"],[173,273.58,4.6,"because correction without connection"],[173,278.22,2.48,"feels like rejection."],[173,282.42,3.56,"Connection is what separates our work from our worth."],[173,285.98,3.28,"It's about valuing people not just for what they do,"],[173,289.26,2.32,"but who they are."],[173,293.1,3.32,"The best practice here is when you use a tone of curiosity,"],[173,296.46,1.8,"you can say something like,"],[173,298.3,2.68,"\"I want you to know it's safe to share.\""],[173,301.46,2.44,"Or use open-ended questions like,"],[173,303.94,1.72,"\"Can you help me understand?\""],[173,305.98,3.36,"Or use phrases like, \"I see you,\""],[173,309.38,1.12,"\"I hear you."],[173,311.1,1.52,"That's hard.\""],[173,313.26,4.08,"All while paraphrasing back to make the employee feel heard."],[173,318.5,4.44,"And when we do, the magic here is that we uncover the root issue."],[173,322.98,2.36,"Maybe it's imposter syndrome."],[173,325.38,2.84,"Maybe it's a personal issue at play."],[173,328.66,1.68,"What makes them feel valued?"],[173,330.38,1.76,"What motivates them?"],[173,332.46,1.88,"All without fixing, lecturing,"],[173,334.38,3.08,"or using the information against them in any way."],[173,338.58,2.36,"It's about talking with them,"],[173,340.94,1.84,"not at them."],[173,343.74,4.68,"And sometimes we find part of contributing to this resistance"],[173,348.42,1.64,"is actually us."],[173,351.1,2.24,"Lean in to that insight."],[173,353.74,1.2,"It's a gift."],[173,356.5,3.72,"Instead of defending your intention,"],[173,360.22,2.24,"model owning your impact."],[173,362.86,1.96,"Saying something like,"],[173,364.86,2.32,"\"It looks like I need to work on that\""],[173,367.18,1.88,"goes a long way."],[173,369.5,3.48,"The first C, connect, is not a nice-to-have."],[173,373.02,3.24,"Even if it takes more than one meeting,"],[173,376.26,2.88,"the only goal is to make an employee feel seen."],[173,380.1,2.44,"And while that's essential,"],[173,382.58,4.72,"not providing any developmental guidance is actually coddling."],[173,388.94,2.2,"The second C is correct."],[173,392.34,2.8,"I once coached a brilliant COO,"],[173,395.14,4.4,"but he would redo his team's work instead of correcting them."],[173,400.38,4.52,"His overfunctioning enabled their under-functioning."],[173,406.06,3.24,"The story he told himself was"],[173,409.34,2.0,"\"feedback was hurting people.\""],[173,412.3,2.32,"But when you look at the definition of a manager,"],[173,414.62,5.12,"which is to steward the organization's goals through people,"],[173,419.78,4.12,"feedback given with psychological safety is actually empowering."],[173,425.02,3.04,"Now that we've separated work from worth,"],[173,428.1,4.0,"we can have a compassionate focus on behavior."],[173,433.22,2.84,"Remember, we are correcting the behavior,"],[173,436.06,1.2,"not the person."],[173,438.66,3.24,"What would it look like for this manager to correct?"],[173,443.06,5.48,"Well, one thing I see a lot of managers do is hint."],[173,449.1,1.44,"Hinting doesn't work."],[173,451.7,5.48,"Effective correction balances clarity and kindness."],[173,458.18,1.32,"No one can read your mind."],[173,459.94,3.56,"Instead of saying \"deadlines are important,\""],[173,463.54,2.76,"we can say \"The deadline was X."],[173,466.34,1.72,"This was submitted on Y."],[173,468.1,4.28,"We need all deadlines met from now on because of a Z impact.\""],[173,477.86,5.24,"Authors Heen and Stone break down the psychology behind why we do this."],[173,483.14,3.6,"The feedback sits at the intersection of two competing human needs."],[173,486.78,3.24,"On one hand, we want to learn and grow."],[173,490.06,3.28,"On another hand, we want to be worthy and enough"],[173,493.34,2.84,"just the way we are, without change."],[173,497.1,2.44,"Vague feedback equals vague commitment"],[173,499.58,2.6,"equals zero chance of change."],[173,502.94,3.2,"And no one is motivated by shame."],[173,506.98,5.0,"So managers are so much more successful when they use the word \"and\""],[173,512.02,1.2,"instead of \"but.\""],[173,513.54,2.76,"\"We\" instead of \"you.\""],[173,517.62,2.72,"\"Your client dedication is impressive"],[173,520.38,4.52,"and can we collaborate to improve communication?\""],[173,526.9,2.52,"Unlike the ineffective feedback sandwich,"],[173,529.46,4.72,"the first C, connect, establishes enough trust for the second C, correct,"],[173,534.18,2.44,"which is that clear, kind guidance."],[173,537.22,3.36,"And because connect and correct is not a one-and-done --"],[173,540.62,2.8,"the third C is cultivating."],[173,544.3,4.36,"Just like a garden needs ongoing care to flourish,"],[173,548.7,5.32,"leadership potential is cultivated by ongoing coaching."],[173,555.26,4.64,"Yet many managers will wait until the last minute to a year-end review"],[173,559.9,2.6,"to give vague feedback,"],[173,562.54,1.92,"which of course erodes trust."],[173,564.94,3.16,"Whereas research shows that real-time, informal,"],[173,568.14,3.4,"frequent feedback is far more effective."],[173,572.94,3.92,"And when we ask our team open-ended coaching questions like,"],[173,576.9,2.36,"\"What do you think we should do?\""],[173,579.3,3.96,"it empowers a whole team to step up with solutions."],[173,583.82,5.16,"Which unlocks the leadership potential of both our team as well as our own"],[173,588.98,3.88,"because we're trading in burnout for a culture of ownership."],[173,596.06,3.88,"This talk is actually not about feedback"],[173,599.94,2.08,"or the latest management trend."],[173,604.38,2.84,"Connect, correct and cultivate"],[173,607.26,2.96,"are these three C’s that we may excel in one --"],[173,610.26,3.32,"but the truth, they’re like a tripod."],[173,613.62,3.68,"And if we don't excel in all three of them,"],[173,617.34,3.2,"our ability to activate leadership falls apart."],[173,621.86,3.6,"But you may think that EQ is fluff,"],[173,625.5,5.56,"that a focus on people moves us further away from the bottom line."],[173,631.98,4.76,"However, I am in the trenches with leaders daily,"],[173,636.78,1.88,"and I know firsthand"],[173,638.7,4.32,"that emotional intelligence is our greatest strategy to results,"],[173,643.02,4.0,"because our work is only as good as our work with other people."],[173,648.78,3.2,"The secret to high performance is simple."],[173,653.42,2.16,"We raise the intrinsic motivation"],[173,655.62,1.8,"and accountability of our teams"],[173,657.46,4.24,"when they first feel seen, heard and valued."],[173,664.18,3.16,"However, leadership isn't just about performance."],[173,669.06,6.68,"I know firsthand that low EQ can create cycles of mistrust,"],[173,675.78,2.04,"disconnection and pain."],[173,681.38,2.64,"I dream of a healthier world"],[173,684.06,1.32,"where we don't just push"],[173,685.42,3.28,"on the world-changing missions of our organizations,"],[173,688.74,5.0,"but the accelerated and unprecedented pace to results"],[173,693.78,1.72,"through non-human means."],[173,695.94,3.04,"We must anchor those achievements"],[173,699.02,2.28,"in what is uniquely human."],[173,701.82,1.48,"Not replacing,"],[173,703.34,3.76,"but retaining connection"],[173,707.14,4.28,"as the cornerstone and the launching pad to all of our progress."],[173,712.38,4.56,"I believe we can raise the emotional intelligence of entire workplaces"],[173,716.98,2.6,"to make them as kind as they are effective."],[173,720.18,4.8,"To create an impact that's both measurable and meaningful."],[173,726.26,1.8,"We're not just leaders,"],[173,728.1,2.12,"we're cycle breakers."],[173,730.86,2.48,"So today I'm asking you,"],[173,733.38,3.72,"what kind of legacy do you want to leave as a leader?"],[173,738.14,2.76,"Will it be defined solely by the \"what,\""],[173,740.94,2.72,"the bottom-line goals,"],[173,743.7,3.68,"or will it transcend to the \"how?\""],[173,747.42,2.24,"How, through connection,"],[173,749.66,3.76,"we created an impact that truly mattered."],[173,754.46,4.76,"Together, we can architect the leadership of tomorrow."],[173,759.66,1.4,"Thank you."],[174,9.6,1.84,"A kilometre from the surface,"],[174,11.44,3.84,"beyond the reach of the sun."],[174,19.56,7.64,"A giant black void, larger than all the rest of the world's habitats combined."],[174,31.04,2.44,"There's life here…"],[174,34.24,3.12,"..but not as we know it."],[174,47.0,5.32,"Alien-like creatures produce dazzling displays of light."],[174,63.92,5.64,"Nearly all animals need to attract mates and repel predators."],[174,71.36,3.52,"This language of light is so widespread here"],[174,74.88,5.36,"that these signals are probably the commonest form of communication"],[174,80.24,3.0,"on the entire planet."],[174,83.36,2.92,"And yet we still know little about them."],[174,110.24,6.56,"Hunters illuminate themselves and, by doing so, attract inquisitive prey."],[174,128.12,3.48,"This is fangtooth."],[174,132.16,5.44,"It has the largest teeth for its size of any fish."],[174,138.4,3.52,"There are pressure sensors all over its head and body,"],[174,141.92,3.6,"which can detect anything moving in the surrounding water."],[174,151.92,3.88,"It's the midnight zone's most voracious fish."],[174,157.52,4.0,"But prey use light as a distraction."],[174,165.24,3.84,"A decoy of luminous ink."],[174,177.16,3.2,"Down here, in this blackness,"],[174,181.52,3.8,"creatures live beyond the normal rules of time."],[174,191.72,2.64,"Siphonophores are virtually eternal."],[174,195.2,3.96,"They repeatedly clone themselves,"],[174,199.16,4.48,"some eventually growing longer than a blue whale."],[174,226.76,4.36,"Down here, it snows."],[174,237.96,6.44,"Continuous clouds of organic debris drift slowly down from above."],[174,250.64,2.28,"This is food,"],[174,252.92,5.04,"and a whole variety of filter feeders depend on it."],[174,260.08,1.48,"Jellyfish."],[174,269.72,2.76,"And delicate sea cucumbers."],[174,291.32,7.72,"The 1% of marine snow they miss eventually settles on the seafloor."],[174,324.96,1.8,"Over millions of years,"],[174,326.76,5.44,"it forms a layer of mud up to a kilometre thick."],[174,335.48,5.76,"It's an empty plain that covers half the surface of our planet."],[174,366.04,4.84,"The deep seabed may at first appear lifeless,"],[174,372.08,5.12,"but it's home to a unique cast of mud-dwellers."],[174,379.48,2.92,"The sea toad."],[174,383.72,4.28,"It is an ambush predator with an enormous mouth…"],[174,389.44,3.2,"..and infinite patience."],[174,402.12,3.4,"This fish has been living for so long here"],[174,405.52,4.6,"that its fins have changed into something more useful."],[174,415.08,2.44,"Feet."],[174,421.12,4.0,"They help it shuffle about on the seafloor."],[174,444.36,3.4,"The flapjack octopus."],[174,451.24,5.32,"It hovers just above the surface of the mud as it delicately sifts through it,"],[174,456.56,2.44,"searching for worms."],[174,461.56,4.8,"But it can jet away at the first sign of danger."],[174,474.2,3.8,"The weirdest, in this world of the strange –"],[174,478.0,4.92,"vampyroteuthis, the vampire squid from hell."],[174,487.92,4.12,"Disturb it, and it only retreats a little distance."],[174,495.8,3.76,"Go after it, and it has a special defence."],[174,503.68,3.48,"To see what it does, you must switch off the lights."],[174,510.16,3.12,"The vampire squid has lights of its own."],[174,516.28,4.2,"Bioluminescent bacteria shine from pockets on its arms"],[174,520.8,2.12,"to confuse its predators."],[174,524.12,1.2,"Are those eyes?"],[174,530.04,2.64,"In fact, they're spots at the end of its mantle."],[174,532.68,3.08,"A bite there would leave the head unscathed."],[174,538.4,2.24,"The threat diminishes"],[174,540.64,4.88,"and vampyroteuthis disappears into the blackness."],[174,552.96,3.0,"Even in the dark zone there is some light."],[174,557.08,7.24,"Turn off the submersible headlights and you see a pyrotechnic display outside."],[174,565.36,2.96,"These lights are created by animals."],[174,568.32,3.0,"This is bioluminescence."],[174,573.32,3.0,"A deep-sea anglerfish flashes in the darkness."],[174,579.96,2.36,"The light is generated by bacteria"],[174,582.32,2.72,"that live permanently inside the lure,"],[174,585.04,3.0,"which attracts prey to these murderous teeth."],[174,591.56,3.8,"There are all sorts of lures out in the darkness."],[174,603.08,3.12,"Come into my mouth, little fish."],[174,611.28,1.96,"And what is the purpose of this lure,"],[174,613.24,5.96,"suspended on a long rod way below its owner's terrifying set of teeth?"],[174,622.32,3.6,"It's difficult to be sure, but then this monster does have"],[174,625.92,3.8,"another giant flashing lure much closer to its mouth."],[174,638.04,3.2,"These fish are called anglers because they use their lures"],[174,641.24,5.24,"in much the same way as fly fishermen use their imitation flies."],[174,651.28,2.68,"For a hunting squid with huge eyes,"],[174,653.96,3.0,"this glimmer is intriguing."],[174,660.96,2.04,"It might just be food."],[174,664.28,2.28,"Many caves are like islands –"],[174,666.76,3.64,"cut off from the outside world and from other caves."],[174,678.72,3.48,"This isolation has resulted in the evolution"],[174,682.2,2.56,"of some very strange creatures."],[174,687.16,2.52,"They are the cave specialists."],[174,689.68,5.44,"Troglobites – animals that never emerge from the caves or see daylight."],[174,708.16,2.64,"These troglobites from Thailand"],[174,710.8,3.68,"are possibly the most specialised creatures on Earth,"],[174,714.56,3.36,"for they live only in cave waterfalls."],[174,718.88,3.16,"The entire population of these cave angelfish"],[174,722.04,4.12,"seems to be restricted to just two small caves."],[174,730.2,2.6,"It's the same story with other troglobites."],[174,732.8,4.96,"There may well be less than 100 Texas cave salamanders in the wild."],[174,754.08,4.04,"And the Belizean white crab is another creature"],[174,758.12,3.04,"that is unique to just one cave system."],[174,766.32,2.12,"Living in perpetual darkness,"],[174,768.44,2.88,"they have all not only lost the pigment in their skin,"],[174,771.6,1.8,"but also their eyes."],[174,775.44,3.72,"It takes thousands of generations for eyes to be lost,"],[174,779.16,4.4,"so these species must have been isolated for a very long time."],[174,788.76,5.08,"But the blind salamander has other highly developed sensory organs."],[174,796.72,5.68,"Receptors in the skin detect minute movements in the water made by its prey."],[174,808.8,5.48,"External gills help it to breathe in water that is particularly low in oxygen."],[174,825.88,5.04,"The cave angelfish feed on bacteria in the fast-flowing water,"],[174,830.92,4.0,"keeping their grip with microscopic hooks on their fins."],[174,845.36,2.64,"Food is often in short supply"],[174,848.0,2.16,"and troglobites like the crab"],[174,850.16,4.0,"have to survive on whatever washes into the cave from outside."],[174,858.68,3.48,"A salamander might not encounter food for several months,"],[174,862.24,1.92,"so when something does come along,"],[174,864.16,2.04,"it can't afford to miss it."],[174,873.04,5.56,"It's astonishing that these extraordinary cave dwellers manage to survive at all."],[174,878.6,2.48,"Million tonnes of sea creatures"],[174,881.08,4.48,"ascend from the deep ocean to search for food near the surface."],[174,891.96,3.8,"They graze on the phytoplankton under cover of darkness."],[174,896.52,2.92,"Even so, they're far from safe."],[174,899.44,1.88,"Other marine hunters follow them,"],[174,901.32,3.92,"some travelling up from hundreds of metres below."],[174,963.72,5.32,"At dawn, the whole procession returns to the safety of the dark depths."],[174,976.44,1.32,"If you want to find"],[174,977.76,4.8,"the greatest number and variety of animals that communicate with light,"],[174,982.56,4.16,"you have to go to the darkest place on Earth."],[174,986.72,3.4,"Somewhere far beyond the reach of the sun's rays,"],[174,990.36,2.36,"where human beings hardly ever go."],[174,992.72,2.0,"To the depths of the ocean."],[174,995.28,2.44,"This is the Johnson-Sea-Link."],[174,997.72,5.52,"At the front, it has an array of remotely controlled television cameras and searchlights."],[174,1003.24,3.8,"I'm sitting beside the pilot in the transparent bubble in the middle,"],[174,1007.04,2.56,"and the whole craft is massively strengthened"],[174,1009.6,3.8,"to withstand the huge pressures of the depths."],[174,1017.04,3.76,"As we go down, it gets darker and darker."],[174,1022.76,4.16,"The water is thick with small floating organisms."],[174,1033.28,5.48,"At 600 feet, the water outside is 20 times atmospheric pressure."],[174,1039.68,3.0,"The temperature is within a few degrees of freezing,"],[174,1042.68,3.0,"and we are far beyond the reach of the sunshine."],[174,1045.8,2.76,"So you might think in such a hostile environment,"],[174,1048.56,2.52,"there would be very few animals living."],[174,1051.08,1.68,"But watch this."],[174,1054.88,5.52,"The chorus of lights is being made by hundreds of small deep-sea creatures,"],[174,1060.4,3.32,"which are flashing in response to my light."],[174,1069.36,3.96,"And now, if I turn on the lights of the submersible,"],[174,1073.32,1.64,"we may catch a glimpse"],[174,1074.96,3.44,"of one of these strange deep-sea creatures as it drifts by."],[174,1079.72,3.72,"The pilot has remote controls for the camera outside the sub,"],[174,1083.72,3.0,"to search for them in the blackness."],[174,1089.96,3.0,"This is a comb jelly as big as a football."],[174,1093.56,4.2,"It's been nicknamed \"Big Red\", but it has yet to be given a proper name."],[174,1098.08,3.0,"It's new to science."],[174,1101.96,2.64,"Another new undescribed comb jelly."],[174,1104.6,2.36,"Although specimens have been brought to the surface,"],[174,1106.96,4.0,"it's only been seen alive through the windows of deep-sea craft like this."],[174,1112.4,3.36,"It uses a pair of long, retractable tentacles to catch fish."],[174,1122.28,3.16,"A jellyfish, Solmissus, two feet across,"],[174,1125.44,2.56,"which, in spite of the changes of pressure,"],[174,1128.0,2.96,"sometimes swims quite close to the surface."],[174,1137.32,2.36,"Kiyohimea, another comb jelly,"],[174,1139.68,4.24,"one that is surrounded by gauzy skirts."],[174,1148.16,2.36,"But for the most sensational spectacle,"],[174,1150.52,3.0,"you have to turn the lights of the submarine off."],[174,1157.92,4.4,"A jellyfish outlined by its own pulsing illuminations."],[174,1171.44,3.52,"A squid, its lights moving as its body throbs."],[174,1174.96,4.48,"Displays like this may serve for defence or to send messages."],[174,1179.44,3.0,"No one knows."],[174,1186.04,2.16,"And most spectacular of all,"],[174,1188.2,6.36,"another jellyfish with its own amazing rhythmic flashing system."],[174,1218.64,2.04,"In the Gulf of Mexico,"],[174,1220.68,4.56,"these eruptions also release a super-salty liquid –"],[174,1232.76,1.68,"brine."],[174,1245.72,2.32,"It's heavier than seawater"],[174,1248.04,3.72,"and it accumulates in great pools on the seafloor."],[174,1257.2,3.76,"It's difficult to make sense of the sight."],[174,1267.04,4.84,"A lake of concentrated saltwater 15 metres deep"],[174,1272.88,2.12,"at the bottom of the sea."],[174,1282.4,3.64,"Around its margin, perhaps even more strangely,"],[174,1286.04,3.0,"there is a profusion of life."],[174,1295.6,4.12,"Giant mussels that can live and grow for a century or more,"],[174,1299.72,1.56,"pack tightly together,"],[174,1301.28,4.52,"dwarfing the shrimps and squat lobsters that feed around them."],[174,1334.0,3.0,"Cutthroat eels, scavengers,"],[174,1337.2,4.16,"come to the shores of the brine lake in search of something edible."],[174,1349.88,3.32,"Some even venture into the brine."],[174,1386.24,1.72,"Spending too long in it"],[174,1387.96,2.68,"can send an eel into toxic shock."],[174,1402.44,2.72,"Its only hope is to rise above it."],[174,1419.96,3.0,"It manages to escape."],[174,1430.76,2.2,"Others are not so lucky."],[174,1439.92,2.96,"The brine embalms their bodies…"],[174,1444.0,3.0,"..and the casualties of decades"],[174,1447.0,2.6,"accumulate around the margins."],[175,4.27,1.83,"Let me tell you about the future."],[175,6.84,4.87,"Predictions are the boxing ring where fights over the future take place."],[175,12.91,1.5,"As the story goes,"],[175,14.45,2.8,"King Louis XI kept an astrologer in court."],[175,17.75,2.04,"One day, the astrologer predicted"],[175,19.82,2.67,"that a lady of the court would die within a week."],[175,23.19,2.97,"When she did, Louis was shaken."],[175,26.59,4.5,"Either the astrologer had murdered the woman to prove his accuracy,"],[175,31.13,3.94,"or he was so prescient that he could threaten Louis himself."],[175,35.8,1.9,"The astrologer had to be murdered."],[175,39.27,2.4,"The king ordered his servants that upon his signal,"],[175,41.7,3.4,"they were to throw the astrologer out the window to a certain death."],[175,45.81,2.84,"You'd be surprised, by the way, by how many seers in history"],[175,48.68,1.4,"have met this fate."],[175,50.08,1.47,"Some advice to astrologers:"],[175,51.58,2.64,"keep away from kings and heights."],[175,55.82,2.17,"When the astrologer arrived to meet Louis,"],[175,58.02,2.07,"the King asked him one last question."],[175,61.12,5.17,"\"Given your prophetic abilities, tell me, how long will you live?\""],[175,67.06,3.7,"Not missing a beat, the astrologer replied,"],[175,70.8,3.27,"“I will die three days before Your Majesty.”"],[175,77.78,2.47,"And Louis never gave the signal."],[175,81.88,3.54,"Did the astrologer find his answer in the stars?"],[175,86.12,1.27,"I don't think so."],[175,88.45,2.9,"I think he understood the power of prediction"],[175,91.39,3.14,"and used it to get himself life insurance."],[175,95.23,1.3,"Smart astrologer."],[175,97.43,3.84,"Even though we tend to associate prediction with knowledge,"],[175,101.3,3.27,"I'd like to invite you to consider the possibility"],[175,104.6,2.27,"that most of the predictions that you encounter"],[175,106.9,1.4,"in an everyday setting"],[175,108.34,3.1,"are closer to the realm of power than that of knowledge."],[175,111.91,3.74,"It might seem that the days in which we sought astrologers"],[175,115.68,3.57,"and soothsayers to tell us about the future are very distant,"],[175,119.28,3.0,"but often we use AI as the new Oracle of Delphi,"],[175,122.32,3.7,"and tech executives whisper in the ears of our leaders,"],[175,126.06,2.07,"much like court astrologers used to."],[175,129.13,3.07,"Granted, the technology is different,"],[175,132.2,2.4,"but the political role is not."],[175,135.5,3.1,"Predictions are often power plays in disguise."],[175,138.64,4.6,"They justify value-laden decisions under the pretense of facts."],[175,144.11,2.94,"Better understanding prediction matters more than ever"],[175,147.08,3.8,"because we're relying on forecasting more than ever with AI."],[175,151.22,2.54,"And based on how we talk about prediction,"],[175,153.78,2.1,"we're being much too naive about it."],[175,156.85,2.5,"But AI is science, you might think."],[175,159.39,2.37,"It's cutting-edge technology, right?"],[175,162.13,3.4,"Well, it depends on the kind of AI and how we use it."],[175,166.13,3.57,"AI can be a great technology to make predictions about molecules"],[175,169.73,3.1,"in the search for new antibiotics,"],[175,172.87,3.24,"but predictions about human beings are fundamentally different"],[175,176.14,1.5,"than those about things."],[175,178.07,3.54,"Predictions about the weather don't influence the weather."],[175,182.11,2.67,"Predictions about people influence people."],[175,185.72,3.04,"Social predictions tend to act like magnets."],[175,188.79,2.87,"They bend reality towards themselves."],[175,191.66,3.04,"They affect the reality they purport to predict."],[175,197.29,2.8,"An algorithmic prediction about future disease"],[175,200.1,2.14,"can make someone's insurance premiums go up,"],[175,202.27,3.24,"leading to worse health outcomes from stress alone."],[175,206.84,4.27,"Predictions sound like descriptions of the world, like facts,"],[175,211.14,1.44,"but they're not."],[175,212.61,2.67,"Analyzed closely as assertions,"],[175,215.31,2.74,"they are what philosophers called “speech acts.”"],[175,218.51,4.0,"That is, language that does something other than describe the world."],[175,222.99,3.0,"When you tell a child to clean up their room,"],[175,226.02,2.07,"you're not describing the state of the room,"],[175,228.09,1.6,"you're issuing an order."],[175,229.73,2.1,"Similarly,"],[175,231.86,4.07,"social predictions are veiled commands."],[175,236.23,2.44,"They implicitly tell us how to act."],[175,239.6,1.17,"For example,"],[175,240.8,3.5,"when a tech executive says that in the future we will use AI"],[175,244.34,2.07,"for everything and everywhere,"],[175,246.44,3.07,"he's trying to get you to act in a way"],[175,249.51,2.27,"that will fulfill his vision of the future."],[175,252.28,2.9,"You know, the one that happens to line his pockets."],[175,256.19,2.17,"And when you believe that prediction,"],[175,258.39,3.37,"as if it were telling you something about the future,"],[175,261.79,2.24,"when you give in to the fear of missing out,"],[175,264.06,1.8,"and you go and you buy the AI"],[175,265.9,2.7,"and you contribute to the self-fulfilling prophecy,"],[175,268.63,3.67,"what you are actually doing is obeying."],[175,273.57,1.97,"Have you noticed how often"],[175,275.57,2.54,"people who make predictions about technology say"],[175,278.14,1.7,"that the future they are describing"],[175,279.88,2.23,"is inevitable?"],[175,282.41,1.6,"That's a red flag."],[175,284.05,2.2,"Those predictions are designed to act"],[175,286.28,2.0,"as conversation stoppers."],[175,288.65,2.0,"They're telling you, \"Don't question me."],[175,290.69,2.44,"Just accept what I'm saying as a fact.\""],[175,293.96,3.14,"I'd like for this talk to be a conversation starter."],[175,297.69,3.44,"I hope it'll persuade you to ask more questions."],[175,302.97,2.17,"Predictions invite manipulation."],[175,306.14,5.0,"Their power to shape the future creates the temptation to tamper with it"],[175,311.14,1.3,"and benefit from it."],[175,312.98,1.6,"Take prediction markets."],[175,315.21,1.64,"The argument for having them"],[175,316.85,2.47,"is that they can be a source of knowledge."],[175,319.35,2.07,"In theory, markets don't lie."],[175,322.05,1.17,"If people make bets"],[175,323.25,2.07,"and they stand to lose if they get it wrong,"],[175,325.36,1.37,"they'll try to get it right."],[175,326.72,1.97,"And by having many people place bets,"],[175,328.73,2.27,"we can harness the wisdom of the crowds."],[175,331.36,2.87,"But that assumes a very naive view of prediction"],[175,334.26,1.87,"as a quest for knowledge."],[175,336.17,3.07,"If you consider prediction as a quest for power,"],[175,339.27,1.97,"a very different picture emerges."],[175,341.94,1.57,"If you have enough money,"],[175,343.54,2.27,"you can use it to influence public perception"],[175,345.84,2.07,"by heavily betting on something."],[175,347.94,2.13,"Politicians have bet on themselves."],[175,350.55,1.53,"In February this year,"],[175,352.12,4.5,"six anonymous accounts earned 1.2 million dollars"],[175,356.65,2.07,"betting for the attack on Iran."],[175,359.42,3.5,"Some of those wallets were funded hours before."],[175,364.96,5.3,"Finally, predictions create and then cover up injustice."],[175,370.93,4.27,"Algorithmic predictions are building this Kafkaesque world"],[175,375.24,2.77,"in which we can no longer contest decisions"],[175,378.01,3.6,"because they're not based on clearly defined criteria."],[175,382.45,1.87,"If I reject your loan application"],[175,384.35,3.17,"because you don't fulfill a particular requirement,"],[175,387.55,2.13,"that's a verifiable fact."],[175,389.72,2.33,"If I'm wrong, you can challenge me."],[175,392.89,2.33,"But if I reject your loan application"],[175,395.26,1.97,"on the basis of a prediction,"],[175,397.26,1.94,"there's no way you can contest that."],[175,399.73,1.7,"Predictions are never facts."],[175,401.46,2.13,"Facts belong to the past."],[175,404.1,2.97,"Predictions are unverifiable, unfalsifiable."],[175,407.1,1.53,"Since they are about the future,"],[175,408.67,2.27,"they cannot be challenged for being false,"],[175,410.97,3.8,"thereby creating the perfect recipe for hidden injustice."],[175,415.61,1.37,"Predictions are often unfair"],[175,417.01,3.14,"because they're not based on who people are,"],[175,420.18,2.64,"but on who we think they will become."],[175,424.12,3.27,"When we predict someone's future as if it was the weather,"],[175,427.42,1.97,"we're treating them with disrespect."],[175,429.86,2.04,"Too much as things,"],[175,431.93,3.37,"and not enough as agents who have a say in that future,"],[175,435.33,2.94,"who can and should be allowed to defy their odds."],[175,439.6,1.97,"We're facing some pretty grim predictions"],[175,441.61,2.44,"from some of our most prominent prophets."],[175,444.04,1.17,"Larry Ellison,"],[175,445.24,2.37,"the chairman of our predictive software company,"],[175,447.61,2.87,"appropriately named Oracle,"],[175,450.48,3.0,"has predicted a modern surveillance state"],[175,453.48,2.3,"in which citizens will be on their best behavior"],[175,455.82,2.3,"because we're being watched all the time."],[175,459.79,3.2,"But the illusion of a world without crime"],[175,462.99,3.64,"is a world filled with a very different kind of crime."],[175,466.66,1.37,"Authoritarianism."],[175,468.56,1.53,"Is that the world we want?"],[175,476.94,3.1,"What can we do to not sleepwalk into it?"],[175,480.51,1.2,"Plenty."],[175,482.01,3.5,"Prophets gain their power from people believing them."],[175,486.02,3.6,"If we decide to defy this prediction instead of obeying it,"],[175,489.62,3.07,"we will choose products that are more respectful of privacy,"],[175,492.72,1.33,"for starters."],[175,495.29,4.3,"Hannah Arendt wrote that it's pointless to argue with a murderer"],[175,499.6,2.94,"about whether their future victim is dead or alive."],[175,502.83,5.11,"The only appropriate response is to rescue the person whose death is predicted."],[175,509.04,4.71,"Well, today's prophets are predicting the death of our democracy,"],[175,513.78,3.27,"and the only appropriate response is to rescue it."],[175,524.05,1.17,"In ancient Rome,"],[175,525.25,3.8,"it was illegal to predict the death of the emperor,"],[175,529.09,1.27,"for the very simple reason"],[175,530.36,3.14,"that they ended up with a murdered emperor on cue."],[175,533.96,2.64,"I'm not suggesting that we do away with prediction."],[175,536.63,4.04,"I'm going to continue to use my weather app every single day."],[175,541.37,1.44,"But we need a public debate"],[175,542.84,3.2,"about the acceptable and unacceptable uses of prediction."],[175,546.08,1.9,"And we're currently not having it."],[175,548.01,2.77,"Meanwhile, dozens of algorithms are making decisions"],[175,550.81,2.5,"about your life right now."],[175,553.82,2.6,"It might be that in the case of insurance,"],[175,556.42,2.67,"we might want to make predictions at a population level,"],[175,559.09,1.2,"but not for individuals"],[175,560.29,3.2,"because it creates unfair, self-fulfilling prophecies."],[175,563.53,3.7,"Plus, if we're being billed according to individualized predictions,"],[175,567.26,2.77,"that means that we’re basically paying for our own way,"],[175,570.03,4.87,"and insurance loses its reason for being solidarity and the pooling of risk."],[175,575.4,3.04,"It might be that in cases in which fairness matters,"],[175,578.44,3.8,"we might prefer transparent and contestable criteria"],[175,582.28,2.54,"to predictive statistical pattern matching."],[175,585.62,2.1,"Let me start to bring things together."],[175,587.75,3.34,"Even though self-fulfilling prophecies are nothing new,"],[175,591.12,2.24,"they are being supercharged by AI"],[175,593.39,2.4,"in ways that make it more urgent than ever"],[175,595.83,2.27,"to think more deeply about predictions."],[175,598.79,2.57,"First, predictions are never facts."],[175,601.4,1.2,"They are speech acts."],[175,603.17,3.14,"Second, they invite manipulation."],[175,606.74,3.57,"Third, they create and cover up injustice."],[175,610.81,3.2,"The one idea that I would like for you to take home today"],[175,614.04,4.0,"is that predictions can be weapons of power."],[175,618.05,3.54,"But they only work if we believe them."],[175,622.15,3.94,"If Oedipus had laughed off the prophecy that he would kill his father"],[175,626.12,1.67,"and marry his mother,"],[175,627.82,2.77,"instead of completely freaking out about it --"],[175,632.1,4.0,"He would have never left for Thebes and made the prophecy come true."],[175,637.4,2.9,"We turn to prophets because we're anxious about uncertainty,"],[175,640.34,1.9,"but uncertainty is good news."],[175,642.27,4.04,"It means that the future is unwritten, that it's ours to write."],[175,646.68,5.24,"And we can face the blank page with creativity, with curiosity,"],[175,651.95,2.54,"with the excitement of a sense of adventure."],[175,655.05,5.07,"Efforts to predict the future go hand-in-hand with efforts to control it."],[175,660.16,3.57,"So beware of prophets and prophecies."],[175,664.39,3.57,"It's only when we acknowledge that we don't know what the future holds"],[175,667.96,1.47,"and act accordingly"],[175,669.47,2.9,"that we can be sure to live in a free society."],[175,672.84,3.24,"Don't bow to people's predictions as if they were facts."],[175,676.91,1.2,"Be like Joe Frazier."],[175,678.51,3.04,"When Muhammad Ali predicted his own victory"],[175,681.54,3.04,"in the 1971 heavyweight championship,"],[175,684.58,2.27,"Frazier took it as a provocation"],[175,686.88,4.07,"and ended up defeating the previously undefeated great Ali"],[175,690.99,1.6,"in the fight of the century."],[175,693.39,4.27,"So next time you hear a gloomy prediction about the social world,"],[175,697.66,1.53,"don't get discouraged."],[175,700.06,2.0,"Find the Joe Frazier within you."],[175,704.43,3.24,"Rebel against tyrannical predictions."],[175,709.27,4.97,"Let's be brave enough to imagine and fight for a better world."],[175,714.88,5.64,"Perhaps then all of us can make the future bright after all."],[175,721.18,1.17,"Thank you."],[176,0.24,4.62,"We have better maps of Mars than we have of our own ocean floor."],[176,4.86,2.5,"And at first glance, this makes no sense, right?"],[176,7.36,4.0,"I mean, Mars is way farther away than the bottom of the ocean."],[176,11.36,4.88,"It turns out though, that it's a lot easier to see through the vastness of space,"],[176,16.24,2.56,"than it is to see through all that water ."],[176,18.8,4.8,"The ocean covers 70% of our planet, and the deepest parts of it are often"],[176,23.6,3.28,"referred to as Earth’s Final Frontier of science."],[176,26.88,3.6,"Now, the people calling it that are mostly the people who study it."],[176,30.48,5.76,"And as a kind of deep sea scientist myself, I’m probably a little—really biased."],[176,36.24,2.4,"But I don’t think that we’re exaggerating!"],[176,38.64,5.2,"We’ve seen less than 0.001% of the ocean floor,"],[176,43.84,4.16,"and the things we have discovered thus far are absolutely bizarre."],[176,48.0,3.44,"That’s why scientists are so eager to investigate more of it."],[176,51.44,4.32,"From reefs where you’d never expect them to be, to massive underwater volcanoes,"],[176,55.76,5.34,"these are five mysteries of the deep sea that researchers are the most desperate to explore."],[176,64.4,3.92,"The Mariana Trench is the deepest ocean trench on the planet,"],[176,68.32,4.48,"plunging almost 11,000 meters at its lowest point: called Challenger Deep."],[176,72.8,4.48,"And I know what you’re thinking: Denali, I’ve heard of people going there before."],[176,77.28,1.68,"That doesn’t sound that unexplored."],[176,78.96,4.32,"Sure, but the Mariana trench is almost 2500 kilometers long,"],[176,83.28,4.16,"and MOST of that 2500 kilometers IS completely unexplored!"],[176,87.44,4.08,"Just because we’ve spent some time at the bottom doesn’t mean we understand it!"],[176,91.52,4.64,"To quote Dr. Drazen, one of the scientists currently studying the Mariana Trench:"],[176,96.16,3.68,"\"That's like trying to understand a mountain by only looking at its summit.”"],[176,99.84,3.44,"The Mariana Trench is our deepest trench, but we’ve discovered at"],[176,103.28,4.72,"least 20 trenches on Earth so far, and there are likely many more out of them out there."],[176,108.0,3.92,"Most of these are formed by what we call in geology, a subduction zone."],[176,111.92,3.28,"A subduction zone occurs when two tectonic plates collide."],[176,115.2,4.72,"The denser of the two plates gets shoved underneath the other one, forming a trench."],[176,119.92,4.6,"The Mariana is in what's called the hadal zone, the deepest region of the ocean."],[176,124.52,5.24,"And at this depth, the weight of the water above creates enormous pressure on the bottom."],[176,129.76,2.32,"That pressure can be measured in atmospheres,"],[176,132.08,4.32,"which increases by 1 atmosphere of pressure for every 10 meters."],[176,136.4,3.84,"This means that by the time we get to the bottom of the Mariana Trench, we’ve reached"],[176,140.24,6.24,"a pressure of over 1,000 atmospheres, that’s about 15,000 lbs per square inch!"],[176,146.48,4.8,"For comparison, that's like the weight of 5 USPS postal trucks"],[176,151.28,3.04,"pressing down on every inch of your body at once!"],[176,154.32,3.2,"And understandably, that makes building equipment that can"],[176,157.52,2.8,"survive on the ocean floor pretty difficult."],[176,160.32,2.08,"So why do we bother trying then?"],[176,162.4,6.24,"Well, there are some things we can only research if we actually see and sample them in person."],[176,168.64,3.28,"Deep trenches serve as carbon sinks, for one thing."],[176,171.92,3.68,"This means that they absorb more carbon than they release and they"],[176,175.6,4.32,"help regulate how much carbon is in our atmosphere at any given time."],[176,179.92,3.76,"This is really important for studying and monitoring climate change."],[176,183.68,4.64,"And famously, the deep sea is unlike any other ecosystem on the planet."],[176,188.32,4.88,"There are so many undiscovered organisms living in the hadal zone that just one study"],[176,193.2,5.2,"on the deepest parts of these trenches revealed more than 5,000 new species."],[176,198.4,4.0,"Many species that live in these zones don't exist anywhere else."],[176,202.4,4.32,"For example, the obviously very majestic Hadal Snailfish,"],[176,206.72,2.24,"one of the deepest living fish in existence."],[176,208.96,1.84,"The deepest one we know about, anyway."],[176,210.8,4.56,"Some things in the ocean aren’t mysteries JUST because of how deep they are though,"],[176,215.36,2.24,"which brings us to the next place on our list:"],[176,217.6,1.92,"The Larsen Ice Shelf."],[176,219.52,2.56,"This ice shelf is located in Antarctica,"],[176,222.08,5.04,"and it’s essentially big blocks of ice extending from the continent into the ocean."],[176,227.12,5.52,"It formed from 11,000 years worth of ocean and terrestrial water freezing onto it."],[176,232.64,6.24,"The Larsen ice shelf is more than 200m thick and it's nearly impossible to explore below it,"],[176,238.88,4.4,"even for deep sea vehicles that actually have been to the Mariana Trench."],[176,243.28,4.72,"So until recently, we’ve known almost nothing about what’s underneath the ice sheet."],[176,248.0,4.08,"But scientists have been able to get a sneak peak in the last couple of decades."],[176,252.08,5.76,"As climate change progresses, parts of the ice shelf break off, and each time a section breaks,"],[176,257.84,3.84,"researchers rush to Antarctica to try and find out what's below them."],[176,261.68,2.32,"In fact, this has happened multiple times."],[176,264.0,6.24,"The first time a chunk broke off was in 1995 when 1500 square kilometers of it drifted away."],[176,270.24,5.2,"This part of the shelf was called Larsen A, and satellite images caught the breakup of the ice."],[176,275.44,4.64,"But it wasn’t until 2002 that a chunk of the shelf called Larsen B broke off"],[176,280.08,3.6,"and we actually got the chance to study the ocean floor beneath it."],[176,283.68,6.0,"Scientists sent camera equipment down 800m to the bottom and they found something incredible:"],[176,289.68,3.84,"the first known chemotrophic ecosystem in Antarctica."],[176,293.52,2.48,"Chemotrophic means that an organism relies"],[176,296.0,4.32,"entirely on chemical reactions for energy instead of photosynthesis."],[176,300.32,4.64,"The ocean floor beneath the ice was covered in clams, other bivalves,"],[176,304.96,3.68,"and white bacterial mats with gas bubbles coming out of them."],[176,308.64,4.48,"The problem was, expeditions to Antarctica are really hard to do."],[176,313.12,2.96,"They’re often planned months or years in advance,"],[176,316.08,3.92,"and by the time scientists get there, where the ice broke off,"],[176,320.0,5.52,"the ecosystem has usually already started to change from how it was before the ice broke."],[176,325.52,3.76,"When you take away all that ice, particles from above start"],[176,329.28,5.68,"to fall down on what were previously pretty isolated organisms and bury those organisms."],[176,334.96,5.44,"Then other species will start to move in and recolonize that part of the ocean floor,"],[176,340.4,4.56,"outcompeting the chemotrophs and changing that ecosystem forever."],[176,344.96,4.8,"So that ecosystem wasn’t pristine by the time the scientists actually got there."],[176,349.76,3.36,"In 2017 though, scientists hit the jackpot."],[176,353.12,5.36,"A part of the Larsen C ice shelf broke off, a sheet of ice bigger than Delaware."],[176,358.48,2.56,"And researchers had been preparing for this for"],[176,361.04,4.4,"years by watching satellite photos for signs of cracks in the ice."],[176,365.44,4.72,"Their diligence finally paid off after they got the first ever glimpse of what these"],[176,370.16,4.56,"ecosystems looked like before other organisms got the chance to move in."],[176,374.72,3.92,"They found these weird geometric patterns covering the seafloor."],[176,378.64,2.88,"And as it turns out, these are nests!"],[176,381.52,2.72,"Made by thousands of these guys … icefish!"],[176,384.24,3.6,"Patterns like this have never been seen before and the researchers"],[176,387.84,5.28,"think that it’s most likely a protective mechanism from predators and other fish."],[176,393.12,3.28,"But why this has only been seen under the ice shelf,"],[176,396.4,4.08,"and WHAT predators may be living down there, we’re still—really—not sure."],[176,400.48,2.8,"It’s amazing how so many species find a way"],[176,403.28,3.84,"to make a home somewhere that’s objectively really hard to live."],[176,407.12,3.44,"And speaking of places it's objectively REALLY hard to live …"],[176,410.56,4.08,"The Red Sea is one of the most unique bodies of water on the planet."],[176,414.64,4.88,"At the bottom, you can find something even stranger than the chemotrophs of Antarctica."],[176,419.52,0.96,"This is a lake."],[176,420.48,3.92,"Or something that looks like a lake, except at the bottom of the ocean."],[176,424.4,2.88,"It’s a lake of dense, salty brine."],[176,427.28,3.92,"So salty in fact, that it can’t mix with the seawater above it,"],[176,431.2,4.08,"and nothing except a select few microbes can survive in the actual brine."],[176,435.28,3.92,"It settles into these pools that can be found more than 1000 meters deep."],[176,439.2,2.88,"The brine pools of the Red Sea formed millions of"],[176,442.08,4.32,"years ago when that area was cut off from the rest of the ocean."],[176,446.4,5.76,"Being cut off caused it to dry up over time and leave concentrated salty deposits behind."],[176,452.16,3.84,"These brine pools are also really hard to access because,"],[176,456.0,4.4,"for one thing, it’s still the deep sea, but they’re also really, really rare."],[176,460.4,3.2,"They’re only found in three known locations: the Red Sea,"],[176,463.6,2.32,"the Mediterranean, and the Gulf of Mexico."],[176,465.92,2.56,"Aside from the microbes in the brine pools,"],[176,468.48,4.64,"there are also several larger species that have evolved really unique survival tactics"],[176,473.12,4.8,"for living directly on the edge of what is essentially a giant deep sea death trap."],[176,477.92,5.2,"Predators like shrimp and hagfish actually sit right on the edge of the brine pool and"],[176,483.12,5.52,"they wait for things like crabs and other small animals to be stunned by the high salt content."],[176,488.64,4.24,"Then they take them as prey before they can fully disappear into the brine,"],[176,492.88,1.44,"which I think is pretty cool!"],[176,494.32,4.16,"As a geologist though, my favorite thing about brine pools is that they preserve"],[176,498.48,2.08,"layers of ocean floor sediment."],[176,500.56,3.2,"And they do it in a way that can’t be done in any other setting."],[176,503.76,5.36,"Most material that ends up this deep in the ocean is made up of really fine particles."],[176,509.12,5.04,"And when a major event like a landslide or a tsunami happens, it brings larger,"],[176,514.16,4.24,"coarser grains farther into the ocean than they would typically reach."],[176,518.4,5.68,"So when scientists see a layer of bigger, coarser particles surrounded by all the normal fine"],[176,524.08,5.44,"particles, that suggests that one of these big events, like a landslide or tsunami, has happened."],[176,529.52,3.44,"This also tells them how often earthquakes and tsunamis happen,"],[176,532.96,4.32,"helping them understand the conditions and regularity of these kinds of events."],[176,537.28,3.6,"It’s an unexpected benefit of studying these deep sea brine pools!"],[176,540.88,3.6,"Next on our list, another unexpected feature of the deep sea."],[176,544.48,2.64,"A coral reef in … Greenland?"],[176,547.12,3.76,"This is Cape Desolation, off the southern coast of Greenland."],[176,550.88,4.72,"And there’s actually a deep sea coral reef here about 800m down."],[176,555.6,5.76,"This reef is made up of a hard, specialized cold-water coral called Eye-coral."],[176,561.36,4.32,"Cold water reefs have also been found in places like Iceland and Norway."],[176,565.68,2.72,"But the Greenland reefs weren’t discovered until"],[176,568.4,4.4,"2012, and they’re among the least explored of these cold water reefs."],[176,572.8,2.8,"This is because it’s … still the deep sea… and,"],[176,575.6,4.48,"also, Greenland's coastal waters are notoriously difficult to navigate."],[176,580.08,5.04,"On the surface, there’s broken, shifting ice, fog, and high winds."],[176,585.12,4.56,"And then beneath the surface, there are really strong, really unpredictable currents."],[176,589.68,5.52,"The corals were actually discovered by accident when a ship sent an instrument down to collect"],[176,595.2,5.36,"water samples and their instrument came back up having been completely smashed by the coral."],[176,600.56,2.08,"Now, I’m sure they were mad about the damage."],[176,602.64,2.56,"But the last time I broke a research instrument"],[176,605.2,3.36,"I didn’t get to say I discovered a new coral species at the same time!"],[176,608.56,4.4,"The existence of these corals is vital for the entire marine ecosystem."],[176,612.96,2.32,"Not just what’s in Greenland but across the globe."],[176,615.28,4.0,"Deep sea corals act as habitat and breeding grounds for a ton"],[176,619.28,4.16,"of marine species like sea bass, rockfish, and snapper."],[176,623.44,4.08,"All of them use different deep sea corals in different parts of their life cycle."],[176,627.52,2.96,"These extreme environments are also home to organisms"],[176,630.48,3.76,"that contain chemical compounds we can use to fight disease."],[176,634.24,2.16,"The corals of Cape Desolation have a"],[176,636.4,4.48,"chemical called Lobophorin K that is actually toxic to cancer cells."],[176,640.88,5.12,"Scientists hope that by studying that compound, we may be able to develop new cancer treatments."],[176,646.0,3.44,"And there are studies happening right now trying to make that a reality!"],[176,649.44,4.56,"It’s one more reason scientists are eager to learn more about the Cape Desolation reef."],[176,654.0,3.6,"Finally, we have my personal favorite location: the Gakkel ridge."],[176,657.6,3.04,"I study underwater volcanoes as part of my PhD,"],[176,660.64,3.28,"and the Gakkel ridge is on my research bucket list for sure…"],[176,663.92,2.4,"So if anyone on that research team is hiring a"],[176,666.32,4.0,"submarine volcanologist in the next 2-3 years… I’m available."],[176,670.32,3.76,"The Gakkel ridge is located between Greenland and Siberia,"],[176,674.08,4.4,"and it runs 1800km across the Arctic ocean floor."],[176,678.48,4.48,"It’s 5,000 meters down at its deepest point, and it’s so hard to get there"],[176,682.96,5.2,"that the first ever submersible dive to this location didn’t happen until 2025."],[176,688.16,4.0,"The sub that made the journey carried 2 scientists and one pilot,"],[176,692.16,3.2,"so only three people have ever seen this thing in person."],[176,695.36,4.24,"It’s also a great example of what's called a mid-ocean ridge."],[176,699.6,5.2,"Those form where two tectonic plates are moving away from each other, and as the plates separate,"],[176,704.8,4.64,"molten rock from the Earth's interior comes up between them and causes volcanism."],[176,709.44,3.44,"People often picture, like, a river of lava running through the middle of"],[176,712.88,3.92,"the ocean floor where they separate, but that's not actually what it looks like."],[176,716.8,3.04,"The reality is that the sea water cools the lava"],[176,719.84,3.04,"into rock almost immediately when it reaches the surface."],[176,722.88,5.2,"It cools so fast that most of the ocean floor is covered in a layer of volcanic glass."],[176,728.08,2.48,"Not obsidian, though … that's a completely different kind"],[176,730.56,4.32,"of volcanic glass with its own distinct chemical composition."],[176,734.88,4.4,"This volcanic glass is called MORB or Mid-Ocean Ridge Basalt glass."],[176,739.28,5.44,"Like everything on this list so far, mid-ocean ridges are really difficult to access."],[176,744.72,4.4,"And this is due to how remote they are and how high the pressure of the deep sea is."],[176,749.12,5.76,"But the Gakkel ridge is also located in the Arctic and is therefore covered by Arctic sea ice."],[176,754.88,3.92,"It’s not as thick as the 200m of the Larsen Ice Shelf,"],[176,758.8,3.76,"but it's more than enough to make it inaccessible to most ships,"],[176,762.56,4.56,"including the ones that would have to carry any underwater vehicles to the dive site."],[176,767.12,1.6,"So why would we risk it then?"],[176,768.72,3.76,"Well, because deep-sea scientists are stubborn by nature."],[176,772.48,5.28,"And because you can thank mid-ocean ridges for just about everything you love about the ocean."],[176,777.76,3.36,"Ridges are a really significant source of iron in our ocean,"],[176,781.12,5.36,"and iron is really important for a process you may have heard of called photosynthesis,"],[176,786.48,2.48,"where light is converted into chemical energy."],[176,788.96,3.04,"Photosynthesis is used by phytoplankton that serve"],[176,792.0,3.04,"as the base for almost the entire marine food chain."],[176,795.04,2.48,"Mid-ocean ridges are also home to one of the"],[176,797.52,4.08,"most unique ecosystems on the planet, hydrothermal vents."],[176,801.6,2.72,"As seawater circulates through the ocean floor,"],[176,804.32,4.08,"molten material within earth's crust starts to heat this water up."],[176,808.4,2.96,"The water then returns to the ocean floor and brings"],[176,811.36,3.36,"a bunch of that heat and nutrients up with it."],[176,814.72,5.12,"This allows thriving communities of really unique animals like giant tube worms,"],[176,819.84,3.28,"pompeii worms, and chemotropic microbes."],[176,823.12,6.54,"The structures of the vent themselves are made of metal sulfide minerals like iron, copper and zinc."],[176,829.66,4.58,"And if you were to look inside the hydrothermal vents, this is what you might see."],[176,834.24,6.4,"The entire inside is covered in a layer of pyrite, known more commonly as Fools Gold."],[176,840.64,2.5,"So, our deep sea has towers of gold …"],[176,843.14,5.5,"well, fools gold … surrounded by an entire ocean floor of volcanic glass."],[176,848.64,2.88,"And I don’t know about you, but to me that sounds pretty magical,"],[176,851.52,3.2,"and it’s why I basically sold my soul to a life researching it."],[176,854.72,4.32,"Hydrothermal vents can even be used to study environments on other planets."],[176,859.04,5.28,"The vents at the Gakkel ridge are some of the only ones on Earth under permanent ice cover."],[176,864.32,3.36,"Which yes, does make them a huge pain to try and get to."],[176,867.68,3.68,"But it also makes them perfect to study what life might be like on"],[176,871.36,3.84,"icy, ocean-covered moons like Europa and Enceladus."],[176,875.2,3.28,"NASA has even funded expeditions there for this exact reason."],[176,878.48,4.8,"The Gakkel ridge also has something that’s never been found at any other mid-ocean ridge."],[176,883.28,4.48,"It’s the location of the only large caldera complex… which the media"],[176,887.76,4.4,"typically calls a ‘supervolcano’ … to ever be discovered at a mid-ocean ridge."],[176,892.16,4.48,"A large caldera complex is essentially a giant volcanic crater,"],[176,896.64,5.68,"and the caldera at the Gakkel ridge is about 80km long and 40km wide."],[176,902.32,6.72,"This volcano could have theoretically erupted as much as 3000 cubic kilometers of material,"],[176,909.04,4.16,"a larger eruption than even the biggest eruption that Yellowstone has had."],[176,913.2,4.4,"Explosive eruptions like that are not impossible underwater, but evidence of"],[176,917.6,6.24,"one that size has never been seen at a mid-ocean ridge before the Gakkel Ridge Caldera discovery."],[176,923.84,4.08,"Even today, there is so much about our ocean floor that remains a mystery."],[176,927.92,1.76,"And everyone in deep-sea science,"],[176,929.68,5.12,"from the scientists & engineers to the chefs and technicians onboard our research vessels,"],[176,934.8,4.64,"we’re all working together to uncover the least explored parts of our ocean."],[176,939.44,6.37,"Maybe by the time I retire, we’ll be able to say we’ve explored 0.002% of it."],[177,0.16,4.56,"Tens of thousands of years ago, gigantic beasts roamed the earth."],[177,4.72,2.64,"Ones way bigger than woolly mammoths or dinosaurs."],[177,7.36,5.44,"They were big enough to dig out this massive lake, cause these giant scratches in the ground,"],[177,12.8,3.12,"and drop these boulders in the middle of a field."],[177,15.92,1.76,"And the best part?"],[177,17.68,3.68,"Some of those big guys are still out here today."],[177,21.36,3.04,"I’m talking about... glaciers!"],[177,24.4,2.65,"Hi! I’m Sage, and this is Crash Course Geology."],[177,31.36,4.64,"Glaciers are enormous masses of ice and snow that flow under their own weight,"],[177,36.0,2.56,"moving very slowly across the earth."],[177,38.56,2.0,"Or, at a glacial pace."],[177,40.56,4.48,"As Miranda Priestly would say, “You know how that thrills me.”"],[177,45.04,3.92,"And they’re not just boring hunks of ice. Glaciers provide habitats,"],[177,48.96,2.24,"hold over half of the world’s freshwater,"],[177,51.2,5.12,"and preserve hundreds of thousands of years worth of history within their layers."],[177,56.32,3.28,"Glaciers are important to the spiritual beliefs of indigenous peoples,"],[177,59.6,2.72,"from Peru to China to Tanzania."],[177,62.32,1.52,"Plus, they’re stunning."],[177,63.84,1.2,"Like, come on!"],[177,65.04,1.04,"How is that real?"],[177,66.08,1.6,"And they’re massive."],[177,67.68,2.32,"So, how does all that ice get there?"],[177,70.0,2.96,"Well, it’s a little more complicated than a giant slush pile"],[177,72.96,2.48,"getting shoved to the edge of a Target parking lot."],[177,75.44,5.84,"Glaciers form when tons of snow falls in an area that’s so cold, the snow never totally melts."],[177,81.28,4.48,"It starts to accumulate year after year, building up until the pile is huge—"],[177,85.76,4.16,"tens of meters deep, and around 4050 square meters in area."],[177,89.92,3.6,"And when there’s that much snow in one place, it gets so heavy,"],[177,93.52,3.04,"its frosty base starts to transform."],[177,96.56,5.44,"First, the snow at the base gets packed so tightly it turns into a state called firn,"],[177,102.0,2.08,"which is technically still snow."],[177,104.08,5.92,"But while fresh snow is about 90 percent air, firn is only about 25 percent."],[177,110.0,3.92,"That tightly-packed state gets us ready for the next transformation."],[177,113.92,2.72,"With increasing pressure, the snow recrystallizes,"],[177,116.64,2.56,"trading its six-armed snowflake shape for dense,"],[177,119.2,2.88,"rounded crystals of firn that can grow bigger over time."],[177,122.08,4.16,"The ice is so dense, it absorbs every color of light except for blue,"],[177,126.24,2.64,"which is what gives glaciers their haunting glow."],[177,128.88,3.6,"That’s the basic process, but it doesn’t always look exactly the same."],[177,132.48,2.32,"Glaciers are super varied in size and shape."],[177,134.8,2.8,"Like, you’re probably familiar with continental glaciers:"],[177,137.6,4.72,"thick domes of ice that stretch thousands of square meters across Earth’s surface."],[177,142.32,5.2,"Sometimes called ice sheets, these vast, frosty expanses exist in only two places today:"],[177,147.52,1.68,"Greenland and Antarctica."],[177,149.2,4.08,"But alpine glaciers, or mountain glaciers, are found on every continent except Australia."],[177,153.28,2.56,"Although, the Kiwis know there are glaciers in New Zealand."],[177,155.84,2.16,"I’m sorry, I don’t know why I thought I could do that."],[177,158.16,3.12,"True to their name, alpine glaciers form high up on mountains"],[177,161.28,2.16,"in bowl-shaped formations called cirques—"],[177,163.44,1.76,"which the glaciers carve themselves!"],[177,165.2,3.76,"If enough ice builds up, these can flow down as valley glaciers."],[177,168.96,4.24,"When the ice reaches flat ground, it can spread out into a lobe called a piedmont glacier."],[177,173.2,4.24,"Like this guy in Greenland, which is called Elephant Foot Glacier, for obvious reasons."],[177,177.44,1.6,"And glaciers are always changing."],[177,179.04,2.8,"Each one can be broken into a few distinct zones."],[177,181.84,4.48,"There's the accumulation zone near the top of the glacier, where new snow collects."],[177,186.32,4.88,"Then there's the ablation zone where snow and ice blow, fall, or melt away."],[177,191.2,3.84,"If more snow accumulates than ablates, the glacier grows."],[177,195.04,4.4,"But if more ice ablates than accumulates, the glacier's front retreats,"],[177,199.44,2.75,"and the length of the glacier shortens over time."],[177,202.24,4.32,"Whether a glacier is lengthening or shortening, it’s constantly flowing from place to place."],[177,206.56,2.72,"As the ice that makes up glaciers deforms and flows,"],[177,209.28,3.28,"it moves the entire glacier from the inside."],[177,212.56,2.88,"That slow, downhill motion is called creep."],[177,215.44,2.0,"Which is also what Dwayne calls me when I sneak up on him"],[177,217.44,1.76,"Sometimes, especially in warmer conditions,"],[177,219.2,3.76,"the glacier’s huge weight causes the bottom to melt, becoming slippery."],[177,222.96,3.6,"And when a slippery-bottomed glacier meets smooth ground, it moves."],[177,226.56,2.0,"Some only move a few millimeters a year, like the Meserve glacier in Antarctica."],[177,228.56,4.08,"But the fastest glacier ever recorded, the Jakobshavn glacier in Greenland,"],[177,232.64,2.72,"moves up to 46 meters a day."],[177,235.36,1.12,"That thing is booking it."],[177,236.48,4.88,"Glaciers sliding over the cold, hard ground is like pushing a brick through a sandbox."],[177,241.36,4.8,"As glaciers flow, bits of sand, gravel, and rock get embedded at the base,"],[177,246.16,2.96,"grating the ground in a process known as abrasion."],[177,249.12,5.12,"This can even leave behind huge claw marks, called glacial striations, like these in Ohio."],[177,254.24,4.88,"Glaciers can also pluck the bedrock— and I don’t mean yanking out its stray eyebrow hairs."],[177,259.12,5.84,"As a glacier drags across uneven ground, it can crack off chunks of rock and carry them away."],[177,264.96,3.6,"Together, abrasion and plucking shape the landscape in a bunch of ways."],[177,268.56,3.76,"They can carve out u-shaped valleys and bowl-shaped cirques, like we saw earlier,"],[177,272.32,2.4,"or even craft flat-faced horns in mountains,"],[177,274.72,2.16,"like the famous Matterhorn in the Swiss Alps."],[177,276.88,3.68,"Eventually, all that crushed rock forms mounds called moraines."],[177,280.56,2.8,"They have different names based on where they form:"],[177,283.36,4.16,"lateral for the ones along the sides of a glacier, and terminal for the ones at the front end."],[177,287.52,3.84,"Sometimes, gravel can build up in the channels of melted ice that flow around a glacier,"],[177,291.36,2.56,"leaving behind winding mounds called eskers, like this one in Minnesota."],[177,293.92,2.08,"And glaciers can move huge boulders,"],[177,296.0,4.96,"carrying them far away from their source rock and leaving them lying around as glacial erratics."],[177,300.96,4.08,"As a certain famous equine would say, “That is a nice boulder.”"],[177,305.04,2.0,"Glaciers can even create lakes."],[177,307.04,3.44,"Sometimes small chunks of ice break off of glaciers as they retreat,"],[177,310.48,3.2,"getting buried in the sediment that washes out from a melting glacier."],[177,313.68,4.08,"Once those buried ice chunks melt, they can leave behind shallow kettle lakes."],[177,317.76,3.28,"Other times, massive glaciers carve basins that fill with water as they melt,"],[177,321.04,2.32,"with moraines around them that keep the water in."],[177,323.36,3.28,"That process can create lakes so big you might even call them “great.”"],[177,326.72,2.96,"But glaciers and the features they create aren’t just cool to look at."],[177,329.68,2.16,"They can also be a window into the past."],[177,331.84,2.64,"Let’s learn about one of my geology rock stars…"],[177,334.48,4.0,"In the 1970s, when Kumiko Goto-Azuma was a high school student in Japan,"],[177,338.48,3.76,"a teacher told her that women couldn’t be professional scientists."],[177,342.24,3.2,"But she didn’t listen— because she wanted to learn about snow."],[177,345.44,2.64,"Years later, after earning her doctorate in engineering,"],[177,348.08,5.2,"she was introduced to ice core research: a method of studying the layers within ice sheets."],[177,353.28,2.08,"Toward the middle of continental glaciers,"],[177,355.36,5.6,"the ice flows so slowly that layers form in a pretty orderly fashion,"],[177,360.96,2.88,"keeping records like the rings inside a tree."],[177,363.84,2.32,"Glaciologists drill vertical holes in the ice,"],[177,366.16,2.8,"pulling out tubes of it that they keep frozen for study."],[177,368.96,2.88,"Scientists like Dr. Goto-Azuma analyze those ice cores"],[177,371.84,4.16,"to learn how Earth’s climate has changed in the past 800,000 years."],[177,376.0,3.84,"Like, a layer’s thickness can show how much precipitation the area saw that year."],[177,379.84,5.2,"And the amount of greenhouse gases in a layer can reveal the air temperature at that time."],[177,385.04,3.76,"Ice cores can even hold remnants of past volcanic eruptions!"],[177,388.8,1.44,"Over her forty-year career,"],[177,390.24,4.64,"Dr. Goto-Azuma has compared ice cores from all over the Arctic and Antarctic,"],[177,394.88,2.48,"studying the climatic cycles that shape glaciers."],[177,397.36,3.28,"Today, she’s a professor at the National Institute of Polar Research in Tokyo,"],[177,400.64,3.44,"where she continues to study ice cores, especially the black carbon—"],[177,404.08,1.84,"AKA soot— that they contain."],[177,405.92,2.48,"Black carbon is released by burning fossil fuels,"],[177,408.4,3.68,"and it can impact how much radiation is reflected by glaciers."],[177,412.08,4.96,"Dr. Goto-Azuma’s work will be especially useful in combating climate change."],[177,417.04,4.64,"Ice cores can tell us a lot about Earth’s past, especially its changing temperature."],[177,421.68,3.68,"Sometime between 120,000 and 11,000 years ago,"],[177,425.36,4.56,"the planet was in a cold stretch of time called a glacial period, also called an ice age."],[177,429.92,3.92,"This was a long chunk of time when glaciers covered vast swaths of the earth."],[177,433.84,1.92,"And I mean long."],[177,435.76,4.72,"That particular ice age took place across more than a hundred thousand years,"],[177,440.48,2.48,"and others lasted millions."],[177,442.96,2.96,"At least five ice ages have happened in Earth’s history."],[177,445.92,3.76,"And even though we associate them with a world full of snow and ice,"],[177,449.68,2.64,"they can have interglacial periods:"],[177,452.32,3.76,"warmer times when glaciers retreat, shaping the earth as they go."],[177,456.08,2.88,"We’re actually in an interglacial period right now!"],[177,458.96,5.88,"While a lot of our glaciers have retreated since the last ice age, many remain intact… for now."],[177,464.85,3.31,"Which brings me to the tough part."],[177,468.16,3.76,"You’ve probably seen some scary headlines about glaciers melting."],[177,471.92,4.64,"So let’s get up to speed on why this is happening and what we can do about it."],[177,476.56,6.0,"Right now, the planet is getting hotter at the rate of about 0.3 degrees Celsius every decade,"],[177,482.56,3.28,"causing continental glaciers to recede."],[177,485.84,4.48,"At higher altitudes, where alpine glaciers are, it’s getting hotter even faster."],[177,490.32,3.2,"And as we all know, when ice gets warm, it melts."],[177,493.52,2.08,"Some scientists estimate that more than a third"],[177,495.6,4.16,"of our remaining glaciers could be gone as early as 2100."],[177,499.76,2.32,"That might sound far off, but plenty of people"],[177,502.08,3.36,"born around 2010 or later could live to see that year."],[177,505.44,2.48,"And that loss would cause some serious problems,"],[177,507.92,5.2,"including rising sea levels that threaten coastal areas, the disruption of ocean ecosystems,"],[177,513.12,2.72,"and a limited supply of freshwater from glaciers—"],[177,515.84,3.36,"which many people rely on, especially in southeast Asia."],[177,519.2,3.92,"This period of warming is different from interglacial periods of the past,"],[177,523.12,3.12,"mainly because of the super rapid pace of change."],[177,526.24,4.16,"Before humans, glaciers receded and temperatures warmed over thousands of years,"],[177,530.4,2.8,"so ecosystems had time to adjust."],[177,533.2,3.92,"But our spike in temperature has happened over the past hundred years,"],[177,537.12,5.76,"thanks to the release of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases from burning fossil fuels."],[177,542.88,5.6,"Our interglacial period could very quickly become a glacier-less period."],[177,548.48,1.04,"That’s not stressful at all!"],[177,549.52,2.16,"I’m fine! Are you fine, Dwayne? ‘Cause I’m fine."],[177,551.68,1.44,"Okay, I’m definitely not fine."],[177,553.12,2.32,"But scientists are working to fix this problem."],[177,555.44,2.48,"Some folks in Switzerland have tried covering melting glaciers"],[177,557.92,2.64,"with massive insulating blankets called geotextiles."],[177,560.56,3.44,"Others have tried using artificial snow to slow down glacial melt,"],[177,564.0,2.0,"shooting it out of cables like a sprinkler."],[177,566.0,2.4,"And some are looking to drill holes in glaciers"],[177,568.4,3.2,"so that water at their base can be pumped out and refrozen."],[177,571.6,4.8,"These strategies are innovative and impressive, but they’re not practical in a lot of ways."],[177,576.4,4.08,"They’re often so expensive that they can’t be scaled up to save whole glaciers,"],[177,580.48,2.24,"especially in lower-income parts of the world."],[177,582.72,3.28,"And they can even cause ecological problems of their own."],[177,586.0,1.52,"So, many scientists say that the most"],[177,587.52,2.16,"straightforward way to slow the pace of climate change"],[177,589.68,2.8,"and keep our planet’s glaciers from disappearing is—"],[177,592.48,3.84,"and say it with me now!— reducing carbon emissions."],[177,596.32,2.72,"Luckily, you’ve got a whole other Crash Course series about that."],[177,599.04,1.49,"Say hey to M for me."],[177,600.56,3.6,"So, I’m not gonna sugarcoat it: Earth’s glaciers are in trouble."],[177,604.16,5.28,"And their loss would have ripple effects on ecosystems, communities, and individual lives—"],[177,609.44,4.24,"including mine and yours— across the globe."],[177,613.68,4.56,"But the more we collectively learn about glaciers and commit to cutting carbon emissions,"],[177,618.24,5.2,"the better chance we have of witnessing these magnificent wonders for generations to come."],[177,623.44,3.92,"Next time, we’ll learn about asteroid impacts of the past… and the future."],[177,627.36,0.96,"See you then."],[177,628.32,2.08,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Geology"],[177,630.4,2.64,"which was filmed at our studio in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[177,633.04,2.32,"and was made with the help of all these chill people."],[177,635.36,3.28,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[177,638.64,2.2,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[178,4.27,2.24,"Picture this: you're 22,"],[178,6.54,3.7,"you just moved to New York City, and you’re out at a bar with friends."],[178,10.81,3.8,"A couple of tequila shots, a lukewarm beer later,"],[178,14.64,2.37,"you go to the bartender to close out your tab."],[178,17.95,6.07,"But then she says the three scariest words known to man:"],[178,25.12,2.47,"“Your card declined.”"],[178,28.26,3.24,"It feels like everyone in the bar overhears."],[178,32.5,2.87,"On one particular night out in 2016,"],[178,35.4,2.77,"I had to wait until 12:01"],[178,38.2,4.44,"for the direct deposit from my paycheck to actually hit my bank account"],[178,42.67,2.64,"to be able to pay my bar tab."],[178,45.34,2.9,"At the time, I was living paycheck to paycheck."],[178,48.68,2.1,"And if I’m being honest,"],[178,50.81,4.84,"the embarrassment I felt that night would stay with me for years."],[178,56.85,3.4,"Even though I had a fancy job on Wall Street,"],[178,60.29,2.84,"my personal finances were a mess."],[178,63.93,3.0,"During the day, I would enact trades on behalf of hedge funds"],[178,66.96,1.57,"worth billions of dollars."],[178,68.9,1.17,"And at night,"],[178,70.07,3.64,"I would go home and wonder if one more pad thai order"],[178,73.74,2.9,"would set me over the financial edge."],[178,77.88,3.34,"Sadly, this is not a unique experience."],[178,82.01,3.9,"Many young people still feel hopelessly lost"],[178,85.92,2.34,"when it comes to our money."],[178,88.79,3.77,"And this is in part due to the existing financial system."],[178,93.46,3.67,"Personal finance education is not taught in K-12."],[178,97.16,2.67,"The past five decades of policy implementation"],[178,99.86,3.77,"has led to a deep divide between the haves and the have-nots,"],[178,103.67,3.1,"and if we're being honest,"],[178,106.8,3.0,"the financial-services industry, as it currently exists,"],[178,109.84,3.47,"is really only set up and incentivized to help,"],[178,113.34,2.3,"well, the already wealthy."],[178,116.88,3.37,"But here's where the story starts to get really good."],[178,120.98,4.3,"There has never been a better time to get financially literate"],[178,125.32,1.2,"than right now."],[178,126.92,4.2,"Knowledge that was once locked behind expensive advisors"],[178,131.16,2.37,"is available at your fingertips."],[178,134.26,1.6,"10 years after bar night,"],[178,135.87,4.67,"I have become the financial educator I so desperately wish I had had back then."],[178,141.17,2.63,"I built my career teaching financial literacy,"],[178,143.81,2.2,"like a friend, across social media."],[178,146.61,1.77,"And as it turns out,"],[178,148.41,3.07,"that boiled-down language and those silly jokes"],[178,151.51,1.2,"and those funny memes,"],[178,152.72,2.44,"they didn't make me any less credible."],[178,155.85,3.84,"But they sure did make me more understandable."],[178,160.52,4.84,"What started as just videos on social media has turned into a podcast,"],[178,165.4,1.74,"not one but two books,"],[178,167.16,3.2,"and if all goes according to plan, a full-blown tech startup."],[178,171.07,3.44,"The financial world has finally opened its doors wide."],[178,175.87,2.3,"But here are the three things"],[178,178.18,2.54,"I wish I had known to do all those years ago,"],[178,180.74,2.44,"when I was so desperately broke"],[178,183.18,3.54,"and wanted so desperately to be very, very rich."],[178,187.52,2.33,"One, learn the language."],[178,189.89,1.3,"The financial language."],[178,191.69,3.17,"Two, build community around money."],[178,195.36,4.37,"And three, find modern solutions for modern problems."],[178,200.36,1.9,"So let's dive into that first point."],[178,202.3,4.64,"It's safe to say learning finance really is like learning a new language."],[178,206.94,4.97,"401(k), IRA, 529, FICO, APY, AGI."],[178,211.91,2.97,"Hello, can someone please help me?"],[178,216.58,2.64,"But my ask of you is really simple."],[178,219.92,4.17,"Don't be afraid to be smart by looking dumb."],[178,224.92,1.64,"Ask what the words mean!"],[178,226.59,4.37,"Don't be afraid to utilize resources like Investopedia or NerdWallet."],[178,231.0,1.17,"And most importantly,"],[178,232.2,3.27,"find a financial mentor that speaks your language."],[178,235.5,3.64,"Someone who has the industry chops to back up those smarts,"],[178,239.17,3.87,"someone who never promises you anything that's too good to be true,"],[178,243.04,1.17,"and certainly someone"],[178,244.24,3.17,"who never gives you that weird feeling in the pit of your stomach."],[178,248.15,2.8,"Here are some of the ways that I have connected with people"],[178,250.98,2.14,"looking for financial guidance."],[178,253.85,2.47,"First off, I nicknamed Jerome Powell \"Fed Daddy\""],[178,256.35,2.0,"to explain rates lowering."],[178,258.62,2.97,"(Video) Fed Daddy just announced we’re lowering interest rates,"],[178,261.59,2.44,"and a lot of people don't even know what that means,"],[178,264.03,1.64,"much less what it means for them."],[178,265.66,2.13,"Translating headlines into action items."],[178,267.8,3.04,"(Video) The federal government didn't clock in today, but I did."],[178,270.83,3.64,"So here's how the government shutdown is going to impact you and your wallet."],[178,274.47,2.77,"And even breaking down how celebs, like Kendrick Lamar,"],[178,277.24,2.0,"get rich and stay rich."],[178,279.28,3.4,"(Video) Kendrick Lamar's family dancing in the \"Not Like Us\" music video"],[178,282.71,2.44,"wasn't just a clapback, it was a genius investment."],[178,285.15,4.77,"When you understand the language, you understand what matters."],[178,289.95,5.54,"And the response to turning finance into fun-nance has been incredible."],[178,296.09,2.34,"And this leads us into our second point."],[178,298.46,3.3,"While it's on financial educators to meet the next generation"],[178,301.8,2.27,"where and how they already live,"],[178,304.1,4.14,"it's on the next generation to build their own financial community."],[178,309.14,1.67,"For years, we have been told"],[178,310.84,4.27,"talking about money is rude, tacky, taboo."],[178,315.95,2.94,"But the last time I checked,"],[178,318.88,4.5,"Bob and Steve seem to have no problem trading investment tips"],[178,323.42,1.97,"as they're teeing off at hole three."],[178,326.19,3.47,"It turns out, it’s really only a faux pas"],[178,329.66,1.23,"when we do it."],[178,331.8,1.44,"It's time to change that."],[178,333.8,2.1,"I, like so many young people,"],[178,335.93,5.94,"clock in daily for a full shift at the looking-at-my-phone factory."],[178,341.9,3.3,"And whether you spend all of your time scrolling TikTok"],[178,345.24,2.04,"or reading Reddit threads,"],[178,347.31,1.4,"it's clear:"],[178,348.75,2.87,"having conversations around money"],[178,351.65,3.14,"takes the power away from the people up top"],[178,354.82,1.83,"and gives it back to us."],[178,357.35,4.57,"The internet has led to the creation of spaces like Fishbowl and Glassdoor,"],[178,361.96,3.84,"where people can share true, real numbers, honest struggles"],[178,365.83,3.0,"and genuine victories without shame or judgment."],[178,368.87,5.0,"And frankly, many of these conversations are still too raw to be had IRL."],[178,374.97,4.77,"This online anonymity is empowering people to work together,"],[178,379.78,2.57,"with their digital accountabili-buddies,"],[178,382.35,2.27,"to make smarter financial choices."],[178,385.28,4.1,"And my hope is that, as this generation gets more comfortable"],[178,389.42,2.63,"creating community around money talk,"],[178,392.09,2.1,"the generation after us --"],[178,394.22,4.87,"they're going to grow up seeing money discussed so openly and authentically"],[178,399.13,1.7,"that they will enter adulthood"],[178,400.86,2.34,"with a level of financial confidence"],[178,403.23,2.67,"that their parents could have only dreamed of."],[178,406.94,3.14,"And speaking of the future, you’ve probably heard the phrase:"],[178,410.11,2.47,"modern problems require modern solutions."],[178,412.61,1.47,"It's never been more true."],[178,414.71,2.07,"If I’m not getting a pension like my dad,"],[178,416.78,4.61,"and I can’t make a household’s ends meet on one income like my dad,"],[178,421.42,4.17,"and I certainly don't plan on working the same job for 40 years like my dad,"],[178,425.62,4.04,"why am I taking my dad's financial advice?"],[178,429.69,1.47,"Or worse,"],[178,431.16,2.54,"using my dad's financial advisor?"],[178,434.26,2.4,"My dad doesn't actually look like that, by the way."],[178,436.67,2.07,"He's going to be so mad if I don't say that."],[178,438.77,1.17,"Sorry, Dad."],[178,439.94,3.87,"Robo-advisors are making sophisticated investment strategies"],[178,443.84,2.13,"accessible to everyone."],[178,446.01,2.94,"Online banks are offering competitive rates"],[178,448.98,4.2,"without the burden of the overhead of brick-and-mortar branches."],[178,453.22,4.94,"And innovative apps are turning complex financial tasks into simple,"],[178,458.19,3.07,"actionable things that you can do from the palm of your hand."],[178,461.82,3.07,"These new platforms are just as regulated and licensed,"],[178,464.93,2.87,"and more often than not, they offer lower fees."],[178,467.83,4.04,"And it’s not just startup hubs or VCs that are taking notice."],[178,472.57,3.2,"Creators and educators are now turning into founders."],[178,476.31,1.17,"For my story,"],[178,477.51,4.3,"what started as just content has turned into an app powered by AI --"],[178,481.85,3.37,"working in conjunction with certified human experts,"],[178,485.25,3.57,"to provide guidance in plain English, like a friend,"],[178,488.85,4.07,"and more importantly, continues to educate first and foremost."],[178,493.56,3.37,"New-age financial brands aren't just a replacement"],[178,496.96,2.6,"for traditional financial services;"],[178,499.6,3.54,"they're purpose-built for the way we live now."],[178,503.43,3.14,"Designed to help you save smarter, invest easier,"],[178,506.57,2.9,"and build wealth faster than ever before."],[178,510.64,2.1,"The future of finance isn't coming."],[178,512.74,2.6,"It's already here and it's working for us."],[178,516.38,1.9,"So to wrap us up,"],[178,518.32,4.44,"a decade has passed since my horrific bar tab incident,"],[178,522.79,2.94,"and I so desperately wish I could go back in time"],[178,525.76,3.07,"and provide past-Vivian with some of the solutions"],[178,528.86,2.3,"available to today-Vivian now."],[178,532.13,4.74,"This shift towards financial accessibility isn’t just changing individual lives;"],[178,536.9,3.54,"it's building an ecosystem where everyone has a fair shot"],[178,540.47,1.27,"at prosperity."],[178,542.37,2.94,"And my dream is that, a decade from now,"],[178,545.34,3.04,"the evolution in financial education will mean:"],[178,548.41,3.04,"there are fewer declined credit cards at bar night,"],[178,551.48,1.9,"a shrinking wealth gap"],[178,553.42,3.37,"and a financial future that is brighter than ever."],[178,557.42,1.17,"Thank you."],[179,6.59,5.92,"In a 2021 study, researchers asked over 500 participants a simple question:"],[179,12.59,2.0,"how does the morning-after pill work?"],[179,15.14,4.88,"Despite these pills widespread use, 60% answered incorrectly."],[179,20.56,2.67,"Emergency contraceptives are often misunderstood."],[179,23.35,4.25,"In fact, even the phrase “the morning-after pill” is a misnomer."],[179,27.9,1.75,"So, how do they work?"],[179,30.28,4.17,"Each month, the brain and the ovaries prepare an egg to be released."],[179,34.53,2.88,"First, a group of immature eggs start to grow"],[179,37.41,3.5,"protective, fluid-filled sacs called follicles."],[179,41.08,4.13,"Around the eighth day, one of these follicles becomes mature or dominant,"],[179,45.21,1.83,"and the others are reabsorbed."],[179,47.42,3.04,"At this point, the brain signals the pituitary gland"],[179,50.46,4.09,"to produce luteinizing hormone, often just called LH."],[179,54.72,3.71,"A big surge in LH tells the dominant follicle to rupture,"],[179,58.43,1.46,"releasing the egg."],[179,60.22,1.75,"This is ovulation."],[179,62.14,4.75,"The egg travels to the fallopian tube, where it stays for about 24 hours,"],[179,66.9,4.8,"and it’s only in this very short window that it can be fertilized by sperm."],[179,72.19,3.54,"The rise of LH also stimulates the production of a hormone"],[179,75.74,1.46,"called progesterone."],[179,77.36,2.59,"When progesterone binds at specific receptors,"],[179,80.16,2.54,"this also helps to signal ovulation."],[179,82.79,4.21,"And high levels of progesterone are vital for preparing the uterine lining"],[179,87.0,3.59,"to support the possible implantation of a fertilized egg."],[179,91.34,3.75,"So, where do emergency contraceptive pills come into play?"],[179,95.34,2.88,"Emergency contraceptives are not abortion pills."],[179,98.3,4.17,"Instead, they work much earlier in the process before pregnancy,"],[179,102.47,2.79,"by disrupting or delaying ovulation."],[179,105.77,1.88,"There are two main types of pills."],[179,107.85,5.88,"The first type, sometimes called Plan B, contains a molecule called levonorgestrel,"],[179,113.86,3.71,"which is structurally very similar to the body’s hormone, progesterone."],[179,117.7,4.46,"When taken, levonorgestrel interferes with the production of LH."],[179,122.33,3.42,"Because LH doesn’t surge, there’s no ovulation."],[179,126.04,4.46,"That said, Plan B needs to be taken before an LH surge."],[179,130.59,3.71,"It can’t prevent ovulation once LH starts rising."],[179,134.96,4.34,"The second type of pill contains a molecule called ulipristal acetate."],[179,139.59,3.59,"It works by blocking progesterone from binding at receptors,"],[179,143.26,1.92,"thereby delaying ovulation."],[179,145.35,4.88,"Ulipristal acetate works even if LH has already begun to rise,"],[179,150.4,3.96,"which means it’s effective in a wider window of time than Plan B."],[179,154.73,3.63,"Since both pills work by blocking or delaying ovulation,"],[179,158.45,3.29,"the name “the morning-after pill” can be misleading."],[179,162.07,2.5,"Emergency contraceptives are most effective"],[179,164.58,2.79,"when taken as soon as possible after sex,"],[179,167.37,3.59,"to better the chance of disrupting ovulation before it occurs."],[179,171.17,2.25,"There is no need to wait until morning."],[179,173.79,4.0,"For this reason, many healthcare providers suggest having them on hand."],[179,178.13,3.46,"Yet emergency contraceptives may still prevent pregnancy"],[179,181.59,3.29,"even if they’re taken 3 to 5 days after sex,"],[179,184.89,1.29,"depending on the pill."],[179,186.39,4.25,"That’s because sperm can live in the reproductive tract for about five days."],[179,190.9,5.3,"So, for example, if a person ovulates three days after having unprotected sex,"],[179,196.36,1.83,"there's still a chance of pregnancy."],[179,198.49,3.34,"Since it's difficult to know exactly where someone is in their cycle,"],[179,201.82,3.63,"it’s best to take emergency contraceptives as soon as possible."],[179,206.12,3.75,"But perhaps the most widely held misconception about these pills"],[179,209.87,3.0,"is that they can harm a person’s future fertility."],[179,213.38,3.17,"Decades of research have shown that this isn’t the case,"],[179,216.71,3.55,"even if emergency contraceptives are taken multiple times."],[179,220.38,3.17,"In fact, the effects of both pills are short lived,"],[179,223.72,2.63,"and they don't provide continued protection."],[179,226.51,3.67,"It's recommended that a person uses condoms for the remainder of their cycle,"],[179,230.18,3.29,"and if they have unprotected sex again, then repeat use."],[179,234.31,3.5,"Of course, emergency contraceptive pills aren’t failproof."],[179,237.94,4.34,"As they neither prevent fertilization nor the implantation of a fertilized egg,"],[179,242.53,3.5,"these pills are ineffective if someone has already ovulated,"],[179,246.2,2.34,"or the LH surge has already peaked."],[179,249.04,3.46,"However, there is a third option that can protect against pregnancy,"],[179,252.5,2.42,"even if a person has already ovulated."],[179,255.58,3.84,"Many people know copper intrauterine devices, or IUDs,"],[179,259.42,2.21,"as a form of long-term birth control,"],[179,261.63,4.96,"but they also work to prevent pregnancy if inserted soon after unprotected sex."],[179,266.89,2.09,"Unlike emergency contraceptive pills,"],[179,269.01,4.75,"a copper IUD can prevent pregnancy before and after fertilization."],[179,274.1,1.58,"Copper is toxic to sperm"],[179,275.69,3.92,"and creates a uterine environment that makes implantation unlikely."],[179,279.77,4.67,"Of course, copper IUDs might be less accessible in an emergency situation—"],[179,284.45,3.21,"they must be inserted by a trained healthcare provider."],[179,287.91,2.92,"But on the upside, they continue to prevent pregnancy"],[179,290.83,2.79,"for at least 10 years while in place."],[179,293.91,2.79,"Most healthcare providers agree that it's more effective,"],[179,296.71,1.67,"as well as less expensive,"],[179,298.46,3.75,"to use a regular form of birth control when trying to avoid pregnancy."],[179,302.55,1.79,"But if or when you need it,"],[179,304.34,3.79,"emergency contraceptives are a safe option to use."],[180,7.04,5.84,"Officer Stone approaches the dimly lit motel he’s been called to investigate."],[180,13.22,3.17,"Outside the shattered windows and broken door,"],[180,16.47,4.55,"the motel manager tells him the room was rented to Frank Carter,"],[180,21.43,3.17,"who was last seen entering the motel three nights earlier."],[180,24.85,2.38,"While Carter doesn't have the cleanest record,"],[180,27.32,2.92,"he's never been arrested for a violent crime before."],[180,30.65,2.59,"Yet as Stone scans the crime scene,"],[180,33.24,6.42,"he sees a disorderly room, visible red-brown stains and a partial shoe track."],[180,40.24,3.71,"All these details hint at a bloody struggle,"],[180,44.21,3.34,"but there aren't enough clues to say exactly what happened."],[180,48.21,3.79,"After calling in a detective and a crime scene investigation team"],[180,52.01,5.8,"to inspect, photograph, sketch, measure, and collect all the obvious evidence,"],[180,57.93,3.04,"the investigators decide their next move."],[180,61.47,3.0,"It's time to apply luminol."],[180,65.44,3.96,"Luminol, also known as 3-aminophthalhydrazide,"],[180,69.52,2.5,"was first introduced as an investigative tool"],[180,72.03,6.46,"in 1937 by a German forensic scientist named Dr. Walter Specht."],[180,79.03,1.63,"Almost a decade earlier,"],[180,80.83,4.09,"research found that when mixed in a solution with an oxidizing agent"],[180,84.91,2.75,"and exposed to certain compounds,"],[180,87.71,4.25,"luminol molecules rapidly oxidize, become excited,"],[180,91.96,3.0,"and emit a faint blue-white glow"],[180,95.13,2.13,"as they return to their normal state."],[180,97.64,3.67,"This process is called chemiluminescence,"],[180,101.31,5.42,"and Specht discovered he could trigger it by exposing luminol to hemoglobin,"],[180,107.1,2.59,"a protein found in red blood cells."],[180,109.98,3.21,"So by spraying the solution in a dark crime scene,"],[180,113.36,4.13,"he could illuminate even the faintest traces of blood."],[180,118.49,3.54,"This is what Officer Stone is hoping to do back at the motel."],[180,122.37,1.46,"The lights are switched off,"],[180,123.83,4.84,"and the forensic team sprays luminol across the walls, floor, and ceiling."],[180,129.08,3.5,"The chemiluminescence only lasts about 30 seconds,"],[180,132.59,2.25,"so our investigators need to keep spraying"],[180,134.88,2.33,"as they track blood through the crime scene."],[180,137.68,1.54,"But if they find a trail,"],[180,139.38,4.46,"the specific patterns of blood could help reconstruct what might have happened."],[180,144.06,4.88,"In some crime scenes, luminol might reveal footprints or drag marks."],[180,149.31,4.5,"It can show larger drips associated with slowly bleeding wounds,"],[180,153.82,3.46,"or fine spatter suggesting high-impact wounds"],[180,157.28,2.71,"from firearms or motorized tools."],[180,160.32,3.29,"The absence of a reaction can be just as informative,"],[180,163.74,4.55,"indicating when people or objects blocked blood from hitting a surface."],[180,168.71,4.09,"And since luminol will react even if the blood is extremely diluted,"],[180,172.92,3.88,"it's almost impossible to clean a bloody crime scene well enough"],[180,176.92,2.38,"to prevent chemiluminescence."],[180,179.72,4.84,"In fact, a skilled investigator can even use these lingering patterns"],[180,184.56,3.13,"to identify attempts at blood removal."],[180,188.43,1.33,"In this case,"],[180,189.77,4.13,"Officer Stone finds spattered spots of blue light on the floor,"],[180,194.11,2.84,"suggesting an impact wound and a struggle."],[180,197.32,4.25,"Shoe track impressions and a glowing handprint on the kitchen countertop"],[180,201.57,2.29,"line up with the drag mark on the floor."],[180,204.32,2.59,"Following the trail from the foot of the bed,"],[180,206.91,2.63,"the investigators end up in the parking lot,"],[180,209.54,3.21,"suggesting the victim was placed inside a vehicle."],[180,213.58,4.46,"These stains make a strong case for the narrative revealed by luminol."],[180,218.21,2.08,"But investigators have to be careful"],[180,220.3,3.71,"since the chemical’s high sensitivity is a double-edged sword."],[180,224.3,3.21,"Not only does luminol react to very old bloodstains,"],[180,227.51,4.92,"with some cases showing chemoluminescence on objects thousands of years old,"],[180,232.65,5.67,"it can also react to cleaning agents like bleach or materials containing copper."],[180,238.86,4.63,"This is why luminol is what's called a presumptive blood test."],[180,243.66,2.79,"It indicates the possible presence of blood,"],[180,246.83,4.09,"but a confirmatory test with chemicals that only react to hemoglobin"],[180,251.08,1.75,"must be done to prove it."],[180,253.83,3.38,"If a positive reaction for blood is confirmed, however,"],[180,257.42,4.5,"DNA tests can be especially useful for identifying and eliminating"],[180,261.92,1.42,"potential suspects."],[180,263.76,4.71,"Officer Stone submitted a DNA sample from the motel for that exact purpose,"],[180,268.72,3.63,"and the results found exactly the clue he needs."],[180,272.6,2.92,"The blood belongs to Frank Carter,"],[180,275.69,4.09,"suggesting that he may be the victim, not the culprit."],[180,280.15,2.38,"Stone is mulling over this new evidence"],[180,282.53,2.79,"when he gets a call from a local state trooper."],[180,285.61,2.54,"After pulling over an erratic driver,"],[180,288.2,3.84,"the trooper noticed what appeared to be bloodstains on the rear bumper."],[180,292.41,1.79,"The car's trunk was empty,"],[180,294.29,4.55,"but luminol testing later revealed traces of blood throughout the car."],[180,299.38,3.71,"One more DNA test confirms Stone’s suspicions:"],[180,303.21,2.63,"Carter was the victim."],[180,306.34,6.21,"And faced with this overwhelming evidence, the car’s driver confessed to the crime."],[181,7.34,2.59,"Whether you memorize a script, or read from one,"],[181,9.92,3.46,"or use a few notes, or don’t use any notes at all,"],[181,13.47,3.46,"a talk that is well-prepared and passionately delivered"],[181,16.93,2.84,"will always impact an audience."],[181,20.27,3.38,"But what does delivering a talk passionately look like?"],[181,24.1,3.21,"It's time to find your voice."],[181,28.07,3.54,"Here’s a radical question: why bother to give a talk?"],[181,31.82,3.96,"Why not simply email the script to every potential member of the audience?"],[181,35.91,2.42,"One of the special things about TED"],[181,38.33,5.5,"is that it shows how talks can offer something more than printed words."],[181,44.0,2.46,"But that doesn’t just happen automatically."],[181,46.54,3.34,"Not every talk offers something extra."],[181,50.21,5.5,"That something extra has to be thought about, invested in, and developed."],[181,55.88,1.75,"It has to be earned."],[181,57.97,1.92,"What is that something extra?"],[181,60.22,1.79,"It’s our humanity."],[181,62.18,4.25,"Our humanity turns information into inspiration."],[181,67.11,4.29,"Here are some of the things the human layer can bring to a text."],[181,71.48,3.0,"Connection: I trust this person."],[181,74.82,3.92,"Engagement: every sentence sounds so interesting!"],[181,79.08,4.59,"Curiosity: I hear it in your voice and see it in your face."],[181,84.16,4.59,"Understanding: the emphasis on that word with that hand gesture,"],[181,88.92,1.21,"now I get it."],[181,90.5,3.54,"Empathy: I can tell how much that hurt you."],[181,94.51,4.05,"Excitement: wow, that passion is infectious!"],[181,98.89,3.84,"Conviction: such determination in those eyes."],[181,103.14,4.55,"Action: I want to be on your team. Sign me up!"],[181,108.44,3.8,"Together, these things create inspiration."],[181,112.69,3.54,"We think of inspiration as the force that tells the brain"],[181,116.24,1.96,"what to do with a new idea."],[181,118.66,4.38,"Many ideas just get filed away, and probably soon forgotten."],[181,123.37,2.04,"Inspiration, on the other hand,"],[181,125.41,2.75,"sets off our mind’s attention alert system."],[181,129.21,4.59,"There are two major things to consider when preparing a talk for an audience."],[181,133.88,3.63,"The first is: what am I doing with my voice?"],[181,137.93,3.71,"And the second is: what am I doing with my body?"],[181,142.39,1.58,"Speak with meaning."],[181,144.22,3.88,"Listen to the opening minute of the TED Talk by George Monbiot."],[181,148.73,1.21,"“When I was a young man,"],[181,149.94,3.0,"I spent six years of wild adventure in the tropics,"],[181,152.94,2.09,"working as an investigative journalist"],[181,155.19,3.0,"in some of the most bewitching parts of the world."],[181,158.78,2.96,"I was as reckless and foolish as only young men can be—"],[181,161.74,1.54,"this is why wars get fought—"],[181,163.33,4.0,"but I also felt more alive than I’ve ever done since."],[181,167.62,1.25,"And when I came home,"],[181,168.87,4.55,"I found the scope of my existence gradually diminishing,"],[181,173.63,3.13,"until loading the dishwasher seemed like an interesting challenge."],[181,178.01,3.75,"And I found myself sort of scratching at the walls of life,"],[181,181.76,4.09,"as if I was trying to find a way out into a wider space beyond."],[181,186.14,3.67,"I was, I believe, ecologically bored.”"],[181,190.14,5.21,"When you hear Monbiot speak, you find yourself pulled into his world."],[181,195.52,4.42,"He uses his voice in a way that adds meaning to every word he says."],[181,200.15,3.17,"That's something you wouldn't get if you read his script in a book."],[181,203.32,4.21,"His words are interesting, but it’s the way he says them"],[181,207.62,3.96,"that gives his audience a feeling of curiosity and astonishment."],[181,211.79,1.21,"How does he do this?"],[181,214.92,5.05,"The key to being an exciting speaker is to create variety in the way you speak—"],[181,220.3,3.59,"variety based on the meaning you’re trying to get across."],[181,224.39,2.04,"So many speakers forget this."],[181,226.43,3.63,"They give a talk in which every sentence sounds the same,"],[181,230.23,3.59,"with a slight rise at the start and a drop at the end."],[181,234.52,2.46,"There are no pauses or changes of pace."],[181,237.15,2.59,"This communicates that no single part of a talk"],[181,239.78,1.79,"matters more than any other part."],[181,241.86,3.92,"It’s just the same thing over and over again until the end."],[181,246.07,3.96,"Listening to a talk like this can feel like being hypnotized."],[181,250.16,2.54,"That is, it puts an audience to sleep."],[181,253.21,2.63,"If your talk is scripted, try this:"],[181,256.0,5.21,"find the most important two or three words in each sentence and underline them."],[181,261.51,4.92,"Then, look for the one word in each paragraph that really matters,"],[181,266.51,1.88,"and underline it twice."],[181,268.81,2.92,"If there are bits that are lighthearted or playful,"],[181,271.85,2.38,"run a wavy pencil line under those."],[181,274.35,1.5,"Look for every question mark,"],[181,275.86,2.42,"and highlight it with a yellow highlighter."],[181,278.52,4.34,"Find the single biggest “Aha” moment in the whole talk,"],[181,282.86,3.38,"and put a great big black blob right before it."],[181,286.78,4.05,"If there's a joke or a funny story, put little pink dots above it."],[181,291.25,2.04,"Now, try reading your script,"],[181,293.29,3.67,"making sure to change your voice in response to each mark."],[181,297.54,4.71,"For example, let yourself laugh a little while looking at the pink dots."],[181,302.71,3.09,"Pause for the big black blob."],[181,306.22,2.71,"And speed up a bit for the wavy pencil line,"],[181,309.43,2.21,"or speak more softly."],[181,312.02,1.25,"How does that sound?"],[181,313.68,1.75,"Now, try one more thing."],[181,316.02,5.55,"Remember all the emotions associated with each section of your talk."],[181,322.11,2.67,"Which bits are you most passionate about?"],[181,325.15,2.17,"Which issues make you angry?"],[181,327.91,1.46,"What makes you laugh?"],[181,329.82,1.71,"What confuses you?"],[181,332.04,4.21,"Read your talk again, letting those emotions out a little as you speak."],[181,337.25,1.5,"Try doing this with a friend."],[181,339.08,3.13,"See what she responds to, or what she rolls her eyes at."],[181,342.75,4.3,"Record yourself reading your talk, then play it back with your eyes closed."],[181,347.3,1.13,"How's it sounding?"],[181,349.14,4.67,"Don’t struggle to add variety to your voice when that doesn’t feel right to you."],[181,353.93,4.21,"Let it come naturally from the passion you feel for your idea."],[181,358.44,1.75,"You want to sound like yourself,"],[181,360.36,3.38,"expressing curiosity and excitement when it's appropriate."],[181,364.03,2.38,"You know the voice you use when you’re catching up"],[181,366.4,2.92,"with friends or family members you haven’t seen in a while?"],[181,369.57,3.55,"That’s the kind of voice you’ll want to use to present your talk:"],[181,373.33,5.84,"real, natural, and unafraid to let it rip if what you’re saying demands it."],[181,379.71,2.54,"It’s important to use your body too."],[181,382.55,2.13,"Sometimes a speaker will give the impression"],[181,384.67,3.92,"that his body is only there to carry his head onto the stage."],[181,389.01,1.5,"Once in front of the audience,"],[181,390.51,2.75,"the body no longer knows what to do with itself."],[181,393.26,3.29,"It stands awkwardly, hands glued to its side,"],[181,396.68,4.04,"or sways back and forth from one leg to the other."],[181,402.06,2.25,"But there are a few things you can think about"],[181,404.32,3.25,"that may help you feel more comfortable on stage"],[181,407.69,2.96,"and make you seem more confident to your audience."],[181,411.24,5.13,"The simplest way to present a talk powerfully is just to stand tall."],[181,416.7,4.25,"Put equal weight on both your feet, keeping your feet a few inches apart."],[181,421.33,4.3,"Use your hands and arms to naturally emphasize whatever it is you’re saying."],[181,426.17,3.67,"This is enough to signal to the audience that you’re calm"],[181,430.05,2.25,"and that you know what you’re talking about."],[181,432.72,3.21,"This is the method used by a majority of TED speakers."],[181,436.26,4.92,"The key is to feel relaxed and to let your upper body move freely."],[181,441.64,1.67,"Good posture helps."],[181,443.4,2.46,"Avoid slouching your shoulders forward."],[181,445.98,3.34,"Standing so openly may feel vulnerable,"],[181,449.49,4.04,"but that vulnerability can be nice for an audience to see."],[181,454.07,2.96,"Some speakers prefer to walk the stage."],[181,457.08,1.13,"It helps them think."],[181,458.45,3.54,"It helps them emphasize key moments, and, this is important,"],[181,462.21,3.5,"they frequently stop to dwell on important points."],[181,466.05,3.67,"It's that change in rhythm that makes the walking method work."],[181,469.8,4.59,"Constant pacing without a change in rhythm can be tiring to watch."],[181,474.68,4.63,"But pacing broken up by moments of stillness can be powerful."],[181,480.14,4.0,"Something to avoid is nervously shifting from leg to leg,"],[181,484.4,3.17,"or walking forward and back a couple of steps,"],[181,487.65,2.17,"in a kind of rocking motion."],[181,490.49,2.75,"Many speakers do this without realizing it."],[181,493.49,1.92,"They may be feeling a little anxious,"],[181,495.41,3.38,"and shifting from one leg to the other eases their discomfort."],[181,498.83,4.8,"But, from the audience’s point of view, it actually highlights that discomfort."],[181,504.0,3.21,"The difference between a speaker who rocks back and forth"],[181,507.21,4.8,"and a speaker who finds stillness can be huge for an audience."],[181,512.3,2.0,"So, move if you want to,"],[181,514.3,3.21,"but only move if you can move with purpose."],[181,518.14,2.04,"Then, when you want to emphasize a point,"],[181,520.35,5.38,"stop moving and address your audience from a stance of calm power."],[181,526.4,3.21,"There are plenty of other ways you can speak with power too."],[181,529.61,3.5,"Dame Stephanie Shirley chose to sit for her talk,"],[181,533.2,3.38,"using a metal stool with one foot tucked back on a rung,"],[181,536.7,1.5,"and notes in her lap."],[181,538.53,2.17,"It looked relaxed and natural."],[181,541.25,3.84,"The late, great neurologist Oliver Sacks also sat for his talk."],[181,545.67,4.21,"Clifford Stoll leapt and darted around the stage with such energy,"],[181,549.88,4.09,"it added an entirely new and unique flavor to his talk."],[181,554.34,1.67,"So there are no rules."],[181,556.09,2.46,"You just have to find a way of being on stage"],[181,558.64,3.29,"that makes you feel comfortable and confident,"],[181,562.14,3.0,"and that doesn’t distract from what you’re saying."],[181,565.56,3.5,"The simple test is to rehearse in front of a small audience,"],[181,569.06,4.05,"and ask them if your body language is getting in the way of your message."],[181,573.44,3.5,"Or, record yourself speaking and watch it back"],[181,576.95,3.67,"to see if you’re doing anything you didn’t know you were doing with your body."],[181,580.66,3.75,"There's a place in the world for all types of presentation styles."],[181,584.41,2.92,"Just make sure your body knows it’s not just there"],[181,587.33,1.63,"to carry your head around."],[181,589.17,3.21,"It's allowed to enjoy its time on stage."],[181,592.88,1.21,"Do it your way."],[181,594.63,1.5,"Don't try to be someone else."],[181,596.26,3.46,"Focus on your idea and your passion for it."],[181,600.1,4.42,"And don't be afraid to let your own personality shine through."],[182,7.0,4.88,"In July of 1969, Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin"],[182,11.88,3.13,"left the first human footprints on the moon."],[182,18.01,5.63,"They also left two pairs of boots, a handful of tools, and four vomit bags."],[182,23.94,5.0,"This lunar litter was far from the last space junk humanity has produced."],[182,29.19,3.92,"In 2006, Suni Williams lost her camera"],[182,33.11,3.96,"while tussling with a stuck solar array on the ISS."],[182,37.2,4.42,"And the following year, a similar job resulted in the loss of a bag"],[182,41.62,3.34,"filled with $100,000 worth of tools."],[182,45.12,3.67,"These accidental satellites typically have short lifespans,"],[182,48.8,3.92,"before falling back to Earth and burning up in our atmosphere."],[182,52.84,4.71,"However, other pieces of space junk, like SpaceX’s Tesla Roadster,"],[182,57.55,3.42,"will be stuck in their orbits for the foreseeable future."],[182,61.72,2.13,"In the early days of space travel,"],[182,63.85,3.63,"this kind of littering was largely considered inconsequential."],[182,68.23,2.08,"But in today's crowded skies,"],[182,70.32,5.0,"orbital debris poses a serious threat to the thousands of satellites"],[182,75.32,3.0,"that underpin Earth’s vital technologies."],[182,78.57,4.8,"Currently, there are over 131 million pieces of debris"],[182,83.37,4.71,"whipping around the planet at an average speed of 10 kilometers a second."],[182,88.25,4.88,"This debris ranges in size from pieces as large as an entire bus,"],[182,93.13,2.88,"to those as small as a grain of sand."],[182,96.3,4.55,"But roughly 1 million pieces are at least one centimeter across,"],[182,100.85,3.79,"which is large enough to severely damage most satellites."],[182,104.85,5.46,"While losing any spacecraft is bad enough, the knock-on effects are even worse."],[182,110.48,5.13,"When satellites crash into debris or each other, such as in 2009,"],[182,115.61,4.8,"when an American communications satellite collided with a defunct Russian satellite,"],[182,120.41,2.92,"they can explode into thousands of pieces."],[182,123.49,3.13,"And if events like these happen often enough,"],[182,126.66,4.67,"the increase in debris could trigger a catastrophic cascade"],[182,131.34,3.0,"that researchers have named the Kessler syndrome:"],[182,134.34,5.21,"a runaway effect which could destroy untold numbers of orbiting spacecraft."],[182,139.68,2.42,"But what does all this mean for people on Earth?"],[182,142.35,3.92,"Well, even if space shrapnel does take down a satellite,"],[182,146.27,2.59,"most debris burns up during re-entry."],[182,148.98,4.59,"So the probability of waking up to a spacecraft in your yard is very small."],[182,154.03,3.75,"That said, some large specimens can survive the trip,"],[182,158.03,6.71,"such as the SpaceX Capsule that landed in an Australian field in August of 2022."],[182,164.91,3.13,"And the odds of something similar happening again"],[182,168.04,2.84,"grows alongside the amount of space junk."],[182,171.08,4.8,"So how can we save our satellites and ourselves from all this trash?"],[182,176.05,3.5,"Part of the solution is to stop creating waste in space,"],[182,179.68,3.09,"but debris is generated in a lot of ways."],[182,182.97,5.71,"In addition to collisions, solar radiation erodes spacecraft surfaces,"],[182,188.81,2.0,"motors spew slag,"],[182,190.98,5.71,"and satellite launches routinely abandon rocket bodies, covers, cowlings,"],[182,196.69,1.88,"and explosive bolts."],[182,200.15,5.76,"Since 1957, we’ve ferried almost 16,000 satellites to space"],[182,205.91,2.71,"using thousands of disposable rockets."],[182,208.75,3.38,"Researchers are finally experimenting with new systems"],[182,212.12,4.3,"that can send up over 100 satellites in a single rocket launch."],[182,216.59,5.51,"Meanwhile, both NASA and private companies are working to prevent existing satellites"],[182,222.09,3.67,"from becoming junk by using servicer satellites"],[182,225.76,2.96,"to refuel, inspect, and repair them."],[182,228.85,4.34,"But we also need to clean up the junk that's already up in orbit."],[182,233.52,2.25,"When a satellite is ready to retire,"],[182,235.77,4.34,"engineers can use controlled re-entry to intentionally burn it up"],[182,240.11,1.29,"in Earth's atmosphere."],[182,241.53,3.75,"Ideally, this would happen as soon as a satellite is out of use,"],[182,245.41,4.8,"but outside the US, current guidelines allow defunct satellites"],[182,250.2,3.04,"to remain in orbit for up to 25 years,"],[182,253.25,2.59,"but this rule has barely been enforced."],[182,256.08,1.96,"Beyond retired spacecraft,"],[182,258.05,3.8,"researchers have identified some particularly dangerous debris,"],[182,261.84,2.71,"so efforts could be made to tackle them first."],[182,264.8,4.0,"Some debris experts have proposed using lasers,"],[182,268.81,2.46,"based both on Earth and in space,"],[182,271.27,4.96,"to nudge small debris to a higher, safer orbit or into the atmosphere."],[182,276.44,4.04,"And private companies are attempting to use space tug boats"],[182,280.48,3.34,"to ferry large debris to less dangerous orbits."],[182,284.61,1.46,"Whatever the solution,"],[182,286.07,5.21,"all these satellites and orbital debris reflect human consumption back on Earth."],[182,291.45,4.04,"So if we want to keep using and exploring this final frontier,"],[182,295.5,5.26,"we'll need to get better at cleaning up our trash both up there and down here."],[183,4.24,3.2,"Whitney Pennington Rodgers: Hi, Jessica. And hello, everyone."],[183,7.47,2.54,"It is so exciting to be able to sit here with you"],[183,10.04,2.07,"for this very first session at TEDNext,"],[183,12.11,1.83,"and to talk about leadership"],[183,13.94,4.21,"in a way that I think will be really meaningful for all of us here."],[183,18.18,3.9,"And maybe that's a really great place to start in this session called Lead."],[183,22.12,2.6,"Could you tell us how you define the term \"leadership,\""],[183,24.75,3.17,"especially as it relates to your work in public health"],[183,27.96,2.57,"and infectious disease epidemiology?"],[183,30.53,2.94,"Jessica Malaty Rivera: Yeah. Thank you so much for having me."],[183,33.5,2.7,"I am so excited to be here in this room with all of you."],[183,36.2,1.53,"Leadership isn't always a word"],[183,37.73,4.67,"that is used necessarily in public health or epidemiology,"],[183,42.41,2.87,"but it probably has been much more in the last several years"],[183,45.31,2.27,"as we've just experienced with the pandemic."],[183,48.45,3.94,"And one thing that I always saw that was needed and lacking,"],[183,52.42,2.14,"and a huge gap in the training that I got,"],[183,54.59,2.97,"was making it make sense to people."],[183,58.22,2.47,"And that was my opportunity to lead."],[183,60.72,3.7,"That was my opportunity to say, \"How can we connect people to this work?\""],[183,64.46,2.9,"It's very siloed. It's very separate."],[183,67.36,3.6,"And if we're talking about things that influence people's decision making"],[183,71.0,3.27,"and risk budgets and relationships,"],[183,74.31,1.67,"and how they build community,"],[183,76.01,3.8,"how can I step in and bridge the gap between academia,"],[183,79.84,3.4,"fancy institutions and the public that it's intending to serve?"],[183,84.18,1.37,"WPR: And, you know, it seems"],[183,85.58,2.33,"like there really is no truer test of leadership"],[183,87.95,3.37,"than in moments where you're doing it in times of unfavorability."],[183,91.32,4.74,"And we know right now that public health and science is being questioned --"],[183,96.06,1.64,"especially in the United States,"],[183,97.69,1.27,"but abroad."],[183,98.96,4.97,"And so I'm interested to hear how you think about courageous leadership"],[183,103.97,4.14,"in times where your work, the very foundation of what you're doing,"],[183,108.14,3.64,"is unsupported, and in some cases, even under attack."],[183,111.81,5.81,"JMR: Yeah. A potentially unpopular opinion is I do think science is political"],[183,117.61,3.17,"because we need good science to inform our politics."],[183,120.78,4.3,"We need data to inform the policies that make decisions"],[183,125.12,3.57,"and make systems available to our public."],[183,128.69,3.94,"And what the difference is, though, is that it shouldn't be politicized."],[183,132.66,2.1,"And so what courageous leadership,"],[183,134.8,4.21,"what being bold in that space has looked like for me, is saying that"],[183,139.04,3.74,"and connecting with lawmakers, and connecting with advocacy groups,"],[183,142.81,5.54,"and even doing a lot of that work myself to make that connection more clear."],[183,148.38,3.84,"The more siloed we are when it comes to science and policy,"],[183,152.25,4.1,"the more opportunity there is for really dangerous mistruths"],[183,156.39,4.37,"and false claims and conspiracies to fill that gap."],[183,160.79,3.97,"And so if we're not using the language of science and translating it"],[183,164.79,3.44,"so that people who are making these laws and policies understand them,"],[183,168.26,4.0,"and the people who are using their votes and voices to elect them,"],[183,172.3,1.64,"then we're missing an opportunity"],[183,173.97,4.67,"to really make sure that everybody is represented in these science policies."],[183,178.68,3.97,"WPR: And I think -- to sort of piggyback on what you’re just saying here --"],[183,182.68,4.14,"you have stood at the center of this tension of communicating"],[183,186.82,2.6,"these complex, unpopular truths"],[183,189.45,4.64,"in what can feel like a very emotionally charged environment."],[183,194.12,4.61,"How do you practice care and courage and leading"],[183,198.76,1.3,"in moments where it feels"],[183,200.1,2.6,"like the political and personal stakes are really high"],[183,202.7,1.87,"for people in your audience?"],[183,204.57,1.94,"JMR: Yeah, it's not easy."],[183,206.54,3.54,"You know, I think that when I look back in the last several years,"],[183,210.07,2.0,"the good outweighs the bad."],[183,212.08,3.87,"But speaking truth, uncomfortable truth for some people,"],[183,215.95,4.67,"has come at great cost to myself, my safety, my family."],[183,220.62,2.87,"I wouldn't go back and not do it,"],[183,223.49,2.77,"but I'd be remiss if I didn't include the fact"],[183,226.29,2.37,"that we're living in some pretty dangerous times"],[183,228.66,3.87,"where we're relitigating established truth,"],[183,232.53,4.07,"and a way that I have sought groundedness,"],[183,236.63,1.6,"a way that I have continued,"],[183,238.24,2.67,"is to remind myself of that why,"],[183,240.9,2.6,"of the purpose that is my children"],[183,243.54,1.6,"who inspired me to do that work."],[183,245.18,2.5,"And in finding solidarity with other scientists"],[183,247.71,2.64,"who are passionate about making science make sense"],[183,250.38,2.74,"and informing people, so that ..."],[183,253.12,2.47,"To me, it's tools in a toolkit."],[183,255.62,2.27,"I don't want to dumb anything down for anybody."],[183,257.92,4.57,"I want to elevate people's literacy in science and complex things."],[183,262.49,2.44,"I'm not expecting people to remember all the things"],[183,264.96,2.5,"that I have to remember about biology and immunology."],[183,267.5,1.8,"That's asking too much."],[183,269.33,3.67,"But I do think that these are important tools"],[183,273.04,1.3,"for people to have"],[183,274.37,4.64,"to navigate really dangerous information ecosystems right now."],[183,279.04,1.94,"WPR: And I think there's a lesson there,"],[183,281.01,3.2,"not in just how you can consume this information and think about it,"],[183,284.25,1.27,"but also as a leader."],[183,285.55,2.84,"And I'd love to hear more about how you actually do this"],[183,288.42,3.27,"because of course, you're doing this from the space of public health,"],[183,291.72,4.94,"but all of us are sort of navigating what feels like a really divisive moment."],[183,296.66,2.44,"And so how do you decide"],[183,299.13,4.44,"what is a great place for you to speak up?"],[183,303.57,1.9,"When is it better for you to be quiet?"],[183,305.5,4.24,"When is it good for you to take a stand? How do you make those decisions?"],[183,309.74,2.2,"JMR: Yeah. I got into public health"],[183,311.94,3.27,"because at the core of public health is altruism."],[183,315.25,4.44,"It is a desire to reduce harm, to prevent disease,"],[183,319.72,3.47,"to increase people's opportunity for long life"],[183,323.22,2.0,"and thriving in that long life."],[183,325.25,4.54,"And so my red line is when information,"],[183,329.83,3.14,"specifically harmful health information,"],[183,333.0,2.4,"challenges or jeopardizes those things."],[183,335.43,1.6,"And I do think that, you know,"],[183,337.03,2.6,"when we're experiencing public health emergencies,"],[183,339.67,2.07,"we're kind of dealing with two outbreaks."],[183,341.74,2.77,"We're dealing with an outbreak of a pathogen"],[183,344.54,1.97,"and an outbreak of information."],[183,346.54,2.44,"And information today is not neutral."],[183,349.01,3.14,"And we need to recognize that that lack of neutrality means"],[183,352.18,3.87,"that that is probably an accelerating driver of that epidemic --"],[183,356.95,3.04,"and it often is. And how do we address both?"],[183,359.99,2.5,"We sometimes get a little myopic with public health"],[183,362.49,2.13,"and just think about the one threat."],[183,364.66,4.11,"And so I do think that having this big picture view"],[183,368.8,1.97,"of tandem outbreaks"],[183,370.8,2.94,"is the way we go forward with this."],[183,373.74,1.87,"It's the way for me to say,"],[183,375.64,2.77,"I can't just think about all the interventions"],[183,378.41,1.27,"that we need to have --"],[183,379.71,3.0,"I need to talk about who doesn't understand this"],[183,382.75,2.57,"and what are they feeling about these things?"],[183,385.35,2.44,"Are they scared? Are they feeling forgotten?"],[183,387.79,2.27,"Have they been hurt by the system in the past?"],[183,390.09,2.13,"So I'm going to work a little bit extra hard"],[183,392.26,1.64,"to regain their trust."],[183,393.92,3.94,"I think a lot about the difference of trust and trustworthiness."],[183,397.86,3.44,"Am I even worthy of the trust of the people I'm speaking to,"],[183,401.33,2.2,"to say these things, instead of asking them,"],[183,403.53,3.7,"\"Just trust me, I'm a scientist. Trust me, I'm an expert.\""],[183,407.24,2.2,"We're now in a different world"],[183,409.47,4.54,"where trust and expertise [are] really subjective."],[183,414.04,4.1,"And people are now choosing to, you know,"],[183,418.18,4.14,"abandon expertise for the sake of things that are more familiar,"],[183,422.32,3.27,"things that they identify with."],[183,425.62,2.9,"And so I do a lot of that ground level listening"],[183,428.56,1.8,"and hearing people"],[183,430.36,3.6,"to understand this is not a one-size-fits-all type of work."],[183,434.0,4.47,"It really is hypertailored and hyperfocused on individuals."],[183,438.5,4.47,"WPR: And, you know, this makes me think about the importance of storytelling."],[183,443.01,2.17,"And I have something that you've said before,"],[183,445.21,1.8,"that data alone doesn't change minds."],[183,447.04,1.27,"Stories do."],[183,448.35,5.94,"And so how do you use storytelling as a tool for courage and leading"],[183,454.28,4.37,"when you're thinking about people potentially not having trust"],[183,458.65,3.9,"in some of the actual data or science behind some of what you're saying?"],[183,462.59,2.1,"JMR: I think about this so much because ..."],[183,464.69,4.87,"There’s a phrase: “Plural of anecdote is not data.”"],[183,469.6,5.07,"It's true. But anecdotes do have a significant influence"],[183,474.7,1.37,"on people's decision making,"],[183,476.07,4.64,"and so we can't ignore the fact that an individual story"],[183,480.74,3.77,"can go a really long way in helping people connect science"],[183,484.55,1.8,"to science decision making."],[183,486.38,1.74,"And I do that a lot myself."],[183,488.12,1.87,"I think it's a slower process,"],[183,489.99,3.54,"which may not be as efficient for many folks in my space,"],[183,493.56,1.27,"but I lead with that."],[183,494.82,3.97,"I don't walk away from the many identities that I have."],[183,498.83,2.87,"On top of being a scientist and a sense maker,"],[183,501.7,2.04,"I'm a mom to three young kids,"],[183,503.77,3.04,"and I use my experience of parenting them"],[183,506.84,4.97,"and making decisions about their health as a way to show my example,"],[183,511.84,3.8,"as a way to show any doubts that I had, any concerns that I had."],[183,515.68,2.97,"And I would be lying if I didn't recognize the fact"],[183,518.68,2.17,"that I had postpartum anxiety,"],[183,520.88,3.97,"and that influenced a lot of the decisions that I made."],[183,524.89,3.84,"It didn't cause me to not make some choices that I fully support,"],[183,528.76,4.3,"but the thought process, I shared that very, very transparently,"],[183,533.1,1.8,"and I think that it can go a long way"],[183,534.93,2.6,"when it comes to connecting the humanity of the people"],[183,537.53,1.77,"who are trained and experts"],[183,539.34,2.07,"to the people that they're trying to serve."],[183,542.41,1.77,"WPR: I think we often hear"],[183,544.21,2.24,"that public health has a messaging problem."],[183,546.44,2.27,"And I think you're speaking to some of the ways"],[183,548.75,2.07,"that you think about how to message things."],[183,550.85,2.04,"Do you think that that's what it is?"],[183,552.92,2.14,"Or is there something bigger happening there"],[183,555.05,1.94,"beyond just the way things are packaged?"],[183,556.99,2.74,"JMR: I think that there is definitely a messaging problem."],[183,559.72,3.3,"And I would probably say it's because of one specific thing."],[183,563.03,2.44,"It's often considered an afterthought."],[183,565.46,1.97,"You know, there are these meaningless --"],[183,567.43,3.0,"I say meaningless now -- sentences that we say in public health."],[183,570.47,2.04,"\"Vaccines work, vaccines save lives.\""],[183,572.54,2.8,"I don't even know if I want to say those sentences anymore"],[183,575.37,2.74,"because it’s really not a vaccine -- it’s a vaccination."],[183,578.14,3.5,"And that's just the word nerd in me making the distinction."],[183,581.68,3.5,"But if you think about the difference between a vaccine and a vaccination,"],[183,585.22,1.33,"what connects those things?"],[183,586.58,3.8,"A message, a trusted message from a trusted messenger."],[183,590.42,3.1,"And so how do we turn those vaccines into vaccinations?"],[183,593.56,1.33,"It's communicating."],[183,594.92,3.34,"It's making sure that the data from those clinical trials makes sense."],[183,598.29,3.57,"It's making sure that the process of getting to these interventions"],[183,601.87,3.17,"is transparent and clear, and without conflicts of interest,"],[183,605.03,3.14,"and without all the things that are constantly scrutinized."],[183,608.21,1.53,"And so how do I do that?"],[183,609.74,2.44,"I translate. I take my time."],[183,612.21,3.5,"It's laborious, but there's not a lot of investment in this."],[183,615.75,5.14,"We spend billions of dollars on the R&D, necessary billions, truly,"],[183,620.92,2.4,"to get these lifesaving interventions."],[183,623.32,3.94,"But then we just think that the messages are going to exist alongside [them]."],[183,627.29,3.17,"And it takes a special kind of training and a special kind of mind"],[183,630.49,3.57,"to know that it's not going to just be accepted,"],[183,634.1,2.64,"and that we are dealing with a very diverse world"],[183,636.77,4.04,"with a very, very, you know, difficult experience."],[183,640.84,3.07,"Now behind anybody who’s lived in the last five years,"],[183,643.91,1.7,"trust should not be assumed."],[183,645.64,4.34,"Trust is a determinant of people's decision making and their health."],[183,650.01,3.14,"So if we're not investing in communications,"],[183,653.18,2.27,"then we're just going to be creating these tools"],[183,655.45,1.9,"that will collect dust and not be used."],[183,657.39,1.94,"WPR: It seems like trust --"],[183,659.32,3.5,"definitely in your space, but I think in so many areas --"],[183,662.83,3.37,"we're seeing that with the rise of misinformation,"],[183,666.2,5.37,"that there is this sort of fracture in the way people trust public figures,"],[183,671.57,3.7,"they trust voices that previously wouldn't have been trusted."],[183,675.3,3.0,"How do you tackle rebuilding that"],[183,678.34,3.0,"and repairing what might have been damaged?"],[183,681.38,2.6,"JMR: Yeah, it's a long game."],[183,684.01,4.4,"You know, I think it takes a lot less time to break trust"],[183,688.45,3.07,"than it does to build it and to repair it."],[183,691.55,2.6,"I know the work of repair as a mom."],[183,694.16,2.87,"I'm constantly doing that and modeling it with my children."],[183,697.03,2.1,"And I keep thinking about the bigger picture"],[183,699.16,2.1,"of, you know, when there's something broken,"],[183,701.3,1.64,"the time it takes to mend that."],[183,702.97,5.97,"There's a lot that needs to be done to recognize the deep hurt and trauma"],[183,708.97,1.6,"of the last five years."],[183,710.57,2.33,"And I think that that's where we look at science"],[183,712.91,2.64,"as needing to be a lot more multidisciplinary."],[183,715.58,3.9,"It's not just the infectious disease people at the helm, right?"],[183,719.52,3.14,"It needs to be the social scientists and the psychologists,"],[183,722.65,2.57,"and the therapists even,"],[183,725.25,3.97,"the politicians and the strategists all kind of together thinking about"],[183,729.26,3.2,"what does this road to regaining trust look like."],[183,732.5,3.14,"And what keeps me up a little bit at night"],[183,735.66,2.74,"is knowing that myself and the scientific community"],[183,738.4,2.1,"[are] not the only one aware of that."],[183,740.54,3.27,"You know, we're seeing like a tug of war over trust,"],[183,743.84,4.17,"and words are losing their meaning, including the word \"trust.\""],[183,748.04,1.44,"Like what is trusted?"],[183,749.51,1.37,"What is “evidence-based?”"],[183,750.91,2.13,"What is “gold standard?”"],[183,753.08,2.9,"And so part of the work that I feel really compelled to do"],[183,755.99,2.67,"is on exposing,"],[183,758.65,2.27,"you know, with deep transparency,"],[183,760.96,2.4,"all of the processes that go behind science."],[183,763.36,2.17,"I mean, “exposing” is maybe an intense word,"],[183,765.53,4.67,"but I say that because people have felt left out of the process"],[183,770.2,3.47,"and just expected to follow suit, to just obey."],[183,773.7,2.1,"And I don't think that that's the right way."],[183,775.84,2.84,"I do think that there is a place for expertise,"],[183,778.71,2.6,"and experts should definitely be influencing"],[183,781.34,2.94,"a lot of these decisions and recommendations."],[183,784.28,1.77,"But when it comes to repair,"],[183,786.08,4.61,"a lot of it is going to go back to the basics of saying, \"I don't know.\""],[183,790.72,1.27,"\"I don't know yet.\""],[183,791.99,2.7,"And that makes a lot of scientists squirm."],[183,794.72,3.04,"You know, the lack of certainty is not a comfortable place."],[183,797.79,1.47,"But if you think about it,"],[183,799.3,3.24,"the lack of certainty is why we all are scientists, right?"],[183,802.53,3.24,"It's me wanting to find an answer to an unknown,"],[183,805.8,4.37,"or to something that I don't fully know is connected to something else."],[183,810.21,2.9,"So how do we go back to the basics of the scientific method,"],[183,813.11,4.37,"of a hypothesis, or of a process of getting to that conclusion"],[183,817.51,4.77,"and not coming in with so much certainty and binary thinking?"],[183,822.28,3.3,"WPR: I think when you think about young scientists"],[183,825.62,1.27,"who are coming up,"],[183,826.92,1.97,"and I think -- I'll even take a step back"],[183,828.92,3.34,"because so much of what you're saying is so relevant to others as well."],[183,832.29,1.8,"But when you think about young people"],[183,834.13,2.44,"who are coming up and looking to communicate ideas,"],[183,836.6,3.8,"what makes you feel good about where things might be headed"],[183,840.4,2.07,"and the direction that they might be taking"],[183,842.5,1.74,"and how they approach these things?"],[183,844.24,2.2,"JMR: I mean, the role of science communicator"],[183,846.44,3.24,"was not something that people were familiar with many years ago."],[183,849.71,3.6,"And even the word \"infodemic\" or \"infodemiology\" was a word"],[183,853.35,2.54,"that was not in a lot of people's lexicon."],[183,855.92,1.27,"And it now is."],[183,857.22,1.27,"And that excites me,"],[183,858.49,2.07,"because I do think that what we need to do"],[183,860.59,3.27,"is train a generation of scientists who can communicate that science,"],[183,863.86,4.84,"because their work is incomplete if it's just sitting behind closed doors"],[183,868.7,1.5,"at fancy institutions."],[183,870.2,3.14,"It's not actually a tool in somebody's toolkit"],[183,873.37,3.57,"unless somebody can repeat that message to somebody sitting next to them"],[183,876.97,1.3,"at the dinner table."],[183,878.31,2.87,"And that's why I feel so passionate about the work that I do,"],[183,881.21,5.5,"that I somehow became a personal scientist to so many people"],[183,886.75,3.17,"who then could refer to me by first name and say,"],[183,889.95,3.04,"\"Well, I heard this from Jessica and she explained it this way,\""],[183,892.99,3.0,"these repeatable messages, this generation, hopefully,"],[183,895.99,4.24,"of creating science communicators in the whole community."],[183,900.26,2.34,"And not everybody has to have the same training,"],[183,902.63,4.2,"but that they can repeat these messages so that everybody can understand,"],[183,906.87,3.7,"that, to me, makes me feel hopeful about the future,"],[183,910.57,4.3,"despite the fraught information ecosystem that we're living in."],[183,914.91,1.47,"WPR: I think that's beautiful."],[183,916.41,2.07,"And when you have these conversations"],[183,918.51,3.9,"with people who maybe feel concerned about how they can break through"],[183,922.45,1.8,"and make a difference,"],[183,924.29,2.74,"what are some of the ways in which you encourage them"],[183,927.06,2.54,"to keep going and to do this work?"],[183,929.63,2.34,"Yeah. The good outweighs the bad."],[183,932.0,1.53,"I have to keep reminding myself"],[183,933.53,4.0,"that, yes, there's violence and death threats and antagonism"],[183,937.53,2.64,"and a lot of scary things."],[183,940.2,2.64,"Oftentimes, we're dealing with keyboard warriors"],[183,942.87,3.9,"and people who maybe are not as brave as they may sound on the internet."],[183,946.78,2.0,"I'm not at all downplaying real threats,"],[183,948.78,4.17,"but I have to keep reminding myself that at the end of the day,"],[183,952.95,2.87,"we have evidence and data to back up the things that we say,"],[183,955.82,1.83,"with that caveat."],[183,957.65,3.07,"That's different than what you're dealing with on the other side."],[183,960.76,3.54,"And I think speaking that truth courageously, repeatedly"],[183,964.33,5.27,"has shown to be beneficial because of what we see."],[183,969.63,1.9,"What's interesting about public health"],[183,971.53,2.44,"is a lot of times what we see is what we don't see."],[183,974.0,2.47,"The interventions are invisible in that sense,"],[183,976.51,2.07,"and they're a victim of their own success."],[183,978.58,4.27,"Because when people are doing well, public health is working,"],[183,982.88,1.27,"and that's what we want."],[183,984.18,2.97,"We don't want people dying in mass and getting sick in mass."],[183,987.18,2.34,"And so how do we make that invisible work visible?"],[183,989.55,3.0,"It's by this type of echoing and communicating"],[183,992.59,2.6,"and inviting more people into that process."],[183,995.23,4.8,"Because if we just assume that it exists and we're not talking about it,"],[183,1000.03,3.4,"that’s when the defunding happens. That’s when the devaluation happens."],[183,1003.47,1.27,"That's when"],[183,1004.73,1.74,"the deprioritization of it happens."],[183,1006.5,1.47,"And that's kind of what got us"],[183,1008.0,2.2,"into the terrible position we were in in 2020."],[183,1010.87,2.97,"WPR: I'd love to know as we're winding down our conversation,"],[183,1013.88,2.5,"just how this -- what this looks like for you."],[183,1016.41,2.47,"How does this show up in practice for you?"],[183,1018.91,4.61,"How do you find the courage to keep going and keep doing this work?"],[183,1023.55,3.04,"JMR: Yeah, I think about all the hats that I wear."],[183,1026.62,2.87,"I think about the fact that I'm not just an epidemiologist"],[183,1029.49,1.3,"and a science communicator."],[183,1030.83,1.47,"I'm a mom."],[183,1032.33,2.07,"I'm a first-generation American."],[183,1034.43,2.94,"I carry all these identities with me."],[183,1037.37,3.64,"And I think that's why I am trusted by some folks."],[183,1041.04,5.57,"And I think a lot about the times that I've had to speak up,"],[183,1046.61,2.2,"when it has felt scary to speak up,"],[183,1048.85,3.7,"whether it's in opposition to an administration"],[183,1052.58,1.77,"or to a specific politician,"],[183,1054.38,5.27,"or to a specific person who is making very dangerous, harmful claims."],[183,1059.66,3.6,"My work requires me to see a lot of dark things."],[183,1063.29,2.8,"You know, the dismantling of public health systems,"],[183,1066.13,2.9,"really dangerous health misinformation."],[183,1069.03,2.6,"Even what we're witnessing over the world."],[183,1071.67,3.07,"You know, I very much have spoken out a lot"],[183,1074.74,1.77,"about what's been happening in Gaza,"],[183,1076.54,3.3,"as it is connected to public health, it is connected to children."],[183,1079.88,3.57,"I am a mom with kids, very young."],[183,1083.45,1.53,"All these things are connected,"],[183,1085.01,2.34,"and I speak about these things in a connected way,"],[183,1087.38,3.44,"because I remember a few words that really ground me."],[183,1091.89,1.7,"And this was really reinforced,"],[183,1093.62,2.47,"most recently, [when] I was in Rwanda with UNICEF"],[183,1096.13,1.77,"visiting a field site."],[183,1097.93,3.7,"Rwanda is just 30 years past the genocide."],[183,1101.66,2.64,"Something that I witnessed 20 years ago,"],[183,1104.33,2.8,"when I was there, 10 years after the genocide,"],[183,1107.17,3.6,"and I remember thinking, how can there be hope here?"],[183,1110.81,2.04,"How can there be repair?"],[183,1112.88,4.47,"And I saw it, and I remembered the question that I asked myself,"],[183,1117.38,1.83,"like, why do I do what I do?"],[183,1119.25,1.74,"What is the thing that I'm good at?"],[183,1121.02,2.7,"And I really, genuinely think I'm good at sense making."],[183,1123.75,3.47,"I'm good at making hard, complicated, uncomfortable things"],[183,1127.26,3.27,"make sense to different types of people."],[183,1130.53,1.6,"And so because of that,"],[183,1132.16,1.77,"it gives me my why,"],[183,1133.96,4.44,"and my why is that speaking up about science,"],[183,1138.4,4.27,"about injustice, about the connection of those two things"],[183,1142.7,1.57,"is always right."],[183,1144.31,2.1,"It's always worth it."],[183,1146.41,4.24,"And so that is how I keep reminding myself of the work that I do,"],[183,1150.68,4.0,"the why that I do it because it's really important to me."],[183,1154.72,3.44,"And I've seen the fruitfulness of it all."],[183,1158.15,1.33,"WPR: That's beautiful."],[183,1159.49,5.44,"And it feels like that's something that maybe all of us can practice."],[183,1164.96,3.44,"Thank you so much for this conversation and for being here with us today."],[183,1168.43,2.04,"JMR: Thank you, Whitney. Thanks, everyone."],[184,0.08,2.32,"If you’ve traveled enough, you’ve inevitably"],[184,2.4,2.4,"been hit by every vacationer's nightmare:"],[184,4.8,3.6,"a cancelled flight. It sucks, but it could be a lot worse."],[184,8.4,2.88,"Like, what if I couldn’t be here in Montana because the"],[184,11.28,2.24,"plane scheduled to bring me here from Boston"],[184,13.52,3.04,"got snowed in at an airport in New Zealand, two days earlier?"],[184,16.56,2.64,"Delays and cancellations are always happening"],[184,19.2,3.52,"somewhere in the world, but through storms, volcanic eruptions,"],[184,22.72,3.52,"and outbreaks of war, airlines are usually able to keep planes"],[184,26.24,2.72,"in the skies and passengers relatively on schedule."],[184,28.96,4.4,"For that reliability, we can thank networks. Not computer networks."],[184,33.36,3.12,"I mean the mathematical kind. And while well-planned networks"],[184,36.48,3.52,"make modern air travel possible, it only takes disruptions in"],[184,40.0,5.0,"a few key places to bring everything to a grinding halt."],[184,46.96,3.84,"The simplest way to imagine an airport network is as a series of points"],[184,50.8,4.08,"joined by lines. Mathematicians call this kind of picture a graph, which,"],[184,54.88,3.6,"in this context, means something different than “a data visualization”."],[184,58.48,3.04,"In math, a graph is a model of how systems like the"],[184,61.52,2.56,"air traffic industry are fundamentally structured."],[184,64.08,3.12,"There are nodes, the points that represent each airport,"],[184,67.2,4.16,"connected by edges, the lines that represent flights between airports."],[184,71.36,2.24,"And when the graph represents something tangible,"],[184,73.6,2.96,"like airports, they’re sometimes called networks,"],[184,76.56,1.84,"and we study them with network theory."],[184,78.4,3.36,"Now you could plot the graph on a map or a grid, however you"],[184,81.76,3.6,"want really. Mathematicians are only interested in the connections."],[184,85.36,2.72,"This might feel like an odd game of connect-the-dots,"],[184,88.08,3.28,"but network theory lets us do things like organize flight patterns"],[184,91.36,3.04,"that withstand interruptions, determine which layovers"],[184,94.4,3.2,"are most common, and understand airline pricing methods."],[184,97.6,2.48,"To do that we need to create the graph."],[184,100.08,3.2,"We could try making a complete graph, meaning that every"],[184,103.28,3.6,"airport in the whole world has a direct flight to every other airport."],[184,106.88,2.08,"In graph language, that means every node"],[184,108.96,2.08,"shares an edge with every other node."],[184,111.04,2.96,"Never needing a layover sounds pretty sweet."],[184,114.0,2.72,"The other perk to this setup is that if one airport"],[184,116.72,3.2,"drops out of the network, any other airport can pick"],[184,119.92,2.56,"up the slack and get you to your destination."],[184,122.48,3.92,"But since there are about 4,000 airports in the system,"],[184,126.4,2.08,"the cost of those conveniences would be"],[184,128.48,3.28,"running more than 16 million flights globally."],[184,131.76,2.08,"Which is why real airports, sadly,"],[184,133.84,2.0,"don’t have direct flights to everywhere."],[184,135.84,3.28,"It doesn’t make economic or environmental sense to allocate"],[184,139.12,3.44,"tons of resources to the tiny percentage of people wanting to"],[184,142.56,3.92,"fly directly from the Caribbean island of Saba to, say, Nepal."],[184,146.48,3.92,"In reality, there are only about 70,000 different direct flights in the"],[184,150.4,3.28,"world, less than one percent of what a complete network would have."],[184,153.68,3.28,"So here’s the challenge for network theorists: how do you assign"],[184,156.96,3.68,"those routes to get people from A to B in the best way possible?"],[184,160.64,1.84,"We need a network that allows people to get"],[184,162.48,2.16,"where they’re going, even without a direct flight."],[184,164.64,3.92,"Paths are the solution, meaning any route between airports, including"],[184,168.56,3.76,"routes that go through additional nodes. In other words, a layover."],[184,172.32,2.24,"Mathematicians call the idea of being able"],[184,174.56,2.72,"to get between any two places connectedness."],[184,177.28,2.96,"One way to calculate the connectedness of a graph is to find the"],[184,180.24,2.24,"largest set of nodes that always has"],[184,182.48,2.24,"some kind of path between any two of them."],[184,184.72,2.96,"Ideally, every airport in the world would be connected to"],[184,187.68,2.48,"the global airport network, so that you have a chance"],[184,190.16,2.48,"to get there, no matter how many layovers it takes."],[184,192.64,2.56,"But there are lots of ways to achieve connectedness."],[184,195.2,2.64,"For instance, say you want a path from Indianapolis,"],[184,197.84,4.72,"in to Missoula, Montana. You could fly from Indianapolis to Portland,"],[184,202.56,3.84,"to Huntsville, to Bismarck and finally to Missoula. Easy peasy!"],[184,206.4,2.08,"Just kidding, we all know that sounds wacky."],[184,208.48,2.64,"Because in practice, closeness also matters."],[184,211.12,2.56,"Closeness is what we call the smallest number of"],[184,213.68,3.12,"individual flights you take to get from one place to another."],[184,216.8,2.32,"If it takes one layover to get from Indianapolis"],[184,219.12,2.24,"to Missoula, that’s a closeness of two."],[184,221.36,4.32,"In our fantasy world of a complete airport network, every flight is direct,"],[184,225.68,3.28,"so every path would have a closeness of one. But in the real world,"],[184,228.96,2.8,"airlines have the challenge of balancing connectedness and"],[184,231.76,3.28,"closeness with their available resources and consumer demand."],[184,235.04,2.8,"The practical solution is a handful of big airports"],[184,237.84,3.2,"whose main job is to connect to a lot of different locations."],[184,241.04,2.8,"Most routes from Indianapolis to Missoula would pass through"],[184,243.84,3.44,"Chicago or Salt Lake City because these big airports have a"],[184,247.28,3.44,"high degree, meaning they’re connected to many other nodes."],[184,250.72,1.6,"Especially compared to an airport like"],[184,252.32,2.48,"Missoula where they have, like, four flights a day."],[184,254.8,3.76,"I kid. I kid. No shade to MSO, I actually love it here."],[184,258.56,4.0,"I love your short lines, I love your eclectic decor, the big bear…"],[184,262.56,2.24,"it’s great! And there are more than four flights a day,"],[184,264.8,1.2,"there are four whole gates now!"],[184,266.0,3.36,"Network analysis shows that carefully implementing high degree"],[184,269.36,3.44,"airports really does a better job of creating shorter flight paths."],[184,272.8,4.56,"In a 2007 paper published in the journal Chaos, Solitons and Fractals,"],[184,277.36,2.72,"a pair of Italian researchers found that real life"],[184,280.08,2.96,"closeness in the Italian airport network was about two."],[184,283.04,2.24,"That means, based on the shape of the network,"],[184,285.28,4.32,"getting between any two airports in Italy typically required one layover."],[184,289.6,2.0,"If the airports were randomly connected,"],[184,291.6,2.24,"the closeness factor would be closer to four."],[184,293.84,3.52,"Without high-degree airports, like Fiumicino in Rome,"],[184,297.36,1.68,"you’d typically need two or three"],[184,299.04,2.08,"layovers to travel elsewhere within Italy!"],[184,301.12,2.08,"Speaking of layovers, we also need"],[184,303.2,2.88,"to take a quick break for this ad."],[184,306.08,2.16,"Thanks to JMP Statistical Discovery"],[184,308.24,2.0,"for supporting this SciShow video!"],[184,310.24,3.6,"JMP helps scientists turn messy, complex data into"],[184,313.84,2.88,"something they can visually explore and understand."],[184,316.72,3.12,"With JMP, you can turn a jumble of numbers into"],[184,319.84,3.6,"patterns, relationships, and outliers in seconds."],[184,323.44,2.72,"You don’t need to be an expert statistician to get started."],[184,326.16,2.48,"You can build these skills with JMP’s free,"],[184,328.64,2.88,"on-demand workshop that lets you get hands-on"],[184,331.52,2.88,"with data visualisation and exploratory analysis."],[184,334.4,3.92,"It’s designed for scientists, engineers, and curious learners alike."],[184,338.32,2.8,"When you feel like the science you’re interacting with isn’t short"],[184,341.12,4.72,"on data, but on clarity, JMP can help rebalance those scales."],[184,345.84,3.12,"Just download the free trial and sample dataset to start"],[184,348.96,4.08,"your journey into data visualisation and exploratory analysis."],[184,353.04,3.12,"It’s all in the link in the description."],[184,356.16,2.48,"Even balancing connectedness and closeness,"],[184,358.64,1.84,"we’re still missing some logistics."],[184,360.48,2.88,"Like the duration of each flight, and the demand for a given route."],[184,363.36,2.96,"So to make our graph even more sophisticated, we can attach"],[184,366.32,3.6,"numbers to each edge in our network, creating a weighted graph."],[184,369.92,1.76,"Weighted graphs help us determine which"],[184,371.68,1.84,"airports should be the high degree ones."],[184,373.52,3.12,"Taking the weights into account, you end up with what’s called a"],[184,376.64,3.2,"hub-and-spoke network that pretty successfully balances"],[184,379.84,2.48,"closeness and connectedness with consumer demand."],[184,382.32,2.24,"The airports with the highest overall demand are"],[184,384.56,3.12,"high-degree hubs that connect to lower-degree airports"],[184,387.68,1.84,"called spokes, which have lower demand."],[184,389.52,2.8,"When you draw the network, it sort of resembles a wheel,"],[184,392.32,0.64,"hence the name."],[184,392.96,4.24,"In 2013, Italian and Spanish researchers found that airlines actually"],[184,397.2,2.64,"select their network shape based on their business model."],[184,399.84,3.36,"For example, the European airline Lufthansa is arranged in the"],[184,403.2,2.4,"hub-and-spoke model, like other major airlines,"],[184,405.6,2.56,"to balance both closeness and connectedness."],[184,408.16,3.52,"Connecting one additional spoke to the hub instantly increases the"],[184,411.68,2.88,"connectedness in the network without adding more layovers on"],[184,414.56,2.72,"average, because most routes will take you through the hub anyway."],[184,417.28,2.64,"This lets airlines serve lots of customers"],[184,419.92,1.92,"coming from a lot of different places."],[184,421.84,2.56,"If you’re going to Singapore, it doesn’t matter much whether"],[184,424.4,2.96,"you’re coming from Lisbon or Warsaw, because the airline"],[184,427.36,3.36,"can bundle you all into the same flight using a layover at Frankfurt."],[184,430.72,3.12,"Aggregating routes in this way creates more consistent schedules"],[184,433.84,2.88,"for anyone at the spokes to get to their final destination."],[184,436.72,3.12,"Meanwhile, the budget airline Ryanair had a much more"],[184,439.84,3.52,"random-looking network, driven by the individual demand between"],[184,443.36,3.36,"any two airports, rather than a high degree of connectedness."],[184,446.72,3.6,"These flights tend to be direct, but focus on smaller airports"],[184,450.32,2.64,"and smaller fleets, which keeps the costs low."],[184,452.96,3.12,"You can’t fly to as many places on Ryanair’s network,"],[184,456.08,3.2,"but budget airlines like this help fill in network gaps where"],[184,459.28,3.2,"the demand is usually low, meaning they can offer lower prices."],[184,462.48,3.04,"So even though we’ve been talking about the overall global airport"],[184,465.52,3.76,"network, it’s actually divided into subnetworks of different airlines,"],[184,469.28,3.52,"each optimizing their own networks for their specific business goals."],[184,472.8,2.88,"And for airlines that care a lot about reliability,"],[184,475.68,2.56,"they also need to account for robustness."],[184,478.24,2.88,"If you’re a business flyer who has a very important meeting"],[184,481.12,3.52,"or video shoot to get to, you’ll probably book an airline"],[184,484.64,2.96,"with a lot of flight options that’s known for being reliable."],[184,487.6,2.72,"But if you’re trying to vacation on a shoestring budget,"],[184,490.32,2.64,"you might take a risk with a budget airline that has fewer"],[184,492.96,2.56,"rebooking options on the chance you miss your flight."],[184,495.52,3.2,"Robustness measures how much the connectedness of the network"],[184,498.72,3.04,"is affected when any given node gets taken out of the system."],[184,501.76,4.0,"For example, the route from Indianapolis to Missoula is robust"],[184,505.76,4.08,"because if a nearby wildfire interrupts your layover at Salt Lake City,"],[184,509.84,1.68,"you can still make it to your SciShow"],[184,511.52,1.92,"shoot by taking a connection through Denver."],[184,513.44,3.04,"Hub-and-spoke models tend to do really well in situations like"],[184,516.48,3.2,"these because most of the airports tend to be low-degree,"],[184,519.68,3.28,"rather than high-degree. If a completely random airport is hit"],[184,522.96,2.56,"by something like a storm or a power outage,"],[184,525.52,2.8,"the odds of it being one of the hub airports is fairly low."],[184,528.32,3.12,"That means fewer people overall end up with disrupted flights,"],[184,531.44,2.56,"because most of the connectedness is still preserved."],[184,534.0,3.36,"A study from 2014 in the journal Transportation Research"],[184,537.36,3.28,"modelled a global network of 3,700 airports."],[184,540.64,4.08,"They found that even removing 13 percent of airports at random only"],[184,544.72,3.28,"reduced the connectedness of the whole network by 22 percent."],[184,548.0,3.44,"There would still be over 3,000 locations you could travel between"],[184,551.44,3.44,"globally if more than one in ten airports were randomly shut down."],[184,554.88,3.2,"But that same study also discussed a huge problem"],[184,558.08,2.8,"with hub-and-spoke networks: targeted attacks."],[184,560.88,3.6,"Precisely because they make the network both close and connected,"],[184,564.48,3.28,"hubs are a huge risk if taken out intentionally by something"],[184,567.76,3.28,"like a cyberattack or someone flying drones near the runway."],[184,571.04,3.52,"If just two and a half percent of the most frequent layover"],[184,574.56,2.8,"airports were taken out of action, then the connectivity"],[184,577.36,3.28,"of the global air network system would drop by over half."],[184,580.64,3.12,"And if you knock out 13 percent of airports, like before,"],[184,583.76,3.28,"but this time targeting the most connected hubs, then it would"],[184,587.04,2.72,"pretty much bring the whole air travel system to a halt."],[184,589.76,1.52,"The connectivity of the network would"],[184,591.28,3.76,"drop from 3,700 airports to just 40."],[184,595.04,3.68,"In other words, the same property that makes global air travel resilient"],[184,598.72,3.76,"to random, low-level problems makes it brittle in extreme events."],[184,602.48,4.56,"For instance, when this infamously difficult to pronounce Icelandic volcano–"],[184,607.04,1.68,"believe me, I tried–"],[184,608.72,4.0,"erupted in 2010, the volcanic ash cloud shut down several"],[184,612.72,3.68,"major hub airports in Europe, leaving 8 million passengers"],[184,616.4,3.76,"stranded and creating nearly 3 billion Euros in losses."],[184,620.16,3.12,"But thankfully, network theory tools can help airlines"],[184,623.28,2.8,"proactively minimize the effects of events like these!"],[184,626.08,5.2,"A 2021 simulation by a European Air Safety organization found that"],[184,631.28,3.6,"since the original eruption, airlines have improved their coordination."],[184,634.88,4.48,"If an event like that icelandic volcano happened today, its negative"],[184,639.36,4.0,"impacts would be cut in half compared to the disruption in 2010."],[184,643.36,3.84,"Networks pop up a lot of places in nature and engineering,"],[184,647.2,3.76,"from cybersecurity to materials science to power grid design."],[184,650.96,3.36,"And the math of network theory is helping scientists plan against"],[184,654.32,9.44,"disasters or design new materials, all by “connecting the dots”."],[185,7.17,4.75,"In 1772, amateur sumo wrestlers in present-day Niigata"],[185,12.13,2.92,"were performing to raise money for a local temple"],[185,15.34,3.34,"when a pair of sumo pros crashed their event."],[185,19.47,2.71,"These wrestlers from the Tokyo sumo guild"],[185,22.18,4.55,"claimed that only their guild had the right to charge audiences admission."],[185,27.19,3.29,"But when the pros tried making off with the ticket money,"],[185,30.48,3.75,"one was killed and the other barely escaped alive."],[185,35.32,3.34,"Back in Tokyo, the guild took their case to a magistrate."],[185,38.99,2.5,"They argued that because they were the only guild"],[185,41.5,3.59,"that adhered to sumo’s most “ancient practices,”"],[185,45.33,3.21,"no other performers in the country were legitimate."],[185,49.05,2.29,"The magistrate accepted this claim."],[185,51.46,1.08,"But surprisingly,"],[185,52.55,5.42,"these so-called “ancient practices” were actually recent inventions."],[185,58.6,3.63,"So, what were these fabricated traditions,"],[185,62.23,4.09,"and how do they relate to sumo’s true history?"],[185,67.02,4.3,"Our earliest reliable records of sumo come from the 8th century,"],[185,71.36,4.38,"when performances were held as entertainment for annual court banquets."],[185,75.99,3.59,"During these events, wrestlers were summoned from across Japan"],[185,79.7,2.67,"as a demonstration of the emperor's influence."],[185,82.75,3.92,"Initially, these wrestlers would punch, scratch, and grab their opponents,"],[185,86.67,3.67,"claiming victory by forcing their hands or knees to the ground."],[185,90.75,4.8,"But eventually, matches began focusing on grappling and tripping techniques."],[185,95.84,3.79,"Over time, power shifted from the emperor and his court"],[185,99.72,2.96,"to their hired soldiers, the samurai,"],[185,102.77,3.75,"and official court sumo ended in 1174."],[185,107.1,5.09,"However, samurai leaders still had soldiers perform less formal sumo bouts"],[185,112.19,2.42,"for exercise and entertainment."],[185,114.78,2.75,"Throughout imperial and samurai rule,"],[185,117.53,5.05,"sumo was performed across Japan for sport, spectacle, and martial training."],[185,122.78,3.92,"But in the 1400s, it took on a new function: fundraising."],[185,127.04,2.71,"Shrines and temples began hosting performances"],[185,129.75,2.5,"to raise money for construction and repairs."],[185,132.29,3.0,"And since skilled performers brought in more customers,"],[185,135.3,3.71,"event promoters were eager to enlist the best wrestlers."],[185,139.72,3.25,"This led performers to create the first sumo guilds,"],[185,143.1,4.21,"ensuring steady employment and improving their working conditions."],[185,147.6,3.13,"Once wrestlers could reliably make money through sumo,"],[185,150.81,4.25,"they became eager to perform for as many paying customers as possible."],[185,155.36,4.21,"Enterprising young men put on rowdy street corner sumo performances,"],[185,159.57,2.54,"hoping to attract the donations of passersby."],[185,162.57,2.79,"Lacking the organization of formal performances,"],[185,165.45,3.21,"these bouts often resulted in fights and injuries"],[185,168.66,2.75,"among both participants and spectators."],[185,171.67,3.8,"So it didn’t take long for samurai authorities to crack down,"],[185,175.46,4.46,"periodically banning all forms of sumo in larger cities."],[185,179.93,5.05,"Wrestlers and organizers knew they needed to make changes to overcome these bans."],[185,185.06,4.55,"First, they established a boundary to separate spectators from participants."],[185,190.06,1.83,"This boundary took many forms,"],[185,191.9,5.05,"but over the 17th century it morphed into a standard ring called the dohyō."],[185,197.28,4.46,"This ring outlined the field of play, introducing a pivotal new rule:"],[185,202.03,5.84,"now matches could also be won by pushing one’s opponent out of the dohyō."],[185,208.29,3.04,"This innovation increased the advantage of heavier wrestlers,"],[185,211.41,3.46,"whose weight already helped them topple their opponents."],[185,215.29,3.96,"But sumo’s journey to become a respectable sport really took off"],[185,219.26,3.17,"when promoters began introducing rituals"],[185,222.43,3.8,"to associate sumo with Japan’s ancient past."],[185,226.68,2.0,"Wrestlers threw salt in the ring"],[185,228.68,3.04,"and rinsed their mouths with water before matches—"],[185,231.85,3.17,"actions that evoked ancient religious ceremonies."],[185,235.56,4.34,"These invented traditions imbued the sport with cultural legitimacy,"],[185,240.07,3.75,"convincing authorities that sumo was above board."],[185,244.24,3.67,"One notable ritual emerged in 1789,"],[185,247.91,3.38,"when the Tokyo guild began having their top-ranked wrestlers"],[185,251.29,5.21,"perform a ring-entering ceremony wearing a sacred rope called the yokozuna."],[185,256.67,2.13,"This innovation became so popular,"],[185,258.8,3.42,"the shōgun himself summoned the troupe for a performance—"],[185,262.51,2.84,"giving legitimacy not just to the Tokyo guild,"],[185,265.51,3.17,"but specifically to their invented traditions."],[185,269.72,3.21,"After the shogunate was overthrown in 1868,"],[185,273.06,4.8,"sumo was seen as a dated relic and briefly fell out of fashion."],[185,278.15,3.42,"But the sport gradually remade itself for the modern era,"],[185,281.57,4.8,"and today, Japan holds six 15-day tournaments every year,"],[185,286.45,3.0,"all of which are broadcast worldwide."],[185,289.79,3.34,"New technology has brought advantages like instant replays,"],[185,293.29,3.59,"while other innovations were introduced to evoke the ancient past."],[185,297.04,1.88,"And over the last century,"],[185,299.05,2.33,"the average body weight in the upper division"],[185,301.38,3.63,"has increased by over 60 kilograms!"],[185,305.59,1.54,"But throughout its changes,"],[185,307.14,4.5,"sumo has sustained its status as a beloved form of entertainment,"],[185,312.06,5.13,"a grand display of strength, and an ode to national traditions—"],[185,317.56,2.34,"both real and invented."],[186,6.75,5.92,"If any of his wives ever birthed a son, he'd be killed immediately."],[186,13.22,5.96,"That was the threat Shemwindo, ruler of the village of Tubondo, had delivered,"],[186,19.35,2.92,"for he wanted all the power to himself."],[186,22.68,2.42,"But he was about to meet his match—"],[186,25.1,5.09,"and enter a rivalry that would take him to the underworld and back."],[186,30.69,2.92,"Six of Shemwindo’s wives had daughters,"],[186,33.78,3.17,"but when Nyamwindo finally went into labor,"],[186,37.12,4.8,"the midwives witnessed a birth unlike any they'd ever seen."],[186,42.29,4.96,"Nyamwindo’s baby, Mwindo, materialized from her belly button,"],[186,47.46,6.3,"wielding a magical conga scepter, already laughing, talking, and walking."],[186,54.38,3.38,"The women agreed to keep the birth a secret."],[186,57.84,5.71,"But a stealthy cricket saw everything and carried the news to Shemwindo."],[186,64.27,3.71,"Outraged, he hurled spears at his newborn,"],[186,68.15,2.29,"but it was like they couldn't touch him."],[186,70.44,5.05,"The tyrannical chief ordered his soldiers to construct a wooden drum,"],[186,75.74,3.8,"wrestle Mwindo into it, and toss it into the river."],[186,79.74,5.17,"From inside, Mwindo cursed his father, vowing to return."],[186,85.21,1.5,"Once he was out of sight,"],[186,86.87,5.34,"Mwindo raised the drum to the water's surface and made for safer shores,"],[186,92.21,4.88,"where his paternal aunt, Iyangura, lived, wedded to a water serpent."],[186,97.34,2.46,"She was far wiser than her brother."],[186,100.1,2.96,"And as Mwindo laid out his plan for revenge,"],[186,103.14,3.13,"she resolved to counsel him on his journey."],[186,106.52,2.46,"But they wouldn’t make the trek alone:"],[186,109.19,4.96,"throughout their quest, they’d be accompanied by different friends and foes."],[186,114.65,4.46,"By the time they reached Tubondo, Shemwindo had already fled."],[186,119.28,6.42,"And though Iyangura advised Mwindo to be merciful, his rage led him instead,"],[186,125.83,3.42,"and he summoned lightning that decimated the village."],[186,129.54,1.67,"In the midst of the chaos,"],[186,131.34,3.84,"an uprooted kikoka plant caught Iyangura’s eye,"],[186,135.46,4.5,"and she realized that her brother had used the plant’s roots"],[186,140.05,2.63,"to escape into the underworld."],[186,143.14,4.21,"Iyangura held fast to one end of a great rope,"],[186,147.39,6.05,"and Mwindo descended into the frigid, subterranean realm after his father."],[186,153.94,5.17,"First, Mwindo reached the lair of Muisa, god of the underworld."],[186,159.28,2.29,"With a glint of cruel mischief,"],[186,161.7,3.67,"Muisa said he’d reveal Shemwindo’s hiding place—"],[186,165.54,6.05,"only if Mwindo planted thousands of banana trees on an arid mountainside."],[186,171.92,5.46,"Unintimidated, Mwindo bewitched countless axes into tilling the soil."],[186,177.88,4.25,"But before he could celebrate his victory, Muisa retaliated,"],[186,182.3,3.34,"sending an enchanted weapon to pin him down."],[186,186.22,4.0,"In turn, Mwindo ordered his scepter to strike back,"],[186,190.52,2.25,"and Muisa lay defeated."],[186,193.06,3.13,"But Mwindo was no closer to finding his father—"],[186,196.44,4.13,"until Hawk appeared and divulged his whereabouts:"],[186,200.78,5.34,"a dark cave guarded by Ntumba, a fearsome aardvark."],[186,206.49,4.88,"But before Mwindo defeated the beast, Shemwindo fled again,"],[186,211.5,4.21,"and the chase persisted through the depths of the underworld."],[186,216.29,3.67,"Soon, Sheburungu, the supreme god of fire,"],[186,220.05,4.63,"materialized before Mwindo and challenged him to a betting game."],[186,225.3,3.34,"Round after round, Mwindo gambled everything—"],[186,228.77,4.3,"Tubondo’s land, people, and property— and lost."],[186,233.9,2.92,"When he thought he had nothing left to wager,"],[186,237.07,3.84,"Mwindo realized these things weren't his to give."],[186,241.32,4.63,"The next round, he pledged his own cherished conga scepter."],[186,246.32,5.3,"And it was this sacrifice that turned his luck and won him the game."],[186,252.08,3.88,"In the same moment, Hawk and Sparrow sounded the alarm,"],[186,256.13,4.09,"warning Mwindo that his father was attempting to escape again."],[186,260.51,4.17,"But this time, Mwindo managed to capture the runaway ruler."],[186,265.05,4.84,"And yet, while Mwindo had thought that seeing his father begging for mercy"],[186,269.89,1.17,"would delight him,"],[186,271.06,3.42,"his thirst for revenge had all but evaporated."],[186,274.94,2.17,"He remembered his aunt's advice,"],[186,277.11,4.88,"and all the people of Tubondo who’d long endured his father’s cruelty,"],[186,282.24,1.46,"then his own."],[186,284.32,4.96,"Shemwindo in tow, Mwindo retraced his path with a new resolve."],[186,289.58,4.88,"First he yielded all that Sheburungu lost in their gambling game,"],[186,294.5,2.63,"receiving his blessing in return."],[186,297.42,5.38,"Then, upon seeing that Ntumba had reconstructed the cave he destroyed,"],[186,302.92,5.0,"Mwindo offered his sympathy and earned Ntumba’s well wishes too."],[186,308.14,4.8,"Mwindo and his father reemerged from the kikoka root transformed."],[186,313.18,4.46,"Mwindo had won the gods’ respect and Shemwindo’s atonement."],[186,317.94,4.59,"With his aunt's guidance, he restored the village and pardoned his father,"],[186,322.74,3.13,"who officially apologized to Tubondo."],[186,326.24,3.63,"And over time, Mwindo gained the people’s trust"],[186,329.87,6.09,"as a kind, just leader who ended age-old cycles of despotism."],[187,6.63,6.38,"When scientists sample the soil, bodies of water, or even the air for DNA,"],[187,13.01,4.3,"they routinely unmask thousands of tiny mysteries—"],[187,17.47,5.96,"unique fragments of fungal DNA that don't match any known species."],[187,23.77,6.71,"This DNA belongs to cryptic organisms, often referred to as “dark fungi,”"],[187,30.65,2.92,"which, like dark matter and dark energy,"],[187,33.74,5.0,"are simultaneously all around us and yet elude discovery."],[187,38.95,6.34,"Scientists estimate that somewhere between 2 and 12 million species of fungi"],[187,45.29,1.58,"remain undiscovered—"],[187,47.0,2.75,"their physical structures yet to be seen,"],[187,49.88,4.55,"either because they’re microscopic, live hidden within other species,"],[187,54.55,2.75,"or for other unknown reasons."],[187,57.93,2.0,"The diverse fungal kingdom,"],[187,59.93,4.04,"which includes everything from baker's yeast to edible mushrooms"],[187,63.98,1.75,"to foot infections,"],[187,65.73,2.29,"has long defied assumptions."],[187,68.19,5.0,"For centuries, biologists mistakenly classified them as plants,"],[187,73.32,2.5,"for what they’d argue were good reasons:"],[187,76.03,4.34,"many grow from the soil atop branching, root-like networks."],[187,80.66,3.09,"But at the biological level, they're very different."],[187,84.04,3.04,"Unlike plants that harness energy from the Sun,"],[187,87.21,6.38,"fungi eat by decomposing matter or siphoning nutrients from other organisms."],[187,94.09,4.59,"Rather than cellulose, their cell walls are made of chitin,"],[187,98.76,4.75,"the same material in exoskeletons of insects, like beetles,"],[187,103.52,2.13,"and crustaceans, like shrimps."],[187,106.02,4.84,"Fungi are actually more closely related to animals than plants,"],[187,111.02,2.25,"sharing a common ancestor"],[187,113.28,4.0,"which first appeared over 1 billion years ago."],[187,118.28,5.5,"Over time, fungi evolved to occupy a range of ecological roles,"],[187,123.79,3.34,"including intertwined relationships with plants."],[187,127.5,4.0,"Many plants can't survive without these fungal partners."],[187,131.71,4.04,"Ectomycorrhizal fungi envelop plant roots,"],[187,135.84,4.96,"trading nutrients they forage in the soil, like phosphorus and nitrogen,"],[187,140.84,2.25,"for carbon to fuel their growth."],[187,143.35,3.84,"Another guild, called arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi,"],[187,147.35,6.51,"goes so far as puncturing plant root cells like straws to aid in this exchange."],[187,153.98,6.55,"This relationship enabled plants to colonize land 470 million years ago,"],[187,160.74,5.5,"when nutrients were far more difficult to obtain from the barren, rocky ground."],[187,166.75,5.59,"Other fungi evolved remarkable strategies to defend against, exploit,"],[187,172.34,1.83,"and manipulate their neighbors,"],[187,174.34,3.38,"often by producing an arsenal of compounds"],[187,177.72,4.05,"like antibiotics, toxins, and even psychedelics."],[187,182.01,4.55,"Many mushrooms in the genus Psilocybe produce psilocybin,"],[187,186.81,3.17,"a molecule that binds to serotonin receptors"],[187,189.98,2.46,"in the bodies and brains of animals."],[187,192.73,3.13,"This causes hallucinogenic effects in humans,"],[187,196.15,5.25,"though psilocybin likely evolved to deter hungry slugs or snails."],[187,201.86,4.84,"Clathrus archeri, a cluster of red tentacle-like mushrooms,"],[187,206.7,2.46,"sometimes called “devil’s fingers,”"],[187,209.16,4.84,"emits a sticky, spore-filled slime called gleba."],[187,214.17,1.88,"It smells like rotting flesh,"],[187,216.25,6.05,"attracting nearby flies that feed on it and carry spores away on their bodies."],[187,222.8,2.79,"Other fungi take a more extreme approach."],[187,225.85,3.63,"Many species of Cordyceps infect ants,"],[187,229.47,3.75,"chemically hijacking their nervous systems with toxins"],[187,233.23,4.63,"and connecting directly to muscle fibers to puppeteer their movements."],[187,238.28,2.46,"The infected ants become zombies,"],[187,240.74,5.67,"compelled to climb to high places and eject millions of fungal spores."],[187,247.16,2.92,"Still other fungi are more self-reliant,"],[187,250.16,3.38,"carving out solitary lives in extreme environments,"],[187,253.58,2.29,"like the walls of nuclear wastelands."],[187,256.29,4.21,"Researchers have observed at least 37 species of fungi"],[187,260.51,2.67,"living among the ruins in Chernobyl,"],[187,263.18,3.13,"home to one of the world’s worst nuclear disasters."],[187,266.47,2.13,"Many are rich in melanin,"],[187,268.6,5.59,"a pigment that can absorb harmful radiation to protect fungal cells and DNA."],[187,274.56,3.92,"But some don’t just endure radiation— they eat it."],[187,278.73,3.79,"Studies conducted on the International Space Station revealed"],[187,282.53,6.55,"that species like Cryptococcus neoformans can convert absorbed gamma radiation"],[187,289.08,1.63,"into useful energy,"],[187,290.79,3.96,"establishing a previously unknown metabolic strategy,"],[187,294.87,3.63,"and redefining the perceived limits of life."],[187,299.42,4.42,"The planet-wide consequences of this fleet of fungal functions"],[187,303.92,2.59,"are still making themselves known."],[187,306.55,4.63,"In the Arctic tundra, fungi form a vast underground network,"],[187,311.39,2.63,"helping plants survive the harsh winters,"],[187,314.06,3.21,"while stockpiling carbon from the atmosphere."],[187,317.81,6.46,"Scientists suspect these soils may be particularly rich in rare, dark fungi."],[187,324.65,3.8,"And these fungal networks, along the plants they support,"],[187,328.45,4.0,"may sequester about 1 trillion metric tons of carbon—"],[187,332.62,3.5,"10 times more than the entire Amazon rainforest—"],[187,336.17,4.25,"potentially keeping Earth resilient against climate change."],[187,341.0,3.67,"The fungal world we see is vast and wild."],[187,345.09,5.17,"Who knows what other wonders might be growing just beyond our view."],[188,3.42,5.8,"I heard this woman singing classical music and everything for me changed."],[188,9.21,2.25,"It was that profound."],[188,11.51,6.38,"It was such an incredibly impactful happening,"],[188,18.39,3.09,"that that day I knew that my life changed."],[188,21.48,2.96,"That day, it gave my life a whole direction"],[188,24.44,3.25,"and I thought whatever this is, this is what I want to do."],[188,32.36,3.0,"Hey you! Yes, you."],[188,36.2,2.17,"What's that light inside of you?"],[188,39.37,4.92,"Is it a dream? A beat? A beautiful sound?"],[188,44.71,1.96,"A heartbreaking song?"],[188,47.92,3.38,"Whatever it is, we're here with working musicians"],[188,51.34,6.51,"to help you grow and share that gift with this wild, wonderful world."],[188,60.6,2.25,"My name is Denyce Graves-Montgomery,"],[188,62.85,4.96,"and I have spent more than 45 years on the stage as an opera singer."],[188,68.61,5.51,"Parallel to that, I have all always been in education and have also taught voice."],[188,74.19,3.46,"Even if you're a natural singer and you know how to sing very, very well,"],[188,77.66,2.54,"some kind of training is going to be necessary."],[188,80.2,3.29,"It will involve having some external ears,"],[188,83.5,2.13,"who can listen to your voice and say:"],[188,85.62,3.25,"your instrument is suited in this range."],[188,89.17,4.3,"So in terms of finding your specific voice type,"],[188,93.71,3.04,"you would go to a teacher or to a coach,"],[188,96.76,3.21,"and they would give you different vocal exercises"],[188,99.97,3.42,"up and down the scale to see what's comfortable,"],[188,103.39,4.92,"and where the voice also shines and where the voice is at its most beautiful."],[188,108.69,2.59,"I'm considered a mezzo soprano."],[188,111.27,3.0,"We have the coloratura soprano, which is the highest soprano."],[188,114.28,2.92,"Then you have soprano, and then you've got the mezzo sopranos,"],[188,117.2,1.67,"the altos, the contraltos."],[188,118.86,2.08,"Then you have the tenors, the countertenors,"],[188,120.95,2.63,"the light tenors, dramatic tenors, the heldentenors,"],[188,123.58,3.63,"all the different classifications, baritones, and then the bases, right?"],[188,127.21,4.92,"There's a lot to learn about your voice and what your voice is suited for."],[188,132.25,3.17,"There are some young people who come to me, for example,"],[188,135.42,3.5,"who might start out as mezzos, who might start out as altos,"],[188,138.93,4.63,"and then we discover that they, in fact, have this incredible range."],[188,143.64,2.09,"And that they, you know, are sopranos."],[188,145.72,6.38,"It takes time for the voice itself to ripen and to grow,"],[188,152.11,4.88,"and to be able to get to that point where you're able to cut"],[188,157.03,3.17,"across a really, really big orchestra."],[188,160.2,3.92,"There has to be a certain amount of heft, there has to be a certain amount of steel"],[188,164.37,2.38,"in the sound to be able to reach to the person"],[188,166.75,2.46,"sitting in the top balcony in the last row."],[188,169.21,3.21,"I am a person who has a big, old honking voice, right?"],[188,172.46,3.25,"My forehead and my cheekbones and my nose"],[188,175.71,3.79,"and all of these incredible resonating cavities"],[188,179.51,3.75,"which creates great resonance that's able to cut."],[188,183.26,3.42,"And when you first start studying, there's the technical part,"],[188,186.68,2.59,"but that continues throughout the whole of your life."],[188,189.39,5.46,"The vocal technique of singing is what we call chiaroscuro."],[188,194.86,2.79,"Chiaro, clear, and scuro, which is darker,"],[188,197.65,2.0,"to create a balanced sound."],[188,199.65,1.96,"And to be able to do that,"],[188,202.2,4.25,"you have to be able to come in contact with what your body is able to do,"],[188,206.45,4.21,"and that is being able to use your body as the microphone."],[188,210.79,3.92,"And the most important thing is technique, technique, technique, technique."],[188,214.71,4.21,"And that would be fundamentally the breath and understanding what that is."],[188,218.92,2.79,"And then understanding how to support that in your body,"],[188,221.72,2.96,"and with your diaphragm and in your rib cage and in the back"],[188,224.68,2.33,"and in your lower abdomen support."],[188,227.01,3.54,"Being able to create that wonderful hollow sound,"],[188,230.56,2.25,"where you're able to use those resonators,"],[188,232.81,2.54,"and then the articulators, the tongue and the teeth,"],[188,235.36,1.5,"and all those different things."],[188,238.07,4.59,"If, for example, if you blow up a balloon, you blow the balloon up."],[188,243.03,1.63,"And you fill the balloon with air."],[188,244.66,4.05,"If you don't hold on to the mouth of the balloon,"],[188,248.83,4.09,"all the air will come out and the balloon will fly all over the place."],[188,253.46,1.79,"And with singing, what you want to do"],[188,255.25,2.71,"is make sure that you hold on to the mouth of the balloon."],[188,257.96,5.67,"So you create air and you've got to use that air,"],[188,263.88,2.13,"and then you've got to spend that air,"],[188,266.01,2.54,"but you got to make sure that you don't use it all up."],[188,268.56,3.79,"And so that's the hand on the mouth of the balloon,"],[188,272.35,3.71,"so that you are able to dole that air out evenly."],[188,276.15,4.04,"So you don't let it all go out on the first phrase,"],[188,280.19,5.05,"but you're able to gauge that breath and spend that breath"],[188,285.24,2.0,"throughout the different phrases."],[188,289.08,3.59,"The bottom line, the bottom, bottom, bottom line:"],[188,294.5,2.25,"if it isn't real, we know."],[188,296.79,3.38,"It has to be real, it has to be honest."],[188,300.17,5.3,"That is the thing that is transformative for people when they go to the theater."],[188,305.59,3.92,"People come and they're riveted because they're seeing something"],[188,309.51,3.63,"in real time happen that is authentic."],[188,315.98,3.0,"And that's the thing that changes people's lives."],[188,318.98,2.25,"The truth. The truth."],[189,6.5,4.42,"Of the many, many things we’re not supposed to snack on,"],[189,10.92,4.38,"perhaps the most common forbidden food is silica gel."],[189,15.64,4.42,"This tiny packet pops up in packaging for everything—"],[189,20.06,4.71,"from TVs and guitar cases to sunscreen and snack foods."],[189,24.94,2.79,"So why is silica gel everywhere,"],[189,27.82,3.0,"and what would actually happen if you ate it?"],[189,31.9,4.84,"Found in the Earth’s crust, silica, also called silicon dioxide,"],[189,36.91,3.34,"is typically mined as raw silica sand,"],[189,40.24,4.38,"which is then washed, dried, sifted, and chemically treated."],[189,45.21,4.09,"Once manufacturers ensure each grain is uniform,"],[189,49.3,3.8,"the processed sand gets mixed with sodium carbonate,"],[189,53.3,5.09,"then heated and combined with an acid that links the silica particles"],[189,58.43,2.5,"into a jelly-like matrix."],[189,61.14,3.67,"This matrix gives the material its porous structure,"],[189,64.89,5.8,"which stays stable after being washed and dried into hard, glassy beads."],[189,70.94,5.34,"The final product is chemically identical to the sand you might find on a beach—"],[189,76.36,3.17,"the only difference is that porous structure."],[189,80.12,6.42,"Inside each silica gel bead is a massive microscopic network of tunnels."],[189,87.0,4.84,"This dramatically increases the surface area of each bead,"],[189,91.96,2.42,"with a single gram of silica"],[189,94.38,5.0,"offering over 700 square meters of surface area."],[189,100.18,3.46,"But this space doesn’t stay empty for long."],[189,103.85,3.67,"The silicon dioxide molecules that make up the gel"],[189,107.52,3.5,"are held together by polar covalent bonds,"],[189,111.02,4.34,"which attract other polar substances, including water."],[189,115.78,3.96,"So when silica gel is placed in a humid environment,"],[189,119.74,3.88,"the water in the air is drawn to the beads’ surface."],[189,123.79,1.83,"From these surface pores,"],[189,125.66,3.79,"the water vapor is then pulled inside the grain"],[189,129.46,3.75,"through a process called capillary condensation."],[189,133.42,5.13,"Each grain can hold up to 40% of its own weight in water,"],[189,138.72,5.46,"allowing a standard 5-gram packet to hold roughly 2 grams of liquid."],[189,144.72,4.42,"And since this liquid is held inside the bead’s internal network,"],[189,149.4,4.88,"even fully saturated silica doesn't feel wet to the touch."],[189,155.44,3.92,"This ability makes silica gel a desiccant,"],[189,159.49,3.25,"a type of substance used to keep things dry."],[189,163.28,3.54,"But these beads can suck up more than just water."],[189,167.0,5.59,"Silica attracts ammonia, sulfur dioxide, and a handful of other gases,"],[189,172.59,4.55,"making it useful in gas masks and other filtration systems."],[189,177.38,2.88,"The pace of this process depends on the environment."],[189,180.47,2.67,"In a very humid enclosed space,"],[189,183.22,3.84,"a packet could reach its saturation point within a day or two,"],[189,187.52,4.05,"but this could take up to a week in a moderately humid environment."],[189,192.02,4.42,"Either way, silica gel beads functionally last forever"],[189,196.44,2.75,"since you can dry them out in a hot oven."],[189,200.45,3.96,"These abilities and silica’s prevalence in Earth’s crust"],[189,204.41,3.55,"have made it the planet’s most popular commercial desiccant"],[189,208.08,3.54,"since we started mass producing it in 1919."],[189,211.79,4.04,"At home, it keeps snacks crispy and extends their shelf life,"],[189,216.04,2.63,"ensures pills and vitamins stay dry,"],[189,218.88,4.34,"and captures any stinky scents from your cat’s litter box."],[189,223.68,5.84,"Museums use silica gel in exhibit cases to prevent metal artifacts from rusting"],[189,229.52,2.88,"and preserved animals from growing mold."],[189,232.81,4.84,"And in outer space, a form of silica gel called aerogel"],[189,237.65,4.05,"is used to insulate rovers from extreme temperatures"],[189,241.78,4.55,"and capture high-velocity comet dust for study back home."],[189,247.03,3.88,"But what about what silica gel would do to you"],[189,250.91,2.67,"if you successfully swallowed a packet?"],[189,253.92,2.59,"The answer is... not much."],[189,256.63,5.13,"Those “Do Not Eat” warnings have more to do with packets being choking hazards"],[189,261.76,2.71,"than any silica-specific concerns."],[189,264.76,3.42,"Besides, your body has so much liquid in it—"],[189,268.35,3.8,"even if the packet did suck up the standard 2 grams,"],[189,272.23,4.25,"you could replenish that much and more with a single glass of water."],[189,276.65,2.67,"And since silica is chemically inert,"],[189,279.4,3.5,"your body won’t break the gel down into something toxic."],[189,283.61,5.09,"That said, downing larger quantities of silica gel would be unpleasant."],[189,288.99,5.21,"Its moisture-wicking properties could dry and irritate mucous membranes"],[189,294.21,1.63,"along the digestive tract,"],[189,296.21,3.34,"resulting in diarrhea and an upset stomach."],[189,299.88,4.63,"Worse still, some silica is coated with cobalt chloride"],[189,304.55,1.96,"to indicate moisture levels,"],[189,306.51,3.34,"and that compound actually is toxic."],[189,310.31,3.92,"But assuming your silica snack is of the standard variety,"],[189,314.27,2.0,"you should survive the ordeal."],[190,8.0,3.92,"Imagine a ship struggling in an endless storm."],[190,12.59,4.46,"Its captain, though strong and commanding, is a terrible navigator."],[190,17.35,2.96,"Neither he nor his crew have been trained to sail,"],[190,20.31,2.54,"yet they relentlessly vie for control"],[190,22.85,2.96,"through bribery, flattery, and even mutiny."],[190,26.44,3.0,"In the crow's nest sits a true navigator."],[190,29.69,3.29,"But her knowledge of the stars, winds, and seasons"],[190,33.03,2.63,"is dismissed by the crew below."],[190,36.37,3.63,"This dysfunctional vessel is the Ship of State—"],[190,40.16,2.46,"a thought experiment devised by Plato"],[190,42.62,2.88,"to illustrate his frustration with democracy."],[190,45.67,3.21,"Specifically, the kind of democracy practiced in Athens"],[190,48.88,2.79,"from the 5th to 4th centuries BCE."],[190,52.01,3.67,"Unlike the forms of representative democracy practiced today,"],[190,55.68,4.0,"where voters elect representatives to enact legislation on their behalf,"],[190,59.97,3.92,"Athenians used a type of direct democracy."],[190,64.35,2.21,"While Athenian jurors and most officials"],[190,66.56,2.88,"were selected by lottery rather than election,"],[190,69.48,5.17,"every law was voted on directly by the citizens of Athens."],[190,74.9,4.0,"Specifically, free men born to Athenian parents—"],[190,78.95,4.92,"women, foreigners, and enslaved individuals had no voting power."],[190,84.45,3.59,"Plato thought this system was destined for ruin."],[190,88.08,2.79,"And despite the many differences between Athens’ government"],[190,90.88,2.09,"and modern representative democracies,"],[190,92.96,3.25,"many of his fears still ring true today."],[190,96.93,3.63,"One of Plato’s two major concerns with direct democracy"],[190,100.56,3.42,"was its vulnerability to charismatic public speakers."],[190,104.22,2.63,"Since laws were voted on in the Assembly,"],[190,106.85,4.25,"confident orators could sway public opinion for their own benefit."],[190,111.57,3.96,"In fact, this kind of speech was considered so powerful"],[190,115.78,4.09,"a class of paid tutors called sophists emerged"],[190,119.87,3.75,"to educate wealthy families in the art of persuasion."],[190,124.25,4.84,"As a result, Plato felt democracy rewarded the strongest speakers,"],[190,129.08,4.42,"not those with true political expertise aimed at public welfare."],[190,134.13,3.63,"His second concern was democracy's obsession with freedom."],[190,138.09,1.29,"In the “Republic,”"],[190,139.38,3.67,"he outlined how the “license to do whatever one wants”"],[190,143.14,4.21,"would lead people to prioritize their own desires over the common good."],[190,147.52,4.17,"In the short term, Plato believed this would weaken societal cohesion"],[190,151.69,2.59,"and erode respect for the rule of law."],[190,154.78,1.25,"But in the long term,"],[190,156.24,4.42,"he worried this ever-increasing chaos would lead to a backlash—"],[190,160.87,3.13,"a yearning for order that would make people vulnerable"],[190,163.99,4.88,"to a charismatic leader promising stability at the cost of liberty."],[190,169.25,1.21,"In Plato’s opinion,"],[190,170.62,5.38,"all democratic systems were doomed to eventually elect a silver-tongued tyrant"],[190,176.17,3.38,"who exploits the very freedoms that made democracy possible."],[190,180.13,2.25,"Plato’s early solutions to these problems—"],[190,182.39,2.79,"including handing power to elite philosopher kings—"],[190,185.31,1.5,"leave much to be desired."],[190,186.97,2.25,"But in his final work, “The Laws,”"],[190,189.39,3.5,"Plato combined democracy and monarchy into a new system"],[190,193.06,2.71,"he hoped would be stable and enduring."],[190,196.4,4.13,"To explain his idea, Plato docked his unruly Ship of State"],[190,200.78,4.13,"and created a new model: the city of Magnesia."],[190,205.53,1.54,"In this imaginary land,"],[190,207.08,4.42,"everything is designed to counteract the issues of direct democracy,"],[190,211.67,4.46,"while empowering citizens to be morally and politically engaged."],[190,216.46,3.21,"Instead of leaving citizens vulnerable to smooth talkers,"],[190,219.67,4.09,"Magnesia weaves persuasion directly into its laws."],[190,224.26,4.84,"Each law is paired with a “prelude” that outlines the logic behind the law"],[190,229.35,2.92,"and connects it to shared societal values."],[190,232.69,3.92,"For example, Plato feared that false beliefs about the gods"],[190,236.61,1.63,"would undermine justice."],[190,238.48,4.46,"So in Magnesia, atheism and impiety are illegal."],[190,243.57,2.88,"But rather than relying solely on the fear of punishment"],[190,246.45,1.5,"to prevent these crimes,"],[190,247.95,3.42,"the laws against them contain philosophical arguments"],[190,251.37,5.13,"designed to convince citizens to adhere to Magnesia’s official religion."],[190,256.96,3.25,"Moral education is everywhere in Magnesia,"],[190,260.42,3.09,"and it’s designed to instill shared responsibility."],[190,264.05,3.46,"This way, citizens can retain the individual freedom they value"],[190,267.51,1.0,"under democracy,"],[190,268.72,3.84,"but their personal goals are more likely to align with the common good."],[190,273.1,4.21,"Even so, Plato still recommends safeguards against corruption."],[190,277.81,4.09,"Many of Magnesia’s most powerful positions are held collectively"],[190,282.07,1.67,"rather than individually."],[190,284.11,2.88,"And strict regulations on buying and selling land"],[190,287.28,4.21,"prevent wealth from pooling in the pockets of an elite minority."],[190,292.0,3.0,"Obviously, Magnesia is far from perfect."],[190,295.0,3.13,"Plato’s educational regime is borderline cultish,"],[190,298.13,3.79,"and its constitution requires extremely time-consuming civic engagement"],[190,301.92,1.29,"from every citizen."],[190,303.67,6.38,"Then again, creating an ideal society probably would take time, attention,"],[190,310.51,1.25,"and education."],[190,312.39,2.79,"Your perfect world might not look like Plato’s,"],[190,315.48,3.46,"but it will likely require a serious commitment from its citizens"],[190,319.06,3.84,"to guide their Ship of State to a brighter horizon."],[191,4.33,1.57,"Four months ago,"],[191,5.9,3.9,"my house was raided by the agents of the Venezuelan regime."],[191,10.31,2.84,"They destroyed the walls,"],[191,13.18,2.2,"the ceilings"],[191,15.41,2.47,"and even killed my dogs."],[191,19.28,3.4,"After that, they decided to take away my Venezuelan nationality,"],[191,22.72,3.74,"something that is prohibited by the Venezuelan Constitution."],[191,27.46,4.14,"What sparked this was a public statement I made"],[191,31.63,2.4,"supporting the law enforcement"],[191,34.03,3.34,"and military buildup against Nicolás Maduro"],[191,37.4,1.9,"that led to his capture"],[191,39.34,1.6,"on January 3."],[191,41.3,1.47,"And maybe the fact"],[191,42.77,6.24,"that my house was only blocks away from Delcy Rodríguez’s,"],[191,49.01,2.5,"interim dictator of Venezuela."],[191,52.25,1.87,"For my family and I,"],[191,54.12,2.8,"the journey of persecution has been very long."],[191,57.52,3.17,"I was elected mayor of Caracas for eight years."],[191,61.73,2.8,"After that, I was banned to run for office."],[191,65.26,2.74,"I had three assassination attempts,"],[191,68.03,2.84,"and in the year 2014,"],[191,70.9,2.74,"when Maduro stole his first election,"],[191,73.67,2.64,"we called for protests, peaceful protests."],[191,76.34,3.04,"People came out by the thousands,"],[191,79.41,2.13,"and Maduro called for my arrest."],[191,82.15,3.07,"I went into hiding, and I had three choices."],[191,85.88,2.24,"I could stay in hiding."],[191,88.15,1.77,"I could leave the country."],[191,89.95,2.2,"Or I could turn myself in."],[191,92.19,3.27,"So I decided to turn myself in."],[191,95.49,4.2,"And I became a political prisoner for seven years."],[191,102.7,2.17,"In solitary confinement,"],[191,104.9,4.44,"the sound I remember most is not screaming."],[191,109.37,1.33,"It's the locks."],[191,110.71,3.64,"One lock, then another, then another."],[191,114.38,2.0,"Nine locks in total."],[191,117.31,3.17,"I was in complete confinement,"],[191,120.48,2.97,"so locks became very present to me,"],[191,123.49,4.87,"and they became the framework through which I started to think about freedom,"],[191,128.39,2.57,"which was the reason I was in prison."],[191,132.13,2.24,"I started to think about locks"],[191,134.4,4.14,"and the framework as the things were taken away from me."],[191,140.07,2.4,"My speech was taken away from me,"],[191,142.51,2.07,"a lock on my freedom of speech."],[191,145.31,3.84,"My freedom to mobility, a lock on my mobility."],[191,150.38,4.14,"The freedom to have a free and fair trial,"],[191,154.55,2.94,"a lock on my freedom and justice."],[191,158.29,1.27,"When they took my home,"],[191,159.59,1.33,"a lock on my property."],[191,160.96,1.57,"When they took my nationality,"],[191,162.53,1.83,"a lock on my identity."],[191,164.86,3.64,"So I started to think about locks as the problem"],[191,168.53,2.17,"and keys as the solution."],[191,171.9,3.37,"While in prison, in solitary confinement,"],[191,175.31,3.84,"I figured out a way to speak to other prisoners."],[191,179.84,3.4,"The only moment I could see other prisoners"],[191,183.28,2.27,"was when I was able to go to mass."],[191,186.35,4.84,"So when I was in mass with two details sitting next to me,"],[191,191.22,2.13,"there was only one moment"],[191,193.39,2.97,"when we stood up and gave the hug of peace."],[191,197.06,1.4,"And when that happened,"],[191,198.46,2.37,"I would stand up"],[191,200.86,3.64,"and tell those prisoners that I knew that were with me,"],[191,204.53,1.94,"I would tell them,"],[191,206.5,2.17,"\"Peace be with you, my brother."],[191,208.67,3.4,"Please tell your wife to find my wife.\""],[191,212.68,3.7,"And in that way, we developed a protocol to communicate within prison."],[191,216.41,2.94,"It took time, a lot of iterations."],[191,219.35,1.53,"But over six months,"],[191,220.92,2.64,"we were able to figure out a way"],[191,223.55,1.97,"to take control of the prison."],[191,225.92,3.47,"A year after we started, there was a spark."],[191,229.43,4.07,"And that spark led to the activation of the protocol."],[191,233.96,1.37,"And for 12 hours,"],[191,235.37,2.94,"we took complete control of that prison."],[191,239.24,4.97,"After that, things became even more lonely and more difficult."],[191,244.71,3.57,"I went [on] a hunger strike for 28 days."],[191,248.31,3.14,"I lost 14 kilograms,"],[191,251.48,2.8,"and then they took everything away from me."],[191,255.29,3.07,"Even my books, which were my universe."],[191,259.19,2.3,"So I was in complete loneliness."],[191,261.93,2.5,"And in that complete loneliness,"],[191,264.46,5.14,"I understood that the one thing they were not able to take away from me"],[191,269.6,2.87,"was my heart and my soul."],[191,272.97,3.7,"The way I felt and the way I thought."],[191,277.64,4.04,"It was a key to my own internal freedom."],[191,282.41,2.13,"By the end of 2020,"],[191,284.58,1.77,"I was able to escape Venezuela --"],[191,286.38,2.4,"something I never wanted to do."],[191,288.82,3.27,"We had to cross several checkpoints,"],[191,292.12,4.04,"and finally I crossed a river and went into Colombia."],[191,296.49,1.87,"And I began exile."],[191,298.39,2.87,"Exile is another form of imprisonment."],[191,301.87,3.17,"And I came out into a very different world."],[191,305.77,2.97,"I came out into a world of COVID,"],[191,308.77,4.07,"and I also came out into a very different world in terms of technology."],[191,312.84,2.5,"I had gone into prison with a BlackBerry."],[191,317.28,3.04,"And I came out into the world of smartphones,"],[191,320.35,3.07,"voice notes, video calls"],[191,323.45,1.27,"and cryptocurrency."],[191,324.72,2.77,"None of that was part of my existence before."],[191,327.52,1.27,"It was shocking."],[191,329.69,1.44,"Some months after that,"],[191,331.16,3.47,"I was a visiting fellow at Stanford,"],[191,334.63,2.84,"and I made a very good friend and he told me,"],[191,337.5,1.27,"\"Listen, man,"],[191,338.8,4.1,"the most important thing that happened while you were away"],[191,342.94,2.17,"was Bitcoin and AI."],[191,345.84,3.07,"Bitcoin, because it's open-source,"],[191,348.91,1.57,"decentralized money"],[191,350.51,2.8,"that you can hold by having your own keys."],[191,353.75,1.23,"And AI,"],[191,354.99,2.5,"because it's going to revolutionize everything,"],[191,357.52,3.54,"the way we use [and] process information."],[191,362.99,2.97,"So I became very interested,"],[191,366.0,2.4,"not about the technology itself,"],[191,368.43,2.5,"because I've never written a line of code in my life."],[191,370.97,3.37,"I didn't know what open-source or decentralized technology was,"],[191,374.37,2.6,"but I became interested in the intersection"],[191,377.01,3.34,"between technology and freedom."],[191,382.15,1.8,"And some months after that,"],[191,383.98,2.4,"I started to meet other people."],[191,387.12,4.74,"Other people like myself that had been political prisoners,"],[191,391.89,3.4,"that had led protests in their own countries,"],[191,395.29,2.14,"that had been forced into exile."],[191,397.76,4.41,"We were very different -- skin color, religion, languages --"],[191,402.17,4.24,"but we were very similar in our struggle for freedom."],[191,407.5,5.04,"So we decided to create the World Liberty Congress,"],[191,412.58,4.61,"which has become the largest global alliance of freedom fighters"],[191,417.21,1.83,"and democracy defenders."],[191,419.48,2.27,"60 countries are represented --"],[191,426.42,4.17,"60 countries represented in the World Liberty Congress."],[191,431.09,4.07,"And that gave me the opportunity to get to know a lot of people."],[191,435.17,4.0,"It gave me the opportunity not just to go to different countries,"],[191,439.17,3.6,"but to share, with different people, about their experiences."],[191,442.81,2.57,"And particularly about the way"],[191,445.41,5.34,"in which technology was being used to fight for freedom."],[191,451.42,3.04,"So rather than tell you about the technology,"],[191,454.49,2.07,"I want to tell you about some of the people"],[191,456.59,2.44,"that have been using this technology."],[191,459.62,2.5,"Maria is a Venezuelan nurse."],[191,462.63,2.84,"During COVID, she was earning $3 per month."],[191,466.3,4.37,"So we figured out a program for nurses and doctors"],[191,470.7,3.64,"to get a $100 monthly support."],[191,474.81,1.17,"But we had a problem."],[191,476.01,4.77,"The financial system was taken by the regime."],[191,480.81,2.17,"It was surveilled by the regime."],[191,483.01,3.0,"So we turned to stablecoins and Bitcoin,"],[191,486.05,1.84,"and we were able to support"],[191,487.88,4.67,"more than 70,000 medical doctors and nurses"],[191,492.56,2.9,"without the intermediation of the regime."],[191,499.3,3.67,"It was not just technology, it was a life support."],[191,503.67,5.3,"It was a way for them to survive in the moment of largest crisis."],[191,510.41,3.27,"Then another friend, Anyan."],[191,513.71,1.87,"Anyan is a journalist."],[191,515.58,4.47,"He's dedicated his career to following conflict zones,"],[191,520.08,3.1,"from Congo to Rwanda to Mexico."],[191,523.92,4.27,"And today he has a network of over a thousand journalists."],[191,528.76,1.64,"But they have a problem."],[191,530.39,4.17,"How do they keep their own information without someone switching it off"],[191,534.6,2.23,"or deleting their information?"],[191,536.83,1.77,"So they turn to Nostr."],[191,539.17,3.24,"Nostr is to communication what Bitcoin is to money."],[191,542.44,4.47,"It's an open-source protocol that nobody owns,"],[191,546.94,2.44,"you can have your own information,"],[191,549.41,2.04,"you can have your own followers,"],[191,551.48,2.04,"you can publish your information,"],[191,553.55,3.34,"and no one can delete that information."],[191,557.35,1.37,"Last January,"],[191,558.75,2.67,"there was an election in Uganda."],[191,562.83,1.9,"My very good friend Bobi Wine,"],[191,564.73,2.47,"founding member of the World Liberty Congress,"],[191,567.23,1.47,"was going to win the election."],[191,568.73,3.74,"There was no doubt that he was going to win the election."],[191,572.97,1.83,"But there was also no doubt"],[191,574.84,3.04,"that they were going to steal the election."],[191,577.91,1.7,"And in order to steal the election,"],[191,579.61,2.47,"one of the things that the regime was going to do"],[191,582.08,2.47,"was to shut down the internet."],[191,585.65,3.07,"So I told Bob, I said,"],[191,588.72,2.7,"\"My friend, there is something called BitChat.\""],[191,591.46,5.07,"BitChat is an open-source protocol that connect phones"],[191,596.56,3.07,"without internet, without cell towers,"],[191,599.66,4.47,"just by Bluetooth mesh network, you are able to connect people."],[191,604.17,4.0,"So for 21 days, Bobi posted about BitChat."],[191,608.17,6.64,"And by Election Day, 1.8 million people in Uganda had downloaded this protocol."],[191,615.51,1.2,"And this protocol --"],[191,618.68,1.94,"And this protocol became their internet."],[191,620.62,2.44,"It became the way for them to communicate."],[191,623.59,1.53,"And it was very powerful."],[191,626.49,2.7,"But Uganda also showed me something else."],[191,629.59,1.6,"We had a backup plan."],[191,631.7,4.1,"So we had helped to smuggle more than 30 Starlinks into Uganda"],[191,635.83,1.27,"before the election."],[191,639.54,3.07,"But the regime knew that something could happen."],[191,644.14,4.4,"So they wrote to Starlink asking for the service to be blocked."],[191,648.58,1.4,"And it was blocked."],[191,650.41,3.94,"The service was blocked, and it was not able to be used."],[191,654.38,2.97,"But that same week, that same week,"],[191,657.39,2.07,"in Iran, there were protesters."],[191,660.46,4.2,"And Starlink was open, the same way it was in Ukraine."],[191,666.06,1.53,"So what that showed me"],[191,667.63,2.0,"is that the technology is not the problem."],[191,669.67,1.37,"The technology is there."],[191,671.07,2.54,"Starlink can use, or any other technology."],[191,673.64,1.8,"The problem was very clear."],[191,675.47,2.54,"The problem was the gatekeeper."],[191,678.34,3.8,"Whenever there is a gatekeeper, there is a problem."],[191,682.18,4.3,"It doesn't matter if the gatekeeper is a government, if it's a bank,"],[191,686.52,1.27,"if it's a company."],[191,687.82,2.67,"The problem is the gate itself."],[191,692.62,5.97,"So what we need to figure out is how we can have these keys"],[191,698.63,3.97,"and the elephant in the room in any conversation today about AI is:"],[191,702.63,1.97,"who holds the keys?"],[191,705.1,2.64,"Who holds the keys to your own information?"],[191,707.74,2.7,"[Do] you hold the information or [does] a corporation"],[191,710.44,1.64,"hold that information?"],[191,712.41,4.04,"Fortunately, in the same way that Bitcoin is working for money"],[191,716.48,3.9,"or Nostr for communication, or BitChat for connectivity,"],[191,720.38,1.94,"today there is sovereign AI."],[191,722.35,2.97,"AI that you can hold in your own machine."],[191,725.36,4.8,"AI that you can manage with your own privacy and that you can decide."],[191,730.19,2.47,"Today hold my own sovereign agent,"],[191,732.7,5.54,"and I am working with other people for them in their autocratic countries"],[191,738.27,1.87,"to have their own agent"],[191,740.17,1.77,"that is completely sovereign."],[191,742.81,3.07,"But keys are hard to master."],[191,746.78,2.54,"I remember when I was in prison,"],[191,749.35,4.87,"waking up to an assault rifle pointed [at] my head."],[191,755.15,4.2,"And several men in the cell just tearing everything apart."],[191,760.19,1.37,"I was very scared."],[191,762.59,6.14,"And the way that fear felt was my heart was pounding."],[191,770.33,3.94,"And they came back again every week, every other week."],[191,774.94,3.94,"So I decided that I needed to do something about it."],[191,778.91,4.91,"And what I decided to do was to figure out how I could control my heart."],[191,784.61,3.24,"Not spiritually, not intellectually,"],[191,787.88,2.1,"but physically control my heart."],[191,790.65,3.0,"So I decided to put together this drill."],[191,794.16,3.5,"I would do push-ups, burpees, jumping jacks."],[191,797.69,3.34,"I would take my heart rate to 80, 90 percent,"],[191,801.03,1.57,"and then I would sit down."],[191,803.07,1.53,"Sit still,"],[191,804.63,1.17,"breathe"],[191,805.8,4.24,"and try to take my heart rate as low as I could,"],[191,810.07,1.5,"as fast as I could."],[191,813.04,3.87,"The agents came back to my cell many times."],[191,816.91,2.04,"But every time they came,"],[191,818.98,1.5,"I felt control."],[191,820.52,4.44,"Because I had found my own internal key to self-control"],[191,824.96,1.33,"and to freedom."],[191,827.02,3.94,"And [in] that same way I was able to find that self-control"],[191,830.99,1.74,"and my own internal key,"],[191,832.76,5.27,"you have the choice to have your keys to your sovereignty"],[191,838.07,3.17,"in the 21st century and in the digital age."],[191,846.21,2.6,"But keys need a purpose."],[191,849.95,2.37,"My purpose, for years,"],[191,852.35,4.24,"has been the freedom of my country, Venezuela."],[191,856.92,5.54,"I hold my keys very closely, but I hold them with the purpose."],[191,862.49,5.11,"And for me, the purpose is the freedom of my country, Venezuela,"],[191,867.63,2.27,"and the freedom of every country"],[191,869.93,3.44,"that today is living under autocratic regimes."],[191,873.4,3.6,"And technology can be a source of freedom"],[191,877.04,4.24,"if everyone is able to find their own keys to identity,"],[191,881.31,2.87,"their own keys to their own freedom."],[191,884.21,1.33,"Thank you very much."],[192,4.24,3.34,"I want to start off tonight with a two-step experiment."],[192,7.61,1.5,"For step one,"],[192,9.11,1.6,"I've got a two-liter bottle here."],[192,10.71,1.83,"It’s half-filled with liquid nitrogen"],[192,12.58,2.84,"that’s negative 320 degrees Fahrenheit."],[192,15.45,3.44,"As you can see, it's violently boiling off into a gas,"],[192,18.92,2.3,"just like boiling water does on a hot stove,"],[192,21.25,2.23,"because to this freezing-cold liquid nitrogen,"],[192,23.52,2.57,"the nice comfy air in this room is super hot."],[192,26.52,1.37,"But here’s the catch:"],[192,28.26,1.87,"the gas form of nitrogen"],[192,30.13,3.54,"occupies 700 times the volume of the liquid,"],[192,33.66,2.9,"so you’ve got to be really careful, as it’s turning into a gas"],[192,36.57,2.27,"you don’t trap it in a small container --"],[192,38.84,1.94,"like if the lid was screwed on top here."],[192,40.77,1.47,"So then for step two,"],[192,42.24,2.2,"I'm going to go ahead and screw the lid on top."],[192,45.18,2.47,"And when I do, if everything goes according to plan,"],[192,47.68,2.14,"the contents of this bottle should experience"],[192,49.85,1.17,"what we refer to at NASA"],[192,51.05,3.07,"as an “energetic, spontaneous, rapid disassembly.”"],[192,55.45,1.2,"It's going to explode."],[192,57.85,1.97,"All right, here we go."],[192,60.32,2.0,"Put that on."],[192,62.33,2.04,"OK, now, this is going to be --"],[192,64.39,1.23,"Oh, you know what?"],[192,65.63,2.9,"What the heck, might as well throw these on top."],[192,69.9,2.4,"This is going to be real boring until it's not."],[192,73.2,2.7,"If you don't like loud noises, cover your ears."],[192,75.94,2.0,"But according to my calculations,"],[192,77.97,1.83,"things could get real interesting here"],[192,79.84,3.1,"in about three, two, one."],[192,83.88,1.17,"One."],[192,86.48,1.17,"One."],[192,88.52,1.4,"I said one."],[192,90.59,3.54,"I’m just kidding -- it’s really impossible to actually calculate exactly --"],[192,94.12,1.17,"Whoa!"],[192,99.73,1.17,"Let’s go!"],[192,103.0,1.83,"How's your jet lag now?"],[192,107.2,2.57,"Alright, my apologies,"],[192,109.77,1.97,"my friends in the front row,"],[192,111.78,3.14,"you should be warned, it will get worse before it gets better."],[192,116.55,2.0,"I'm like science Gallagher up here."],[192,120.28,1.94,"So a lot of people would look at that --"],[192,122.22,1.17,"Thank you, yeah."],[192,123.39,1.83,"You keep those as souvenirs, actually."],[192,125.22,2.07,"Please don’t throw them back the whole time."],[192,128.03,2.34,"A lot of people look at that and be like,"],[192,130.36,1.97,"\"Oh, neat, the ping-pong balls exploded.\""],[192,132.33,2.37,"But what actually happened is so much cooler."],[192,134.7,3.1,"Because as more and more of that nitrogen turned into a gas,"],[192,137.8,2.87,"those gas molecules got more and more crowded"],[192,140.67,2.8,"as they pushed back on the plastic molecules"],[192,143.51,2.77,"comprising the walls of the two-liter bottle."],[192,146.31,1.53,"And then eventually,"],[192,147.88,2.64,"the pressure got so great, it got so crowded,"],[192,150.55,4.2,"the walls of the two-liter bottle lost the game of molecular Red Rover,"],[192,154.78,2.17,"and all the gas came rushing out."],[192,157.29,1.3,"But where could it go?"],[192,158.62,2.9,"Because it can’t go down or to the sides --"],[192,161.56,1.74,"trash can's too thick."],[192,163.33,3.0,"Therefore, the only option is to go up,"],[192,166.36,2.34,"and since all the ping-pong balls are in the way,"],[192,168.73,2.07,"they just happen to go along for the ride."],[192,171.47,1.57,"But here's the really cool part."],[192,173.07,4.04,"This is exactly how cannons fire cannonballs."],[192,178.47,3.07,"It's how Nerf blasters fire Nerf darts."],[192,182.25,3.17,"It's how T-shirt cannons fire T-shirts."],[192,187.08,1.97,"And for my friends in the front row,"],[192,189.09,2.1,"it’s how straws fire spit wads."],[192,191.82,1.17,"I'm just kidding."],[192,194.76,3.44,"The trick for all those is you simply build up enough pressure,"],[192,198.19,1.94,"you suddenly release it in one direction"],[192,200.16,3.4,"and plop whatever you want in the way to go along for the ride."],[192,203.6,2.94,"And I love when I get like, that aha moment."],[192,206.57,2.77,"When I understand the world around me with a new framework."],[192,209.37,3.24,"But even more than that, I love giving that to other people."],[192,212.64,3.77,"This is why, for the last 15 years, once a month,"],[192,216.45,3.6,"I've uploaded a free science or engineering video to YouTube"],[192,220.05,2.37,"to my now 75 million subscribers"],[192,222.45,2.8,"who also have a taste for those aha moments."],[192,225.76,1.24,"And the videos --"],[192,229.29,4.04,"The videos can range anywhere from an elephant toothpaste volcano"],[192,233.36,1.94,"that sets a world record"],[192,235.33,3.3,"to a satellite that's orbiting above us right now that we built,"],[192,238.67,2.57,"that you can upload your picture to for free,"],[192,241.27,2.0,"and it will display it on a screen"],[192,243.31,4.2,"and then give you a selfie in space with the Earth photobombing you."],[192,249.48,1.57,"Or even a soccer goalie [that]"],[192,251.08,2.44,"moves back and forth at 40 miles an hour,"],[192,253.55,2.54,"and then we track your shot so effectively,"],[192,256.12,4.44,"not even the world's greatest player can score on it."],[192,261.89,1.44,"Sorry, Ronaldo."],[192,264.89,2.24,"And 16 billion views later,"],[192,267.16,2.77,"what I've learned is I can't teach you"],[192,269.93,2.1,"if I don't have your attention."],[192,272.07,2.94,"But if I can get your attention with something remarkable,"],[192,275.04,3.47,"well, now I suddenly have something to attach the learning to."],[192,279.04,2.04,"This is why it kills me if someone's like,"],[192,281.08,2.3,"\"Oh, I don't like science because it's boring.\""],[192,283.41,1.5,"But at the same time, I get it."],[192,284.92,2.04,"Because instead of learning about pressure"],[192,286.98,1.97,"the way I did with these ping-pong balls,"],[192,288.95,2.24,"a lot of the times it’s something like this --"],[192,291.19,3.64,"with curiosity-murdering phrases"],[192,294.86,2.97,"like, \"consider a rigid container of volume V.\""],[192,299.56,2.24,"It’s a freaking trash can, all right?"],[192,303.37,3.44,"And don’t get me wrong, there is a time and a place for equations,"],[192,306.84,3.8,"but for a generation that is growing up on TikTok and YouTube,"],[192,310.67,1.57,"you have to make it matter"],[192,312.28,2.94,"with something that gets them to lean forward in their seat."],[192,315.25,3.94,"Something that ignites a little curiosity fire in their brain."],[192,319.65,2.07,"And we need that today more than ever."],[192,322.12,3.4,"I like to call this style of teaching “hiding the vegetables.”"],[192,327.19,2.4,"For example, it's really boring if you make a video"],[192,329.63,1.37,"about the scientific method."],[192,330.99,2.37,"But what's not boring is if you make a video"],[192,333.4,1.74,"about a 15-ton Jell-O pool"],[192,335.17,2.24,"you can actually belly flop onto."],[192,337.4,2.74,"And in the process, I'm going to sneak in"],[192,340.14,2.37,"all six steps of the scientific method."],[192,343.24,4.34,"What's also really boring is memorizing an equation about terminal velocity."],[192,347.61,1.4,"But what's not boring"],[192,349.05,2.87,"is you point out that squirrels are one of the only mammals"],[192,351.95,1.97,"that can survive a fall from any height"],[192,353.95,1.94,"at their terminal velocity."],[192,355.89,3.6,"And then, in an effort to keep them from stealing all your birdseed,"],[192,359.52,3.97,"you set up an eight-part Ninja Warrior obstacle course in your backyard,"],[192,363.49,1.5,"and in the process,"],[192,365.03,1.74,"what you might find"],[192,366.8,3.44,"is they will voluntarily demonstrate this principle for you."],[192,372.8,1.27,"It's a very true story."],[192,375.67,4.57,"There's also a ton of super boring microcontroller tutorial videos online."],[192,380.28,3.47,"So when someone came by and stole a package from my porch,"],[192,383.75,2.27,"I decided to dedicate a year of my life"],[192,386.05,4.14,"to making the world's least boring microcontroller tutorial video."],[192,390.22,2.77,"Step one: you make a bait package like this."],[192,392.99,3.57,"And on the outside, you have something like a picture of headphones."],[192,396.56,2.87,"But inside, instead of headphones, you have this contraption."],[192,399.46,4.1,"And at the top there is a cup that spins a pound of the world’s finest glitter."],[192,403.57,1.83,"You have four phones that will record,"],[192,405.4,2.54,"then upload their footage to the cloud in real time,"],[192,407.94,2.0,"and as the pièce de résistance:"],[192,409.94,3.1,"a very uncharitable amount of fart spray."],[192,415.48,1.3,"That's foreshadowing."],[192,417.81,2.84,"And then step two: you have the thief come by,"],[192,420.65,2.74,"and when he lifts the lid, takes it home --"],[192,423.42,3.57,"of course -- it sprays the glitter followed by the fart spray."],[192,427.02,2.27,"I should point out, by the way,"],[192,429.33,3.74,"this whole video was sort of a modern-day homage"],[192,433.1,1.8,"to the greatest film of a generation."],[192,434.93,2.04,"I'm talking, of course, about \"Home Alone.\""],[192,437.57,3.84,"And so the thief here, who’s pretending,"],[192,441.44,2.07,"is none other than my buddy Macaulay Culkin."],[192,446.28,1.33,"And so then for step three:"],[192,447.61,1.8,"what you and Macaulay are going to do"],[192,449.45,1.77,"is you're going to make 20 of these,"],[192,451.25,1.97,"put them on porches all across America,"],[192,453.25,1.37,"and it won't take long"],[192,454.62,3.57,"before you start getting back some incredible footage like this."],[192,459.79,1.44,"(Video) Hello."],[192,464.06,2.4,"Machine: Keep the change, you filthy animal."],[192,468.13,1.2,"Person 1: Whoa!"],[192,472.94,1.24,"Machine: Hello."],[192,476.84,1.17,"You guys give up?"],[192,478.01,1.8,"Or are you thirsty for more?"],[192,483.18,3.6,"Operation “Whoever Smelt It, Dealt It” initiated."],[192,491.45,1.23,"Person 2: Oh."],[192,492.72,2.7,"Machine: Operation \"Butt Trumpet\" initiated."],[192,497.93,1.2,"Person 2: Oh."],[192,502.0,1.5,"Person 2: Alright, alright."],[192,503.5,1.83,"Alright, we get it, OK."],[192,506.34,2.14,"Smells like ... in here."],[192,516.05,1.97,"Mark Rober: By the way, I was watching."],[192,518.35,3.1,"And when that thief was about to lift the lid on the glitter bomb,"],[192,521.49,3.9,"not a single one of you was looking down or was on your phones."],[192,525.42,1.87,"I had your undivided attention."],[192,527.32,2.6,"And that makes for the most fertile brain soil,"],[192,529.93,3.77,"because learning is best when it’s attached to a visceral experience."],[192,533.73,3.47,"Basically, if you feel something here, you remember it here."],[192,537.23,3.04,"This is why memorizing a boring equation is hard."],[192,540.3,2.74,"But you know, remembering that song you sang as a teenager"],[192,543.04,2.33,"that made you feel all those feels, that’s easy --"],[192,545.38,2.97,"and you remember every single lyric, you know, 30 years later."],[192,549.01,3.07,"And to be fair, I didn’t come up with this style of teaching --"],[192,552.12,1.44,"all great teachers know this."],[192,553.58,1.6,"For me, it was Mr. Malloy."],[192,555.22,2.74,"He was my high school statistics teacher."],[192,557.99,2.27,"Hello, Mr. Malloy, if you're watching this."],[192,560.29,1.14,"And he was great,"],[192,561.46,2.84,"not just because he had a killer Doctor Evil impression --"],[192,565.63,2.27,"Or because, and I mean this with all sincerity,"],[192,567.93,3.27,"he had way better hair than whatever I was trying to figure out here."],[192,573.64,1.87,"He was great because he made it matter."],[192,575.5,2.64,"Like encouraging us to use what we learned in statistics"],[192,578.17,2.04,"to predict where our arch-rival soccer team"],[192,580.24,1.84,"was going to kick their penalty kicks."],[192,582.08,1.47,"And it worked."],[192,583.55,3.27,"He was so good at attaching emotions to learning."],[192,586.85,2.77,"He was so good at hiding the vegetables."],[192,590.49,3.57,"And the thing is, with a really good teacher, their impact is immeasurable."],[192,594.09,2.0,"Just like Mr. Malloy impacted his students"],[192,596.13,2.54,"and we're now, in turn, impacting the world."],[192,598.7,1.67,"That's why I love teachers."],[192,600.4,2.6,"I think they have the most important job on the planet."],[192,603.03,1.77,"In fact, please."],[192,608.37,2.0,"I've said for like a decade,"],[192,610.41,2.64,"my dream job is to be a middle-school science teacher,"],[192,613.08,1.44,"and I will be."],[192,614.51,1.83,"But until that time --"],[192,616.38,1.37,"and buckle up,"],[192,617.75,3.14,"because this is the first time the world is hearing about this --"],[192,620.92,2.54,"for the last two and a half years,"],[192,623.49,3.04,"myself, with a team of about 50 people,"],[192,626.56,2.77,"including some of the best science teachers in the country,"],[192,629.36,2.84,"have been secretly working on a full science curriculum,"],[192,632.23,3.34,"and it uses all the tricks I’ve learned from the past 15 years"],[192,635.6,1.53,"on how to hide the vegetables."],[192,641.94,4.3,"And the reason we're doing this is because it breaks my heart"],[192,646.24,2.4,"when I see teachers who get paid salaries [that],"],[192,648.64,1.83,"as a society, we should be ashamed of,"],[192,650.51,3.0,"spending their own money on resources that totally suck."],[192,659.12,1.7,"So it's got everything they'd need,"],[192,660.86,2.67,"from banger videos the kids are going to beg to rewatch,"],[192,663.56,1.87,"that demonstrate the science --"],[192,665.46,2.7,"yes, that is a wrench in an MRI machine,"],[192,668.2,1.83,"because how else am I supposed to talk"],[192,670.03,3.07,"about the invisible magnetic fields all around us?"],[192,673.14,2.94,"It's got ready-to-teach classroom slide decks"],[192,676.11,2.17,"that are super clean and easy to use."],[192,678.31,2.57,"And then it’s got these really thought-out"],[192,680.91,2.1,"curated science demos"],[192,683.01,2.84,"where the kids get to viscerally interact with the science"],[192,685.88,1.27,"that the teachers can make"],[192,687.18,2.7,"just with stuff they have lying around their classroom."],[192,689.92,2.94,"The idea is that together we teach the classroom,"],[192,692.86,1.8,"and the teacher becomes the hero,"],[192,694.69,3.07,"as we engage the students in real science and engineering"],[192,697.76,1.44,"they actually care about."],[192,699.7,2.67,"We're starting with grades three through eight."],[192,702.37,2.7,"It exceeds all the state science standards,"],[192,705.1,1.33,"but most importantly,"],[192,706.47,3.0,"it’s exactly what science class is meant to be:"],[192,709.81,1.53,"super freaking fun."],[192,711.37,3.7,"(Video) Your teacher and I are about to launch this class into hyperdrive."],[192,717.18,1.24,"Let’s go!"],[192,724.29,2.0,"We’ll build stuff,"],[192,726.29,2.4,"ask a ton of questions"],[192,728.73,2.3,"and even smash a few things along the way."],[192,732.5,2.9,"If everything goes according to plan,"],[192,735.43,3.04,"you'll see the world around you in an entirely new way."],[192,739.3,1.77,"Kristin Bell: Oh my God, that’s crazy."],[192,741.07,2.44,"MR: Buckle up. It’s going to be a wild ride."],[192,745.24,2.5,"It's basically magic,"],[192,747.78,1.2,"but science."],[192,750.48,1.77,"Which makes it cooler than magic."],[192,753.75,1.74,"Mr. Beast: I knew it felt too light."],[192,757.52,1.77,"MR: What do you guys want to do now?"],[192,765.53,1.4,"The world has evolved,"],[192,766.96,2.77,"and teaching science is long overdue for disruption."],[192,769.77,2.07,"I'm happy to report that of the teachers"],[192,771.87,2.94,"who are assigned to teach the pilot lessons to their students,"],[192,774.8,3.57,"because of our ability to explain complicated things simply"],[192,778.41,4.54,"and for our very unique experience in solving the science motivation gap"],[192,782.98,1.4,"for 15 years,"],[192,784.41,2.5,"95 percent of the teachers said that when we're done,"],[192,786.95,2.87,"they would want this to be their full science curriculum."],[192,789.85,3.17,"And so the idea is you combine incredible teachers"],[192,793.02,1.2,"with incredible resources"],[192,794.26,1.4,"to get this explosive output,"],[192,795.69,1.67,"sort of like you'd expect to see"],[192,797.36,2.3,"if you combine this bucket of boiling water"],[192,799.7,2.5,"with this trash can of freezing-cold liquid nitrogen."],[192,804.0,2.4,"So to all those teachers out there in the trenches,"],[192,806.44,2.64,"I want you to know reinforcements are on the way."],[192,809.64,3.94,"In fact, a bunch of the videos and lessons are already available right now,"],[192,813.61,2.8,"and we'll finish the rest over the next four years,"],[192,816.45,3.97,"because I know this will be the most important thing I do my whole life."],[192,820.45,5.64,"And even though this is going to cost us $60 million to make,"],[192,826.12,3.97,"my official declaration tonight is every single lesson plan,"],[192,830.09,1.47,"every teacher training,"],[192,831.56,1.97,"every original class demo"],[192,833.56,1.97,"will cost exactly zero dollars"],[192,835.57,3.44,"and be 100 percent free for all teachers forever,"],[192,839.0,3.64,"as we work to ignite those brain fires of curiosity"],[192,842.67,2.9,"in the next generation of big problem solvers."],[192,847.18,1.33,"Thank you so much."],[192,852.12,1.17,"Thank you."],[192,856.72,1.17,"Thank you."],[193,5.44,1.77,"What’s up, everybody?"],[193,8.3,1.7,"I'm Keke Palmer."],[193,10.51,2.4,"You might know me from the spelling bee movie"],[193,12.91,1.94,"\"Akeelah and the Bee,\""],[193,14.88,4.74,"my Nickelodeon TV show \"True Jackson VP.\""],[193,19.65,1.83,"Jordan Peele's \"Nope.\""],[193,22.85,4.04,"Maybe my viral meme where I was “Sorry to that man.”"],[193,28.83,3.37,"Or more recently, my new TV show, \"The Burbs,\""],[193,32.23,1.84,"streaming now on Peacock."],[193,36.47,3.64,"I've been working in front of the camera for over 20 years now."],[193,40.64,2.87,"But today, I'm going to share my story with you."],[193,44.21,4.1,"Not as a survivor soliloquy, but to expose a pattern,"],[193,49.01,2.6,"because survival can be so effective,"],[193,51.65,2.74,"you don't realize when it's no longer serving you."],[193,55.02,2.4,"I grew up in Robbins, Illinois."],[193,58.05,3.4,"And Robbins, by definition, is a food desert."],[193,61.49,3.57,"The liquor store is often where I picked up my lunches before school --"],[193,65.1,1.7,"Flamin’ Hot Cheetos and a pop --"],[193,67.43,3.47,"a meal the teachers over at my Catholic school often criticized."],[193,71.67,2.97,"Even still, my family had love."],[193,75.21,3.57,"We was cash-poor but rich in culture and pride."],[193,79.34,2.97,"My mother was a substitute teacher for disabled children."],[193,82.35,3.57,"She sang for churches and did backup singing for extra cash."],[193,86.28,3.5,"My father worked in the factory at a polyurethane company."],[193,89.82,2.87,"He had Carhartt before it was fashionable, OK?"],[193,94.46,3.8,"But they fell in love doing speech interp and theater --"],[193,98.3,3.14,"things circumstance slowly made no space for."],[193,101.73,1.74,"The love was there."],[193,103.47,1.64,"The joy was there."],[193,105.1,2.6,"But even with both of my parents working multiple jobs,"],[193,107.7,1.7,"it wasn't enough."],[193,109.44,2.5,"When I was eight, we moved somewhere a little nicer"],[193,111.98,4.6,"and qualified for Section 8 -- which is a subsidized housing program."],[193,117.35,4.3,"I remember being told not to mention my father when the assessor came by,"],[193,121.65,2.64,"because it would reduce the support we needed."],[193,125.46,3.44,"I didn't understand the system, but I understood the stakes."],[193,129.43,1.67,"Stability was fragile,"],[193,131.13,1.47,"survival was urgent --"],[193,132.63,1.17,"and in that urgency,"],[193,133.83,1.8,"I learned that protecting the whole"],[193,135.63,3.0,"sometimes meant shrinking parts of ourselves."],[193,139.67,2.74,"Growing up in a place where access is limited,"],[193,142.44,2.27,"hamming it up became my pastime."],[193,144.74,2.27,"A dream passed down."],[193,148.21,1.17,"Then suddenly,"],[193,149.38,4.4,"performing was a gift that granted my family more access."],[193,154.19,2.87,"See, only a child could fit through the gatekeeper’s gates --"],[193,157.09,3.84,"especially a child like me that was so eager to please."],[193,160.93,2.33,"So when I started auditioning and booking,"],[193,163.29,2.67,"it became clear I was the one who could do it."],[193,166.53,4.14,"I could do something I enjoyed and lift some weight off my parents."],[193,172.14,1.47,"So we did it."],[193,173.6,3.17,"We moved to LA for my career."],[193,177.31,3.87,"We drove four days and three nights from Illinois to California."],[193,181.18,1.83,"My dad withdrew his pension,"],[193,183.01,3.94,"the church and extended family gave us what they could, and we was off."],[193,187.72,3.0,"And right away, it seemed to be the right decision."],[193,190.72,4.2,"In the first year, I starred in a movie alongside William H. Macy"],[193,194.93,1.57,"and got a SAG nomination."],[193,197.43,2.07,"Then -- go ahead, clap."],[193,204.17,3.67,"Then I got a self-titled Disney Channel pilot,"],[193,207.87,2.67,"and I starred in my own movie."],[193,211.44,4.57,"Suddenly, we had access to a life that didn't require constant vigilance."],[193,216.05,4.67,"Each opportunity gave way to a world we never knew was possible."],[193,220.72,1.77,"We no longer shared rooms,"],[193,222.52,1.64,"we had a car that worked,"],[193,224.19,1.87,"my parents weren't stressed about bills"],[193,226.09,3.54,"or their ability to get the best education for me and my three siblings."],[193,230.46,4.07,"It got to the point where my career became the center of our orbit,"],[193,234.56,2.33,"and not because we chased success,"],[193,236.93,2.37,"but because it bought us freedom."],[193,241.57,3.84,"That's when performing stopped being something I did for fun"],[193,245.44,1.94,"and something we relied on."],[193,248.11,2.1,"Messing that up wouldn’t have just cost me --"],[193,250.21,1.94,"it would have put our freedom at risk."],[193,252.18,3.3,"And we already knew what it was like to live without it."],[193,255.52,2.9,"So I adapted."],[193,259.12,2.23,"Not all at once, but over the years."],[193,261.39,3.67,"By the time I landed my own TV show, I was undoubtedly the breadwinner,"],[193,265.1,2.0,"and my job was just that."],[193,267.7,2.34,"There was no time for outside activities,"],[193,270.03,2.67,"no time for vacation, no time for pause."],[193,273.3,2.77,"And as the pressure got greater,"],[193,276.11,2.14,"stage became my home."],[193,278.81,3.2,"Performing was the safest way for me to be free."],[193,282.05,1.2,"In my roles,"],[193,283.28,2.74,"I could embody joy -- even briefly."],[193,286.35,2.57,"I could be “True Jackson, VP,”"],[193,288.95,3.9,"“Working at a grown-up job never really knew I could work this hard.”"],[193,294.46,2.3,"At the time, it was just a theme song I wrote."],[193,296.79,2.27,"I didn't know how I was transmuting."],[193,299.76,2.34,"In my roles, I could be sad."],[193,302.33,1.6,"I was allowed to be frustrated --"],[193,303.93,2.54,"although often disguised as humor."],[193,307.2,3.67,"Performing was safe because it didn't make people feel guilty"],[193,310.91,3.47,"about watching me carry the weight of adulthood far too early."],[193,315.78,3.17,"As the years flew by, I didn’t just perform on stage --"],[193,319.72,1.94,"I started performing off it, too."],[193,323.22,3.77,"I began designing a character to survive my life."],[193,327.99,3.0,"That character is Keke Palmer --"],[193,331.63,3.37,"approachable, capable, funny --"],[193,335.03,3.67,"a small container my full range could exist inside of"],[193,338.74,2.07,"without overwhelming anyone."],[193,343.07,1.33,"And it worked."],[193,344.91,4.3,"That character has carried me through 23 years in this industry,"],[193,349.25,3.37,"through childhood fame, the transition into adulthood,"],[193,352.62,2.64,"through success I could have never imagined."],[193,355.29,3.84,"I even wrote a \"New York Times\" best selling book about how I did it,"],[193,359.16,2.47,"how I became a \"Master of Me.\""],[193,363.66,1.64,"By every external measure,"],[193,365.33,1.9,"I made the system work for me."],[193,368.46,3.07,"And then I had a son."],[193,372.34,1.6,"His name is Leodis,"],[193,373.97,1.64,"and every year, my son and I"],[193,375.64,2.17,"do these elaborate Halloween costumes."],[193,377.84,2.47,"And listen, he's really good."],[193,380.34,1.6,"Like, he commits."],[193,383.15,1.37,"He knows how to perform --"],[193,384.55,1.9,"they've become full-on productions,"],[193,386.45,2.77,"and it's a cool way to share what I do with him."],[193,389.95,1.37,"We have a lot of fun."],[193,392.32,3.8,"But this past year after it was over, I noticed something."],[193,396.49,1.27,"He was exhausted."],[193,398.1,2.17,"And not the kind where you just fall asleep --"],[193,400.26,4.4,"the kind where you keep running and running and yelling and screaming."],[193,405.2,3.2,"I thought once we got into the car he'd fall asleep, but he didn't."],[193,408.44,2.9,"He couldn’t, and that scared me."],[193,411.81,1.5,"So I pulled over,"],[193,413.34,2.3,"took him out of his seat and held him real tight."],[193,415.68,1.44,"And he was fighting me."],[193,417.11,2.07,"I kept saying, \"It's OK to rest,"],[193,419.22,1.94,"you can rest. I’ve got you.”"],[193,421.72,2.87,"After one last slap to my face, he fell asleep."],[193,426.06,1.33,"When we got home,"],[193,427.42,3.4,"I still had work to do, but I had one hour free."],[193,431.5,2.84,"So I laid down, closed my eyes,"],[193,434.33,3.24,"and before I knew it, the hour was gone."],[193,437.9,1.2,"I hadn't slept one bit."],[193,439.14,1.17,"My mind kept running."],[193,440.34,2.67,"Then my mom walks in saying it’s time to go,"],[193,443.01,1.64,"and I get angry with her."],[193,444.68,1.77,"She has no clue what’s going on --"],[193,446.48,1.17,"now I'm crying,"],[193,447.64,1.84,"feeling this delayed sense of grief,"],[193,449.51,2.04,"realizing I'm acting like my son"],[193,451.55,3.04,"and expecting my mother to do what she never could."],[193,455.22,2.04,"Not because she didn’t love me,"],[193,458.59,4.54,"but because survival taught her to value propulsion."],[193,463.99,3.04,"Moving forward mattered more than being healed."],[193,467.87,2.17,"My mother was terrified I wouldn't survive,"],[193,470.07,2.13,"so she gave me what she knew:"],[193,472.94,1.67,"survival skills."],[193,475.34,2.33,"And sure, when I was younger, she'd say,"],[193,477.71,1.83,"\"We can go back to Chicago,\""],[193,479.58,2.34,"but going back didn’t feel like rest --"],[193,481.94,1.44,"it felt like erasure."],[193,484.98,1.87,"Stopping was always on the table"],[193,486.88,2.64,"alongside going back to how we were living before."],[193,492.09,2.7,"So stopping never felt like a choice --"],[193,495.73,1.37,"just an ultimatum."],[193,498.46,1.9,"I wasn't trying to be exceptional."],[193,501.63,1.6,"I was trying to be reliable."],[193,504.87,2.84,"I carried the load, not because I had to,"],[193,507.74,2.97,"but because I couldn’t un-know what was at stake."],[193,512.01,2.47,"Once you've seen life on the other side of poverty,"],[193,514.51,2.07,"you can't unsee the contrast."],[193,517.45,3.34,"I couldn't live with the fact that we had a shot and I didn't take it,"],[193,520.82,2.0,"so I didn't fail."],[193,526.19,2.34,"I just didn't know when it was complete."],[193,532.33,1.2,"Somewhere along the way,"],[193,533.56,3.17,"I started believing I was a thing that saved us."],[193,537.3,2.7,"I was Keke Palmer."],[193,541.8,3.8,"I built an entire way of moving through the world around staying alert,"],[193,545.61,2.27,"staying useful, staying on."],[193,548.61,2.77,"I was reflexively disembodied,"],[193,551.41,2.87,"constantly juggling everything thrown at me."],[193,554.92,2.67,"I got so good at letting my body run on autopilot [that]"],[193,557.62,2.6,"I would have these huge gaps in my life"],[193,560.22,1.9,"where I lacked recall."],[193,563.23,2.7,"I remember one time I was doing \"Cinderella\" on Broadway,"],[193,565.93,3.9,"and I couldn’t remember how I got to the stage while on stage."],[193,570.67,2.57,"It's clear that system didn't know how to stop."],[193,573.67,1.17,"It’s like a computer --"],[193,574.87,2.34,"it works great so you never turn it off."],[193,577.24,2.0,"You don’t even let it restart for updates,"],[193,579.28,2.94,"so you never know just how much better it could be."],[193,583.18,1.33,"That was me:"],[193,585.12,3.87,"a billboard for hyper-functioning -- with style, of course."],[193,591.86,1.87,"But the pattern finally broke."],[193,594.86,4.04,"When I held my son and told him to rest,"],[193,600.36,4.64,"that was a small moment, but it ended something old,"],[193,605.03,3.54,"something that had been running for generations."],[193,610.41,5.07,"When adaptive intelligence outlives the conditions it was built for,"],[193,615.48,2.1,"it turns into compulsion --"],[193,618.28,3.47,"productivity without presence."],[193,623.69,2.27,"What I want to share with you"],[193,625.99,2.0,"is that survival can be so effective,"],[193,627.99,3.44,"you don't realize when it's no longer needed in your life."],[193,632.9,2.77,"You might think you need to earn more,"],[193,635.7,3.1,"prove more, secure one more opportunity,"],[193,638.84,1.64,"collect one more accolade,"],[193,640.5,4.0,"or just keep moving long enough until you finally feel safe."],[193,646.18,2.24,"When in reality,"],[193,648.41,1.9,"you don't need another achievement."],[193,651.35,1.6,"You need a break."],[193,654.65,1.27,"OK?"],[193,661.42,5.11,"You need a break long enough to look around, take stock"],[193,666.56,3.14,"and feel gratitude for what you've already built."],[193,671.3,3.24,"It's important we check the systems we're still running on."],[193,674.57,2.57,"Some of the functions that saved you"],[193,677.17,4.3,"may be keeping you from the very you you were always trying to save."],[193,682.48,4.2,"My parents survived inside of systems that never fully saw them."],[193,687.62,4.21,"Learning how to live instead of just surviving"],[193,691.82,3.3,"became my way of returning some of that visibility."],[193,697.73,2.7,"I went to Bali this past year"],[193,700.43,3.14,"and finally spent some one-on-one time with that little girl"],[193,703.6,3.04,"who left Robbins, Illinois all those years ago."],[193,707.64,4.57,"So please allow me to reintroduce myself."],[193,713.38,5.04,"My name is Lauren Keyana Palmer,"],[193,718.45,4.34,"and I’m the CEO of the Keke Palmer Company --"],[193,722.82,2.9,"a company I created out of nothing"],[193,725.72,2.77,"with my mother, my father"],[193,728.52,1.57,"and my three siblings."],[193,737.53,4.67,"I'm just a girl that wanted herself and her family out of poverty,"],[193,742.21,1.77,"and once we was out,"],[193,744.01,2.14,"I forgot to let myself free."],[193,747.11,2.24,"Yet I'm here today,"],[193,749.38,2.14,"grateful to say"],[193,751.55,2.4,"my parents showed me how to survive."],[193,754.82,3.0,"I showed them how to dream,"],[193,757.85,3.2,"and my son is showing me"],[193,761.06,1.5,"how to live."],[193,766.0,1.2,"Thank you."],[194,7.21,3.34,"While cleaning out your eccentric uncle's attic,"],[194,10.55,3.79,"you find a chest with a sparkling potion."],[194,14.63,5.84,"The attached tag declares drinking this liquid will make you immortal."],[194,20.77,3.0,"Your body will be frozen at its current age,"],[194,23.77,3.96,"and you'll become immune to disease and serious injuries."],[194,28.02,3.38,"These effects would be final and irreversible,"],[194,31.65,4.63,"guaranteeing your existence at least until the heat death of the universe."],[194,36.32,2.33,"So, the instructions are clear—"],[194,38.91,3.09,"the only question is, do you drink the potion?"],[194,42.91,2.71,"Generally, people consider death to be bad—"],[194,45.92,5.05,"so bad, in fact, that our lives are filled with precautions against it."],[194,51.13,2.96,"We often avoid overtly dangerous activities,"],[194,54.17,5.88,"and we willingly accept inconveniences to further reduce our chances of dying."],[194,60.6,4.55,"But if you were immortal, you could live as dangerously as you like."],[194,65.27,4.46,"You could pursue any goal on as long a timeline as you need."],[194,70.15,2.92,"You’d be free to enjoy a boundless horizon"],[194,73.07,4.34,"of exploration, pleasure, fulfillment, and novelty,"],[194,77.53,3.75,"all without the fear of death hanging over you."],[194,81.95,4.17,"Despite this, British ethicist Bernard Williams"],[194,86.17,3.29,"thinks there are plenty of reasons not to drink,"],[194,89.63,5.13,"most notably because an immortal life would be unbearably boring."],[194,95.34,3.59,"Think of how your favorite things can become less enjoyable"],[194,98.93,1.5,"with frequent repetition."],[194,100.81,5.09,"Your 16th chocolate truffle doesn't bring as much pleasure as the first,"],[194,106.06,4.59,"and you might grow sick of truffles altogether if you ate them every day."],[194,111.15,1.33,"Following this logic,"],[194,112.65,4.8,"Williams argues that repeating the same activities for all eternity"],[194,117.61,2.21,"would be horribly monotonous."],[194,120.28,3.5,"On the other hand, philosopher John Martin Fischer"],[194,123.79,5.09,"argues that while some pleasurable experiences do have diminishing returns,"],[194,128.88,1.13,"others don’t."],[194,130.08,3.71,"Surely watching a beautiful sunset would remain fresh,"],[194,133.8,1.83,"even after thousands of years?"],[194,135.97,3.96,"And since we already tend to forget things in our human lifespan,"],[194,140.09,3.04,"it seems reasonable that after several centuries"],[194,143.14,3.71,"you could enjoy a previously exhausted experience."],[194,147.35,2.54,"Besides, even if some pleasures fade,"],[194,149.94,3.63,"the pursuit of certain goals could last forever."],[194,153.94,4.63,"Fischer and Benjamin Mitchell-Yellin point out that even an immortal"],[194,158.57,5.17,"couldn’t read every novel ever written while keeping up with new releases."],[194,164.03,3.79,"And J.J. Wisnewski imagines an immortal musician"],[194,167.96,4.67,"who learns all existing instruments and any new ones that are invented."],[194,172.96,1.96,"And those are just personal goals—"],[194,174.92,3.75,"an immortal dedicated to advancing various social causes"],[194,178.67,1.92,"would likely find their hands busy"],[194,180.59,3.71,"navigating a constantly shifting cultural landscape."],[194,184.76,4.59,"Alternatively, perhaps the novelty sustaining your immortal life"],[194,189.35,1.63,"could come from within,"],[194,190.98,2.63,"as you change what you value and pursue."],[194,193.98,1.71,"After decades of painting,"],[194,195.69,3.88,"you might decide to become an astronaut or a chef or a farmer,"],[194,199.78,3.21,"each transformation opening up new experiences."],[194,203.28,4.63,"However, Williams questions if anyone would actually want to live"],[194,207.91,2.13,"such an ever-changing lifestyle."],[194,210.46,3.04,"Sampling every experience life has to offer"],[194,213.5,4.88,"would require living untethered to a core set of values or ambitions."],[194,218.71,4.84,"And not only would this be profoundly destabilizing to your sense of self,"],[194,223.68,2.0,"over a long enough timeline"],[194,225.68,4.04,"you’d probably come into conflict with your past selves."],[194,229.85,4.3,"In such a situation, would you even care about your continued survival?"],[194,234.48,6.01,"Immortality skeptics often cite the painful prospect of outliving loved ones"],[194,240.49,1.92,"as a reason to remain mortal."],[194,242.86,3.67,"But what about knowing that the current version of yourself"],[194,246.53,4.34,"could effectively die by morphing into someone totally different?"],[194,251.37,3.17,"Williams argues that living within the constraints"],[194,254.54,2.67,"of our own individual character"],[194,257.38,3.17,"is part of what makes life meaningful."],[194,260.96,3.63,"Thinking about what makes life meaningful is very relevant here."],[194,264.88,3.09,"After all, the ideal outcome of immortality"],[194,267.97,4.8,"is the ability to maximize life and the good things it contains."],[194,273.06,4.88,"But philosopher Samuel Scheffler argues that temporal scarcity—"],[194,277.94,4.8,"meaning the lack of time— is essential to our valuing anything."],[194,282.94,4.25,"It’s precisely the fact that our time on Earth is limited"],[194,287.2,2.63,"that forces us to form our values,"],[194,289.83,4.13,"and determine what things are worth doing with the time we have."],[194,294.5,4.3,"For Scheffler, immortality undermines the very possibility"],[194,298.79,1.29,"of a meaningful life."],[194,300.96,1.96,"For these reasons, and many more,"],[194,302.92,5.09,"some theorists consider eternal life more of a curse than a blessing."],[194,308.18,4.96,"Then again, a couple of centuries of learning, loving, and chocolate truffles"],[194,313.18,1.75,"doesn’t sound that bad, does it?"],[194,315.14,3.17,"So, will you drink your uncle’s potion?"],[195,4.04,1.92,"Anuj Gupta: Be a little more aggressive."],[195,6.0,1.21,"Get after that."],[195,7.21,3.13,"Neil Frauenglass: I mean, I might as well be up for the challenge."],[195,10.34,1.17,"AG: No, seriously?"],[195,12.59,2.13,"NF: What actually brings people together?"],[195,15.14,1.88,"Oh, I knew you should have gotten this."],[195,17.01,1.29,"You would have said food!"],[195,27.73,1.54,"NF: Hi, I'm Neil Frauenglass,"],[195,29.32,2.79,"I'm the chief marketing officer at Visit Philadelphia,"],[195,32.15,4.59,"and I believe the success of democracy is up to all of us participating."],[195,36.74,2.63,"AG: Anuj Gupta, I'm president of the Welcoming Center,"],[195,39.41,2.5,"and I believe democracy can still work."],[195,41.91,2.17,"Producer: Today, we're playing Idea Knockdown."],[195,44.08,1.75,"Red blocks are rapid-fire questions,"],[195,45.83,3.0,"and black blocks have questions that go a little deeper."],[195,48.88,3.29,"Whoever knocks down the tower will have to do a mystery challenge."],[195,52.22,2.13,"AG: Go for a lower piece, Neil, come on."],[195,59.56,1.54,"NF: OK."],[195,61.1,1.67,"Is debate overrated?"],[195,62.81,1.17,"Absolutely not."],[195,64.02,3.13,"We need to understand what other people have to say"],[195,67.19,2.25,"and understand other opinions, right?"],[195,69.48,2.04,"It takes a lot of animals to make up a zoo."],[195,71.53,1.75,"AG: We're also not always right."],[195,73.32,1.38,"Shh. No! Stop!"],[195,74.7,2.25,"OK, here we go."],[195,77.32,2.13,"It's like I'm talking to my dog."],[195,79.49,3.04,"Do you think most people actually want to participate?"],[195,82.54,4.92,"I think we're at a point where most people feel"],[195,87.46,3.59,"like there is no benefit to participating."],[195,91.09,4.21,"If they felt like there was a benefit, then the answer would be yes."],[195,95.34,3.34,"NF: I think a lot of people want to participate"],[195,98.72,3.59,"and they don't know how to do it"],[195,102.31,2.54,"in a way that is meaningful."],[195,104.89,1.13,"AG: Mhm."],[195,106.06,4.25,"You know, writing letters to your councilperson,"],[195,110.36,3.59,"seeing what's happening in the local news and speaking out,"],[195,113.94,2.59,"rallying neighbors together."],[195,116.53,4.67,"We have to actively participate in democracy."],[195,121.24,1.17,"It can't be passive."],[195,122.45,1.25,"Otherwise it won't work."],[195,128.33,4.13,"Are people divided or just disconnected?"],[195,132.46,3.21,"People are certainly divided,"],[195,135.72,3.67,"and they're probably divided because they are disconnected."],[195,140.09,4.0,"We are just getting further and further apart from each other,"],[195,144.14,2.63,"and we cannot find common ground."],[195,146.77,2.59,"And one of the things that we talk about so much,"],[195,149.35,1.88,"if you ask one more question,"],[195,151.23,2.84,"you can begin to find that common ground"],[195,154.07,1.75,"and understand that we,"],[195,155.86,2.0,"many of us, want the same things."],[195,157.86,3.04,"That division is just a symptom of the disconnection."],[195,160.95,1.17,"AG: Totally agree."],[195,165.75,1.25,"It’s not easy."],[195,169.58,2.25,"It's a good thing I wasn't a civil engineer."],[195,175.34,3.63,"Can young people fix what older generations broke?"],[195,179.01,4.13,"Yes, if they don't adopt the same habits of older generations."],[195,183.18,4.8,"And they bring their optimism to bear"],[195,188.02,2.34,"on whatever that issue is,"],[195,190.4,3.29,"to allow them to break past those practices."],[195,193.69,3.17,"NF: And we've seen proof of that over hundreds of years."],[195,196.86,2.25,"We talk all the time in Philly"],[195,199.15,2.42,"about this pursuit of a more perfect union."],[195,201.57,1.88,"And we're doing that, right?"],[195,203.49,4.3,"You and I are a younger generation from our parents and trying to better it."],[195,207.83,1.25,"We're here right now."],[195,209.08,2.34,"So I think it is an emphatic yes."],[195,213.17,1.21,"AG: There you go."],[195,214.38,1.25,"Nice, that was easy."],[195,219.63,2.5,"NF: What actually brings people together?"],[195,222.18,1.92,"Oh, I knew you should have gotten this."],[195,224.09,1.21,"You would have said food!"],[195,225.31,1.21,"AG: Food, yeah."],[195,227.31,2.42,"NF: Food is a huge one that brings people together."],[195,229.77,1.88,"I think another one is family,"],[195,231.64,1.58,"shared sense of community."],[195,233.27,3.38,"Ultimately, I think all the things are incredibly personal"],[195,236.69,2.96,"and around people that we know."],[195,239.69,2.17,"And maybe that's why it's a challenge, right?"],[195,241.86,2.13,"Because as the aperture gets wider"],[195,244.03,3.63,"and you interact with more and more people that you don't know,"],[195,247.7,1.71,"it becomes more challenging"],[195,249.41,3.42,"and you're not sure if you have a shared sense of values."],[195,252.83,3.0,"And that's when we start to put up walls, maybe."],[195,255.88,3.88,"AG: Like, the structural integrity is increasingly in question here."],[195,260.51,1.21,"Ooh."],[195,264.01,3.34,"How do you know if a democracy is working?"],[195,267.93,5.5,"Certainly one way is if we find solutions,"],[195,273.48,1.75,"even if they're not perfect,"],[195,275.27,3.92,"to some of our larger, more pressing issues,"],[195,279.19,4.13,"if we can at least find a pathway to try and solve it,"],[195,283.32,3.46,"even if the thing doesn't ultimately work,"],[195,286.82,2.63,"that suggests to me that democracy is still working,"],[195,289.45,4.42,"because we’ve got a variety of interests"],[195,293.87,3.09,"to coalesce around a path"],[195,297.0,2.46,"and to say, yes, we're going to try this."],[195,299.46,2.54,"NF: Somebody once told me that a democracy is working"],[195,302.01,2.17,"if we're actively disagreeing with each other."],[195,304.22,1.17,"AG: Yeah."],[195,305.38,2.84,"NF: And that really hit home, right?"],[195,308.26,3.46,"If we're all agreeing, we're probably not in a democracy."],[195,311.77,3.71,"And the fact that we can speak out and we can disagree with each other,"],[195,315.52,2.25,"that's what the democratic process is all about."],[195,317.81,5.5,"AG: But so long as that disagreement doesn't stop attempts to work together."],[195,323.36,1.17,"NF: Yeah."],[195,324.86,1.42,"Oh, yeah."],[195,326.32,1.83,"I knew one of these had to be easy."],[195,333.45,2.54,"We'll make it harder on you to place that."],[195,336.04,6.42,"What's one thing people have more power over than they realize?"],[195,342.5,1.88,"I do think people have more power"],[195,344.38,2.13,"over what happens in their local community"],[195,346.55,1.25,"than they realize."],[195,347.8,1.96,"Every morning when I'm walking the dogs,"],[195,349.76,2.84,"I see something and I email my councilman."],[195,352.64,1.17,"AG: Yeah."],[195,353.81,2.5,"NF: And everybody could do that every single day."],[195,356.31,1.17,"AG: Yes, I know."],[195,357.52,1.17,"NF: Every day."],[195,358.73,2.54,"It's as easy as making that call or sending that note"],[195,361.27,1.33,"to make change."],[195,362.65,5.0,"And I see people complain more than take the action to do it."],[195,367.65,5.71,"AG: I would elevate that exact same dynamic to Capitol Hill."],[195,373.37,3.38,"I can tell you, as a former Capitol Hill staffer,"],[195,376.79,2.5,"I was chief of staff to a member of the House,"],[195,379.29,3.75,"[and] every week I asked my staff to pull a report"],[195,383.05,2.0,"of the emails, the phone calls,"],[195,385.09,2.38,"the faxes that still come in"],[195,387.47,4.42,"and handwritten letters on any particular issue,"],[195,391.89,1.79,"bill, letter."],[195,393.72,1.75,"And I would factor that in,"],[195,395.48,4.71,"particularly those that came in from residents in the district,"],[195,400.23,5.46,"I would factor those in into what bills I would put the congressman’s name on."],[195,405.74,4.09,"All it took was that one constituent to draw my attention to it,"],[195,409.82,2.79,"and I put the congressman on the bill to support it."],[195,412.66,1.17,"NF: That's great."],[195,413.83,2.79,"AG: So it actually still works."],[195,416.66,3.59,"NF: I'll be a little more reckless, but not completely reckless."],[195,424.13,1.71,"Should voting be mandatory?"],[195,427.46,1.17,"AG: Yes."],[195,428.63,1.17,"NF: Yes."],[195,429.84,1.84,"AG: Oh, man, it's wobbly."],[195,432.64,2.08,"NF: You're giving the people what they want."],[195,437.27,2.92,"AG: Is compromise a sign of strength or weakness?"],[195,440.23,1.17,"It's a sign of strength."],[195,441.4,3.21,"It's a sign that the system is working."],[195,444.61,6.21,"We have made everything, every position, every policy, a dogmatic choice."],[195,450.86,2.71,"That is not the way the system was intended to work."],[195,460.37,1.38,"NF: OK."],[195,461.75,3.71,"Is the problem that people don't care"],[195,465.46,3.25,"or they feel like their voice doesn't matter?"],[195,468.76,3.42,"I really think it's that people feel like their voice doesn't matter."],[195,472.18,5.0,"You know, I think so many people care about their communities,"],[195,477.18,2.42,"their cities, their states, their country."],[195,480.02,1.54,"But I think they feel like"],[195,481.6,2.59,"they don't have a voice in anything."],[195,484.19,3.21,"AG: We could look at Philadelphia as a microcosm of that."],[195,487.44,1.63,"In our lifetime,"],[195,489.11,3.96,"we used to see turnout higher than 70 percent"],[195,493.07,1.67,"in elections for mayor"],[195,494.78,1.46,"in the city of Philadelphia."],[195,496.28,1.83,"We're now down to 30 percent."],[195,498.16,3.13,"How did that happen in such a short period of time?"],[195,501.33,1.29,"We're not that old."],[195,502.67,3.38,"It happened because people started to check out of the system"],[195,506.08,1.46,"for one reason or another,"],[195,507.55,3.42,"probably because they feel like their issues aren't being addressed."],[195,511.01,1.21,"NF: Yeah."],[195,518.68,3.13,"AG: How do you invite people into civic engagement?"],[195,521.81,1.96,"I think you lower the temperature."],[195,523.81,3.63,"If you start from the position"],[195,527.48,2.92,"[that] you’re either on one side or another of this issue,"],[195,530.4,3.25,"you have just alienated a whole group of people."],[195,533.7,4.46,"So I think you can broaden the umbrella"],[195,538.2,4.42,"and get more people in to have conversation"],[195,542.66,1.96,"by bringing the temperature down."],[195,545.21,1.38,"Boom."],[195,546.92,1.17,"No, seriously?"],[195,548.84,1.21,"Come on."],[195,557.39,1.5,"Oh, gosh."],[195,558.89,2.59,"NF: It's going to be you, my friend."],[195,561.97,1.21,"It's going to be you."],[195,563.23,4.13,"OK, what is one thing worth rebuilding right now?"],[195,567.4,3.34,"I think we need to rebuild"],[195,570.73,2.71,"our understanding of truth"],[195,573.49,6.26,"so that we can have common ground on what is real and true,"],[195,579.78,3.67,"and common ground on what is fake and untrue."],[195,584.12,3.0,"Without that, we can't ever come together."],[195,591.25,1.17,"AG: Sorry."],[195,595.05,2.34,"Is social media good for democracy?"],[195,597.76,1.25,"N-O."],[195,599.05,1.17,"No. Period."],[195,600.26,2.0,"I feel very, very strongly about that."],[195,602.31,3.34,"I think it was one of the most destructive forces"],[195,605.64,3.46,"towards an effective democracy I've seen in my lifetime."],[195,613.48,1.42,"Be a little more aggressive."],[195,614.9,1.21,"Get after that."],[195,616.82,2.96,"NF: Well, I mean, I might as well be up for the challenge here."],[195,619.82,1.21,"See if I could do it."],[195,627.62,1.29,"AG: There we go."],[195,638.38,1.17,"NF: OK."],[195,639.55,3.5,"My challenge is ..."],[195,643.64,2.54,"I need to fly a kite with a stranger."],[195,646.77,1.17,"Done."],[195,647.98,1.13,"Let's do this."],[195,649.14,1.17,"AG: Good luck."],[195,650.81,2.46,"NF: Guys, I lost a bet -- will you help me?"],[195,653.27,1.71,"You don't know, you're not helpful?"],[195,654.98,1.58,"No, OK, OK, OK, no problem."],[195,656.61,2.17,"He's like, \"Honey, look the other way."],[195,658.82,1.17,"Go past them.\""],[195,660.03,1.13,"That's OK."],[195,662.28,1.88,"Oh, you do want to help me?"],[195,664.2,1.17,"Person: Sure."],[195,665.41,1.42,"NF: She's in, she's in."],[195,666.83,1.92,"Do you notice the breeze totally stopped"],[195,668.79,1.67,"now that I handed you the kite?"],[195,670.5,1.13,"Ready?"],[195,674.0,2.29,"Do you need to, shoot it up in the air, maybe?"],[195,676.34,1.75,"Person: Maybe."],[195,678.13,2.29,"NF: Oh, breeze, breeze there's breeze right now."],[195,680.47,1.38,"Person: Ah, I don't know."],[195,681.89,1.25,"NF: Which way is it going?"],[195,683.18,1.5,"I don't know what we're doing."],[195,687.64,1.71,"Yeah yeah yeah!"],[195,689.39,1.58,"You got it, you got it!"],[195,691.02,1.63,"Did you see, that had liftoff?"],[195,692.65,1.21,"That had liftoff."],[195,693.86,1.71,"That totally counts, right?"],[195,695.57,1.83,"Person: You can do it."],[195,702.49,1.38,"Oh."],[195,705.83,1.21,"NF: I thought you had it."],[195,707.08,1.17,"Person: I don't know."],[195,708.25,2.08,"NF: I thought you had it for a little bit."],[195,710.37,1.54,"Person: Like, maybe like that."],[195,711.92,2.0,"We're going to do one more time."],[195,717.05,1.21,"NF: I think you did it."],[195,718.63,1.75,"Person: We did it, we tried."],[195,720.42,1.25,"That's what counts, right?"],[195,721.72,1.79,"NF: We did it, this is awesome."],[196,14.6,2.52,"Christmas Island in the Indian Ocean."],[196,18.76,1.32,"For millions of years,"],[196,20.08,3.36,"this remote speck of land has been ruled"],[196,23.44,1.32,"by crabs."],[196,30.84,2.72,"Their ancestors came from the sea,"],[196,33.56,4.44,"but most have now adopted a land-based existence."],[196,46.84,1.84,"Given there are so many of them,"],[196,48.68,3.04,"they get along relatively harmoniously."],[196,53.76,3.24,"They're the gardeners and caretakers"],[196,57.0,2.72,"of a tiny crab utopia."],[196,63.2,3.68,"Once a year, they must all return to the sea to breed."],[196,66.88,2.4,"And the march of the red crabs"],[196,69.28,3.96,"is one of the greatest natural spectacles on Earth."],[196,84.68,2.44,"There are 50 million of them."],[196,93.84,4.32,"It's an event that has brought the island worldwide fame."],[196,109.08,1.64,"But in recent years,"],[196,110.72,4.24,"millions of red crabs haven't managed to reach the sea."],[196,120.68,3.16,"An invader has occupied this island."],[196,126.68,2.04,"Yellow crazy ants."],[196,129.76,2.16,"They escaped from visiting ships"],[196,131.92,2.12,"and, with no predators to control them,"],[196,134.04,4.44,"they have now created vast super-colonies in the forest."],[196,144.2,3.2,"When migrating red crabs march into their territory,"],[196,147.4,1.24,"the ants attack,"],[196,150.56,3.12,"squirting acid into the crabs' eyes and mouths."],[196,164.08,2.32,"The crabs have no defence."],[196,170.16,2.92,"Blinded and confused,"],[196,173.08,1.32,"they're doomed."],[196,185.6,3.6,"Humans brought these ant invaders here,"],[196,189.2,2.4,"and now humans are having to control them."],[196,195.44,5.92,"Isolated communities may evolve for millions of years in relative peace."],[196,202.44,2.64,"But when new challenges arrive,"],[196,205.08,2.04,"they can struggle to cope."],[196,209.28,3.56,"Of all the species that have become extinct in recent years,"],[196,213.84,2.96,"around 80% have been islanders."],[197,6.96,4.3,"Andre and Leslie are a deaf couple who have recently decided to have a child,"],[197,11.3,5.17,"and they’re considering using a process called preimplantation genetic diagnosis."],[197,16.68,3.42,"In PGD, an embryo is screened for genetic conditions"],[197,20.1,2.46,"before being transferred into the uterus,"],[197,22.56,2.71,"allowing prospective parents to exclude embryos"],[197,25.27,2.17,"with unwanted genetic conditions."],[197,27.77,5.46,"However, Andre and Leslie are considering using PGD to ensure deafness"],[197,33.24,1.08,"in their child."],[197,34.7,3.42,"While the couple is uneasy exercising such profound control"],[197,38.12,2.63,"over their child’s future, as deaf parents,"],[197,40.74,2.67,"they feel they could provide better guidance to a child"],[197,43.41,4.25,"that would share their lived experience and grow up immersed in deaf culture."],[197,48.42,3.54,"But is it ethical for these two to select for deafness?"],[197,53.17,3.96,"The rise of genetic technologies like PGD have led philosophers"],[197,57.14,1.88,"to consider this kind of question."],[197,59.22,1.96,"And to make sense of these dilemmas,"],[197,61.18,4.25,"many thinkers rely on the “Principle of Procreative Beneficence.”"],[197,65.73,2.63,"Coined by philosopher Julian Savulescu,"],[197,68.48,2.59,"this principle says any prospective parent"],[197,71.07,2.33,"able to select their future child’s traits,"],[197,73.4,3.92,"should give their child the best chance at the best life."],[197,77.91,3.59,"But defining the best life is incredibly tricky."],[197,81.83,4.71,"After all, what exactly makes a life better or worse?"],[197,87.75,1.38,"For many hearing people,"],[197,89.13,3.84,"it’s common to think that deafness must make a life worse—"],[197,93.09,2.75,"regardless of how good the rest of that life may be."],[197,96.17,1.88,"This perception of disability—"],[197,98.05,3.71,"that people with a disability are worse off just for being disabled—"],[197,101.76,4.67,"is what philosopher Elizabeth Barnes calls the “bad difference view.”"],[197,106.6,3.25,"And many philosophers, deaf activists, and deaf individuals"],[197,109.86,2.46,"argue that this view is false."],[197,112.61,3.13,"Barnes herself favors the “mere-difference view,”"],[197,116.07,3.96,"which says that being disabled is neither inherently good nor bad—"],[197,120.24,2.0,"it's merely different."],[197,123.03,4.21,"For example, just as hearing people can have auditory experiences of music"],[197,127.25,1.46,"that deaf people can’t,"],[197,128.83,2.79,"deaf people can experience music through vibrations"],[197,131.67,1.88,"in ways hearing people can't."],[197,133.75,3.04,"The two are simply different experiences."],[197,137.09,4.13,"And Barnes would say the difficulties faced by people with a disability"],[197,141.22,3.34,"most often don’t come from these different experiences,"],[197,144.81,3.04,"but rather, from the ways in which our society"],[197,147.85,3.09,"is not designed for people with disabilities."],[197,151.69,2.92,"Andre and Leslie know their lived experience is different"],[197,154.61,1.33,"from a hearing couple’s—"],[197,156.11,3.63,"in fact, it’s a big part of why they believe they’re better positioned"],[197,159.74,1.46,"to parent a deaf child."],[197,161.82,2.17,"As lifelong members of the deaf community,"],[197,164.08,5.05,"they’ve cherished specific experiences and rituals unique to deaf culture."],[197,169.41,3.38,"So not only do they want their child to be a part of that community,"],[197,172.88,3.46,"but they’re also worried about how to support and guide a child"],[197,176.34,4.75,"that spends most of their time navigating culture they’re largely removed from."],[197,181.64,3.0,"Philosopher Robert Sparrow thinks it's very reasonable"],[197,184.64,3.29,"to believe that parents might be better suited to raise children"],[197,187.93,2.54,"in the culture that they themselves are a part of."],[197,190.56,2.59,"And there's reason to believe that a child who grows up"],[197,193.15,3.92,"feeling understood by their parents and secure in their family unit"],[197,197.24,2.33,"would indeed have a better life."],[197,199.86,4.13,"If this logic is sound, the Principle of Procreative Beneficence"],[197,204.03,5.05,"seems to say that some deaf parents should try to have deaf children."],[197,209.75,5.25,"However, following this principle’s logic to the extreme raises serious issues."],[197,215.29,3.59,"After all, disability is just one of the countless traits"],[197,218.88,4.38,"that can prevent a child from having the best chance at the best life."],[197,223.64,3.09,"Our world is full of discrimination that makes life worse"],[197,226.72,3.46,"for people of certain genders, sexualities, and races."],[197,230.35,3.46,"But it’s clearly wrong to prevent people in these groups from being born"],[197,233.81,3.63,"just because existing prejudices would make their lives worse."],[197,237.61,2.63,"As bioethicist Adrienne Asch worries,"],[197,240.24,3.63,"this attitude suggests the harmful and disrespectful idea"],[197,243.95,3.84,"that the lives of people with a disability are less worth living."],[197,247.91,3.17,"Surely, the better approach is to get rid of the prejudice"],[197,251.08,1.83,"and the accessibility issues,"],[197,252.91,2.71,"rather than the people who experience them."],[197,256.04,2.29,"But intolerance can’t be dismantled overnight,"],[197,258.34,3.0,"and Andre and Leslie are wondering what to do now."],[197,261.67,4.34,"So maybe they owe it to their future child to help them navigate the world as it is—"],[197,266.18,1.71,"selecting against deafness,"],[197,267.93,2.92,"so their child won’t experience that prejudice."],[197,271.18,2.5,"Clearly, even with tools like PGD,"],[197,273.69,3.54,"it's incredibly difficult to ensure the best life for your child."],[197,277.27,3.59,"But maybe genetic intervention is misguided in the first place?"],[197,280.99,4.09,"After all, if parenting is actually an exercise in acceptance"],[197,285.07,1.42,"rather than control,"],[197,286.49,5.59,"perhaps the best path for Andre and Leslie is to simply leave things to chance."],[198,3.38,2.46,"The gods granted Pandora many gifts,"],[198,5.95,2.82,"and one abominable box."],[198,9.12,5.08,"Curiosity got the better of her and chaos was unleashed upon the world."],[198,14.46,2.18,"But that's only the beginning of the story..."],[198,16.81,2.73,"What if instead of accepting the new world order,"],[198,19.54,2.12,"Pandora tried to fix it?"],[198,23.79,4.02,"Pandora’s Legacy is a jigsaw unlike any you’ve experienced before."],[198,28.0,4.05,"You’ll start by assembling the pieces and find areas missing."],[198,32.57,3.07,"To unlock the next section and advance the story,"],[198,35.64,4.01,"you’ll need to solve puzzles embedded within the jigsaw."],[198,39.95,2.54,"The puzzles, crafted by renowned constructors,"],[198,42.49,1.74,"are deep and challenging."],[198,44.46,2.24,"Some will reward you with physical artifacts,"],[198,46.7,1.69,"that you'll then you’ll then figure out how to use"],[198,48.4,2.43,"in ways that will surprise and delight you—"],[198,51.05,1.82,"just like in an escape room."],[198,68.96,2.31,"1,200 plus pieces to assemble."],[198,71.27,1.87,"17 boxes to unlock."],[198,73.32,2.42,"16 devious puzzles to solve."],[198,75.88,2.6,"1 infernal box."],[198,82.96,2.52,"Turn your living room into an escape room;"],[198,85.48,2.02,"let loose the curiosities inside."],[198,87.63,3.66,"This epic experience is available on Kickstarter now!"],[198,91.36,2.85,"Do you dare open Pandora's Box?"],[199,3.62,2.77,"I was one of those overachiever kids."],[199,6.72,1.4,"12 things going at once,"],[199,8.14,2.04,"already dreaming of changing the world."],[199,10.19,4.47,"And I was so excited to go to summer school at Yale"],[199,14.68,1.44,"when I was 16."],[199,16.13,1.38,"I didn't say I was cool."],[199,18.97,3.17,"The classes were tough and thought-provoking."],[199,22.15,3.25,"But that's when something strange started happening to me."],[199,25.42,2.09,"I'd be sitting in class, completely engaged,"],[199,27.53,3.24,"when suddenly, a fog rolled in."],[199,31.36,2.35,"My thoughts fuzzed, my senses dulled,"],[199,33.75,2.04,"the world blurred,"],[199,35.8,3.14,"like someone was slowly turning a dimmer switch on my brain."],[199,39.59,2.05,"I'd pinch my thigh, tap my foot,"],[199,41.67,3.32,"anything to fight this overwhelming urge to sleep."],[199,45.01,2.72,"Once I bit my tongue so hard it bled."],[199,48.36,2.6,"And then the thought would hit me like a hammer."],[199,51.0,3.94,"\"If I don't go to sleep right now, I'm going to die.\""],[199,55.84,2.35,"So somehow, quietly, I'd excuse myself,"],[199,58.21,1.57,"slip into a bathroom stall,"],[199,59.79,2.82,"lean my head against the wall and fall asleep."],[199,63.65,1.87,"When I told my family doctor, she said,"],[199,65.55,2.13,"\"You're busy. Busy people are tired.\""],[199,67.7,1.17,"It felt dismissive,"],[199,68.88,2.85,"but also like some secret initiation into adulthood."],[199,72.74,2.54,"I had big dreams, a lot to accomplish,"],[199,75.31,2.99,"so I figured I just had to get better at being awake."],[199,78.99,2.37,"When I started working in professional politics,"],[199,81.38,2.32,"exhaustion was a badge of honor."],[199,84.38,2.44,"The later you stayed up, the more coffee you drank,"],[199,86.85,1.72,"the more worthy you felt."],[199,88.6,2.75,"Society convinced us that if we were tired,"],[199,91.37,1.69,"we were doing it right."],[199,94.34,3.47,"In 2008, I joined the Obama Advance team,"],[199,97.83,1.8,"the people responsible for everything"],[199,99.65,2.9,"from logistics to aesthetics on the campaign."],[199,102.57,1.67,"But the thing about Advance,"],[199,104.25,2.07,"you're in a new city every four to six days,"],[199,106.34,1.49,"regularly pulling all-nighters,"],[199,107.84,2.59,"on behalf of the future president of the United States."],[199,110.81,2.25,"The stakes were high, it was fast-paced,"],[199,113.09,2.44,"everyone was exhausted."],[199,115.55,5.46,"I was surrounded by the best, the brightest and the busiest."],[199,121.5,3.82,"But I never saw them sneaking into the bathroom to nap like I did."],[199,126.93,2.04,"I thought I must be lazy,"],[199,128.99,3.42,"that I lacked the willpower everyone else seemed to have."],[199,133.52,3.57,"And underneath all that drive,"],[199,137.1,3.99,"I felt utterly alone in my inability to keep up."],[199,142.42,3.05,"As the years passed, I kept bringing up my sleepiness to doctors"],[199,145.49,4.14,"who kept pushing the same glossy handouts on improving my sleep hygiene."],[199,149.65,2.12,"My gut knew something else was going on."],[199,152.63,1.47,"And then came the dreams."],[199,154.95,2.2,"I'd wake up frozen in bed,"],[199,157.17,2.2,"convinced something terrifying was in the room."],[199,159.41,2.8,"I could see it, but I couldn't move or scream."],[199,162.23,3.54,"One doctor said it was just me manifesting stress from my job."],[199,166.33,3.07,"One night I woke to a child whispering,"],[199,169.42,1.99,"\"Can I hold your hand?\""],[199,173.17,2.67,"The next day I saged my entire bedroom,"],[199,175.86,1.77,"like, it never crossed my mind"],[199,177.64,1.89,"I might have a neurological disorder."],[199,182.36,1.78,"At 35, I hit my wall."],[199,184.7,2.3,"I could fall asleep, but I couldn't stay asleep."],[199,187.04,1.47,"I was awake most of the night,"],[199,188.52,2.47,"falling back asleep just before my alarm went off."],[199,191.02,3.69,"Most days I felt like a lunatic zombie, barely holding it together."],[199,194.73,1.4,"I would drop things suddenly,"],[199,196.16,2.47,"in the middle of stressful meetings at work."],[199,198.65,3.27,"At my wit's end, I begged my general practitioner for help."],[199,202.52,3.57,"She ordered an at-home sleep test, which came back negative for sleep apnea."],[199,206.12,1.45,"And I was told, \"You're fine.\""],[199,207.89,1.5,"I marched back into her office"],[199,209.41,2.39,"with a full defense that something else was wrong."],[199,212.13,1.99,"She stopped me mid-sentence and admitted,"],[199,214.13,3.15,"\"We're officially beyond both our knowledge of sleep.\""],[199,217.3,1.78,"I was referred to a sleep specialist,"],[199,219.12,3.8,"which is a doctor trained to diagnose and treat sleep disorders."],[199,223.54,1.77,"And two months later,"],[199,225.34,3.19,"after 19 years of symptoms,"],[199,228.56,2.74,"shame and self-doubt,"],[199,231.33,1.78,"I was diagnosed with narcolepsy,"],[199,233.15,4.05,"a chronic neurological condition of the brain's sleep-wake cycle."],[199,237.22,3.15,"And before you say, \"19 years, that's extreme.\""],[199,240.82,1.44,"It's not."],[199,242.27,4.04,"The average diagnosis period for narcolepsy is eight to 15 years."],[199,246.79,3.32,"And that's because in medical schools worldwide,"],[199,250.13,2.15,"doctors receive, on average,"],[199,252.3,4.92,"less than three hours of education on sleep"],[199,257.64,4.3,"total across their entire medical training."],[199,261.98,3.07,"No wonder no doctor thought my sleepiness was an issue"],[199,265.06,2.12,"or asked any follow-up questions."],[199,268.35,2.79,"Narcolepsy has five distinct symptoms that, on their own,"],[199,271.15,1.9,"seem completely unrelated."],[199,273.07,3.57,"Excessive sleepiness that can feel like you've been awake for 48 hours,"],[199,276.66,2.19,"even after a full night’s rest:"],[199,278.86,3.67,"nighttime hallucinations, sleep paralysis, disrupted sleep"],[199,282.55,1.58,"and sometimes cataplexy --"],[199,284.17,2.95,"a loss of muscle tone with strong emotions."],[199,287.15,2.17,"Which is why I don't have slides in this talk,"],[199,289.34,2.64,"because I was afraid I would drop the remote."],[199,292.01,4.96,"I never walked into a doctor’s office and listed them all together"],[199,296.98,4.39,"because there was no way for me to know they belonged to the same story."],[199,301.67,2.64,"The same way there was no way for me to know"],[199,304.34,2.57,"my tired was different."],[199,307.44,3.15,"But for one in five of us, our tired is different."],[199,310.99,1.5,"While narcolepsy might be rare,"],[199,312.51,3.5,"it’s estimated [that] 20 percent of the population has a sleep disorder --"],[199,316.35,2.3,"everything from sleep apnea to chronic insomnia,"],[199,318.67,1.42,"restless leg syndrome"],[199,320.1,2.75,"and other conditions that quietly steal rest."],[199,323.19,3.27,"And hundreds of millions of people worldwide"],[199,326.48,2.3,"are walking around exhausted,"],[199,328.79,3.14,"untreated and thinking it’s normal to feel this way."],[199,332.48,2.2,"We've been taught to push through exhaustion"],[199,334.72,1.78,"instead of asking about it."],[199,336.88,2.25,"This isn't an epidemic of laziness."],[199,339.5,2.64,"It's a failure of diagnosis."],[199,342.93,2.72,"We dismiss people with sleep disorders all the time."],[199,345.68,3.25,"We think sleep apnea only affects middle-aged men with thick necks"],[199,348.95,1.28,"and square jaws."],[199,351.43,3.87,"But it can affect anyone, at any age and any size."],[199,355.32,3.27,"Now, thankfully, we've started to recognize that sleep is vital."],[199,358.61,3.94,"One of the most important things you can do for your physical or mental health."],[199,362.58,4.14,"When you get enough sleep, your body performs quiet miracles."],[199,366.75,3.84,"Repairing cells, balancing hormones, clearing toxins from your brain."],[199,370.89,3.54,"Sleep is where creativity is born, memories are filed"],[199,374.46,2.95,"and the subconscious gets to color outside the lines."],[199,377.83,2.55,"Sleep is the engine of productivity."],[199,380.88,2.27,"Now good sleep hygiene matters."],[199,383.17,2.92,"But for the one in five of us living with a sleep disorder,"],[199,386.1,2.74,"sleep hygiene alone isn't a cure."],[199,388.85,2.34,"No perfect bedtime routine, no weighted blanket,"],[199,391.21,1.64,"no lavender spray on your pillow"],[199,392.86,1.95,"will keep your airway open at night."],[199,395.66,4.77,"Because when the problem is neurological, structural or biochemical,"],[199,400.47,3.47,"without medical intervention, your body pays the price."],[199,404.42,2.35,"Aside from brain fog, loss of productivity"],[199,406.81,2.42,"and the dangers of drowsy driving,"],[199,409.26,2.85,"over time, when sleep disorders go undiagnosed,"],[199,412.14,3.55,"they silently break the body down from the inside out,"],[199,415.71,2.32,"fueling inflammation, accelerating aging,"],[199,418.05,1.87,"driving insulin resistance"],[199,419.94,2.49,"and raising the risk of heart attack,"],[199,422.45,2.25,"stroke [and] even Alzheimer’s."],[199,425.26,2.04,"This isn't just exhaustion."],[199,427.61,3.75,"It’s a slow erosion of health, focus"],[199,431.38,1.45,"and longevity."],[199,433.7,1.8,"But it's also an identity issue."],[199,436.45,3.45,"I didn't become a whole person until I was diagnosed with narcolepsy."],[199,439.92,1.89,"Before that, I was working or sleeping."],[199,441.82,1.78,"There was nothing in between."],[199,443.62,3.82,"But with treatment, the world came alive to me."],[199,447.46,3.69,"Colors got brighter, [and] I felt a full range of emotions."],[199,451.17,1.77,"I started having hobbies."],[199,454.79,1.8,"I found my voice."],[199,457.82,3.47,"Before my narcolepsy diagnosis, I didn't think I was creative."],[199,461.88,3.52,"In the last eight years of treatment, I've written two pilot scripts,"],[199,465.41,2.25,"[written] and published a whole book,"],[199,467.7,1.57,"became a keynote speaker"],[199,469.3,2.64,"and had articles published in national outlets."],[199,473.2,4.54,"Before my diagnosis, my world felt extraordinarily limited."],[199,478.53,3.9,"Now there's no limit to my capacity."],[199,482.45,3.29,"So consider this your activation,"],[199,485.75,3.02,"your permission slip, your reclamation,"],[199,488.8,4.42,"your literal wake-up call to question not just your sleep,"],[199,493.24,2.44,"but how you feel during the day."],[199,495.69,2.55,"If something is off, ask for help,"],[199,498.26,1.37,"demand answers,"],[199,499.67,2.05,"see a sleep specialist."],[199,502.03,1.95,"Not just for your health,"],[199,504.0,3.65,"but for the idea, the project, the relationship,"],[199,507.69,3.0,"the version of you that's waiting to wake up."],[199,511.13,3.24,"Because I want you to live your biggest,"],[199,514.38,1.35,"most beautiful life --"],[199,515.73,1.33,"full of color,"],[199,517.1,1.99,"curiosity and awe."],[199,519.1,2.89,"Wild and wide awake."],[199,523.09,2.59,"So I'm asking you,"],[199,525.71,2.7,"is your tired different?"],[200,0.66,3.09,"I don’t think you have to have any kind of formal training at all"],[200,3.75,1.08,"to be a songwriter."],[200,4.83,1.33,"Again, I work with people,"],[200,6.17,3.84,"brilliant people with huge hits all the time that don't play an instrument."],[200,10.01,1.83,"So don't let that dissuade you."],[200,13.05,3.96,"Hey, you! Yes, you. Is there music inside of you?"],[200,17.05,3.17,"We’ve recruited working musicians from throughout the industry"],[200,20.22,6.26,"to help you hear it, hold it, and share it with this wild and wonderful world."],[200,28.15,3.29,"I think growing up, I assumed, like many people,"],[200,31.44,3.54,"that the artists that I loved and listened to wrote all of their own music."],[200,34.99,3.0,"I didn't realize that there were other people behind the scenes,"],[200,37.99,2.42,"and that it really takes a village to write a song."],[200,40.41,3.71,"Some artists write everything themselves, but most of the artists that you hear"],[200,44.12,1.0,"are a collaboration."],[200,45.12,3.21,"The first artist that I was aware of that wrote songs for themselves"],[200,48.34,2.59,"and also wrote songs for other people, was Carole King."],[200,50.92,3.46,"And then I heard that she had had a whole career before she was an artist"],[200,54.38,1.63,"of writing songs for other people."],[200,56.01,3.21,"That was kind of like a light bulb went off for me where I was like,"],[200,59.22,2.92,"that can be a job? That’s something that you can actually do?"],[200,62.14,1.63,"When it comes to making a song,"],[200,63.77,3.92,"and people realizing their favorite artist did not write that song,"],[200,67.86,2.84,"that’s sometimes the job of a songwriter,"],[200,70.69,4.67,"is to make sure that it feels like it came from your favorite artist."],[200,75.66,2.79,"And that is songwriting at its best."],[200,78.45,1.83,"It's to make sure that it's authentic."],[200,80.28,1.75,"There's two different ways of going."],[200,82.04,3.09,"Whether you’re pitching a song, meaning you’re selling it,"],[200,85.12,2.13,"just randomly and doing that,"],[200,87.25,4.17,"or whether you're getting in and taking a really collaborative effort together."],[200,92.34,3.71,"So there are two ways that in pop music that songs,"],[200,96.05,1.63,"let’s call it getting placed."],[200,98.14,2.71,"The first way is you are in a room with an artist."],[200,101.06,3.59,"It’s a producer or two, or a songwriter or two,"],[200,104.64,2.04,"and an artist— only one."],[200,106.89,2.79,"And you're making that piece of music with the artist"],[200,109.69,1.92,"and you are collaborating with them."],[200,111.65,3.21,"You are basically helping, bringing their vision to life."],[200,115.03,3.42,"Sometimes artists come in and they know exactly what they want to say"],[200,118.45,1.42,"and they don't need any help."],[200,119.87,2.75,"And you’re kind of like artwork or you’re just supportive."],[200,122.62,2.71,"And sometimes they come in and they don't know what to say"],[200,125.33,1.96,"and you are helping them figure that out."],[200,127.33,1.38,"So that's the first way."],[200,128.71,2.38,"The second way is what’s called a pitch song,"],[200,131.09,3.42,"where you and a group of other writers and producers,"],[200,134.67,3.09,"just a group of musicians get together and you write a song."],[200,137.84,5.88,"And then that song then gets pitched to an artist or multiple artists"],[200,143.72,4.0,"to see if it is something that they would like to sing themselves"],[200,147.73,1.25,"and hopefully release."],[200,150.35,2.34,"When you go in to do a pitch song,"],[200,152.69,3.21,"which is a song that you write without the artist in the room,"],[200,155.94,2.67,"you take the brand of the artist into consideration,"],[200,158.61,2.0,"you take the genre into consideration."],[200,160.7,2.25,"Oftentimes, the label will send you a brief,"],[200,162.95,4.13,"and that is sort of details of themes that the artist wants to explore."],[200,167.62,1.54,"Placing a pitch song,"],[200,169.29,6.05,"it’s kind of like going in your closet, regardless of your shape, size, and style"],[200,175.59,1.63,"and pulling out a suit,"],[200,177.38,5.34,"and then you hand it to this artist and you go, I hope this fits you perfectly."],[200,182.89,2.08,"Start to think about the key of the song."],[200,184.97,3.04,"You start to think about the vocal range of this artist."],[200,188.02,2.54,"Are they the type of artist that singing all the runs,"],[200,190.56,1.88,"and are they the vocalists of our time,"],[200,192.44,2.63,"or is it giving a different kind of vocal style?"],[200,195.07,2.25,"If I do a little bit of research on an artist"],[200,197.32,2.59,"and I see that they’re going through a breakup"],[200,199.9,3.71,"or they came from a certain kind of a background,"],[200,203.62,2.84,"I try to find things that I have in common with the artists"],[200,206.45,2.59,"so that I can write from an authentic place for me."],[200,210.08,3.21,"Let’s just say they’re in the room— we’ll pick that adventure."],[200,213.29,2.25,"The next question becomes, how's your day?"],[200,215.54,1.88,"Starts off with another conversation:"],[200,217.42,2.54,"where are we right now in this moment?"],[200,220.09,4.46,"For me, I like to get an idea of what is your idea of success as an artist?"],[200,225.1,4.42,"Because a hit song is very, very different based off of the artist, right?"],[200,229.6,3.21,"A Billboard Hot 100 hit song or a Grammy nomination."],[200,232.81,3.13,"Two different forms of success— both very successful."],[200,235.94,4.63,"So let's say the artist is more concerned with popularity"],[200,240.74,3.17,"and having the hottest record on the Billboard Hot 100,"],[200,243.91,1.96,"because that’s all that that means—"],[200,245.87,3.17,"if he goes to number 1, this is the most popular song in the world."],[200,249.04,3.21,"I say, okay, well, I know it needs to be catchy,"],[200,252.5,2.34,"I know it needs to be relatable,"],[200,255.04,2.75,"and I know that it needs to be straight to the point."],[200,257.92,3.75,"That also gives me a lot of reference on whether we're going to have a bridge"],[200,261.67,1.38,"on this song or not."],[200,263.05,5.09,"It also gives me some perspective as to how relatable it needs to be."],[200,268.18,4.25,"You’re more likely to connect to typical kind of pop chords"],[200,272.44,2.33,"that we kind of hear over and over and over again."],[200,274.77,4.59,"We've listened to the same chords for however many years."],[200,279.4,4.46,"However, how you put them together is what makes something feel familiar enough"],[200,284.07,1.67,"to resonate with people,"],[200,285.74,2.63,"but not familiar enough that you’re in a lawsuit."],[200,288.37,3.04,"Versus the person who is wanting to make an artistic statement."],[200,291.41,2.04,"And they might go a little bit weird,"],[200,293.46,3.25,"like some of our jazz artists or our punk artists."],[200,297.17,3.13,"There’s very different intentions as an artist, right?"],[200,300.3,3.5,"And I think that it’s very important for songwriters specifically"],[200,303.8,3.29,"to have all of these things going through their mind"],[200,307.09,1.88,"because that's how we make it work."],[200,310.72,2.29,"Most of the times I start off with the melody."],[200,313.02,5.3,"However, there's some times where I come in and I'm like, this is the lyric."],[200,318.31,3.13,"And that might have started because of a conversation."],[200,321.52,1.63,"So each one is different."],[200,323.15,3.96,"But for me, melody is king and then lyrics come later."],[200,327.49,4.04,"A lot of my early songwriting experiences in the industry"],[200,331.54,3.25,"were in rooms where someone would play a track."],[200,334.87,3.5,"And then, the writers in the room will all go into the booth"],[200,338.38,4.25,"and freestyle a bunch of melodies, and then you build the song from there."],[200,342.96,3.17,"But the last five years now I've been living in Nashville,"],[200,346.13,2.75,"where the songwriting process is the exact opposite,"],[200,348.89,4.34,"where you come in with a title and a concept for the song,"],[200,353.39,4.96,"and then you build the song more from a lyrical and conceptual perspective."],[200,358.44,3.5,"And then almost try to retrofit some of those lyrics"],[200,362.07,2.75,"to melodies that might be able to house them,"],[200,364.82,3.38,"because the lyric is most important here in Nashville."],[200,368.86,1.75,"If you want to get into songwriting,"],[200,370.62,4.21,"I think it's important to listen to a lot of music and to study the structure"],[200,374.83,3.96,"and think about rhyme scheme and dynamics between the different parts,"],[200,378.79,3.17,"and just notice what the songs that you like are doing."],[200,382.17,2.04,"And leave your ego at the door."],[200,384.34,1.92,"Do what's best for the art."],[200,386.42,2.63,"Nobody’s going to know who wrote what in the room."],[200,389.05,3.34,"Nobody’s going to know who came up with this and who came up with that."],[200,392.39,1.0,"Nobody cares."],[200,393.39,5.05,"The point is, if somebody in the room has a better idea than you— great!"],[200,398.44,3.0,"You get to be a part of that and you get to learn from that."],[200,401.44,4.34,"So, I think the most important thing is to learn how to collaborate,"],[200,405.78,2.67,"know your strengths, know your weaknesses,"],[200,408.57,3.67,"and try to find people that can sort of fill in the gap for you."],[200,412.24,1.96,"So if you're not super strong on melody,"],[200,414.2,1.79,"find somebody who’s strong on melody."],[200,416.0,2.5,"And the two of you together can make something great."],[200,418.5,4.0,"My advice for anybody that wants a career as a songwriter or a producer"],[200,422.5,2.63,"would be to work very, very, very hard at it."],[200,425.46,1.0,"Just keep on working."],[200,426.63,2.46,"Just keep on going, keep on writing songs."],[200,429.09,2.71,"When you write that song that you think is your best song,"],[200,431.8,3.09,"I promise you, you’ve got better; just keep on doing it."],[200,435.01,4.92,"It is a sport and it’s a muscle and it needs to be exercised, and keep on going,"],[200,439.94,2.79,"and you will end up in a place musically"],[200,442.73,2.25,"that you probably didn’t even know existed."],[201,0.76,2.54,"Imagine you just died."],[201,4.37,4.04,"You need to get your spouse a message that could save their life."],[201,9.64,1.47,"Here's the problem."],[201,11.14,4.41,"The only person who can hear you is Whoopi Goldberg."],[201,16.88,1.33,"Yes."],[201,18.21,2.9,"It's true, this is the plot of the movie \"Ghost.\""],[201,22.15,4.47,"Patrick Swayze is trying to communicate with Demi Moore through a psychic."],[201,26.99,1.4,"But imagine that's you."],[201,29.83,3.8,"What could you say in just a few urgent seconds"],[201,33.66,2.74,"that would convince your spouse it's really you?"],[201,36.97,1.6,"Patrick's first attempt fails."],[201,38.6,1.2,"\"Tell her I love her.\""],[201,39.84,2.1,"Too obvious, too generic."],[201,42.41,3.27,"But then he knows, it’s just one word."],[201,46.24,1.27,"\"Ditto.\""],[201,47.54,4.87,"Because “ditto” was their secret code for “I love you.”"],[201,52.85,3.37,"Something that only made sense inside the private world"],[201,56.25,1.83,"the two of them had built together."],[201,59.66,3.9,"I think of that private world as a relationship's lovemotif."],[201,64.63,3.64,"Just as a leitmotif is a recurring musical theme"],[201,68.27,2.0,"that identifies a character,"],[201,70.27,6.51,"a lovemotif is a recurring set of shared experiences and meanings"],[201,76.81,2.6,"that uniquely define a relationship."],[201,80.14,2.7,"Your lovemotif includes the words"],[201,82.88,3.94,"that would pass your own version of the “ditto test,”"],[201,86.85,4.04,"but also your weird in-jokes,"],[201,90.92,2.37,"your distinctive moral code."],[201,94.29,3.94,"It turns out lovemotifs matter a lot."],[201,99.13,2.47,"I’m a psychological scientist who [has] studied love"],[201,101.6,1.8,"for nearly 30 years."],[201,103.4,2.17,"I wrote a book, \"The All-or-Nothing Marriage,\""],[201,105.57,2.3,"to explain how the best relationships work."],[201,107.87,4.0,"I cohost a podcast that analyzes romantic movies"],[201,111.91,2.57,"from the perspective of relationship science."],[201,114.51,1.97,"It's called \"Love Factually.\""],[201,117.71,6.04,"But it was only recently that I discovered just how important lovemotifs are."],[201,124.72,2.9,"In fact, they matter more today than ever before."],[201,127.66,3.67,"Because a lovemotif isn't just something you do."],[201,132.66,2.97,"It helps to define who you are."],[201,137.43,4.54,"These days, around much of the world, we feel increasingly responsible --"],[201,142.0,2.97,"obligated -- to be true to ourselves."],[201,146.01,2.67,"And in almost any long-term relationship,"],[201,148.71,4.57,"you'll have moments where it feels like that pursuit of authenticity"],[201,153.32,2.4,"is at odds with your relationship commitment."],[201,156.19,2.27,"How should we think about those moments?"],[201,158.49,1.4,"In the past,"],[201,159.92,3.44,"couples who got divorced felt ashamed for getting divorced."],[201,164.19,2.97,"These days, it's increasingly the opposite."],[201,167.2,1.47,"We feel shame for staying"],[201,168.66,2.8,"if it makes us feel like we're not authentic."],[201,171.5,2.47,"But there's an assumption lurking here"],[201,174.0,3.5,"that in those moments,"],[201,177.54,3.17,"we struggle to be true to ourselves"],[201,180.74,3.1,"and true to our relationship at the same time."],[201,184.55,3.2,"We see this assumption in the movie \"La La Land.\""],[201,188.28,3.67,"Ryan Gosling and Emma Stone have a sparkling connection."],[201,191.99,2.3,"And yet, the relationship fails."],[201,195.29,2.9,"Not because there's no love,"],[201,198.23,3.04,"not because they don't understand or support each other,"],[201,201.3,4.44,"but because in those me-versus-us moments,"],[201,205.77,2.4,"the relationship never gets a voice."],[201,209.04,3.07,"Emma has this exciting opportunity overseas and Ryan says,"],[201,212.14,2.23,"“You’ve got to give this everything you’ve got."],[201,214.38,1.5,"It's your dream.\""],[201,215.91,3.37,"But he tells her, \"I've got to follow my own plan."],[201,219.32,1.37,"Stay here.\""],[201,221.05,5.27,"This logic, as irrefutable as they find it, is flawed."],[201,226.99,3.54,"That's not how authenticity actually works."],[201,232.5,2.07,"Before we unpack that,"],[201,234.56,1.53,"I'd like to call your attention"],[201,236.1,3.4,"to something kind of amazing that we often overlook."],[201,240.7,2.7,"At the end of a dinner party with other couples,"],[201,243.44,2.8,"most of us feel lucky that the person we go home with"],[201,246.28,1.77,"is our own partner."],[201,251.18,3.04,"Where does that sense of compatibility come from?"],[201,254.52,3.37,"We're told it comes from finding the right person."],[201,258.39,3.8,"The idea is that we possess a constellation of attributes --"],[201,262.23,2.9,"our personality, our habits, our preferences --"],[201,265.16,3.74,"and we need to find somebody whose attributes fit with our own."],[201,269.27,1.87,"It’s as if we’re a gear,"],[201,271.17,1.37,"and we need to keep shopping"],[201,272.54,3.2,"until we find a gear that's the right shape and size for us,"],[201,275.77,2.17,"one that will minimize friction"],[201,277.97,1.74,"as we rotate through life together."],[201,279.71,1.97,"We can even find one on our phone."],[201,284.08,3.37,"My “Love Factually” cohost, Paul Eastwick, and I"],[201,287.48,4.71,"have spent decades studying this issue across tens of thousands of couples."],[201,292.19,1.53,"Here’s the surprise:"],[201,294.16,4.47,"compatibility is not something that you find."],[201,299.23,1.3,"In some of our studies,"],[201,300.53,3.17,"including at speed-dating events that I emceed,"],[201,303.7,3.77,"we asked singles to tell us about the attributes they were seeking."],[201,308.07,3.77,"Things like physical attractiveness or sense of humor or kindness."],[201,312.24,4.0,"And then we introduced them to a bunch of potential partners"],[201,316.25,1.53,"who varied on those attributes."],[201,317.81,4.1,"Some of them were hotter or funnier or kinder than others."],[201,323.35,6.07,"You'd expect that those people who told us they cared a lot about how,"],[201,329.46,3.24,"say, physically attractive a partner was"],[201,332.73,2.14,"would be more attracted to hotter partners"],[201,334.9,2.54,"than people who said they didn't care as much."],[201,338.6,1.67,"You'd be wrong."],[201,340.27,3.3,"The attributes that we think we're looking for"],[201,343.61,4.57,"cease to matter once we meet a live flesh-and-blood partner."],[201,348.68,1.97,"Same thing with similarity."],[201,350.68,2.27,"It feels like we should be especially compatible"],[201,352.95,1.94,"with people who are more similar to us."],[201,355.55,1.23,"We aren't."],[201,357.49,2.84,"What actually matters"],[201,360.36,2.6,"is something smaller and more profound."],[201,364.76,1.74,"A phrase gets repeated."],[201,367.8,1.84,"A joke sticks."],[201,370.2,2.37,"A ritual forms,"],[201,372.57,2.13,"and a private world takes shape."],[201,376.04,5.27,"Two people across countless tiny interactions,"],[201,381.34,2.7,"building something that never existed before."],[201,385.38,1.47,"That's compatibility."],[201,387.55,2.14,"Not finding the right person."],[201,390.95,2.1,"Building your private world together."],[201,393.82,2.37,"I'll give you an example from my own relationship."],[201,396.19,2.67,"It dates back to our earliest months together."],[201,398.86,3.04,"Our \"ditto\" was \"belly full of wine.\""],[201,402.3,2.7,"That's a line from a Beatles song called \"Her Majesty,\""],[201,405.04,1.37,"which includes the couplet"],[201,406.4,4.0,"\"I want to tell her that I love her a lot, but I got to get a belly full of wine.\""],[201,411.11,3.8,"Whenever I wanted to tell Alison that I loved her,"],[201,414.94,2.33,"or was grateful for her,"],[201,417.31,2.97,"\"belly full of wine\" delivered."],[201,421.28,6.27,"And as couples repeat and riff on these tiny moments,"],[201,427.56,2.47,"they cease to be random moments."],[201,430.83,2.5,"They become a lovemotif."],[201,434.33,1.67,"They become who we are."],[201,436.97,1.8,"That's pretty cool,"],[201,438.8,4.1,"because it offers a happy answer to that question from earlier"],[201,442.94,1.97,"about whether we can be true to ourselves"],[201,444.94,2.04,"and true to our relationship."],[201,447.41,3.9,"And once our relationship becomes part of our identity,"],[201,451.31,3.64,"being true to it stops feeling like self-denial."],[201,456.09,2.47,"It starts feeling like authenticity."],[201,459.79,3.57,"Which is why we need to find our lovemotif."],[201,464.56,4.64,"And why, once we've found it, we need to nurture and protect it."],[201,470.13,3.54,"If our partner starts pulling away from our private world,"],[201,473.7,2.3,"it’s not just disappointing --"],[201,476.04,1.37,"it's devastating."],[201,477.71,2.6,"Because what we're losing,"],[201,480.34,3.2,"what’s being rejected isn’t just an overture --"],[201,483.58,1.3,"it's an identity."],[201,485.68,2.57,"Maybe you've seen the movie \"(500) Days of Summer.\""],[201,488.75,2.84,"In one scene, Joseph Gordon-Levitt and Zooey Deschanel"],[201,491.62,2.7,"are play-acting domestic life at IKEA."],[201,494.66,2.47,"She jokingly says, “The kitchen sink is broken.”"],[201,497.16,2.2,"He says, \"Well, that's OK because --\""],[201,499.4,1.97,"and he runs over to the next display --"],[201,501.36,2.27,"\"That's why we bought a home with two kitchens.\""],[201,504.13,2.97,"They race over to the IKEA bedding department,"],[201,507.14,1.9,"and they have sort of a romantic moment"],[201,509.04,2.33,"until they discover that they're being watched."],[201,511.41,4.24,"It's playful, it's romantic, it's theirs."],[201,516.48,4.3,"But later, when Joseph reaches out for that ritual"],[201,520.82,2.77,"on a subsequent shopping trip,"],[201,523.62,1.53,"she won't meet him there."],[201,525.59,4.34,"What breaks your heart for him isn’t just the rejection --"],[201,529.93,3.77,"it's the loss of a private world that no longer exists."],[201,534.96,5.37,"And now we can see what went wrong with Emma and Ryan in “La La Land.”"],[201,540.34,4.94,"They realized too late that loyalty to their private world,"],[201,545.31,3.6,"to their playful banter, to their dancing,"],[201,548.95,2.6,"wouldn't have been a betrayal of authenticity."],[201,552.05,2.4,"It would have been an expression of it."],[201,555.05,4.17,"They have that final, haunting glance in the jazz club,"],[201,559.22,3.7,"and we see the Parisian life they might have lived."],[201,563.49,3.74,"The apartment, the child, the ordinary Tuesdays."],[201,567.86,2.64,"Theirs wasn't a failure of love."],[201,571.23,2.04,"It was a failure to protect a world."],[201,574.57,3.27,"And a beautiful relationship became a wistful memory."],[201,578.21,4.64,"In one study, we asked couples to tell us about their own lovemotif."],[201,583.28,4.27,"They told us about their October-long horror movie calendar."],[201,588.25,2.1,"They told us about the special song"],[201,590.39,2.37,"they sing to their baby daughter every Shabbat."],[201,592.79,2.34,"And this one is my favorite:"],[201,595.16,3.5,"they told us about surfing on a shrimp sandwich."],[201,600.96,2.13,"My colleague Lydia Emery and I"],[201,603.13,2.97,"kept tabs on these couples every day for weeks."],[201,606.1,3.7,"We're still finalizing the article, but the results are clear."],[201,609.84,4.8,"Couples who leaned more strongly into their lovemotif were happier."],[201,614.64,1.3,"And in fact,"],[201,615.98,3.64,"those who leaned more strongly into their lovemotif"],[201,619.65,1.97,"on a given day were happier"],[201,621.65,3.07,"than on days when they didn't lean as much into it."],[201,624.75,2.37,"Turns out it's not just about date nights."],[201,627.59,3.54,"It's about savoring your private world together."],[201,631.76,2.67,"Alison left me this Post-it note a decade ago."],[201,634.8,4.91,"The reason I can show it to you today is that it still lives in my cabinet,"],[201,639.74,1.57,"where I see it all the time."],[201,642.34,1.67,"I've moved offices since then."],[201,644.71,2.34,"This Post-it note moved with me."],[201,647.74,2.33,"That's the thing about a lovemotif."],[201,650.88,1.74,"To the outside world,"],[201,652.65,1.47,"looks trivial."],[201,654.12,2.67,"From the inside, it's sacred."],[201,658.19,3.4,"We’re often told that relationships lose something over time:"],[201,662.16,2.5,"the novelty, the excitement, the passion."],[201,665.2,1.2,"It's true."],[201,666.43,2.07,"Some of that stuff goes."],[201,670.23,3.27,"But something deeper grows."],[201,674.1,4.5,"That shared history, that private world that means nothing to anyone else"],[201,678.61,1.4,"and everything to you."],[201,680.68,1.43,"That world matters."],[201,682.61,1.2,"So how do we build it?"],[201,683.81,1.7,"We've already seen the three moves."],[201,685.55,1.7,"First, find it."],[201,687.82,1.9,"Pay attention to those moments"],[201,689.72,2.77,"when something uniquely \"us\" is starting to emerge."],[201,692.99,1.53,"Next, feed it."],[201,694.56,2.94,"Revisit those moments, nurture them into rituals,"],[201,697.53,1.83,"into inside jokes."],[201,699.39,2.54,"And finally, fight for it."],[201,701.97,4.4,"Against distraction, against drift,"],[201,706.4,4.1,"against the delusion that the tiny stuff can’t really matter."],[201,710.97,2.77,"That's how those shared symbols,"],[201,713.78,2.4,"something as small as a Post-it note,"],[201,716.21,2.94,"become protectors of your private realm."],[201,720.88,3.24,"Someday, one of you will be gone."],[201,725.22,4.41,"And the other will be left holding a hundred tiny symbols"],[201,729.66,2.8,"that mean nothing to anyone else and everything to you."],[201,734.2,1.74,"Please,"],[201,735.97,2.84,"while there's still time,"],[201,738.8,2.44,"build your lovemotif."],[201,742.11,2.27,"So when it matters most,"],[201,744.41,2.37,"one word will be enough."],[201,747.68,1.17,"Thank you."],[202,6.96,1.75,"If you want to learn a fish’s age,"],[202,8.71,1.75,"simply take one of its scales"],[202,10.46,2.92,"and count the number of bands radiating from its center."],[202,13.43,4.5,"For trees, you can count their rings; for narwhals, their tusk layers;"],[202,18.06,3.17,"and for blue whales, their layers of earwax."],[202,21.64,1.38,"When it comes to humans,"],[202,23.14,3.67,"scientists have yet to find any visible traits that mark our age"],[202,26.81,2.67,"with anywhere near the same specificity."],[202,29.65,4.25,"But in the past few decades, they’ve discovered small, invisible markers"],[202,33.91,3.13,"hidden within the body that do change over time."],[202,37.03,5.59,"And they may hold even more information about our health, history, and future."],[202,43.0,3.38,"The first of these markers was discovered in the 1990s."],[202,46.46,3.84,"Telomeres are repetitive sequences at the ends of DNA strands"],[202,50.3,2.75,"that protect chromosomes from fusing together."],[202,53.3,4.75,"However, each time a cell divides, part of the telomere is cut off."],[202,58.3,4.71,"These small losses can add up over time, and once telomeres get too short,"],[202,63.06,3.38,"cells lose their ability to replicate and eventually die."],[202,66.77,4.67,"This gradual shrinking process led scientists to identify telomere length"],[202,71.44,4.3,"as a promising, albeit imperfect, marker of aging."],[202,76.16,1.75,"As technology has advanced,"],[202,77.91,4.38,"scientists learned how to detect even more discreet changes to DNA,"],[202,82.29,4.84,"like the addition of individual molecular tags called methyl groups."],[202,87.29,3.38,"This DNA methylation can control gene expression,"],[202,90.67,2.71,"including switching genes on and off."],[202,93.55,2.25,"It can occur rapidly and frequently,"],[202,95.8,4.46,"allowing cells to respond to different situations and perform normal functions."],[202,100.39,3.5,"Yet researchers noticed that at certain sites in the genome,"],[202,103.89,4.88,"methylation levels tend to change more gradually and predictably as we age."],[202,109.02,2.04,"The reason for this is still unknown,"],[202,111.06,2.75,"but these changes may be associated with stress"],[202,114.03,3.34,"or caused by random errors during DNA replication."],[202,117.86,4.46,"By measuring DNA methylation patterns from hundreds or thousands of these sites,"],[202,122.41,3.67,"scientists have developed what they call epigenetic clocks"],[202,126.08,1.92,"as another way to measure aging."],[202,128.67,3.96,"A third way our bodies change over time is through inflammation."],[202,132.63,2.92,"Proteins that are important components of the immune system"],[202,135.55,2.17,"generally increase when we get sick,"],[202,137.72,2.5,"then decrease again once our body has recovered."],[202,140.34,2.96,"But as we get older, their baseline levels rise,"],[202,143.31,2.92,"resulting in chronic low-level inflammation."],[202,146.39,2.75,"This process is called inflammaging."],[202,149.52,2.54,"Scientists are still unsure why it occurs,"],[202,152.19,4.38,"but it could be due to repeated encounters with pathogens and other stressors."],[202,156.78,3.67,"Another explanation could be the process of cellular senescence:"],[202,160.45,4.17,"when cells get old and stop dividing, they secrete inflammatory proteins."],[202,164.74,2.13,"And by measuring the level of these proteins,"],[202,166.87,4.63,"scientists have developed yet another clock that can accurately measure aging."],[202,172.0,4.55,"While all three of these markers are great additions to the age estimation toolbox,"],[202,176.55,3.63,"they don’t perfectly correlate with chronological age."],[202,180.22,4.34,"Instead, they are best at estimating something called biological age."],[202,184.81,3.59,"Biological age is a measure of how well your body is functioning,"],[202,188.56,2.92,"how much damage your cells and organs have accumulated,"],[202,191.48,2.96,"and your overall risk for certain health problems."],[202,194.73,1.83,"Unlike chronological clocks,"],[202,196.69,3.5,"biological clocks tick at different rates for different people"],[202,200.2,3.34,"due to genetic differences as well as health, stress,"],[202,203.53,2.34,"and many other environmental factors."],[202,205.87,2.34,"And different tissues and organs within the body"],[202,208.2,2.92,"may even biologically age at different speeds."],[202,211.54,3.34,"In many ways, these clocks are still a work in progress."],[202,215.04,3.04,"For example, most experts recommend against"],[202,218.09,2.59,"personalized biological age testing."],[202,220.88,2.75,"While these tests are commercially available,"],[202,223.64,3.88,"the value and accuracy of these measurements remain unclear."],[202,227.81,3.5,"Currently, these tools work best at the population level,"],[202,231.31,2.88,"when data from many individuals are analyzed."],[202,234.65,3.13,"However, many scientists hope to change this in the future,"],[202,237.78,3.38,"as accurately tracking this information could revolutionize"],[202,241.15,1.54,"personalized healthcare."],[202,242.99,3.75,"For example, one day a person's accelerated biological age"],[202,246.87,4.25,"might help doctors detect an unknown health condition much earlier."],[202,251.5,3.13,"And other researchers are searching for ways to slow down,"],[202,254.62,3.09,"or even turn back, these biological clocks."],[202,257.71,3.88,"Though so far, most attempts at this have been largely unsuccessful."],[202,262.05,5.55,"Besides, researchers still don’t know if telomeres, inflammation, or epigenetics"],[202,267.6,2.04,"are actually to blame for aging,"],[202,269.64,4.25,"or if they are simply neutral markers of other processes happening in the body."],[202,274.23,3.13,"We may be learning to read the language of aging,"],[202,277.36,3.67,"but we still have a way to go in understanding its grammar."],[203,7.46,2.33,"Everyone needs time to themselves,"],[203,9.88,3.38,"and peaceful solitude has stress-relieving benefits."],[203,13.47,5.96,"But being alone takes on an entirely different dimension when it creeps up"],[203,19.43,2.13,"or is forced upon you."],[203,22.14,4.59,"When that's the case, the effects can be surprisingly extensive."],[203,27.07,4.88,"And though different people experience distinct effects at different times,"],[203,32.07,6.42,"symptoms tend to become more severe and persistent the longer one's isolated."],[203,38.99,5.13,"When someone is involuntarily confined to one space indefinitely—"],[203,44.29,4.92,"for days, weeks, months, or even years—"],[203,49.34,3.09,"alone and without productive tasks,"],[203,52.59,4.17,"their body will likely undergo numerous changes."],[203,57.39,3.29,"Let's take a look at what may happen and why."],[203,61.64,4.84,"Early on, stress hormones may spike, and as time passes,"],[203,66.48,2.25,"that stress can become chronic."],[203,69.02,4.34,"Social interactions and meaningful activities are essential"],[203,73.36,1.5,"for emotional stability."],[203,75.28,3.84,"This may be because they provide us with what researchers call"],[203,79.12,2.25,"“social reality testing”—"],[203,82.12,5.17,"a sort of sounding board where we can gauge how rational our perceptions are."],[203,87.79,5.05,"So, when someone’s deprived of those kinds of communication and tasks,"],[203,92.92,4.05,"their sense of identity and reality becomes threatened."],[203,97.22,3.84,"Their thoughts spiral and their impulses take the reins,"],[203,101.14,4.8,"setting the stage for depression, obsessions, suicidal ideation,"],[203,105.94,3.88,"and, for some, delusions and hallucinations."],[203,110.4,5.42,"Over time, this prolonged agitation can cause the brain's limbic system,"],[203,115.82,2.25,"which regulates fear and stress,"],[203,118.16,3.63,"to become especially responsive and hyperactive."],[203,122.41,2.54,"Meanwhile, the prefrontal cortex,"],[203,125.04,3.29,"the brain’s hub for reasoning and moral judgment,"],[203,128.46,1.08,"may shrink,"],[203,129.58,3.96,"impairing one's focus, memory, and cognition."],[203,134.34,5.51,"Overall, the balance shifts from rational thinking towards emotionality."],[203,140.14,4.88,"And as someone remains in this state, the imbalance becomes ingrained,"],[203,145.1,5.88,"making them more prone to bouts of anxiety, rage, and irrational actions."],[203,151.69,4.13,"Isolation will also affect other parts of the person's health."],[203,155.99,4.0,"They may lose sense of time and have difficulties sleeping."],[203,160.57,4.8,"They're more likely to experience heart palpitations, headaches, dizziness,"],[203,165.45,1.63,"and hypersensitivity."],[203,167.54,1.96,"And they may also lose weight"],[203,169.5,4.42,"because of stress-induced digestive issues and poor appetite."],[203,174.5,4.88,"One can attempt to cope by establishing the healthiest routine possible"],[203,179.38,2.21,"under the extreme circumstances,"],[203,181.59,3.67,"including robust exercise, reading, and writing."],[203,185.72,2.13,"But that can only do so much."],[203,188.1,4.46,"The United Nations, many human rights organizations, and experts"],[203,192.73,4.96,"classify this kind of forced, prolonged isolation as torture."],[203,198.36,4.96,"And yet, it’s something imprisoned people in many countries endure."],[203,203.66,4.3,"Also called “solitary confinement” or “restrictive housing,”"],[203,208.04,3.42,"the practice is most common in the United States."],[203,211.92,7.01,"In 2019, more than 120,000 US prisoners lived in solitary confinement,"],[203,219.05,5.76,"spending 22 to 24 hours a day in mostly windowless cells"],[203,224.8,2.88,"spanning roughly six by nine feet."],[203,228.1,4.13,"Quaker groups introduced solitary confinement to US prisons"],[203,232.23,1.88,"in the late 1700s,"],[203,234.15,2.59,"as an alternative to corporal punishment,"],[203,236.73,3.67,"believing it could bring about reflection and penitence—"],[203,240.49,1.75,"hence “penitentiary.”"],[203,243.03,2.59,"But the practice quickly faced criticism,"],[203,245.62,3.42,"from public figures all the way to the Supreme Court."],[203,249.66,1.92,"Charles Dickens, for one,"],[203,251.58,5.38,"condemned solitary confinement as “worse than any torture of the body.”"],[203,257.63,3.5,"Its use dwindled, but then, in the 1980s,"],[203,261.21,3.54,"alongside more punitive, tough-on-crime laws,"],[203,264.93,3.0,"US prison populations skyrocketed."],[203,268.56,1.75,"As prisons grew crowded,"],[203,270.31,4.25,"incidences of protests, rebellions, and violence grew,"],[203,274.69,6.21,"and prison authorities increasingly used solitary confinement to maintain control."],[203,281.82,3.34,"Many people have been placed in solitary confinement"],[203,285.16,5.34,"for minor, nonviolent infractions, like talking back to prison guards."],[203,290.7,3.42,"And solitary confinement is harmful to everyone,"],[203,294.25,4.8,"but many who’ve experienced it have pre-existing mental health disorders,"],[203,299.05,2.13,"which it only exacerbates."],[203,301.67,3.75,"Solitary confinement also seems to have lasting effects"],[203,305.43,4.09,"that make readjusting to life outside of a cell difficult."],[203,309.93,4.84,"People who have gone through solitary confinement are three times more likely"],[203,314.77,3.09,"to show signs of post-traumatic stress disorder."],[203,318.06,4.21,"And they commonly report experiencing shifts in their personalities,"],[203,322.28,4.8,"increased anxiety and paranoia in otherwise ordinary situations,"],[203,327.07,3.84,"and difficulty concentrating and connecting with others."],[203,331.16,3.25,"Some states have restricted the use of solitary confinement"],[203,334.45,4.88,"in cases involving serious mental illness, children, or pregnancy,"],[203,339.54,4.55,"and some have adopted 15 or 20 day limits for everyone."],[203,344.8,4.5,"But laws that regulate solitary confinement aren’t always enforced—"],[203,349.3,3.21,"and prison authorities have created loopholes."],[203,353.01,5.38,"Yet solitary confinement does immense damage that is contrary to rehabilitation,"],[203,358.48,2.67,"while failing to reduce prison violence."],[203,361.48,3.75,"Meanwhile, other countries have centered more humane approaches."],[203,365.24,5.21,"Norway, for example, imprisons far fewer people per capita than the US"],[203,370.57,4.46,"while spending five times more per prisoner on accommodations,"],[203,375.12,2.5,"classes, and work-release programs."],[203,377.92,5.3,"Norway also sees far fewer people return to prison after release,"],[203,383.34,3.09,"with one of the world’s lowest rates of recidivism,"],[203,386.63,3.71,"indicating we tend to get better together."],[204,4.79,3.04,"Not long ago, I was giving my dog a walk."],[204,7.88,1.5,"His name is Martini."],[204,11.09,1.46,"That wasn't even a joke."],[204,13.55,1.21,"My wife was out of town,"],[204,14.76,1.29,"and I left my phone inside"],[204,16.1,2.17,"so I could get away from the incessant buzzing"],[204,18.27,2.21,"and took him on a nice long walk."],[204,20.52,1.17,"And I came back inside,"],[204,21.73,3.29,"and I had one of those moments where your stomach sinks,"],[204,25.02,4.88,"because I looked at my phone and I had a lot of missed calls."],[204,30.23,4.42,"And my first thought was, my wife is pissed about something."],[204,34.7,1.63,"It's a lot of calls."],[204,36.37,2.67,"But then I looked, and it wasn't just a lot of calls,"],[204,39.03,5.67,"it was hundreds of phone calls and numbers I didn't recognize,"],[204,44.75,2.29,"some of them unknown numbers."],[204,47.42,3.13,"And I decided to figure out what this was."],[204,50.59,1.83,"And I opened my phone,"],[204,52.47,4.34,"and I opened the voicemails to see if these people had left messages."],[204,58.39,3.34,"(Audio) Person 1: What you're doing to President Trump is disgusting."],[204,61.73,1.75,"You are disgusting people."],[204,63.52,2.09,"You're evil and you're going to go down."],[204,65.64,2.63,"Person 2: You, my friend, are a piece of shit."],[204,68.31,1.54,"You are a traitor."],[204,70.19,2.21,"Person 3: You're pushing for anti-Trump?"],[204,72.44,1.63,"You dumb motherfucker!"],[204,75.99,3.75,"We will squash you like a fucking peanut, bitch."],[204,79.78,1.84,"You're done, you're done."],[204,81.62,2.38,"So eat a dick and die."],[204,84.58,1.92,"Person 4: Miles, we're going to dox you."],[204,86.54,2.46,"You're not going to be able to walk down the street."],[204,89.04,1.25,"You're an anti-American."],[204,90.34,1.25,"Leave the country."],[204,91.63,1.88,"You're not welcome here anymore."],[204,93.97,2.59,"You're anti-American, you hate your country."],[204,96.55,1.21,"Get out."],[204,97.8,2.92,"Person 5: Because you will deserve the wrath of hell."],[204,101.51,2.29,"And I think you will get what's coming to you."],[204,104.89,1.17,"God willing."],[204,106.48,3.0,"Miles Taylor: If you can believe it, those were the nice ones."],[204,110.98,4.3,"So anyway, before I get to that, let me take you back in time."],[204,115.57,1.71,"Why did I end up in Washington, DC?"],[204,117.32,2.04,"Like a lot of people,"],[204,119.37,2.46,"after the attacks of September 11, 2001,"],[204,121.83,1.54,"I wanted to come to DC"],[204,123.41,3.29,"to make sure a day like that never happened again."],[204,126.71,2.38,"That was going to be the full focus of my career."],[204,129.08,1.38,"And I came into Washington,"],[204,130.46,3.79,"the lowest place you can possibly come in on the totem pole"],[204,134.3,2.13,"as a young messenger on Capitol Hill,"],[204,136.47,5.0,"a page messenger delivering envelopes during my junior year in high school."],[204,141.47,2.59,"But despite being the lowest rung on the totem pole,"],[204,144.1,3.42,"I had the best desk in Washington, DC."],[204,147.56,1.46,"And I'm not joking about that."],[204,149.02,2.5,"Better than the Resolute desk inside the White House,"],[204,151.56,2.59,"because my desk was in the back of the chamber,"],[204,154.19,2.04,"the back of the citadel of democracy,"],[204,156.24,3.71,"where I had a perch to see the comings and goings of Congress"],[204,159.95,2.59,"in the wake of a catastrophic attack."],[204,163.12,1.17,"And if you take a look,"],[204,164.33,3.63,"that little red circle has a little guy inside of it"],[204,168.0,1.38,"who's me, sitting there,"],[204,169.37,2.5,"watching the President's State of the Union address."],[204,171.92,2.34,"And I'm going to tell you what I saw in Washington"],[204,174.29,1.17,"in that time period."],[204,175.46,1.17,"I saw unity."],[204,177.38,2.5,"I was a young person who was very inspired"],[204,179.88,3.71,"by seeing members of Congress walk across the literal aisle"],[204,183.6,3.63,"to work together on legislation to protect this country."],[204,188.39,1.13,"But times change."],[204,189.56,1.17,"Things fade."],[204,190.77,1.29,"Everything fades."],[204,192.1,1.33,"Fast forward in time."],[204,193.48,2.21,"I find myself in 2017"],[204,195.69,3.88,"as the chief of staff of the US Department of Homeland Security."],[204,200.11,3.79,"It's not a time of unity anymore in Washington."],[204,203.95,4.84,"I took that job because of the worst sales pitch anyone's ever made for someone"],[204,208.79,2.54,"to join their office in history."],[204,211.92,2.5,"My boss, John Kelly, said, \"Miles,"],[204,214.46,4.0,"it's not as bad as it looks inside the Trump administration.\""],[204,220.55,2.13,"\"It is so much worse.\""],[204,224.18,2.96,"And I still took that job because I understood what he meant."],[204,227.18,2.42,"I understood he meant we need people who understand"],[204,229.6,2.75,"how government functions, how national security functions,"],[204,232.4,1.58,"real conservatives to come in"],[204,234.02,2.08,"and help steady the ship of these agencies."],[204,236.15,2.42,"But I soon saw what he saw and what others saw,"],[204,238.61,2.08,"which is that in meetings with the President"],[204,240.69,1.63,"in the White House Situation Room,"],[204,242.32,1.79,"in the Oval Office, on Air Force One,"],[204,244.12,2.09,"I met a man who I had not known previously,"],[204,246.24,3.59,"and I found him to be reckless and impulsive at best,"],[204,249.87,2.96,"and at worst, on days members of Congress,"],[204,252.87,3.5,"cabinet secretaries, walked out of the Oval Office with ashen faces,"],[204,256.42,1.33,"and they said things like,"],[204,257.8,3.59,"\"The man is a threat to the fabric of our republic,\""],[204,261.42,2.34,"I knew there was something more serious going on."],[204,263.8,3.04,"Now I’m not going to talk about the first Trump administration,"],[204,266.85,1.38,"but if there was one theme,"],[204,268.22,3.88,"I had to spend most of my time not focused on the 250,000 men and women"],[204,272.14,1.83,"of the Department of Homeland Security"],[204,273.98,2.29,"I was responsible for helping oversee,"],[204,276.27,6.71,"but one man who was regularly engaged or attempting to engage in illegal acts."],[204,283.03,2.63,"There was no deep state inside the Trump administration."],[204,285.7,2.29,"There were people willing to speak truth to power"],[204,288.03,2.46,"and prevent the President from doing illegal things,"],[204,290.5,3.5,"not to prevent him from implementing a lawful agenda."],[204,294.0,1.75,"But I grew very frustrated"],[204,295.79,3.5,"because these conversations were happening among us,"],[204,299.34,2.13,"a group of unelected bureaucrats,"],[204,301.51,1.71,"navel gazing, wringing our hands,"],[204,303.26,3.29,"complaining about how unfit the President was for office."],[204,306.59,3.29,"It was not our job to decide if the President was unfit for office."],[204,309.93,2.96,"We would not decide if he got re-elected in a second term."],[204,312.93,1.58,"That's what you would decide."],[204,314.52,2.04,"And I felt like it was very important,"],[204,316.56,3.67,"if these gray-beard wise men wouldn't go out and say it in public,"],[204,320.23,1.29,"that someone needed to."],[204,321.57,4.84,"So I decided I would go say what we were talking about in private"],[204,326.45,1.25,"in public."],[204,328.07,4.38,"Now I did that at first anonymously,"],[204,332.5,1.5,"as many of you know."],[204,334.0,1.25,"And I put on that mask"],[204,335.25,5.0,"not because I was afraid to stand by my opinions,"],[204,340.25,2.08,"but because I'm a student of history."],[204,342.84,3.88,"And my favorite book is a compendium of essays called \"The Federalist Papers.\""],[204,349.85,2.08,"Total best seller, all the authors are dead."],[204,353.93,4.59,"And the Founding Fathers wrote anonymous essays"],[204,358.52,2.25,"to sell the American public on the Constitution,"],[204,360.81,3.59,"not because they were scared to associate themselves with those words,"],[204,364.44,3.54,"but because they knew it would create a spectacle"],[204,367.99,1.38,"and it would draw attention."],[204,369.37,3.55,"And to them, it was the most important issue of their time"],[204,372.91,3.21,"to get the American people to pay attention to this conversation."],[204,376.12,1.17,"I did the same thing."],[204,377.33,2.0,"I'm not comparing myself to those founders,"],[204,379.38,2.67,"but I used the same device, and it worked."],[204,382.05,1.17,"You paid attention."],[204,383.25,1.88,"We started a national conversation"],[204,385.17,4.04,"about how the President's own lieutenants didn't think he was fit for office."],[204,389.26,2.0,"That was an important conversation to have,"],[204,391.26,3.46,"whether you agreed with us or didn't agree with us."],[204,395.56,1.71,"One person also noticed."],[204,399.19,3.13,"And in a seven-letter, all-caps tweet,"],[204,402.36,2.04,"he said “TREASON?”"],[204,405.44,2.42,"And I was grateful that there was a question mark."],[204,409.4,2.96,"Because treason is punishable by death in the United States."],[204,412.37,3.0,"So at least if there was a question,"],[204,415.41,1.42,"I had a chance."],[204,418.21,1.67,"The President of the United States"],[204,419.92,2.96,"subsequently said he wanted the author found"],[204,422.92,2.5,"and turned in for national security reasons."],[204,425.46,1.21,"But as I later found out,"],[204,426.71,1.63,"the White House gave up the search"],[204,428.34,2.34,"because lawyers in the White House said"],[204,430.72,3.04,"\"This article was First Amendment- protected speech."],[204,433.81,1.42,"We can't pursue this person."],[204,435.22,2.67,"It's not treason, we're not going to arrest them.\""],[204,437.93,1.5,"But that was not enough for me."],[204,439.48,3.34,"In 2020, I felt like I needed to take off the mask"],[204,442.86,1.5,"because behind the mask,"],[204,444.36,1.92,"I was sending the signal that it was OK"],[204,446.27,2.38,"to sit in the dark and put your opinions out there"],[204,448.69,1.38,"and not take accountability."],[204,450.07,1.17,"Before that election,"],[204,451.24,1.17,"I needed to go out there"],[204,452.45,3.59,"and tell you the specific things that I saw"],[204,456.08,3.17,"to buttress those claims I made in that piece,"],[204,459.29,2.46,"and let you make up your damn minds"],[204,461.79,2.79,"about whether this guy deserved to be reelected."],[204,465.38,1.83,"Once again,"],[204,467.25,1.25,"he noticed."],[204,469.92,1.79,"We'll get to that in a second."],[204,473.3,2.63,"And he said, \"At campaign rallies,"],[204,475.97,3.96,"bad things are going to happen to Miles Taylor.\""],[204,480.73,1.38,"And he was right."],[204,482.56,1.21,"They did."],[204,483.81,2.42,"His supporters made sure of it."],[204,487.52,2.5,"As a consequence of his rhetoric,"],[204,490.07,3.21,"as a consequence of accusing me of treason,"],[204,493.28,2.59,"I had to leave my home on Capitol Hill,"],[204,495.87,2.79,"I lost my job that I had taken in the private sector"],[204,498.66,1.58,"after I left the administration,"],[204,500.25,4.34,"I lost my life savings spending it on lawyers,"],[204,504.58,2.67,"my security, friends."],[204,507.3,1.54,"And on election night 2020,"],[204,508.88,3.75,"I found myself in a safe house in Northern Virginia,"],[204,512.67,3.54,"under armed guard with a pistol under my pillow."],[204,516.93,4.46,"Because so many of my fellow Americans believed I should die"],[204,521.39,4.13,"for criticizing the President of the United States."],[204,526.31,2.25,"Fast forward to April 9 of this year."],[204,528.57,2.84,"Of course, Donald Trump lost that election in 2020."],[204,531.4,2.25,"He won, he came back to power."],[204,533.7,1.13,"And on that day,"],[204,534.86,3.71,"I was out and about again with Martini,"],[204,538.58,2.96,"which means, you know something bad's going to happen."],[204,541.58,1.33,"He was a dirty Martini,"],[204,542.96,2.13,"I had to take him to the groomer's."],[204,545.08,3.29,"We got him cleaned, I came home, and I got a message from a journalist"],[204,548.38,1.38,"who said, \"Turn on the news."],[204,549.75,3.59,"The President of the United States is talking about you in the Oval Office.\""],[204,553.34,2.96,"And I pulled it up on my phone, and there was Donald Trump,"],[204,556.3,2.59,"and there was Donald Trump declaring"],[204,558.93,2.08,"not that I might be guilty of treason --"],[204,561.01,1.54,"the question mark was gone."],[204,562.98,2.09,"He told the American people and the world"],[204,565.1,3.59,"that he believed I was guilty of the highest crime contemplated"],[204,568.69,1.75,"in the United States Constitution,"],[204,570.48,3.67,"a reminder -- a crime punishable by death."],[204,574.2,2.96,"And as legal scholars later told me,"],[204,577.2,3.75,"it was the first time in 249 years of the American Republic"],[204,580.99,1.88,"that a president of the United States"],[204,582.91,4.42,"has issued an executive order to investigate one of his critics"],[204,587.38,3.21,"for First Amendment-protected speech."],[204,591.96,2.38,"Now you would be right to ask a question."],[204,594.92,2.5,"If treason is tantamount to murder,"],[204,597.47,2.17,"why am I standing here right now?"],[204,600.18,2.09,"Why am I not in handcuffs?"],[204,602.81,2.63,"Why am I not in a jail cell?"],[204,606.23,4.09,"And whether you agree with me or not, I will tell you the answer."],[204,611.07,2.46,"The answer is because the US justice system"],[204,613.57,2.71,"has not caught up to the President's view"],[204,616.32,3.88,"that criticism of a president is subversive,"],[204,620.24,2.63,"that criticism should be criminalized,"],[204,622.87,2.84,"that you need a permission slip to criticize the president."],[204,625.75,2.59,"The justice system hasn't caught up to that."],[204,628.37,1.13,"Yet."],[204,631.79,1.29,"But you know who did?"],[204,634.63,3.88,"The same people before that followed his dog whistles."],[204,639.01,1.38,"They were paying attention."],[204,641.05,2.92,"(Audio) Person 1: Miles Taylor, what the fuck are you thinking"],[204,643.97,2.59,"going against Trump, you and your cronies?"],[204,646.56,2.84,"Person 2: I really don't appreciate you putting out garbage."],[204,649.39,5.38,"Do you want to be looking at defamation of character, etc.,"],[204,654.82,1.67,"for putting out lies,"],[204,656.49,3.92,"because everybody's on it and on you."],[204,660.99,3.17,"Person 3: Your names are going to go viral"],[204,664.16,5.25,"because you are treasonous ilk to our country."],[204,670.17,2.13,"Person 4: You are what is wrong with America."],[204,672.34,1.63,"You, sir, are a piece of shit."],[204,674.0,2.5,"Person 5: We don't forgive traitorship."],[204,676.55,1.83,"Person 6: I just want to let you know"],[204,678.38,3.17,"that your efforts to try and defeat the GOAT,"],[204,681.59,2.92,"the greatest president of all times,"],[204,684.51,1.42,"is not going to work."],[204,686.47,1.46,"You guys poked the bear."],[204,687.98,1.5,"You woke the sleeping giant."],[204,690.81,1.42,"We're coming, my man."],[204,693.94,4.46,"MT: These folks are not getting invited to Thanksgiving at the Taylor household."],[204,698.82,3.67,"But I will tell you, joking aside, those consequences were real."],[204,702.91,3.71,"Everything that we had rebuilt since 2020, when our lives were detonated,"],[204,706.66,2.29,"was kicked over again because of all of this."],[204,708.96,1.21,"The address of our home"],[204,710.16,2.5,"that we had moved to to protect my family was doxed."],[204,712.67,1.42,"Our phone numbers were doxed."],[204,714.09,2.25,"My wife asked me, do we need to sell the house"],[204,716.38,2.08,"to be able to pay for the legal fees?"],[204,718.51,2.42,"The business that I had built in the wake of this,"],[204,720.97,3.42,"that cut checks for 50 people was destroyed,"],[204,724.39,2.71,"and those 50 people no longer received paychecks."],[204,727.1,2.75,"But the hardest for me as a father and a husband"],[204,729.85,2.71,"was watching not just me get death threats,"],[204,732.6,1.21,"my wife be threatened,"],[204,733.85,5.09,"my one-year-old daughter threatened and her image posted on the internet."],[204,738.94,1.25,"That's not OK."],[204,741.03,1.79,"And it forced us to take legal action"],[204,742.82,2.29,"against people we've never met in this country."],[204,745.7,2.0,"Even today, I will tell you the truth,"],[204,747.74,3.75,"my security advisor said he did not want me to come speak to you today."],[204,751.87,1.17,"And we know why."],[204,753.04,1.79,"Because of the environment we are in."],[204,754.88,1.63,"But think about that for a second."],[204,756.5,1.96,"The irony that a speech about free speech"],[204,758.5,3.21,"can't be given in the land of the free without fear of reprisal."],[204,763.38,3.38,"What's so remarkable about my experience,"],[204,766.8,2.34,"and the reason I shared it with you today,"],[204,769.18,2.96,"is that it is unremarkable now."],[204,773.06,3.25,"Because what has happened to me is happening from people"],[204,776.36,1.96,"at the member of Congress level,"],[204,778.32,3.59,"all the way down to state representatives and Parks and Recreation directors"],[204,781.9,1.54,"and you name it in this country."],[204,783.49,2.84,"Poll workers, who are threatened with crowdsourced violence,"],[204,786.32,2.63,"members of Congress, who are scared to hold town halls"],[204,788.95,3.92,"because of their constituents on the Left and on the Right."],[204,792.91,2.17,"I’m going to give you a data point about this:"],[204,795.08,1.29,"10X."],[204,796.38,2.09,"The year Donald Trump was elected president,"],[204,798.46,2.92,"there were 1,000 death threats a year to members of Congress,"],[204,801.42,1.58,"a thousand violent threats."],[204,803.05,1.75,"By the time he left office,"],[204,804.84,4.13,"that had gone up tenfold to 10,000 death threats a year."],[204,809.01,2.34,"And this is roughly where the number has hovered."],[204,811.39,5.0,"And it is indicative of what we are seeing in other parts of our society."],[204,816.39,3.34,"But I didn't need the data points to know this."],[204,819.73,2.25,"I'll tell you what my data point was."],[204,821.98,2.63,"My data point was a desk."],[204,825.28,2.5,"On January 6, 2021,"],[204,827.78,1.29,"you all know what happened."],[204,829.12,4.63,"What you probably don't know is that a desk was slid"],[204,833.79,1.79,"from the corner of the house chamber."],[204,835.62,2.29,"The desk I had sat at as a congressional page"],[204,837.92,3.25,"in a Washington unified against a foreign enemy,"],[204,841.17,1.79,"and it was slid in front of the door,"],[204,842.96,5.34,"and it was the last line of defense against a violent mob of insurrectionists."],[204,849.18,2.5,"I get chills still talking about that"],[204,851.68,5.21,"because of how quickly our world changed in that time period."],[204,858.1,3.38,"Now we know a lot of scary things have happened recently."],[204,861.48,4.34,"We saw the assassination of a top political commentator on the right."],[204,865.86,2.75,"Just last week, the top Democrat in Congress"],[204,868.61,2.21,"had an assassination plot foiled against him."],[204,870.87,1.75,"And so it begs the question:"],[204,872.62,5.0,"who is responsible for the price of dissent being so high in America?"],[204,877.66,4.04,"Why are people fearing for their lives to speak their political opinions?"],[204,882.92,2.84,"And if you think my answer is Donald Trump,"],[204,885.8,1.5,"you would be wrong."],[204,888.17,3.88,"Unfortunately, my answer is you."],[204,893.43,1.17,"Or some of you."],[204,895.06,3.67,"NPR just came out with a survey the other week that showed"],[204,898.73,1.21,"one in three,"],[204,899.98,5.09,"one in three Americans now believe that political violence would be justified"],[204,905.11,2.63,"to put the country back on track."],[204,908.99,2.04,"I'm going to give you another one in three."],[204,911.03,2.46,"One in three Americans own a cat."],[204,916.37,1.25,"One in three."],[204,917.66,1.83,"Think about that."],[204,919.83,1.67,"Now I want to ask you a question."],[204,921.54,3.67,"Which political affiliation do you think is more likely to hold this view?"],[204,925.25,1.17,"Is it the Democrats?"],[204,927.84,1.29,"Is it the Republicans?"],[204,930.38,1.38,"You're all wrong."],[204,932.6,3.13,"It's Democrats and Republicans in equal measure statistically."],[204,936.31,1.88,"Now you might be sitting there saying,"],[204,938.18,2.04,"\"Miles, but I'm not one of those one third."],[204,940.89,1.17,"I'm not one of those."],[204,942.1,2.29,"I didn't call your phone that day.\""],[204,944.77,3.0,"At least I don't think any of you did."],[204,949.95,2.25,"And again, statistically, you're probably right."],[204,952.2,2.21,"You probably fit into this category."],[204,955.7,4.67,"Because a recent survey found that two out of three Americans"],[204,960.37,4.55,"are now actively admitting to self-censoring their views"],[204,964.96,1.67,"out of fear of reprisal."],[204,967.0,2.96,"It's not just people inside the beltway, it's you."],[204,970.63,2.67,"We're all doing it, we're all guilty of it."],[204,973.3,4.75,"The same survey found that the vast majority of Americans agree in private"],[204,978.1,2.54,"on almost all the controversial issues in our society:"],[204,980.64,2.42,"immigration, abortion, climate change, you name it."],[204,983.06,2.84,"Survey found majorities agree on those issues,"],[204,985.94,1.63,"but they're scared to say it,"],[204,987.61,4.92,"including they're afraid to repudiate political violence."],[204,993.24,4.38,"So who's responsible for the price of dissent being so high in this country?"],[204,997.62,1.17,"It's not Donald Trump."],[204,998.83,1.96,"It's not even the one third of Americans"],[204,1000.79,3.92,"who believe we might have to use political violence against one another."],[204,1004.71,1.38,"It's the two thirds."],[204,1008.0,1.67,"In my opinion,"],[204,1009.71,4.0,"the greatest threat to democracy today"],[204,1013.76,1.13,"is anonymity."],[204,1015.93,2.75,"And I understand that that's ironic coming from me."],[204,1019.72,1.21,"I get it."],[204,1022.64,1.29,"It's anonymity."],[204,1024.52,2.46,"We are wearing our figurative masks."],[204,1027.02,2.17,"We are scared to tell the truth."],[204,1029.23,1.13,"And you know what?"],[204,1030.4,3.63,"When we're scared to tell the truth, intimidation works."],[204,1034.07,2.59,"It works very, very well."],[204,1037.12,2.0,"And I'm going to close by saying this."],[204,1039.45,3.5,"The question is, how do we fix it then?"],[204,1044.87,2.0,"I'm going to give you an economics lesson."],[204,1046.92,1.75,"Oh, God, this talk just got awful."],[204,1051.38,2.29,"If you remember supply and demand curves."],[204,1053.72,1.54,"In any marketplace,"],[204,1055.26,3.17,"when supply and demand meet, that's what determines the price, OK?"],[204,1058.47,2.96,"If as many people want pencils as there are pencils out there,"],[204,1061.43,1.21,"that's the price."],[204,1062.64,1.54,"I don’t know, $1.50 a pencil."],[204,1065.06,3.88,"If the price of dissent in this country is high today,"],[204,1068.98,1.46,"how do you lower it?"],[204,1070.48,2.38,"Economics teaches you there are only two ways."],[204,1072.9,1.17,"That's it."],[204,1074.07,2.5,"There [are] two ways to lower that price."],[204,1076.61,1.21,"One."],[204,1079.91,1.46,"You could decrease demand."],[204,1081.79,1.71,"You decrease demand for pencils,"],[204,1083.54,2.13,"we don't want them anymore, we're using pens."],[204,1085.66,1.33,"The price will go down."],[204,1087.04,4.3,"But when it comes to dissent and truth and debate in this country,"],[204,1091.38,3.09,"if you believe in what the Founders said about America,"],[204,1094.46,1.29,"you don't want to do that."],[204,1095.76,2.71,"We don't want to decrease the demand for truth and debate"],[204,1098.51,4.25,"and the possibility of reaching accord on important issues."],[204,1102.81,5.71,"So the only way to lower the price in any marketplace"],[204,1108.56,1.67,"is to increase the supply."],[204,1112.57,1.21,"That is you."],[204,1114.94,2.71,"You are the good in that marketplace."],[204,1118.49,1.71,"We need more dissent."],[204,1120.24,4.84,"We need people unafraid to step forward and speak the truth."],[204,1125.41,3.0,"And so I will close by just saying this."],[204,1128.41,3.92,"There [are] a lot of scary things happening right now in this country,"],[204,1132.38,1.46,"a lot of scary things."],[204,1134.59,4.63,"And I understand the inclination to ask, \"What is going on here?\""],[204,1140.3,1.88,"But before you answer that question,"],[204,1142.22,5.13,"I want you to ask yourself and the people around you another question."],[204,1148.18,2.34,"Am I anonymous still?"],[204,1152.4,3.96,"Because if the answer is yes to that, then you also know the solution."],[204,1156.4,1.5,"It's within your control."],[204,1157.94,3.42,"And that solution is, it's time to take off the mask."],[204,1161.74,1.17,"Thank you."],[205,4.33,4.47,"Debbie Millman: Rose, let's talk a little bit about your origin story."],[205,9.44,3.1,"You grew up in Santa Clara Pueblo,"],[205,12.58,4.17,"surrounded by generations of artists and thinkers."],[205,16.75,2.0,"Your mother, Roxanne Swentzell,"],[205,18.78,6.01,"and your grandmother both forged paths that united making and meaning."],[205,25.49,4.54,"When did you first sense that art could also be"],[205,30.06,2.0,"a kind of language for survival?"],[205,32.93,1.24,"Rose B. Simpson: Hm."],[205,34.2,1.7,"DM: I'm going real deep, real fast."],[205,35.9,2.27,"RBS: That's really good, I love that."],[205,39.4,4.54,"I would like to say first that there wasn't a difference between art and life."],[205,44.27,3.14,"That everything was a creative process"],[205,47.45,2.33,"and everything was applied"],[205,49.78,2.2,"and everything had intention and meaning."],[205,52.02,2.27,"So what we did"],[205,54.32,2.74,"and how we moved through the world"],[205,57.09,4.7,"had an invested interest"],[205,61.79,5.14,"in creating a reality that was aesthetic."],[205,67.93,5.27,"So I think that my mother's an incredible sculptor."],[205,73.64,3.1,"And she took the ability"],[205,76.77,2.7,"that she had been given to work in ceramics."],[205,79.51,3.7,"That came from generations and generations before us."],[205,83.25,5.24,"And she used it to communicate in a way that she was needing to,"],[205,88.52,2.0,"and it became our livelihood."],[205,91.19,2.3,"She supported our family with her sculpture."],[205,94.56,4.04,"But it was like the art world was something very strange"],[205,98.63,2.84,"because she was also making the pottery that we ate out of."],[205,101.47,6.97,"She used her ability to craft earth to build our home,"],[205,108.47,1.5,"to grow our food."],[205,110.01,4.8,"And so that utility and relationship with it"],[205,114.84,2.57,"was innate in all walks of our life."],[205,118.48,4.74,"DM: You've described your childhood home as experimental."],[205,123.25,4.04,"And I understand that your mother once turned off the electricity"],[205,127.32,2.5,"to see how the family could adapt."],[205,130.93,1.47,"How'd that go?"],[205,132.43,2.14,"RBS: Oh, it was really frustrating"],[205,134.56,3.67,"when I had to catch the bus at 6:30 in the morning."],[205,138.77,2.37,"But I am so grateful."],[205,141.87,4.1,"My mom is always, still to this day,"],[205,146.01,4.17,"searching for ways to root"],[205,150.21,4.71,"and to figure out how to apply the innate values"],[205,154.95,5.57,"of relationship to Earth and being, in all walks of life."],[205,160.56,3.2,"And so part of that was growing all our own food"],[205,163.79,3.57,"and figuring out how we could live sustainably."],[205,167.4,4.64,"And we have the privilege of living in our ancestral home,"],[205,172.07,3.77,"having seed and relationship to spirituality"],[205,175.87,3.37,"that can foster the farming."],[205,179.24,3.57,"So those seeds have been adapted to that environment for a very long time."],[205,183.28,4.14,"We have tradition that's passed down for a really long time"],[205,187.45,3.94,"to be able to support ourselves in the high desert of northern New Mexico."],[205,192.32,2.84,"And turning off the electricity"],[205,195.19,2.1,"was one step towards remembering"],[205,197.33,2.94,"what it's like to not be dependent on a system."],[205,201.03,4.54,"And now because of the fact that she turned off the electricity,"],[205,205.6,2.17,"she homeschooled us for a long time,"],[205,207.81,1.27,"we grew our own food,"],[205,209.07,2.07,"I can actually hear electricity."],[205,211.14,1.17,"DM: Really?"],[205,212.34,3.0,"RBS: Yeah, I'm really sensitive to like --"],[205,215.38,1.5,"yeah, it's all --"],[205,216.88,3.34,"we adapt to all the things that we add to our lives."],[205,220.25,1.23,"And when we take it away,"],[205,221.49,3.44,"then we start realizing how much we're affected by it."],[205,225.26,3.8,"DM: What did those early experiences teach you about self-reliance"],[205,229.06,3.24,"or the connection between self-reliance and imagination?"],[205,234.73,3.7,"RBS: I feel like understanding true sustainability"],[205,238.47,2.37,"means that we always have a choice."],[205,241.14,2.54,"Because my mom didn't put us into the school system,"],[205,243.71,4.94,"she, you know, intentionally homeschooled me and my brother from early on,"],[205,248.68,2.4,"and we chose to go to school later."],[205,251.12,1.83,"And now we keep going to school."],[205,254.35,3.54,"And I think that was a way"],[205,257.89,4.1,"of her building a capacity in us to choose."],[205,262.39,1.17,"There's always a choice."],[205,263.59,2.6,"We're not victims to the world that we live in."],[205,266.2,1.33,"We can always,"],[205,267.56,3.44,"if we're taught how to be sustainable and how to innovate"],[205,271.04,2.8,"and figure out how to survive in any situation,"],[205,273.84,5.14,"then we are in our agency when we navigate the world around us, right?"],[205,279.01,2.14,"DM: That requires self-reliance"],[205,281.18,6.77,"that is not always something that we're learned to cultivate as children."],[205,287.99,1.8,"How do you sustain that?"],[205,289.82,2.07,"RBS: How do I sustain that?"],[205,291.92,5.17,"I love to remember that I don't need the things I think I need, right?"],[205,297.13,3.7,"Like we’re told and told and told that we need X, Y and Z."],[205,300.86,3.7,"And every single day, I realize it's a choice I'm making."],[205,304.57,3.6,"And then I'm in my power in relationship to it."],[205,308.2,3.67,"As long as I don't need it, it doesn't rule me, doesn't own me."],[205,311.88,3.94,"And so if I maintain my relationship"],[205,315.85,3.27,"with the natural world, my food sources, etc.,"],[205,319.15,3.84,"then I am actively engaging in the relationships that I'm making."],[205,323.02,4.24,"And that includes the art world, that includes car culture,"],[205,327.29,4.94,"that includes education, that includes all the decisions,"],[205,332.26,1.7,"you know, being here today."],[205,333.96,5.67,"DM: What intrigues you most about car culture?"],[205,339.67,5.57,"Like, are you a \"Fast and Furious\" fan or is it more spiritual?"],[205,345.71,2.6,"RBS: At one point, I did like “Fast and Furious” a lot,"],[205,348.31,1.47,"and I watched all the movies."],[205,350.31,2.07,"\"Gone in 60 Seconds\" is my jam."],[205,353.35,1.24,"DM: So you've got range."],[205,355.92,3.7,"RBS: I grew up in Espanola, New Mexico, which is a town that’s adjacent --"],[205,359.66,2.87,"it's actually sandwiched between two reservations,"],[205,362.53,1.44,"two tribal nations,"],[205,363.99,2.6,"Santa Clara Pueblo and Ohkay Owingeh,"],[205,366.63,1.77,"so the town is ..."],[205,369.3,1.97,"The youth culture is very mixed"],[205,371.3,4.64,"between indigenous communities and then the local Hispanic communities."],[205,375.97,1.3,"And so we grew up together."],[205,377.27,5.17,"So like, my youth culture was very much, like lowriders"],[205,382.48,4.87,"and like, what would be the cholo culture Hispanic community there."],[205,387.95,2.34,"And you know, as a little kid,"],[205,390.32,3.0,"my mom had a ’52 Willys truck that she built our house with,"],[205,393.36,2.5,"and there was no room for the kids in the front seat."],[205,395.86,3.2,"So we used to sit in the bed of the truck as she drove through town."],[205,399.1,1.17,"And I'd be, you know,"],[205,400.3,1.87,"watching all the cars pile up behind us"],[205,402.2,2.04,"because we went max like, 40 miles an hour."],[205,404.27,1.94,"And I'd be looking at all the nice cars."],[205,406.24,3.4,"And in my head I was like, when I grow up, I'm going to have a nice car."],[205,409.67,2.74,"That was just like, you know, the goal, right?"],[205,412.41,1.17,"DM: Did that happen?"],[205,413.61,1.14,"RBS: Oh, yeah."],[205,415.11,1.17,"Oh yeah."],[205,416.28,2.0,"DM: Tell us about what's in your garage."],[205,419.55,3.37,"RBS: I have two custom cars"],[205,422.95,3.4,"that I built for myself --"],[205,427.72,2.9,"In order to have an aesthetic experience."],[205,430.66,1.27,"DM: What does that mean?"],[205,431.96,1.6,"RBS: Relational aesthetics to me,"],[205,433.6,2.47,"when I was in graduate school the first time,"],[205,436.1,1.64,"I wrote my thesis,"],[205,437.77,3.37,"and I studied what Indigenous aesthetic means, right?"],[205,441.17,4.91,"And the closest I found was actually Japanese aesthetics"],[205,446.11,3.1,"that I was [writing] about and aesthetics of the everyday"],[205,449.21,2.47,"and the intentionality of all that we do"],[205,451.71,2.64,"and applied aesthetics to our lived environment."],[205,454.38,1.3,"And growing up in Espanola,"],[205,455.72,3.27,"where the cruise line on Sunday is everybody get in their nice car"],[205,458.99,2.34,"and you put on some good tunes, and you lean back,"],[205,461.32,1.4,"and you are present,"],[205,462.76,1.8,"you're enjoying your community,"],[205,464.56,3.07,"you're having a sense of self-worth, you're enjoying the sunset."],[205,467.66,2.54,"You have a good drink from Sonic, right?"],[205,471.7,2.37,"That feeling is actually presence,"],[205,474.47,5.94,"and that feeling is when aesthetic gets reapplied to our life"],[205,480.44,2.87,"and when we make those aesthetic decisions"],[205,483.31,2.84,"that we are in a state of agency, right?"],[205,486.18,2.74,"We're a state of empowerment in ourselves, right?"],[205,488.95,4.34,"And so I was building myself to have that moment"],[205,493.32,2.9,"to create that aesthetic experience for myself"],[205,496.23,1.9,"that I felt was reminiscent"],[205,498.16,3.37,"of what I knew of applied Indigenous aesthetics."],[205,501.56,2.67,"Where it's not in a white cube on a white box,"],[205,504.27,1.57,"in some other building somewhere"],[205,505.87,2.1,"where you don't necessarily have access to."],[205,507.97,1.17,"It's for everyone."],[205,509.17,2.44,"Everyone has access to that experience."],[205,512.14,3.44,"DM: But your work does move between disciplines,"],[205,515.58,4.2,"ceramics, metal, automotive restoration, performance,"],[205,519.82,2.14,"and now includes all of them."],[205,521.99,3.5,"Now, did you always imagine those boundaries dissolving,"],[205,525.52,2.2,"or did that happen more organically"],[205,527.72,2.54,"over the work that you've done in your practice?"],[205,531.16,4.41,"RBS: I always wonder if I stop being an artist"],[205,535.6,2.37,"in the way that the world is \"arting.\""],[205,538.0,2.34,"I would still be doing stuff."],[205,540.37,2.4,"I would still be going from one place to the next"],[205,542.81,1.5,"and making things constantly."],[205,544.31,4.94,"And I feel like I'm always interested in how -- I'm a dreamer."],[205,549.28,3.7,"I constantly am imagining the next best thing,"],[205,553.02,3.34,"I'm laying there, like, that window should be over there"],[205,556.39,1.17,"and how can I do that?"],[205,557.59,2.54,"And like, I look at a car and I don't see the car,"],[205,560.16,1.5,"I see what it could be, right?"],[205,561.69,2.67,"Like, I look at a garden and I don't see the garden,"],[205,564.36,1.17,"I see what it could be,"],[205,565.56,1.27,"and then I begin."],[205,567.4,3.4,"And I feel like I will always be doing that no matter what."],[205,570.83,4.44,"I'm always going to be searching and whether that's in the world around me"],[205,575.3,3.5,"and how to better that and how to listen to it,"],[205,578.84,2.34,"ask how I can be of service of it,"],[205,581.21,2.07,"and then do work."],[205,583.88,2.1,"And the satisfaction of stepping back"],[205,586.02,2.4,"and seeing how something transformed,"],[205,588.42,3.6,"that is that applied aesthetic to the world around me in all things."],[205,592.05,2.17,"And I feel like that is also internal."],[205,594.26,3.57,"So it's also that internal investigation"],[205,597.86,3.24,"of our psychological and spiritual spaces."],[205,602.43,4.3,"DM: You've called cars vessels."],[205,607.6,4.5,"And many of your figures, many of the sculptures you make,"],[205,612.14,4.07,"you've called vessels for transformation."],[205,616.88,2.1,"What did these vessels hold?"],[205,620.08,1.2,"RBS: Consciousness."],[205,621.32,1.17,"DM: Tell us about that."],[205,622.99,4.57,"RBS: I am a vessel and I am aware and I am moving"],[205,627.56,3.9,"and I am making decisions intentionally in this world."],[205,631.93,5.44,"And I make ceramic vessels that are hollow inside,"],[205,637.4,1.53,"and they are watching,"],[205,638.97,2.54,"and they are doing work, and they are independent,"],[205,641.54,1.64,"and they make their own decisions,"],[205,643.17,2.34,"and they move through the world with a job to do."],[205,645.51,1.4,"And so do cars."],[205,646.94,3.67,"And so do the houses we live in, the spaces we inhabit,"],[205,650.61,2.1,"they’re all watching, they’re all listening,"],[205,652.72,2.23,"and they're all making decisions."],[205,654.99,1.4,"If we are aware."],[205,657.29,1.97,"DM: Tell me how you become aware."],[205,662.12,1.57,"RBS: I like to ask,"],[205,663.73,4.94,"and I think the first thing that we can do is begin to ask."],[205,669.8,2.23,"And then wait for an answer."],[205,672.37,5.84,"And I think we have so consistently built a muscle inside of ourselves"],[205,678.24,4.67,"of prioritizing a human interaction"],[205,682.91,3.34,"that we've stopped understanding and believing"],[205,686.25,4.61,"that we can be in communication with that which is beyond human, right?"],[205,691.25,3.3,"And so I make anthropomorphized vessels in ceramics"],[205,694.59,1.17,"so that it's a bridge,"],[205,695.79,3.0,"because humans really like to talk to other humans"],[205,698.79,1.97,"and have interactions with those."],[205,700.8,3.14,"And if we can see an anthropomorphized face"],[205,703.97,1.74,"and start feeling and listening,"],[205,705.74,3.8,"then we can begin building that muscle of communication"],[205,709.54,2.44,"with that which we have deemed inanimate."],[205,711.98,4.0,"DM: One of the things that I've noticed about your work, particularly \"Seed,\""],[205,715.98,4.67,"the installation that was in two spaces in New York City,"],[205,720.68,3.6,"was that the sculptures that you make, these vessels,"],[205,724.32,1.67,"the eyes are always open."],[205,726.99,2.14,"Sometimes the mouths are open as well,"],[205,729.13,2.33,"but most of them are closed."],[205,731.46,3.74,"I'm assuming that these are very deliberate, conscious decisions."],[205,735.63,1.17,"Can you talk about that?"],[205,736.83,4.04,"Because though they're anthropomorphized, they feel very real."],[205,740.9,5.0,"They don't feel there's a step between real humanity,"],[205,745.94,1.97,"anthropomorphized humanity."],[205,747.94,2.07,"So can you talk a little bit about that?"],[205,751.25,2.0,"RBS: When I make my figurative work,"],[205,753.28,3.74,"I don't always include things I don't think are necessarily important"],[205,757.05,2.77,"like arms or hair,"],[205,759.82,3.57,"but they do have the senses."],[205,763.43,2.77,"So they have ears, nose, mouth, eyes,"],[205,766.23,1.97,"because I want them to be,"],[205,768.2,4.0,"to have what they need to soak in the world around them"],[205,772.24,2.64,"and to create a relationship with that."],[205,774.9,4.37,"And that we see that in that, we will also build a relationship"],[205,779.31,3.74,"understanding that they are sensing us, right?"],[205,783.78,2.07,"And so that is why I choose to do that."],[205,787.18,4.57,"And you know, when I cut the eyes into the clay the first time,"],[205,791.79,2.5,"I always say \"Hello, welcome.\""],[205,795.86,3.77,"DM: There's been a lot of writing in the last couple of years"],[205,799.66,4.94,"about how artists of any discipline"],[205,804.63,4.04,"are vessels for creative communication."],[205,808.71,3.04,"Rick Rubin talked about that in his book \"The Creative Act,\""],[205,811.77,2.74,"Elizabeth Gilbert talked about that in her TED talk,"],[205,814.54,2.54,"about how we have to be open"],[205,817.08,3.54,"to the idea of creativity coming through us,"],[205,820.62,2.97,"that we are the muse for creativity"],[205,823.59,4.4,"and allow that creativity to be born through our work."],[205,828.52,1.64,"Do you feel that way as well?"],[205,830.19,1.17,"RBS: Totally."],[205,831.36,2.0,"DM: How do you feel like that happens?"],[205,833.4,1.4,"I'm waiting, you know,"],[205,834.83,3.74,"I'm like, come on, come on, come through."],[205,838.6,2.4,"How does that feel?"],[205,841.4,3.0,"RBS: For instance, when I'm doing a public art piece,"],[205,844.41,1.37,"I have to go to the place."],[205,845.81,1.47,"I have to sit there."],[205,847.31,1.37,"And I ask."],[205,849.45,1.37,"And I wait for an answer."],[205,850.85,1.53,"I say, what needs to be here,"],[205,852.41,1.2,"what needs to be told,"],[205,853.65,2.47,"what story needs to be, you know,"],[205,856.12,1.67,"manifested here to make change."],[205,857.82,1.8,"And how can I be of service?"],[205,860.26,2.23,"And then I wait, and I listen, and it comes."],[205,862.83,1.17,"DM: Always?"],[205,863.99,1.17,"RBS: Real fast."],[205,865.2,1.5,"It's like truth."],[205,866.7,1.17,"There's only truth."],[205,867.9,1.8,"And when you're in alignment with it,"],[205,869.7,2.7,"it’s like you just tuned into it, and boom, you found it."],[205,872.4,2.17,"And then you're like, OK, time to get started."],[205,874.57,4.87,"And so when I enter into the studio, and, you know, I get into that place,"],[205,879.48,2.47,"it's like, OK, what needs to be done?"],[205,881.98,1.33,"What needs to be said?"],[205,883.35,1.74,"How can I be of service?"],[205,885.08,4.37,"When I first met my 1985 Chevy El Camino --"],[205,889.45,1.17,"DM: Is that Maria?"],[205,890.65,1.13,"RBS: Maria."],[205,891.82,3.87,"She, you know, when I first painted her,"],[205,895.69,1.37,"the first day I painted her,"],[205,897.06,1.9,"she's a satin black with a gloss clear,"],[205,899.0,2.17,"made to look like our traditional pottery,"],[205,901.2,3.64,"I brought her home, and I was sitting on the porch with a folding chair,"],[205,904.87,3.17,"and I was looking at her and I was like, I didn't do this."],[205,908.07,1.4,"This wasn't me."],[205,909.84,4.87,"She used me to make her into what she was always meant to be."],[205,914.74,1.33,"And I just listened."],[205,916.65,4.27,"And it's incredible to see and give the power back"],[205,920.95,3.04,"and say, thank you for choosing me"],[205,924.02,2.77,"to be a part of this process of your becoming."],[205,927.66,3.57,"DM: How involved do you feel you are"],[205,931.26,3.8,"in determining the aesthetic?"],[205,935.1,4.71,"If there's something that you're making, are you in the process while making,"],[205,939.8,2.13,"also evaluating the making?"],[205,941.97,3.4,"RBS: Oh, I love the subject of aesthetic."],[205,946.08,2.87,"I like teaching aesthetics to students, etc.,"],[205,948.98,3.9,"because have you ever heard the term \"don't yuck my yum?\""],[205,953.38,4.71,"So to me, aesthetic is when we let go of our thoughts"],[205,958.12,3.4,"and find our yum."],[205,962.33,3.47,"And sometimes what we thought was our yum isn't."],[205,965.79,1.8,"If we sit with it long enough, right?"],[205,967.6,4.84,"So it's like, how do you refine and refine and refine your yum"],[205,972.47,3.4,"until it's just mhm, mhm."],[205,975.9,1.47,"Right? What is that?"],[205,977.41,2.44,"And how do we live in that long enough"],[205,979.88,3.34,"to where it's like, no, it's actually just to the left."],[205,983.25,1.7,"And if you close your eyes,"],[205,984.98,4.27,"you feel it click, and that’s where it is, right?"],[205,989.28,3.67,"That is the finding of it, is tuning."],[205,993.36,1.17,"It's tuning to it."],[205,994.52,3.2,"DM: I think also having that self-reliance all the way,"],[205,997.76,2.9,"going all the way back to your origin story,"],[205,1000.7,4.07,"to know that you can trust your sense and your judgment."],[205,1004.77,2.4,"RBS: Yeah, and it's a muscle."],[205,1007.17,2.17,"I think it's a muscle and we have to build it."],[205,1010.71,4.6,"DM: You've talked about animacy, the soul of things."],[205,1015.31,4.44,"And you blur the line between making and being."],[205,1019.78,4.24,"And a lot of that, as you said, is about listening."],[205,1024.05,4.87,"What kind of silence do you hope your work leaves behind?"],[205,1031.53,2.27,"RBS: The silence I hope my work leaves behind"],[205,1033.83,6.31,"is the one that is full of information,"],[205,1040.17,4.0,"because there's so much to learn"],[205,1044.21,1.24,"when we shut up."],[205,1047.04,1.47,"There's so much."],[205,1049.68,1.6,"And my pieces,"],[205,1051.31,3.87,"unless there are 410-horsepower 350, going really fast,"],[205,1055.22,1.24,"are very quiet."],[205,1057.05,3.04,"And in that silence, there's connection."],[205,1061.62,1.6,"DM: Thank you, Rose Simpson."],[205,1063.23,1.44,"RBS: Thank you for having me."],[206,4.24,3.8,"So in summer 2018, I went on a first date."],[206,8.41,1.4,"Now, in my early 30s,"],[206,9.84,2.57,"this, of course, was not my first first date."],[206,12.41,3.8,"And so I wasn't expecting much except for some beers and tacos."],[206,16.75,4.57,"That was until I was too many beers in and too deep in conversation."],[206,22.45,2.7,"My question to him at the end of the night:"],[206,25.19,2.4,"\"Do you plan to vaccinate your kids?\""],[206,31.13,1.17,"His reply?"],[206,32.33,3.07,"\"Sure, except for the flu vaccine,"],[206,35.43,2.47,"because the flu vaccine has viruses in it,"],[206,37.94,2.8,"and every time I take it, I get sick.\""],[206,41.37,2.94,"I guess he didn't realize that he was sitting across the table"],[206,44.34,2.54,"from a woman who had just invented a novel flu vaccine"],[206,46.91,2.64,"that was then slated for clinical trial."],[206,49.58,1.13,"But --"],[206,52.45,2.74,"At the same time, I was a research fellow"],[206,55.22,3.87,"at the National Institutes of Health Vaccine Research Center."],[206,59.09,1.2,"And so normally,"],[206,60.29,3.1,"that answer would have sent me packing onto my next first date,"],[206,63.43,1.67,"but I like this guy."],[206,65.5,3.84,"And so I stayed and I answered a few questions"],[206,69.37,1.64,"after getting another beer."],[206,71.67,4.14,"I told him that, yes, the flu vaccine does have viruses in it,"],[206,75.84,1.5,"but those viruses are not alive"],[206,77.34,3.07,"and so they technically cannot \"make you sick.\""],[206,80.91,2.74,"Sure, you may feel a little groggy after getting the shot,"],[206,83.65,3.47,"but that's just a sign that your immune system is working."],[206,87.72,2.24,"And I asked him a simple question."],[206,90.32,2.87,"Did he know that there are 200 or more viruses"],[206,93.22,3.94,"that can cause flu-like symptoms or the common cold?"],[206,97.19,1.74,"Of course, he didn't have an answer,"],[206,98.96,3.0,"but that's what you get when you take a vaccinologist on a date."],[206,103.33,3.47,"Surprisingly, he was not offended"],[206,106.84,2.14,"and he asked me out on a second date."],[206,109.64,3.47,"And I got more than a second date out of this guy."],[206,113.44,2.0,"I got my first understanding"],[206,115.48,4.3,"of what I now like to call “vaccine inquisitiveness.”"],[206,121.12,3.34,"It wasn't that he was just reluctant to get himself vaccines"],[206,124.49,2.4,"or to get his future children vaccinated."],[206,127.29,2.9,"It was that he had questions, concerns,"],[206,130.19,2.44,"misinformation buried deep down,"],[206,132.66,2.84,"for which he needed an expert like myself"],[206,135.53,3.17,"to answer without scorn and without judgment."],[206,139.44,4.5,"Nevertheless, I went back into the lab and I did what I do, making vaccines."],[206,144.54,3.14,"And then on December 31 of the next year,"],[206,147.71,4.24,"there was a report of a mysterious virus in Wuhan, China."],[206,152.32,2.67,"It had sickened 27 people."],[206,155.55,3.57,"This was a moment that my team had prepared for."],[206,159.52,1.97,"You see, not only was I on the flu team,"],[206,161.53,4.14,"but I was also the scientific lead of the coronavirus vaccine team."],[206,170.33,3.67,"You see those orange proteins on the surface of this coronavirus?"],[206,174.04,1.6,"Those are called spike proteins."],[206,175.67,3.44,"And those proteins are responsible for driving immunity."],[206,179.14,3.1,"So we developed a spike protein with a small sequence change"],[206,182.28,4.87,"that made it an ideal vaccine candidate in case of a pandemic."],[206,187.59,1.77,"And that's exactly what happened."],[206,190.19,2.07,"In January of 2020,"],[206,192.29,4.04,"that virus that was circulating was deemed to be a coronavirus."],[206,196.36,1.77,"We joined forces with Moderna,"],[206,198.16,1.47,"and we promised ourselves"],[206,199.66,5.87,"that we were going to get into a phase-1 clinical trial in only 100 days."],[206,206.37,2.1,"A purely scientific goal, by the way."],[206,208.5,2.94,"But as I look back and I write this speech,"],[206,211.47,3.14,"I think there was probably a little bit of ego attached to it too."],[206,215.04,2.2,"But that's OK because we did it."],[206,218.82,1.23,"I mean."],[206,223.79,3.37,"After countless hours in the lab,"],[206,227.16,2.64,"testing that vaccine in mice and monkeys"],[206,229.83,4.17,"and determining that it was safe and effective enough to go into humans,"],[206,234.0,3.27,"that vaccine, mRNA-1273,"],[206,237.3,2.64,"or what you now know to be called Spikevax,"],[206,239.97,5.31,"went into a phase-1 clinical trial in only 66 days, a world record."],[206,248.68,1.23,"At the end of the year,"],[206,249.91,3.97,"that vaccine was determined to be 94 percent effective against disease."],[206,254.35,2.6,"So we were proud of ourselves, obviously."],[206,257.59,1.67,"But what was the gut punch for me"],[206,259.29,1.77,"was that 20 percent of people"],[206,261.09,4.21,"were what the WHO would call vaccine hesitant."],[206,265.3,2.07,"Now, vaccine hesitancy is not new."],[206,267.96,1.97,"Even prior to the pandemic,"],[206,269.97,5.17,"vaccine hesitancy was deemed to be one of [the] top 10 global health threats."],[206,275.81,1.53,"Top 10 global health threats."],[206,277.37,5.44,"And that is something that even me, making vaccines, was not going to fix."],[206,283.45,3.17,"And the truth of the matter is that vaccinologists, like myself,"],[206,286.65,2.9,"had long ignored this population of people"],[206,289.55,4.0,"who just were not going to take vaccines for one reason or the other."],[206,293.92,3.07,"We were too busy publishing our data in world-renowned journals,"],[206,297.03,1.8,"kind of talking to ourselves."],[206,299.73,2.54,"And the other truth of the matter is"],[206,302.3,2.27,"that we made some honest communication mistakes"],[206,304.6,1.57,"during the pandemic."],[206,306.2,2.44,"And I'll be the first to just talk about myself."],[206,309.17,1.74,"The first time I got on TV,"],[206,310.94,3.54,"I said that the vaccine was going to curb transmission."],[206,314.85,2.47,"And at that time it did."],[206,317.85,4.0,"But what I should have said is that over time, as the virus mutates,"],[206,321.88,3.0,"the vaccine may not be as good at curbing transmission."],[206,325.66,3.67,"And then we should have taken just a little bit more care"],[206,329.36,5.8,"and tenderness to talk to people about the possibility of vaccine injury."],[206,335.2,3.74,"Even if vaccine injury didn't turn up in our clinical trials,"],[206,338.97,2.07,"there was always this possibility,"],[206,341.04,3.3,"and those types of stats didn't matter to someone"],[206,344.34,2.97,"who was reading headlines about vaccine injury"],[206,347.34,2.57,"and deciding whether they were going to get themselves"],[206,349.95,2.33,"or their loved ones vaccinated."],[206,352.72,5.44,"You see, some people had questions and concerns that were genuinely valid."],[206,358.52,3.87,"And some people actually had criticisms that were genuinely valid, too."],[206,362.39,2.74,"Except for that one time the internet went ablaze"],[206,365.16,2.5,"when they said my hair was ugly on CNN."],[206,368.87,1.94,"I like to think that I'm pretty cute."],[206,373.64,2.34,"Seriously though,"],[206,376.01,3.14,"one of the major concerns was that we were moving too fast."],[206,379.14,2.27,"And so I'm going to stand right here --"],[206,381.44,1.2,"is this the middle? --"],[206,382.68,3.44,"In the middle of this TED circle and say,"],[206,386.12,4.41,"there was so much research that was done prior to the pandemic."],[206,390.55,2.97,"People studied coronavirus’s basic biology."],[206,393.56,3.17,"The Nobel laureate studied how to make mRNA feasible"],[206,396.76,2.2,"to deliver medicines into the body."],[206,398.96,1.8,"We even did our thing, too."],[206,400.8,4.94,"Nevertheless, all of that research pushed the coronavirus vaccine out quicker,"],[206,405.77,1.94,"safer and more effectively."],[206,408.27,2.37,"All of that notwithstanding,"],[206,410.67,3.77,"some people still had questions that needed to be answered."],[206,414.88,3.74,"Some people were still vaccine inquisitive."],[206,418.65,2.2,"No, they were not anti-vax"],[206,420.88,2.6,"and no, they weren't even anti-science."],[206,423.82,2.2,"They were unconvinced."],[206,426.66,3.9,"And it was our job as scientists to make them convinced."],[206,430.56,2.84,"Except that failed communication lingered on,"],[206,433.43,4.41,"and I'm afraid that maybe some of that failed communication"],[206,437.87,4.47,"is the reason why today we have a rise in vaccine refusal."],[206,442.81,2.14,"Kindergartners are showing up to kindergarten"],[206,444.94,2.27,"without their full vaccine regimens."],[206,447.24,2.6,"Measles is on the rise in many communities."],[206,449.88,1.17,"And measles, by the way,"],[206,451.08,3.7,"is a vaccine-preventable disease."],[206,455.99,3.5,"And for me, that's really unfortunate"],[206,459.52,4.4,"because I know that there are 23 viral families"],[206,463.96,2.84,"that have the potential to infect humans."],[206,466.83,3.34,"So it's not a question of whether there will be another pandemic."],[206,470.53,1.94,"It's a matter of when."],[206,473.14,4.04,"And so I guess I'm lucky because I will always have a job."],[206,477.61,4.07,"But as a vaccine inventor at Harvard University,"],[206,481.71,5.24,"my lab studies how to make nanoparticles or small protein balls into vaccines."],[206,486.95,3.7,"We put viral proteins on the surfaces of those nanoparticles,"],[206,490.69,1.87,"kind of in a click-and-bait way,"],[206,492.59,3.17,"with small linkers that allow us to drive those viral proteins"],[206,495.79,3.1,"directly to the cells in the immune system that we need"],[206,498.93,2.57,"to make ideal vaccines for pandemics."],[206,501.53,3.14,"We could put just about any type of viral protein on the surface,"],[206,504.7,2.47,"many of them at the same time,"],[206,507.17,1.5,"so that we can make vaccines"],[206,508.67,4.2,"that are hypothetically good against many viruses in one shot."],[206,513.24,1.17,"Great."],[206,514.44,4.0,"Vaccine technology is going to go on and on and on,"],[206,518.48,2.44,"but I like to believe that vaccine hesitancy"],[206,520.95,2.47,"does not have to go on and on and on with it."],[206,523.92,4.87,"If researchers would just open their minds to vaccine inquisitiveness,"],[206,528.83,3.9,"in the same way that we open our mind to scientific inquisitiveness."],[206,533.53,3.27,"If community and science were to meet."],[206,537.57,1.97,"And that's not really that revolutionary."],[206,539.57,2.77,"We already know that when community and science meet,"],[206,542.37,2.4,"science advances faster,"],[206,544.81,3.5,"vaccine refusal turns into vaccine acceptance."],[206,549.25,4.34,"Actually, when [a] vaccine inquisitive meets a scientist on a date,"],[206,553.62,1.74,"it turns into a marriage."],[206,560.76,4.37,"You know, one thing about this is that my husband still has questions"],[206,565.13,3.3,"every single time we take our children to get their vaccines."],[206,568.87,2.7,"And I still have to find the answers."],[206,571.57,2.5,"Not just as a scientist,"],[206,574.07,2.3,"but now as a mother,"],[206,576.41,3.2,"because I'm concerned about my children's safety"],[206,579.64,3.07,"and my children's lives depend on it."],[206,583.18,1.13,"Thank you."],[207,3.93,1.9,"Teenagers today are"],[207,7.91,1.17,"amazing."],[207,9.91,2.17,"You didn't think I was going to use that word,"],[207,12.08,1.2,"because as we age,"],[207,13.31,2.64,"our adult brains have a harder time making this"],[207,15.95,2.9,"teenager-equals-amazing connection."],[207,20.15,2.0,"I've always worried that I'd reach the age"],[207,22.19,3.1,"where I'd look back and immediately judge the youth of today"],[207,25.32,1.97,"for what they wear"],[207,27.29,1.7,"or how they spend their time,"],[207,29.03,2.74,"lament the loss of my free-range childhood,"],[207,31.8,2.3,"and wish that they would spend more of their time"],[207,34.13,1.27,"doing things that we did,"],[207,35.43,2.8,"like, crash our cars and excessively drink."],[207,38.23,1.17,"Right?"],[207,41.17,1.37,"True story."],[207,43.17,1.87,"But I'm beginning to think"],[207,45.04,2.44,"that I'm immune from judging teenagers in this way,"],[207,47.51,3.44,"and I think it's because I now know too much about them."],[207,50.98,1.44,"So let me explain."],[207,52.42,1.6,"I'm a developmental psychologist,"],[207,54.02,3.3,"and for the past 25 years -- that's a long time --"],[207,57.35,2.74,"I've been analyzing trends in teen mental health."],[207,60.12,1.74,"Since 2008,"],[207,61.89,3.54,"we’ve been working with thousands of 10 to 14-year-olds"],[207,65.43,2.34,"getting information every day from their phones"],[207,67.8,2.9,"on how they're feeling, how they spend their time,"],[207,70.73,1.17,"who they're with."],[207,71.94,1.17,"With their consent,"],[207,73.14,2.77,"we look at their school records, we track their sleep data,"],[207,75.94,1.67,"we look at their step count,"],[207,77.64,1.84,"and we see what they're doing online."],[207,80.08,2.9,"Importantly, we listen to them"],[207,83.01,1.47,"about what upsets them"],[207,84.48,1.64,"and what they need to be well."],[207,87.08,2.54,"It has been a really incredible"],[207,89.62,2.6,"and at times, a wild ride."],[207,93.02,2.7,"It's also been an illuminating one"],[207,95.76,3.54,"because one of the most consistent things we have found"],[207,99.33,3.6,"is that the stories that you and I are told repeatedly, every day"],[207,102.93,1.53,"about teenagers today --"],[207,104.47,1.37,"that they are lost,"],[207,105.87,1.64,"that they are worse off than ever,"],[207,107.5,4.17,"that somehow smartphones and social media have destroyed them and their brains."],[207,112.48,1.77,"It doesn't match the data."],[207,114.28,2.04,"And it doesn't match what they tell us."],[207,116.31,2.47,"There is a massive gap."],[207,120.08,2.07,"And as I've tried to make sense of this gap,"],[207,122.15,3.47,"I keep coming back to a simple truth that we all know:"],[207,126.62,1.6,"scary stories sell."],[207,129.16,1.5,"They always have."],[207,130.69,2.14,"And the more often you hear something,"],[207,132.86,2.87,"the more likely you are to believe that it's true."],[207,135.73,1.27,"I'm going to say it again."],[207,137.03,2.2,"The more often you hear something,"],[207,139.27,2.57,"the more likely you are to believe that it's true."],[207,143.81,3.1,"And scary stories are really easy to sell to parents."],[207,146.94,1.6,"We are an anxious lot."],[207,149.45,2.14,"The hypervigilance -- it hits us."],[207,151.58,3.34,"When we hold that tiny baby in our arms,"],[207,154.92,1.3,"our entire world changes."],[207,157.02,3.07,"It hits again when they hit puberty,"],[207,160.09,4.17,"and they begin to change in these dramatic and rapid ways."],[207,164.29,2.5,"When our sense of control over them"],[207,166.83,4.04,"and what comes next begins to feel less certain."],[207,172.04,1.43,"I get this."],[207,173.5,3.1,"My 15-year-old is six-foot-four."],[207,177.44,2.57,"My 12-year-old is embracing independence"],[207,180.01,2.2,"like she's leaving for college next week."],[207,184.15,1.17,"But the good news is,"],[207,185.35,2.4,"is that the real story about teens today"],[207,187.78,1.5,"is far more positive"],[207,189.32,3.04,"and far less frightening than the stories we all read."],[207,192.96,4.2,"They have made remarkable progress on metrics that really matter."],[207,197.16,5.11,"And we know a lot about how to keep them happy, safe, and well."],[207,202.93,1.87,"OK, the bad news."],[207,206.3,4.44,"The bad news is: we are in the middle of an adult mental health crisis."],[207,212.01,3.34,"And caregiver mental health is the most important predictor"],[207,215.35,2.7,"of teen mental health by far."],[207,218.08,2.13,"So if we are concerned about teens today,"],[207,220.22,4.54,"we need to invest in real and meaningful ways in the adults around them."],[207,225.19,2.64,"Many of them are simply not OK."],[207,228.49,1.57,"So I'll come back to that."],[207,230.09,4.6,"But first, I want to share with you a few science-based facts about kids today."],[207,234.7,1.94,"And I'm going to focus on American teens"],[207,236.67,4.27,"because that's where a lot of the stories that you and I are hearing come from."],[207,241.64,3.54,"So in the past 20 years, we've had some major wins."],[207,245.64,4.1,"Rates of teen violence, alcohol use, pregnancy"],[207,249.78,2.6,"have plummeted to historic lows."],[207,252.95,3.7,"You are looking at the most educated generation ever"],[207,256.69,1.8,"in terms of high school graduation."],[207,259.59,2.44,"Young people are inventors."],[207,262.06,1.94,"They’re activists. They’re leaders."],[207,264.03,1.47,"They’re amazing singers."],[207,265.53,2.5,"They’re Olympians."],[207,268.03,1.3,"They're amazing."],[207,270.3,2.94,"But they’re also telling us that they’re sadder,"],[207,273.27,3.34,"and they’e more worried about the world that they’re growing up in."],[207,276.61,5.21,"So they report increased concerns about safety at school, climate change,"],[207,281.85,2.2,"racism, their future."],[207,284.75,3.84,"In our studies, what we find is the most frequently reported stressors"],[207,288.58,4.3,"are conflict in their home and pressure to do well at school."],[207,292.92,1.6,"And those are also the things"],[207,294.52,2.57,"that predict their mental health day to day."],[207,297.46,1.5,"Now,"],[207,299.0,1.64,"since 2008,"],[207,300.66,3.0,"we've seen an uptick in youth suicide risk."],[207,304.37,2.04,"But perhaps this shouldn't be surprising,"],[207,306.44,3.1,"because adult suicide has been increasing dramatically"],[207,309.57,2.77,"in the United States since 1999."],[207,312.98,3.47,"Remember when I said that adult mental health"],[207,316.48,2.77,"and caregiver mental health is the most important predictor"],[207,319.28,1.27,"of child mental health?"],[207,320.55,1.23,"Well, with that in mind,"],[207,321.82,1.87,"I want to take a look at this slide."],[207,323.72,4.77,"This graph here shows you that between 2011 and 2021,"],[207,328.49,4.54,"the rate of overdoses due to drug use among parents more than doubled."],[207,333.96,2.4,"People ask me all the time:"],[207,336.37,2.47,"what could have happened during this period"],[207,338.87,3.1,"other than social media coming online?"],[207,343.51,2.27,"The answer is that adults were in distress"],[207,345.81,1.67,"and parents were dying."],[207,347.98,3.1,"OK, let's go back to that elephant in the room."],[207,351.75,3.4,"Social media and smartphones also increased during this period."],[207,355.89,1.47,"But here's the weird thing."],[207,357.39,1.5,"In our longitudinal studies,"],[207,358.92,4.87,"social media does not emerge as a major predictor of teen mental health."],[207,364.36,2.44,"Many others find the same,"],[207,366.83,1.83,"concluding, and I'm going to quote,"],[207,368.7,3.9,"that social media is one of the “least influential factors”"],[207,372.64,1.64,"in predicting teen mental health."],[207,374.74,4.27,"For most adolescents, like boys, there's no association."],[207,379.04,3.8,"And for girls, what we find is that girls that are depressed"],[207,382.85,3.1,"go on to use social media more --"],[207,385.98,2.0,"but not the other way around."],[207,387.99,4.3,"Social media does not meaningfully predict future mental health problems."],[207,393.19,5.0,"Now, despite this, adults are quickly converging on banning social media"],[207,398.23,1.64,"for under-16s"],[207,399.9,4.91,"as a simple and singular solution to solving the youth mental health crisis."],[207,404.83,1.64,"And here is the crazy part:"],[207,407.0,2.54,"there’s not one single study to date"],[207,409.57,2.44,"that has actually tested whether shutting it off"],[207,412.04,2.04,"impacts their mental health."],[207,414.11,2.57,"And when we do this among adults,"],[207,416.71,2.5,"we find on average impacts"],[207,419.25,3.07,"that are close to or indistinguishable from zero."],[207,423.05,4.84,"I know, there's a big distance between the story we're told and the data."],[207,428.62,1.7,"The National Academies of Sciences,"],[207,430.36,2.77,"one of the most well-respected organizations in the world,"],[207,433.13,1.53,"convened an expert panel,"],[207,434.7,1.94,"and they came to this conclusion also."],[207,437.17,1.24,"OK."],[207,438.4,2.17,"So before you start throwing stuff at me,"],[207,440.6,2.47,"I want to clarify a few things."],[207,444.81,2.4,"I'm not saying there are no harms online."],[207,447.24,3.84,"I'm not saying big tech does not require massive overhaul."],[207,451.11,2.1,"Both things can be true."],[207,453.25,2.5,"We need to clean up the online world for everyone and"],[207,455.75,2.5,"especially our children --"],[207,458.29,3.07,"and social media is not the major driver"],[207,461.39,1.57,"of teen mental health problems"],[207,462.99,2.07,"for most adolescents today."],[207,466.13,3.74,"But instead of cleaning up the online world"],[207,469.87,3.5,"and prosecuting perpetrators of online harm,"],[207,473.4,4.11,"regardless of the office or the position in society that they hold,"],[207,480.01,2.54,"we are punishing victims."],[207,482.58,3.54,"We're kicking them out of the spaces they go to be with friends,"],[207,486.15,1.7,"to consume youth culture,"],[207,487.85,4.77,"and yes, sadly, many times to escape people that are harming them offline."],[207,496.23,2.94,"We've already kicked teenagers out of public spaces."],[207,499.2,1.64,"In the US, we've created a society"],[207,500.86,4.04,"where firearms are the number one killer of our children,"],[207,504.94,3.37,"and now we're telling our kids that we're going to take away the spaces"],[207,508.34,2.84,"that they're going to virtually gather and create community,"],[207,511.21,2.77,"because adults broke that, too?"],[207,515.45,3.04,"Yeah, I'm saying adults broke the internet"],[207,518.51,2.87,"and they're trying to fix it by kicking kids off."],[207,522.82,4.21,"So a social media ban might feel good for the adults in the room,"],[207,527.06,3.2,"but teens tell me, and I believe them --"],[207,530.29,1.47,"it's not going to work."],[207,531.79,3.7,"It'll push them into less safe and less regulated spaces,"],[207,535.5,2.74,"and it will prevent us from doing what we really need"],[207,538.27,1.7,"to help them be well."],[207,540.84,2.3,"So if a social media ban might make things worse,"],[207,543.17,1.67,"what would make things better?"],[207,544.88,5.21,"So first, we need to invest in the adults around children."],[207,550.48,4.54,"Did you know that the ratio of counselors to students in US middle schools"],[207,555.02,2.04,"is 1 to 500?"],[207,558.42,2.74,"Spending millions of dollars on yonder pouches"],[207,561.19,1.64,"to lock up kids' phones"],[207,562.86,1.7,"is not going to solve that."],[207,564.56,1.4,"We need to take that money."],[207,566.0,1.27,"We need to hire teachers."],[207,567.26,1.4,"We need to hire counselors."],[207,568.66,2.87,"And we need to pay them well."],[207,577.11,3.5,"Building a healthy human requires investment in children"],[207,580.64,1.77,"and the adults around them."],[207,582.45,1.37,"Full stop."],[207,585.78,6.27,"Second, we need to build spaces that are welcoming and safe for all teens,"],[207,592.05,3.07,"to build the skills they need for the future."],[207,595.16,2.8,"So my colleague Stephen Schueller spends his days"],[207,597.96,4.5,"designing digital mental health services for rural communities"],[207,602.47,3.74,"and opening up youth drop-in centers across the state of California"],[207,606.24,1.7,"for young people who need it."],[207,608.61,2.8,"We know young people are more likely to go online"],[207,611.41,1.7,"when they're anxious and depressed."],[207,613.14,2.3,"We need to be there to build the services"],[207,615.48,1.67,"and supports that they need,"],[207,617.18,2.97,"instead of just focusing on taking things away."],[207,621.42,2.17,"And three,"],[207,623.62,5.51,"please do not believe everything that you hear about young people today."],[207,629.86,4.17,"A favorite pastime of adults has been shaming young people."],[207,634.03,2.9,"But this generation is not lost or destroyed."],[207,636.97,2.07,"They are resilient."],[207,639.04,2.5,"They are resilient because they are succeeding"],[207,641.54,2.87,"despite the suffering of adults around them."],[207,644.44,2.4,"And if we want to help them to continue to succeed,"],[207,646.88,2.6,"we set high expectations"],[207,649.51,3.2,"and then we need to support them in getting them there."],[207,652.75,3.74,"This is the magic combination that has always worked in teaching,"],[207,656.52,2.13,"in coaching, in parenting."],[207,659.12,1.87,"It still works with teens today."],[207,661.02,1.77,"Tech has not changed that."],[207,663.93,1.64,"And finally,"],[207,665.6,3.1,"just so you believe me, I'm going to say it again."],[207,668.7,1.8,"Big tech does not get a pass."],[207,671.3,3.17,"This cannot fall to parents and teachers."],[207,674.47,3.2,"We require more regulation, but bans don’t do that."],[207,677.71,2.07,"They let companies off the hook."],[207,680.48,3.47,"Instead, we need to fund teachers."],[207,683.98,3.54,"We need to build communities and spaces for children"],[207,687.55,2.84,"and teenagers to play and to learn."],[207,690.42,3.6,"And we need to deliver effective digital mental health services"],[207,694.06,2.24,"and digital literacy programs to young people"],[207,696.33,2.0,"where and when they need them."],[207,699.16,3.07,"And we're going to pay for all of this"],[207,702.26,2.57,"with a big old tax on tech."],[207,704.87,1.2,"Right?"],[207,710.24,4.4,"Did I mention that I'm Canadian and I do not mind taxing the rich?"],[207,717.01,4.37,"But if our goal is to support young people,"],[207,721.42,2.37,"and I assume that we all share that,"],[207,723.82,1.27,"then we will fail"],[207,725.12,3.74,"if all we do is focus on tech and taking things away."],[207,729.53,4.67,"If all we see when we look at our kids is their phone,"],[207,734.23,3.07,"we’re going to fail to see what they really need from us."],[207,737.33,1.17,"Thank you."],[208,0.24,5.28,"Strange things are happening around the Pacific Ocean right now. A 10-foot great white shark"],[208,5.52,5.28,"spotted dangerously close to a group of children swimming north of Los Angeles. Hundreds of hungry"],[208,10.8,5.44,"pelicans are flocking to Peruvian ports and coastal markets. The worst winter storm front"],[208,16.24,6.96,"to hit Chile in more than a decade. And all signs point to El Niño as a major reason why. A number"],[208,23.2,6.48,"of climate models are calling for the possibility of a Super El Niño brewing this summer. El Niño"],[208,29.68,6.8,"is not uncommon. This climate phenomenon occurs every few years, but this one could be different."],[208,36.48,6.64,"It's been called a potential Super or Godzilla El Niño, and it could fuel extreme and dangerous"],[208,43.12,6.0,"weather conditions globally through 2027. So, now seems like a good time to explain the science"],[208,49.12,8.72,"behind El Niño, how strong this one might be, and how it could impact our global ecosystem."],[208,57.84,6.56,"An El Niño gets declared when trade winds shift and temperatures rise in the Pacific Ocean. The"],[208,64.4,6.24,"trade winds along the equator are constantly blowing from the east to the west. They're"],[208,70.64,6.72,"called the trade winds because hundreds of years ago, the sailing ships could rely on those steady"],[208,77.36,6.08,"winds to move them across the Pacific. And they essentially pile up warm water in the"],[208,83.44,7.92,"far western Pacific. When they slow down, even briefly, they can allow that warm water to slide"],[208,91.36,6.24,"backwards under the surface of the ocean. All of that water will become warm as those trade winds"],[208,97.6,5.04,"weaken. And then when they hit about a half a degree centigrade, about 1 degree Fahrenheit"],[208,102.64,6.88,"over the central Pacific, then our team here will declare an El Niño event. Noah says the"],[208,109.52,6.24,"El Niño has officially begun. El Niño is a part of the naturally recurring climate pattern known"],[208,115.76,7.28,"as the El Niño Southern Oscillation or ENSO. That acronym ENSO, El Niño Southern Oscillation,"],[208,123.04,6.88,"encompasses the entire El Niño and La Niña system. La Niña is the opposite phase with cooler than"],[208,129.92,8.32,"average surface temperature and generally we see swings between the two. They tend to recur on a 2"],[208,138.24,6.8,"to 7 year cycle. There's also a neutral phase in the middle when neither El Niño nor La Niña are"],[208,145.04,6.56,"present. So El Niño is just a normal part of this constantly replaying cycle in the ocean."],[208,151.6,5.52,"But what's not normal is how strong scientists are predicting that this current El Niño could"],[208,157.12,5.2,"become. We do have a grading system. We actually grade it based on the sea surface temperatures in"],[208,162.32,8.0,"the central Pacific. Greater than 2° C, which is about 3.8° F. It's a very strong El Niño° C,. We"],[208,170.32,6.16,"have an 81% chance of being in a very strong El Niño. That's the highest odds I've seen for that."],[208,176.48,7.92,"The atmospheric response scales with that. So, if that temperature is much much warmer than average,"],[208,184.4,8.96,"then that's a stronger El Niño and has a stronger influence over global atmospheric circulations."],[208,193.36,5.6,"A contributing factor to the strength of this El Niño is what's known as a Kelvin wave. A"],[208,198.96,8.56,"9,000 mile long warm water mass moving across the Pacific. That's a whopping 7.5° C or 13.5°"],[208,207.52,7.52,"F warmer than the average. That warm water moving under the surface, it rises up as it moves to the"],[208,215.04,7.28,"east and provides a a source of heat for the surface and therefore feeds an El Niño event."],[208,222.32,7.2,"We've only seen an El Niño with this much strength a few times in the past. From 1877 to 1878,"],[208,229.52,5.84,"an El Niño caused a famine that killed millions of people. From 1982 to 1983,"],[208,235.36,5.12,"a super-powered El Niño brought drought to nearly every continent and destructive floods in North"],[208,240.48,7.36,"and South America. And another from 1997 to 1998 caused widespread record rainfalls, mudslides,"],[208,247.84,5.92,"wildfires, and coral bleaching. So, what can we expect with the strength of the current El"],[208,253.76,6.48,"Niño continuing to gather over the coming months? The World Meteorological Organization's seasonal"],[208,260.24,6.32,"outlook is predicting temperatures and rainfall far outside of global averages. And a stronger"],[208,266.56,7.28,"El Niño means more intense shifts in weather. It makes a parade of storms more likely to happen."],[208,273.84,5.04,"Because El Niño can shift rain away from where it normally falls. It's not just that wetness"],[208,278.88,6.32,"or dryness increases, but where it increases, in places that aren't necessarily used to dealing"],[208,285.2,4.64,"with those conditions. You can get a lot of these kind of pattern changes. There's parts of"],[208,289.84,5.6,"western Chile, the Atacama desert that, you know, they don't get rain maybe once every 25 years,"],[208,295.44,6.56,"but they do when it's an El Niño. More wetness into Southern California, across Arizona, Texas."],[208,302.0,4.96,"The potential risk of extreme weather events is higher given the strength of this El Niño;"],[208,306.96,4.72,"droughts in southern Asia, flooding in the Americas, and hurricanes forming in the East"],[208,311.68,5.68,"Pacific Ocean. They can bring a lot more rainfall to the, you know, Peru, Ecuador, and Chile,"],[208,317.36,4.8,"and have devastating consequences there. We see drier conditions in Indonesia, which can"],[208,322.16,5.68,"contribute to wildfires. The far eastern Pacific can be, the water can be quite a lot warmer than"],[208,327.84,7.2,"average and that has a lot of implications for fish and for species that depend on fish. Those"],[208,335.04,4.32,"sharks that are showing up on the beaches of Southern California? They're heading north of"],[208,339.36,5.04,"their usual habitat because their normal hangouts have gotten too warm, and the fish that they eat"],[208,344.4,5.76,"are migrating to cooler, more nutrient-rich water. And as this El Niño gathers strength,"],[208,350.16,4.56,"it appears it will continue through the winter, when it's expected to peak. North America,"],[208,354.72,5.2,"southern Australia, and some other regions like that. We expect those impacts to be greater in"],[208,359.92,8.32,"the winter. More rain, especially in the southern tier of the US. Historically, we have seen snowier"],[208,368.24,5.76,"winters during El Niño, largely because of that increased moisture. But even with that foresight,"],[208,374.0,19.52,"we still face a test of what some are calling one of the strongest El Niño ever recorded."],[209,7.46,2.92,"It's 6:00 in the morning, pitch black outside."],[209,10.55,2.92,"My 14-year-old son is fast asleep in his bed."],[209,13.59,3.34,"I flip on the light and physically shake the poor boy awake."],[209,17.18,2.42,"because I know that, like ripping off a Band-Aid,"],[209,19.6,2.25,"it's better to get it over with quickly."],[209,22.1,2.75,"Sound brutal, but perhaps familiar?"],[209,25.27,5.09,"Every morning I ask myself, how can I, knowing what I know,"],[209,30.65,1.88,"be doing this to my own son?"],[209,32.78,2.5,"You see, I’m a sleep researcher."],[209,35.57,4.0,"So I know that I’m depriving my son of sleep he desperately needs"],[209,39.62,1.96,"as a rapidly growing teenager."],[209,41.75,1.92,"I also know that by waking him up"],[209,43.66,4.0,"hours before his natural biological clock tells him he’s ready,"],[209,47.67,2.59,"I’m literally robbing him of the type of sleep"],[209,50.25,5.71,"most associated with learning, memory consolidation, and emotional processing."],[209,56.05,2.92,"But it's not just my kid that's being deprived of sleep."],[209,59.01,3.67,"Sleep deprivation among American teenagers is an epidemic."],[209,62.93,4.21,"Only about 1 in 10 gets the 8 to 10 hours of sleep per night"],[209,67.15,3.25,"recommended by sleep scientists and pediatricians."],[209,70.48,4.13,"The major factor preventing teens from getting the sleep they need"],[209,74.61,2.13,"is a matter of public policy."],[209,77.32,4.96,"Across the country, many schools are starting around 7:30 a.m. or earlier,"],[209,82.41,3.67,"despite the fact that major medical organizations recommend"],[209,86.08,4.46,"that middle and high schools start no earlier than 8:30 a.m."],[209,90.92,2.54,"These early start policies have a direct effect"],[209,93.46,3.04,"on how little sleep American teenagers are getting."],[209,96.84,4.0,"They’re also pitting teenagers in a fundamentally unwinnable fight"],[209,100.85,1.67,"against their own bodies."],[209,103.31,1.54,"Around the time of puberty,"],[209,104.85,3.42,"teenagers experience a delay in their biological clock,"],[209,108.56,3.46,"which determines when we feel most awake and when we feel most sleepy."],[209,112.15,3.88,"This is driven in part by a shift in the release of the hormone melatonin."],[209,116.07,4.88,"Teenagers’ bodies wait to start releasing melatonin until around 11 p.m.,"],[209,121.12,4.13,"which is two hours later than what we see in adults or younger children."],[209,125.7,3.79,"This means that waking a teenager up at 6 a.m."],[209,129.5,5.17,"is the biological equivalent of waking an adult up at 4 a.m."],[209,135.09,3.59,"Now, on the unfortunate days when I have to wake up at 4 a.m.,"],[209,138.68,1.17,"I’m a zombie."],[209,139.84,1.33,"Functionally useless."],[209,141.26,1.33,"I can't think straight."],[209,142.64,3.34,"I’m irritable, and I probably shouldn’t be driving a car."],[209,146.47,4.17,"But this is how many American teenagers feel every single school day."],[209,151.02,1.67,"Many of the, shall we say,"],[209,152.81,4.05,"unpleasant characteristics that we chalk up to being a teenager—"],[209,156.86,3.09,"moodiness, irritability, laziness, depression—"],[209,160.16,2.96,"could be a product of chronic sleep deprivation."],[209,163.28,2.71,"For many teens battling chronic sleep loss,"],[209,166.0,5.0,"their go-to strategy to compensate is consuming large quantities of caffeine"],[209,171.0,3.96,"in the form of venti Frappuccinos, or energy drinks and shots."],[209,175.21,6.3,"So essentially, we've got an entire population of tired but wired youth."],[209,182.14,2.29,"Advocates of sleep-friendly start times"],[209,184.43,4.09,"know that adolescence is a period of dramatic brain development,"],[209,188.68,2.17,"particularly in the parts of the brain"],[209,190.85,3.42,"that are responsible for those higher order thinking processes,"],[209,194.27,4.17,"including reasoning, problem solving, and good judgment."],[209,198.53,4.17,"In other words, the very type of brain activity that’s responsible for reining in"],[209,202.7,5.59,"those impulsive and often risky behaviors that are so characteristic of adolescence."],[209,208.62,1.75,"They know that, like the rest of us,"],[209,210.37,2.46,"when teenagers don’t get the sleep they need,"],[209,212.83,2.92,"their brains, their bodies, and behavior suffer."],[209,215.88,1.5,"They can't concentrate."],[209,217.67,1.5,"Their attention plummets,"],[209,219.38,3.79,"and many will even show behavioral signs that mimic ADHD."],[209,223.55,3.84,"But the consequences of teen sleep loss go well beyond the classroom,"],[209,227.64,3.38,"sadly contributing to many of the mental health problems"],[209,231.02,2.46,"that skyrocket during adolescence."],[209,234.15,2.96,"In our work with teens from LA Unified School District,"],[209,237.11,4.21,"we found that teens with sleep problems were 55% more likely"],[209,241.32,2.29,"to have used alcohol in the past month."],[209,243.82,3.54,"In another study with over 30,000 high school students,"],[209,247.49,2.92,"they found that for each hour of lost sleep,"],[209,250.45,3.75,"there was a 38% increase in feeling sad or hopeless,"],[209,254.5,3.67,"and a 58% increase in teen suicide attempts."],[209,258.75,1.5,"And if that's not enough,"],[209,260.38,2.63,"teens who skimp out on sleep are at increased risk"],[209,263.01,3.84,"for a host of physical health problems that plague our country,"],[209,266.85,3.96,"including obesity, heart disease, and diabetes."],[209,271.1,3.92,"Then there’s the risk of putting a sleep-deprived teen behind the wheel."],[209,275.44,4.04,"Studies have shown that getting five hours or less of sleep per night"],[209,279.48,5.34,"is the equivalent of driving with a blood alcohol content above the legal limit."],[209,285.36,3.92,"Researchers in this area have produced tremendous science"],[209,289.29,2.79,"showing the benefits of later start times."],[209,292.12,1.92,"The findings are unequivocal."],[209,294.08,3.21,"Teens from districts with later start times get more sleep."],[209,297.67,2.17,"They're more likely to show up for school."],[209,299.84,3.59,"School absences dropped by 25% in one district,"],[209,303.55,2.08,"and they're less likely to drop out."],[209,305.93,2.54,"Not surprisingly, they do better academically."],[209,308.85,4.8,"Standardized test scores in math and reading go up by 2 to 3 percentage points."],[209,313.89,4.88,"That's as powerful as reducing class sizes by one third fewer students."],[209,319.11,4.38,"Their mental and physical health improves, and their families are happier."],[209,323.57,4.05,"Even their communities are safer because car crash rates go down—"],[209,327.66,2.75,"a 70% reduction in one district."],[209,330.87,3.17,"Now, given these tremendous benefits, you might think,"],[209,334.04,2.0,"well, this is a no brainer, right?"],[209,336.29,3.88,"So why have we as a society failed to heed this call?"],[209,340.88,3.38,"Delaying start times presents many logistical challenges."],[209,344.26,4.84,"Updating bus routes, increased transportation costs, impact on sports."],[209,349.3,2.21,"Care before or after school."],[209,351.85,2.88,"But these are problems we have to work through."],[209,354.98,4.96,"They are not valid excuses for failing to do the right thing for our children,"],[209,360.23,4.67,"which is to start middle and high schools no earlier than 8:30 a.m."],[209,365.15,3.46,"Districts around the country, big and small, who have made this change,"],[209,368.61,3.38,"they’ve found that these fears are often unfounded"],[209,371.99,3.25,"and far outweighed by the tremendous benefits"],[209,375.25,2.5,"for our student health and performance"],[209,377.75,2.21,"and our collective public safety."],[210,0.48,1.68,"Earthquake!"],[210,2.16,2.96,"The word alone can paint intense pictures."],[210,5.12,4.24,"Giant cracks splitting the ground open, swallowing cars and buildings."],[210,9.36,3.92,"The after-effects of tsunamis, landslides, and fires."],[210,13.28,1.12,"Scary stuff."],[210,14.4,3.36,"Some earthquakes are strong enough to be deadly."],[210,17.76,4.8,"But of the roughly one million detectable quakes that shake our planet every year,"],[210,22.56,3.44,"most are too mild to even rattle even a teacup."],[210,26.0,2.56,"So, can we predict the bad ones?"],[210,28.56,2.72,"Hi, I’m Sage, and this is Crash Course Geology."],[210,35.8,3.32,"Let's start with a simple question: what’s an earthquake?"],[210,39.12,5.12,"Basically, it’s any sudden shaking of the ground caused by waves of energy in the Earth."],[210,44.24,5.84,"That energy can come from landslides, churning magma, or even meteorite impacts."],[210,50.08,4.56,"But usually, an earthquake is the fault of… a fault."],[210,54.64,2.56,"No, Dwayne, not like The Fault in Our Stars."],[210,57.2,2.56,"Faults are underground cracks in Earth’s crust,"],[210,59.76,3.44,"where blocks of rock slide and scrape against each other."],[210,63.2,4.16,"They can range from thousands of kilometers long, to smaller than your fingernail."],[210,67.36,2.48,"It’s no coincidence that some of the most active,"],[210,69.84,3.92,"earthquake-prone faults sit at the edges of tectonic plates—"],[210,73.76,4.72,"those massive slabs of crust and bits of the upper mantle that surf atop the lower mantle."],[210,78.48,3.6,"Tectonic plates are moving all the time, very slowly."],[210,82.08,3.44,"This movement puts stress on the rock within Earth’s crust."],[210,85.52,4.8,"As stress builds, it causes strain, or physical changes, to that rock—"],[210,90.32,2.64,"Pushing it, pulling it, or shearing it."],[210,92.96,3.84,"Some movement along faults happens at such a tiny, steady creep"],[210,96.8,2.96,"that there’s not enough energy to generate an earthquake."],[210,99.76,1.36,"But under great stress,"],[210,101.12,5.12,"brittle rocks in the crust can bend only so far before something’s gotta give."],[210,106.24,4.64,"One side of the fault slips against the other, and all that pent-up energy breaks loose."],[210,110.88,3.28,"That energy rolls out in waves that jiggle the ground—"],[210,114.16,2.56,"sometimes drastically changing the surface."],[210,116.72,1.28,"That’s an earthquake."],[210,118.0,2.0,"It’s basically rock stress relief."],[210,120.0,2.72,"It’s good to shake it off sometimes, right Dwayne?"],[210,122.72,3.04,"But if most earthquakes are so mild we can’t even feel them,"],[210,125.76,1.68,"how do we know they’re happening?"],[210,127.44,4.96,"For that, we turn to seismologists, geologists who specialize in studying seismic waves:"],[210,132.4,3.68,"the energy that’s unleashed after a slip and breakage on a fault."],[210,136.08,5.52,"Seismologists use GPS, radar satellites, and super sensitive machines called seismographs"],[210,141.6,3.68,"to detect and analyze these waves as they travel underground."],[210,145.28,2.56,"Global networks of these machines record seismic data,"],[210,147.84,5.2,"which then get shared and studied through a facility called SAGE —no big deal."],[210,153.04,4.08,"Seismologists help us not only better understand how earthquakes happen,"],[210,157.12,3.28,"but also estimate the risks of one happening."],[210,160.4,2.56,"Now, there’s more than one type of seismic wave—"],[210,162.96,2.48,"and some are more dangerous than others."],[210,165.44,2.48,"First, there are body waves."],[210,167.92,3.44,"Body waves begin from an earthquake’s hypocenter or focus:"],[210,171.36,3.6,"the underground point on a fault where the rock cracks."],[210,174.96,4.48,"Then they take a curving path through Earth’s insides before reaching the surface."],[210,179.44,1.76,"There are two kinds of body waves."],[210,181.2,2.08,"A p-wave, or primary wave,"],[210,183.28,2.8,"compresses and squeezes the particles it moves through."],[210,186.08,4.24,"It’s sort of like what happens when you pull a Slinky out on a flat surface from both ends,"],[210,190.32,1.92,"and push on one side."],[210,192.24,4.16,"P-waves are the speediest waves, so they’re the ones that hit first."],[210,196.4,2.56,"They're what causes that initial jolt in an earthquake,"],[210,198.96,1.84,"but they usually aren’t very destructive."],[210,200.8,3.68,"S-waves, on the other hand, are secondary— and often quite damaging."],[210,204.48,4.56,"They move through rock more slowly, and they shear the particles side to side, or up and down,"],[210,209.04,2.64,"sort of like if I jiggle the Slinky like this."],[210,211.68,4.56,"Buildings, especially older ones, struggle to withstand this horizontal waggle,"],[210,216.24,2.16,"sometimes causing them to topple."],[210,218.4,1.28,"So those are the body waves."],[210,219.68,3.76,"But there’s another major type of seismic wave: surface waves,"],[210,223.44,3.04,"which travel along the, well, surface!"],[210,226.48,2.32,"And even though surface waves are the slowest,"],[210,228.8,3.92,"they can be the most destructive because they produce an undulating pattern"],[210,232.72,3.76,"with up-and-down, back-and-forth, and side-to-side motion."],[210,236.48,4.56,"But not all big earthquakes necessarily produce strong shaking at the surface."],[210,241.04,3.12,"Like, seismic waves from an earthquake deep underground"],[210,244.16,2.96,"largely peter out by the time they reach the surface."],[210,247.12,4.24,"Seismic waves are more intense the closer you are to an earthquake's epicenter—"],[210,251.36,3.56,"the point that's directly above where the fault slipped."],[210,254.96,2.32,"The type of ground that seismic waves move through"],[210,257.28,2.32,"can also affect the intensity of an earthquake."],[210,259.6,5.6,"Like, in 2011, an earthquake in Virginia was felt up to 600 miles away from its epicenter."],[210,265.2,5.28,"But an even stronger earthquake in California was felt only about 250 miles away."],[210,270.48,2.32,"The reason? Older rocks!"],[210,272.8,3.68,"Seismic waves on the U.S. East Coast travel farther because the rocks there"],[210,276.48,2.24,"are much older than the rocks on the West Coast."],[210,278.72,3.04,"The faults in these older rocks have had more time to heal,"],[210,281.76,4.72,"which lets the waves cross them more effectively, and be felt much farther away."],[210,286.48,3.68,"Meanwhile, those young whippersnapper rocks out on the west coast have newer faults,"],[210,290.16,2.72,"which means seismic waves can’t spread as effectively."],[210,292.88,1.52,"When it comes to measuring earthquakes,"],[210,294.4,3.2,"scientists use a system called moment magnitude, or mW—"],[210,297.6,2.4,"an update to the more familiar Richter scale."],[210,300.0,4.08,"Moment magnitude describes the absolute energy released during an earthquake."],[210,304.08,2.16,"It’s reached through a combination of seismic data"],[210,306.24,2.72,"and the physical shifts in the land that happen during an earthquake."],[210,308.96,4.16,"Usually, this is referred to as just “magnitude,” or how much energy was released in general."],[210,313.12,3.12,"The bigger the area of the fault that breaks, the bigger the magnitude."],[210,316.24,4.16,"Often, when a big earthquake first makes the news, its magnitude is just a rough estimate."],[210,320.4,2.96,"A truly accurate measurement can take days to calculate."],[210,323.36,1.92,"Even though it might not sound like it at first,"],[210,325.28,4.96,"the difference between just one “order of magnitude” can be striking."],[210,330.24,2.8,"Magnitude is measured on a logarithmic scale,"],[210,333.04,5.2,"so each whole number represents a ten-fold jump in energy."],[210,338.24,1.52,"Let’s put that in spaghetti terms,"],[210,339.76,4.0,"à la my favorite earthquake geologist, Dr. Wendy Bohon."],[210,343.76,1.76,"Just go with me."],[210,345.52,2.32,"Let's say a 4.0-magnitude earthquake is"],[210,347.84,3.6,"equivalent to the energy it takes to snap one noodle."],[210,351.44,1.2,"Don’t get mad at me, Italians."],[210,352.64,2.64,"It’s for science."],[210,355.28,12.24,"If that’s the case, then a 5.0-magnitude event would be like snapping 32 noodles."],[210,367.52,10.96,"And a 6.0-magnitude earthquake would be like snapping roughly a thousand noodles."],[210,379.68,2.72,"Yeah, I don’t think I can do that."],[210,382.4,2.32,"I’m just gonna clean this up."],[210,384.72,3.92,"So the higher up the scale you go, even by just a point or two,"],[210,388.64,2.16,"the more drastic the consequences."],[210,390.8,4.16,"Consider the 2009 earthquake that struck the city of L’Aquila, Italy."],[210,394.96,4.8,"It was eventually recorded as a 6.2 on the moment-magnitude scale."],[210,399.76,4.48,"The quake brought 20,000 buildings crashing to the ground—"],[210,404.24,7.24,"killing over 300 people, injuring 1,500, and leaving 65,000 houseless."],[210,411.52,4.88,"It left a lot of people asking, “Was this damage preventable?”"],[210,416.4,4.48,"For weeks before the quake, the people of L’Aquila felt smaller tremors in the city."],[210,420.88,3.28,"But a government commission tasked with monitoring earthquake risk"],[210,424.16,3.04,"reassured the public that the shaking was “normal,”"],[210,427.2,2.24,"and a bigger event was unlikely."],[210,429.44,3.36,"Sadly, those tremors turned out to be foreshocks—"],[210,432.8,4.8,"smaller earthquakes that sometimes precede a serious event."],[210,437.6,5.84,"In light of the tragedy, that government official and six scientists were accused of giving, quote,"],[210,443.44,5.6,"“inexact, incomplete, and contradictory information” that downplayed the true risks."],[210,449.04,1.44,"Three years after the earthquake,"],[210,450.48,2.8,"an Italian court convicted them of manslaughter"],[210,453.28,2.64,"for not properly warning the public of the danger."],[210,455.92,1.28,"But here’s the thing:"],[210,457.2,7.12,"we can’t know if something’s a foreshock until after the bigger earthquake happens."],[210,464.32,4.4,"It’s common for small earthquakes not to lead up to a worse event."],[210,468.72,4.64,"And it’s possible for a strong earthquake to happen with no foreshocks preceding it."],[210,473.36,3.44,"So can we tell when a tremor is nothing to worry about—"],[210,476.8,2.96,"and when it’s the lead-up to something more dangerous?"],[210,479.76,4.0,"And how do seismologists even estimate earthquake risk?"],[210,483.76,6.8,"Well, it’s nuanced. You see, “prediction” has a very specific meaning in earthquake-speak."],[210,490.56,1.76,"To predict an earthquake means"],[210,492.32,5.84,"you’ve pinned down the exact time, date, location, and severity."],[210,498.16,4.96,"No one, has ever predicted an earthquake."],[210,503.12,0.88,"Not once."],[210,504.0,2.88,"In the words of the U.S. Geological Survey,"],[210,506.88,7.2,"“We do not know how, and we do not expect to know how any time in the foreseeable future.”"],[210,514.08,3.92,"In other words, you can’t just schedule an earthquake reminder on your Google Calendar."],[210,518.0,4.72,"What scientists can do is forecast earthquake risk in a certain area."],[210,522.72,1.52,"It’s a lot like a weather report."],[210,524.24,3.92,"A meteorologist can’t tell you if or when it will rain,"],[210,528.16,4.08,"only that there might be rain somewhere during a certain period."],[210,532.24,2.24,"Forecasts deal in probability."],[210,534.48,2.24,"It’s an estimate of the long-term odds of"],[210,536.72,4.16,"an earthquake happening on a specific fault sometime in the future,"],[210,540.88,4.72,"based on how much time has passed between big earthquakes in a particular spot."],[210,545.6,3.6,"Which is why gathering and analyzing seismic data is so important!"],[210,549.2,2.48,"They’re what forecasts are based on."],[210,551.68,3.6,"They’re also based on literally digging into the"],[210,555.28,2.96,"past for evidence of shaken-up sediment in the rock record,"],[210,558.24,4.0,"which tells us about the cadence of ancient earthquakes."],[210,562.24,2.88,"There’s always some uncertainty in forecasting—"],[210,565.12,2.64,"much like what happened with the L’Aquila earthquake."],[210,567.76,4.16,"Over five thousand members of the scientific community signed an open letter"],[210,571.92,3.6,"to Italy’s president calling it “manifestly unfair”"],[210,575.52,2.0,"to bring criminal charges against scientists"],[210,577.52,2.8,"for not acting on information that, quote,"],[210,580.32,4.88,"“the international scientific community would consider inadequate for [...] issuing a warning.”"],[210,585.2,3.97,"In light of those facts, the charges against the scientists were dropped in 2014."],[210,589.2,3.04,"And though we’ll probably never have an earthquake crystal ball,"],[210,592.24,1.76,"there are ways we can prepare."],[210,594.0,2.4,"Places like Japan, Mexico, and California"],[210,596.4,5.28,"have developed early warning systems that notify people at the first sign of seismic waves,"],[210,601.68,5.68,"giving even a few precious seconds to get to a safer spot before the big shock hits."],[210,607.36,1.92,"Many areas require new buildings to be"],[210,609.28,3.2,"constructed with earthquake-resistant techniques and materials,"],[210,612.48,1.68,"so they’re less likely to collapse."],[210,614.16,2.32,"And because earthquakes can happen anywhere,"],[210,616.48,4.72,"we can all personally prepare by getting familiar with the seismic risk where we live"],[210,621.2,4.0,"and learning what to do if an earthquake ever affects our area."],[210,625.2,3.68,"Earthquakes can happen anywhere on Earth, at any time—"],[210,628.88,3.76,"and unfortunately, we have no way of predicting exactly when or where."],[210,632.64,4.56,"But by studying and tracking seismic data, scientists continue to refine forecasts,"],[210,637.2,1.28,"better assess the risks,"],[210,638.48,4.72,"and even research how to prevent and protect us from the most damaging shocks."],[210,643.2,3.2,"Next time, we’ll learn more about earthquake preparedness."],[210,646.4,0.8,"See you then."],[210,647.2,2.48,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Geology,"],[210,649.68,2.52,"which was filmed at our studio in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[210,652.2,2.92,"and was made with the help of all these nice people."],[210,655.12,3.84,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[210,658.96,4.72,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[211,6.79,5.8,"In 2023, a US grocer recalled over 10,000 cases of broccoli cheddar soup"],[211,12.59,3.71,"over concerns they contained too much of an unintended ingredient."],[211,16.39,2.34,"That ingredient? Bugs."],[211,19.31,3.04,"We know insects regularly come into contact with our food—"],[211,22.35,4.96,"but how many are you actually eating? And is it okay?"],[211,27.98,2.29,"Let’s start with an extreme example:"],[211,30.48,4.67,"figs and their conspicuously close bond with certain bugs."],[211,35.32,3.79,"Around 80 million years ago, wasps started pollinating figs."],[211,39.24,4.42,"And today, each of the approximately 750 fig tree species"],[211,43.66,3.59,"depends on at least one unique species of tiny wasps."],[211,47.54,3.46,"Pollinator-plant relationships can get hyper-specific."],[211,51.21,3.96,"And figs guard their flowers especially tight for fig wasps."],[211,55.51,2.0,"Technically, a fig isn't a fruit,"],[211,57.51,2.75,"but a fleshy bundle derived from stem tissues"],[211,60.26,4.38,"that holds hundreds of internal flowers— like a hidden garden."],[211,65.19,3.75,"Humans typically harvest one species: the common fig."],[211,69.19,2.38,"Its breeding system, called gynodioecy,"],[211,71.61,3.29,"is seen in less than 1% of flowering plants."],[211,75.11,4.59,"It works with some common fig trees having seed-producing female parts,"],[211,79.7,2.0,"while others, called caprifigs,"],[211,81.7,4.92,"have both female seed-producing and male pollen-producing parts."],[211,86.92,3.29,"Wasps get involved when a female fig wasp full of eggs"],[211,90.21,2.54,"follows odor cues to a common fig tree"],[211,92.75,4.75,"and thrusts herself into the minuscule hole at a developing fig’s base."],[211,97.64,3.59,"From there, depending on whether it's a caprifig or a female fig,"],[211,101.31,2.04,"things go one of two ways,"],[211,103.35,4.34,"the outcome being either more wasps or more figs."],[211,108.19,4.3,"If it’s a caprifig, the wasp deposits her eggs into the flowers’ ovaries,"],[211,112.57,1.13,"then dies."],[211,113.9,4.42,"Instead of developing seeds, those flower ovaries turn into galls"],[211,118.32,3.0,"that nurture the wasp’s developing offspring."],[211,121.62,4.09,"Wingless and blind, the males hatch first, open the remaining galls,"],[211,125.7,2.42,"and fertilize the developing females—"],[211,128.29,5.42,"yes, oftentimes their sisters, unless another wasp laid eggs here."],[211,134.38,4.21,"Next, the males dig exit pathways they never use themselves"],[211,138.63,2.5,"because they die before leaving the fig."],[211,141.47,2.96,"Finally, the already-fertilized females hatch,"],[211,144.6,3.84,"exit through the male-made holes, getting coated with pollen on the way,"],[211,148.48,2.17,"and fly off to other figs."],[211,151.1,2.88,"If a wasp winds up in a female fig, however,"],[211,153.98,3.75,"she can’t lay her eggs because the flowers are structured differently."],[211,157.9,2.08,"So, she dies without offspring—"],[211,160.11,4.67,"but she did pollinate the fig’s flowers, so the tree can reproduce."],[211,164.99,3.21,"Female wasps don’t know which kind of fig they’re entering—"],[211,168.21,4.5,"and whether it’ll give her offspring or use her to make its own—"],[211,172.84,3.54,"because fig trees smell the same, regardless of sex."],[211,176.67,4.13,"This ensures that a good portion of common figs can also reproduce"],[211,180.8,2.25,"and not just further wasp-kind."],[211,183.6,4.05,"That was how things went— until humans intervened."],[211,187.85,3.25,"Archaeological records suggest that people in the Jordan Valley"],[211,191.1,3.55,"grew figs some 11,400 years ago,"],[211,194.65,3.17,"possibly making them the first domesticated crop."],[211,198.07,3.42,"When a genetic mutation emerged that allowed the tree’s fruit to ripen"],[211,201.49,4.3,"without being pollinated, people began propagating it with cuttings."],[211,205.91,3.59,"And suddenly the common fig wasn’t beholden to wasps;"],[211,209.66,2.29,"it had a new partner to multiply with."],[211,212.42,1.96,"The crop spread far and wide,"],[211,214.38,4.88,"and today we harvest more than 1.3 million tons of figs annually."],[211,219.97,1.96,"So how many wasps are we eating?"],[211,222.13,1.75,"Well, store-bought fresh figs"],[211,223.89,4.59,"are typically of the common fig varieties that ripen without pollinators,"],[211,228.56,1.38,"so they’re wasp-free."],[211,230.27,4.13,"Many that are sold dried, however, still require pollination."],[211,234.6,4.05,"But, of these, we usually don’t eat caprifig fruits,"],[211,238.65,2.67,"where the mother wasp and her male offspring die."],[211,241.44,3.09,"Instead, we eat dried figs from female trees,"],[211,244.53,3.84,"which may contain a female wasp that attempted—and failed—"],[211,248.37,1.29,"to lay her eggs in it."],[211,250.2,3.0,"However, it’s also possible that the moisture and enzymes"],[211,253.21,2.96,"figs naturally release break her body down."],[211,256.46,3.59,"Big picture, though, bugs are often harvested with our produce"],[211,260.05,2.75,"or attracted to food processing facilities."],[211,262.84,2.33,"Eating them is kind of inevitable."],[211,265.34,3.5,"The US Food and Drug Administration actually permits certain amounts"],[211,268.85,2.63,"of bug bits in different food products."],[211,271.48,5.05,"For example, no more than 30 insect fragments per 100 grams of peanut butter,"],[211,276.77,4.17,"or over 2,500 aphids in 10 grams of hops."],[211,281.15,4.75,"Some estimates hold that Americans eat around a kilogram of insects annually—"],[211,285.95,3.88,"without incident, and maybe even a little added nutrition."],[211,290.08,4.42,"After all, insects feature in over 2 billion people's traditional diets"],[211,294.5,1.71,"and are relatively sustainable."],[211,296.29,2.13,"So, maybe chew on that."],[212,18.75,2.54,"This is a glimpse into one of my artworks,"],[212,21.32,1.37,"\"Vanishing.\""],[212,22.69,1.17,"What you see,"],[212,23.89,4.77,"this is a tribute to the species extinguished by us, humanity."],[212,29.19,4.3,"I dedicate my career to translating the invisible crisis of our time"],[212,33.53,3.04,"as vividly as the images before you."],[212,37.17,3.77,"That includes climate change,"],[212,40.94,2.04,"artificial intelligence,"],[212,43.01,3.2,"and even the crisis of our own humanity."],[212,46.94,3.04,"We're living in an era of wicked problems."],[212,50.01,3.54,"The crises are so massive that we cannot grasp them."],[212,54.19,1.7,"We can feel a paper cut,"],[212,55.92,3.1,"but we cannot feel a collapsing planet."],[212,59.62,2.2,"In an endless chase of progress,"],[212,61.86,4.8,"we have turned our nature and technology into monumental threats."],[212,67.1,5.97,"Paradoxically, we have devastated the very habitat we live in"],[212,73.07,3.14,"and jeopardized the very intelligence that makes us human."],[212,77.51,2.57,"The result is great anxiety."],[212,80.64,4.41,"But we must ask ourselves, was this collapse truly unseen?"],[212,85.55,3.07,"We have more data than any generation in history,"],[212,88.62,2.44,"yet reality feels further away."],[212,91.09,3.47,"We're drowning in information but starving for meaning."],[212,94.92,2.4,"The chain of understanding is broken,"],[212,97.36,3.34,"leaving our crisis so cold and abstract."],[212,101.2,3.9,"We have lost the connection and the ability to care."],[212,105.64,3.04,"Living at the intersection of art and technology,"],[212,108.71,3.8,"I felt an irresistible urge to rebuild that bridge."],[212,112.94,2.27,"My mission became clear:"],[212,115.25,2.77,"to render the invisible visible."],[212,118.55,4.71,"I want to translate these wicked problems into physical experiences"],[212,123.29,1.77,"so that we can truly care."],[212,125.96,3.24,"But how do we translate the intangibles?"],[212,129.19,1.77,"Let me take you on my journey."],[212,134.37,2.97,"I began with the most vital data set:"],[212,137.37,1.57,"the Earth,"],[212,138.97,2.34,"specifically, our water."],[212,142.21,1.77,"Look at this single tiny drop."],[212,144.01,2.4,"It holds all the Earth's fresh water."],[212,146.88,3.64,"Half of humanity will lack clean water by 2050."],[212,150.55,2.1,"And many already do."],[212,152.65,2.9,"To expose the crisis of this vital resource,"],[212,155.59,2.27,"I spent an intense year with Google."],[212,158.32,3.94,"They were searching for an artist to bridge the gap between complex data"],[212,162.29,1.64,"and creative storytelling."],[212,164.29,1.97,"And an amazing door opened."],[212,166.3,5.17,"NASA joined the team to offer me an exclusive access to the satellite data."],[212,172.27,3.54,"This rare gift became the soul of my artwork"],[212,175.84,1.64,"\"Passage of Water.\""],[212,177.84,2.74,"My goal is to turn these NASA numbers"],[212,180.61,2.07,"into visceral experiences,"],[212,182.71,2.57,"dramatize them so that we can truly see"],[212,185.32,3.44,"and feel what is happening to our fresh water."],[212,189.22,1.17,"Look at this."],[212,190.39,4.04,"This is the raw satellite data that captures the water situation."],[212,194.82,3.24,"We have the most sophisticated technology of our time."],[212,198.63,3.54,"But who can actually understand what is happening here?"],[212,202.2,2.74,"What happens if we change the interface?"],[212,205.3,4.34,"I spent months obsessing over how to turn these number’s grave,"],[212,209.67,3.0,"and now I can present the outcome to you."],[212,213.31,3.17,"What you see is 20 years of water patterns"],[212,216.51,2.27,"turned into a breathing landscape."],[212,219.25,1.77,"This is “Passage of Water.”"],[212,221.05,5.37,"It lives on Google's platform, designed for you to fully interact with."],[212,227.22,4.74,"Let's see the dramatic change between 2002"],[212,232.0,1.8,"and 2023."],[212,234.8,3.27,"We are losing our fresh water fast,"],[212,238.1,2.2,"and we are the reason."],[212,240.74,2.2,"Here, the numbers become instinct."],[212,242.97,2.5,"You don't just see the data, you feel it."],[212,246.44,5.04,"And this is where artistic creativity becomes an essential, not a luxury."],[212,251.51,4.54,"It is a language that allows me to tackle those wicked problems"],[212,256.05,2.34,"and turn them into something we can grasp."],[212,259.12,2.67,"We brought this project to COP28."],[212,261.82,3.04,"There we spoke with climate scientists,"],[212,264.89,1.27,"policymakers,"],[212,266.16,4.14,"Indigenous people who face water scarcity every day."],[212,271.1,2.8,"Seeing their reactions in front of my artwork"],[212,273.9,1.8,"gave me absolute certainty."],[212,276.71,2.07,"Art is the vital bridge,"],[212,278.81,3.67,"a missing link that has undeniable power"],[212,282.51,3.2,"to restore our lost connection."],[212,285.75,3.94,"But now we face [an] even deeper crisis,"],[212,289.72,4.2,"one that challenges the very definitions of human intelligence."],[212,294.39,1.8,"The unfathomable AI."],[212,296.86,3.74,"Here you are, 2026, and AI is everywhere."],[212,301.0,3.2,"The technology is advancing at a staggering pace,"],[212,304.23,3.17,"and we have to accept an uncomfortable truth."],[212,307.44,2.9,"The machines are replacing parts of us."],[212,311.14,4.14,"But the real danger is hidden behind the slick interfaces."],[212,315.31,5.04,"AI gives us brilliant results, but the logic, the process --"],[212,320.38,2.04,"that remains a total mystery."],[212,322.95,3.94,"And that is exactly why we call it a black box."],[212,327.56,2.6,"If these algorithms are deciding our future,"],[212,330.19,2.57,"shouldn't we talk about the invisibles,"],[212,332.76,2.57,"the hidden logic governing our lives?"],[212,335.9,4.04,"That is why I created this project with my students in my research lab."],[212,340.27,4.41,"We wanted to open the black box to illuminate what's inside."],[212,345.34,2.27,"The title of this project is \"SoTA,\""],[212,347.64,2.07,"short for state of the art."],[212,349.75,1.77,"In the world of engineering,"],[212,351.55,4.27,"it means to describe the gold standard of machine capability."],[212,356.35,3.87,"AI that generates our images or the text isn’t just magic,"],[212,360.26,2.44,"it's a complex mathematical structure."],[212,363.09,2.17,"Digital neurons cluster into a network,"],[212,365.3,3.14,"processing data through multiple hidden layers."],[212,368.77,4.34,"But aren't you curious if these architectures are all the same?"],[212,373.77,3.5,"You might assume that they are all identical grids,"],[212,377.31,1.67,"but as we looked deeper,"],[212,379.01,2.17,"we found [them] far more diverse."],[212,381.55,5.77,"What you see is living architecture of 118 AI models visualized."],[212,387.79,5.04,"See how digital neurons connect and fire with [their] own unique geometry."],[212,393.26,4.6,"The shape decides how to generate images, text, or sounds."],[212,397.89,1.8,"To understand their intelligence,"],[212,399.7,2.04,"we need to see the shape first."],[212,402.77,1.94,"Let's take a closer look at \"SoTA.\""],[212,405.0,5.61,"I will show you how LLM calculates and completes a single sentence."],[212,411.34,1.84,"This is how a machine works."],[212,413.21,4.27,"A complex mathematical prediction governed by its architecture."],[212,418.08,2.87,"This is the invisible logic of our time."],[212,421.38,3.57,"When we bring the hidden machinery into the physical world,"],[212,424.95,2.97,"the artificial finally becomes tangible."],[212,429.06,4.1,"To experience the shape of AI is to demystify it."],[212,433.8,3.57,"As you can see here, you can access this artwork on your mobile."],[212,438.04,2.7,"Don’t just watch them -- play with the models."],[212,441.17,2.7,"This isn't just about overcoming anxiety."],[212,444.31,5.27,"It is the very first step to reclaiming our human agency."],[212,450.11,3.7,"But we don't want to stop at mere understanding."],[212,453.85,2.1,"Clarity is just the foundation."],[212,456.75,4.97,"Our true task is not to see the presence, but to shape the future."],[212,462.69,5.44,"While my previous works primarily existed within digital projections and displays,"],[212,468.17,3.54,"I felt a strong yearning to pull this intelligence"],[212,471.74,1.74,"into tangible forms."],[212,473.97,2.67,"I wanted to make an overwhelming experience,"],[212,476.67,2.44,"something that you don't just look at,"],[212,479.14,3.1,"but something you inhabit with your body."],[212,483.31,4.77,"We are at a threshold to co-design our future with AI."],[212,488.38,1.94,"We must ask,"],[212,490.32,3.04,"\"What is the shape of future AI?\""],[212,493.96,3.17,"I built a physical answer to that question,"],[212,497.16,2.4,"and this is \"Light Architecture.\""],[212,519.95,3.5,"In \"Light Architecture,\" AI takes a physical breath."],[212,524.22,2.47,"These modules are no longer flat pixels."],[212,526.72,3.5,"They are living neurons, and I call them neural parts."],[212,530.83,2.5,"They pulse with light, move through space,"],[212,533.33,2.13,"running on the exact logic of AI."],[212,536.07,1.8,"Here you don't just watch a model."],[212,537.9,2.64,"You stand inside the mind of a machine."],[212,542.31,2.27,"Together they form a circular network,"],[212,544.61,3.77,"feeding data into a central projection to manifest the final outputs."],[212,548.91,3.97,"See the light spills outward, filling the voids."],[212,553.22,6.14,"It transforms the entire room into one giant living black box."],[212,560.19,1.87,"Why a circular formation?"],[212,562.76,3.1,"Because I found my inspiration in [the] human brain."],[212,566.26,4.44,"Our brains have an incredible mechanism moving data from [the] cortex"],[212,570.73,4.97,"to deeper layers with the flexibility that AI cannot yet replicate."],[212,576.31,1.8,"But it makes me wonder,"],[212,578.11,2.97,"with this incredible power in our heads,"],[212,581.08,2.0,"are we genuinely training it?"],[212,583.55,2.8,"Or [are] we simply leaning on AI,"],[212,586.35,4.3,"paralyzed by the fear of being replaced by a machine,"],[212,590.65,4.17,"only to let our creativity and curiosity wither?"],[212,595.36,2.87,"I designed a circular formation to raise a question."],[212,599.0,3.97,"What if AI learns this neuromorphic fluidity?"],[212,603.63,2.04,"What would that future look like?"],[212,606.2,4.1,"And more importantly, is that the future we want?"],[212,615.78,2.9,"As you walk through the blinding lights"],[212,618.72,1.94,"in absolute darkness,"],[212,620.68,2.2,"you feel the vastness of AI."],[212,624.05,4.04,"Is our fate to become just data providers?"],[212,628.83,3.24,"Is this intelligence lighting our way"],[212,632.1,2.9,"or pulling us deeper into the dark?"],[212,643.44,3.7,"I shared how I transformed these wicked problems"],[212,647.18,2.37,"into immersive experiences."],[212,649.55,1.44,"This is who I am."],[212,652.08,3.07,"I'm an artist who builds bridges."],[212,656.45,1.9,"I collaborate with scientists."],[212,659.29,1.94,"I build systems,"],[212,661.22,3.24,"and I connect the physical and the virtual."],[212,665.93,5.67,"My art is about building spaces where you can step inside the unseen."],[212,673.14,4.0,"I do this because I believe one simple truth:"],[212,678.14,3.4,"that we are all deeply entangled."],[212,690.59,3.5,"Nature, humanity, technology."],[212,694.42,2.2,"They are not separate entities."],[212,696.66,4.44,"It is not “us” versus “them” or human versus machine."],[212,701.5,3.0,"It is one living network."],[212,705.1,2.6,"And embracing this connection is the only way"],[212,707.74,2.6,"to solve our wicked problems."],[212,711.48,2.84,"Let this light be my final message."],[212,714.81,4.87,"I hope it shines not just on our data but on a shared vision."],[212,720.75,1.57,"By standing together,"],[212,722.35,2.07,"we can face the unknown"],[212,724.45,1.74,"and create a world"],[212,726.22,2.2,"where we can finally belong."],[212,731.09,1.17,"Thank you."],[213,8.63,1.63,"In the 1940s,"],[213,10.26,3.17,"the radio was a transformative technology."],[213,14.01,1.96,"But it wasn't an immediate hit."],[213,16.76,4.71,"That's because the radios of the early 1900s looked like this:"],[213,21.52,4.5,"unwieldy contraptions of exposed wires and glowing vacuum tubes,"],[213,26.02,2.0,"like a science-fair project gone wrong."],[213,28.02,4.71,"It wasn't until 1929 when radio started to look like this,"],[213,32.74,1.92,"with its sleek Bakelite --"],[213,34.66,2.13,"that’s a kind of plastic shell --"],[213,36.78,2.54,"that they became insanely popular --"],[213,39.33,2.13,"but at a critical cost."],[213,42.33,1.38,"Transparency."],[213,44.12,4.0,"What happens when technology becomes a black box"],[213,48.13,3.42,"and we stop being able to see what's going on inside?"],[213,52.05,1.71,"Fast forward 100 years."],[213,54.26,4.46,"Today, the sci-fi artificial intelligence devices of our dreams"],[213,58.72,3.25,"have been woven into the very fabric of modern life."],[213,62.02,3.67,"But like the radio, these systems are “bakelited.”"],[213,66.35,2.96,"We've hidden the machine behind frictionless interfaces,"],[213,69.36,1.96,"beneath friendly voices"],[213,71.36,1.79,"and inside children's toys."],[213,73.49,1.25,"Over the past decade,"],[213,74.78,3.29,"I've had a lot of conversations with kids about AI"],[213,78.12,3.67,"as an MIT researcher and founding member of Day of AI,"],[213,81.83,4.17,"a nonprofit creating opportunities for millions of children around the world"],[213,86.0,2.63,"to learn about how AI works."],[213,89.17,1.67,"And here's what I've been seeing."],[213,91.38,4.38,"These devices mimic intelligence and friendliness so well"],[213,95.8,2.21,"that children learn to trust them --"],[213,98.01,2.25,"sometimes more than they trust themselves."],[213,100.85,3.38,"And there is real danger of children becoming overly reliant on"],[213,104.27,3.21,"or inappropriately attached to their smart toys."],[213,108.15,3.71,"That said, with some intentional choices on the part of adults,"],[213,111.9,3.29,"we can help children see the technology for what it really is,"],[213,115.19,3.21,"objects that they can play with, or even program,"],[213,118.45,3.46,"rather than entities that they should look to for all the answers."],[213,122.37,1.67,"My journey exploring kids"],[213,124.08,1.58,"and their relationships with AI"],[213,125.7,2.0,"began early in my days at MIT,"],[213,127.71,4.21,"working on this fluffy, adorable social robot, Tega."],[213,133.67,1.92,"So Tega being a social robot means"],[213,135.63,3.46,"that its goal is to help people with their goals."],[213,139.09,2.13,"Social robots are used throughout healthcare,"],[213,141.26,2.25,"customer service and education."],[213,143.51,1.17,"In Tega's case,"],[213,144.72,3.29,"we were building it to see if a robot could help young children"],[213,148.02,1.88,"as they were learning how to read."],[213,149.94,3.04,"Let me show you a quick clip of Tega in action."],[213,153.4,2.25,"Student: Hm, I'm lavender."],[213,157.32,2.42,"Tega: What are we trying to find?"],[213,159.74,3.8,"Student: We are trying to find lavender-color stuff."],[213,164.83,1.38,"Tega: OK."],[213,167.33,2.67,"The word was lavender?"],[213,170.04,1.13,"Student: Yes."],[213,172.5,2.5,"And what is lavender, though?"],[213,177.76,2.54,"Tega: Lavender is purple."],[213,180.34,1.13,"Student: Yes."],[213,182.34,2.96,"Just like my friend loves lavender."],[213,186.02,1.63,"Tega: Hmm."],[213,190.31,1.84,"Randi Williams: So as you saw,"],[213,192.15,3.96,"it's Tega's job to keep the child engaged in the story."],[213,196.11,3.09,"Tega will ask questions, add funny commentary,"],[213,199.24,3.54,"and of course, weave in that target vocabulary for the week."],[213,202.78,2.09,"And it was my job to introduce Tega"],[213,204.91,2.17,"and get children oriented to how to use it."],[213,207.62,3.0,"So one day I come into the study room and I say, \"Hi,"],[213,210.66,2.0,"my name is Randi, this is Tega."],[213,213.0,2.38,"You're going to read some stories with Tega today."],[213,215.42,1.5,"Let me show you how it works.\""],[213,216.96,3.17,"And the girl I was working with, a kindergartener, stops me."],[213,220.13,1.46,"\"No thanks,\" she says."],[213,222.63,1.58,"\"I know all about robots."],[213,224.26,2.67,"I have an Alexa at home, and she's my best friend."],[213,226.97,1.33,"Watch this.\""],[213,228.35,2.88,"And then she proceeded to show me how my robot worked."],[213,232.69,1.92,"What do we make of a response like that?"],[213,234.65,3.29,"Well first, I thought it was kind of impressive"],[213,237.98,3.42,"that this girl who, again, was learning how to read,"],[213,241.4,1.92,"could effortlessly navigate the internet"],[213,243.36,1.88,"thanks to voice technology."],[213,245.24,4.67,"And for context, I had to know how to read, write and type"],[213,249.95,2.25,"before I could ask Jeeves a question."],[213,252.25,1.38,"But for her, not so much."],[213,254.08,2.33,"But secondly, I was a little bit disappointed"],[213,256.46,3.34,"that this girl didn't think that my super cool robot was so super cool."],[213,260.42,4.3,"And when I took a step back, I realized it wasn't just her."],[213,265.22,3.67,"We are raising a generation of children who are growing up with AI"],[213,268.93,1.42,"and smart toys,"],[213,270.39,4.5,"and they tend to see these devices not as virtual assistants or technology,"],[213,274.94,1.71,"but as friends,"],[213,276.69,3.34,"distinct beings with a mind, a will and a soul."],[213,280.65,1.5,"And that made me really curious"],[213,282.19,3.25,"about what children make of this technology."],[213,285.49,3.21,"How much do they understand about what's going on?"],[213,288.7,1.54,"So to investigate that question,"],[213,290.29,1.13,"I ran a study."],[213,291.45,3.96,"I had 30 children between the ages of three and 11"],[213,295.42,1.25,"come together to interact"],[213,296.71,2.75,"with a whole bunch of different smart toys all at once."],[213,299.46,4.3,"We had toy robots, talking dolls, text-based chatbots,"],[213,303.8,2.04,"and of course, plenty of virtual assistants"],[213,305.84,1.83,"like Alexa and Google Home."],[213,308.26,3.04,"One of the girls in the study, her favorite animal"],[213,311.35,1.29,"was sloths."],[213,312.68,1.29,"She loves sloths."],[213,314.02,3.38,"She'd been telling us all day how much she loves sloths."],[213,317.4,3.09,"And so naturally, when she got hold of an Alexa,"],[213,320.52,2.67,"she wanted to learn more about sloths from Alexa."],[213,323.24,1.96,"So she picks up Alexa and says,"],[213,325.2,2.42,"\"Alexa, what do sloths eat?\""],[213,328.16,2.38,"And then Alexa, very helpfully, replied,"],[213,330.54,2.54,"\"I'm sorry, I don't know how to help you with that.\""],[213,334.2,1.96,"The girl looked disappointed for a second"],[213,336.21,2.34,"and then all of a sudden perked right back up."],[213,338.58,3.04,"\"Oh, I know,\" she says, and she picked up a second Alexa."],[213,342.0,1.63,"\"I'll see if this one knows.\""],[213,345.43,4.96,"The limitations of AI are not only frustrating and confusing for children,"],[213,350.39,3.71,"but research in child-robot interaction shows that it can make them vulnerable"],[213,354.1,2.38,"to even more serious risks,"],[213,356.52,4.42,"like manipulation or accidental exposure to inappropriate content"],[213,360.94,1.54,"or security breaches."],[213,363.15,1.83,"And for me personally,"],[213,365.03,3.0,"even the devices that are marketed as educational"],[213,368.07,5.42,"or screen-free are still corrupt because they're consumer products."],[213,373.49,3.04,"And they seem to be designed as something"],[213,376.54,3.0,"that can be endlessly engaging or addictive."],[213,379.54,3.46,"They want to become your child's best friend and gain their trust"],[213,383.05,1.96,"so that they can advertise to them."],[213,385.42,1.38,"Or better yet,"],[213,386.84,3.42,"sell that trust back to you for $20 a month."],[213,390.3,4.0,"The bottom line is that smart toys open a digital door"],[213,394.35,2.96,"into children's playtime, bedroom and hearts."],[213,397.98,2.5,"And if you're thinking about opening that door,"],[213,400.52,3.29,"my advice to you would be to take a very careful look"],[213,403.82,1.88,"at what's on the other side."],[213,406.49,5.17,"The good news is that we can mitigate the risks of AI to children"],[213,411.7,2.13,"by leaning into something they already do."],[213,414.54,2.96,"Kids are natural reverse engineers."],[213,417.5,1.17,"Over and over again,"],[213,418.71,3.17,"I've watched children playing with AI devices"],[213,421.88,1.21,"trying to break it,"],[213,423.13,3.55,"just asking seemingly absurd questions like,"],[213,426.67,1.96,"\"Alexa, how old are you?\""],[213,429.43,2.71,"Or “Cosmo” -- which is a toy robot car --"],[213,432.14,1.38,"\"Can you jump?\""],[213,433.85,3.46,"Or \"Hey Google, is it OK if I eat you?\""],[213,438.77,2.38,"And these questions, while silly,"],[213,441.14,2.75,"also are an example of children probing the machine"],[213,443.9,2.0,"about its very nature."],[213,445.9,3.25,"Play is inherent to how children understand the world,"],[213,449.15,4.17,"which means that maybe we can use it to help them understand what's happening"],[213,453.37,2.75,"with the technologies they're interacting with."],[213,456.62,2.88,"What if children could take apart"],[213,459.54,1.83,"and reconstruct their smart toys"],[213,461.42,3.92,"the same way that they take apart and reconstruct physical blocks?"],[213,465.38,4.46,"That was the idea behind another social robot that I worked on, “PopBot.”"],[213,470.47,4.5,"As you can see, PopBot’s body is made completely out of LEGO bricks,"],[213,475.01,2.63,"and it's kind of like a typical smart toy,"],[213,477.68,4.09,"except I built it to make the most complex ideas of modern AI --"],[213,481.81,3.5,"logical reasoning, machine learning, generative AI --"],[213,485.31,4.17,"easy to understand through hands-on, child-driven play."],[213,490.32,3.46,"The idea was, if a four-year-old could understand AI,"],[213,493.82,1.79,"then anyone can."],[213,496.45,2.46,"So for example, to learn about machine learning,"],[213,498.95,4.09,"what children would do is they would play Rock, Paper, Scissors against the robot."],[213,503.04,3.25,"Let me show you a quick clip of what that looks like with PopBots."],[213,506.63,1.29,"Person: OK, go ahead."],[213,507.92,1.54,"Student: Scissors... shoot."],[213,509.5,1.79,"I choose rock."],[213,511.84,1.17,"PopBot: Rock."],[213,513.01,2.75,"You chose rock, and I choose rock."],[213,516.85,1.38,"PopBot: It's a tie."],[213,518.26,1.13,"Student: Rock!"],[213,519.89,1.42,"Student 2: It's a tie!"],[213,522.52,2.34,"RW: The beauties of a real-life classroom study."],[213,525.86,4.21,"So before children could start playing against their PopBot,"],[213,530.07,3.5,"first they had to teach their PopBot the rules of Rock, Paper, Scissors."],[213,533.57,1.71,"And then, as they played the game,"],[213,535.32,2.17,"rounds like that, over and over again,"],[213,537.53,3.46,"what PopBot would try to do is pick up on their pattern"],[213,541.04,3.0,"so that it could predict what move it could play to beat them."],[213,544.54,2.21,"And if you're between the ages of four and six,"],[213,546.79,3.59,"pretty soon it gets hard, if not impossible, to beat your PopBot."],[213,550.92,3.29,"There was one boy who was very frustrated by this,"],[213,554.26,2.88,"and so I planted a mischievous idea in his head."],[213,557.72,2.88,"I said, \"Well, what if you taught your PopBot"],[213,560.6,2.21,"the wrong rules of Rock, Paper, Scissors?"],[213,562.81,3.21,"That way as you played it, you would win every single time.\""],[213,567.23,1.46,"And he really liked that idea."],[213,570.19,3.67,"Our little act of AI sabotage created a unique opportunity"],[213,573.86,3.04,"to talk about who teaches AI the rules."],[213,576.91,1.42,"Well, people do."],[213,578.32,1.29,"And so I asked him, \"Well,"],[213,579.66,4.0,"if you can teach your PopBot the wrong rules of Rock, Paper, Scissors,"],[213,583.66,3.29,"how do you know Alexa was taught the right ones?\""],[213,588.13,3.59,"Which led to a very insightful conversation where he discussed"],[213,591.71,1.79,"how much we can trust AI"],[213,593.55,3.38,"and the importance of verifying what it says, with an adult’s help,"],[213,596.93,3.38,"just to make sure that Alexa has been taught all the right rules."],[213,601.68,6.34,"The big idea that I want children to understand is that AI is not magic."],[213,608.02,2.34,"It's a set of rules written by people."],[213,611.02,1.29,"And it's up to us,"],[213,612.32,2.67,"those of us who have been around for a bit longer,"],[213,615.03,3.25,"to make sure that children aren't being programmed by their toys,"],[213,618.32,2.79,"but rather things work the other way around."],[213,621.16,4.17,"We need to raise a generation of children who know that they are the ones"],[213,625.33,3.67,"who get to write, and even rewrite, the rules of AI."],[213,629.92,2.88,"And this work starts at the dinner table."],[213,633.17,4.55,"So you're all sitting down and a question gets asked to the nearest AI chatbot"],[213,637.76,1.58,"or smart toy."],[213,639.68,3.84,"When it responds, rather than just accepting that answer,"],[213,643.51,4.42,"wonder, \"I wonder why it answered the way it did.\""],[213,648.35,1.17,"Or ask aloud,"],[213,649.56,2.75,"\"Do we agree or disagree with that answer?\""],[213,653.15,4.3,"\"Who do we think wrote the rules to make it work the way that it does?\""],[213,658.36,1.17,"And the thing is,"],[213,659.53,2.71,"you don't need to know the answers to all these questions"],[213,662.24,1.71,"or become some sort of AI expert --"],[213,663.95,1.25,"that’s not the point."],[213,665.91,1.5,"What children need is a model"],[213,667.46,2.88,"of what it means to be a curious user of AI."],[213,670.88,2.17,"They need someone who will sit down with them,"],[213,673.09,1.13,"explore the machine,"],[213,674.25,1.25,"poke at its limits,"],[213,675.5,1.83,"challenge its responses,"],[213,677.38,1.33,"and most importantly,"],[213,678.72,2.38,"dare to rewrite its rules."],[213,681.89,3.5,"And I've been thinking about this a lot in the context of what's happening"],[213,685.39,1.21,"in children's toy boxes,"],[213,686.6,1.21,"but the bigger picture is"],[213,687.85,2.54,"that we should all be treating the everyday AI systems"],[213,690.39,2.21,"that we interact with the same way."],[213,693.48,4.5,"I 100 percent believe that we can help children build safer relationships"],[213,698.03,1.46,"with their smart toys,"],[213,699.53,4.04,"and we all need to build better relationships between ourselves"],[213,703.62,3.04,"and the technologies we interact with every day."],[213,706.7,4.09,"I believe in a world where no one is left in the dark by technology,"],[213,710.83,4.5,"and where everyone has the inherent right to play a part"],[213,715.34,2.71,"in shaping the systems that shape our lives."],[213,718.63,1.29,"Thank you."],[213,726.35,2.59,"Chris Duffy: Randi, fantastic talk."],[213,728.98,3.59,"I'd love to ask you just one question here, which is,"],[213,732.56,1.21,"before I was a parent,"],[213,733.81,3.75,"I had some very black-and-white thinking around screens."],[213,737.61,3.71,"I had this pristine vision of, I would never use screens with my child at all."],[213,741.36,2.34,"And then as I think many parents have experienced,"],[213,743.7,3.0,"that crashed into the reality of needing to put sunscreen on him"],[213,746.74,2.13,"or to clip his nails."],[213,748.91,2.84,"I'm wondering, AI, as a new technology"],[213,751.75,1.54,"and these smart toys,"],[213,753.29,2.71,"how do you see parents balancing"],[213,756.04,3.25,"the ideals and the best case of what we want"],[213,759.34,3.21,"versus the practical realities of kids being exposed to these things"],[213,762.55,3.04,"in ways that are sometimes out of our control, even as a parent?"],[213,765.64,2.84,"RW: Yeah, I really love that question."],[213,768.47,4.96,"I think, first, because it gives so much grace to the fact"],[213,773.44,1.21,"that parenting is hard"],[213,774.69,1.63,"and there are a lot of challenges."],[213,776.31,2.46,"I think, in the world of artificial intelligence,"],[213,778.82,3.42,"something I think a lot about is human wisdom, right?"],[213,782.28,3.71,"And we've actually been kind of struggling with this problem for a long time."],[213,785.99,4.38,"There were books and then the radio and then TV and then the internet,"],[213,790.37,2.67,"and now it's artificial intelligence."],[213,793.04,3.17,"And I think the advice from then still rings true now,"],[213,796.25,3.34,"which is, don't leave children on their own to figure it out."],[213,799.63,1.13,"Rules are great,"],[213,800.8,3.75,"but better is to help your child learn how to interact with them"],[213,804.59,2.46,"in a way that works with your family's values"],[213,807.09,2.42,"and the ways that you want to engage in the world."],[213,809.51,2.96,"So if you're willing to take that journey with your child,"],[213,812.48,1.88,"I think that could do a lot of good."],[213,814.35,2.08,"CD: Thank you, that's a great answer."],[213,816.48,1.92,"Another big round of applause for Randi."],[214,0.0,7.0,"Transcriber: Maki Sugimoto"],[214,3.58,1.75,"Sometimes citizens think,"],[214,5.33,3.63,"“Oh, this is my personal problem or a personal issue."],[214,9.42,4.38,"So it’s kind of a waste of time to speak up.”"],[214,13.84,1.29,"But this is not true."],[214,15.51,5.34,"If someone speaks up and others agree with it,"],[214,20.85,3.8,"that personal issue becomes social issue."],[214,25.02,1.92,"With my administration,"],[214,26.98,3.92,"the city government actually did not make a town vision."],[214,31.78,5.25,"Instead of that, citizens created the vision by themselves"],[214,37.08,4.96,"and we, local government, supported the process,"],[214,42.04,2.84,"because [a] city is citizens --"],[214,46.25,2.29,"[a] city is not the government."],[214,49.75,1.71,"I am Takashima Ryosuke,"],[214,51.51,2.0,"the youngest mayor in Japanese history."],[214,54.09,1.96,"I’m the mayor of Ashiya City."],[214,60.72,2.21,"I’m a mayor, but I’m not a politician."],[214,63.44,3.13,"My stance is actually very simple."],[214,66.56,4.25,"I just listen to as many voices as possible,"],[214,70.82,1.96,"especially (from) younger generations."],[214,72.78,1.84,"The reason is very simple:"],[214,74.65,3.54,"students under 18 cannot vote."],[214,79.03,1.71,"So I visited schools,"],[214,80.78,2.46,"like junior high schools and elementary schools,"],[214,83.29,2.0,"to hear their voices."],[214,85.33,5.71,"But it’s so hard for kids, for students, to speak their opinions to a mayor --"],[214,91.09,2.29,"even if I am the youngest mayor."],[214,93.84,3.46,"So what I did was eat school lunch together,"],[214,100.1,4.17,"and I repeatedly visited schools."],[214,104.27,3.63,"I didn’t want to be a formal visit,"],[214,107.94,2.0,"but rather a casual visit,"],[214,109.98,3.25,"casual chat with teachers and students."],[214,114.03,4.09,"I could create the kind of friendly atmosphere,"],[214,118.11,4.75,"and I got more information rather than [in] a formal visit."],[214,123.24,5.71,"For example, some students told me their school gym was so hot."],[214,128.96,4.75,"So we decided to install air conditioners"],[214,133.75,2.29,"in every single school gym."],[214,136.97,4.84,"I wanted to make schools a place to learn and practice democracy."],[214,142.22,3.17,"And it's all about communication and dialogue."],[214,146.27,5.38,"I wanted to encourage people to speak up, listen to their voices,"],[214,151.69,2.71,"and then reflect their voices."],[214,154.44,3.92,"The main method is holding dialogue meetings."],[214,158.66,4.34,"It’s a meeting with me, mayor, and citizens"],[214,163.03,3.13,"sit down together in a circle and talk with each other."],[214,166.62,4.55,"And sometimes the mayor does not even speak up."],[214,171.54,5.38,"Just listens or facilitates what citizens want to talk about."],[214,177.59,4.55,"So, for example, Ashiya is a very healthy city."],[214,182.18,5.26,"Recently, we had the world's oldest person in Ashiya city."],[214,187.43,4.34,"Around 30 percent of the citizens are over 65."],[214,191.81,1.21,"In the dialogue meetings,"],[214,193.06,4.42,"I heard elderly people say that the community centers were too busy"],[214,197.49,2.42,"to answer their questions about care."],[214,200.36,3.71,"So I wanted to increase the number of community centers,"],[214,204.12,2.08,"but we needed some budget."],[214,206.24,2.54,"So before we had a monetary gift for elderly people,"],[214,208.83,3.63,"so we decided to change our monetary gift system,"],[214,212.5,1.54,"from a one-time monetary gift"],[214,214.09,2.75,"to creating [a] new community center"],[214,216.88,2.67,"where elderly people and their families"],[214,219.55,2.38,"get some advice on wellness."],[214,222.8,3.63,"Some people say monetary gifts are a kind of symbol of respect."],[214,227.35,1.63,"So some people argued"],[214,229.02,3.96,"that I had no respect for elderly people."],[214,233.48,1.71,"But through the dialogue sessions,"],[214,235.19,3.25,"I explained why this was important"],[214,238.49,4.63,"and why this actually benefited for elderly people."],[214,244.12,4.46,"And I also cut my future monetary gift."],[214,249.16,3.5,"So this was not like others’ problem;"],[214,252.71,1.63,"this was our problem."],[214,254.88,3.67,"Finally, they (came to) understand why we needed to change."],[214,259.05,6.13,"They changed their opinion because they understood the process."],[214,266.22,4.3,"During cherry blossom season, when cherry blossoms bloom beautifully,"],[214,270.56,3.5,"local shops wanted to close the street"],[214,274.06,3.96,"to enjoy food under the cherry blossoms."],[214,278.02,3.96,"But for local residents, that proposal was simply inconvenient,"],[214,282.36,1.58,"so it was controversial."],[214,284.78,6.59,"But when local citizens and local shops actually joined the process"],[214,291.41,3.34,"and citizens proposed the plan,"],[214,294.75,2.25,"the proposal was a success."],[214,297.58,4.55,"So citizens actually created the vision of (our) town,"],[214,302.17,1.33,"not the government."],[214,304.47,1.58,"People tend to think"],[214,306.09,3.75,"that the ones who make society better are the government"],[214,309.89,1.92,"or someone else."],[214,311.85,1.5,"and not ourselves."],[214,313.94,2.21,"I want to change that mindset."],[214,316.6,1.63,"It is everyone."],[214,318.56,3.38,"It's every single person who can make society better"],[214,321.99,3.0,"and who can contribute to making society better."],[214,324.99,1.67,"That is what I want to do."],[214,330.37,1.21,"Thank you."],[215,7.04,2.13,"Something strange happened here."],[215,9.34,4.5,"Two decades after some ants arrived on this Kenyan savanna,"],[215,14.09,4.67,"lions were killing many more buffalo and far fewer zebras."],[215,19.1,3.54,"But how do these seemingly disparate events relate?"],[215,22.93,1.79,"Well, let’s rewind."],[215,25.15,4.63,"Around the early 2000s, a species called big-headed ants"],[215,29.78,2.75,"landed in Kenya’s Laikipia Plateau,"],[215,32.74,3.17,"probably hitching rides on imported produce."],[215,36.2,4.46,"Since their suspected origins on an island in the Indian Ocean,"],[215,40.79,6.05,"big-headed ants have become among the world’s most widespread invasive species."],[215,46.96,4.88,"Their colonies can have multiple queens and reproduce year round,"],[215,51.88,4.25,"expanding rapidly into extensive super-colonies"],[215,56.13,3.38,"sustained by voracious foraging and hunting."],[215,60.18,5.05,"They prey on other insects, spiders, and even small birds."],[215,65.35,3.42,"This all spells trouble for many of their new neighbors."],[215,69.11,6.96,"And in Laikipia, perhaps most notably, lots of native acacia ants are nearby."],[215,76.36,6.21,"Their existence is closely entwined to the area’s whistling-thorn acacia trees,"],[215,82.58,2.29,"which are clad in spiky armor."],[215,85.0,2.42,"But this isn’t enough to defend the trees"],[215,87.42,3.84,"against savanna herbivores that could eat them to death."],[215,91.38,3.04,"And that’s where acacia ants come in."],[215,94.8,5.3,"Acacia trees are myrmecophytes— one of around 700 plant species"],[215,100.1,3.79,"that have evolved mutualistic relationships with ants."],[215,104.27,6.13,"In Laikipia, some acacia ant species claim specific whistling-thorn trees"],[215,110.4,1.63,"and live within them,"],[215,112.23,4.3,"boring holes and nesting in the bulbous, hollow structures"],[215,116.53,3.25,"at the base of the tree’s modified thorns."],[215,120.07,5.46,"The acacias provide the ants shelter, nectar, and other nutritious secretions,"],[215,125.54,2.92,"often in return for fierce defense."],[215,128.71,2.63,"When the ants sense certain vibrations,"],[215,131.34,3.92,"they secrete alarm pheromones from their mandibular glands"],[215,135.26,6.13,"and swarm out of their nests in droves, poised to sting and bite intruders—"],[215,141.51,4.84,"be it invading insects or the tender trunks and mouthparts"],[215,146.35,1.92,"of grazing herbivores."],[215,148.56,3.59,"Whistling-thorn acacias and their associated ants"],[215,152.15,4.3,"have historically dominated hundreds of thousands of square kilometers"],[215,156.44,1.13,"in East Africa."],[215,157.65,3.92,"But researchers observed significant changes to the landscape"],[215,161.57,5.25,"as whistling-thorn trees dropped by over 70% in some areas"],[215,166.83,2.46,"between 2000 and 2020."],[215,169.46,1.79,"Something was going on here."],[215,172.79,3.34,"These changes seemed to lead back to the introduction"],[215,176.13,3.75,"of those tiny, ferocious big-headed ants."],[215,180.01,3.38,"They threaten competitors, attacking in swarms,"],[215,183.39,2.25,"killing adult acacia ants,"],[215,186.01,1.63,"eating their eggs and young,"],[215,187.72,2.92,"and essentially decimating their populations."],[215,190.77,3.25,"And without their usual militant ant residents,"],[215,194.15,4.71,"acacias then become especially vulnerable to defoliation,"],[215,199.03,4.5,"breaking, and toppling by large herbivores like elephants."],[215,205.58,4.75,"Big-headed ants also nest in the ground, tunneling near tree roots,"],[215,210.33,3.42,"and can cause water stress among acacia trees"],[215,213.79,3.84,"and limit their ability to photosynthesize and store sugars."],[215,218.05,4.25,"And this is where lions, zebras, and buffalo come in."],[215,222.68,3.46,"Zebras are among Laikipia lions’ main prey,"],[215,226.22,4.46,"while water buffalo are less ideal because they pose more of a threat."],[215,230.98,2.25,"To up the odds of a zebra hunt,"],[215,233.27,4.34,"East African lions tend to rely on the art of ambushing,"],[215,237.73,3.59,"covertly stalking their prey before finally pouncing."],[215,241.57,2.25,"For that, they require cover."],[215,244.2,4.8,"Lions in Laikipia are three times more likely to catch zebras"],[215,249.0,3.67,"in tree-heavy areas than in open grasslands,"],[215,252.66,3.21,"where zebras can better spot them with time to escape."],[215,256.17,3.84,"But because of how big-headed ants threaten acacia trees,"],[215,260.01,3.63,"their presence has been shown to increase the visibility"],[215,263.72,5.59,"across a savannah landscape almost threefold within just three years."],[215,269.51,3.21,"So, between 2003 and 2020,"],[215,272.85,5.21,"as big-headed ant activity led to more and more acacia trees collapsing,"],[215,278.06,1.79,"grasslands spread,"],[215,280.03,5.71,"and lion kills went from being 67% to 42% zebra"],[215,285.87,3.5,"and 0 to 42% water buffalo."],[215,289.99,4.92,"It seems lions switched to their second, more dangerous choice of prey."],[215,295.5,4.0,"So: big-headed ants threatened acacia ants,"],[215,299.67,4.67,"leaving acacia trees defenseless against herbivores like elephants,"],[215,304.34,3.71,"which altered the landscape, leaving less cover for lions,"],[215,308.14,2.13,"which made zebras tougher targets,"],[215,310.39,3.0,"so lions started going for water buffalo."],[215,313.68,2.92,"Putting these ecological pieces together,"],[215,316.6,5.46,"scientists realized this also probably wouldn’t be the chain reaction’s end."],[215,322.23,5.42,"The impact switching prey will have on local lion life remains to be seen."],[215,327.87,5.46,"Plus, other animals, like superb starlings and endangered black rhinos,"],[215,333.45,2.13,"also rely on acacias."],[215,336.04,5.76,"And all of it began with tiny ants sending ripples through an ecosystem"],[215,341.8,4.21,"long stabilized by an intricate web of relationships,"],[215,346.18,3.79,"its underpinnings exposed through total disruption—"],[215,350.14,2.5,"no matter how small the start."],[216,3.87,1.8,"Hiya."],[216,5.7,1.8,"So my name is TED."],[216,9.57,1.74,"You're all very welcome to my world."],[216,11.34,5.0,"And I'm going to sing you three songs."],[216,16.81,2.97,"The first one is for joy,"],[216,19.82,3.54,"and the second one is for humanity,"],[216,23.39,3.27,"and the last one is for the heart."],[216,27.46,2.54,"So they're really depressing, you're welcome, see ya."],[216,34.43,1.2,"OK."],[216,37.53,1.77,"Alright, it's called \"To my bones.\""],[216,51.31,3.5,"[Music] We’re kissing the lips of strangers"],[216,54.82,3.8,"We're hugging whoever next we meet"],[216,58.66,3.47,"Oh, life, I love you to my bones"],[216,64.23,3.3,"When everything's shut, we're listless"],[216,67.53,4.0,"Take them home and cook them dinners and sing"],[216,71.53,3.17,"Oh, life, I love you to my bones"],[216,77.54,1.5,"Dominoes and medicine"],[216,79.04,1.7,"A two you are and new you win"],[216,80.78,1.5,"Take a seat and let it grow"],[216,82.31,2.4,"The world will love you"],[216,86.48,1.17,"Woo!"],[216,87.69,1.27,"Elephants of memory"],[216,88.95,1.74,"An alphabet of chemistry"],[216,90.72,3.2,"All of these things have made me"],[216,93.96,3.24,"Roscoe and Beenie,"],[216,97.23,2.74,"Laughter and no TV"],[216,100.0,4.0,"I've got punch lines and fisticuffs"],[216,104.03,2.4,"And tons and tons of luck."],[216,106.77,3.84,"I've got firesides and dutch gold"],[216,110.97,2.97,"There's no such thing as too old"],[216,113.94,3.17,"And my fortune is my home"],[216,117.81,2.4,"My home and all I know"],[216,121.72,4.91,"Oh, yeah, because, because"],[216,126.66,3.3,"oh, life, I love you to my bones"],[216,129.99,3.17,"Oh, life, I love you to my bones"],[216,133.2,3.3,"Oh, life, I love you to my bones"],[216,136.5,3.4,"Oh, life, I love to my bones"],[216,139.94,3.07,"Oh, life, I love you to my bones"],[216,143.04,3.5,"Oh, life, I love you to my bones"],[216,146.54,3.27,"Oh, life, I love you to my bones"],[216,149.85,3.34,"Oh, life, I love you to my bones"],[216,161.66,1.17,"Thanks very much."],[216,169.93,1.27,"Thank you again."],[216,173.14,2.1,"OK, this next song is brilliant."],[216,179.58,2.0,"It's called \"As the River Flows.\""],[216,181.61,4.61,"And it's in support of migrants and refugees"],[216,186.25,1.83,"and in defiance of racism."],[216,196.99,2.77,"[Music] And as the river flows,"],[216,199.8,3.77,"well you and I are made of water"],[216,203.6,3.07,"Surging into history"],[216,206.67,3.2,"Universal mystery"],[216,209.87,3.9,"All in all, we cannot choose"],[216,213.81,2.33,"The life that we were given"],[216,216.45,3.54,"So we move across the land"],[216,219.98,1.8,"To sky, to sea"],[216,222.22,3.5,"Looking for more than we were offered"],[216,225.76,1.83,"Are we not allowed?"],[216,228.06,4.07,"Are you still where your mother pushed you out?"],[216,232.86,1.5,"I doubt that this is true"],[216,234.76,3.84,"If you can move, then I will too"],[216,240.37,6.84,"To to to the land,"],[216,247.88,1.74,"To the sea"],[216,249.65,3.54,"To safety, to safety, to safety"],[216,253.22,1.67,"To the land"],[216,254.92,1.7,"to the mouth of the sea"],[216,256.65,1.74,"To the land"],[216,258.42,1.74,"To the sea"],[216,260.19,2.97,"To safety, to safety, to safety"],[216,263.16,2.8,"Overcoming mystery"],[216,266.0,3.3,"Overcoming mystery"],[216,274.17,2.0,"What are you scared of"],[216,276.21,2.07,"Aren't you brave enough"],[216,278.31,3.64,"To pave a basic decency"],[216,281.98,1.3,"for human kind?"],[216,283.95,2.94,"A baby lost its family"],[216,286.92,1.64,"Lost its life"],[216,291.65,2.77,"Don't turn your face away"],[216,294.46,5.67,"Don't tell me borders are for jobs and civic order"],[216,300.13,3.44,"When I know you see it differently"],[216,304.4,2.74,"You want your palette white"],[216,307.17,2.54,"You want it cleaned"],[216,313.84,1.67,"Walk a mile"],[216,315.55,2.6,"Walk 2,000 miles"],[216,318.18,5.11,"Walk 3,000 miles to to the land"],[216,323.92,1.74,"To the sea"],[216,325.69,2.8,"To safety, to safety, to safety"],[216,329.09,2.04,"Overcoming mystery"],[216,332.53,2.9,"To safety, to safety, to safety"],[216,335.83,1.74,"Overcoming mystery"],[216,342.07,3.24,"Overcoming mystery"],[216,345.34,3.5,"Overcoming mystery"],[216,348.88,3.7,"Overcoming mystery"],[216,352.58,3.44,"Overcoming mystery"],[216,356.02,3.54,"Overcoming mystery"],[216,359.59,3.4,"Overcoming mystery"],[216,363.03,3.44,"Overcoming mystery"],[216,366.5,3.4,"Overcoming mystery"],[216,369.93,3.44,"Overcoming mystery"],[216,388.08,2.34,"To safety, to safety, to safety"],[216,390.45,2.64,"To safety, to safety, to safety"],[216,393.12,2.5,"To safety, to safety, to safety"],[216,395.66,2.6,"To safety, to safety, to safety"],[216,398.3,2.44,"To safety, to safety, to safety"],[216,400.76,3.84,"To safety, to safety, to safety"],[216,416.78,1.3,"Wow."],[216,418.48,1.37,"Thank you so much."],[216,421.42,2.8,"I would like to invite my lovely partner on stage for this,"],[216,424.22,2.24,"Tracey K, please come on stage."],[216,433.03,1.7,"This one is for your heart."],[216,439.57,2.13,"Well, I’ll tell you the truth"],[216,443.44,5.24,"As I only do, my friend"],[216,451.48,3.27,"I'll been living my youth"],[216,455.72,2.6,"As I live till my end"],[216,458.36,2.1,"My friend"],[216,463.66,2.84,"I've been battered and bruised"],[216,466.93,5.71,"But it's only a few that have stayed or left"],[216,473.54,3.07,"And that's nothing a hammer"],[216,476.64,4.0,"A nail or my mind couldn't cure"],[216,483.48,2.94,"But I've left people hang"],[216,486.45,2.37,"On a stick or a word"],[216,488.85,4.14,"If their grip grew tight"],[216,493.39,2.97,"All the bottles of battles"],[216,496.39,4.87,"The beds that I've shared they're all mine"],[216,501.9,3.3,"But the more you hold on to me"],[216,507.24,4.5,"The less you can have of me"],[216,514.58,3.0,"The more you hold on to me"],[216,519.45,4.64,"The less I am yours to keep"],[216,532.3,4.54,"Yet the more you hold on to me"],[216,538.97,4.97,"I'll try not to leave."],[216,545.61,1.2,"Peace on earth."],[216,546.81,1.44,"Thank you so much."],[216,558.39,2.0,"Hello everybody, my name is Jacob Collier,"],[216,560.39,2.1,"and I am this year's music curator for TED."],[216,562.53,2.13,"And the musician you just heard, Wallis Bird,"],[216,564.7,1.94,"is one of my dearest musical companions."],[216,566.63,1.17,"She is crazy."],[216,567.83,3.1,"I don't know how you felt, but the first time I heard Wallis sing,"],[216,570.97,1.3,"I had tears and goosebumps."],[216,572.27,1.7,"She's like a roof-raising musician."],[216,573.97,3.44,"She accompanied me on tour, opening for some shows a couple of years ago,"],[216,577.44,1.53,"and I knew then what I know now,"],[216,579.01,3.57,"which is that she possesses that rare gift that not many musicians possess,"],[216,582.58,3.44,"which is the ability to win over any crowd with her totally wild energy,"],[216,586.05,1.64,"her joy, and her colors."],[216,587.72,3.44,"Her voice to me is like this chameleon."],[216,591.19,2.67,"It can change color, it can change shape, it's stretchy."],[216,593.86,2.4,"It's this magnificent instrument of joy."],[216,596.29,3.27,"And I think one of my favorite moments of the talk you just witnessed"],[216,599.56,2.84,"was that moment where she goes higher and higher and higher,"],[216,602.4,2.34,"and the energy just builds and builds and builds."],[216,604.74,1.97,"She really is unlike any musician I know."],[216,606.74,2.37,"Hope you enjoyed. Lots of love, cheerio, bye."],[217,7.04,6.17,"In 1900, Greek divers stumbled upon a 2,000-year-old shipwreck"],[217,13.22,4.75,"whose contents would shake our understanding of the ancient world."],[217,18.47,4.88,"Among the remains were fragments of mangled wood and corroded metal,"],[217,23.64,6.01,"which archaeologists soon realized contained an intricate series of gears,"],[217,29.65,5.38,"resembling technology not believed to exist until centuries later."],[217,35.57,4.8,"By the 1950s, scientists began to understand what they'd found."],[217,40.79,4.46,"These fragments were part of a device that could calculate the movements"],[217,45.25,3.29,"of the Sun, Moon, and planets."],[217,48.71,3.13,"Now known as the Antikythera mechanism,"],[217,51.96,5.46,"this instrument appears to be the oldest geared device ever discovered"],[217,57.43,3.13,"and humankind’s first computer."],[217,61.39,3.71,"Most modern devices, like your phone or laptop,"],[217,65.1,2.5,"are examples of digital computers."],[217,67.81,4.5,"These systems store information in strings of zeros and ones"],[217,72.32,3.09,"that they can then convert into images on a screen"],[217,75.4,1.54,"or sounds in a speaker."],[217,77.53,5.75,"But more generally, a computer refers to any device that stores information"],[217,83.29,3.46,"and can convert it from one format into another."],[217,87.25,3.21,"And long before the rise of digital computing,"],[217,90.63,4.8,"human technology was dominated by analog computers."],[217,96.09,2.38,"Rather than using binary bits,"],[217,98.59,4.88,"analog computers receive and store signals from the real world,"],[217,103.47,2.38,"such as light or mechanical motion,"],[217,106.19,3.84,"and convert that information into something “readable.”"],[217,110.52,4.0,"Think of how a vinyl record stores impressions of sound."],[217,114.82,3.92,"The recording device— a vinyl cutting system—"],[217,118.74,2.88,"writes mechanical analogs of the world:"],[217,121.99,4.75,"the physical force of sound waves carves grooves into a disc,"],[217,126.96,4.09,"which a record player then reads back into sound."],[217,131.63,4.75,"The Antikythera is the first known example of analog computing."],[217,136.67,1.38,"So how did it work?"],[217,139.13,3.46,"Ancient Greek astronomers knew that celestial bodies"],[217,142.6,3.25,"zoom predictably around the solar system."],[217,146.14,3.42,"But to forecast events like solar eclipses,"],[217,149.69,3.34,"they'd need to perform calculations by hand."],[217,153.44,3.79,"This device likely made that process much easier."],[217,157.78,4.88,"Setting the dial to a specific day drove a series of gears"],[217,162.66,3.46,"to calculate that day's astronomical personality."],[217,166.45,4.46,"Metallic hands pointed to the positions of the Sun, Moon,"],[217,170.92,3.63,"and what seemed to be the five then-known planets"],[217,174.54,3.84,"relative to the 12 constellations of the zodiac."],[217,178.63,3.04,"And the Antikythera’s features didn’t end there:"],[217,182.05,6.84,"another gear system likely spun a colored sphere representing the lunar phase."],[217,189.23,6.01,"On another face, one of the five dials revolved according to the Saros cycle—"],[217,195.44,4.88,"the approximately 6,585.3 days"],[217,200.32,3.04,"it takes for the Sun, Moon, and Earth"],[217,203.49,3.29,"to return to their exact relative positions."],[217,207.08,4.88,"There was even a dial that indicated the timing of Ancient Greek festivals."],[217,212.96,5.3,"For the Antikythera, computation lived in the connections between gears."],[217,218.42,3.17,"Each celestial body follows specific rules"],[217,221.59,3.29,"that govern its motion, positions, and quirks—"],[217,225.05,3.84,"like changing speeds and the Moon’s wobbly orbit."],[217,229.27,3.92,"The fact that these ancient gears were precisely designed"],[217,233.27,4.8,"to compute not just one, but many, of these rules simultaneously"],[217,238.15,2.0,"has astounded researchers."],[217,240.32,4.75,"For example, the handshake between 13 gears automatically"],[217,245.07,5.5,"that recreated our Moon’s orbit was only seconds off from reality."],[217,251.54,2.63,"Analog computers, like the Antikythera,"],[217,254.17,4.3,"are, in fact, more efficient than digital computers."],[217,258.8,6.67,"For example, take a digital 4K movie, represented by 100 billion binary digits."],[217,265.76,3.84,"These bits are fundamentally devoid of an identity."],[217,269.85,4.17,"They must be assembled by software on your TV or laptop,"],[217,274.14,5.25,"and converted into the colors, motion, and sound that simulate the real world."],[217,280.11,3.92,"While the exactness of binary code makes it reliable,"],[217,284.15,5.17,"digital logic requires abundance at the expense of efficiency."],[217,289.58,4.55,"And the output is always just an approximation of reality."],[217,294.87,3.67,"In contrast, consider an analog scale—"],[217,298.79,1.58,"when you step on this device,"],[217,300.38,3.54,"the force of your body weight presses down on a spring"],[217,304.01,2.96,"which rotates a dial to reveal a number."],[217,307.35,5.63,"This “computer” converts mechanical force directly into numbers we can read."],[217,313.35,1.96,"It’s a continuous input:"],[217,315.35,6.05,"weighing 150 pounds moves the spring more than weighing 149 pounds does."],[217,321.61,3.63,"And by acting continuously rather than discretely,"],[217,325.49,6.13,"many experts argue that analog computers are more real than digital ones."],[217,332.41,4.09,"This elegance of faithfully representing real information"],[217,336.5,2.67,"has renewed interest in analog tech,"],[217,339.25,2.79,"and is even inspiring new technologies."],[217,342.25,5.92,"In the future, analog computing may improve the efficiency of digital systems,"],[217,348.26,3.59,"proving what's old can be new again."],[218,4.27,2.4,"Chris Anderson: Great to see you, welcome to TED."],[218,6.67,4.27,"Now, Silvana, you've been passionate about science for quite a long time."],[218,10.97,1.7,"Tell me about this picture."],[218,13.78,3.07,"Silvana Konermann: So this picture is when I was 15."],[218,16.88,2.4,"So I was born in a small town in Switzerland."],[218,19.32,3.34,"My parents weren't into science, but somehow I got really fascinated"],[218,22.69,1.27,"just with nature around me"],[218,23.99,3.5,"and also just how we worked as humans and our biology."],[218,27.52,3.9,"So I really wanted to find a way to be able to get into a lab"],[218,31.46,1.17,"to do some science."],[218,32.66,1.77,"It was actually pretty tricky for me,"],[218,34.47,2.34,"but eventually I talked one of my science teachers"],[218,36.83,3.34,"into convincing one of his colleagues to let me go into the lab."],[218,40.2,3.67,"And so this is me with that first science project"],[218,43.91,3.2,"where I went on to win the national competition,"],[218,47.14,2.94,"and then also the European Union competition."],[218,50.11,3.6,"And I think that's really where, you know, I got, I think,"],[218,53.72,3.37,"the confidence to continue with science since then."],[218,57.09,2.7,"CA: But there's a drawback to being a scientific prodigy,"],[218,59.79,3.27,"which is that you end up feeling like you might have a responsibility"],[218,63.06,1.53,"to do something with that."],[218,64.63,2.0,"And I think you've had that your whole life"],[218,66.66,1.67,"and you’ve thought about"],[218,68.36,2.9,"what are the biggest problems you could work on."],[218,71.3,2.27,"Tell us about this graph here."],[218,73.6,1.44,"SK: Yeah, absolutely."],[218,75.07,1.5,"So I have been, you know,"],[218,76.57,2.3,"doing science now for more than 20 years."],[218,78.91,3.84,"But I did want to say, this is actually my first real public appearance."],[218,82.78,4.47,"So I am very much, usually behind the scenes."],[218,89.52,3.54,"Yeah, a problem that I’ve really been thinking a lot about"],[218,93.09,3.94,"since undergrad -- I did my undergrad in Switzerland"],[218,97.03,1.64,"in biology neuroscience --"],[218,98.66,2.57,"and I learned more about Alzheimer’s disease."],[218,101.97,1.77,"And through learning"],[218,103.77,3.84,"how there are these big changes in the brain that are happening,"],[218,107.64,2.04,"a lot of what is known about"],[218,109.71,3.0,"late stages of the disease, how severe it is,"],[218,113.71,4.2,"and then the lecture though ended with basically:"],[218,117.95,3.14,"but we have no idea really how it’s starting."],[218,121.12,1.67,"We still don’t have a therapy."],[218,122.82,2.07,"And that was now, a long time ago,"],[218,124.92,3.74,"I guess, 17 years ago or so."],[218,128.69,3.1,"And that really stuck with me because"],[218,131.79,2.17,"why didn’t we understand how it’s starting?"],[218,134.0,1.4,"Why didn’t we have a therapy?"],[218,135.43,3.54,"There are all these very observable changes happening."],[218,139.34,3.74,"And so that got me interested in disease biology"],[218,143.11,4.04,"and specifically complex diseases, where Alzheimer’s is a complex disease."],[218,147.14,2.6,"It sounds [like] “Oh, it’s just complicated,”"],[218,149.78,1.44,"but that's not what it means."],[218,151.25,3.0,"It means that there are multiple different risk factors."],[218,154.28,4.24,"And basically every patient has a unique combination of risk factors"],[218,158.56,1.13,"for a disease --"],[218,159.72,3.74,"that’s different from an infection where you have one cause."],[218,163.46,4.3,"CA: And several of these diseases here are similarly fundamentally complex."],[218,167.8,1.17,"SK: That's right."],[218,169.0,3.44,"So heart disease, many cancers, obviously not accidents,"],[218,172.47,2.57,"but stroke and Alzheimer’s disease --"],[218,175.07,1.83,"these are all complex diseases."],[218,176.94,2.07,"CA: And so that's why it's been so resistant"],[218,179.01,3.34,"to dramatic advancements"],[218,182.35,1.7,"in medical science in recent years."],[218,184.08,4.24,"SK: Basically, all of these have a combination of genetic changes"],[218,188.35,1.5,"[and] environmental factors."],[218,189.89,1.5,"And each patient is unique."],[218,191.39,2.2,"They have a unique combination of risk factors."],[218,193.62,4.47,"And so we’ve been really struggling as a scientific community"],[218,198.13,4.27,"understanding what do all these different patients have in common"],[218,202.4,3.6,"that we could target and then fix the disease?"],[218,206.67,4.07,"CA: But you're seeing now an opportunity to have a different kind of assault"],[218,210.77,1.2,"on these diseases."],[218,211.98,1.17,"What has changed?"],[218,213.18,2.17,"SK: I think there are now three things"],[218,215.38,3.27,"that have come together just in the last one or two years"],[218,218.68,4.37,"that make it possible to understand such a complex problem"],[218,223.05,2.57,"like Alzheimer’s disease and other diseases like it --"],[218,225.62,2.54,"and [those are], at a high level, three areas,"],[218,228.16,3.6,"if you summarize it really quickly: measuring,"],[218,231.79,1.87,"changing and understanding."],[218,233.66,4.3,"And so measuring -- what that means for us is really single-cell sequencing."],[218,238.0,4.34,"So this is a technology that allows us to look at one cell at a time"],[218,242.37,3.74,"and take a snapshot of key dynamic processes in the cell"],[218,246.14,2.17,"which is the RNA expression of the cell."],[218,248.34,2.6,"So basically, RNA is like the language of the cell,"],[218,250.98,3.07,"and this takes a snapshot one cell at a time"],[218,254.05,1.94,"of what's going on inside it."],[218,255.99,3.0,"And then the second step, which is changing,"],[218,259.32,3.34,"we need to have the ability to change something very precise --"],[218,262.69,3.14,"so changing one gene at a time [to]"],[218,265.86,4.1,"stop it from making the RNA or changing it to upregulate the RNA."],[218,270.0,3.37,"This is the area that I’ve been working on now for 15 years --"],[218,273.37,1.83,"CRISPR technology --"],[218,275.24,2.8,"and as a field, we’ve made a lot of advancements."],[218,278.07,2.74,"And now we can do this across all the genes in the genome."],[218,280.84,2.07,"We can make these changes in a targeted way."],[218,282.95,3.57,"And it is really only possible very recently."],[218,286.55,2.87,"And then finally, of course, AI"],[218,289.45,3.4,"is at the forefront of everything, especially today."],[218,293.49,1.6,"But we’ve just seen over --"],[218,295.12,2.57,"I would say really the last two years --"],[218,297.73,2.5,"that ... it’s really working."],[218,300.23,3.97,"AI can help us understand these kinds of processes."],[218,305.0,1.5,"CA: So if I understand right,"],[218,306.54,5.74,"just as AI has cracked understanding human language,"],[218,312.28,2.87,"you see a possibility that AI can be used"],[218,315.14,3.6,"to understand the language of our own cells, RNA."],[218,318.78,1.97,"SK: Yeah, exactly,"],[218,320.78,1.9,"that's basically the core principle."],[218,322.72,3.7,"And for that, you need to be able to measure it"],[218,326.46,2.0,"and change it in this targeted way."],[218,328.49,3.4,"But as an analogy, the field was doubting this at the time."],[218,331.93,1.4,"I mean, even six years ago,"],[218,333.36,4.54,"people were not sure that you could really scale"],[218,337.94,3.4,"these large language models, just based on language"],[218,341.34,2.04,"and kind of predicting language,"],[218,343.41,3.94,"to actually build a conception of the world essentially,"],[218,347.38,4.84,"and at least approximate intelligence clearly pretty well."],[218,352.22,3.87,"So this is the key insight for the last six years,"],[218,356.12,5.17,"which is that a model can learn so much just from human language."],[218,361.32,3.57,"And similarly, we can apply that concept to RNA,"],[218,364.89,2.27,"which is basically the language of the cell --"],[218,367.16,2.17,"especially the dynamic language of the cell --"],[218,369.33,1.7,"because it's changing all the time."],[218,371.03,2.14,"It reflects what's happening to the cell,"],[218,373.2,2.3,"but also it reflects the cell's genetics."],[218,375.54,3.3,"CA: Is it approximately the same level of complexity as human language"],[218,378.88,1.53,"or much more so or less?"],[218,380.41,3.07,"SK: It's hard to say, but I will say one key difference for me,"],[218,383.51,4.04,"and I think this is why AI can be so powerful for biology,"],[218,387.58,3.1,"is that human language is generated by humans, right?"],[218,390.72,1.5,"So we understand it, right?"],[218,392.26,1.4,"We came up with it."],[218,394.76,3.5,"RNA language, or the biological language, has evolved --"],[218,398.3,2.74,"it was not generated by humans."],[218,401.06,2.44,"So it's basically impenetrable for us."],[218,403.53,3.17,"We can predict the left side: “to be or not to be” we know [is]"],[218,406.74,2.1,"Shakespeare -- we can complete it."],[218,408.84,3.4,"On the right side [though], no human really could complete that, right?"],[218,412.24,1.97,"But AI doesn't care."],[218,414.48,1.77,"CA: To try and crack it,"],[218,416.25,2.67,"I think you have to take the same stance"],[218,418.92,3.44,"of just getting huge amounts of data."],[218,422.39,2.67,"Talk about that process."],[218,425.09,1.17,"SK: Yeah, absolutely."],[218,426.29,3.17,"I mean, really what we learn, again, for large language models,"],[218,429.49,2.0,"is just they're very hungry."],[218,431.86,1.4,"They’re very data-hungry."],[218,434.0,3.87,"And really we’ve been generating data"],[218,437.9,4.1,"for these language models for thousands of years."],[218,442.04,1.97,"They're using all human languages"],[218,444.04,4.47,"that's been generated over generations and civilizations."],[218,448.55,2.74,"In biology, we don't have anything similar to that, right?"],[218,451.31,3.44,"Especially when you're thinking about, we need these precise measurements"],[218,454.79,1.3,"kind of one cell at a time."],[218,456.12,2.87,"And we also need to know what actually happened to that cell"],[218,458.99,3.14,"because we're trying to build a predictive model, a dynamic model,"],[218,462.16,3.3,"that can predict how a cell will change when something happens to it."],[218,465.5,2.13,"And so we need to generate that data set."],[218,467.66,4.27,"And that's kind of really core to being able to build any useful model here."],[218,472.34,4.44,"CA: So give a sense of how you actually do this."],[218,476.81,3.1,"SK: Essentially, this is really combining those first two elements"],[218,479.94,1.17,"I was talking about,"],[218,481.11,2.04,"which is making a targeted change."],[218,483.18,2.3,"In this case, we're using CRISPR technology"],[218,485.52,2.8,"to turn a gene off or to turn it on."],[218,488.35,3.77,"And we're doing that one gene at a time for one cell at a time."],[218,492.15,4.14,"And then we’re measuring the outcome using single-cell RNA sequencing"],[218,496.29,2.13,"so we’re capturing what happened to the cell."],[218,498.46,2.94,"CA: So you do what you call a perturbation of the cell."],[218,501.43,1.53,"And then you measure the output."],[218,503.0,3.34,"How many experiments like that do you need to do,"],[218,506.34,1.33,"what's your plan?"],[218,507.7,3.5,"SK: So our plan is to do at least a billion of these experiments."],[218,511.24,1.6,"So it's a lot of experiments."],[218,513.91,1.6,"Over the next four years."],[218,515.55,2.44,"CA: And you're not talking about, like in software,"],[218,517.98,2.47,"you're talking about a billion actual, biological --"],[218,520.45,2.14,"SK: Yeah, they're all physical experiments."],[218,522.62,2.04,"I mean, I'm a biologist,"],[218,524.69,1.5,"an experimental biologist."],[218,526.19,2.44,"We're working with a lot of experiments in the lab."],[218,528.66,3.54,"And yet the way that we can do this is kind of using some tricks"],[218,532.2,1.64,"that make this much more scalable."],[218,533.86,3.9,"We’re not actually like, running a billion little"],[218,537.77,1.94,"individual reactions."],[218,540.17,4.14,"We're able to use kind of different bar-coding technologies"],[218,544.34,4.1,"to run these experiments in this bigger pools"],[218,548.45,3.2,"and then back out what happened [and] what we did to the cells."],[218,551.68,3.94,"CA: OK, so if things work out as you hope --"],[218,555.62,2.97,"I guess you’re already seeing evidence that it’s working out."],[218,558.59,1.27,"Once you gather that data,"],[218,559.89,4.57,"you're able to get from the model something truly amazing."],[218,564.76,1.17,"Talk about that."],[218,565.93,1.6,"SK: So just to give a sense"],[218,567.56,2.44,"of why I feel that we can do the billion experiments"],[218,570.03,2.47,"is we've done about 60 million experiments so far."],[218,572.54,1.74,"CA: You've done 60 million, right."],[218,574.3,2.24,"SK: So we feel pretty good we can keep going."],[218,576.97,3.9,"But yeah, the whole point of this is that we want to learn."],[218,580.91,3.3,"If I have this cell, and I make this change,"],[218,584.25,1.24,"what happens to the cell?"],[218,585.51,3.8,"Really my motivation for generating this model"],[218,589.35,2.67,"is ultimately for human health."],[218,592.05,3.54,"And so for that, we can now have a disease state."],[218,595.63,2.57,"And importantly, this can be, for example,"],[218,598.23,2.37,"a certain cell in Alzheimer’s disease --"],[218,600.63,3.04,"let’s say it’s an immune cell in the brain: microglia."],[218,603.7,2.4,"And we can measure, what that looks like,"],[218,606.1,2.44,"not just for one patient, but across many patients."],[218,608.57,3.07,"And this data is out there, so we don't even have to generate it."],[218,611.67,2.9,"And so we can see, OK, all these diseased cells,"],[218,614.61,3.27,"then we can have all the healthy cells, but again across people."],[218,617.91,2.77,"And then we can ask the model, OK, the model knows how"],[218,620.72,1.83,"to change cells, right?"],[218,622.59,2.4,"So what intervention, what genetic change,"],[218,625.02,4.2,"what chemical change do I need to make to convert all the diseased cells"],[218,629.26,2.57,"across all the patients with the same disease"],[218,631.86,1.97,"back to the healthy cells?"],[218,633.86,3.37,"CA: So that's an amazing sort of prediction."],[218,638.03,2.4,"Like, if you truly understand the language of DNA,"],[218,640.47,3.74,"the model can predict something that medicine has never known before,"],[218,644.24,2.04,"because the answer to doing that"],[218,646.31,2.27,"might be quite a complex series"],[218,648.58,2.07,"of interventions are needed for that cell."],[218,650.65,2.0,"It's not like you just give it an aspirin."],[218,652.68,2.74,"SK: It could be that it's a complex combination of things,"],[218,655.45,3.34,"or it's really just a question even of picking the correct one, right?"],[218,658.79,2.0,"There’s, you know, 20,000 possibilities --"],[218,660.79,2.67,"could be up or down to 40,000 possibilities."],[218,663.46,4.14,"And normally, the way this target identification"],[218,667.63,1.47,"and biomedicine works today"],[218,669.13,2.44,"is really this kind of guess and check approach."],[218,671.6,2.87,"So you have a hypothesis, one gene,"],[218,674.5,2.07,"then you're spending a few years on checking"],[218,676.57,1.74,"whether that's the right one, right?"],[218,678.34,2.0,"So if you have 40,000 things to pick from,"],[218,680.38,2.04,"even if you just have to pick a single one,"],[218,682.45,1.47,"that takes forever, right?"],[218,683.95,2.8,"And that's why we haven't cured these diseases yet."],[218,688.12,3.2,"CA: So what are you going to do with this model"],[218,691.35,2.74,"as you gradually refine it?"],[218,694.12,1.83,"I mean, [as] I understand,"],[218,695.96,2.37,"you think of this as basically a virtual cell,"],[218,698.33,3.1,"[that] is what you’re creating -- it’s almost more than that."],[218,701.43,5.31,"It's like a universal virtual cell that researchers can,"],[218,706.77,3.04,"whatever cell they’re working on, use your model."],[218,709.81,2.24,"Talk about what you're planning to do with it."],[218,712.08,3.07,"SK: The whole point of it is that it is a universal virtual cell,"],[218,715.14,3.54,"which means that it needs to learn how to generalize to a new kind of cell"],[218,718.72,3.2,"or a new state of a cell, a new disease, for example,"],[218,721.92,4.17,"without having seen data, training data for that new cell type."],[218,726.12,1.97,"So that is a very challenging task."],[218,728.12,2.3,"And that's why we're really thinking hard"],[218,730.46,2.5,"about how to do these experiments."],[218,733.0,2.74,"But ultimately, I mean, this is what we're seeing here is,"],[218,735.77,2.67,"you know, the vision is that this is actually real."],[218,738.47,1.94,"So this is our state designer."],[218,740.4,2.17,"So we have already built our first model"],[218,742.61,3.07,"that came out eight months ago."],[218,745.71,1.3,"It's not very good."],[218,747.48,2.44,"So I mean to be clear, it is state-of-the-art,"],[218,749.95,2.4,"it's the best model at the time that was published."],[218,755.25,2.97,"But it has a really long way to go"],[218,758.25,4.07,"to be at the accuracy that I think it needs to be to be really useful."],[218,763.06,3.64,"But this is an interface that uses that model that we have today."],[218,766.7,1.84,"And so what you can do is you can say,"],[218,768.53,2.1,"“OK, I have this cell that I’m starting with."],[218,770.63,2.74,"And then, you know, I want to change this about the cell.”"],[218,773.37,3.57,"And then it spits out these different changes"],[218,776.94,2.27,"that you can make to the cell"],[218,779.24,3.1,"that are most likely to shift it the way you want."],[218,782.35,2.1,"CA: So you're not holding on to this yourself"],[218,784.48,1.44,"or licensing it to companies,"],[218,785.95,1.87,"you're making this generally available."],[218,787.85,1.2,"SK: That's right, yeah."],[218,789.09,2.6,"So we have a few ways that we really want people --"],[218,795.09,1.94,"People to be able to interact with it"],[218,797.06,1.9,"and also follow along."],[218,798.96,1.37,"So one is"],[218,800.36,3.87,"we're going to be releasing this tool later this year for people to try."],[218,804.27,2.5,"We'll give caveats like, this is not very accurate"],[218,806.8,2.2,"or this is going to be 20 percent accurate,"],[218,809.04,4.3,"but also we're going to iterate over the next four years."],[218,813.34,3.57,"We’re also hosting a “Virtual Cell Challenge” every year"],[218,816.95,1.2,"for the whole community."],[218,818.18,3.17,"We have 1,000 teams participating in the first one,"],[218,821.38,3.14,"and that's really to move the whole field forward,"],[218,824.55,2.24,"to get to where I think we need to be."],[218,827.09,1.17,"CA: So this is amazing."],[218,828.29,1.64,"The amazing work in your institute"],[218,829.96,3.6,"is really going to catalyze research worldwide"],[218,833.6,2.04,"because you're making this tool available."],[218,835.63,2.54,"Some people looking at that may go, “Well, wait a sec,"],[218,838.17,1.7,"isn’t that a little bit dangerous?”"],[218,839.87,1.77,"Some of the people playing with this"],[218,841.64,2.3,"may not have humanity’s best interests at heart."],[218,843.94,1.3,"What do you say to that?"],[218,845.27,3.14,"SK: That’s definitely a question that I think, as you know, we got"],[218,848.45,2.0,"during the Audacious process."],[218,850.45,2.07,"And I think the key thing to keep in mind is"],[218,852.51,2.34,"that this is really just for human cells."],[218,854.85,1.33,"In theory,"],[218,856.22,3.04,"someone could build this kind of tool for a virus."],[218,859.29,2.67,"And I would say, don't do that, that's a bad idea,"],[218,861.99,3.17,"because yes, then you can absolutely use it"],[218,865.2,2.07,"to create something that would be dangerous."],[218,867.26,4.7,"But this really just allows you to shift human cells into a different state."],[218,872.0,3.57,"And I think that would be pretty difficult to abuse."],[218,875.57,2.54,"CA: And in principle, if a nasty virus did come along"],[218,878.14,1.7,"and this model is working properly,"],[218,879.84,2.57,"that's a way of giving us one of the quickest --"],[218,882.45,3.24,"SK: Yeah, I mean, it will tell you, for example,"],[218,885.72,3.3,"how the virus is targeting this gene in this cell right now."],[218,889.05,3.97,"OK, we know what happens to the cell when that's getting targeted."],[218,893.06,2.97,"So yeah, it will absolutely help us to defend."],[218,896.06,1.17,"CA: So here's your team."],[218,898.09,1.33,"Tell us about them."],[218,899.8,4.3,"SK: Yeah, so Arc was only started in 2022 --"],[218,904.1,1.9,"[which was] when we decided to launch."],[218,906.04,2.0,"2022 is really when we got up and running."],[218,908.07,2.44,"So it's only been four years, but we've grown a lot."],[218,910.54,2.4,"CA: So that's the before and after over four years?"],[218,912.98,2.64,"SK: That’s just one year of growth -- the last year."],[218,915.64,1.77,"So we're over 300 people now."],[218,917.41,2.47,"And I think one thing we really wanted"],[218,919.92,1.47,"to be able to achieve with Arc"],[218,921.38,3.07,"is to bring people together from different disciplines"],[218,924.49,4.8,"and have AI and biology under one roof in one institute."],[218,929.29,3.64,"And we started this just around the right time when"],[218,932.96,3.8,"we could see what machine learning was going to mean for biology."],[218,937.23,3.6,"CA: Silvana, I got so excited to see the Audacious community get behind this"],[218,940.87,3.34,"and really help you expand this vision."],[218,944.24,5.74,"It's hard to imagine a bolder effort at really tackling what humanity needs"],[218,950.01,2.97,"and in making us all feel better about AI."],[218,954.08,3.44,"So someone here who's got in their family,"],[218,957.55,4.4,"they've got Alzheimer's or they've got heart disease or whatever,"],[218,961.99,1.33,"what would you say to them?"],[218,963.33,2.64,"SK: I mean, I would say that I really think"],[218,966.0,3.04,"the medicine is going to transform"],[218,969.03,1.7,"for these kinds of diseases, right?"],[218,970.77,1.83,"Maybe not in three months."],[218,972.63,1.8,"So you have to be a little patient."],[218,974.47,3.07,"But I think that within four years, five years,"],[218,977.57,5.24,"we will be able to have these models that are accurate enough to be useful."],[218,982.85,2.6,"And then it's a totally different way of doing biology."],[218,985.48,2.87,"It's not kind of, one hypothesis at a time, right?"],[218,988.38,2.77,"A field like Alzheimer's can get really bogged down"],[218,991.19,2.64,"by just focusing on one dominant hypothesis"],[218,993.86,1.33,"that might be wrong."],[218,995.23,1.23,"And with these models,"],[218,996.49,4.07,"you can actually take a comprehensive data-driven look, you know,"],[218,1000.6,2.94,"[at] all the things that we could be targeting with a drug,"],[218,1003.57,1.97,"what's going to happen with all of them,"],[218,1005.57,2.87,"and then which of them is going to be the most effective one?"],[218,1008.47,2.7,"It's just a totally different way of tackling the problem"],[218,1011.21,2.0,"that I think is so exciting."],[218,1013.24,3.5,"CA: Silvana, thank you for your incredible vision, for sharing it with us."],[218,1016.78,2.74,"Thank you, really, just fantastic."],[218,1019.55,1.24,"SK: Thank you so much."],[219,6.96,3.67,"You’re a male firefly and it seems you’re hitting it off with this female."],[219,10.63,3.21,"She’s sending you the right signals back— but what’s this?"],[219,14.01,2.0,"Suddenly, she’s lunging at you."],[219,16.18,1.21,"That’s not normal."],[219,17.47,2.33,"Let’s pause here to understand what’s going on"],[219,19.81,2.0,"and see if you'll make it out alive."],[219,22.39,4.17,"The first firefly is thought to have lived over 100 million years ago,"],[219,26.69,2.84,"illuminating the night with a glowing green light."],[219,29.9,3.29,"And today, there are around 2,000 firefly species,"],[219,33.2,1.46,"found all over the world,"],[219,34.78,3.75,"from forests and grasslands to marshes and deserts."],[219,39.49,3.63,"All fireflies produce light at some point in their lives."],[219,43.29,4.42,"They do this with a reaction-catalyzing enzyme called luciferase"],[219,47.71,2.38,"and a molecule called luciferin,"],[219,50.13,4.05,"which undergoes a luminous transformation when it interacts with oxygen."],[219,54.47,4.3,"Meanwhile, many firefly species also produce unpleasant tasting,"],[219,58.76,3.59,"toxic, defensive chemicals like lucibufagins."],[219,62.73,3.67,"So it's thought that glowing might help signal their toxicity"],[219,66.52,2.5,"and deter predators from eating them."],[219,69.44,3.42,"Developing fireflies also light up to varying degrees."],[219,72.86,3.84,"In fact, fireflies generally spend just a few weeks in their adult bodies"],[219,76.7,2.84,"and actually pass most of their lives as larvae."],[219,79.83,4.5,"During this stage, many species are highly active, fierce predators"],[219,84.33,2.0,"that use venom to paralyze prey,"],[219,86.37,3.54,"then externally liquefy their victims with their saliva."],[219,90.38,4.0,"Immature fireflies also tend to glow when they're disturbed."],[219,94.63,4.55,"When they finally become adults, their light skills help dissuade predators—"],[219,99.18,3.34,"but they're also usually channeled towards mating."],[219,102.89,3.29,"There are some exceptions, like so-called “dark” fireflies,"],[219,106.19,1.5,"which are active during the day"],[219,107.69,3.13,"and rely on pheromones instead of light for courtship."],[219,111.23,4.5,"But how it goes in many cases is that males fly around, flashing,"],[219,115.74,3.25,"while females observe until spotting the right one."],[219,119.36,2.42,"Different species display different colors—"],[219,121.78,3.04,"including reds, yellows, greens, and blues—"],[219,124.87,4.42,"all resulting from variation in the luciferase enzyme’s structure."],[219,129.46,5.26,"They also flash in unique patterns, from rapid flickers to sustained glows."],[219,135.05,1.25,"In some species,"],[219,136.3,4.21,"males synchronize their flashing once they reach great enough numbers,"],[219,140.51,3.42,"resulting in grand displays that help reduce visual clutter"],[219,143.93,1.83,"for their female spectators."],[219,145.89,4.0,"And females of just about every species are looking for something different."],[219,150.19,4.38,"Cattail flash-train firefly females, for example, prefer faster flickers,"],[219,154.57,4.55,"while ignited firefly females select for longer light pulses."],[219,159.57,1.33,"Compared to males,"],[219,160.91,4.5,"female fireflies tend to keep their bioluminescent signals pretty simple."],[219,165.49,3.75,"Generally, an interested female will point her lantern at a chosen male"],[219,169.25,2.84,"and respond to his flashes, drawing him in."],[219,172.17,4.67,"Successful matches may mate for hours, attached at the abdomen."],[219,176.92,2.42,"But things don’t always go so smoothly,"],[219,179.63,2.88,"which takes us back to that conspicuous lunge."],[219,182.68,3.0,"Many fireflies don't even eat as adults,"],[219,185.68,5.09,"instead channeling all the energy they stored as larvae into finding mates."],[219,191.02,3.84,"But that's not the case for the females of one genus of fireflies."],[219,195.07,2.04,"They are called femme fatales,"],[219,197.11,4.88,"and they're predators that hunt the adult males of other firefly species."],[219,202.24,1.67,"Despite being much larger,"],[219,203.95,3.79,"femme fatales lure males by mimicking the unique flashing patterns"],[219,207.75,2.42,"the females of their species give off."],[219,210.33,3.75,"Then, instead of mating, they attack and eat them."],[219,214.34,3.59,"But femme fatales are after more than just nutritious snacks:"],[219,218.05,2.59,"they lack their own natural toxins"],[219,220.63,4.55,"and are able to sequester those of other species when they consume them."],[219,225.3,4.21,"And this similarly helps make them unappealing to would-be predators."],[219,229.77,4.5,"But the males they aim to prey on seem to also have defense strategies."],[219,234.4,3.42,"One research team observed that males often approached femme fatales"],[219,237.82,4.04,"with caution and dodged attacks by dropping several centimeters."],[219,242.03,4.71,"In fact, they estimated that less than 10% of femme fatale hunts are successful."],[219,247.16,3.96,"Interestingly, this isn't the only time firefly flashes are channeled"],[219,251.12,2.42,"towards more deceptive means."],[219,253.79,4.8,"Certain Chinese orb-weaver spiders have been observed to wrap fireflies"],[219,258.59,1.17,"in their webs,"],[219,259.75,4.05,"keeping them alive so they attract others to the sticky trap."],[219,264.26,5.09,"The spiders even seem to make captured males adopt female flashing patterns"],[219,269.35,3.46,"in order to bait other males on the search for a mate."],[219,273.23,1.75,"So, as a male firefly,"],[219,275.02,4.04,"while the right bright signal could mean the hard-fought promise of progeny,"],[219,279.19,4.34,"it's best to beware of the treacherous tricks light can play."],[220,0.0,3.42,"Fourteen billion dollars."],[220,3.68,2.72,"That’s the incomprehensible amount of money"],[220,6.4,4.09,"that was spent on just federal political campaigns in 2024."],[220,10.88,2.96,"And generally with each passing election cycle,"],[220,13.84,1.97,"that number only keeps growing."],[220,16.37,2.46,"But get this – most Americans"],[220,18.83,2.07,"don’t donate to political campaigns."],[220,20.91,4.21,"And a whole chunk of the billions thrown around in the last election"],[220,25.12,1.76,"were donated by…"],[220,26.88,1.92,"well, we don’t exactly know."],[220,28.8,2.64,"So where’s all this money really coming from?"],[220,31.44,1.92,"And where is it actually going?"],[220,33.36,2.88,"Hi, I’m Kousha Navidar, this is Crash Course,"],[220,36.24,3.76,"and we’re answering Big Questions on U.S. Government."],[220,44.04,3.24,"Fourteen billion dollars is a ton of money."],[220,47.28,2.72,"And some of it comes from pretty obvious sources,"],[220,50.0,3.77,"like spending by the Democratic and Republican National Committees."],[220,54.23,3.19,"But the rest of it has to come from a bunch of different places."],[220,57.68,1.5,"Now, the last time I checked,"],[220,59.18,1.54,"there’s no mention of"],[220,60.72,3.12,"campaign fundraising in the Constitution."],[220,63.84,1.0,"Wait, hold on."],[220,67.74,2.26,"[mumbling] We the people…"],[220,70.0,1.58,"Yep, still true!"],[220,72.4,2.08,"In fact, when the thing was first written,"],[220,74.48,3.68,"the idea of a politician asking regular folks for money"],[220,78.16,1.71,"was seen as peak cringe."],[220,80.0,3.51,"It was okay to hand out free whiskey to voters, though."],[220,83.92,0.73,"It was a different time."],[220,85.0,3.8,"By the mid-1800s, campaign fundraising started catching on."],[220,88.8,2.96,"And pretty soon, money from wealthy donors and businesses"],[220,91.76,2.88,"had become big enough of a deal that over time,"],[220,94.64,3.52,"lawmakers began to implement some actual rules around it."],[220,98.16,3.2,"Today, the way you or I might be the most familiar with"],[220,101.36,5.2,"financially supporting a campaign is through campaign contributions—"],[220,106.56,3.36,"those are direct donations to a candidate."],[220,109.92,1.37,"These can come from"],[220,111.29,2.53,"individual donors, like you and I,"],[220,113.82,2.26,"or by Political Action Committees,"],[220,116.08,2.0,"also known as PACS."],[220,118.41,1.78,"But in 1971,"],[220,120.2,1.95,"the Federal Election Campaign Act"],[220,122.15,2.49,"laid out requirements for campaigns"],[220,124.64,2.24,"to report all their donations,"],[220,126.88,2.9,"and an amendment implemented in 1974"],[220,129.78,3.38,"introduced strict caps on how much"],[220,133.16,3.8,"and how often someone can give to individual candidates"],[220,136.96,2.8,"and their campaigns per election cycle."],[220,139.76,4.88,"Like, did you know campaigns can’t take more than $50 cash"],[220,144.64,1.92,"if it’s given anonymously?"],[220,146.56,6.72,"And a single person can’t give more than $3500 to a campaign each election?"],[220,153.28,5.28,"Meanwhile, for PACS, contributions can’t surpass $5,000."],[220,158.56,4.24,"Plus, each state can set its own limits for how much you can contribute"],[220,162.8,1.84,"in their state-wide elections."],[220,165.36,1.68,"There are a lot of rules,"],[220,167.04,2.09,"and when it comes to federal elections"],[220,169.13,2.31,"it’s up to the Federal Election Commission,"],[220,171.44,2.8,"or FEC, to make sure they get followed."],[220,174.24,1.96,"So in 2024,"],[220,176.2,4.04,"out of that fourteen billion dollars spent on federal campaigns,"],[220,180.24,3.88,"campaign contributions only made up about 4.7 billion."],[220,184.48,3.76,"But there’s a whole other way to spend money on campaigns:"],[220,188.24,1.48,"independent spending."],[220,189.92,4.72,"That’s where, instead of giving directly to a campaign or party,"],[220,194.64,5.6,"donors spend as much of their own money as they’d like to boost advertising,"],[220,200.24,5.44,"organize rallies, or even help fund outside groups in support of a candidate–"],[220,205.68,2.7,"and those FEC rules don’t apply."],[220,208.97,1.11,"Now, it might seem like"],[220,210.08,3.44,"a sort of shifty loophole in the system."],[220,213.52,3.36,"But the fact is, it got the OK from the Supreme Court."],[220,216.88,3.68,"In the 2010 case Citizens United v. FEC,"],[220,220.56,4.48,"the court ruled th at independent spending by corporations, unions,"],[220,225.04,3.28,"and nonprofits counts as free speech."],[220,228.32,4.24,"And later that same year, Speechnow.org v. FEC"],[220,232.56,3.68,"built on that decision to include individuals, too."],[220,236.24,3.28,"So really, the only limits to how much you can spend"],[220,239.52,3.28,"to influence an election is how deep your pockets are."],[220,242.8,2.72,"And this decision had huge consequences."],[220,245.52,4.24,"Since 2010, independent spending has become a powerful,"],[220,249.76,3.84,"quickly growing way of putting your money into your politics–"],[220,253.6,3.12,"especially for corporations and nonprofits."],[220,256.72,3.01,"It led to the creation of new organizations"],[220,259.73,2.75,"whose express purpose is to inject"],[220,262.48,3.84,"massive amounts of independent spending money into elections."],[220,266.32,2.29,"And it’s paved the way for corporations"],[220,268.61,2.35,"and the very-wealthy to have more influence"],[220,270.96,1.52,"than ever on elections—"],[220,272.48,2.88,"while simultaneously getting around the rules"],[220,275.36,2.56,"that were supposed to keep donations transparent."],[220,277.92,3.76,"The most common independent spending organizations are Super PACs,"],[220,281.68,3.2,"also known as independent expenditure-only committees."],[220,284.88,3.76,"These are organizations like Make America Great Again Inc;"],[220,288.64,1.36,"WinSenatePAC;"],[220,290.0,3.68,"Democracy PAC, created and funded by billionaire George Soros;"],[220,293.68,4.0,"and America PAC, funded by trillionaire Elon Musk."],[220,297.68,3.52,"Super PACS aren’t allowed to collaborate with the candidates they like"],[220,301.2,1.92,"or directly contribute to them –"],[220,303.12,2.87,"because then that would be a campaign contribution"],[220,305.99,2.0,"and have to follow the FEC rules—"],[220,308.4,4.96,"and Super Pacs do also report who their donors are to the FEC."],[220,313.36,4.24,"But there’s no limit to how much a Super PAC can raise and spend."],[220,317.6,6.32,"In fact, about 20% of total spending in the 2024 election came from Super PACs."],[220,323.92,3.6,"But hold on to your ballots, cause we’re not done here yet."],[220,327.52,1.26,"There’s yet another,"],[220,328.78,1.92,"[scary music builds] much more secretive way"],[220,330.7,1.84,"[scary music builds] that billions of dollars"],[220,332.54,3.2,"[scary music builds] get poured into elections:"],[220,335.74,3.54,"[music cuts abruptly] 501c4s and 501c6s."],[220,339.28,1.44,"That was anticlimactic."],[220,340.72,2.64,"501c4s and 501c6s"],[220,343.36,3.44,"are another type of independent spending organization."],[220,346.8,3.52,"Basically, they’re politically active nonprofits,"],[220,350.32,1.76,"like Heritage Action,"],[220,352.08,2.24,"the Planned Parenthood Action Fund,"],[220,354.32,1.6,"or the NRA,"],[220,355.92,3.68,"with no limits on how much they can accept from a single donor"],[220,359.6,3.12,"and no requirement to reveal who gave it—"],[220,362.72,4.24,"which is why you also might hear them called “dark money” groups."],[220,366.96,3.68,"Much like Super PACS, groups like these aren’t allowed"],[220,370.64,3.2,"to coordinate directly with campaigns,"],[220,373.84,4.16,"but they still spent almost two billion on the 2024 election."],[220,378.0,4.32,"And while there are some suggested restrictions on dark money groups,"],[220,382.32,3.2,"like they aren’t supposed to spend more than half their budget"],[220,385.52,1.84,"on political advocacy,"],[220,387.36,4.8,"the reality is they can get around rules like that by funding issue ads –"],[220,392.16,4.16,"which are meant to influence voters by advocating around certain issues"],[220,396.32,3.6,"that happen to align with certain candidates."],[220,399.92,3.52,"And since there’s no way of knowing where the money’s coming from,"],[220,403.44,2.8,"it can be hard for viewers to decipher"],[220,406.24,4.72,"the perspective or motive behind the information in the ad itself."],[220,410.96,4.4,"So, independent spending by Super PACS and dark money groups are responsible"],[220,415.36,3.04,"for billions of dollars of election spending."],[220,418.4,3.87,"That’s a whole lot of money– and where is it actually going?"],[220,422.56,1.52,"Well, like I mentioned earlier,"],[220,424.08,3.12,"all that Super PAC and dark money group cash"],[220,427.2,4.48,"can’t go directly to candidates themselves, so that all gets funnelled into"],[220,431.68,3.52,"independent issue ads and political outreach."],[220,435.2,4.16,"But we also need to look into where exactly all those"],[220,439.36,3.2,"campaign contributions are going, too."],[220,442.56,1.92,"To find out, let’s focus on"],[220,444.48,2.88,"congressional races with our good friend,"],[220,447.36,2.94,"Congressperson John J. Johnson."],[220,450.3,1.46,"[JOHN J JOHNSON] Yes, sorry,"],[220,451.76,2.48,"I am back to back for the next 4 years."],[220,454.24,3.1,"[KOUSHA] Just give him a quick minute."],[220,457.34,2.5,"[JOHN J JOHNSON] Ok, thanks, Kousha."],[220,459.84,2.96,"You look very handsome. They let you sleep, don’t they??"],[220,462.8,4.08,"Now, when it comes to spending on congressional races,"],[220,466.88,4.56,"not every candidate –or every race– attracts the same amount of funding."],[220,471.44,2.4,"The more competitive a race is,"],[220,473.83,3.04,"the more dollars are generally gonna get spent."],[220,476.88,3.44,"Which makes sense—in a tight race, your money actually stands a chance"],[220,480.32,1.44,"of swaying the outcome."],[220,481.76,2.0,"It can feel like a good investment."],[220,483.76,3.28,"So you won’t find Democrats pumping millions of dollars into"],[220,487.04,3.12,"a Congressional race in deeply Republican Idaho,"],[220,490.16,2.72,"where the money’s unlikely to help them win."],[220,492.88,2.42,"And when it comes to individual candidates,"],[220,495.3,2.06,"thanks to the incumbency advantage,"],[220,497.36,4.08,"folks who are running for reelection tend to be able to fundraise more"],[220,501.44,5.12,"due to name recognition and connections that come with having already done the job."],[220,506.56,2.96,"Which helps explain why a whopping 90%"],[220,509.52,4.16,"of Congresspeople like me who run for reelection win."],[220,513.68,3.28,"But once a campaign’s got those benjys in the bank,"],[220,516.96,1.2,"what do they do with them?"],[220,518.16,4.72,"Well, a decent chunk of it goes toward keeping the campaign-machine chugging along:"],[220,522.88,3.6,"paying for our staff, our office space, supplies."],[220,526.48,3.36,"Sending you desperate texts at all hours of the day and night."],[220,529.84,1.28,"You know, stuff like that."],[220,531.12,6.56,"And because elections in the US last a really long time,"],[220,537.68,2.64,"this can end up being a pretty significant amount."],[220,540.32,2.17,"Some of it even gets spent on raising"],[220,542.5,2.96,"more money to help support the campaign marathon."],[220,546.53,1.71,"But far and away,"],[220,548.24,4.0,"the biggest chunk of cash gets spent on ads telling voters"],[220,552.24,3.52,"why I’m the best choice— or why my opponent stinks."],[220,555.76,2.39,"All in all, it’s estimated that you need"],[220,558.15,2.73,"a campaign chest of at least one million dolllars"],[220,560.88,1.75,"to win a Congressional race–"],[220,562.62,1.36,"[phone buzzing] totally separate from"],[220,563.99,1.68,"[phone buzzing] independent spending from"],[220,565.66,1.12,"[phone buzzing] Super PACS and"],[220,566.78,1.28,"[phone buzzing] Dark Money Groups."],[220,568.07,1.77,"[phone buzzing] Sorry, I gotta take this."],[220,569.84,1.52,"Go for Johnson!"],[220,571.36,2.68,"[KOUSHA] Aaaaand he’s gone."],[220,574.08,2.64,"So we know where campaign money goes,"],[220,576.72,2.4,"but you have to wonder what’s actually in it"],[220,579.12,4.0,"for those billionaires, corporations, and special interest groups—"],[220,583.12,3.6,"these folks throwing millions of bucks at these campaigns."],[220,586.72,4.88,"And are their reasons for donating different from regular voters like you and me,"],[220,591.6,3.2,"donating spare change toward a campaign we believe in?"],[220,594.8,4.0,"It’s interesting: when regular people like you and me finally say,"],[220,598.8,3.04,"“you know what, I will put some of my hard-earned money toward this"],[220,601.84,2.24,"Congressperson who keeps texting me,”"],[220,604.08,1.76,"it’s usually for the reasons you think:"],[220,605.84,3.28,"we like that candidate’s vibe and want to help them win."],[220,609.12,4.28,"Although sometimes there’s some negative partisanship behind these donations, too"],[220,613.4,3.0,"— a strong hatred for the opposing party,"],[220,616.4,4.8,"motivating people to support anybody running against “the bad guy.”"],[220,621.2,4.08,"And as politics become increasingly nationalized,"],[220,625.28,2.96,"some folks might turn to surrogate representation,"],[220,628.24,3.92,"where they donate to a candidate who isn’t even running in their area."],[220,632.16,3.92,"Like when progressives in Missouri give to Bernie Sanders in Vermont,"],[220,636.08,4.4,"or conservatives in California give to Ted Cruz in Texas–"],[220,640.48,1.44,"especially when they feel like"],[220,641.92,2.56,"the options in their own state don’t represent them."],[220,644.48,1.28,"You can think of it as saying,"],[220,645.76,2.64,"“if you can’t have my vote, at least have my money.”"],[220,648.4,3.76,"And surrogate representation isn’t just for the little guys;"],[220,652.16,4.08,"sometimes those high-level spenders will also drop the big bucks"],[220,656.24,3.04,"in areas where they aren’t actually based."],[220,659.28,2.16,"But when it comes to big money donors"],[220,661.44,3.36,"who actually drive the election fundraising machine,"],[220,664.8,4.72,"the theoretical motivations are a little different, and a little murkier."],[220,669.52,5.44,"One possibility is that these big funders are essentially buying a candidate’s support"],[220,674.96,1.36,"once they’re in office."],[220,676.32,5.44,"One theory proposes that these big donations are basically a legal bribe."],[220,681.76,2.8,"I fund you, you get elected,"],[220,684.56,4.24,"you vote like I want you to on issues that matter to me,"],[220,688.8,2.64,"my corporation, or my special interest."],[220,691.44,5.44,"Or, in the case of Elon Musk recently, gift me my own government task force."],[220,696.88,1.84,"It sounds believable, right?"],[220,698.72,3.36,"But when political scientists have actually researched the issue,"],[220,702.08,4.32,"they haven’t found strong evidence that donations actually translate directly"],[220,706.4,3.28,"to a “yea” or “nay” on key legislation–"],[220,709.68,1.68,"although they do seem to influence"],[220,711.36,2.96,"which bills make it onto the agenda in the first place."],[220,714.32,4.72,"So maybe big donors aren’t after buying votes, per se–"],[220,719.04,3.92,"it’s more a matter of getting a vote to happen in the first place."],[220,722.96,2.48,"Putting money toward like-minded candidates"],[220,725.44,3.12,"is also a way to make connections around"],[220,728.56,2.8,"issues a donor and a candidate have in common–"],[220,731.36,4.56,"like the Planned Parenthood Action Fund supporting a pro-choice candidate."],[220,735.92,4.0,"It’s a little like buying someone a coffee at a networking event."],[220,739.92,4.56,"So when the NRA donates to a candidate who opposes gun control,"],[220,744.48,4.4,"it’s possible that the organization isn’t really “buying” the candidate’s support,"],[220,748.88,3.04,"but instead making important political connections"],[220,751.92,2.96,"and backing somebody who already shares their views,"],[220,754.88,3.92,"who’ll be more likely to support their organization’s interests."],[220,758.8,3.92,"And once those connections are made, it makes sense for an organization"],[220,762.72,3.52,"to continue to fund that candidate– so when the time comes"],[220,766.24,2.24,"and that successfully elected lawmaker"],[220,768.48,2.56,"needs to craft some new policies,"],[220,771.04,2.56,"they know where to turn for advice."],[220,773.6,2.08,"All of these theories can be great things"],[220,775.68,2.16,"for the large donors themselves–"],[220,777.84,3.92,"but they’re not necessarily in the best interest of the people."],[220,781.76,2.08,"Even though that’s the thing elections"],[220,783.84,2.77,"are supposed to be about in the first place, right?"],[220,787.62,1.55,"It does that."],[220,789.17,1.63,"Why can’t you just work??"],[220,792.88,3.68,"The boatloads of money it takes to win an election these days"],[220,796.56,2.0,"is a pretty new phenomenon."],[220,798.56,4.24,"But it’s not all doom and gloom for us regular people."],[220,802.8,1.36,"Money is important,"],[220,804.16,3.04,"but it’s not the only thing that makes an election go round."],[220,807.2,4.56,"And while people like you and I may not have the big bucks,"],[220,811.76,3.68,"we have two other things that are absolutely crucial"],[220,815.44,3.44,"when it comes to counterbalancing the advantage all those dollars"],[220,818.88,4.4,"can give a candidate: our voices and our votes."],[220,823.28,2.16,"So, why don’t more people use them?"],[220,825.44,1.92,"We’ll get into that next time on"],[220,827.36,3.28,"Crash Course: Big Questions on US Government."],[220,830.64,3.28,"This episode of Crash Course Big Questions on U.S. Government"],[220,833.92,2.72,"was produced in partnership with USAFacts,"],[220,836.64,2.16,"a non-partisan civic tech project"],[220,838.8,2.96,"that makes government data easier to understand."],[220,841.76,2.72,"For more resources that go along with these videos,"],[220,844.48,3.68,"go to usafacts.org/crashcourse."],[220,848.16,4.0,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Big Questions on U.S. Government,"],[220,852.16,2.16,"which was filmed in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[220,854.32,2.96,"and was made with the help of all these nice people."],[220,857.28,4.32,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[220,861.6,2.37,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[221,0.0,2.54,"Formula One drivers race sitting down."],[221,2.54,1.88,"They’re driving after all."],[221,4.42,3.0,"So why do they have to work out like this?"],[221,8.13,2.75,"Driving an F1 car requires incredible cardio"],[221,10.88,3.04,"ability, stamina, and a shocking amount of strength."],[221,14.43,3.5,"Every driver would qualify for the National Hockey League."],[221,17.93,2.96,"They would all qualify for the Apollo space program, which is pretty amazing."],[221,20.94,1.13,"A lot of people assume"],[221,22.06,3.0,"that winning an F1 race is just about having the fastest car,"],[221,26.03,3.46,"but the physics of driving an F1 car put massive demands"],[221,29.49,4.37,"on the human body. Every lap the car is trying to pull their head sideways,"],[221,33.86,5.3,"slam them forward, and cook them inside a cockpit that can be over 130°F."],[221,39.16,3.38,"If you or I did it, it would be life threatening, as in"],[221,42.54,3.54,"we would probably pass out, faint or and have a crash."],[221,46.21,4.3,"If the driver can't withstand those forces for an entire race, they just can't win."],[221,50.51,2.38,"So I wanted to see what F1 drivers"],[221,52.89,3.75,"do to train their bodies to withstand those kinds of physics."],[221,60.98,1.42,"Since F1 is such an"],[221,62.4,4.09,"international sport, there aren't many specialized trainers in the US."],[221,66.48,2.04,"So with the help of Pete McKnight, an"],[221,68.53,3.84,"F1 performance expert currently based in the United Arab Emirates,"],[221,72.36,2.13,"I'll be working out with David Dunlop."],[221,74.49,4.59,"David works with NFL players and told me that the training regimen for F1 drivers"],[221,79.08,3.0,"isn't actually that different than what football players do."],[221,82.25,2.75,"With Formula one training, we're still looking to get them"],[221,85.0,2.13,"generally physically prepped the same way we would"],[221,87.13,3.29,"with most other athletes. I can train an F1 driver."],[221,90.51,3.84,"I can train an NFL player, and 80% of the things can be the same."],[221,94.34,5.25,"Formula One drivers are strong guys because they have to work their full body"],[221,99.6,4.5,"to be, you know, generally athletic. But what really sets F1 drivers"],[221,104.1,3.0,"apart from other athletes is their neck strength."],[221,107.15,3.63,"An F1 car is under pressure that you've never experienced while just"],[221,110.78,0.75,"driving around the block."],[221,112.53,1.08,"They're driving around"],[221,113.61,3.09,"really tight corners at really high speeds."],[221,116.87,1.96,"Let's take turn one at Suzuka,"],[221,118.83,1.58,"the F1 course in Japan."],[221,120.41,3.5,"When an F1 car rounds that turn, it generates"],[221,123.92,3.42,"centrifugal force, pushing sideways and outwards."],[221,127.38,0.92,"On this turn,"],[221,128.29,1.54,"drivers are experiencing"],[221,129.84,4.17,"5 G's of force, meaning five times the normal force of gravity."],[221,134.01,5.38,"So a driver's head, which weighs an average of 11 pounds, feels like it weighs 55."],[221,139.39,4.3,"So as you go around the corner you need to work against the"],[221,143.73,4.46,"force that is kind trying to throw your head and neck out outwards."],[221,148.19,2.09,"And the importance is you need to keep your head"],[221,150.28,3.34,"in the neutral position because by moving your head, it changes"],[221,153.61,3.09,"your vision and your line of sight, which changes your racing line."],[221,156.7,3.75,"And that could impact their speed or even cause them to crash."],[221,161.58,0.54,"Just resist."],[221,162.12,2.29,"Resist. Keep fighting. Keep fighting. Keep fighting."],[221,164.41,2.67,"We usually don't isolate the neck that much. Even in football."],[221,167.08,2.13,"That's because we can usually get the traps"],[221,169.21,3.34,"and the other neck muscles by doing heavy compound movements like deadlifts,"],[221,172.55,2.5,"shoulder movements overhead, any sort of thing like that."],[221,175.05,3.04,"When it comes to F1, we're trying to keep their relative body"],[221,178.14,3.09,"size small so that they can fit in the car and way as little as possible,"],[221,181.22,1.54,"while still giving them the best chance"],[221,182.76,3.38,"for their body to withstand the G forces that are common in F1."],[221,186.14,3.34,"For context, I'm struggling to lift 10 pounds here."],[221,189.86,2.38,"An F1 driver can do a lot more than that."],[221,192.23,3.21,"On average, these guys can lift about 90 pounds."],[221,195.44,1.96,"and these guys are not big guys."],[221,197.41,3.75,"They're like five foot seven to maximum six foot."],[221,201.2,1.96,"If you're bigger than that, you can't fit in the car."],[221,203.16,3.34,"They weigh about 170 pounds, but they've got the"],[221,207.21,4.75,"the neck strength of like a rugby prop forward or a lineman in football."],[221,211.96,2.13,"Building the strength to withstand"],[221,214.09,3.8,"5 G's of force is just one part of racing. To survive in the cockpit"],[221,217.93,2.84,"drivers need cardiovascular endurance, as well."],[221,220.76,4.59,"In general, a driver's training would be made up of two thirds"],[221,225.39,3.55,"cardiovascular training and one third strength training, roughly."],[221,228.94,4.3,"Despite the fact that you sat there in a seat, kind of strapped in"],[221,233.44,2.84,"your heart rate is elevated for the whole duration of the race."],[221,236.28,4.46,"And the race lasts between 90 minutes and two hours."],[221,240.95,3.54,"They end up burning about 1500 calories in a single race."],[221,244.49,3.42,"That's roughly the equivalent of what you burn while running a half marathon."],[221,247.91,2.34,"And that comes from a few different areas."],[221,250.25,5.25,"It comes from the the danger of the sport, that cognitive demand"],[221,255.55,3.71,"of having to be zoned in and concentrating the whole time"],[221,259.26,1.67,"because you're driving super quick"],[221,260.93,3.88,"and there's 21 other cars on the track, and there's a high level of jeopardy."],[221,264.81,1.67,"If something goes wrong, you're in trouble."],[221,266.47,4.21,"And while this mental strain builds, their body is also heating up."],[221,270.69,3.75,"Our data indicates that probably the primary stressor placed on"],[221,274.44,0.96,"drivers is heat."],[221,275.4,3.59,"So when drivers race, they wear a fire protective suit,"],[221,278.99,3.0,"which is great because it protects them in the event of a fire,"],[221,281.99,4.17,"but it is so insulating that it actually inhibits their ability"],[221,286.16,3.13,"to cool themselves through the process of evaporation."],[221,289.41,3.54,"Which means once they're inside a cockpit, which can be anywhere"],[221,292.96,3.53,"from 100 to 130°F, they get very hot."],[221,296.49,5.19,"Drivers are able to withstand these temperatures because of the cardio exercise"],[221,301.68,0.79,"they do."],[221,302.47,5.92,"When you are in the heat or hot and you're exercising, your heart rate will go up."],[221,308.39,3.92,"So it's taking blood from your core that is hot and putting it"],[221,312.31,4.34,"into your extremities like your arms, your skin to help cool yourself."],[221,316.73,3.92,"One way of measuring cardio fitness is called VO2 max,"],[221,320.65,4.0,"which refers to the amount of oxygen your body can use during exercise."],[221,324.66,5.09,"The higher your VO2 max, the more efficiently your heart, lungs, and muscles work."],[221,329.79,4.83,"So if you have a higher VO2 max, you will also have a higher heat tolerance."],[221,334.62,3.63,"So you can cool yourself and you can do the work to drive the car."],[221,338.25,3.09,"So that's why most professional race car"],[221,341.34,3.04,"drivers have a VO2 max somewhere between"],[221,344.38,4.88,"60 and 72mL/kg/min which is very similar"],[221,349.26,3.92,"to elite level cyclists, runners."],[221,353.19,3.84,"It's getting borderline Olympic athlete level, which is very cool."],[221,357.02,4.21,"And then right before a race, F1 drivers practice their reflexes to be able"],[221,361.24,4.17,"to make those split second decisions while driving over 160mph."],[221,365.7,4.09,"So I'm not going to give you any sort of heads up here."],[221,370.58,3.54,"So I have to imagine that it's 130 degrees,"],[221,374.12,3.04,"I've been driving for two hours, and I have to be able to react this quick."],[221,377.21,1.88,"Yeah, exactly. Okay, great."],[221,382.84,1.71,"I didn't crash the car, I think."],[221,384.8,2.42,"This video is presented by the Ford Motor Company."],[221,387.22,5.34,"Ford has been studying the brutal physics of F1 for decades, from 1967 to 2004."],[221,392.6,5.59,"The Ford-Cosworth DFV engine won 176 races, and every one of those victories"],[221,398.19,3.96,"led to insights that were implemented directly into the cars everyday drivers"],[221,402.15,0.75,"take on the road."],[221,402.9,3.92,"After a 22 year break, Ford is back in F1, because they know true"],[221,406.82,3.09,"capability is proven in the extremes. To build better"],[221,409.91,3.84,"cars, Ford tests them on the most demanding circuit in the world."],[221,413.75,1.33,"Check the link in the description"],[221,415.08,3.37,"to learn more about Ford's latest lineup of vehicles."],[221,418.49,2.64,"Now, Ford didn't have any editorial input on the content of this video,"],[221,421.13,2.71,"but their support does make videos like this possible."],[221,424.09,1.38,"Race day impacts"],[221,425.47,3.88,"drivers in ways even the best training can't prepare them for."],[221,429.55,6.13,"Normally, they will lose somewhere between 4 and 6 pounds of sweat."],[221,435.69,4.13,"In some cases that's over 2 to 3% your body mass."],[221,440.06,2.96,"The American College of Sports Medicine says if you've lost that much,"],[221,443.02,2.42,"you're in trouble. And if that sounds dangerous,"],[221,445.44,4.21,"it is. As dehydration builds, cognitive performance can decline."],[221,449.66,3.34,"As you get dehydrated, you're losing"],[221,452.99,3.21,"electrolytes, you're losing blood volume."],[221,456.2,4.0,"And that will reduce blood flow to the brain as your core temperature"],[221,460.21,4.59,"heats up, your ability to respond correctly and fastly is compromised."],[221,465.05,4.21,"This is why we say that heat is the greatest threat to drivers."],[221,469.26,3.38,"They're just drained. You get out of the car and you are empty."],[221,472.68,1.38,"You've got nothing left to give."],[221,474.06,3.59,"The overall exhaustion is something it's hard to quantify."],[221,477.64,1.58,"But you've got to remember,"],[221,479.23,3.5,"these guys have, they've been driving like 10 or 15 years."],[221,482.73,2.33,"They're very well conditioned to the heat."],[221,485.07,2.75,"They've been driving in hot countries and hot conditions."],[221,487.82,1.38,"Although there have been occasions"],[221,489.2,4.0,"where they've got out of the car and they've collapsed, we haven't recorded"],[221,493.2,3.17,"any instances of people passing out behind the wheel and there"],[221,496.37,1.07,"being a bad crash because of that."],[221,497.44,2.02,"F1 can look effortless."],[221,499.54,4.71,"Fast cars, champagne, and races are some of the most glamorous events in the world."],[221,504.25,3.13,"But underneath that is a constant fight against physics."],[221,507.38,5.42,"Every lap demands strength, endurance, focus, and the ability to do all of that"],[221,512.85,4.49,"while your body is literally being pulled in multiple directions at once."],[221,517.37,0.5,"In a way,"],[221,517.93,3.71,"an F1 drivers job is keeping the laws of physics from winning."],[221,521.65,4.09,"I think there's a reason why there are only 20 Formula One drivers in the world,"],[221,525.77,3.17,"because there's probably only that number that can actually do it."],[221,534.83,1.25,"Thanks so much for watching."],[221,536.08,1.25,"If you're not already a member."],[221,537.33,3.84,"Head over to Patreon.com/Vox to get access to more stories."],[221,541.29,3.63,"Stay updated on our work and help support ambitious reporting."],[221,544.92,2.63,"It's so important to support independent journalism."],[221,547.55,3.34,"Your membership allows us to make really cool videos, research"],[221,550.88,3.17,"interesting stories, and also dig into the investigative reporting."],[221,554.18,2.25,"And if it doesn't make financial sense for you to support us"],[221,556.43,2.71,"today, you can still follow us on Patreon for free"],[221,559.14,3.63,"and stay up to date on all of the great reporting going on at Vox."],[221,562.77,0.5,"Thanks!"],[222,4.13,3.27,"The internet is an absolute miracle."],[222,7.87,2.07,"And for the last 30 years,"],[222,9.94,3.24,"it has been driven by [a] very consistent"],[222,13.21,1.94,"and fundamental business model"],[222,15.15,3.37,"that has given us everything amazing about the internet"],[222,18.55,2.67,"and everything not so amazing as well."],[222,21.25,3.87,"I run a company that provides a lot of the internet's infrastructure"],[222,25.16,2.3,"and as a result, means three things."],[222,27.49,6.34,"One, we've had this unique view into what is going on online."],[222,34.6,4.74,"Two, we care enormously about the success of the internet."],[222,39.7,2.47,"And three, I can share with you"],[222,42.21,3.14,"some of how that underlying business model is changing"],[222,45.38,1.67,"and why it's important."],[222,47.08,2.37,"Because as that business model changes,"],[222,49.48,3.64,"it's going to have a dramatic impact on what the internet is,"],[222,53.15,3.5,"but beyond that, on what content is created,"],[222,56.65,1.94,"on what journalists get compensated for,"],[222,58.62,2.64,"on how researchers are able to do their research."],[222,61.29,1.47,"And so it's up to all of us"],[222,62.79,2.94,"as that business model of the internet changes,"],[222,65.76,3.24,"to make sure it changes for something we want"],[222,69.03,3.04,"and can learn from the mistakes that we had in the past."],[222,72.07,3.3,"So to review the past, let's look at what that business model was."],[222,75.41,3.57,"And we all know it basically, which was: the internet was built on"],[222,79.01,2.74,"we took an idea, and we put it online,"],[222,81.78,3.57,"and then we drove traffic to that idea."],[222,85.72,3.04,"And then from that traffic, we either sold things,"],[222,88.79,2.27,"subscriptions or ads."],[222,91.52,2.54,"And it's been pretty amazing to watch"],[222,94.06,4.0,"how that has very much driven the growth of the web"],[222,98.09,1.97,"and the growth of the internet over time."],[222,100.06,2.94,"And it survived a number of dramatic platform shifts."],[222,103.0,2.34,"We all started browsing the internet on browsers,"],[222,105.34,1.53,"on our desktop or laptop."],[222,106.9,2.9,"That transitioned to be increasingly social media,"],[222,109.84,3.14,"which was the interface through which we consumed content."],[222,113.44,2.84,"And then we went to consuming it through our mobile devices."],[222,116.28,2.04,"And yet, through all of this,"],[222,118.31,3.47,"the humble banner ad has basically financed it all."],[222,121.82,2.97,"Traffic has been the currency of the internet,"],[222,124.79,4.64,"and that's what everyone has built their content to try to achieve."],[222,129.46,2.64,"And if we want to look for who's the Superman,"],[222,132.1,3.14,"who's the superhero that has driven this"],[222,135.27,2.27,"for the last 27 years,"],[222,137.53,2.13,"that has been Google."],[222,139.7,4.04,"Google built not only the search engine to index how the internet works,"],[222,143.74,2.97,"but then they built the monetization system"],[222,146.71,4.34,"to actually ensure that content creators got paid"],[222,151.08,2.4,"for the traffic that they generated."],[222,153.52,3.7,"The internet owes a ton to Google in the last 27 years."],[222,157.72,1.3,"But the world is changing."],[222,159.06,2.54,"AI is the topic du jour,"],[222,161.62,2.3,"and what I think a lot of people don't understand"],[222,163.96,2.47,"is that AI is yet another platform shift."],[222,166.46,3.8,"Think about how much more of the way that you consume information already"],[222,170.3,1.77,"is looking at your AI chatbot,"],[222,172.1,3.3,"and how much more of that will be the case in the future."],[222,175.44,3.07,"We can see it in a lot of things we do kind of normally,"],[222,178.54,4.14,"like, this is a Google search from the early days of Google."],[222,182.71,1.17,"Not even that long ago,"],[222,183.91,3.44,"where I would type something in, like, when was the first TED conference?"],[222,187.38,2.94,"And then I would get back basically a treasure map"],[222,190.35,2.1,"where I would have to continue to do the work"],[222,192.49,2.04,"and click on the links, generating traffic"],[222,194.53,1.67,"to find what my answer was."],[222,196.23,3.27,"The search wasn't over when the results returned."],[222,199.53,3.2,"That was just the beginning, and you had to follow the treasure map."],[222,202.77,1.67,"That's what a search engine is."],[222,204.47,1.87,"Today we have something very different."],[222,206.34,3.3,"We have something that's closer to what I would call an answer engine,"],[222,209.67,2.5,"where you're getting the answer directly on the page."],[222,212.18,2.47,"And what's amazing about that is that as a consumer,"],[222,214.68,1.5,"it's better for you."],[222,216.21,2.1,"But what's terrifying about it"],[222,218.35,3.8,"is that it means traffic isn't going to the publishers' sites,"],[222,222.19,2.57,"and traffic is the currency of the internet."],[222,225.06,4.07,"And so 18 months ago, publishers called us and said, “Are you seeing this, too?”"],[222,229.13,1.77,"And I have to confess,"],[222,230.89,2.07,"we weren't really paying a ton of attention,"],[222,233.0,2.3,"but we went into our logs, we went into our data,"],[222,235.33,1.94,"we pulled out that information and said,"],[222,237.27,2.54,"“How much harder is it to get traffic"],[222,239.84,2.5,"based on the same amount of content today"],[222,242.37,2.1,"than it was with the Google of old?”"],[222,244.51,2.84,"And the answer is, it's 5.2 times harder"],[222,247.34,3.07,"to get traffic for the same amount of content that was out there."],[222,250.45,1.2,"But that's the good news."],[222,251.68,1.17,"That's with Google."],[222,252.88,1.94,"What is it with OpenAI?"],[222,254.85,1.67,"750 times harder."],[222,256.55,1.33,"What is it with Anthropic?"],[222,257.89,2.37,"32,000 times harder."],[222,260.66,1.97,"The answer engines are giving the answers"],[222,262.66,2.07,"and people aren't following the footnotes."],[222,264.76,1.53,"And this was 18 months ago."],[222,266.3,2.5,"What’s happened since, has it gotten better or worse?"],[222,268.8,3.74,"Do you trust AI more today than you did 18 months ago?"],[222,272.54,3.44,"The answer is most people do, and as a result,"],[222,276.01,3.97,"it's actually gotten twice as hard to get traffic to content that's there."],[222,280.01,2.0,"You're not following the footnotes anymore."],[222,282.05,3.44,"You're actually trusting that AI systems are giving you the right answer,"],[222,285.48,1.37,"and that's destroying media."],[222,286.85,2.24,"Across the board, we're seeing reported numbers"],[222,289.09,3.64,"... of between a 90 and 30 percent decline"],[222,292.72,1.64,"in the traffic to their sites,"],[222,294.39,2.57,"and this is extremely problematic"],[222,296.99,2.97,"because I think journalists deserve to be able to eat."],[222,301.8,3.67,"And fundamentally, if the internet is built on traffic"],[222,305.5,4.04,"and this new business model isn't delivering traffic,"],[222,309.54,2.57,"then I'm not sure how media is going to survive"],[222,312.14,2.7,"unless we create a new business model."],[222,314.88,3.4,"And the good news is the AI companies want journalism."],[222,318.31,2.0,"They want people out there doing research."],[222,320.32,1.4,"We all want journalism."],[222,321.72,1.94,"We want people out there doing research."],[222,323.69,3.2,"And so I think, I'm actually kind of optimistic"],[222,326.92,2.2,"that there might be a solution to this problem."],[222,329.13,1.74,"And here's the way I think about it."],[222,330.89,1.74,"For the first time in human history,"],[222,332.66,2.87,"we have a mathematical model of all of human knowledge."],[222,335.57,3.64,"It's not perfect, but that's essentially what all of the LLMs are."],[222,339.2,2.5,"And I think of it like a giant block of Swiss cheese."],[222,341.74,2.1,"There's a lot of cheese, that's what we know."],[222,343.87,2.17,"But we also have a lot of holes in the cheese."],[222,346.04,1.7,"Those are the things we don't know."],[222,347.78,1.57,"And the thing that is incredible"],[222,349.35,2.64,"is that what all of you want, what I want,"],[222,351.98,1.7,"what the AI companies want"],[222,353.68,3.97,"is they want people out there filling in the holes in the cheese."],[222,358.09,2.97,"They don’t want you writing stories that already exist --"],[222,361.06,2.17,"they want you actually creating new knowledge."],[222,363.23,2.6,"And the AI companies, I've talked to every one of them,"],[222,365.83,3.74,"they're all willing to actually pay for people who are creating new knowledge,"],[222,369.57,2.87,"creating new ideas, ideas worth spreading."],[222,372.44,2.3,"That's what this new system would reward."],[222,374.77,5.41,"Compare that to what the business model of media is for the last 10 years,"],[222,380.18,2.77,"which is not filling in the holes in the cheese,"],[222,382.98,4.84,"but instead, basically, lighting the cheese on fire, right?"],[222,387.85,2.0,"Incendiary cheese, right?"],[222,389.89,3.1,"Yet another story about what happened at 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue,"],[222,393.02,2.0,"told in the most inflammatory way possible."],[222,395.06,1.8,"I've sat with media executives"],[222,396.89,4.67,"who brag about the fact that they create content for as little as possible,"],[222,401.6,3.9,"and then write headlines that create the largest cortisol response"],[222,405.54,1.9,"so that you will click on them."],[222,407.47,2.1,"That's not a world we should be celebrating."],[222,409.61,2.34,"That's not a world that's making the world better."],[222,411.98,2.1,"If, on the other hand, we actually celebrated"],[222,414.11,1.83,"who is making the cheese bigger,"],[222,415.98,1.17,"who's filling in that --"],[222,417.15,3.3,"Imagine in the future that you actually had AI companies"],[222,420.45,1.17,"that were measuring"],[222,421.65,3.17,"who actually advanced human knowledge mathematically"],[222,424.86,2.0,"over the course of the last year the most,"],[222,426.86,1.27,"and we gave them an award."],[222,428.12,1.83,"We could call it the Big Cheese award."],[222,431.03,1.17,"I'm not kidding."],[222,432.23,1.53,"Like, how amazing would that be?"],[222,433.8,2.77,"And you might be thinking, OK, Matthew, you're crazy."],[222,436.57,3.04,"Like, I'm the person who thinks AI is just destroying the world,"],[222,439.64,2.04,"and technology always makes things worse."],[222,441.67,3.07,"I'm going to share just two anecdotes that make me have some hope"],[222,444.77,1.77,"that this might actually be the case."],[222,446.54,1.44,"The first starts 23 years ago."],[222,448.01,2.97,"23 years ago, the music industry had a huge problem:"],[222,450.98,2.64,"Napster, Kazaa -- everyone was stealing music."],[222,453.65,3.6,"And then Steve Jobs stepped on stage, almost 23 years ago to this day,"],[222,457.25,2.44,"and he said, “We’re going to do something different."],[222,459.69,2.04,"We're going to charge 99 cents per song."],[222,461.76,2.5,"We're going to actually say that you should be paying"],[222,464.26,1.53,"for the music that you consume.”"],[222,465.8,2.07,"Now that’s not the business model that won,"],[222,467.9,3.37,"but it was the start, and it turned into what is today Spotify"],[222,471.3,2.1,"and all the other systems that are out there,"],[222,473.44,2.87,"which is basically $10 a month, all-you-can-eat music,"],[222,476.34,1.17,"and it's pretty amazing."],[222,477.54,6.11,"Last year, Spotify alone sent over $10 billion to music creators"],[222,483.65,1.53,"in revenue."],[222,485.68,4.21,"And again, that's in addition to titles, TikToks, YouTube,"],[222,489.89,2.37,"Apple Music, everybody else,"],[222,492.26,3.27,"more money is going to music creation than any time in human history."],[222,495.53,2.63,"So technology can be pie-expanding."],[222,498.8,1.84,"In fact, I thought it was so important"],[222,500.63,2.4,"that I flew up to Stockholm to meet with Daniel Ek,"],[222,503.07,3.24,"the founder of Spotify, and he said, \"You know, it's pretty amazing."],[222,506.3,3.84,"We know when someone goes to Spotify and they type in Taylor Swift,"],[222,510.17,1.24,"\"Shake It Off\""],[222,511.44,2.27,"that we can give them what they're looking for.\""],[222,513.74,2.04,"But every once in a while, someone comes in"],[222,515.78,1.37,"and types in something like,"],[222,517.18,3.34,"'I want a song about how much fun it is to dance disco with your cat.'"],[222,521.78,2.24,"And nobody's written that."],[222,524.05,1.97,"But they know that nobody's written that."],[222,526.06,2.64,"And so they actually go back and take those queries"],[222,528.73,2.1,"and then publish them back to music creators."],[222,530.86,3.44,"And this is the moment you're all going to think you're doing life wrong."],[222,534.3,1.83,"But there's a guy who lives in Denmark"],[222,536.17,1.6,"who makes €40 million a year"],[222,537.8,2.24,"writing songs for unfulfilled Spotify queries."],[222,542.11,2.1,"And I think that's beautiful."],[222,544.24,1.87,"That's filling the holes in the cheese."],[222,546.11,1.5,"Because when I look for a song,"],[222,547.64,1.8,"what I'm looking for is an emotion."],[222,549.48,3.17,"And this person is helping me find a song to resonate that way."],[222,552.65,2.7,"And I think that's a model for what could happen as well."],[222,555.38,2.44,"Another story starts a little bit closer to home."],[222,557.85,4.04,"So this is the Park Record newspaper."],[222,562.99,2.23,"The first place I ever saw my name in print --"],[222,565.23,2.24,"my hometown newspaper from Park City, Utah."],[222,567.46,1.33,"It was dying."],[222,568.8,2.77,"So three years ago, my wife and I bought it."],[222,571.6,3.44,"We did not buy it because we thought it was going to be a great business."],[222,575.07,2.74,"We bought it because we thought local news was important."],[222,577.81,1.77,"In fact, we had no financial KPIs --"],[222,579.58,3.9,"the only KPI we focused on was how much could we increase voter turnout."],[222,583.48,3.5,"And the answer was, we got it from the low 20 percent to over 60 percent"],[222,586.98,1.74,"in the last election in three years,"],[222,588.75,1.17,"which is amazing, right?"],[222,591.12,1.7,"Local news matters."],[222,592.86,1.74,"But here's the crazy part."],[222,595.23,1.77,"Terrible business, right?"],[222,597.36,1.43,"Except it's not."],[222,598.83,3.2,"We're likely going to make more in 2026"],[222,602.07,2.1,"off licensing our content to AI companies"],[222,604.2,2.44,"than we do off digital advertising. Why?"],[222,606.67,2.2,"Because if you're traveling to Park City, Utah,"],[222,608.87,2.5,"and you want to know what's the great new restaurant,"],[222,611.41,2.0,"what's the cool band that's coming to town,"],[222,613.41,1.6,"we're the only ones that have it."],[222,615.04,1.97,"So think about how amazing that would be."],[222,617.01,1.6,"Think about how if in the future,"],[222,618.65,2.77,"the business model of the new AI-driven internet"],[222,621.42,2.74,"actually rewards the media we're all craving,"],[222,624.19,2.64,"local, unique, original content."],[222,627.26,2.0,"That's what I'm super excited about."],[222,629.89,1.27,"But there are problems."],[222,631.56,1.24,"Challenges."],[222,632.83,2.24,"It's kind of like in the superhero films,"],[222,635.1,4.0,"where the hero of the last movie becomes the villain of the next."],[222,639.1,3.14,"And so our good friends at Google ..."],[222,642.27,1.17,"Ugh."],[222,644.77,1.3,"They are holding this back."],[222,646.11,3.0,"They believe that they have a God-given right"],[222,649.15,1.43,"to all of the content online."],[222,650.58,2.0,"They shouldn't have to pay for any of it."],[222,652.62,1.5,"And they have a huge advantage."],[222,654.12,1.7,"In fact, it's hard to believe this,"],[222,655.85,2.2,"but every other AI company was actually started"],[222,658.05,1.64,"to oppose Google, right?"],[222,659.69,1.57,"That’s what OpenAI started with."],[222,661.29,2.1,"Anthropic was just a spinoff of OpenAI, xAI."],[222,663.43,3.6,"All of this was all to say, we can't let Google win this entire game."],[222,667.03,1.83,"How much of an advantage do they have?"],[222,668.9,1.43,"Well we looked at this again,"],[222,670.37,3.14,"and we found that every single page that OpenAI sees,"],[222,673.54,1.74,"Google sees three."],[222,675.3,2.94,"Four in Microsoft’s case,"],[222,678.27,1.47,"five in Anthropic's case,"],[222,679.74,3.1,"21 or 22 in xAI's case."],[222,682.88,2.5,"An enormous advantage that Google has,"],[222,685.38,2.04,"and they want to hold on to that advantage."],[222,687.45,2.0,"In fact, they're playing incredibly dirty."],[222,689.45,1.7,"They're saying to content creators,"],[222,691.19,2.84,"\"You can't stop us from training on your content"],[222,694.06,2.34,"or we will take you out of search.\""],[222,697.36,1.2,"Horrible."],[222,698.6,2.07,"Superman, how far you’ve fallen!"],[222,702.9,2.67,"And so I was pretty depressed, I have to confess,"],[222,705.6,2.77,"thinking we were on the cusp of creating something amazing:"],[222,708.4,2.74,"a better internet."],[222,711.58,4.87,"And yet, I have to just realize that no AI company's top priority"],[222,716.48,1.6,"is paying content creators."],[222,718.65,1.64,"What's their top priority?"],[222,721.32,1.4,"Beating Google."],[222,723.15,1.7,"Which gave me an idea."],[222,725.65,1.9,"Kind of a wonderful idea."],[222,728.12,2.67,"An idea which is going on right now, and it's working."],[222,730.83,1.74,"Which is:"],[222,732.6,2.33,"don’t charge for all the content that’s out there,"],[222,734.97,3.8,"just charge the AI companies for the ability to catch Google."],[222,739.27,1.83,"And I don't think that's the end game,"],[222,741.14,2.23,"but I think it might be the iTunes moment."],[222,743.37,3.87,"It might be the point that shows us content is worth paying for."],[222,747.28,1.4,"Everyone should be out there,"],[222,748.68,2.87,"and we can create a better business model for the internet."],[222,751.55,3.54,"And so I'm hopeful that on this stage, not too long from now,"],[222,755.12,2.5,"I get to hand out one of the first Big Cheese awards,"],[222,757.62,1.3,"and I really appreciate it."],[222,758.96,1.2,"Thank you."],[223,0.0,2.64,"Pompeii is a story of survival."],[223,2.8,2.88,"<i>The Romans had a really</i> <i>active approach to religion.</i>"],[223,5.68,0.48,"<i>Mhmm.</i>"],[223,6.16,2.8,"<i>They believed you could</i> <i>appeal to the gods directly.</i>"],[223,8.96,2.72,"<i>‘Fighting’ can also mean you’re</i> <i>fighting against the envionment</i>"],[223,11.88,0.92,"<i>to get to something you need.</i>"],[223,13.2,3.2,"<i>Or you want to, maybe, rescue a loved one.</i>"],[223,16.4,3.08,"<i>The eruption released more</i> <i>thermal energy than atomic bombs</i>"],[223,19.48,3.4,"<i>dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, combined.</i>"],[223,22.88,0.84,"<i>Herculaneum.</i>"],[223,23.72,1.52,"<i>All those people are under threat.</i>"],[223,25.24,1.16,"<i>All those people need to be rescued.</i>"],[223,32.52,5.72,"I live, breathe, excavate, teach, in Rome."],[223,38.24,3.56,"So, I’m pleased to be able to spend a lot of time in Herculaneum -"],[223,41.8,2.76,"one of the best sites that you can explore"],[223,44.56,2.92,"to understand the daily life of the ancient Romans."],[223,47.48,2.36,"<i>You said this place was a beach</i> <i>resort, so where’s the sea?</i>"],[223,49.84,3.8,"<i>Well, we are on the beach,</i> <i>and the sea was right there.</i>"],[223,53.68,1.32,"<i>That’s the power of the eruption.</i>"],[223,55.0,4.24,"<i>It’s going to radically change the whole</i> <i>geography of the entire Bay of Naples.</i>"],[223,59.24,2.4,"People don’t know as much about Herculaneum..."],[223,61.64,0.8,"but they should!"],[223,62.72,3.88,"Because the preservation in Herculaneum, the neighboring city,"],[223,66.6,4.56,"has to do with the nature of the pyroclastic material that covered the site."],[223,71.56,4.32,"It was more like a hot mud, so the material ends up being better-preserved."],[223,75.88,2.8,"<i>Unlike Pompeii, when they</i> <i>excavate Herculaneum,</i>"],[223,78.88,2.08,"<i>they basically don’t find any bodies.</i>"],[223,81.24,3.04,"<i>So, something did happen on this beach, right?</i>"],[223,84.56,3.32,"<i>There does seem to have been some</i> <i>sort of an attempt of an evacuation.</i>"],[223,89.0,3.64,"My job is working as a survival psychologist,"],[223,92.64,4.08,"looking at how people respond to disasters."],[223,97.2,4.48,"<i>So in disaster psychology, what I’m</i> <i>looking at is people under extreme stress.</i>"],[223,101.68,1.6,"Like a natural disaster."],[223,103.28,3.48,"From all of your studies, can we apply these predictions to people from the past,"],[223,106.76,0.4,"do you think?"],[223,107.4,3.12,"Yeah, I don’t think people have changed very much in 2,000 years."],[223,110.84,2.56,"We know that there are about ten percent of people"],[223,113.4,3.32,"who will manage to be cool, calm, and collected"],[223,116.72,2.24,"and respond effectively."],[223,119.24,1.64,"We have a Praetorian."],[223,120.88,2.92,"He is exactly the sort of person you’d need in a situation like this."],[223,124.8,1.12,"Into the boat sheds!"],[223,126.44,0.84,"Now!"],[223,127.72,2.92,"And then, we have the eighty percent of people."],[223,131.12,2.2,"They don’t really know how to respond."],[223,133.6,4.24,"<i>They’re waiting for direction and other</i> <i>people to basically help rescue them.</i>"],[223,137.84,1.4,"<i>Julia Felix.</i>"],[223,139.24,2.0,"<i>She’s the leader of her household.</i>"],[223,142.24,1.56,"<i>Okay, we need to focus.</i>"],[223,144.56,1.32,"<i>Get them inside!</i> <i>Now!</i>"],[223,145.88,1.96,"<i>Even in the heat of an emergency,</i>"],[223,148.2,4.08,"<i>people are capable of engaging in</i> <i>what we call, “Pro-Social Behavior.”</i>"],[223,152.28,3.08,"<i>So, we see people helping</i> <i>and supporting each other.</i>"],[223,155.36,1.2,"<i>Rescuing each other.</i>"],[223,156.56,1.44,"<i>Friends.</i> <i>Family.</i>"],[223,158.36,1.64,"<i>Or even total strangers.</i>"],[223,160.0,1.32,"<i>They rescue one another.</i>"],[223,161.6,0.68,"<i>Yes.</i>"],[223,164.16,1.64,"I’m interested in how we use"],[223,165.8,1.76,"the rocks and fossils we find"],[223,167.92,2.88,"beneath our feet to reconstruct Earth history."],[223,170.8,1.84,"Four point five billion years of it."],[223,173.0,2.2,"<i>On that day two thousand years ago,</i>"],[223,175.4,2.2,"<i>magma, molten rock, is rising up.</i>"],[223,177.92,2.68,"<i>Pushing against the rocks</i> <i>and fracturing them, until...</i>"],[223,180.6,0.6,"<i>Explosion!</i>"],[223,181.2,1.48,"<i>Well, eruption, actually.</i>"],[223,182.96,2.32,"Pompeii is incredibly unique,"],[223,185.6,4.88,"just as a place of record of the way in which these geohazards,"],[223,190.48,4.8,"volcanic eruptions, can immediately, and in a very devastating way, affect human life."],[223,196.08,3.36,"<i>Six hundred million tons of</i> <i>superheated rock and magma</i>"],[223,199.76,3.04,"<i>was blown twenty miles up into the atmosphere.</i>"],[223,203.0,1.4,"<i>And within minutes,</i>"],[223,204.64,3.6,"<i>that rock and magma would</i> <i>start to rain down on Pompeii.</i>"],[223,209.2,3.36,"And I think that you could really argue that the story of Pompeii is"],[223,212.56,2.08,"the birth of volcanology, in a way."],[223,214.64,4.92,"And you can argue that it’s, therefore, one of the founding principles of geology, itself."],[223,219.56,3.08,"<i>The eruption column now becomes so vast, so dense,</i>"],[223,222.64,2.8,"<i>that it was actually stopping sunlight reaching</i>"],[223,225.44,1.2,"<i>Herculaneum...</i>"],[223,226.64,1.08,"<i>Pompeii...</i>"],[223,227.72,1.92,"<i>well, the whole Bay of Naples.</i>"],[223,229.64,2.0,"<i>Soon, it will be completely buried.</i>"],[223,231.64,1.12,"<i>But there’s still time, right?</i>"],[223,232.76,2.96,"<i>There’s still some chance</i> <i>that they can evacuate?</i>"],[223,236.04,1.12,"<i>It’s a slim chance.</i>"],[223,237.56,4.64,"I’ve worked on basically three main topics throughout my career."],[223,242.48,1.64,"Household archaeology,"],[223,244.16,3.52,"interactions between Egypt and the Greco-Roman world,"],[223,247.88,3.4,"and the archaeology of religion and ritual."],[223,251.52,2.24,"<i>Romans had many, many gods.</i>"],[223,253.84,2.68,"<i>- Yeah.</i> <i>- Including a lot that originally came from other cultures,</i>"],[223,256.52,1.84,"<i>- like Egypt, in this case.</i> <i>- Yeah.</i>"],[223,259.08,2.72,"There is so much Egyptian influence at Pompeii."],[223,262.16,3.48,"Objects imported from Egypt. Practices imported from Egypt."],[223,265.92,2.92,"Local products that are meant to look Egyptian."],[223,268.84,3.56,"The most dramatic example is the temples of Isis, where"],[223,272.4,3.56,"an Egyptian god was being worshipped in this community."],[223,276.12,2.72,"<i>Isis was a cosmic creator.</i>"],[223,279.04,2.68,"<i>She could embody all of the gods in one.</i>"],[223,282.2,3.92,"<i>She’s certainly a god you might want to</i> <i>have on your side during a natural disaster.</i>"],[223,287.24,2.68,"<i>There was someone praying</i> <i>here during the eruption.</i>"],[223,290.64,0.72,"<i>Mother.</i>"],[223,291.72,1.12,"<i>Protector.</i>"],[223,293.52,1.6,"<i>Come to me, Isis.</i>"],[223,296.4,2.0,"I hope that the viewers are curious"],[223,298.4,2.68,"to learn more about this incredible lost culture."],[223,301.08,2.6,"I think the experience of being in Pompeii"],[223,303.68,3.84,"can produce a real empathy for the people who lived there."],[223,307.52,4.4,"These people felt all of the things that you would feel if you were in the same situation."],[223,311.92,1.88,"It isn't just about the ruins."],[223,313.8,8.4,"It's about human stories and those emotions that translate from 79 AD to the present day."],[224,0.16,3.2,"For most of human existence, if you needed major surgery,"],[224,3.36,2.0,"there was a solid argument for you to just say,"],[224,5.36,4.24,"“No thanks, I’ll just take my chances with this rotten tooth or cancerous tumor”."],[224,9.6,3.76,"Sure, we had some methods of pain relief…like super addictive opium,"],[224,13.36,3.68,"also addictive alcohol, and a bucket of ice, or hypnosis."],[224,17.04,2.8,"But a lot of the time, surgeons just didn’t bother."],[224,19.84,3.2,"The best they could do was get in and out as fast as possible."],[224,23.04,1.92,"And then, anesthesia came along."],[224,24.96,2.64,"To both numb the pain and knock you unconscious."],[224,27.6,4.64,"Anesthesia changed surgery forever, even if we’re still a little hazy on how it works."],[224,32.24,3.92,"But the man who introduced it to the world wasn’t a celebrated scientist."],[224,36.16,3.65,"In fact, you could say he put the con in unconscious."],[224,43.03,2.17,"On October 13, 1804,"],[224,45.2,4.32,"a Japanese surgeon named Seishū Hanaoka removed a tumor from a woman’s breast."],[224,49.52,4.96,"To dull her senses and put her to sleep, he gave her an herbal concoction called Mafutsusan."],[224,54.48,4.56,"And this wasn’t just something he threw together, he had been working on this recipe for years."],[224,59.04,1.6,"To many medical historians,"],[224,60.64,3.76,"this was the first documented use of a surgery performed with anesthesia."],[224,64.4,4.72,"But around the same time Hanaoka was experimenting with his solution to the surgery problem,"],[224,69.12,3.6,"a brazen young research assistant in England was laying the groundwork for"],[224,72.72,4.64,"the global anesthesia revolution…which wouldn’t happen for another four decades."],[224,77.36,3.44,"Which brings us to nitrous oxide, a.k.a. laughing gas."],[224,80.8,4.32,"Although our Script Editor JD can testify that…when she had to inhale a bunch prior"],[224,85.12,3.36,"to her wisdom teeth extraction…she didn’t utter as much as a chuckle."],[224,88.48,3.6,"The discovery of nitrous oxide is usually credited to Joseph Priestley,"],[224,92.08,3.44,"based on some experiments he ran in 1772."],[224,95.52,2.4,"But since he was a chemist, not a doctor,"],[224,97.92,5.36,"his discovery was really only celebrated in a “cool, this molecule exists” kind of way."],[224,103.28,2.96,"Certainly not in a “This is a medical marvel” kind of way."],[224,106.24,4.08,"Nearly three decades later, Humphrey Davy did what scientists liked to do back then."],[224,110.32,4.32,"By which I mean he experimented on himself, huffing a bunch of different gases to help"],[224,114.64,4.24,"his boss at the Pneumatic Medical Institution find something that could help cure diseases."],[224,118.88,2.08,"Yes John, including tuberculosis."],[224,120.96,3.04,"Davy was playing a gaseous game of Russian roulette."],[224,124.0,2.88,"But compared to the corrosive nitric oxide and poisonous"],[224,126.88,3.68,"carbon monoxide that he’d already sampled, nitrous oxide was a win."],[224,130.56,3.52,"He described the experience as “thrilling”...including how in the"],[224,134.08,4.0,"middle of his high, he exclaimed “Nothing exists but thoughts!”"],[224,138.08,2.0,"Which is certainly a thought."],[224,140.08,4.32,"With such a high Yelp review, of course all his friends wanted to give it a try."],[224,144.4,1.2,"You know, for “science”."],[224,145.6,3.76,"And Davy, ever the researcher, collected and published almost 20"],[224,149.36,3.68,"accounts from the people participating in their own personal “experiments”."],[224,153.04,2.32,"With that many people trying it, Davy finally"],[224,155.36,3.84,"noticed the symptom most important for this episode: the drug could ease pain."],[224,159.2,1.84,"In his book published in 1800,"],[224,161.04,3.84,"Davy reports nitrous oxide relieving his own head and tooth aches."],[224,164.88,4.56,"He also wrote that the gas might have a future in surgery, but he never followed up on that."],[224,169.44,5.6,"Instead, people dabbled in nitrous oxide for both recreational and scientific whims until 1844,"],[224,175.04,2.96,"when it caught the eye of a dentist named Horace Wells."],[224,178.0,4.08,"Or rather, a man high on laughing gas caught his eye…who had injured"],[224,182.08,3.12,"himself on a bit of furniture, and walked away none the wiser."],[224,185.2,1.76,"Wells was immediately inspired."],[224,186.96,2.56,"The next morning, he sourced some of the gas for himself,"],[224,189.52,2.64,"and had one of his teeth extracted by a dental student."],[224,192.16,2.4,"He called it the “greatest discovery ever made”."],[224,194.56,3.28,"After using it on his own dental patients, Wells was so gassed up"],[224,197.84,4.08,"about its potential that he approached one of the most famous surgeons in the country:"],[224,201.92,2.48,"John Warren, at Massachusetts General Hospital."],[224,204.4,4.08,"He wanted a public demonstration to show how great nitrous oxide was."],[224,208.48,4.96,"Warren agreed, but the details of this 1845 demonstration are a bit murky."],[224,213.44,3.68,"Doctors and dentists were apparently not the best record keepers at the time."],[224,217.12,2.32,"And some of the first-hand accounts that historians"],[224,219.44,2.8,"rely on come from decades after the actual event."],[224,222.24,4.32,"But the thing all sources agree on is that it was…a phenomenal disaster."],[224,226.56,4.43,"Since Wells only had so many teeth of his own…and he also had to run the demo…"],[224,230.99,2.61,"a student volunteered to have one extracted."],[224,233.6,3.12,"In front of a large audience, Wells then administered the gas,"],[224,236.72,5.04,"the procedure began, and then…according to reports…the student shouted in pain."],[224,241.76,2.24,"The audience was not forgiving."],[224,244.0,5.2,"They criticized the entire affair, and after that, Wells kind of slinks out of our story."],[224,249.2,4.48,"He tried to explain that he’d simply failed to administer enough gas, but it's pretty"],[224,253.68,4.88,"tough to recover from such a blunder in front of so many medical professionals."],[224,258.56,3.68,"Enter, William Morton: a dentist and medical student in Boston."],[224,262.24,2.64,"He had both trained under and worked with Wells,"],[224,264.88,2.24,"and by some accounts helped organize the demonstration."],[224,267.12,2.08,"But that failed demo didn’t stop Morton from"],[224,269.2,4.08,"picking up right where Wells left off… just not with nitrous oxide."],[224,273.28,2.4,"Morton was putting his money on sulfuric ether."],[224,275.68,3.28,"Which, despite the name, doesn't actually have any sulfur in it."],[224,278.96,3.04,"But to make it, you can mix sulfuric acid and ethanol."],[224,282.0,1.52,"I’ll just call it “ether” from now on."],[224,283.52,5.92,"In Morton’s day, the word “ether” all by itself just vaguely meant any super volatile liquid."],[224,289.44,3.28,"If a compound readily vaporized, then it was dubbed an ether."],[224,292.72,1.84,"But these days, when people say “ether”,"],[224,294.56,3.2,"they almost always mean the stuff that Morton was experimenting with."],[224,297.76,2.8,"Now, sulfuric ether was far from a new drug."],[224,300.56,3.76,"First documented in the 1500s, it acts on receptors in the central"],[224,304.32,4.64,"nervous system to slow the brain down and induce calm, euphoria, and sedation."],[224,308.96,3.98,"With that combo of effects, it was immediately clear this had to be used in surgery…"],[224,313.92,0.56,"Just kidding."],[224,314.48,3.04,"It was quickly adopted as a party drug, and used for miscellaneous"],[224,317.52,4.32,"medical needs like headaches, respiratory infections, convulsions, and childbirth."],[224,321.84,4.64,"Exactly what inspired Morton to turn to ether as an anesthetic is up for debate."],[224,326.48,1.52,"He said it was all his own idea."],[224,328.0,4.53,"But his fellow dentist Wells, and another colleague…a chemist, Charles Jackson…"],[224,332.53,4.75,"they both argued that they had discussed using ether as anesthesia, too, and deserved some credit."],[224,337.28,5.26,"Meanwhile, one Dr. Crawford Long had been using it since 1842 in the state of Georgia."],[224,342.54,3.46,"So Morton definitely wasn’t the first to use ether for surgery,"],[224,346.0,2.48,"but he sure delivered it to fame with his showmanship."],[224,348.48,3.2,"Morton followed in the footsteps of his former teacher, and requested a"],[224,351.68,2.48,"public demonstration of ether's efficacy."],[224,354.16,3.52,"Yes, with the same Dr. John Warren presiding and everything."],[224,357.68,5.2,"It takes a special kind of person to see Wells’s failure and think “I wanna do that”,"],[224,362.88,4.24,"but Morton had a reputation for seizing any opportunity he thought he could monetize."],[224,367.12,5.04,"In the past, that usually meant forging letters of recommendation or scamming creditors."],[224,372.16,2.48,"In other words, he was a bit of a Con Man."],[224,374.64,2.24,"But he was willing to give peddling ether a try."],[224,376.88,3.04,"Dr. Warren either loved to let dentists embarrass themselves,"],[224,379.92,4.8,"or really wanted to find an anesthetic that worked, because he agreed to another demo."],[224,384.72,3.71,"He even raised the stakes, because instead of a tooth extraction,"],[224,388.43,3.25,"this time it was going to be a tumor removal from someone’s neck."],[224,391.68,4.0,"According to records, Morton took this responsibility super seriously,"],[224,395.68,4.64,"as evidenced by the fact that he picked up a glass inhaler the morning of the surgery…which"],[224,400.32,4.48,"he special-ordered to conceal the fact that he was using something as straightforward as ether."],[224,404.8,1.46,"Also, he showed up late."],[224,406.26,2.7,"He didn’t even have time to test the device beforehand."],[224,408.96,1.44,"But Morton was a betting man."],[224,410.4,4.32,"On October 16, 1846, he used ether to put a patient to sleep"],[224,414.72,3.52,"before Warren tagged in to remove a 3-inch tumor."],[224,418.24,3.36,"By all accounts, the demonstration was a huge success."],[224,421.6,3.44,"The patient didn’t react when he was first cut into, and even during the"],[224,425.04,5.68,"more invasive parts of surgery he only moved a bit, later claiming to have felt minimal pain."],[224,430.72,4.08,"More surgeries under ether were performed at Mass General over the next few days."],[224,434.8,4.8,"None of them were perfect, but the patients fell asleep, and reported far less pain than usual."],[224,439.6,3.36,"Hospitals were quick to make the use of ether in surgeries standard."],[224,442.96,2.24,"And just one month after Morton’s demo,"],[224,445.2,3.6,"it was rebranded as an anesthetic by Oliver Wendell Holmes."],[224,448.8,2.0,"No, not the Supreme Court justice."],[224,450.8,0.96,"That guy’s dad."],[224,451.76,4.96,"Now, October 16th is celebrated as “Ether Day”, or alternatively, “World Anaesthesia Day.”"],[224,456.72,5.2,"Even though it wasn’t the first use of ether, it was its really first public debut."],[224,461.92,2.24,"And it marked a turning point where anesthesia"],[224,464.16,2.88,"started being used in tons of procedures around the world."],[224,467.04,3.2,"Morton, meanwhile, got credit for putting on the demonstration,"],[224,470.24,2.64,"and proving that ether was an effective anesthetic."],[224,472.88,4.4,"But ether was public domain, as far as intellectual property law was concerned."],[224,477.28,5.28,"So despite his best efforts to try and disguise it, patent it, sell the flimsy inhaler,"],[224,482.56,4.88,"and lobby the government for a reward, it didn’t turn into the golden goose he’d hoped for."],[224,487.44,3.28,"All his efforts strained his relationships with Wells and Jackson,"],[224,490.72,2.88,"and tarnished his reputation with the greater medical community."],[224,493.6,5.12,"Whatever his true intentions deep down inside, it sure looked like he was just in it for the money."],[224,498.72,2.24,"Not, you know, trying to alleviate people’s suffering."],[224,500.96,2.96,"And speaking of suffering, all three of these men…Morton,"],[224,503.92,3.08,"Wells, and Jackson, all had pretty tragic endings."],[224,507.0,1.32,"But I’ll spare you the details."],[224,508.32,2.8,"As for ether, it spent about a century in the limelight,"],[224,511.12,2.72,"as a ton of anaesthetic alternatives sprouted up."],[224,513.84,5.36,"For example, chloroform was introduced just a year after ether, by Scottish doctor James Simpson."],[224,519.2,2.0,"He was looking for something to aid in childbirth."],[224,521.2,4.64,"In 1853, Queen Victoria received chloroform during her own labor and delivery,"],[224,525.84,2.88,"so it was quickly welcomed into the surgical theater."],[224,528.72,3.84,"But by the 1930s, it was clear that chloroform posed a risk to heart health,"],[224,532.56,3.2,"and wasn’t as safe as ether, so it was phased out."],[224,535.76,2.8,"It was also around this time that anesthesiology became a"],[224,538.56,4.0,"proper scientific field, which encouraged the development of more anesthesia drugs."],[224,542.56,3.28,"The gas cyclopropane was introduced in 1933."],[224,545.84,3.84,"Patients could recover very quickly from it, but it was super explosive,"],[224,549.68,2.48,"so it didn’t improve much on ether’s dangers."],[224,552.16,5.04,"Over the following decades, drugs like halothane, xenon, and isoflurane were introduced."],[224,557.2,2.72,"Some of those are still used in general anesthesia, today."],[224,559.92,2.88,"There are also different categories of anesthetics, now."],[224,562.8,1.86,"Local anesthetics like lidocaine,"],[224,564.66,4.14,"and then liquid anaesthetics delivered via IV like propofol or ketamine."],[224,568.8,1.04,"You name ‘em, we got ‘em."],[224,569.84,4.96,"We can also craft drug cocktails to make sure patients have all the relief they require."],[224,574.8,4.72,"General anesthesia usually involves a combo of IV drugs and inhaled gases."],[224,579.52,2.48,"Anesthesia has come a long way."],[224,582.0,4.88,"But what hasn’t really made nearly as much progress is how anesthesia actually works."],[224,586.88,1.68,"It’s not for lack of trying."],[224,588.56,4.24,"Scientists have spent a lot of time investigating several different hypotheses."],[224,592.8,4.56,"Like maybe anesthesia drugs reduce the signals traveling between your brain’s neurons to the"],[224,597.36,4.8,"point where there’s just not enough metaphorical hands on deck to maintain consciousness."],[224,602.16,2.56,"Or maybe, your brain is a delicate ecosystem of"],[224,604.72,3.12,"signals saying things like “Do this” and “Stop that”."],[224,607.84,4.0,"And it’s not a reduction of signalling, but an imbalance that knocks you out."],[224,611.84,2.64,"Studies also suggest your unconscious self might"],[224,614.48,3.44,"remember things without ever sharing the receipts with your conscious self."],[224,617.92,3.36,"And none of this even touches on what happens when anesthesia fails,"],[224,621.28,4.32,"and a patient wakes up in the middle of surgery but unable to move or speak."],[224,625.6,3.04,"But at least now people are going to school and learning this stuff,"],[224,628.64,2.64,"so you don’t have to rely on a man who showed up late to your"],[224,631.28,4.4,"surgery with a contraption meant to hide a mysterious miracle gas."],[225,7.79,4.63,"In the 2022 World Cup, while Argentina took home the title,"],[225,12.47,3.5,"there was another clear winner: the gambling industry."],[225,16.6,5.46,"Spectators around the globe wagered an estimated $35 billion,"],[225,22.14,4.13,"making it one of the most bet-on events in sports history."],[225,26.69,5.76,"Over the past decade, sports gambling has become more popular than ever."],[225,32.7,5.21,"Mobile apps make it easier to place bets anywhere or anytime,"],[225,38.03,3.38,"especially for young people in their early 20s,"],[225,41.41,3.71,"who make up the fastest-growing gambling demographic."],[225,45.54,3.42,"The industry's success, however, is no coincidence."],[225,49.05,5.71,"In fact, sports betting apps work just like the games you'd find in a casino."],[225,54.93,3.13,"They’re strategically designed in ways that are known"],[225,58.05,2.17,"to keep you playing and paying—"],[225,60.31,3.84,"ensuring that, in the end, the house always wins."],[225,64.48,3.84,"So, what exactly are the tricks these apps use?"],[225,68.65,7.01,"It’s estimated that Americans lost $60 billion gambling online and in casinos"],[225,75.7,2.17,"in 2022 alone."],[225,77.95,2.79,"While losing might seem like a deterrent,"],[225,80.99,5.88,"infrequent wins are actually a key reason gambling can feel so enticing."],[225,87.0,3.09,"One of the best ways to motivate any behavior—"],[225,90.09,4.75,"whether posting on social media, swiping on a dating app, or placing a bet—"],[225,94.97,5.46,"is to grant a reward at random and unpredictable intervals."],[225,100.72,5.51,"The reason for this likely lies in uncertainty’s effect on the brain."],[225,106.6,4.25,"Dopamine is a neurotransmitter that mediates feelings of pleasure"],[225,110.98,5.17,"and is released when we do things we enjoy and when we receive rewards."],[225,116.65,4.38,"Its release is what motivates us to seek out the sensation again."],[225,121.28,4.21,"In certain brain areas, this dopamine release is highest,"],[225,125.5,2.29,"not when the rewards are the biggest,"],[225,127.79,4.5,"but rather when you're unsure whether you'll even receive them."],[225,132.63,3.59,"This draw to unpredictability can be a good thing."],[225,136.3,3.96,"Evolutionarily, it may have been beneficial for our ancestors"],[225,140.26,3.25,"to seek out situations with uncertain rewards,"],[225,143.64,2.75,"like investigating new locations for food."],[225,146.52,4.75,"But when it comes to something like a casino game or a sports betting app,"],[225,151.77,4.38,"this behavior can create a vicious and expensive cycle."],[225,156.86,3.46,"Another feature these games manipulate is timing."],[225,160.41,4.3,"Slot machines, for example, are notoriously mesmerizing,"],[225,164.74,4.96,"thanks in part to how quickly you move from placing a bet to seeing the result"],[225,169.71,1.63,"to placing your next one."],[225,171.54,2.84,"This frequency, paired with uncertainty,"],[225,174.38,4.59,"is thought to induce what researchers call dark flow."],[225,179.38,4.5,"You may feel as if you are in the zone, completely absorbed in the game."],[225,184.06,5.55,"Yet a key characteristic of any flow state is that it often feels more difficult"],[225,189.6,2.21,"to stop than to keep going."],[225,192.44,5.59,"To capitalize on this, sports betting apps have introduced in-play bets,"],[225,198.03,3.17,"which are wagers that users can place during the game"],[225,201.24,2.38,"on moment-to-moment events."],[225,204.28,4.13,"Finally, near-misses can feel particularly thrilling."],[225,208.75,3.34,"This is when, for example, a slot machine reveals"],[225,212.08,3.04,"a cherry... a cherry... and then a seven."],[225,215.71,5.92,"Brain imaging studies show that the anticipation built up in that last reveal"],[225,221.68,3.92,"activates the same brain regions as winning does."],[225,225.93,4.3,"So even though near-misses are ultimately losses,"],[225,230.23,4.13,"they actually increase our desire to continue playing."],[225,234.6,3.38,"That’s why some slot machines are actually designed"],[225,237.98,3.09,"to hit near-misses more often than they should."],[225,241.49,3.8,"Sports can naturally lead to near-miss situations,"],[225,245.41,4.3,"yet gambling apps have found ways to exploit them further."],[225,250.2,1.54,"Parlays, for example,"],[225,251.79,5.3,"are when bets on multiple events or outcomes are tied into the same wager."],[225,257.3,4.71,"If any single bet loses, then the entire wager is lost,"],[225,262.22,3.17,"easily creating a near-miss situation."],[225,266.05,3.42,"While these tactics work on brains of all ages,"],[225,269.6,3.04,"young people may be especially at risk."],[225,273.27,1.33,"During adolescence,"],[225,274.6,4.92,"dopamine-related reward regions of the brain mature rapidly,"],[225,279.65,4.21,"making teens more vulnerable to developing gambling problems."],[225,284.15,4.8,"And while 18 is the legal age limit for gambling in most countries,"],[225,289.04,5.13,"these same psychological tactics are now increasingly found in games"],[225,294.17,2.88,"marketed directly to teens and children."],[225,297.38,3.46,"For example, many games contain loot boxes,"],[225,300.84,4.71,"where players can spend real money to gain unpredictable prizes."],[225,305.93,5.05,"The global gambling industry racks in hundreds of billions of dollars"],[225,310.97,1.0,"every year."],[225,311.97,5.3,"Yet it’s estimated that anywhere from 15 to 50% of that revenue"],[225,317.27,3.13,"comes from people with gambling problems."],[225,320.94,3.8,"We can’t change that our brains are drawn to gambling tactics."],[225,325.03,3.84,"The industry clearly knows this and is cashing in."],[226,189.72,2.4,"Helen Walters: OK, alright."],[226,192.12,3.0,"You guys, we can't let him go without asking him some questions,"],[226,195.16,2.3,"which is really unkind, given all of the dancing,"],[226,197.49,1.17,"but here you are."],[226,198.7,1.57,"There's a microphone, my friend."],[226,200.3,1.2,"Sean Bankhead: Thank you."],[226,202.0,1.37,"Hi."],[226,203.4,1.14,"HW: Hi, Sean."],[226,204.57,1.17,"SB: How are you?"],[226,205.74,2.13,"HW: I've never felt so cool adjacent."],[226,209.27,2.27,"SB: Thank you for having me, this is incredible."],[226,211.54,1.67,"HW: Come in the light."],[226,215.68,1.2,"SB: Let me wipe my sweat."],[226,216.88,2.74,"HW: That was ridiculous and amazing."],[226,219.65,1.64,"And a song by Victoria Monét."],[226,221.32,2.17,"Can you tell us about that choreography?"],[226,223.52,1.84,"SB: Woo! I'm going to catch my breath."],[226,226.49,1.17,"Thank you."],[226,227.66,4.4,"So myself and Victoria knew that we wanted to pay homage"],[226,232.06,4.04,"to so many different things that have inspired us in pop culture:"],[226,236.13,4.24,"HBCU culture, Black culture, college culture,"],[226,240.4,3.34,"skate culture, even meme culture"],[226,243.77,1.67,"and things that you see on Twitter"],[226,245.44,2.0,"that are just really funny and memorable."],[226,247.44,3.74,"And we decided to make a kind of a mixing pot of a lot of different,"],[226,251.18,2.0,"really cool things that have inspired us."],[226,253.22,1.9,"And that’s how “On My Mama” came about."],[226,257.19,3.54,"HW: So would you say that that is a driving force in your work in general,"],[226,260.76,2.67,"is trying to connect choreography to the current moment?"],[226,263.46,1.27,"SB: One million percent."],[226,264.73,2.3,"I am standing here today"],[226,267.03,2.74,"because of all the incredible choreographers and artists"],[226,269.8,2.5,"that inspired me, back from Missy Elliott,"],[226,272.34,1.64,"Janet Jackson, Michael Jackson,"],[226,274.0,1.37,"Aaliyah, Usher."],[226,275.41,2.74,"And watching their music videos"],[226,278.18,2.0,"gave me the tools and the inspiration"],[226,280.21,3.3,"to try to keep carrying the torch of what's hot, what's new,"],[226,283.55,2.37,"and making sure that dance and choreography"],[226,285.92,2.44,"is still very prevalent in pop culture."],[226,288.35,4.47,"HW: So we should be clear, that video went super bonkers online."],[226,292.86,3.1,"So why do you think that some dance videos go viral?"],[226,297.23,1.37,"SB: There's a couple things."],[226,298.63,3.3,"One, I think luck or God, thank you."],[226,301.96,2.1,"Still making things that are hot."],[226,304.1,2.3,"One thing, I think,"],[226,306.44,3.1,"I always put 1,000 percent of my heart"],[226,309.57,3.17,"and time and energy into anything I create."],[226,312.78,2.9,"So if I'm stepping into a project, I want to kill it."],[226,315.68,3.2,"I want to put 1,000 percent of myself into the project."],[226,318.92,2.8,"And people can feel heart, they can feel passion."],[226,321.75,2.44,"And I think it translates through the screen."],[226,324.19,2.67,"And I think another thing is,"],[226,326.89,2.17,"oddly enough, the song is called \"On My Mama,\""],[226,329.09,4.04,"and I always love having at least one move or one groove"],[226,333.16,1.7,"that my mama can do, in a video."],[226,336.87,4.4,"But I think it allows dancers and non-dancers alike to feel like,"],[226,341.31,1.17,"\"Oh, I like that move."],[226,342.51,2.17,"I can do that whenever the song comes on.\""],[226,344.68,1.5,"And I think it just translates."],[226,346.21,1.64,"It's catchy, it's trendy."],[226,347.85,2.5,"HW: Which move was it in that one?"],[226,351.98,1.44,"I'm asking for your mum."],[226,353.45,1.4,"SB: Let's do this one."],[226,354.88,1.2,"HW: Oh no, no, let's not."],[226,356.12,2.07,"SB: Here we go, we could do this right here."],[226,358.19,1.2,"Real easy."],[226,359.42,2.0,"Step together, step together."],[226,361.46,1.14,"OK, step --"],[226,365.13,1.17,"That was amazing."],[226,366.33,3.04,"HW: That was really horrific, but thank you, I appreciate that."],[226,369.37,1.57,"Now tell us about your friends."],[226,370.97,1.17,"Your amazing friends."],[226,372.14,2.74,"SB: Oh, please, this next generation of incredible talent."],[226,374.9,2.07,"I've been watching these kids take my class."],[226,377.01,4.04,"Kimoni, Cam, Corey and Devonte."],[226,381.58,2.07,"Thank you guys for assisting me."],[226,390.59,1.27,"Thank you guys, love you."],[226,391.89,1.57,"Thank you for showing up for me."],[226,393.46,3.07,"The next generation of dancers, choreographers from Atlanta."],[226,396.86,1.27,"Thank you for having me."],[227,3.54,2.04,"Not only am I humming on an airplane,"],[227,5.58,4.88,"I’m also the friend you’ll be walking with and I’ll be like, (singing) voo-zhhh."],[227,11.09,1.96,"The instruments on your body;"],[227,13.05,3.04,"you just play it all day, you know what I mean?"],[227,16.8,2.09,"Hey, you! Yes, you."],[227,19.02,1.79,"Is there music inside of you?"],[227,20.85,3.21,"We’ve recruited working musicians from throughout the industry"],[227,24.06,6.13,"to help you hear it, hold it, and share it with this wild and wonderful world."],[227,31.15,4.34,"My parents met in a gospel choir, they’re both gospel singers."],[227,35.7,4.05,"And they always joke that, in the Breland family,"],[227,39.74,1.96,"if you can't sing, you'll get sent back."],[227,41.83,5.38,"My dad showed me a tape of a musical called “Les Miserables,” and I got hooked."],[227,47.46,3.21,"I was the new girl at a lot of schools,"],[227,50.67,3.96,"and I would just take my guitar to some beautiful location"],[227,54.88,3.54,"and sing myself into being okay."],[227,58.72,4.25,"The way that I came to singing was through school choir,"],[227,62.98,4.17,"and it didn't hurt that I was also surrounded by some of the most eccentric"],[227,68.11,4.42,"singer-songwriters on the planet through my family’s music shop."],[227,73.74,4.63,"A great vocal performance is first and foremost feeling."],[227,78.99,3.55,"Louis Armstrong does not have a huge range."],[227,83.29,2.17,"Nat King Cole— not a huge range."],[227,86.08,4.88,"No acrobatics, but you could feel the emotion in the words."],[227,91.05,4.38,"Oftentimes, it’s not the best vocalist that’s going to win on a song."],[227,95.59,2.67,"It’s whoever’s voice is able to best convey"],[227,98.26,1.96,"whatever story it is that they’re telling"],[227,100.22,2.96,"and whatever emotion it is that they’re trying to convey."],[227,103.31,4.63,"Like Alison Krauss, I feel like she's not singing notes."],[227,108.1,4.63,"The notes of existence are emanating from her."],[227,112.78,4.84,"Someone like a Zach Bryan, who maybe isn’t the best vocalist in the world—"],[227,117.61,1.63,"and he would tell you that—"],[227,119.36,3.04,"but he’s telling these stories and you believe him."],[227,122.49,4.46,"And I love Leonard Cohen, who's got this baritone speaking,"],[227,127.04,2.38,"melodic spoken delivery."],[227,129.75,2.38,"And then you get to your Ella Fitzgeralds,"],[227,132.13,3.09,"who had all the range and they had feeling."],[227,135.38,5.34,"So I try to look at other, more acrobatic vocalists like Aretha Franklin,"],[227,140.72,3.84,"Celine Dion, Beyoncé, Stevie Wonder."],[227,144.76,3.59,"I like to hear vocalists reach and explore in real time."],[227,148.6,1.83,"I'm so attracted to Brazilian music"],[227,150.44,3.38,"because there's a certain style of singing"],[227,153.82,4.0,"in bossa nova, specifically, that’s very intimate."],[227,157.82,3.75,"And it’s like the singers basically whispering into the microphone."],[227,161.66,3.34,"I think I was attracted to that because my voice suited it,"],[227,165.08,3.63,"and also because I didn’t always want to perform loudly,"],[227,168.71,2.88,"I wanted to write for myself."],[227,174.09,1.96,"The way that I’ve come into singing,"],[227,176.05,2.33,"and going from learning how to sing as a kid"],[227,178.38,2.96,"to singing every night on stage,"],[227,181.84,4.55,"is, for one, I’ve trusted my voice and trusting your range"],[227,186.39,3.34,"and bravely exploring where your voice can go."],[227,189.85,2.0,"Because we’re all born with an instrument."],[227,191.9,2.96,"Let’s figure it out, let’s put it through the paces, right."],[227,194.98,2.17,"We can all sing “Happy Birthday,” right?"],[227,197.15,3.71,"But what’s going to make your singing it different from the way I sing it?"],[227,201.11,1.46,"It’s how we color it."],[227,203.41,1.5,"It's how we phrase it."],[227,204.91,4.38,"And there are a myriad of choices that one can make."],[227,209.37,3.5,"And that’s why I say you have to be curious and you have to be interested."],[227,212.88,4.59,"You have to be excited about all of the many choices that you can have"],[227,217.59,2.13,"in the way that you tell a story."],[227,222.01,4.63,"Chest voice is the part of your voice that is most natural to speech."],[227,226.64,4.09,"So I'm talking here in this chest voice."],[227,230.81,2.54,"That’s the voice from your chest, literally."],[227,233.48,3.17,"Falsetto is thin and high."],[227,236.65,1.63,"So, (singing) heyyyy."],[227,238.28,4.38,"You can hear a flip into falsetto if I go: (singing) ahhhh-ahhh-oooh."],[227,242.7,2.25,"You’re kind of thinning out the air."],[227,244.99,3.46,"Head voice is when you go high and you mix."],[227,248.49,3.25,"So it’s like a, (singing) meee-eaah-eaah."],[227,251.75,3.17,"There's a word called squillo in Italian."],[227,255.04,3.29,"It's bringing chest voice up into your upper register."],[227,258.5,2.71,"You can project very loudly like, (singing) haaaa!"],[227,261.26,3.96,"Or you can do something more breathy like, (singing softly) haaaa."],[227,265.3,2.79,"Riffs— (singing) mmhmm."],[227,268.14,4.84,"All the melismas, I think is the technical word, but not to be used ad nauseam."],[227,272.98,2.17,"You know when someone’s doing too much of that"],[227,275.15,1.58,"and it’s really annoying, right?"],[227,277.11,3.29,"Great singers give you the melody and then once in a while—"],[227,280.4,1.42,"I’m talking to myself—"],[227,281.82,1.29,"we’ll add a little moment."],[227,285.07,1.29,"It's experimentation."],[227,286.37,3.71,"Experimentation is a really big part of getting familiar with your instrument."],[227,290.2,4.05,"I decide what part of my voice I'm using based on the song."],[227,294.5,4.71,"Based on the moment, based on the lyric, based on the feeling I’m trying to emote,"],[227,299.21,2.63,"or the intensity I’m trying to put into it."],[227,301.96,3.13,"Am I trying to be intimate? Am I trying to be a little sexy?"],[227,305.34,2.92,"Then I'll use a little bit more of my breathiness in my voice."],[227,308.26,4.5,"But if I'm trying to be more energetic and more excited,"],[227,313.06,1.96,"or more even frustrated,"],[227,315.23,5.34,"then I’ll get in my higher register and go (singing) naaa-naaa-d-daa-daaa."],[227,320.61,3.13,"And you develop your voice by finding yourself"],[227,323.74,3.13,"and finding where singing feels natural."],[227,326.86,3.29,"I might like singing coloratura mezzo-soprano,"],[227,330.16,4.63,"but that’s not what my voice likes, and that’s not what my personality likes."],[227,335.0,5.96,"My personality likes the fiery, sort of femme fatale women,"],[227,341.3,2.38,"those bad, bad, bad girls."],[227,345.38,1.63,"My dad specifically was like, hmm,"],[227,347.01,4.05,"I think you should take some voice lessons to hone in your skills"],[227,351.35,2.13,"and not wear yourself out so quickly."],[227,353.81,2.5,"When you practice and your voice cracks,"],[227,356.56,2.75,"realize that that’s your voice getting stronger."],[227,359.77,3.21,"So it’s important to practice off stage."],[227,362.98,2.29,"Because anytime your voice cracks,"],[227,365.28,4.0,"anytime you make a mistake and you go over it again and it sounds right,"],[227,369.62,2.54,"that’s actually, the mistake is good."],[227,372.24,1.21,"It boils down to:"],[227,374.29,4.63,"are you comfortable and are you singing and using your vocal muscle"],[227,379.42,3.21,"in the most natural way possible"],[227,382.63,2.17,"so that it's not creating damage?"],[227,384.88,1.83,"I have a really soft voice,"],[227,386.71,3.21,"and I really like the tone that I hit when I’m soft."],[227,390.39,3.88,"And the reason that I like the tone is my vocal folds are barely touching."],[227,394.43,3.13,"But it’s really hard to be accurate with pitch"],[227,397.85,2.63,"when you’re singing all soft and quiet and cute."],[227,401.15,3.67,"When you’re singing all hard and vocal folds touching and strong,"],[227,404.9,3.0,"you can actually hit the note much easier."],[227,407.94,4.75,"And so I learned that I have to actually train in strength,"],[227,412.87,2.21,"with the vocal folds completely touching."],[227,415.16,2.08,"When I train like that constantly,"],[227,417.25,5.88,"when I go back to singing in this way, with the vocal folds barely touching,"],[227,423.42,3.59,"I can actually be a lot more accurate with pitch because I’m stronger."],[227,427.17,4.17,"So some of the ones that’ll help loosen up your vocal cords can be things like,"],[227,431.43,2.17,"(rolling lips)."],[227,433.6,1.92,"A lip roll or a tongue roll:"],[227,435.51,1.75,"(rolling tongue)."],[227,437.73,3.88,"(Vocal exercises) veee-eeeoo, or ooo-ooooh."],[227,441.69,3.38,"(Vocal exercises) vvvhhhh. Zzzzhhhh."],[227,445.19,5.84,"(Vocal exercises) eee-iii-aaahh, or, ney-ney-ney-ney-ney-ney-ney-ney."],[227,451.11,3.92,"(Vocal exercises) Ah-ah-ah-ah-ah-ah-aaahh."],[227,455.03,5.17,"Haa-ah-ah-ah-ah-ah-ah-aaahhh."],[227,460.37,1.63,"And kind of warm my way up."],[227,462.12,3.71,"(Vocal exercises) Yeee-eee-eehh-eee."],[227,466.09,4.75,"And try to get rid of the little break between the falsetto and the chest voice."],[227,472.51,4.67,"Singing, especially doing a 90-minute set, is a marathon exercise,"],[227,477.18,1.5,"so you got to treat it as such."],[227,478.72,2.21,"The term vocal cord is a misnomer."],[227,481.1,1.84,"They're actually folds of muscle."],[227,483.23,5.92,"So if you put a lot of pressure or strain on that musculature,"],[227,489.24,4.42,"you can develop nodes, and nodes will make it painful to sing."],[227,493.86,4.5,"They'll limit how malleable and flexible those folds can be."],[227,498.54,5.05,"You want to avoid strain because obviously, like any athlete,"],[227,503.58,2.84,"if you strain a muscle, you have to take time off."],[227,506.79,3.79,"I know that my voice sings better when it's rested."],[227,511.26,6.26,"I know that the quality of my voice is better when it stays hydrated."],[227,517.72,1.75,"The thing is, I sing every day."],[227,519.47,5.17,"So, the daily practice of singing is, in and of itself, a caretaking method"],[227,524.64,3.88,"because you’re saying to your voice, hey, build up to this stamina."],[227,528.65,2.96,"When I’m on stage and I’m starting to feel the nerves,"],[227,531.78,2.13,"your voice doesn’t work the same way."],[227,533.99,3.63,"So that’s where practice and repetition becomes really important,"],[227,537.62,3.5,"because I may feel a little nervous, but my voice, for the most part,"],[227,541.12,3.09,"knows what it needs to do, even in a moment of stress."],[227,544.25,5.13,"Your body changes and your voice changes as you continue to grow and to mature."],[227,549.59,5.46,"The scariest part to me, though, about exploring one's voice for a lifetime,"],[227,555.38,6.51,"is that I do feel strongly that, having sung since I was— professionally—"],[227,561.89,3.5,"since I was 20 years old, to now, I’m 55,"],[227,566.77,2.25,"I feel like I'm just coming into my voice."],[228,7.17,2.96,"Great public speaking is like magic."],[228,10.17,3.67,"Whether it's a presentation for school, a talk for your community,"],[228,13.88,2.71,"or a video message for family and friends,"],[228,16.6,4.92,"a good talk can electrify an audience and even change the world."],[228,21.68,1.92,"It all starts with an idea."],[228,24.14,2.42,"Ideas change everything."],[228,26.86,4.8,"They bring people together, spark curiosity, and inspire action."],[228,32.07,4.38,"The right idea can ripple across the planet at the speed of light."],[228,36.82,2.71,"But what is an idea, exactly?"],[228,39.99,5.38,"Your number one mission as a speaker is to take something that matters deeply to you"],[228,45.5,3.46,"and rebuild it in the minds of your listeners."],[228,49.42,1.92,"That's something is an idea."],[228,51.67,3.21,"Think of it like a gift you give your audience;"],[228,54.88,3.92,"something they can walk away with, value, and be changed by."],[228,59.14,4.46,"Your idea doesn’t need to be a scientific discovery or a genius invention"],[228,63.6,1.0,"to be great."],[228,64.64,3.17,"You can share instructions for a special skill you have."],[228,67.9,3.0,"Or a story from your life and the lessons it taught you."],[228,71.07,2.59,"Or a vision you have for the future."],[228,73.86,2.92,"Or just a reminder of the things that matter most."],[228,77.11,4.25,"An idea is anything that can change how people see the world."],[228,81.45,3.29,"If you can conjure up an exciting idea in someone's mind,"],[228,85.0,2.42,"you have done something wondrous."],[228,87.67,3.88,"A little piece of you has become part of them."],[228,92.17,4.42,"In March 2015, a scientist named Sophie Scott gave a TED Talk:"],[228,96.84,2.46,"“What I’m going to do now is just play some examples"],[228,99.3,1.79,"of real human beings laughing."],[228,101.1,2.71,"And I want you just to think about the sounds people make"],[228,103.81,3.54,"and how odd that can be, and in fact, how primitive laughter is as a sound."],[228,107.35,2.75,"It's much more like an animal call than it is like speech."],[228,110.11,3.59,"So here we’ve got some laughter for you— the first one is pretty joyful.”"],[228,128.29,4.04,"Within minutes, Sophie had the entire audience cracking up."],[228,132.88,3.38,"She's one of the world's leading researchers on laughter."],[228,136.63,4.21,"She was showing the audience just how weird a phenomenon laughter is."],[228,141.3,3.0,"“Now, this next guy, I need him to breathe."],[228,144.31,3.5,"There’s a point in this when I’m like you’ve got to get some air in there,"],[228,147.81,2.21,"because he just sounds like he’s breathing out."],[228,158.32,1.83,"This hasn’t been edited, this is him.”"],[228,167.29,3.71,"“More like an animal call than speech,” as Sophie put it."],[228,171.25,2.42,"Sophie’s talk was a lot of fun to listen to,"],[228,173.92,3.38,"but she gave her audience something more than just a good time."],[228,177.51,2.79,"She changed the way they think about laughter."],[228,180.47,5.59,"Sophie's core idea is that laughter exists as a way human beings form bonds"],[228,186.06,1.08,"with one another."],[228,187.27,3.63,"Her research shows that laughing strengthens relationships."],[228,191.27,3.38,"Nobody who listened to Sophie's talk will ever hear laughter"],[228,194.65,1.42,"the same way again."],[228,196.23,3.21,"A laugh isn’t just a silly sound in reaction to a joke—"],[228,199.44,3.8,"it’s a biological process through which we can connect with one another."],[228,203.37,2.75,"Sophie gave her audience a gift."],[228,206.2,3.59,"She gave them an idea that will be part of them forever."],[228,210.29,3.38,"In order for an audience to receive the gift of an idea,"],[228,213.83,4.84,"a speaker has to deliver the idea in a way that the audience can understand."],[228,218.88,1.42,"How does a speaker do that?"],[228,220.34,3.5,"Well, it can be helpful to think of a talk as a journey that a speaker"],[228,223.84,1.63,"and an audience take together."],[228,225.68,3.38,"You, the speaker, are the trusty tour guide."],[228,229.47,1.5,"To be a good tour guide,"],[228,230.98,2.92,"a speaker must start where the audience is,"],[228,234.1,3.17,"and must be careful not to lose anyone by rushing ahead"],[228,237.28,2.17,"or constantly changing direction."],[228,239.49,3.96,"The goal is to lead the audience to a beautiful new place,"],[228,243.45,1.42,"step by step."],[228,245.03,2.34,"And this is done using language."],[228,247.58,3.75,"Language is a very powerful tool. Let’s prove it."],[228,251.5,3.8,"Imagine an elephant with its trunk painted bright red,"],[228,255.46,1.58,"waving the trunk to and fro"],[228,257.04,3.38,"in sync with the shuffling steps of a giant orange parrot,"],[228,260.42,3.13,"dancing on the elephant’s head and shrieking over and over:"],[228,263.55,2.33,"“let’s do the fandango!”"],[228,266.14,2.71,"You have just formed in your mind an image of something"],[228,268.85,2.29,"that has never existed in history,"],[228,271.14,3.34,"except in the minds of the people who have heard that sentence."],[228,274.85,2.5,"A single sentence can do that."],[228,278.32,3.17,"The fact that we can transfer ideas in this way"],[228,281.49,2.63,"is why speaking skills are so important."],[228,284.36,2.67,"Language builds our world."],[228,287.16,2.5,"Our ideas make us who we are."],[228,289.87,4.34,"And speakers who have figured out how to spread their ideas into others’ minds"],[228,294.21,3.13,"have the power to make an incredible impact."],[228,297.63,2.67,"Do you have ideas that deserve a wider audience?"],[228,300.55,3.25,"Focusing on what gift you would like to give your audience,"],[228,303.8,2.09,"or what journey you might lead them on,"],[228,306.01,3.17,"are two great ways to start preparing your talk."],[229,0.72,4.08,"How did over one billion years of rock go missing?"],[229,4.8,3.68,"The Grand Canyon contains one of the most complete geological records on Earth."],[229,8.48,4.08,"Its layers are an open book laying out the sordid history of our planet."],[229,12.56,1.68,"But not the whole history."],[229,14.24,3.44,"There are millions of years missing, even over one billion years in some locations."],[229,17.68,3.52,"That’s quite a lot of missing pages…"],[229,21.2,1.36,"So… where did they go?"],[229,22.56,2.96,"Hello. I'm Sage, and this is Crash Course Geology."],[229,30.0,1.92,"Ah, the Grand Canyon."],[229,31.92,2.96,"It’s big. It’s grand. It’s canyon-y."],[229,34.88,3.36,"The Indigenous Paiute describe it as a “mountain lying down.”"],[229,38.24,4.72,"And nineteenth-century geologist John Wesley Powell called it a “library of the gods.”"],[229,42.96,0.96,"And for good reason!"],[229,43.92,3.28,"The stripes on the canyon’s walls are storytellers called strata,"],[229,47.2,3.68,"layers of rock that record what the planet was like in the deep past."],[229,50.88,2.88,"These are the three main chunks of strata in the Grand Canyon:"],[229,53.76,5.6,"the Vishnu Basement Rocks, the Grand Canyon Supergroup, and the Layered Paleozoic Rocks."],[229,59.36,3.2,"A beautiful array of igneous, metamorphic, and sedimentary."],[229,62.56,1.12,"Lookin’ good, ol’ GC!"],[229,63.68,3.28,"Strata can tell us about all sorts of geologic activity:"],[229,66.96,5.04,"floods, tectonic movements, changes in sea level, volcanic eruptions, and even more."],[229,72.0,4.32,"And because strata are well-preserved and exposed in the Grand Canyon,"],[229,76.32,4.08,"geologists can read these rocks to uncover the story of our planet—"],[229,80.4,5.2,"including the mystery of why some chapters are missing."],[229,85.6,5.76,"The layers know something… but they’re just not talking."],[229,91.36,3.52,"Or maybe… I just gotta speak their language."],[229,94.88,3.92,"If strata are chapters in Earth’s biography, then sediment is the alphabet:"],[229,98.8,3.84,"worn-down fragments of rock that get moved and deposited somewhere else."],[229,102.64,1.76,"That’s where the story really begins."],[229,104.4,2.21,"I mean, we can’t read anything without the ABCs!"],[229,106.61,1.15,"Through sedimentology,"],[229,107.76,4.64,"geologists study how rocks transform into sediment and sediment transforms into rocks."],[229,112.4,2.56,"Which goes a little something like this:"],[229,114.96,3.2,"weathering wears down rocks, creating sediment."],[229,118.16,6.88,"Erosion by water, wind, ice, or gravity physically removes that sediment and puts it somewhere else."],[229,125.04,2.64,"Often, it’s deposited in basins:"],[229,127.68,2.96,"areas of land that sit at slightly lower elevations"],[229,130.64,1.52,"than the surrounding landscape."],[229,132.16,3.68,"Like the massive Colorado River Basin, home to the Grand Canyon."],[229,135.84,3.12,"When conditions are right, sediment can get buried so deep"],[229,138.96,3.2,"that it turns into solid sedimentary rock."],[229,142.16,4.96,"Layers of rock pile up as beds, layered beds become strata, and voilà—"],[229,147.12,2.64,"another juicy chapter in Earth’s tell-all memoir."],[229,149.76,1.52,"Dwayne and I are in a book club."],[229,151.28,2.48,"Oh, and by the way, if you’re also really into rocks,"],[229,153.76,1.68,"you should check out the SciShow Rocks Box."],[229,155.44,2.4,"A cool rock delivered straight to your door every month!"],[229,157.84,2.32,"I mean, hat could be better than that?"],[229,160.16,1.36,"Check out the link below to learn more."],[229,161.52,3.68,"Except Earth is constantly editing and rewriting the record."],[229,165.2,2.88,"In a lot of places, erosion washes away the evidence,"],[229,168.08,3.12,"or the strata are simply buried too deep to observe."],[229,171.2,2.24,"But not in the Grand Canyon!"],[229,173.44,4.0,"Here, the rocks are both very old and very well-preserved."],[229,177.44,6.0,"We can use it to look back 270 million years ago at a minimum."],[229,183.44,5.04,"But most of the canyon itself was carved only in the past 5 to 6 million years"],[229,188.48,2.16,"by erosion from the Colorado River."],[229,190.64,1.44,"It’s kind of a brainteaser—"],[229,192.08,5.36,"the Grand Canyon itself is much, much younger than even the youngest rocks it’s made of."],[229,197.44,4.8,"Only in geology can something that happened 5 million years ago qualify as “recent history.”"],[229,202.24,2.72,"Also, sedimentary rocks hold clues to what a"],[229,204.96,2.56,"place was like at the time the sediment was deposited."],[229,207.52,3.36,"Ripples, mud cracks, and angled layers called cross-beds"],[229,210.88,3.68,"can tell us which way the wind was blowing or water was flowing,"],[229,214.56,1.6,"how intensely it was doing so,"],[229,216.16,3.23,"and if there was a dry spell after sediment was deposited."],[229,219.44,1.6,"But beyond a snapshot in time,"],[229,221.04,5.68,"rocks can also tell a bigger story over millions, even billions, of years."],[229,226.72,3.36,"Stratigraphy, or the study of the geological record through strata,"],[229,230.08,2.32,"is how geologists understand the relationships"],[229,232.4,3.52,"between layers of rock and the events from the deep past."],[229,235.92,2.0,"Come on, Dwayne, we’re going to the bottom of this baby."],[229,237.92,2.8,"No, I didn’t rent a mule— you know their teeth weird me out."],[229,240.72,1.28,"But yes, I will carry you."],[229,242.0,3.84,"At the canyon’s top layer are sedimentary strata that show periods"],[229,245.84,2.72,"when the landscape was at the bottom of a shallow sea."],[229,248.56,2.16,"Sediment deposited during those watery times"],[229,250.72,2.72,"contains fossils of marine organisms that died here."],[229,253.44,3.68,"But we can also tell there were times when sea levels dropped and the waters retreated,"],[229,257.12,2.88,"because those sedimentary layers have no marine fossils."],[229,260.0,1.84,"Deeper down and further back in the past,"],[229,261.84,3.92,"we can find evidence of early single-celled life in that middle section of rock"],[229,265.76,2.0,"called the Grand Canyon Supergroup."],[229,267.76,0.64,"Free band name."],[229,268.4,1.36,"And at the very bottom of the canyon,"],[229,269.76,2.56,"the metamorphic and igneous Vishnu Basement layer"],[229,272.32,1.52,"— better free band name—"],[229,273.84,4.88,"tells us what Earth’s crust was like over 1.3 billion years ago,"],[229,278.72,4.8,"as volcanic island chains smashed into what was then the edge of our continent."],[229,283.52,3.28,"But stratigraphy doesn’t just tell us what happened in Earth’s history."],[229,286.8,4.0,"It can also help us figure out when things happened, through relative dating."],[229,290.8,3.12,"Relative dating uses relationships between strata to determine whether"],[229,293.92,3.68,"an event in the rock record happened before or after other events."],[229,297.6,2.48,"This technique can’t put a number on how old rocks are—"],[229,300.08,4.0,"that’s what absolute dating methods are for, which we’ll get into in the next episode."],[229,304.08,1.92,"But it can help us put a timeline together."],[229,306.0,1.04,"Oh what’s that, Dwayne?"],[229,307.04,2.72,"Your dating app keeps recommending 500 million-year-old rocks,"],[229,309.76,2.4,"even though you put your age limit at 200 million?"],[229,312.16,2.32,"I think we’re talking about different kinds of dating, buddy."],[229,314.48,4.16,"Geologists have a few guiding principles for figuring out how old a layer of rock is."],[229,318.64,2.72,"One of them is the principle of faunal succession."],[229,321.36,4.32,"They use fossils of species that were only alive during a specific time period"],[229,325.68,2.48,"to infer the age of the rocks they’re found in."],[229,328.16,2.4,"Like, trilobites were on Earth for a limited time—"],[229,330.56,3.68,"and different types of these creepy crawlies had brief moments to shine."],[229,334.24,4.64,"So when the same type of trilobites are found in rock layers in two different places,"],[229,338.88,2.16,"we can assume they’re around the same age."],[229,341.04,4.56,"Also, we can assume that higher-up rock layers formed after the rocks below them."],[229,345.6,2.4,"That follows the principle of superposition:"],[229,348.0,3.84,"rocks below other rocks are older than the layers above."],[229,351.84,2.4,"It’s kind of like making sand art."],[229,354.24,3.2,"You can tell I added the orange sand first because it’s at the bottom,"],[229,357.44,2.72,"and the green sand last because it’s at the top."],[229,360.16,4.56,"And each layer of sand in between is in the exact chronological order I poured them in."],[229,364.72,2.64,"Then there’s the principle of original horizontality:"],[229,367.36,3.92,"sediment beds are deposited in flat-ish lines, pulled by gravity."],[229,371.28,4.56,"So if sedimentary rock layers don’t rest roughly horizontally, we can assume something—"],[229,375.84,2.8,"like shifting tectonic plates—"],[229,378.64,3.36,"knocked them into a wonky shape after the sediment solidified into stone."],[229,382.0,2.08,"The same goes for layers of igneous rock."],[229,384.08,4.96,"Sometimes, one layer of rock just barges into another at an angle."],[229,389.04,2.48,"According to the principle of cross-cutting relation,"],[229,391.52,4.64,"rock layers doing the interrupting must be younger than the rock being interrupted."],[229,396.16,1.76,"Classic younger sibling energy."],[229,397.92,3.76,"Let’s take a look at some of these principles at play in the Grand Canyon."],[229,401.68,4.0,"We can assume the Vishnu Basement Rocks are older than any rocks above them,"],[229,405.68,2.24,"based on the principle of superposition."],[229,407.92,5.28,"And the chunk of igneous rock must be younger than the metamorphic rock it intruded into,"],[229,413.2,2.48,"because of the principle of cross-cutting relation."],[229,415.68,3.6,"Above the basement rocks, the Grand Canyon Supergroup tilts at an angle."],[229,419.28,2.4,"From the principle of original horizontality,"],[229,421.68,4.16,"we can assume geological forces shoved those rocks out of position."],[229,425.84,1.12,"And above that?"],[229,426.96,5.36,"The layers lie horizontally, so we know that they haven’t been disturbed since they first formed."],[229,432.32,6.88,"All that covers a massive timespan: the rocks at the bottom are roughly 1.75 billion years old,"],[229,439.2,3.12,"and the ones on top are around 270 million."],[229,442.32,2.48,"That’s older than the oldest dinosaurs!"],[229,444.8,1.04,"But I know what you’re thinking:"],[229,445.84,3.28,"Sage, when are we going to talk about the billion years of missing rock?"],[229,449.12,1.49,"Now! Now is the time my friends!"],[229,450.61,2.91,"The Grand Canyon’s rock record is astonishing."],[229,453.52,4.72,"But it actually covers less geologic time than the giant chunks that are missing!"],[229,458.24,3.2,"These gaps in the timeline are called unconformities,"],[229,461.44,4.96,"boundaries between two sections of rock that represent a gap in the geological record."],[229,466.4,4.64,"The results look a bit different depending on the rocks that were around at the time."],[229,471.04,3.68,"And these different types of unconformities have different names."],[229,474.72,2.64,"Like our first example: a nonconformity."],[229,477.36,2.16,"I know, it's annoyingly similar to “unconformity”."],[229,479.52,0.8,"Just go with me."],[229,480.32,5.28,"A nonconformity is a surface where erosion wears down a layer of igneous or metamorphic rock,"],[229,485.6,2.96,"before new sediments are eventually deposited on top."],[229,488.56,1.28,"You can see that here:"],[229,489.84,2.96,"The rocks at the bottom are the Grand Canyon's metamorphic basement"],[229,492.8,3.12,"that formed about 1.75 billion years ago."],[229,495.92,2.72,"But the top of these rocks were eroded,"],[229,498.64,4.8,"so the timeline doesn’t pick up again until just over one billion years later,"],[229,503.44,2.32,"when the layers of sedimentary rock begin."],[229,505.76,2.56,"You can also get a disconformity,"],[229,508.32,4.16,"where erosion wears down the top horizontal layers of sedimentary rock."],[229,512.48,4.96,"Then more sediment washes in, stacking other horizontal layers on top."],[229,517.44,3.76,"And if the older rocks were tilted at an angle before they're eroded,"],[229,521.2,3.28,"you've got yourself an angular unconformity."],[229,524.48,4.24,"The most famous gap in the Grand Canyon’s timeline happens along these two spots,"],[229,528.72,3.52,"where the Layered Paleozoic Rocks meet the Vishnu Basement Rocks—"],[229,532.24,3.36,"or, in some places, touch the Grand Canyon Supergroup."],[229,535.6,2.96,"It’s called… the Great Unconformity."],[229,538.56,0.88,"I don’t know what to tell ya."],[229,539.44,2.56,"Geologists don’t, like, take a class on how to name stuff."],[229,542.0,6.64,"In some spots, the Great Unconformity skips about 250 million years of the geological record."],[229,548.64,4.8,"In others, as much as 1.2 billion years of history are just missing."],[229,553.44,6.0,"That’s about 25 percent of Earth’s biography, either erased or never recorded."],[229,559.44,3.2,"And while the Grand Canyon might be the easiest place to see it,"],[229,562.64,3.2,"this gap exists in rock layers everywhere."],[229,565.84,1.52,"It’s just usually hidden."],[229,567.36,3.6,"So what’d Earth do, take a gap year that turned into a cool one billion years?"],[229,570.96,1.84,"Well, there's a couple theories."],[229,572.8,2.24,"One possibility is that powerful glaciers"],[229,575.04,4.16,"eroded the landscape during a period when Earth’s surface was totally frozen."],[229,579.2,1.92,"Another theory proposes that when a past"],[229,581.12,2.96,"supercontinent called Rodinia began to break apart,"],[229,584.08,4.56,"the land shot upward, and erosion wiped away a billion years of evidence,"],[229,588.64,3.12,"kinda like a giant Etch A Sketch."],[229,591.76,4.32,"And still others theorize that what looks like one global event actually took place"],[229,596.08,4.88,"over multiple periods of massive erosion or little sediment deposition."],[229,600.96,3.04,"It’s a mystery geologists haven’t quite solved yet!"],[229,604.0,2.4,"You’ve evaded me this time, Great Unconformity."],[229,606.4,1.52,"But one day… I’ll catch ya."],[229,608.08,4.24,"The Grand Canyon’s stunning layers might be a familiar sight in road trip photo albums."],[229,612.32,2.48,"But they’re so much more than that."],[229,614.8,3.64,"They’re the best record we’ve got of what happened in our planet’s distant past."],[229,618.44,2.76,"And let me tell you, it’s a real page-turner."],[229,621.2,1.68,"Even when some chapters are missing,"],[229,622.88,3.44,"reading rocks helps us understand Earth’s many phases and changes,"],[229,626.32,3.52,"shaped by forces that still act on the planet today."],[229,629.84,4.64,"Rocks have stories to tell us— and stories they’re keeping to themselves."],[229,634.48,1.28,"At least for now."],[229,635.76,3.36,"Next time, we’re headed back in geologic time. I’ll see you then."],[229,639.12,2.24,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Geology"],[229,641.36,2.24,"which was filmed at our studio in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[229,643.6,2.32,"and was made with the help of all these nice people."],[229,645.92,3.12,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[229,649.04,2.0,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[230,0.08,4.96,"There’s a beach in Hawaii that shouldn’t exist, geologically speaking."],[230,5.04,1.76,"The sand is green."],[230,6.8,2.72,"Regular sand is made of lots of different materials,"],[230,9.52,2.08,"but the most common ones are pieces of"],[230,11.6,4.8,"the minerals quartz and feldspar, plus shells and fragments of other stuff."],[230,16.4,4.0,"Papakōlea Beach in Hawaii, however, is made of something else."],[230,20.4,2.4,"Fragments of a mineral called olivine."],[230,22.8,3.92,"Finding pieces of olivine that are big enough to see with the naked eye"],[230,26.72,2.56,"is somewhat uncommon, so you can imagine"],[230,29.28,3.52,"how rare it would be to find an entire beach made out of it."],[230,32.8,4.4,"And the story of how so much of this mineral got in one spot and formed"],[230,37.2,4.77,"a giant green beach actually begins far below the beach itself."],[230,45.67,2.65,"Like the other minerals that make up sand,"],[230,48.32,3.04,"olivine doesn’t start off small and sand sized."],[230,51.36,4.64,"Bigger pieces of it had to be broken down into those tiny pieces over time."],[230,56.0,2.72,"It takes a very specific set of events for"],[230,58.72,3.04,"olivine to make its way to Earth's surface at all."],[230,61.76,1.76,"And when it manages to do so,"],[230,63.52,4.24,"it's usually found inside a kind of volcanic rock called basalt."],[230,67.76,5.12,"Globally, most basalts don’t have large, visible olivine crystals."],[230,72.88,3.36,"If there are any, they’re usually really really tiny …"],[230,76.24,2.72,"like microscopically tiny most of the time."],[230,78.96,4.32,"And when bigger pieces of olivine do manage to make their way to the surface,"],[230,83.28,2.08,"they don’t usually last very long."],[230,85.36,3.52,"This makes the odds of them forming an entire beach pretty low."],[230,88.88,4.64,"To understand why big bits of olivine often don’t make their way to the surface,"],[230,93.52,4.0,"we need to travel to where most olivine on the planet actually is,"],[230,97.52,2.16,"and that's in the Earth's upper mantle."],[230,99.68,3.44,"In fact, over half of the upper mantle is olivine,"],[230,103.12,2.4,"where it’s part of a rock called peridotite."],[230,105.52,2.24,"The Earth's upper mantle ranges from"],[230,107.76,4.16,"just below the crust to as deep as about 400km."],[230,111.92,3.92,"And this puts peridotite under a LOT of heat and pressure."],[230,115.84,1.92,"It’s not pure liquid, though."],[230,117.76,4.88,"It’s still semi-solid but under certain conditions it will start to melt."],[230,122.64,2.4,"When it melts, it forms magma,"],[230,125.04,3.28,"and that magma will start to make its way to the surface."],[230,128.32,3.12,"This journey to the surface makes it start to cool again,"],[230,131.44,4.08,"and this causes minerals to slowly crystallize out of the magma."],[230,135.52,4.0,"The first mineral to crystallize out of most magmas is olivine."],[230,139.52,3.92,"But those olivines that crystallize are much denser than the magma"],[230,143.44,3.2,"they’re crystallizing in, so as time goes on,"],[230,146.64,3.28,"they end up sinking to the bottom of the magma reservoir."],[230,149.92,4.96,"Now different minerals crystallize and separate from the liquid as the magma"],[230,154.88,5.2,"continues to cool, and this is a process that geologists call fractional crystallization."],[230,160.08,2.8,"The longer the magma sits in the reservoir,"],[230,162.88,4.0,"the more time all of the olivine has to settle to the bottom."],[230,166.88,5.36,"If a magma reservoir then ends up erupting after sitting there for a really long time,"],[230,172.24,5.36,"the lava that comes out probably won’t have much olivine in it because it’s coming"],[230,177.6,5.6,"from the top part of the reservoir first and all of the olivine is at the bottom by now."],[230,183.2,5.68,"But, if the magma reaches the surface really fast, before the olivine has settled,"],[230,188.88,3.36,"then more of it can reach the surface during an eruption,"],[230,192.24,2.32,"carried in a host rock of basalt."],[230,194.56,2.8,"This rock from Iceland is a good example."],[230,197.36,4.24,"It’s a piece of something called an Olivine Picrite Hyaloclastite,"],[230,201.6,3.68,"and I was so excited when I found that I actually started crying,"],[230,205.28,3.44,"which also why I put a picture of it on my sweater vest."],[230,208.72,2.08,"This rock is not for Rocks Box, though."],[230,210.8,3.28,"This one is mine, so you can’t have it, but you can look at it."],[230,214.08,5.12,"The olivine picrite part of the name just means that it has a TON of olivine in it,"],[230,219.2,3.04,"and the hyaloclastite part means that it erupted"],[230,222.24,4.88,"underneath a glacier and forms a kind of glassy rind on the surface."],[230,227.12,2.8,"Not all magma can reach the surface fast enough"],[230,229.92,2.56,"to become an olivine picrite, though."],[230,232.48,4.24,"Most magma sits around in the crust for a while before erupting."],[230,236.72,4.72,"And even if a volcano does manage to erupt lava with a ton of olivine,"],[230,241.44,3.92,"the amount of olivine can change even in the middle of an eruption."],[230,245.36,4.64,"There was an eruption in Iceland in 2021 where only the earliest lava"],[230,250.0,3.6,"that erupted contained any visible olivine crystals."],[230,253.6,4.16,"So the early lavas formed from deeper, hotter magma that traveled"],[230,257.76,4.72,"really quickly to the surface and allowed the olivine to make the journey with it."],[230,262.48,2.88,"This is actually the eruption I have tattooed on my arm."],[230,265.36,3.6,"I visited the site in 2021 and got this sample,"],[230,268.96,4.24,"which does have some olivine, from right about here."],[230,273.2,3.76,"But the lava that came from the eruption later on looked completely different,"],[230,276.96,2.56,"and had almost no visible olivine."],[230,279.52,3.28,"Like this one, which was collected right here."],[230,282.8,2.72,"By the time the last lava reaches the surface,"],[230,285.52,2.24,"the olivine has settled out already."],[230,287.76,2.64,"Plus, the original magma had mixed with magma"],[230,290.4,3.28,"from a different reservoir with less olivine in it."],[230,293.68,2.88,"While this makes it harder for olivine to get to the surface,"],[230,296.56,3.04,"it also makes it a really valuable tool for volcanologists."],[230,299.6,4.72,"Olivine is so sensitive to the timing of an eruption that we can use it to"],[230,304.32,2.8,"help us understand how long the magma that"],[230,307.12,3.2,"carried it sat in the reservoir before it erupted."],[230,310.32,4.08,"This is actually something I’m doing with my own rock samples for my PhD."],[230,314.4,4.4,"I study rocks called PUBs or plagioclase ultraphyric basalts,"],[230,318.8,2.64,"which is basically just convoluted geology speak"],[230,321.44,3.84,"for having a lot of these big white plagioclase crystals in it."],[230,325.28,3.6,"However, they do also have a TINY bit of olivine in them,"],[230,328.88,5.12,"and by calculating how long the olivine sat in the magma before it erupted,"],[230,334.0,2.56,"we’re able to figure out how long the lava"],[230,336.56,3.44,"as a whole was stored in its magma reservoir."],[230,340.0,2.4,"We were also able to figure out that the magma"],[230,342.4,3.6,"that made this rock wasn’t just stored in one place."],[230,346.0,2.8,"It started out in a really deep magma reservoir,"],[230,348.8,2.56,"and then moved to a slightly shallower one,"],[230,351.36,2.88,"and then moved again to an even shallower one"],[230,354.24,2.0,"before finally erupting at the surface."],[230,356.24,2.16,"And it was possible to learn all of that by"],[230,358.4,4.72,"looking at the chemistry of both the olivine and the plagioclase in the rocks."],[230,363.12,3.84,"So olivine is essentially like a speedometer for the eruption."],[230,366.96,3.52,"But now that we’ve got these olivine crystals up on the surface,"],[230,370.48,2.32,"how do they become part of a green beach?"],[230,372.8,3.68,"Like everything else in geology, it’s all about location."],[230,376.48,4.4,"See, the olivine on that beach erupted from the Hawaiian volcano Mauna Loa,"],[230,380.88,5.2,"and Hawaii is well known for having lots of olivine rich volcanic eruptions."],[230,386.08,5.04,"Basically, the magma beneath the Hawaiian islands usually erupts really quickly,"],[230,391.12,2.32,"too quickly for the olivine to settle to the"],[230,393.44,2.72,"bottom of the reservoir or mix with anything else."],[230,396.16,2.8,"This allows all that beautiful olivine to actually"],[230,398.96,2.4,"end up in the volcanic rock at the surface."],[230,401.36,3.2,"Crystals of olivine are pretty susceptible to erosion,"],[230,404.56,3.6,"but the basalt they’re found in is even more vulnerable to it."],[230,408.16,2.48,"So when a volcano is located on a coast,"],[230,410.64,4.16,"the basalt that forms the volcano itself is going to get worn away."],[230,414.8,3.28,"If you’ve heard of the famous black sand beach in Iceland,"],[230,418.08,3.84,"that black sand is made of eroded bits of volcanic rock"],[230,421.92,3.6,"and it does have some olivine crystals in the sand there,"],[230,425.52,2.4,"but it’s nothing compared to Papakōlea Beach."],[230,427.92,2.08,"The reason for this comes down to the"],[230,430.0,3.12,"shape of the bay the Hawaiian beach is located in."],[230,433.12,2.64,"On other volcanic coasts, like Iceland,"],[230,435.76,3.6,"most of the olivine is going to get carried out to sea and sink,"],[230,439.36,2.0,"just like it does in the magma reservoir."],[230,441.36,3.28,"But at Papakōlea Beach, the half moon shape of the bay"],[230,444.64,4.56,"creates a circular wave pattern that keeps bringing the dense olivine"],[230,449.2,2.64,"back up onto the beach every time it sinks."],[230,451.84,3.6,"This concentrates it in a way that isn’t seen anywhere else in the world."],[230,455.44,2.64,"This beach only exists thanks to a geographic"],[230,458.08,2.8,"quirk and a well-timed volcanic eruption."],[230,460.88,0.96,"Pretty cool, right?"],[230,461.84,1.92,"And if you’re a Rocks Box subscriber,"],[230,463.76,3.12,"you’ll get your own sample of olivine in basalt this month!"],[230,466.88,2.56,"Now, they are not from Papakōlea Beach."],[230,469.44,3.52,"That’s a protected landmark, and taking rocks from there is illegal."],[230,472.96,2.16,"So instead, we found some ethically and legally"],[230,475.12,2.16,"collected samples you can have at home."],[230,477.28,3.12,"You could even theoretically do the same chemical analysis"],[230,480.4,3.36,"that I did to find out how long yours took to erupt …"],[230,483.76,2.72,"if you can manage to get the grant money for that kind of thing."],[230,486.48,4.32,"Every month, Rocks Box subscribers receive a piece of geologic history in the mail."],[230,490.8,2.4,"Whether they’re minerals, rocks, or fossils,"],[230,493.2,3.76,"each item teaches you about our planet or what has lived on it in some way."],[230,496.96,2.16,"If you’ve been wanting to join the Rocks Box club,"],[230,499.12,1.68,"some new subscriptions just opened up"],[230,500.8,3.28,"and you can find them at Complexly.store/rocks."],[230,504.08,4.4,"Or if you prefer to pick out your own, we also have minerals for individual sale"],[230,508.48,4.4,"and plenty of other merch, like t-shirts, jeweler’s loupes, and stickers."],[230,512.88,3.36,"To learn more about the Rocks Box or just go rock shopping,"],[230,516.24,2.64,"visit Complexly.store/rocks."],[230,518.88,1.04,"Thanks for watching!"],[231,6.96,5.21,"In the 1970s, psychoanalyst Samuel Dunkell proposed a curious,"],[231,12.18,3.96,"albeit almost completely unsubstantiated, theory"],[231,16.14,3.63,"that the position you sleep in reflects your personality."],[231,20.02,4.3,"For example, preferring to snooze in the so-called royal position"],[231,24.31,1.92,"indicated self-confidence,"],[231,26.23,4.5,"while sleeping semi-fetal meant you’re sensible and well adjusted."],[231,31.15,3.21,"While Dunkell’s ideas lack any definitive proof,"],[231,34.45,2.75,"how we sleep can impact our health."],[231,37.45,2.92,"So, what’s the best position to sleep in?"],[231,40.79,3.75,"Sleep positions go by countless creative names—"],[231,44.54,4.38,"the zombie, mountain climber, free faller, and soldier—"],[231,49.67,4.75,"yet sleep experts typically simplify them into four basic types:"],[231,54.8,3.13,"left side, right side, stomach, or back."],[231,58.76,1.29,"Until the age of one,"],[231,60.06,3.59,"it is recommended that babies sleep on their backs to lower their risk"],[231,63.64,1.79,"of choking and suffocation."],[231,65.64,3.59,"As young children, we start to move around more in our sleep,"],[231,69.4,3.25,"usually ending up on our sides and backs."],[231,72.82,4.71,"And this tendency to avoid sleeping on our stomachs only grows with age."],[231,77.74,3.54,"By adulthood, most people prefer side-sleeping,"],[231,81.45,2.25,"while fewer favor back-sleeping,"],[231,83.7,3.59,"and stomach-sleeping is the least preferred overall."],[231,87.46,4.0,"But of course, individual sleep styles are highly personal:"],[231,91.59,2.67,"some people are sworn belly sleepers,"],[231,94.34,4.3,"while others can't nod off unless they're sprawled out on their backs."],[231,99.05,3.46,"How we spend the night affects our bodies in several ways."],[231,102.6,4.42,"Different sleep postures shift the relative positions of our internal organs,"],[231,107.23,3.67,"which can either relieve or worsen certain health issues."],[231,111.23,4.21,"Others can leave the spine unsupported, straining nearby tissue,"],[231,115.57,5.0,"and preventing the muscles in the back, hips, or neck from fully relaxing."],[231,120.91,3.92,"This can then lead to tension and stiffness in the morning."],[231,125.37,4.5,"Take, for example, the least popular position: sleeping prone."],[231,130.17,4.88,"Of the four, this also tends to be the least recommended by sleep experts,"],[231,135.13,3.67,"as it's thought to stretch the natural curvature of your spine."],[231,139.09,1.79,"Yet there are unique situations"],[231,140.89,3.04,"when lying on your stomach may be beneficial."],[231,144.26,3.79,"For patients with certain respiratory conditions, for example,"],[231,148.06,3.67,"it allows the back of the lungs to expand more freely."],[231,152.15,2.04,"Sleeping on your back, on the other hand,"],[231,154.19,3.13,"is often preferred by those with lower back pain."],[231,157.94,2.79,"When paired with a small pillow under the knees,"],[231,160.74,4.25,"this position allows the spine to maintain its natural curve."],[231,165.33,4.0,"However, it may be less than ideal for your sleep partner."],[231,169.54,4.5,"For some people, lying on their back can cause the soft tissues of the throat"],[231,174.04,2.96,"and tongue to collapse into the airway."],[231,177.42,5.09,"Then, as the body tries to pull air through, these tissues vibrate,"],[231,182.51,2.79,"creating the sound we call snoring."],[231,185.56,5.0,"This is also why people with sleep apnea are often told to avoid this position."],[231,190.85,3.17,"This condition already involves pauses in breathing,"],[231,194.19,2.84,"and back sleeping can increase the likelihood"],[231,197.03,4.21,"that the airway collapses completely, blocking airflow."],[231,201.74,3.84,"Today, most experts generally recommend side-sleeping,"],[231,205.74,3.71,"particularly when combined with a pillow between the knees,"],[231,209.46,1.79,"to better align the spine."],[231,211.46,4.38,"But as it turns out, which side you lie on may make a difference."],[231,216.04,3.54,"For example, studies show that sleeping on the left side"],[231,219.59,4.42,"often reduces heartburn symptoms for those with acid reflux."],[231,224.34,3.71,"Your stomach is primarily located on the left side of your body,"],[231,228.18,3.25,"so this position situates it below the esophagus,"],[231,231.52,4.42,"allowing gravity to naturally keep stomach acid from flowing back up."],[231,236.19,3.25,"However, for people with congestive heart failure,"],[231,239.65,3.13,"who often have an enlarged left ventricle,"],[231,243.07,3.17,"sleeping on the right side can help relieve chest pressure"],[231,246.24,2.63,"and improve their overall quality of sleep."],[231,249.5,3.46,"Clearly, there’s no one-size-fits-all answer."],[231,253.16,4.0,"And attempting to reap the benefits of any one position can be tough,"],[231,257.34,4.25,"since most people shift positions 20 to 30 times per night"],[231,261.59,1.96,"without even knowing it."],[231,263.63,3.88,"Pillows and other sleep training devices can help you stay in one place."],[231,267.51,3.42,"But fortunately, unless you have a specific concern,"],[231,270.93,2.84,"sleep movement isn't always a bad thing."],[231,273.98,4.09,"It's how your body naturally relieves pressure, responds to discomfort,"],[231,278.06,2.29,"and ultimately prevents injury."],[231,280.74,2.92,"So, the next time you find yourself drifting"],[231,283.65,3.46,"to the free faller, soldier, or zombie,"],[231,287.12,2.13,"you can rest assured."],[232,4.3,6.21,"I am overwhelmed by all the conversations that we’ve been having --"],[232,10.54,1.5,"especially about AI."],[232,12.91,3.24,"I worry about what we're doing."],[232,17.45,2.1,"I worry about where we're going."],[232,20.32,5.94,"I worry that we're in danger of building this brave new world"],[232,26.29,3.47,"with all this data and no wisdom."],[232,31.2,1.24,"All this data --"],[232,33.83,1.4,"but no wisdom."],[232,35.83,2.37,"Is there room for wisdom?"],[232,38.23,4.17,"Where is the wisdom going to come from in this brave new world?"],[232,42.41,3.07,"And so I want to talk about a different kind of AI."],[232,45.48,5.17,"I want to talk about the AI that's absolutely necessary for us"],[232,50.68,2.37,"to give our children the future they deserve."],[232,53.08,4.14,"I want to talk about Ancient Intelligence AI."],[232,58.05,4.87,"I want to talk about Ancestral Intelligence AI."],[232,62.96,6.37,"Do our ancestors have anything to teach us about entering new eras"],[232,69.33,1.87,"and creating new worlds?"],[232,71.23,4.04,"Or should we just push all the old folks on one side?"],[232,75.94,2.8,"I'm a ninth-generation American,"],[232,78.78,1.77,"but I'm the first one in my family"],[232,80.54,3.2,"that was born with all my rights recognized by this government."],[232,83.78,1.8,"Why? Why?"],[232,85.62,3.47,"Because my mother and my father did everything they could"],[232,89.09,2.54,"to make sure that I grew up in a better world."],[232,91.66,2.44,"And I want to do that for my children."],[232,94.09,1.3,"But I'm afraid ..."],[232,96.63,1.74,"I don't know how to do that."],[232,98.36,1.57,"I'm worried ..."],[232,100.53,5.21,"because a new human civilization is being born,"],[232,105.77,2.3,"and my children are going to grow up in it."],[232,108.54,3.1,"And it is very different than the one I was born into."],[232,112.84,1.44,"I have four children."],[232,114.31,2.24,"Two of them are still sleeping in diapers."],[232,117.21,1.47,"Here's reality for them."],[232,119.55,1.17,"Just as a parent."],[232,121.99,1.47,"Their first crushes"],[232,124.82,2.67,"probably are not going to be a rock star or an athlete."],[232,127.53,4.14,"Their first crushes may well be an AI,"],[232,131.69,1.5,"may well be an AI."],[232,133.2,3.17,"That's a different human civilization than the one I grew up in."],[232,136.37,3.34,"As they get older, when they decide to start families,"],[232,139.74,4.7,"they might open up a laptop or a holographic interface"],[232,144.47,4.14,"and use biotech tools to design my grandchildren."],[232,149.75,3.17,"That's a different human civilization."],[232,152.95,3.17,"With longevity tech, and all these health breakthroughs,"],[232,156.15,4.61,"they might be running marathons at the age of 90 or 120."],[232,160.79,4.84,"When, heaven forbid, it's time for them to be buried,"],[232,165.66,2.33,"my children, who I'm looking at right now,"],[232,168.03,5.5,"might be buried on the Moon or on Mars,"],[232,173.57,1.6,"because within a hundred years,"],[232,175.21,2.47,"we're going to be a fully spacefaring civilization."],[232,177.71,2.6,"That's a different human civilization."],[232,180.34,1.5,"But I have a question about it."],[232,181.88,1.74,"Will it be human?"],[232,184.81,1.57,"And will it be civilized?"],[232,186.95,3.44,"The world that we are building, will it be human,"],[232,190.42,1.74,"and will it be civilized?"],[232,192.19,2.0,"Will it be a world of human flourishing,"],[232,194.22,3.14,"where all these technologies are helping people do great things?"],[232,197.36,4.67,"Or will be a world where robots with no hearts"],[232,202.07,2.64,"dominate human beings with no agency?"],[232,206.24,1.94,"That's my question."],[232,208.21,2.97,"Humanity now has a math problem"],[232,211.21,2.44,"that we're going to have to come together to solve,"],[232,213.68,3.27,"just what I would call the adaptation gap."],[232,218.05,5.34,"Technology is now proceeding on an exponential curve."],[232,223.42,6.11,"Robotics, biotech, AI, quantum computing, space exploration,"],[232,229.56,5.07,"these technologies are now growing their capability on an exponential curve."],[232,234.66,2.37,"Some of the breakthroughs"],[232,237.07,4.3,"that would have been the breakthrough of a decade just a few years ago"],[232,241.41,2.04,"are not even the breakthrough of the day."],[232,243.47,2.0,"Today, you can't even win a Twitter cycle,"],[232,245.51,2.2,"because by noon, something else has come out."],[232,247.75,2.5,"But humans don't develop that quickly."],[232,250.28,4.0,"Humans evolve on more of a linear slope."],[232,254.85,1.6,"Humans are not that much smarter,"],[232,256.49,4.1,"that much that much faster than we were 5,000 years ago,"],[232,260.62,1.67,"let alone five weeks ago."],[232,263.09,3.64,"And this gap is a gap that terrifies me."],[232,266.76,4.24,"This is the gap that, if we don't handle it properly,"],[232,271.04,3.14,"can lead to social unrest,"],[232,274.17,2.3,"where people don't have what they need,"],[232,276.47,1.64,"and they're left behind,"],[232,278.14,4.57,"and they feel that the future doesn't need them at all."],[232,282.75,3.04,"Now what I see a lot of people trying to do"],[232,285.78,5.84,"is to figure out some way to decelerate technology,"],[232,291.62,3.1,"is to figure out some way to use legislation and laws"],[232,294.76,3.37,"to decelerate the technology so we don't get left behind."],[232,298.13,2.07,"But I have bad news for you."],[232,301.76,2.47,"I spent a lot of time in Washington, D.C."],[232,307.0,5.0,"If you think that Congress can catch a train moving that fast"],[232,312.04,1.3,"and do something about it,"],[232,313.38,2.8,"you have never met a US senator, OK?"],[232,317.28,2.94,"They're not exactly lickety-split type people."],[232,320.55,2.0,"So --"],[232,323.65,3.94,"I think the better move is to accelerate humanity."],[232,327.62,1.5,"I think the better move"],[232,329.16,2.24,"is to accelerate humanity."],[232,331.43,1.83,"And there's a way to do that,"],[232,333.3,6.14,"but it requires both sides of the tech divide to come back"],[232,339.44,2.64,"to Ancient Intelligence,"],[232,342.11,2.07,"Ancestral Intelligence."],[232,344.21,2.67,"On the tech side, let me talk to my sisters and brothers"],[232,346.91,1.5,"in the technology community,"],[232,348.44,2.47,"and offer a prayer and an invitation."],[232,351.92,5.44,"We are going to have to have a new deal between Big Tech and humanity."],[232,357.39,5.04,"The original New Deal was between government and the people."],[232,362.46,2.64,"That's where the power was, the power was in government."],[232,365.13,3.44,"The future used to be written in laws in Washington, D.C."],[232,368.6,3.37,"Now the future is written in code, in Silicon Valley."],[232,372.0,2.64,"And so there has to be a new deal, a new agreement,"],[232,374.67,4.44,"a new social contract between Big Tech and human beings."],[232,380.54,2.64,"But right now, we're not on path to doing that."],[232,384.92,3.94,"Number one, our tech companies keep telling us things"],[232,388.85,1.64,"that we just don't believe."],[232,391.62,2.84,"They keep saying, \"Well, all this abundance,"],[232,394.49,2.14,"these robots are going to work over overtime,"],[232,396.66,1.47,"everything's going to be fine."],[232,398.13,3.2,"It's going to be so much abundance. Everything's going to be fine.\""],[232,401.37,1.23,"Nobody believes you."],[232,404.23,1.6,"Literally nobody believes you."],[232,407.04,1.97,"You can just stop saying that."],[232,410.27,3.64,"Abundance by itself doesn't solve your problems."],[232,413.94,1.74,"By the way, the opposite."],[232,415.71,3.84,"Just wheeling out a wheelbarrow into this room"],[232,419.58,3.0,"called AI, full of diamonds and gold,"],[232,422.62,3.67,"technically makes the room richer,"],[232,426.32,3.2,"but if I dump it out on this side of the room only ..."],[232,429.56,1.33,"you're not richer."],[232,430.93,2.44,"You're miserable."],[232,433.4,3.64,"Abundance without participation,"],[232,437.07,5.47,"abundance without inclusion, feels like scarcity."],[232,442.57,2.17,"It feels like scarcity."],[232,444.77,2.6,"You think you're scaling abundance,"],[232,447.41,4.3,"but we experience it as you scaling scarcity."],[232,451.75,2.74,"If you dump out all the good on one side of the room"],[232,455.79,3.54,"the people on the other side of the room, they're not just miserable."],[232,460.09,2.27,"At some point, their baby asks them a question."],[232,462.36,3.87,"\"Mommy, Daddy, how come we don't have what they have?\""],[232,467.46,3.0,"And now you're not just unhappy, you're ashamed."],[232,471.03,1.37,"You're humiliated."],[232,472.9,3.47,"I've worked in some of the toughest prisons in this world."],[232,476.41,4.37,"The one thing you don't want to do is take somebody who already has nothing"],[232,480.81,1.77,"and then humiliate them."],[232,482.61,3.04,"They become the most dangerous person in that prison."],[232,485.68,1.84,"Even if they only weigh 100 pounds."],[232,487.55,2.87,"We're not just risking mass unemployment,"],[232,490.42,2.6,"we're risking mass humiliation."],[232,493.06,1.9,"And that has to be avoided."],[232,494.96,3.67,"There's another reason that coming back to the people"],[232,498.63,5.0,"who embody Ancestral Intelligence at the grassroots level"],[232,503.67,1.53,"will be helpful:"],[232,505.24,2.94,"because it’d be helpful for you."],[232,508.2,4.94,"You are in a very difficult situation, if you're in the tech world."],[232,513.18,2.3,"You have tremendous power."],[232,515.51,2.34,"You have tremendous responsibilities."],[232,518.35,3.87,"You're not just cofounding companies and cofounding firms."],[232,522.25,3.4,"You're cofounding a new human civilization."],[232,526.56,2.04,"And there's a lot of temptation --"],[232,529.16,4.97,"money, fame, power, sex."],[232,534.83,3.1,"These things can ruin a person."],[232,538.6,3.87,"These things can ruin a nation."],[232,542.47,4.71,"And it's the old ways, Ancient Intelligence."],[232,548.04,3.74,"Don't underestimate those mosques"],[232,551.82,3.97,"and temples and synagogues and churches"],[232,555.82,3.24,"and Native American ceremonies and prayer circles"],[232,559.09,4.67,"for you to come home and get your head on straight."],[232,563.79,2.07,"Because you being in a greed and speed trap,"],[232,565.9,5.81,"where you feel the only values that matter are greed and speed."],[232,571.74,1.47,"It is not good for you."],[232,573.24,1.9,"It’s not good for your decision-making."],[232,575.14,1.53,"It’s not good for your heart."],[232,576.67,4.97,"Don't underestimate these little Black churches"],[232,581.68,4.3,"that had nothing but praying hands and marching feet,"],[232,585.98,1.83,"and changed a whole nation."],[232,587.82,2.23,"Don't underestimate your grandma."],[232,590.09,2.6,"Don't underestimate ancestral wisdom."],[232,592.72,3.27,"We need you to come back, and you need us too."],[232,596.03,1.23,"You need us too."],[232,597.29,3.57,"There is a way forward together."],[232,601.7,1.74,"And now on the other side."],[232,604.4,3.04,"I do want to say something to my friends on the grassroots side."],[232,608.67,4.84,"Yes, we want our tech folks to come back to Ancestral Intelligence --"],[232,613.54,3.3,"Ancient Intelligence, grassroots intelligence."],[232,617.91,3.84,"But we've gotten a little bit away from that stuff, too."],[232,622.65,3.94,"Those of us who work at the grassroots,"],[232,626.59,3.24,"whose hearts are with people who don't have very much,"],[232,629.86,2.77,"whose hearts are with people who have to choose"],[232,632.66,3.44,"between fewer pills or fewer meals this week."],[232,636.73,1.7,"That's where our hearts are."],[232,639.4,1.33,"Our hearts are with people"],[232,640.77,4.0,"whose main enemy isn't another rival tech firm,"],[232,644.81,3.0,"it's the check engine light."],[232,648.98,2.07,"The check engine light."],[232,651.08,2.04,"When that light comes on,"],[232,653.15,3.57,"and you don't have enough money to go and get your car looked at,"],[232,656.72,2.4,"that's the enemy of the people that we care about."],[232,659.16,2.13,"And I get it, I get it,"],[232,661.32,3.7,"but I want to say something to my grassroots sisters and brothers."],[232,665.8,1.53,"We've been a bit much."],[232,670.1,4.34,"We’ve been a bit much this last 10 years."],[232,674.47,3.9,"We've been kind of hard to be around."],[232,678.41,3.37,"Been a little bit loose with the name-calling."],[232,681.81,5.57,"Everybody's a racist and a sexist and a homophobe and a transphobe."],[232,687.42,1.64,"Everybody's some kind of phobe,"],[232,689.09,2.47,"to the point that now people are phobic about us,"],[232,691.59,5.64,"and don't want to be around us, because we're calling people out,"],[232,697.26,4.34,"and forget, sometimes, to call them in and to call them up."],[232,701.63,2.37,"We forget, on the grassroots side --"],[232,705.43,1.24,"We --"],[232,707.74,3.74,"You know, we say we're holding people accountable,"],[232,711.48,1.74,"but are we holding people?"],[232,714.54,1.44,"Are we holding people?"],[232,716.75,4.84,"I think we replaced \"I have a dream\""],[232,721.62,2.2,"with \"I have a complaint.\""],[232,727.12,1.5,"That wasn't a speech."],[232,729.33,1.5,"That wasn't a speech."],[232,731.89,4.34,"We forget that Dr King was a futurist."],[232,736.97,1.6,"He wasn't talking about history."],[232,738.6,2.57,"Dr King was trying to make tomorrow better."],[232,741.2,2.57,"Don't get temporal dissociation."],[232,743.81,3.64,"Dr King was a futurist trying to make tomorrow better."],[232,747.48,1.37,"That's why we love him."],[232,748.88,1.97,"And he said he has \"a dream.\""],[232,751.58,1.5,"A dream is about tomorrow."],[232,753.78,2.27,"And the thing about it, Ancestral Intelligence"],[232,756.09,2.54,"... is when you have a dream,"],[232,758.62,3.2,"and you’re trying to make something very hard happen,"],[232,761.86,2.64,"you don’t want to multiply enemies."],[232,765.46,2.44,"You don't want to cancel anybody."],[232,767.93,1.97,"You don't want to call anybody out."],[232,769.93,4.44,"When you have a dream for your children and a dream for all children,"],[232,774.4,3.17,"and a dream for the children of all species,"],[232,777.61,3.4,"you want to multiply friends."],[232,781.04,2.1,"You want to multiply friends."],[232,783.18,1.7,"And so any politics"],[232,784.91,4.37,"that has us calling people out and multiplying enemies"],[232,789.32,3.94,"is not consistent with Ancestral Intelligence."],[232,793.29,3.74,"And so both sides have to come back to the table"],[232,797.06,2.04,"and create a new world,"],[232,799.13,3.94,"a new human civilization that is human and is civilized."],[232,803.1,1.3,"What would it look like?"],[232,804.43,4.57,"It would look like more incubators and accelerators"],[232,809.04,2.57,"and venture studios and funds"],[232,811.61,6.94,"directed to pairing up grassroots folks and engineers,"],[232,818.58,3.0,"people with knowledge of technology,"],[232,821.62,3.0,"but also knowledge of what's happening at the grassroots level."],[232,824.62,4.0,"It would look like Andre Peart, who was formerly incarcerated,"],[232,828.62,3.84,"paired up with engineers, built something called Untapped Solutions."],[232,832.5,3.0,"They built a LinkedIn for formerly incarcerated people,"],[232,835.53,2.04,"getting people off the street and employed."],[232,837.6,4.14,"Now they're deploying AI agents to help social workers."],[232,841.77,3.74,"Noella Sudbury, a lawyer, teamed up with engineers,"],[232,845.51,4.41,"using AI to expunge people's prison records."],[232,849.95,4.87,"It would look like Phaedra Ellis-Lamkins and Diana Frappier at Promise."],[232,854.85,5.51,"They have built a billion-dollar company to help government not be more lethal,"],[232,860.36,2.54,"but more frugal and more helpful."],[232,862.93,4.37,"It would look like Tadzio Smith, my buddy at Rapport,"],[232,867.33,3.84,"a company backed by Reid Hoffman that I'm supporting,"],[232,871.2,5.84,"that's using AI to increase EQ, and not just IQ, inside of firms."],[232,877.04,2.37,"It would look like John Hope Bryant,"],[232,879.44,6.17,"who just launched HOPE AI to get thousands of grassroots people AI-literate."],[232,885.65,4.87,"He's going to get a billion people literate in AI in the next ten years,"],[232,890.55,1.8,"so we can create even more companies."],[232,892.39,2.54,"It would look like hope."],[232,894.96,1.53,"It would look like hope."],[232,896.53,4.8,"And so what I want to say to you, in closing, is this."],[232,902.87,2.54,"On the tech side,"],[232,905.43,2.3,"a little less greed and speed."],[232,908.61,5.44,"A little bit more sharing and caring on the grassroots side,"],[232,914.08,6.07,"a little bit less shame and blame, a little bit more space and grace."],[232,921.08,3.74,"If we put those two things together,"],[232,924.85,6.91,"what we'll have is a movement that has so much magic and so much genius,"],[232,931.79,3.14,"and so much hope and so much love,"],[232,934.97,1.57,"and so much power"],[232,936.57,5.21,"that what we create out of that will, believe it or not,"],[232,941.8,4.7,"someday be the Ancestral Intelligence"],[232,946.54,1.9,"for our children."],[232,949.04,1.3,"Thank you very much."],[233,8.67,6.01,"On October 17th, 1902, Paris detectives arrived at a grisly scene."],[233,14.93,3.55,"Joseph Reibel had been murdered at his workplace."],[233,18.85,3.54,"With no eyewitnesses, the officers had little to work from,"],[233,22.43,3.29,"until they discovered a shard of broken glass"],[233,25.9,2.46,"with several bloody fingerprints."],[233,28.69,3.67,"An investigator manually searched the police station's fingerprint records"],[233,32.36,2.5,"and eventually found a match."],[233,35.16,3.38,"Henri Scheffer, who had been arrested for theft the year before,"],[233,38.62,3.38,"was detained and later confessed to the murder."],[233,42.29,2.29,"This marked the first time in Europe"],[233,44.58,4.0,"that investigators solved a crime using fingerprints alone."],[233,48.92,1.33,"Over a century later,"],[233,50.25,3.5,"fingerprints remain one of the most common types of evidence"],[233,53.76,1.38,"in criminal courts."],[233,55.13,2.25,"But just how reliable are they?"],[233,57.8,2.5,"Humans, like other climbing species,"],[233,60.39,3.8,"are born with patterns of raised ridges and recessed furrows"],[233,64.19,3.92,"not only on their fingers but also along their hands and feet."],[233,68.11,2.96,"These friction ridges help provide a firmer grip,"],[233,71.15,4.38,"especially on wet surfaces, and increase our sensitivity to touch."],[233,75.61,2.84,"They begin to develop in the womb at about seven weeks,"],[233,78.45,5.05,"when the skin on the hands and feet swell to form smooth raised pads."],[233,83.79,4.3,"At around ten weeks, deeper layers begin to grow faster than the upper layers,"],[233,88.08,2.71,"causing the pads to buckle and fold."],[233,90.88,3.8,"This folding is guided by genetics and environmental factors"],[233,94.67,2.71,"and continues over the next several months."],[233,97.51,3.96,"Scientists have yet to fully understand the exact environmental factors at play,"],[233,101.47,2.63,"but it’s their randomness that leads to the formation"],[233,104.1,2.0,"of unique fingerprint patterns."],[233,106.44,4.34,"In fact, many experts believe it’s improbable that any two fingerprints,"],[233,110.77,2.34,"past or present, are exactly alike."],[233,113.28,2.67,"They're even different among identical twins."],[233,116.03,3.25,"People have likely known about the individual nature of fingerprints"],[233,119.28,1.0,"for centuries,"],[233,120.28,4.8,"but it wasn’t until the late 1800s that scientists began studying and classifying"],[233,125.08,1.42,"their different features."],[233,126.62,5.17,"By the early 1900s, prosecutors began utilizing fingerprints in courts,"],[233,131.92,3.71,"forever changing how detectives approach and analyze crime scenes."],[233,135.84,4.42,"Today, investigators often begin by searching for visible prints."],[233,140.39,2.0,"These include patent prints,"],[233,142.39,3.29,"left by transferable substances like blood or dirt,"],[233,145.85,4.34,"and plastic prints, which are impressions in soft, malleable materials,"],[233,150.27,3.54,"like caulking, wax, or even a newly painted fence."],[233,154.15,3.63,"However, most fingerprints aren't visible to the naked eye."],[233,157.94,1.71,"These are called latent prints,"],[233,159.66,4.17,"and they’re composed of the water, oils, proteins, and salts"],[233,163.83,2.38,"that ridge patterns leave on surfaces."],[233,166.33,2.67,"They can be revealed by dusting with a fine powder,"],[233,169.0,2.38,"which sticks to the water and oil trail."],[233,171.5,2.42,"If surfaces are porous or difficult to dust,"],[233,173.92,3.46,"investigators use chemical developers like Ninhydrin,"],[233,177.38,2.59,"which reacts with proteins left by fingers."],[233,180.51,4.75,"Forensic scientists continue to develop more sensitive and surface-specific tools."],[233,185.26,4.09,"For example, one experimental technique uses an electrical charge"],[233,189.35,3.54,"to capture the corrosion that fingerprint salts leave on metals,"],[233,192.98,1.88,"even if they have been wiped down."],[233,195.27,2.25,"Once investigators have collected prints,"],[233,197.53,3.13,"then they get to work matching them with potential suspects."],[233,200.74,4.17,"In certain cases, investigators will use automated computing systems"],[233,204.91,1.88,"that can narrow down potential matches"],[233,206.79,2.46,"found in national fingerprint databases."],[233,209.41,3.46,"Certified experts then compare minor fingerprint details,"],[233,212.96,2.92,"such as how the ridges branch and intersect,"],[233,215.96,1.83,"and the spacing of oil pores."],[233,218.0,3.54,"Any result must be blindly verified by a second expert"],[233,221.55,3.25,"before information is handed over to justice departments."],[233,225.14,3.25,"The fact that fingerprint patterns are unique for every individual"],[233,228.39,3.46,"makes them particularly strong evidence in criminal cases."],[233,231.94,3.04,"However, the system isn't infallible."],[233,235.31,4.0,"Prints are often smudged, distorted, or overlapped with other prints,"],[233,239.44,1.96,"which can make matching more challenging."],[233,241.49,1.67,"And it’s prone to human error—"],[233,243.49,4.46,"one 2011 study found that fingerprint experts falsely identified"],[233,247.99,3.71,"two different fingerprints as a match 0.1% of the time."],[233,251.91,5.21,"While this may seem low, the stakes are high for a wrongly accused defendant."],[233,257.63,4.0,"Further, there’s no universal standard on the number of ridge details"],[233,261.63,2.34,"that experts must analyze to make a match."],[233,264.18,3.55,"Many investigators stress that no conviction should be made"],[233,267.76,1.79,"on fingerprint evidence alone."],[233,270.18,3.71,"For these reasons, forensic experts continue to work to improve"],[233,273.89,2.21,"and standardize the fingerprinting process."],[233,276.4,2.71,"After all, when it comes to investigating crime,"],[233,279.15,2.88,"fingerprints have certainly left their mark."],[234,7.38,3.25,"You enter your living room, then pause."],[234,11.01,5.17,"You feel your mission’s momentum— but its aim is suddenly lost."],[234,16.51,4.38,"So, what just happened? And why does it keep happening?"],[234,21.27,1.71,"Well, you’re not alone;"],[234,23.23,5.92,"this is actually a common phenomenon scientists have termed the doorway effect."],[234,29.94,2.25,"According to psychological models,"],[234,32.2,6.96,"our brains parse life’s continuous flow of time and activity into distinct events."],[234,39.53,5.92,"As a situation unfolds, the brain develops a working model of the event."],[234,45.71,4.67,"It likely does this by employing its default mode network,"],[234,50.42,2.34,"the brain regions that work together"],[234,52.76,3.21,"to accomplish many resting thought processes."],[234,56.55,2.54,"Then, when there's a big enough change,"],[234,59.26,4.13,"the brain is thought to identify an event boundary,"],[234,63.6,3.88,"at which point it stores the prior event model,"],[234,67.48,3.46,"and begins developing a new working model."],[234,71.48,3.29,"During this switch, called an event update,"],[234,74.9,2.38,"parts of the default mode network"],[234,77.28,4.17,"seem to interact with the memory-storing hippocampus,"],[234,81.62,4.5,"perhaps transporting the experience into longer-term memory."],[234,86.71,4.13,"In the process, the brain clears its short-term memory,"],[234,90.84,4.42,"making room for the new information that’s already pouring in."],[234,95.67,4.38,"But sometimes that can be stuff you weren't quite done with."],[234,100.43,3.46,"It’s still not fully clear if the doorway effect,"],[234,103.93,3.13,"also called the location updating effect,"],[234,107.35,2.84,"befalls some people more than others."],[234,110.4,4.63,"But one study found that age had very little bearing."],[234,115.4,3.5,"Scientists are working to better understand the phenomenon"],[234,119.07,3.0,"by pinpointing the conditions that trigger it,"],[234,122.16,5.42,"which, yes, includes doorways, both physical and virtual."],[234,128.21,4.38,"In one study, college students in real and virtual spaces"],[234,132.75,5.63,"were asked to pick up objects, walk set distances carrying them out of view,"],[234,138.47,2.67,"then try to recall what they were holding."],[234,142.01,5.63,"Time and distance remained constant, but some traveled through a doorway."],[234,148.19,5.5,"Those who did tended to respond slower and be more likely to forget."],[234,154.44,2.46,"And passing through multiple doorways"],[234,156.9,3.34,"seems to worsen the memory-thwarting effect."],[234,160.74,3.25,"It's also not helped by multitasking."],[234,164.24,1.88,"In another VR experiment,"],[234,166.12,4.21,"participants were first asked to memorize the colors and shapes"],[234,170.33,1.42,"of different objects."],[234,172.0,5.42,"Then, some moved across the same room while others entered another."],[234,178.09,3.59,"When they were asked to also complete a counting task,"],[234,181.89,3.34,"those who traveled through the doorway had a harder time"],[234,185.22,2.21,"recalling the original objects."],[234,187.81,5.51,"Moving through the doorway distracted seemed to somehow overload their memory."],[234,193.86,3.88,"But the effect plagues people beyond literal doorways."],[234,197.94,5.5,"Looking through clear walls into adjacent rooms also appears to do the trick."],[234,203.7,4.59,"And really, whatever triggers an event update is thought to do the same:"],[234,208.66,3.29,"an alarm ringing; a friend changing the subject;"],[234,212.17,5.38,"or a shift in location, time, motivation, or computer tab."],[234,217.92,5.05,"Likewise, when people come upon an event boundary while reading stories,"],[234,223.05,3.13,"like a sentence describing a considerable time jump,"],[234,226.43,4.05,"researchers have observed that their reading speed may slow,"],[234,230.69,2.29,"their neural activity increases,"],[234,232.98,3.34,"and they have more difficulty answering questions"],[234,236.31,2.54,"about what a character was just doing."],[234,239.36,3.46,"But this isn't always the case with event updating."],[234,243.03,4.55,"In certain circumstances, doorways actually seem to aid memory."],[234,248.12,3.59,"In one study, participants were given lists of words,"],[234,251.71,1.25,"and in between them,"],[234,252.96,4.46,"they either moved spots within one room or walked into another."],[234,257.92,5.13,"Those who switched rooms wound up recalling the words more faithfully."],[234,263.76,5.26,"The key difference in a doorway’s ability to strengthen versus muddle memory"],[234,269.31,4.67,"might be the act of separating information into discrete events"],[234,274.35,2.75,"versus sharing it among multiple."],[234,277.44,3.17,"This may result in clearer compartmentalization"],[234,280.74,3.34,"that helps reduce competition and interference"],[234,284.07,2.46,"when trying to recall the information."],[234,286.82,5.63,"Generally, event updating is the brain attempting to adapt to new situations."],[234,292.7,3.67,"But the doorway side effect can be disorienting."],[234,297.17,4.5,"So, how can we best pick our train of thought back up?"],[234,302.3,3.5,"Thankfully, the confusion tends to resolve on its own."],[234,306.22,3.46,"But retracing your steps may help jog your memory."],[234,309.97,2.84,"And people have also suggested that attempts"],[234,312.81,3.63,"at deliberately preventing the doorway effect can help,"],[234,316.64,4.75,"consciously keeping their goal in mind— perhaps repeating it verbally."],[234,322.03,4.63,"Really, whatever helps you stay caught up so your head doesn’t get ahead of you."],[235,7.46,3.75,"In 2018, a non-profit gave every adult"],[235,11.21,4.05,"in western Kenya’s Ahenyo village $500."],[235,15.51,4.25,"Most of their families had lived in extreme poverty for generations,"],[235,19.77,4.8,"and this sum was roughly equivalent to most recipients’ annual salaries."],[235,24.69,3.71,"Despite all this, the money came with no strings attached"],[235,28.4,4.05,"outside a commitment to speak with researchers after two years."],[235,32.78,4.13,"They hoped this influx of cash would lift the villagers out of poverty."],[235,37.2,3.29,"But they also knew this could easily be the latest"],[235,40.49,3.79,"in a long line of failed philanthropic interventions."],[235,44.87,4.21,"In the 1960s, charitable organizations began ramping up"],[235,49.09,1.71,"their philanthropic efforts,"],[235,50.92,4.96,"spending billions funding education, job training, agricultural development,"],[235,56.05,3.29,"infrastructure projects, and health care programs"],[235,59.35,2.38,"in attempts to help poor countries."],[235,62.52,4.3,"These programs hoped to create a springboard of knowledge and capital"],[235,66.94,4.46,"that would foster financial independence and bolster struggling economies."],[235,71.73,3.75,"But when economists started studying this kind of aid"],[235,75.49,2.63,"in the late 90s and early 2000s,"],[235,78.2,2.42,"they made some surprising discoveries."],[235,81.45,3.54,"After running various randomized control trials,"],[235,85.0,4.92,"where one group received education or job training and another group did not,"],[235,90.0,4.63,"the researchers found this kind of aid often had minimal impact."],[235,95.01,3.13,"School supplies failed to improve education."],[235,98.3,2.71,"Job training didn’t always raise incomes."],[235,101.14,5.38,"And the benefits of nutrition education varied dramatically from group to group."],[235,107.06,4.96,"These disappointing results even extended to newer philanthropic models."],[235,112.44,5.21,"At this time, many theorists advocated heavily for microfinance,"],[235,117.78,5.59,"a model that offered small loans to aspiring entrepreneurs in weak economies."],[235,123.79,2.25,"But while microfinance recipients"],[235,126.04,3.21,"consistently repaid their loans with interest,"],[235,129.25,3.92,"the programs failed to meaningfully raise their incomes."],[235,133.5,3.92,"All these failures led researchers to consider a strategy"],[235,137.43,2.08,"many considered ridiculous:"],[235,139.63,1.92,"direct cash giving."],[235,141.76,2.38,"Most philanthropists saw this approach"],[235,144.14,3.29,"as the worst kind of shortsighted philanthropy."],[235,147.52,3.21,"They assumed recipients would quickly spend the cash"],[235,150.73,2.33,"and then end up back where they started."],[235,153.23,3.63,"But when researchers returned to Ahenyo two years later,"],[235,156.9,1.71,"the results were astonishing."],[235,158.95,3.59,"Business revenues were up 65%."],[235,163.2,2.84,"Families saved more and ate more."],[235,166.66,1.83,"Kids were doing better in school."],[235,168.54,3.67,"There was less alcoholism, depression, domestic violence,"],[235,172.29,2.54,"and inequality between families."],[235,175.13,2.96,"And these impacts weren’t unique to Ahenyo."],[235,178.51,1.17,"Since this study,"],[235,179.68,5.55,"direct cash giving has become one of the most researched poverty interventions,"],[235,185.22,6.05,"and it's consistently shown impacts that often exceed traditional aid programs."],[235,191.44,4.55,"In fact, a subsequent study spanning hundreds of Kenyan villages"],[235,195.98,4.0,"found the surrounding economy grew by more than twice"],[235,199.99,3.92,"what was given out just a year after the cash transfers."],[235,204.53,3.67,"However, direct cash giving isn’t a silver bullet."],[235,208.45,5.21,"Poverty is a generational issue that requires long-term changes to solve;"],[235,213.88,2.71,"and since this intervention is relatively new,"],[235,216.8,3.71,"we still don’t fully understand the effects of cash giving"],[235,220.51,1.79,"on extended timelines."],[235,223.18,3.92,"For example, a Ugandan study beginning in 2008"],[235,227.1,3.88,"found that while a cash transfer improved some families’ earnings"],[235,231.06,1.79,"over the first four years,"],[235,232.98,3.88,"the positive effect disappeared after the next five years."],[235,236.98,4.92,"Then it returned again under the pressure of the COVID-19 pandemic."],[235,242.15,5.8,"Clearly, we still have a lot to learn about how cash giving unfolds over time."],[235,248.62,2.54,"But regardless of what we learn in the future,"],[235,251.16,3.25,"the theory for why direct cash giving works"],[235,254.42,2.84,"can help change how we think about poverty today."],[235,257.63,3.67,"Where traditional aid programs assume that philanthropists"],[235,261.3,2.58,"have the best knowledge of a community's needs,"],[235,264.05,4.42,"cash giving programs believe the people experiencing poverty"],[235,268.6,2.71,"best understand what they need to escape it."],[235,272.31,2.42,"For example, perhaps for one person,"],[235,274.73,4.0,"repairing their home is more important to long-term success"],[235,278.73,1.67,"than starting a new business."],[235,280.65,3.5,"And for another, ensuring their child can finish school"],[235,284.15,3.09,"might allow them to bring in more money in the future."],[235,287.62,3.0,"Fortunately, we can afford this kind of help."],[235,290.79,5.96,"Today, wealthy countries spend $200 billion a year in international aid,"],[235,296.75,3.46,"and philanthropists have a trillion and a half more"],[235,300.21,2.29,"sitting in private foundations."],[235,302.96,3.75,"We already have the means to eliminate extreme poverty."],[235,307.22,4.63,"But doing so will require these institutions to trust the expertise"],[235,311.85,3.04,"of the people actually living in these conditions."],[236,0.24,4.48,"If you get eaten, it’s pretty safe to assume that’s the end for you.…"],[236,4.72,2.48,"Except in the surprisingly frequent cases"],[236,7.2,4.4,"where you pop back out and go on living your life like nothing ever happened."],[236,11.6,4.64,"Animals have an incredible number of strategies to survive being eaten,"],[236,16.24,4.48,"from wiggling through a predator’s gills to riding it out all the way"],[236,20.72,4.08,"through the digestive system and miraculously living to tell the tale."],[236,24.8,2.32,"Yes I am talking about butts!"],[236,27.12,2.08,"With all of their death-defying maneuvers,"],[236,29.2,4.61,"these Shawshank-level escape artists might make you lose your lunch."],[236,37.35,3.05,"Ok, ok, I know that not everyone will agree with me"],[236,40.4,3.76,"and the experts that this first example even counts as being eaten."],[236,44.16,2.24,"But we’re starting with the earliest evacuations"],[236,46.4,2.48,"and building to the most unbelievable here"],[236,48.88,4.16,"and I think the predator had intent to eat, and that’s what matters."],[236,53.04,4.64,"It’s a battle of attrition between catfish and water scavenger beetles."],[236,57.68,3.12,"A catfish captures its lunch: a delicious beetle."],[236,60.8,2.72,"The next moment, the beetle is back out in the water,"],[236,63.52,2.56,"swimming around right in front of the catfish’s face."],[236,66.08,2.24,"But the catfish isn’t deterred yet."],[236,68.32,3.52,"It snatches up the beetle again, right into its hungry maw."],[236,71.84,3.76,"And, just like before, the beetle appears back in the open water,"],[236,75.6,2.8,"and definitely not in the catfish’s empty stomach."],[236,78.4,2.56,"Researchers described these catfish as looking"],[236,80.96,2.56,"\"as frustrated as a fish can look.\""],[236,83.52,1.12,"And who could blame them?!"],[236,84.64,2.8,"In one study, the beetles made it out of the catfish’s mouth"],[236,87.44,1.76,"about 70% of the time!"],[236,89.2,3.52,"At some point, you just have to say that beetle isn’t worth it!"],[236,92.72,3.6,"Now, we don’t know exactly how the beetles have gotten so good at this,"],[236,96.32,2.24,"but the researchers have a few ideas."],[236,98.56,2.72,"The beetles might scramble around the catfish mouth"],[236,101.28,1.28,"until they get an opening,"],[236,102.56,1.84,"or maybe they cling to the side of the mouth"],[236,104.4,2.32,"and irritate the catfish into spitting them back out."],[236,106.72,2.24,"Better out than in I always say!"],[236,108.96,1.68,"That was my Shrek impression..."],[236,110.64,1.52,"But, whatever they’re doing in there,"],[236,112.16,2.08,"we know they need to be small to pull it off."],[236,114.24,2.08,"Big beetles rarely survived."],[236,116.32,2.8,"And that might be because the catfish had an easier time"],[236,119.12,2.32,"manipulating those chonky boys in their mouth …"],[236,121.44,0.88,"to be gulped."],[236,122.32,2.64,"So for catfish with a taste for tiny beetles,"],[236,124.96,3.68,"let this serve as a lesson on the importance of always chewing your food."],[236,128.64,3.2,"But aquatic predators also have a few more exit points."],[236,131.84,0.88,"Like the gills!"],[236,132.72,2.96,"When the ominously named Dark Sleeper fish swallows"],[236,135.68,1.92,"a young Japanese eel,"],[236,137.6,2.4,"things start looking pretty bleak for the eel."],[236,140.0,1.68,"But a little more than half the time,"],[236,141.68,1.76,"the eel manages an escape!"],[236,143.44,3.92,"First, a little eel tail peeks out of the fish’s gills."],[236,147.36,2.56,"And I’d imagine that’s not very comfortable for the fish"],[236,149.92,4.96,"because it starts rubbing against nearby surfaces to get the rowdy would-be meal out of there."],[236,154.88,1.12,"Now, I don’t know about you,"],[236,156.0,2.64,"but if I were escaping from inside a fish,"],[236,158.64,1.52,"my first thought would be"],[236,160.16,2.8,"“You’ve really gotten yourself into a pickle now, Ceri.”"],[236,162.96,2.08,"Quickly followed by my second thought,"],[236,165.04,3.6,"which would be to exit head first or at least facing forward."],[236,168.64,2.24,"But these eels take the opposite approach."],[236,170.88,1.6,"And I guess we can’t knock them for it"],[236,172.48,1.92,"because about half of the time it works."],[236,174.4,3.04,"Generally, when an eel is escaping a predator out in the open,"],[236,177.44,4.16,"they’ll retract their head in response to a threat and rapidly swim backwards."],[236,181.6,2.72,"So when they’re escaping from inside the predator,"],[236,184.32,2.4,"it seems this tactic works just as well,"],[236,186.72,3.44,"slotting themselves into the gills and letting them wriggle from there."],[236,190.16,3.52,"The eel’s approach seems well suited for their long, thin bodies."],[236,193.68,3.12,"Other, similar shaped elongate catfish and deep sea eels"],[236,196.8,2.56,"have been said to escape from sharks with this same method."],[236,199.36,1.6,"My body, in comparison,"],[236,200.96,3.52,"is far too human to pull off that kind of maneuver."],[236,204.48,2.24,"But being big comes with different advantages"],[236,206.72,1.2,"when you’re trying to escape."],[236,207.92,2.64,"When a boa constrictor swallows an iguana,"],[236,210.56,2.56,"it probably thinks it has dinner taken care of."],[236,213.12,2.72,"Surely after five minutes, the iguana is dead."],[236,215.84,3.52,"But in at least one such encounter observed by scientists,"],[236,219.36,3.44,"the boa constrictor’s eyes might have been too big for its stomach."],[236,222.8,2.56,"At about 40% of the snake’s mass,"],[236,225.36,2.72,"the iguana was an exceptionally large meal."],[236,228.08,2.64,"And after an alarming amount of time,"],[236,230.72,2.24,"the snake spat out the large iguana …"],[236,232.96,1.92,"who made a full recovery by the way."],[236,234.88,3.68,"The experts who described this in the journal “The Herpetological Bulletin”"],[236,238.56,3.2,"think that certain adaptations allowed the iguana to survive"],[236,241.76,1.84,"under those extreme circumstances."],[236,243.6,2.4,"Like how reptiles can change their metabolic rate"],[236,246.0,2.0,"and thus their need for oxygen,"],[236,248.0,2.8,"which was probably not plentiful inside the snake."],[236,250.8,3.36,"But being big isn’t the only thing that can get you spat out."],[236,254.16,4.08,"Before we get to the next escape, though, all science needs funding,"],[236,258.24,2.08,"even the science of not being eaten."],[236,260.32,1.6,"So here’s a quick ad."],[236,261.92,2.4,"This SciShow video is supported by DeleteMe,"],[236,264.32,3.52,"the subscription service that provides set it and forget it peace of mind."],[236,267.84,2.72,"When you sign up, you tell them what information you want gone,"],[236,270.56,2.16,"and their privacy experts get it done,"],[236,272.72,3.6,"reducing your risk of phishing, doxing, swatting, and harassment."],[236,276.32,4.64,"Then they’ll continuously remove your data using their own tech and no third parties."],[236,280.96,2.0,"Or, if you set it and /don’t/ forget it,"],[236,282.96,4.4,"DeleteMe makes real human privacy advisors available for custom requests"],[236,287.36,1.28,"and help when you need it."],[236,288.64,2.8,"This service has been used by judges, journalists,"],[236,291.44,1.52,"and our executive producer!"],[236,292.96,2.32,"They reviewed over 1,000 records for her"],[236,295.28,3.84,"and saved her 13 hours of reviewing and removing listings."],[236,299.12,2.08,"That’s time she can spend on more fun things"],[236,301.2,1.84,"like creating awesome SciShow videos!"],[236,303.04,3.92,"And they’ll review yours too for 20% off DeleteMe consumer plans"],[236,306.96,3.44,"when you go to joindeleteme.com/SCISHOW"],[236,310.4,2.4,"and use promo code SCISHOW at checkout,"],[236,312.8,2.32,"or use the QR code on-screen."],[236,315.12,3.28,"Some animals make it all the way to their predator’s stomach"],[236,318.4,1.84,"before initiating their escape plan."],[236,320.24,2.24,"You may have heard of the bombardier beetle."],[236,322.48,3.36,"They’re somewhat famous for blasting toxic chemicals out their butt"],[236,325.84,1.2,"to dissuade predators."],[236,327.04,1.92,"And on top of just being toxic,"],[236,328.96,4.96,"the chemicals are boiling hot, often coming out at 100 degrees Celsius!"],[236,333.92,2.8,"So unsuspecting toads that try to eat these beetles"],[236,336.72,1.92,"are in for the shock of a lifetime."],[236,338.64,2.56,"The beetles create their dramatic effect a bit like"],[236,341.2,2.96,"the ol’ mixing mentos and diet coke together trick."],[236,344.16,3.04,"Rather than a single chemical that can be fired at will,"],[236,347.2,3.76,"they mix two separate chambers of chemicals together to oxidize them"],[236,350.96,2.56,"and create a boiling and explosive effect."],[236,353.52,1.52,"And, luckily for the beetles,"],[236,355.04,3.28,"this reaction can happen even from inside a predator."],[236,358.32,3.2,"You can hear the spray going off inside the toad!"],[236,361.52,1.24,"it's super freaky."],[236,362.76,2.76,"Researchers studying this defense prevented the beetles"],[236,365.52,3.2,"from releasing their toxic chemicals, and sure enough,"],[236,368.72,1.36,"the beetles were eaten. …"],[236,370.08,1.28,"And stayed eaten."],[236,371.36,4.96,"But without any tampering, up to 57% of beetles were spat up by toads,"],[236,376.32,2.16,"even after 100 minutes."],[236,378.48,3.52,"If you were impressed by the iguana’s longevity in a boa constrictor,"],[236,382.0,2.08,"that’s nothing compared to these beetles."],[236,384.08,3.28,"It’s not the most dramatic escape on our list, though."],[236,387.36,1.44,"That belongs to the creatures"],[236,388.8,2.96,"who make it all the way through the digestive system."],[236,391.76,1.28,"It’s time for butts!"],[236,393.04,2.4,"A lot of animals use this method of escape."],[236,395.44,1.6,"First up are stick insects."],[236,397.04,3.04,"Birds are all too happy to eat adult stick insects."],[236,400.08,2.08,"And those guys don’t tend to escape."],[236,402.16,1.2,"But their babies do!"],[236,403.36,5.04,"Viable stick insect eggs have been observed passing through Bulbul bird guts intact."],[236,408.4,2.0,"They survive the bird’s digestive system"],[236,410.4,2.96,"and then hatch successfully into healthy young insects."],[236,413.36,3.04,"After, you know, ejecting out the other end of the bird."],[236,416.4,2.0,"If you know what I mean…"],[236,418.4,0.88,"cloaca."],[236,419.28,4.24,"Those eggs basically come pre-packaged in their own escape pods."],[236,423.52,2.0,"Stick insects have tough egg cases"],[236,425.52,3.44,"that are generally regarded as a big factor in their success."],[236,428.96,3.12,"So the sturdiness of the eggs probably contributes to them"],[236,432.08,2.16,"getting through bird digestive systems."],[236,434.24,2.88,"Plus, the mothers are capable of parthenogenesis,"],[236,437.12,3.44,"so they don’t need to find another insect to fertilize those eggs."],[236,440.56,4.16,"But other animals can create a sealed off escape pod for themselves."],[236,444.72,3.2,"Various species of snail can retreat into their shells"],[236,447.92,3.28,"and drift through their predator’s gut like a lazy river"],[236,451.2,1.84,"until they come out on the other end."],[236,453.04,3.52,"Probably not as relaxing as the lazy rivers you and I are familiar with,"],[236,456.56,1.28,"but it’s survivable...just a little poopier.."],[236,457.84,2.96,"And as you’d expect, the shell is important here."],[236,460.8,1.84,"Like its overall integrity."],[236,462.64,2.0,"One publication in the journal “Science”"],[236,464.64,3.76,"found that any crack or puncture in the shell doomed a snail,"],[236,468.4,2.48,"which often got digested by a hungry fish."],[236,470.88,2.32,"But if your shell stays intact,"],[236,473.2,3.04,"there’s an additional feature that helps snails make the journey."],[236,476.24,1.68,"It’s called an operculum."],[236,477.92,3.12,"Some snails have this thing like a hinged door to the shell"],[236,481.04,2.16,"that they can close to seal everything up."],[236,483.2,3.28,"By sealing themselves in and slowing their metabolism,"],[236,486.48,2.32,"they can tolerate low-oxygen conditions"],[236,488.8,3.04,"long enough to make it through the fish’s digestive system."],[236,491.84,4.0,"Even fish like the Spiny Sculpin that are adapted to eat tough,"],[236,495.84,2.4,"hard prey like snails and crustaceans"],[236,498.24,3.28,"will poop out live snails that took advantage of this feature."],[236,501.52,2.4,"Other shellfish like limpets and hermit crabs"],[236,503.92,2.88,"that don’t have an operculum are eaten and digested"],[236,506.8,1.12,"without too much trouble."],[236,507.92,3.92,"Moral of the story: If you find yourself on a treacherous journey,"],[236,511.84,2.72,"you’re going to want a properly sealed escape pod."],[236,514.56,2.24,"And for animals that don’t have an escape pod,"],[236,516.8,2.8,"speed needs to be on their side if they’re going to make it all the way"],[236,519.6,1.92,"through someone else’s digestive system."],[236,521.52,4.96,"One type of water beetle can crawl through a frog’s GI tract in just six minutes!"],[236,526.48,1.92,"Okay, so that’s the record."],[236,528.4,2.16,"Most of them take a bit longer than that."],[236,530.56,2.72,"But one study found that the beetles who make it through alive"],[236,533.28,2.08,"do it within around 4 hours."],[236,535.36,4.24,"And, I know, that’s still too long if you need to do things like breathe."],[236,539.6,2.64,"But researchers think that being aquatic helps them out there."],[236,542.24,3.76,"They’re adapted to keep a small pocket of air with them when submerged,"],[236,546.0,3.44,"which likely helps them in the poorly oxygenated frog stomach."],[236,549.44,3.52,"As well as having a tough exoskeleton and a streamlined body."],[236,552.96,4.16,"So these beetles can get through the frog guts fast and stay alive the whole time,"],[236,557.12,3.28,"with about a 90% survival rate after getting eaten!"],[236,560.4,2.0,"Incredibly, for many animals,"],[236,562.4,2.88,"being swallowed isn’t necessarily a death sentence."],[236,565.28,2.56,"But it doesn’t sound all that fun either. …"],[236,567.84,2.64,"Unless you really want to get back at a catfish for something."],[236,570.48,2.48,"So if you’re an animal with a predator after you,"],[236,572.96,5.28,"maybe try to avoid being eaten in the first place."],[237,0.16,5.12,"In the late 1800s, the height of luxury was a ship like the PS Portland."],[237,5.28,4.88,"She had mahogany furniture, plush velvet, and comfortable staterooms to provide an"],[237,10.16,3.28,"unforgettable journey between Boston and Portland."],[237,13.44,4.0,"The ship was a large, wooden paddle steamer that could transport up to"],[237,17.44,3.04,"700 passengers on this eight hour journey."],[237,20.48,6.32,"But on the night of November 26, 1898 a massive storm ripped across her path."],[237,26.8,4.56,"Huge waves up to 9 meters high washed across the boat’s deck,"],[237,31.36,4.48,"tipping it to the side and straining the drive mechanism to the point of breaking."],[237,35.84,4.4,"That night, all 192 passengers and crew lost their lives."],[237,40.24,4.24,"Bodies and ship wreckage washed up on the shores of Cape Cod for months."],[237,44.48,1.36,"It was a tragedy."],[237,45.84,2.72,"But the PS Portland’s story doesn’t end there."],[237,48.56,1.84,"Scientists would study it for decades,"],[237,50.4,4.8,"and it would reveal secrets about the strange afterlife of a ship at the bottom of the ocean."],[237,58.48,3.12,"On that evening in 1898, the PS Portland became"],[237,61.6,4.72,"one of over 200 shipwrecks now in The Gloucester Basin of Massachusetts Bay."],[237,66.32,3.6,"The Portland rests in what’s now a marine protected area known as"],[237,69.92,2.8,"the Stellwagen Bank National Marine Sanctuary."],[237,72.72,2.56,"It was designated as a sanctuary partially because"],[237,75.28,4.32,"the shipwrecks here kind of act as time capsules for maritime history."],[237,79.6,3.92,"Plus, these shipwrecks have turned out to be great resources for studying what happens"],[237,83.52,4.56,"after a ship sinks, and the impacts that wrecks have on the local marine ecosystem."],[237,88.08,1.76,"Studies about the Portland, specifically,"],[237,89.84,6.48,"started with a 2002 to 2005 baseline survey, with follow-up surveys in 2019 and 2020."],[237,96.32,2.24,"The studies were a collaboration between The Woods"],[237,98.56,3.36,"Hole Oceanographic Institution and Marine Imaging Technologies."],[237,101.92,3.2,"These surveys—together with studies of other shipwrecks in the bay and"],[237,105.12,3.92,"beyond—help us piece together what happens after a ship sinks."],[237,109.04,1.92,"And we’ll start with the sinking itself."],[237,110.96,5.2,"The ship’s structure is rapidly changing as wind and waves tear at the sinking ship."],[237,116.16,3.92,"Obviously some parts are damaged before the ship even reaches the bottom,"],[237,120.08,2.32,"due to collisions or artillery attacks."],[237,122.4,3.84,"And researchers know from video surveys of the Portland that much of"],[237,126.24,5.12,"the structure above her deck is missing: its chimneys, crew area and flagpoles."],[237,131.36,2.96,"The next phase depends on where the ship sinks."],[237,134.32,2.08,"The Portland landed on muddy sediment,"],[237,136.4,3.44,"likely stirring up a plume of nutrients settled on the seafloor."],[237,139.84,4.64,"That slow-mo mushroom cloud of sediment can be an absolute feast for bacteria."],[237,144.48,5.6,"But a cushy landing in sediment is anticlimactic compared to, say, landing on rocks or coral."],[237,150.08,4.32,"In those cases, the wreck can disturb or destroy pieces of an existing ecosystem."],[237,154.4,3.44,"Either way, the initial crash landing might break the ship up more,"],[237,157.84,2.08,"or parts might get buried in the sediment."],[237,159.92,3.68,"And how deep the shipwreck descends influences what communities might get"],[237,163.6,3.76,"disrupted when it lands, as well as everything that happens next."],[237,167.36,4.32,"Deeper water, for example, means darker, colder and different levels of oxygen."],[237,171.68,4.8,"That’s important because once the ship has settled in its final resting place, the decay can begin."],[237,176.48,2.24,"Wooden ships, like the Portland, become infested"],[237,178.72,4.56,"with marine borers like Limnoria and shipworm—the real Kraken of the sea."],[237,183.28,3.04,"Shipworm are equipped with rows of sharp teeth and"],[237,186.32,3.84,"shells on either side of their head to scrape away at wood."],[237,190.16,4.48,"That means if they have their preferred environment, with oxygen and relatively warm,"],[237,194.64,5.2,"salty water, these little guys can decimate most of a wooden ship within a couple years!"],[237,199.84,2.88,"They’re not exactly picky about their habitat or diet."],[237,202.72,5.76,"Which, by the way, makes them a huge problem not just for shipwrecks we want to preserve and study"],[237,208.48,5.76,"but any ocean-adjacent infrastructure, like piers, docks or the dikes that protect cities."],[237,214.24,4.16,"But borers aren’t the only life to call shipwrecks home early on."],[237,218.4,4.64,"Fungi and bacteria also move in, but a bit more quietly than the borers."],[237,223.04,2.64,"These microbes start munching from the outside,"],[237,225.68,4.24,"and are helped along by swirling sand and ocean currents that erode the"],[237,229.92,4.32,"wood’s texture and create new nooks and crannies for the microbes to thrive in."],[237,234.24,3.12,"The bacteria and fungi do more than just degrade the wood,"],[237,237.36,4.08,"they actually shape it into a new habitat by creating a biofilm."],[237,241.44,3.12,"Inside the biofilm are the microorganisms themselves,"],[237,244.56,5.04,"plus a protective goo they secrete, made of long chains of sugar-based molecules."],[237,249.6,2.64,"The gooey layer is its own tiny community,"],[237,252.24,3.36,"with chemical reactions, nutrient sharing, and even gene swapping!"],[237,255.6,4.24,"Some of the non-wood-eating bacteria that join these biofilm colonies can"],[237,259.84,2.96,"even help protect the ship from eroding away entirely."],[237,262.8,3.92,"Now, you might think that metal ships are immune to these microscopic critters."],[237,266.72,1.6,"But that’s not the case."],[237,268.32,4.72,"Consider one of the most famous metal shipwrecks of all time, the RMS Titanic."],[237,273.04,3.6,"After more than 100 years, it’s now covered in a biofilm of marine"],[237,276.64,4.16,"fungi and bacteria that have been slowly consuming the ship’s metal."],[237,280.8,2.16,"Additional bacteria have joined the party,"],[237,282.96,3.44,"perhaps having drifted over from nearby hydrothermal vents."],[237,286.4,5.68,"Using the Titanic’s iron, they’ve formed meter long rusticles and sprawling rust flowers."],[237,292.08,4.88,"Those bacteria are so aggressive that the rust spreads out from the ship around 4"],[237,296.96,4.56,"inches each year and now reaches 6 inches down into the sediment."],[237,301.52,3.6,"This bacteria-built rust art comes at the cost of around"],[237,305.12,4.48,"130 to 200 kilograms of ship iron every day."],[237,309.6,4.24,"Large swathes of the Titanic may be totally dissolved by 2192."],[237,313.84,2.56,"But to keep us afloat so that we don’t go the way of these shipwrecks,"],[237,316.4,2.64,"we need to show you a quick ad."],[237,319.04,4.08,"Thanks to our Presidents of Science, Binorthedrunkdwarf, Charlie Stanley,"],[237,323.12,4.24,"Harry Plumley, and Raz Tirosh for supporting this SciShow video!"],[237,327.36,3.6,"They make it possible for you to watch these videos and learn for free."],[237,330.96,4.72,"And they generally contribute to the spread of knowledge and curiosity far and wide."],[237,335.68,3.84,"Their donations, and all of the donations made on SciShow’s Patreon,"],[237,339.52,2.08,"pay for what you’re watching right now."],[237,341.6,3.28,"And they’re tax deductible because we’re a non-profit!"],[237,344.88,4.72,"To make multiple high quality, fact-based, and beautifully designed videos every week,"],[237,349.6,2.8,"it takes writers, editors, fact-checkers, producers,"],[237,352.4,4.64,"animators, sound designers…and a whole team that gets compensated for their hard work."],[237,357.04,3.04,"And our Presidents of Science help provide that compensation."],[237,360.08,2.88,"To join our Presidents of Science in financially supporting these"],[237,362.96,5.2,"videos that you’re enjoying, you can go to patreon.com/SciShow."],[237,368.16,1.44,"Let’s get back to the Portland."],[237,369.6,1.84,"Even as the flesh of the ship is ripped away,"],[237,371.44,4.72,"and its skeleton begins to dissolve, the shipwreck still has plenty left to give."],[237,376.16,4.96,"Because next it becomes a home for bigger creatures in the form of an artificial reef."],[237,381.12,3.68,"The shipwreck provides a scaffold for all sorts of life, by adding structure"],[237,384.8,3.84,"and height to areas that otherwise would just be muddy, sandy, and flat."],[237,388.64,3.2,"The Portland became something like a neighbourhood, with different species"],[237,391.84,3.28,"moving into different areas of the ship and establishing new homes."],[237,395.12,4.48,"Suspension feeders, like a zooplankton-eating anemone, occupied the top part of the ship,"],[237,399.6,3.12,"taking advantage of currents that keep the water and food flowing."],[237,402.72,2.72,"Larvae, on the other hand, mostly nestled under the ship,"],[237,405.44,3.52,"where they’re protected from being buried alive in the sandy seafloor,"],[237,408.96,3.12,"but also sheltered from currents that would sweep them away."],[237,412.08,3.76,"The crevices of the midship provided a perfect home for fish to hide in,"],[237,415.84,4.4,"while other fish feed on the furry layer of algae that coats the wreck’s frame."],[237,420.24,3.36,"Species like Atlantic Cod, Acadian redfish, Cusk,"],[237,423.6,5.28,"Winter Flounder and Spiny Dogfish, and many more have all been spotted hanging about the Portland."],[237,428.88,3.92,"But as the ship settled and moved over time, those communities changed, too."],[237,432.8,6.72,"For example, between the 2002-2005 baseline survey and 2019/2020, researchers saw that"],[237,439.52,5.84,"timber and railings had ripped off and fallen to the seafloor, plus new fishing nets had appeared."],[237,445.36,4.96,"Researchers don’t know exactly where those nets came from, but their appearance might be partly"],[237,450.32,6.4,"because of changes in fishing regulations in 2010 and increased scallop fishing starting in 2017."],[237,456.72,4.56,"The policies changed, and around the same time, the biological community changed, too."],[237,461.28,2.48,"On their way down, those new fishing nets probably"],[237,463.76,3.36,"scraped off some of the taller organisms clinging to the ship."],[237,467.12,2.88,"It might have missed species like the breadcrumb sponge"],[237,470.0,2.88,"that tucks in flatter to its growing surface."],[237,472.88,4.4,"And nets generally mean fewer invertebrates can find a foothold on the wreck,"],[237,477.28,3.92,"since it’s tricky to cling onto such a flimsy and flailing surface."],[237,481.2,3.92,"Plus researchers noticed fewer suspension feeders clinging to the top of the ship,"],[237,485.12,3.44,"since much of the upper structure had tumbled to the sea floor."],[237,488.56,4.24,"And surfaces that were once horizontal had shifted to a more vertical position,"],[237,492.8,4.72,"which meant deep sea sponges that prefer an upright surface had been able to move in."],[237,497.52,4.64,"The kinds of fish hanging around the Portland were also different from the ones in 2002,"],[237,502.16,4.48,"but researchers couldn’t pin that to any specific change in the ship’s structure."],[237,506.64,4.88,"It’s more likely the fish were reacting to climate change, overfishing, or competition in the region."],[237,511.52,3.68,"The funny thing about shipwrecks is that they were never supposed to be there."],[237,515.2,2.8,"But by introducing these new structures to the environment,"],[237,518.0,3.04,"they end up functioning like biological stepping stones."],[237,521.04,3.76,"Basically, a way for species to move long distances by jumping—or in this"],[237,524.8,3.04,"case swimming—between these stepping stone oases."],[237,527.84,3.52,"See, once all those algae, fungi, and fish move into a shipwreck,"],[237,531.36,5.36,"the benefits soon start spilling out, like bounty overflowing from a sunken treasure chest."],[237,536.72,3.84,"The effect is a kind of “halo of life”, as one marine biologist put it,"],[237,540.56,2.08,"extending out past the shipwreck itself."],[237,542.64,3.52,"That’s because these habitats don’t just abruptly end where the shipwreck"],[237,546.16,3.84,"does—although it’s obviously more concentrated on the wreck itself."],[237,550.0,5.84,"The habitat gradually fades, creating a transition zone between the wreck and the empty seafloor."],[237,555.84,1.92,"Like suburbs between a city and the country!"],[237,557.76,2.4,"If the distance between those “halos” is small enough,"],[237,560.16,4.4,"then organisms can move from one habitat’s transition zone to another one nearby."],[237,564.56,3.6,"No one’s studied whether this stepping stone effect has happened with The Portland"],[237,568.16,3.6,"specifically, but we have observed the effect around other wrecks."],[237,571.76,5.2,"For all their benefits, it’s tricky to say if shipwrecks are a good or bad thing ecologically."],[237,576.96,4.16,"Corroding metal or toxic fuel leaks can cause a whole bunch of environmental damage."],[237,581.12,2.08,"But there might be quieter downsides, too."],[237,583.2,5.2,"Ships might unknowingly transport invasive species to a new area, like how shipwrecks in the Atlantic"],[237,588.4,6.16,"ocean have helped spread invasive sun corals up to 200 kilometers from their original habitat."],[237,594.56,4.0,"Everywhere these corals set up camp, they outcompete and then shove out other species."],[237,598.56,4.72,"Another potential downside of all this moving around is that the exciting new habitat of a"],[237,603.28,5.6,"shipwreck could attract fish from nearby, leaving an ecological gap in their former neighborhood."],[237,608.88,1.92,"But on the plus side, these shipwrecks provide"],[237,610.8,3.84,"a great opportunity for species that wouldn’t otherwise be able to thrive."],[237,614.64,2.88,"In some cases, they even become biodiversity hotspots."],[237,617.52,5.2,"The Portland’s afterlife is just one example of how life can emerge from these watery graves—its"],[237,622.72,5.04,"habitat halo is different even from other shipwrecks in the same marine sanctuary!"],[237,627.76,3.84,"Every ship has a second life as varied and unique as its first."],[237,631.6,4.0,"Even though the Portland has been below the waves for more than 125 years,"],[237,635.6,2.24,"its afterlife is far from over."],[237,637.84,2.48,"It might look entirely different again in ten years!"],[237,640.32,2.48,"As the top slowly sags and is eaten away,"],[237,642.8,4.48,"it’s turning what was once a mountain terrain into a flatter, eroded hill."],[237,647.28,2.24,"The species that live there will have to adapt, too."],[237,649.52,3.04,"Eventually, the Portland will crumble into a pile of dust,"],[237,652.56,3.21,"just as metal shipwrecks will completely rust away."],[237,655.77,3.59,"All ships will dissolve into the ocean, having donated their nutrients"],[237,659.36,4.0,"and minerals to the marine creatures that kept them company during their afterlife."],[237,663.36,1.92,"In the end, very little will remain."],[237,665.28,3.6,"The oldest known shipwreck, which sits off the coast of Dokos Island,"],[237,668.88,4.88,"Greece, is now just a smattering of clay pots arranged in the shape of a ship."],[237,673.76,0.64,"Spooky."],[237,674.4,3.52,"Although a shipwreck’s life after death is, geologically speaking,"],[237,677.92,5.52,"very brief, its ecological impact can last for many generations of marine creatures."],[237,683.44,4.24,"And for all those critters, these ships that sadly never reached home"],[237,687.68,3.1,"ultimately became a treasured safe harbor."],[238,3.87,5.0,"I want you to spend the next 10 minutes being absurdly self-indulgent,"],[238,8.91,3.37,"which is advice I would almost never otherwise give people."],[238,12.64,3.44,"I want you to not think about the things you can't control."],[238,16.11,3.87,"AI, politics, your parents, your social media feed,"],[238,20.02,1.74,"your Tinder alerts."],[238,22.95,3.57,"By locking in on you for the next 600 seconds,"],[238,26.56,2.4,"I'm very confident that you're going to walk away"],[238,28.99,4.91,"with what I think is the single best hack for longevity, purpose"],[238,33.9,2.9,"and living the type of lives that we want to live."],[238,37.87,5.64,"First, who is this middle-aged dude preaching a gospel of self-indulgence?"],[238,44.34,2.14,"Well, the 20-year-old version of me,"],[238,46.48,5.27,"I'm very confident, was a much less impressive version of you."],[238,52.68,1.2,"Three years into college,"],[238,53.92,4.14,"I had a 1.491 grade point average that I was dragging around."],[238,58.09,3.34,"I was smoking a pack of Camel Lights a day."],[238,61.46,1.83,"I was drinking prolifically."],[238,63.33,4.04,"At night, I was delivering pizzas in my diesel VW Rabbit."],[238,68.2,4.7,"I was remarkably unhealthy, and I was remarkably unremarkable."],[238,73.47,1.33,"Within a decade,"],[238,74.84,2.13,"I'm interviewing presidents,"],[238,77.01,3.24,"I’m covering the White House for The Wall Street Journal"],[238,80.28,2.07,"and The Washington Post."],[238,82.35,4.8,"Soon after, I’m starting and running companies. First Politico, then Axios."],[238,87.15,1.57,"So what the hell happened?"],[238,89.75,4.57,"And in those dark times and in now, some very good times,"],[238,94.36,3.74,"I really tried to become a student of what does it take to be successful"],[238,98.13,3.04,"and what makes great people great?"],[238,101.47,3.74,"What brings them real success?"],[238,105.24,3.57,"And there is a commonality to all of them."],[238,109.37,6.61,"That all of them maniacally focus on controlling life's variables"],[238,116.01,1.83,"that you can control."],[238,117.85,2.17,"You control you."],[238,120.05,1.97,"That became my mantra,"],[238,122.05,2.6,"and that became my map for life."],[238,125.16,1.87,"When I watched these people,"],[238,127.06,3.07,"whether they were teachers, doctors, entrepreneurs --"],[238,130.16,4.57,"every day, they were maniacal about constructing their success"],[238,134.76,2.0,"and constructing their greatness"],[238,136.8,3.84,"piece by piece, day by day, on their terms."],[238,141.07,1.87,"If we're being honest,"],[238,142.97,5.11,"a lot of us just surrender to others or to other things."],[238,148.11,4.04,"We say, well, we can't do that because of my parents, my childhood,"],[238,152.18,4.94,"my circumstances, my DNA, my crappy luck."],[238,158.12,1.17,"We just can't do it"],[238,159.32,3.3,"because we're under control of others or other things."],[238,162.66,1.43,"And it's just BS."],[238,164.09,2.57,"And it's just not a good way to live."],[238,166.66,1.57,"You control you,"],[238,168.26,3.04,"it's just a much better, brighter way to live."],[238,171.3,2.84,"And so I want to walk you through five different things"],[238,174.17,2.94,"that you can do that are free,"],[238,177.14,1.7,"that you can start today"],[238,178.88,3.04,"and that are available to every single person in this room."],[238,182.88,2.4,"You control today."],[238,186.35,2.9,"From the moment you wake up to the moment you go to bed,"],[238,189.25,3.77,"you have scores of decisions to make that only you make."],[238,193.52,3.27,"Do you doomscroll, or do you meditate?"],[238,196.83,3.34,"Do you eat Lucky Charms, or do you eat a healthy breakfast?"],[238,200.7,2.07,"When someone snaps at you, do you snap back,"],[238,202.8,2.07,"or do you show a little bit of grace?"],[238,204.9,3.37,"At night, do you get hammered, or do you get some sleep?"],[238,209.01,2.74,"You make each one of those decisions."],[238,211.78,3.64,"And every one either leads to happiness and feeling better about yourself,"],[238,215.45,2.2,"or sadness and feeling like crap."],[238,217.65,1.64,"Nobody wants to feel like crap."],[238,219.78,5.07,"So how did I get from Oshkosh and my 1.491"],[238,224.85,2.3,"to DC and then having some success?"],[238,227.56,3.64,"It was studying other people who were smarter than me,"],[238,231.23,2.2,"who were better than me, more talented than me,"],[238,233.43,1.17,"better read than me,"],[238,234.63,3.07,"who just knew a lot more than me, who were healthier than me."],[238,237.74,1.43,"And then I tried to copy them."],[238,239.2,2.13,"If they read a book I hadn’t read, I read it."],[238,241.34,2.37,"Or if they used a word I’d never heard in Oshkosh,"],[238,243.71,4.07,"I wrote it down on a piece of paper, and I would work it into my vocabulary."],[238,247.81,3.57,"If they were fitter than I were, I'd try to copy their habits."],[238,251.41,3.27,"I started to take control of what we can control."],[238,254.72,2.34,"You control you."],[238,257.09,2.27,"You control your reactions."],[238,259.39,3.44,"The best advice I ever got was five words:"],[238,262.86,2.04,"Do the next right thing."],[238,265.56,1.47,"Do the next right thing."],[238,267.06,1.94,"It's really hard to be a good person."],[238,269.33,1.6,"It's hard to be moral."],[238,271.54,2.17,"It's hard to do all the things right."],[238,273.7,2.57,"It's pretty easy to do the next right thing."],[238,276.94,1.6,"And if you do that,"],[238,278.57,2.0,"if you do things that would make you proud,"],[238,280.61,2.64,"your parents proud, your kids proud, your friends proud,"],[238,283.28,1.6,"you do that and then do it again,"],[238,284.88,2.2,"suddenly, you're a pretty good person."],[238,287.12,3.2,"And then if you do it in bad times,"],[238,290.32,2.1,"you could become a great person."],[238,292.42,2.07,"My personal motto of the last 15 years is:"],[238,294.52,2.4,"When shit happens, shine."],[238,296.93,4.1,"Meaning, in the worst of times, when really bad things happen,"],[238,301.06,3.07,"that's when I really want to do the next right thing."],[238,304.77,2.3,"That helped me through one of the hardest periods"],[238,307.1,1.17,"of my professional life."],[238,308.31,1.9,"I had started Politico, it was my baby."],[238,310.21,1.17,"It's emotional."],[238,311.41,3.34,"And, you know, like a lot of these start-ups, it was a success."],[238,314.78,3.7,"And then there was turmoil, and I felt like I had to leave."],[238,319.02,1.87,"And it's emotional."],[238,320.92,2.23,"And I could have said and done things"],[238,323.19,1.64,"that would come back to haunt me."],[238,324.82,3.0,"In every minute of every day during that period, I thought,"],[238,327.86,2.87,"I'm going to do the next right thing."],[238,330.76,3.74,"I'm not going to do things that are going to come back and hurt me or haunt me."],[238,334.5,1.8,"And it helped me get there."],[238,336.77,3.7,"Three, you control your reality."],[238,341.57,2.77,"That might sound weird, but you do."],[238,344.38,3.4,"What you read, what you watch, what you listen to."],[238,347.81,2.0,"That forms your reality."],[238,350.15,3.9,"Your podcasts, your YouTube channels, your newsletters, your friends."],[238,354.08,2.37,"It's all a reality-shaping machine."],[238,357.29,2.74,"The inputs affect your outputs."],[238,360.42,3.04,"If you're inputting a lot of misery,"],[238,363.49,2.2,"a lot of doom and gloom,"],[238,365.73,1.5,"a lot of trivial stuff,"],[238,367.26,4.07,"you might be a doomy and gloomy kind of person and kind of trivial."],[238,372.0,1.3,"But change that."],[238,373.34,2.97,"If you control your reality,"],[238,376.34,3.07,"all you got to do is realize, listen, there's more good,"],[238,379.41,3.84,"high-quality information available to everybody in this room"],[238,383.25,2.07,"than at any point in humanity."],[238,385.35,2.97,"It's all free, and it's not even close."],[238,388.35,4.04,"If there's something you're curious about, you can Google it or ChatGPT it."],[238,392.76,2.1,"If you want to know what Elon Musk thinks"],[238,394.89,1.97,"or Mel Robbins would advise"],[238,396.89,1.6,"or Taylor Swift feels,"],[238,398.5,4.17,"there's probably a podcast or a video or an interview with them."],[238,402.7,3.1,"You can search that, you can change the inputs."],[238,405.84,3.94,"You can put healthy, edifying content into your mind,"],[238,409.81,4.2,"have a better reality that you then share with other people."],[238,414.51,3.44,"Four, you control how you're seen."],[238,418.75,3.8,"This one might seem a little too absurdly self-indulgent,"],[238,422.59,1.97,"but it's actually a magic trick."],[238,425.12,2.3,"You control how you're seen."],[238,427.42,1.5,"When you're in a group setting,"],[238,428.96,3.37,"especially if it's tense or it's emotional,"],[238,432.36,3.54,"think about how other people are experiencing you."],[238,436.5,3.77,"The way I do it is I almost envision myself having this out-of-body experience."],[238,440.27,2.94,"Watching me through the eyes of others."],[238,443.24,2.07,"Our own eyes deceive."],[238,445.34,2.1,"Our own eyes lie."],[238,447.48,4.54,"But if you look at how other people feel and experience you,"],[238,452.05,2.57,"then you know the impression that you're leaving,"],[238,454.65,2.14,"that you want to leave."],[238,457.32,2.4,"And it really helps you, I think,"],[238,459.76,1.53,"be the person you want to be"],[238,461.29,3.9,"and have other people experience you as the person that you want to be."],[238,465.8,1.9,"This technique helped me through"],[238,467.73,2.27,"what was probably the hardest period of my life,"],[238,470.0,2.0,"but the most wondrous period of my life."],[238,472.04,4.34,"It’s about seven years ago, and we adopted a teenage boy,"],[238,476.41,2.94,"and I had two teenagers already at home."],[238,479.68,2.4,"And he had lost his parents,"],[238,482.08,1.57,"it was a rough part of his life."],[238,483.68,4.14,"And he's a young guy doing what you do when you have a rough period of life."],[238,487.85,1.9,"And I remember thinking, you know what?"],[238,489.79,1.7,"He's going to be watching me,"],[238,491.49,1.74,"my kids are going to be watching me,"],[238,493.26,1.67,"my wife's going to be watching me."],[238,494.93,1.8,"If I show love,"],[238,496.73,1.7,"if I show forgiveness,"],[238,498.46,4.2,"if I show persistence, if I show grit,"],[238,502.7,4.14,"that's going to echo through how they experience what we're going through."],[238,506.84,2.84,"And if I'm rattled or I'm pessimistic,"],[238,509.71,2.2,"it would have an opposite consequence."],[238,512.28,2.7,"And that process, I really believe,"],[238,515.01,5.57,"had a very small part in turning my son into this amazing, thriving,"],[238,520.58,3.34,"grateful college athlete today."],[238,524.29,2.2,"And I think that applies to all of us."],[238,527.19,4.44,"The fifth thing that you control is you control your destiny."],[238,531.66,2.54,"You control where you're going."],[238,534.23,3.64,"One of the things I hate the most is when I run into people"],[238,537.9,3.57,"and they just feel they're like a bobber or a log on a river,"],[238,541.5,2.5,"and you just have to go with the current."],[238,544.47,1.24,"It's not true."],[238,545.74,1.97,"You get to control where you go."],[238,547.74,3.24,"You can control if you want to go against the current,"],[238,551.01,2.1,"if you want to go upstream."],[238,553.78,2.2,"That is fully in your control."],[238,556.02,3.97,"And I want to end today with a writing assignment."],[238,559.99,1.67,"Not that you need to do right now,"],[238,561.69,2.2,"but maybe do it later today or later this week."],[238,564.39,3.94,"I want you to imagine yourself on your deathbed,"],[238,568.37,1.7,"thinking about your life."],[238,570.5,2.6,"And did you live a life that you were proud of?"],[238,573.8,1.97,"Did you live a life that you could feel,"],[238,575.81,1.9,"\"You know what? I did it my way.\""],[238,577.74,2.47,"I did it the right way."],[238,581.01,2.34,"And write down the three things that it would take"],[238,583.38,2.2,"for that answer to be: “Yes, that was it."],[238,585.58,1.9,"I lived the life I wanted to live.\""],[238,588.02,4.0,"Then write down the three things that it would take to do that"],[238,592.05,1.33,"between now and then,"],[238,593.42,3.0,"to actually achieve the life that you want to achieve."],[238,597.26,1.7,"That's your North Star."],[238,599.0,2.04,"That puts you ahead of 95 percent on Earth"],[238,601.06,5.0,"in terms of taking ownership of what you want to do with your own life."],[238,607.47,1.37,"Man, I'm blessed."],[238,609.14,2.74,"I grew up, I had four grandparents"],[238,611.91,1.94,"who lived in my hometown."],[238,613.84,2.2,"I have two parents who are still alive."],[238,616.08,3.27,"I have an amazing wife, three wondrous children"],[238,619.38,3.47,"and tons of really great friends and colleagues"],[238,622.85,2.23,"who poured a lot of knowledge"],[238,625.09,2.7,"and a lot of love into my life."],[238,627.79,3.1,"Richard Powers, in his book \"Bewilderment,\" says,"],[238,630.93,1.47,"maybe the purpose of life"],[238,632.43,4.3,"is about leaving little pieces of ourselves behind in others."],[238,636.77,2.2,"And I hope today that I've left a little piece"],[238,638.97,3.5,"of what all those people poured into me, with you,"],[238,642.47,4.37,"and that you can leave little pieces of yourself in many others,"],[238,646.88,2.87,"and you can do it because you control you."],[238,649.78,1.37,"So get ’er done."],[238,652.68,1.74,"Thank you."],[239,6.88,5.38,"You're about to buy some bargain bin headphones when you spot a real bargain."],[239,12.47,5.38,"This pair is more expensive, but it's selling for half its usual price."],[239,18.01,3.09,"Surely this is the better buy, right?"],[239,21.48,1.04,"It’s possible!"],[239,22.68,5.17,"However, it’s also possible you’re falling for a psychological trick"],[239,28.11,5.46,"retailers use to influence what you buy and what you’re willing to pay."],[239,34.49,5.0,"From the late 1800s, economic theory often assumed that consumers"],[239,39.49,4.84,"were rational actors who pursued the optimal financial choice."],[239,44.62,4.0,"But while consumers are reasonably rational on average,"],[239,48.63,4.88,"they’re also vulnerable to impulse purchases, savvy salespeople,"],[239,53.55,5.26,"and other external factors that make them, well, irrational."],[239,59.22,4.09,"20th century psychologists investigating these factors"],[239,63.31,5.09,"learned that human decision making is rarely perfectly logical."],[239,68.69,6.63,"And by the 1970s, this research informed the birth of behavioral economics,"],[239,75.49,5.46,"a new field exploring the intersection of economics and psychology."],[239,81.66,4.17,"Behavioral economics operated off a new assumption,"],[239,85.83,4.25,"introduced by economic sciences laureate Herbert Simon."],[239,90.5,3.13,"His model, called “bounded rationality,”"],[239,93.71,4.34,"argued that consumers have limited information, limited time,"],[239,98.14,2.75,"and various psychological constraints"],[239,100.89,3.25,"that lead them not to the optimal financial choice,"],[239,104.35,2.63,"but simply one they feel good about."],[239,107.73,2.71,"So how do we decide what's good enough?"],[239,110.73,1.75,"Rather than using logic,"],[239,112.57,4.5,"behavioral economists say we fall back on heuristics—"],[239,117.24,4.38,"mental shortcuts for making decisions easier and faster."],[239,121.83,2.75,"For example, the anchoring heuristic"],[239,124.58,3.21,"suggests that the first piece of information you get"],[239,127.92,3.54,"becomes your baseline for future decision making."],[239,131.54,3.59,"So if a menu's first item costs $50,"],[239,135.21,4.0,"you'll compare other prices against that reference point."],[239,139.59,6.13,"Psychologists like Daniel Kahneman thought heuristics predict many consumer choices—"],[239,145.85,3.84,"which means they can be manipulated to control them."],[239,149.98,5.3,"Case in point, while those discounted headphones might be 50% off,"],[239,155.28,4.88,"it’s also possible the original price was inflated to begin with."],[239,160.74,4.8,"Anchoring is just one of many heuristics retailers exploit."],[239,165.66,4.3,"Consider this: which of these cleaning products would you buy?"],[239,170.42,1.46,"How about now?"],[239,172.17,3.84,"Both are equally effective and basically cost the same,"],[239,176.17,4.5,"but careful language and pricing makes one more appealing."],[239,180.97,2.09,"This is the framing effect,"],[239,183.05,3.04,"and it’s one of several psychological phenomena"],[239,186.1,4.0,"that short circuit our ability to assign value."],[239,190.6,1.71,"Here’s an example of another:"],[239,192.44,4.25,"some American grocery stores tried encouraging customers"],[239,196.69,4.59,"to bring reusable shopping bags by offering a 5 cent credit."],[239,201.53,4.5,"But this proved less effective than charging a 5 cent fee"],[239,206.16,2.0,"for using disposable bags."],[239,208.5,1.04,"In other words,"],[239,209.54,6.21,"the desire to avoid losing 5 cents was more motivating than gaining 5 cents."],[239,216.17,2.5,"This is called loss aversion,"],[239,218.67,3.54,"and retailers trigger this fear of losing value"],[239,222.22,4.38,"by emphasizing a sale’s scarcity or looming deadline."],[239,227.31,5.92,"All these tactics suggest our perception of value is more important"],[239,233.23,2.5,"than what something is actually worth."],[239,236.27,3.17,"And that effect goes deeper than you might think."],[239,239.69,4.59,"In a 2008 study, subjects were put into MRI machines"],[239,244.28,5.92,"and treated to samples from five bottles of increasingly expensive wine."],[239,250.58,3.46,"Participants reported preferring the expensive samples,"],[239,254.08,3.54,"and MRI images confirmed this claim."],[239,258.0,4.13,"But in reality they only tasted three bottles,"],[239,262.26,5.5,"with the two cheapest pulling double duty as the two priciest."],[239,267.97,4.05,"Of course, retailers don’t just manipulate your perception,"],[239,272.23,2.96,"they control the spaces you shop."],[239,275.73,4.75,"Supermarkets often place high-margin items at eye level"],[239,280.48,3.25,"and design stores to guide you past them."],[239,284.15,5.25,"Behavioral economist Richard Thaler and legal scholar Cass Sunstein"],[239,289.54,3.42,"call this subtle product placement “nudge theory,”"],[239,292.95,2.46,"and it works online as well."],[239,295.92,4.55,"Sites might nudge you by suggesting related items to purchase"],[239,300.55,2.54,"or by pre-selecting add-ons."],[239,303.42,4.25,"So if retailers are boxing you in, what can you do about it?"],[239,307.85,2.25,"First, shop slower."],[239,310.47,4.38,"Heuristic-based decision making is quick and instinctive,"],[239,314.94,6.05,"so slowing down to run the numbers can pull back the curtain on a flashy deal."],[239,321.61,3.84,"Deciding what a product is worth to you before shopping"],[239,325.45,2.5,"can help set your own anchors."],[239,328.78,3.38,"And always ask yourself why you want this deal."],[239,332.45,3.04,"Buying something just because it’s a bargain"],[239,335.58,3.71,"means you might not be in control of your choice."],[239,340.0,5.59,"Above all, remember that retailers and advertisers aren’t just selling products—"],[239,345.8,2.0,"they’re designing your choices."],[239,347.88,3.92,"And once you understand the psychology behind their designs,"],[239,351.81,3.96,"you might gain something even more valuable than a discount:"],[239,355.89,3.29,"the ability to choose on your own terms."],[240,6.79,2.67,"The year is 100 CE,"],[240,9.76,6.09,"and 11-year-old Julia Lucilla is playing with her ivory doll Pompeia."],[240,16.55,2.71,"Named after the Roman Emperor Trajan’s wife,"],[240,19.39,3.04,"Pompeia is only 20 centimeters tall,"],[240,22.43,3.46,"with joints at her arms, legs, knees, and elbows,"],[240,26.02,3.13,"and a bevy of miniature clothes and accessories."],[240,29.98,4.25,"With her doll in hand, Julia hosts elaborate dinner parties,"],[240,34.24,2.21,"helps Aeneas escape Troy,"],[240,36.45,3.46,"and accompanies her father on work trips to Egypt."],[240,40.29,3.54,"This ancient scene is likely familiar to any modern parent,"],[240,43.83,4.38,"and that’s because kids have been going on imaginary adventures with their toys"],[240,48.46,2.17,"for thousands of years."],[240,52.51,3.96,"While the most common ancient playthings would likely have been sticks and rocks,"],[240,56.47,3.96,"evidence of their use for play is archaeologically invisible."],[240,60.6,5.05,"However, archaeologists have found material, visual, and written evidence"],[240,65.64,2.67,"for toys across the ancient world."],[240,68.98,3.04,"In Anatolia circa 3000 BCE,"],[240,72.03,2.71,"miniature toy carriages raced through the dirt"],[240,74.74,2.0,"at the hands of energetic youngsters."],[240,77.07,2.46,"A thousand years later, in the Indus Valley,"],[240,79.66,1.75,"a toddler giggled gleefully"],[240,81.41,4.13,"at the chirping sound created by their dove-shaped terracotta whistle."],[240,85.87,2.42,"Alongside whistles and wheeled toys,"],[240,88.42,2.88,"dolls are another common ancient plaything,"],[240,91.63,2.92,"though they can be a little harder to identify."],[240,95.76,3.09,"While some archaeologists think ancient female figurines"],[240,98.84,3.5,"found across Afro-Eurasia may have been children’s toys,"],[240,102.39,2.96,"others believe them to be sacred fertility idols."],[240,106.35,4.17,"But we do know dolls like Pompeia were popular in Greece and Rome,"],[240,110.77,4.5,"with hair and clothes designed to reflect changing adult fashions."],[240,115.49,2.46,"And thousands of years later in the Arctic,"],[240,117.95,5.13,"Inuit children carried fur-clad dolls carved of wood or walrus tusk."],[240,124.41,3.84,"But perhaps the most common ancient toy is the humble ball."],[240,128.71,1.38,"Thousands of years ago,"],[240,130.08,4.63,"children in ancient Egypt, China, Greece, and Mesoamerica"],[240,134.71,2.04,"kicked and threw balls made of everything"],[240,136.76,3.46,"from leather and linen to papyrus and palm fiber."],[240,140.26,2.75,"The rules for most of their games have been lost to time,"],[240,143.01,4.71,"however some kids may have imitated adult games we know more about."],[240,147.94,3.84,"For example, ancient Greek children might have played episkuros,"],[240,151.86,1.96,"a team sport where players competed"],[240,153.82,4.59,"to push their opponents outside the pitch by hurling the ball as far as possible."],[240,158.78,2.92,"Meanwhile, Mesoamerican kids might have played a game"],[240,161.7,4.13,"where players try to hit a rubber ball through a hoop using only their hips."],[240,165.95,3.54,"Then again, in some cultures, this game had religious significance,"],[240,169.5,4.21,"so it's possible those children steered clear of this sacred sport."],[240,174.09,3.59,"In any case, rougher, physical play was often limited"],[240,177.67,2.54,"by ancient gender expectations,"],[240,180.22,4.17,"with young girls being left out of rowdier ball games in Rome and Egypt."],[240,184.76,2.88,"Spartan girls circa 400 BCE"],[240,187.64,3.5,"did engage in the same rigorous physical activities as boys,"],[240,191.15,3.38,"but largely because adults believed it would strengthen their bodies"],[240,194.52,1.42,"for childbearing."],[240,196.73,4.92,"Fortunately, this wasn’t the only reason ancient adults encouraged kids to play."],[240,201.82,3.88,"Athenian philosopher Plato observed that toys like building blocks"],[240,205.7,2.79,"and miniature farming implements were useful tools"],[240,208.5,2.71,"for learning the skills needed in adulthood."],[240,211.62,4.8,"That said, many ancient cultures expected children to give up their games and toys"],[240,216.42,1.13,"as they grew up."],[240,217.71,4.21,"A common Roman phrase described a child “giving up nuts”"],[240,221.93,2.5,"when taking up their adult responsibilities"],[240,224.43,3.25,"since nuts were used in a variety of simple games."],[240,228.06,3.79,"And young Roman girls left their dolls as offerings to the gods"],[240,231.85,2.13,"as part of preparing for their wedding."],[240,234.44,2.54,"However, other childhood games resembled those"],[240,236.98,3.25,"men and women continued to enjoy into adulthood."],[240,240.53,4.88,"One, called knucklebones, was popular across the ancient Mediterranean."],[240,245.82,2.96,"Usually crafted from the ankle bones of sheep or pigs,"],[240,248.87,2.38,"they could be used as jacks or dice,"],[240,251.33,3.04,"with different faces earning different point values."],[240,254.71,1.63,"In addition to games of chance,"],[240,256.33,3.71,"ancient adults played a variety of strategic board games,"],[240,260.05,2.54,"which may have been introduced during childhood."],[240,262.68,3.13,"Some particularly well-recorded examples include"],[240,265.8,3.21,"the Royal Game of Ur in ancient Mesopotamia,"],[240,269.14,3.34,"Go in China, Senet in Egypt,"],[240,272.48,5.26,"and Ludus latrunculorum— or, the Game of Soldiers— in Rome."],[240,278.61,4.88,"Today, playful kids follow the same instincts as their ancient ancestors."],[240,284.45,5.0,"They continue to make up ball games, invent stories for their favorite dolls,"],[240,290.12,3.21,"and send toy cars skittering across the floor."],[240,295.42,3.92,"Because the simplest kinds of play never get old."],[241,6.96,3.96,"Pandora was having a decisively bad day."],[241,11.13,2.63,"She didn’t set out to open the box;"],[241,13.76,3.46,"if anything, she’d resisted with every ounce of her will."],[241,17.35,2.92,"But her curiosity got the better of her,"],[241,20.39,3.67,"and a cavalcade of evils of every shape and size spilled out."],[241,24.19,3.79,"By the time she'd slammed the front door of her home shut,"],[241,28.07,6.63,"only 10 minor imps— of wrath, greed, gluttony, and sloth varieties—"],[241,34.86,3.04,"and three greater demons remained."],[241,38.2,4.17,"Pandora tried desperately to get them under control,"],[241,42.37,3.04,"but they resisted and pounded at the windows."],[241,45.5,2.13,"“This is hopeless!” she cried."],[241,48.09,4.42,"The first greater demon turned to Pandora and said,"],[241,52.51,4.42,"“Hope? Hope is still here. I just made her look like an imp."],[241,56.93,2.59,"Because that’s the kind of bad guy I am!”"],[241,59.64,4.92,"He whispered in the ears of the two other demons and continued."],[241,64.94,1.67,"“To make things interesting,"],[241,66.6,5.46,"I just told Abaddon which variety of imp Hope is,"],[241,72.19,5.25,"and Beelzebub how many eyes she has. Bye!”"],[241,77.91,3.04,"Abaddon taunted Beelzebub by saying,"],[241,81.08,2.75,"“I know you don’t know which one she is.”"],[241,84.16,4.75,"Beelzebub responded, “Well, I didn’t before, but now I know.”"],[241,89.08,5.38,"To which Abaddon replied in a huff, “Then I also know which one she is.”"],[241,94.8,4.71,"And both left in a hurry, without so much as a farewell."],[241,100.1,5.67,"Pandora knows her only chance to fix this mess is to capture Hope"],[241,105.85,3.25,"before the imps all escape her home."],[241,109.44,1.71,"But which one is she?"],[241,111.15,2.5,"Pause here to figure it out yourself. Answer in 3"],[241,113.65,2.13,"Answer in 2"],[241,116.15,2.21,"Answer in 1"],[241,118.66,4.92,"This problem originally took the world by storm in 2015"],[241,123.66,4.96,"when Dr. Joseph Yeo Boon Wooi posed it as Cheryl’s birthday problem,"],[241,128.71,1.29,"and for good reason."],[241,130.0,5.42,"The logic requires simultaneously modeling three different perspectives—"],[241,135.51,6.51,"ours, Abaddon’s and Beelzebub’s, whom we’ll call A and B from now on."],[241,142.76,6.09,"This puzzle is much more approachable if we start by doing what Pandora could not:"],[241,149.1,3.25,"cramming all of these imps into a box..."],[241,152.44,2.29,"in this case, of the table variety."],[241,155.15,4.25,"One key thing to note is that unlike many logic problems,"],[241,159.45,2.38,"the order of statements matters here,"],[241,161.82,4.8,"because the two demons’ knowledge changes over the course of the dialogue."],[241,166.75,2.29,"So let's start with the first statement"],[241,169.04,3.38,"and milk it for all it's worth before moving on."],[241,172.75,4.92,"In it, A says to B, “I know you don’t know which one she is.”"],[241,178.38,3.21,"At this point, A knows the imp type,"],[241,181.59,5.46,"but since there are multiple imps of each type, A doesn’t know who Hope is."],[241,187.68,2.34,"The eyes, however, work differently;"],[241,190.23,4.84,"if Hope had 3 or 5 eyes, B would know who she is."],[241,195.82,4.42,"This step is where a lot of people succumb to a common pitfall."],[241,200.49,3.71,"Because the important takeaway here isn’t to just eliminate"],[241,204.2,2.38,"the three- and five-eyed imps."],[241,206.74,4.21,"It’s to eliminate the entire rows of the table that they’re in."],[241,211.29,1.0,"Why?"],[241,212.29,5.38,"The trick is to think about B’s perspective from A’s perspective."],[241,218.17,6.17,"Through A’s eyes, the entire table boils down to one of these four options,"],[241,224.39,3.04,"because he knows which type the imp is."],[241,227.72,4.96,"If he knew Hope was a greed imp, she could have five eyes."],[241,233.23,5.3,"There’s only one imp with five eyes, so B could know who Hope is."],[241,238.73,2.92,"But A knows that B doesn’t know,"],[241,241.7,4.75,"so greed isn’t possible, nor, by the same reasoning, is wrath."],[241,247.24,5.55,"Now we’re down to just five possible imps, and we can move on to the next statement,"],[241,252.83,3.21,"which we have to consider through B’s eyes."],[241,256.38,5.17,"Critically, B is an expert logician, so anything we’ve figured out—"],[241,261.55,3.04,"like that wrath and greed are off the table—"],[241,264.76,1.71,"he’s also figured out."],[241,266.93,3.88,"That, plus knowing how many eyes Hope has,"],[241,270.81,2.5,"allows him to figure out who she is."],[241,273.52,4.59,"So she must be in a column that has only one imp left,"],[241,278.23,3.8,"meaning we can eliminate both one-eyed imps."],[241,282.28,5.3,"Finally, let’s examine the last statement and switch back to A’s perspective."],[241,287.95,2.75,"For A to now know who Hope is,"],[241,290.87,3.0,"there must be only one option left in that row."],[241,294.21,4.96,"Therefore, Hope must be the six-eyed sloth imp."],[241,299.8,2.13,"Before anyone else can escape,"],[241,301.92,4.17,"Pandora leaps into action and wrestles Hope to the ground."],[241,306.34,2.04,"The world may be a mess,"],[241,308.39,5.21,"but at least she’s got a glimmer of... well, you know."],[242,7.0,5.59,"For over four decades, Walter Cavanagh held a truly credit-worthy record:"],[242,12.8,2.75,"the most credit cards held by one person."],[242,16.39,6.96,"What began as a bet between friends led him to open over 1,400 cards,"],[242,23.39,2.38,"ranging from traditional bank-issued"],[242,25.9,4.67,"to those from airlines, gas stations, and even ice cream shops."],[242,31.03,2.59,"Most people have far fewer credit cards."],[242,34.24,3.8,"While useful for secure purchasing, handling emergencies,"],[242,38.03,1.96,"and building credit scores,"],[242,40.08,3.75,"credit cards can make it difficult to avoid overspending."],[242,44.62,5.25,"Globally, the outstanding credit card debt exceeds trillions of dollars."],[242,49.88,1.46,"And this is no accident."],[242,51.88,3.75,"Credit card companies intentionally design these systems"],[242,55.63,5.88,"to encourage spending and lock you into a cycle of interest and fees."],[242,61.89,3.54,"So, how can you avoid their most common traps?"],[242,66.35,2.46,"It starts with card selection."],[242,69.31,5.46,"Lenders often aggressively advertise perks like cash back, travel miles,"],[242,74.78,1.17,"and discounts."],[242,76.11,2.79,"But these offers only seem generous."],[242,79.08,4.92,"In reality, only a small portion of cardholders actually benefit—"],[242,84.16,3.59,"those who pay off their entire balance every month."],[242,88.33,1.67,"But according to one survey,"],[242,90.25,5.42,"46% of American cardholders carry a revolving balance."],[242,96.13,3.42,"For these users, the cost of interest and fees"],[242,99.55,3.67,"almost always exceeds the value of rewards."],[242,103.39,3.34,"If you think you may fall into this category,"],[242,106.77,3.38,"financial experts recommend getting a card"],[242,110.15,4.38,"with the lowest annual percentage rate, or APR."],[242,115.11,3.38,"This number is essentially the cost of borrowing money"],[242,118.49,1.46,"from the credit card company."],[242,120.24,2.79,"You pay that cost through interest charges,"],[242,123.03,5.46,"which kick in as soon as you carry any balance past a statement due date."],[242,129.5,6.17,"While APRs are listed as yearly rates, for most cards, interest accrues daily."],[242,135.92,3.8,"For example, a card with a 35% APR"],[242,139.8,5.25,"charges about 0.095% interest per day."],[242,145.47,3.21,"So a revolving balance of $1,000"],[242,148.9,5.05,"would accumulate approximately $29 in interest over a month."],[242,154.23,4.42,"Meanwhile, a card with a lower APR, say 18%,"],[242,158.82,4.21,"would charge about $15 in interest during the same period."],[242,163.74,2.25,"This difference can add up over time."],[242,166.41,5.13,"If you were to make a $50 monthly payment towards that $1,000 balance,"],[242,171.75,5.75,"the card with the higher APR would take an extra $325"],[242,177.84,3.21,"and an extra seven months to pay off."],[242,181.51,3.29,"And interest fees can be even more complicated."],[242,184.89,3.92,"If you carry a balance and actively use a card,"],[242,188.94,3.79,"new purchases start accruing interest immediately,"],[242,193.06,3.46,"even if you pay off those purchases before the statement date."],[242,196.98,3.17,"In other words, once you start to carry a balance,"],[242,200.28,4.59,"it’s much more difficult to avoid interest charges going forward."],[242,205.33,5.59,"And even after a bill is fully paid, you might still get one more charge,"],[242,211.21,2.79,"something called residual interest,"],[242,214.34,3.5,"for the days between your statement and payment date."],[242,218.76,5.46,"Low APR cards are the best option for minimizing these interest charges;"],[242,224.34,4.92,"however, be wary of cards that offer exceptionally low APR"],[242,229.52,2.0,"for an introductory period."],[242,231.77,5.09,"These flashy offers can mask things like complicated fee structures,"],[242,236.9,1.67,"steep late charges,"],[242,238.61,5.96,"and high default APRs that kick in once the introductory period ends."],[242,245.12,4.67,"While many countries require companies to disclose these conditions,"],[242,249.87,2.75,"they're often buried in the fine print."],[242,253.21,2.42,"Payment structures can also be confusing."],[242,255.63,3.42,"For example, many people mistake the minimum payment"],[242,259.05,3.25,"as the standard or even recommended amount to pay."],[242,262.63,3.88,"In reality, it typically only covers interest, fees,"],[242,266.68,2.25,"and a small percentage of the balance."],[242,269.18,3.34,"As a result, sticking to just the minimum payment"],[242,272.64,6.42,"can make even a low balance of $1,000 take over 18 years to pay off."],[242,279.57,4.96,"Paying the full card balance each month is the best way to avoid fees"],[242,284.53,2.59,"and actually benefit from rewards."],[242,287.45,1.71,"But it's not always possible."],[242,289.37,3.92,"So what should you do if you find yourself in credit card debt?"],[242,293.79,5.17,"The first step is to assess your finances and create a repayment plan."],[242,299.38,2.59,"If the debt is on a high APR card,"],[242,302.17,3.75,"transferring your balance to a lower APR card may help."],[242,306.13,3.71,"Just be sure to check for balance transfer fees."],[242,310.06,2.71,"If you’re juggling debt on multiple cards,"],[242,312.89,3.71,"it may be tempting to pay off the lowest balance first."],[242,316.94,4.84,"However, the most financially efficient strategy will always be"],[242,321.78,4.88,"to put any extra money towards the card with the highest APR,"],[242,326.91,1.5,"regardless of balance."],[242,328.99,3.25,"The key is to chip away one card at a time—"],[242,332.54,4.67,"just don’t be like Walter and open a thousand more in the meantime."],[243,6.84,5.84,"In March 2024, US veterinarians were facing down a mystery."],[243,13.09,3.67,"On dairy farms in Texas and nearby states,"],[243,17.01,5.8,"cows were losing their appetite and producing thick, discolored milk."],[243,23.06,4.71,"At the same time, cats on these farms were being diagnosed"],[243,27.77,2.84,"with an unknown neurological disease."],[243,31.07,5.63,"For weeks, all their tests for common cattle diseases came back negative"],[243,36.82,4.09,"until lab results finally uncovered the culprit:"],[243,41.24,4.42,"influenza A subtype H5N1 —"],[243,45.75,2.88,"more commonly known as bird flu."],[243,49.42,6.88,"This wasn’t the first time a virus adapted for birds infected non-avian animals."],[243,56.47,4.8,"In fact, every human influenza pandemic on record"],[243,61.31,3.84,"has been linked to some strain of bird flu."],[243,65.73,5.88,"So what is it about these viruses that makes them so frighteningly flexible?"],[243,72.23,6.26,"Of the four groups of flu viruses, all bird flus fall into group A."],[243,78.74,1.21,"Within this group,"],[243,79.95,6.17,"the viruses are organized into subtypes defined by two viral proteins."],[243,86.54,3.79,"Hemagglutinin binds to host cell receptors,"],[243,90.42,3.88,"allowing the virus to enter the cell and replicate."],[243,94.72,4.88,"And neuraminidase helps those new viruses leave the cell."],[243,99.93,4.13,"Currently, we’ve discovered 18 types of hemagglutinin"],[243,104.22,2.71,"and 11 types of neuraminidase,"],[243,107.14,5.34,"and different subtypes of influenza A use different combinations."],[243,112.73,5.59,"For example, the combo of hemagglutinin 5 and neuraminidase 1"],[243,118.32,2.84,"is called H5N1."],[243,121.78,6.59,"These proteins are necessary for influenza A to enter and exit host cells,"],[243,128.46,4.55,"so they generally determine which animals a strain can infect."],[243,133.5,3.88,"But birds, especially wild aquatic birds,"],[243,137.38,4.34,"have receptors for most known versions of these proteins,"],[243,142.05,4.05,"making them susceptible to nearly all combinations."],[243,146.56,4.46,"Worse still, these waterfowl shed the virus through their feces"],[243,151.1,1.79,"into lakes and ponds,"],[243,152.9,5.34,"increasing the odds of infection for any bird using those bodies of water."],[243,158.82,4.96,"And since the viruses don’t usually make these birds actively sick,"],[243,164.03,3.21,"they can act as reservoirs for disease—"],[243,167.33,3.13,"flying around and spreading their strain."],[243,170.96,4.75,"Thankfully, humans are mostly safe from all these flus."],[243,175.92,3.96,"We only have receptors for a few viral proteins,"],[243,179.97,2.71,"protecting us from most combinations."],[243,183.01,5.09,"And while bird flus can sometimes bind to receptors in our airways,"],[243,188.27,3.92,"our more vulnerable receptors are deep in our lungs."],[243,192.52,5.42,"This makes infection less likely and transmission less efficient"],[243,198.07,5.21,"compared to common human flus that target receptors in our nose and throat."],[243,203.74,4.88,"Unfortunately, influenza has other tricks up its sleeves."],[243,208.83,2.25,"Viruses have antigens—"],[243,211.08,4.92,"specific molecules that our immune system can recognize and attack"],[243,216.0,2.21,"to fight against an infection."],[243,218.42,4.21,"But two processes are constantly changing these molecules"],[243,222.63,2.38,"and challenging our immune systems."],[243,225.47,2.54,"The first is antigenic drift,"],[243,228.1,5.3,"where antigens change slowly over time due to random mutations."],[243,233.94,4.34,"This makes it harder for immune systems to recognize the virus,"],[243,238.48,3.63,"but the process is relatively slow and trackable."],[243,242.32,4.34,"In fact, we manage the antigenic drift of human flu"],[243,246.74,2.59,"by updating our flu shots every year."],[243,249.75,5.92,"The much more sudden and potentially dangerous process is antigenic shift."],[243,256.17,6.3,"This happens when two or more different influenza viruses meet inside a host"],[243,262.47,3.0,"and swap entire gene segments,"],[243,265.64,4.8,"instantly creating a new virus with all-new abilities—"],[243,270.68,3.75,"including the potential to bind to different receptors"],[243,274.48,2.59,"and infect different species."],[243,277.69,3.71,"This is why bird flus are so concerning."],[243,281.65,4.75,"Since birds are vulnerable to so many influenza A subtypes,"],[243,286.57,4.0,"they’re natural reservoirs for antigenic shifts."],[243,290.95,2.79,"Consider our dairy farm mystery."],[243,293.96,5.71,"While H5N1 used to infect mostly birds, in the past few years,"],[243,299.67,3.96,"it’s been found in over 70 mammal species,"],[243,303.67,5.88,"including cats, dairy cows, foxes, seals, and bears."],[243,309.81,6.8,"Each new mammalian infection gives the virus more chances to drift or shift,"],[243,316.77,4.8,"potentially spilling over into humans, and igniting a pandemic."],[243,322.03,3.92,"And since our immune systems aren’t trained to recognize"],[243,325.95,2.25,"avian influenza proteins,"],[243,328.28,2.84,"the effects of these viruses can be deadly."],[243,331.49,1.83,"So, should we panic?"],[243,333.87,1.0,"Not yet."],[243,335.08,5.17,"Despite the fact that influenza is ubiquitous in birds across the globe,"],[243,340.46,4.84,"bird flu infections in humans are still relatively rare."],[243,345.97,3.04,"What's most important is to stay vigilant."],[243,349.22,5.42,"Scientists are working around the clock to monitor ongoing viral evolution,"],[243,354.93,3.92,"test vaccines, and strengthen biosecurity."],[243,359.48,2.96,"Everything could change with a single outbreak,"],[243,362.61,6.05,"ao the only way to win this viral arms race is to stay one flap ahead."],[244,12.84,2.59,"Order, order! Who do we have on the stand today?"],[244,15.43,4.55,"Alexander... the Great, eh? Well, what’s so great about him?"],[244,20.6,3.25,"Your honor, the better question is, what's not great about him?"],[244,23.85,1.71,"He was the King of Macedon"],[244,25.56,4.88,"and considered a living demigod by Egyptians and Greeks alike."],[244,30.73,3.88,"He conquered Persia when it was one of Earth's largest empires."],[244,34.7,3.13,"And he was a thoughtful student of Aristotle,"],[244,37.91,4.5,"creative enough to untie the impossibly tight Gordian knot"],[244,42.41,1.46,"by cutting it in half."],[244,44.29,1.79,"The Gordian knot is a myth—"],[244,46.08,4.92,"just like the countless other stories invented to enhance Alexander’s legend!"],[244,51.21,3.42,"Calling him “great” just plays into this propaganda"],[244,54.63,3.54,"and glorifies his ruthless military ambitions,"],[244,58.26,3.09,"such as his unprovoked invasion of Persia."],[244,61.68,2.75,"Alexander invaded Persia to free Greeks!"],[244,64.44,1.63,"A century before his time,"],[244,66.06,4.05,"Persia had sacked Athens and conquered countless Greek lands."],[244,70.27,3.96,"So after Alexander’s father Philip expanded Macedonian control"],[244,74.24,1.29,"over much of Greece,"],[244,75.53,4.67,"he planned a final campaign to liberate those still under Persian rule."],[244,80.45,2.5,"“Liberate” is a generous word—"],[244,82.95,2.88,"those Greeks were just going from one despot to another!"],[244,86.21,2.92,"Not that they ever even experienced Philip's leadership,"],[244,89.13,5.0,"since Alexander had his father assassinated to take power for himself."],[244,94.26,1.71,"Objection, Your Honor."],[244,95.97,2.08,"We don’t know for sure who killed Philip."],[244,98.05,3.8,"Some evidence suggests he was murdered by a humiliated ex-lover."],[244,102.22,4.38,"Oh, please— that “disgruntled lover” story is ridiculous."],[244,106.77,5.67,"Before his death, Philip had taken a new Macedonian wife to father a new heir."],[244,112.57,4.71,"So it’s far more likely Alexander had his father murdered to take the throne"],[244,117.28,3.67,"and reap the glory from Philip's planned campaign."],[244,121.16,1.58,"That's one messy family."],[244,122.99,1.63,"Regardless of Philip's fate,"],[244,124.62,3.59,"Alexander was hardly riding his father's coattails."],[244,128.37,3.67,"His tactical brilliance was essential for defeating Persia."],[244,132.42,2.67,"For example, at the Battle of Issus,"],[244,135.09,3.5,"he strategically positioned his troops along the riverbank,"],[244,138.63,4.55,"forcing his enemies onto a narrow plain that disrupted their formation,"],[244,143.18,4.21,"limited their maneuverability, and neutralized their numerical advantage."],[244,148.1,2.96,"So what if he was a military genius?"],[244,151.06,5.59,"His power-hungry conquering spree left a wake of widespread death and ruin."],[244,157.11,3.63,"Actually, his conquests were neither bloody nor destructive!"],[244,160.99,3.25,"The Persian governor of Egypt surrendered without a fight."],[244,164.37,4.92,"And after most victories, he usually expanded existing infrastructure."],[244,169.5,4.38,"Not in Persepolis! He raised the Persian capital to the ground!"],[244,174.21,3.0,"He only did that to maintain Greek support—"],[244,177.47,4.38,"razing Persepolis was considered payback for what Persia did to Athens."],[244,181.97,5.51,"Besides, Persepolis was the only city he destroyed in over a decade of campaigning."],[244,187.6,2.34,"Typically, Alexander founded cities."],[244,190.19,5.13,"And befitting his massive ego, he named over 20 of them after himself."],[244,195.73,1.79,"That’s a lot of Alexandrias."],[244,197.99,4.0,"And it’s a huge part of why his legacy seems so impressive when,"],[244,201.99,2.79,"in reality, he only conquered one empire."],[244,204.91,1.92,"He just thought he'd conquered the world"],[244,206.83,2.38,"because he had such a narrow conception of it."],[244,209.46,3.71,"He didn’t even know India existed until he stumbled upon it."],[244,213.58,2.54,"And when he tried his hand at conquest there,"],[244,216.38,3.04,"his soldiers rebelled and forced him to turn back."],[244,219.88,3.09,"It’s true his men mutinied after being pushed too far,"],[244,222.97,3.67,"but Alexander and his forces had a deep respect for each other."],[244,226.81,3.84,"He fought alongside them in battle, led cavalry charges,"],[244,230.64,4.09,"and never asked them to do anything he wouldn't do himself."],[244,234.9,4.25,"Part of his greatness was his ability to cultivate talented individuals."],[244,239.49,3.5,"If only he paid similar attention to all his subjects."],[244,242.99,4.38,"His attempts at cultural assimilation led to resentment and rebellion"],[244,247.37,2.92,"from Greeks, Persians, and Macedonians."],[244,250.41,5.38,"And he left Macedon in the hands of a regent for almost his entire reign."],[244,256.17,2.25,"That regent was well regarded."],[244,258.42,4.3,"And in his defense, Alexander’s diverse subjects all expected their king"],[244,262.72,2.29,"to behave in very different ways."],[244,265.09,3.13,"The Macedonians saw him as a hereditary king"],[244,268.22,2.79,"who needed public support to remain in power,"],[244,271.02,2.63,"so they expected him to respect their wishes."],[244,273.81,2.75,"Since the Greeks refused to be ruled by a king,"],[244,276.56,3.17,"they thought he should behave as a first among equals."],[244,279.86,2.79,"And the Egyptians and Persians believed he should act"],[244,282.65,2.84,"as a semi-divine absolute monarch!"],[244,285.66,2.5,"It was an impossible balancing act."],[244,288.16,5.17,"And since he died suddenly of an unidentified illness at just 32 years old,"],[244,293.46,4.38,"he simply didn't live long enough for us to judge his governing abilities."],[244,298.17,1.92,"I'll be the judge of what we can judge."],[244,300.3,1.29,"Of course, Your Honor."],[244,301.76,3.21,"I just think failing to plan for his own death"],[244,304.97,2.84,"tells how uninvested he was in ruling."],[244,308.01,4.63,"On his deathbed, he declared his empire should go “to the strongest,”"],[244,312.85,4.05,"setting the stage for decades of destructive power struggles."],[244,317.19,2.21,"Now you're buying into the legends."],[244,319.4,4.71,"Those final words were likely fabricated to suggest Alexander's approval"],[244,324.11,1.88,"for the conflicts that followed."],[244,326.15,1.92,"But you can hardly judge a man by those"],[244,328.07,3.88,"who invoke his name to justify their own ambitions."],[244,332.12,1.96,"If we want to talk about legacy,"],[244,334.12,4.09,"let’s give him credit for ushering in the Hellenistic Age,"],[244,338.29,3.92,"where Greek cultural influence spread across Europe and Asia."],[244,342.42,4.05,"Like it or not, Alexander fundamentally transformed the world."],[244,346.59,1.0,"Fair enough."],[244,347.59,5.21,"But it’s hard to judge anyone clearly when they cast a shadow this, well, great."],[244,353.22,3.46,"Whether he was a ruthless conqueror or an embattled leader,"],[244,356.69,3.42,"Alexander the Great is undeniably a legend."],[244,360.11,6.3,"But trying to separate fact from fiction is all part of putting history on trial."],[245,4.25,3.34,"Sally Kohn: Oh, no, no, no, no."],[245,7.88,1.33,"Jeff Maurer: Wow."],[245,9.76,1.83,"I didn't think that was going to work."],[245,11.59,2.09,"SK: I really liked that sound you just made."],[245,13.72,1.17,"I hate this question."],[245,14.89,1.63,"JM: I love that, that is awesome."],[245,16.51,1.21,"SK: I believe it."],[245,17.72,1.96,"Would you dance with me for a second?"],[245,28.69,1.46,"SK: Hi, my name is Sally Kohn."],[245,30.19,3.09,"I am a media trainer and a communications strategist."],[245,33.32,2.25,"I am a democracy superfan,"],[245,35.62,5.17,"and I think democracy can work better for our lives and our wallets."],[245,40.83,3.42,"JM: Hi, my name is Jeff Maurer, and I work in defense tech"],[245,44.29,3.25,"and getting the most disruptive technologies to advance"],[245,47.54,1.67,"into our warfighters."],[245,49.21,2.13,"Director: Today we're playing Idea Knockdown."],[245,51.38,1.75,"Red blocks are rapid-fire questions,"],[245,53.13,3.04,"and black blocks have questions that go a little deeper."],[245,56.22,3.17,"Whoever knocks down the tower will have to do a mystery challenge."],[245,61.31,1.71,"SK: Huh!"],[245,63.02,1.38,"JM: Now on top."],[245,65.1,1.17,"Yay!"],[245,66.31,1.63,"SK: Alright, red card, here we go."],[245,67.94,2.84,"It says, is there a version of democracy"],[245,70.78,2.0,"that actually works for everyday people?"],[245,72.78,1.17,"Why, yes."],[245,73.99,3.34,"I think there is a version of democracy that works for everyday people,"],[245,77.32,2.38,"but it requires getting money out of politics"],[245,79.7,3.79,"and changing our expectations of what democracy is meant to do"],[245,83.54,2.09,"to actually prioritize working people."],[245,85.67,1.17,"JM: Nice."],[245,86.88,2.54,"SK: Look at me, I'm stepping back, come on."],[245,89.46,1.79,"JM: Oh, that feels like a winner."],[245,91.25,1.17,"There we go."],[245,92.42,1.71,"SK: Winner, winner, chicken dinner."],[245,94.13,1.83,"JM: Right there. OK, red card."],[245,96.01,4.55,"Should there be a wealth limit for those running for office?"],[245,100.6,2.0,"SK: Oh, interesting."],[245,102.64,2.42,"JM: I think we could definitely have a couple beers"],[245,105.06,2.0,"and talk about this for a very long time."],[245,107.06,1.17,"Who's got the beers?"],[245,108.23,3.09,"SK: You don't think there should be limits for anything, correct?"],[245,111.32,1.46,"That's the libertarian in you."],[245,112.78,1.21,"JM: Yeah, that's true."],[245,113.98,3.17,"I don't necessarily think that wealth on its own is inherently bad,"],[245,117.2,4.04,"and I don't necessarily like that our leadership skews"],[245,121.28,1.21,"toward the ultra-wealthy."],[245,122.49,3.34,"And so there is a disconnect between the people representing us"],[245,125.83,1.63,"and the people being represented."],[245,127.46,2.84,"And I don't think our founders would have liked that either,"],[245,130.29,1.33,"even though they had --"],[245,131.67,3.04,"SK: That's hard to say because they were all landed white dudes."],[245,135.59,1.38,"But our values,"],[245,137.01,2.34,"our values and our history never quite matched up."],[245,139.39,3.09,"JM: What it says and what we do are two totally different things."],[245,142.47,1.88,"And that's the part that we should fix."],[245,144.35,2.08,"So it's not that I believe in wealth limits,"],[245,146.48,3.09,"it's that I believe we should, again, take money out of politics."],[245,149.6,3.67,"And I think that will get us more candidates and more representatives"],[245,153.32,2.04,"who are truly representative of the people."],[245,155.36,1.21,"And guess what?"],[245,156.61,2.75,"We're not all millionaires and nor should be our Congress."],[245,159.41,2.04,"Oh, wow, that's a good one."],[245,161.49,2.34,"SK: Well, it was loose, so I went with it."],[245,164.99,3.67,"And that's looking a little shaky."],[245,169.29,1.21,"OK."],[245,170.5,5.17,"Do you think the next generation will have more opportunities than yours?"],[245,175.67,5.17,"We are on a knife's edge as a nation, at least in the United States."],[245,180.88,2.0,"I think this is true around the world too,"],[245,182.93,3.59,"when you think about, for instance, cuts to USAID and aid"],[245,186.56,3.34,"and the sort of disintegration of the global world order,"],[245,189.89,2.59,"like, we can make a decision both as the United States"],[245,192.52,1.63,"and as a global community,"],[245,194.19,4.71,"to pay forward the wealth, the opportunity,"],[245,198.9,2.17,"the good stuff, the roads, the schools."],[245,201.53,5.67,"Or to allow extreme wealth and extreme inequality to pile up."],[245,207.2,1.83,"Of course, then we haven't even gotten"],[245,209.08,2.09,"into the impacts of climate and all of that."],[245,211.17,1.54,"So I like, truly, truly,"],[245,212.75,2.71,"truly pray we, as a world, come to our senses,"],[245,215.46,2.29,"but it does keep me up at night."],[245,219.97,1.58,"Yeah, I think they're all stuck."],[245,221.55,1.21,"Oh, oh."],[245,222.76,1.79,"JM: Oh, yeah, here we go."],[245,224.6,3.17,"SK: Don't get too cocky before you put it down."],[245,227.81,1.17,"JM: That's true."],[245,229.48,3.38,"What's one thing worth rebuilding right now?"],[245,232.9,1.88,"I am going to go with trust."],[245,234.77,1.33,"Trust and integrity."],[245,236.15,2.0,"Those two things kind of go hand in hand."],[245,238.57,2.17,"Without those, we just can't do anything else."],[245,240.74,2.59,"There's no functioning and there's no working together,"],[245,243.32,2.63,"which means we can't do anything greater than ourselves."],[245,245.99,1.63,"SK: All right, I like that answer."],[245,247.66,1.42,"I'm making a bold choice."],[245,249.12,1.96,"JM: That is -- Wow, look at you."],[245,252.08,3.04,"SK: I'm really trying to set up my opponent here."],[245,256.46,2.08,"I'm, like, afraid to touch the cards."],[245,258.96,1.38,"JM: I can pass one to you."],[245,260.38,1.38,"SK: No, we're good."],[245,261.8,2.63,"Why is authoritarianism winning globally?"],[245,264.43,1.67,"Well, it's not friggin' winning."],[245,266.14,2.79,"It just happens to be having a moment."],[245,268.97,1.58,"JM: A little fascism flare-up."],[245,270.56,4.34,"SK: Authoritarianism is flaring up around the globe."],[245,275.27,2.71,"Not because of anything authoritarianism is doing right,"],[245,277.98,2.21,"but because of what democracy is getting wrong,"],[245,280.24,3.25,"and in particular, that democracy is not delivering like, real,"],[245,283.53,5.17,"tangible improvements in people's lives, in particular financial improvements."],[245,288.74,3.71,"And there is a massive correlation between countries"],[245,292.45,3.46,"that have extreme income inequality"],[245,295.92,1.75,"and see erosions of democracy,"],[245,297.71,2.09,"which is understandable because, like,"],[245,299.84,3.59,"why would you vote for a system that isn't working for you?"],[245,303.47,2.59,"JM: Oh, I think I’ve got a red in my hair."],[245,306.05,1.25,"Oh, maybe I don't."],[245,309.18,1.17,"We're going to go slow."],[245,310.35,1.46,"This might take a few minutes."],[245,311.85,1.46,"SK: Waiting with bated breath."],[245,313.31,1.63,"I don't know what bated breath is."],[245,314.98,1.25,"JM: We should Google that."],[245,318.36,2.0,"We did it. Now I just need to pick a card."],[245,320.36,3.09,"SK: It's like this weird feeling of like, I'm both happy for you,"],[245,323.44,1.17,"and sad for myself."],[245,324.61,1.71,"Now I have to -- Alright, go ahead."],[245,326.36,1.75,"JM: Ooh, this one's going to be fun."],[245,328.12,2.92,"What's an overused word in politics?"],[245,331.08,3.42,"This one is a pet peeve for me, particularly: bipartisan."],[245,335.5,3.71,"We have so many different factions, so many different Americans,"],[245,339.21,2.67,"so many different perspectives and points of view,"],[245,341.92,4.8,"there is no way that only two parties can represent them."],[245,346.76,2.17,"And I think Americans, right at this moment,"],[245,348.97,2.5,"are seeing a lot of that frustration."],[245,351.47,4.25,"And so when we talk about partisan, left, right, bipartisan, particularly,"],[245,355.77,3.09,"that is just a distraction"],[245,358.9,3.17,"for the fact that we are not getting our voices heard."],[245,362.11,2.84,"SK: I just, you know, here's the problem, Jeff,"],[245,364.99,4.13,"is that I’m a slightly competitive person and don’t like losing."],[245,369.16,1.21,"Yeah."],[245,370.7,3.92,"JM: Oh, oh, wait a minute, wait a minute."],[245,376.41,1.17,"SK: Oh."],[245,377.58,1.88,"JM: That's impressive."],[245,380.54,1.38,"SK: Thank goodness."],[245,382.5,3.59,"Trying to make me do some embarrassing stuff in the streets of Philadelphia,"],[245,386.13,1.71,"it's 99 friggin' degrees out there."],[245,387.84,2.25,"What's the version of your city or country"],[245,390.09,1.79,"you're still holding out hope for?"],[245,391.93,1.75,"I have a lot of hope."],[245,393.68,2.67,"I think hope is like,"],[245,396.39,4.55,"one of the most important civic tools."],[245,400.98,1.75,"Hope and love, right?"],[245,402.77,3.96,"I think if you love your country"],[245,406.78,2.46,"and you love all --"],[245,409.28,2.17,"all of the people in it,"],[245,411.45,2.75,"and you have hope that things can be better,"],[245,414.24,1.96,"I think nothing is impossible."],[245,416.25,1.5,"JM: Can I just say I love that?"],[245,417.75,1.25,"That is awesome."],[245,419.04,1.17,"SK: I believe it."],[245,420.25,3.55,"I mean, I, will say the flip side, and I feel this intensely now,"],[245,423.84,3.29,"is how much of our society is held together by norms:"],[245,427.17,3.09,"good behavior, decency, kindness, respect,"],[245,430.26,3.84,"and how consequential it can be"],[245,434.14,1.38,"when those are not upheld."],[245,435.51,1.83,"But I think most people,"],[245,437.39,3.09,"and I live in a really politically diverse community,"],[245,440.52,1.79,"everybody comes to the four-way stop"],[245,442.36,1.96,"and most everybody is like,"],[245,444.36,1.13,"\"Oh no, you go first.\""],[245,445.52,3.67,"And I just think that's the best of us."],[245,449.24,2.75,"And it gives me hope for all the rest."],[245,451.99,1.33,"JM: So hard on this one."],[245,454.7,1.25,"OK, here we go."],[245,455.99,2.21,"SK: We didn't think we would be this good."],[245,465.0,1.25,"Yeah, that was fantastic."],[245,466.25,1.25,"JM: Let's pick a red card."],[245,467.55,1.79,"Is privacy still possible?"],[245,469.34,1.88,"Ooh, that's a good one."],[245,472.3,1.71,"Sadly, I think the answer is no."],[245,474.01,4.38,"And I think that is what's creating so much of the angst"],[245,478.43,3.46,"and societal unrest in many ways."],[245,481.94,3.88,"The more that we're tracked and monitored, the more uncomfortable we get."],[245,485.86,2.79,"And technology, unfortunately, empowers that."],[245,488.65,3.75,"And we can look at what some of the big authoritarian technocracies are doing,"],[245,492.45,1.38,"and it's scary."],[245,493.87,2.04,"SK: The surveillance state is worrying."],[245,495.95,3.04,"However, I'm going to need some privacy soon when I cry."],[245,499.04,1.25,"So --"],[245,505.13,2.46,"JM: Oh, you did find one."],[245,508.05,1.38,"SK: Thank you, thank you."],[245,510.38,1.29,"JM: And you fixed the OCD."],[245,511.67,1.5,"SK: I did. I had to."],[245,513.22,1.33,"JM: I'm very proud of that."],[245,514.55,1.25,"SK: It's driving me crazy."],[245,515.85,3.5,"Is there a version of success that doesn't require someone else to lose?"],[245,519.39,3.09,"We all win when we all win, right?"],[245,522.52,2.88,"We can't have a country, any country,"],[245,525.4,1.29,"we can't have a world --"],[245,526.73,4.71,"Think about all of our great technological advances, all of our inventions,"],[245,531.44,3.34,"or think about climate change, right?"],[245,534.82,4.09,"Like, is there a version of success that doesn't require someone else to lose?"],[245,538.95,3.21,"It's almost like you want to ask the inverse question, right?"],[245,542.21,5.42,"We cannot survive as a species anywhere if we don't fix climate change."],[245,547.67,1.17,"We cannot."],[245,548.88,2.54,"So no one can succeed in that formula."],[245,551.42,2.71,"It's like, the wrong way to think"],[245,554.17,4.67,"of the interwoven web of humanity, which was always interwoven."],[245,558.89,1.71,"But it's like super --"],[245,560.6,2.67,"We're all connected now."],[245,563.27,2.0,"Oh, oh, no, no, no."],[245,565.31,1.83,"No, no, no."],[245,572.19,1.46,"JM: OK, here we go, alright."],[245,573.69,1.92,"Keep our OCD record strong here."],[245,575.65,1.17,"OK."],[245,578.03,1.25,"OK."],[245,579.33,2.34,"SK: Don't touch the table again."],[245,581.7,1.17,"JM: That's true."],[245,582.91,1.58,"What would you tell a 15-year-old"],[245,584.5,1.71,"about how the world actually works?"],[245,586.21,1.17,"Two sentences:"],[245,587.42,2.09,"Not the way you hope it does."],[245,589.54,1.29,"SK: But it could be better."],[245,590.84,1.33,"JM: But it could be better."],[245,592.21,2.59,"And, you have a responsibility in making it better."],[245,594.8,1.25,"Boom."],[245,596.09,1.38,"SK: Alright."],[245,597.47,1.5,"JM: I don't envy you right now."],[245,599.01,1.75,"SK: OK, wait, wait, whoa, whoa!"],[245,600.8,1.25,"JM: Careful."],[245,602.06,2.09,"SK: Pushing it back, pushing --"],[245,607.23,1.17,"JM: Good job."],[245,608.4,1.46,"SK: That really hurt my ego."],[245,610.52,1.17,"Mystery challenge."],[245,611.69,1.25,"JM: What have we got?"],[245,613.28,2.25,"SK: I hope I get to keep my clothes on."],[245,616.15,4.75,"Recruit people to join your \"political party.\""],[245,622.16,3.13,"Oh, no, it's not like a political, political party."],[245,625.33,4.13,"It's like a, get people to dance in my \"political party\" on the street."],[245,629.5,3.21,"JM: So we're going to go dancing, because that was way too much fun,"],[245,632.71,1.38,"and you were such a partner."],[245,634.13,1.38,"SK: You're going to help me?"],[245,635.51,1.83,"JM: I'm voluntarily going to help you."],[245,637.34,2.71,"SK: You're going to embarrass yourself in public with me?"],[245,640.05,1.92,"JM: I am going to hold the card for you."],[245,641.97,1.21,"Let's do this."],[245,643.22,1.13,"SK: Let's go."],[245,647.81,2.17,"Well, now it's feeling embarrassing."],[245,651.86,3.25,"Do you want to come over and dance with me in my political party?"],[245,655.15,1.21,"Come on over."],[245,658.24,1.13,"Person 1: What is this?"],[245,659.4,1.88,"SK: I'm Sally, nice to meet you."],[245,661.32,1.33,"So we're giving TED talks."],[245,662.66,2.09,"Person 1: It's my dream to be on a TED talk."],[245,664.78,2.75,"SK: Well, this is the closest you might get for right now."],[245,667.54,1.88,"Which is part of our TED dance party."],[245,669.41,1.5,"Are you ready? Let's go."],[245,671.33,1.21,"Ooh."],[245,673.17,1.42,"Here we go."],[245,674.63,1.17,"There we go."],[245,675.84,1.58,"JM: I don't know what it means --"],[245,677.42,1.83,"SK: Hey, do you want to dance with me?"],[245,679.3,1.38,"Person 2: No!"],[245,680.68,1.17,"JM: Why not?"],[245,681.89,1.42,"SK: These people look fun."],[245,683.35,1.63,"Would you dance with me on video?"],[245,684.97,1.25,"Person 3: I can't dance."],[245,686.26,1.63,"SK: Come on, I can't dance either."],[245,689.6,2.21,"Look how much fun we can have in Philadelphia."],[245,691.81,1.67,"More people, join the party."],[245,693.52,2.5,"What do you mean you can't dance?"],[245,696.02,1.5,"Oh, she is -- OK."],[245,697.57,1.75,"Alright, we're really dancing now."],[245,699.32,2.54,"But, I mean, that was more than I was bargaining for."],[245,701.91,1.63,"Give us a ringer."],[245,703.57,2.21,"A ringer, oh, come on."],[245,705.83,1.33,"JM: He's beaten all of us."],[245,710.29,2.04,"SK: Thank you, you're my last challenge."],[245,712.38,1.38,"Thank you so much."],[246,3.53,3.84,"Soundtrack for the apocalypse is silent."],[246,8.44,2.8,"We felt the weight of that total absence of sound"],[246,11.28,3.8,"when we arrived in the devastated neighborhoods of Altadena"],[246,15.11,1.53,"and the Palisades"],[246,16.65,3.27,"after the January 2025 firestorm."],[246,20.79,4.6,"Our small crew of two or three was usually alone for blocks and blocks."],[246,25.92,4.71,"There was no sound of cars or children or people or pool pumps"],[246,30.66,2.1,"or electricity across the lines."],[246,33.3,1.7,"Even the birds were gone."],[246,36.03,5.04,"I'm best known for having pioneered something called immersive journalism,"],[246,41.1,2.44,"which uses emerging technologies"],[246,43.54,2.54,"to create new stories that represent our world"],[246,46.11,1.77,"the way it really is:"],[246,47.91,3.07,"fully dimensional and not flat."],[246,51.68,3.27,"People then can experience stories with their whole body"],[246,54.98,1.94,"and not just with their eyes."],[246,58.52,1.47,"Also, as a journalist,"],[246,60.02,5.0,"I feel that we have a moral responsibility to bear witness to injustice,"],[246,65.06,2.37,"to wonder, to the human condition."],[246,68.0,1.77,"With immersive journalism,"],[246,69.8,3.64,"I am merging those new technologies with that code of ethics."],[246,74.0,1.84,"I'm also founding director"],[246,75.87,2.44,"for the Narrative and Emerging Media program"],[246,78.31,2.13,"at Arizona State University."],[246,80.48,1.53,"So when the fires broke out,"],[246,82.05,3.04,"I mobilized my colleagues and my graduate students,"],[246,85.12,1.83,"and we went out to cover,"],[246,86.95,2.77,"using our Cronkite journalism news passes"],[246,89.75,3.44,"what firemen called “Nuketown.”"],[246,94.12,2.27,"When we got there, the fires were basically out,"],[246,96.39,2.34,"so there wasn't anything anyone could really do."],[246,98.76,2.9,"But when we'd run into a fire crew, they would rush over,"],[246,101.7,3.14,"and we thought, \"Wow, they're going to question our press passes.\""],[246,104.83,3.0,"But no, they just wanted to help in any way that they could."],[246,107.87,4.14,"And in our case, it was just water or granola bars."],[246,113.54,3.77,"All of us were suffering from shock and sorrow"],[246,117.31,4.44,"as the sole witnesses for the devastation around us."],[246,123.79,1.84,"It was grueling work."],[246,125.66,2.67,"The air was toxic, it was incredibly hot."],[246,128.69,2.67,"We were wearing hazmat suits and heavy boots"],[246,131.4,1.74,"and double-filtered masks."],[246,133.73,3.64,"We were using specialized equipment designed by Mike Caronna,"],[246,137.4,3.84,"who is a photojournalist and the former general manager for Reuters."],[246,141.27,4.2,"We had 3D-printed special parts that ... clamped to the top,"],[246,145.51,2.17,"middle and bottom of a pole,"],[246,147.71,2.84,"so we could shoot in every direction all at once."],[246,151.25,3.37,"Each one of those dots represents a shot that we took,"],[246,154.62,3.74,"and then [they] would be fused together using artificial intelligence."],[246,158.39,4.44,"I also used my phone to make very painstaking captures."],[246,163.19,2.9,"You have to use your camera like a paintbrush"],[246,166.13,2.27,"and get every bit, every angle,"],[246,168.43,1.3,"and at the same time,"],[246,169.77,2.6,"make sure that you're not stepping on upturned nails"],[246,172.4,2.37,"or broken glass or shards of metal."],[246,175.77,2.6,"This is my graduate student, Ashley Buschhorn."],[246,178.78,3.3,"She was trying to capture a cactus that had been really weirdly burned"],[246,182.11,1.57,"only on one side."],[246,187.38,2.6,"I'd often look up and see my colleague Mary Mathieson,"],[246,189.99,3.74,"and she’d been taking the gear into these incredibly dangerous situations,"],[246,193.76,2.67,"determined to get the best scan possible."],[246,196.43,2.07,"We scanned fallen angels,"],[246,201.26,1.27,"buddhas,"],[246,205.47,1.8,"melted trash cans,"],[246,209.14,1.27,"Teslas"],[246,213.01,1.4,"[and] surf wagons."],[246,217.58,3.14,"We filmed until the light was gone, every day."],[246,222.42,1.74,"We even used our headlights"],[246,224.19,3.27,"to capture this car that was sliding down the mountain."],[246,227.49,2.3,"We didn't know if it would be there the next day."],[246,230.86,1.5,"More than once,"],[246,232.36,3.77,"I'd have to leave the crew working and go sit in the car by myself."],[246,237.37,2.7,"These weren't just places on a map."],[246,240.07,2.07,"I grew up in and around these neighborhoods,"],[246,242.17,3.07,"and often we were scanning the homes of friends of mine."],[246,245.28,1.5,"They hadn't even been back yet,"],[246,246.81,2.54,"they didn't even know the status of their own places."],[246,250.11,1.33,"Like the cello teacher."],[246,251.91,2.2,"This is a home we brought my son to"],[246,254.12,1.7,"for years, every weekend."],[246,259.26,3.84,"This is a home where I held the baby shower for my first child."],[246,267.6,2.5,"This is my sister's good friend's equipment."],[246,270.47,2.44,"She taught horseback riding at Will Rogers Park,"],[246,272.9,1.4,"and when the fires broke out,"],[246,274.34,2.47,"she rushed over to go rescue the horses,"],[246,276.84,1.77,"and then her own home burned,"],[246,278.64,2.8,"and everything she owned was also destroyed."],[246,281.44,1.94,"This was a particularly difficult moment"],[246,283.38,4.91,"because I was standing next to the wall that had collapsed over a bedroom,"],[246,288.32,3.4,"where I said goodbye to one of my best friends about a year earlier."],[246,291.75,2.5,"She had died of cancer the next day."],[246,294.26,2.27,"Her partner escaped with a small suitcase"],[246,296.56,3.34,"and a loaf of bread from their favorite bakery."],[246,299.93,2.5,"The thumbnail was completely black,"],[246,302.47,1.53,"which is incredibly strange"],[246,304.0,3.84,"because none of my other thumbnails were like that on any of my scans."],[246,307.87,2.04,"There was also some incredible stories."],[246,309.91,3.97,"This was a house where my son spent most weekends during high school"],[246,313.91,1.64,"with a bunch of his buddies."],[246,315.55,2.9,"One of his buddies had been there the night before the fire,"],[246,318.45,3.34,"and he left his car in front of the house and took an Uber home"],[246,321.82,1.6,"because they'd been out partying."],[246,323.45,2.1,"They saw on Instagram an image"],[246,325.56,3.1,"which seemed to indicate his car was totally intact."],[246,329.13,3.24,"So I called him when we got there to describe what we were seeing,"],[246,332.4,1.64,"and it was extraordinary."],[246,334.06,2.24,"Other than ashes on top, his car looked [to be]"],[246,336.3,2.23,"in perfect condition."],[246,338.57,2.87,"He later got into the car and just drove it away."],[246,342.11,2.44,"Then there was the Bank of America building,"],[246,344.57,2.6,"where a lot of people had their safe deposit boxes,"],[246,347.21,1.97,"and they were told they'd been destroyed."],[246,349.18,3.34,"They'd lost family heirlooms and Holocaust papers."],[246,352.92,4.5,"But it turns out, underneath all this crazy rubble --"],[246,357.45,1.67,"everything was safe."],[246,359.12,2.6,"And here we were, taking these images,"],[246,361.76,2.04,"and there was nobody there guarding it."],[246,365.96,3.0,"Niantic, the company that makes Pokémon Go,"],[246,368.97,1.97,"learned about the work we were doing,"],[246,370.97,2.2,"and they used our material"],[246,373.17,3.54,"to try to help folks who'd been impacted by the fire."],[246,377.07,2.3,"And as a journalist, that's what you want, right?"],[246,379.38,1.44,"You want your work to be used"],[246,380.81,3.2,"in a way that can make an impact, that can help others."],[246,385.12,1.6,"I also had a third motivation"],[246,386.75,2.64,"about why I was doing the work I was doing."],[246,390.85,3.2,"I really understood personally the helplessness"],[246,394.06,3.94,"and just the feelings of loss,"],[246,398.03,2.5,"because a year and a half earlier,"],[246,400.53,1.6,"I'd had my own fire."],[246,411.98,3.94,"This was my home studio where I'd lost everything personally"],[246,415.91,2.6,"and professionally from pretty much my entire life."],[246,418.55,3.47,"During COVID, I had closed up my offices in Santa Monica"],[246,422.05,1.9,"and I'd brought everything here."],[246,423.99,1.64,"I mean, no one was coming to work,"],[246,425.66,3.14,"so this is where I thought it would be safe."],[246,432.0,1.2,"It turned out,"],[246,433.23,3.04,"my electric bicycle battery had exploded."],[246,437.94,2.94,"I lost projects and equipment."],[246,441.31,3.67,"I lost photos and video archives,"],[246,444.98,3.27,"diaries, family memories."],[246,449.41,1.3,"I inhaled smoke."],[246,450.71,1.3,"I burned my gums."],[246,452.05,1.57,"I damaged my lungs."],[246,453.65,3.37,"And I now have what might be a permanent neuropathy"],[246,457.05,1.3,"in my esophagus."],[246,460.06,5.27,"When I looked at the remains of what had been decades of my life,"],[246,465.36,1.77,"I just thought it was all gone."],[246,468.76,3.6,"But a few strange things actually survived."],[246,472.4,3.54,"This is a photo I took in college of one of my best friends."],[246,476.71,2.44,"My husband of 27 years ago,"],[246,479.14,4.24,"he wrote me this love letter after our first date and it said,"],[246,483.41,3.6,"\"I think I see a kindred spirit in you, and if you invite me,"],[246,487.05,2.3,"I'll cross the country in a heartbeat.\""],[246,489.39,3.67,"Well, I invited him and he asked me to marry him on our second date."],[246,493.59,3.3,"I also used my scans with my battle with the insurance company."],[246,496.89,2.04,"Like, they didn't want to pay on this sofa"],[246,498.96,1.53,"because I didn’t have a receipt,"],[246,500.5,2.07,"and the receipt had been burned in the fire."],[246,502.57,2.6,"My scans showed very clearly there was a sofa in there,"],[246,505.17,1.37,"and they had to pay on that."],[246,506.54,2.33,"And it was one of the reasons why I was out there,"],[246,508.91,1.3,"scanning everybody's homes."],[246,510.24,4.07,"I wanted them to be able to use what we had in any way that they could."],[246,514.75,3.24,"Another thing strangely survived the fire."],[246,518.01,2.67,"In 2009, I was at an event"],[246,520.68,2.47,"at the University of Southern California,"],[246,523.19,2.5,"and Mark Hamill was at the adjacent table."],[246,526.09,3.07,"Now, I grew up in LA, and normally I don't bug celebrities."],[246,529.19,3.1,"I just let them go about their lives peacefully, right?"],[246,532.33,2.77,"But my son was six and I couldn't resist."],[246,535.13,2.64,"I asked Mark Hamill to sign the program card."],[246,538.74,1.77,"And that flammable,"],[246,540.54,2.34,"fragile piece of paper,"],[246,542.91,2.57,"it somehow survived a lithium-battery fire"],[246,545.51,1.7,"that was so hot"],[246,547.24,2.8,"that the fire folks said it had to burn its course."],[246,550.41,4.6,"And there it was, like, it drifted right on top of the hellscape"],[246,555.05,2.07,"that used to be my entire life."],[246,558.49,2.9,"And not only that, Mark Hamill wrote a message."],[246,563.19,2.94,"\"The force will be with you always."],[246,566.16,1.74,"Your pal, Mark Hamill.\""],[246,568.53,2.87,"I can't help but take that message literally."],[246,572.17,3.0,"The force, it's within us all."],[246,575.74,2.7,"And it's not what we lose that defines us."],[246,578.88,1.4,"It's who we love"],[246,580.31,2.67,"and how we take that love forward."],[246,584.05,1.53,"I know personally,"],[246,585.62,2.54,"when you’re looking at what [are] the remains"],[246,588.18,2.27,"of what had been a vibrant life,"],[246,590.49,2.64,"you can't help but feel like everything is gone."],[246,594.22,2.57,"But silence is not emptiness."],[246,597.13,3.14,"It's a place to reflect and remember,"],[246,600.3,3.57,"to imagine the wild ways we can rebuild"],[246,603.9,3.0,"and to consider what really endures."],[246,607.57,4.24,"And now, as we come to the one-year anniversary of the fires,"],[246,611.81,3.57,"I'm calling on everyone to feel the force,"],[246,615.41,2.4,"to think about that love within you"],[246,617.85,2.23,"and help support our friends,"],[246,620.12,2.2,"our neighbors, our communities."],[246,622.99,3.24,"Together, we can rebuild Los Angeles,"],[246,626.26,2.2,"better and more resilient."],[246,629.66,1.17,"Thank you."],[247,7.25,4.88,"You are the only you that's existed in all of human history."],[247,12.3,3.13,"Your experiences are yours and yours alone."],[247,15.55,3.79,"Some of those experiences have taught you things that are absolutely"],[247,19.35,1.84,"worth sharing with an audience."],[247,21.31,2.33,"And that's what we're here to learn how to do."],[247,23.81,3.92,"Once you've found an idea that you're excited to share with an audience,"],[247,27.73,2.67,"you're ready to start putting a talk together."],[247,32.03,3.17,"The purpose of a talk is to say something meaningful."],[247,35.36,2.21,"But many talks never quite do that."],[247,37.99,5.3,"The number one reason this happens is that a speaker does not have a proper plan"],[247,43.29,1.83,"for the talk as a whole."],[247,45.46,4.38,"They may have planned what to say point by point or sentence by sentence,"],[247,49.88,3.29,"but did not plan how everything in the talk would link up"],[247,53.26,2.34,"to deliver a meaningful message."],[247,56.55,5.26,"There’s a helpful word that people use to analyze plays, movies, and novels."],[247,61.89,1.67,"It applies to talks, too."],[247,63.89,2.21,"The word is throughline."],[247,66.73,2.79,"The throughline of a talk is the main idea"],[247,69.52,2.79,"that ties together everything the speaker presents."],[247,72.57,2.34,"Every talk should have a throughline."],[247,75.61,3.09,"That doesn't mean a talk must only cover one topic,"],[247,78.78,4.67,"or only tell a single story, or proceed in only one direction."],[247,83.75,2.09,"It just means that everything in the talk"],[247,85.83,3.29,"should connect to support the main idea."],[247,89.84,2.79,"Here’s the start of a talk without a throughline:"],[247,92.96,4.46,"“I want to share with you some experiences I had during my recent trip to Cape Town,"],[247,97.47,3.13,"and then make a few observations about life on the road.”"],[247,101.18,4.96,"Now here’s the start of a talk where the throughline is made clear from the start:"],[247,106.52,4.13,"“On my recent trip to Cape Town, I learned something new about strangers,"],[247,110.65,2.96,"when you can trust them, and when you definitely can’t."],[247,113.61,3.96,"Let me share with you two very different experiences I had.”"],[247,118.28,4.0,"The version without a throughline might work for your family,"],[247,122.28,4.75,"but the version with a throughline is more exciting for a general audience."],[247,127.62,3.0,"Here are the throughlines of some popular TED Talks:"],[247,131.34,2.96,"“More choice actually makes us less happy.”"],[247,134.71,4.0,"“Vulnerability is something to be treasured, not hidden from.”"],[247,139.68,5.3,"“Let’s bring on a quiet revolution— a world redesigned for introverts.”"],[247,145.35,5.21,"“A history of the universe in 18 minutes shows a journey from chaos to order.”"],[247,151.06,3.84,"“Terrible city flags can reveal surprising design secrets.”"],[247,155.44,3.0,"“A ski trek to the South Pole threatened my life"],[247,158.45,2.29,"and changed my sense of purpose.”"],[247,161.99,3.67,"Remember lesson one when we compared a talk to a journey"],[247,165.66,2.42,"that a speaker and an audience go on together?"],[247,168.37,1.5,"If a talk is a journey,"],[247,169.87,3.71,"then the throughline is the path that journey takes."],[247,173.92,5.42,"Following the path of a throughline makes sure there are no impossible leaps."],[247,179.72,1.17,"By the end of the talk,"],[247,180.88,4.8,"the speaker and the audience have arrived together at a satisfying destination."],[247,186.6,2.38,"So, how do you figure out your throughline?"],[247,189.35,5.21,"Pick an idea that can be properly explored in the time you have to give your talk."],[247,194.86,5.17,"Then make sure everything you include in your talk links back to this main idea."],[247,200.24,5.38,"Creating a great talk that fits into a limited period of time can be hard work."],[247,205.78,2.96,"But there’s a right way and a wrong way to go about it."],[247,209.16,4.3,"The wrong way is to include all the points you think you need,"],[247,213.5,2.04,"but cover them as briefly as possible—"],[247,215.59,3.04,"maybe skipping out on details or examples."],[247,219.09,3.75,"You can create a short script this way with every topic you want to cover"],[247,222.84,2.09,"included in summary form."],[247,224.93,3.63,"You may even think there’s a throughline connecting it all together."],[247,228.77,4.3,"But throughlines that connect a great many things don’t often work."],[247,233.19,4.42,"If you rush through many different topics without exploring them deeply,"],[247,237.69,2.54,"your points won’t land with any force."],[247,240.49,1.5,"It’s a simple equation:"],[247,242.2,3.21,"overstuffed equals under-explained."],[247,246.53,2.38,"To say something meaningful in a talk,"],[247,248.91,3.5,"you have to take the time to do at least two things."],[247,252.62,3.88,"First, you have to show why what you have to say matters."],[247,256.96,2.34,"What is the question you're trying to answer?"],[247,259.34,2.21,"What's the problem you're trying to solve?"],[247,261.72,2.96,"What's the experience you're trying to share?"],[247,265.22,3.54,"Second, you have to flesh out each point you make"],[247,268.76,3.21,"with real examples, stories, and facts."],[247,272.44,4.92,"This is how an idea that’s important to you can be built in someone else’s mind."],[247,278.4,2.42,"To give a really good talk,"],[247,280.86,3.29,"you may have to cut back on how many topics you want to cover"],[247,284.24,4.5,"and instead focus on a single connected thread— a throughline—"],[247,288.91,3.13,"that you have time to present thoroughly and completely."],[247,292.54,5.13,"This is the right way to make a great talk fit into a limited amount of time."],[247,298.08,3.63,"You may make fewer points than you would without a throughline,"],[247,301.71,3.71,"but the points you do make will have more of an impact."],[247,305.68,1.88,"Less can be more."],[247,309.1,2.17,"Choosing a throughline will help you determine"],[247,311.26,3.84,"which topics to include in your talk and which to leave out."],[247,315.31,4.38,"It will help you filter out anything that doesn't connect to your main idea."],[247,319.94,2.42,"If you’re having trouble focusing your throughline,"],[247,322.36,5.55,"a good exercise is to try to say it in no more than 15 words."],[247,328.24,4.38,"What is the precise idea you want to build inside your listeners?"],[247,332.83,2.63,"What do you want them to take away from your talk?"],[247,336.25,4.13,"Here are some questions to ask yourself as you’re working out your throughline:"],[247,340.84,2.46,"Is this a topic that means something to me?"],[247,343.46,2.08,"Does it inspire curiosity?"],[247,345.97,3.21,"Does it offer the audience a new way of looking at something?"],[247,349.26,3.09,"Is my talk a gift? Does it ask a question?"],[247,352.56,3.5,"Is the information fresh or unexpected in some way?"],[247,356.39,3.71,"Can I truly explain the topic in the time I have,"],[247,360.11,2.21,"complete with necessary examples?"],[247,362.69,3.88,"Do I know enough about the topic, or do I need to do some research?"],[247,366.74,2.46,"Does this topic connect to my experience?"],[247,369.49,3.92,"What are the 15 words that capture my talk?"],[247,373.54,3.92,"Would those 15 words make someone interested to hear my talk?"],[247,378.46,2.59,"A speaking coach named Abigail Tenembaum"],[247,381.04,2.75,"recommends testing your throughline out on someone."],[247,384.21,3.09,"Saying everything you'd like to include in your talk out loud"],[247,387.43,2.25,"will help you notice which bits are clear,"],[247,389.88,2.34,"which bits could use more explanation,"],[247,392.22,3.59,"and which bits should be cut in order for your central message"],[247,395.81,2.08,"to land more powerfully."],[247,398.6,1.54,"Once you have your throughline,"],[247,400.15,2.33,"you’re ready to plan what you’ll attach to it."],[247,402.69,3.96,"Whether your time limit is two minutes, 18 minutes, or an hour,"],[247,406.78,5.51,"remember: only cover as much as you have time to really explore in depth."],[248,4.29,2.0,"So there's this very famous song."],[248,6.34,1.79,"I'm sure you're all familiar with it."],[248,8.17,2.59,"It’s called “My Way” by Frank Sinatra."],[248,11.59,1.79,"Now I love it,"],[248,13.43,3.88,"but there's one line in that song that has always bothered me."],[248,18.18,1.21,"It goes like this."],[248,20.81,1.38,"(Sings) “Regrets,"],[248,22.23,2.46,"I've had a few."],[248,25.02,4.59,"But then again, too few to mention.\""],[248,30.9,1.21,"Regrets."],[248,32.82,1.17,"I've had a few."],[248,35.16,3.34,"But then again, too few to mention?"],[248,38.95,1.29,"Now hang on a second."],[248,40.74,4.46,"I am pretty certain that Frank Sinatra had more than a few regrets"],[248,46.42,2.92,"Including hanging out with shady mafiosos."],[248,49.38,1.21,"So there's that."],[248,52.09,4.0,"But that song, and society in general,"],[248,56.14,4.09,"encourages us to minimize regret,"],[248,60.27,3.38,"as if they really don't have an impact on our lives."],[248,64.31,1.75,"But that's just not true."],[248,66.48,1.33,"For many of us,"],[248,67.81,2.92,"regrets are at the core of our identity"],[248,70.78,2.84,"and have a huge impact on our work,"],[248,73.61,2.96,"our relationships and how we live our lives."],[248,78.12,2.34,"I certainly have a few."],[248,80.49,2.17,"Some aren't very important."],[248,83.08,2.42,"I regret eating a box of donuts"],[248,85.54,2.59,"right before my annual physical last year."],[248,90.05,2.17,"Some are a little bit more serious."],[248,92.21,5.09,"I regret not spending more time with my son after my divorce."],[248,99.01,3.84,"But I have one deep regret"],[248,102.85,4.55,"that goes right to the heart of what I'm talking about today."],[248,109.98,2.17,"You see,"],[248,112.19,1.79,"I'm an accidental killer."],[248,115.44,2.46,"When I was a young man,"],[248,117.91,2.88,"I was driving home late one night"],[248,120.78,2.75,"with my high school sweetheart, Michelle."],[248,124.5,4.84,"And I was paying more attention to her than I was to my driving."],[248,130.84,2.29,"And I went off the road,"],[248,133.13,1.79,"and I hit a telephone pole."],[248,136.47,1.25,"And she died."],[248,140.01,3.0,"For decades after that,"],[248,143.01,3.34,"there were times where I was completely overwhelmed"],[248,146.35,4.13,"and brought to my knees by incredible guilt,"],[248,150.48,4.17,"paralyzing shame and sorrow and regret."],[248,155.53,1.5,"A lot of self-loathing."],[248,157.78,2.04,"Not very much self-respect."],[248,160.74,3.75,"And a desperate need to do whatever I could"],[248,164.54,3.04,"to pay back the life that I took."],[248,169.58,3.96,"And for many years, I thought I was the only one in this situation."],[248,174.88,1.25,"I was wrong."],[248,177.34,1.58,"Every single year,"],[248,178.97,3.25,"people in the US accidentally kill someone."],[248,182.76,4.3,"And many more are seriously injured or permanently disabled"],[248,187.1,4.25,"because someone made an unintentional mistake,"],[248,191.4,1.96,"just like me."],[248,194.94,4.5,"We all belong to a club that we never wanted to join."],[248,200.95,3.8,"And recently I found out that that club has a name."],[248,205.49,2.59,"It's called the Hyacinth Fellowship."],[248,208.58,2.38,"And last fall, I had the opportunity"],[248,210.96,4.09,"to attend their first annual in-person gathering in Detroit."],[248,215.88,3.29,"All perfectly normal, reasonable,"],[248,219.22,3.63,"caring people from all walks of life"],[248,222.89,4.5,"coming together to try to figure out a way"],[248,227.39,4.59,"to move forward and to cope through their own accidental killings."],[248,233.4,3.09,"One woman I met there, let's call her Marie,"],[248,236.48,4.04,"she was just one year out from having had a motorcyclist"],[248,240.53,2.96,"T-bone, her car, dying instantly."],[248,244.32,4.17,"And she told us that she had recently found out"],[248,248.54,3.8,"that two cars had collided in that exact same spot"],[248,252.37,2.04,"not long after her accident,"],[248,254.42,2.13,"and both the drivers had died."],[248,257.5,4.96,"And she said her very first thought was,"],[248,262.51,2.04,"\"Thank goodness they both died,"],[248,264.59,2.92,"because then no one has to feel the guilt.\""],[248,268.72,4.3,"And we all nodded because we knew exactly how she felt."],[248,274.56,3.75,"And one of the realizations I took away with me from that gathering"],[248,278.36,3.17,"is how ill-equipped we are as a society"],[248,281.53,1.88,"to deal with regret."],[248,284.49,4.55,"Now accidentally killing someone is very, very serious."],[248,289.99,2.71,"But none of us are immune to regret."],[248,293.41,1.5,"Maybe, as I've been talking,"],[248,294.96,2.54,"some of you here have been thinking about something"],[248,297.54,1.92,"that happened in your life."],[248,299.5,4.34,"Some unintentional harm you caused someone that you truly regret,"],[248,303.84,3.34,"that you wish you could take back and fix somehow."],[248,308.22,1.88,"The thing of it is,"],[248,310.14,2.84,"most of us don't talk about our regrets"],[248,313.02,1.92,"until maybe we're on our deathbed,"],[248,314.98,3.13,"trying to own our mistakes and make amends."],[248,318.77,1.83,"But by then it's usually too late."],[248,321.53,2.54,"Or if we do talk about them,"],[248,324.11,2.67,"we often get some really bad advice."],[248,328.12,6.09,"I remember one woman coming up to me at the funeral and saying,"],[248,334.2,1.33,"\"Don't blame yourself."],[248,335.58,1.54,"This isn't your fault."],[248,337.12,1.96,"God wanted her in heaven.\""],[248,340.88,3.38,"Now I know she was trying to make me feel better,"],[248,344.3,2.25,"and I know she truly believed that."],[248,347.18,2.17,"But at that moment, all I could think was,"],[248,349.39,1.67,"\"Are you kidding me?\""],[248,352.72,2.38,"But the really troubling piece of advice"],[248,355.14,4.92,"that I got over and over and over again"],[248,360.06,3.04,"was that I needed to forgive myself."],[248,364.49,1.17,"Huh."],[248,366.28,1.33,"Forgive myself."],[248,368.95,2.5,"That is the last thing I want to do."],[248,372.45,1.96,"You see, to me,"],[248,374.41,4.59,"forgiving myself is somehow making an excuse for my failure"],[248,379.04,2.84,"or deliberately letting myself off the hook"],[248,381.88,2.59,"so I could feel better about me."],[248,385.59,3.25,"No, I was wholly responsible for what happened that night."],[248,388.88,1.5,"And I always will be."],[248,391.43,2.63,"So forgiving myself is off the table."],[248,395.06,5.42,"But what I always needed to know was how do I move forward in a positive way?"],[248,401.9,4.55,"One thing I discovered is you can regret something"],[248,406.44,2.34,"and hold yourself accountable for it,"],[248,408.78,2.29,"but that doesn't mean you have to punish yourself"],[248,411.12,1.42,"for the rest of your life."],[248,414.54,2.84,"You could learn from your regrets."],[248,417.37,4.04,"You could try to transform them or try to do something good with them."],[248,422.42,3.38,"You know, one of the best pieces of advice I ever got"],[248,425.84,3.59,"came from a friend of mine in the form of two questions,"],[248,429.47,2.79,"and he asked me this right after the accident."],[248,432.3,3.63,"He said, \"So, dude, this happened to you."],[248,436.72,1.54,"What have you learned?"],[248,438.31,1.88,"And what are you going to do about it?”"],[248,441.6,1.79,"Pretty harsh, right?"],[248,444.4,1.67,"Still, he had a point."],[248,446.99,5.13,"But I was too overcome with sorrow and regret and shame"],[248,452.12,2.08,"to really think it through."],[248,455.91,2.25,"And that's the thing about regrets."],[248,458.7,2.17,"We can try to ignore them,"],[248,460.87,2.17,"we can try to dismiss them,"],[248,463.08,4.42,"we can even let them overwhelm us and undermine our relationships"],[248,467.55,2.38,"and our work for the rest of our lives."],[248,470.55,3.04,"Or we can choose a different path forward."],[248,474.51,1.21,"To own our mistakes,"],[248,475.76,3.88,"to do whatever we can to help those we harmed"],[248,479.68,1.67,"and make amends any way we can,"],[248,481.39,4.25,"and somehow try to make a positive difference in the world."],[248,487.11,2.71,"So the question then becomes, how?"],[248,491.11,4.0,"Well, for many years, I tried to do \"good works\""],[248,495.16,3.84,"in my work as a professional theater artist and in my writing"],[248,499.04,3.34,"and in my training programs on leadership and on respect."],[248,504.0,3.38,"But looking back on it now,"],[248,507.42,4.13,"I think that all that good work I was doing"],[248,511.55,2.92,"was unconsciously disingenuous."],[248,515.6,4.13,"You see, as good as they were, those things I was doing,"],[248,519.77,4.21,"I think I was just doing them to make myself feel better about me"],[248,523.98,2.79,"and to convince other people that I'm a good guy."],[248,527.65,2.84,"Which I am, just ask my three-month-old grandson."],[248,533.57,6.05,"Eventually, after doing some research on something called “moral injury,”"],[248,539.66,2.67,"which is where we intentionally"],[248,542.37,4.71,"or unintentionally break our own personal moral code,"],[248,547.13,2.71,"I realized that if I could make"],[248,549.84,2.54,"a powerful, positive difference in the world"],[248,552.42,4.8,"in the way that Michelle would have if she had lived"],[248,557.26,3.42,"or would have told me to do, if she could,"],[248,560.72,4.42,"then all the good I was doing would be wholly about her"],[248,565.14,2.42,"and not about assuaging my guilt."],[248,568.69,2.46,"So what I did was, seven years ago,"],[248,571.15,4.25,"I started an art scholarship in her name at our high school."],[248,576.07,2.92,"She wanted to be a professional artist,"],[248,578.99,4.55,"and it feels like that's exactly what she would have wanted me to do."],[248,593.63,2.5,"And you know what I regret now?"],[248,599.39,3.04,"Is not coming to that realization sooner."],[248,605.48,5.46,"Don't try to make the world better to make yourself feel better about you."],[248,610.98,3.92,"Change the world in the way that those you hurt would have done"],[248,614.9,3.42,"or would have told you to do if they could."],[248,619.87,3.63,"The woman from the Hyacinth Fellowship I told you about,"],[248,623.49,5.09,"well, she is now actively researching practical ways"],[248,628.62,3.88,"to make our roadways safer for motorcyclists."],[248,633.55,4.38,"And many in the group have become lay pastors and counselors and mentors"],[248,637.97,2.96,"to those who are still working their way through"],[248,640.93,2.46,"their own accidental killings."],[248,645.47,1.25,"Regrets."],[248,647.43,1.88,"We all have a few, right?"],[248,650.02,1.38,"And that's OK."],[248,653.23,1.79,"You can't change your past."],[248,656.74,3.92,"But you can use it to correct the future."],[248,662.37,1.17,"Thank you."],[249,7.0,1.54,"Dropping out of light speed,"],[249,8.55,5.3,"a fleet of rebel spaceships is greeted by a frenzy of laser blasts—"],[249,13.97,3.63,"illuminating the sector with brilliant bursts of light."],[249,18.01,3.92,"Science fiction has shown us scenes like this for decades,"],[249,22.02,5.38,"but the lasers in our daily lives seem far less dazzling and destructive."],[249,27.86,6.09,"Laser eye surgery can cut tissue, and laser cutters can shape wood or plastic,"],[249,33.95,3.84,"but neither seems strong enough to pierce a spaceship."],[249,38.2,5.34,"And laser pointers are more likely to annoy a cat than an evil empire."],[249,43.66,4.63,"So could sci-fi laser weaponry ever exist?"],[249,48.29,3.17,"And how do lasers even work in the first place?"],[249,52.01,3.54,"Lasers are devices that produce laser beams,"],[249,55.55,2.42,"which are ultimately just a form of light."],[249,58.05,2.79,"But these beams have special properties."],[249,61.06,1.0,"For example,"],[249,62.06,3.25,"the ordinary light from this incandescent bulb"],[249,65.31,2.42,"is composed of a rainbow of colors,"],[249,67.73,2.0,"each of a different wavelength."],[249,69.98,3.55,"And the particles that make up the light, called photons,"],[249,73.53,3.46,"randomly emanate outward in all directions."],[249,77.28,4.09,"Laser beams, on the other hand, are much more organized."],[249,81.49,3.71,"They're generated through a process called stimulated emission,"],[249,85.21,4.09,"which is part of the acronym that gave this tech its name."],[249,89.79,4.96,"Every laser houses a few photons, and when the device is turned on,"],[249,94.84,4.09,"it begins exciting a large number of electrons."],[249,99.22,4.92,"Stimulated emission begins when these particles collide,"],[249,104.14,6.26,"causing each electron to emit a second synchronized photon of the same color."],[249,110.65,4.34,"This photon then strikes other excited electrons,"],[249,114.99,4.92,"which in turn emit more and more of the same type of photon."],[249,120.24,6.26,"Eventually, this chain reaction generates an organized stream of light particles"],[249,126.54,2.0,"known as a laser beam."],[249,129.12,5.3,"Stimulated emission imbues these beams with two unique properties."],[249,134.42,1.46,"They’re monochromatic,"],[249,135.88,3.09,"meaning their light is composed of a single color."],[249,139.05,1.92,"And they’re coherent,"],[249,141.05,3.17,"meaning their light waves are all synchronized"],[249,144.26,2.38,"and travel in the same direction."],[249,147.23,4.92,"These two traits give laser beams all kinds of special abilities."],[249,152.23,4.38,"Whereas the light from a standard bulb spreads out to fill a room,"],[249,156.94,3.09,"coherent laser beams stay narrow,"],[249,160.2,5.71,"allowing some to travel over distances as large as thousands of kilometers."],[249,166.29,4.71,"Coherence also makes laser beams great for carrying information."],[249,171.29,3.5,"Since their light waves are perfectly synchronized,"],[249,174.79,3.21,"it's easy to detect any changes in the beam."],[249,178.42,3.88,"For example, when you shine laser light on a barcode,"],[249,182.47,3.54,"the scanner can precisely measure the reflected light"],[249,186.01,3.84,"and convert the pattern of black and white into data."],[249,190.39,5.84,"We can also encrypt a Morse code-like chain of data into laser light"],[249,196.32,3.29,"by creating the beam in short pulses,"],[249,199.61,4.96,"and laser light’s high frequency lets it carry much more information"],[249,204.57,1.42,"than radio waves."],[249,206.37,4.09,"Scientists rely on the precision of laser measurements"],[249,210.46,3.17,"in guidance systems that help robots navigate;"],[249,213.71,4.8,"spectroscopy tools that study the composition of different materials;"],[249,218.59,3.46,"and even in a gravitational wave observatory"],[249,222.05,2.79,"that measures ripples in space-time."],[249,225.26,2.75,"Being monochromatic and coherent"],[249,228.06,4.42,"are also what gives laser beams their destructive potential."],[249,232.56,4.96,"Whereas ordinary light distributes its energy across a large physical space"],[249,237.53,2.04,"and a spectrum of colors,"],[249,239.57,5.71,"laser light concentrates its energy into a narrow, single-color beam."],[249,245.49,4.75,"However, there are limits to how powerful these beams can get."],[249,250.62,3.75,"First, stimulated emission is incredibly fragile."],[249,254.58,4.92,"As lasers increase in power, the more internal heat they generate."],[249,259.8,2.63,"Heat de-excites electrons,"],[249,262.51,3.59,"rendering them unable to participate in the chain reaction"],[249,266.1,1.79,"that produces laser light."],[249,268.14,2.33,"So when there's too much excess heat,"],[249,270.56,4.04,"the mechanism gets disrupted and the laser stops working."],[249,275.06,4.34,"Furthermore, while laser beams can travel huge distances,"],[249,279.52,4.38,"doing so causes the beams to get scattered and absorbed"],[249,283.99,2.96,"by air molecules and other particles,"],[249,287.16,3.67,"weakening the light until it disappears entirely."],[249,291.12,1.63,"For all these reasons,"],[249,292.75,5.96,"blasters and laser cannons will likely remain in the realm of science fiction."],[249,299.05,3.5,"However, this doesn't mean lasers can't hurt us."],[249,302.88,4.75,"On a clear night, laser beams can damage small nearby targets"],[249,307.64,3.84,"like drones, mortar shells, and balloons."],[249,311.85,6.09,"High power lasers found in factories, machine shops, labs, and medical clinics"],[249,317.94,3.88,"can instantly burn and cut nearby skin."],[249,322.15,4.21,"And even the humble laser pointer can damage eye tissue"],[249,326.36,1.83,"with continued exposure."],[249,328.62,3.63,"So while lasers can't take down a starship,"],[249,332.41,3.63,"they still need to be used with care and caution."],[250,6.96,3.04,"For decades, Yvonne and Zaina have been happily married"],[250,10.01,4.05,"and co-leading a non-profit developing a cure for a life-threatening disease."],[250,14.18,4.38,"This work is largely funded by profits from a successful company Yvonne founded."],[250,18.89,2.92,"But one day the couple is involved in a tragic car accident,"],[250,21.89,1.83,"and Yvonne is rushed to the hospital."],[250,23.89,2.92,"The doctors can only do so much, and on her deathbed,"],[250,26.81,2.92,"Yvonne asks Zaina to promise her two things."],[250,30.19,3.21,"First, she wants Zaina to continue using the company’s funds"],[250,33.4,2.42,"to support their non-profit and its research."],[250,35.99,3.54,"Second, Yvonne recalls a conversation from years earlier"],[250,39.58,2.71,"where the two pledged that no matter what the future held,"],[250,42.29,1.42,"they would never remarry."],[250,43.87,4.3,"And in her final moments, Yvonne asks Zaina to recommit that pledge."],[250,48.34,4.04,"Holding her wife closely, Zaina promises to uphold Yvonne’s wishes."],[250,53.17,3.0,"In the following decade, Zaina keeps these promises."],[250,56.72,3.34,"But now several large pharmaceutical companies are likely to cure"],[250,60.06,1.71,"this disease at any moment."],[250,61.93,2.0,"Additionally, after years of mourning,"],[250,63.94,2.54,"Zaina has finally entered a new relationship."],[250,66.56,1.46,"She's happy with her partner,"],[250,68.02,3.04,"and while she's not sure if she's even ready to remarry,"],[250,71.07,1.83,"she feels the weight of her promise."],[250,73.53,4.46,"Given these new circumstances, should Zaina continue keeping these promises?"],[250,78.45,3.46,"Philosophy has a lot to say about the value of keeping promises."],[250,82.12,4.17,"For example, philosopher Tim Scanlon argues that promising"],[250,86.29,3.21,"is essentially about creating interpersonal expectations."],[250,89.71,3.96,"Making a promise assures another person that we’ll act in a certain way"],[250,93.67,2.21,"which they can incorporate into their plans."],[250,96.01,2.42,"But since Yvonne can no longer make plans,"],[250,98.43,3.71,"it seems like Zaina’s decision only affects herself, right?"],[250,102.43,1.04,"Maybe not."],[250,103.81,4.96,"Philosophers like John Rawls and David Hume argue that keeping promises"],[250,108.77,2.92,"isn’t as much about protecting the individuals involved"],[250,111.69,3.46,"as preserving the societal value of promising itself."],[250,115.44,4.05,"Hume sees promises as tools for signaling trustworthiness,"],[250,119.66,3.29,"so breaking them makes the practice less reliable overall."],[250,123.37,3.88,"Similarly, Rawls thinks keeping promises is a matter of fairness."],[250,127.54,2.54,"If you've benefited from this social practice,"],[250,130.08,3.46,"then justice requires you to uphold your end of the bargain."],[250,133.71,3.71,"But do these social concerns apply to promises we’ve made to the dead?"],[250,137.55,1.46,"And even if they do,"],[250,139.01,4.09,"shouldn’t Zaina’s decision on this personal matter focus more on Yvonne"],[250,143.14,3.04,"than on what Zaina might abstractly owe society?"],[250,146.47,2.04,"These questions don't have clear answers,"],[250,148.56,2.5,"and they might not even be relevant to this case."],[250,151.27,4.55,"Most philosophers agree that promises obtained through coercion or deceit"],[250,155.82,1.13,"aren’t binding."],[250,157.11,3.54,"And one could argue that Zaina’s promises were made under duress."],[250,160.87,2.34,"Who could say “no” to their dying spouse?"],[250,163.58,2.67,"On the other hand, Yvonne’s deathbed wishes"],[250,166.25,4.21,"were neither threatening nor particularly unreasonable given their history."],[250,170.92,2.92,"If we assume both promises are legitimate,"],[250,173.92,4.0,"we might think about Zaina’s dilemma through the lens of self-determination."],[250,178.17,3.63,"English philosopher John Locke believes people have a natural right"],[250,181.8,4.46,"to self-ownership that limits how much other people can determine what we do."],[250,186.39,2.63,"And his followers might say that making a promise"],[250,189.02,2.08,"doesn’t overrule this natural right,"],[250,191.48,3.46,"especially about something as central to Zaina’s self-determination"],[250,194.94,1.29,"as the choice to remarry."],[250,196.78,2.33,"Then again, what about Yvonne's rights?"],[250,199.36,2.0,"Did they all disappear when she died,"],[250,201.66,4.13,"or does Yvonne still have authority over her legacy, her relationship,"],[250,205.78,1.67,"or especially her money?"],[250,210.16,3.67,"Many countries have legal codes protecting the assets of the dead,"],[250,213.83,3.46,"and money is a powerful tool for pursuing self-determination."],[250,217.88,3.54,"So at a minimum, perhaps Zaina should respect Yvonne’s right"],[250,221.43,2.25,"to determine how her money will be spent."],[250,224.05,4.05,"At the same time, Zaina feels strongly that Yvonne would want those funds"],[250,228.1,1.75,"to go to where the need is greatest,"],[250,229.93,2.71,"like research into other less understood diseases."],[250,232.9,1.75,"And since the facts have changed,"],[250,234.65,3.29,"maybe Zaina is justified in updating Yvonne’s wishes."],[250,238.32,4.09,"But perhaps all this talk of ownership, authority, and self-determination"],[250,242.41,1.38,"is too impersonal."],[250,244.12,4.3,"Feminist philosopher Natalie Stoljar and Catriona Mackenzie"],[250,248.49,4.17,"argue that we construct our identities in part through societal relationships,"],[250,252.71,4.55,"meaning that Zaina’s identity and values are entangled with her marriage to Yvonne."],[250,257.34,3.5,"So perhaps honoring that marriage and the promises made within it"],[250,260.92,3.09,"could be a way for Zaina to affirm her own identity."],[250,264.38,4.59,"So, given all these considerations, what would you do in Zaina’s position?"],[251,3.17,2.0,"Welcome..."],[251,6.13,1.25,"to Africa."],[251,8.51,0.79,"Welcome..."],[251,12.89,2.29,"to Earth's wild home."],[251,22.52,2.63,"See a new side to animals you know."],[251,28.03,2.54,"And meet some you've never seen before."],[251,41.04,2.79,"This is the definitive look"],[251,45.71,2.04,"at the wildest continent"],[251,49.47,1.21,"on our planet."],[251,57.1,1.54,"There's no place..."],[251,64.94,1.88,"like Africa."],[252,6.92,3.29,"A figure perches, hunched in reflection."],[252,10.46,3.59,"But this canonical sculpture isn't just contemplation incarnate."],[252,14.43,5.0,"French sculptor Auguste Rodin intended it to represent a specific person—"],[252,19.43,4.63,"and fit into a much larger piece featuring the fiery pits of Hell—"],[252,24.39,3.71,"a project that obsessed him during the last decades of his life."],[252,28.52,5.0,"So, who was “The Thinker” and what was he actually thinking?"],[252,35.24,2.38,"Rodin's path to renown was rocky."],[252,37.95,2.71,"He grew up in a working-class neighborhood in Paris,"],[252,40.66,3.04,"applied to the esteemed school of fine arts,"],[252,43.83,2.46,"and was rejected three separate times."],[252,46.75,2.42,"After several years working as a craftsman,"],[252,49.21,4.96,"he submitted his first sculpture to Paris’ Salon— and was denied."],[252,54.51,5.55,"It wasn't until 1877, when he was 35 and fresh off a visit to Italy,"],[252,60.06,2.96,"dazzled by the Renaissance sculptures on display,"],[252,63.35,2.84,"that Rodin completed his first major work."],[252,66.69,3.63,"However, critics accused him of casting the lifelike sculpture"],[252,70.32,1.71,"directly from the model."],[252,72.48,3.63,"But he hadn’t, and other artists vouched for him."],[252,76.45,5.17,"As the controversy concluded, however, Rodin drastically shifted his style."],[252,82.54,3.34,"Rather than render academically realistic forms,"],[252,86.04,3.54,"he began creating rougher, more expressive surfaces."],[252,90.21,3.34,"Advances in camera technology had recently made it possible"],[252,93.55,1.83,"to capture perfect likeness,"],[252,95.67,3.84,"but Rodin argued that artistic renderings, though less precise,"],[252,99.68,1.17,"were more truthful."],[252,101.22,3.21,"Like artists helming the burgeoning movements of Cubism,"],[252,104.43,2.25,"Abstraction, and Impressionism,"],[252,106.69,5.92,"Rodin was poised to modernize sculpture, lending new life to classical forms."],[252,113.15,3.46,"And in 1880, he received his life-defining commission:"],[252,116.74,3.42,"a monumental doorway for a new French museum"],[252,120.16,5.67,"intended to echo the “Gates of Paradise” by Renaissance sculptor Lorenzo Ghiberti."],[252,126.5,4.09,"Rodin proposed its antithesis: \"The Gates of Hell,”"],[252,130.84,5.21,"a swirling, infernal composition featuring over 200 tormented souls."],[252,136.34,3.25,"It was inspired by Dante Alighieri’s “Inferno,”"],[252,139.84,3.67,"a 14th-century poetic journey through the nine circles of Hell"],[252,143.72,2.79,"and its doomed inhabitants’ downfalls."],[252,147.52,2.75,"Rodin began “The Gates” in clay,"],[252,150.27,2.46,"sculpting small, interlocking figures,"],[252,152.73,3.71,"his studio filling with fragments to be rearranged, combined,"],[252,156.44,2.71,"or enlarged as independent works."],[252,159.53,4.88,"Breaking with tradition, he left visible traces of the creative process."],[252,165.04,2.92,"However, the museum was never built."],[252,168.08,3.84,"And the project became a sprawling obsession of endless revision."],[252,172.46,3.79,"But it was one that would yield some of Rodin’s greatest sculptures—"],[252,176.34,5.3,"individual elements from “The Gates” that were isolated, refined, and scaled up."],[252,182.64,3.42,"Like many artists, Rodin had a team of studio assistants"],[252,186.06,1.92,"who were talented in their own right."],[252,188.31,3.13,"For “The Gates,” he favored an ancient technique,"],[252,191.6,3.84,"the lost-wax method, to go from clay to bronze."],[252,196.03,3.09,"For each sculpture, his team made various molds,"],[252,199.11,3.09,"beginning with plaster and moving into hollow wax replicas"],[252,202.2,1.42,"they’d coat and heat,"],[252,203.78,3.96,"melting away the wax, before pouring molten bronze in."],[252,208.25,4.38,"Finally, they’d break the outer shell to reveal the solid metal sculpture within."],[252,213.0,4.05,"Complex compositions were cast in sections and soldered together."],[252,217.63,3.13,"Then, Rodin’s team would finish the surface,"],[252,220.93,2.38,"applying a chemical patina."],[252,224.47,4.42,"Within “The Gates of Hell,” forms described in Dante’s “Inferno”"],[252,228.97,2.58,"writhed in sin-struck anguish:"],[252,231.98,4.84,"lovers Paolo and Francesca grappling eternally in forbidden lust"],[252,237.02,3.84,"and political traitor Count Ugolino cannibalizing his sons"],[252,240.86,2.25,"in his final desperate moments."],[252,243.49,4.05,"Rodin also found infernal inspiration in other works,"],[252,247.7,5.05,"like the carnal themes explored in a poetry collection by Charles Baudelaire."],[252,253.67,5.13,"But above all of this hellish chaos was to be a single seated figure—"],[252,259.09,1.29,"not just any man,"],[252,260.42,1.88,"but the author of “Inferno,”"],[252,262.76,3.5,"Dante, himself, pondering the suffering below,"],[252,266.26,5.8,"considering human nature’s great pitfalls, the weight bearing down on his fist."],[252,272.64,5.42,"Rodin originally called him “The Poet,” then “The Thinker.”"],[252,279.15,5.21,"First cast on its own in 1888, “The Thinker” became a sensation."],[252,284.7,1.25,"Out of context,"],[252,285.95,4.17,"the figure came to be seen less as Dante wrestling with sin and damnation,"],[252,290.45,1.79,"and more of an everyman;"],[252,292.58,5.59,"a universal symbol of the human mind’s ability to reflect, doubt, and create;"],[252,298.59,4.17,"or even France itself, striving to balance its values."],[252,303.3,4.42,"In 1904, a life-sized “Thinker” was installed in public—"],[252,307.97,4.42,"not overlooking Hell, but crowning a cultural monument."],[252,312.81,3.34,"And it soon became one of the world’s most famous sculptures."],[252,316.56,4.25,"But much as “The Thinker” remains eternally consumed by contemplation,"],[252,321.15,3.5,"Rodin’s “Gates of Hell” remain unfinished."],[252,325.07,2.58,"Despite 37 years of work,"],[252,327.7,6.21,"the first bronze cast of “The Gates” was completed nearly a decade after his death."],[253,0.0,3.09,"Prime Minister of India Narendra modi is cracking down"],[253,3.09,3.71,"on the recent Gen Z cockroach party protests."],[253,6.8,6.55,"We are witnessing a massive crackdown on student activists across the country."],[253,13.35,2.0,"Police and states governed by Modi's"],[253,15.35,3.63,"BJP have reportedly arrested over 100 students in the days"],[253,18.98,1.96,"since the protest was officially called off."],[253,20.94,4.3,"An online movement with one of the most unusual names in politics,"],[253,25.23,3.13,"the Cockroach Junta party took to the streets of New Delhi"],[253,28.36,3.13,"in protest, and helped force India's education"],[253,31.49,4.0,"minister, a man who is one of Modi's top advisors, to resign."],[253,35.74,3.46,"It was a big win for the social media fueled youth movement,"],[253,39.66,1.71,"but it may come at a cost."],[253,41.37,3.25,"Prime Minister Modi was one of the first Indian politicians"],[253,44.63,3.09,"to make social media central to his political strategy."],[253,47.8,2.29,"Modi was so politically savvy."],[253,50.09,5.25,"He understood the intrinsic power of these tools well before everybody else."],[253,55.35,1.84,"Now, in the wake of the Cockroach"],[253,57.18,3.88,"Party protests, India's government is censoring social media,"],[253,61.1,4.13,"removing posts related to the Cockroach Janta Party and their success."],[253,65.23,4.84,"Social media has been the only avenue that that people have had to voice"],[253,70.07,5.42,"their discontent, disagreement and criticism of the government."],[253,75.49,4.09,"The same internet that fueled Prime Minister Modi's rise to power"],[253,79.58,4.38,"is increasingly being used to control a movement challenging his government."],[253,85.17,1.21,"In 2014,"],[253,86.38,3.17,"Narendra modi was elected to be the Prime Minister of India,"],[253,89.63,4.0,"having campaigned on a platform of economic growth and anti-corruption."],[253,93.8,4.17,"He was able to get this message out using what was then a cutting edge tool:"],[253,98.14,0.63,"the internet."],[253,98.77,6.55,"In 2014, it was doing simple things like creating mass registries of people,"],[253,105.31,3.8,"cell phone numbers, and communicating with them via SMS."],[253,109.11,2.38,"These are things that are kind of commonplace in America,"],[253,111.49,1.04,"but in 2014, they were"],[253,112.53,1.02,"they were quite new."],[253,113.55,3.06,"And then in the 2019 election, he doubled down on this strategy."],[253,116.7,5.59,"He said that the election of 2019 is going to be fought on mobile phone."],[253,122.29,4.17,"Today, Prime Minister Modi has the largest social media following of any world"],[253,126.46,1.29,"leader currently in office."],[253,127.75,3.29,"So of course, nowadays, if you if you look at any political party"],[253,131.05,3.0,"in India, they will be using a variety of social media tools,"],[253,134.09,3.34,"but they've all been kind of playing catch up to where Modi was."],[253,137.43,3.88,"Now in 2026, there's growing disillusionment with the promises"],[253,141.31,1.61,"that Prime Minister Modi made."],[253,142.92,2.6,"And it all came to a head after a leaked exam."],[253,145.52,5.74,"In India, every person who aspires to go to college or to finish high school even"],[253,151.25,2.61,"needs to pass a centrally administered exam."],[253,153.86,4.09,"What happened in this particular case was the exam paper was leaked in advance,"],[253,157.95,3.34,"which forced the authorities to call off the exam, to postpone"],[253,161.29,1.88,"it, reschedule it for a different time."],[253,163.16,4.21,"India has seen dozens of national and state exams canceled, annulled,"],[253,167.38,3.59,"or reconducted because of leaks or other breakdowns in the testing system."],[253,171.04,3.92,"This latest example led to widespread outrage and even suicides."],[253,175.34,3.25,"This is happening in a time where nearly 40% of graduates"],[253,178.59,3.67,"between 15 and 25 years old in India are unemployed,"],[253,182.31,3.92,"making these exams crucial to any chance of securing a stable future."],[253,186.23,2.06,"I think a lot of young Indians have been kind of"],[253,188.29,3.69,"led to believe this gospel of meritocracy,"],[253,191.98,4.38,"that if they work hard and they study hard, and they do well on these exams,"],[253,196.36,3.71,"they'll get a place at a college. And that will allow them"],[253,200.07,4.42,"to get a well-paying job, and become sort of part of this kind of,"],[253,204.54,3.09,"you know, aspirational middle class or upper middle class."],[253,207.62,2.79,"The fact that these exams don't work the way they should,"],[253,210.42,2.79,"the fact that they're not enough jobs being created,"],[253,213.21,2.41,"the fact that even if you're lucky enough to get a job,"],[253,215.79,2.13,"it may not pay what you think you're worth."],[253,217.93,4.0,"There have been this kind of gathering sense of frustration amongst"],[253,221.97,1.52,"kind of India's Gen Z."],[253,223.61,2.07,"There are youngsters like cockroaches."],[253,225.77,2.42,"They don't get any employment."],[253,228.19,1.54,"They don't have any place in profession."],[253,229.73,4.09,"This comment from India's Chief Justice in May of 2026"],[253,233.82,3.09,"landed poorly with India's already frustrated youth."],[253,236.9,3.84,"It became the catalyst that launched the satirical social media account"],[253,240.74,3.54,"for the Cockroach Janta Party or the Cockroach People's Party."],[253,244.53,0.75,"And it went viral."],[253,245.29,1.0,"The Instagram account"],[253,246.29,3.8,"for the Cockroach Janta Party gained over 26 million followers."],[253,250.33,4.23,"That's a quarter of the number of Instagram followers Prime Minister Modi has"],[253,254.56,3.57,"Tech and social media and politics has been a mainstay of Indian"],[253,258.13,3.04,"campaigning elections for at least a dozen years."],[253,261.18,4.0,"But we haven't really seen this kind of very,"],[253,265.26,5.25,"you know, young, slick, AI infected kind of viral memes"],[253,270.6,3.8,"play such a central role in kind of mass politics."],[253,274.4,2.13,"So this was, I think, an inflection point."],[253,276.53,3.46,"And, you know, it was it was a kind of poignant one, because here you have"],[253,279.99,3.96,"a ruling party, a government, which has had such a large"],[253,283.95,3.75,"technological advantage over the opposition, over its opponents,"],[253,287.75,4.59,"being kind of outsmarted, outplayed by a leaderless,"],[253,292.5,3.63,"brand new group of kind of dilettantes who are protesting"],[253,296.13,3.88,"but who are very conversant in the current technology."],[253,300.09,3.25,"This kind of played out like a David versus Goliath story."],[253,303.43,3.42,"Despite attempts by Modi's government to shut down internet and"],[253,306.85,4.0,"cell phone service to curb the protests, the Cockroach Janta Party"],[253,310.89,3.67,"succeeded in forcing one of Modi's closest ministers to resign."],[253,314.65,3.34,"But this rare concession appears to have prompted a broader crackdown."],[253,317.98,4.5,"It's the latest move from a government that has become increasingly restrictive"],[253,322.49,0.83,"of free speech."],[253,323.32,3.29,"Over the last five years, the Indian government"],[253,326.62,3.88,"has built what I call the infrastructure for censorship."],[253,330.5,4.8,"And they've created a criminal liability for social media platforms"],[253,335.33,3.42,"in that an employee of, let's say, a company like Facebook"],[253,338.8,3.29,"or Twitter can be sent to jail if they don't comply with orders"],[253,342.09,1.28,"from the Indian government."],[253,343.37,4.19,"Following the protests, Delhi Police filed charges over social media posts"],[253,347.56,4.21,"they deemed derogatory, malicious and defamatory, demanding the platforms"],[253,351.77,3.77,"take them down, as well as providing the information of the users."],[253,355.54,5.16,"And police used AI powered facial recognition software to identify protesters."],[253,360.69,1.83,"Some of the protesters are still in jail."],[253,362.53,1.38,"Freedom of speech, is suffering."],[253,363.9,1.08,"Hi friends."],[253,364.99,1.88,"Prime Minister Modi has made it clear"],[253,366.87,3.42,"that he is not accepting this movement's criticisms of his government."],[253,370.54,3.59,"In a recent Instagram video, he told the CJP that abuse solves"],[253,374.12,3.13,"nothing and that he forgives the misguided protesters."],[253,377.25,5.76,"I think the legacy of the Cockroach Janta Party protests is going to be that"],[253,383.01,4.91,"it's going lead to the the strengthening of censorship laws in India."],[253,388.01,2.71,"And this isn't exactly new in Indian politics."],[253,390.72,2.99,"Every government tries to censor social media in India."],[253,393.71,4.02,"Every government tries to suppress criticism and"],[253,397.77,1.23,"and the media in India."],[253,399.0,3.7,"The question is, how much further will Modi's government go"],[253,402.69,2.57,"to censor its people and restrict free speech?"],[253,405.52,2.51,"And they're going to try and evolve mechanisms"],[253,408.04,2.6,"to try and ensure that this never happens again."],[253,410.64,2.97,"The real success of the Cockroach Junta party may be"],[253,413.61,2.97,"how it pushed new issues to the forefront of Indian politics."],[253,416.58,1.87,"I think one of the puzzles for people"],[253,418.45,3.6,"who've studied Indian politics for a long time is why haven't basic human rights,"],[253,422.09,3.17,"like health and education kind of been on the political agenda?"],[253,425.26,3.29,"And, you know, I think one legacy is that these protests"],[253,428.55,3.46,"have really made those issues mass issues."],[253,432.01,2.75,"I feel like the older generations are kind of accepted."],[253,434.77,3.63,"The fact that this is just how the country works and learn to live with it."],[253,438.4,4.42,"But I like the fact that kids are not willing to stick with that."],[253,443.15,4.7,"They could start to drive party platforms, what politicians talk about,"],[253,447.85,0.84,"what voters demand."],[253,448.7,0.42,"And I think,"],[253,449.12,3.88,"you know, if that were to happen, that would be just a massive sea change."],[253,458.79,1.25,"Thanks so much for watching."],[253,460.04,1.25,"If you're not already a member."],[253,461.29,3.84,"Head over to Patreon.com/Vox to get access to more stories,"],[253,465.26,3.63,"stay updated on our work, and help support ambitious reporting."],[253,468.88,2.63,"It's so important to support independent journalism."],[253,471.51,3.34,"Your membership allows us to make really cool videos, research"],[253,474.85,3.17,"interesting stories, and also dig into the investigative reporting."],[253,478.14,2.25,"And if it doesn't make financial sense for you to support us"],[253,480.4,2.71,"today, you can still follow us on Patreon for free"],[253,483.11,3.63,"and stay up to date on all of the great reporting going on at Vox."],[253,486.74,0.5,"Thanks!"],[254,3.67,3.09,"Meghan Sullivan: It strikes me, as somebody who's a huge space --"],[254,6.79,1.13,"Ariel Ekblaw: Nerd."],[254,7.96,1.58,"MS: Fangirl is what I would say,"],[254,9.55,3.5,"is that having these really concrete and ambitious goals,"],[254,13.05,3.13,"like going to the moon or putting people on the space station,"],[254,16.22,2.92,"sometimes forces us to focus our attention on solving problems"],[254,19.14,2.75,"that then help us solve problems back here on earth."],[254,21.93,2.29,"AE: This is exactly what we want to do in space."],[254,42.5,1.25,"AE: Off we go."],[254,45.08,2.79,"What does the common good actually mean"],[254,47.92,1.88,"and how do we build towards it?"],[254,50.13,3.92,"MS: I think a lot of people assume that the common good means"],[254,54.05,3.34,"finding the kind of least common denominator that we can agree on,"],[254,57.43,1.71,"like just splitting the difference"],[254,59.14,3.04,"or kind of, finding like, going down to the basics."],[254,62.23,2.0,"I think that is a huge mistake."],[254,64.85,4.88,"The philosophers that I study and love, the ancient Greeks, like Aristotle,"],[254,69.77,2.17,"believe that the common good, the good itself,"],[254,71.94,1.92,"is something that you're moving towards."],[254,73.9,3.17,"It's a common goal and nobody is there yet."],[254,77.62,3.04,"So when we talk about prioritizing the common good,"],[254,80.66,3.09,"I think we're not trying to figure out,"],[254,83.75,2.71,"of all the things that we believe and care about now,"],[254,86.5,1.29,"how do we sort of settle --"],[254,87.83,1.96,"AE: And figure out what we already share."],[254,89.84,1.17,"MS: Exactly."],[254,91.05,2.21,"Instead, we need to be having this conversation"],[254,93.26,2.09,"of where do we collectively want to move to,"],[254,95.34,2.96,"which presumably is very different from where we currently are,"],[254,98.34,1.63,"which is probably not that great."],[254,100.31,1.83,"AE: I think this is really compelling."],[254,102.18,2.63,"In our field for space, we think about the space commons"],[254,104.85,1.96,"and try to define what is the public good"],[254,106.85,3.92,"in the context of a really broad geographical expanse,"],[254,110.77,2.29,"like the near-Earth cocoon of space."],[254,113.07,2.0,"And we don't have a real space commons yet."],[254,115.11,1.25,"It does exist as a space,"],[254,116.36,1.96,"but we haven't defined all the norms yet."],[254,118.36,1.17,"And so I love this,"],[254,119.57,2.5,"this is a way that we can think about moving together"],[254,122.12,2.17,"towards the definition of maybe a future goal."],[254,124.29,3.13,"An aspirational version of humanity's commons."],[254,127.46,1.67,"MS: And maybe trying to figure out"],[254,129.17,3.13,"what it means to be an interplanetary species."],[254,132.29,3.29,"We might imagine, like, exercise our imagination"],[254,135.59,3.46,"about how to be a better planetary species in the process."],[254,139.09,1.5,"AE: Love that."],[254,142.89,1.21,"MS: Alright."],[254,145.68,3.09,"What do we owe each other as humans?"],[254,148.81,1.46,"AE: Hmm."],[254,150.31,5.3,"I think we should owe each other the alternative to the Hobbesian view."],[254,155.65,5.0,"The idea that human nature is fundamentally wrought at its base,"],[254,160.66,3.88,"or somehow pegged to our lowest basis instinct."],[254,164.54,3.79,"I think we should owe each other our highest version of ourselves"],[254,168.37,3.0,"or our more honorable version of ourselves."],[254,171.38,1.21,"MS: Oh, I like that."],[254,172.59,1.42,"So you disagree with Hobbes"],[254,174.0,3.42,"that life should be nasty, brutish, and short."],[254,177.47,1.63,"That's pretty, pretty depressing."],[254,179.09,2.84,"AE: It should be elevated, you know, delightful and long."],[254,181.93,2.79,"MS: One of my favorite 20th-century philosophers"],[254,184.76,2.38,"is [a] philosopher named Iris Murdoch."],[254,187.18,2.09,"And she has a good answer to this question."],[254,189.31,4.46,"She says, what we owe another person is to be willing to look at them again."],[254,194.31,2.5,"Which means like, oftentimes we meet people,"],[254,196.86,2.59,"and we form our assumptions and stereotypes about them."],[254,199.49,2.17,"We put them in a little category in our brain,"],[254,201.66,2.71,"and then we interact with them according to that category."],[254,204.41,1.75,"AE: We put them in a box too early."],[254,206.16,1.17,"MS: Oh my gosh."],[254,207.37,1.88,"And she's like, 80 percent of the time,"],[254,209.29,1.79,"your first impression is pretty dumb."],[254,211.08,2.25,"AE: This is something I have learned in my life."],[254,213.38,1.5,"I think for the last 10 years,"],[254,214.88,2.04,"I thought I was great at first impressions"],[254,216.92,2.34,"and then judging people in a positive sense,"],[254,219.3,1.29,"but quickly and decisively."],[254,220.63,1.71,"And I think I have learned recently"],[254,222.34,2.42,"that I really do need to give people a second look."],[254,224.76,1.17,"I love this comment."],[254,225.93,4.0,"MS: And just being able to like, say, alright, I've got a vibe on you."],[254,229.93,2.84,"I'm going to just put this on the shelf and just take it --"],[254,232.81,2.0,"And she's like, really look at the person."],[254,234.86,2.46,"Just make yourself see like, there's a person there."],[254,237.32,3.09,"So I think if we did that, maybe things would start to go better."],[254,242.28,1.42,"AE: That was a great toss."],[254,245.37,3.59,"If you could design a better world for humans from scratch,"],[254,249.0,1.54,"what's the first rule?"],[254,250.58,4.71,"MS: You know, I come at philosophy from a Catholic perspective."],[254,255.34,3.5,"And in the Jewish tradition and the Christian tradition,"],[254,258.88,3.5,"there was a fundamental rule that was laid down at the beginning"],[254,262.38,2.42,"that then we just promptly disregarded,"],[254,264.84,5.84,"which is you will love your neighbor as another self, like, as yourself,"],[254,270.73,1.46,"like, you're going to put love"],[254,272.19,3.09,"at the basis of all the rest of your decision making."],[254,275.27,1.63,"And pretty much as soon"],[254,276.94,3.38,"as that kind of fundamental operating principle was laid down,"],[254,280.36,3.04,"we all started ignoring it and like, revising it."],[254,283.86,4.21,"So I don't know if I would revise the original code,"],[254,288.12,3.46,"but I would maybe enforce it a little bit harder."],[254,291.58,2.09,"I don't know if that's allowed as an answer."],[254,293.67,1.21,"What would you do?"],[254,294.92,2.29,"AE: I think in the context of space environments,"],[254,297.25,2.29,"we're often in isolated, confined environments,"],[254,299.59,2.42,"tiny, constrained little spaces."],[254,302.05,2.67,"So if I was going to start with a new rule for humanity,"],[254,304.72,1.88,"I love the idea of an oldie but goodie,"],[254,306.64,3.25,"the golden rule: treat others how you would like to be treated."],[254,309.89,2.59,"And I think that that actually would play out very well"],[254,312.52,3.38,"in a constrained space where what goes around comes around,"],[254,315.89,1.63,"all happens very quickly,"],[254,317.56,3.71,"with kind of a small, you know, proto group of humans in a space habitat."],[254,321.28,2.88,"It's like almost a tiny little, maybe not a civilization,"],[254,324.19,2.46,"but a tiny little society in its own right."],[254,326.66,3.25,"So yeah, something like recommitting to the golden rule"],[254,329.95,2.17,"and this sense of really treating other people"],[254,332.12,3.38,"in the way in which you would like to be received and seen and treated."],[254,335.54,1.13,"MS: I love it."],[254,338.42,1.46,"You're going to love this one."],[254,339.92,4.63,"What is a hope you have for humans in the next ten years?"],[254,345.43,2.0,"AE: I have an aspiration for humans:"],[254,347.47,3.88,"that more people get to experience the overview effect,"],[254,351.35,2.54,"which really means democratizing access to space"],[254,353.89,2.84,"so that more humans can fly up to low-Earth orbit,"],[254,356.77,2.96,"look back down at the Earth and see the Earth from space."],[254,359.77,3.29,"And even really serious military astronauts,"],[254,363.11,4.34,"very, you know, impeccably well-trained, brave people,"],[254,367.49,3.0,"they start to cry when they see the Earth from space."],[254,370.53,3.29,"They lose control over their own emotions because it is so moving."],[254,373.83,3.25,"And I would love to share that experience with more people."],[254,377.12,1.29,"That's a hope I would have."],[254,378.46,2.96,"MS: I think, on my end, similar hope --"],[254,381.42,1.79,"that we’d have a kind of realization,"],[254,383.21,4.96,"I'm very excited about the AI decade finally taking root."],[254,388.18,4.63,"And I consider myself kind of pragmatic optimist about artificial intelligence."],[254,392.81,1.79,"But my biggest hope"],[254,394.64,3.59,"is that we would use this powerful technology"],[254,398.27,2.29,"that we now have at our disposal"],[254,400.61,4.84,"to help a whole lot more folks realize what it means to be genuinely human,"],[254,405.49,1.92,"that like, kind of humans be the star."],[254,408.07,1.38,"It heightens the difference,"],[254,409.45,2.25,"and also, there's so much about our lives pre-AI"],[254,411.74,1.33,"that [is] dehumanizing,"],[254,413.12,3.04,"so many ways in which we learn, in which we work,"],[254,416.16,1.38,"that have never enabled us"],[254,417.54,3.88,"to really express what's special about being a human being."],[254,421.46,2.42,"I'd love to think that now we have this technology,"],[254,423.88,2.92,"we would choose to develop it in a way where humans,"],[254,426.84,3.09,"and our capabilities and virtues would be the star of the show."],[254,429.93,1.96,"I don't know that that's going to happen."],[254,431.93,1.38,"I'm kind of afraid it's not,"],[254,433.35,1.46,"but maybe if we had the chance"],[254,434.81,2.84,"to see where we stand in the perspective of the universe,"],[254,437.68,1.5,"it would go better."],[254,441.4,3.34,"AE: What is the biggest barrier to human thriving?"],[254,444.73,2.0,"MS: The biggest barrier to human thriving?"],[254,446.78,2.5,"There are some really big barriers,"],[254,449.28,5.05,"but I think probably the biggest one is our inattention."],[254,455.45,4.09,"You know, we're rational animals,"],[254,459.54,5.21,"we have extraordinarily great capabilities in us,"],[254,464.75,3.67,"but the only way that we realize them, unlike most animals,"],[254,468.42,2.92,"which kind of mature and develop their powers no matter what,"],[254,471.34,4.67,"a squirrel becomes a good squirrel with very little effort."],[254,476.06,1.79,"Humans require a great deal of effort"],[254,477.89,2.5,"to become good versions of what we're supposed to be,"],[254,480.39,2.96,"and [that] requires a lot of attention, not just to ourselves,"],[254,483.35,2.54,"but attention on the part of our parents and teachers."],[254,485.9,3.75,"And I think when we fail to flourish,"],[254,489.65,5.34,"it's not typically because we've had some malicious plan"],[254,495.03,1.75,"that's screwed up our lives."],[254,496.78,2.5,"But really, we just never had the opportunity"],[254,499.33,1.63,"to focus on the good plan."],[254,500.95,2.0,"So I would think distraction."],[254,502.96,1.25,"How about you?"],[254,504.21,1.38,"AE: That's a great response."],[254,505.58,2.84,"Like, we are really high-intensity creatures"],[254,508.46,1.96,"in the sense we need a lot of attention."],[254,510.42,2.17,"We need a lot of explicit care and development"],[254,512.59,2.25,"to become maybe the best version of ourselves."],[254,514.84,1.38,"For me, it's interesting."],[254,516.22,2.04,"I think of this sometimes in the context"],[254,518.26,3.09,"of why do we spend so much money on space exploration"],[254,521.35,2.0,"if some of the barriers to human thriving"],[254,523.35,2.13,"are things like poverty,"],[254,525.52,1.54,"war, climate change,"],[254,527.11,2.04,"there's a lot of things at a societal scale"],[254,529.15,3.38,"that might be keeping us from thriving in the way that we really could."],[254,532.53,1.71,"And then there arises the question,"],[254,534.28,2.71,"why would we spend money on space at all in that context."],[254,536.99,3.04,"And I think one of the things we could do to help human thriving"],[254,540.08,2.71,"is we can use space for the benefit of life on Earth."],[254,542.79,3.92,"So before we go [and] be a sci-fi spacefaring species,"],[254,546.75,2.92,"maybe we earn our right to go do that someday"],[254,549.67,2.09,"by showing that we can take really good care"],[254,551.76,1.58,"of our own planet first."],[254,553.34,3.46,"So try to do things in space that would help with human thriving."],[254,556.8,2.13,"And my latest favorite one is biology."],[254,558.97,3.29,"It turns out biology is really different when you're floating in orbit"],[254,562.27,1.54,"than when you're on the ground."],[254,563.81,2.88,"And so we might be able to do things like cancer cures,"],[254,566.73,1.88,"Alzheimer's, organoids, testing,"],[254,568.65,3.34,"all kinds of interesting things that would maybe help humans thrive"],[254,571.98,2.63,"in a more concrete, immediate way on Earth,"],[254,574.65,2.46,"but leveraging space technology."],[254,577.16,2.5,"MS: It strikes me as somebody who's a huge space --"],[254,579.66,1.17,"AE: Nerd?"],[254,580.83,1.79,"MS: Fangirl is what I would say,"],[254,582.62,3.92,"is that having these really concrete and ambitious goals,"],[254,586.58,3.09,"like going to the moon or putting people on the space station"],[254,589.71,1.96,"sometimes forces us to focus our attention"],[254,591.67,3.5,"on solving problems that then help us solve problems back here on earth."],[254,595.17,2.29,"And having a big vision like that,"],[254,597.47,2.54,"sometimes [is] exactly the kick in the pants you need"],[254,600.01,3.13,"to feel motivated to take on something that in your ordinary life"],[254,603.14,2.21,"might not feel like it deserves your attention."],[254,605.35,2.25,"AE: This is exactly what we want to do in space."],[254,608.52,1.29,"Producer: Nice."],[254,610.98,4.63,"MS: How do we build systems that influence the good in each other?"],[254,615.61,2.34,"AE: Systems that influence the good in each other"],[254,617.99,3.25,"also have to assume good intentions at the base."],[254,621.28,3.29,"And so being able to think about what is the architecture of policy"],[254,624.58,2.04,"that comes out of the US government,"],[254,626.66,2.59,"or ways that we could unify people to get them there."],[254,629.29,2.0,"And I've recently become really passionate"],[254,631.29,2.25,"about something completely outside of my domain,"],[254,633.59,1.71,"so maybe I'm a little over my skis,"],[254,635.34,4.38,"which is an idea of a national service, not necessarily a military draft."],[254,639.72,3.34,"My parents are both ex-military -- I’m very supportive of the military,"],[254,643.05,1.58,"but something that would be like"],[254,644.64,3.42,"every young person does something for a year when they're 18."],[254,648.06,3.09,"And it really unites red and blue-state kids"],[254,651.15,1.58,"and urban and rural kids."],[254,652.73,1.75,"And it could be Teach for America --"],[254,654.48,2.17,"it could be something that's service-oriented."],[254,656.65,1.88,"Also, maybe, military service-oriented,"],[254,658.53,1.54,"but something like that I think."],[254,660.07,3.17,"Once you get to know the people of your community and your village"],[254,663.24,2.79,"and your society and your civilization a little bit better,"],[254,666.04,2.79,"I think you can do a better job of finding the good in them"],[254,668.87,2.5,"and then maybe building good systems around them."],[254,671.42,1.17,"MS: I love that."],[254,673.42,3.38,"I think on my end, there are a few institutions"],[254,676.84,1.63,"that really roll up their sleeves"],[254,678.47,3.46,"and do this work of forming people's characters."],[254,681.93,1.75,"One is education,"],[254,683.72,3.75,"another would be, like, church and religious organizations."],[254,687.52,1.38,"And a third is the media."],[254,688.93,3.13,"And everybody likes to like, talk about how awful, you know,"],[254,692.06,2.08,"whatever, fill in the blank social media is,"],[254,694.15,3.67,"but, you know, genuinely print media,"],[254,697.86,3.71,"the news and various sources still very much form people’s outlook"],[254,701.57,1.17,"and what they hope for"],[254,702.74,2.46,"and what they view as their sort of purpose in life."],[254,705.24,5.92,"And I think we have sorely neglected those institutions,"],[254,711.16,2.88,"and a lot of people are really on the brink of nihilism"],[254,714.09,1.21,"... right now"],[254,715.29,3.29,"about whether we could still imagine amazing schools,"],[254,718.63,3.04,"whether we could still imagine on the weekends,"],[254,721.68,1.79,"you going and being with other humans"],[254,723.51,2.5,"and doing some kind of collective activity."],[254,726.05,3.13,"And I think if we really want to design systems"],[254,729.18,1.88,"that will bring out the good in people,"],[254,731.06,2.84,"we've got to recognize that we are social animals"],[254,733.94,3.54,"and that these human-level institutions need to remain strong"],[254,737.52,2.54,"and need our investment and our attending."],[254,740.07,2.34,"So I would love to see just, you know,"],[254,742.4,2.42,"a renaissance of hope in what we're able to build"],[254,744.87,1.5,"in those kinds of institutions."],[254,746.41,3.21,"AE: I love this and just reinvesting in bringing humans together"],[254,749.66,1.75,"through these different institutions"],[254,751.41,2.54,"in the ways that we used to come together and convene"],[254,753.96,2.21,"and gather more, religious and non-religious."],[254,756.21,2.34,"I would love to do that with you, let's start it."],[254,758.59,1.33,"MS: Let's do it."],[254,760.72,1.13,"AE: Thank you so much."],[254,761.88,3.13,"It was a delight talking to you and learning more about your work."],[254,765.01,1.5,"MS: I had so much fun."],[255,0.0,1.76,"Maybe you’ve noticed:"],[255,1.76,2.48,"The echo chambers are loud."],[255,4.24,2.0,"The divisions, wide."],[255,6.24,3.28,"Our Fox News dads and our MSNBC moms"],[255,9.52,2.48,"may as well be living in different dimensions."],[255,12.0,2.96,"And our majestic eagle friend is looking…"],[255,14.96,2.48,"way less majestic than when we last saw him."],[255,17.44,1.28,"You okay there, Baldy?"],[255,18.72,3.28,"Stop picking at your feathers, you’re only gonna make it worse."],[255,22.0,4.08,"So, how did we get here, screaming at each other across"],[255,26.08,2.56,"an ever-growing cavernous divide?"],[255,28.64,3.36,"And what does that mean for the fate of this country?"],[255,32.0,2.88,"Hi! I'm Kousha Navidar, this is Crash Course,"],[255,34.88,0.96,"and we’re answering"],[255,35.84,2.17,"Big Questions on U.S. Government."],[255,42.56,1.84,"While it’s true that the media"],[255,44.4,2.0,"we consume contributes greatly"],[255,46.4,2.96,"to our super-polarized political culture,"],[255,49.36,2.0,"To understand the full scope"],[255,51.36,1.12,"of what’s going on,"],[255,52.48,2.48,"we have to take an even wider view,"],[255,54.96,1.6,"because this thing we’ve got going on"],[255,56.56,1.84,"goes back a long time."],[255,58.4,2.8,"For decades, there’s been a widening divide between"],[255,61.2,4.16,"the two major political parties in terms of political ideology –"],[255,65.36,2.24,"where people fall on the spectrum of beliefs"],[255,67.6,2.08,"about how society should work."],[255,69.68,0.72,"Very simply,"],[255,70.4,1.28,"from left to right,"],[255,71.68,2.0,"liberal to conservative."],[255,73.68,2.56,"And when we say liberal and conservative,"],[255,76.24,3.44,"we’re not actually talking about Democrats and Republicans."],[255,79.68,1.28,"At least, not yet."],[255,80.96,1.44,"Liberal really just means"],[255,82.4,2.8,"falling on the left side of our political spectrum."],[255,85.2,1.52,"These folks tend to support"],[255,86.72,2.08,"more government involvement in people’s lives."],[255,88.8,2.96,"Conservatives, on the right side of the spectrum,"],[255,91.76,3.44,"want the government to conserve the status quo,"],[255,95.2,2.88,"which usually means less intervention both socially"],[255,98.08,1.12,"and economically."],[255,99.2,2.32,"So, for example, a liberal person"],[255,101.52,3.04,"would probably support government-funded healthcare,"],[255,104.56,1.6,"while a conservative person"],[255,106.16,3.04,"might think that’s something better left to private companies."],[255,109.2,4.0,"In a centrist political culture, most voters hold moderate views,"],[255,113.2,2.88,"falling somewhere between liberal and conservative."],[255,116.08,3.44,"Sure, you’ve got your fair share of leftists and right-wingers,"],[255,119.52,3.84,"but the largest cluster of voters are hanging out in the middle."],[255,123.36,2.8,"And in an election, they could swing either way."],[255,126.16,4.16,"But in a culture of political polarization, like ours,"],[255,130.32,1.6,"voters are camped out"],[255,131.92,2.32,"farther away from each other in their views."],[255,134.24,2.96,"These days, we tend to think of the political spectrum"],[255,137.2,2.0,"as falling along party lines,"],[255,139.2,2.4,"with “liberal” meaning the same thing as “Democrat”"],[255,141.6,2.44,"and “conservative” meaning the same thing as “Republican”"],[255,144.04,4.41,"– with a vast, seemingly uncrossable canyon between them."],[255,148.48,3.36,"But the left-right divide between the parties wasn’t"],[255,151.84,1.44,"always this clear-cut."],[255,153.28,0.96,"So what happened?"],[255,154.24,1.6,"To help explain this,"],[255,155.84,3.68,"let’s head over to my colleague, historian Buff."],[255,159.52,3.24,"Just Buff. That’s his whole name."],[255,162.76,3.04,"[BUFF] Hi there, Kousha."],[255,165.8,2.2,"[KOUSHA] You are not what I was expecting."],[255,168.0,1.16,"[BUFF] What do you mean?"],[255,169.16,1.64,"[KOUSHA] Don’t worry about it, Buff."],[255,170.8,0.92,"Go ahead, friend."],[255,171.72,1.72,"[BUFF] You got it, Kousha."],[255,173.44,1.6,"Well, back in the day,"],[255,175.04,2.08,"political parties didn’t really mean"],[255,177.12,1.84,"the same thing they do now."],[255,178.96,2.56,"There were liberals and conservatives sprinkled"],[255,181.52,1.6,"throughout both parties."],[255,183.12,1.52,"I’ll give you an example."],[255,184.64,1.68,"In the wake of the Civil War,"],[255,186.32,2.48,"in the period known as Reconstruction,"],[255,188.8,2.96,"who do you think led the charge to expand voting"],[255,191.76,2.0,"and civil rights for Black Americans?"],[255,193.76,2.88,"It was liberal Republicans from New England."],[255,196.64,2.48,"And who do you think instituted Jim Crow laws"],[255,199.12,2.24,"that made it harder for Black Americans"],[255,201.36,1.76,"to exercise those rights?"],[255,203.12,3.12,"It was conservative Southern Democrats."],[255,206.24,2.8,"In fact, the voting records of Southern Democrats"],[255,209.04,4.32,"in Congress skewed significantly more conservative than their"],[255,213.36,3.92,"Northern Republican brethren until well into the 20th century!"],[255,217.28,2.32,"And here’s an interesting way that shows up:"],[255,219.6,3.68,"In 1964, President Lyndon B. Johnson,"],[255,223.28,1.36,"himself a Democrat,"],[255,224.64,3.12,"signed the Civil Rights Act into law."],[255,227.76,2.16,"The act made it illegal to discriminate"],[255,229.92,2.8,"and segregate by race in public spaces,"],[255,232.72,4.08,"and it generally enjoyed pretty bipartisan support in Congress."],[255,236.8,2.72,"But the folks who didn’t support it?"],[255,239.52,1.84,"Most weren’t Republicans–"],[255,241.36,2.72,"they were Dems from Johnson’s own party."],[255,244.08,3.6,"When white, conservative Southern Democrats saw"],[255,247.68,4.88,"Democratic presidents like LBJ and his predecessor, JFK,"],[255,252.56,4.16,"embracing civil rights legislation, it sent a message:"],[255,256.72,1.76,"Democrats became the party"],[255,258.48,3.04,"more associated with the civil rights movement."],[255,261.52,2.08,"So those conservative Democrats"],[255,263.6,3.36,"steadily left the party for the Republican party"],[255,266.96,2.48,"(or died out– Congress was pretty old,"],[255,269.44,1.68,"even back in the 60s)."],[255,271.12,3.44,"Meanwhile, those liberal Republicans decided"],[255,274.56,3.76,"to fully align themselves with the party of civil rights,"],[255,278.32,3.84,"and made the swap over to the Dems."],[255,282.16,3.76,"Back to you, Kousha. [bumps mic – whispers “sorry”]"],[255,285.92,1.36,"[KOUSHA] Thanks, Buff."],[255,287.28,3.6,"That is just one example of how party alignment shifted"],[255,290.88,1.6,"during the mid 20th century,"],[255,292.48,2.24,"giving us the system we know today,"],[255,294.72,2.56,"with Democrats being pretty universally liberal,"],[255,297.28,1.2,"or left-leaning,"],[255,298.48,2.96,"and Republicans being pretty universally conservative,"],[255,301.44,1.04,"or right-leaning–"],[255,302.48,4.72,"with a, you guessed it, ever-widening gap between them."],[255,307.2,3.6,"These days, when Congress does manage to get legislation passed,"],[255,310.8,2.56,"it’s often not with the same bipartisan support"],[255,313.36,1.52,"of the days of yore;"],[255,314.88,3.12,"like, 2025’s One Big Beautiful Bill Act"],[255,318.0,2.0,"passed through the House with support from"],[255,320.0,3.92,"218 out of 220 total House Republicans–"],[255,323.92,2.8,"and, count it!-- zero Democrats."],[255,326.72,4.48,"The 1960s set us off on a journey where “conservative” and “liberal”"],[255,331.2,2.08,"have become more and more synonymous with"],[255,333.28,2.0,"“Republican” and “Democrat.”"],[255,335.28,2.08,"And things have started to turn into kind of a"],[255,337.36,3.52,"self-perpetuating spiral of polarized intensity."],[255,340.88,3.84,"As the parties become more associated with specific values,"],[255,344.72,3.44,"like liberal support for LGBTQ+ rights"],[255,348.16,2.56,"or conservative support for the second amendment,"],[255,350.72,2.16,"they attract people with those same values,"],[255,352.88,4.08,"which then enforce those “conservative” or “liberal” associations."],[255,356.96,4.0,"All of this makes it a lot easier for people to fit neatly"],[255,360.96,2.72,"into slots along the left-right continuum,"],[255,363.68,3.12,"a process known as ideological sorting."],[255,366.8,2.16,"Essentially, it’s become a lot easier"],[255,368.96,1.84,"to guess which party somebody supports"],[255,370.8,2.16,"just by their stance on individual things"],[255,372.96,3.2,"like abortion, taxes, gun control,"],[255,376.16,2.08,"foreign policy, the environment,"],[255,378.24,2.88,"or, importantly, their go-to news network–"],[255,381.12,3.41,"and a lot harder for voters to hang out in the center."],[255,384.56,3.12,"But it’s not just that Democrats and Republicans have grown apart"],[255,387.68,2.4,"in terms of how they think about issues."],[255,390.08,3.36,"They’ve also grown apart in terms of who they are as people."],[255,393.44,3.44,"Until recently, you couldn’t reliably predict someone’s"],[255,396.88,2.24,"political party based on their gender,"],[255,399.12,2.16,"or race, or anything like that."],[255,401.28,2.32,"But thanks to what’s known as social sorting,"],[255,403.6,1.92,"for the past two decades or so,"],[255,405.52,1.44,"certain identities have become"],[255,406.96,3.04,"increasingly tied to one party over another."],[255,410.0,1.6,"Take gender, for example."],[255,411.6,2.32,"Across the entire U.S. population,"],[255,413.92,3.04,"men today are more likely to identify as Republicans,"],[255,416.96,3.04,"and women are more likely to identify as Democrats."],[255,420.0,2.56,"To be clear, that doesn’t mean there aren’t men or women"],[255,422.56,2.56,"who buck the trend — there definitely are –"],[255,425.12,3.92,"it’s just that gender is becoming a stronger predictor."],[255,429.04,1.36,"And so is race."],[255,430.4,1.44,"White voters are more likely"],[255,431.84,2.0,"than any other racial or ethnic group"],[255,433.84,1.84,"to identify as Republicans,"],[255,435.68,1.76,"while Black voters are more likely"],[255,437.44,3.04,"than any other group to identify as Democrats."],[255,440.48,3.76,"So even though women in general more often vote Democrat,"],[255,444.24,3.6,"if we look at exit poll data on votes cast by white women"],[255,447.84,3.04,"in the 2024 election, the Republican candidate"],[255,450.88,3.28,"Donald Trump comes out 4 percentage points higher"],[255,454.16,2.0,"than his opponent, Kamala Harris."],[255,456.16,2.08,"On top of all these other factors,"],[255,458.24,2.64,"there is a growing geographic divide, too."],[255,460.88,3.92,"People in rural areas increasingly identify as Republicans,"],[255,464.8,1.68,"while people in urban areas"],[255,466.48,2.08,"identify as Democrats."],[255,468.56,2.96,"So, thanks to the combined effects of ideological sorting"],[255,471.52,2.56,"and social sorting, our politics aren’t just"],[255,474.08,3.41,"something we believe– they’re becoming who we are."],[255,477.52,2.48,"So, it didn’t take just one ingredient"],[255,480.0,1.92,"to cook up our current situation–"],[255,481.92,2.96,"it was decades of shifting party ideology,"],[255,484.88,1.52,"calcifying identities,"],[255,486.4,2.88,"and increasing partisanship from both sides."],[255,489.28,1.76,"And as the gap between us widens,"],[255,491.04,3.28,"it’s harder and harder to find common ground."],[255,494.32,2.4,"What do we have left —gardening?"],[255,496.72,2.64,"The Bachelor? Bachelor in Paradise?!"],[255,499.36,3.28,"Of course, political polarization has way bigger consequences"],[255,502.64,3.12,"than, like, “it’s hard to know what to talk about at family dinner.”"],[255,505.76,3.76,"As political affiliation becomes more closely linked to other"],[255,509.52,1.68,"core parts of who people are,"],[255,511.2,2.56,"it stops being just a political identity"],[255,513.76,1.84,"and becomes something new:"],[255,515.6,4.96,"a mega-identity, or one identity and set of values to rule all others."],[255,520.56,3.28,"The term “mega-identity” was coined by political scientist"],[255,523.84,2.48,"Liliana Mason in the 2010s."],[255,526.32,2.96,"It describes a pattern where someone’s partisan identity —"],[255,529.28,3.2,"whether they define themselves as Republican or Democrat –"],[255,532.48,2.24,"affects how they see and interact with people"],[255,534.72,2.4,"that identify the other way."],[255,537.12,0.88,"And as a result,"],[255,538.0,4.64,"we’re more likely to think bad stuff about the “other side”"],[255,542.64,2.16,"—or even view them as a threat."],[255,545.04,2.4,"Research has shown a rise in what’s known"],[255,547.44,2.24,"as affective polarization–"],[255,549.68,2.48,"a kind of polarization that goes way beyond"],[255,552.16,3.04,"whether or not the two sides disagree on policy issues,"],[255,555.2,1.52,"dipping into big emotions"],[255,556.72,3.2,"like distrust, animosity, and contempt."],[255,559.92,2.4,"In other words, the more polarized we get,"],[255,562.32,3.28,"the less we recognize each other as 3-D human beings,"],[255,565.6,3.2,"and the more we cast each other as 2-D bad guys."],[255,568.8,3.44,"And that has direct consequences on election day."],[255,572.24,2.24,"Voters are increasingly motivated less"],[255,574.48,3.04,"by what they like about their own party’s platform,"],[255,577.52,5.04,"and more by negative partisanship, or their hatred for the other party."],[255,582.56,3.12,"This isn’t new, by any means— negative campaign ads"],[255,585.68,3.28,"were swaying your grandpappy's vote before you were born."],[255,588.96,3.28,"But as Republicans and Democrats have gotten more polarized,"],[255,592.24,3.6,"negative partisanship has become increasingly powerful."],[255,595.84,3.44,"More and more, we’re out there voting against someone"],[255,599.28,1.52,"instead of for someone–"],[255,600.8,3.44,"to the point where we might start to ignore candidates’"],[255,604.24,1.92,"actual political platforms."],[255,606.16,1.84,"And I know we’ve been talking about national stuff,"],[255,608.0,3.84,"but the inability to divorce politics and partisanship"],[255,611.84,2.96,"can also affect voting habits at the more local level,"],[255,614.8,1.68,"leading to nationalization."],[255,616.48,5.6,"Basically, as the parties have divided into these two big, at-odds camps,"],[255,622.08,3.44,"voters increasingly cast ballots based on what they know about"],[255,625.52,2.32,"the national party they identify with"],[255,627.84,2.08,"(or identify against),"],[255,629.92,2.64,"not necessarily what’s happening in their own town."],[255,632.56,3.2,"And, unfortunately for us, our country,"],[255,635.76,2.24,"and our little eagle friend Baldy,"],[255,638.0,2.64,"all these effects of increasing partisanship"],[255,640.64,2.24,"feed back into the causes."],[255,642.88,1.84,"It’s a vicious cycle,"],[255,644.72,4.72,"creating a feedback loop that’s really shi- bad."],[255,649.44,3.84,"Because not only is it seeping into how we, the citizens,"],[255,653.28,1.2,"interact with each other,"],[255,654.48,2.08,"but how the people running our government"],[255,656.56,2.37,"work together to actually get stuff done."],[255,658.96,3.76,"Just because something’s hard doesn’t mean it’s not possible."],[255,662.72,2.08,"Our government, and our country as a whole,"],[255,664.8,1.76,"has weathered plenty of challenges"],[255,666.56,1.92,"and come out on the other side"],[255,668.48,2.16,"with a desire to be better."],[255,670.64,1.6,"And today, there are plenty of people,"],[255,672.24,2.0,"both in the government and on the street,"],[255,674.24,3.28,"willing to reach across the aisle to work together for a better future."],[255,677.52,4.48,"Today’s super-polarized politics have been brewing for decades."],[255,682.0,1.68,"But it’s not the end."],[255,683.68,3.68,"And there are plenty of people thinking there must be a better way."],[255,687.36,2.8,"So, next time, that’s exactly what we’re asking:"],[255,690.16,3.68,"“Why does the U.S. only have two major political parties?”"],[255,694.16,3.2,"This episode of Crash Course Big Questions on U.S. Government"],[255,697.36,2.72,"was produced in partnership with USAFacts,"],[255,700.08,2.24,"a non-partisan civic tech project"],[255,702.32,2.96,"that makes government data easier to understand."],[255,705.28,6.32,"For more resources that go along with these videos, go to usafacts.org/crashcourse."],[255,711.6,1.52,"Thanks for watching this episode of"],[255,713.12,2.48,"Crash Course Big Questions on U.S. Government,"],[255,715.6,2.16,"which was filmed in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[255,717.76,2.96,"and was made with the help of all these nice people."],[255,720.72,4.4,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[255,725.12,2.42,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[256,0.0,3.2,"- Oh, my God! - That was absolutely phenomenal."],[256,3.36,1.16,"Just wow!"],[256,65.4,3.36,"That was like being in the middle of a Fast and Furious race."],[256,68.92,3.28,"At one point, one orca was coming up and it must have had a seal in its mouth."],[256,72.44,3.92,"And the other orca came up and did a handbrake turn and was like, gggrrrrr."],[256,76.36,1.44,"They went nose to nose."],[256,77.8,3.16,"This one's swimming forwards, this one's swimming backwards,"],[256,80.96,3.24,"and they're sharing a seal. It was ridiculous."],[256,84.2,1.8,"But it's amazing how tender they are."],[256,86.0,2.88,"One catches the seal and then they all gently"],[256,88.88,2.4,"share it with one another and with the baby."],[256,91.64,1.4,"It's absolutely phenomenal."],[256,94.04,2.0,"Just… Just wow."],[256,104.48,2.04,"We are right now with the pod of orcas"],[256,106.52,3.12,"and they've just taken a picture of one orca that's been tagged."],[256,109.88,2.12,"And it means that it's one of those orcas"],[256,112.04,2.28,"that has been released here in the Russian Far East."],[256,114.32,4.64,"That means that despite all the arguments from the captive industry,"],[256,118.96,4.8,"orcas actually can be released and safely returned into their natural environment"],[256,123.76,3.4,"and live happy life in where they belong to."],[256,207.6,2.48,"- A six-tonne orca slaps its tail"],[256,210.08,2.28,"with awesome destructive power."],[256,217.96,3.88,"Under the water, tail slaps are used to stun the herring."],[256,236.36,3.8,"Some fish come too quickly and escape back to the safety of the shoal."],[256,243.08,2.44,"But most are picked off by the orcas,"],[256,245.52,1.44,"one by one."],[256,258.44,2.84,"Soon, the water glitters with fish scales,"],[256,261.28,3.68,"as the orcas methodically consume the ball of fish."],[256,273.28,2.52,"By breaking up the herring into a smaller shoal,"],[256,275.8,2.2,"the orcas have done the humpbacks a favour,"],[256,278.64,3.68,"turning the usual relationship between the animals on its head."],[256,284.88,1.08,"Everywhere else on Earth,"],[256,285.96,2.44,"orcas are the humpbacks' biggest enemy."],[256,289.04,2.0,"But here,"],[256,291.04,3.08,"the humpbacks cash in on the colossal fish supper."],[256,306.56,2.88,"And the humpbacks aren't the only ones to gatecrash the orcas' party."],[256,315.12,4.72,"Massive fin whales can swallow 3,000 herring in a single gulp."],[256,336.24,3.68,"Deadly enemies feed side by side,"],[256,339.92,2.64,"a unique scene only possible here"],[256,342.6,2.12,"because of the warmth of the Gulf Stream."],[256,348.76,3.0,"- The smaller females travel as a group,"],[256,351.76,3.48,"keeping a watchful eye in all directions."],[256,358.92,3.76,"They know that killers lurk in the depths."],[256,365.32,3.68,"The surface is a dangerous place to linger,"],[256,369.0,2.56,"especially if you're alone."],[256,397.6,1.24,"Orca."],[256,399.6,1.68,"Killer whales."],[256,403.64,2.12,"The sea lion is wounded,"],[256,405.76,2.32,"but the killers keep their distance."],[256,411.32,4.48,"His powerful jaws are still a threat to the hungry orca."],[256,419.44,3.16,"He struggles towards the safety of land…"],[256,432.12,3.32,"..but his injuries are slowing him down."],[256,460.48,3.52,"Blow by blow, they wear him down."],[256,477.28,3.16,"It's a carefully coordinated attack."],[256,486.12,2.56,"One killer distracts his attention,"],[256,488.68,3.36,"while another hits his soft underside."],[256,514.28,3.04,"The richest seas on Earth can be treacherous."],[256,518.24,4.72,"Here's another good example of whales outsmarting people, in the Mediterranean."],[256,523.6,3.52,"On the receiving end is another prized fishing industry."],[256,529.84,4.44,"Not herring this time, but giant bluefin tuna."],[256,541.84,5.2,"A fish as big as this will net the crew at least $2,000."],[256,549.28,2.04,"The boats are following a long tradition."],[256,551.84,3.32,"Every summer, after spawning, bluefin tuna"],[256,555.16,3.16,"migrate from the Mediterranean to the Atlantic Ocean."],[256,561.84,2.48,"Each year, the fishermen are waiting."],[256,564.32,2.96,"And so are the killer whales."],[256,582.28,4.0,"Concrete blocks take the bait down to 200 metres,"],[256,586.28,4.12,"because tuna swim very deep, as well as fast."],[256,601.44,1.56,"With the line paid out,"],[256,603.0,3.12,"the crew can only wait and hope"],[256,606.12,2.2,"and watch the whales."],[256,625.84,2.2,"A fish has been hooked."],[256,630.84,1.68,"The sound of the winch"],[256,632.52,2.6,"is a signal to the waiting whales."],[256,635.12,4.28,"The fishermen will have to work as fast as possible to bring in the line."],[256,645.36,3.24,"The whales simply bide their time."],[256,651.44,2.16,"By the time the fish nears the boat,"],[256,653.6,2.12,"all the fight has gone out of it."],[256,658.64,3.76,"This is as easy a meal as a killer whale could hope for."],[256,680.84,2.76,"The whales can strip the tuna in minutes."],[256,684.56,4.32,"All that remains for the fishermen are leftovers."],[256,716.12,4.56,"This piracy on the high seas doesn't do much for the whales' popularity,"],[256,720.68,3.8,"but this is just too good an opportunity for them to miss."],[256,739.04,4.76,"These killer whales began stealing fish in this way around 30 years ago."],[256,743.8,4.24,"Now, the culture has spread among all the members of two local pods."],[256,748.48,2.28,"The fishing boats don't stand a chance."],[256,751.16,4.12,"For sea lions to venture into deep water here is very dangerous indeed."],[256,755.68,3.24,"It's much safer to stay in the shallows, if they can."],[256,788.24,1.56,"In one or two places,"],[256,789.8,3.72,"channels enable the whales to get really close to the beach."],[256,793.52,2.48,"Those are the danger spots."],[256,824.32,1.32,"To get off the beach,"],[256,825.64,2.6,"the killer has to thrash its body."],[256,833.24,3.4,"No other whale deliberately beaches itself in this way"],[256,836.64,3.68,"or has perfected this method of getting back to the sea."],[256,856.68,2.8,"As long as the sea lions stay well up the beach,"],[256,859.48,2.08,"you might think they would be safe."],[256,861.56,3.04,"But the hungry whales are very daring."],[256,878.64,3.4,"Now, several of the whales are hunting in a group."],[256,903.04,3.24,"That sea lion was keeping just ahead of one of the whales,"],[256,906.28,3.64,"but was caught by another it probably hadn't seen."],[256,976.16,3.68,"This savage beating may be to separate hide from flesh."],[256,1011.56,2.64,"Not much remains of the porpoise"],[256,1014.2,2.24,"after the whales are through with it."],[256,1020.92,3.84,"Killer whales don't just target prey smaller than themselves."],[256,1026.64,6.08,"Off the coast of California, a grey whale and her baby are in serious danger."],[256,1039.68,5.68,"Once again, computer images give the underwater view of an extraordinary hunt."],[256,1046.8,2.72,"The grey whale calf is just a few weeks old."],[256,1050.16,4.68,"It's following its mother north, towards the summer feeding grounds in Alaska."],[256,1056.28,2.04,"They've reached Monterey Bay,"],[256,1058.32,2.88,"where a deep trench cuts in from the sea."],[256,1065.56,2.48,"As they move beyond the trench wall,"],[256,1068.04,2.48,"the grey whales lose their cover."],[256,1073.04,4.72,"Out in the bay, the killer whales are listening for that telltale sound –"],[256,1077.76,2.52,"the breathing of a great whale."],[256,1086.68,2.2,"Immediately, their behaviour changes."],[256,1089.32,3.44,"The killers close ranks, like a hunting wolf pack."],[256,1104.2,2.76,"The killer whales have caught up with the mother and calf."],[256,1108.12,2.72,"And now the chase is on."],[256,1113.68,3.84,"The grey whales try to outswim their attackers."],[256,1119.8,3.72,"Fast swimming pushes up the grey whales' breathing rate."],[256,1123.52,3.0,"A breathless calf is easier to catch."],[256,1127.92,1.84,"The killers work as a team."],[256,1130.8,3.92,"Their first goal is to separate the baby from its mother."],[256,1138.08,2.84,"The grey whale mother is a formidable adversary."],[256,1142.12,4.96,"She could seriously harm a killer whale with just one slap of her massive tail fluke."],[256,1156.56,3.96,"One of the attackers pushes between mother and calf."],[256,1181.32,1.4,"The strategy is working."],[256,1183.8,2.12,"The grey whales are forced apart."],[256,1190.76,3.64,"With the baby separated, the killer whales change strategy."],[256,1194.72,3.6,"Now, their goal is to drown the calf."],[257,41.1,3.37,"I see trees"],[257,46.41,1.77,"Of green"],[257,51.62,2.07,"Red roses too"],[257,57.35,2.0,"I see them bloom"],[257,61.42,2.97,"For me and you"],[257,67.2,3.04,"And I think to myself"],[257,74.5,4.3,"What a wonderful world"],[257,89.15,2.3,"All the colors of the rainbow"],[257,92.92,2.1,"So pretty in the sky"],[257,97.09,5.17,"Are also on the faces of people going by"],[257,104.27,4.74,"I see friends shaking hands saying,"],[257,109.01,2.34,"Saying, \"How do you do?\""],[257,113.94,2.67,"They're really saying"],[257,118.22,3.04,"I love you"],[257,135.2,4.24,"I hear babies crying"],[257,142.87,2.2,"I watch them grow"],[257,146.51,6.81,"They'll know much more than I'll ever know"],[257,158.76,4.1,"And I think to myself"],[257,166.9,4.0,"What a wonderful"],[257,174.5,1.74,"World"],[257,198.29,1.87,"Thank you very much, everybody."],[257,200.73,1.57,"Isn't that a beautiful song?"],[257,202.7,1.33,"I just love it."],[257,204.03,3.24,"You know, music is a language you already know."],[257,207.27,2.67,"And there's something deeply powerful that happens"],[257,209.97,1.77,"when we come together to make it."],[257,211.78,1.5,"Something connective,"],[257,213.31,3.2,"something transformative, something vibrational."],[257,216.51,1.8,"It's a feeling that moves us."],[257,218.35,2.37,"Now I have a name for this feeling."],[257,221.55,2.54,"I call it the wiggle."],[257,226.79,3.04,"Now what’s the difference between a wiggle and a straight line?"],[257,229.83,2.94,"Well we all know how a straight line feels, right?"],[257,232.8,1.67,"Rigid, unyielding,"],[257,234.5,1.94,"inflexible, linear."],[257,236.47,2.2,"Like a Roman road or the hand of a clock."],[257,238.7,1.9,"It's set, it's certain."],[257,241.74,4.67,"But the wiggle, on the other hand, is soft and flexible and fluid."],[257,246.88,3.5,"The wiggle is smooth and constantly in motion, right?"],[257,251.05,2.94,"So where the straight line is firm and brittle,"],[257,254.02,2.3,"the wiggle is supple and resilient --"],[257,256.32,2.6,"like water -- constantly changing shape,"],[257,258.96,2.27,"but stronger than almost anything."],[257,261.26,4.41,"As the great Alan Watts used to say, there are no straight lines in nature."],[257,266.63,3.64,"Now I've learned many of the principles of wiggledom"],[257,270.27,1.74,"through the process of making music"],[257,272.04,2.33,"because music is inherently wiggly."],[257,274.4,2.87,"Music is made out of a bunch of those things, there."],[257,277.31,2.57,"Essentially, music is just wiggling air."],[257,279.91,1.64,"That's what's going on with music."],[257,281.58,1.27,"And as musicians,"],[257,282.85,3.24,"we are constantly oscillating between different states, right?"],[257,286.12,2.5,"So high to low"],[257,288.65,2.3,"Or loud to quiet."],[257,292.12,3.3,"Thick to thin."],[257,297.29,1.94,"Music is constantly in motion."],[257,299.26,2.77,"And everything in music is learned through play."],[257,302.53,3.94,"Now there are three essential types of wiggle in music"],[257,306.5,1.97,"that I'd like to talk to you about today."],[257,308.47,2.27,"The first is called melody, what we call melody."],[257,310.77,3.3,"Now a melody draws a line from one place to another"],[257,314.08,2.0,"and tells a story as it goes."],[257,316.08,4.0,"So Louis Armstrong walks down the street, looks around and he goes,"],[257,320.12,1.7,"\"I see trees of green.\""],[257,321.85,1.24,"Yeah?"],[257,323.09,2.33,"Not, \"I see trees of green.\""],[257,325.46,1.2,"Not that."],[257,326.66,1.7,"\"I see trees of green.\""],[257,328.79,1.24,"It's a tree."],[257,330.59,2.07,"\"I see skies are blue.\""],[257,333.06,1.6,"It's the sky, right?"],[257,335.2,2.74,"\"Clouds are white,\" there's a little cloud up there."],[257,338.4,1.33,"\"The bright blessed day,"],[257,339.74,1.3,"The dark sacred night.\""],[257,341.04,1.83,"Is that not the sun going down?"],[257,342.91,1.5,"It is."],[257,344.44,3.14,"A good melody arc tells a story and draws a line that moves us."],[257,347.61,1.94,"That's the first kind of wiggle."],[257,349.98,3.44,"Now the second kind of wiggle is what they call rhythm."],[257,353.42,3.64,"Now rhythm is about time, but it's also about the body."],[257,357.09,1.5,"So Louis does not sing this:"],[257,358.62,1.53,"\"I see trees of green."],[257,360.19,2.54,"Red roses too, I see --\""],[257,362.76,1.3,"No, sir."],[257,364.39,2.47,"Instead he goes, \"I see trees green.\""],[257,371.33,1.27,"Right?"],[257,372.6,1.67,"I just saw a bunch of you do this."],[257,374.94,2.87,"Rhythm physically moves the body."],[257,377.81,1.64,"And with that motion and play,"],[257,379.48,2.47,"the melody has something to be in conversation with."],[257,381.98,1.5,"And suddenly we have space,"],[257,383.48,2.97,"and we have form, and we have proportion and dynamics"],[257,386.45,1.97,"and that kind of storytelling."],[257,388.45,1.3,"It's beautiful."],[257,389.79,3.07,"So rhythm moves us and our melody through time."],[257,392.89,1.87,"That's the second kind of wiggle."],[257,394.79,3.7,"Now the third kind of wiggle is my personal favorite,"],[257,398.53,1.97,"and it's what they call harmony."],[257,400.5,2.67,"Now harmony is about relationships."],[257,403.53,1.94,"It's the wiggles between the wiggles."],[257,405.94,2.74,"And by adding these chords, little combinations of notes,"],[257,408.7,1.57,"like clusters of notes,"],[257,410.27,2.8,"we get to take our melody and our rhythm"],[257,413.11,2.8,"and decide how we want these things to feel."],[257,415.95,3.4,"OK, so with this song, we start home."],[257,419.38,1.97,"We begin home, right?"],[257,424.65,1.27,"Begin to move."],[257,427.52,2.0,"Somewhere close by, quite warm."],[257,431.56,1.33,"A bit of darkness there."],[257,435.0,1.27,"Lightening."],[257,437.33,1.14,"Saddening."],[257,439.4,2.04,"And then something extraordinary happens."],[257,441.81,2.07,"Our melody note stays the same."],[257,443.91,3.04,"\"And I think to myself,\" right, one melody note."],[257,447.41,3.1,"But the chord, which could happily stay here,"],[257,450.55,1.2,"becomes this."],[257,451.78,4.54,"\"And I think to myself,\" did you feel the wiggle?"],[257,457.02,1.27,"It's special, isn't it?"],[257,458.32,2.94,"This note just completely changed color."],[257,462.76,1.14,"Didn't it?"],[257,463.93,1.53,"That's the power of harmony."],[257,465.5,2.8,"So with a chord, I can squint,"],[257,468.33,1.94,"I can reassure,"],[257,470.3,2.2,"I can enchant,"],[257,472.54,3.04,"I can purify,"],[257,475.61,1.43,"I can terrify."],[257,479.64,2.04,"So that when I get home, in the end ..."],[257,484.11,3.1,"It’s all the more beautiful because we’ve traveled somewhere new."],[257,487.25,1.9,"Harmony tells an emotional story"],[257,489.19,2.44,"through tension, release, dissonance and consonance"],[257,491.65,2.7,"by combining many different notes together."],[257,494.39,1.47,"That's the third wiggle."],[257,495.89,3.57,"So you may say, “But Jacob, why does it make us wiggle so?"],[257,500.0,2.47,"How do these notes make us feel these things?”"],[257,502.5,3.54,"And I would say, well, because music is about so much more than just notes."],[257,506.04,1.57,"Music is about life."],[257,507.64,2.97,"In music, we learn that being in harmony with the world"],[257,510.64,3.04,"doesn't mean everyone is singing the same note at the same time."],[257,513.68,3.27,"It's about embracing the dissonance and following the heart to home."],[257,517.55,2.27,"We learn that being in time with the world"],[257,519.85,2.0,"doesn't have to mean stepping into line."],[257,521.85,2.9,"It's about motion and knowing when your time is right."],[257,524.79,1.83,"Leaving space for the rest."],[257,526.96,3.47,"We learn that being in tune is not about being perfect."],[257,530.93,1.53,"It's about following a line"],[257,532.5,2.04,"and being open to where it might take you."],[257,534.53,1.77,"Because here's the truth of it."],[257,536.33,2.24,"Life does not work in straight lines."],[257,538.6,3.0,"Life is full of curveballs, full of ups and downs."],[257,541.64,2.77,"And either we yield and we bend and we soften."],[257,544.88,1.97,"Or we stiffen up and we break."],[257,547.41,2.33,"So when the world feels like a straight line,"],[257,549.78,1.3,"be a wiggler."],[257,551.11,1.44,"It's in your nature, trust me."],[257,552.58,1.74,"You already know how to wiggle."],[257,555.45,1.5,"You just need to remember."],[257,574.04,2.0,"I think to myself,"],[257,577.31,4.3,"What a wonderful world"],[257,586.58,1.5,"Yes"],[257,589.49,2.5,"I think to myself"],[257,597.69,4.17,"What a wonderful"],[257,606.07,2.14,"World"],[257,608.24,1.83,"World"],[257,610.07,1.53,"World"],[258,0.08,1.6,"Let’s go to ancient Egypt."],[258,1.68,3.92,"This is the Egyptian pharaoh, Tutankhamun, AKA King Tut."],[258,5.6,2.8,"And this was found inside his tomb."],[258,8.4,5.12,"It’s an ornate breastplate, and the pièce de résistance is that gemstone in the middle."],[258,13.52,2.64,"It depicts the god Ra as a beetle."],[258,16.16,1.68,"But what kind of gem is it?"],[258,17.84,1.12,"And how did it form?"],[258,18.96,5.44,"For decades after the tomb’s discovery in 1922, no geologist could find a solid answer."],[258,24.4,1.68,"But now, we have one."],[258,26.08,4.8,"That gem formed because of a rock from space."],[258,30.88,3.22,"Hi! I’m Sage, and this is Crash Course Geology."],[258,38.65,1.99,"They sparkle. They shine."],[258,40.64,3.2,"And sometimes, they’re found in a literal treasure trove."],[258,43.84,2.0,"I’m talkin’ about gems."],[258,45.84,2.88,"We value these stones for their distinctive qualities—"],[258,48.72,3.2,"they’re hard to break, hard to find, and simply beautiful."],[258,51.92,2.0,"And they can be made of a bunch of different stuff."],[258,53.92,2.0,"Most often, they’re composed of minerals:"],[258,55.92,4.32,"those inorganic materials that make up things like quartz, ruby, and garnet."],[258,60.24,2.48,"Minerals have a specific, crystalline structure;"],[258,62.72,3.68,"they’re made of atoms that are arranged in an orderly, repeating pattern."],[258,66.4,1.76,"So that makes all minerals crystals."],[258,68.16,6.32,"And when a crystal is valued specifically for its durability, rarity, and beauty, we call it a gem."],[258,74.48,3.04,"No, Dwayne, you’re not a gemstone. …"],[258,77.52,2.16,"Yes, I still love you just the way you are."],[258,79.68,2.24,"But gems can also be made of other stuff,"],[258,81.92,2.72,"including organic materials that come from living creatures —"],[258,84.64,3.76,"like a pearl from an oyster, or petrified wood from a tree."],[258,88.4,2.56,"It all depends on what we deem valuable."],[258,90.96,4.8,"Because of those qualities, people have been entranced by gems for a long, long time –"],[258,95.76,3.76,"coveting them, wearing them, attributing powers and curses to them,"],[258,99.52,4.96,"stealing them from palaces, graves, and renowned Parisian museums."],[258,104.48,3.52,"Like, people across the globe have treasured opals for centuries;"],[258,108.0,4.08,"some Indigenous Australians believe they were left behind in the footprints of the Creator,"],[258,112.08,2.32,"who rode to Earth on a rainbow."],[258,114.4,3.36,"So maybe it’s no surprise we’ve always wondered where they came from in the first place."],[258,117.76,2.32,"Or that Taylor Swift named a song after them."],[258,120.08,2.4,"Technically, the song is “Opalite,” and opalite is man-made."],[258,123.12,2.72,"This was a whole thing in the writers’ room, there was Swifty on Swifty violence."],[258,125.84,1.2,"We don’t have time for it right now."],[258,127.04,3.52,"We curious humans have been studying gems for thousands of years,"],[258,130.56,2.4,"trying to figure out why and how they form."],[258,132.96,2.4,"Like, way back in the ‘70s CE,"],[258,135.36,3.6,"Roman philosopher Pliny the Elder was the gemstone expert."],[258,138.96,1.2,"Some of his ideas were right."],[258,140.16,2.8,"But he did say that you could only break a diamond by"],[258,142.96,2.8,"hammering it with a tool that had been soaked in goat’s blood."],[258,145.76,3.52,"So, you know, grain of salt."],[258,149.28,2.4,"But for a long time, that research was mostly guesswork."],[258,151.68,3.12,"It took new technologies for us to find the answers we were searching for."],[258,154.8,2.72,"Let’s dig a little deeper…"],[258,157.52,1.84,"After the discovery of King Tut’s tomb,"],[258,159.36,3.52,"it took another decade for someone to discover a similar gem in nature."],[258,162.88,2.72,"British surveyor Patrick Clayton found a bunch of yellow-green stones"],[258,165.6,4.48,"in a part of the Sahara called the Great Sand Sea, along the border of Egypt and Libya."],[258,170.08,1.52,"He assumed it was a type of quartz."],[258,171.6,3.04,"Later, the gem became known as Libyan desert glass."],[258,174.64,3.6,"Geologists had a range of wild theories about the glass’s origins,"],[258,178.24,2.64,"from lightning strikes to lunar volcanoes."],[258,180.88,3.84,"As technology advanced, scientists have slowly teased out some clues."],[258,184.72,5.52,"For one, researchers estimated that the Libyan desert glass was about 28 million years old."],[258,190.24,3.92,"They uncovered evidence of its high-pressure and high-temperature beginnings —"],[258,194.16,2.4,"so high that some of the minerals within the glass,"],[258,196.56,2.08,"like those dark streaks of cristobalite,"],[258,198.64,3.36,"couldn’t have possibly formed from ordinary happenings on Earth."],[258,202.0,6.64,"They found chemical traces that suggest bits of iron from within the gem weren't from our planet."],[258,208.64,4.4,"Essentially: the gem likely formed from a meteorite slamming into the desert sand,"],[258,213.04,5.36,"causing the sand to melt and re-crystallize, along with those mysterious dark streaks."],[258,218.4,4.0,"Put another way, the very same gem that was buried with an Egyptian king"],[258,222.4,3.25,"likely came from Earth tango-ing with a space rock."],[258,225.65,2.03,"But the mystery hasn’t been completely solved."],[258,227.68,3.2,"Some researchers think that the meteorite didn’t actually hit Earth’s surface,"],[258,230.88,3.28,"but rather broke apart in a low-altitude explosion above it."],[258,234.16,1.36,"Though there's still much to learn,"],[258,235.52,2.96,"it’s safe to say that we know a lot more about gems than we used to."],[258,238.48,2.32,"Gemologists— scientists who study gems—"],[258,240.8,4.32,"use the Mohs hardness scale to measure a gem’s hardness, or resistance to scratching."],[258,245.12,4.16,"They also study gems’ toughness, or resistance to chipping or fracturing;"],[258,249.28,3.52,"and their stability, or resistance to heat, chemicals,"],[258,252.8,2.72,"and light that can cause a gem’s color to fade."],[258,255.52,3.2,"Side note: you might have heard gems described as semi-precious or precious,"],[258,258.72,2.16,"supposedly based on their rarity."],[258,260.88,1.52,"But don't let those words fool you —"],[258,262.4,4.16,"there are so many exceptions to the rule, those labels are basically meaningless."],[258,266.56,3.36,"Like, diamonds are considered precious gems, but depending on the type,"],[258,269.92,3.68,"they could be worth less than supposedly “semi-precious” garnet."],[258,273.6,3.04,"Aside from color, we’ve also ironed out some other qualities of gems,"],[258,276.64,4.32,"like brilliance, luster, transparency, and fire."],[258,280.96,2.8,"Wow, Dwayne! You are dazzling."],[258,283.76,1.2,"Did you get some work done?"],[258,284.96,3.76,"Modern geology has also helped us learn a lot about where gems come from."],[258,288.72,2.96,"They can form in many different ways, not just by meteoric explosion…"],[258,291.68,3.2,"or your pet rock bedazzling itself."],[258,294.88,4.88,"Some gems come from lightning, like fulgurite, which forms when lightning strikes sand."],[258,299.76,3.84,"And lots of gemstones, like emerald, aquamarine, and topaz,"],[258,303.6,4.64,"form as molten rock cools super slowly underground."],[258,308.24,6.72,"Other gems form similarly to how rock candy is made, just with minerals instead of sugar."],[258,314.96,4.72,"Hot water circulates through deep cracks in Earth's surface, dissolving minerals as it flows."],[258,319.68,1.68,"When water eventually cools down,"],[258,321.36,2.8,"crystals like quartz and fluorite can begin to grow."],[258,324.16,3.28,"Don’t eat gems, though, even when they look delicious."],[258,327.44,2.24,"And if crystals form in cavities underground,"],[258,329.68,4.88,"you've got yourself a geode— a hunk of rock lined with crystals on the inside."],[258,334.56,2.48,"When the layers of rock above it eventually wear away,"],[258,337.04,2.32,"that ball of crystals reaches the surface."],[258,339.36,3.2,"Then, you can crack that puppy open to see what sparkles inside!"],[258,342.56,1.04,"No no no don’t worry!"],[258,343.6,1.2,"I wouldn’t crack you open."],[258,344.8,1.12,"Also you’re not a geode."],[258,345.92,0.8,"There’s nothing in there."],[258,346.72,3.44,"However they form, minerals all need the same basic things –"],[258,350.16,4.96,"the right temperature, pressure, time, space, and ingredients."],[258,355.12,2.64,"Lots of those ingredients are pretty common chemical elements,"],[258,357.76,2.0,"like silicon, carbon, and oxygen."],[258,359.76,3.44,"But sometimes, tiny traces of other elements get in there."],[258,363.2,2.72,"Those impurities are often what determine a gemstone’s color,"],[258,365.92,1.84,"which can sometimes change its name."],[258,367.76,3.84,"Ruby and sapphire — two paragons of gem high society —"],[258,371.6,3.2,"are actually just different colors of the same mineral, corundum."],[258,374.8,2.88,"Other weird stuff can happen to baby gemstones too."],[258,377.68,2.64,"Like, the different colored bands in this watermelon tourmaline"],[258,380.32,3.44,"formed when the environment around the growing crystals shifted."],[258,383.76,5.04,"Or sometimes, different fluids or gases get trapped in the crystal, creating inclusions,"],[258,388.8,5.76,"like the needle-thin hairs of the mineral rutile that form the \"star\" in this star ruby."],[258,394.56,4.64,"Inclusions can also offer us information about where the gem formed geographically."],[258,399.2,1.84,"For example, we can tell certain emeralds come"],[258,401.04,2.88,"from Colombia because of parisite inclusions in them."],[258,403.92,2.05,"It’s a type of mineral, not a flesh-eating bug."],[258,405.97,1.79,"The more we humans learn about gems,"],[258,407.76,1.44,"the more fascinated we become."],[258,409.2,2.64,"People today are just as into them as old King Tut—"],[258,411.84,0.8,"maybe even more."],[258,412.64,4.64,"As of 2025, gemstones were a 36 billion dollar industry."],[258,417.28,3.68,"And the value of individual gems can really vary."],[258,420.96,3.04,"Often, gem-sellers cut and polish them for sale,"],[258,424.0,1.92,"creating flat surfaces called facets"],[258,425.92,3.68,"that can enhance those beautiful qualities like brilliance and fire."],[258,429.6,2.96,"On the other hand, inclusions can make gems less valuable."],[258,432.56,3.04,"Unless they’re really cool-looking, like in this rainbow lattice sunstone."],[258,435.6,4.08,"But as early as the 1870s, scientists have tried to get around those long, complex,"],[258,439.68,2.56,"geological processes by making their own gems."],[258,442.24,4.72,"Imitation gems, known as simulants, are made of materials that only look like certain gems."],[258,446.96,2.0,"Think: glass cut into the shape of a diamond."],[258,448.96,3.04,"Or a hunk of granite covered in rhinestones."],[258,452.0,1.68,"No! I said you looked great!"],[258,453.68,4.08,"But we've also harnessed science to grow gems that are so much like the real thing…"],[258,457.76,1.76,"that they kind of are."],[258,459.52,2.24,"These synthetic gems are grown in a lab by"],[258,461.76,3.36,"mimicking the conditions in which gems grow in the wild."],[258,465.12,2.56,"But the lab is way more efficient."],[258,467.68,3.36,"In nature, rubies grow over millions of years,"],[258,471.04,4.32,"but in the lab, they can grow in as little as three hours."],[258,475.36,2.0,"You also don’t have to dig them out of the ground,"],[258,477.36,2.88,"which makes them a lot easier to come by."],[258,480.24,3.52,"People still prize natural gems— the proof is in the price tags."],[258,483.76,3.04,"But many are turning to synthetics as a more sustainable option."],[258,486.8,2.96,"Processes that create synthetic gems don’t use or degrade"],[258,489.76,2.96,"nearly as much land as natural mining does—"],[258,492.72,2.08,"though they do require a lot of energy."],[258,494.8,3.6,"And gem mining can sometimes coincide with human rights abuses,"],[258,498.4,4.16,"like forced labor and displacement of Indigenous peoples."],[258,502.56,3.6,"These considerations are big and very complex—"],[258,506.16,2.56,"we’ve shared some resources below to dig deeper."],[258,508.72,3.52,"As for how to tell if a gem was made inside the earth or in a lab?"],[258,512.24,2.16,"Well, the average shopper probably can’t."],[258,514.4,5.04,"High-end gem dealers often send their wares to experts who use tools like microscopes,"],[258,519.44,3.35,"UV lamps, lasers, and X-rays to tell the difference."],[258,522.8,3.76,"Whether we’re coveting the sparkly jewelry worn by celebs at the Met Gala"],[258,526.56,3.2,"or perusing the crystal stand at the farmer’s market,"],[258,529.76,2.88,"we humans are mesmerized by gems."],[258,532.64,1.92,"And that connects us to the people of the past,"],[258,534.56,2.8,"who saw just as much beauty in them as we do today."],[258,537.36,2.0,"As we move into the future of the gem trade,"],[258,539.36,5.04,"we’ll have to ask ourselves even more questions about what we value and why."],[258,544.4,4.88,"Next time, we’ll talk about one very special gem: diamonds."],[258,549.28,3.6,"I’ll see you then."],[258,552.88,2.16,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Geology"],[258,555.04,2.28,"which was filmed at our studio in Indianapolis, Indiana"],[258,557.32,2.28,"and was made with the help of all these rocking people."],[258,559.6,3.36,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[258,562.96,6.0,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[259,8.21,1.88,"Welcome to Risky Business,"],[259,10.09,5.25,"the game where we find out how far you’ll go to win a mystery prize!"],[259,15.34,2.75,"I’m your host, Will Baywontbay,"],[259,18.14,3.42,"and today’s contestants are 21-year-old Imani,"],[259,21.56,3.84,"16-year-old Johann, and 12-year-old Rachel."],[259,25.48,2.42,"Our first challenge is simple:"],[259,28.07,3.09,"will you eat the cookie on your podium?"],[259,31.28,1.79,"Two of you have sugar cookies,"],[259,33.07,3.92,"but one was made with painfully spicy ghost peppers."],[259,37.41,4.67,"As a reminder, our players aren’t influenced by seeking audience approval"],[259,42.16,3.34,"since Risky Business never gets televised!"],[259,45.5,1.42,"And that’s risky."],[259,47.96,5.05,"Now let’s see what Predicto-Bot 9000 thinks our contestants will do."],[259,53.42,1.54,"Like most daily choices,"],[259,54.97,2.96,"this challenge involves weighing risks against rewards."],[259,57.93,2.17,"But since our grand prize is a mystery,"],[259,60.1,2.67,"the contestants don’t know half of this equation."],[259,62.89,4.38,"People are usually opposed to taking risks for uncertain rewards."],[259,68.69,2.54,"However, Rachel may have an advantage."],[259,71.32,4.88,"People aged 12 to 18 face a lot of unfamiliar circumstances,"],[259,76.2,4.0,"and one of the primary ways they learn to navigate new environments"],[259,80.2,1.58,"is by taking risks."],[259,81.87,4.55,"This means Johann and Rachel are already primed to risk it all."],[259,86.54,3.34,"But research shows that younger adolescents are less likely"],[259,89.88,2.34,"to shy away from uncertain rewards."],[259,92.34,4.13,"This could be because the brain regions associated with decision making"],[259,96.47,1.54,"are still developing."],[259,98.14,3.96,"Or perhaps they’ve simply experienced less failure overall,"],[259,102.14,2.71,"making them less afraid of negative outcomes."],[259,104.93,4.63,"Whatever the answer, this doesn’t mean young people take risks impulsively."],[259,109.69,2.71,"In fact, one decision making study"],[259,112.4,4.21,"found that while 8 to 12 year olds gambled on unknown options"],[259,116.61,4.3,"more than older participants, they still hesitated to do so."],[259,121.41,4.13,"Well, it looks like no one here hesitated to bite their cookie—"],[259,125.54,1.88,"and Johann’s bit back!"],[259,127.5,4.42,"Hopefully our mystery prize will be a lifetime supply of milk."],[259,132.25,4.25,"Speaking of prizes, you’ll have a chance to win one in our next challenge!"],[259,136.63,2.75,"This swimming pool is full of honey,"],[259,139.38,4.59,"and you’ll have to swim through it to grab your $100 prize box!"],[259,144.1,1.29,"While you deliberate,"],[259,145.43,3.04,"let’s see what the PB-9000 has to say."],[259,148.85,3.13,"People are generally attracted to new experiences,"],[259,151.98,2.46,"and it’s highly unlikely that our contestants"],[259,154.44,2.21,"have ever gone swimming in honey."],[259,156.78,5.3,"Some research even suggests new experiences can trigger brain activity"],[259,162.07,2.04,"similar to getting a reward,"],[259,164.12,3.04,"regardless of how pleasant that experience is."],[259,167.29,3.67,"That said, I think Imani has a few disadvantages."],[259,171.29,4.8,"We already know adolescents frequently take risks to learn about the world."],[259,176.09,5.17,"Well, that same drive makes them especially attracted to new experiences."],[259,181.38,5.05,"And at 16 years old, Johann is in peak reward sensitivity range,"],[259,186.47,3.17,"meaning he’s likely to over value the prize."],[259,189.73,4.25,"Conversely, Imani’s longer life experience means she’s able to build"],[259,193.98,4.92,"a robust internal model to simulate her choices’ potential outcomes."],[259,198.94,3.59,"Generally, this helps adults make complex decisions"],[259,202.53,1.75,"with long-term consequences."],[259,204.45,3.75,"But more experience can also lead to more worries."],[259,208.33,3.38,"And some studies have found people struggling with anxiety"],[259,211.71,4.13,"tend to give extra weight to potential negative outcomes,"],[259,215.84,1.71,"even if they’re unlikely."],[259,218.21,1.13,"Thanks, PB."],[259,219.34,3.29,"As predicted, while Johann and Rachel took the plunge,"],[259,222.63,4.17,"this situation was too sticky for our oldest competitor."],[259,227.14,2.34,"That takes us to our final challenge!"],[259,229.47,4.63,"To see the big prize, our contestants will have to cross this balance beam"],[259,234.1,2.88,"without falling into the pit of poison ivy."],[259,237.28,4.63,"And to make things even spicier, we’ve brought their schoolmates to watch!"],[259,241.95,1.83,"What do you think will happen, PB?"],[259,244.03,4.59,"Younger people often discount the long-term consequences of their choices,"],[259,248.62,3.75,"so Rachel might not be as worried about the poison ivy,"],[259,252.37,3.84,"But Johann is driven by an even stronger influence."],[259,256.29,4.8,"During adolescence, our desire for group acceptance is at an all-time high,"],[259,261.3,3.09,"and this can lead people to take surprising risks"],[259,264.38,2.29,"to secure the respect of their peers."],[259,266.72,3.46,"This is especially true from ages 14 to 16,"],[259,270.18,4.8,"when the brain regions responsible for emotional control are more developed"],[259,274.98,2.67,"than those handling cognitive control."],[259,277.94,3.21,"What's more, this cognitive control is weaker"],[259,281.15,2.96,"when adolescents know their peers are watching."],[259,284.36,4.8,"So with his friends standing by, Johann is very likely to risk it all."],[259,289.33,2.58,"Well, I’m excited to see— oh uh."],[259,291.91,3.13,"I’m being told there’s a bear in the honey pool?!"],[259,295.42,1.79,"Uh, we’ll be right back!"],[260,8.92,4.46,"In 1315, Scotland’s King Robert the Bruce decided to lay siege"],[260,13.38,2.04,"to England's Carlisle Castle."],[260,15.8,2.21,"Robert expected a swift victory,"],[260,18.01,4.09,"but his men arrived to find a relentless downpour."],[260,22.68,2.29,"Struggling against muck and mud,"],[260,25.1,2.84,"they tried to fill the moat with corn husks"],[260,28.07,2.38,"only for the husks to float away."],[260,31.11,3.59,"Then Robert ordered the construction of a massive siege tower,"],[260,34.91,3.09,"but the English responded with wooden scaffolds"],[260,37.99,2.17,"that heightened the castle walls."],[260,40.83,5.0,"Their final push to scale those walls ended with English arrows raining down"],[260,45.83,1.88,"on the sodden Scots."],[260,48.09,3.54,"At a time when sieges could last months or even years,"],[260,51.63,3.38,"Robert retreated after just 11 days."],[260,55.55,3.88,"This siege is a prime example of how medieval European castles"],[260,59.43,3.0,"could stand firm against enemy onslaughts."],[260,62.93,3.0,"But castles weren’t just for passive defense;"],[260,66.14,3.29,"they were symbols of power, private residences,"],[260,69.61,5.21,"and hubs of governance intended to impress and intimidate."],[260,74.99,5.34,"In fact, they were just as likely to be used for offensive military tactics."],[260,80.74,3.71,"Castles regularly served as bases for launching attacks"],[260,84.45,3.88,"and as a way to control local populations with promises of shelter"],[260,88.58,2.25,"or threats of violence."],[260,91.55,6.21,"While citadels and fortified palaces could be found as early as the 8th century BCE,"],[260,98.01,2.0,"European castles as we think of them"],[260,100.01,4.71,"started appearing in large numbers in the late 9th century CE."],[260,104.98,3.75,"At this time, the ruling Carolingian Empire was breaking down."],[260,108.98,1.42,"And as the monarch’s control"],[260,110.4,3.63,"over his 1 million square kilometer realm disintegrated,"],[260,114.15,4.5,"nobles began building castles to solidify their regional power."],[260,119.03,4.42,"These early wooden castles followed the motte and bailey design,"],[260,123.7,3.21,"featuring a central tower built atop a steep mound"],[260,126.91,2.13,"with a walled yard at the base."],[260,129.5,3.21,"But over the following centuries, as castles became centers"],[260,132.71,3.21,"of social, cultural, military, and political life,"],[260,136.3,3.29,"their designs grew more varied and complex."],[260,139.97,5.34,"By the 12th century, many wooden motte and baileys had been rebuilt with stone,"],[260,145.39,2.63,"increasing their defense and longevity."],[260,148.64,2.92,"Subsequent castles built in stone from the start"],[260,151.56,4.84,"used curtain walls and gatehouses to enclose the castle yard."],[260,156.78,5.09,"And their walls and towers were often coated in a layer of protective limewash,"],[260,161.99,4.46,"covering their stony grey with a range of colorful tones."],[260,167.33,1.67,"Alongside their scale,"],[260,169.21,3.71,"visual beauty was a way for castles to project power."],[260,173.5,2.42,"Castle Caernarfon in northern Wales"],[260,175.92,2.96,"was built with bands of different colored stone."],[260,179.18,3.04,"And Crusader armies building castles in the Middle East"],[260,182.34,4.67,"often incorporated elements of intricate Islamic and Byzantine architecture."],[260,187.35,3.29,"But as European castles became more widespread,"],[260,190.81,2.13,"so did technologies to defeat them."],[260,193.1,3.04,"Fire had long threatened wooden motte and baileys,"],[260,196.15,2.88,"and while their stone counterparts were more resistant,"],[260,199.03,3.63,"their underlying wooden structure was still vulnerable to arson."],[260,203.2,2.79,"Battering rams cracked castle doors,"],[260,205.99,4.09,"siege towers and mining operations thwarted castle walls,"],[260,210.08,4.25,"and trebuchets launching stones up to 130 kilograms"],[260,214.34,2.25,"endangered entire structures."],[260,217.34,2.88,"Rectangular castle towers were especially vulnerable"],[260,220.22,3.79,"since their square corners had fewer load bearing stones."],[260,224.84,3.75,"Fortunately, when the Crusaders returned to Europe from the Middle East,"],[260,228.6,2.96,"they brought home architectural innovations,"],[260,231.56,3.55,"including the design for concentric castles."],[260,235.27,4.04,"Featuring numerous rings of defensive walls around a central tower,"],[260,239.53,5.55,"this approach created a circular fortress with multiple layers of siege security."],[260,245.41,3.92,"From the 13th century onward, this design reigned supreme,"],[260,249.58,4.25,"even standing up to the small cannons that had begun spreading across Europe."],[260,254.25,1.58,"But by the 15th century,"],[260,255.84,3.17,"the high towers that once rained arrows on attackers"],[260,259.09,3.29,"became easy targets for powerful new cannons."],[260,263.38,3.25,"While castle builders adapted by making squatter structures"],[260,266.64,2.0,"with walls up to three meters thick,"],[260,268.64,3.17,"this kind of construction was prohibitively expensive."],[260,272.31,1.83,"By the early 16th century,"],[260,274.14,3.0,"most local nobles couldn’t keep up with the costs,"],[260,277.15,3.0,"and became reliant on strong centralized rulers"],[260,280.36,4.67,"who could afford to support modern defenses and large standing armies."],[260,285.41,5.34,"This financial and political pressure, alongside advances in gunpowder weapons,"],[260,290.75,2.88,"put an end to castles’ military dominance."],[260,293.83,3.0,"Most of these fortresses were converted to residences,"],[260,296.96,2.5,"administrative centers, or prisons,"],[260,299.67,4.59,"and today many still serve as tourist attractions and private estates."],[260,304.97,3.84,"But while they no longer protect their inhabitants from sieging armies,"],[260,308.97,3.21,"they still project the grandeur and authority"],[260,312.22,4.21,"that made medieval castles so effective for centuries."],[261,8.52,2.96,"It's July in the deserts of Nevada"],[261,11.48,1.88,"in the western United States."],[261,15.0,2.68,"The hottest time of the year."],[261,30.84,2.88,"Bands of wild horses, mustang,"],[261,33.72,4.8,"are converging on one of the last remaining waterholes for miles."],[261,43.4,4.16,"Now, water not only offers them the chance to drink,"],[261,47.56,2.32,"it can also bring power."],[261,52.72,3.24,"If a stallion can control access to water,"],[261,55.96,3.0,"he will have secured mating rights to the entire herd."],[261,60.88,2.92,"So stallions try to dominate these pools,"],[261,63.8,2.44,"fighting off rivals who venture too close."],[261,79.52,1.68,"A stranger."],[261,82.6,2.28,"He's travelled 10 miles to be here"],[261,84.88,4.2,"because the pools where he's come from have already dried up."],[261,92.4,3.0,"With him come his females."],[261,97.0,1.96,"If he can't provide them with water,"],[261,98.96,2.28,"they will leave him for the white stallion"],[261,101.24,3.08,"who already dominates this pool."],[261,114.52,2.88,"So he will have to fight."],[261,177.04,2.12,"There is everything to lose."],[261,180.36,5.4,"A broken leg or a shattered jaw would mean a slow and painful death."],[261,205.88,2.12,"A missed kick"],[261,208.0,2.04,"and it's all over."],[261,211.56,2.64,"The new arrival has won."],[261,215.76,3.28,"And his prize is more than just a chance to drink."],[261,220.12,3.16,"He has provided for his herd and, in the process,"],[261,223.28,3.04,"stolen his rival's females."],[261,227.4,3.44,"The white stallion's rule is over."],[262,4.3,2.6,"Elise Hu: Tell us a little bit about what led you here"],[262,6.94,4.04,"and what it means to you to be a digital wellness entrepreneur."],[262,11.01,5.14,"Larissa May: The beginning of digital wellness started with digital sickness."],[262,16.18,2.47,"I literally was scrolling to death."],[262,19.48,4.71,"I was a college student spending 10 to 12 hours a day behind my screen."],[262,24.22,4.54,"I was scrolling more than I was walking, sleeping, or socializing."],[262,28.76,2.44,"And luckily, I had an RA that saved my life."],[262,31.2,2.44,"But I was going to the psychiatrist every day,"],[262,33.66,2.54,"and I found it really interesting"],[262,36.2,2.47,"that they asked me about drugs, sex and alcohol,"],[262,38.7,3.3,"but not the drug that was in my pocket."],[262,42.04,4.57,"And that was the beginning of the big idea, 10 years ago,"],[262,46.64,6.54,"which was maybe digital wellness is the new wellness, in that as humans,"],[262,53.22,4.57,"we have to cultivate a healthy relationship with technology"],[262,57.82,2.23,"if we want to be around for another millennia."],[262,60.09,2.77,"EH: Right. How did it show up for you,"],[262,62.89,2.37,"when you said that you were kind of feeling sick?"],[262,65.3,2.37,"LM: Well I think the thing that I’ve learned"],[262,67.7,2.07,"in the 10 years of exploration"],[262,69.77,2.64,"is that screens are really just symptoms."],[262,72.44,2.6,"So many young people -- and adults too --"],[262,75.04,2.7,"we go to our screens because it's more comfortable"],[262,77.78,2.07,"than actually dealing with the emotions --"],[262,79.88,3.67,"whether it's sadness, depression, anxiety."],[262,83.58,3.3,"And for me, I just didn't have the skills,"],[262,86.92,3.57,"the emotional resilience in the digital age that I needed."],[262,90.49,3.77,"But I was given a device and was told to, \"Hey, go through this life milestone,"],[262,94.29,2.07,"go to college, figure out how to be a human."],[262,96.39,4.24,"And by the way, you're going to have a stage in your pocket the entire time.\""],[262,100.66,1.77,"And then I started looking into it."],[262,102.47,1.77,"I think most of us, we look around,"],[262,104.27,2.1,"we see our kids, our parents on their phones."],[262,106.4,4.24,"And then I realized, do we want to chase our dreams?"],[262,110.67,1.94,"Or do we want to be behind these things"],[262,112.64,3.67,"that are making billions of dollars for a couple of guys in Silicon Valley?"],[262,116.35,2.74,"EH: You have said, and I'm quoting you to you,"],[262,119.12,2.8,"\"The systems were not in place for the younger generations"],[262,121.95,3.6,"to develop healthy relationships with technology from the start.\""],[262,125.59,2.07,"So a system that was sort of broken here."],[262,127.69,1.37,"What did you mean by that?"],[262,129.06,1.8,"And are things getting better,"],[262,130.9,2.3,"now that we're in this age where we realize it?"],[262,133.23,1.8,"There's a lot of books about this,"],[262,135.03,3.6,"and a lot of discourse about social media and sort of the risks of it."],[262,138.64,4.84,"LM: The fact that I started this with $250 in my dorm room,"],[262,143.51,4.37,"and I’m now on this stage, alone, is a win."],[262,147.88,2.67,"It’s a win for the movement that TED believes"],[262,150.58,3.37,"that digital wellness is critical for the future of humanity."],[262,153.98,3.64,"Now when we talk about systems and systems change,"],[262,157.66,4.14,"a lot of you might be in a state where there might be a phone-free ban."],[262,161.82,2.0,"And across the nation,"],[262,163.86,2.54,"because we haven't had a national legislation"],[262,166.43,1.6,"in 30 years past,"],[262,168.06,3.4,"almost 40 states, this back to school, said,"],[262,171.5,2.44,"\"We want to ban phones now.\""],[262,173.97,1.67,"There was a problem with that."],[262,175.67,3.07,"Creating a policy that you can't have phones in school"],[262,178.78,2.0,"does not fix all of the problems."],[262,180.81,2.67,"And so what I did was say --"],[262,183.51,2.84,"we actually started this with the governor in New York --"],[262,186.38,4.74,"\"Why don't we build the next generation of digital wellness activist athletes?\""],[262,191.16,2.27,"Let's train them, let's give them grants,"],[262,193.46,3.97,"and let's have them help us implement and create a new story"],[262,197.46,4.74,"and a new brand for digital wellness, from \"phone free\" into \"phone-free fun.\""],[262,202.2,3.9,"And so what I believe is that we have to put young people at the center,"],[262,206.14,3.14,"but we also have to educate all stakeholders."],[262,209.31,2.6,"We need to ensure, in the future,"],[262,211.91,2.5,"that there are financial incentives for big tech"],[262,214.44,2.47,"to actually edit these platforms,"],[262,216.95,2.4,"in the same way that we've created tax incentives"],[262,219.35,3.94,"for the environmental movement, because nothing has changed,"],[262,223.32,1.67,"but humans are changing."],[262,225.39,1.64,"EH: OK. Two follow-ups to that."],[262,227.06,2.17,"One is, what are young people saying to you?"],[262,229.26,2.23,"LM: Well young people want to be heard."],[262,231.53,3.5,"They want to be in the rooms where decisions are being made about them,"],[262,235.06,2.1,"because they're the ones that are now forced"],[262,237.2,1.97,"to take their tests on iPads."],[262,239.2,2.6,"There is no longer that period of time"],[262,241.84,3.54,"where you're feeling stressed to take a test, then you're relieved,"],[262,245.38,2.13,"and you don't get your grade back for a week."],[262,247.54,2.04,"These kids are getting it from all sides --"],[262,249.61,3.34,"socially, academically, a lot of pressure from their parents --"],[262,252.95,1.87,"and they're the ones that give me hope,"],[262,254.85,2.07,"that say, \"Hey, we want to be the leaders."],[262,256.92,3.14,"We want to show our school how to not just take away phones,"],[262,260.09,2.9,"but how to make sure the kids that use phones at lunch"],[262,263.03,2.04,"because they didn't have a place to fit in"],[262,265.06,1.64,"can sit at our friendship table.\""],[262,266.73,1.83,"Or \"We want to take phones away,"],[262,268.6,3.74,"and we actually want to make screen-free proms and make this feel fun.\""],[262,272.37,3.4,"Because if it's all about abstinence and the lack thereof,"],[262,275.81,2.9,"you're never going to get kids on board."],[262,278.71,1.7,"EH: I get it, I grew up in Texas,"],[262,280.41,2.27,"where abstinence was taught in schools"],[262,282.71,2.04,"-- look at how well that worked."],[262,286.12,4.94,"We're talking a lot about systems and then both kind of big picture,"],[262,291.09,2.94,"but also a lot that can be done individually"],[262,294.06,3.47,"and how we can make changes in our own families or in our own homes."],[262,297.53,3.34,"I guess my next question is why is the onus on us?"],[262,300.9,3.14,"You know, there's so many conversations about, like,"],[262,304.07,4.5,"what we need to be doing to help our kids become more literate,"],[262,308.61,3.97,"what we need to be doing to help solve for this anxious generation"],[262,312.61,2.64,"or so-called anxious generation in our own homes."],[262,315.28,2.87,"What do those incentives look like at a system-wide level?"],[262,318.15,4.24,"Like, how do we incentivize technology makers and companies"],[262,322.39,3.3,"to do something different that would be healthier for the rest of us?"],[262,325.69,2.27,"LM: I’ve been trying to do that for 10 years."],[262,327.99,2.47,"We still don't have a national legislation passed,"],[262,330.49,4.24,"and AI is moving faster than any other social media platform in history."],[262,334.76,2.57,"So at this point, as humans,"],[262,337.37,1.47,"the way I think about this"],[262,338.87,2.77,"is that technology is more like food than anything."],[262,341.67,2.6,"We have our own media metabolisms."],[262,344.31,1.6,"Every child is different."],[262,345.94,1.87,"Every family is different."],[262,347.85,3.1,"We must learn how to consume technology"],[262,350.98,3.17,"in a way that is going to support us rather than hurt us,"],[262,354.15,4.8,"because unfortunately, the systems are not built for us to thrive."],[262,358.99,3.0,"But the hope is that we can retrain young minds."],[262,362.02,4.44,"And if we start early, well -- I can't believe I'm going to say this --"],[262,366.46,1.97,"the average toddler,"],[262,368.43,3.97,"40 percent of them have devices before they're even in preschool."],[262,372.44,4.74,"So if we're not starting then, the moment a child sees a blue light,"],[262,377.21,2.57,"then we're going to be losing ourselves to big tech."],[262,379.81,2.5,"And, guess what, it's no longer about billionaires."],[262,382.35,3.2,"It's now about trillionaires and everyone else."],[262,385.58,5.21,"EH: How do you want kids as young as age six to be educated?"],[262,390.82,2.1,"LM: I've been doing it for the last decade,"],[262,392.96,4.94,"and what I believe is that wicked problems need wicked games,"],[262,397.93,2.13,"and that if we want to drive behavior change,"],[262,400.06,3.8,"we have to do it in a playful way that kids are going to engage with."],[262,403.9,3.97,"We know that just having PowerPoint slides and telling kids what not to do"],[262,407.9,1.17,"doesn't work."],[262,409.11,1.74,"So I wanted to rewrite that playbook"],[262,410.87,3.24,"and put young people at the center of designing, creating,"],[262,414.14,3.8,"and now I’m even building technology that young people are helping me design --"],[262,417.98,1.64,"because I know what's out there."],[262,419.65,4.77,"Surveillance and stalking our kids is not enough to support them emotionally."],[262,424.42,1.7,"And our world is broken."],[262,426.16,2.34,"EH: How should we be supporting them emotionally?"],[262,428.49,2.77,"LM: Well I think one of the things I always say"],[262,431.3,2.44,"is that if you’re a parent or a grandparent,"],[262,433.76,3.24,"or even just someone that lives with someone you love,"],[262,437.03,3.4,"is that you should never have more devices than people in a room."],[262,440.47,3.97,"And what I mean by that is that if you want to watch something,"],[262,444.48,1.17,"watch it together."],[262,445.64,2.24,"If you want to scroll, scroll together."],[262,448.11,3.44,"When you're going to bed with your partner and you're on your devices,"],[262,451.55,2.1,"or you’re sitting in a room with your kids --"],[262,453.65,2.77,"a term I call “phubbing” -- when you’re all on your phones."],[262,456.42,1.0,"EH: Guilty."],[262,457.42,3.4,"LM: I mean, we’ve all been there, but it’s one of the great golden rules"],[262,460.82,1.5,"that we can start implementing."],[262,462.33,2.54,"And at the end of the day, the kids are looking at us,"],[262,464.86,3.6,"and we are modeling that future, and we need to be able to show them"],[262,468.46,2.44,"even though the headlines say that you’re a victim"],[262,470.9,2.1,"and that you're anxious and you're depressed,"],[262,473.04,2.67,"that's not a story that is going to empower someone"],[262,475.74,1.47,"to be up on the stage."],[262,477.24,2.0,"I pulled myself out of that story."],[262,479.28,2.6,"And so I want the next generation of young people"],[262,481.91,1.67,"to know that it is possible,"],[262,483.61,3.57,"and that when social media is used as a tool, it can change the world."],[262,487.22,3.87,"EH: Do you draw a distinction between social media"],[262,491.12,5.11,"or just digital tools for consumption versus creation?"],[262,496.26,2.04,"LM: I love that you asked this question,"],[262,498.33,2.34,"and I'm going to give my tip at the end on this."],[262,500.66,4.5,"But ultimately, the way that technology was designed"],[262,505.17,3.27,"was for us to be passive consumers."],[262,508.81,3.9,"We were never trained to interact with it any other way."],[262,512.74,3.34,"And therefore, many of us, starting at a young age,"],[262,516.08,3.1,"are caught in this doom loop and this vicious cycle"],[262,519.22,2.44,"of \"I'm going to die,\" \"the world is going to end,\""],[262,521.68,1.53,"\"I'm never going to have a job,\""],[262,523.25,2.67,"\"What's the point of studying? The world is burning."],[262,525.96,1.27,"Kids are dying.\""],[262,527.26,4.04,"However, I believe, and I know, that when we retrain our minds,"],[262,531.33,2.94,"and we teach our kids to engage with technology,"],[262,534.3,5.27,"with an intention to lead with creativity and the imagination muscle,"],[262,539.6,2.67,"that we can actually activate joy"],[262,542.31,3.54,"and fight against the doom loop into the joy loop,"],[262,545.88,2.57,"which is really where we take back our creative power"],[262,548.48,1.33,"and say, \"You know what?"],[262,549.85,4.47,"I'm going to be the creator of my own digital story and my own digital world."],[262,554.35,1.94,"And instead of you hacking me, Big Tech,"],[262,556.29,3.64,"I'm going to hack my devices so I can take back control of my life.\""],[262,559.96,4.87,"EH: OK. Which brings us to Ginko and how you have decided to work with AI."],[262,564.86,2.5,"Why did you decide to use an AI?"],[262,567.4,2.24,"And can you tell us a little bit more about it?"],[262,569.67,1.33,"LM: Absolutely."],[262,571.03,2.5,"In looking at all of these tools in the ecosystem,"],[262,573.57,2.57,"I saw that parents were stalking kids,"],[262,576.17,2.6,"but there was basically broken product-market fit."],[262,578.81,3.97,"Because kids, I’m going to be really honest -- they’re smarter than all of us."],[262,582.81,3.7,"They break through everything. They create their own Wi-Fi routers."],[262,586.55,3.27,"So the truth is that your kids are always going to win."],[262,589.85,2.37,"What I saw in the market is that --"],[262,592.25,4.21,"we’ve seen a lot of OpenAI’s decisions lately and it’s a little bit scary."],[262,596.46,2.84,"I know a lot of kids that have also lost their lives"],[262,599.33,2.47,"because of how AI impacted them,"],[262,601.83,2.07,"and the way that social media impacted me."],[262,603.93,4.14,"And what I knew is that parents didn't have a doctor-backed compass"],[262,608.11,1.47,"for the digital world."],[262,609.57,2.7,"What do you do if your kid is being groomed?"],[262,612.31,2.84,"But also, they didn’t have a safe place to go."],[262,615.14,3.84,"The reason why I wanted to create an AI-based tool was:"],[262,619.02,3.57,"one, because I had clinical research that I could build it with."],[262,622.62,2.14,"Two, I could make it HIPAA-compliant"],[262,624.79,3.64,"so people could trust us with their family secrets."],[262,628.46,1.83,"But third, because I wanted"],[262,630.33,4.54,"to build a tool that could take the data from a child's device,"],[262,634.9,2.87,"take those insights, and make early predictions for parents"],[262,637.77,2.47,"of things that could go wrong."],[262,640.24,3.07,"Because technology shows us signals."],[262,643.34,2.54,"And if Big Tech is making billions of dollars"],[262,645.91,1.87,"and selling our children face masks,"],[262,647.81,4.47,"we, as parents, should be able to take that data, get those insights and know,"],[262,652.32,3.24,"\"Hey, if Lars is moving really quickly on her device"],[262,655.59,3.67,"and experiencing a lot of mood issues and looking at different content ...\""],[262,659.29,2.2,"My parents, back when I was struggling,"],[262,661.52,3.4,"might have been able to get a notification that said"],[262,664.96,2.17,"\"Hey, you should take Ginko to the parent,"],[262,667.16,2.27,"to the doctor's office, and show them,"],[262,669.43,4.34,"because we think she might be dealing with anxiety or depression or ADHD.\""],[262,673.8,2.23,"And that is the big gap right now,"],[262,676.04,3.94,"is that there is such a disconnect between our children's inner worlds,"],[262,680.01,2.1,"the digital world, and our family's world."],[262,682.14,1.6,"And I want to rewrite that story."],[262,683.78,3.84,"EH: So basically, if you are a parent with a child who has a device,"],[262,687.65,2.04,"or more than one device,"],[262,689.72,1.5,"you can use Ginko, is that how?"],[262,691.25,2.34,"LM: Just ask Ginko, yeah."],[262,693.62,1.5,"Really, our vision, you know,"],[262,695.12,3.7,"after spending a decade building half the story with young people,"],[262,698.83,3.27,"I realized the next frontier of digital wellness"],[262,702.13,2.64,"is bringing it into the healthcare space."],[262,705.13,2.37,"This is not something that's going away."],[262,707.54,1.33,"And in fact, in five years,"],[262,708.9,3.27,"we’re going to be sitting, wishing that [we had] asked people"],[262,712.21,1.3,"to put their phones away,"],[262,713.54,3.24,"because our tech is going to be embedded in our bodies."],[262,716.81,1.67,"And so what we need to figure out"],[262,718.51,3.44,"is how do we preserve the part of us that makes us human."],[262,722.25,2.04,"EH: What is your answer to that question?"],[262,724.32,3.74,"LM: Well I think we really need to get out of the consumer seat"],[262,728.09,3.3,"and into the driver's seat, when it comes to technology."],[262,731.39,1.5,"And what I mean by that"],[262,732.9,4.54,"is that you can literally retrain your mind or your child's mind"],[262,737.47,2.84,"by engaging with technology 20 minutes a day"],[262,740.34,1.44,"and doing something joyful --"],[262,741.8,2.27,"sparking the imagination loop."],[262,744.11,3.84,"Instead of having your child scrolling passively on YouTube, sit down"],[262,747.98,3.24,"[and] let them tell you a story and make it together on AI,"],[262,751.25,2.57,"and [then] let them watch it appear like magic."],[262,753.85,1.37,"That is joy."],[262,755.22,2.37,"That is how technology can be used."],[262,757.62,5.24,"But more importantly, it will awaken our senses instead of suffocating them."],[262,764.13,3.24,"EH: If I asked you to just paint a picture"],[262,767.4,2.37,"of a more affirmative vision of the future,"],[262,769.77,3.2,"in which we have all of this digital technology"],[262,773.0,1.5,"and AI at our fingertips,"],[262,774.54,4.77,"but it isn't so suffocating, or at least not so fraught,"],[262,779.31,1.3,"what would that look like?"],[262,780.64,2.5,"LM: It would look like young people believing"],[262,783.18,2.47,"that they have a right to their mind."],[262,786.08,1.2,"EH: Say more about that."],[262,787.28,2.9,"LM: What I mean is that, like I said,"],[262,790.22,3.04,"kids have just been taught to be consumers of technology."],[262,793.29,2.3,"I am watching kids every day say,"],[262,795.62,2.47,"\"I'm going to put my phone down"],[262,798.09,3.0,"and I'm going to take back control of my thoughts."],[262,801.13,2.33,"I'm going to think before I search."],[262,803.5,3.57,"I'm going to reflect before I say.\""],[262,807.1,2.6,"Technology has, quite honestly,"],[262,809.74,1.9,"told us more about our own stories"],[262,811.67,2.64,"than we've let ourselves reflect on our own."],[262,814.34,2.5,"And I know that in this new future,"],[262,816.88,2.1,"we will have no minds left behind"],[262,819.01,2.4,"if we continue to give young people the tools"],[262,821.45,2.2,"to rewrite that for the next generation."],[262,823.69,1.27,"EH: Before we let you go,"],[262,824.96,3.54,"what are some small, actionable steps that we can take"],[262,828.52,2.5,"to get to this more affirmative vision?"],[262,831.03,3.57,"LM: Swap screens with analog fun."],[262,834.63,1.57,"Have a notebook and paper."],[262,836.23,2.6,"Get your knitting. Put your phone down."],[262,838.87,1.17,"Keep that at your desk."],[262,840.07,2.27,"It's a great way to tap into yourself."],[262,842.34,3.47,"The second thing is -- and this is hard for all of us --"],[262,845.84,4.24,"challenge yourself, one day a week, to not use any AI."],[262,850.75,2.67,"EH: And that's from an AI founder, you're saying this."],[262,853.45,3.94,"LM: Absolutely, and the truth is we have to retrain our brains"],[262,857.42,2.6,"to actually answer our own questions."],[262,860.06,4.1,"The third thing is actually try to do a screen-free scavenger hunt"],[262,864.19,1.53,"where, if you have a family,"],[262,865.76,3.9,"have your kids write down a list of things that they want to find in the world,"],[262,869.7,2.6,"leave your phones at home, and just go out and do it."],[262,872.34,1.24,"Go be free."],[262,873.6,3.5,"Escape the wild, wild web and get some screen peace,"],[262,877.14,1.97,"which is really just peace of mind."],[262,879.14,2.14,"EH: Alright, you heard it from Larissa May --"],[262,881.31,1.94,"escape the wild, wild web."],[262,883.25,1.5,"Larissa May, thank you so much."],[262,884.75,1.2,"LM: Thank you."],[263,4.27,2.9,"On Valentine's Day 2022,"],[263,7.2,4.84,"a pontoon bridge appeared near the border of Belarus and Ukraine."],[263,12.74,2.97,"One day, an empty river,"],[263,15.75,2.67,"and the next day, a bridge."],[263,19.08,3.3,"I’m the cofounder of an Earth imaging company,"],[263,22.42,1.17,"and with our satellites,"],[263,23.59,3.07,"we can see the entire world every day."],[263,27.19,3.07,"And from space, we were able to detect that bridge,"],[263,30.26,1.2,"zoom in,"],[263,31.5,4.07,"and that picture was broadcast around the world the next morning."],[263,35.97,3.57,"Everyone could see that something was changing."],[263,41.1,3.44,"And then ten days later, Russia invaded Ukraine."],[263,45.18,4.3,"Thanks to satellite imagery, many governments and NGOs,"],[263,49.51,2.07,"researchers and journalists,"],[263,51.62,3.9,"they all had access to the same daily facts,"],[263,55.55,2.44,"the same baseline of reality,"],[263,58.02,3.17,"and the same possibility, at least in theory,"],[263,61.23,2.6,"of seeing clearly enough to respond together."],[263,64.39,1.83,"And with that shared view of reality,"],[263,66.26,3.14,"we’re no longer arguing if something is there"],[263,69.43,2.87,"but deciding what to do because it is there."],[263,72.84,3.2,"In the days that followed that Valentine's Day bridge,"],[263,76.07,4.07,"Western nations coordinated for severe sanctions against Russia"],[263,80.18,2.24,"and unified in their support for Ukraine."],[263,82.88,1.8,"But for months beforehand,"],[263,84.72,2.7,"we saw an increase of military activity"],[263,87.45,2.2,"and tanks amassing at the border."],[263,90.35,2.04,"The signals were there."],[263,93.32,3.67,"Perhaps the allies stopped the worst from happening,"],[263,97.03,1.6,"but they didn't stop the war."],[263,100.4,2.67,"And I sat with that for a really long time."],[263,104.33,3.54,"The satellites, they worked exactly as they were designed."],[263,107.87,1.57,"They showed the emerging threat."],[263,109.47,1.53,"They saw the threat."],[263,111.58,3.27,"But the institutions that were supposed to respond,"],[263,114.88,1.64,"they weren't built for this."],[263,117.38,3.5,"The alliances, the frameworks, the coordination mechanisms,"],[263,120.92,2.97,"they weren't built to handle signal at this speed"],[263,123.92,2.3,"or to incorporate this amount of information."],[263,126.66,3.77,"And we built this extraordinary eye,"],[263,130.46,3.7,"an independent source of truth about what's happening on the planet."],[263,134.66,4.3,"But an eye that's not connected to a functional nervous system"],[263,139.0,3.17,"and a body capable of response,"],[263,142.21,1.8,"that eye is just a witness."],[263,144.61,3.9,"But fortunately, new technologies are helping to close that gap."],[263,148.55,2.87,"When you take all of this earth data"],[263,151.41,3.74,"and pair it with the analytical power of AI,"],[263,155.15,3.57,"we can build extremely different things,"],[263,158.76,3.07,"action in an entirely new way."],[263,162.43,3.77,"We're entering into an era that we call planetary intelligence,"],[263,166.23,3.6,"where new capabilities in action can happen at the speed of change."],[263,170.33,4.0,"Now, my cofounder, Will, was on this spot in 2014,"],[263,174.34,1.97,"and he shared with you all our dream."],[263,176.81,3.4,"What if we could image the whole world every day?"],[263,181.21,3.17,"And he came back in 2018 with a new dream."],[263,184.41,3.6,"What if we can use AI to understand how the world is changing?"],[263,189.09,3.24,"And today, that vision is becoming a reality."],[263,192.89,1.87,"And before I share that with you today,"],[263,194.76,2.14,"I want to provide a little bit of context."],[263,197.46,2.9,"The space sector is going through a renaissance right now."],[263,200.4,3.27,"We have reusable rockets, thousands of satellites."],[263,204.24,3.77,"And for the first time in over 50 years,"],[263,208.04,2.14,"we just sent humans to the moon and back."],[263,210.57,3.47,"NASA is inspiring us once again with this picture"],[263,214.04,2.87,"to gain perspective that we live on this beautiful,"],[263,216.95,2.67,"precious spaceship Earth that we call home."],[263,220.42,3.64,"And with hundreds of satellites that are all monitoring the planet,"],[263,224.05,2.07,"they're getting connected to each other,"],[263,226.16,2.5,"and we're putting a lot more compute next to it."],[263,228.66,2.94,"It's beginning to build a planetary nervous system."],[263,232.96,1.6,"And here on the ground,"],[263,234.56,5.0,"we're all in amazement as AI just continues to accelerate at rapid speed."],[263,240.4,1.64,"And like many of you here,"],[263,242.07,2.34,"I'm holding on to that polarity"],[263,244.44,3.17,"between the sheer awesomeness of this technology,"],[263,247.65,1.67,"and also a bit of anxiety"],[263,249.31,2.7,"about how this comes into the world in the right way."],[263,252.65,2.84,"We have to get governance right,"],[263,255.49,2.0,"and we have to get alignment right."],[263,257.82,2.84,"For AI to work for everyone"],[263,260.69,2.84,"and the larger living world,"],[263,263.53,3.17,"we need it to speak the language of the planetary systems"],[263,266.7,1.6,"to which we're all embedded."],[263,268.67,4.34,"It must understand our infrastructure and human activity,"],[263,273.04,6.11,"but also the impacts on the water, the trees and the soil."],[263,279.78,2.3,"So let me show you what this looks like."],[263,283.01,3.27,"So when you combine all of this Earth data"],[263,286.32,2.77,"with AI and large language models,"],[263,289.12,4.3,"we are allowing for global change to happen in near real time."],[263,293.46,2.27,"You can see change in just about everywhere"],[263,295.76,2.77,"over vast distances and time."],[263,299.26,4.64,"It's helping to close that gap between awareness and action."],[263,304.27,3.67,"A farmer can understand how healthy their soil is."],[263,308.24,3.67,"An insurer can create a dynamic and custom policy"],[263,311.91,3.44,"based on the real-time environmental risk of the asset."],[263,315.35,1.77,"And a finance minister,"],[263,317.15,4.41,"they can account for the true cost of their financial economy"],[263,321.58,3.47,"along with their rich, living, natural ecosystems."],[263,325.42,4.04,"So planetary intelligence guides our actions"],[263,329.46,1.23,"much in the same way"],[263,330.73,4.17,"that the eye connected to a nervous system guides the body"],[263,334.93,4.5,"so that humanity is no longer a witness to what's happening."],[263,339.47,3.97,"We can become stewards for the planet and our society."],[263,344.04,2.8,"And we're already seeing glimmers of planetary intelligence"],[263,346.88,1.8,"happening all over the world."],[263,348.71,3.14,"It is already measuring global change in near real time,"],[263,351.88,3.34,"guiding human intervention where it's needed the most."],[263,355.25,1.8,"So let me show you a few examples."],[263,357.09,1.8,"First, let's go to Brazil."],[263,358.92,2.74,"The Brazilian Amazon is huge."],[263,361.69,1.83,"It's five million square kilometers,"],[263,363.56,2.97,"roughly two thirds the size of the United States,"],[263,366.53,3.1,"and it's brimming with rich biodiversity,"],[263,369.67,2.47,"countless Indigenous communities."],[263,372.17,4.54,"And it's the home of about 20 percent of the planet's freshwater supply."],[263,377.61,2.94,"But deep in the dense forests of the Amazon,"],[263,380.58,3.54,"there are illegal activities that are happening all the time."],[263,384.15,3.4,"Fields are cleared for illicit crops."],[263,387.58,2.67,"Runways are cut for narco traffickers."],[263,390.75,4.0,"Riverbeds are illegally mined and poisoning downstream communities."],[263,394.79,2.8,"And deforestation still happens every day."],[263,397.89,1.43,"So how do you stop that?"],[263,400.1,3.2,"You can't manually patrol that with boots on the ground"],[263,403.3,1.64,"and planes and paperwork."],[263,405.94,3.74,"So our team worked with local partners and the Brazilian federal police"],[263,409.71,4.07,"to create a system that monitors the entire Brazilian Amazon every day."],[263,414.24,3.24,"And it looks for a signal of illegal activity."],[263,417.48,2.5,"For instance, an unpermitted road."],[263,420.95,4.47,"And today, over 100 000 people across the Brazilian government,"],[263,425.46,2.5,"from a ranger to federal courts,"],[263,427.96,1.74,"they use this system"],[263,429.69,1.94,"and over the last three years,"],[263,431.66,4.04,"they’ve seized four billion dollars’ worth of illegal equipment in the Amazon."],[263,435.73,1.47,"And most importantly, I think,"],[263,437.23,3.24,"over wherever this was deployed,"],[263,440.47,3.6,"deforestation rates decreased by over 50 percent."],[263,449.85,2.94,"Now let's see what this looks like at the speed of change."],[263,452.82,1.4,"Let's go to Kenya."],[263,454.25,4.37,"A herder has kept his cattle on the same semi-arid land"],[263,458.62,1.8,"for generations."],[263,460.46,3.34,"And livestock to pastoral families is not just their income,"],[263,463.83,2.54,"it's their savings, their insurance,"],[263,466.4,2.3,"the accumulated wealth of a lifetime."],[263,469.2,1.97,"But every few years, the rains fail."],[263,471.6,1.64,"And when they do,"],[263,473.27,1.47,"the grass dries up,"],[263,474.77,2.57,"the cattle weaken and they start to die."],[263,477.64,3.84,"So one bad drought can wipe out the economic foundation of a family"],[263,481.51,1.27,"in a matter of weeks."],[263,483.45,3.34,"But now, a new drought insurance program is coming together."],[263,487.38,2.94,"The satellite is able to read the health of the grass."],[263,490.35,2.74,"If that health falls below a critical threshold,"],[263,493.09,2.37,"an automatic payment is sent via mobile money"],[263,495.46,2.7,"to every pastoralist in the region affected"],[263,498.16,3.1,"so that they can go and buy supplemental food for their animals."],[263,501.76,3.04,"So the herder doesn't need to understand satellite imagery."],[263,504.83,2.27,"He doesn't even need to file a claim."],[263,508.17,2.7,"Satellite reads the grass, a number crosses a line,"],[263,510.87,1.77,"money appears on his phone,"],[263,512.64,3.44,"he moves his herd, he buys supplemental food,"],[263,516.11,3.54,"and the crisis that would have unfolded doesn't."],[263,520.58,3.67,"Over a million and a half herders across the Horn of Africa"],[263,524.29,2.74,"are now protected with this drought insurance program."],[263,531.73,1.4,"So this is what it looks like"],[263,533.13,3.74,"when you use a signal from space to guide human action"],[263,537.23,2.23,"in real time to changing events."],[263,539.97,3.74,"The eye detects change and the body moves to prevent harm."],[263,545.31,3.1,"And I think there's something deeper going on here."],[263,549.11,4.61,"Planetary intelligence doesn't just change what we do,"],[263,553.72,2.84,"but it changes how we know the world"],[263,556.59,1.53,"and our place within it."],[263,559.06,2.74,"It allows for us to see with the whole in mind."],[263,562.59,4.6,"And dissolves the false separation between our culture and our society"],[263,567.23,2.1,"and that of the larger living planet."],[263,570.63,2.0,"And we're living through a time right now"],[263,572.67,2.94,"where many of our institutions and our alliances"],[263,575.61,2.44,"are fracturing and falling apart."],[263,578.71,3.14,"And this is extremely painful"],[263,581.85,1.17,"because the problems"],[263,583.05,3.14,"that these institutions were built to address"],[263,586.22,1.67,"are only intensifying."],[263,588.22,2.5,"But I believe that planetary intelligence"],[263,590.72,3.0,"can help us transform our institutions."],[263,594.96,4.0,"Purpose-built for the speed and the complexity of the 21st century."],[263,600.1,3.34,"We can all build new products and institutions"],[263,603.43,2.94,"that allow for us to see change in real time."],[263,606.4,3.8,"And it can transform just about every sector of our society,"],[263,610.24,3.17,"from finance to infrastructure,"],[263,613.44,3.8,"decision-making and accountability mechanisms,"],[263,617.28,2.1,"all at the speed of change,"],[263,619.38,2.67,"in order for us to build a more resilient future"],[263,622.09,3.9,"so that the next time tanks amass at a border,"],[263,626.02,2.13,"there will be no pontoon bridge."],[263,629.53,3.6,"I started my career believing that information would save us."],[263,633.83,3.57,"I now know that only coordination actually will."],[263,638.67,2.04,"We gave the earth eyes."],[263,641.44,1.97,"We're connecting it to the brain."],[263,643.81,4.57,"But now, now the only thing left to do is to build the body,"],[263,648.41,3.8,"the trust, the alliances, the institutions,"],[263,652.25,2.97,"that is brave enough to move when the mind signals."],[263,656.32,1.24,"Thank you."],[263,664.49,3.04,"DS: Thank you so much for watching Robbie’s incredible TED Talk."],[263,667.56,2.17,"I'm Divya Siddharth, one of the guest curators"],[263,669.77,2.04,"for Robbie’s session this year at TED2026,"],[263,671.8,2.13,"along with the incredible Audrey Tang."],[263,674.5,2.1,"And I think the reason we were so excited"],[263,676.64,1.77,"to share Robbie's work with the world"],[263,678.44,2.84,"is because we think it's a huge part of what the world needs"],[263,681.28,2.7,"to collectively govern and understand our future with AI."],[263,684.01,3.07,"One of the reasons this is an important talk to have on stage now"],[263,687.08,2.7,"is information is power."],[263,689.82,2.17,"And the incredible thing that Planet has done"],[263,692.02,2.47,"is build a very powerful information source"],[263,694.52,3.74,"and make it, in as much as they can, available to everyone."],[263,698.29,2.07,"And so they not only create the information,"],[263,700.36,1.24,"which lots of people do,"],[263,701.63,2.1,"but they actually let people benefit from it."],[263,703.77,2.17,"And that's, in the age of AI, not always true."],[263,705.97,2.9,"Audrey Tang: When you see the Earth from the outer space,"],[263,708.9,1.57,"we become better people"],[263,710.51,5.0,"because we can then imagine that we're on this fragile spaceship."],[263,715.54,3.1,"And that really changed me to want to wage peace"],[263,718.68,3.0,"much more forcefully than we wage war."],[264,4.37,2.5,"There is a mahogany podium"],[264,6.87,2.94,"at the Supreme Court of the United States."],[264,10.47,2.5,"One person died there,"],[264,12.98,1.57,"mid-argument,"],[264,14.58,1.14,"a stroke."],[264,16.08,4.6,"Another collapsed there, dying soon thereafter."],[264,21.48,1.53,"That's the podium."],[264,23.45,2.9,"It also happens to be where I practice law."],[264,26.39,2.77,"The most powerful court on earth."],[264,29.19,3.34,"Nine minds ready to attack --"],[264,32.93,2.4,"and you stand 10 feet away from them."],[264,35.37,2.74,"There are no prepared speeches in this court."],[264,38.13,4.37,"Instead, 50 questions thrown at you in 30 minutes."],[264,42.54,3.87,"I'm making hundreds of decisions in real time."],[264,46.78,2.74,"Every argument I choose to make"],[264,49.55,1.33,"or not make,"],[264,50.91,3.77,"every word, every pause, every tone."],[264,55.55,1.5,"There are no rewinds."],[264,57.66,2.77,"Flinch and the justices pounce."],[264,60.89,1.47,"That's my courtroom."],[264,62.39,3.2,"But each of you has something like that."],[264,65.63,2.64,"A place in which words matter."],[264,68.3,2.04,"The right words can win"],[264,70.37,2.87,"and the wrong words [make a] huge difference."],[264,73.67,2.77,"Five months ago, I stood before that podium"],[264,76.47,2.7,"asking the Supreme Court to do something"],[264,79.21,2.67,"it had never done in its history:"],[264,81.88,6.11,"declare a president’s four-trillion-dollar signature initiative unconstitutional."],[264,94.02,2.04,"And I had a secret."],[264,97.03,2.04,"April 2, 2025."],[264,99.1,4.17,"The president dusts off a 1977 law"],[264,103.3,3.8,"and imposes tariffs on virtually every country on earth."],[264,107.47,1.87,"No congressional vote --"],[264,109.37,1.9,"nothing like that whatsoever --"],[264,111.31,1.47,"just his word."],[264,112.81,1.7,"And here’s what’s at stake:"],[264,114.55,5.54,"if the president can command the global economy by yelling emergency,"],[264,120.08,1.6,"what can't he do?"],[264,121.72,3.5,"Checks and balances don't just bend, they break."],[264,126.29,2.1,"I was hired to kill it."],[264,129.26,5.34,"Legal scholars, commentators [and] my own colleagues said it was impossible."],[264,135.1,5.14,"They said the president has nominated three of the justices on the court,"],[264,140.27,3.64,"and three others were appointed by Republican presidents."],[264,143.94,3.17,"They're not going to go against their president, they said."],[264,147.88,1.27,"I thought that was wrong."],[264,149.18,1.43,"But the real problem"],[264,150.61,2.9,"was that the Supreme Court never in its history,"],[264,153.55,2.17,"in 237 years,"],[264,155.75,4.87,"has declared a signature initiative of the president unconstitutional."],[264,161.29,5.0,"I was hired to do what no lawyer had done in 237 years."],[264,167.0,1.3,"My first thought?"],[264,168.33,1.3,"\"Hell, yes.\""],[264,171.07,1.53,"My second thought?"],[264,172.6,2.57,"“What in the world is wrong with me?”"],[264,175.21,1.9,"People have died at that podium,"],[264,177.14,3.37,"and I'm about to tell the world's most powerful man"],[264,180.54,2.67,"he can't do what he just did?\""],[264,184.05,4.2,"I had the self-preservation instincts of a moth near a bug zapper."],[264,192.09,3.67,"So for months, I prepared for the argument of my life."],[264,196.29,2.47,"Three weeks before that argument,"],[264,198.8,4.77,"one of my own teammates decided to try and take me down"],[264,203.6,2.3,"so that he could argue the case."],[264,205.9,4.24,"He campaigned, he lobbied, he made calls."],[264,211.38,2.6,"Just a few days before the argument,"],[264,214.01,1.67,"about two weeks,"],[264,215.71,3.37,"The Washington Post runs an editorial somehow,"],[264,219.08,2.74,"and I’m going to read this to you word for word:"],[264,221.85,2.0,"\"Strategic mistake.\""],[264,224.96,1.8,"I read it over breakfast."],[264,227.86,2.07,"Look, I don't begrudge the guy."],[264,229.96,2.44,"I mean, whatever."],[264,235.13,3.97,"I had more important things to do because I wasn't replaced."],[264,239.14,2.77,"Up I walked to that mahogany podium,"],[264,241.94,1.47,"and I won."],[264,243.41,2.74,"The President's tariffs declared unconstitutional."],[264,250.78,2.47,"OK, look, I know how this sounds."],[264,253.28,5.07,"Lawyer wins big case, gets a fancy TED talk invitation,"],[264,258.39,2.3,"talks for 14 minutes about how great he is."],[264,260.69,1.87,"I’ve seen that guy at dinner parties --"],[264,262.59,1.7,"nobody stays for dessert."],[264,264.83,2.44,"So that's not what this is."],[264,267.26,5.84,"This is the behind-the-scenes story of four teachers that helped me connect."],[264,273.84,4.34,"And it's also about one secret that I've never told anyone about"],[264,278.21,2.37,"when I walked out of that courtroom."],[264,280.61,2.47,"[The] first connection I needed was with myself."],[264,283.61,2.0,"I was terrified of blowing the case."],[264,285.65,3.1,"And that Washington Post editorial didn’t help matters."],[264,289.12,2.77,"A month before the argument, I met Ben."],[264,292.42,2.3,"Ben coaches sports legends,"],[264,294.76,2.84,"Andre Agassi, Olympians and the like."],[264,297.59,2.17,"His whole thing is about “game day.”"],[264,299.8,4.34,"That moment when everything you’ve been preparing for either shows up,"],[264,304.17,1.43,"or it doesn't."],[264,305.64,2.37,"Ben's first question to me:"],[264,308.04,1.6,"\"What are you afraid of?\""],[264,309.67,2.27,"Now look, at that point, I’d argued 52 cases."],[264,311.98,1.64,"I’d saved the Voting Rights Act."],[264,313.64,2.97,"I'd struck down the Guantanamo military tribunals."],[264,316.65,5.11,"But Ben forced me to admit a truth I'd buried from myself."],[264,322.09,2.57,"Every time I walked into the court,"],[264,324.69,3.64,"I looked at those portraits on the walls and thought:"],[264,328.36,1.83,"they don’t look like me,"],[264,330.23,1.57,"I don't belong here."],[264,332.36,3.44,"Imposter syndrome doesn't care about how many cases you won."],[264,335.83,2.3,"It cares about only your doubts."],[264,338.74,1.37,"Ben didn’t dismiss this."],[264,340.14,1.17,"He worked with it."],[264,341.34,3.47,"He had me write down five adjectives"],[264,344.84,3.64,"and visualize them every day before our pretend court."],[264,348.51,2.47,"About 18 hours before the argument,"],[264,351.01,3.34,"Ben calls and says, “How are you feeling?”"],[264,354.38,3.44,"And I say, \"Honestly, I'm terrified."],[264,357.82,3.07,"I've got to do a great job."],[264,360.92,3.14,"I've got to remember 500 things."],[264,364.09,3.24,"I've got to deliver an argument for history.\""],[264,367.36,4.8,"Ben says, “You know, change the vowel, use an ‘e’ instead of an ‘o.’”"],[264,372.2,1.74,"He says, \"What do you get to do?\""],[264,373.97,1.64,"And instantly it pours out of me:"],[264,375.64,2.7,"\"I get to defend the Constitution of the United States."],[264,378.34,2.4,"I get to, the son of immigrants,"],[264,380.78,2.3,"remind the country of what it's about."],[264,383.11,4.07,"I get to defend my parents' vision of America.\""],[264,393.49,1.44,"One letter."],[264,394.93,4.8,"The terror didn't disappear, but it transformed into joy."],[264,400.5,1.94,"So was Ben the secret,"],[264,402.47,3.74,"an elite sports coach, who teaches people about mindset?"],[264,406.5,1.17,"No."],[264,407.7,1.24,"But he got me ready."],[264,408.97,4.0,"The second thing I needed was connection to information at scale."],[264,413.01,3.74,"I assembled the most relentless legal team in the country."],[264,416.78,4.91,"They stress-tested every argument until only the best ones survived."],[264,422.22,1.33,"But I needed more."],[264,423.59,3.14,"I needed someone who was absolutely relentless."],[264,427.39,1.67,"I found Harvey."],[264,429.36,2.07,"Harvey reads the 200th tariff case"],[264,431.46,2.2,"the same way as he reads the first."],[264,436.1,1.27,"Shoot."],[264,437.37,2.57,"Honestly, this is my first time using PowerPoint."],[264,439.97,1.67,"I've given hundreds of speeches --"],[264,442.84,2.57,"I didn't want to use it,"],[264,445.44,1.2,"but they told me to."],[264,446.64,1.17,"So anyway --"],[264,449.01,2.1,"The picture's not coming up, but that's fine."],[264,451.15,1.4,"Don't worry about it."],[264,452.58,1.53,"So let's see."],[264,456.52,2.23,"OK, fine, good, we're good."],[264,458.75,1.84,"You know, a month before the argument,"],[264,460.62,4.04,"Harvey told me that I should expect a question from Justice Barrett"],[264,464.66,1.17,"about license fees."],[264,465.86,2.47,"And the yellow is what Harvey told me to predict,"],[264,468.33,3.14,"and blue is what Justice Barrett actually said at the argument."],[264,471.5,1.57,"It's almost verbatim."],[264,473.1,2.44,"So Harvey taught me peripheral vision:"],[264,475.57,1.83,"the idea [that] if you read a lot,"],[264,477.41,2.64,"you can see patterns and come up with stuff"],[264,480.04,3.6,"and anticipate the angles of attack before it arrived."],[264,483.68,4.77,"So was this secret a team of relentless lawyers who never slept,"],[264,488.49,2.14,"who pressure-tested everything?"],[264,490.65,2.0,"Closer, but that's not it either."],[264,492.69,2.14,"The third thing I needed was the hardest."],[264,494.86,2.34,"And it’s something we’ve been talking about today:"],[264,497.23,1.17,"connection."],[264,498.43,4.67,"Here, I needed to connect with nine very skeptical legal minds"],[264,503.13,1.87,"and to do so in real time."],[264,505.37,3.84,"Enter Liz, my improv coach."],[264,509.84,2.33,"What does improv have to do"],[264,512.21,2.87,"with the Supreme Court of the United States?"],[264,515.51,1.24,"Everything."],[264,517.31,2.6,"Liz's secret:"],[264,519.95,2.34,"“Neal, you need to actually listen."],[264,523.69,1.3,"Actually listen.”"],[264,525.32,1.83,"She taught me to quiet my own thoughts"],[264,527.19,3.24,"and to trust myself to come up with the words"],[264,530.46,2.44,"after the other person had spoken."],[264,532.93,2.07,"That’s the essence of “yes, and.”"],[264,535.03,3.14,"Absorb the question and then build on it."],[264,538.2,1.67,"When the justices attacked,"],[264,539.87,3.34,"I validated their concerns and then bridged back."],[264,543.54,2.27,"The interrogation became a dialogue."],[264,545.84,1.94,"The room's energy flipped."],[264,548.88,2.33,"(Audio) NKK: This power, as Justice Gorsuch said,"],[264,551.25,1.37,"as Justice Barrett said,"],[264,552.65,2.44,"is going to be stuck with us forever."],[264,555.12,2.17,"Justice Alito, I think you've said many times,"],[264,557.29,1.7,"the purpose isn't what you look at,"],[264,558.99,2.67,"you look to actually what the government is doing."],[264,561.66,1.44,"Thank you, Justice Kavanaugh."],[264,563.09,2.3,"So, five answers on the Nixon precedent."],[264,565.39,2.34,"Tariffs are constitutionally special"],[264,567.73,2.87,"because our Founders feared revenue raising,"],[264,570.6,1.33,"unlike embargoes."],[264,571.97,2.37,"There was no Boston Embargo Party,"],[264,574.34,2.4,"but there was certainly a Boston Tea Party."],[264,577.14,3.24,"Justice Sotomayor, I wish I had an hour to talk about this with you,"],[264,580.38,3.87,"because this argument by the government is wrong every which way."],[264,584.28,2.04,"Justice Alito: I wonder if you ever thought"],[264,586.35,2.6,"that your legacy as a constitutional advocate"],[264,588.99,5.14,"would be \"the man who revived the non-delegation argument?\""],[264,594.16,1.5,"NKK: Heck, yes, Justice Alito."],[264,595.66,3.57,"So was the secret an improv coach"],[264,599.26,2.8,"who taught a lawyer to “yes, and” the justices?"],[264,602.8,1.77,"That would be a hell of a TED talk."],[264,604.6,2.44,"But no, that's not it either."],[264,608.8,2.84,"Liz taught me the power of connection."],[264,612.14,2.07,"And the fourth teacher,"],[264,614.24,1.17,"the fourth teacher,"],[264,615.45,2.77,"the one who taught me the most important thing."],[264,618.25,2.8,"The thing we forget: to connect with yourself."],[264,621.65,2.57,"Enter Bob, my meditation coach."],[264,624.82,3.67,"Now I am just about the last person to meditate."],[264,628.52,3.6,"I thought meditation was for people who own crystals."],[264,632.13,1.83,"I do not own crystals."],[264,635.9,1.17,"But --"],[264,638.4,3.47,"Way before, way before the tariffs argument,"],[264,641.9,1.3,"I started working with Bob,"],[264,643.24,4.17,"and he had me, 20 minutes a day, focus on a single word."],[264,647.44,1.3,"He didn't send an app."],[264,648.78,2.97,"He actually rented an apartment a block from the court."],[264,651.78,4.2,"And we worked together every day, focusing on that word."],[264,656.49,4.04,"Bob didn't just give me a mantra, he gave me a weapon."],[264,661.09,4.04,"When I walked into court that day, the static was cleared."],[264,665.13,2.8,"I was calm. I was dangerous."],[264,668.6,3.24,"Was Bob the secret, the crystal-free meditation coach?"],[264,672.64,1.2,"No."],[264,674.87,1.24,"But close."],[264,676.14,4.04,"Because Bob, like Ben,"],[264,680.18,1.77,"like Liz,"],[264,681.98,1.17,"are human."],[264,684.31,2.5,"That fourth teacher"],[264,686.82,1.17,"is not."],[264,689.29,2.64,"Harvey is an AI."],[264,692.49,6.94,"A bespoke system I'd been building with a legal AI company for the last year."],[264,699.46,4.57,"And I trained it on every question asked by a Supreme Court justice"],[264,704.07,1.77,"in the last 25 years"],[264,705.87,3.5,"and everything they've written, every opinion, every concurrence,"],[264,709.37,3.4,"every dissent, every separate opinion."],[264,712.81,2.87,"And in that, patterns emerged."],[264,716.58,4.44,"It predicted the contours of the very argument I would face."],[264,721.42,5.41,"It knew that Justice Gorsuch would ask me about the taxing power."],[264,727.36,3.47,"It knew Justice Kavanaugh was going to grill me"],[264,730.86,2.07,"on tariffs versus embargoes."],[264,733.73,4.1,"It nailed Justice Barrett's worry about tariff refunds."],[264,738.5,1.74,"And the Chief Justice?"],[264,740.9,1.94,"It didn’t just predict his question,"],[264,742.87,2.5,"it predicted a possible escape route."],[264,745.41,3.0,"How the Chief Justice could vote for us"],[264,748.45,3.24,"and at the same time protect the institution"],[264,751.68,3.37,"he had spent his entire career defending."],[264,755.09,3.2,"Harvey glimpsed that narrow door,"],[264,758.32,2.04,"I held the door open,"],[264,760.39,2.24,"the Chief Justice walked through it,"],[264,762.66,2.1,"riding a six-to-three opinion,"],[264,764.79,1.5,"striking down the tariffs."],[264,766.93,3.5,"Harvey even predicted Justice Gorsuch's separate opinion,"],[264,770.47,2.9,"striking down the tariffs, almost verbatim."],[264,773.9,1.97,"Now I want to be precise about something."],[264,775.91,2.64,"I'm a lawyer, precision really matters."],[264,778.58,2.7,"What we were doing was not some trick."],[264,781.31,3.17,"We weren't pulling some fast one over on the court"],[264,784.51,2.24,"when we predicted these things."],[264,787.52,3.44,"Because predictability is what we want,"],[264,790.99,1.9,"especially in courts."],[264,793.36,3.87,"A justice who returns to the same principles"],[264,797.23,2.97,"case after case, year after year,"],[264,800.23,2.2,"is a justice with character."],[264,802.93,3.64,"Predictability is just consistency made visible."],[264,806.6,2.7,"It is, in every sense, a compliment."],[264,809.67,2.9,"What Harvey found in these justices was not weakness."],[264,813.08,1.33,"It was integrity."],[264,814.88,3.17,"But if I had just parroted Harvey's output,"],[264,818.08,1.9,"I would have lost the case 10-zero,"],[264,820.02,2.0,"and there aren’t even 10 justices."],[264,823.89,2.8,"Because AI has a shadow side."],[264,826.69,2.3,"When a tool is powerful,"],[264,829.02,2.17,"when a tool is powerful, you've all seen it,"],[264,831.23,1.53,"people stop thinking."],[264,832.8,1.77,"\"The computer says so.\""],[264,834.6,3.07,"Four words, human judgment ends,"],[264,837.67,2.74,"then people just fold like a cheap lawn chair."],[264,840.9,2.34,"The machine thinks, the human just nods,"],[264,843.27,2.57,"and in that nod somewhere, we disappear."],[264,845.84,1.64,"My legal team never nodded."],[264,847.48,2.34,"Harvey was not some god,"],[264,849.85,2.0,"it was our sparring partner --"],[264,851.88,3.4,"brilliant, tireless, occasionally insufferable --"],[264,855.32,1.33,"but not a god."],[264,856.69,1.64,"Harvey asked the questions,"],[264,858.36,1.57,"we found the answer[s]."],[264,860.36,2.44,"Now this is bigger than just law."],[264,862.83,1.2,"It's about all of us."],[264,864.06,4.0,"For centuries, the expert was the person who read the most,"],[264,868.1,2.1,"who remembered the most, who'd seen the most,"],[264,870.23,2.97,"the seasoned doctor, the experienced lawyer."],[264,873.24,2.44,"Their edge was accumulated knowledge."],[264,876.47,3.1,"AI is making that edge nearly worthless."],[264,881.08,2.37,"Not because humans no longer matter,"],[264,883.48,2.54,"but because that particular advantage,"],[264,886.05,3.9,"pattern recognition across vast data and breadth of knowledge,"],[264,889.95,2.34,"is now available to anyone."],[264,892.89,3.64,"AI can analyze, AI can predict."],[264,897.23,2.57,"But the one thing AI can't do"],[264,899.83,3.2,"is the thing that actually won that argument."],[264,903.43,1.27,"Connect."],[264,905.34,2.64,"That's the last irreplaceable human skill."],[264,908.0,3.3,"Persuade one person to change their mind"],[264,911.34,2.94,"by appealing to something beneath the surface."],[264,914.31,1.74,"Adjust not just the argument,"],[264,916.08,1.94,"but the delivery, the pause, the tone,"],[264,918.01,2.67,"the look that says, \"I hear you."],[264,920.72,1.57,"And here is my answer.\""],[264,923.52,2.0,"You know, at one moment in the argument,"],[264,925.55,4.34,"Justice Barrett asked a question that Harvey hadn't predicted."],[264,929.93,1.7,"And I remember it felt like"],[264,931.66,4.27,"she and I were the only two people in that marble and mahogany room."],[264,936.47,3.4,"And in the half-second before I answered,"],[264,939.9,2.4,"I did something no algorithm can do."],[264,942.97,1.13,"I looked at her."],[264,944.14,1.3,"I really looked."],[264,945.48,2.17,"I wanted to understand her worry."],[264,948.68,2.14,"And I answered the worry."],[264,951.78,3.27,"That lesson is true for all of us."],[264,955.09,2.47,"You don't just got to do it, you get to do it,"],[264,957.55,3.37,"in an interview, in a negotiation,"],[264,960.96,3.14,"in a conversation that could save a marriage or end one."],[264,964.46,3.0,"Any place in which you need to reach another human"],[264,967.46,1.7,"and actually connect."],[264,969.67,5.54,"The question AI poses to every one of us is not will you be replaced?"],[264,975.57,1.17,"The question is,"],[264,976.77,4.1,"what is the irreducibly human thing that you do?"],[264,981.75,1.3,"Go deeper into it."],[264,983.71,3.24,"Not to \"survive AI,\""],[264,986.98,2.0,"but to come home to yourself."],[264,989.45,1.87,"That's where your edge lives."],[264,991.89,3.57,"So Ben taught me to reframe,"],[264,995.49,2.37,"Harvey gave me foresight,"],[264,997.89,3.4,"Liz taught me to listen and Bob taught me stillness."],[264,1002.47,1.9,"Four teachers,"],[264,1004.4,2.0,"four connections,"],[264,1006.44,1.33,"one argument."],[264,1008.27,2.17,"An argument that some have called"],[264,1010.44,4.3,"the most important decision the Supreme Court has made in a century."],[264,1015.28,2.94,"When I walked into the court that day,"],[264,1018.25,5.17,"I never felt more like I was exactly where I was meant to be."],[264,1024.15,3.17,"I brought to the podium no mountain of legal notes,"],[264,1027.36,3.34,"just an email from Liz about the power of connection."],[264,1030.73,1.97,"And on the top of that,"],[264,1032.73,3.74,"in my own handwriting, scrawled my parents' names,"],[264,1036.5,3.14,"my children's names, my wife's name."],[264,1039.64,2.44,"The people I was fighting for."],[264,1042.97,2.84,"My father was my first audience."],[264,1047.04,2.9,"He didn't get to live to see this argument,"],[264,1049.98,3.47,"but as I walked out of the courtroom afterwards,"],[264,1053.48,1.8,"past those marble walls,"],[264,1055.32,3.77,"past the portraits of people who didn't look like me,"],[264,1059.12,2.64,"I got a text on my phone,"],[264,1061.79,1.74,"an email from Ben."],[264,1064.19,1.43,"\"So happy for you, Neal!"],[264,1066.2,2.8,"I think your dad was watching over you too.\""],[264,1070.53,2.4,"The newest technology,"],[264,1072.97,2.14,"the oldest human wisdom,"],[264,1075.11,1.77,"the most powerful court."],[264,1077.94,4.24,"I get to do that."],[265,0.08,3.12,"If we all had guts that did what guts are supposed to do,"],[265,3.2,1.76,"we wouldn’t be making this video."],[265,4.96,3.04,"Unfortunately, several of the people who make SciShow"],[265,8.0,3.92,"are all too aware that their guts could be doing a much better job."],[265,11.92,1.84,"And since you’re watching this video,"],[265,13.76,3.12,"you might also be a member of the Slacking Gut Club."],[265,16.88,2.8,"Membership comes with inflammatory bowel disease,"],[265,19.68,3.52,"or if you’re a top tier member, C. diff infections!"],[265,23.2,1.92,"But it might not be your gut’s fault."],[265,25.12,2.64,"This stuff often comes down to the tiny organisms"],[265,27.76,3.04,"that call your gut home: your gut microbiota."],[265,30.8,3.28,"Some people just have worse gut microbiota than others."],[265,34.08,1.52,"And if you’re one of those folks,"],[265,35.6,2.93,"here’s what researchers know about how to get a better one."],[265,42.07,2.65,"Welcome to the Slacking Gut Club orientation."],[265,44.72,3.92,"There are a lot of new names and …faces? …flagella? to get to know."],[265,48.64,4.72,"A healthy gut microbiota has up to 2,000 different species of bacteria,"],[265,53.36,2.32,"viruses, archaea, and fungi in it."],[265,55.68,4.0,"And the microbiome is the collective genes and products of all those guys."],[265,59.68,3.84,"Your gut microbiome helps maintain all sorts of important stuff in your body,"],[265,63.52,3.2,"like your metabolism, mucus barriers, and immune responses."],[265,66.72,2.56,"So if you’re lucky, you have thousands of different kinds of"],[265,69.28,4.24,"microorganisms inside your gut right now keeping everything running smoothly."],[265,73.52,2.56,"And luck is definitely a factor."],[265,76.08,2.64,"You’re not totally in control of who resides in your gut."],[265,78.72,3.12,"You get some of your microbiota from birth, some from your diet,"],[265,81.84,3.76,"and some from hospitalizations or conditions you pick up throughout the years."],[265,85.6,3.12,"Plus a ton of other factors that come with living."],[265,88.72,2.24,"So everybody’s gut microbiome is different."],[265,90.96,3.76,"But if you have one that puts you solidly in the Slacking Gut Club,"],[265,94.72,2.72,"you’d probably like to turn it into a better one."],[265,97.44,3.44,"And there are experts trying to help make that happen for you... and me."],[265,100.88,2.0,"The idea that’s gained the most traction is"],[265,102.88,2.48,"known as microbiota replacement therapy."],[265,105.36,3.92,"If you have medical concerns related to your sub-par gut microbiota,"],[265,109.28,2.24,"you can sample someone else’s healthier"],[265,111.52,2.64,"gut microbiota and take on its good qualities."],[265,114.16,2.0,"Technically you can do this in a few ways."],[265,116.16,1.84,"But some are more effective than others."],[265,118.0,4.96,"Like, you can try to seed a good gut microbiota with over-the-counter probiotics."],[265,122.96,2.4,"This is a pretty easy and accessible option."],[265,125.36,3.36,"But the downside is: data’s not really on your side."],[265,128.72,2.48,"So far, probiotics have not consistently been"],[265,131.2,2.32,"found to help much with your gut microbiome."],[265,133.52,2.08,"One problem is that we don’t know yet how to"],[265,135.6,2.72,"grow some gut microbes outside your body."],[265,138.32,3.12,"And if we can’t grow them, we can’t put them into a probiotic."],[265,141.44,3.44,"Also, there are only so many microbes in probiotics."],[265,144.88,2.72,"And generally, healthy guts have a ton of microbial"],[265,147.6,2.8,"diversity in them that you won’t get from that limited boost."],[265,150.4,4.0,"Which means probiotics might not be the quick fix you were hoping for."],[265,154.4,4.48,"Instead, you might find success with a fecal microbiota transplant."],[265,158.88,2.64,"That’s when you get a person to donate their poop,"],[265,161.52,2.4,"complete with gut microbiota galore."],[265,163.92,3.04,"Then, the professionals water down your donor poop and"],[265,166.96,3.92,"filter that mixture into a syringe to shoot it up your butt."],[265,170.88,4.56,"Not once. Not twice. But lots of times for weeks."],[265,175.44,4.0,"What’s a little sharing poop between friends with the help of some doctors?"],[265,179.44,0.72,"Am I right?"],[265,180.16,3.52,"And, I promise it’s not joke, it really is healing."],[265,183.68,2.8,"Depending on what problem you’re trying to solve with the transplant,"],[265,186.48,1.84,"the treatment can work wonders."],[265,188.32,2.88,"For example, a 2015 paper reported that patients"],[265,191.2,4.16,"with active ulcerative colitis received fecal microbiota transplants"],[265,195.36,2.48,"and achieved remission in a matter of weeks."],[265,197.84,3.12,"Those folks had significantly less inflammation down there,"],[265,200.96,3.04,"and some even had no inflammation by the end of the study."],[265,204.0,1.84,"Now, the effects might not last forever."],[265,205.84,3.84,"But they still can help you live a healthier, happier life for years."],[265,209.68,2.24,"And when the effect starts wearing off,"],[265,211.92,2.72,"you can get another transplant to prolong the benefits."],[265,214.64,3.04,"Just think about it like pen pals, but poop pals."],[265,217.68,3.04,"So I’m a science communicator and I’m used to talking about poop,"],[265,220.72,4.32,"and it can be off-putting to think about someone else’s poop inside of you."],[265,225.04,3.04,"But you have to give it to those scientific results!"],[265,228.08,3.04,"And there may be more palatable transplant options coming out."],[265,231.12,3.36,"Researchers are working on modifying this therapy into capsules,"],[265,234.48,2.48,"so you can swallow a pill instead of going through"],[265,236.96,2.56,"the whole “syringe full of poop water” ordeal."],[265,239.52,2.4,"The thing is, though, the ick factor isn’t the"],[265,241.92,2.64,"only thing keeping us all from perfect poops."],[265,244.56,3.6,"Unfortunately, not all fecal transplants are equally curative."],[265,248.16,3.44,"In that 2015 study, only 9 of the 38 patients"],[265,251.6,3.36,"who got a fecal microbiota transplant entered remission."],[265,254.96,2.88,"And the researchers have some ideas about why."],[265,257.84,2.0,"For one thing, timing might be a factor."],[265,259.84,2.8,"Three of the four patients who had just started reporting"],[265,262.64,3.28,"symptoms of ulcerative colitis entered remission after treatment."],[265,265.92,4.32,"And three quarters is a much higher success rate than the six out of 34 patients"],[265,270.24,3.12,"who achieved remission after longer term ulcerative colitis."],[265,273.36,3.68,"So if you get a fecal microbiota transplant in your first year of symptoms,"],[265,277.04,1.92,"you might have better chances of remission."],[265,278.96,2.08,"But that’s not the only factor here."],[265,281.04,2.48,"There’s also microbial diversity to consider."],[265,283.52,4.4,"Research suggests that the benefit you get from a fecal microbiota transplant"],[265,287.92,4.4,"may be dependent on getting a good donor with a really diverse microbiome."],[265,292.32,4.56,"Back to that 2015 study, of the nine patients who achieved remission,"],[265,296.88,1.68,"seven used the same donor."],[265,298.56,2.56,"I guess you could call this guy a super pooper!"],[265,301.12,2.32,"I mean, the scientists didn’t, but we could."],[265,303.44,2.88,"The researchers actually used the term super-donor,"],[265,306.32,3.36,"and these folks have particularly diverse gut microbiomes."],[265,309.68,2.64,"And it’s not like the rest of us, non super-doners,"],[265,312.32,3.68,"are walking around with only a few kinds of microscopic gut roommates."],[265,316.0,3.12,"One study found that stool donors who don’t produce responses"],[265,319.12,4.16,"in their recipients still have almost 800 species to brag about!"],[265,323.28,4.72,"It’s just that the donors who get remission results have more like 950 species."],[265,328.0,4.0,"And that extra diversity in donor microbes really seems to make a difference."],[265,332.0,4.08,"Now, if you’re trying to increase the microbial diversity in your gut for best results,"],[265,336.08,3.84,"you might consider combining multiple poops from different donors."],[265,339.92,1.28,"But when it comes to remission,"],[265,341.2,4.96,"a rag tag team of average poopers doesn’t compare to a single super donor."],[265,346.16,3.44,"Some experts are taking this super donor idea and running with it."],[265,349.6,2.48,"But before we get to what they’re doing with that super poop,"],[265,352.08,2.4,"it’s time for a quick ad."],[265,354.48,2.64,"Thanks to Zocdoc for sponsoring this SciShow video!"],[265,357.12,3.84,"Zocdoc is a free website where you can search and compare high quality,"],[265,360.96,3.6,"in-network doctors and click to instantly book an appointment."],[265,364.56,6.08,"With over 150,000 providers across more than 200 specialties in all 50 US states,"],[265,370.64,2.96,"you can book an appointment within 24 to 72 hours."],[265,373.6,2.64,"When the SciShow script writer on staff has medical needs,"],[265,376.24,1.6,"she goes to Zocdoc first."],[265,377.84,3.28,"She’s used them to find a new doctor when she moved to a new city."],[265,381.12,2.8,"Then again when her amazing doctor she found on Zocdoc"],[265,383.92,2.48,"switched practices and she needed to start over again."],[265,386.4,1.84,"And she used them for her husband too"],[265,388.24,2.0,"when he had a medical need crop up suddenly."],[265,390.24,2.24,"With Zocdoc, she’s been able to find professionals that"],[265,392.48,1.52,"specialize in the stuff she needs."],[265,394.0,2.72,"And make sure they’re in network with her insurance."],[265,396.72,3.44,"And book an appointment in a few minutes for a date that’s not a year out."],[265,400.16,2.56,"You don’t have to sit on the phone listening to the worst hold music"],[265,402.72,3.44,"you’ve ever heard or have a 20 minute long conversation with a bot."],[265,406.16,4.24,"Zocdoc lets you handle everything yourself on their free, straightforward website."],[265,410.4,3.92,"With a really short time commitment on Zocdoc, you can get the ball rolling today."],[265,414.32,1.84,"Taking care of your health just got easier."],[265,416.16,3.28,"Head to zocdoc.com/SCISHOW to get started"],[265,419.44,2.16,"and check that appointment off your to-do list."],[265,421.6,6.08,"Thanks again to Zocdoc for sponsoring today’s video!"],[265,427.68,2.0,"The way fecal donations work today,"],[265,429.68,3.2,"donors already need to be pre-screened to make sure they don’t have"],[265,432.88,3.12,"any nasty bugs in them that would cause more harm than good."],[265,436.0,2.08,"So that step could be expanded to screen"],[265,438.08,2.56,"for the presence of the most beneficial microbes."],[265,440.64,4.24,"In a perfect world, we could identify super donors and use the best poop"],[265,444.88,2.56,"for all fecal microbiota transplants."],[265,447.44,1.52,"We just need to know which species"],[265,448.96,2.8,"are good to have around and which should be avoided."],[265,451.76,2.88,"Researchers have identified a couple of kinds of bacteria"],[265,454.64,3.2,"that generally show up when patients don’t achieve remission."],[265,457.84,2.0,"Some of the microbes you’d want to keep around"],[265,459.84,3.28,"are classified as Clostridium cluster IV bacteria."],[265,463.12,4.8,"These bacteria, associated with prolonged clinical remission from inflammatory"],[265,467.92,3.76,"bowel disease, are known for their work supporting your immune system."],[265,471.68,2.16,"So they’re top notch microbes."],[265,473.84,2.56,"But there are a lot of them in that giant category."],[265,476.4,4.4,"And if you were searching for one keystone gut species that you really want in your"],[265,480.8,4.08,"donor stool, that species would depend on what problem you’re trying to solve."],[265,484.88,4.32,"For example, if you’re getting a fecal microbiota transplant because of your IBS,"],[265,489.2,2.8,"maybe you’d want to place your bets on Bifidobacterium."],[265,492.0,3.36,"It can be tempting to work toward highly personalized therapies"],[265,495.36,2.08,"that focus on specific microbes."],[265,497.44,3.28,"But not all experts are on board with that idea because some say"],[265,500.72,4.32,"species richness is more important than any specific species being transferred."],[265,505.04,4.08,"If we do make our way toward more personalized fecal microbiota transplants,"],[265,509.12,3.36,"maybe it would look less like worrying about which species are transplanted"],[265,512.48,3.2,"and more like ensuring that the donors and recipients are compatible."],[265,515.68,3.12,"Your gut microbiota influences your immune system."],[265,518.8,2.72,"And your immune system influences your gut microbiota."],[265,521.52,3.12,"And if your immune system mounts a supercharged response"],[265,524.64,3.92,"to the transplanted microbes, you may just be incompatible with that donor."],[265,528.56,3.28,"Luckily, there are other ways to modify your gut microbiota"],[265,531.84,1.6,"that don’t involve transplants."],[265,533.44,2.16,"You can totally change your gut microbiota"],[265,535.6,2.88,"within 24 hours by changing what you eat."],[265,538.48,2.16,"In a study published in the journal “Nature,”"],[265,540.64,3.52,"volunteers began eating either entirely animal based foods,"],[265,544.16,3.6,"with the exception of coffee, or entirely plant based foods."],[265,547.76,2.32,"None of them had been following those diets beforehand"],[265,550.08,3.12,"and they all measurably changed what they ate during the study."],[265,553.2,4.0,"People in the animal group added fat and protein to their overall food intake."],[265,557.2,2.16,"And people in the plant group added fiber."],[265,559.36,3.36,"After just five days, their gut microbes started resembling"],[265,562.72,3.04,"what you’d see in carnivores and herbivores."],[265,565.76,4.32,"Not just by increasing or decreasing the proportions of microbes they already had,"],[265,570.08,2.4,"but also by introducing totally new ones."],[265,572.48,2.48,"The animal based diet had a bigger effect on"],[265,574.96,3.76,"how many kinds of microbes increased or decreased in abundance."],[265,578.72,2.64,"But a lot of change isn’t necessarily good."],[265,581.36,2.32,"For example, there’s one genus that happens to be"],[265,583.68,2.56,"associated with remission of Crohn’s disease."],[265,586.24,3.44,"And for at least one of the animal eaters, the genus decreased."],[265,589.68,4.0,"So an animal based diet might be lacking some positive gut microbes."],[265,593.68,2.56,"And what’s worse, it might promote some negative ones."],[265,596.24,2.0,"People who ate the animal based diet had"],[265,598.24,3.12,"more microbes associated with inflammatory bowel disease."],[265,601.36,3.44,"So please talk to your doctor before making any drastic changes."],[265,604.8,2.72,"But this data suggests you might lean on plants"],[265,607.52,2.4,"to help create a nice environment in your gut."],[265,609.92,3.6,"And an important caveat is that the changes to your gut microbiota"],[265,613.52,2.32,"go away almost as fast as they arrive."],[265,615.84,3.2,"So you do have to keep up these diets to maintain the effects."],[265,619.04,3.84,"And if changing your diet is impractical or impossible for you,"],[265,622.88,2.0,"there’s still another trick you can try."],[265,624.88,3.36,"Exercise has also been shown to quickly change your gut microbiota."],[265,628.24,4.48,"In studies, excercise has changed the composition of gut microbiota in rodents"],[265,632.72,2.8,"and increased its diversity in professional athletes."],[265,635.52,4.88,"But I’m assuming that you, like me, are not a rodent or a professional athlete."],[265,640.4,2.24,"So here’s some research on amateur runners"],[265,642.64,3.12,"that might be more relevant to your life, my life, you know:"],[265,645.76,3.28,"The study, published in the journal “Frontiers in Microbiology,”"],[265,649.04,2.88,"found that running just one half marathon changed"],[265,651.92,3.28,"the composition of gut microbiota in the people who participated."],[265,655.2,3.76,"That’s based on samples taken before and after the race."],[265,658.96,2.4,"So literally one day made a difference."],[265,661.36,4.56,"As for which microbes changed, one example is an increase in a type of bacteria"],[265,665.92,2.72,"that helps your cells perform their basic functions,"],[265,668.64,3.28,"like signaling, storing energy, and maintaining their membranes."],[265,671.92,2.4,"As if you needed another reason to exercise."],[265,674.32,3.92,"But in that study, they didn’t separate the effects of diet and exercise."],[265,678.24,3.68,"So that half marathon alone didn’t necessarily cause all of those changes."],[265,681.92,4.96,"Remember, your gut microbiome is unique to you just like mine is unique to me."],[265,686.88,1.76,"So you might have to go through a period of"],[265,688.64,3.12,"bouncing ideas around with your doctor and trying things out."],[265,691.76,4.88,"Whether it’s a new diet, new exercise, or finding a compatible super donor for"],[265,696.64,2.56,"your next fecal microbiota transplant,"],[265,699.2,2.8,"there are a lot of ways to take care of your gut."],[265,702.0,4.96,"As the super donor researchers say, it’s not a “one stool to rule them all” approach."],[266,0.24,3.36,"This charming little creature is Tullimonstrum gregarium,"],[266,3.6,1.84,"but its friends call it the Tully Monster."],[266,5.44,4.16,"It’s an extinct species that lived along an ancient coastline in what is now Illinois,"],[266,9.6,1.84,"more than 300 million years ago."],[266,11.44,3.68,"At first glance, you might think it’s some sort of strange fish with stalk eyes,"],[266,15.12,2.4,"or maybe a slug with a claw on its face,"],[266,17.52,6.16,"or maybe even some kind of odd ocean-dwelling worm, but what it really is … we have no idea."],[266,23.68,0.83,"No, really."],[266,24.51,5.17,"Over the past 70 years, paleontologists have found literally thousands of Tully Monster fossils,"],[266,29.68,4.96,"and they’ve analyzed them over and over using the most sophisticated techniques available,"],[266,34.64,2.88,"and the more we learn, the more confusing this animal is."],[266,37.52,2.32,"So, let’s follow in these scientists’ footsteps"],[266,39.84,5.02,"and explore exactly what it takes to stump multiple generations of paleontologists."],[266,48.16,3.6,"Ever since the mid-1800s, folks in Illinois have reported finding"],[266,51.76,3.12,"well-preserved fossils in and around local coal beds."],[266,54.88,5.12,"Starting in the early 1900s, these beds became the focus of extensive coal mining operations,"],[266,60.0,3.12,"which uncovered even more of these fossils, and over time,"],[266,63.12,2.72,"an incredible ancient ecosystem came into view."],[266,65.84,4.24,"Nowadays, these rock layers are known as the Mazon Creek fossil beds, and they date"],[266,70.08,4.0,"to a little over 300 million years ago, during the Late Carboniferous Period."],[266,74.08,2.64,"At that time, Illinois was just south of the equator,"],[266,76.72,2.72,"and it was a tropical paradise for all sorts of life."],[266,79.44,4.16,"The fossils of Mazon Creek tell the story of an ancient river delta system."],[266,83.6,5.28,"From swamp and shoreline deposits, paleontologists have identified the remains of horsetails,"],[266,88.88,4.56,"early trees, insects, amphibians, and some of the earliest known reptiles."],[266,93.44,3.36,"And in the adjacent ocean deposits, there are fossils of jellyfish,"],[266,96.8,4.48,"crustaceans, and lots of fish, including sharks, lampreys, hagfish, and more."],[266,101.28,3.04,"Mazon Creek is an incredibly rich fossil locality."],[266,104.32,4.96,"Hundreds of thousands of fossils have been found, representing some 700 ancient species."],[266,109.28,3.36,"Most of these fossils are found hidden away in iron-rich nodules,"],[266,112.64,4.4,"which split open to reveal incredibly well-preserved fossils, often including"],[266,117.04,3.2,"the remains of soft tissues like internal organs and eyeballs."],[266,120.24,5.04,"In 1958, a fossil collector named Francis J. Tully brought a very unusual"],[266,125.28,4.8,"Mazon Creek fossil animal to the attention of paleontologists at the Field Museum in Chicago."],[266,130.08,5.12,"These animals were small, ranging in size from around 6 centimeters to over 30 centimeters long,"],[266,135.2,4.72,"with a squishy torpedo-shaped body composed of many segments like a worm or a centipede."],[266,139.92,3.44,"On its tail are broad, triangular flaps that look like tail fins."],[266,143.36,3.12,"Just behind its head is a straight structure that cuts across the body"],[266,146.48,3.76,"and seems to support a pair of round organs on either end – this is often"],[266,150.24,4.48,"called the “transverse bar.” And on its face is a long, spiky proboscis."],[266,154.72,3.84,"Now you might be thinking, that sounds like a really strange creature to me,"],[266,158.56,4.16,"but surely Illinois paleontologists had some idea what sort of critter this was."],[266,162.72,1.12,"And you’d be wrong."],[266,163.84,3.52,"Baffled by this bizarre species, local paleontologists and fossil"],[266,167.36,3.84,"hunters took to calling it “Tully’s monster,” or “The Tully Monster.”"],[266,171.2,4.96,"Eventually, in 1989, the Tully Monster would be named the official state fossil of Illinois."],[266,176.16,4.08,"In 1966, the Tully Monster appeared in a scientific publication written"],[266,180.24,4.56,"by E.S. Richardson Jr, who described the fossils in all their unusual glory,"],[266,184.8,4.88,"and who gave the species an official scientific name: Tullimonstrum gregarium."],[266,189.68,3.92,"But Richardson mostly side-steps the question of the Tully Monster’s identity,"],[266,193.6,4.48,"simply calling it “wormlike,” and leaving the details to be argued by other scientists."],[266,198.08,1.44,"Like a coward."],[266,199.52,1.52,"And argue they did!"],[266,201.04,4.4,"Paleontologists have been debating the true identity of the Tully Monster ever since."],[266,205.44,3.2,"We’ll get to why it’s so hard after this short break!"],[266,209.28,2.16,"This SciShow video is supported by DeleteMe,"],[266,211.44,3.2,"the subscription service that provides set it and forget it peace of mind."],[266,214.64,4.08,"When you sign up, their privacy experts reduce your risk of phishing,"],[266,218.72,2.8,"doxing, swatting, and harassment."],[266,221.52,4.48,"They’ll continuously remove your data using their own tech and no third parties."],[266,226.0,3.92,"Or, if you set it and don’t forget it, DeleteMe makes real human"],[266,229.92,3.84,"privacy advisors available for custom requests and help when you need it."],[266,233.76,4.08,"This service has been used by judges, journalists, and our executive producer!"],[266,237.84,5.2,"They reviewed over 1,000 records for her and saved her 34 hours of reviewing and removing listings."],[266,243.04,6.8,"You can get 20% off DeleteMe consumer plans when you go to joindeleteme.com/SCISHOW and"],[266,249.84,4.79,"use promo code SCISHOW at checkout, or use the QR code on screen."],[266,255.2,3.76,"Believe it or not, identifying fossil animals is often pretty straightforward."],[266,258.96,1.44,"You just have to find the distinguishing"],[266,260.4,3.04,"characteristics of whatever group of life that animal belongs to."],[266,263.44,3.76,"When paleontologists can’t identify an ancient animal, it’s usually because the"],[266,267.2,4.72,"fossils aren’t preserved very well, and those distinguishing features are missing or obscured."],[266,271.92,4.24,"There are many, many such cases, but the Tully Monster isn’t one of them."],[266,276.16,2.48,"We know a ton about this species’ anatomy."],[266,278.64,2.56,"Because Thousands of Tully Monster fossils have been found,"],[266,281.2,2.08,"and lots of them are really well-preserved."],[266,283.28,4.24,"Scientists have been able to deduce that the transverse bar is a pair of stalked eyes,"],[266,287.52,4.72,"and that its long proboscis was flexible and ended in a toothy, mouth-like structure."],[266,292.24,2.16,"They’ve even observed its internal organs!"],[266,294.4,2.16,"With all this information to work with,"],[266,296.56,4.16,"you’d think figuring out the Tully Monster’s evolutionary relationships would be easy."],[266,300.72,4.64,"But the problem is, the features we can identify just don’t make sense together."],[266,305.36,1.92,"There’s no one group they all fit with."],[266,307.28,4.56,"It’s clearly an animal, and it has bilateral symmetry, meaning its left and right half"],[266,311.84,4.16,"are a mirror image – just like us and insects and worms and lots of others."],[266,316.0,2.56,"This narrows things down a bit, but not much."],[266,318.56,4.16,"Some scientists have suggested the Tully Monster might be a relative of a group called ribbon"],[266,322.72,5.36,"worms, also known as proboscis worms, which – as you might have guessed – have a proboscis!"],[266,328.08,3.28,"But these worms don’t have the same shape of proboscis that the"],[266,331.36,3.12,"Tully Monster does … also, they don’t have eyes."],[266,334.48,2.8,"Others have compared it with polychaetes, or bristle worms,"],[266,337.28,3.52,"which also have a proboscis and they have eyes, but the Tully"],[266,340.8,4.48,"Monster doesn’t have the bristly appendages or the distinctly-shaped head of these worms."],[266,345.28,3.52,"Some have pointed out that the Tully Monster’s proboscis looks similar to"],[266,348.8,4.4,"the toothy, tongue-like radula of molluscs like octopus and snails,"],[266,353.2,3.84,"but the Tully Monster is missing a lot of the internal organs we see in molluscs."],[266,357.04,4.08,"Still others have compared the Tully Monster to the bizarre animals of the Cambrian Period,"],[266,361.12,4.48,"like the stalk-eyed, claw-faced Opabinia, which are thought to be cousins of arthropods."],[266,365.6,5.52,"But these animals are separated in time from the Tully Monster by around 200 million years."],[266,371.12,1.92,"Somewhere within that squishy body are the"],[266,373.04,2.8,"features that link the Tully Monster to its true cousins,"],[266,375.84,4.88,"but paleontologists have had a really hard time figuring out exactly which features those are."],[266,380.72,4.8,"The reality is that the Tully Monster doesn’t look very much like any other known animals."],[266,385.52,4.4,"Over the course of its evolution, it’s either lost some key features of its ancestors,"],[266,389.92,4.72,"or it’s evolved new features that are similar to those of its distant relatives … or both!"],[266,394.64,3.92,"The end result is an animal that has become very different from its closest cousins."],[266,398.56,3.76,"For example, one author in 2025 suggested a possible relationship"],[266,402.32,2.32,"with a group of mollusks called sea elephants."],[266,404.64,4.56,"These are a type of sea slug with long, round bodies, a flexible proboscis,"],[266,409.2,3.68,"and a pair of well-developed eyes – all pretty similar to the Tully Monster."],[266,412.88,3.76,"But the Tully Monster is missing some of the iconic features of sea elephants,"],[266,416.64,2.72,"such as the swimming fin and stabilizing organs."],[266,419.36,4.16,"So if it is a sea slug, it must have evolved away those features at some point."],[266,423.52,4.56,"And if it isn’t a sea slug, it’s evolved a bunch of sea-slug-like features which"],[266,428.08,2.96,"were probably used for sea-slug-like behaviors."],[266,431.04,1.76,"That’s what makes this so tricky."],[266,432.8,3.36,"You have to figure out which features come from its ancestors in its group,"],[266,436.16,1.44,"and which happened along later."],[266,437.6,4.56,"As the decades have passed, paleontologists have taken a closer and closer look at the Tully"],[266,442.16,4.88,"Monster – down to the molecular scale – trying to find the clues to finally solve this puzzle."],[266,447.04,4.32,"And in 2016, it seemed for a moment that the answer had finally been found."],[266,451.36,5.52,"That year, two separate studies found evidence to suggest that the Tully Monster … was a fish!"],[266,456.88,3.76,"The first of those studies took a scanning electron microscope to the Tully Monster’s"],[266,460.64,4.96,"eyes and found that they were made up of layers of tiny structures called melanosomes."],[266,465.6,4.32,"This structure, they pointed out, was only known from one group: vertebrates."],[266,469.92,3.52,"The other study examined over a thousand Tully Monster specimens,"],[266,473.44,2.88,"paying close attention to the structure of its various features."],[266,476.32,4.88,"Among their findings was that one of its internal structures, a long, narrow feature which had been"],[266,481.2,3.84,"previously identified as part of the guts, was actually the remains of a notochord."],[266,485.04,3.84,"A notochord is a rod of tissue that runs down the back of chordate animals,"],[266,488.88,2.72,"a group that includes vertebrates and their closest relatives."],[266,491.6,3.76,"That study also identified a handful of other vertebrate features, including angled"],[266,495.36,4.4,"blocks of muscle tissue, gill slits, and vertebrate-like teeth on that proboscis."],[266,499.76,3.28,"With this collection of anatomical features, they suggested that the"],[266,503.04,4.56,"Tully Monster belongs to an early branch of aquatic vertebrates – that is, fish!"],[266,507.6,4.16,"More specifically, they proposed it was a jawless fish closely related to lampreys."],[266,511.76,3.92,"That team even ran a statistical comparison between the features of a bunch of different"],[266,515.68,4.08,"fossil critters, and the numbers grouped the Tully Monster with lamprey-like fish."],[266,519.76,2.88,"Now, you might be wondering how all those distinguishing features could"],[266,522.64,4.4,"have gone unnoticed for so long, and the researchers wondered the same thing."],[266,527.04,2.88,"They pointed out that fossilization might have been getting in the way."],[266,529.92,4.48,"Some of these features had been degraded so much that they had become unrecognizable."],[266,534.4,3.28,"And the Tully Monster’s tail – which is flattened top-to-bottom instead"],[266,537.68,4.16,"of side-to-side like in fish – could have become twisted during the burial process,"],[266,541.84,2.56,"giving it a falsely un-fish-like appearance."],[266,544.4,4.32,"And so, 2016 was the year that the Tully Monster was officially identified as a fish."],[266,548.72,1.92,"It was a very neat and satisfying solution"],[266,550.64,4.32,"to this long-standing mystery … perhaps too neat and satisfying."],[266,554.96,4.64,"In the years that followed, numerous other studies came out which cast doubt on … well,"],[266,559.6,1.84,"just about all of those findings."],[266,561.44,1.76,"Some paleontologists pointed out that those"],[266,563.2,2.64,"vertebrate features couldn’t have been vertebrate features."],[266,565.84,3.68,"The supposed notochord extends in front of the Tully Monster’s eyes,"],[266,569.52,1.92,"which doesn’t happen in vertebrates."],[266,571.44,2.32,"The supposed gill slits are too widely spaced and"],[266,573.76,3.12,"some are present in the wrong parts of the body, like near the tail."],[266,576.88,2.08,"And those alleged muscle segments don’t run"],[266,578.96,2.48,"the full length of the body like they do in vertebrates."],[266,581.44,4.16,"And on top of all that, there are lots of fish preserved in Mazon Creek, including jawless"],[266,585.6,4.8,"fish like lampreys and hagfish – and none of them ever preserve those anatomical features."],[266,590.4,2.8,"They had those features, but we can’t see them."],[266,593.2,3.36,"So, either the Tully Monster had these iconic vertebrate features,"],[266,596.56,2.88,"but they were all unprecedented shapes and fossilized uniquely"],[266,599.44,4.4,"well for some reason … or those are not iconic vertebrate features after all."],[266,603.84,3.2,"That same study also pointed out some problems with the statistical"],[266,607.04,2.72,"comparison that linked the Tully Monster to fish."],[266,609.76,3.6,"See, a statistical analysis is only as good as the data it uses."],[266,613.36,2.24,"And some of that data had apparently been based"],[266,615.6,3.2,"on outdated interpretations of some of the Mazon Creek fossils."],[266,618.8,4.32,"That analysis also barely included any invertebrate animals, so the only things"],[266,623.12,4.32,"the Tully Monster could be compared to were vertebrates and their closest relatives."],[266,627.44,4.64,"So, jawless fish might have been the best available match, but the options were limited."],[266,632.08,4.48,"A few years later, a 2019 study revisited the topic of the Tully Monster’s eyes."],[266,636.56,2.72,"As I said, the 2016 study found that the eye"],[266,639.28,3.36,"structure of the Tully Monster was a lot like vertebrate eyes."],[266,642.64,2.0,"But it turns out not much research had been done"],[266,644.64,3.6,"on the eye structures of other animals, like squid or octopus."],[266,648.24,4.8,"The 2019 study found that these animals also have eyes composed of melanosome"],[266,653.04,3.92,"layers, meaning this is not a sure-fire identifier of a vertebrate animal."],[266,656.96,4.08,"They also did a spectroscopic analysis on the Tully Monster’s eyes to determine"],[266,661.04,4.0,"their chemical composition, and its chemistry didn’t match fish or octopus!"],[266,665.04,4.16,"And so, just a few years after it seemed the mystery had been solved, a whole pile"],[266,669.2,4.72,"of scientific evidence had accumulated to say that the Tully Monster wasn’t in fact, a fish."],[266,673.92,4.96,"But then in 2020, another team of researchers did their own chemical analysis on the various"],[266,678.88,4.88,"fossilized tissues of the Tully Monster and a whole bunch of other Mazon Creek fossil animals."],[266,683.76,4.08,"They found that these chemical signals could reliably distinguish between invertebrate and"],[266,687.84,4.08,"vertebrate animals, and the Tully Monster had the chemistry of a vertebrate animal!"],[266,691.92,2.48,"And just like that, vertebrates rejoined the long"],[266,694.4,3.44,"list of potential suspects for the Tully Monster’s identity."],[266,697.84,4.16,"It has some features that seem to say vertebrate, and others that seem to say absolutely not."],[266,702.0,2.88,"The Tully Monster is either a very unusual fish,"],[266,704.88,3.44,"or an invertebrate that evolved some very fish-like features."],[266,708.32,1.28,"And we still don’t know which."],[266,709.6,3.76,"The story of the Tully Monster is an incredible tale of scientific mystery."],[266,713.36,4.56,"But more than that, it’s a beautiful demonstration of how paleontologists do what they do."],[266,717.92,3.84,"Sorting through the tangle of debates and hypotheses reveals a whole bunch of the"],[266,721.76,3.28,"different tools and techniques that scientists use to identify fossils,"],[266,725.04,2.8,"as well as the shortcomings and potential flaws of those methods."],[266,727.84,4.24,"The Tully Monster forces us to look past the question of how you can identify a fossil,"],[266,732.08,4.64,"to the much more interesting question of why there are some fossils we can’t seem to figure out."],[266,736.72,2.72,"Someday, surely, paleontologists will find the"],[266,739.44,2.4,"right combination of clues to put this debate to rest."],[266,741.84,2.24,"And as much as that will be a day for celebration,"],[266,744.08,3.52,"it will be bittersweet to see the end of this fascinating scientific discussion."],[266,747.6,3.62,"Fortunately, there are plenty of other mystery fossils out there."],[267,8.6,5.24,"I've been working on the climate issue for almost 30 years."],[267,14.64,1.56,"For most of that time,"],[267,16.24,4.12,"I worked with the Japanese climate organization called Kiko Network."],[267,21.04,3.8,"Of course, it's been filled with ups and downs,"],[267,24.88,4.4,"but today I would like to share some of the moments when we succeeded."],[267,30.2,5.32,"One turning point came after the Fukushima nuclear disaster in 2011."],[267,36.0,4.8,"All of Japan's nuclear reactors shut down all of a sudden,"],[267,40.84,3.68,"and the country's energy system stood at a critical crossroads."],[267,45.24,2.64,"While people were waking to nuclear risks,"],[267,47.92,2.92,"attention to climate change faded in return."],[267,51.4,1.48,"Then, soon after,"],[267,52.92,4.0,"new plans to develop coal-fired power plants began to surge."],[267,57.4,1.84,"The number went up to 10,"],[267,59.24,2.0,"20 and 50."],[267,61.84,1.8,"That was a really scary trend"],[267,63.68,3.0,"considering the long-term CO2 emissions' impact."],[267,67.6,1.16,"At that time,"],[267,68.8,3.12,"I'd been focusing on the UN climate negotiations"],[267,71.96,1.84,"and national climate policy."],[267,74.28,3.04,"But I turned my focus to work on coal."],[267,79.2,1.4,"Why coal?"],[267,80.96,2.8,"Even people close to me ask this question."],[267,84.6,4.72,"Despite the fact that more than 150 units of coal-fired power plants"],[267,89.36,2.28,"are in operation in Japan,"],[267,91.64,4.12,"that fact was just unknown and invisible to people."],[267,96.44,1.72,"Kind of like CO2."],[267,98.56,3.16,"So we started to create a nationwide map"],[267,101.76,3.64,"to track every coal project and made it public."],[267,106.08,5.12,"Also, we visited communities where projects are located"],[267,111.24,1.76,"and spoke with people."],[267,113.04,1.56,"But on many occasions,"],[267,114.6,2.32,"people didn't care much about the climate"],[267,116.96,1.84,"and new coal projects."],[267,118.84,3.08,"People cared more about their daily issues"],[267,121.92,1.96,"such as jobs,"],[267,123.92,2.44,"income or child care."],[267,127.28,2.68,"So we talked not only about climate change,"],[267,129.96,3.36,"but also about economic and financial risks"],[267,133.36,2.64,"and health impacts from air pollution."],[267,137.28,2.92,"Everything I tried was new to me."],[267,140.72,5.16,"So I reached out to experts, lawyers, professors and NGOs,"],[267,145.92,2.4,"both in Japan and abroad."],[267,148.32,2.48,"I didn't hesitate to ask for help."],[267,151.64,3.24,"And eventually, we developed networks."],[267,155.8,3.56,"With a combination of citizens’ engagement,"],[267,159.36,3.28,"data tracking and strategic communication,"],[267,162.64,2.56,"gradually people started to act."],[267,166.96,2.96,"In 2020, I took another action."],[267,170.36,3.56,"We submitted a shareholder proposal to Mizuho,"],[267,173.96,1.84,"one of Japan's largest banks"],[267,175.8,3.56,"and the biggest coal financier globally at that time."],[267,180.16,3.84,"We asked the company to disclose its business plan,"],[267,184.0,2.08,"aligned with the Paris Agreement."],[267,186.4,3.6,"That means there is no room to finance new coal."],[267,191.44,1.52,"In Western countries,"],[267,192.96,4.36,"shareholder activism on climate had already gained momentum,"],[267,197.36,3.84,"but in Japan it was the very first of its kind."],[267,201.84,3.96,"I spent months studying best practices overseas."],[267,206.68,4.24,"I still remember the conversation I had with my friend in Australia"],[267,210.96,2.32,"on the day before the shareholder meeting."],[267,213.64,1.4,"He said,"],[267,215.04,4.72,"\"You will probably get 17 percent support, so I'm betting.\""],[267,219.8,1.88,"And I said, \"Oh no,"],[267,221.72,3.92,"if we get 10 percent, that will already be a miracle.\""],[267,226.88,1.36,"To my surprise,"],[267,228.24,3.88,"the proposal received 34 percent support from shareholders."],[267,234.52,3.44,"Including large institutional investors."],[267,237.96,3.16,"That became quite big news across the country."],[267,242.12,3.2,"As a result of those multiple works,"],[267,245.36,2.76,"out of 50 proposed coal projects,"],[267,248.12,3.96,"17, totaling nine gigawatts, were cancelled."],[267,255.52,5.04,"This cancellation prevented 50 million tons of CO2 per year"],[267,260.6,3.84,"and 1.7 billion tons over their lifetimes."],[267,264.48,4.24,"That's equivalent to taking more than eight million cars off the road"],[267,268.72,2.04,"every year for 40 years."],[267,275.8,2.64,"Of course, the success wasn't ours alone."],[267,278.48,1.8,"Many people took action, too."],[267,280.88,2.12,"But many cancelled projects"],[267,283.04,3.4,"overlapped with regions where we worked intensively."],[267,287.04,4.4,"I can tell that our collaborative efforts worked."],[267,292.64,4.44,"Civil society in Japan is often considered small and powerless."],[267,297.64,2.04,"But even as a small group,"],[267,299.68,3.8,"what seems impossible is not necessarily impossible."],[267,304.12,1.28,"That's what I learned."],[267,307.04,2.92,"But this is not the end of my journey."],[267,310.0,4.04,"Stopping one third of new coal projects is just one step."],[267,314.56,3.48,"We still heavily depend on fossil fuels in Japan."],[267,318.88,2.04,"The real challenge for us now"],[267,320.96,4.84,"is to transform our entire economy away from fossil fuels."],[267,326.28,2.64,"That means saying no isn't enough."],[267,329.44,5.28,"We need to build a positive, strong yes for systemic change."],[267,335.56,5.64,"But we still face various arguments such as: renewable energy is expensive,"],[267,341.24,1.32,"it's not reliable."],[267,342.56,3.12,"Those are very live discussions still in Japan."],[267,345.72,3.52,"And some people in Japan oppose renewable energy"],[267,349.28,3.12,"because of concerns about local environmental damage."],[267,352.88,5.28,"In addition, many people prioritize other issues than climate,"],[267,358.16,1.68,"and people are busy too."],[267,360.84,3.08,"We need to remove all of these barriers."],[267,364.68,1.64,"So I took another step."],[267,366.8,4.6,"I founded an independent think tank, Climate Integrate,"],[267,371.44,6.24,"to provide fact-based information that can accelerate energy transition."],[267,378.0,2.56,"Moving beyond just saying no,"],[267,380.56,3.92,"I sit down and talk with people with different views --"],[267,384.48,2.04,"in government and in companies --"],[267,386.56,4.08,"and then try to build conviction in people to say yes"],[267,390.68,1.48,"for renewable energy."],[267,393.0,4.16,"We started by conducting a comprehensive analysis,"],[267,397.2,2.96,"uniquely applied to the Japanese context."],[267,400.52,1.64,"And with that,"],[267,402.2,3.48,"we are more confident that it is definitely possible"],[267,405.68,3.08,"to shift our energy system to renewables"],[267,408.8,3.68,"with abundant potential of solar and wind."],[267,413.12,2.08,"Having that clear vision,"],[267,415.24,2.88,"we show the facts and convey the facts."],[267,419.32,4.0,"We also started to collaborate with cities starting from three."],[267,424.28,3.16,"Toyooka city in the western area"],[267,427.44,3.8,"is known for its onsen hot springs and ski resort."],[267,431.64,2.72,"It is already experiencing climate change,"],[267,434.4,3.88,"such as reduced snowfall or extreme heat."],[267,439.0,2.16,"The city has now challenged itself"],[267,441.2,3.52,"to become a model of sustainable tourism"],[267,444.76,4.24,"by incorporating a nature-positive approach and decarbonization."],[267,450.0,2.48,"Ichikawa city, near Tokyo,"],[267,452.52,5.08,"is an area of high population density with a lot of residential buildings."],[267,457.96,3.72,"The city now promotes installing rooftop solar"],[267,461.72,3.92,"and improving energy efficiency for its housing,"],[267,465.68,2.48,"particularly targeting young families."],[267,469.12,2.76,"Sakata city, located in the north,"],[267,471.92,3.76,"is an area that has old coal-fired power plants,"],[267,475.72,3.4,"but it is also a very good location for offshore wind."],[267,479.56,4.88,"This city aims to revitalize its economy through offshore-related business"],[267,484.44,5.4,"and achieve a just transition away from fossil fuels and coal power."],[267,491.48,5.12,"I don't yet know if these efforts will get us to our vision,"],[267,496.6,4.24,"but even though I changed my approach from no to yes,"],[267,500.84,2.12,"what I do for renewable energy"],[267,503.0,4.2,"is actually very similar to what I did for my coal work."],[267,507.88,4.68,"I meet people, enhance dialogue and develop networks."],[267,513.6,1.64,"From my experiences,"],[267,515.28,2.56,"I can tell that change happens"],[267,517.88,3.04,"when people are engaged with facts and stories."],[267,521.28,3.4,"Now, my vision is to help Japan ..."],[267,524.72,4.12,"promote renewable energy up to 90 percent or more, ..."],[267,528.84,2.76,"achieve full decarbonization"],[267,531.64,3.72,"and extend that impact across Asia."],[267,536.16,5.36,"I know it is very challenging, but I also know that I'm not alone."],[267,541.52,3.8,"I have an incredible team and network of experts,"],[267,545.36,2.08,"advocates and believers."],[267,547.44,2.92,"We are all united by a shared conviction"],[267,550.4,2.56,"that this work must be done."],[267,553.36,2.36,"So I am always hopeful."],[267,555.76,3.68,"Even living in a country where change seems difficult."],[267,560.8,1.88,"So my journey continues."],[267,562.72,2.68,"I continue seeking my own ways."],[267,566.0,3.68,"Likewise, you can also find your own way."],[267,570.2,6.08,"And I truly believe that together, we can change the world from no to yes."],[267,576.76,3.08,"So I hope you will join me on this journey forward."],[267,580.2,1.24,"Thank you."],[268,6.33,4.21,"Roughly 50% of people will break a bone at some point in their lives."],[268,10.71,3.17,"But whether the cause is as mundane as a slip on the ice"],[268,13.97,3.25,"or as dramatic as a tightrope-walking accident,"],[268,17.22,2.59,"broken bones tend to heal the same way."],[268,20.35,4.92,"The human body's 200 plus bones come in many shapes and sizes,"],[268,25.31,5.71,"referred to by medical professionals as short bones, long bones, flat bones,"],[268,31.07,3.46,"and the catch-all category of irregular bones."],[268,34.99,1.29,"Despite their differences,"],[268,36.28,4.46,"bones generally have a rigid structure composed of a protein matrix"],[268,40.74,1.54,"that’s dotted with bone cells"],[268,42.29,2.96,"and reinforced with minerals containing calcium."],[268,45.33,3.75,"Inside these stiff exteriors is spongy marrow,"],[268,49.09,2.88,"containing stem cells that can divide and differentiate"],[268,51.96,2.04,"into specialized cell types."],[268,54.34,3.17,"Bones are also penetrated by blood vessels and nerves,"],[268,57.51,3.96,"which relay the searing pain signal associated with the break."],[268,61.97,4.38,"Since these nerves are encased in hard tissue they’re very difficult to study,"],[268,66.56,4.34,"so we still don't know exactly why broken bones hurt so much."],[268,71.07,3.13,"But we do know that long bones are the most likely to break"],[268,74.19,2.88,"since they often take the brunt of the impact from a fall."],[268,77.24,3.5,"Whether an accident causes a clean break or a splintering crack,"],[268,80.95,3.75,"any kind of fracture makes the blood vessels inside the bone rupture,"],[268,84.91,2.59,"causing internal bleeding and inflammation."],[268,87.83,3.92,"This signals the body to start the first step of the healing process:"],[268,91.75,1.58,"the inflammatory phase."],[268,93.88,3.63,"For the next week, the body floods the injury site with immune cells"],[268,97.51,1.58,"which remove damaged tissue"],[268,99.09,3.34,"and help prepare the site for healthy new tissue to grow."],[268,102.77,4.38,"The cells also release signaling molecules that recruit stem cells to the area."],[268,107.39,3.88,"As they arrive, these stem cells differentiate into chondrocytes."],[268,111.4,2.54,"These cells use the clotted blood at the break site"],[268,113.94,3.21,"as a scaffold to build callus made of cartilage."],[268,117.49,4.17,"Cartilage can grow very quickly, making it a useful temporary patch."],[268,121.78,3.09,"But it’s much weaker than bone, so over the following weeks,"],[268,124.87,2.25,"some of the chondrocytes and stem cells develop"],[268,127.12,3.75,"into special bone-building cells called osteoblasts"],[268,130.88,2.63,"that can create a stronger bony callus."],[268,133.8,3.8,"Once the bony callus is complete, the remodeling phase can begin."],[268,138.13,1.42,"Over the next few months,"],[268,139.59,2.96,"another type of cell eats away at the bony callus"],[268,142.64,2.67,"as osteoblasts lay down new bone tissue."],[268,145.52,2.67,"This phase is where most of the bone healing happens,"],[268,148.19,2.71,"restoring the injury site to its previous shape."],[268,151.44,4.04,"But the healing timeline varies widely depending on a patient's diet,"],[268,155.48,3.09,"how much rest they’re getting, and how messy the break is."],[268,158.91,2.21,"A clean break generally heals the fastest,"],[268,161.12,4.09,"and doctors use casts and splints to keep bones aligned as they heal."],[268,165.49,2.59,"But if the bone has been splintered into tiny fragments"],[268,168.08,1.67,"and significantly displaced,"],[268,169.75,3.46,"surgery may be required to put those pieces back in place."],[268,173.54,2.54,"And if a bone does heal while misaligned,"],[268,176.09,3.0,"a surgeon would need to refracture it, realign it,"],[268,179.09,4.05,"and then use pins, plates, or screws to hold the bone together."],[268,183.68,1.83,"When the remodelling phase is over,"],[268,185.51,2.25,"there may be a slight bump at the fracture site,"],[268,187.77,2.25,"but this typically resolves over time,"],[268,190.02,3.0,"leaving the bone just as strong as it was before."],[268,193.94,3.59,"Naturally, the strength of our bones varies from person to person."],[268,197.53,3.67,"And older populations are more likely to break their bones in the first place,"],[268,201.24,3.09,"since bone density tends to decrease with age."],[268,204.62,2.5,"Researchers are still investigating why this happens,"],[268,207.12,3.67,"but fortunately, we already know the best way to keep bones strong."],[268,211.0,3.59,"Even when they aren’t healing, bones are constantly remodeling—"],[268,214.63,1.88,"adjusting their density and strength"],[268,216.5,2.21,"to the amount of force they’re typically under."],[268,218.8,3.0,"So exercises like walking, running, and weightlifting"],[268,221.8,2.46,"all stimulate your bones to grow denser."],[268,224.59,3.38,"Just make sure whatever activity you're doing is relatively safe,"],[268,227.97,3.46,"because there’s nothing humorous about a broken humerus."],[269,6.88,6.17,"One morning in France in 1885, a dog attacked 9-year-old Joseph Meister,"],[269,13.26,2.34,"biting him in 14 places."],[269,15.93,4.67,"He survived— but his mother realized he was probably now infected with rabies,"],[269,20.81,2.63,"an almost certain death sentence."],[269,23.94,1.46,"She rushed him to Paris,"],[269,25.52,4.38,"knowing his life depended on a rumored experimental medicine."],[269,30.48,5.46,"Today, without proper intervention, rabies remains deadly in almost all cases,"],[269,36.24,3.21,"making it the most lethal infectious disease."],[269,39.87,3.84,"However, modern advances have afforded us a much deeper understanding"],[269,43.71,4.75,"of the mechanisms that make rabies so dangerous and treatment-resistant,"],[269,48.59,2.92,"and have revealed the best methods to save lives."],[269,52.55,5.09,"Rabies is caused by the roughly 20 virus species in the Lyssavirus genus,"],[269,57.93,2.88,"all of which attack the mammalian nervous system."],[269,61.22,4.55,"Lyssavirus rabies, the most abundant, can infect any mammal."],[269,66.19,3.71,"Lyssaviruses mainly persist long term in bat populations."],[269,70.23,3.71,"Besides bats, rabies virus has also adapted to carnivores"],[269,73.94,2.59,"like foxes, raccoons, and skunks,"],[269,76.95,3.46,"and most human rabies infections come from dogs."],[269,80.91,3.5,"Infection begins with the transfer of virus-laden saliva"],[269,84.41,2.04,"into the new host's tissue."],[269,86.79,4.67,"This could happen with a deep bite wound or a simple lick to certain spots."],[269,91.92,1.71,"Once inside its new host,"],[269,93.63,4.25,"the virus particles, called virions, set out for the brain."],[269,98.05,2.42,"If the infection site is closer to the head,"],[269,100.47,1.88,"things may escalate faster."],[269,102.77,5.76,"Rabies virions may lay low for days, weeks, months, or in rare cases, years,"],[269,108.69,3.13,"replicating without causing any immediate symptoms."],[269,112.53,2.75,"Virions also suppress certain immune responses,"],[269,115.36,2.75,"making it harder to clear an infection locally."],[269,118.78,2.34,"Meanwhile, the virions begin to travel,"],[269,121.12,1.42,"entering local nerve cells"],[269,122.53,4.0,"and exploiting the existing transport mechanisms along their axons,"],[269,126.83,2.84,"traveling up to 100 millimeters a day."],[269,130.42,2.54,"Their protected position here in the nerve cells"],[269,132.96,3.34,"helps shield the virions from the host’s immune system."],[269,136.8,2.42,"Once they’ve made it to the central nervous system,"],[269,139.22,3.54,"the infection enters a new, devastating stage."],[269,142.97,4.59,"Clinical symptoms develop as rabies virions rapidly proliferate,"],[269,147.64,3.84,"spreading into the peripheral nervous system and its connective tissues."],[269,151.77,3.92,"First, the escalating infection registers with flu-like symptoms."],[269,155.74,3.63,"Where a different neurological infection might cause inflammation"],[269,159.36,3.8,"that opens the blood-brain barrier and incites an immune response,"],[269,163.62,1.88,"rabies viruses do not,"],[269,165.87,3.88,"so immune cells and therapies cannot easily or quickly reach them there."],[269,170.12,2.75,"Then, as the disease damages the nervous system,"],[269,172.96,1.83,"things take one of two turns."],[269,175.09,1.84,"In paralytic rabies,"],[269,176.92,4.55,"symptoms like a vacant expression, weakness, and paralysis set in."],[269,181.89,1.92,"Furious rabies, meanwhile,"],[269,183.8,3.88,"is associated with hyperactivity, uncontrolled movements,"],[269,187.72,3.17,"hallucinations, aggression, and paralysis."],[269,191.1,2.92,"As virions accumulate in the salivary glands,"],[269,194.02,2.38,"the host secretes excess saliva."],[269,196.53,3.63,"The infection also often causes hydrophobia in people,"],[269,200.28,3.29,"where throat spasms make swallowing water painful and difficult,"],[269,203.91,2.5,"further concentrating the virus in the saliva"],[269,206.45,2.38,"and increasing the odds of transmission."],[269,209.54,3.29,"Some hosts are especially effective at transmitting the virus;"],[269,213.21,2.0,"humans, however, aren’t,"],[269,215.21,2.33,"and rarely transmit rabies themselves,"],[269,217.67,3.71,"for example, in cases of tissue or organ transplantation."],[269,222.09,2.17,"Regardless of whether transmission occurs,"],[269,224.26,5.0,"this period of acute illness is almost always followed by coma and death."],[269,229.64,3.84,"Once symptoms have begun, there is currently no cure for rabies."],[269,233.98,3.96,"However, we do have highly effective preventative methods"],[269,237.94,1.79,"if they’re delivered early enough—"],[269,239.74,3.0,"and Joseph Meister was the first to benefit."],[269,243.45,3.29,"For years, French scientist Louis Pasteur and colleagues"],[269,246.78,2.46,"had been trying to develop a rabies vaccine."],[269,249.58,3.5,"They collected the spinal cords of rabies-virus-infected rabbits,"],[269,253.33,1.83,"dried them to weaken the virus,"],[269,255.17,3.67,"and injected the resulting mixtures into dogs and other animals."],[269,259.38,3.46,"Meister’s mother got him to Pasteur two days after the attack."],[269,263.13,4.09,"He received a course of inoculations, and though the procedure was risky,"],[269,267.26,1.25,"he beat the odds,"],[269,268.51,4.25,"becoming the first rabies-vaccinated person to survive."],[269,273.39,3.46,"Today, two series of vaccinations are available:"],[269,277.27,2.84,"pre- and post-exposure prophylaxis."],[269,280.57,3.46,"Each is highly effective at preventing rabies virus infections"],[269,284.03,2.04,"from reaching the central nervous system,"],[269,286.32,4.09,"before symptoms develop and the virus has done irreparable harm."],[269,290.79,4.3,"The very few people known to have survived rabies after the onset of symptoms"],[269,295.08,2.46,"have done so due to intensive care,"],[269,297.79,3.13,"usually sustaining neurological damage in the process."],[269,301.42,4.96,"Around 59,000 people are still estimated to die of rabies annually,"],[269,306.38,2.75,"though the number is sadly suspected to be higher"],[269,309.22,3.92,"due to insufficient medical access in rabies-burdened areas."],[269,314.14,2.84,"Scientists are still looking into new therapies."],[269,316.98,4.25,"But the way rabies virions elude the immune system and damage cells"],[269,321.4,3.09,"makes the infection extremely difficult to treat."],[269,324.74,3.17,"And because the Lyssavirus has so many natural hosts,"],[269,327.99,2.46,"it's impossible to eradicate the disease."],[269,330.83,3.17,"This makes preventative measures especially essential."],[269,334.29,2.96,"Even unconfirmed, potential exposures—"],[269,337.25,3.84,"like the mere presence of a bat in one’s house while they’ve been sleeping—"],[269,341.09,2.75,"warrant serious medical investigation."],[270,6.88,5.21,"Ever since Albert Einstein published his Special Theory of Relativity in 1905,"],[270,12.09,4.55,"one equation has been the bane of humans hoping to explore the stars:"],[270,17.01,2.29,"E=mc²."],[270,19.89,4.3,"In addition to informing our understanding of gravity, space, and time,"],[270,24.31,5.92,"this formula implies that traveling at or beyond light speed is impossible."],[270,30.61,2.59,"And given how expansive the universe is,"],[270,33.2,5.71,"this speed limit severely restricts our ability to zip around the cosmos."],[270,39.16,3.34,"But while most physics textbooks describe this speed limit,"],[270,42.5,3.38,"their explanations don’t always tell the whole story."],[270,46.12,1.5,"In Einstein’s equation,"],[270,47.63,4.88,"E stands for energy, m for mass, and c for a constant—"],[270,52.67,3.09,"specifically, the speed of light in a vacuum."],[270,56.18,2.29,"C squared is a huge number,"],[270,58.47,3.42,"which means it requires enormous amounts of energy"],[270,61.89,4.09,"to move even small amounts of mass close to the speed of light."],[270,66.31,4.96,"This relationship is why the only particles that can travel at light speed"],[270,71.28,3.92,"are those with no mass at all, such as photons."],[270,75.57,3.21,"That’s the short answer for why objects with mass"],[270,78.78,2.29,"can’t reach or exceed light speed."],[270,81.24,2.63,"But to make full use of Einstein's equation,"],[270,83.95,3.5,"physicists often include one more variable."],[270,87.96,3.42,"This gamma represents the Lorentz Factor,"],[270,91.59,4.8,"which models how an object’s velocity changes the way that object"],[270,96.38,4.42,"experiences time, length, and other physical properties."],[270,101.35,5.34,"Now, when an object’s velocity is a very small percentage of the speed of light,"],[270,106.77,4.67,"this variable resolves to 1, so it doesn’t impact the equation."],[270,112.15,3.29,"However, when an object is moving fast enough,"],[270,115.53,2.71,"this denominator drops to 0."],[270,118.49,4.34,"Since dividing by 0 is impossible, this breaks the equation"],[270,122.91,3.5,"and makes the variables therein mathematically impossible—"],[270,126.62,2.29,"hence the unbreakable speed limit."],[270,129.25,3.59,"But what does it actually mean for this math to break down?"],[270,133.17,1.0,"To answer that,"],[270,134.17,4.8,"we need to understand the physical system its modeling: spacetime."],[270,139.34,3.38,"After Einstein published his theory of special relativity,"],[270,142.81,5.0,"his mentor Hermann Minkowski realized that— if his student was right—"],[270,147.81,4.17,"it would mean space and time were not two separate entities,"],[270,152.11,1.88,"but one connected system."],[270,154.32,5.17,"And everything in the universe travels through space and time simultaneously."],[270,159.82,4.67,"However, traveling through one of these vectors limits the speed at which"],[270,164.49,1.63,"we can travel through the other."],[270,166.66,3.46,"To picture this, imagine moving north at a fixed speed."],[270,170.33,3.0,"You could turn to travel east at the same speed,"],[270,173.34,4.96,"but moving northeast would mean you move in both directions more slowly."],[270,178.59,3.34,"The tradeoffs are the same when we move through spacetime."],[270,182.39,4.46,"Since our typical movement through space is so much slower than the speed of light,"],[270,186.89,4.46,"we mostly perceive moving through time at a relatively steady speed."],[270,191.81,4.75,"But if an object managed to move through space at the speed of light,"],[270,196.73,2.42,"it would no longer move through time."],[270,199.49,3.96,"This is the kind of time dilation charted by the Lorentz Factor,"],[270,203.57,3.88,"which models how time slows down for objects moving"],[270,207.45,2.17,"at incredibly high velocities."],[270,210.0,4.8,"This nuance is just one of several hiding in E=mc²."],[270,215.0,4.42,"For example, the c in Einstein’s equation refers specifically"],[270,219.42,4.55,"to the speed of light in a “vacuum,” which outer space approximates."],[270,224.22,4.67,"But light’s speed is actually defined by what it’s traveling through."],[270,229.22,2.54,"For example, when light travels through water,"],[270,231.77,3.59,"its speed is reduced by about 25%."],[270,235.61,5.51,"And scientists can propel low mass particles like charged electrons"],[270,241.24,3.5,"through water at speeds faster than these photons."],[270,245.28,5.17,"This means that underwater, some particles can travel faster than light;"],[270,250.54,6.17,"and doing so emits a ghostly blue glow known as Cherenkov radiation."],[270,257.09,1.58,"Despite these loopholes,"],[270,258.75,4.42,"the major takeaway of E=mc² remains true."],[270,263.59,4.75,"As far as we know, we still can't travel faster than light in a vacuum."],[270,268.64,5.42,"But this hasn't stopped scientists from theorizing what might happen if we did."],[270,274.56,3.09,"If you were on a spacecraft approaching light speed,"],[270,277.73,2.79,"your vision would likely become kaleidoscopic."],[270,280.69,3.29,"The direction your ship moved would appear blue-shifted,"],[270,284.2,3.75,"while the things next to and behind you would be red-shifted."],[270,288.37,4.34,"And if you were somehow able to reach or exceed light speed,"],[270,292.91,3.75,"it might even manifest as some kind of time travel—"],[270,296.67,3.13,"potentially letting you chat with Einstein himself"],[270,299.84,3.71,"to rewrite our fundamental understanding of physics."],[271,7.0,4.63,"Morgan and her twin sister Lisa have been inseparable since birth."],[271,11.8,2.04,"So nobody was surprised"],[271,13.84,4.05,"when they moved into identical houses right next to each other."],[271,18.18,1.33,"The only difference?"],[271,19.56,4.13,"Morgan bought her house, while Lisa is renting hers."],[271,24.06,4.21,"For generations, conventional wisdom has been that home ownership"],[271,28.36,2.25,"is financially better than renting."],[271,30.86,4.71,"But let’s follow the twins and see how things play out over the years,"],[271,35.74,2.75,"based on historical trends in the US."],[271,39.79,3.84,"As Morgan and Lisa move into their two-bedroom houses,"],[271,43.62,3.0,"their upfront costs look a little different."],[271,46.88,3.59,"Morgan buys her house for $400,000."],[271,50.67,3.25,"Like most buyers, she takes out a mortgage—"],[271,54.13,4.67,"a loan that she'll pay back to a lender over time with interest."],[271,59.14,2.96,"For this, she needs to make a down payment—"],[271,62.1,3.79,"a lump sum that’s some percentage of the total home price."],[271,66.06,6.26,"Between Morgan’s 10% down payment and a few extra fees, known as closing costs,"],[271,72.32,3.17,"Morgan spends a total of $50,000."],[271,75.61,1.0,"Next door,"],[271,76.61,6.3,"Lisa signs a 12-month lease to rent her house for $1,800 a month."],[271,83.0,3.8,"To get her keys, she’ll also pay a security deposit,"],[271,86.83,3.54,"bringing her total to $3,600—"],[271,90.5,2.71,"just a fraction of Morgan’s payment."],[271,93.63,5.34,"Five years in, Morgan and Lisa have added a few more members to the family."],[271,99.14,4.63,"Lisa’s rent has been steadily climbing, about 2% every year."],[271,104.1,5.09,"Her monthly rent is now over $2,000, including a new pet fee."],[271,109.56,5.17,"This brings her five year total to around $112,000."],[271,115.19,3.17,"Meanwhile, Morgan’s still paying off her mortgage,"],[271,118.41,4.21,"a 30-year loan with a fixed interest rate of 6%."],[271,123.12,4.5,"This type of long-term fixed-rate loan is common in the US,"],[271,127.67,3.63,"though loans with adjustable rates over shorter periods"],[271,131.38,2.84,"are much more typical in the rest of the world."],[271,134.8,3.13,"Longer mortgages lower monthly payments"],[271,137.93,2.75,"by spreading them over an extended period."],[271,140.93,3.59,"Morgan's monthly total, including taxes and insurance,"],[271,144.56,2.96,"is around $2,500 per month."],[271,147.85,4.92,"But a large fraction of early payments goes towards interest."],[271,152.9,5.13,"So five years in, she’s spent $150,000 on her property—"],[271,158.32,2.5,"$38,000 more than Lisa."],[271,161.2,5.71,"Despite this, she's only chipped about $25,000 off her mortgage."],[271,167.96,3.71,"The good news for Morgan is that the market has changed"],[271,171.67,4.25,"and interest rates have dropped, allowing her to refinance."],[271,176.21,4.5,"Basically, she takes out a new mortgage at the lower interest rate"],[271,180.72,1.71,"to replace her old one."],[271,182.68,2.04,"Her new rate is 5%,"],[271,184.85,5.3,"bringing her total monthly cost down to around $2,300."],[271,190.69,5.51,"15 years in, Lisa’s rent now exceeds Morgan’s monthly payments."],[271,196.32,2.79,"But this isn’t the only cost to consider."],[271,199.44,4.42,"During an intense storm season, flash floods hit their neighborhood."],[271,204.03,3.17,"As a homeowner, Morgan pays for repairs,"],[271,207.2,3.04,"while Lisa lets her landlord handle everything."],[271,210.62,4.71,"At this point, Morgan has spent over $500,000 on her house,"],[271,215.46,4.21,"while Lisa has paid around $380,000."],[271,220.17,5.09,"After 30 years, Morgan is finally done paying off her mortgage."],[271,225.47,3.5,"To get there, she spent about $1 million,"],[271,229.1,5.09,"including the price of the house, interest, taxes, and insurance."],[271,234.44,3.29,"But she's also had to pay for repairs and upkeep,"],[271,237.9,3.71,"bringing her total to $1.4 million."],[271,241.9,4.67,"Meanwhile, Lisa’s rent is now $3,300 a month,"],[271,246.57,4.09,"and she's spent around $900,000 in rent."],[271,251.0,2.71,"But before we calculate final tallies,"],[271,253.71,4.0,"there’s one more cost to consider: opportunity cost."],[271,258.05,4.13,"When Morgan chose to spend $40,000 on a down payment,"],[271,262.17,2.92,"she missed out on putting that $40,000"],[271,265.09,3.04,"into a stable, diversified retirement account."],[271,268.56,4.42,"That’s what Lisa did, and it’s grown into $500,000."],[271,273.44,1.42,"Morgan’s not sweating it,"],[271,274.85,3.29,"because her home turned out to be a pretty good investment,"],[271,278.15,3.54,"as it's now worth $1.3 million."],[271,282.53,6.42,"In the end, Morgan spent $1.4 million on a $1.3 million house,"],[271,289.2,3.21,"putting her at a loss of $100,000."],[271,292.87,7.01,"Lisa spent $900,000 in rent, but made $460,000 in investments,"],[271,300.21,4.96,"putting her at a total loss of $440,000."],[271,306.05,3.17,"In this situation, Morgan came out on top."],[271,309.47,2.46,"Plus, she’s paid off her mortgage,"],[271,312.02,5.17,"so she only needs to pay taxes, insurance, and maintenance costs going forward."],[271,317.44,2.5,"But Lisa won out in other ways."],[271,320.02,3.34,"She had lower monthly expenses for over 10 years,"],[271,323.4,2.88,"which allowed her to spend on hobbies and travel."],[271,326.7,4.46,"In another scenario, she might have put that extra cash into retirement"],[271,331.16,2.54,"or investments and allowed it to grow."],[271,334.12,2.67,"And Lisa wasn't tied to one location."],[271,336.92,3.67,"In fact, she just got a job opportunity in a new city"],[271,340.59,2.67,"and is finally feeling ready to branch out."],[272,4.43,3.17,"Right now, the world is in an AI race."],[272,8.24,3.3,"Companies, governments, universities"],[272,11.57,4.41,"are all racing to build bigger models, smarter systems."],[272,16.01,1.47,"And behind the scenes,"],[272,17.48,3.74,"they are racing to build more data centers to power AI."],[272,22.05,1.4,"But there's a problem."],[272,24.05,3.64,"We are running head first into the limits of our infrastructure."],[272,28.02,3.24,"The power grid includes all the infrastructure,"],[272,31.26,2.33,"power plants, transmission lines and all"],[272,33.63,3.8,"to generate and deliver power to our homes, our businesses,"],[272,37.47,2.37,"and now to AI data centers."],[272,40.24,1.7,"In the United States,"],[272,41.97,4.0,"the grid operators are reporting that new AI data center projects"],[272,46.01,4.54,"are requesting power loads equal to entire cities."],[272,50.88,3.14,"In some regions, utilities simply can't keep up."],[272,55.15,4.37,"So when you hear “AI data center,” what comes to mind?"],[272,60.32,2.8,"For many, it's one thing:"],[272,63.16,1.87,"energy hogs."],[272,65.43,1.67,"And they are not wrong."],[272,67.53,4.67,"AI is dramatically accelerating the electricity demand of data centers."],[272,72.2,2.4,"Just training GPT-4"],[272,74.64,4.47,"is estimated to have consumed around the annual electricity use"],[272,79.14,1.77,"of thousands of US homes."],[272,81.38,3.24,"In another striking example, in Ireland,"],[272,84.65,3.77,"nearly 20 percent of the nation's electricity"],[272,88.45,2.7,"is drawn by data centers today."],[272,92.16,2.27,"And these are not just statistics."],[272,95.19,1.8,"They are also community stories."],[272,97.93,3.0,"In the data center alley in Virginia,"],[272,100.93,3.1,"residents recently saw higher electricity bills,"],[272,104.03,4.54,"20 percent higher already compared to just a few years ago,"],[272,108.61,5.24,"as utilities scramble to serve massive new AI facilities."],[272,113.84,5.17,"So energy-hog label seems well deserved."],[272,119.78,1.67,"But that's only half the story."],[272,121.95,1.6,"Here is the new view."],[272,124.52,4.2,"These facilities are not just energy-hungry brains."],[272,128.73,5.37,"They can also be the muscles of the grid, flexing on demand."],[272,134.13,2.54,"Unlike our homes or hospitals,"],[272,136.7,4.34,"AI data centers run jobs that are predictable,"],[272,141.04,2.9,"controllable and often delayable."],[272,144.57,4.64,"That makes them ideal to help balance supply and demand on the grid."],[272,150.41,3.67,"By making AI data centers power-flexible,"],[272,154.12,3.3,"we can connect them much more rapidly to the grid,"],[272,157.45,5.61,"while at the same time making electricity more affordable and resilient."],[272,164.06,4.64,"What's more, the AI boom is arriving"],[272,168.7,3.64,"just as the renewable boom is also taking off."],[272,172.74,3.94,"Wind and solar don't follow our schedules,"],[272,176.67,1.7,"but data centers can."],[272,178.81,3.54,"Which means we can align the rise of AI"],[272,182.38,2.04,"with the rise of clean energy,"],[272,184.41,3.0,"if we are bold enough to rethink their role."],[272,188.35,4.34,"All this transformation to power flexibility"],[272,192.72,2.2,"didn't just come out of thin air."],[272,195.69,3.2,"It builds on decades of research"],[272,198.93,1.97,"on energy-efficient computing,"],[272,200.9,3.57,"scheduling, optimization and many others."],[272,205.24,1.8,"I've lived this journey myself."],[272,207.91,2.0,"Early in my career,"],[272,209.91,4.1,"I asked a question that many found unrealistic."],[272,214.65,5.34,"Could computer systems adapt their behavior"],[272,220.02,2.94,"depending on power grid needs,"],[272,222.99,3.7,"but without breaking their performance promise"],[272,226.72,1.3,"to their users?"],[272,229.06,2.9,"At the time, this sounded radical"],[272,232.0,5.74,"because why would we ever design a system that would slow itself down"],[272,237.77,1.33,"on purpose?"],[272,239.57,2.13,"But then came the breakthroughs."],[272,242.34,2.47,"First, we discovered"],[272,244.84,2.67,"not all computing tasks are urgent."],[272,247.98,2.34,"Some can wait for minutes or hours,"],[272,250.35,5.17,"and some can be slowed down without anyone really noticing it."],[272,255.55,1.57,"For example,"],[272,257.12,5.0,"a researcher analyzing hundreds of medical images with AI"],[272,262.13,3.2,"may be OK with waiting just a little longer."],[272,265.36,3.34,"Or, if you are fine-tuning your AI model"],[272,268.73,1.94,"over the course of the next few days,"],[272,270.67,4.47,"you may be OK with slowing it down for just a few hours."],[272,275.17,3.04,"This inherent flexibility in computing"],[272,278.24,2.64,"gives us the flexibility we need to manage power."],[272,280.88,1.8,"Second,"],[272,282.68,1.6,"we reframed the problem."],[272,285.01,2.14,"Instead of asking"],[272,287.18,3.54,"how do we compute as fast as possible,"],[272,290.75,1.53,"we asked,"],[272,292.32,4.97,"how do we make computer systems meet the constraints of the power grid,"],[272,297.29,5.07,"while at the same time still delivering on user performance agreements?"],[272,302.37,2.3,"This shift led to new strategies:"],[272,304.7,1.57,"capping power,"],[272,306.3,1.77,"shifting workloads"],[272,308.07,5.27,"and provisioning the data center as a flexible reserve to the grid."],[272,313.38,5.57,"A key aspect here is that we do keep the performance promise to users,"],[272,318.98,1.9,"so it's not arbitrary."],[272,320.92,3.6,"User experience remains as a key target."],[272,324.56,3.0,"And better yet, it becomes more predictable."],[272,328.12,5.07,"So we built prototypes on real data-center servers,"],[272,333.2,1.23,"and they worked."],[272,334.43,3.5,"Systems that could follow a power target"],[272,337.97,2.2,"while still delivering results."],[272,340.2,2.27,"But all this journey wasn't smooth."],[272,342.47,4.97,"There were paper rejections, funding rejections,"],[272,347.44,3.07,"colleagues telling me this would never work."],[272,351.42,4.14,"Well, since I was a kid, I was told I'm a persistent person."],[272,355.59,1.87,"Perhaps stubborn at times."],[272,358.69,4.54,"And bold ideas require persistence"],[272,363.26,4.27,"because change almost always looks impossible"],[272,367.56,1.67,"before it looks obvious."],[272,369.83,3.9,"So you take that feedback, you reframe it again and again,"],[272,373.74,1.3,"and you keep building."],[272,375.61,1.3,"You keep proving."],[272,376.91,4.5,"So what began as scribbles on a whiteboard 12 years ago,"],[272,381.41,2.84,"is now running on real AI data centers."],[272,385.01,1.44,"Why does this matter now?"],[272,386.48,3.3,"Because the power grids challenge"],[272,389.79,2.27,"isn't just to generate more power."],[272,392.39,1.44,"It's about timing."],[272,394.66,4.54,"Solar gives us a glut of electricity at noon,"],[272,399.2,2.44,"but demand might peak in the evening."],[272,401.67,3.57,"Wind might be abundant one day and scarce the next."],[272,405.94,5.47,"Nuclear takes decades and billions of dollars to build"],[272,411.44,3.7,"and is often hard to locate in urban areas."],[272,415.14,2.64,"Batteries are critical,"],[272,417.81,3.27,"but scaling them is costly, slow,"],[272,421.12,2.04,"and often not environmentally clean."],[272,423.65,6.77,"Meanwhile, AI data centers themselves face five to seven-year wait times"],[272,430.46,1.97,"just to connect to the grid"],[272,432.46,1.6,"in places like Virginia."],[272,434.06,1.24,"In AI time,"],[272,435.33,3.1,"where technologies shift in a major way every six months,"],[272,438.47,2.04,"five to seven years is an eternity."],[272,441.14,2.0,"So here's the opportunity."],[272,443.14,2.07,"With the right orchestration,"],[272,445.24,3.44,"AI data centers can be flexible today."],[272,448.68,4.97,"No waiting, no new massive power infrastructure construction."],[272,453.68,4.3,"They can soak up excess solar in the afternoon,"],[272,458.02,2.27,"scale down at peak times"],[272,460.29,3.27,"and act as virtual batteries today."],[272,463.59,1.37,"And the stakes are real."],[272,464.96,2.6,"Take Texas, August 23."],[272,467.9,2.2,"During a brutal heat wave,"],[272,470.13,4.64,"the rising electricity demand pushed the grid to its limits."],[272,475.54,4.47,"Wholesale electricity prices spiked over 800 percent"],[272,480.04,1.9,"in a single afternoon."],[272,482.28,3.9,"So flexible loads, if they were widely available,"],[272,486.18,2.1,"could have reduced the costs"],[272,488.32,3.87,"and could have prevented the emergency alerts that went to the consumers."],[272,492.22,2.4,"So we have two opportunities here."],[272,494.99,4.27,"One, we can make current data centers flexible"],[272,499.3,1.33,"and help prevent blackouts"],[272,500.66,1.74,"and reduce electricity costs."],[272,503.57,3.27,"Two, and perhaps the more significant,"],[272,506.84,4.24,"by making future data centers power-flexible,"],[272,511.11,2.4,"we can connect them much earlier"],[272,513.54,2.87,"without waiting for major power grid upgrades."],[272,516.71,3.34,"If we ignore this opportunity,"],[272,520.08,2.97,"we are not just wasting renewable energy"],[272,523.09,2.84,"and we are not just raising our electricity bills."],[272,526.49,3.07,"We are also slowing AI adoption,"],[272,529.59,1.9,"making it delayed,"],[272,531.5,3.67,"more expensive and less accessible to society."],[272,535.83,1.6,"But there's a catch."],[272,538.13,3.84,"Orchestrating this flexibility is not easy."],[272,542.37,1.8,"Prices change hourly."],[272,545.21,2.3,"Workloads may arrive unpredictably."],[272,548.45,3.4,"Grid rules change across states, across countries."],[272,552.42,2.13,"So no human operator"],[272,554.59,3.84,"and no single fixed data center management policy can keep up."],[272,558.46,3.97,"This is where AI itself comes back into the story."],[272,563.29,4.44,"The very technology driving this unforeseen demand"],[272,567.73,4.14,"is also probably the only thing smart enough to tame it."],[272,571.9,4.54,"AI can learn patterns, anticipate grid needs"],[272,576.47,4.04,"and coordinate across data centers, across utilities,"],[272,580.51,2.5,"even nations in real time."],[272,583.45,1.23,"Imagine a data center"],[272,584.72,2.17,"or a whole network of them,"],[272,586.92,1.64,"as an orchestra,"],[272,588.59,3.54,"with hundreds of instruments, all playing at once."],[272,592.76,3.57,"Left on their own, it can sound like chaos."],[272,597.26,2.2,"But bring in a conductor,"],[272,599.5,2.44,"suddenly all that noise turns into music."],[272,602.47,3.54,"The conductor in this case is AI."],[272,606.8,3.34,"AI can direct data center operation"],[272,610.17,5.31,"so that the data center can precisely match power constraints,"],[272,615.51,3.84,"depending on what the grid needs, what power is available"],[272,619.35,1.5,"and what users demand."],[272,621.25,2.3,"The result is harmony."],[272,624.42,2.9,"Reliable electricity, efficient computing"],[272,627.36,3.14,"and a system that works beautifully together."],[272,630.53,3.14,"And that's exactly what we've built."],[272,633.7,4.14,"We built software that slows down, speeds up,"],[272,637.87,2.8,"or pauses workloads in a data center,"],[272,640.67,2.97,"or shifts workload among data centers."],[272,643.67,5.8,"Our conductor platform tunes performance and power at real time,"],[272,649.51,4.8,"all the while respecting user and cloud-provider performance needs."],[272,654.32,3.6,"In this way, by flexing when needed,"],[272,657.95,5.37,"we can connect AI data centers much faster to the grid."],[272,663.89,3.77,"Make better use of the available power in the power grid"],[272,667.7,2.84,"and enable faster AI adoption."],[272,671.13,1.57,"I've been inside this story"],[272,672.74,2.6,"from an idea that once seemed impossible"],[272,675.37,2.07,"to prototypes in a lab,"],[272,677.47,2.0,"to systems now running in the field,"],[272,679.48,2.07,"and I believe this is just the beginning."],[272,681.58,3.27,"AI is already reshaping how we compute,"],[272,684.88,3.37,"but it could also reshape how we power the world."],[272,688.65,4.37,"So the question isn't how much energy AI consumes."],[272,693.66,4.77,"The real question is how much flexibility, resilience"],[272,698.46,2.44,"and clean power can AI unlock?"],[272,701.33,3.0,"If we are bold enough to rethink AI data centers,"],[272,704.37,3.8,"the very machines that now seem like a burden"],[272,708.17,1.9,"could be our greatest assets"],[272,710.11,2.77,"in building a sustainable AI future."],[272,714.14,1.17,"Thanks."],[273,6.63,6.76,"At age 44, Xie Qiuping cut her hair for the first time in over three decades."],[273,14.18,4.17,"At the time, her impressive locks extended over five meters—"],[273,18.72,3.84,"earning the Guinness World Record for the world's longest hair."],[273,23.77,2.04,"But even with this record-setting feat,"],[273,26.36,3.67,"her hair might not have reached its maximum possible length."],[273,30.73,4.0,"So just how long can human hair actually grow?"],[273,35.87,2.13,"To get to the bottom of this question,"],[273,37.99,2.84,"let's start at the bottom of a hair follicle."],[273,41.45,4.34,"Follicles are tube-shaped structures nestled roughly half a centimeter"],[273,45.79,1.63,"below the skin's surface."],[273,47.71,2.71,"At their base is what's called the hair bulb,"],[273,50.59,3.38,"and it contains rapidly dividing matrix cells."],[273,54.42,4.8,"As they divide, these develop into specialized hair cells called trichocytes,"],[273,59.35,4.34,"which harden as they fill themselves with cable-like keratin proteins."],[273,63.94,2.17,"As more and more trichocytes are produced,"],[273,66.1,4.55,"they form a strand of hair that emerges from the follicle and grows upwards."],[273,71.28,2.5,"All the hair on your body grows this way,"],[273,73.78,4.34,"but not all hair will grow to the same length, shape, or color."],[273,78.57,3.13,"Those properties are determined by yet another type of cell"],[273,81.7,3.29,"within the hair bulb: the fibroblast."],[273,85.5,4.38,"Fibroblasts instruct the matrix cells to grow specific kinds of hair"],[273,89.88,1.58,"for each part of the body."],[273,91.84,4.04,"This is why doctors doing hair implants usually use follicles"],[273,95.88,1.79,"from the same body part,"],[273,97.89,2.46,"though they'll sometimes change things up."],[273,100.76,4.09,"For example, eyelashes are some of the shortest hairs on your body"],[273,104.85,2.5,"at an average length of one centimeter."],[273,107.6,5.34,"So to give people longer eyelashes, doctors might implant scalp follicles,"],[273,112.98,3.54,"which grow at a rate of about 15 centimeters per year."],[273,116.95,3.5,"So these patients will have to trim their new lashes regularly"],[273,120.45,2.88,"to avoid a blinding curtain of hair."],[273,124.5,2.88,"In general, our follicles are very resilient."],[273,127.37,3.17,"Even if you pluck hair out with your fingers or some wax,"],[273,130.63,4.25,"you’ll likely only damage matrix cells, not fibroblasts."],[273,135.17,2.96,"So given time, those hair bulbs will repair themselves"],[273,138.13,2.04,"and begin producing new hair."],[273,140.34,4.21,"However, each strand of hair will only grow for so long."],[273,145.1,3.25,"We don't fully understand how follicles measure time,"],[273,148.35,4.71,"but in most cases they'll stop growing a strand after 5 to 7 years."],[273,153.48,3.67,"This means that at the rate of 15 centimeters a year,"],[273,157.36,3.84,"the average person’s scalp hair could reach a maximum length"],[273,161.37,1.46,"of around one meter."],[273,163.99,4.59,"The precise duration and speed of hair growth varies from person to person"],[273,168.62,3.92,"depending on genetics, hormones, and various health conditions,"],[273,172.79,4.21,"and it’s difficult to know exactly what combination of these factors"],[273,177.09,3.09,"let Xie Quiping reach her record."],[273,180.97,4.38,"But however long your locks may be, when a follicle stops growing,"],[273,185.35,3.88,"the hair falls out— shedding from our heads slowly,"],[273,189.44,3.17,"or in clumps during showers and hair brushing."],[273,193.23,4.29,"After a hair is lost, fibroblasts will trigger the growth of a new strand"],[273,197.53,2.67,"to fill out your scalp— usually."],[273,200.61,2.96,"As we age, follicles run out of certain cell types,"],[273,203.57,2.5,"which can lead to changes in hair growth."],[273,206.24,5.42,"For example, when they run out of pigment-producing melanocyte stem cells,"],[273,211.83,2.75,"follicles begin producing gray hairs."],[273,214.96,4.42,"And since there are only a handful of melanocyte stem cells per follicle"],[273,219.38,1.13,"to begin with,"],[273,220.51,3.21,"they tend to run out relatively early in a person’s life."],[273,224.51,3.63,"Similarly, when follicles run out of epithelial stem cells,"],[273,228.14,3.84,"they begin to shrink and grow shorter, thinner strands"],[273,232.06,2.88,"before eventually shutting down production altogether."],[273,235.23,2.04,"This is what leads to baldness,"],[273,237.28,3.17,"though the condition can also be accelerated by testosterone,"],[273,240.44,2.79,"which can prevent fibroblasts’ instructions"],[273,243.24,2.38,"from reaching a follicle’s cells."],[273,246.45,2.25,"While it’s completely natural for hair growth"],[273,248.7,2.13,"to change over the course of our lives,"],[273,250.83,3.38,"there are ways to take more control of our scalps."],[273,254.67,3.25,"Some baldness remedies remove excess testosterone,"],[273,257.96,3.59,"and special shampoos can address the coarse, brittle strands"],[273,261.55,2.25,"reported by many people with gray hair."],[273,264.38,4.92,"Since our bodies already produce hair at the fastest possible biological speed,"],[273,269.47,2.96,"there's currently no way to grow hair faster."],[273,273.02,4.09,"But even if you’re unlikely to beat Xie Qiuping’s record,"],[273,277.36,3.5,"with time, the right treatments, and some genetic luck,"],[273,281.15,3.42,"you should be able to grow some luscious locks of your own."],[274,0.16,2.64,"If you’re looking for a guaranteed way to lose a little weight,"],[274,2.8,1.92,"you could head to the middle of the Indian Ocean."],[274,4.72,2.56,"Without any change to your exercise or diet,"],[274,7.28,3.28,"you’d stand on a scale in Sri Lanka and be a few grams lighter!"],[274,10.56,2.32,"This isn’t caused by any changes to your mass,"],[274,12.88,2.72,"but rather by how much gravity you’re experiencing."],[274,15.6,4.24,"The strength of gravity at the Earth’s surface varies depending on where you are."],[274,19.84,4.16,"And yeah, a few grams might not seem like much on the scale of a person."],[274,24.0,3.6,"But when you get to the scale of the Earth, it can make a pretty big difference."],[274,27.6,2.72,"If you prefer a colder climate, there’s also a giant,"],[274,30.32,2.24,"moving gravity hole under Antarctica."],[274,32.56,2.4,"It might even be the reason the continent froze over!"],[274,34.96,4.83,"And the thing is, scientists still aren’t entirely sure why these holes are there at all."],[274,43.28,3.36,"Look at any picture of Earth from space and you’ll see a sphere."],[274,46.64,2.0,"But the Earth isn’t really a sphere."],[274,48.64,4.32,"Way back in 1687, Isaac Newton suggested our planet is both"],[274,52.96,3.92,"slightly flattened at the poles and fattened at the equator,"],[274,56.88,3.2,"on account of the forces generated as the Earth spins."],[274,60.08,4.8,"And by the 1730s, careful measurements confirmed that on a broad scale,"],[274,64.88,1.6,"the Earth is an ellipsoid."],[274,66.48,2.4,"Unfortunately, for many practical purposes,"],[274,68.88,3.2,"saying the Earth is an ellipsoid also isn’t enough."],[274,72.08,1.92,"Obviously, when you zoom way in,"],[274,74.0,4.0,"you’ve got the changing relief of mountains, valleys and oceans."],[274,78.0,2.0,"But there’s something else at play, too."],[274,80.0,4.48,"In 1828, German mathematician Carl Friedrich Gauss was trying to"],[274,84.48,3.28,"accurately map and measure his native kingdom of Hanover,"],[274,87.76,2.88,"so the government could work out how to tax everyone properly."],[274,90.64,2.0,"Of course this all started as a way to make money."],[274,92.64,3.52,"He used triangulation and plumb lines to level his equipment,"],[274,96.16,3.76,"but over large distances, the triangles he drew didn’t match up."],[274,99.92,3.44,"This led Gauss to propose a new, purely mathematical definition"],[274,103.36,3.6,"for the Earth’s shape, called its equipotential surface."],[274,106.96,2.8,"Just like the term “equidistant” describes points that are"],[274,109.76,2.48,"all of the same distance from a reference point,"],[274,112.24,3.12,"the term “equipotential” describes points that experience"],[274,115.36,2.16,"the same gravitational force as each other."],[274,117.52,3.92,"On the Earth, objects experience gravity pulling stuff towards the center,"],[274,121.44,4.48,"and a centrifugal inertial force throwing stuff outwards due to rotation."],[274,125.92,4.32,"So the equipotential surface is the place where these two things are balanced."],[274,130.24,3.04,"It’s a theoretical surface that wraps around the Earth,"],[274,133.28,3.44,"where if you placed a marble it wouldn’t roll in any direction."],[274,136.72,2.96,"Another way to think of it is that it’s the shape the oceans"],[274,139.68,3.84,"would take if they were completely calm, with no waves or tides."],[274,143.52,2.8,"Water is pulled down by gravity, and bulged out"],[274,146.32,2.72,"near the equator by the centrifugal inertial force."],[274,149.04,4.48,"So practically, the equipotential surface defines the mean local sea level."],[274,153.52,2.8,"Fifty years after Gauss, another German mathematician"],[274,156.32,4.32,"coined the friendlier word geoid to describe this theoretical mean sea level."],[274,160.64,3.52,"Now, if the world worked according to Newton’s mathematics alone,"],[274,164.16,3.68,"the geoid would be a smooth, featureless ellipsoid."],[274,167.84,1.76,"But it’s a bit more complicated than that."],[274,169.6,1.52,"Gravity is linked to mass."],[274,171.12,4.88,"And more mass means more gravity, so wherever you have big lumps of stuff,"],[274,176.0,3.92,"you get slightly more gravity than when you have less stuff."],[274,179.92,3.52,"But ultimately, density is the thing that matters, and on Earth,"],[274,183.44,3.52,"there are a lot of things that can create density variations."],[274,186.96,3.36,"Ice is less dense than water, which is less dense than rock."],[274,190.32,3.2,"Continental crust is less dense than oceanic crust."],[274,193.52,3.04,"And hot rocks and magma are less dense than cooler rock."],[274,196.56,3.52,"Each of these features on or inside the Earth creates"],[274,200.08,2.32,"what geologists call gravity anomalies,"],[274,202.4,3.76,"where the gravity reading is higher or lower than we’d expect it to be"],[274,206.16,2.4,"if the earth was one homogenous blob."],[274,208.56,3.52,"And these anomalies create so-called geoid undulations,"],[274,212.08,3.68,"which are literal height differences from the idealised ellipsoid."],[274,215.76,2.08,"So it might be a mathematical surface,"],[274,217.84,3.36,"but the geoid does physically affect the height of the oceans."],[274,221.2,2.16,"Where gravity is higher beneath the surface,"],[274,223.36,3.2,"water is pulled towards those areas, creating higher sea level."],[274,226.56,3.04,"And where gravity is lower, there’s less pull compared to other areas,"],[274,229.6,3.36,"so sea level drops, creating a literal hole in the ocean."],[274,232.96,3.84,"Even though the concept of the geoid was established in the 19th century,"],[274,236.8,3.6,"it was actually a long time before we had a way of accurately measuring it,"],[274,240.4,3.6,"because it requires a lot of very precise measurements"],[274,244.0,2.24,"made on the scale of the whole planet."],[274,246.24,2.32,"You can do it by hand, using a combination"],[274,248.56,2.32,"of astronomical and earthly measurements."],[274,250.88,3.36,"But the big leap in ability came with the help of satellites."],[274,254.24,5.76,"LAGEOS, launched in 1976, takes the prize for the funkiest of all satellite designs."],[274,260.0,2.24,"It had no electronics or moving parts."],[274,262.24,4.72,"It was basically just a giant golf ball with angled mirrors in each of its dimples,"],[274,266.96,1.6,"like a cosmic disco ball."],[274,268.56,2.96,"Ground stations would shoot lasers up to it and the mirrors"],[274,271.52,3.04,"would reflect them back, and the return time would tell scientists"],[274,274.56,2.24,"the precise distance to the satellite."],[274,276.8,4.32,"And that distance would change according to the variable pull of gravity on Earth,"],[274,281.12,2.0,"like the geoid projected into space."],[274,283.12,5.12,"Then, in 1992, the TOPEX/Poseidon satellite was able to map the geoid itself,"],[274,288.24,3.92,"by sending radar signals down from the satellite to the ocean surface"],[274,292.16,2.0,"to “see” the geoid undulations."],[274,294.16,3.68,"And since 2000, there have been multiple pairs of satellites"],[274,297.84,3.44,"that each have one satellite measure gravity’s effects on the other."],[274,301.28,1.84,"Thanks to these instruments, we now have"],[274,303.12,2.88,"a great high-res map of our planet’s geoid."],[274,306.0,4.4,"And one of the most famous representations of it is known as the Potsdam potato,"],[274,310.4,3.28,"named after the German center for geosciences that put it together."],[274,313.68,4.16,"Even after 200 years, Germany is still the place to go to understand gravity."],[274,317.84,3.12,"And just like all that science, we need funding to keep going."],[274,320.96,2.08,"So here’s an ad."],[274,323.04,2.32,"Since you’re still watching, you’re probably someone"],[274,325.36,1.68,"who likes learning science online."],[274,327.04,3.04,"And if you’re also a student in grade 5 through undergrad,"],[274,330.08,3.28,"or someone helping those students through the learning process,"],[274,333.36,3.28,"then this video’s sponsor, Brilliant, was made for you."],[274,336.64,3.28,"Brilliant is an online personal tutor for students in math,"],[274,339.92,1.76,"science, and engineering classes."],[274,341.68,4.4,"You know, really complicated stuff that a lot of folks will use AI to get through."],[274,346.08,2.48,"But Brilliant’s tutor, Koji, is the opposite."],[274,348.56,2.48,"Koji never gives you the answer. Instead,"],[274,351.04,4.32,"Koji will ask questions and guide students to find the answer themselves."],[274,355.36,4.56,"To get started with Brilliant’s tutor for free, click the link below or scan the QR code."],[274,359.92,2.64,"You can upgrade to Premium to unlock all courses."],[274,362.56,2.16,"And right now, SciShow viewers can save"],[274,364.72,5.92,"20% off an annual subscription at brilliant.org/scishow"],[274,370.64,3.76,"The Potsdam potato reshapes the surface of the Earth to its geoid,"],[274,374.4,2.24,"to show where the gravity highs and lows are."],[274,376.64,3.04,"In reality, the anomalies are nowhere near this big."],[274,379.68,2.72,"They’re often far smaller than the real hills and valleys"],[274,382.4,1.84,"that shape our planet’s surface."],[274,384.24,4.0,"But the visualisation helps to show just how varied the geoid can be."],[274,388.24,4.32,"And scientists haven’t gone to all this effort over the decades just for the fun of it."],[274,392.56,4.72,"Knowing the precise shape of the geoid is critical for making GPS actually useful."],[274,397.28,4.24,"Because satellite-based GPS calculates your position using pure geometry,"],[274,401.52,2.16,"and maps it to the ideal ellipsoid."],[274,403.68,2.88,"But in places where there are big gravity variations,"],[274,406.56,2.56,"the altitude can be off by a significant amount."],[274,409.12,3.68,"So a second calculation built into the system adds in the information"],[274,412.8,4.32,"from the geoid, correcting the altitude relative to the real local sea level."],[274,417.12,4.0,"But having a picture of the geoid is also a window into the Earth’s insides."],[274,421.12,3.04,"By comparing geoid measurements with geological features,"],[274,424.16,4.8,"geoscientists can map density differences in places our drills could never reach,"],[274,428.96,3.12,"helping us to understand what’s going underneath the surface."],[274,432.08,2.56,"Interestingly, gravity anomalies don’t always line up"],[274,434.64,2.4,"with the landscape in the way you might expect."],[274,437.04,4.88,"For instance, massive mountain ranges are a big lump of extra stuff on the surface,"],[274,441.92,2.08,"so you’d imagine they'd create more gravity."],[274,444.0,3.2,"But they’re actually made of low-density continental crust,"],[274,447.2,2.16,"and often have deep roots that displace"],[274,449.36,2.16,"the higher density mantle underneath them."],[274,451.52,3.68,"As a result, the biggest mountain ranges like the Himalayas"],[274,455.2,2.8,"actually correspond to a negative gravity anomaly."],[274,458.0,1.44,"A small gravity hole."],[274,459.44,2.56,"Instead, the highest gravity hills on the geoid"],[274,462.0,3.6,"are found where there’s a surplus of dense material beneath the surface."],[274,465.6,2.96,"There’s a significant high around Iceland, in the middle of the Atlantic,"],[274,468.56,3.44,"related to the thickening of denser oceanic crust that breaches the surface here."],[274,472.0,2.72,"Another geoid high is located in the Western Pacific,"],[274,474.72,1.52,"centered on Papua, New Guinea."],[274,476.24,3.04,"A combination of factors contribute to its higher than average gravity."],[274,479.28,2.72,"It’s a place where oceanic tectonic plates are colliding,"],[274,482.0,2.16,"so you’ve got a thickening of dense crust,"],[274,484.16,3.28,"plus an excess of dense mantle material near the surface."],[274,487.44,2.24,"But the volcanic arc setting also means"],[274,489.68,3.04,"newly created oceanic crust is piled on as well."],[274,492.72,4.88,"This shows there isn’t always a clear recipe for high or low geoid undulations."],[274,497.6,3.04,"Another case in point is the Indian Ocean Geoid Low,"],[274,500.64,3.36,"the commonly cited lowest point on the entire geoid."],[274,504.0,5.36,"Centered just over the tip of India, the geoid is 106 meters below the ellipsoid."],[274,509.36,4.8,"And it’s not only the deepest, but also the largest gravity hole on the Potato,"],[274,514.16,3.44,"spanning more than 15,000 kilometers in diameter."],[274,517.6,2.88,"Scientists still aren’t entirely sure why it exists."],[274,520.48,3.28,"Without anything obvious on the surface, there must be some seriously"],[274,523.76,2.88,"low density stuff going on within Earth’s mantle…"],[274,526.64,2.48,"that massive layer of rock beneath the crust."],[274,529.12,2.88,"One option is there’s something called a slab graveyard"],[274,532.0,3.52,"at the bottom of the mantle that’s so big and heavy it’s pushed the"],[274,535.52,4.0,"core-mantle boundary down, displacing the even denser outer core"],[274,539.52,3.2,"below the mantle, while simultaneously sending a plume"],[274,542.72,2.64,"of less dense hot mantle material upwards."],[274,545.36,2.96,"Unfortunately, the image resolution this deep in the Earth"],[274,548.32,2.0,"just isn’t good enough to say for sure."],[274,550.32,3.76,"But the Indian ocean isn’t the only place you’ll find yourself light on your feet."],[274,554.08,3.6,"There’s another gravity hole, under the Ross Sea near Antarctica."],[274,557.68,4.24,"And according to a study published in 2025, this one’s been getting deeper."],[274,561.92,2.64,"The scientists behind that study used earthquake waves"],[274,564.56,4.48,"to scan the interior of the planet, just like we use sonar to map the ocean floor."],[274,569.04,2.48,"This produced a deep earth density map that"],[274,571.52,2.8,"closely matches the distribution of gravity anomalies."],[274,574.32,4.08,"Just like in the Indian ocean, it looks like the lower gravity in Antarctica"],[274,578.4,4.24,"is caused by a combination of crustal slabs sinking to the ocean floor,"],[274,582.64,2.16,"and hot plumes closer to the surface."],[274,584.8,2.56,"But their study also revealed something surprising:"],[274,587.36,2.08,"Antarctica’s hole was on the move."],[274,589.44,2.8,"With their reconstruction of the deep mantle in this area,"],[274,592.24,2.32,"they ran a simulation that showed how the density"],[274,594.56,2.32,"and gravity anomalies changed over time."],[274,596.88,4.56,"Apparently, this gravity hole has existed for at least the last 70 million years,"],[274,601.44,2.48,"but between 50 and 30 million years ago,"],[274,603.92,3.6,"it strengthened and migrated from the south Atlantic to where it is now."],[274,607.52,2.32,"The scientists suggest this happened as a result of"],[274,609.84,3.52,"the hot plume underneath Antarctica becoming stronger."],[274,613.36,3.92,"And in fact, it represented such a big movement of mass within the mantle,"],[274,617.28,3.6,"that it was enough to shift the entire planet’s axis of rotation."],[274,620.88,2.32,"Coincidentally, this shift also coincides with"],[274,623.2,2.4,"the timeline for Antarctica freezing over."],[274,625.6,2.72,"The team suggests this gravity hole may have played a part."],[274,628.32,3.6,"Lowered gravity means that water moves away from the region to areas of"],[274,631.92,3.36,"higher gravity, pulling the local sea level down to a lower level."],[274,635.28,2.8,"This, in turn, exposes more of the continental shelf,"],[274,638.08,2.4,"which alters ocean currents and weather systems,"],[274,640.48,2.16,"and which may have contributed to Antarctica’s"],[274,642.64,2.48,"climatic isolation and the growth of ice sheets."],[274,645.12,3.04,"In all, the measurement and understanding of geoid undulations"],[274,648.16,3.6,"is a pretty young science, despite its long theoretical foundation."],[274,651.76,4.0,"We’ve not yet had a chance to see how much or how fast these gravity hills and"],[274,655.76,4.32,"valleys move, but this Antarctica study hints at the importance of finding out."],[274,660.08,3.6,"Our planet’s lumpy potato may not be a reasonable weight-loss plan,"],[274,663.68,1.49,"but it sure is useful."],[275,0.24,3.52,"Get ready, because you’re about to look a dinosaur in the face."],[275,3.76,1.28,"This is a nodosaur."],[275,5.04,3.36,"In its belly, you can still see specks of its last meal."],[275,8.4,0.8,"Fern salad."],[275,9.2,2.32,"From the traces of melanin in its bumpy skin,"],[275,11.52,3.12,"we can wager that it was reddish-brown with a light underbelly."],[275,14.64,3.92,"And those toe beans still look squishable— even though they’ve turned to stone."],[275,18.56,1.12,"Don’t you just wanna squeeze ‘em?"],[275,19.68,4.8,"Turns out, the worst day of this nodosaur’s life was a very lucky day for making a fossil."],[275,24.48,2.74,"Hi, I’m Sage and this is Crash Course Geology."],[275,31.6,0.96,"This is poop."],[275,32.56,1.12,"And it’s a fossil."],[275,33.68,2.0,"This is a bug bedazzled in fool’s gold."],[275,35.68,0.96,"And it’s a fossil."],[275,36.64,2.64,"This baby cave lion unearthed from permafrost?"],[275,39.28,1.28,"This worm sperm?"],[275,40.56,3.04,"This ant with a parasitic mushroom growing out of its butt?"],[275,43.6,1.2,"All fossils."],[275,44.8,5.36,"Fossils are preserved remains or traces of organisms that are at least 10,000 years old."],[275,50.16,4.56,"But they aren’t just shells and bones, ammonite necklaces and t-rex thighs."],[275,54.72,3.6,"Fossils can also preserve skin, feathers, or flowers."],[275,58.32,3.68,"They can freeze footprints, tunnels, and nests in time."],[275,62.0,1.92,"Even whole baby mammoths!"],[275,63.92,3.84,"Fossils are the closest we’ll ever get to long-gone organisms."],[275,67.76,4.64,"And often, they’re a snapshot of their last, worst days."],[275,72.4,4.56,"But even though everyone dies, very few fossilize."],[275,76.96,3.92,"Actually becoming a fossil is like winning the geological jackpot."],[275,80.88,3.84,"You see, fossils require very specific conditions to exist at all."],[275,84.72,4.16,"Most fossils form when living things are quickly trapped and buried in sediment,"],[275,88.88,1.84,"like mud, sand, or ash."],[275,90.72,1.84,"That’s what happened to our nodosaur friend."],[275,92.56,2.08,"I mean, I consider it a personal friend."],[275,94.64,4.72,"About 110 million years ago, the nodosaur died… somehow."],[275,99.36,5.28,"Its body floated down a river, then into the sea, as it… bloated with gas."],[275,104.64,4.8,"Eventually, the body burst and sank belly-up to the seafloor,"],[275,109.44,2.4,"where mud swirled around and buried it."],[275,111.84,1.92,"After that initial burial, there are a few different"],[275,113.76,1.6,"ways fossils can form."],[275,115.36,2.24,"The first path is permineralization."],[275,117.6,4.08,"That’s when minerals from the water in the ground leech into the pores of buried wood,"],[275,121.68,5.12,"bone, shells, or soft tissue, replace them, and leave behind a heavier, denser 3-D mold."],[275,126.8,2.4,"As sediment kept layering over our nodosaur,"],[275,129.2,4.56,"minerals infused and replaced much of its armored skin, bones, and squishy bits,"],[275,133.76,3.6,"which eventually hardened into stone. Now that’s what I call rock-hard abs."],[275,137.36,3.36,"Often, as minerals replace the original tissue, they change color."],[275,140.72,3.84,"And on rare occasions, they can actually opalize and get all glossy."],[275,144.56,1.04,"Ooooh, shiny."],[275,145.6,4.24,"Even so, it can be hard to tell permineralized fossils from regular rocks."],[275,149.84,2.64,"So allow me, your Internet science talk-talk clown,"],[275,152.48,4.48,"to let you in on a professional secret that real paleontologists use in the field:"],[275,156.96,0.8,"you lick ‘em."],[275,157.76,0.48,"No, really."],[275,158.24,1.28,"Bone is more porous than rock."],[275,159.52,3.2,"So, if it sticks to your tongue, it’s probably fossilized bone."],[275,162.72,3.6,"Dwayne, may I?"],[275,166.32,0.88,"Actually, you know what?"],[275,167.2,2.88,"I like our relationship as it is."],[275,170.08,2.0,"By the way, if you think minerals are as neat as I do,"],[275,172.08,2.48,"you’re gonna want to check out the SciShow Rocks Box."],[275,174.56,2.32,"They send cool rocks right to your home!"],[275,176.88,1.6,"Check out the link below to learn more."],[275,178.48,3.76,"Okay, so replacement by minerals— that’s the first path to fossil-hood."],[275,182.24,2.4,"Another possible path is the ol’ C and C:"],[275,184.64,1.68,"compression and carbonization,"],[275,186.32,3.6,"which usually happens to lightweights like leaves, fish, and insects."],[275,189.92,4.4,"First, layers of sediment bury an organism until it’s flatter than the acting in a B-movie."],[275,194.32,1.52,"That’s the compression part."],[275,195.84,3.2,"Then, as the organic bits decompose, carbonization begins,"],[275,199.04,4.88,"sparking chemical reactions that transform its remains into a filmy carbon silhouette."],[275,203.92,1.04,"Rest in peace."],[275,204.96,3.68,"But not all burial by sediment involves preservation of body parts."],[275,208.64,4.24,"Trace fossils preserve impressions of organisms’ behaviors from long ago,"],[275,212.88,2.32,"like walking, wiggling, or burrowing."],[275,215.2,3.04,"And they’re no less amazing than preserved skin and bones."],[275,218.24,3.76,"Like, 11,500 years ago in what’s now New Mexico,"],[275,222.0,2.56,"fossils formed from the footprints of little kids"],[275,224.56,4.0,"who stopped to play in the fresh tracks of a giant ground sloth."],[275,228.56,0.88,"And honestly?"],[275,229.44,2.16,"Every time I think about this I feel like crying."],[275,231.6,0.72,"I love us."],[275,232.32,1.44,"We are very cute animals."],[275,233.76,2.08,"And some of the rarest, wildest,"],[275,235.84,4.24,"most jaw-dropping fossils don’t involve getting buried under sediment at all."],[275,240.08,4.08,"In fact, there’s quite a few methods to choose from. Let’s hear a word from our sponsor…"],[275,244.16,3.2,"Are you dead? Or about to be?"],[275,247.36,2.56,"We here at the Preservation Station"],[275,249.92,5.12,"offer bespoke fossilization services for the discerning organism."],[275,255.04,6.4,"Whether it’s an amber tomb, or a desert cave, or a 10,000-year deep-freeze,"],[275,261.44,6.32,"we’ll match you with a special environment that will preserve all, or most, of your body,"],[275,267.76,4.4,"so you can look almost as good as the day you died."],[275,272.16,3.36,"When it works, it really works."],[275,275.52,1.2,"When it doesn’t?"],[275,276.72,2.32,"Eh. You’re already dead."],[275,279.04,4.8,"So don’t delay, slow the decomposition of your body today."],[275,286.08,0.88,"Sign me up!"],[275,286.96,3.84,"Sometimes, in really specific cases, fossils preserve whole bodies, skin and all."],[275,290.8,3.92,"Like when resin oozes off a tree and seals up a little bug before it can crawl away—"],[275,294.72,3.92,"hardening into tiny, transparent geologic coffins we call amber."],[275,298.64,3.84,"Or when a creature dies in a desert cave and dries out, becoming a mummy."],[275,302.48,4.88,"Some exceptional fossils have formed from organisms that landed in tar pits or bogs,"],[275,307.36,2.24,"while others have been found frozen in ice."],[275,309.6,2.56,"That slows down decomposition by a lot—"],[275,312.16,3.57,"keeping skin, hair, and even whiskers intact against all odds."],[275,315.73,2.19,"And I do mean all odds."],[275,317.92,5.2,"In the scheme of everything that’s lived and died on our planet, fossilization is very rare."],[275,323.12,4.4,"Usually, organisms rest in pieces, which get broken down and recycled—"],[275,327.52,3.12,"either by other organisms or geological processes."],[275,330.64,4.0,"But two main factors increase the odds of winning the fossilization lottery."],[275,334.64,3.12,"Number one: rapid burial by sediment soon after death."],[275,337.76,3.6,"That makes it more likely the remains will settle in place, undisturbed."],[275,341.36,3.12,"And number two: burial of a tough body part."],[275,344.48,4.88,"Hard bits like shells, bones, and teeth have better odds of fossilizing than soft tissue,"],[275,349.36,2.08,"which decomposes very easily."],[275,351.44,2.72,"But it’s not just extraordinary to become a fossil."],[275,354.16,4.32,"It’s extraordinary for a fossil to stick around long enough for us to find it."],[275,358.48,3.52,"Erosion and weathering are constantly wiping the geologic record clean"],[275,362.0,1.52,"like a big Etch A Sketch."],[275,363.52,5.6,"Because Earth’s surface is constantly changing, the fossil record will always be incomplete."],[275,369.12,2.72,"So the nodosaur fossil really beat the odds."],[275,371.84,1.68,"First, it became a fossil."],[275,373.52,4.96,"Then, it lay undisturbed for millions of years, 250 feet below the surface."],[275,378.48,2.24,"And then, it was discovered by chance in 2011,"],[275,380.72,4.16,"when some oil-sands miners in Alberta, Canada, drilled into it by accident."],[275,384.88,2.64,"As one of them put it, they were “like kids on Christmas morning.”"],[275,387.52,2.24,"I would be, too, if I got to help dig up a dinosaur."],[275,389.76,0.8,"Lucky ducks."],[275,390.64,4.0,"When we are lucky enough to find fossils, we can learn a lot about how our planet—"],[275,394.64,3.2,"and the life it supports— have changed over time."],[275,397.84,3.68,"Let’s check out one of my geology rock stars…"],[275,401.52,5.2,"Roughly 400 million years ago, a fishy critter managed to leave the sea and walk on land."],[275,406.72,3.04,"If it hadn’t, you and I wouldn’t be here right now."],[275,409.76,1.28,"But which came first?"],[275,411.04,2.16,"Living on land, or evolving legs?"],[275,413.2,3.2,"Paleontologist Jennifer Clack spent a lot of time thinking about that mystery—"],[275,416.4,2.32,"when she wasn’t busy riding her motorcycle."],[275,418.72,4.4,"By the 1980s, scientists understood how today’s back-boned land animals—"],[275,423.12,1.76,"including humans— came to be."],[275,424.88,3.04,"They evolved from the first tetrapods, or creatures with four legs,"],[275,427.92,4.8,"which themselves evolved from the first back-boned creatures that arose in ancient seas."],[275,432.72,3.44,"But nobody understood exactly how the transition from living in water"],[275,436.16,2.8,"to walking on land had happened."],[275,438.96,3.04,"So in 1987, Clack headed to Greenland with some colleagues"],[275,442.0,3.04,"and returned with a trove of early tetrapod fossils,"],[275,445.04,3.28,"including a big-mouthed beast she named Boris."],[275,448.32,4.64,"It took years for Clack to extract Boris from the chunk of rock he was fossilized in."],[275,452.96,2.72,"But what she discovered was incredible."],[275,455.68,1.92,"At the back of Boris’s skull,"],[275,457.6,2.48,"rod-like structures suggested the creature had"],[275,460.08,4.24,"arteries for breathing through both gills and lungs."],[275,464.32,3.28,"Boris also had legs with feet shaped like paddles."],[275,467.6,3.2,"They were too small to fully support the animal’s weight for walking,"],[275,470.8,2.48,"but they definitely were not fins!"],[275,473.28,1.36,"As Clack herself put it:"],[275,474.64,3.6,"Boris was basically “a fish with fingers.”"],[275,478.24,4.48,"With this fossil, Clack solved the mystery of how our fishy ancestors"],[275,482.72,2.8,"transitioned from the ocean to the shore."],[275,485.52,2.8,"The legs and lungs came first."],[275,488.32,2.08,"Then came life on land."],[275,490.4,2.88,"Fossils like Boris and the nodosaur are as close as we can get"],[275,493.28,3.28,"to witnessing the dramas of the living things that came before us."],[275,496.56,4.21,"Sometimes, really exceptional troves of fossils turn up, like Earth’s storage units."],[275,500.88,3.52,"Except they hold way cooler stuff than a couch that doesn’t fit in your apartment."],[275,504.4,0.5,"Literally."],[275,504.9,4.46,"They’re called Lagerstätte, which comes from the German words for “storage” and “place.”"],[275,509.36,6.32,"Lagerstätten can be full of lots of fossils, or a few really well-preserved fossils, or both."],[275,515.68,1.6,"Like, in the Canadian Rocky Mountains,"],[275,517.28,4.96,"the Burgess Shale rock formation teems with fossils from more than half a billion years ago,"],[275,522.24,1.52,"during the Cambrian period."],[275,523.76,3.6,"Some of those early critters are the relatives of today’s animals, including us."],[275,527.36,4.4,"Hi, great-great-great-great-grandpa!"],[275,531.76,1.2,"And others, like Hallucigenia,"],[275,532.96,3.92,"are so weird it took scientists over three decades just to figure out where the head was."],[275,536.88,3.76,"I think I’m supposed to pet… that end?"],[275,540.64,3.04,"Other fossils in the Burgess Shale are evolutionary dead-ends—"],[275,543.68,3.44,"lifeforms that died out en masse, near the end of the Cambrian."],[275,547.12,1.6,"We’re not exactly sure why."],[275,548.72,4.12,"But understanding which life survived and evolved in the aftermath of past mass"],[275,552.84,3.64,"extinctions can help us understand the repercussions of ecological change today."],[275,556.48,3.92,"Pour one out for the trilobites, y’all."],[275,560.4,2.08,"Fossils also tell us the story of Earth itself,"],[275,562.48,2.8,"and how our life-sustaining mutha has changed over time."],[275,565.28,2.32,"Like, we know that the rocks in today’s mountain ranges"],[275,567.6,2.4,"formed from ancient seafloors that were forced upwards,"],[275,570.0,2.64,"thanks to sea creature fossils found on mountaintops."],[275,572.64,3.76,"And fern and reptile fossils found on opposite sides of the world helped us"],[275,576.4,2.21,"figure out that continents move."],[275,578.64,3.28,"Fossils are like Polaroid pictures of a dead thing’s last day on Earth."],[275,581.92,3.12,"And they’re also clues to how life has changed and adapted over time,"],[275,585.04,4.16,"in a big, wild story that includes us— and is still unfolding today."],[275,589.2,3.04,"They invite us to imagine a time and place beyond the little slice"],[275,592.24,1.36,"we get to live and breathe in."],[275,593.6,3.68,"So let’s hear it for the few, the elite, the fossilized."],[275,597.28,2.32,"Shout-out to the ant with a mushroom in its butt."],[275,599.6,2.36,"Next time, grab your parka; we’re talking about glaciers."],[275,601.96,1.16,"I’ll see you then."],[275,603.12,2.08,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Geology"],[275,605.2,2.16,"which was filmed at our studio in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[275,607.36,2.16,"and was made with the help of all these awesome people."],[275,609.52,2.8,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[275,612.32,2.07,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[276,7.0,3.92,"In the 1500s, there was a little dog called turnspit"],[276,11.01,2.13,"whose job was to roast meat."],[276,13.63,3.84,"The dog was a widespread kitchen attendant until the mid-1800s,"],[276,17.6,2.5,"when new tech took the breed's trade."],[276,20.73,4.05,"The turnspit was just one of a vast constellation of dog breeds,"],[276,24.77,1.79,"past and present."],[276,26.9,2.17,"The immense physical variety of dogs"],[276,29.07,3.46,"makes them among the world’s most diverse species,"],[276,32.7,2.88,"and likely the one with the greatest range in size."],[276,36.28,3.67,"A Pomeranian, for example, might weigh two kilograms,"],[276,39.99,3.46,"while a Mastiff could tip the scale to 100."],[276,43.91,1.58,"So, why is this?"],[276,45.58,3.38,"And what does a dog’s breed actually say about them?"],[276,49.67,2.13,"All dogs descend from ancient wolves"],[276,51.8,4.17,"that started living around humans at least 15,000 years ago."],[276,56.26,5.13,"Dogs are the first domesticated species, by a landslide of thousands of years."],[276,61.68,4.92,"Over time, it's thought that humans began relying on dogs for specific tasks"],[276,66.65,3.04,"and selectively breeding them for particular traits,"],[276,69.94,4.09,"fueling the dramatic diversification of dogs planet-wide."],[276,74.53,4.71,"By 11,000 years ago, they’d already assumed an array of appearances."],[276,79.41,4.0,"And finally, formalized dog breeding took off in Victorian England."],[276,84.12,2.88,"Instead of mating dogs to emphasize their function,"],[276,87.0,4.34,"people selectively inbred dogs to achieve particular features"],[276,91.34,3.13,"and established overarching breed standards."],[276,94.76,3.59,"But while maintaining specific lines of inheritance and characteristics"],[276,98.34,1.88,"is important to kennel clubs,"],[276,100.26,2.13,"breed genetics are complex."],[276,102.85,4.25,"In one study where scientists sequenced the genomes of different dog breeds,"],[276,107.39,2.71,"10 major groups emerged that roughly aligned"],[276,110.11,2.79,"with the lineage’s historic working role—"],[276,113.28,3.67,"from pointing and flushing to scent-tracking and retrieving."],[276,117.36,1.21,"Within different breeds,"],[276,118.57,4.25,"scientists may be able to identify distinct genetic signatures,"],[276,122.83,3.79,"but it's difficult to untangle how those genetic signatures"],[276,126.66,2.75,"actually shape breed-defining traits."],[276,129.62,1.58,"This is especially true"],[276,131.38,5.38,"when it comes to parsing how much a dog’s breed informs their personality,"],[276,137.22,4.34,"as defined by their consistent behaviors across a range of scenarios."],[276,142.35,2.92,"When researchers in one study compared the genetic data"],[276,145.27,1.54,"from many different dogs,"],[276,146.81,3.54,"they found about 9% of a dog’s personality traits"],[276,150.35,2.96,"were attributed specifically to its breed."],[276,153.82,2.88,"Some of the traits in the study that were most heritable"],[276,156.69,3.38,"seemed to be derived from long-standing predatory behaviors"],[276,160.07,2.38,"also exhibited in wolves."],[276,162.62,3.38,"This is seen in how border collies stalk with their eyes,"],[276,166.0,2.96,"and how golden retrievers chase and grab-bite."],[276,169.29,3.38,"And other traits also seem to have gotten ingrained along the way."],[276,172.96,3.54,"For example, breeds like Portuguese water dogs"],[276,176.5,3.25,"do tend to be especially comfortable getting wet;"],[276,180.59,2.59,"retrievers have an aptitude for fetch;"],[276,183.18,3.38,"and for dogs like huskies, malamutes, and hounds,"],[276,186.56,1.96,"howling seems habitual."],[276,188.81,2.54,"How well a dog responds to human direction"],[276,191.35,3.54,"was another of the most heritable behavioral traits by breed,"],[276,194.98,2.25,"with border collies standing out."],[276,198.03,3.21,"Other behaviors— including circling before pooping,"],[276,201.24,2.67,"and how easily provoked or aggressive a dog is—"],[276,204.12,2.88,"don’t appear to be super breed-specific."],[276,207.45,5.21,"So, while pit bull terriers are widely considered to be more aggressive dogs,"],[276,212.96,2.96,"this research casts doubt on their reputation."],[276,216.88,2.79,"Toy breeds did appear to be more independent"],[276,219.67,1.96,"and less sociable with other dogs,"],[276,221.72,2.34,"but non-genetic factors could be at work."],[276,224.51,4.46,"For example, smaller dogs might not initiate much play with other dogs"],[276,228.97,3.42,"because they're small or how they're socialized,"],[276,232.44,4.0,"and not necessarily due to how their genes wire their brains."],[276,237.11,3.0,"The same study found that, beyond breed,"],[276,240.19,5.51,"less than 25% of a dog's personality was accounted for by their genes."],[276,246.49,4.21,"So, genetics play a role, but they’re not nearly everything."],[276,251.21,3.84,"This is also illustrated in service dog breeding programs,"],[276,255.04,3.54,"which deliberately breed certain dogs based on their temperament."],[276,258.75,3.04,"This yields lots of dogs suited for service—"],[276,261.92,4.05,"but because of individual variation, some still don't make the cut."],[276,266.64,4.3,"As much as humans have heavily tinkered with canine characteristics,"],[276,271.23,4.55,"dog behavior is shaped by a complex mix of their genes and environment."],[276,276.23,3.21,"And breed alone appears to be a pretty poor proxy"],[276,279.57,3.96,"for all that makes up the personality of a given pup."],[277,8.2,1.68,"We don't live in a perfect world."],[277,9.92,4.92,"I wish we did, especially when it comes to fighting climate change."],[277,14.88,2.64,"Sure, we have 30 percent of the world on clean energy,"],[277,17.52,3.08,"but we still depend a lot on fossil fuels for the near future."],[277,21.76,2.2,"Take the Science Based Targets initiative."],[277,24.0,3.36,"It's a leading decarbonization initiative for businesses."],[277,27.4,1.68,"Businesses have taken a target,"],[277,29.08,4.52,"on an average, of 6.4 percent emission reductions per year,"],[277,33.6,2.4,"year on year, till they go net zero."],[277,36.72,1.12,"That's great news."],[277,39.92,3.68,"But what happens to the other 93.6 percent?"],[277,43.6,3.6,"My fight is on that 93.6 percent."],[277,47.92,2.08,"Here's the red triangle, is what I call it."],[277,50.04,4.24,"What happens between now and then?"],[277,55.32,2.24,"Who takes care of these emissions?"],[277,57.6,2.88,"Who's taking responsibility for these emissions"],[277,60.52,2.48,"that we are leaving out there in the atmosphere?"],[277,64.16,1.92,"Here's where carbon credits come in."],[277,67.28,3.16,"One carbon credit equals one ton of carbon"],[277,70.48,3.32,"that is either removed, reduced or avoided."],[277,73.84,1.96,"These don't come from thin air."],[277,75.84,4.16,"They're third-party audited, certified using international standards."],[277,80.68,4.08,"These credits can then be bought by companies"],[277,84.8,2.52,"to offset their emissions."],[277,87.32,3.0,"Not the 6.4 percent they are to reduce --"],[277,90.36,1.72,"it's for this 93.6,"],[277,92.12,1.84,"and that's the important point."],[277,94.0,1.52,"We're not saying do not reduce."],[277,95.56,2.52,"You reduce, but go beyond,"],[277,98.12,2.52,"because this red triangle is important."],[277,101.32,4.28,"This is also not a license to pollute."],[277,105.64,5.08,"This allows companies to take responsibility for these emissions, yes,"],[277,110.76,4.36,"but it also allows these companies to work beyond their boundaries"],[277,115.16,2.8,"and help things like the energy transition,"],[277,118.0,4.84,"things like planting mangroves in some other country for the communities,"],[277,122.88,1.68,"the frontline communities"],[277,124.6,2.92,"who bear the brunt of climate change every day of their lives."],[277,127.52,3.8,"I actually call them the first responders, not the vulnerable."],[277,132.08,2.36,"It's an important distinction to make."],[277,135.88,5.88,"Solutions can and should reach every part of the planet,"],[277,141.8,3.8,"from the tropics of Papua to the deserts of the Sahel,"],[277,145.64,1.92,"to the settlements of the Amazon,"],[277,147.6,1.32,"everywhere."],[277,149.56,2.6,"Think of carbon credits as a bridge."],[277,154.16,1.32,"An agile bridge."],[277,155.52,2.32,"It gets you funding right now."],[277,157.88,2.24,"We need finance now ..."],[277,161.0,1.8,"and that's an important concept."],[277,163.76,3.56,"Think back to the red triangle, the 93.6."],[277,168.76,6.56,"So sometimes, we’re called the Wild Wild West, the carbon markets."],[277,175.92,3.8,"I have been working in carbon for almost two decades now,"],[277,179.76,3.96,"and I have seen the difference it can make."],[277,185.32,1.32,"I'll take you to Indonesia."],[277,186.64,4.48,"There was this NGO that reached out to us in 2021 and said,"],[277,191.16,3.16,"\"We want to build a wall of mangroves.\" I said, \"Why?\""],[277,194.36,3.72,"So in 2004, when the tsunami hit all those islands"],[277,198.12,2.16,"which had standing mangroves,"],[277,200.32,3.08,"the waves, they were tackled faster,"],[277,203.44,3.8,"and hence, there was less destruction in those islands."],[277,207.28,2.68,"Again, you have to remember that public climate finance"],[277,209.96,2.2,"is slow to reach these last-mile communities,"],[277,212.16,3.28,"because it's steeped in bureaucracy and geopolitics."],[277,215.44,2.56,"Geopolitics, people, the world we live today."],[277,219.56,4.08,"And they came to us, and we made it happen, through carbon credits."],[277,223.68,2.44,"There you go, a wall of mangroves, baby mangroves,"],[277,226.16,1.6,"but they grow up really quickly."],[277,228.24,4.64,"Sometimes, these credits, and I hear this a lot,"],[277,232.92,1.28,"\"It's a scam.\""],[277,235.6,3.8,"And I get it, because there have been some high-profile failures"],[277,239.4,2.76,"about carbon markets that you might have heard about."],[277,242.76,2.96,"And I get it, but not all projects are failures,"],[277,245.76,3.16,"and not all failures are the end of the story."],[277,250.08,2.28,"Let me talk about one of my failures."],[277,252.84,3.08,"The same mangroves we planted in Indonesia,"],[277,255.96,2.64,"out of those, in one area of Sumatra,"],[277,258.64,3.2,"there was this massive flood in the fall of 2024."],[277,261.88,2.4,"Those walls of mangroves disappeared."],[277,264.32,2.96,"The floods came in and took all those trees."],[277,267.32,2.24,"Does that mean all those carbon credits"],[277,269.56,2.24,"that we claimed for the projects were bogus?"],[277,272.36,3.72,"No, because we account for these risks."],[277,276.12,2.08,"In any carbon standards and methodologies,"],[277,278.24,1.88,"we have things like dispersed planting,"],[277,280.12,2.6,"risk management and buffers."],[277,282.72,1.8,"Buffers is an important concept."],[277,284.56,3.36,"Every project keeps aside about 15 to 20 percent of carbon credits"],[277,287.96,2.2,"for exactly this reason."],[277,290.2,3.08,"So the credit and the claim stands."],[277,293.64,3.6,"This is important, because most of climate action"],[277,297.28,3.04,"will happen in places where the risk is high."],[277,301.0,2.36,"That's the way it should be done."],[277,303.4,1.88,"But yes, we recognize these risks,"],[277,305.32,3.64,"and we account for these risks every time we develop these projects."],[277,309.0,1.36,"And this is not just for us."],[277,310.4,2.08,"Most carbon projects would have to do that."],[277,312.48,3.2,"A good project, if done properly, can make a massive difference."],[277,317.6,1.8,"I often hear about ..."],[277,321.72,1.16,"\"Polluters pay.\""],[277,322.92,2.6,"I go to conferences and I see people holding up signs,"],[277,325.56,1.32,"\"Polluters pay.\""],[277,326.92,3.52,"And I almost question, saying, \"Polluters pay, but pay who?\""],[277,331.84,1.16,"Right?"],[277,333.04,3.4,"So carbon markets actually allow for these polluters to pay directly"],[277,336.48,2.88,"to the people who are taking climate action."],[277,339.92,4.16,"This is important: directly to people who are taking climate action."],[277,344.12,4.64,"And the planet, frankly, could not care where your decarbonization comes from."],[277,349.12,1.16,"Audience: Woo!"],[277,351.12,1.48,"Sandeep Roy Choudhury: Right?"],[277,352.6,1.28,"So case in point,"],[277,353.88,2.68,"it costs 75 dollars to reduce one ton of carbon,"],[277,356.56,2.0,"on average, in the European Union."],[277,359.2,3.8,"In Southeast Asia, that average is 15 dollars a ton."],[277,363.8,4.96,"You're getting five times more impact, value for your buck,"],[277,368.8,5.28,"and you're getting I don't know how many more times of the social impact."],[277,374.12,2.48,"I have seen it firsthand."],[277,376.64,2.2,"Let me take you to Kashmir,"],[277,378.88,1.76,"[to] a distant village in India,"],[277,380.68,1.56,"where the grid will never reach."],[277,382.24,2.16,"Solar home lighting systems here."],[277,384.44,1.28,"Same project in Indonesia."],[277,385.76,4.16,"Women microenterprises making batik products using mangrove pigments."],[277,389.92,1.48,"Livelihoods."],[277,391.96,3.44,"Meet Injara. He’s an ex-poacher in Madagascar,"],[277,395.44,1.44,"now plants mangroves with us."],[277,397.24,1.16,"Social change."],[277,399.76,1.76,"A football team in Kigoma, Tanzania."],[277,401.56,4.28,"These are women who plant agroforestry systems with us in Kigoma,"],[277,405.88,1.16,"but play football."],[277,407.08,2.2,"We have village-level football competitions."],[277,409.28,4.52,"Now community building cannot be decoupled from nature restoration."],[277,413.84,2.0,"This is a very important point."],[277,415.88,1.6,"And this is a scale up [program]."],[277,417.48,3.08,"This clean cooking concrete kitchen [program] in rural Bangladesh"],[277,420.56,3.0,"was languishing at about 500,000 households in 2012."],[277,423.6,4.28,"We took it up to 6.5 million households in the next few years."],[277,427.92,3.08,"Scale is of essence on climate action,"],[277,431.04,1.64,"and that's where markets can help."],[277,433.92,2.04,"This is all possible because,"],[277,435.96,3.52,"when climate action is rooted in people and possibilities,"],[277,439.48,2.6,"markets allow us to explore those possibilities."],[277,443.72,3.36,"Sometimes, I hear about the accounting."],[277,447.12,2.4,"\"Ah, but the accounting is not solid.\""],[277,449.52,3.6,"I get it, but which part of climate science is solid accounting?"],[277,453.16,2.64,"The science improves, so does the accounting."],[277,455.84,3.52,"We also worry about things like Indigenous rights,"],[277,459.4,3.84,"consent on local populations, benefit-sharing mechanisms."],[277,463.28,2.04,"Please understand that we worry about it;"],[277,465.36,1.4,"so do the carbon standards."],[277,467.88,3.04,"A carbon finance is not a grant, it's a transaction."],[277,470.96,3.48,"It talks to the equality of the transactor and the transacted."],[277,474.48,3.76,"A farmer transitioning to a cleaner practice is being paid for a service."],[277,478.28,1.76,"It's not a dole-out."],[277,480.08,3.92,"These people, these programs have nowhere else to go for money."],[277,485.36,3.4,"So companies will reduce what they can,"],[277,488.8,3.04,"and they will invest in carbon credits for what they can't."],[277,491.88,1.96,"It's the right thing to do."],[277,493.88,2.8,"But today, all of the market detractors make it impossible,"],[277,496.68,3.12,"or make it so much harder for companies who are actually trying."],[277,501.0,1.6,"This will not solve this problem."],[277,502.6,5.64,"We have to keep trying, for mistakes will happen, and that's OK."],[277,508.28,2.76,"What is not right is to let go of the only tools that work"],[277,511.08,1.44,"for the Global South."],[277,512.84,4.72,"Today, we are making inaction feel safer than action."],[277,518.68,1.92,"That's not how this gets solved."],[277,520.64,3.32,"So let's fix what needs fixing."],[277,524.0,3.36,"Let's take responsibility for all those emissions"],[277,527.4,1.48,"and leave no emissions behind."],[277,528.88,1.36,"Price every ton."],[277,530.28,2.96,"That's the only responsible thing to do."],[277,533.28,1.16,"Thank you."],[278,4.92,1.58,"In August 2017,"],[278,6.54,3.13,"the genocide in Myanmar had exploded overnight."],[278,10.01,3.96,"The military were killing Rohingya people, setting their villages on fire."],[278,14.34,3.88,"This was part of the ethnic cleansing of the Rohingya people."],[278,18.26,4.42,"The women and men who had escaped were now flooding across the border,"],[278,22.73,2.96,"carrying infants and young children on their backs."],[278,26.02,1.42,"Within four months,"],[278,27.44,3.54,"over 850,000 people crammed into refugee camps"],[278,31.03,1.25,"in Bangladesh,"],[278,32.28,1.96,"in a town called Cox's Bazar."],[278,34.78,2.84,"I am Bangladeshi, and I live in Dhaka."],[278,37.95,4.96,"I have spent my career working to make sure children have access to play"],[278,42.91,3.38,"when everything else in their lives has been in chaos."],[278,46.33,4.55,"I have worked with children for over three decades now through BRAC,"],[278,50.92,1.58,"the world's largest NGO."],[278,52.92,4.13,"While the Rohingya refugees were arriving by the thousands in Bangladesh,"],[278,57.09,2.79,"I got an urgent call from my BRAC colleague."],[278,59.93,4.8,"He told me I had to go visit the refugee camps in Cox's Bazar"],[278,64.77,2.71,"and see how the children were doing."],[278,67.48,3.59,"I thought about how much those kids had been through."],[278,71.11,3.09,"They saw things that no child should ever see."],[278,74.61,3.04,"I wondered how would they ever recover."],[278,78.28,4.0,"I believed play might help them reconnect with what they lost:"],[278,82.33,2.59,"their culture and their sense of home."],[278,85.33,2.67,"I believed play might heal them."],[278,88.0,5.13,"And what we discovered over time was that it was not just any kind of play"],[278,93.17,1.83,"that would help them heal."],[278,95.01,4.21,"It was specifically play from their own Rohingya culture"],[278,99.22,3.63,"that helped them feel pride, gain confidence"],[278,102.89,4.42,"and experience joy after being violently forced to leave behind their home."],[278,107.94,2.17,"So I'll go back to the beginning."],[278,110.15,5.71,"After that phone call in 2017, I traveled to the camps in Cox's Bazar."],[278,115.9,3.67,"Aid workers had set up safe spaces for the children."],[278,119.57,2.84,"I found children drawing guns in their art."],[278,122.74,2.0,"That was not unusual,"],[278,124.74,2.59,"but I saw something that surprised me."],[278,127.33,4.0,"Someone had donated Scrabble sets and Uno cards for the children."],[278,131.38,1.88,"It was a generous gesture,"],[278,133.29,3.17,"but the children had never seen these games before."],[278,136.47,2.79,"They didn’t know the rules. They didn’t speak the language."],[278,139.3,3.09,"Even the adult facilitators didn't know how to play."],[278,142.43,2.75,"They were arranging the Scrabble tiles into patterns"],[278,145.22,2.34,"because that's what made sense to them."],[278,147.98,4.17,"Standing there, I thought, there's got to be a better way."],[278,152.15,4.67,"These kids were used to wide-open spaces with trees and flowers,"],[278,156.82,2.13,"and now they were crammed into a refugee camp"],[278,158.94,1.88,"with a million other people."],[278,161.82,5.05,"Every shelter was a copy-paste of the next: bamboo frames, tin roof,"],[278,166.91,3.63,"blue and black plastic tarp held back the monsoon rain."],[278,170.54,3.75,"The air smelled like still water and human waste."],[278,174.67,2.38,"But none of the kids ever complained."],[278,177.34,2.38,"And there were no signs of frustration."],[278,179.76,2.96,"Instead, some kids were just withdrawn."],[278,182.76,1.54,"Others were restless."],[278,184.35,3.0,"Some wouldn't engage with kids or with adults."],[278,187.39,1.75,"This was painful to watch,"],[278,189.18,3.42,"but typical of kids who have gone through such extreme conditions."],[278,192.65,4.92,"But even the kids who were withdrawn would come alive and express more joy"],[278,197.57,3.0,"when they were playing in a certain way:"],[278,200.95,2.04,"the Rohingya way."],[278,202.99,3.25,"So for the next few months, we watched them"],[278,206.28,2.38,"and we asked lots of questions."],[278,208.66,1.79,"What did you play in Myanmar?"],[278,210.5,1.29,"How did you play it?"],[278,211.79,1.5,"How did you decorate your home?"],[278,213.33,1.17,"Why?"],[278,214.54,1.33,"What does it mean?"],[278,215.92,2.46,"Children, parents, grandparents and siblings"],[278,218.42,1.88,"would show and tell us."],[278,220.34,1.17,"From the kids,"],[278,221.51,2.71,"we heard the stories their grandparents told."],[278,224.26,3.21,"We heard rhymes that their siblings knew by heart."],[278,227.51,1.42,"We watched physical games"],[278,228.97,2.54,"that their parents did when they were little,"],[278,231.52,2.75,"and we watched them make traditional Rohingya art,"],[278,234.27,4.63,"the kind of art that would be hanging in every household back home."],[278,239.36,1.17,"And from that,"],[278,240.57,3.38,"we built what we now call the “Humanitarian Play Lab:”"],[278,243.95,3.5,"small welcoming spaces for kids three to five,"],[278,247.49,2.79,"decorated by the Rohingya community itself,"],[278,250.29,3.17,"with hand-embroidered cloth hanging from the ceiling"],[278,253.46,3.8,"and artwork of flowers and homes and animals,"],[278,257.29,1.92,"images that the children recognize"],[278,259.25,3.25,"from a life they were forced to leave behind."],[278,262.55,4.0,"Each Play Lab is facilitated by young Rohingya women,"],[278,266.6,4.38,"who spend up to five hours simply playing with the kids."],[278,271.02,3.63,"Each Play Lab is also supported by other women"],[278,274.64,2.29,"trained in psychosocial support"],[278,276.98,2.67,"to help kids manage their emotions."],[278,279.69,3.09,"At 9am, those kids leave their home"],[278,282.82,3.13,"to walk to the Play Lab through narrow passageways."],[278,285.99,3.79,"It takes them only three minutes to step into a different world"],[278,289.83,1.79,"while still in the camp."],[278,291.66,2.38,"A Rohingya woman, their play facilitator,"],[278,294.08,2.09,"greets 30 young children."],[278,296.17,2.59,"She asks, \"How are we today?"],[278,298.79,1.63,"Are we all OK?"],[278,300.42,2.34,"Here we are, like friends and siblings."],[278,302.8,4.21,"At home, respect those who are older and love those younger.\""],[278,307.05,2.67,"Then she asks, \"Who wants to lead the rhyme?\""],[278,309.76,2.25,"One child steps forward to lead."],[278,312.35,2.13,"Hands are up, bodies move."],[278,314.52,1.79,"They chant, they play,"],[278,316.35,2.04,"they jump, they giggle,"],[278,318.4,1.42,"they listen to stories,"],[278,319.86,1.42,"they draw."],[278,321.27,1.79,"One of the girls is Noor."],[278,323.11,1.88,"Noor is just eight years old."],[278,325.03,3.96,"Five years ago, Noor was undernourished, withdrawn and shy."],[278,328.99,3.17,"She was physically weak with continuous diarrhea."],[278,332.2,3.29,"When Noor started coming to the Play Lab at the age of three,"],[278,335.54,1.54,"she sat quietly."],[278,337.12,1.29,"She watched."],[278,338.46,3.13,"Then she learned the rope game from the facilitator."],[278,341.63,3.0,"The game she plays is a traditional Rohingya one."],[278,345.13,2.0,"Two kids hold a long rope,"],[278,347.18,1.71,"flipping it back and forth,"],[278,348.93,2.84,"while Noor leaps over it without touching it,"],[278,351.81,2.38,"reciting a rhyme while she's jumping."],[278,354.18,1.46,"She's tracking the rope,"],[278,355.68,1.88,"she's controlling her feet"],[278,357.56,1.54,"and she keeps the words coming,"],[278,359.14,1.38,"all at once."],[278,362.02,1.46,"Noor kept playing."],[278,363.52,2.42,"As she played, she became stronger."],[278,365.94,4.13,"Her mother noticed that Noor became less shy and [more] confident,"],[278,370.12,1.46,"and once Noor learned it,"],[278,371.62,1.79,"her mother encouraged her,"],[278,373.41,4.59,"telling her to jump higher, go further, be bolder."],[278,378.0,3.8,"When you watch her jump rope, you forget for a moment where you are."],[278,381.83,1.58,"She doesn't just jump."],[278,383.46,1.5,"She flies."],[278,385.0,3.75,"Now Noor walks around the camp with a rope in her hand,"],[278,388.8,2.59,"just in case she finds a space to jump."],[278,391.76,2.63,"She may still be shy to speak to strangers,"],[278,394.43,2.88,"but she will jump in front of any audience."],[278,397.73,4.09,"That's not a small thing for a kid who used to fade into the background."],[278,401.81,2.38,"Noor's mother played this game as a girl."],[278,404.23,2.92,"\"This is not a Bangladeshi game,\" she says."],[278,407.19,3.25,"\"We Rohingya call this 'Rosi Fal Doní.'"],[278,410.49,2.21,"This has made my daughter stronger."],[278,413.03,1.84,"She's not scared anymore."],[278,414.91,2.29,"She has courage.\""],[278,417.83,1.63,"Then there is Muhammad."],[278,419.5,2.25,"Muhammad is about seven years old."],[278,421.79,2.54,"He lives with his parents and four siblings,"],[278,424.38,2.92,"and he is known as the seesaw boy in his neighborhood."],[278,427.84,2.92,"This game is not the seesaw that we imagine."],[278,430.8,3.75,"The Rohingya seesaw is where two kids wrap their legs around each other"],[278,434.6,1.29,"like acrobats"],[278,435.93,3.46,"and swing together, going up and down."],[278,439.85,4.0,"At the Play Lab, he learned something that was even more important"],[278,443.9,2.71,"than the skill to balance during the game."],[278,447.02,3.34,"Muhammad learned that he needs to be careful"],[278,450.4,1.71,"while playing the seesaw game."],[278,452.49,2.75,"One wrong move and the other kid will get hurt."],[278,456.12,4.75,"Children learn to pay attention to someone else's body before their own."],[278,460.91,2.71,"So he prefers not to play with the younger kids,"],[278,463.62,1.79,"not because he doesn't want to,"],[278,465.46,3.25,"but because he is concerned that he might hurt them"],[278,468.75,1.67,"because of his strength."],[278,471.01,3.67,"Playing taught him to pay attention to how others might feel."],[278,475.18,4.34,"He became more thoughtful of his mother and his younger siblings."],[278,479.97,1.96,"When he gets biscuits in school,"],[278,481.98,3.42,"he chooses to bring [them] for his younger siblings instead,"],[278,485.44,2.59,"and he cleans the house and washes the pots"],[278,488.02,2.67,"so that his mother can get some rest."],[278,491.11,2.13,"He hopes to be a teacher someday."],[278,493.74,2.29,"Now let's talk about Tuhinur."],[278,496.03,2.0,"Tuhinur is four years old."],[278,498.49,2.92,"As a two-year-old, she was shy and quiet."],[278,501.41,3.75,"Other children teased her and called her Fuli, meaning fat."],[278,505.21,1.67,"She never responded."],[278,506.92,2.21,"She cried silently to her mother."],[278,509.46,2.96,"Her mother, Yasmin, worried how she would raise her daughter"],[278,512.47,1.54,"to be more assertive."],[278,514.42,3.04,"When Yasmin became a facilitator at the Play Lab,"],[278,517.51,1.79,"she brought Tuhinur with her."],[278,519.76,4.63,"Soon, Yasmin noticed that Tuhinur would be the first to raise her hand"],[278,524.39,1.42,"to lead the Kabia,"],[278,525.85,2.17,"a traditional Rohingya rhyme"],[278,528.06,2.84,"with hand movements and body gestures."],[278,530.9,2.38,"Tuhinur learned the entire rhyme"],[278,533.28,2.42,"and when and how to move her body"],[278,535.74,2.75,"and when and how to add hand movements."],[278,538.83,2.25,"She now leads the Kabia."],[278,541.41,2.0,"She stands at the front,"],[278,543.41,3.0,"reciting the words in a clear, loud voice"],[278,546.42,4.04,"and moving her hand and body in a way that the other kids follow."],[278,551.17,2.5,"Some of those following her are older."],[278,553.72,1.88,"She doesn't seem to notice."],[278,556.51,2.67,"Leading the Kabia meant doing this perfectly"],[278,559.22,2.5,"so that others could learn and follow."],[278,562.14,2.25,"She became the lead at age three."],[278,564.98,3.92,"Tuhinur loves leading the rhyme so much that she will practice at night"],[278,568.94,3.38,"with her shadow on the bamboo wall and dance with it."],[278,572.9,3.34,"Yasmin says Tuhinur is bold"],[278,576.28,2.92,"and no one can tease her anymore."],[278,580.16,1.88,"These are just a few examples,"],[278,582.08,2.96,"and we've been running the Play Lab since 2017,"],[278,585.08,1.54,"reaching 50,000 children"],[278,586.66,3.88,"across 350 humanitarian Play Labs in Cox's Bazar."],[278,590.54,4.3,"We've also used this approach in the Rhino refugee camp in Uganda."],[278,594.84,2.88,"We've been working on it for the last five years"],[278,597.72,3.54,"and will be scaling and setting up 150 Play Labs,"],[278,601.3,3.84,"reaching 63,000 children in Uganda."],[278,605.18,2.75,"I believe this work really matters."],[278,608.35,5.67,"The Play Lab helps children to get ready to enter school feeling confident"],[278,614.07,1.46,"and proud."],[278,615.57,1.42,"When I visit the camp now,"],[278,617.03,5.46,"parents say “Shukriya,” or thank you, for recognizing Rohingya play."],[278,622.49,2.54,"The children here didn't choose to be refugees."],[278,625.08,3.29,"They didn't choose to leave behind their home and where they belonged."],[278,628.37,1.21,"But through play,"],[278,629.58,3.92,"we’ve helped them rediscover their own culture --"],[278,633.54,1.79,"the Rohingya culture."],[278,635.38,1.38,"And in doing so,"],[278,636.76,2.59,"they showed us something extraordinary:"],[278,639.38,2.46,"their ability to find confidence,"],[278,641.89,1.54,"joy and pride,"],[278,643.43,3.04,"even when a place is so unfamiliar."],[278,646.52,2.54,"And that is something that every child deserves,"],[278,649.1,2.38,"not just the children in Cox's Bazar."],[278,651.52,5.25,"Every child everywhere deserves the right to play their own play."],[278,656.78,1.17,"Thank you."],[279,8.21,2.0,"Consider these six plants."],[279,10.26,4.42,"It might not seem like going out on a limb to call them all trees."],[279,14.89,3.71,"But botanically speaking, only three make the cut."],[279,18.93,1.88,"Take a guess which."],[279,24.02,3.79,"Generally, true trees are tall, woody plants"],[279,27.82,2.79,"that have leaves and one load-bearing trunk."],[279,30.9,2.46,"They begin their lives with primary growth,"],[279,33.4,4.55,"where a soft stem develops upwards until secondary growth kicks in"],[279,37.95,2.79,"and the stem bulks out into a woody trunk."],[279,41.04,3.79,"From there, trees keep growing taller from their crown,"],[279,44.92,4.46,"and wider, so new rings expand their trunks annually."],[279,49.92,3.71,"Working off that rubric, identifying a tree might seem simple."],[279,53.8,1.71,"But not so fast."],[279,55.76,4.8,"What makes or breaks a tree can come down to some pretty specific characteristics,"],[279,60.68,4.0,"based on how the plant develops as a result of how it evolved."],[279,64.89,5.05,"We only see trees among seed plants, which consist of two groups:"],[279,69.94,2.25,"gymnosperms and angiosperms."],[279,72.23,4.04,"All gymnosperms are woody plants, and some of those are trees."],[279,76.36,2.54,"All angiosperms are flowering plants—"],[279,78.91,3.59,"some of which are woody, and some of those are trees."],[279,82.79,2.5,"Plants assume a variety of forms."],[279,85.29,1.5,"Tree is one of them."],[279,86.96,5.3,"There are also shrubs, which grow as clusters of larger, woody stems—"],[279,92.34,4.21,"like lavender, an angiosperm, and ephedra, a gymnosperm."],[279,96.84,3.67,"And then some angiosperms, like mint, are herbs,"],[279,100.51,3.75,"because they grow from herbaceous, non-woody stems."],[279,104.52,4.13,"But mint and lavender are actually part of the same family—"],[279,108.86,4.3,"one that also includes hardwood teak trees."],[279,113.69,4.46,"That’s because these planty forms are all evolutionarily tangled up,"],[279,118.16,4.29,"and all trees don’t comprise one closely related group,"],[279,122.45,1.58,"like insects or mammals."],[279,124.25,4.67,"Many plants actually charted entirely different evolutionary paths"],[279,128.92,2.17,"to reach tree status."],[279,131.42,1.54,"Apple trees, for example,"],[279,132.96,4.75,"are closer kin to rose shrubs and herbaceous strawberry plants,"],[279,137.72,5.46,"than to avocados and guava trees, which are also pretty distantly related."],[279,143.56,4.67,"Over millions of years, some plant lineages became less tree-like,"],[279,148.23,4.0,"while others became increasingly arborescent."],[279,152.77,4.05,"But not every lineage has what it takes to become a tree."],[279,157.65,2.88,"All plants exhibit primary growth."],[279,160.62,3.88,"And all gymnosperms also display secondary growth,"],[279,164.49,1.25,"making them woody."],[279,166.12,3.63,"However, angiosperms tend to fall into two groups,"],[279,169.75,2.54,"and only one yields woody plants."],[279,172.54,4.88,"All angiosperms sprout early leaves distinct from those that grow later;"],[279,177.59,5.3,"some— dicots— sprout two seed leaves, while monocots sprout just one."],[279,182.89,3.79,"Monocots and dicots also grow differently from there."],[279,187.14,3.38,"Monocots don’t undergo woody secondary growth,"],[279,190.6,2.04,"so none of them are trees."],[279,192.9,5.71,"Some dicots, however, do, and some woody dicots are trees,"],[279,198.74,1.96,"though plenty aren't."],[279,201.28,5.09,"But why can dicots grow wood and be trees— and monocots can’t?"],[279,206.58,3.25,"Well, the earliest angiosperms were probably woody,"],[279,209.83,3.34,"much like their non-flowering relatives, the gymnosperms,"],[279,213.17,1.83,"which likely evolved first."],[279,215.29,5.09,"Dicots seem to have kept the propensity for woodiness embedded in their DNA,"],[279,220.47,3.67,"even as the characteristic has switched off at different points,"],[279,224.22,3.54,"while monocots seem to have lost it completely."],[279,228.81,2.42,"So, okay, back to these six."],[279,231.56,4.46,"Each has its quirks, but these are the true trees of the bunch."],[279,236.23,2.54,"The Brazilian grape tree just looks like that"],[279,238.78,3.63,"because its fruit grows directly from its trunk and branches—"],[279,242.53,2.08,"a trait called cauliflory."],[279,244.99,4.04,"Giant baobabs’ bulbous trunks store water."],[279,249.12,3.54,"And bristlecone pines, some of the oldest trees,"],[279,252.66,3.13,"grow slow and sturdy in their cold habitats,"],[279,255.84,2.79,"twisted by millennia of high winds."],[279,259.09,3.59,"The remaining three plants grow in tree-ish shapes"],[279,262.68,2.29,"but lack critical qualities."],[279,265.55,4.25,"All three are angiosperms, and more specifically, monocots."],[279,269.93,5.13,"Their stems are tall and thick, but made of herbaceous primary growth,"],[279,275.19,2.25,"not wood from secondary growth."],[279,277.65,3.59,"Bananas have pseudostems with soft centers,"],[279,281.24,3.13,"surrounded by hardened, overlapping leaves,"],[279,284.36,3.75,"and are related to bird-of-paradise and ginger plants."],[279,288.66,3.38,"Joshua trees are succulents, like agaves."],[279,292.2,3.84,"And palms are pretty closely related to grasses."],[279,296.21,4.3,"However, among angiosperm dicots that do produce wood,"],[279,300.5,3.71,"the line between shrub and tree can be blurry."],[279,304.63,2.75,"Characteristics like height and trunk diameter"],[279,307.43,1.92,"might be used to reach a verdict."],[279,309.81,3.0,"But because those metrics change over time,"],[279,312.81,5.21,"a plant like juniper may switch from shrub to tree within its own lifetime."],[279,318.36,4.88,"Plant evolution is twisty and complex, and tree-ness is no exception."],[279,323.53,3.25,"And even the arborescent plants that don't technically make it"],[279,326.78,1.63,"into the official tree club"],[279,328.41,3.21,"are certainly tree enough by other measures."],[279,331.66,2.54,"So, no shade to them."],[280,4.6,5.96,"They're setting off on the greatest overland trek made by any animal."],[280,32.84,2.92,"But wherever grass-eaters travel,"],[280,35.76,2.72,"predators lie in wait."],[280,44.72,1.48,"Here they are –"],[280,47.16,2.2,"Arctic wolves."],[280,55.68,1.92,"They must seize their chance"],[280,57.6,2.4,"while the caribou pass through their territory."],[280,96.0,2.2,"The wolf runs at the herd,"],[280,98.2,3.0,"trying to flush out the weak or the slow."],[280,110.16,2.08,"A calf is separated."],[280,120.04,2.84,"At full tilt – 40 miles an hour –"],[280,122.88,2.2,"the wolf is just faster."],[280,133.16,2.96,"But the calf has stamina."],[280,165.12,2.16,"Only a few weeks old,"],[280,167.28,3.64,"and this calf's will to survive is remarkable."],[280,175.92,2.28,"And it needs to be,"],[280,178.2,3.24,"for these young caribou have now started a journey"],[280,181.44,2.6,"that will last a lifetime."],[280,186.68,5.12,"Forever chasing the seasonal growth of the grass on which they depend."],[280,200.52,1.92,"Like all grassland creatures,"],[280,202.44,3.04,"they are at the mercy of these unpredictable"],[280,205.48,3.16,"but ultimately bountiful lands."],[280,214.36,4.08,"Grass can survive some of the harshest conditions on Earth –"],[280,218.44,2.84,"flood, fire and frost –"],[280,221.28,1.96,"and still flourish."],[280,232.96,3.6,"So it is that grasslands provide a stage"],[280,236.56,4.04,"for the greatest gatherings of wildlife on planet Earth."],[281,4.37,3.94,"A couple of years ago, I stood on the streets in eastern Ukraine,"],[281,8.34,4.4,"in Kramatorsk, in the aftermath of a Russian cruise missile strike"],[281,12.74,2.34,"on a residential apartment building."],[281,16.28,4.1,"I remember the front of the building was blown off."],[281,20.42,3.44,"You could see a living room and a kitchen inside, belongings burnt,"],[281,23.85,2.47,"scattered on the floor, mixed in with the rubble."],[281,26.36,3.44,"There were some weapons inspectors on one side,"],[281,29.83,2.07,"an excavator in the middle,"],[281,31.93,1.8,"and of course, in amongst it all,"],[281,33.76,4.17,"the journalists were there, trying to interview those involved."],[281,37.97,3.97,"One lady in particular had soot on her face."],[281,42.74,1.37,"It was very moving to watch."],[281,44.14,2.07,"She gathered her belongings from the rubble,"],[281,46.21,1.5,"and she put them on the floor,"],[281,47.71,2.44,"and she sat on them for safekeeping."],[281,50.18,5.27,"These are the sorts of scenes that are broadcast around the world every night."],[281,55.45,1.8,"And I sat and drew this image --"],[281,57.29,2.0,"that’s her in the black at the front."],[281,59.79,2.3,"I found them to be so moving."],[281,62.13,5.71,"And let me tell you -- the destruction is truly captivating."],[281,69.4,2.2,"But around the back of the same building,"],[281,71.64,4.24,"I found a library of books had been blasted into a rose garden,"],[281,75.91,6.34,"and in amongst the thorns and the roses stood Petro, age 70."],[281,82.28,3.14,"On his morning walk, he couldn't go any further."],[281,85.45,3.27,"He felt it was his duty to stop, pick up the books,"],[281,88.75,1.8,"stack them in neat little piles."],[281,91.96,2.23,"And as I drew him, he challenged me,"],[281,94.22,2.37,"and he said \"I was brought up"],[281,96.63,3.17,"that if I see a loaf of bread on the floor, I'll pick it up."],[281,99.83,1.44,"It's the same with books."],[281,101.3,2.07,"They are food for the soul.\""],[281,104.1,3.74,"I've drawn mass graves in Ukraine,"],[281,107.87,2.6,"a cesarean in Afghanistan"],[281,110.51,2.33,"and the war in Syria."],[281,112.84,4.17,"And over two decades, I’ve had one realization over and over,"],[281,117.05,2.97,"that there are many truths to war."],[281,120.05,5.34,"But on that morning in Kramatorsk, in Ukraine, it was so stark."],[281,125.39,2.1,"It was Petro that captured my heart,"],[281,127.53,3.84,"this gentle soul standing amidst atrocity."],[281,132.56,1.64,"This is what I do."],[281,134.23,4.27,"I draw for magazines and newspapers, in ink, on location."],[281,138.54,3.57,"And over weeks and weeks in Ukraine,"],[281,142.14,4.34,"it became my mission to be slow, to delve deeper."],[281,146.51,5.71,"And I found that the news can tell us what happened,"],[281,152.25,2.64,"but the drawing can show us how it felt."],[281,155.85,5.14,"In Kyiv, I met Madame Olga, aged 99."],[281,161.03,4.47,"She was too old to get out of her bed when the air-raid sirens rang."],[281,165.53,3.0,"I remember she had a Bible tucked under her pillow."],[281,168.57,3.34,"She was very muddled, she was confusing Putin and Hitler,"],[281,171.94,4.47,"and she held the translator's hands about six inches from her face."],[281,176.44,4.67,"And in this moment of wonderful clarity, she said,"],[281,181.15,3.94,"\"If you be quiet, I will tell you the story from the beginning.\""],[281,185.12,2.5,"She was born in 1923."],[281,187.65,3.0,"She was taken to a collective farm with her father."],[281,190.69,1.87,"She survived the Holodomor --"],[281,192.56,2.87,"the great famine which killed four million Ukrainians."],[281,196.03,5.47,"When World War II started, she worked as a slave for a Nazi family."],[281,201.53,4.04,"She recounted the story about one time they fed her worms."],[281,205.6,3.27,"She ran away, on another occasion, to the police station,"],[281,208.87,3.37,"but the policeman returned her straight back to the house."],[281,212.28,4.91,"When the war ended, she began to walk home to Ukraine."],[281,217.18,3.67,"She met her husband on the way -- future husband, I should say."],[281,220.88,1.2,"They got married."],[281,222.12,1.17,"She had four children."],[281,223.32,2.3,"She lived another 80 years."],[281,225.66,2.44,"If it hadn't been for the process of drawing,"],[281,228.09,2.17,"I wouldn’t have shared this moment with Olga,"],[281,230.29,2.07,"and I wouldn't be able to share it with you."],[281,232.4,2.77,"She died a couple of months after we met,"],[281,235.2,3.57,"peacefully, in her sleep, before her 100th birthday."],[281,240.24,3.27,"I believe the news is failing us."],[281,245.04,4.04,"It promotes destruction before humanity."],[281,249.88,2.54,"It's polarizing and fatiguing."],[281,252.45,2.24,"It gives the illusion of certainty."],[281,254.72,3.9,"You might feel helpless or hopeless."],[281,258.62,2.9,"We'd certainly be forgiven for turning it off."],[281,262.23,4.5,"My antidote is to wander the streets and stand on corners."],[281,266.73,3.2,"I work with local translators and fixers"],[281,269.97,2.87,"to interview the unsung and the understated."],[281,273.6,2.2,"Like Oleg, 23-year-old"],[281,275.81,4.2,"mixed martial arts fighter and football fan from Ukraine."],[281,280.01,4.37,"He joined the military to fight the Russians with his football friends."],[281,284.42,3.57,"But on this day, I drew him being served borscht"],[281,288.02,3.8,"by his mother, Lisa, the English teacher from that town,"],[281,291.82,3.24,"and he posed for me with his Kalashnikov,"],[281,295.09,3.87,"but also, more unusually, with his guinea pig, Almond."],[281,300.8,5.47,"And throughout the sitting, he whispered to Almond affectionately,"],[281,306.3,1.44,"\"You are a big pig.\""],[281,309.87,1.27,"\"You are a big pig.\""],[281,315.81,3.6,"It's people like Sergei that remind me that these stories are continuing"],[281,319.45,1.57,"as I stand in front of you."],[281,321.05,2.5,"Perhaps the most ordinary of them all."],[281,323.59,6.31,"An architect whose house fell on his head when it was hit by a Russian missile."],[281,329.93,4.1,"And I met him in the hospital, bandage wrapped around his head."],[281,334.06,3.87,"I asked if I could draw him and the nurse translated."],[281,337.97,3.7,"And he looked at me down his nose and he grinned from ear to ear, and said,"],[281,341.7,2.67,"\"Yes, but I will also draw you.\""],[281,345.78,3.9,"And, very formally, a date was arranged for the next day,"],[281,349.71,2.54,"and I came back and we sat in one of the wardrooms."],[281,352.25,3.54,"The windows were still sellotaped up in case there was an explosion."],[281,355.79,1.87,"And we sat and we drew each other."],[281,357.69,2.9,"We didn't share any common language."],[281,360.62,2.74,"We were both shy about our drawings."],[281,363.39,6.11,"Like all good artists, he blamed his tools for the quality of his picture."],[281,369.53,6.21,"And he'd probably be secretly proud to know that I'm showing you this."],[281,382.91,3.27,"Sergei, this is your story."],[281,386.22,4.37,"Of course, these are only four stories from Ukraine."],[281,390.62,5.61,"But over the years, in Syria and Tajikistan, Gaza and Iraq,"],[281,396.26,3.77,"I have met and drawn hundreds of other people."],[281,401.0,2.67,"But why, in the age of immediate news,"],[281,403.7,3.34,"do you need to know about Almond, the guinea pig,"],[281,407.04,1.8,"or Sergei the architect"],[281,408.87,2.64,"or Olga, the great-great-grandmother?"],[281,411.88,3.4,"Or Hamdo, Lisa, Dimitri?"],[281,416.18,2.23,"Well perhaps it’s obvious:"],[281,418.42,2.77,"I believe they could so easily have been one of us."],[281,422.95,2.9,"They are the true consequences of war."],[281,427.32,2.9,"And I tell you about them, I share them with you tonight"],[281,430.26,5.81,"because they are ordinary people who had terror poured on their lives"],[281,436.1,2.2,"and resisted it with their hearts."],[281,440.14,4.97,"I would like to finish with a confession."],[281,445.14,2.04,"More of an admission, if you like,"],[281,447.21,3.24,"but a difficult one for artists, I think, to admit."],[281,450.48,4.37,"As it turns out, it isn't really about the painting at all."],[281,454.88,6.21,"That's just an excuse to sit a little longer, to give time."],[281,461.12,2.14,"We've heard about listening accurately today."],[281,463.29,1.83,"This is about observing,"],[281,465.16,2.17,"seeing properly."],[281,467.33,2.84,"Drawing is the only way I know how to do that."],[281,470.2,3.34,"But today, I invite you --"],[281,474.3,2.27,"more than that actually, if I'm allowed --"],[281,476.57,4.44,"I urge you to witness these stories and not consume them."],[281,481.48,2.47,"To be deliberately slow"],[281,483.95,2.84,"and to cherish human connection."],[281,487.75,1.27,"Thank you for listening."],[282,4.42,3.24,"4:35 am, the alarm goes off."],[282,8.7,3.24,"5 am, a 50K training ride."],[282,12.98,3.68,"6:30 am, a strength session,"],[282,16.7,1.92,"often fighting through muscle fatigue"],[282,18.66,3.24,"and sometimes even period cramps."],[282,22.78,2.48,"8 am, breakfast,"],[282,25.3,3.72,"with a nutrition plan calculated to the gram."],[282,29.98,3.8,"9 am, off work or university."],[282,33.82,2.12,"Because many female athletes"],[282,35.98,3.52,"juggle jobs or study with their sporting careers."],[282,40.66,5.52,"Now that's a fairly typical morning for many elite female athletes."],[282,46.22,1.6,"Strong,"],[282,47.86,2.44,"disciplined, driven"],[282,50.3,2.0,"and constantly measured."],[282,53.98,3.44,"Isn't the female body extraordinary?"],[282,58.42,1.56,"It adapts."],[282,60.02,1.16,"It endures."],[282,61.94,2.0,"It creates life."],[282,65.14,2.92,"It recovers from injury, responds to training"],[282,68.1,3.12,"and achieves feats of skill, strength and power"],[282,71.22,2.0,"once thought impossible."],[282,73.82,2.52,"And yet, in 2025,"],[282,76.38,2.24,"women are still judged more for how they look"],[282,78.62,1.68,"than for what they can do."],[282,81.42,3.72,"Judged more for how they look"],[282,85.14,1.76,"than for what they can do."],[282,87.98,1.24,"At the top of their game,"],[282,89.22,4.6,"up to 70 percent of elite female athletes report body image concerns."],[282,94.22,2.48,"You heard me, up to 70 percent."],[282,97.22,2.0,"Now this is women with Olympic medals,"],[282,99.26,2.44,"premiership trophies, world records,"],[282,101.74,4.92,"and still they feel the pressure to look a certain way,"],[282,106.7,4.0,"which is often at direct opposition with how they need to build their bodies"],[282,110.74,1.56,"for competitive success."],[282,114.18,2.44,"Now this topic is such a passion of mine"],[282,116.62,3.32,"because I live and work in this space every day."],[282,120.98,4.88,"I am surrounded by some of the most intelligent, hard working,"],[282,125.9,2.44,"resilient women in sport."],[282,129.02,1.6,"Women who can outrun,"],[282,130.66,3.96,"outlift and outthink most people in the room,"],[282,134.62,3.88,"and yet they battle a world that comments on their appearance"],[282,138.54,1.6,"before their capability."],[282,141.14,3.28,"How is this fair, I ask you."],[282,146.3,2.08,"At the top of her game at world number one,"],[282,148.42,1.4,"Ash Barty walked away,"],[282,149.86,3.56,"choosing to define success on her own terms."],[282,154.1,3.84,"Elite Olympic gymnast Simone Biles reminded the world"],[282,157.98,3.8,"that mental and physical well-being are performance foundations,"],[282,161.82,1.48,"not weaknesses."],[282,163.62,4.12,"And here in Australia, AFLW players are juggling work,"],[282,167.78,3.36,"study and public commentary about how they look in their uniform"],[282,171.18,2.6,"when all they want to do is play."],[282,174.82,1.52,"Why is this still happening?"],[282,177.38,4.32,"Why are we so comfortable judging athletes on aesthetics?"],[282,182.3,1.84,"And what are we going to do about it?"],[282,185.34,2.64,"Now this talk isn’t just about elite athletes."],[282,188.42,3.68,"It's about what happens when performance and appearance collide."],[282,192.14,2.04,"Yes, in sport,"],[282,194.18,3.12,"but also in schools, in workplaces,"],[282,197.34,1.92,"and even in our own homes."],[282,200.46,3.16,"When we celebrate appearance over capability,"],[282,203.66,2.48,"we send that message to our daughters,"],[282,206.14,3.08,"our sons and our colleagues"],[282,209.26,2.36,"that how we look matters more than what we do."],[282,211.66,2.2,"And that doesn't just hold athletes back."],[282,214.9,1.64,"That holds all of us back."],[282,218.9,1.84,"So to start to make a real difference,"],[282,220.74,3.12,"we have to understand where the pressure and commentary comes from"],[282,223.9,1.76,"in the first place."],[282,225.7,2.44,"Now let's talk about the sources of pressure."],[282,228.18,1.92,"Some are external."],[282,230.1,2.56,"The uniform that feels exposing,"],[282,232.7,2.52,"the commentator that talks about how she looks"],[282,235.22,1.6,"before how she plays,"],[282,236.86,4.2,"the constant scrolling through social media of the perfect body."],[282,241.94,2.0,"And others are internal."],[282,244.34,3.08,"The comparison of skin folds between teammates."],[282,247.9,2.8,"The coach that says, \"You need to be leaner\""],[282,250.7,2.28,"with very little context."],[282,253.5,3.92,"And the athlete herself, chasing unattainable goals."],[282,258.62,2.84,"And then there is language,"],[282,261.5,5.2,"the invisible force that can fuel or fracture performance."],[282,267.82,1.64,"I once worked with an athlete"],[282,269.46,3.2,"who crumbled after being told, “You’re too heavy to compete.”"],[282,274.38,2.16,"That same athlete later thrived"],[282,276.58,2.04,"when the conversation was reframed"],[282,278.66,5.48,"to “Improving your repeat sprint ability will give you a competitive edge."],[282,284.46,3.32,"Let's get to work on a training and fueling program"],[282,287.82,1.88,"and make that happen.\""],[282,291.5,2.0,"In sport and in life,"],[282,293.54,2.84,"language is not just communication,"],[282,296.38,1.68,"it's a performance tool."],[282,299.06,2.84,"Research has shown that even subtle word choices"],[282,301.94,2.16,"from coaches, parents and peers"],[282,304.1,4.92,"can significantly impact an athlete’s confidence, motivation"],[282,309.06,1.92,"and even risk of disordered eating."],[282,312.06,5.32,"The words that we use are the stories that athletes tell themselves."],[282,319.7,4.36,"Now I'm going to talk about quite a taboo topic of physique."],[282,324.82,3.48,"Now I know this might be controversial, but here we go."],[282,328.9,2.6,"Physique matters."],[282,331.5,1.76,"At the highest level,"],[282,333.3,3.24,"different sports require different builds."],[282,336.54,2.2,"Lean mass for power in football,"],[282,338.78,2.48,"strength-to-weight ratio in gymnastics,"],[282,341.3,1.76,"lighter frames for endurance."],[282,343.06,1.48,"So no,"],[282,344.54,3.8,"in elite sport, we should not ban body composition assessment"],[282,348.38,4.28,"because if used correctly, it is an invaluable performance tool."],[282,353.98,2.2,"However, the damage occurs"],[282,356.22,2.8,"when those numbers are used as an identity."],[282,359.86,1.48,"The public weigh ins,"],[282,361.38,2.56,"the comparison between teammates"],[282,363.98,2.6,"when measurement is used as punishment."],[282,367.06,2.8,"That's when science becomes shame."],[282,370.82,2.04,"And we've all seen the fallout."],[282,373.58,3.2,"Serena Williams, 23-time Grand Slam champion,"],[282,376.78,3.4,"was told her power made her look too muscular."],[282,381.26,3.32,"AFLW player Tayla Harris was body shamed"],[282,384.62,2.52,"for an image that should have been celebrated"],[282,387.18,2.92,"as pure athletic brilliance."],[282,390.62,1.96,"And even Olympic champion Emma McKeon"],[282,392.62,3.48,"has spoken openly about the pressure of appearance in swimming."],[282,397.14,1.52,"So the take home?"],[282,398.7,4.28,"Yes, physique and performance may be linked,"],[282,402.98,3.32,"but appearance should never overshadow capability."],[282,408.22,2.96,"So what happens when the balance tips the wrong way?"],[282,411.54,2.52,"Well, athletes can under-fuel."],[282,414.54,2.96,"And from here, we see the cascade of events."],[282,417.78,1.8,"Hormonal dysregulation,"],[282,419.62,1.28,"menstrual dysfunction,"],[282,420.94,2.92,"fatigue, bone loss, slower recovery."],[282,423.9,1.2,"The result?"],[282,425.42,1.52,"Performance declines,"],[282,426.98,2.76,"strength plateaus, injuries mount."],[282,430.82,3.08,"And this isn't just specific to athletes."],[282,434.86,1.8,"Think about your own life."],[282,437.26,3.48,"When was the last time you skipped a meal,"],[282,440.74,2.4,"trained exhausted or didn't sleep well?"],[282,443.86,1.64,"Did you perform better?"],[282,446.66,1.4,"Well neither do they."],[282,448.38,2.52,"Except their livelihoods depend on it."],[282,452.5,4.28,"Prolonged low-energy availability has been shown to reduce power output,"],[282,456.82,2.72,"endurance capability and decision making."],[282,459.58,4.56,"And that is why we cannot separate body image from performance."],[282,466.14,3.68,"So is this all doom and gloom, or how do we fix it?"],[282,470.94,3.52,"Well we build environments that are performance-focused"],[282,474.5,1.68,"and athlete-centered."],[282,476.86,1.16,"So what does that mean?"],[282,478.06,1.48,"Well it means education"],[282,479.58,3.56,"in fueling, in energy availability, in menstrual health,"],[282,483.18,1.92,"and not just for athletes."],[282,485.1,1.6,"For coaches as well."],[282,487.78,1.96,"It means individualization"],[282,489.74,5.76,"because optimal body composition is specific to the individual athlete."],[282,496.38,2.8,"Language protocols that are private,"],[282,499.18,2.12,"functional and constructive."],[282,502.02,2.92,"And choice in testing,"],[282,504.98,2.28,"in uniform and in timing."],[282,508.94,1.4,"And here's the thing."],[282,510.66,3.44,"When we do these things, performance improves."],[282,514.9,2.68,"I've seen teams transform."],[282,517.62,2.52,"Confidence rises, engagement lifts,"],[282,520.18,1.48,"results follow."],[282,522.46,2.76,"And this is not about lowering standards"],[282,525.26,2.28,"or wrapping athletes up in cotton wool."],[282,528.14,5.08,"It's about raising awareness so we can pursue that excellence safely,"],[282,533.26,2.56,"intelligently and sustainably."],[282,537.22,2.36,"Now some sports are leading the way in this space."],[282,539.62,2.08,"IOC safe sport initiatives"],[282,541.7,2.64,"and professional organizations like Swimming Australia,"],[282,544.38,3.12,"who have invested in education, language training"],[282,547.54,1.68,"and measurement standards."],[282,549.26,2.16,"But it takes everyone,"],[282,551.42,3.2,"coaches, administrators and the public."],[282,556.5,3.2,"Now you might still be sitting there thinking, really?"],[282,559.7,2.04,"What does this have to do with me?"],[282,562.74,3.36,"Again, this is not just about elite sport."],[282,566.14,4.24,"All of us have felt the weight of appearance in some way."],[282,571.26,5.68,"For women, that might be to be smaller, thinner, more feminine."],[282,576.98,4.52,"For men it may be to be taller, bigger, more masculine."],[282,582.18,1.28,"Think about it."],[282,584.06,4.72,"When was the last time you compared yourself in the mirror,"],[282,588.82,2.08,"at the gym or online?"],[282,592.34,1.6,"Praised for losing weight"],[282,593.98,3.64,"when no one is actually asking if you're happy or healthy?"],[282,598.62,2.16,"Or took 20 photos before posting one"],[282,600.82,2.56,"because you didn't like the way you looked?"],[282,604.54,3.08,"Now imagine that pressure times by a thousand."],[282,607.66,3.72,"Your body constantly scanned, discussed"],[282,611.38,2.48,"and linked directly to your contract."],[282,615.02,2.12,"And men aren't immune to this either."],[282,618.1,2.12,"Research has shown that male athletes"],[282,620.26,3.2,"and teenagers also struggle with body image."],[282,623.5,1.68,"Maybe not to be thinner,"],[282,625.18,4.6,"but to be broader, stronger, more muscular, more defined."],[282,630.14,1.8,"Different pressure,"],[282,631.98,1.24,"same story."],[282,634.66,3.72,"The reality is, we're all living under performance expectations"],[282,638.42,1.48,"shaped by appearance."],[282,640.38,2.32,"But for young women getting into sport out there,"],[282,642.7,1.16,"I want you to know"],[282,643.9,2.68,"your body is not the barrier to success."],[282,647.14,2.12,"It is the vehicle that will get you there."],[282,651.06,1.48,"So what's the takeaway?"],[282,653.26,3.28,"That it's time to redefine female performance"],[282,656.58,2.96,"not by size, shape or aesthetics,"],[282,659.58,4.76,"but by power, capability, confidence and resilience."],[282,665.18,1.88,"For coaches and parents,"],[282,667.1,1.92,"use language that builds."],[282,669.38,3.68,"For teachers, focus on effort and progress."],[282,673.46,4.36,"For fans, celebrate strength and courage and not appearance."],[282,678.18,1.52,"And for friends and colleagues,"],[282,679.7,3.76,"notice when you praise looks over capability."],[282,683.5,2.4,"Because words shape beliefs,"],[282,685.94,2.2,"beliefs shape behaviors,"],[282,688.18,2.12,"and behaviors shape performance."],[282,691.58,4.8,"When we shift the conversation, we don't just create healthier athletes,"],[282,696.38,2.32,"we create stronger performances."],[282,699.18,1.6,"And outside of sport,"],[282,700.82,5.36,"we create a society where women feel free to show up unapologetically,"],[282,706.18,1.84,"just as they are,"],[282,708.06,1.84,"and still perform at their best."],[282,710.62,1.16,"Thank you."],[283,7.71,6.55,"In the early 1900s, physicists Niels Bohr and Hendrika Johanna van Leeuwen"],[283,14.38,2.42,"separately discovered something shocking."],[283,17.39,6.21,"According to every known rule of physics, magnetism was impossible."],[283,25.02,1.83,"This finding made no sense."],[283,27.07,4.13,"Humans had relied on magnetic compasses for a thousand years,"],[283,31.19,3.34,"and anyone who had ever held two magnetized metals"],[283,34.53,3.92,"could tell you that some invisible force was acting on them."],[283,38.78,3.0,"Bohr and van Leeuwen knew they were missing something—"],[283,42.08,1.04,"but what?"],[283,43.87,5.42,"By the 1920s, physicists had learned quite a bit about the fundamentals of matter."],[283,49.46,2.79,"They knew that every material is made of atoms,"],[283,52.26,5.34,"each comprised of a nucleus containing protons and orbited by electrons."],[283,57.93,2.38,"They also knew that both of these particles"],[283,60.31,2.25,"have a few fundamental properties,"],[283,62.56,3.75,"including a small amount of mass, an electrical charge,"],[283,66.31,2.17,"and some amount of magnetism."],[283,69.11,4.75,"The origin of these intrinsic properties remains an ongoing mystery."],[283,73.86,5.0,"But the result is that every electron and proton is a tiny magnet"],[283,78.95,3.13,"that works just like their full-size counterparts."],[283,82.33,5.0,"Both have invisible magnetic fields that radiate out in closed loops"],[283,87.46,1.75,"from one end to the other."],[283,89.5,3.46,"These magnetic poles are attracted to the dense field lines"],[283,92.96,2.29,"of the opposite pole of other magnets,"],[283,95.26,3.79,"and repelled by fields flowing in the opposite direction."],[283,99.55,2.71,"Of course, researchers still had questions."],[283,102.6,3.09,"What determines the direction of these magnetic fields?"],[283,105.81,3.21,"And if every atom is composed of tiny magnets,"],[283,109.11,2.29,"why isn't everything magnetic?"],[283,112.28,3.79,"The answers emerged with the birth of quantum mechanics:"],[283,116.28,4.75,"a new branch of physics exploring how the laws of nature work differently"],[283,121.03,1.58,"at the sub-atomic level."],[283,123.41,4.84,"In 1926, a pair of Dutch physicists discovered that—"],[283,128.25,3.13,"in addition to mass, charge, and magnetism—"],[283,131.59,3.46,"particles had another fundamental property:"],[283,135.59,1.79,"quantum spin."],[283,138.01,4.04,"This is a confusing label because while nothing is actually spinning,"],[283,142.22,4.0,"spin does refer to a kind of innate direction"],[283,146.27,2.5,"that influences a particle’s behavior."],[283,149.23,4.5,"And in the 1930s, Nobel Prize laureate John H. Van Vleck"],[283,153.82,2.5,"discovered that this property held the answer"],[283,156.32,3.09,"to countless questions about magnetism—"],[283,159.78,2.59,"including Bohr and van Leeuwen’s."],[283,162.53,3.25,"Basically, the direction of a particle’s spin"],[283,166.08,3.04,"determines the direction of its magnetic field."],[283,169.54,4.21,"And since electrons have much stronger magnetic fields than protons,"],[283,173.75,4.96,"the direction of their spin determines whether or not something is magnetic."],[283,179.3,4.42,"If enough of a material's electrons spin in the same direction,"],[283,184.06,3.42,"their many tiny magnetic fields will align,"],[283,187.72,5.55,"turning the material into a magnet with a field pointing in that direction."],[283,193.77,3.59,"The more aligned electrons, the stronger the magnet."],[283,197.94,1.04,"On the other hand,"],[283,198.99,3.84,"if a material is full of electrons with competing spins"],[283,203.03,2.38,"their fields cancel each other out,"],[283,205.62,2.84,"leaving you with something non-magnetic."],[283,209.04,3.29,"Most elements have electrons that come in pairs"],[283,212.33,2.46,"and spin in opposite directions,"],[283,214.79,2.84,"which is why most materials aren’t magnetic."],[283,218.13,4.0,"But iron, nickel, cobalt, and a few rare metals"],[283,222.34,3.25,"prefer to leave some of their electrons unpaired."],[283,225.85,2.71,"This doesn’t automatically make them magnetic—"],[283,228.56,2.29,"just because their electrons are unpaired"],[283,230.85,2.67,"doesn’t mean they always spin in the same direction."],[283,233.85,3.42,"However, they can be nudged into alignment"],[283,237.28,2.33,"by interacting with neighboring atoms."],[283,239.74,2.5,"And researchers can kick-start this process"],[283,242.24,2.63,"by applying a strong magnetic field"],[283,245.12,5.84,"or by heating and cooling the material inside a weaker magnetic field."],[283,251.71,5.3,"These metals will stay magnets permanently unless their electrons are scrambled"],[283,257.0,3.88,"by intense heat or an extremely strong magnetic field."],[283,261.34,4.96,"But this magnetization process can make anything magnetic temporarily."],[283,266.72,5.67,"Felix Bloch and Edward Mills Purcell took advantage of this in 1945"],[283,272.39,4.5,"when they invented nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy."],[283,277.31,2.75,"This technology uses a strong magnetic field"],[283,280.07,3.42,"to temporarily align a material's protons,"],[283,283.78,4.84,"then a weaker field to knock specific molecules out of alignment."],[283,288.95,3.38,"The machine then tracks the electromagnetic energy"],[283,292.33,3.63,"expended by the jostled protons as they realign,"],[283,296.29,4.17,"allowing researchers to map the material's molecular structure."],[283,301.34,4.0,"This invention laid the groundwork for MRI machines,"],[283,305.55,4.25,"which use a similar approach to magnetize the water in our bodies"],[283,309.81,1.83,"and map our tissues."],[283,312.06,5.88,"Of course, NMR and MRI are just two of countless modern technologies"],[283,317.94,1.88,"that rely on magnetism."],[283,320.27,4.67,"Computer hard drives, electric motors, speakers, wireless communication—"],[283,325.32,4.21,"all these inventions prove that not only does magnetism exist,"],[283,329.53,4.38,"but studying this phenomenon remains endlessly attractive."],[284,7.21,3.55,"Many of you here have probably heard of the 10,000 hours rule."],[284,10.96,2.54,"It’s the idea that to become great in anything takes"],[284,13.51,2.42,"10,000 hours of focused practice."],[284,15.97,2.54,"So you’d better get started as early as possible."],[284,18.64,3.63,"The poster child for this story is Tiger Woods."],[284,22.48,3.42,"His father famously gave him a putter when he was seven months old."],[284,25.94,3.25,"Fast forward to the age of 21— he’s the greatest golfer in the world."],[284,29.19,1.92,"Quintessential 10,000 hours story."],[284,31.32,2.21,"Another is that of the three Polgar sisters,"],[284,33.53,3.17,"whose father decided to teach them chess in a very technical manner"],[284,36.7,1.38,"from a very early age."],[284,38.16,3.04,"Two of his daughters went on to become grandmaster chess players"],[284,41.2,2.75,"I got curious: if this 10,000 hours rule is correct,"],[284,43.96,2.59,"then we should see that elite athletes get a head start"],[284,46.54,1.79,"in so-called deliberate practice."],[284,48.34,2.33,"And in fact, when scientists study elite athletes,"],[284,50.67,2.92,"they see that they spend more time in deliberate practice."],[284,53.72,1.29,"Not a big surprise."],[284,55.01,3.38,"When they actually track athletes over the course of their development,"],[284,58.39,1.42,"the pattern looks like this:"],[284,59.81,3.54,"the future elites tend to have what scientists call a sampling period,"],[284,63.35,2.25,"where they try a variety of physical activities."],[284,65.6,3.42,"They gain broad general skills and delay specializing"],[284,69.02,2.54,"until later than peers who plateau at lower levels."],[284,71.86,3.21,"That doesn’t really comport with the 10,000 hours rule, does it?"],[284,75.07,2.84,"So I started to wonder about other domains that we associate"],[284,77.91,3.21,"with obligatory early specialization, like music."],[284,81.12,1.88,"Turns out the pattern is often similar."],[284,83.0,3.8,"The exceptional musicians didn’t start spending more time in deliberate practice"],[284,86.79,2.79,"than the average musicians until their third instrument."],[284,89.58,2.13,"They too tended to have a sampling period."],[284,91.71,3.5,"Even musicians we think of as famously precocious, like Yo-Yo Ma."],[284,95.22,3.21,"So this got me interested in exploring the developmental backgrounds"],[284,98.43,2.0,"of people whose work I had long admired."],[284,100.43,2.34,"Duke Ellington shunned music lessons as a kid"],[284,102.77,2.25,"to focus on baseball and painting and drawing."],[284,105.02,2.67,"Mariam Mirzakhani wasn’t interested in math as a girl,"],[284,107.69,1.54,"dreamed of becoming a novelist,"],[284,109.23,3.79,"and went on to become the first and so far only woman to win the Fields Medal,"],[284,113.03,2.25,"the most prestigious prize in the world in math."],[284,115.28,4.46,"Vincent van Gogh had five different careers before flaming out spectacularly,"],[284,119.74,1.08,"and, in his late 20s,"],[284,120.83,3.25,"picked up a book called “The Guide to the ABCs of Drawing.”"],[284,124.2,3.34,"Claude Shannon was an electrical engineer at the University of Michigan"],[284,127.54,2.79,"who took a philosophy course just to fulfill a requirement."],[284,130.33,3.34,"And in it he learned about a near century-old system of logic"],[284,133.67,3.17,"by which true and false statements could be coded as ones and zeros"],[284,136.84,1.54,"and solved like math problems."],[284,138.47,2.34,"This led to the development of binary code,"],[284,140.8,3.0,"which underlies all of our digital computers today."],[284,143.81,3.8,"Frances Hesselbein took her first professional job at the age of 54,"],[284,147.6,2.54,"and went on to become the CEO of the Girl Scouts."],[284,150.27,1.79,"Here’s an athlete I’ve followed."],[284,152.06,2.59,"He tried some tennis, some skiing, wrestling."],[284,154.65,1.88,"His mother was actually a tennis coach,"],[284,156.53,3.42,"but she declined to coach him because he wouldn’t return balls normally."],[284,159.95,1.5,"And he kept trying more sports:"],[284,161.45,2.92,"handball, volleyball, soccer, badminton, skateboarding."],[284,164.45,1.5,"So who is this dabbler?"],[284,166.12,1.46,"This is Roger Federer."],[284,167.83,3.09,"Every bit as famous as an adult as Tiger Woods."],[284,170.92,3.38,"And yet even tennis enthusiasts don't usually know anything"],[284,174.29,1.46,"about his developmental story."],[284,175.8,1.0,"Why is that?"],[284,176.8,2.88,"I think it’s partly because the Tiger story is very dramatic,"],[284,179.68,2.34,"but also because it seems like this tidy narrative"],[284,182.01,3.84,"that we can extrapolate to anything that we want to be good at in our own lives."],[284,185.97,3.25,"But it turns out that in many ways, golf is a uniquely horrible model"],[284,189.23,2.21,"of almost everything that humans want to learn."],[284,191.44,2.71,"Golf is the epitome of what the psychologist Robin Hogarth"],[284,194.15,1.71,"called a kind learning environment."],[284,195.86,4.17,"Next steps and goals are clear; rules that are clear and never change."],[284,200.36,3.54,"When you do something, you get feedback that is quick and accurate."],[284,204.12,2.46,"Chess, also a kind learning environment."],[284,206.74,3.09,"On the other end of the spectrum are wicked learning environments"],[284,209.83,3.75,"where next steps and goals may not be clear— rules may change."],[284,213.63,2.54,"You may or may not get feedback when you do something,"],[284,216.17,2.04,"it may be delayed, it may be inaccurate."],[284,218.21,3.5,"Which one of these sounds like the world we're increasingly living in?"],[284,221.72,3.88,"So if hyper-specialization isn’t always the trick in a wicked world, what is?"],[284,225.72,1.75,"That can be difficult to talk about,"],[284,227.47,3.96,"because sometimes it looks like meandering or zigzagging or keeping a broader view."],[284,231.44,1.54,"It can look like getting behind."],[284,232.98,2.58,"But if we look at research on technological innovation,"],[284,235.56,3.13,"it shows that increasingly the most impactful patents are authored"],[284,238.69,2.33,"by teams that include individuals"],[284,241.03,3.25,"who have worked across a large number of different technology classes"],[284,244.28,2.17,"and often merge things from different domains."],[284,246.45,3.5,"Someone whose work I've admired, who was sort of on the forefront of this,"],[284,249.95,1.79,"is a Japanese man named Junpei Yokoi."],[284,251.75,2.79,"Yokoi didn't score well in his electronics exams at school,"],[284,254.54,3.29,"so he had to settle for a low-tier job as a machine maintenance worker"],[284,257.84,1.71,"at a playing card company in Kyoto."],[284,259.55,3.21,"He combined some well-known technology from the calculator industry,"],[284,262.76,3.0,"with some well-known technology from the credit card industry,"],[284,265.76,1.17,"and made handheld games."],[284,266.93,2.17,"And it turned this playing card company,"],[284,269.1,2.96,"which was founded in a wooden storefront in the 19th century,"],[284,272.06,1.79,"into a toy and game operation."],[284,273.85,2.21,"You may have heard of it, it’s called Nintendo."],[284,276.06,1.96,"His magnum opus was the Game Boy."],[284,278.32,2.92,"We probably don't make as many of those people as we could,"],[284,281.24,2.71,"because we don't tend to incentivize anything that doesn't"],[284,283.95,2.0,"look like a head start or specialization."],[284,286.12,3.96,"And naturally, I think there are as many ways to succeed as there are people,"],[284,290.08,3.84,"but I think we tend only to incentivize and encourage the Tiger path,"],[284,293.91,2.46,"when increasingly, in a wicked world, we need people"],[284,296.38,1.88,"who travel the Roger path as well."],[284,298.38,3.04,"Or as the eminent physicist and mathematician and writer"],[284,301.42,1.54,"Freeman Dyson put it:"],[284,302.96,3.63,"“For a healthy ecosystem, we need both birds and frogs."],[284,306.72,3.0,"Frogs are down in the mud seeing all the granular details."],[284,309.72,2.79,"The birds are soaring up above, not seeing those details,"],[284,312.52,2.09,"but integrating the knowledge of the frogs.”"],[284,314.6,1.04,"And we need both."],[284,315.64,4.25,"The problem, Dyson said, is that we’re telling everyone to become frogs."],[284,320.15,3.59,"And I think in a wicked world, that's increasingly shortsighted."],[285,3.9,2.52,"Last July I was in Rome."],[285,7.62,3.92,"I walked from my hotel to the Trevi Fountain"],[285,11.58,4.68,"on the cobbled streets lined with beautiful cafes,"],[285,16.3,3.48,"bustling with residents and travelers,"],[285,19.78,3.36,"drinking their espressos and cheering and greeting each other."],[285,24.06,5.28,"Small souvenir shops selling beautiful replicas of the Colosseum,"],[285,29.38,3.04,"the Sistine Chapel ceiling paintings."],[285,32.46,1.24,"What an atmosphere."],[285,34.9,2.4,"I soaked it all in,"],[285,37.34,2.16,"reached the Trevi Fountain,"],[285,39.54,3.92,"grabbed my favorite pistachio gelato"],[285,43.5,2.56,"and looked at the marvelous views."],[285,46.1,2.56,"A perfect Roman holiday."],[285,49.9,2.48,"Suddenly my alarm rang"],[285,52.42,2.0,"and I woke up from my dream."],[285,56.5,1.24,"I was in Rome."],[285,58.54,3.04,"I did walk from my hotel to the Trevi Fountain."],[285,63.38,1.32,"But I felt rushed."],[285,67.02,2.12,"The cafes were crowded."],[285,70.02,2.0,"People had to even queue outside."],[285,73.06,2.96,"The shopkeepers had no time to entertain visitors."],[285,76.9,2.44,"I reached the Trevi Fountain"],[285,79.38,4.0,"and forgot [to] grab my favorite pistachio gelato"],[285,83.42,1.84,"or find a bench to sit."],[285,86.46,1.8,"I couldn't get to the steps."],[285,88.66,4.2,"Thousands and thousands of people all around."],[285,93.66,1.64,"Have you felt the same way?"],[285,96.34,3.08,"When you reach your dream destination,"],[285,99.46,3.04,"you feel as if it has been overrun."],[285,102.54,3.32,"You feel as if it has become a selfie queue."],[285,105.86,2.16,"I had the same feeling."],[285,108.06,1.84,"And this is not an isolated incident."],[285,111.34,3.16,"I had the same exact experience in Tokyo"],[285,114.54,3.08,"when I went there during the cherry blossom season."],[285,118.26,4.92,"Tourists crowd every park in the city"],[285,123.22,1.84,"to get a glimpse of the famous sakura."],[285,127.14,4.8,"I asked a resident: “How does [you] feel seeing so many tourists all around?”"],[285,133.5,3.76,"He told me that he doesn't bring his family anymore"],[285,137.3,1.64,"for a picnic under the sakura."],[285,140.18,2.12,"That response broke my heart."],[285,143.78,4.0,"We are loving our favorite destinations to death."],[285,149.22,1.44,"We are overrunning them."],[285,152.62,2.12,"And residents are pushing back"],[285,154.74,2.12,"because it's impacting their livability."],[285,157.86,3.36,"Their cost of living is increasing, congestion on the road,"],[285,161.26,3.16,"rental prices, food prices."],[285,164.46,1.48,"They are pushing back."],[285,165.94,2.88,"And they're pushing back like in Barcelona,"],[285,168.82,2.44,"with the graffiti asking tourists to go home."],[285,172.5,2.92,"In Barcelona, in Italy, in Portugal,"],[285,176.74,1.92,"residents are pushing back."],[285,179.9,2.2,"I have been researching the travel industry"],[285,182.1,1.6,"for [the] last 20 years"],[285,183.7,4.68,"and my two research subjects, the residents and the travelers,"],[285,188.42,2.76,"are on a collision course like never before."],[285,192.26,2.8,"We have a travel crisis."],[285,195.82,5.12,"The travel industry employs about 300 million people globally,"],[285,200.94,4.24,"and it’s going to be a $15 trillion industry by 2040."],[285,205.78,1.96,"[It’s] too large, [with] too many"],[285,207.74,2.4,"livelihoods at stake for us to ignore this crisis."],[285,211.3,2.36,"But let me pause and ask you a question."],[285,214.58,2.08,"Why do we travel?"],[285,216.66,2.16,"We travel for joy."],[285,219.5,5.16,"Joy by experiencing the diversity this world has to offer."],[285,224.66,2.68,"Joy by experiencing different lives."],[285,227.38,1.84,"Joy through a shared connection."],[285,230.1,3.0,"My daughter, son and wife are here,"],[285,233.14,4.56,"and I want them to experience the same joy I experienced in my Roman dream."],[285,237.7,2.88,"I want all of you to experience the same joy."],[285,241.54,3.24,"We need to bring joy back in travel."],[285,246.26,1.96,"But it's a complex problem."],[285,248.26,4.92,"We have millions of tourists getting inspired by social media"],[285,253.22,3.68,"and planning their travel destinations, and planning their travel itineraries."],[285,256.9,3.92,"We have the travel industry, which is looking for short-term profits,"],[285,260.82,3.76,"and we have cities who don't know how to manage the tourist inflow."],[285,265.78,2.68,"There is a path forward, though."],[285,268.5,5.32,"With the use of technology like AI and simple behavior changes,"],[285,273.82,3.2,"we could bring joy back in travel."],[285,277.86,5.2,"Now imagine if we were to make the entire travel industry's objective,"],[285,283.1,2.8,"the airlines, the hotel, the travel tour operators,"],[285,285.9,5.88,"to bring joy to both residents as well as the travelers."],[285,291.78,2.12,"And they have everything at their disposal,"],[285,293.9,2.64,"from technology to people to skilling."],[285,296.58,2.08,"They will rise to the occasion."],[285,298.66,1.32,"They will do three things:"],[285,300.02,3.0,"one, help manage crowd,"],[285,303.06,2.6,"help discover authentic experiences"],[285,305.66,3.44,"and help drive a really, really respectful connection"],[285,309.14,3.24,"between the resident and the traveler."],[285,312.42,1.24,"On your last trip,"],[285,315.34,2.2,"did you feel rushed?"],[285,317.58,2.56,"Did you feel like you were going through a checklist,"],[285,320.18,2.44,"moving from one place to another place?"],[285,322.62,3.0,"Did it feel like a chore or did you feel joy?"],[285,327.26,1.28,"We all are super busy"],[285,330.1,2.44,"and we all land to the same destinations"],[285,332.58,2.16,"during the same peak season times"],[285,336.02,2.8,"without realizing quieter seasons,"],[285,338.82,2.68,"quieter times, or hidden paths."],[285,341.54,2.4,"What if technology could help us figure that out?"],[285,345.14,3.32,"FeelFlorence is an app by the city of Florence,"],[285,348.5,3.68,"which helps do exactly this for the travelers."],[285,353.26,4.8,"It uses the data from sensors, from cameras,"],[285,358.1,2.8,"to understand crowded locations,"],[285,360.9,4.32,"and recommends personalized travel itineraries for tourists."],[285,365.26,3.48,"It sends user alerts to avoid crowded locations."],[285,368.74,1.28,"When this happens,"],[285,370.86,2.16,"it balances the load,"],[285,373.06,3.28,"and it is beneficial for both the tourists,"],[285,376.38,1.72,"as well as the residents."],[285,379.02,1.56,"Cities are going down that path."],[285,380.58,1.28,"Amsterdam."],[285,381.86,2.88,"And even religious cities like Mecca or Varanasi"],[285,384.74,3.44,"are increasingly using technology to manage crowd."],[285,389.78,4.0,"What was your last unforgettable trip?"],[285,394.58,5.04,"Was it the famous landmark, [the] Instagrammable picture,"],[285,399.62,2.16,"or was it a shared smile with someone?"],[285,403.3,2.0,"Or was it a story that somebody told you,"],[285,405.34,1.32,"of course, in the backdrop"],[285,406.66,3.04,"of beautiful nature or a beautiful surrounding."],[285,410.9,1.92,"I asked the same question to my friend."],[285,414.66,3.6,"She said, \"If I ignore my bachelorette party outside,"],[285,419.94,2.96,"my last unforgettable trip was in Vietnam."],[285,424.58,2.72,"A fisherman took me on a boat ride,"],[285,428.34,1.88,"told me stories about Vietnam,"],[285,430.26,3.56,"told me stories about the life he has lived there."],[285,433.82,2.16,"And I cannot forget that.\""],[285,436.02,1.36,"What a beautiful connection."],[285,438.86,1.28,"For me,"],[285,440.94,3.88,"it was my experience in Istanbul, in Turkey,"],[285,444.82,3.24,"where a cheerful cafe owner took interest in me"],[285,448.1,2.88,"and told me the history of Turkish tea,"],[285,451.02,2.16,"how to make it and how to drink it."],[285,453.78,4.56,"Whenever I think about Turkey or Istanbul, my heart fills with joy."],[285,459.42,2.72,"This is what we crave. This is what we seek."],[285,464.02,2.76,"I'm sure some of you have been stranded at airports"],[285,466.78,2.36,"because of airline disruptions."],[285,469.18,1.48,"The airline has two options:"],[285,470.66,4.12,"one to check you into [a] hotel overlooking the terminal,"],[285,474.78,3.28,"or maybe organize a Turkish tea-making workshop."],[285,478.94,2.36,"Maybe some of us will like it, and if we like it,"],[285,481.34,2.68,"we will become a loyal customer of the airline."],[285,485.5,1.76,"Same is the case with hotels."],[285,487.3,3.16,"If hotels can use technology to give custom concierges,"],[285,491.62,3.48,"which speaks in your language, curates local suggestions,"],[285,495.14,1.76,"helps you discover hidden gems,"],[285,498.22,2.32,"and walks along with you,"],[285,500.58,4.8,"you will become a loyal customer of the hotel."],[285,505.42,2.36,"Imagine the possibilities"],[285,509.66,5.2,"of uncovering authentic experiences that give us true joy."],[285,516.38,2.56,"Many residents in the destinations we love"],[285,520.34,2.2,"don’t see the direct benefits of a traveler."],[285,524.78,2.12,"They only see the negative side."],[285,526.9,4.0,"I know we are contributing to the local economy through our money."],[285,532.14,1.32,"But that may not be enough."],[285,534.66,4.76,"And even if we want to contribute through our time,"],[285,539.46,1.56,"we don't know how to do it."],[285,542.42,3.12,"Residents feel like how I used to feel as a child"],[285,545.58,3.24,"when unexpected guests arrived in my house,"],[285,548.82,3.88,"especially if they didn't carry any special gifts for me."],[285,552.7,2.24,"I barely uttered a smile"],[285,554.94,3.32,"and I wished that they [would] leave as soon as possible."],[285,558.3,3.76,"I'm sure many of you, if you could find a way"],[285,562.1,2.52,"to contribute through your time, you would."],[285,565.3,2.4,"Again things are changing on this front."],[285,567.7,2.68,"Golden Volunteer is an example of a platform"],[285,570.42,3.4,"which connects volunteers with organizations and projects"],[285,573.82,1.36,"looking for volunteers."],[285,575.94,3.68,"It, in fact, goes to the extent of doing a volunteer background check."],[285,579.62,3.92,"If we can unleash a “gratitude marketplace” at scale,"],[285,585.26,5.36,"we will create a deep connection between the traveler and the resident."],[285,590.62,3.2,"Copenhagen is going on that path with CopenPay."],[285,593.82,1.88,"You get points."],[285,595.7,3.24,"If you do gardening, you get points. If you do cycling,"],[285,598.94,3.64,"you can use those points for redeeming [at] attractions."],[285,603.62,4.48,"If you had the opportunity to contribute with your time,"],[285,608.14,1.36,"what would you do?"],[285,609.54,1.72,"Some of you are animal lovers."],[285,612.5,4.24,"You may want to be part of the elephant rescue centers in Thailand,"],[285,616.74,3.92,"or want to be part of sea turtle rescue centers in India."],[285,621.74,2.56,"If you're an environment or nature lover,"],[285,624.34,2.36,"you may want to be part of coral reef preservation"],[285,626.7,2.72,"in the Great Barrier or in Maldives."],[285,629.46,2.04,"And if you are a history lover like me,"],[285,633.14,3.84,"you may be wanting to be a part of restoration"],[285,637.02,2.08,"of an 800-year-old temple like this."],[285,640.1,4.04,"In fact, I will seek such an opportunity and bake it in my itinerary"],[285,644.18,3.48,"because I will love to be part of something like this."],[285,647.66,1.8,"The possibilities are infinite."],[285,651.14,1.8,"The travel industry is going to grow."],[285,654.54,5.24,"It's one industry that is probably encouraging tolerance"],[285,659.78,1.64,"in this highly polarized world."],[285,662.9,1.28,"It's for us"],[285,666.06,4.8,"to shape positively the destinations we love"],[285,670.86,2.6,"and the people who call it their home."],[285,675.1,4.2,"I've talked about the tools at hand, and I've given you a few examples."],[285,679.34,3.44,"I want to leave with one question."],[285,682.78,5.92,"On your next trip, will you be a tourist or a joyful guest?"],[285,688.7,4.2,"It's time we bring joy back in travel."],[285,692.9,1.28,"Thank you."],[286,0.16,2.48,"For centuries, it was worth its weight in gold."],[286,2.64,1.76,"People built cities to trade it."],[286,4.4,1.76,"They fought wars over it."],[286,6.16,6.08,"And to this day, you and I and every animal on Earth literally can’t live without it."],[286,12.24,2.96,"I’m talking about this: salt!"],[286,15.2,2.32,"Every grain you’ve ever eaten comes from this—"],[286,17.52,3.52,"a mineral called halite, that forms from the evaporation of saltwater."],[286,21.04,2.0,"But salt isn’t the only mineral with a story."],[286,23.04,4.0,"And in more ways than you might realize, the story of minerals connects to you."],[286,27.04,2.76,"Hi, I’m Sage, and this is Crash Course Geology."],[286,34.21,1.71,"Minerals are heroes."],[286,35.92,2.08,"They make life itself possible."],[286,38.0,2.64,"Some stink, or glow under a blacklight."],[286,40.64,2.96,"Some are magnetic… others poisonous."],[286,43.6,3.52,"And some keep the shredded cheese in the fridge from clumping together."],[286,47.12,2.0,"One of their more important responsibilities."],[286,49.12,2.88,"But what exactly are they?"],[286,52.0,1.84,"Well, hold onto your bustamite."],[286,53.84,1.92,"I’m about to say a lot of words."],[286,55.76,2.56,"A mineral is an inorganic material with a solid,"],[286,58.32,4.24,"crystalline structure and a specific chemical composition formed by geological processes."],[286,62.56,6.16,"An “inorganic material” is not made from the carbon molecules associated with living things."],[286,68.72,3.12,"“Solid” means it’s neither liquid nor gas."],[286,71.84,2.32,"“Crystalline” means the atoms are arranged in"],[286,74.16,3.6,"one of six orderly patterns called crystal systems."],[286,77.76,1.84,"Having a “specific chemical composition”"],[286,79.6,3.68,"means it can be described with a particular recipe of chemical elements."],[286,83.28,4.56,"And “formed by geological processes” means it’s cooked up in nature."],[286,87.84,3.92,"There are a lot of minerals out there— over six thousand that we know of!"],[286,91.76,5.36,"Some are extremely rare, found only in strange or inaccessible corners of the earth."],[286,97.12,4.16,"I bet some are even in your house right now,"],[286,101.28,3.36,"like nahcolite (which you might know as baking soda)."],[286,104.64,3.44,"Or ice (which you might know as, yeah, ice)."],[286,108.08,2.16,"I know, right? Ice is a mineral!"],[286,110.24,2.56,"But some things are mineral posers."],[286,112.8,3.52,"Like, opal and pearl seem like minerals, but they"],[286,116.32,2.24,"don’t check off all the qualities in the definition."],[286,118.56,1.92,"Or, let me show you some other examples."],[286,120.48,3.92,"Mineral oil has “mineral” in the name, but it’s a liquid."],[286,124.4,5.44,"Rock candy is crystalline, but it’s organic and not formed in nature."],[286,129.84,1.92,"And glass isn’t a mineral, either."],[286,131.76,1.68,"I gotta put this back in the cupboard."],[286,133.44,2.0,"And we all know a rock when we kick one,"],[286,135.44,3.6,"but “rock” has a specific definition in geology-speak."],[286,139.04,3.36,"It’s a solid made of one or more minerals smushed together."],[286,142.4,3.68,"Like, Dwayne here is granite: a mix of mostly quartz and feldspar."],[286,146.08,4.96,"He’s also a reminder of the vastness of geologic time and my own human frailty…"],[286,151.04,2.56,"which I contend with by putting googly eyes on him."],[286,155.04,3.04,"So we’ve got some seriously cool minerals on this planet."],[286,158.08,2.16,"And there are a few main ways that they form."],[286,160.24,3.68,"One is when magma—molten rock under the earth’s surface—or lava—"],[286,163.92,3.68,"molten rock that breaks through the surface—cools down and crystallizes."],[286,167.6,3.04,"Another is when a rock is heated and compressed so much,"],[286,170.64,3.36,"its minerals transform into other minerals."],[286,174.0,2.0,"That also happens to me when I'm stuck on a hot,"],[286,176.0,2.96,"crowded bus and then I transform into SWEATY SAGE."],[286,178.96,4.0,"Living things can even build minerals in their bones and shells!"],[286,182.96,5.36,"Like, one of the main materials in your teeth and bones is the mineral hydroxyapatite."],[286,188.32,2.16,"And then you’ve got another classic way minerals form:"],[286,190.48,3.44,"when water evaporates or cools down, creating solid crystals."],[286,193.92,1.12,"That’s how salt happens."],[286,195.04,4.64,"It’s also how filmy, white gunk ends up on your faucet in your bathroom."],[286,199.68,1.44,"That’s mostly calcium carbonate!"],[286,201.12,3.76,"Some minerals just need a couple weeks of neglecting a bathroom cleaning schedule to form,"],[286,204.88,4.8,"while others take a super long time— with bonkers results."],[286,209.68,5.36,"Like, check out these gypsum crystals the size of trees found in Chihuahua, Mexico."],[286,215.04,5.44,"It likely took nearly a million years to form just"],[286,220.48,3.76,"one of these babies in this hot cave once full of water."],[286,224.24,0.96,"That’s your cousin?"],[286,225.2,1.44,"I don’t see the resemblance."],[286,226.64,3.76,"Luckily, you don’t need to go hundreds of feet below the earth to get up-close and personal"],[286,230.4,1.2,"with cool minerals."],[286,231.6,1.76,"They’re all over the planet!"],[286,233.36,3.68,"And mineralogists — geologists who specialize in minerals —"],[286,237.04,3.44,"identify them by their chemical composition and physical properties."],[286,240.48,4.25,"But sometimes this takes quite a bit of… investigation."],[286,244.73,3.45,"*jazzy music*"],[286,248.19,3.65,"Some minerals are tricksters. Con-men. Tough to identify."],[286,251.84,5.6,"My white whale is apatite—from the Greek word meaning “to deceive”."],[286,257.44,4.08,"And deceive it does."],[286,261.52,4.4,"On the other hand, some minerals have special properties that can help reveal their identity."],[286,265.92,5.36,"Like, alexandrite shines emerald green in the sun, but glows ruby red under incandescent light."],[286,271.28,2.72,"Sulfur can smell like a burning match when heated up"],[286,274.0,3.12,"or take on a rotten egg stank from reactions with moisture."],[286,277.12,3.12,"Calcite and dolomite fizz when they touch hydrochloric acid."],[286,280.24,2.8,"And sylvite has a salty-bitter-metallic taste."],[286,283.04,3.84,"So if you ever see a grown adult licking rocks, they’re probably just a geologist."],[286,286.88,0.8,"Hopefully."],[286,287.68,3.68,"Usually, though, there’s not just one thing that gives away a mineral’s identity."],[286,291.36,3.12,"Geologists have to examine a bunch of different physical properties"],[286,294.48,2.48,"to figure out what minerals a rock is made of."],[286,296.96,1.57,"Let’s check out some cool minerals…"],[286,299.36,3.84,"The first property I notice is luster — how light interacts with the surface."],[286,303.2,3.6,"Some minerals are shiny and metallic-looking, like this pyrite,"],[286,306.8,3.52,"but others reflect very little light at all, like kaolinite."],[286,310.32,3.12,"This fluorite here has a glass-like luster."],[286,313.44,4.24,"It’s also got a lovely pale color— although it can glow brightly under a blacklight."],[286,317.68,1.92,"Sometimes, color’s a huge tell—"],[286,319.6,5.52,"like, malachite is always shades of green, and azurite is usually deep blue."],[286,325.12,2.8,"But most of the time, color alone won’t tell you much."],[286,327.92,4.08,"Like, quartz and fluorite are sometimes the exact same shade of purple."],[286,332.0,2.24,"And depending on the trace elements it contains,"],[286,334.24,2.32,"quartz can also be yellow, pink, or blue."],[286,336.56,1.92,"So we’ve also got to look at the streak:"],[286,338.48,4.32,"the color of the powder left behind when the mineral is scratched against a ceramic plate."],[286,342.8,3.04,"Like, hematite creates a rusty brown powder."],[286,345.84,4.8,"And despite its gold-ish color, this pyrite leaves a green-ish black streak."],[286,350.64,2.48,"But some minerals don’t leave a streak at all."],[286,353.12,4.96,"That’s because minerals vary in their hardness: how resistant they are to being scratched."],[286,358.08,5.28,"Geologists use the Mohs Hardness scale to assign minerals a hardness between one and ten."],[286,363.36,3.44,"Talc is a one, so soft you could scratch it with your fingernail."],[286,366.8,4.48,"Diamond is a ten, so hard the only thing that can scratch it is another diamond."],[286,371.28,2.96,"If I were to scratch this fluorite, a penny wouldn’t leave a mark,"],[286,374.24,2.39,"but a pocket knife would."],[286,376.63,2.65,"So I’d guess it would be around a 4 on the Mohs scale."],[286,379.28,4.08,"Another property to check for is cleavage, meaning how minerals break in smooth,"],[286,383.36,3.68,"flat planes, along weak atomic bonds within their crystal structures."],[286,387.04,3.84,"Some minerals break in parallel lines, others in multiple directions."],[286,390.88,3.76,"But some, like quartz and olivine, don’t split in a clean fashion."],[286,394.64,4.16,"This muscovite breaks into thin sheets or flakes."],[286,398.8,1.6,"Up next: density."],[286,400.4,2.0,"That’s hard to measure in a precise way,"],[286,402.4,3.6,"so geologists often rely on a related property: specific gravity."],[286,406.0,3.68,"That’s the ratio of the mineral’s mass to an equal volume of water."],[286,409.68,4.08,"Geologists figure this out by suspending mineral samples in water, and taking measurements."],[286,413.76,1.36,"We don’t have time for that right now,"],[286,415.12,5.76,"so I’m going to rely on the advanced technique of picking it up… and feeling how heavy it is."],[286,420.88,0.8,"Science!"],[286,421.68,3.44,"Last but not least, you can tell minerals apart by their crystal habit,"],[286,425.12,3.12,"or the appearance of a crystal or cluster of crystals."],[286,428.24,2.24,"There’s some wild shapes out there."],[286,430.48,5.84,"Cubes, blobs, blades, branches, even crystals that grow like sheets or rose petals."],[286,436.32,2.96,"This chalcedony has a botryoidal crystal habit,"],[286,439.28,2.16,"which is what makes it look like a bunch of grapes."],[286,441.44,0.96,"And there you have it!"],[286,442.4,2.69,"Everything you need to know about that cool rock you just found."],[286,445.12,4.48,"Minerals are all around us—from a cool rock you pick up outside, to the salt in your pantry."],[286,449.6,3.12,"But minerals are also a big deal for life itself."],[286,452.72,1.2,"In the early universe,"],[286,453.92,2.8,"there were only about twelve minerals around,"],[286,456.72,3.28,"which formed in the wake of exploding stars."],[286,460.0,6.8,"When our solar system burst into existence, that number grew to about 250 types of minerals."],[286,466.8,5.12,"And Earth’s baby years sparked the formation of over a thousand more,"],[286,471.92,2.24,"thanks to weathering, the movement of water,"],[286,474.16,5.28,"the churning and cooling of magma, and the pressure from colliding tectonic plates."],[286,479.44,5.6,"And when life made its debut billions of years ago, mineral diversity exploded."],[286,485.04,3.04,"The earliest forms of life released oxygen into the atmosphere,"],[286,488.08,4.16,"triggering chemical reactions that created thousands of new minerals."],[286,492.24,3.04,"Some living things began making minerals themselves, like we talked about earlier."],[286,495.28,5.2,"Minerals created soil as bits of weathered rocks mixed with decaying critters, supporting plants,"],[286,500.48,3.44,"land animals, and the formation of even more kinds of minerals."],[286,503.92,4.8,"It was this amazing feedback loop that created a metaphorical gold mine."],[286,508.72,3.44,"As far as we know, Earth is the only place life has ever formed."],[286,512.16,3.44,"And if it weren’t for minerals, life as we know it wouldn’t exist."],[286,515.6,4.48,"But also: if it weren’t for life, minerals as we know them wouldn’t exist."],[286,520.08,4.48,"It’s estimated there are ten times more minerals on Earth than anywhere else in the"],[286,524.56,1.36,"solar system."],[286,525.92,2.53,"And life might be one of the reasons why."],[286,528.48,4.0,"Minerals are everywhere, and their past is linked to our own."],[286,532.48,4.72,"Some minerals are the size of telephone poles; others are too tiny to see with the naked eye."],[286,537.2,3.6,"Some dazzle with their rarity; others, we encounter every day."],[286,540.8,3.36,"Some have been part of the human story for thousands of years,"],[286,544.16,2.4,"and some have been around longer than life itself."],[286,546.56,4.88,"Still others are lurking in the depths and corners of the earth, waiting to be discovered."],[286,551.44,3.68,"Next time, we’ll ask the question: where do rocks come from?"],[286,555.12,5.12,"I’ll give you a hint: it’s when two or more minerals love each other very much…"],[286,560.24,1.36,"well, kinda."],[286,562.32,0.72,"See you then."],[286,563.04,2.32,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Geology,"],[286,565.36,2.72,"which was filmed at our studio in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[286,568.08,2.56,"and was made with the help of all these cool people."],[286,570.64,3.84,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[286,574.48,2.1,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[287,0.92,4.32,"Low on oxygen, her skin starts to pale."],[287,7.84,2.32,"She's suffocating."],[287,15.6,2.92,"An Octopus oliveri."],[287,20.0,2.68,"This pool is her lifeline,"],[287,23.64,3.96,"providing the oxygen she needs to breathe."],[287,32.68,1.24,"But her prey…"],[287,34.76,1.8,"..is outside."],[287,38.32,3.84,"So what she does is astonishing."],[287,48.52,2.6,"She leaves the safety of her pool…"],[287,52.32,2.16,"..to hunt on land."],[287,60.76,1.76,"Blennies."],[287,74.24,1.6,"Got one!"],[287,81.64,2.2,"Just as she's getting the hang of it…"],[287,85.44,2.12,"..she needs to breathe."],[287,87.92,3.76,"But she's made a potentially fatal error."],[287,94.72,4.32,"She has strayed dangerously far inland."],[287,100.0,2.88,"And in the midday sun,"],[287,102.88,2.8,"the rockpools she relies on"],[287,105.68,2.96,"are evaporating."],[287,111.08,4.92,"Low on oxygen, her skin starts to pale."],[287,143.2,2.12,"She's suffocating."],[287,182.6,2.68,"The heat of summer has driven her"],[287,185.28,2.92,"to the limit of survival."],[288,6.84,5.09,"In the 1930s, Danish landscape architect Carl Theodor Sørensen"],[288,12.09,4.38,"noticed that his neighborhood’s children loved playing in old building sites."],[288,17.05,2.84,"Watching kids swing from exposed beams,"],[288,19.89,3.63,"scavenge for scrap materials, and hammer together bridges"],[288,23.69,3.29,"inspired him to transform an abandoned housing estate"],[288,27.19,2.63,"into a dedicated junk playground."],[288,30.11,2.84,"Sørensen’s site became a huge success,"],[288,33.24,1.88,"and soon countries throughout Europe"],[288,35.11,3.0,"were making playgrounds out of derelict building yards"],[288,38.2,2.79,"and even former World War I bomb sites."],[288,41.66,2.46,"These environments might look dangerous,"],[288,44.12,1.92,"especially to 21st century parents"],[288,46.04,2.92,"who tend to be far more anxious about their childrens’ safety"],[288,48.96,1.5,"than previous generations."],[288,50.76,3.5,"But research has found these seemingly scary setups"],[288,54.26,4.38,"teach kids important lessons that standard playgrounds don’t."],[288,59.26,4.09,"Play is profoundly important for childhood development."],[288,63.35,3.67,"Creating games and following rules helps teach problem solving."],[288,67.31,4.38,"Moving our bodies through varied terrain helps hone our motor control."],[288,71.82,4.84,"And playing with others lets us practice language and refine our social skills."],[288,77.11,2.46,"Since play comes naturally to all of us,"],[288,79.58,3.67,"children are likely to develop these skills in most environments."],[288,83.25,5.71,"But researchers have identified a few factors that can maximize these benefits."],[288,89.04,2.38,"Chief among them is freedom."],[288,91.59,2.54,"Play is all about experimentation,"],[288,94.13,4.55,"and kids need the agency to explore and shape their surroundings"],[288,98.68,2.13,"without adults getting in the way."],[288,101.47,2.29,"The next factor is novelty—"],[288,104.35,4.46,"kids are always hungry for new and unpredictable play elements."],[288,109.31,4.3,"Finally, they need time to see their plans through."],[288,114.4,2.54,"While we could all use more time to play,"],[288,117.11,2.96,"the best play environments offer freedom and novelty"],[288,120.07,4.0,"that kids can explore as much or as little as they like."],[288,124.75,3.63,"But traditional playgrounds don’t offer much flexibility here."],[288,128.83,4.71,"Swings, slides, and climbing frames are hardly novel structures."],[288,133.96,2.5,"They also have relatively low affordance—"],[288,136.72,1.63,"a term used by play researchers"],[288,138.34,3.34,"to indicate how open-ended an object’s design is."],[288,142.22,2.88,"Items with high affordance, like a sandbox,"],[288,145.22,2.38,"can be interacted with in many ways."],[288,147.94,3.25,"Meanwhile, objects with low affordance, like a slide,"],[288,151.35,2.71,"were designed with a single use in mind."],[288,154.78,5.0,"Even when kids are creative enough to find new uses for low affordance objects,"],[288,160.03,2.5,"they're still more limited playthings."],[288,163.03,1.38,"To address these problems,"],[288,164.41,3.63,"some designers are looking back at Europe's junk playgrounds"],[288,168.04,2.63,"to create modern adventure playgrounds—"],[288,170.83,3.54,"big, open spaces full of high affordance structures"],[288,174.38,1.96,"and free play possibilities."],[288,176.92,5.71,"Berlin’s Kolle 37 features scrap and building tools for kids to manipulate."],[288,182.97,2.34,"In Tokyo’s muddy Harapa Park,"],[288,185.47,3.79,"children shape water slides, sink holes, and splash pools"],[288,189.27,2.04,"out of the natural landscape."],[288,192.06,2.59,"And in London’s Glamis Adventure Playground"],[288,194.9,4.71,"kids burn things in fire pits, with the help of play worker employees."],[288,200.07,3.96,"While some parents worry these unpredictable spaces are too dangerous,"],[288,204.12,2.46,"play researchers like Ellen Sandseter"],[288,206.58,3.84,"would say this so-called risky play is essential."],[288,210.91,5.21,"Giving kids the space to experiment with fast speeds, high heights and getting lost"],[288,216.13,2.96,"helps them develop better judgment and confidence."],[288,219.38,5.71,"In fact, risky play is how kids learn to manage risk and keep themselves safe."],[288,225.68,3.46,"One study even found that kids were more likely to get seriously injured"],[288,229.14,2.04,"in traditional playgrounds—"],[288,231.19,3.25,"perhaps because adventure playgrounds encourage them to be more aware"],[288,234.44,1.38,"of their surroundings."],[288,236.11,4.67,"Risky play also helps develop skills for managing mental health."],[288,240.94,2.79,"Experimenting with small doses of uncertainty"],[288,243.74,2.88,"gets kids used to life being unpredictable,"],[288,246.91,3.75,"helping them better manage anxiety for years to come."],[288,251.75,3.55,"Adventure playgrounds aren't the only places for risky play."],[288,255.71,4.92,"Nature playgrounds encouraging kids to explore creeks, caves, and tree climbing"],[288,260.63,2.0,"combine the perks of risk-taking"],[288,262.63,2.67,"with the health benefits of connecting with nature."],[288,265.47,3.5,"Meanwhile, other designers are working to bring more freedom and novelty"],[288,268.97,1.67,"to traditional designs."],[288,270.85,3.92,"This Philadelphia park features a 30-person mega-swing,"],[288,274.94,2.88,"and Nebraska’s Omaha Riverfront Playground"],[288,277.81,4.42,"uses a ribbon of multi-layered decking as a play spine,"],[288,282.4,4.55,"warping up and down to reveal secret tunnels and rope forests."],[288,287.37,3.75,"So the real key is to design a variety of playgrounds,"],[288,291.12,2.08,"supporting a wide range of activities"],[288,293.2,4.13,"that promote fun, agency, and a dash of uncertainty—"],[288,297.79,3.84,"inviting kids to fill in the possibilities."],[289,8.2,2.84,"When you sit in your car or any car,"],[289,11.08,3.32,"do you ever wonder how many parts that car is made of?"],[289,14.72,1.28,"Anyone?"],[289,16.88,1.8,"Oh, great."],[289,18.68,2.08,"Do you also ever wonder"],[289,20.76,2.64,"what does it really take to put all this together?"],[289,23.44,1.2,"Anyone?"],[289,25.28,1.48,"Oh, great."],[289,27.52,1.28,"Now let me get to it."],[289,29.52,3.8,"How many of you have ever built a car from scratch?"],[289,34.8,2.2,"Oh, great, good to see you."],[289,37.48,1.16,"Absolutely."],[289,38.64,2.64,"And I’m also honored to say that I have,"],[289,41.64,2.04,"an electric one at that."],[289,44.32,2.36,"Growing up as a follower of four boys"],[289,46.72,1.8,"earned me an early ticket"],[289,48.56,3.32,"to the fascinating world of speed and cars."],[289,51.88,4.6,"There's something thrilling, something very powerful about, you know,"],[289,56.52,3.04,"the vehicles, the elegance of the design,"],[289,59.56,2.84,"the engineering, putting it all together."],[289,62.88,2.6,"But what really excites me the most today"],[289,65.48,2.12,"is the future of mobility."],[289,67.64,2.04,"Electric mobility."],[289,70.32,3.6,"I was first introduced to the electric vehicle world"],[289,73.92,1.76,"during my time as a student"],[289,75.72,2.0,"at Makerere University."],[289,78.16,2.16,"Kiira Motors of Uganda"],[289,80.36,2.8,"was the very first company on this continent"],[289,83.2,2.04,"to dream beyond fuel."],[289,88.32,5.76,"In 2011, Kiira Motors built Africa's very first electric car."],[289,94.92,1.28,"Doesn't it look great?"],[289,97.28,1.16,"And this is at a time"],[289,98.48,3.4,"when the rest of Africa was not paying any attention whatsoever"],[289,101.92,1.68,"to the electric vehicle space,"],[289,103.6,3.6,"or even the urgency of addressing the climate crisis."],[289,107.64,1.8,"I was privileged to join this team,"],[289,109.48,3.28,"and we moved on to produce Africa's very first"],[289,112.8,2.12,"plug-in hybrid electric vehicle."],[289,114.96,1.44,"Would you buy that?"],[289,116.4,1.2,"Audience: Yes."],[289,117.6,1.16,"Doreen Orishaba: Great."],[289,118.8,1.92,"And we also proceeded to build"],[289,120.72,3.88,"Africa's first solar electric bus prototype."],[289,128.48,1.88,"As you can imagine, at that time,"],[289,130.4,3.6,"we’re talking about more than 10 years ago,"],[289,134.04,1.6,"some people used to think"],[289,135.64,3.16,"maybe this electric vehicle stuff,"],[289,138.8,2.08,"these are toys for the Western world."],[289,140.92,2.28,"But for us, we were driven by the vision"],[289,143.2,4.24,"that Africa should contribute to the future of electric mobility"],[289,147.48,2.12,"and not just be a consumer."],[289,149.92,3.36,"Electric buses are now a very prime opportunity."],[289,153.64,2.88,"Buses are the lifeline in African cities,"],[289,156.52,5.04,"moving over 60 percent of the population every single day."],[289,161.56,3.96,"And public transport in most of the regions in Africa,"],[289,165.52,1.92,"it's led by the private sector."],[289,167.48,2.76,"So we have electric mobility start-ups"],[289,170.28,4.0,"that are driving, really, the vision of electric buses."],[289,175.84,5.0,"This is happening here in Kenya, in Rwanda, in Nigeria."],[289,181.44,3.92,"But at the end of the day, despite all these efforts,"],[289,185.36,5.96,"scaling electric mobility is facing huge challenges."],[289,192.16,2.04,"Talk of range anxiety,"],[289,194.24,3.28,"the high upfront cost of electric vehicles"],[289,197.56,2.08,"and limited charging infrastructure"],[289,199.68,4.04,"is really slowing down the adoption of electric mobility."],[289,203.72,1.8,"But these challenges are not new."],[289,205.56,3.56,"They are the same hindrances that stopped the electric vehicle"],[289,209.16,2.6,"from growing in the 1890s."],[289,211.8,2.08,"However, the times are different."],[289,213.92,1.76,"We cannot afford to wait."],[289,215.72,3.96,"The planet is telling us that we need to act really soon,"],[289,219.72,4.2,"and we are proving that there is a way to overcome these challenges."],[289,224.56,1.52,"I am Doreen Orishaba,"],[289,226.12,2.8,"the Managing Director of BasiGo, Rwanda."],[289,228.92,2.76,"At BasiGo, we provide electric buses"],[289,231.72,4.24,"as a cost-effective alternative to the diesel bus."],[289,237.04,3.12,"We started out here in Nairobi in 2022,"],[289,240.2,2.52,"and we've scaled now into Rwanda"],[289,242.76,4.4,"with now a total fleet of 77 buses between both countries."],[289,247.2,1.44,"And every single day,"],[289,248.68,5.52,"we are enabling the commute of over 30,000 passengers between both countries."],[289,258.24,1.16,"Thank you."],[289,259.44,3.16,"We've built this by overcoming some of these challenges."],[289,263.36,2.48,"Number one, we are cheaper than diesel."],[289,266.32,2.16,"Electricity in both Kenya and Rwanda"],[289,268.48,4.92,"is locally generated from renewables like hydro and wind."],[289,273.44,3.56,"That not only makes electricity much cheaper,"],[289,277.0,1.56,"but also cleaner,"],[289,278.6,3.48,"because remember that diesel is all imported here."],[289,282.12,3.56,"So electricity is cheaper, it's cleaner and it's more stable."],[289,287.2,2.2,"We design for the local needs."],[289,290.56,4.24,"Have you ever wondered what kind of specification do we need for the bus"],[289,294.84,2.12,"to be deployed in any of these markets?"],[289,297.4,1.68,"We do not make assumptions."],[289,299.92,2.96,"We pilot with a small fleet"],[289,302.92,3.12,"and then scale based on the local needs."],[289,306.4,2.96,"We're getting an optimized battery capacities,"],[289,309.4,2.76,"the suspension systems, the drive systems,"],[289,312.2,2.28,"and make sure that at the end of the day,"],[289,314.52,4.44,"we are using the real-life route data in order to scale."],[289,319.96,3.88,"I recall when we were doing the market survey in Rwanda,"],[289,323.88,3.2,"one of the top questions that we always received was,"],[289,327.12,4.24,"\"Are you sure your electric bus will be able to handle Kigali's Hills?\""],[289,332.08,5.12,"It's been 18 months now of our operation and that question is now history."],[289,337.84,2.76,"As you can see, the bus is able to go up that hill."],[289,343.24,5.64,"We equip our buses with high-torque motors for the steep inclines"],[289,348.92,2.72,"and regenerative braking for the descent"],[289,351.68,3.48,"to be able to harvest the energy back into the battery."],[289,355.16,3.04,"And then this boosts the efficiency of our buses"],[289,358.24,2.76,"as well as minimizing the brake wear."],[289,362.68,3.84,"One thing that really excites me about our Nairobi fleet"],[289,366.52,4.72,"is we are locally assembling these electric buses right here."],[289,375.32,3.04,"I hope each of you has had a chance to ride on the bus,"],[289,378.4,1.72,"and we hope that you like it."],[289,381.36,3.36,"These buses have been built with lots of love and passion."],[289,385.84,4.0,"And you know, local assembly not only helps reduce the cost,"],[289,389.88,5.24,"but we are enabling to build the critical local manufacturing capacity"],[289,395.16,1.4,"on the planet"],[289,396.56,2.44,"while also creating jobs."],[289,399.04,1.36,"At the end of the day,"],[289,400.44,3.36,"it's going to be about, what can we all do together?"],[289,404.36,2.32,"How do we handle the range anxiety?"],[289,407.8,3.36,"We are building up charging infrastructure."],[289,411.72,1.96,"Let me ask you, what's your dream car?"],[289,413.68,1.2,"Did you ever think about,"],[289,414.92,1.6,"\"Oh, now that I'm buying the car,"],[289,416.52,3.08,"I also need to build a gas station for it.\""],[289,419.6,1.24,"Right?"],[289,420.88,1.8,"No one ever thinks about that."],[289,422.72,3.12,"It should be no different for the electric vehicles."],[289,425.88,4.08,"As BasiGo, we go out and build the charging infrastructure,"],[289,430.0,2.28,"we have a dedicated charging infrastructure team"],[289,432.28,3.68,"that identifies and builds out the charging stations."],[289,436.0,1.48,"We handle all the permitting,"],[289,437.52,1.64,"all the compliance requirements,"],[289,439.2,2.88,"so that our partners do not have to do that."],[289,442.12,1.6,"And we've built stations"],[289,443.76,4.48,"in strategically located places right near the bus routes."],[289,448.28,2.48,"At our stations, we're able to recharge our buses"],[289,450.8,2.48,"in about one and a half to two hours,"],[289,453.32,2.92,"removing this entire burden from the operators."],[289,457.44,2.32,"Think about service and maintenance."],[289,459.8,2.92,"We take this burden away from the operator as well."],[289,463.2,3.2,"You know, new technology can be intimidating, right?"],[289,466.4,3.24,"Especially when there is no local available capacity"],[289,469.64,1.96,"to be able to support that."],[289,471.64,5.0,"So as BasiGo, we have set up service centers in both Kenya and Rwanda,"],[289,476.68,4.32,"fully equipped with trained staff, specialized tools,"],[289,481.04,4.8,"as well as part inventory to minimize the downtime of these buses."],[289,486.48,5.8,"However, our biggest innovation is in the financing of these buses."],[289,493.68,3.32,"We provide the buses through a lease model"],[289,497.04,1.76,"that we call Pay-As-You-Drive,"],[289,498.84,5.88,"taking away the need for the operators to invest heavily at the start."],[289,506.52,2.64,"I've seen firsthand the transformation"],[289,509.2,3.56,"that electric mobility is bringing to Kenya and Rwanda."],[289,512.8,4.72,"The smiles and the awe of the passengers as they experience the BasiGo bus."],[289,517.52,2.8,"A ride so quiet and yet so revolutionary."],[289,520.88,1.84,"The relief of the operators"],[289,522.76,5.32,"being able to scale without being choked by the heavy bank loans."],[289,528.8,3.48,"But I'll be honest and say, is it a smooth ride?"],[289,532.28,1.24,"Perhaps not."],[289,534.0,3.76,"We're experiencing different challenges,"],[289,537.8,2.68,"right from product iteration to the flooding"],[289,540.52,2.36,"that we recently faced here in Nairobi,"],[289,542.88,1.96,"which is a direct threat to our operation"],[289,544.88,2.4,"because of where our batteries are placed."],[289,547.28,1.6,"But then it's a reminder"],[289,548.92,2.64,"of why we are doing this."],[289,551.6,1.36,"Climate change."],[289,553.0,1.4,"And I'll be honest and say,"],[289,554.44,1.16,"at the end of the day,"],[289,555.64,2.72,"we wouldn't trade those experiences for anything else."],[289,558.4,3.2,"It is what's bringing us together as a team."],[289,563.88,2.64,"Given where we are,"],[289,566.56,5.32,"Africa really has the potential to be able to lead"],[289,571.88,4.0,"in this electric-mobility movement across the globe."],[289,577.68,2.84,"Think about here in East Africa,"],[289,580.52,2.92,"our electricity is over 90 percent renewable."],[289,584.16,3.04,"That means deploying an electric vehicle here"],[289,587.24,4.16,"has much greater impact than anywhere else in the world."],[289,591.96,4.48,"One of our electric buses replacing a diesel bus"],[289,596.44,3.52,"enables the mitigation of up to 50 tonnes of CO2"],[289,600.0,2.0,"every single year."],[289,602.36,1.6,"That is five times more"],[289,604.0,4.2,"than what the Tesla is able to mitigate in California."],[289,608.76,1.88,"And what is that speaking to?"],[289,611.16,2.84,"Africa has already proven that, you know,"],[289,614.04,4.28,"we are able to lead in electric vehicle mobility."],[289,618.36,4.96,"We do not have to follow the path of fossil fuel that we followed before."],[289,624.48,4.0,"We don’t have to catch up on the electric vehicle mobility movement."],[289,628.52,2.16,"We can actually lead this."],[289,631.24,4.72,"So far, we have proven that, you know, zero-carbon transport is possible."],[289,636.0,5.48,"It's viable here in Africa with the greatest environmental impact,"],[289,641.52,1.88,"with greater social impact"],[289,643.4,2.08,"and with no government subsidy."],[289,652.72,4.76,"Africa definitely has the potential to be able to lead"],[289,657.48,1.84,"in some of these other sectors"],[289,659.36,4.24,"that were previously stated to be out of the reach for us."],[289,664.16,3.88,"I hope you will be on board as we make this a reality."],[289,668.08,1.16,"(Swahili) Thank you."],[290,0.16,4.8,"Almost all the planet’s earthquakes and volcanic eruptions happen in one place."],[290,4.96,2.4,"The Ring of Fire!"],[290,7.36,1.68,"No really, that’s what it’s called."],[290,9.04,0.88,"Dope, I know."],[290,9.92,3.6,"Nearly the whole Pacific Ocean is bordered by this treacherous region."],[290,13.52,3.44,"We’re talking the sites of some of the biggest earthquakes ever recorded."],[290,16.96,3.2,"Massive volcanoes in Indonesia, Russia, even Antarctica."],[290,20.16,4.96,"Huge explosions and fractures that happen on land and underwater."],[290,25.12,0.48,"But why?"],[290,25.6,2.16,"What makes the Ring of Fire so… fiery?"],[290,27.76,2.35,"Hi, I’m Sage, and this is Crash Course Geology."],[290,34.4,3.92,"The Ring of Fire came about thanks to a billions-of-years-long process"],[290,38.32,2.96,"where all the continents, all the ocean floors,"],[290,41.28,3.68,"everything that makes up Earth’s outermost layer shifts around,"],[290,44.96,2.24,"pulling apart and crashing into each other."],[290,47.2,1.36,"What’s that, Dwayne?"],[290,48.56,0.48,"Oh, yeah."],[290,49.04,2.0,"That does sound like one of your family reunions."],[290,51.04,1.4,"Let’s break down how all that works."],[290,52.44,3.72,"The Earth has three main layers: crust, mantle, and core."],[290,56.16,2.24,"Its rigid outer shell, called the lithosphere,"],[290,58.4,2.96,"is made up of the crust and the uppermost part of the mantle."],[290,61.36,2.96,"But the lithosphere isn’t just one solid piece of earth."],[290,64.32,1.68,"It’s broken up into plates:"],[290,66.0,2.8,"huge slabs of rock that are puzzle-pieced together."],[290,68.8,2.56,"They’re generally composed of both continental lithosphere,"],[290,71.36,3.12,"which makes up the land we can see, and oceanic lithosphere,"],[290,74.48,1.92,"which makes up the seafloor."],[290,76.4,2.64,"Continental lithosphere is made up of mostly thick,"],[290,79.04,1.04,"lightweight granite,"],[290,80.08,4.8,"while oceanic lithosphere is made of denser, heavier rocks like basalt and gabbro."],[290,84.88,4.0,"Roughly 95 percent of Earth’s surface is made up of seven major plates,"],[290,88.88,1.84,"with the rest covered by much smaller ones."],[290,90.72,2.4,"And underneath those plates is the asthenosphere,"],[290,93.12,3.92,"where intense heat from Earth’s core warms up the mantle rocks so much"],[290,97.04,2.96,"that they flow around and bend like taffy."],[290,100.0,2.8,"The hottest rock creeps upwards toward the surface,"],[290,102.8,2.88,"and the colder stuff at the top sinks back down,"],[290,105.68,2.88,"creating convection currents, or heat-based flow cycles."],[290,108.56,3.12,"And those currents power important forces that move Earth’s plates,"],[290,111.68,4.4,"in one big, geologic slow dance called plate tectonics."],[290,116.08,2.72,"Plate tectonics shapes our world—literally."],[290,118.8,5.28,"And all the action happens where plates meet: at their edges, called plate boundaries."],[290,124.08,3.28,"Like, at divergent boundaries, plates move away from each other."],[290,127.36,5.04,"As oceanic plates diverge, the hot mantle rock wells upward and some erupts as lava,"],[290,132.4,2.72,"which cools and hardens into new oceanic crust."],[290,135.12,4.16,"This process is called seafloor spreading, and it’s mainly generated by ridge push,"],[290,139.28,3.6,"the gravity-driven force that causes the new seafloor to sink downward"],[290,142.88,2.0,"and pushes away the older crust."],[290,144.88,4.32,"When continental plates diverge, the earth’s crust thins and breaks apart,"],[290,149.2,1.92,"creating features called rift valleys."],[290,151.12,1.92,"We can see this happening in East Africa,"],[290,153.04,5.6,"where the Nubian and Somalian plates started pulling apart about 25 million years ago."],[290,158.64,4.4,"Meanwhile, at convergent boundaries, plates move toward each other."],[290,163.04,4.0,"When a dense oceanic plate meets a more buoyant continental plate,"],[290,167.04,2.56,"a process called subduction happens:"],[290,169.6,4.72,"the denser plate slides underneath the other one, returning that rock to the mantle."],[290,174.32,1.92,"This is primarily thanks to slab pull:"],[290,176.24,2.64,"the gravitational force that yanks plates downward."],[290,178.88,2.48,"And this is largely what happens in the Ring of Fire;"],[290,181.36,2.0,"it’s full of subduction zones."],[290,183.36,5.2,"As the oceanic plates slide deep underground, they release water into the hot rocks above,"],[290,188.56,1.68,"which has an explosive result."],[290,190.24,4.4,"That water can cause the overlying rocks to melt, driving tons of volcanic activity."],[290,194.64,3.68,"And if two dense, oceanic plates meet at a convergent boundary,"],[290,198.32,2.88,"subduction can create huge depressions in the surface,"],[290,201.2,2.64,"like the Ring of Fire’s Mariana Trench,"],[290,203.84,3.28,"which contains the deepest point in the world!"],[290,207.12,1.52,"It’s on Dwayne’s bucket list."],[290,208.64,3.2,"Now, when two continental plates converge, watch out."],[290,211.84,3.04,"‘Cause instead of subducting, they’ll smash into each other,"],[290,214.88,4.0,"forming massive mountain ranges like the Alps and the Himalayas."],[290,218.88,4.8,"When continental plates converge with oceanic ones, the continental plate usually wins,"],[290,223.68,3.12,"staying on the surface as the denser stuff is forced down."],[290,226.8,2.24,"But it’s not always a competition."],[290,229.04,4.8,"Sometimes plates move past each other at spots called transform boundaries."],[290,233.84,2.4,"You can think of these plates like pieces of sandpaper."],[290,236.24,3.44,"They don't easily slide past each other."],[290,239.68,3.84,"Eventually, that stress reaches a point where the ground moves with a jolt—"],[290,243.52,1.52,"AKA an earthquake."],[290,245.04,2.8,"The San Andreas Fault in California is a transform boundary"],[290,247.84,1.92,"that has had many earthquakes,"],[290,249.76,2.32,"like the Loma Prieta earthquake in 1989,"],[290,252.08,2.88,"which folks in my neck of the woods still talk about."],[290,254.96,2.0,"But that kind of ground rattling can happen at"],[290,256.96,2.96,"all types of plate boundaries when there’s enough stress."],[290,259.92,1.6,"Plate movements have shaped what Earth's"],[290,261.52,2.64,"surface looks like throughout our planet's history."],[290,264.16,2.08,"In fact, at multiple times in Earth's past,"],[290,266.24,2.48,"the shifting tectonic plates have caused all the"],[290,268.72,3.12,"continents to collide and form one giant “supercontinent,”"],[290,271.84,4.8,"the most recent one being Pangea around two to three hundred million years ago."],[290,276.64,2.53,"It’s like seeing Earth in her awkward phase."],[290,279.2,4.08,"So yeah, plate tectonics has been happening for a long time,"],[290,283.28,6.64,"keeping the earth’s surface in constant motion for something like 3 billion years."],[290,289.92,4.72,"The continents move an average of about 1.5 centimeters a year."],[290,294.64,2.4,"That means that, in all of recorded human history,"],[290,297.04,5.12,"the ground below you has probably shifted about 76 meters."],[290,302.16,2.64,"That’s only about as wide as a professional soccer field."],[290,304.8,2.96,"That creeping movement is way too slow to feel."],[290,307.76,2.16,"So, how do we know it’s actually happening?"],[290,309.92,3.68,"Way back in the 1910s, German geologist Alfred Wegener looked at a map of the world"],[290,313.6,4.72,"and noticed that the continents looked like they fit together, almost like puzzle pieces."],[290,318.32,3.44,"And he built a theory to explain what he was seeing – continental drift."],[290,321.76,3.12,"He believed that the continents were moving slowly around the earth,"],[290,324.88,4.32,"together and apart, drifting through the crust like icebergs in the ocean."],[290,329.2,4.88,"He’s even credited with coming up with the name “Pangea,” meaning “all of the earth.”"],[290,334.08,3.04,"But the geological community dunked on Wegener pretty hard."],[290,337.12,3.6,"At the time, most geologists thought that continents formed and moved vertically,"],[290,340.72,2.4,"not horizontally, driven by the heating and"],[290,343.12,2.16,"cooling of Earth’s surface way back when it formed."],[290,345.28,4.24,"But Wegener was onto something, and his idea laid a foundation that other geologists built on."],[290,349.52,2.93,"Let’s hang out with some of my geology rock stars…"],[290,352.45,1.73,"*guitar riff*"],[290,354.18,3.26,"For a long time, we had no clue what the seafloor looked like."],[290,357.44,1.6,"Most geologists thought it was just flat."],[290,359.04,3.28,"But in the 1950s and ‘60s, American geologist Marie Tharp"],[290,362.32,3.04,"and her collaborator Bruce Heezen used sonar to map it."],[290,365.36,4.16,"Given the culture at the time, Bruce got to gather data aboard research ships,"],[290,369.52,2.8,"while Marie was forced to stay on land and make the maps."],[290,372.32,1.36,"‘Cause she was a lady."],[290,373.68,2.96,"With pens, ink, and rulers as her tools,"],[290,376.64,4.4,"she and her colleague Hester Haring methodically plotted each data point."],[290,381.04,4.88,"And what the team discovered was incredible: not a flat, barren wasteland,"],[290,385.92,5.92,"but a 40,000-mile underwater mountain range that circled the globe like a seam on a baseball."],[290,391.84,4.56,"The mid-ocean ridge turned out to be a series of divergent plate boundaries,"],[290,396.4,2.96,"where the mantle wells up as plates pull apart,"],[290,399.36,3.12,"forming huge mountains and driving volcanic eruptions."],[290,402.48,3.04,"This was a major point in favor of plate tectonics theory."],[290,405.52,1.36,"Over the course of several years,"],[290,406.88,1.28,"Tharp and Heezen worked with"],[290,408.16,1.68,"Austrian artist Heinrich Berann"],[290,409.84,3.92,"to paint an accurate map of the entire seafloor,"],[290,413.76,4.72,"and in 1977, the World Ocean Floor panorama was published."],[290,418.48,5.36,"Twenty years later, Tharp was named one of the four greatest cartographers of the 20th century"],[290,423.84,1.52,"by the Library of Congress."],[290,425.36,2.88,"In 1999, Tharp said that, at the start,"],[290,428.24,4.24,"“I had a blank canvas to fill with extraordinary possibilities.”"],[290,432.48,1.68,"And she did just that."],[290,434.16,2.32,"It took decades—and many lines of evidence—"],[290,436.48,3.2,"for the theory of plate tectonics to develop and gain consensus."],[290,439.68,3.76,"But the combined efforts of geologists across the world brought us to the conclusion"],[290,443.44,2.88,"that the earth really does move underneath our feet."],[290,446.32,4.64,"And that consensus didn’t look like a bunch of geologists saying, “Meh, sounds about right.”"],[290,450.96,2.4,"Science historian Naomi Oreskes called it"],[290,453.36,4.8,"the first theory ever to be accepted in the entire history of earth science."],[290,458.16,2.48,"Can you imagine that level of harmony?"],[290,460.64,3.76,"It’d be like if every film critic agreed on the best movie ever made."],[290,464.4,1.48,"Dwayne’s favorite: “Shrek 2.”"],[290,465.92,3.04,"Today, we know that plate tectonics shaped the planet we know and love."],[290,468.96,4.64,"It’s why Europe is warm enough for people to live there, while Antarctica is uninhabited."],[290,473.6,1.28,"Unless you count the penguins."],[290,474.88,3.44,"Regardless, knowing about plate tectonics helps us prepare our communities"],[290,478.32,3.76,"for disasters like volcanic eruptions, earthquakes, and tsunamis."],[290,482.08,2.32,"And it gives us a window into Earth’s past."],[290,484.4,2.32,"Earth’s plates are still moving, right now!"],[290,486.72,2.16,"And we’re still learning more about them."],[290,488.88,4.24,"Like, we don’t know exactly what our planet will look like in the distant future."],[290,493.12,3.04,"Some predict that, 250 million years from now,"],[290,496.16,4.0,"Earth may be home to another supercontinent similar to good ol’ Pangea."],[290,500.16,2.56,"And we don’t know how long plate tectonics will last."],[290,502.72,3.44,"One day, when the earth gets old enough to cool way down inside,"],[290,506.16,4.8,"plate tectonics will come to a stop, reshaping the world in a big way, one last time."],[290,510.96,4.96,"That could look like mountains eroding into plateaus, Earth’s magnetic field stripping away,"],[290,515.92,2.16,"and continents sinking into the ocean."],[290,518.08,2.8,"But that’s potentially billions of years away."],[290,520.88,2.64,"Plate tectonics isn’t going anywhere anytime soon,"],[290,523.52,3.01,"and neither is our massive, magnificent planet."],[290,526.53,2.03,"Our planet moves us."],[290,528.56,5.28,"And not just in the tear-jerking “Wow, Earth really is something!” kind of way."],[290,533.84,2.64,"The land beneath us is constantly shifting,"],[290,536.48,3.76,"creating something new every day—albeit very slowly."],[290,540.24,1.68,"Go at your own pace, girl."],[290,541.92,3.68,"As long as the seafloor keeps spreading and gravity keeps pulling,"],[290,545.6,4.8,"Earth’s plates will be carrying us around, shaping and reshaping the world as we know it."],[290,550.4,3.84,"Next time, we’ll talk about how plate tectonics builds mountains."],[290,554.24,0.72,"See you then."],[290,554.96,2.08,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Geology,"],[290,557.04,2.48,"which was filmed at our studio in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[290,559.52,2.88,"and was made with the help of all these spectacular people."],[290,562.4,3.28,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[290,565.68,1.91,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[291,4.3,3.7,"I was one month out from my Olympic race in Rio,"],[291,8.04,1.53,"the 10,000 meters,"],[291,9.61,1.5,"when I couldn't hit my splits"],[291,11.14,2.74,"in a very important workout."],[291,13.91,2.47,"And I thought,"],[291,16.41,1.5,"what am I going to do?"],[291,17.95,1.37,"Am I good enough?"],[291,19.88,1.8,"I cried in lane one."],[291,21.72,1.67,"Was I even worth it?"],[291,23.42,2.47,"If I couldn't do this in front of my five teammates,"],[291,25.89,3.4,"could I do it in front of thousands of people on the world stage?"],[291,29.93,3.4,"I begged my watch to change its mind"],[291,33.36,3.0,"when my coach, who was also an Olympian,"],[291,36.37,4.3,"turned to me and said something calmly and confidently,"],[291,40.7,1.94,"\"Take your watch off,\" he said."],[291,42.67,1.17,"\"What?\""],[291,43.84,1.6,"\"Take your watch off.\""],[291,45.44,2.1,"He never said this to me before,"],[291,47.55,2.37,"so I thought maybe I wasn't good enough."],[291,49.91,3.2,"And he said, “No, Lex, it’s the ‘rule of thirds.’”"],[291,53.78,2.1,"“What’s the ‘rule of thirds’?” I asked."],[291,55.92,1.87,"And then he told me the best advice"],[291,57.82,2.5,"I've ever gotten in my entire life."],[291,60.83,1.64,"The rule of thirds"],[291,62.49,3.4,"is that when you're chasing a dream or doing anything hard,"],[291,65.93,3.24,"you're supposed to feel good a third of the time,"],[291,69.2,1.87,"OK a third of the time"],[291,71.1,2.33,"and crappy a third of the time."],[291,74.07,5.57,"If you felt too good all the time, it might be a sign that the ratio's off,"],[291,79.68,1.83,"and you're not pushing yourself enough"],[291,81.51,2.34,"to go beyond the boundaries of your potential"],[291,83.88,1.67,"and to the great unknown."],[291,86.25,1.83,"You might need to dial things up."],[291,88.59,3.84,"But if you feel too bad all the time,"],[291,92.46,4.71,"that might mean that you are fatiguing or doing something unsustainable,"],[291,97.16,3.04,"and you might need to actually dial things back."],[291,100.6,4.6,"If you're within this ratio, then the bad days aren't bad."],[291,105.2,2.74,"They just mean you're chasing a dream."],[291,108.47,2.57,"That day, my coach had me take my watch off"],[291,111.08,1.97,"because it wasn't about pace,"],[291,113.08,1.74,"it was about effort."],[291,116.05,2.77,"And I love my bad days now."],[291,119.48,2.9,"So this rule has really changed my perspective"],[291,122.42,1.9,"that day and for the rest of my life."],[291,124.66,1.83,"That day, I got back on the track,"],[291,126.52,2.74,"and I finished the workout giving 100 percent effort"],[291,129.29,3.17,"even when I didn't feel 100 percent fast."],[291,133.1,4.3,"And then I went to the Olympics, and I broke a national record,"],[291,137.43,1.87,"and I ran a personal best."],[291,140.5,1.27,"And it was awesome."],[291,141.81,1.33,"It was awesome."],[291,143.17,1.87,"The Olympics is awesome."],[291,145.07,1.9,"My coach was right, OK?"],[291,147.01,1.2,"He was right."],[291,148.25,2.97,"And this rule has not only helped me in my running,"],[291,151.22,1.94,"but it's how I live the rest of my life."],[291,153.15,1.8,"It's how I wrote my book \"Bravey,\""],[291,154.95,2.2,"it’s how I’ve directed three movies"],[291,157.15,2.74,"and it's even how I look at myself day to day,"],[291,159.92,1.17,"how I feel,"],[291,161.09,2.77,"how I think about my mood and my emotions."],[291,164.53,3.3,"OK, a few years after the Olympics -- I guess I wasn’t done running."],[291,167.83,3.17,"I went and entered this big ultramarathon."],[291,171.03,1.74,"And that means it's over a marathon."],[291,172.77,1.44,"And it was not just any race."],[291,174.21,1.87,"It was the hardest race in the country,"],[291,176.11,3.1,"the Leadville 100-mile race in the Rocky Mountains."],[291,179.24,3.4,"And it's 16 times the distance of my Olympic race,"],[291,182.65,2.17,"over 15,000 feet of elevation."],[291,184.81,3.47,"And only half the people who start finish this race, statistically."],[291,188.28,2.6,"You go all night, it's wild."],[291,190.92,2.67,"And so it's the opposite of the Olympics, right?"],[291,193.62,2.94,"It's not about pace or time or place."],[291,196.89,3.64,"It's about being present and just continuing to move."],[291,200.56,3.6,"And there was no coach out there to monitor my thirds."],[291,204.7,1.17,"And during this race,"],[291,205.9,4.1,"I felt every feeling under the sun and under the moon."],[291,210.04,5.51,"And you know, it was like I was a brave, capable voyager in one moment,"],[291,215.55,2.5,"and then I was barely hanging on in others."],[291,218.65,2.9,"A past me would have felt really down"],[291,221.55,2.3,"about the really hard moments."],[291,223.85,2.9,"And there were some really hard moments in the woods."],[291,226.79,4.17,"But now I understood this was a part of a bigger picture of success."],[291,231.36,2.64,"Now in the same race, there was a point at which I felt"],[291,234.03,1.8,"like I couldn't run anymore."],[291,235.87,4.04,"Like, I felt like I had metal rods in my legs, and I had to walk."],[291,239.94,3.2,"And I actually walked the last 40 miles of that race"],[291,243.14,1.94,"through the night, all night."],[291,245.11,1.2,"But I didn't give up."],[291,246.84,5.11,"And it really taught me to let go of control"],[291,251.95,2.8,"and just believe in the process"],[291,254.78,2.84,"and be really, really present."],[291,258.79,4.64,"If we step back, the rule of thirds can help us see the picture"],[291,263.43,2.24,"of a bigger dream-chasing journey"],[291,265.7,1.94,"and evaluate and assess."],[291,267.67,1.57,"And in a moment of pain,"],[291,269.27,2.34,"it can help us stay on our own team,"],[291,271.63,1.77,"even when there's no coach"],[291,273.44,3.5,"or anyone else there to tell you that it was meant to be this way."],[291,278.11,3.27,"Instead of asking yourself to be the best,"],[291,281.41,2.77,"ask yourself to try your best"],[291,284.21,1.8,"and don't give up."],[291,286.45,1.33,"OK?"],[291,287.79,1.4,"It's a bad day."],[291,289.22,1.94,"It's not a bad life."],[291,292.86,3.9,"I think that that day that my coach taught me the rule of thirds"],[291,296.79,2.77,"must have been his good third,"],[291,299.6,5.04,"because that day he taught me how to understand and coach myself."],[291,305.1,1.17,"Thank you."],[292,7.0,4.71,"Troy’s princess, Cassandra, wasn’t cruel to her soon-to-be sister-in-law,"],[292,11.72,2.88,"the legendary beauty Helen, out of spite."],[292,15.01,4.75,"It was because she was suffering under the weight of a tortuously twisted curse,"],[292,20.06,4.17,"which haunted her with appalling visions linked to Helen’s arrival."],[292,25.23,4.17,"Helen was already married to the Spartan king Menelaus, after all."],[292,29.69,4.3,"And when Cassandra’s brother, Prince Paris, prepared to sail to Sparta,"],[292,34.36,4.88,"she foresaw his calamitous attachment to Helen and begged him to stay."],[292,39.53,1.71,"But he’d merely dismissed her,"],[292,41.33,3.38,"fallen for Helen, and returned to Troy with her in tow."],[292,46.38,2.21,"Beset by violent premonitions,"],[292,48.63,3.75,"Cassandra then warned that Menelaus’ vengeful crusade"],[292,52.38,4.38,"would lead to the brutal fall of their family and the city of Troy."],[292,57.22,2.29,"But once again, people ignored her."],[292,60.14,1.88,"In fact, they always did."],[292,62.27,2.59,"And this was the crux of her curse."],[292,65.14,3.88,"As a young woman she had been pursued by the god Apollo."],[292,69.19,2.54,"He offered her the gift of prophecy."],[292,71.86,6.01,"But when she withdrew from his advances, he twisted his gift into a maddening hex:"],[292,78.32,6.01,"from that day on, Cassandra would be able to precisely see and describe the future—"],[292,84.58,3.0,"but her prophecies would never be believed."],[292,87.88,4.17,"Despite this, Cassandra resolved to always speak the truth,"],[292,92.25,3.13,"even as her family fell deeper into denial."],[292,96.34,3.42,"Her dire warnings became increasingly repellent to her father,"],[292,100.01,1.58,"Trojan King Priam."],[292,101.97,2.42,"And when the war began, as she’d predicted,"],[292,104.68,3.09,"he declared that her meddling was bad for morale"],[292,108.02,2.17,"and confined her to a high tower."],[292,110.69,4.71,"From this unobstructed lookout, Cassandra watched everything unfold."],[292,115.65,2.25,"Over 10 agonizing years,"],[292,117.91,4.75,"she witnessed her own grisly visions of senseless destruction come to pass"],[292,122.66,1.54,"on the battlefield below."],[292,124.62,3.92,"When dawn broke one day to reveal a huge wooden horse"],[292,128.54,1.75,"in place of the Greek encampment,"],[292,130.59,3.96,"the Trojans believed that the victory had finally been secured."],[292,134.88,2.0,"Their enemies appeared to have fled,"],[292,136.88,3.59,"leaving this inscrutable token of peace behind."],[292,140.64,3.25,"But as others streamed into the streets to celebrate,"],[292,144.01,2.71,"Cassandra realized the insidious truth."],[292,147.14,2.71,"Brandishing a battle-axe and a flaming torch,"],[292,149.94,3.09,"she descended from the tower and ran at the wooden horse,"],[292,153.11,2.46,"alerting everyone that it was a trick."],[292,155.94,3.75,"Instead of heeding Cassandra’s warnings, they wrestled her to the ground"],[292,159.82,3.25,"and scoffed that she was getting more paranoid with age,"],[292,163.45,4.59,"then proceeded to tug the behemoth gift horse inside the city’s walls."],[292,168.66,3.21,"As Troy fell into an oblivious slumber that night,"],[292,172.25,3.0,"Greek soldiers descended from the wooden horse's belly."],[292,175.59,4.42,"All had gone according to plan, and they scattered among the city streets,"],[292,180.09,6.13,"intoxicated with their victory, to unleash a torrent of pent up, violent chaos."],[292,186.89,4.84,"Cassandra had seen her city burn and heard her people’s screams many times,"],[292,192.15,3.13,"but that didn't make the reality any easier to bear."],[292,195.65,1.83,"Her nightmare had come to pass."],[292,197.94,4.46,"As dawn neared, the commander of the Greek army, Agamemnon himself,"],[292,202.45,2.84,"arrived to claim Cassandra as his prize."],[292,205.7,1.63,"He dragged her back to his ship,"],[292,207.37,3.25,"boasting relentlessly of his victory as they set sail."],[292,211.04,3.34,"But Cassandra told him plainly that he'd be punished."],[292,214.59,1.92,"Earlier in the years of battle,"],[292,216.5,3.13,"Agamemnon had sacrificed his daughter to the war effort,"],[292,219.88,2.88,"hoping the gods would favor the Greeks in return."],[292,223.18,4.8,"Now Cassandra saw that his wife, Clytemnestra, awaited his return,"],[292,228.31,1.83,"poised for revenge."],[292,230.6,2.75,"But just as certainly as she saw that Agamemnon"],[292,233.35,2.0,"was sailing towards his own murder,"],[292,235.61,3.67,"Cassandra saw that she would also be killed in the fallout."],[292,239.86,2.92,"As always, she confronted the grim fate."],[292,243.03,3.54,"In her lonely pact with the truth, no matter how ugly,"],[292,246.7,2.42,"Cassandra had always stood apart."],[292,249.58,3.46,"After all, was it not her but everyone else"],[292,253.21,3.88,"who had fallen victim to the curse, living in deceit’s shadow,"],[292,257.38,3.5,"oblivious to reality until the bitter end?"],[293,4.13,5.11,"I had just turned 22 when I finished my first semester of graduate school"],[293,9.24,2.7,"at the Iowa Writers' Workshop."],[293,11.94,3.74,"I was also taking classes in the printmaking program,"],[293,15.71,4.14,"ambitious, young, art-loving thing that I was."],[293,19.88,3.3,"I had flown from Iowa City to Chicago O'Hare,"],[293,23.22,4.04,"where I'd change planes and go home to Nashville for Christmas."],[293,27.29,2.9,"I had my Hermes 3000 typewriter with me,"],[293,30.19,5.44,"technically portable at 14 pounds because I wrote stories."],[293,35.67,2.8,"I also had a shoulder bag of zinc plates,"],[293,38.5,3.07,"which I planned to engrave over the break."],[293,42.51,3.47,"Have you ever traveled with a bag of zinc plates?"],[293,47.08,2.1,"They're a lot heavier than a typewriter."],[293,50.68,4.71,"In O'Hare, I got very, very lost."],[293,55.42,2.5,"I put my typewriter down,"],[293,57.95,2.4,"stood there lopsided, looking at my ticket,"],[293,60.39,4.24,"when a young man walked up and asked me if I needed help."],[293,65.86,5.04,"Time changes memory, but I remember him clearly."],[293,70.9,4.17,"He had on khaki pants and a pink Oxford shirt."],[293,75.07,4.54,"He had straight sandy blond hair and wire-rimmed glasses."],[293,79.61,2.27,"He looked like the young John Denver."],[293,82.61,1.33,"I gave him my ticket."],[293,85.02,4.41,"\"You are really lost,\" he said."],[293,89.45,3.6,"And then he took my extraordinarily heavy bag"],[293,93.09,1.27,"from my shoulder"],[293,94.39,1.8,"and the typewriter from my hand,"],[293,96.19,2.67,"and said he would walk me to my gate."],[293,100.63,1.8,"Side note."],[293,102.47,2.54,"This was 1986."],[293,105.03,3.4,"I was shy and I was plain."],[293,108.47,4.8,"I was not the kind of girl whose typewriter was carried by men"],[293,113.28,1.7,"who look like John Denver."],[293,116.35,4.47,"And my heart expanded with the wonder."],[293,121.65,2.74,"Together, we traversed many concourses,"],[293,124.42,1.9,"and I began to worry about the time."],[293,126.32,3.04,"I said he shouldn't risk missing his flight"],[293,129.36,2.0,"so that I could make mine."],[293,131.96,2.8,"That's when he told me he didn't have a flight."],[293,135.77,1.97,"I asked him if he worked in the airport,"],[293,137.74,1.57,"and he said, \"Yeah, sort of.\""],[293,139.34,2.17,"He said he was a Hare Krishna."],[293,141.54,1.8,"Audience: Ah!"],[293,147.91,4.77,"What a beautiful world it was"],[293,152.72,3.47,"when you could still get lost in an airport,"],[293,156.22,5.21,"when zinc plates, sharp as meat cleavers, filled your carry-ons."],[293,161.46,6.51,"When Hare Krishnas, those dancing, chanting members of a religious sect"],[293,168.0,3.24,"roamed freely from gate to gate."],[293,174.37,3.97,"I was terrified."],[293,179.51,4.0,"But of what? That he'd kidnap me and make me a vegetarian?"],[293,183.55,2.24,"I was already a vegetarian."],[293,187.59,4.14,"I had to keep walking with him because he had my typewriter,"],[293,191.75,4.0,"and I was in love with my typewriter."],[293,197.03,5.74,"In those days, there were no screens updating travelers as to departure times,"],[293,202.8,3.44,"so I didn't know that my flight was two hours delayed"],[293,206.24,1.43,"until I reached the gate."],[293,208.67,5.34,"The Hare Krishna laid my burdens down and said he'd wait with me."],[293,216.75,6.54,"Would I have chosen to spend two hours in O'Hare with a Hare Krishna?"],[293,224.49,1.4,"No."],[293,226.46,3.9,"But I lacked the courage to bolt."],[293,231.19,3.4,"I decided that given the circumstances,"],[293,234.63,4.1,"the only thing I could do was listen."],[293,239.74,3.44,"The ability to really listen to another person"],[293,243.21,2.8,"is an essential skill for a novelist."],[293,246.04,3.9,"It's an essential skill for all human beings."],[293,249.95,2.74,"And what the Hare Krishna told me"],[293,252.72,5.67,"was one of the most remarkable things I had ever heard in my life."],[293,259.59,1.23,"He said,"],[293,261.66,2.27,"\"Imagine"],[293,263.96,3.67,"loving God so much"],[293,267.67,5.3,"that you would be willing to stand in an airport all day"],[293,273.0,2.87,"so that you could tell people about God's love.\""],[293,277.34,4.04,"All day long, people rushed past him,"],[293,281.41,4.91,"even after he had forsaken his traditional saffron robes"],[293,286.35,2.07,"to mitigate first impressions."],[293,288.45,2.9,"They buried their faces in their newspapers"],[293,291.39,1.94,"as soon as he started to speak."],[293,293.32,3.4,"And still he kept showing up"],[293,296.76,5.34,"because God's love was the greatest thing he had ever known,"],[293,302.13,1.7,"and he wanted to share it."],[293,305.9,3.0,"When I finally made it home to Nashville,"],[293,308.91,3.7,"I told this story to everyone --"],[293,312.61,2.6,"in fact, I told it for years."],[293,316.18,5.14,"Two hours in an airport with a Hare Krishna."],[293,323.05,1.74,"But as I got older,"],[293,325.82,5.27,"I could see myself becoming that Hare Krishna,"],[293,332.16,5.51,"wanting to testify about the greatest love in my life,"],[293,338.5,1.24,"which is reading."],[293,341.2,5.24,"So great is my need to share this love"],[293,346.44,6.17,"that it outweighs my significant need for privacy."],[293,352.62,2.64,"I gave up printmaking when I left Iowa,"],[293,355.29,4.21,"but books have been my steadfast companions."],[293,359.49,5.14,"My solace, my teachers, my joy."],[293,364.66,3.97,"I can't imagine what life would be without reading."],[293,368.67,5.97,"And so pretty much every day, in every situation I find myself in,"],[293,374.67,2.64,"I'm out there sharing the good news."],[293,378.91,5.21,"For a long time, my love for books was more cloistered, less zealous."],[293,384.11,3.27,"But in 2011, all of that changed."],[293,387.42,3.1,"The two major bookstores in Nashville closed,"],[293,390.55,3.94,"and after waiting around for someone else to open a bookstore,"],[293,394.49,1.87,"I decided to do it myself."],[293,397.49,4.8,"This was not the fulfillment of a lifelong dream."],[293,402.33,3.4,"It was more like irritation."],[293,407.3,4.27,"People love to tell me"],[293,411.57,2.44,"that bookstores are dead,"],[293,414.04,3.9,"that books themselves are hobbling towards the dust heap"],[293,417.95,2.34,"of cultural irrelevance."],[293,421.38,2.7,"Heresy, I say!"],[293,424.12,4.1,"Books are the rock on which I built my church."],[293,434.33,5.77,"Like the Hare Krishna, I didn't do this because of what I needed."],[293,440.14,3.44,"I had books, I would always have books."],[293,443.57,3.44,"I fought for books because you need them."],[293,448.28,4.24,"The summer before we opened Parnassus Books, I went on tour."],[293,452.55,4.3,"I had been going on tour regularly since 1992."],[293,456.89,5.5,"This one, for a novel I wrote called \"State of Wonder,\""],[293,462.43,2.84,"was going to be my fact-finding mission."],[293,465.3,3.84,"I collected information from all the booksellers I knew,"],[293,469.17,4.8,"but the most valuable came from my friend Daniel Goldin"],[293,474.0,3.34,"at Boswell Books in Milwaukee."],[293,477.37,4.27,"He told me people were desperate"],[293,481.68,3.97,"to buy anything that was hanging from the ceiling,"],[293,485.68,1.8,"which is true."],[293,487.48,4.5,"More importantly, he told me to put the children's section"],[293,491.99,3.24,"as far away from the front door as possible,"],[293,495.26,2.1,"so that when a child makes a break for it,"],[293,497.36,2.67,"you have the maximum opportunity to catch her."],[293,501.53,2.77,"Daniel talked a lot about the children's section,"],[293,504.33,3.34,"which was not a part of bookstores I knew much about."],[293,508.87,3.57,"\"If you want customers,\" he said,"],[293,512.44,1.9,"\"you have to raise them yourself.\""],[293,516.01,1.5,"That made sense."],[293,517.55,2.5,"Small children coming to story time,"],[293,520.08,3.17,"being read to, learning to read themselves,"],[293,523.29,3.74,"will grow up to be great customers."],[293,527.06,3.04,"15 years later, I can attest that this is true."],[293,530.13,3.24,"We have raised up a raft of customers."],[293,534.93,5.34,"But over time, this charming bit of wisdom has changed."],[293,540.3,3.37,"I don't think of it as cultivating shoppers anymore."],[293,543.71,2.57,"I think of it as cultivating readers."],[293,546.28,4.44,"And not just for the books I write or the books I sell,"],[293,550.75,3.37,"but for all books."],[293,554.12,1.3,"I've come to believe"],[293,555.45,4.47,"that if you're interested in living in a society where people read,"],[293,559.96,4.5,"it's also your responsibility to cultivate readers."],[293,566.83,1.87,"What does that even mean?"],[293,569.7,4.94,"Well reading to children the same way people read to us."],[293,575.4,5.41,"You can buy books for children who are in Title I schools"],[293,580.84,2.84,"who might not have books of their own."],[293,583.71,2.7,"We do a lot of this at Parnassus."],[293,587.99,3.94,"You can speak out against book banning."],[293,595.56,4.3,"Because books are not the things"],[293,599.9,2.67,"endangering our children."],[293,607.0,3.94,"And speak up for teachers and librarians"],[293,610.97,3.1,"who are doing the jobs that they've been trained for."],[293,614.95,3.37,"Also, you can just read a book."],[293,619.42,2.77,"Do you wish that people read books"],[293,622.22,3.67,"instead of, say, constantly scrolling on their phones?"],[293,625.92,2.57,"Then read books!"],[293,628.49,2.64,"Do you want your children to be readers?"],[293,631.16,1.8,"Then model that behavior."],[293,633.0,6.34,"Of course you're going to read to them, but they also have to see you read."],[293,640.47,3.17,"Like all the people who told me not to open a bookstore,"],[293,643.67,3.17,"you may be wondering if books are even relevant"],[293,646.88,3.04,"in this golden age of technology."],[293,650.55,2.24,"Think of it this way."],[293,652.78,5.71,"Every piece of information coming out of your computer or phone"],[293,658.49,2.2,"is a single thread."],[293,660.72,3.87,"At any given moment, you are holding countless threads"],[293,664.63,4.2,"which range in quality from vital to worthless."],[293,669.97,5.14,"What a novelist does is take all of those threads"],[293,675.14,2.77,"and weaves them into a tapestry."],[293,679.54,1.23,"Maidens,"],[293,681.71,1.37,"unicorns,"],[293,684.18,1.23,"pear trees."],[293,686.52,2.17,"This is no small job,"],[293,688.72,2.33,"but when it's done right,"],[293,691.09,4.4,"the outcome is both beautiful and enduring."],[293,695.49,4.44,"Novels teach us empathy by putting us into another person's life,"],[293,699.96,4.57,"and they define our history by showing us how we've changed"],[293,704.53,2.3,"and will continue to change."],[293,706.87,2.13,"Think of Harriet Beecher Stowe."],[293,710.07,3.37,"Now think of John Updike."],[293,713.44,3.1,"And if this wasn't reason enough to love books,"],[293,716.58,6.11,"they also help us develop and preserve what I like to call a long-format brain."],[293,722.72,5.0,"The problems of the world cannot be seen one thread at a time,"],[293,727.76,2.67,"nor can they be solved."],[293,730.46,3.24,"The ability to think with depth and complexity"],[293,733.7,5.11,"is greatly enhanced by reading more than 280 characters at a time."],[293,738.84,2.67,"If you haven't read a novel in a while,"],[293,742.64,2.0,"it may feel strange at first,"],[293,744.67,1.4,"but stick with it."],[293,746.08,3.0,"What you put in is what you'll get back."],[293,750.88,2.94,"What surprised me most about owning a bookstore --"],[293,753.85,2.54,"and practically everything about it surprises me --"],[293,756.42,3.0,"is that it's not just books people come in for."],[293,760.22,6.21,"Reading -- that solitary endeavor -- has proven to be a means of connection."],[293,766.46,2.3,"Our monthly book club at Parnassus"],[293,768.8,3.87,"is now so large we've broken it into three sections."],[293,772.7,3.8,"We also have a classics book club and a romance book club,"],[293,776.54,4.77,"and once a year those two groups come together to read a classic romance,"],[293,781.34,2.17,"most recently \"Pride and Prejudice.\""],[293,784.85,4.87,"Our author events have ranged from Jeff Kinney to Percival Everett,"],[293,789.75,5.84,"to Barbara Kingsolver, to R. F. Kuang, to Ina Garten to Bono."],[293,795.62,2.8,"And whether it's presidential histories"],[293,798.46,1.27,"or mysteries,"],[293,799.76,4.64,"what we see is that books give people the means by which to connect."],[293,804.43,4.91,"The conversations may start with books, but they go everywhere."],[293,809.37,3.07,"Reading shines the light"],[293,812.44,2.94,"that disrupts the dark isolation"],[293,815.41,2.87,"so many people find themselves in."],[293,819.25,2.54,"Go to your local bookstore"],[293,821.78,1.83,"and see what I'm talking about."],[293,826.89,3.3,"Every time I change planes in O'Hare,"],[293,831.6,2.04,"I think about the Hare Krishna."],[293,835.3,3.0,"A person I was afraid of,"],[293,839.2,2.1,"a person I had nothing in common with,"],[293,842.21,4.24,"a person whose only intention was to help me,"],[293,846.44,2.33,"both to find my gate"],[293,848.78,3.44,"and to find a force in the world greater than myself."],[293,853.45,3.2,"Through experience, we had both come to see"],[293,856.65,4.61,"that we were part of the larger human fabric."],[293,861.29,1.37,"He, through his faith,"],[293,862.66,2.04,"and me,"],[293,864.69,1.64,"through a different kind of faith."],[293,867.63,4.1,"Maybe this time we would sit at the gate a little bit longer,"],[293,871.74,2.57,"and I would ask him what he was reading."],[293,875.3,5.94,"I would tell him how much I admired the courage of his convictions."],[293,882.51,3.77,"I would thank him for helping me find my way."],[294,15.08,4.44,"Meadows that only a few weeks ago were buried beneath the snow"],[294,19.52,2.08,"are now full of life."],[294,39.96,1.6,"But in these mountains,"],[294,41.56,2.84,"the good times will not last long."],[294,45.76,3.32,"So the bears must feed as fast as they can."],[294,52.6,1.4,"During the summer months,"],[294,54.0,4.96,"an adult can put on 180 kilos gorging on plants,"],[294,58.96,2.12,"and, if they can catch them…"],[294,62.68,2.04,"..a marmot or two."],[294,68.04,4.36,"But, just now, the bears have something else on their minds."],[294,76.0,1.56,"It's becoming warmer"],[294,77.56,2.84,"and the bears are keen to shed their thick winter coats."],[294,84.16,3.52,"Mothers show the cubs what to do about this."],[294,97.64,2.4,"They'll soon catch on."],[294,102.24,3.76,"Some trees, it seems, are particularly suitable for rubbing."],[294,107.24,1.96,"Bears have their favourites"],[294,109.2,3.48,"and will travel long distances to visit them."],[294,115.0,3.12,"Some itches just have to be scratched."],[294,139.12,4.64,"There are now around 30 bears in this one valley."],[294,149.4,5.44,"As they rub, each leaves an individual and recognisable scent."],[294,158.32,4.4,"So the tree soon carries a list of who's around,"],[294,162.72,2.44,"which might help individuals to avoid a fight."],[294,167.2,2.68,"To best spread their scent,"],[294,169.88,2.04,"they really have to put their back into it."],[294,213.92,2.12,"But summer is short."],[294,216.4,3.56,"Itches satisfactorily scratched,"],[294,219.96,2.44,"it's time to eat."],[294,224.76,1.24,"In a couple of months,"],[294,226.0,2.52,"they will have to return to their dens to hibernate."],[294,229.44,3.16,"So now they must put on as much weight as they can."],[295,7.42,1.88,"Plastic is everywhere."],[295,9.46,4.17,"It’s in our clothes, our food— even the air we breathe."],[295,13.76,4.92,"So, it’s no surprise that plastic is also in our bodies."],[295,18.97,4.55,"But how exactly do these microscopic particles affect our health?"],[295,24.44,6.26,"Micro and nanoplastics generally enter our bodies in one of three ways:"],[295,30.9,2.33,"through the air, through our skin,"],[295,33.36,3.55,"and most commonly, through what we eat and drink."],[295,37.24,3.25,"While processed foods have the most plastic,"],[295,40.62,3.71,"particles have infiltrated our farms and seas,"],[295,44.46,4.59,"making their way into most seafood, meat, and produce."],[295,49.46,4.09,"Plastic packaging sheds particles into whatever it touches—"],[295,53.76,5.63,"a single liter of bottled water can contain over 200,000 of them."],[295,59.6,5.96,"Even metal cans and paper wrappers often hide plastic linings."],[295,65.77,4.42,"At home, particles enter our food from plastic cutting boards"],[295,70.19,1.75,"and Teflon cookware."],[295,72.19,3.71,"Plastic storage containers are especially bad—"],[295,76.07,4.59,"microwaving food in these can release millions of particles"],[295,80.7,1.5,"into your leftovers."],[295,82.7,3.92,"The amount of plastic you breathe depends on your environment."],[295,86.92,6.21,"Urban areas and indoor spaces contain more airborne particles than rural ones,"],[295,93.21,4.59,"but most people inhale tens of thousands of particles a day."],[295,98.26,5.92,"Finally, beauty and personal care products let plastics and toxic chemicals"],[295,104.18,1.71,"seep into our skin."],[295,106.77,6.76,"Once inside, nanoplastics are small enough to pass through most cell membranes"],[295,113.69,2.33,"and settle deep in our tissues."],[295,116.32,2.63,"Our bodies recognize these invaders"],[295,118.95,3.71,"and trigger inflammation to remove the foreign particles."],[295,122.83,4.25,"But since our natural defenses can’t break down plastic,"],[295,127.29,3.79,"the inflammatory response can trigger repeatedly,"],[295,131.25,1.96,"causing long term damage."],[295,133.84,4.55,"For example, particles circulating through the respiratory system"],[295,138.38,4.0,"can trigger widespread inflammation that impairs breathing"],[295,142.39,3.0,"and contributes to asthma and pneumonia."],[295,145.85,5.25,"Worse still, inflammation brings an influx of blood to these tissues,"],[295,151.1,3.88,"which plastics use to hitch a ride throughout the body."],[295,155.36,5.51,"Particles have been detected in our livers, spleens, muscles, bones,"],[295,161.03,1.71,"and even our brains."],[295,163.53,5.17,"It's hard to say exactly how much plastic is in any one person,"],[295,168.83,3.34,"but these particles are just the tip of the iceberg."],[295,172.42,5.55,"There are over 16,000 chemicals involved in plastic production,"],[295,178.05,4.42,"and whenever plastic enters your body, some of these chemicals do too."],[295,182.84,5.63,"The vast majority of these have health impacts we don’t yet understand,"],[295,188.68,3.25,"making it tricky to link specific health issues"],[295,191.98,2.75,"with specific chemicals and plastics."],[295,194.9,6.13,"However, researchers have identified a few particularly dangerous groups."],[295,201.86,6.13,"Endocrine disrupting chemicals— such as phthalates, PFAS, and BPA—"],[295,208.12,5.21,"are known to alter hormonal activity, wreaking havoc across the body."],[295,213.79,3.5,"By activating or inhibiting hormone receptors,"],[295,217.42,3.46,"EDCs can hijack our metabolisms,"],[295,221.01,4.17,"increasing the risk for obesity and type 2 diabetes."],[295,225.39,5.38,"They can mimic testosterone and estrogen, confusing our body’s hormonal balance."],[295,231.1,5.13,"In women, high phthalate levels have been linked with pregnancy complications."],[295,236.56,5.42,"And in men, high BPA exposure has been connected to lower sperm count."],[295,242.45,4.63,"In fact, research suggests these chemicals have contributed"],[295,247.08,4.55,"to the global decline in sperm count over the past 50 years."],[295,252.21,5.25,"Today, most people encounter these chemicals before they're even born."],[295,257.84,6.88,"A study on over 300 pre-teens found that exposure to EDCs in utero"],[295,264.88,4.8,"may have impacted when they entered puberty over a decade later."],[295,270.43,3.92,"It's easy to feel overwhelmed by this plastic plague."],[295,274.6,4.63,"After all, there are no medical interventions for getting this material"],[295,279.23,1.38,"out of our bodies."],[295,280.69,4.63,"And data suggests these particles accumulate inside us"],[295,285.41,4.38,"faster than we excrete them through sweat, urine, and feces."],[295,290.04,4.0,"But small choices you make each day can reduce"],[295,294.04,2.21,"how much plastic enters your body."],[295,296.83,1.63,"When buying clothes,"],[295,298.46,4.71,"look for pieces made from natural fibers without toxic chemicals."],[295,303.38,3.59,"Swap your plastic cutting boards and storage containers"],[295,306.97,3.96,"for wood, stainless steel, and glass replacements."],[295,311.26,2.13,"Eliminate single use plastic"],[295,313.39,4.21,"and buy fresh, unpackaged foods whenever possible."],[295,318.27,3.8,"The more people stop buying plastic, the less of it we'll see."],[295,322.23,4.17,"But fixing a problem this big requires big solutions."],[295,326.57,4.3,"Just like gasoline, plastic is a petrochemical product"],[295,330.99,2.79,"made and sold at a massive scale."],[295,334.25,5.42,"So it’s going to take legislation on the local, state, and country level"],[295,339.67,2.04,"to make this material safer"],[295,341.84,5.51,"and regulate just how much of it we put into our world and our bodies."],[296,0.13,4.15,"In the wilds of Chile, there are mountains that look like… Oreos?"],[296,4.28,1.76,"These are Los Cuernos del Painé."],[296,6.04,3.16,"Their unusual appearance draws thousands of visitors every year."],[296,9.2,4.24,"With a thick light stripe between two dark layers, they’re out here looking like a snack."],[296,13.44,4.4,"But besides looking cool, they actually have a lot to teach us about geology’s star subject…"],[296,17.84,2.84,"That’s right, it’s the rock episode!"],[296,20.68,1.6,"Hit it Dwayne! *guitar riff*"],[296,22.28,2.96,"Hi! I'm Sage, and this is Crash Course Geology."],[296,29.76,2.76,"Los Cuernos are amazing not just for their unique visual features,"],[296,32.52,2.84,"but because they showcase every part of the rock cycle."],[296,35.36,1.36,"*guitar riff*"],[296,36.72,4.08,"Listen, Dwayne, if you do that every time I say “rock” in this episode, we'll be here for a while."],[296,40.8,1.92,"Just tone it down a little bit, pal."],[296,42.72,4.04,"The rock cycle is the process that recycles Earth’s raw materials over time."],[296,47.4,4.8,"The rock cycle makes it possible for any kind of rock to transform into another kind of rock."],[296,52.2,3.88,"It’s one of the forces that continuously freshen up Earth’s surface."],[296,56.08,2.44,"It’s the reason why, when you hold a rock in your hand,"],[296,58.52,3.2,"you’re not holding something that’s frozen in time forever."],[296,61.72,5.12,"A rock’s current identity isn’t what it once was… or what it will be in the future."],[296,66.84,3.56,"But first: what really is a rock?"],[296,71.32,4.56,"Stonehenge. The Blarney Stone. Emma Stone."],[296,75.88,5.03,"Since the dawn of time, rocks have held many meanings for many — What?"],[296,81.37,1.03,"We don’t have that much time?"],[296,84.2,1.56,"What am I supposed to do with these?"],[296,87.76,1.24,"As I was saying."],[296,89.0,3.2,"In geology, the word “rock” has a specific meaning:"],[296,92.2,2.44,"a solid cluster of one or more minerals."],[296,94.64,4.12,"Minerals, by the way, are solid crystals with a specific chemical composition,"],[296,98.76,2.24,"formed by geological processes."],[296,101.0,1.4,"Dwayne one of us is gonna have to change."],[296,105.19,1.73,"Stubborn as a rock!"],[296,106.92,2.28,"*hold music*"],[296,109.2,1.68,"I think of rocks kinda like dogs."],[296,110.88,4.24,"You have your purebreds, like quartzite, which is mostly just the mineral quartz."],[296,115.12,3.0,"Then you’ve got your mutts, which are a mix of different minerals."],[296,118.12,3.8,"Like, Dwayne is granite, which is mostly quartz, feldspar, and mica –"],[296,121.92,4.28,"but can also have a lot of other minerals such as hornblende, zircon, and magnetite."],[296,126.2,2.84,"But even the rock-mutts still fall into three basic types,"],[296,129.04,2.32,"classified by how they formed within the rock cycle:"],[296,131.36,2.28,"sedimentary, like this super sandstone,"],[296,133.64,2.36,"igneous, like this beautiful basalt,"],[296,136.0,4.96,"and metamorphic, like this nice, well, gneiss!"],[296,140.96,4.0,"All three of these rock types were involved in the formation of Los Cuernos."],[296,144.96,1.6,"Let’s start at the bottom."],[296,146.56,1.64,"Rock bottom, am I right?"],[296,148.2,2.2,"I think I just have to accept the puns into my life at this point."],[296,150.4,3.36,"This is sedimentary rock—which is created when bits of rocks and minerals,"],[296,153.76,3.56,"called sediment, pile up and solidify into a solid mass."],[296,157.32,3.2,"About 90 million years ago, before these mountains were mountains,"],[296,160.52,5.04,"their oldest rocks formed from tiny grains of mud and sand at the bottom of an ancient sea."],[296,165.56,4.28,"Over time, this sediment was buried and squeezed like a geological panini"],[296,169.84,1.64,"until it formed a solid mass."],[296,171.48,1.92,"Like, imagine if all the stuff at the bottom of"],[296,173.4,3.36,"your locker slowly fused together over the school year."],[296,176.76,2.6,"Paperclips, clarinet reeds, emo sweaters —"],[296,179.36,2.88,"all combining into a single locker rock."],[296,182.24,3.36,"It’s sorta like that, just on a much longer timescale."],[296,185.6,2.24,"The sedimentary rocks at Los Cuernos are clastic—"],[296,187.84,3.32,"they formed from bits of other rocks that broke off, drifted away,"],[296,191.16,3.96,"and got compacted deep underground over time."],[296,195.12,2.84,"If you want to get fancy—and I always do—"],[296,197.96,3.28,"you’ve gotta know there’s more than one way to make a sedimentary rock."],[296,201.24,2.48,"And I’ve got examples of all of them in my pockets."],[296,203.72,2.36,"It’s cool rock time."],[296,206.08,2.16,"Alright Dwayne, it’s your time to shine!"],[296,208.24,1.88,"*guitar riff*"],[296,210.12,3.32,"This is limestone. Get a load of that puppy."],[296,213.44,2.64,"It’s a sedimentary rock that forms biochemically,"],[296,216.08,3.96,"meaning from the shells or bones of once-living organisms."],[296,220.04,0.84,"Cool, right?"],[296,220.88,3.76,"This one—you know it, you love it, you eat it— it’s rock salt."],[296,224.64,1.92,"It’s a sedimentary rock that forms chemically,"],[296,226.56,2.76,"from dissolved minerals that separate from water."],[296,229.32,3.16,"Finally, this lump of coal is a sedimentary rock"],[296,232.48,4.16,"that forms organically from the compressed tissues of once-living things."],[296,236.64,3.64,"Can you believe this rock is made of leaf goo?"],[296,240.28,2.28,"Like, how is our planet a real place!?"],[296,242.56,2.08,"Let’s turn it over to… igneous rock:"],[296,244.64,3.0,"by far the most common rock type on Earth’s surface."],[296,247.64,0.6,"What’s that, Dwayne?"],[296,249.0,1.88,"Sure, I guess classic rock is pretty common too."],[296,251.68,5.2,"What? No blink-182 is not classic rock! I’m not going through this again with you!"],[296,256.88,0.8,"Sorry about that."],[296,257.68,4.6,"Anyway, igneous rock forms when molten rock cools down and crystallizes into a solid."],[296,262.28,3.4,"Geologists give molten rock different names depending on where it’s hanging out:"],[296,266.2,4.12,"“magma” is when it’s underground, and “lava” Is when it’s above ground."],[296,270.32,2.72,"Remember that thick, light stripe on Los Cuernos?"],[296,273.04,1.6,"We can thank igneous rock for that!"],[296,274.64,4.96,"Around 12.5 million years ago, three pulses of magma pumped out of Earth’s depths"],[296,279.6,4.16,"and slid in between the sedimentary rock layers that now make up much of the mountain."],[296,283.76,1.56,"As the magma cooled over time,"],[296,285.32,3.36,"it formed a special type of igneous formation called a laccolith."],[296,288.68,2.2,"It’s giving… Belted Galloway cattle."],[296,290.88,1.76,"Look at those fuzzy little guys!"],[296,292.64,3.24,"The igneous rock at Los Cuernos is intrusive."],[296,295.88,4.84,"That means it formed from magma that rose up and cooled very slowly,"],[296,300.72,3.04,"inside other rocks below Earth’s surface."],[296,303.76,2.4,"It intruded, if you will."],[296,306.16,7.76,"But some igneous rocks, like the basalt and this pumice here, form in a way that’s extrusive."],[296,313.92,3.2,"They’re created when magma exits up to the earth’s surface,"],[296,317.12,3.24,"becoming lava, where it cools and hardens into rock."],[296,320.52,3.28,"Our last stop on the tour of Los Cuernos is metamorphic rock,"],[296,323.8,3.52,"which forms when intense temperature, pressure, hot fluids,"],[296,327.32,5.96,"or a combo of these causes one kind of rock to transform into a different kind of rock."],[296,333.28,3.04,"In other words, a metamorphosis happens."],[296,336.32,4.12,"Like a beautiful rock butterfly. A rockafly. Butterrock? You get it."],[296,340.44,3.04,"At Los Cuernos, when the magma intruded into the mountain,"],[296,343.48,2.68,"it oozed between layers of sedimentary rock."],[296,346.68,5.12,"As it did so, the magma’s heat baked the rock it touched, causing it to change identity."],[296,352.32,2.0,"Those rocks became metamorphic."],[296,355.0,3.88,"Sometimes, metamorphic rock takes on a foliated, or stripey, appearance."],[296,358.88,3.28,"Gneiss, like the one I have here, has visible streaks,"],[296,362.16,6.2,"because intense pressure flattened and rearranged its mineral crystals into even bands."],[296,368.36,2.96,"But at Los Cuernos, the metamorphic rocks are non-foliated."],[296,371.32,2.96,"That’s because the rocks weren’t squished by extreme pressure,"],[296,374.28,2.52,"only warped by intense heat—"],[296,376.8,2.48,"so the mineral crystals never got pushed into stripes."],[296,379.4,5.2,"Los Cuernos is cool, but these mountains show us just one way the rock cycle can play out."],[296,384.6,3.76,"Many different factors can cause rocks to bop back and forth in the rock cycle,"],[296,388.36,1.52,"changing identities over time."],[296,389.88,2.8,"Igneous rock can become sedimentary, then metamorphic,"],[296,392.68,2.28,"then back to igneous or sedimentary again."],[296,394.96,2.44,"Rocks go through more phases than me in middle school."],[296,397.4,1.4,"Just look at Dwayne!"],[296,398.8,2.84,"His journey started underground millions of years ago,"],[296,401.64,3.08,"when magma collected in a bleb under Earth's surface,"],[296,404.72,2.76,"slowly cooling to form our igneous buddy."],[296,407.48,4.36,"Over many years, tectonic shifts pushed the land—and Dwayne—upward."],[296,412.6,5.56,"As the land rose, other slow but mighty forces freed Dwayne from his rocky prison:"],[296,418.16,1.6,"weathering and erosion."],[296,419.76,4.24,"You see, elements like water, wind, and ice act as hammers,"],[296,424.0,3.8,"sculpting rocks by breaking them down in a process called weathering."],[296,427.8,2.0,"This can also happen through chemical processes."],[296,429.8,4.92,"Like, rain is slightly acidic, so raindrops can spark chemical reactions as they land on rocks,"],[296,434.72,1.92,"transforming the minerals within them."],[296,436.64,2.72,"And even living things can cause weathering!"],[296,439.36,2.96,"Trees weather rocks by creating cracks with their roots."],[296,442.32,3.88,"People cause weathering by walking over the same patch of rocks for years."],[296,446.2,5.92,"Even tiny, unassuming lichen weather rocks, by oozing acids from their root-like structures."],[296,452.12,5.52,"We have got to change the Game to Rock, Paper, Scissors, Wind, Water, Lichen."],[296,457.64,3.24,"Weathering creates sediment, the stuff that eventually forms sedimentary rock."],[296,460.88,4.76,"And then comes erosion, the process that washes away sediment and moves it somewhere else."],[296,465.64,3.24,"Those two processes explain how Dwayne broke off from his home cliff"],[296,468.88,2.24,"and tumbled down to where I was hiking."],[296,471.12,3.24,"When I was like “Hey! Cool rock!” And the rest is history."],[296,474.36,2.54,"So yeah, nothing stays the same forever."],[296,476.9,2.85,"The rock cycle is happening all around us, all the time."],[296,479.76,1.04,"When you pick up a rock,"],[296,480.8,1.32,"you’re touching something that’s part of the"],[296,482.12,2.88,"same cycle that formed the Rainbow Mountains in China,"],[296,485.0,2.36,"and the Giant’s Causeway in Northern Ireland,"],[296,487.36,3.88,"and the Wave in Arizona, which honestly looks like something out of a Dr. Seuss book."],[296,491.24,3.08,"No matter where you live, the rock cycle shapes the land around you."],[296,494.32,3.24,"Understanding how rocks form— and transform—"],[296,497.56,3.52,"can give you a whole new perspective on this planet we call home."],[296,501.08,3.8,"Next time, we’re taking a magnifying glass to gems!"],[296,504.88,0.76,"See you then."],[296,505.64,1.52,"Alright, Dwayne. Let’s go!"],[296,507.16,2.88,"Rock, Paper, Scissors, Wind, Water, Lichen—"],[296,510.04,1.56,"oh wait, you don’t have hands."],[296,511.6,2.52,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Geology"],[296,514.12,2.6,"which was filmed at our studio in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[296,516.72,3.32,"and was made with the help of all these super cool people."],[296,520.04,3.32,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[296,523.36,2.12,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[297,0.21,1.17,"The data is in."],[297,1.38,1.13,"More and more Americans"],[297,2.5,1.46,"are turning away from alcohol"],[297,3.96,2.54,"and the ripple effects are big."],[297,6.51,1.17,"Wineries in California"],[297,7.67,1.58,"are going out of business."],[297,9.26,1.79,"Alcohol companies are scrambling"],[297,11.05,1.21,"to make NA products."],[297,12.26,0.58,">> How does it affect"],[297,12.85,0.71,"the businesses, though,"],[297,13.55,1.63,"that rely on selling alcohol?"],[297,15.18,1.5,">> Many are adapting right now."],[297,16.68,1.5,">> We basically have modified"],[297,18.18,1.58,"our entire beverage program"],[297,19.77,0.92,"to accommodate"],[297,20.69,2.0,"more nonalcoholic drinkers."],[297,22.69,2.13,">> Heineken made history in 2023,"],[297,24.82,1.83,"advertising alcohol-free beer"],[297,26.65,1.71,"in the Super Bowl for the first time."],[297,28.36,0.5,"But this"],[297,28.86,1.54,"decline has also led to the boom"],[297,30.41,1.25,"of a whole new industry:"],[297,31.66,1.58,"nonalcoholic wines."],[297,33.24,0.83,"Though the price tag on"],[297,34.08,2.25,"these NA wines can be pretty steep,"],[297,36.33,0.88,"often costing"],[297,37.2,2.09,"even more than wine with alcohol."],[297,39.29,1.25,"But there's a good reason for"],[297,40.54,2.38,"that markup."],[297,42.92,1.17,"So I went to visit my friend"],[297,44.09,2.0,"Dean, the co-owner of an NA bottleshop"],[297,46.09,0.75,"in LA,"],[297,46.84,1.83,"to get the insider's perspective"],[297,48.67,2.17,"on why wine without alcohol"],[297,50.84,1.96,"is likely worth the price."],[297,56.31,1.04,"So funny story:"],[297,57.35,1.25,"Dean actually used to"],[297,58.6,1.67,"work with us here at Vox,"],[297,60.27,1.17,"and it turns out you can go"],[297,61.44,1.33,"from making Vox videos"],[297,62.77,3.0,"to being a small business owner."],[297,66.52,1.46,"Hey, Dean!"],[297,67.98,0.71,"Hey, What's up?"],[297,68.69,1.63,"Beautiful shop."],[297,70.32,2.21,"Thank you. Welcome in."],[297,72.53,1.42,"Wow."],[297,73.95,2.71,"Honestly, I don't know if I would be able"],[297,76.66,3.0,"to tell that this doesn't have alcohol."],[297,79.79,0.46,"Yeah."],[297,80.25,0.83,"I mean,"],[297,81.08,0.96,"I always tell people"],[297,82.04,1.71,"it's like you're not really,"],[297,83.75,3.0,"like, losing anything when you"],[297,86.92,1.46,"drink nonalcoholic"],[297,88.38,0.92,"wine."],[297,89.3,1.25,"I feel like the only thing you're losing"],[297,90.55,1.58,"is kind of"],[297,92.13,1.17,"the cluster headache"],[297,93.3,2.04,"you get later on from alcoholic wine."],[297,95.34,1.75,"This tastes like a pool party,"],[297,97.1,1.0,"you know?"],[297,98.1,0.58,"The artistry"],[297,98.68,0.88,"kind of comes in"],[297,99.56,2.71,"with reintroducing the flavors"],[297,102.27,3.17,"and the aroma and the texture"],[297,105.48,2.13,"that kind of gets removed"],[297,107.61,2.04,"sometimes with the alcohol."],[297,109.65,1.58,"Human beings have been cultivating wine"],[297,111.24,1.21,"for thousands of years."],[297,112.44,1.71,"And in those thousands of years,"],[297,114.16,2.04,"wine has changed a lot."],[297,123.41,0.96,"Since we've opened."],[297,124.37,1.83,"We've seen a huge evolution"],[297,126.21,1.42,"in not only the market,"],[297,127.63,1.42,"but the consumer as well."],[297,129.04,2.71,"Only about 54% of people in the US"],[297,131.76,0.83,"drink alcohol."],[297,132.59,2.75,"And of the people that do drink alcohol,"],[297,135.34,0.75,"a lot of them say"],[297,136.09,1.08,"they want to drink less."],[297,137.18,1.63,"So I kind of embarked on this journey"],[297,138.81,1.54,"into nonalcoholic stuff"],[297,140.35,2.63,"through like medical reasons,"],[297,142.98,1.54,"like I had a heart condition"],[297,144.52,1.88,"that I had to get"],[297,146.4,1.21,"corrective surgery for."],[297,147.6,1.88,"So kind of overnight,"],[297,149.48,1.92,"I had to just stop drinking."],[297,151.4,1.21,"And Dean's not alone."],[297,152.61,0.92,"Alcohol consumption"],[297,153.53,1.79,"in the US has been declining every year"],[297,155.32,1.38,"since 2022,"],[297,156.7,1.96,"and that cutback is due to a few factors."],[297,158.66,1.17,"One is health concerns"],[297,159.83,0.62,"like what Dean"],[297,160.45,2.0,"experienced, and another is related"],[297,162.45,1.79,"to mental health and anxiety."],[297,164.25,1.46,"And a big part of this shift"],[297,165.71,2.04,"is spearheaded by younger consumers"],[297,167.75,1.75,"choosing to drink less."],[297,169.5,0.38,"I think"],[297,169.88,1.46,"younger people are just not taking"],[297,171.34,1.17,"to drinking in the same way."],[297,172.5,1.38,"In terms of market share,"],[297,173.88,1.29,"millennials are actually kind"],[297,175.18,2.17,"of the highest adopters of NA,"],[297,177.34,1.79,"but we're also seeing a lot of like"],[297,179.14,0.96,"older generations"],[297,180.1,1.67,"and baby boomers coming in"],[297,181.76,2.17,"whose doctors are telling them"],[297,183.93,0.38,"it's time"],[297,184.31,1.04,"to stop drinking"],[297,185.35,2.09,"those couple glasses of whiskey at night."],[297,187.44,1.08,"With all these shifts,"],[297,188.52,1.75,"Dean saw an opportunity."],[297,190.27,1.71,"There were a lot of nonalcoholic"],[297,191.98,0.75,"options out there,"],[297,192.73,1.25,"but they were kind of spread out."],[297,193.99,0.83,"You had to go to, like,"],[297,194.82,1.04,"a bunch of different stores"],[297,195.86,1.33,"or buy stuff online."],[297,197.2,1.42,"I was kind of missing"],[297,198.62,2.21,"that independent cool,"],[297,200.82,1.71,"like wine shop experience."],[297,202.53,3.29,"And so we just decided to open the store"],[297,205.87,1.54,"that I wished existed"],[297,207.41,1.88,"when I kind of started on this journey"],[297,209.29,0.63,"into non-alc"],[297,209.92,1.21,"in the first place."],[297,211.13,0.75,"In fact,"],[297,211.88,1.96,"US sales of nonalcoholic beer,"],[297,213.84,1.13,"wine and spirits"],[297,214.96,2.04,"have grown over 20% every year"],[297,217.01,1.46,"since 2022."],[297,218.47,1.25,"Researchers estimate that the"],[297,219.72,1.13,"no-alcohol market"],[297,220.84,3.34,"will grow to over $4 billion by 2028,"],[297,224.18,2.04,"but the price tag is a problem."],[297,226.23,1.13,"Sometimes customers come in"],[297,227.35,2.17,"and they are browsing our wine"],[297,229.52,1.33,"and they're like,"],[297,230.85,0.33,"you know,"],[297,231.19,1.71,"this is the same price"],[297,232.9,1.5,"as some alcoholic wine that I see"],[297,234.4,0.42,"at the store."],[297,234.82,1.0,"Like, why is it the same"],[297,235.82,1.67,"if there's no alcohol in it?"],[297,237.49,1.38,"It's actually much more expensive"],[297,238.86,0.46,"to create"],[297,239.32,1.67,"nonalcoholic wine, especially"],[297,240.99,1.54,"good nonalcoholic wines."],[297,245.58,0.5,"And I know,"],[297,246.08,1.17,"logically, it would seem that"],[297,247.25,1.38,"NA wine should cost less"],[297,248.62,1.58,"because you're removing something"],[297,250.21,1.46,"rather than adding to it,"],[297,251.67,0.5,"but..."],[297,252.17,2.54,"If making wine is difficult,"],[297,254.75,0.75,"making wine"],[297,255.5,1.0,"without alcohol"],[297,256.51,2.25,"is like the next level of challenge."],[297,258.76,1.17,"This is Dr. Mihnea,"],[297,259.93,0.79,"a food scientist"],[297,260.72,0.92,"who is one of the world's"],[297,261.64,2.67,"leading researchers of dealcoholized wine."],[297,264.31,1.63,"She also works with Swedish"],[297,265.93,1.96,"NA wine company, Oddbird."],[297,267.89,2.29,"Wine has been with us for 8000 years."],[297,270.19,1.83,"It's been part of our culture,"],[297,272.02,4.59,"of our celebration, of our scientific"],[297,276.78,1.67,"knowledge and discoveries."],[297,278.44,1.5,"So how exactly does"],[297,279.95,1.88,"the science of removing alcohol work?"],[297,281.82,1.21,"The first patents"],[297,283.03,3.29,"of removing alcohol started in 1890s,"],[297,286.37,0.88,"and you can do it"],[297,287.25,1.71,"through different technologies"],[297,288.95,0.88,"like vacuum distillation"],[297,289.83,1.42,"or reverse osmosis"],[297,291.25,1.63,"or spinning cone."],[297,292.88,1.13,"The earliest technology"],[297,294.0,1.54,"was vacuum distillation,"],[297,295.55,1.0,"which basically means"],[297,296.55,0.96,"increasing (correction: decreasing) pressure"],[297,297.5,0.88,"to lower the boiling"],[297,298.38,2.17,"point of alcohol for evaporation."],[297,300.55,1.67,"So that will allow"],[297,302.22,2.88,"for the wine to start boiling"],[297,305.1,3.0,"at a lower temperature, below 30 degrees,"],[297,308.1,0.83,"and that"],[297,308.93,0.67,"will allow"],[297,309.6,3.0,"for the rest of the compound in the wine"],[297,312.81,1.17,"to not"],[297,313.98,1.71,"evaporate and stay there"],[297,315.69,2.17,"and not being like destroyed."],[297,317.86,2.59,"So vacuum distillation uses maybe"],[297,320.44,0.96,"higher energy,"],[297,321.4,3.0,"but it's easier to preserve the aroma."],[297,324.41,2.71,"And it's a very clean technology."],[297,327.12,1.25,"To my knowledge,"],[297,328.37,0.58,"right now,"],[297,328.95,2.38,"the most used is vacuum distillation."],[297,331.33,1.29,"Especially for Oddbird."],[297,332.62,0.96,"this is what we use"],[297,333.58,1.42,"because we really work"],[297,335.0,1.42,"with low intervention."],[297,336.42,1.79,"Then there's reverse osmosis,"],[297,338.21,1.42,"which involves using very fine"],[297,339.63,1.17,"membranes to filter out"],[297,340.8,1.75,"the hundreds of compounds in wine,"],[297,342.55,1.33,"including ethanol."],[297,343.88,2.54,"And these different compounds"],[297,346.43,2.0,"has different size."],[297,348.43,2.88,"And this membrane separates"],[297,351.31,1.75,"by size of the compound."],[297,353.06,1.25,"So first separates"],[297,354.31,2.79,"water, ethanol, or alcohol."],[297,357.11,1.46,"And then comes the other compounds."],[297,358.57,1.63,"You take the remaining components"],[297,360.19,3.0,"recombine them to recreate the wine."],[297,363.36,0.92,"Finally there's"],[297,364.28,1.38,"the spinning cone column,"],[297,365.66,2.04,"the most modern and advanced technique."],[297,367.7,1.13,"It basically combines"],[297,368.83,1.0,"the methods in vacuum distillation"],[297,369.83,2.09,"and reverse osmosis"],[297,371.91,2.25,"in a staircase of connected cones."],[297,374.17,1.17,"You put a lot of pressure,"],[297,375.33,3.13,"but you also have a lot of like membranes,"],[297,378.46,2.04,"thin membranes that are in cone,"],[297,380.5,1.42,"and they spin."],[297,381.92,1.92,"It first captures"],[297,383.84,1.54,"most of the volatile aroma."],[297,385.38,2.96,"It starts to trap them in the membranes."],[297,388.35,2.5,"And then remove the ethanol"],[297,390.85,1.08,"or the alcohol."],[297,391.93,2.54,"And then it returns all the aromas to wine."],[297,394.48,0.71,"I would say that"],[297,395.19,2.0,"there is no single technology"],[297,397.19,1.46,"that is simply the best,"],[297,398.65,4.09,"but the quality of the final wine depends"],[297,403.07,0.79,"very much"],[297,403.86,1.67,"on the quality of the original wine."],[297,405.53,1.88,"So what you do with your grapes."],[297,407.41,2.5,"So doesn't matter which technology"],[297,409.91,2.0,"you are going to choose to do."],[297,411.91,1.38,"It's about what"],[297,413.29,1.63,"what kind of wine want to make."],[297,414.91,0.79,"So yes,"],[297,415.71,1.21,"alcohol was removed,"],[297,416.92,1.33,"but the product has actually become"],[297,418.25,2.33,"even more complex than it was before."],[297,420.59,0.58,"A quick word"],[297,421.17,1.38,"from the sponsor of this video."],[297,422.55,1.0,"This video is presented"],[297,423.55,1.42,"by Verizon Business."],[297,424.97,0.96,"Verizon Small Business"],[297,425.93,1.13,"Days are happening now."],[297,427.05,1.21,"If you're a small business owner,"],[297,428.26,0.71,"Verizon Business"],[297,428.97,1.46,"experts are ready to meet with you"],[297,430.43,1.08,"one on one."],[297,431.51,1.17,"They'll act as extra support"],[297,432.68,1.04,"to make sure you've got the best"],[297,433.72,1.13,"tech, the best plans,"],[297,434.85,1.38,"and to give you special offers"],[297,436.23,1.46,"to help your business succeed."],[297,437.69,1.79,"Score a meeting with a Verizon Business"],[297,439.48,1.29,"expert during Small Business"],[297,440.77,1.54,"Days from August 17th"],[297,442.32,1.07,"through August 30th."],[297,443.64,2.06,"Go to verizon.com/smallbusiness"],[297,445.69,1.33,"to learn more."],[297,447.03,0.88,"By the way,"],[297,447.9,1.46,"Verizon Small Business has no say"],[297,449.37,2.0,"in the editorial content of this video,"],[297,451.37,1.04,"but their support"],[297,452.41,1.25,"does allow us to do stories"],[297,453.66,2.25,"that feature cool entrepreneurs like Dean."],[297,455.91,3.0,"And now back to the video."],[297,460.63,0.75,"For small business"],[297,461.38,0.83,"owners like Dean,"],[297,462.21,1.63,"their space is a lot more than just"],[297,463.84,0.58,"a shop."],[297,464.42,1.63,"It's a destination to gather,"],[297,466.05,1.29,"to build community,"],[297,467.34,1.29,"which in a lot of ways"],[297,468.63,2.09,"has always been the goal of drinking."],[297,470.72,3.54,"We do free tastings every weekend"],[297,474.26,1.25,"so that people can kind of"],[297,475.52,2.71,"actually experience these things,"],[297,478.23,0.46,"instead of"],[297,478.69,1.67,"just kind of hearing about them."],[297,480.35,1.25,"But also, we do a lot of stuff"],[297,481.61,2.88,"that's not revolving around beverages."],[297,484.48,1.58,"We do like a book club,"],[297,486.07,1.08,"we do a monthly poetry"],[297,487.15,1.38,"workshop, we do game nights,"],[297,488.53,1.38,"we do craft nights."],[297,489.91,1.21,"They're just coming to play."],[297,491.12,0.79,"But then they're like,"],[297,491.91,1.92,"oh, this is a cool, comfortable space."],[297,493.83,1.5,"And you know, this beverage that I tried"],[297,495.33,0.75,"is really tasty."],[297,496.08,0.63,"And these snacks"],[297,496.7,1.04,"I got were really delicious."],[297,497.75,2.67,"And then they would want to come back."],[297,500.42,0.79,"What's happening"],[297,501.21,3.96,"in the wine industry today is it's facing"],[297,505.17,1.63,"one of the biggest transformation"],[297,506.8,1.46,"actually in its history."],[297,508.26,3.0,"In 2024, global wine consumption fell"],[297,511.3,4.21,"to around 214 million hectoliters."],[297,515.51,2.08,"So it was like the lowest level"],[297,517.6,1.67,"in more than 60 years."],[297,519.27,0.75,"You know, innovation"],[297,520.02,3.63,"matters in this situation."],[297,523.69,2.34,"This is when we need to rethink"],[297,526.02,1.58,"and get out of the box."],[297,527.61,1.71,"And I'm not saying that"],[297,529.32,2.67,"the future is only wine without alcohol."],[297,531.99,2.96,"I think they can go hand in hand. And wine"],[297,534.95,1.63,"without alcohol can be also,"],[297,536.58,4.0,"the future lab of the wine industry."],[297,541.67,0.83,"Why not?"],[297,542.5,1.41,"Reinvent ourselves."],[297,547.05,1.21,"Cheers to you."],[297,548.26,2.17,"May everyone have the Vox video"],[297,550.42,1.21,"to small business"],[297,551.63,2.25,"owner pipeline dream come true."],[297,553.89,3.0,"You know we're living the dream."],[297,560.1,1.79,"That is delicious."],[298,7.13,4.17,"How can a bigger tube fit inside a smaller tube?"],[298,11.72,1.83,"This might sound like a riddle,"],[298,13.84,5.05,"but it’s a practical dilemma in our snake-eat-snake world."],[298,19.43,6.3,"This eastern kingsnake, for example, has a mouthful of a Texas rat snake,"],[298,25.9,3.54,"but the rat snake’s longer than it is,"],[298,29.69,4.17,"so how can the kingsnake possibly swallow it whole?"],[298,34.11,5.09,"This is just one of countless predatory dilemmas snakes have solved."],[298,39.53,4.92,"Since slithering onto the scene some 150 million years ago,"],[298,44.62,5.92,"evolving length and limblessness out of their ancestral lizard forms,"],[298,50.59,2.71,"snakes have diversified rapidly."],[298,53.59,3.13,"Their noodly bodies and flexible heads"],[298,56.72,4.13,"granted them access to novel places and prey."],[298,61.18,4.38,"And today, there are nearly 4,000 snake species,"],[298,65.73,4.46,"spanning habitats high and low, wet and dry."],[298,70.73,5.96,"All are carnivorous, but their diets range from fish eggs to alligators."],[298,77.03,6.21,"Jaw-dropping anatomy allows most snakes to swallow their meals whole."],[298,83.5,3.13,"Because their jaw bones aren’t fused like ours"],[298,86.83,2.75,"but connected by an elastic ligament"],[298,89.75,3.88,"and the bones on the sides of their lower jaws flare apart,"],[298,93.67,3.21,"they can dramatically stretch their maws."],[298,97.22,5.71,"Reticulated pythons can even achieve 180-degree gapes."],[298,103.27,5.17,"Sharp, curved teeth also line many snakes’ jaws,"],[298,108.56,2.63,"keeping prey from wriggling out."],[298,111.44,3.63,"And to prevent suffocation when choking down a big meal,"],[298,115.07,3.92,"many snakes shift the position of their airways’ entrance"],[298,118.99,4.04,"and isolate which rib cage regions they inhale with."],[298,123.54,5.96,"Pythons are also equipped with stretchy tissue along their jaws,"],[298,129.62,5.21,"enabling them to spread four times wider than their own skulls."],[298,135.09,5.3,"They’ve been documented using this skill to eat hyenas, alligators,"],[298,140.47,4.25,"and— yes— even humans, whole."],[298,145.27,3.25,"Their skin might sag after all the stretching"],[298,148.64,3.96,"and it could take weeks to metabolize meals this large,"],[298,152.73,5.17,"but it seems their intestines have special cells for helping digest bones,"],[298,158.07,5.76,"and they can sometimes survive for over a year on just one feast."],[298,164.41,6.3,"African egg-eating snakes, meanwhile, consume large, intact bird eggs,"],[298,170.83,6.96,"piercing their shells in their esophagi using inward-facing vertebral spines."],[298,178.26,4.3,"But while many snakes make a show of swallowing things whole,"],[298,182.55,5.25,"crab-eating snakes often pry their prey’s limbs off"],[298,187.81,1.54,"one-by-one"],[298,189.69,4.92,"and blindsnakes decapitate their termite targets,"],[298,194.73,4.38,"probably to prioritize their more digestible bodies."],[298,199.57,6.3,"Some blindsnakes actually use chemical secretions to repel their prey,"],[298,206.24,3.54,"because it allows them to hang out in ant colonies,"],[298,210.04,3.63,"snacking on their inhabitants without being attacked."],[298,213.96,3.88,"Their skills have even been noticed by other species."],[298,218.71,5.05,"Eastern screech owls sometimes place blindsnakes in their nests,"],[298,223.97,4.42,"where they eat the insects that might otherwise harm their young."],[298,228.97,6.3,"In fact, baby owls with a blindsnake nestmate have higher rates"],[298,235.27,2.08,"of growth and survival."],[298,237.61,3.71,"The same can't be said for other species."],[298,241.74,4.88,"Turtle-headed sea snakes scrape fish eggs off coral reefs."],[298,246.87,5.13,"And cat-eyed snakes hunt red-eyed tree frog eggs."],[298,252.46,2.29,"However, even as embryos,"],[298,254.79,4.46,"the developing frogs can sense an attacking snake’s vibrations"],[298,259.3,2.84,"and may prematurely hatch to escape."],[298,262.47,5.0,"It’s a sacrifice— but at least it’s not certain death by serpent."],[298,267.89,4.09,"Garter snakes, meanwhile, go after western newts"],[298,272.1,4.88,"even though their skin is packed with a potent neurotoxin."],[298,277.52,2.71,"Just one bite could kill a person,"],[298,280.57,3.75,"but garter snakes swallow the newts unscathed,"],[298,284.65,5.13,"since modified proteins in their nerve cells prevent the toxin from binding."],[298,290.24,4.17,"Not only that, but since the toxin may remain in their livers"],[298,294.42,1.46,"for weeks afterwards,"],[298,295.88,6.34,"it may even end up lending garter snakes protection against their predators."],[298,303.38,5.0,"However, snakes more often make their own toxins,"],[298,308.6,4.59,"injecting or spitting venom from specialized glands"],[298,313.18,3.59,"out of grooved or syringe-like fangs."],[298,317.23,5.26,"Philippine cobra venom is full of fast-acting neurotoxins"],[298,322.49,3.29,"that cause paralysis and respiratory failure."],[298,326.07,5.8,"West African saw-scaled viper venom is a cocktail of compounds"],[298,331.87,3.84,"that cause profuse bleeding and tissue death."],[298,336.29,5.63,"And while inland taipans are thought to possess the world's most potent venom,"],[298,341.92,4.0,"they tend to reserve it for quickly killing rodents."],[298,346.59,5.8,"But we still haven’t answered how smaller snakes can consume bigger ones."],[298,353.26,5.17,"It turns out that the kingsnake does this with an elegant trick."],[298,358.56,3.42,"Once it runs out of space in its flexible stomach,"],[298,362.19,3.54,"it stretches and compresses its spine,"],[298,365.74,6.51,"shoving the rattlesnake into a kinked, zig-zag shape within its digestive tract."],[298,372.87,5.05,"So a post-meal X-ray may look like this."],[298,378.5,4.96,"Sorry, we know the truth can be hard to swallow."],[298,383.96,2.96,"Unless, of course, you're a snake."],[299,7.13,4.21,"In 1851, the Prussian government feared that Friedrich Fröbel’s"],[299,11.34,4.38,"scandalous new approach to education might teach disobedience"],[299,15.72,2.0,"and lead to peasant rebellion."],[299,18.22,2.34,"The name of Fröbel’s school?"],[299,20.89,1.08,"Kindergarten."],[299,22.43,3.63,"Its controversial curriculum included singing and dancing,"],[299,26.06,2.71,"gardening, painting, and playing."],[299,29.69,1.83,"Prussia banned kindergarten,"],[299,31.82,5.13,"enforcing a strict system of supervised classrooms and government-approved lessons"],[299,36.95,3.59,"that clearly separated learning time from play time."],[299,40.95,1.58,"And over the next century,"],[299,42.66,4.0,"the Prussian model inspired public school systems around the world."],[299,47.29,2.29,"But while it might seem traditional now,"],[299,50.0,3.63,"the belief that play and learning are at odds with each other"],[299,53.63,1.96,"is a relatively new idea."],[299,56.72,2.0,"Before the last few centuries,"],[299,58.72,4.3,"children around the world spent most of their time learning through play:"],[299,63.43,3.84,"observing and mimicking adults, roaming their surroundings,"],[299,67.27,3.13,"and sharing what they learned with friends and family."],[299,70.82,3.34,"Many communities and educators still believe play"],[299,74.15,2.67,"is one of the best teachers we have,"],[299,76.82,2.17,"and they’ve taken a variety of approaches"],[299,78.99,3.55,"to keep this kind of self-directed learning alive."],[299,83.41,1.42,"In his Kindergartens,"],[299,85.08,3.0,"Fröbel provided specially designed toys"],[299,88.21,3.59,"to help kids learn concepts like volume, density,"],[299,91.96,2.08,"and even the principles of geometry."],[299,94.55,4.96,"And today, Montessori schools continue to embrace toy-based learning."],[299,100.35,3.42,"Montessori toys— or materials, as they call them—"],[299,103.85,4.59,"are rigorously designed to isolate and teach specific concepts."],[299,108.6,2.46,"For example to learn math,"],[299,111.06,4.59,"babies and toddlers might explore dimension with fat wooden cylinders,"],[299,115.74,4.55,"then unlock more spatial reasoning with a set of pink, stackable blocks."],[299,120.99,3.88,"Older children tackle a set of brown stairs to learn about height,"],[299,125.04,5.05,"while a series of red rods reveals the secrets of length and balance."],[299,130.75,4.59,"Teachers gently guide this exploration by asking students questions"],[299,135.34,3.17,"and suggesting material specific activities—"],[299,138.51,2.88,"like arranging the rods in order of size."],[299,141.85,3.5,"But Montessori classrooms also have a range of ages"],[299,145.35,2.46,"and low teacher to student ratios"],[299,147.81,3.59,"to encourage students to explore the materials together"],[299,151.4,1.75,"and learn from each other."],[299,154.11,5.09,"Reggio Emilia schools also give students sensory objects to explore together,"],[299,159.2,4.09,"but they're especially focused on letting the students direct their learning."],[299,164.08,5.38,"Every corner of a Reggio classroom is designed to reward curiosity,"],[299,169.62,2.59,"allowing students to follow their interests"],[299,172.21,3.88,"and express themselves with a variety of artistic tools."],[299,176.88,3.67,"Even when Reggio teachers do assign formal projects,"],[299,180.76,3.34,"they start by asking students open-ended questions—"],[299,184.1,2.75,"from how to make a model ship float or sink"],[299,187.06,2.38,"to how they should decorate an art studio."],[299,190.19,4.67,"Then they design the project to explore the student’s assumptions and interests,"],[299,194.86,2.92,"weaving in their curriculum along the way."],[299,199.07,4.55,"Skeptics might argue that self-directed learning lets kids avoid the subjects"],[299,203.62,1.58,"they’re not as interested in,"],[299,205.2,2.46,"leading some students to fall behind."],[299,207.87,3.63,"But most of these schools use an interdisciplinary approach"],[299,211.5,2.46,"to ensure no subject is left behind."],[299,214.34,4.05,"For example, in project-based Laboratory Schools,"],[299,218.59,3.0,"the day might start by solving a number puzzle,"],[299,221.72,4.84,"then using the answers to compose a piece of music or three dimensional art."],[299,227.14,4.59,"By choosing to explore overlapping concepts through multiple projects,"],[299,231.89,3.13,"teachers can show different practical applications"],[299,235.02,3.84,"that make abstract ideas feel concrete and useful."],[299,239.49,4.17,"In all these models, giving students more agency comes with challenges."],[299,243.91,1.88,"Different kids have different needs,"],[299,245.78,3.29,"so educators have to be sensitive and able to adapt."],[299,249.54,2.96,"From rearranging the room to redirecting attention,"],[299,252.5,2.42,"teachers need to respond to each student"],[299,254.92,3.38,"while maintaining a safe, playful environment for all."],[299,259.05,4.13,"This requires buckets of emotional intelligence, prep time,"],[299,263.26,4.25,"and trust from both parents and their school’s administration."],[299,268.85,3.84,"But for many educators and learners, the effort is worth it."],[299,273.14,3.21,"A 2023 analysis of over 30 studies"],[299,276.36,4.25,"found that Montessori education outperformed traditional schools"],[299,280.61,1.96,"in language and math learning,"],[299,282.78,4.0,"as well as in developing creativity and social skills."],[299,287.24,2.5,"And these benefits go beyond early learning."],[299,289.95,3.25,"Many universities are moving from lecture-based learning"],[299,293.25,4.13,"towards long-term projects and roundtable discussions."],[299,297.96,2.84,"MIT’s Lifelong Kindergarten"],[299,300.8,3.25,"tries to extend a playful, creativity-first approach"],[299,304.05,1.83,"to learners of all ages,"],[299,306.18,3.38,"giving students a variety of physical and digital tools"],[299,309.56,3.75,"to explore, experiment, and express themselves."],[299,314.02,5.46,"Some architecture and engineering students even use Fröbel’s special toys"],[299,319.48,3.09,"to hone their design principles and spatial reasoning—"],[299,322.94,3.84,"showing that it’s not only children who learn through play."],[300,7.13,3.46,"Exploring how to use your voice and body on stage"],[300,10.59,4.55,"will help you tackle the most important piece of giving a powerful talk:"],[300,15.51,1.96,"connecting with your audience."],[300,17.85,1.58,"People aren't computers."],[300,19.68,3.71,"They're social creatures with all kinds of unique traits."],[300,23.77,6.3,"Your first job as a speaker is to build a trusting human bond with your audience."],[300,30.28,1.5,"There are many ways to do that."],[300,31.94,2.09,"Here are five suggestions:"],[300,34.28,2.42,"1. Make eye contact."],[300,36.87,3.84,"Make eye contact with members of your audience right from the start of your talk"],[300,40.7,3.17,"and continue making eye contact throughout."],[300,44.83,3.17,"Humans learn a lot about one another through eye contact."],[300,48.25,4.34,"Our brains can detect the tiniest muscle movements in someone else's eyes."],[300,52.97,3.67,"We use that information to figure out all sorts of things—"],[300,56.76,4.34,"like how that person is feeling and whether or not we can trust them."],[300,61.77,3.88,"And while we’re doing that, they’re figuring out the same things about us."],[300,65.89,4.21,"Scientists have even shown that when two people stare at one another,"],[300,70.27,3.71,"their brains start to adopt the same emotional state."],[300,74.28,4.13,"So when we make eye contact, our minds link up."],[300,80.24,2.5,"2. Show vulnerability."],[300,82.91,2.96,"One of the best ways to put an audience at ease"],[300,85.87,2.84,"is to reveal your own vulnerability."],[300,88.92,2.71,"It’s like a tough cowboy walking into a saloon"],[300,91.63,3.59,"and opening his coat to show he's not carrying any weapons."],[300,95.34,1.71,"Everyone relaxes."],[300,97.64,2.29,"Take this example from Ethan Lisi,"],[300,99.97,3.79,"who spoke at a TED-Ed Student Talks event in 2020."],[300,104.72,5.05,"“Another common stereotype that is often associated with autism"],[300,109.86,2.54,"is that autistic people lack empathy."],[300,113.36,2.0,"And again, this is not true."],[300,116.03,2.09,"I actually have lots of empathy."],[300,118.49,2.17,"I’m just not really good at showing it."],[300,121.91,2.13,"Whenever a friend is trying to tell me"],[300,124.04,2.59,"some of the struggles that they’re going through,"],[300,126.75,3.04,"I often don’t know how to express my reply."],[300,130.13,4.84,"And that is why I don't show as much empathy as my non-autistic friends do."],[300,138.18,5.34,"Emotional expression, however much or however little, is difficult for me."],[300,144.56,2.79,"And that is because I am bursting inside"],[300,147.35,3.84,"with every single emotion one feels at all times."],[300,152.11,3.0,"Though, of course, I cannot express it that way."],[300,155.4,4.21,"Otherwise, let’s say happiness, for example,"],[300,159.99,4.38,"would come out as a huge burst of gleeful wheezing, hand flapping,"],[300,164.37,2.38,"and loud vocal ‘woohoo!’"],[300,169.0,1.71,"Whereas you may just smile.”"],[300,172.54,3.04,"Ethan's willingness to open up about something vulnerable"],[300,175.63,2.29,"helps the audience open up as well;"],[300,178.13,4.42,"allowing them to see him, and perhaps the world, from a new perspective."],[300,182.8,1.42,"We feel connected."],[300,184.22,2.54,"We're ready and eager to learn more."],[300,187.35,3.13,"Sharing something vulnerable is a powerful tool"],[300,190.48,2.75,"a speaker can use to connect to an audience."],[300,193.27,3.5,"But, as with anything powerful, it should be handled with care."],[300,197.07,3.63,"It is possible to share too much personal information in a talk,"],[300,200.78,3.0,"in a way that makes the audience feel uncomfortable."],[300,204.03,2.5,"Make sure any vulnerable moments you include"],[300,206.53,3.63,"are there to help support the message you are trying to deliver."],[300,210.33,3.71,"If they aren't doing that, it's probably best to leave them out."],[300,214.34,3.25,"Vulnerability related to your message is powerful."],[300,217.8,3.67,"Sharing something personal just for the sake of it is not."],[300,221.93,4.13,"If you're not sure whether or not a vulnerable moment is appropriate,"],[300,226.26,2.88,"try your talk out on an honest friend."],[300,229.85,3.17,"3. Make them laugh, but not squirm."],[300,233.19,3.38,"Concentrating on a talk can be hard work for an audience."],[300,236.65,3.5,"Humor is a wonderful way to keep everyone with you."],[300,240.49,2.71,"Remember Sophie Scott’s talk from Lesson One?"],[300,243.61,3.75,"If Sophie is right, part of the reason laughter exists"],[300,247.45,2.71,"is that it helps humans form bonds."],[300,250.2,3.71,"When you laugh with someone, you both feel you're on the same side."],[300,254.17,3.25,"It's a fantastic tool for building a connection."],[300,257.55,2.0,"Some of the very best speakers in the world"],[300,259.55,3.29,"spend a lot of time building this connection in their talks."],[300,263.05,1.71,"However, take caution."],[300,264.93,2.13,"Spending a lot of time on funny stories,"],[300,267.18,3.29,"while still delivering a meaningful message, is difficult."],[300,270.6,2.09,"It's not recommended for most talks."],[300,272.85,3.75,"But, if you can find just one short story that makes people smile,"],[300,276.73,3.0,"it may unlock the rest of what you have to say."],[300,281.61,2.59,"4. Park your ego."],[300,284.74,4.59,"Would you want to listen to someone who seems completely full of themselves?"],[300,289.66,4.13,"Nothing hurts a talk more than the sense that the speaker is showing off."],[300,294.29,3.88,"If an audience gets that impression early on in a talk, look out."],[300,298.38,2.29,"They will likely stop listening."],[300,300.92,2.88,"TED Speaker Salman Khan put it beautifully,"],[300,304.18,1.29,"“Be yourself."],[300,305.47,3.92,"The worst talks are the ones where someone is trying to be someone they aren’t."],[300,309.56,2.67,"If you are generally goofy, then be goofy."],[300,312.39,2.63,"If you are emotional, then be emotional."],[300,315.56,4.0,"The one exception to that is if you are arrogant and self-centered,"],[300,319.77,2.71,"then you should definitely pretend to be someone else.”"],[300,322.57,3.84,"Ego can show up in a lot of ways the speaker may not even be aware of,"],[300,326.61,4.17,"such as: name dropping, telling stories just to show off,"],[300,331.12,3.71,"boasting about your achievements, or making the talk all about you."],[300,335.08,4.17,"Remember that the purpose of your talk is to gift an idea,"],[300,339.33,2.08,"not tell everyone how great you are."],[300,341.96,3.09,"Still, these things can be hard to catch yourself on."],[300,345.22,3.42,"Every leader needs someone she can count on for honest feedback."],[300,348.64,3.88,"Someone who's not afraid to tell her the truth about how she's coming across,"],[300,352.72,1.63,"even if that's difficult to hear."],[300,354.48,3.46,"A good way to know whether or not your talk seems boastful"],[300,357.94,2.46,"is to read it for that trusted person."],[300,360.61,1.63,"Give them the chance to say,"],[300,362.23,2.33,"“That was great in parts, but honestly,"],[300,364.57,2.34,"you came across a little full of yourself.”"],[300,368.95,2.29,"5. Tell a story."],[300,371.7,2.63,"We’re born to love stories."],[300,374.5,4.13,"They create interest, empathy, emotion, and a sense of mystery."],[300,378.71,4.0,"They can help an audience understand why a topic matters to you,"],[300,382.88,3.13,"and encourage them to care about that topic, too."],[300,386.51,3.17,"Powerful stories can be great at any stage of a talk."],[300,389.8,3.79,"A great way to open, a great way to make your point in the middle,"],[300,393.6,3.5,"and sometimes (though less often) a great way to end."],[300,397.31,2.63,"The stories that work best to connect with the audience"],[300,399.94,4.21,"are the stories about you personally, or about the people close to you."],[300,404.36,4.88,"Tales of failure, awkwardness, bad luck, danger or disaster,"],[300,409.36,3.5,"when told honestly, can really hook an audience."],[300,412.95,2.0,"They start really listening."],[300,415.12,2.42,"They start to share some of your emotions."],[300,417.66,1.58,"They start to care about you."],[300,419.33,2.21,"They start to like you."],[300,421.83,1.25,"But be careful."],[300,423.17,2.71,"The stories you tell must be authentic to you."],[300,426.05,3.96,"Again, stories that seem boastful will push an audience away."],[300,430.51,3.67,"Ask yourself: is it the real me telling this story?"],[300,434.35,3.34,"A good test is to imagine whether or not you would tell the story"],[300,437.68,1.58,"to a group of friends."],[300,439.44,1.79,"If so, how would you tell it?"],[300,441.39,3.59,"Friends are good at knowing when you’re trying to be someone other than you."],[300,445.23,2.67,"And so are audiences."],[300,448.03,2.5,"Be yourself and you won't go wrong."],[300,451.15,3.75,"The suggestions and tools in this lesson should all be used"],[300,454.91,4.0,"as part of a real desire to connect to your audience."],[300,459.04,2.75,"It's that connection that's important."],[300,462.46,3.63,"You’re a human, and your listeners are humans."],[300,466.55,4.5,"Think of them as friends and reach out with your gift."],[301,0.08,5.52,"In 1844, a group of government surveyors discovered something… weird."],[301,5.6,3.76,"They were trudging through Michigan’s Upper Peninsula, toward the shore of Lake Superior,"],[301,9.36,2.88,"when suddenly, their compasses went haywire."],[301,12.24,2.72,"Now, I’ve seen enough horror movies for this to raise some red flags."],[301,14.96,5.36,"When the compasses get funky, there’s something funkier afoot."],[301,20.32,4.24,"What the surveyors found was a big surprise, though not a supernatural one."],[301,24.56,3.68,"It was a whole lot … of iron."],[301,28.24,4.4,"And that iron would change what we know about the history of the world."],[301,32.64,2.68,"Hi! I’m Sage, and this is Crash Course Geology."],[301,39.68,2.16,"To get started, we have to turn back the clock."],[301,41.84,2.16,"And I don’t mean back to 1844."],[301,44.0,5.6,"I mean way back— before compasses, or Michigan, or human beings existed at all."],[301,49.6,3.04,"We’re going back in geologic time."],[301,52.64,3.64,"Geologic time, also called deep time, is the history of Planet Earth."],[301,56.28,1.08,"You know, no big deal."],[301,57.36,2.88,"It started about 4.6 billion years ago,"],[301,60.24,4.96,"when a cloud of dust and gas smushed in on itself so hard it turned into a planet."],[301,65.2,4.0,"I’ll say that number again: 4.6 billion."],[301,69.2,2.56,"It’s hard to wrap your head around, right?"],[301,71.76,7.6,"Like, if you started counting right now, you’d get to 4.6 billion in about 147 years."],[301,79.36,2.16,"Well, if you could magically live that long."],[301,81.52,3.16,"\"The human brain isn’t built to understand numbers that big."],[301,85.11,4.41,"[Dwayne] Sage, you are far too young to explain geologic time."],[301,89.52,0.96,"Allow me."],[301,90.48,3.2,"[Sage] Whoa! Okay! You’re using your Big Voice!"],[301,93.68,2.08,"So, um... alright!"],[301,95.76,0.92,"Take it away, Dwayne."],[301,96.68,2.6,"[Dwayne] Let’s dig a little deeper…"],[301,99.28,5.76,"The geologic timescale is broken down into several, different-sized portions."],[301,105.04,6.64,"The largest are eons, which last from hundreds of millions to billions of years."],[301,111.68,2.96,"Our story starts in the Hadean Eon,"],[301,114.64,4.72,"when the earth was born and covered in an ocean of molten rock."],[301,119.36,3.36,"Today, we’re living in the Phanerozoic Eon."],[301,122.72,5.84,"Eons are broken down into eras, each hundreds of millions of years long."],[301,128.56,2.64,"And really hard to get tickets to."],[301,131.2,7.44,"For example, inside the Phanerozoic Eon, the Mesozoic Era was the famous “age of reptiles”"],[301,138.64,4.4,"when dinosaurs, crocodiles, and pterosaurs roamed the Earth."],[301,143.04,5.92,"Eras are further broken down into periods, which last tens of millions of years,"],[301,148.96,2.56,"and mark major geologic events,"],[301,151.52,4.96,"like the extinction of the dinosaurs at the end of the Cretaceous Period."],[301,156.48,6.88,"In fact, all of Earth’s five major extinctions have marked the end of a geologic period."],[301,163.36,4.24,"Then there are epochs, lasting several million years."],[301,167.6,5.2,"Epochs capture shifts in characteristics like climate and biodiversity."],[301,172.8,3.52,"For instance, we’re now in the Holocene epoch,"],[301,176.32,4.64,"characterized by human civilization and climate change."],[301,180.96,1.44,"Back to you, Sage."],[301,182.4,3.44,"[Sage] Wow, I think you have a career in voiceovers, Dwayne."],[301,185.84,1.13,"[Dwayne] Thanks pal."],[301,186.97,3.59,"[Sage] For the vast majority of geologic history, humans weren’t here—"],[301,190.56,4.96,"the earliest members of our species only arrived on the scene some 300,000 years ago."],[301,195.52,1.92,"So how do we know all of this?"],[301,197.44,4.96,"In our last episode, we talked about how layers of rock can help be a window into Earth’s past."],[301,202.4,4.48,"Geologists study those rocks to piece together the major events that shaped our planet."],[301,206.88,1.36,"To get an idea of how that works,"],[301,208.24,2.72,"let’s take another look at what those guys dug up in the U.P."],[301,210.96,2.0,"That’s Michigan-speak for “Upper Peninsula.”"],[301,212.96,1.28,"Shout-out yoopers."],[301,214.24,2.56,"What those government surveyors had stumbled on was this:"],[301,216.8,2.16,"a banded iron formation, or BIF,"],[301,218.96,2.8,"which was what threw off the magnets inside their compasses."],[301,221.76,4.16,"But it took decades to come up with a solid guess for where it came from."],[301,225.92,3.68,"The standard hypothesis these days is that, billions of years ago,"],[301,229.6,3.36,"water-soluble iron floated throughout early oceans,"],[301,232.96,3.2,"possibly because of volcanic activity on the seafloor."],[301,236.16,3.36,"At that point, there likely wasn’t much oxygen around."],[301,239.52,2.4,"But then, somewhere around two and a half billion years ago,"],[301,241.92,2.72,"we got a lot of oxygen, all at once."],[301,244.64,3.28,"Possibly from bacteria in those early oceans photosynthesizing:"],[301,247.92,2.72,"eating up carbon dioxide and spitting out oxygen."],[301,250.64,2.32,"That event is known as “The Great Oxidation.”"],[301,252.96,4.88,"And oxygen loves to bond with iron— it’s why exposed iron gets rusty."],[301,257.84,1.68,"When dissolved iron is oxidized,"],[301,259.52,3.44,"it changes color to a shiny black or reddish solid."],[301,262.96,3.68,"And it precipitates out of, or separates from, water."],[301,266.64,3.12,"So, all that new oxygen would have bonded with that dissolved iron"],[301,269.76,2.8,"and sunk it down onto the ocean floor."],[301,272.56,2.16,"As for their distinctive zebra stripes,"],[301,274.72,5.2,"those came from super iron-rich layers alternating with more silica-rich layers."],[301,279.92,4.0,"But, to this day, we don’t fully know why."],[301,283.92,3.68,"Maybe those layers formed because of bacterial growth cycles, or some other reason."],[301,287.6,4.96,"But BIFs stopped forming on a large scale around 1.85 billion years ago."],[301,292.56,2.48,"That means we don’t have modern versions of BIFs"],[301,295.04,2.8,"to compare them to and help us answer those questions."],[301,297.84,2.88,"What we do know is that during the Great Oxidation,"],[301,300.72,2.96,"so much oxygen eventually built up in the oceans,"],[301,303.68,4.0,"that it rose out of the water and into the atmosphere."],[301,307.68,3.44,"This set the stage for increasingly complex life on earth—"],[301,311.12,5.2,"from oxygen-breathing bacteria to multicellular plants, to human beings."],[301,316.32,3.2,"Banded iron formations have shown up all across the globe,"],[301,319.52,2.0,"but there are still many mysteries about them."],[301,321.52,4.96,"Like, not all BIFs seem to have formed during the same period in exactly the same way,"],[301,326.48,2.72,"including some that formed before the Great Oxidation."],[301,329.2,3.04,"Still, they’ve made a huge impact on the planet."],[301,332.24,4.8,"Most of the iron we use today, especially to make steel, comes from BIFs."],[301,337.04,1.92,"And they’re an exciting example of how"],[301,338.96,3.84,"rock records can reveal our billions-of-years-long geologic history."],[301,342.8,3.52,"But we haven’t always known the earth was billions of years old."],[301,346.32,5.2,"In fact, we didn’t have an accurate age for our planet until the 1950s."],[301,351.52,1.84,"And it was a major shake-up."],[301,353.36,1.52,"Before that, most people believed—"],[301,354.88,2.96,"for a variety of reasons, both scientific and religious—"],[301,357.84,3.28,"that the planet was only a few thousand years old and was formed quickly,"],[301,361.12,2.08,"as the result of a single event."],[301,363.2,3.2,"This belief is part of a school of thought called catastrophism,"],[301,366.4,4.72,"which promotes the idea that sudden, violent events— like floods, volcanic eruptions,"],[301,371.12,3.28,"or asteroid impacts— are what ultimately shaped our planet."],[301,374.4,4.32,"But in the 1700s, a Scottish geologist named James Hutton developed a new theory."],[301,378.72,2.64,"He said: “the past history of our globe must be"],[301,381.36,2.56,"explained by what can be seen to be happening now.”"],[301,383.92,2.64,"This theory became known as uniformitarianism:"],[301,386.56,2.64,"the idea that the processes happening on Earth today"],[301,389.2,3.2,"are the same processes that happened throughout its history."],[301,392.4,3.28,"In other words, Earth's many features were crafted by slow,"],[301,395.68,4.16,"continuous processes, not one-time, dramatic events."],[301,399.84,4.56,"So, for many years, the pendulum swung from catastrophism to uniformitarianism—"],[301,404.4,3.12,"especially thanks to an 1830 book called “Principles of Geology,”"],[301,407.52,3.12,"written by Hutton stan Charles Lyell."],[301,410.64,3.84,"And uniformitarianism did help geologists learn about our planet."],[301,414.48,1.84,"Like the principle of faunal succession—"],[301,416.32,3.52,"the idea that fossils of various organisms follow one after another"],[301,419.84,2.72,"in a specific order throughout geologic time."],[301,422.56,2.72,"That information came from the observations of fossils,"],[301,425.28,2.24,"especially by English surveyor William Smith."],[301,427.52,5.12,"Because the uniformitarian view said geological processes had always worked the same,"],[301,432.64,3.92,"Smith could assume that the deepest-down layers of fossils were probably the oldest,"],[301,436.56,2.32,"and the ones toward the top were pretty new."],[301,438.88,2.16,"Which meant that, if a rock contained a fossil,"],[301,441.04,4.16,"you could match it up with other fossils like it, and figure out when it formed."],[301,445.2,4.8,"But as time wore on, it became clear that catastrophism wasn’t entirely bunk science."],[301,450.0,3.52,"Huge, one-time events do shape Earth’s geology,"],[301,453.52,3.92,"like the floods that formed the Channeled Scablands in Washington State."],[301,457.44,5.87,"Earth’s landscape was shaped by both continuous processes and major catastrophes."],[301,463.31,3.41,"Now, proving those theories was the tricky part."],[301,466.72,3.68,"Faunal succession gave us some early clues to the ages of rocks,"],[301,470.4,2.24,"but it could only tell us so much."],[301,472.64,1.6,"It’s a type of relative dating,"],[301,474.24,3.52,"which involves looking at the relationships between rocks or fossils."],[301,477.76,2.64,"Like, it can tell you if rocks are older or younger"],[301,480.4,3.36,"than other rocks based on which layer appears on top."],[301,483.76,2.8,"But it can’t tell you how old they actually are."],[301,486.56,2.56,"To do that, geologists now use absolute dating,"],[301,489.12,4.32,"a way of figuring out the age of rocks themselves based on how they change over time."],[301,493.44,4.16,"It’s a method of geochronology, or the study of when past geologic events occurred."],[301,497.6,3.52,"The main type of absolute dating geochronologists use is called radiometric,"],[301,501.12,1.6,"or isotopic, dating."],[301,502.72,2.08,"It’s about to get real science-y in here."],[301,504.8,4.88,"Here’s how it works. Isotopes are elements with different numbers of neutrons in their nuclei."],[301,509.68,4.8,"And some of them are radioactive, which means their nuclei are unstable—"],[301,514.48,1.92,"over time, they decay."],[301,516.4,3.76,"The “parent” isotope gets replaced by its “daughter products,”"],[301,520.16,2.08,"the decayed form of the isotope."],[301,522.24,3.12,"And thanks to lots of physics research,"],[301,525.36,4.16,"we know that replacement happens at a constant rate for each element,"],[301,529.52,2.08,"until the parent is totally gone."],[301,531.6,6.32,"So when a rock contains radioactive isotopes, we can figure out how long it’s been decaying."],[301,537.92,4.0,"If there’s tons of the parent isotope in there, it must be pretty new."],[301,541.92,5.6,"But if it’s mostly the daughter isotope, it must have been decaying for a while."],[301,547.52,6.16,"And since we know exactly how fast stuff decays, we can figure out exactly how long."],[301,553.68,4.56,"Absolute dating is what lets us be absolutely sure about the age of rocks."],[301,558.24,2.96,"But not all rocks have the elements required for absolute dating."],[301,561.2,4.32,"So both relative and absolute dating can be used to figure out how old a rock is,"],[301,565.52,2.56,"or when a geologic event happened."],[301,568.08,4.0,"Radiometric dating was what allowed American geochemist Clair Patterson and his colleagues"],[301,572.08,5.92,"to accurately estimate the earth’s age for the first time in 1955."],[301,578.0,5.92,"They compared the radioactive breakdown of uranium on Earth's surface and in meteorites."],[301,583.92,1.04,"Super chill stuff."],[301,584.96,4.64,"Knowing that Earth is around 4.6 billion years old completely shifted the way we think of time"],[301,589.6,1.36,"and our place within it."],[301,590.96,3.2,"‘Cause like I said at the top, it’s almost impossible"],[301,594.16,2.48,"for humans to conceive of such a number."],[301,596.64,4.8,"To the earth, we’re just… a blip."],[301,606.0,1.12,"What was I saying?"],[301,607.12,4.24,"But some scientists say that actually thinking about deep time and your relationship to it"],[301,611.36,3.28,"can be kind of… good? Not in a “99 percent of"],[301,614.64,1.52,"doctors say considering your own mortality"],[301,616.16,2.16,"decreases your chance of heart attack” kind of way."],[301,618.32,4.08,"More like: thinking about deep time can provide us some much needed perspective."],[301,622.4,1.84,"Like, think of your neighborhood."],[301,624.24,2.56,"Imagining what it might have looked like millions of years ago—"],[301,626.8,2.64,"or what it’ll look like millions of years from now—"],[301,629.44,4.37,"can provide you a sense of awe and wonder that you can carry into the rest of your life."],[301,633.84,3.28,"From banded iron formations to radioactive meteorites,"],[301,637.12,5.36,"rocks have a lot to teach us about the past 4.6 billion years on Earth."],[301,642.48,3.2,"Looking back into deep time can be kind of spooky,"],[301,645.68,5.28,"but it’s one of the coolest ways we can understand the planet’s past, present,"],[301,650.96,2.24,"and future— and our place in it."],[301,653.2,4.4,"Next time, we’ll check out some of the best records of life on Earth: fossils."],[301,657.6,0.8,"See you then."],[301,658.4,2.24,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Geology,"],[301,660.64,2.36,"which was filmed at our studio in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[301,663.0,2.36,"and was made with the help of all these nice people."],[301,665.36,3.12,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[301,668.48,2.72,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[302,0.0,2.16,"It’s about to get freaky."],[302,2.16,1.92,"Gargantuan tubeworms."],[302,4.08,1.84,"Foot-long clams."],[302,5.92,3.76,"Shrimp with eyes that can detect radiation."],[302,9.68,2.4,"And I’m not talking about the special at Red Lobster."],[302,12.08,3.12,"These are just a few of the stranger-than-fiction creatures"],[302,15.2,3.52,"you can find in the deep, dark depths of the ocean."],[302,18.72,4.72,"The ocean is teeming with life, even its most hostile parts."],[302,23.44,6.0,"It fosters the most spectacular and diverse lifeforms imaginable— including us."],[302,29.44,3.6,"Just ‘cause we live on land doesn’t mean we’re not homies with our salty neighbors, okay?"],[302,33.04,2.24,"So, how does she do it?"],[302,35.28,2.64,"What makes the ocean so… life-y?"],[302,37.92,2.5,"Hi, I’m Sage, and this is Crash Course Geology."],[302,44.83,4.37,"THE OCEAN! More than 70 percent of our planet is covered in this enormous, salty,"],[302,49.2,1.36,"plankton-filled wonderland."],[302,50.56,2.48,"Seriously, there’s a lot of plankton in there."],[302,53.04,3.2,"We’ve been fascinated by this massive body of water for centuries."],[302,56.24,2.72,"One of the earliest studies of the ocean started in 1872,"],[302,58.96,4.32,"when the British HMS Challenger hit the seas for a 1,250-day journey"],[302,63.28,3.52,"that covered over 68,000 nautical miles."],[302,66.8,3.92,"You could consider it an early feat of oceanography, the study of the physics,"],[302,70.72,2.8,"chemistry, biology, and geology of the oceans."],[302,73.52,3.84,"Later, tools like sonar, submarines, satellites, and submersibles"],[302,77.36,3.04,"let us gather data from above and send stuff below—"],[302,80.4,4.16,"everything from cameras to robots to “Titanic” director James Cameron."],[302,84.56,1.2,"Voluntarily, of course."],[302,85.76,2.16,"He was really vibing down there!"],[302,87.92,2.56,"So today, we know quite a bit about the ocean."],[302,90.48,2.56,"And it isn’t just one uniform mass of saltwater."],[302,93.04,4.72,"Some parts are way saltier than others, or way hotter or way colder."],[302,97.76,4.72,"Some parts are home to thousands of species, while others support almost none at all."],[302,102.48,3.84,"And when it comes to the seafloor, it’s not just flat and sandy."],[302,106.32,2.32,"There’s a lot going on with the ocean’s bathymetry,"],[302,108.64,2.37,"or the topography of the ocean floor."],[302,111.01,1.95,"Now, I can’t take you on a tour of it—"],[302,112.96,3.2,"the Crash Course budget is a little too tight for a full-on submersible."],[302,116.16,3.2,"But I bet I know someone who would make the trip… Dwayne!"],[302,119.36,2.8,"You wanna go on a lil adventure?"],[302,122.16,3.76,"Dwayne starts his journey on the coast, where the land meets the sea."],[302,125.92,4.16,"As he strikes out onto the seafloor, he’ll roll along the continental shelf:"],[302,130.08,3.12,"a part of the ocean floor that’s still continental crust."],[302,133.2,4.8,"It's stretched out and topped with a thick layer of sediment accumulated over thousands of years."],[302,138.0,3.04,"Continental crust is thicker and more buoyant than oceanic crust,"],[302,141.04,2.16,"so it “floats” higher on top of the mantle."],[302,143.2,3.84,"Combined with the thick layer of sediments, the ocean is shallower on the shelf,"],[302,147.04,2.64,"usually less than two hundred meters deep."],[302,149.68,4.72,"Here, you’ll find algae, seaweed, and tons of different kinds of fish and sea creatures."],[302,154.4,1.68,"Hey, look! Dwayne found Nemo!"],[302,156.08,2.48,"From here, things go downhill fast."],[302,158.56,1.44,"That’s the continental slope:"],[302,160.0,4.16,"the transition zone between the continental shelf and the deeper basin of the ocean."],[302,164.16,3.04,"It’s made of thinner, denser oceanic crust."],[302,167.2,3.04,"A few kilometers under the surface, it starts to smooth out again,"],[302,170.24,2.24,"in an area called the continental rise:"],[302,172.48,3.04,"a zone of sediments accumulated at the base of the slope."],[302,175.52,4.32,"And when Dwayne hits the end, he’ll find himself in… the abyss."],[302,179.84,1.76,"Or at least the abyssal plain."],[302,181.6,0.88,"No, don’t worry, pal!"],[302,182.48,2.48,"You have location sharing on, we won’t lose you out there."],[302,184.96,3.68,"The abyssal plain is the flat-ish bottom of the ocean basin."],[302,188.64,4.08,"It’s mostly made of a volcanic rock called basalt and covered in a layer of sediment."],[302,192.72,3.52,"It tends to hover around 4-and-a-half kilometers deep."],[302,196.24,2.48,"Here, it’s hard for much of anything to survive."],[302,198.72,4.8,"It’s cold, it’s dark, and the pressure is up to 600"],[302,203.52,1.6,"times what it is on the surface."],[302,205.12,1.44,"It’s ok, Dwayne can handle it."],[302,206.56,1.44,"Regardless of where you dive in,"],[302,208.0,3.6,"there are plenty of cool landforms to explore under the sea."],[302,211.6,1.44,"Like submarine canyons."],[302,213.04,2.48,"When the sea level’s low, they’re carved by"],[302,215.52,2.88,"rivers cutting through the continental shelf and slope."],[302,218.4,3.84,"Then, they’re deepened by submarine avalanches called turbidity currents."],[302,222.24,2.8,"And there are underwater volcanoes called seamounts,"],[302,225.04,4.32,"where violent, submarine, volcanic eruptions built up mountains on the seafloor."],[302,229.36,4.24,"You’ll find lots of unique sealife here, from bubblegum coral and squat lobsters,"],[302,233.6,3.2,"to loggerhead sea turtles and rare sei whales."],[302,236.8,1.6,"Alright, buddy, time to come to the surface."],[302,238.4,1.2,"Come out of the water."],[302,239.6,2.64,"Now, Dwayne just took a trip along a passive margin,"],[302,242.24,4.48,"which is where continental crust meets oceanic crust on the same tectonic plate."],[302,246.72,0.56,"Pretty chill."],[302,247.28,2.8,"But if he had toured, say, South America's western coast,"],[302,250.08,3.76,"he would have found a much narrower continental shelf at an active margin."],[302,253.84,4.24,"That’s the edge of a tectonic plate, where lots of earthquakes rattle the seafloor."],[302,258.08,2.28,"Yeah, I’m not gonna put you through that bud."],[302,260.36,1.4,"And there are other things I’d worry about"],[302,261.76,2.48,"if Dwayne had kept wandering the bottom of the ocean."],[302,264.24,1.6,"Like hydrothermal vents."],[302,265.84,2.96,"They’re underwater chimneys as high as 55 meters"],[302,268.8,5.44,"that send up blackened plumes of particles and water hot enough to melt solid metal."],[302,274.24,3.04,"Can you imagine if that’s what happened when they picked a new pope?"],[302,277.28,2.88,"Hydrothermal vents are created by plate tectonics:"],[302,280.16,3.36,"the theory that earth’s outermost layer is broken up into chunks,"],[302,283.52,2.88,"called tectonic plates, that move across the mantle."],[302,286.4,2.96,"We got way more into plate tectonics in an earlier episode."],[302,289.36,4.88,"But the important thing to know today is that it plays a big role in marine geology."],[302,294.24,4.4,"One place hydrothermal vents form is where oceanic plates pull apart."],[302,298.64,2.64,"Here’s how it happens: at these divergent boundaries,"],[302,301.28,5.44,"lava erupts in the gaps and then cools and hardens into new oceanic crust, or seafloor."],[302,306.72,3.36,"All that tectonic movement can crack deep fissures in the crust,"],[302,310.08,1.68,"where water and magma mix."],[302,311.76,3.12,"The liquid heats up, dissolving minerals from the surrounding rocks."],[302,314.88,2.08,"And when that super-heated fluid comes back up"],[302,316.96,2.56,"and meets the frigid temperatures at the seafloor,"],[302,319.52,3.04,"the minerals form stubby spires and towering chimneys—"],[302,322.56,2.16,"and voilà, a hydrothermal vent!"],[302,324.72,4.08,"You’ll find a lot of them along underwater mountain ranges called mid-ocean ridges."],[302,328.8,3.52,"Hydrothermal vents pump heat from deep in the earth into the oceans,"],[302,332.32,4.32,"crafting hot, mineral-rich zones for super weird creatures to call home."],[302,336.64,3.2,"That, and the fact that these vents are often so deep in the ocean"],[302,339.84,2.96,"that there’s no light for photosynthesis."],[302,342.8,2.48,"Instead, bacteria are chemosynthetic,"],[302,345.28,2.88,"meaning they feed on the chemicals from the vents,"],[302,348.16,2.64,"and they serve as the base of the food chain there."],[302,350.8,3.92,"Two-meter-long tubeworms, orange-shelled mussels,"],[302,354.72,4.88,"and fuzzy crabs all happily thrive around hydrothermal vents."],[302,359.6,4.32,"Marine geologists study these ecosystems to try to figure out how life began on Earth,"],[302,363.92,2.48,"and how it might exist on other planets."],[302,366.4,4.72,"Like, some research has found primitive microorganisms living by hydrothermal vents—"],[302,371.12,3.92,"a sign that life could’ve started in those environments."],[302,375.04,4.72,"And if chemical-eating organisms can exist in hostile Earth environments,"],[302,379.76,4.0,"maybe they can survive elsewhere in the solar system, too."],[302,383.76,3.04,"Hydrothermal vents can also form at convergent boundaries,"],[302,386.8,3.68,"where a continental plate and an oceanic plate collide,"],[302,390.48,2.4,"plunging one into the mantle beneath the other."],[302,392.88,4.56,"That process creates a trench, which forms as one plate dives beneath another,"],[302,397.44,3.36,"bending the seafloor to create a deep v-shaped depression."],[302,400.8,4.24,"And if that happens with two oceanic plates, the trench is massive."],[302,405.04,3.76,"That's how we got the Challenger Deep: the deepest point in the world,"],[302,408.8,2.24,"located in the Pacific’s Mariana Trench."],[302,411.04,4.08,"It reaches nearly 11,000 meters below sea level—"],[302,415.12,5.68,"so deep that Mount Everest could fit inside with room to spare."],[302,420.8,3.2,"Well, at least that’s how deep we think it is."],[302,424.0,0.96,"‘Cause it turns out,"],[302,424.96,2.08,"measuring it is surprisingly difficult."],[302,427.04,4.4,"Since the HMS Challenger, we’ve been trying to measure it, starting with a weighted rope,"],[302,431.44,2.72,"then graduating to sonar technology in the fifties."],[302,434.16,3.04,"The most accurate estimate as of this filming in 2026"],[302,437.2,6.72,"came from a series of submersible dives in 2020, which clocked it at 10,935 meters deep."],[302,443.92,1.92,"But it’s hard to know for sure."],[302,445.84,3.12,"We can’t exactly drop a yardstick down there to get the exact answer."],[302,448.96,3.92,"What we do know is that, just like at hydrothermal vents,"],[302,452.88,2.48,"life exists in deep sea trenches."],[302,455.36,3.44,"And that life is weird with a capital W."],[302,458.8,5.6,"We’re talking transparent sea cucumbers, saucer-sized single-celled organisms,"],[302,464.4,4.05,"and shrimp-like critters that eat sunken wood."],[302,468.48,4.16,"But the ocean’s geology doesn't just create hotspots of life on the seafloor."],[302,472.64,5.04,"It affects life everywhere on Earth, especially through currents."],[302,477.68,4.96,"Currents are continuous, directed oceanic movements that carry water across the globe."],[302,482.64,3.92,"They’re driven by wind, water density, tides, and Earth’s rotation,"],[302,486.56,4.08,"and they’re affected by bathymetry, which can change their speed and direction."],[302,490.64,1.84,"For example, about three million years ago,"],[302,492.48,4.0,"tectonic shifts formed the isthmus of Panama, which connected North and South America."],[302,496.48,1.84,"This stretch of land deflected the warm,"],[302,498.32,3.12,"salty ocean current that once flowed through the gap."],[302,501.44,3.52,"It redirected that water northward, which warmed up Europe."],[302,504.96,5.36,"And currents can have a cooling effect, too, like when Antarctica broke off from South America."],[302,510.32,2.64,"This allowed frigid waters to circle the continent,"],[302,512.96,2.08,"creating a barricade for warmer waters,"],[302,515.04,3.44,"and chilling Antarctica into the frozen landscape we know today."],[302,518.48,4.4,"One of the most important contributions of ocean currents is the distribution of nutrients."],[302,522.88,4.16,"Upwelling currents bring nutrient-rich waters from deep in the ocean upward"],[302,527.04,3.44,"to feed microorganisms at the base of marine food webs."],[302,530.48,4.0,"Currents can even move animals themselves, like a watery superhighway,"],[302,534.48,4.64,"distributing populations of everything from plankton to fish eggs to sea turtle hatchlings,"],[302,539.12,2.4,"influencing how these creatures evolve."],[302,541.52,5.04,"Oceans pump tons of water around the world in a so-called “global conveyor belt” of currents,"],[302,546.56,3.44,"mostly driven by changes in temperature and saltiness."],[302,550.0,4.56,"Basically, water that's saltier and colder is more dense and sinks,"],[302,554.56,2.32,"traveling along the deep ocean bottom."],[302,556.88,5.12,"While water that's less salty and warmer is less dense and upwells."],[302,562.0,5.2,"But as we unleash greenhouse gases into the atmosphere, Arctic temperatures are warming."],[302,567.2,4.48,"Which means ocean waters are cooling slower and glaciers are melting faster,"],[302,571.68,2.4,"flushing fresh water into the mix."],[302,574.08,3.12,"All of that slows down the conveyor belt."],[302,577.2,3.2,"The ocean also acts as a huge carbon sink,"],[302,580.4,3.44,"storing carbon dioxide so that it doesn’t warm the atmosphere."],[302,583.84,2.96,"That’s a massive deal for a lot of reasons."],[302,586.8,4.64,"Like, the temperature of the earth determines what kind of creatures can live here,"],[302,591.44,1.04,"including us."],[302,592.48,2.24,"But as surface water warms and currents slow,"],[302,594.72,4.8,"the surface waters saturate with carbon dioxide and can’t absorb any more."],[302,599.52,2.8,"Many scientists recommend cutting carbon emissions"],[302,602.32,3.92,"to prevent large-scale disruption of currents and ocean environments."],[302,606.24,3.33,"You can learn more about that in Crash Course Climate and Energy."],[302,609.57,3.15,"Earth’s oceans are still full of mysteries."],[302,612.72,3.04,"As of June 2024, we’ve mapped only about a"],[302,615.76,2.72,"quarter of the world’s seafloor in high resolution."],[302,618.48,2.08,"That means there’s a whole three-quarters"],[302,620.56,4.32,"that could be hiding even more creepy little earth-aliens."],[302,624.88,2.32,"Dwayne wants to see more of those fuzzy crabs."],[302,627.2,3.12,"The ocean is much more than a big pool of saltwater."],[302,630.32,3.36,"And the seafloor isn’t just an underwater, sandy beach, either."],[302,633.68,3.6,"The bottom of the ocean is a maze of slopes, trenches, and formations"],[302,637.28,2.32,"that show us plate tectonics in action."],[302,639.6,3.28,"It creates habitats for creepy creatures and affects the currents"],[302,642.88,2.88,"that are essential to Earth’s climate and life."],[302,645.76,2.4,"Our planet wouldn’t be the same without it."],[302,648.16,3.36,"In our next episode, we’ll tackle volcanoes."],[302,651.52,0.72,"See you then."],[302,652.24,2.32,"Thanks for watching this episode of Crash Course Geology"],[302,654.56,2.72,"which was filmed at our studio in Indianapolis, Indiana,"],[302,657.28,2.4,"and was made with the help of all these nice people."],[302,659.68,3.68,"If you want to help keep Crash Course free for everyone, forever,"],[302,663.36,2.23,"you can join our community on Patreon."],[303,1.04,3.09,"Across the globe, a rising number of people…"],[303,4.13,1.25,"- Thank you."],[303,5.38,3.84,"..are embracing the wild side of their garden,"],[303,9.22,2.0,"by growing native plants"],[303,11.22,2.46,"and leaving lawns uncut,"],[303,13.68,2.59,"which is boosting biodiversity."],[303,17.27,2.96,"Over the next 10 years in the US alone,"],[303,20.23,2.75,"this could increase carbon dioxide storage"],[303,22.98,3.21,"by an extra 1.4 gigatonnes,"],[303,26.19,2.29,"helping climate and health."],[303,37.12,2.13,"18-year-old Esther Bonney"],[303,39.25,2.54,"is co-founder of Nurture Natives."],[303,42.46,4.42,"They encourage people to breathe new life into their backyards."],[303,46.88,2.25,"[Esther] In 2019, I lost my older brother,"],[303,49.13,2.63,"and throughout the next couple of months,"],[303,51.76,3.17,"I just spent hours and hours walking along my local creek,"],[303,54.93,2.0,"just noticing the native plants,"],[303,56.93,3.17,"the leaves blooming and the insects buzzing all around."],[303,61.39,3.21,"They brought so much joy, so much healing."],[303,64.61,3.63,"I found a true passion for native plants and native pollinators,"],[303,68.23,2.84,"so I came up with Nurture Natives."],[303,71.57,4.3,"It started as a passion project and now it's grown into a youth-led nonprofit."],[303,76.58,2.29,"[Narrator] Native plants are important"],[303,78.87,4.8,"because they support local wildlife and strengthen ecosystems."],[303,83.67,3.29,"So projects like Esther's are crucial"],[303,86.96,3.21,"to help protect regional species from decline."],[303,92.38,1.33,"Across the pond,"],[303,93.72,3.0,"another grassroots movement is flourishing."],[303,96.93,2.29,"In just one century,"],[303,99.22,5.96,"the UK has lost 97% of its species-diverse meadowlands."],[303,105.73,2.13,"But in the village of Drayton,"],[303,107.86,4.38,"residents are taking action by doing less…"],[303,112.24,2.33,"- Ooh, musk mallow."],[303,114.57,3.96,"..as the community embraces the No Mow movement."],[303,118.53,3.42,"Neighbours now compete not for the neatest lawn,"],[303,121.95,2.71,"but for the wildest."],[303,124.67,2.46,"- I'm here as the biodiversity judge,"],[303,127.13,2.04,"to go and check out people's gardens."],[303,130.0,3.42,"So the first thing that I'll do is make a list of the whole site"],[303,133.42,1.79,"and all the different species,"],[303,135.22,2.04,"and then I'll see what the overall diversity is."],[303,138.35,1.88,"Now I'm very envious of some of our neighbours,"],[303,140.22,2.21,"who've got some orchids coming through."],[303,142.43,1.84,"- The only thing we've introduced is the rattle."],[303,144.27,2.08,"Everything else just appeared."],[303,146.35,2.29,"- I think it adds to a lawn rather than detracts."],[303,148.65,1.75,"It's just my way of getting out of a job perhaps."],[303,150.4,1.08,"Yes, there is that."],[303,153.9,2.92,"[Sarah] I'm a real plant addict."],[303,156.82,2.13,"Ever since I was a little girl,"],[303,158.95,4.46,"I had visions of working as a conservationist in the Amazon."],[303,163.41,5.17,"Being part of this feels like I'm doing my little bit to inspire other people"],[303,168.59,3.09,"to look after their own little wild lawn jungle."],[303,171.67,3.63,"[Narrator] One study has found that lawns left unmown"],[303,175.3,3.0,"can nearly double their number of plant species"],[303,178.3,2.79,"in the first 18 months."],[303,182.56,2.25,"Fostering a colourful comeback"],[303,184.81,3.42,"isn't the only reason to encourage wild species."],[303,188.23,2.88,"[Esther] There are so many benefits to having native plants."],[303,191.11,5.38,"They're adapted to local conditions, local climate, so they're very resilient."],[303,196.49,3.46,"Native plants have such deep roots, they can soak up so much more water,"],[303,199.95,4.42,"and then stormwater run-off isn't an issue, flooding is less of an issue."],[303,204.37,3.42,"If wildfires are happening, then the natives are used to that."],[303,207.79,2.38,"If you have non-natives, they're not going to survive the fire"],[303,210.17,2.33,"because that's not what they evolved with."],[303,212.5,3.92,"[Narrator] Not only do non-natives fare worse in a wildfire,"],[303,216.42,4.88,"because they tend to be drier, they literally add fuel to the flames."],[303,224.6,2.96,"[Esther] A huge advantage of native plants is they're cheaper."],[303,227.56,3.29,"It is incredibly easy to care for native plants."],[303,230.85,6.05,"They're used to thriving without any extra fertilisers or pesticides or even extra watering."],[303,236.9,3.84,"[Narrator] Using fewer chemicals also helps create a habitat"],[303,240.74,2.21,"that welcomes in wildlife."],[303,244.28,4.67,"Pollinators help plants reproduce and boost biodiversity."],[303,249.58,4.63,"But as much as 35% of insect pollinators"],[303,254.21,2.25,"face extinction globally,"],[303,256.46,2.04,"partly due to habitat loss"],[303,258.51,2.63,"and the use of pesticides."],[303,261.13,3.92,"[Sarah] Recreating these diverse habitats in your own garden"],[303,265.06,3.46,"can be a way of providing more space for pollinators,"],[303,268.52,3.38,"and, hopefully, see an uptake in those numbers."],[303,271.9,2.42,"[Narrator] Simply by mowing every three months"],[303,274.31,2.29,"rather than every two weeks,"],[303,276.61,6.13,"one study recorded a 170% increase in pollinator numbers."],[303,282.74,2.96,"[Sarah] That increases the diversity of plant species,"],[303,285.7,3.54,"and that in turn increases the ability to capture carbon"],[303,289.25,2.96,"because of the complex root structures in the soil."],[303,293.0,2.08,"[Esther] If you think about turf grass,"],[303,295.09,2.17,"it maybe has four or five inches of roots,"],[303,297.25,4.5,"and natives, they have five, 10, 15 feet of roots"],[303,301.76,4.13,"and the carbon that those roots are storing is so much more."],[303,308.47,4.5,"[Narrator] By reducing erosion and allowing water to soak in more readily,"],[303,312.98,4.96,"such long roots also help to keep the soil itself healthy."],[303,319.69,3.21,"And why should we care about healthy soil?"],[303,322.9,3.67,"Well, it's the second-largest carbon store on Earth,"],[303,326.58,1.92,"after our oceans."],[303,333.37,2.42,"[Sarah] It can be really hard sometimes to think"],[303,335.79,2.96,"that what you are doing alone is significant."],[303,338.75,3.67,"But actually on, like, a global scale, if everyone was doing this,"],[303,342.43,5.25,"it could really change so much in terms of benefit to wildlife,"],[303,347.68,2.96,"but also change people's opinions on nature"],[303,350.64,1.92,"and how they view the planet."],[303,352.56,2.34,"And I do think it starts with small."],[303,355.98,2.88,"You don't have to have a big lawn to take part."],[303,358.86,3.25,"You can use planters or just a small patch of lawn,"],[303,362.11,2.92,"or even pots in your house."],[303,365.03,2.04,"[Narrator] So, whatever your space,"],[303,367.07,1.46,"give it a go."],[303,368.53,3.42,"Because there are proven benefits to tuning in to nature."],[303,373.46,2.71,"Studies show that having your hands in soil"],[303,376.17,2.88,"can shift your mood from stressed to calm."],[303,379.84,3.96,"The scent of roses has been found to lower the stress hormone cortisol."],[303,384.63,3.25,"And a 10% increase in urban greenery"],[303,387.89,3.29,"can boost happiness by up to 18%."],[303,393.73,3.5,"[Sarah] We've seen a definite increase in people wanting to take part."],[303,397.23,3.25,"There are people doing it all over the UK."],[303,400.48,4.09,"We're even starting to get people from Australia and America."],[303,407.66,4.63,"[Narrator] Access to nature has also been linked to happier communities."],[303,412.29,4.71,"[Esther] The biggest thing for me about our native seedling giveaways is the community."],[303,418.67,2.33,"Here you are! Enjoy planting them."],[303,422.3,4.21,"[Narrator] So the next time you look out at your garden, imagine this –"],[303,426.51,3.79,"2% of the United States is covered by lawn."],[303,430.3,4.38,"That's around half the area of all US national parks."],[303,434.68,4.13,"Letting just half of that lawn grow out into more natural greenery"],[303,438.81,3.13,"would dramatically boost biodiversity,"],[303,441.94,1.29,"reduce water use"],[303,443.23,4.17,"and sequester literally millions of tonnes of carbon each year."],[303,448.32,1.88,"So step outside"],[303,450.2,3.54,"and unleash the benefits of your own backyard."],[304,2.08,1.87,"How can you shape the future?"],[304,3.95,4.75,"At TED, we believe it's by shaping the future of ideas."],[304,8.7,3.23,"And that future is in the hands of young people,"],[304,11.93,3.13,"sharing their vision to make an impact."],[304,15.06,2.74,"That's why we created TED-Ed Student Talks,"],[304,17.8,5.82,"empowering over a million students to develop and share their ideas with the world."],[304,23.63,2.37,"And now we're taking it further:"],[304,25.99,2.75,"proudly announcing TED Summer School,"],[304,28.74,3.52,"a transformative journey guiding curious young minds"],[304,32.26,2.62,"to become the thought leaders of tomorrow."],[304,35.36,4.82,"Ambitious students from around the world will work with TED-trained tutors"],[304,40.18,4.92,"to develop original ideas and gain expert storytelling skills,"],[304,45.09,2.27,"culminating in a standout moment"],[304,47.36,3.81,"showcasing the power and potential of your voice."],[304,51.86,2.43,"We're partnering with Immerse Education,"],[304,54.29,2.58,"a trusted leader in academic enrichment,"],[304,56.87,3.13,"to offer a safe, supportive experience,"],[304,60.0,2.54,"in person around the world,"],[304,62.54,6.06,"and virtually with the same live, small group mentorship— 100% remote."],[304,68.86,3.48,"We're empowering young thinkers to share their ideas"],[304,72.34,4.08,"with clarity, purpose, and impact."],[304,76.42,5.75,"Sign up now to apply and learn more about merit and need-based scholarships."],[304,82.18,4.76,"TED Summer School. Find your voice and take the world stage."],[305,6.96,6.3,"In 326 BCE, the mighty army of Alexander the Great was exhausted."],[305,13.34,3.29,"Tired of monsoon rains and fruitless fighting in India,"],[305,16.68,4.17,"Alexander’s forces mutinied and demanded a retreat."],[305,21.06,2.13,"But their bad luck followed them home."],[305,23.27,5.63,"While marching along the Indus River, the water’s current suddenly reversed"],[305,29.15,4.59,"and a massive wave crashed down on the weary soldiers."],[305,34.45,3.17,"This unexpected event was a tidal bore,"],[305,37.83,6.05,"a phenomenon that occurs when extremely high tides push seawater up a river."],[305,44.12,2.67,"The wave was likely quite the shock for Alexander,"],[305,46.79,3.54,"who was accustomed to the Mediterranean’s mild tides."],[305,50.34,4.84,"But tidal bores are just one of many ways tides can surprise."],[305,55.93,5.42,"2,000 years after Alexander, Isaac Newton deciphered the laws of gravity"],[305,61.35,3.92,"and offered the first gravitational explanation of tides."],[305,65.73,2.21,"As Newton correctly identified,"],[305,67.94,4.25,"tides are choreographed by the motions of celestial objects,"],[305,72.36,4.63,"and Earth’s tides in particular are mostly driven by the Moon."],[305,77.49,5.21,"Many coastal communities connected lunar and tidal activity long before Newton,"],[305,82.83,4.88,"but the precise nature of this relationship is actually quite nuanced."],[305,88.42,4.3,"The attractive force of gravity gets weaker with distance,"],[305,92.92,5.46,"so the Moon’s gravity tugs strongest on the side of the Earth that faces it."],[305,98.55,5.84,"There, gravity pulls the oceans up into what’s called a tidal bulge."],[305,105.14,5.5,"Yet at the same time, another tidal bulge forms on the planet’s opposite side."],[305,110.9,3.09,"This might seem like gravity defying behavior."],[305,114.15,3.88,"But that’s because we often think of the Moon as orbiting the Earth,"],[305,118.28,3.84,"when in reality, the Earth and Moon orbit each other"],[305,122.16,5.09,"around a shared center of mass roughly 1,700 kilometers"],[305,127.25,2.13,"below the planet’s surface."],[305,130.17,4.5,"In this context, the Earth is like a child holding on to a carousel."],[305,134.92,3.17,"And just like a rider’s hair flies out behind them,"],[305,138.09,5.71,"Earth's water stretches away to create that second tidal bulge."],[305,144.18,4.25,"Within that orbit, the Earth rotates once a day,"],[305,148.69,3.96,"moving points on its surface in and out of these bulges."],[305,153.4,5.46,"This results in two daily high tides when areas are inside each bulge,"],[305,158.95,4.42,"and two daily low tides when places are between them."],[305,163.91,4.67,"But as Newton recognized, it’s not just the Moon’s gravity that pulls on Earth—"],[305,168.71,3.34,"our Sun tugs the tides, too."],[305,172.34,4.92,"In fact, the Sun is why tidal strength varies with the phases of the Moon."],[305,177.76,3.92,"Lunar phases coincide with different gravitational lineups"],[305,181.68,2.0,"of the Moon, Sun, and Earth."],[305,183.97,4.0,"For example, high tides are highest when the Moon is full,"],[305,188.14,2.75,"creating extreme spring tides."],[305,191.06,3.79,"And low tides are lowest when the Moon is half-full,"],[305,194.94,2.63,"making tiny neap tides."],[305,197.86,3.0,"Subtleties in the orbits of these celestial bodies"],[305,200.86,3.63,"introduce even more complexities and tidal varieties."],[305,204.66,4.71,"And the strength of all these tides depends on the local landscape."],[305,209.83,4.42,"Flat, enclosed lakes and seas generate the weakest tides,"],[305,214.34,3.92,"while bays and narrow inlets produce the strongest."],[305,218.59,2.75,"Well, at least the strongest tides on Earth—"],[305,221.59,2.29,"there are even more dramatic tidal forces"],[305,223.89,3.34,"on our solar system's other celestial bodies."],[305,227.6,3.42,"Millennia of Jupiter and Saturn’s gravitational kneading"],[305,231.02,5.38,"has generated enough heat on their respective moons of Enceladus and Europa"],[305,236.4,3.46,"to create oceans beneath their icy crusts."],[305,240.19,4.88,"Jupiter’s moon Io endures the strongest tidal forces in the solar system,"],[305,245.12,2.88,"fueling intense volcanic activity."],[305,248.29,4.46,"And in other planetary systems, some planets orbit so close to their stars"],[305,252.75,3.8,"that extreme tidal forces lock them in place."],[305,256.71,4.42,"This tidal locking can leave the sun-facing hemisphere boiling"],[305,261.26,2.92,"while the other freezes in eternal night."],[305,264.47,4.92,"You won’t find a half-melting, tidally-locked planet in our solar system,"],[305,269.56,4.75,"but given enough time, tidal forces would lock the Earth to the Moon."],[305,274.69,3.92,"As Earth’s oceans churn to keep pace with the Moon,"],[305,278.61,4.46,"the water creates friction that slows our planet's rotation."],[305,283.32,2.42,"And after roughly 50 billion years,"],[305,285.74,2.96,"this process will have slowed Earth down enough"],[305,288.7,3.0,"for it to become tidally locked to the Moon."],[305,292.08,1.71,"But before you start to sweat,"],[305,293.87,3.79,"you can take solace in the knowledge that the Sun will have already died"],[305,297.67,4.25,"and taken Earth with it billions of years earlier."],[306,4.3,3.2,"There's a fundamental paradox right at the core of human life."],[306,7.87,2.3,"On the one hand, decades of research have shown"],[306,10.21,2.0,"that we are highly social creatures"],[306,12.24,2.33,"who are made happier and healthier by reaching out"],[306,14.61,3.97,"and connecting with other people in the moments, the days, the weeks,"],[306,18.62,2.27,"the months and the years of our lives."],[306,21.65,4.14,"And yet, on the other hand, just look around a little bit."],[306,25.82,2.7,"It's not clear that all of us have gotten this memo."],[306,28.83,2.9,"Every day there are opportunities, big and small, to reach out"],[306,31.76,3.24,"and connect with other people that we choose not to take."],[306,35.0,1.74,"We avoid talking to strangers."],[306,37.13,1.74,"We lean back and type to each other"],[306,38.87,2.3,"rather than leaning in and talking to each other."],[306,41.6,3.87,"Once talking, we stick to shallow talk, to small talk, rather than going deeper."],[306,45.51,1.87,"We feel grateful but don’t express it."],[306,47.38,3.67,"[We] want to reach out to offer support to someone in need but hold back."],[306,51.08,2.5,"We’d like to be open and honest in our relationships,"],[306,53.62,3.0,"but all too often keep our true selves to ourselves."],[306,57.22,3.3,"If being socially connected is so darn good for us,"],[306,60.56,3.1,"then why do we so often seem to be so darn unsocial?"],[306,64.86,2.97,"This paradox hit me like a freight train one morning"],[306,67.86,1.83,"while I was on an actual train,"],[306,69.7,2.54,"commuting into my office at the University of Chicago,"],[306,72.27,2.6,"where I work as a professor of behavioral science."],[306,74.91,3.07,"That morning on the train began like every other I'd been on"],[306,78.01,1.33,"for years beforehand --"],[306,79.38,1.27,"all filing onto the train,"],[306,80.64,3.44,"everybody in a desperate search for their own little acreage of solitude"],[306,84.11,1.13,"right along the window."],[306,85.28,3.04,"I think we'd have sat outside the train if that was possible."],[306,89.72,3.07,"Heaven forbid you’d sit next to somebody and start a chitchat --"],[306,92.79,1.1,"of course, you creep."],[306,93.89,2.9,"Or worse yet, somebody would come and sit down next to you --"],[306,96.79,1.3,"surely some kind of weirdo."],[306,98.09,1.77,"But then there we all were."],[306,99.9,1.57,"Highly social creatures,"],[306,101.5,3.1,"made happier and healthier by connecting with other people,"],[306,104.64,1.17,"now sitting hip-to-hip"],[306,105.8,2.17,"with another perfectly reasonable human being."],[306,108.0,4.17,"And what did we do for the next 30 to 45 minutes with each other?"],[306,115.91,1.47,"We chose to ignore each other."],[306,117.41,2.4,"You could have heard a pin drop that morning."],[306,120.62,1.2,"That morning, a woman,"],[306,121.82,2.74,"who was about 15 to 20 years older than I was at the time,"],[306,124.59,1.2,"sat down next to me,"],[306,125.82,1.53,"dressed professionally for work"],[306,127.36,3.6,"and wearing just this fabulous, killer, stylish red hat."],[306,131.0,2.27,"I'm never going to forget this red hat."],[306,134.2,2.23,"I put other people in experiments for a living,"],[306,136.43,2.74,"but this morning I decided to put myself in an experiment"],[306,139.2,1.97,"and pay close attention to what happened."],[306,141.21,1.37,"Instead of keeping to myself"],[306,142.57,2.4,"and doomscrolling on my phone or checking my email,"],[306,144.97,1.94,"I'd try to have a conversation with her,"],[306,146.94,2.4,"try to help us get to know each other a little bit."],[306,149.35,3.34,"Turn this 30-minute dull ride into something a little more interesting."],[306,152.72,2.5,"Turn a stranger into a momentary acquaintance."],[306,155.25,2.84,"The second I had that thought about that experiment,"],[306,158.09,2.4,"my brain started screaming at me all the reasons"],[306,160.52,2.47,"why this was a really, really bad idea."],[306,163.03,2.0,"\"Clearly, she doesn't want to talk to you,"],[306,165.06,2.04,"otherwise she'd already be talking to you."],[306,167.1,2.2,"She's going to think you're some kind of creep."],[306,169.3,2.7,"You probably don't even have anything in common with her,"],[306,172.04,2.7,"and you’ve got nothing to even start with, smarty pants.”"],[306,174.74,2.77,"Nevertheless, I decided the experiment must continue,"],[306,177.54,2.24,"so I ignored that part of my brain."],[306,179.78,2.1,"I turned to her and I said,"],[306,181.88,1.53,"\"Hi, my name is Nick."],[306,183.71,1.37,"I love your hat."],[306,185.12,1.57,"I have one just like it.\""],[306,188.28,1.2,"Yeah, huh?"],[306,191.39,2.4,"Now look, I know that’s not going to make its way"],[306,193.79,2.04,"into the conversation-starter Hall of Fame,"],[306,195.86,1.4,"but it didn't seem to matter."],[306,197.26,1.5,"She turned to me, a big smile,"],[306,198.8,1.37,"her face all lit up --"],[306,200.2,2.24,"she looked like a different person."],[306,202.47,2.77,"And from there, the conversation just flowed really easily."],[306,205.24,1.94,"[We] found things that we had in common,"],[306,207.17,3.07,"we talked about our families, our work, our hope for the future."],[306,210.27,2.13,"30-minute train ride just went like that."],[306,212.44,3.17,"I was done, and I got up to leave, and she stopped me and she said,"],[306,215.61,3.27,"\"Thank you so much for taking the time to talk with me this morning.\""],[306,220.78,1.67,"I've forgotten a lot of details"],[306,222.45,2.04,"about how that conversation actually went,"],[306,224.52,2.97,"but I've never forgotten how that conversation made me feel."],[306,227.49,2.94,"Not just good, but surprisingly good."],[306,230.83,3.14,"The contrast between my beliefs about how that would go"],[306,233.96,2.8,"and how it actually went was pretty sizable."],[306,237.47,2.44,"And there ... in that gap"],[306,239.94,2.7,"was a potential resolution to this paradox."],[306,243.21,3.54,"Social connection, after all, isn't something that just happens to us."],[306,246.78,1.17,"It's a choice we make."],[306,247.98,1.47,"It's a choice we make at times"],[306,249.45,2.84,"to reach out and approach other people, to engage with them,"],[306,252.32,1.5,"or to hold back and avoid them."],[306,253.82,2.57,"It might, in fact, arguably be the choice we make,"],[306,256.42,1.87,"the most important choice we make,"],[306,258.32,1.5,"because how we make that choice"],[306,259.86,2.74,"over and over and over again in our lives so routinely"],[306,262.63,2.3,"determines so much about our happiness,"],[306,264.93,1.9,"our health and our success in life."],[306,267.26,3.67,"But highly social creatures like us might avoid reaching out"],[306,270.97,2.64,"and engaging with other people mistakenly"],[306,273.64,4.24,"if we underestimate just how positively our attempts to connect might turn out."],[306,279.74,4.2,"That morning changed both my career and my character."],[306,284.58,1.64,"In my career,"],[306,286.22,2.67,"my wonderful collaborators and I"],[306,288.89,2.84,"have now conducted well over 100 experiments"],[306,291.72,3.37,"with over 30,000 people of all ages and nationalities,"],[306,295.12,2.77,"and found that my tendency to be overly pessimistic"],[306,297.93,1.57,"is not unique to me."],[306,299.53,3.47,"It's something we see over and over again in varying shades and magnitudes"],[306,303.0,3.07,"across different contexts that vary a little bit across people --"],[306,306.07,1.9,"but that consistent signal is there."],[306,308.34,1.8,"In one of our very first experiments,"],[306,310.14,1.5,"we went back to a train station"],[306,311.68,2.07,"on the line that I ride into work every day,"],[306,313.74,1.83,"and we recruited a group of commuters,"],[306,315.58,1.44,"and we asked them to predict"],[306,317.05,2.14,"how they would feel on the train that morning"],[306,319.22,2.37,"if they kept to themselves in solitude,"],[306,321.62,3.1,"or if they turned to the person who sat next to them that morning"],[306,324.75,2.4,"and tried to have a conversation, tried to connect."],[306,327.16,1.77,"The results here were crystal clear:"],[306,328.93,2.77,"people thought talking to a stranger was a really bad idea."],[306,331.73,2.6,"They thought they would have a more pleasant experience"],[306,334.37,3.34,"that left them feeling happier if they kept to themselves in solitude,"],[306,337.74,3.34,"than if they turned to the stranger to connect with them on the train."],[306,341.11,2.23,"But when we recruited another sample of people"],[306,343.37,2.54,"and actually randomly assigned them in an experiment"],[306,345.94,2.4,"to either keep to themselves in solitude"],[306,348.35,2.74,"or to try to connect with a person sitting next to them --"],[306,351.08,2.54,"rather than just imagine it, to actually do it --"],[306,353.65,2.17,"we found exactly the opposite results."],[306,356.35,2.5,"The people we had instructed randomly"],[306,358.89,1.9,"to keep to themselves that morning"],[306,360.82,2.54,"reported having a less pleasant and happy commute"],[306,363.36,3.2,"than those we asked to connect with the person sitting next to them."],[306,366.56,3.2,"People’s beliefs about social interaction here weren’t just wrong --"],[306,369.77,1.43,"they were precisely backward."],[306,371.2,3.37,"But notice that if you believe that talking with a stranger would suck,"],[306,374.61,1.23,"you wouldn't try it,"],[306,375.87,3.4,"and then you'd never find out that you might be wrong about that."],[306,379.31,3.5,"Pessimistic beliefs in that way are self-fulfilling."],[306,384.18,3.84,"This little experiment was just the tip of a very large iceberg"],[306,388.05,2.7,"that came into view for us in many ensuing years."],[306,390.79,2.5,"We've now seen this tendency to be overly pessimistic"],[306,393.32,1.77,"over and over and over again."],[306,395.12,3.27,"We’ve now had more than 4,500 people"],[306,398.43,2.27,"not just have conversations with a stranger,"],[306,400.73,2.44,"but to have deep conversations with a stranger and"],[306,403.2,1.47,"talk about things like,"],[306,404.7,2.87,"\"Can you tell me what you're most grateful for in your life?\""],[306,407.57,2.24,"Or \"Can you tell me about one of the last times"],[306,409.84,1.87,"you cried in front of another person?\""],[306,412.34,2.77,"So when I show people these questions in these experiments,"],[306,415.14,2.67,"I can feel a sense of dread spreading over the room"],[306,417.85,2.07,"when I put these questions up on the board,"],[306,419.92,1.53,"people start eyeing the exits --"],[306,421.45,2.37,"wishing they hadn't come to this session today."],[306,423.85,2.84,"But then when I actually put them into the experiments,"],[306,426.72,1.8,"in the conversation,"],[306,428.56,2.37,"the trouble I have is getting them to come back."],[306,430.96,3.74,"These conversations go much better than people expect that they will."],[306,435.17,1.8,"This is also true when we have people"],[306,437.0,3.87,"who disagree about the most divisive political issues that divide us today"],[306,440.9,2.07,"talk about those political disagreements --"],[306,442.97,1.7,"even those political disagreements,"],[306,446.98,2.3,"go better than people expect them to."],[306,449.81,3.4,"We find in our research that we have tremendous power"],[306,453.25,3.47,"to create meaningful social connection every day of our lives."],[306,456.75,3.34,"But if we underestimate how positively our efforts to reach out"],[306,460.09,1.6,"and connect with someone will go,"],[306,461.73,2.37,"we won't use that power that we have."],[306,466.86,2.1,"We see this tendency for misplaced pessimism"],[306,469.0,2.34,"also showing up beyond conversation."],[306,471.94,2.0,"When we ask people to think of a compliment"],[306,473.97,1.5,"they could give to their friend"],[306,475.47,2.74,"and then actually deliver that compliment to their friend,"],[306,478.24,2.1,"they leave their friend feeling more uplifted"],[306,480.34,2.0,"than the complimenters imagined they would."],[306,482.38,3.17,"When we ask people to express their gratitude to someone they love,"],[306,485.55,2.27,"they leave their recipients feeling even better"],[306,487.82,2.47,"than the expressers predict they would."],[306,491.09,3.77,"Performing random acts of kindness, reaching out and asking for help,"],[306,494.89,2.13,"expressing support to someone in need,"],[306,497.06,2.24,"being open and honest in our relationships"],[306,499.3,2.47,"all tend to be received, on average,"],[306,501.8,3.74,"more favorably, more positively by the people we're reaching out to"],[306,505.57,2.77,"than the people who are reaching out expected to."],[306,510.24,1.33,"How much more time"],[306,511.61,2.27,"would you spend reaching out to lift somebody up"],[306,513.88,4.14,"if you knew just how much good in that moment you could actually do?"],[306,520.58,2.47,"No research I've ever been involved with"],[306,523.09,2.37,"has changed the way I live my life more than this."],[306,526.56,2.47,"My train rides are almost never silent anymore."],[306,529.06,2.64,"I met amazing people on planes and in cabs."],[306,531.86,2.54,"Even just walking around town is more pleasant for me,"],[306,534.4,2.7,"whether I’m on campus or [at] work or in a grocery store,"],[306,537.1,2.77,"because I’ve made a habit of walking around with my head up"],[306,539.87,1.97,"smiling and saying hello to other people,"],[306,541.84,2.34,"and I get, in return, a lot more smiles and hellos"],[306,544.17,1.17,"when I'm walking around."],[306,545.34,2.54,"When I feel grateful, I write a note and send it off."],[306,547.91,2.4,"When I need help, I’m less reluctant to ask for it."],[306,550.31,1.87,"When I know someone needs some support,"],[306,552.18,2.4,"I’m not as embarrassed to reach out and offer it --"],[306,554.59,2.27,"even if there's nothing I can do in that moment."],[306,556.85,2.84,"It's made me a more open, friendlier person and as a result,"],[306,559.69,2.0,"changed all of my relationships."],[306,561.69,2.64,"I've turned countless strangers into friends"],[306,564.33,1.64,"or into acquaintances at least --"],[306,566.0,1.33,"even if just for a moment."],[306,567.97,1.7,"My friendships are better."],[306,569.7,1.64,"I think my marriage is stronger."],[306,572.5,1.7,"I think I'm a better father."],[306,575.27,2.07,"These changes didn't happen to me overnight."],[306,577.34,2.07,"They happened, of course, slowly, over time."],[306,579.41,1.53,"Just like you move a mountain --"],[306,580.94,2.77,"not by pushing it all at once, but one shovelful at a time."],[306,583.75,3.64,"The way you change how you approach other people happens slowly, over time."],[306,587.42,1.67,"One choice after another."],[306,589.12,1.17,"One small choice,"],[306,590.29,2.8,"as you learn where your mistaken beliefs about other people"],[306,593.12,2.17,"might be holding you back needlessly."],[306,595.33,3.44,"And then you develop habits that then become part of your character"],[306,598.79,1.67,"and part of who you are."],[306,601.87,2.24,"Overcoming my misplaced pessimism, though,"],[306,604.13,3.47,"has also affected how I've made some big choices"],[306,607.64,2.07,"that I have been a part of in my life,"],[306,609.74,3.0,"including when pain struck my family."],[306,614.61,1.74,"So 10 years ago,"],[306,616.35,4.87,"my wife Jen was three months into her pregnancy"],[306,621.25,3.17,"when we learned that our daughter, who we had already named Sophie,"],[306,624.42,1.2,"had Down syndrome."],[306,625.62,1.37,"And three months after that,"],[306,626.99,1.87,"we learned that our daughter had died"],[306,628.86,1.7,"before she could be born."],[306,631.89,1.64,"Losing our daughter was horrible."],[306,633.56,1.53,"It was absolutely horrible."],[306,635.7,2.13,"And we mourned that loss for many months,"],[306,638.53,2.57,"until one morning Jen and I were talking"],[306,641.14,2.7,"and she asked whether we could,"],[306,643.84,2.24,"whether we should, whether we might"],[306,646.08,2.44,"consider adopting a child with Down syndrome."],[306,649.88,1.7,"And there it was, the choice."],[306,652.92,2.0,"Do you reach out and connect with someone?"],[306,654.95,1.97,"Do you engage with them, do you approach,"],[306,656.92,2.2,"or do you hold back and avoid it?"],[306,660.59,2.77,"Jen and I had already adopted two children into our family,"],[306,663.39,2.17,"and so we had some sense of how this might go."],[306,665.6,2.44,"Nevertheless, this choice caught me off guard."],[306,668.07,1.47,"I wasn't there at that moment."],[306,669.53,1.5,"My mind wasn't there yet."],[306,671.07,2.87,"And so I had all the pessimistic fears that you might have"],[306,673.97,2.27,"when you think about connecting with a stranger,"],[306,676.24,2.04,"or having a deep conversation with someone,"],[306,678.31,2.04,"except multiplied by 100 or 1,000."],[306,680.71,1.83,"How well would this go?"],[306,682.58,1.67,"Would we be able to handle this?"],[306,684.25,1.8,"Would we be able to connect, to love,"],[306,686.05,3.27,"to parent the stranger we were bringing into our lives"],[306,689.35,1.47,"with all of these challenges"],[306,690.85,2.97,"that seemed to me at the time very hard and difficult."],[306,693.86,2.24,"How would this child respond to us?"],[306,697.76,1.53,"My first thought was,"],[306,699.33,2.0,"I don't think we can do this."],[306,701.36,1.67,"I'm not sure I can do this."],[306,703.9,2.64,"But my second thought then started turning to my data,"],[306,706.57,2.33,"as researchers will tell you, can happen."],[306,709.84,4.44,"And I started thinking about thousands and thousands of data points,"],[306,714.31,2.64,"of people underestimating the joys they would experience"],[306,716.95,2.47,"when they reach out to engage with, to connect with,"],[306,719.45,2.24,"to pull someone else close to them."],[306,722.65,2.84,"And it gave me data-driven courage"],[306,725.52,2.07,"that, yeah, we can do this together."],[306,727.62,2.54,"I happened to marry a superhero, too."],[306,730.56,1.77,"We can do this together."],[306,732.66,1.64,"And it won't just be good."],[306,734.3,1.77,"I bet it'll be surprisingly good."],[306,737.57,2.1,"And so about a year after that,"],[306,739.7,2.9,"Jen and I boarded a flight to China"],[306,742.64,2.2,"with our four other children,"],[306,744.84,2.17,"where we were going to meet Lindsey,"],[306,747.04,4.17,"two years old, born to a woman we will never meet,"],[306,751.21,3.77,"with big dark eyes and just a relentless smile,"],[306,755.02,3.5,"despite a really, really hard start in her life."],[306,759.62,2.23,"We reached out to Lindsey"],[306,761.86,2.34,"and Lindsey reached back to us."],[306,764.66,4.5,"She's been bringing love and smiles like that into our lives for years since."],[306,769.8,1.9,"Now I want to be clear,"],[306,771.74,3.37,"raising a child with an intellectual disability is hard."],[306,775.14,1.17,"It's really hard."],[306,776.34,1.53,"Lindsey is not just one handful."],[306,777.87,1.7,"She is both arms completely full."],[306,781.18,2.77,"But she's also enriched and blessed our lives"],[306,783.98,3.97,"so far beyond what my pessimistic expectations beforehand"],[306,787.98,1.94,"ever possibly could have imagined."],[306,792.86,3.4,"Connecting with other people is one of the most consistently enjoyable,"],[306,796.29,2.6,"enlightening and enriching experiences we'll ever have."],[306,798.89,2.24,"And yet, all too often, our choice to reach out"],[306,801.13,3.07,"and connect with somebody is thwarted by overly pessimistic fears"],[306,804.2,1.8,"about how other people might respond."],[306,806.0,1.8,"Being overly pessimistic doesn't mean"],[306,807.84,3.17,"we should reach out all the time, or that it always turns out well;"],[306,811.04,1.13,"of course not."],[306,812.17,2.27,"It means we tend to underestimate the likelihood"],[306,814.48,2.07,"that it will turn out well, and as a result,"],[306,816.55,2.37,"we tend to hold ourselves back a little too often."],[306,818.91,1.94,"I've found in my life and in my research"],[306,820.88,2.7,"that testing some of those beliefs that hold us back"],[306,823.62,3.14,"can reveal places where we're making mistakes about other people"],[306,826.79,1.8,"and show us how to reach out,"],[306,828.62,4.1,"empower us to reach out a little bit more often than we might otherwise,"],[306,832.73,4.27,"to make both our own lives and those we reach out to a little bit better."],[306,837.83,2.34,"You want to change your life for the better?"],[306,840.6,2.94,"I suggest keeping some data-driven courage in mind."],[306,844.01,1.53,"And when in doubt,"],[306,845.58,1.17,"reach out."],[306,847.14,1.44,"Thank you so much."],[307,7.17,4.0,"It's just before dawn on a Saturday in summer 1906,"],[307,11.3,4.04,"and 16-year-old Alice Sutton’s mind is already spinning—"],[307,15.47,2.96,"tonight’s plans could cost her her job."],[307,18.64,1.96,"But for now, there's work to do."],[307,21.48,4.17,"Alice gets ready slowly, thinking about how her life has changed."],[307,25.86,3.84,"Only a week ago, she was the lone servant in a modest home."],[307,29.86,4.5,"Now she’s an under-housemaid at Wroxton Abbey in Oxfordshire, England,"],[307,34.41,3.92,"the grand estate of Sir John and Lady Ann Ashby-Blythe."],[307,38.74,2.38,"As one of the lower-ranking indoor servants,"],[307,41.12,2.09,"she reports to the housemaids"],[307,43.21,3.84,"and, ultimately, the housekeeper, Mrs. Fletcher."],[307,47.25,4.0,"Her responsibilities include scrubbing floors, sweeping fireplaces,"],[307,51.26,1.84,"and other cleaning tasks."],[307,53.38,4.34,"She’s one of over a million women in domestic service in England—"],[307,57.72,3.59,"making up almost 30% of the female workforce."],[307,61.64,3.17,"All but the poorest households have at least one servant,"],[307,65.14,3.17,"while estates like Wroxton employ dozens."],[307,68.65,3.92,"She waits for Roberta, her nosy roommate, to leave."],[307,72.86,3.71,"When all’s clear, Alice slips a stick of chalk into her pocket."],[307,76.91,5.21,"A buzz of excitement fills the kitchen as everyone discusses tonight’s guest:"],[307,82.45,2.21,"Prince Arthur, the king’s brother."],[307,84.91,4.0,"Tonight is Alice’s chance to prove herself to her new employers."],[307,89.21,1.79,"As she finishes her breakfast,"],[307,91.0,3.38,"Alice watches as a kitchen maid carefully stirs eggs,"],[307,94.38,2.96,"ensuring the yolks stay perfectly centered."],[307,97.59,5.51,"Like many of the tasks servants perform, it requires painstaking precision."],[307,103.35,3.21,"And Alice thinks it’s the sort of pointless demand"],[307,106.56,3.34,"only someone who has never had to do it could dream up."],[307,110.61,3.09,"Alice and another under-housemaid, Louisa,"],[307,113.69,4.17,"head to Sir John and Lady Ann’s rooms to perform their morning chores:"],[307,118.32,2.71,"filling the water basins, opening the curtains,"],[307,121.03,2.04,"and carrying out the chamber pots."],[307,123.54,3.75,"The two girls had become fast friends after meeting months ago"],[307,127.33,3.5,"at a gathering of the Women’s Social and Political Union."],[307,131.34,3.46,"Since 1869, British women who owned property"],[307,134.8,2.59,"had been allowed to vote in local elections—"],[307,137.63,1.75,"but not in parliamentary ones."],[307,140.14,3.13,"Many hoped the Liberal Party, back in power since December,"],[307,143.35,2.46,"would expand voting rights for all women,"],[307,145.97,1.83,"but they kept deferring the issue."],[307,148.14,3.42,"This included Alice’s employer, Sir John, who,"],[307,151.65,2.29,"while a critic of Britain’s imperial project,"],[307,153.94,2.92,"had yet to voice an opinion on women's suffrage."],[307,157.49,4.84,"But now, women of all classes were demanding deeds, not words,"],[307,162.32,1.21,"from the government—"],[307,163.87,4.5,"chaining themselves to railings, smashing windows, and defacing churches."],[307,168.79,2.13,"Alice and Louisa hadn't gone that far."],[307,171.08,1.0,"Not yet."],[307,172.42,3.0,"Alice's afternoon is spent in the downstairs entryway,"],[307,175.42,2.75,"washing each tile in the grand mosaic with milk"],[307,178.17,3.34,"so it would gleam in the candlelight as the guests arrived."],[307,182.26,1.42,"As the dinner hour nears,"],[307,183.68,3.38,"she guides footmen carrying intricately carved butter sculptures"],[307,187.06,1.21,"to the dining room,"],[307,188.27,2.67,"and notices Roberta pulling Luisa aside."],[307,191.23,2.96,"Alice lingers, trying to listen in on their conversation,"],[307,194.19,2.79,"but Mrs. Fletcher admonishes her for dawdling."],[307,197.78,3.0,"By the time Prince Arthur's motorcar finally arrives,"],[307,200.86,3.09,"Alice has spent over 12 hours on her feet."],[307,204.2,4.0,"As the dinner bell rings out, she realizes it's now or never."],[307,208.79,2.71,"She and Louisa slip out and run to the nearby church."],[307,211.62,3.13,"Alice scrawls “Votes For Women” across the wall"],[307,214.84,2.42,"while Louisa strews pamphlets about."],[307,218.17,2.59,"Suddenly, Roberta emerges from the shadows."],[307,220.93,2.17,"Before Alice can stammer an excuse,"],[307,223.09,2.46,"Roberta hurls a rock at one of the windows."],[307,226.64,2.46,"The three girls stand back and smile,"],[307,229.27,2.63,"imagining Sir John and the prince’s reactions"],[307,231.94,2.5,"when they arrive for church the next morning."],[307,234.6,3.67,"With the prince there, the act might even make the newspapers."],[307,238.69,1.42,"They rush back to the Abbey,"],[307,240.11,2.71,"arriving just in time to help clean up after dinner."],[307,243.07,3.5,"Alice heads to bed, exhilarated and exhausted,"],[307,246.87,3.29,"unaware of how much would change in the years to come."],[307,250.45,4.13,"From 1914, the First World War fundamentally altered the rhythms"],[307,254.58,1.21,"of domestic life."],[307,256.04,3.75,"Working-class women like Alice took on roles in factories,"],[307,259.8,2.17,"offices, and civil service."],[307,262.47,2.46,"Many never returned to domestic work."],[307,265.59,3.96,"In 1918, some women over 30 were finally allowed to vote"],[307,269.56,1.58,"in parliamentary elections."],[307,271.31,3.5,"But another decade would pass before all women could vote"],[307,274.81,2.04,"on equal terms with men."],[307,277.19,4.59,"By the mid-20th century, rising taxes, falling agricultural income,"],[307,281.86,4.0,"and a shrinking workforce led many grand estates to be sold off—"],[307,286.49,4.13,"relics of a system whose grandeur depended on deep inequalities"],[307,290.79,3.29,"and the invisibility of the laborers who sustained it."],[308,3.9,3.7,"During COVID, everyone was sequestered in their own corners of the world."],[308,7.61,2.47,"I had the strange fortune of being sequestered here,"],[308,10.07,1.3,"aboard the Ocean Explorer,"],[308,11.41,2.64,"one of the most advanced research vessels on the planet,"],[308,14.08,2.2,"filming a show for National Geographic."],[308,16.28,3.4,"The ship was designed not just for research but also for storytelling."],[308,19.72,1.94,"My job was to travel the world"],[308,21.68,4.07,"and show people what exploration is like from the perspective of an engineer."],[308,25.76,2.77,"When I tell people this, they usually have three questions:"],[308,28.56,1.17,"What was it like?"],[308,29.76,2.6,"What did you learn? And how did you get the job?"],[308,32.4,1.33,"I'll go backward."],[308,34.37,2.44,"I wasn't always a great engineer."],[308,36.83,1.4,"I wasn't even a good student,"],[308,38.27,2.27,"I spent more time tinkering than doing homework."],[308,40.57,1.24,"But I love engineering,"],[308,41.84,2.4,"and that has led me on an incredible journey."],[308,44.27,2.8,"With friends, I built low-cost underwater robots"],[308,47.08,2.07,"designed to democratize exploration,"],[308,49.15,3.54,"and that democratization drew the attention of the show's producers."],[308,52.72,2.3,"You can watch the show to see what we discovered,"],[308,55.05,2.94,"but for me, the most powerful moments"],[308,57.99,2.54,"were seeing things that no one had ever seen before,"],[308,60.56,2.17,"using the tools that we had built."],[308,63.69,1.14,"In the Azores,"],[308,64.86,1.64,"we teamed up with Rui Peredo,"],[308,66.53,3.87,"[a] renowned whale biologist who spent decades studying sperm whales."],[308,70.43,3.2,"These giants can dive to over a mile deep to hunt,"],[308,73.67,3.64,"and we know very little about what they do when they're down there."],[308,77.34,3.4,"So to find out, Rui had put together a very DIY tag."],[308,80.78,2.34,"He used a taken-apart action camera,"],[308,83.15,1.97,"it had a light, there was a radio beacon,"],[308,85.12,3.34,"and it had suction cups designed to stick to the whale for a few hours"],[308,88.49,2.0,"and then pop off and float to the surface."],[308,90.52,2.7,"It was very DIY, but even on an advanced research ship,"],[308,93.22,3.47,"building your own tools is often the way to get the information you need."],[308,96.73,1.2,"It barely worked."],[308,97.93,3.4,"We had to spend nights soldering and improvising to try to get it going,"],[308,101.36,2.64,"and at like two in the morning, we finally got it going."],[308,104.03,2.6,"And while I overslept, Rui was already out on the boat."],[308,106.64,1.4,"He placed the tag on a whale,"],[308,108.04,2.27,"and I remember him radioing back the tag was on."],[308,110.31,1.47,"OK, a huge amount of suspense:"],[308,111.78,3.27,"Would it ever come back to the surface, or would it flood with water?"],[308,115.05,2.1,"Would the battery die, would the camera work?"],[308,117.18,1.13,"We had no idea."],[308,118.35,1.53,"But it did come up, we found it."],[308,119.92,3.9,"We took it back to the ship, and fingers were crossed."],[308,123.85,1.53,"Finally we opened it up."],[308,125.42,1.27,"There was no water inside."],[308,126.69,3.2,"Oh my God, we pulled out the SD card and like, with bated breath,"],[308,129.89,3.34,"we put it in the computer and waited for the files to load."],[308,133.23,3.44,"And oh my God, we had footage from the back of a sperm whale."],[308,138.47,1.23,"Rui and I were losing it."],[308,139.74,3.14,"The first thing we saw was the whale's head and back"],[308,142.91,1.6,"as she descended into the bottom."],[308,144.54,2.13,"You could hear the water rushing by"],[308,146.71,2.07,"as she swam faster and faster into the deep."],[308,148.78,2.24,"Particles rushed by the camera."],[308,151.05,2.77,"And the water pressure was so immense from the speed"],[308,153.85,2.6,"that eventually the suction cups started to come loose."],[308,156.45,2.84,"I thought that was going to be it, we were going to lose it,"],[308,159.29,1.97,"but one suction cup miraculously held on,"],[308,161.26,1.97,"and it caused the tag to rotate backward."],[308,163.23,2.2,"And now we could see the whale’s massive fluke,"],[308,165.43,2.04,"these can be 16 feet across on some whales,"],[308,167.5,2.13,"driving her into the depths."],[308,170.07,1.5,"And as it got deeper,"],[308,171.57,3.2,"we started hearing the camera’s microphone clicking."],[308,174.81,1.4,"You guys hear that?"],[308,176.24,1.23,"That is echolocation."],[308,177.47,2.1,"That is the sound of the sperm whale hunting"],[308,179.61,2.57,"by listening for echoes bouncing off of prey."],[308,182.68,1.17,"I couldn't believe it."],[308,183.85,2.57,"And it didn't seem like she caught anything that time."],[308,186.42,2.64,"But as she came shallower and the light grew brighter,"],[308,189.09,2.8,"we were just like, amazed at what we were seeing."],[308,191.89,1.83,"It didn't seem like this was possible."],[308,193.76,2.37,"And then we started hearing a different sound."],[308,196.16,2.07,"It was a rapid series of clicks."],[308,198.26,1.83,"Dot dot dot, dot, that’s called codas."],[308,200.13,3.2,"This is the way sperm whales use to communicate with each other."],[308,203.37,2.27,"So we were hearing her talking to another whale."],[308,205.67,1.83,"We could not believe our ears,"],[308,207.54,2.27,"and then we couldn't believe our eyes."],[308,209.84,1.3,"Rui and I were losing it."],[308,211.18,2.07,"The other whale came into the shot,"],[308,213.28,1.87,"and they were talking back and forth."],[308,215.18,3.04,"They were swimming and bumping alongside each other for minutes."],[308,218.25,4.3,"We watched in disbelief ... this exchange of these two whales."],[308,222.59,2.54,"Friends, family, lovers,"],[308,225.16,1.64,"we can never know for sure."],[308,226.82,3.67,"But what we were witnessing was something no one had ever seen before."],[308,230.53,4.14,"I remember seeing the bond that they had with my eyes"],[308,234.7,2.07,"and also feeling it with my heart."],[308,238.6,3.0,"The footage we had seen was not just data."],[308,242.21,1.83,"This was an experience of life."],[308,244.07,2.4,"It was reminding me why exploration really matters."],[308,246.51,3.47,"It's not just about understanding the world with our logical minds."],[308,250.01,2.1,"I believe exploration has huge potential"],[308,252.15,2.7,"to allow us to experience things with emotion"],[308,254.88,2.64,"and feel the context of why we’re here on Earth."],[308,257.86,1.94,"We saw that they even dive together,"],[308,259.82,2.27,"and that is really something that moved me."],[308,262.73,1.37,"That one last dive"],[308,264.13,4.2,"[showed] that maybe it’s not even such a lonely place down there after all."],[308,268.37,4.14,"We are all here together, and that is something extremely powerful."],[308,273.0,2.2,"So we are living in an era now"],[308,275.24,3.1,"where our tools can give us amazing ability"],[308,278.38,2.64,"to understand the world in ways never before possible."],[308,281.01,3.37,"The same advances that have put computers in our pockets"],[308,284.42,3.24,"and access to almost unlimited information on our screens"],[308,287.69,3.2,"can also allow us to explore in brand-new ways."],[308,291.79,3.6,"Our tools no longer limit what we can understand."],[308,295.43,3.34,"It's more that our understanding is limited by curiosity."],[308,299.4,2.67,"So the question isn’t what can we explore."],[308,302.1,1.74,"So much is already within our reach."],[308,303.87,1.27,"The real question is,"],[308,305.17,2.2,"what will we wonder about next?"],[308,307.4,1.17,"Thank you very much."],[309,6.96,3.67,"Somewhere on a farm in Iowa in 2010, a hen lays an egg."],[309,11.05,1.5,"In just a few short weeks,"],[309,12.63,2.96,"this egg will be part of a massive infection event:"],[309,15.6,3.88,"thousands of people will fall ill, millions of eggs will be recalled,"],[309,19.47,3.42,"and several egg industry titans will ultimately land in jail,"],[309,22.93,3.04,"all thanks to a microscopic but mighty bacterium."],[309,26.48,2.92,"Salmonella infects millions worldwide each year,"],[309,29.4,3.04,"causing fever, stomach cramps, and diarrhea."],[309,32.53,1.88,"And these effects can be extreme:"],[309,34.99,3.54,"Salmonella is the leading cause of hospitalizations and deaths"],[309,38.53,1.25,"from food poisoning."],[309,40.04,4.5,"So, let’s follow this microbe to find out how it makes so many people sick."],[309,44.75,2.38,"We begin in the chicken's digestive tract,"],[309,47.13,2.54,"a major source of all Salmonella infections."],[309,49.75,3.54,"In chickens, Salmonella bacteria often go undetected,"],[309,53.42,3.5,"allowing them to spread to eggs either through the developing yolk"],[309,56.93,1.63,"or by passing through feces,"],[309,58.64,2.0,"which can then contaminate shells."],[309,60.85,2.13,"Under unhygienic farming conditions,"],[309,62.98,3.88,"this Salmonella-laden feces may also infect or contaminate"],[309,66.85,1.79,"other animals and crops,"],[309,68.69,2.13,"causing various food-linked outbreaks."],[309,70.94,4.84,"Meanwhile, chicken meat can be exposed to intestinal Salmonella during processing."],[309,76.07,1.75,"On its journey from farm to plate,"],[309,77.99,4.17,"the microbe can survive extreme cold, wet, and dry conditions."],[309,82.41,2.84,"However, once it moves into a human body,"],[309,85.25,2.96,"Salmonella reveals its true talents for survival."],[309,88.54,1.83,"The first hurdle is the stomach."],[309,90.84,3.71,"Here, most bacterial invaders are killed off by stomach acid."],[309,94.84,3.34,"But Salmonella cells can detect acidic conditions,"],[309,98.18,3.13,"which triggers the production of acid shock proteins."],[309,101.56,2.71,"These molecules shield the bacteria from damage"],[309,104.27,2.79,"just long enough for it to pass into the intestines."],[309,107.31,2.46,"Salmonella then faces the next gauntlet,"],[309,109.9,4.21,"as intestinal cells swiftly unleash microbe-destroying immune cells."],[309,114.57,4.21,"But once again, the bacteria detect these changes with inbuilt sensors."],[309,118.91,3.88,"And embedded within Salmonella’s genome are pathogenicity islands,"],[309,123.03,3.59,"clusters of adaptive genes that launch the next phase of attack."],[309,127.21,2.71,"They signal the construction of a specialized system"],[309,129.92,2.34,"that resembles a needle and syringe."],[309,132.59,3.75,"Within seconds, it injects molecules called effector proteins"],[309,136.34,1.58,"into the intestinal cells,"],[309,137.97,3.63,"causing them to change their structure and swallow up the Salmonella."],[309,141.8,3.88,"Once inside, Salmonella can then exploit the cell machinery"],[309,145.68,1.83,"to replicate and spread."],[309,148.44,3.38,"But these invaded intestinal cells don’t go down without a fight—"],[309,151.9,3.75,"as soon as this breach begins, they release cytokines,"],[309,155.65,3.96,"chemical messengers that prompt the immune system to launch into action."],[309,160.07,3.84,"Fleets of white blood cells seek out and destroy Salmonella microbes"],[309,163.91,1.29,"and infected cells."],[309,165.33,3.17,"This inflammatory response is also what leads to symptoms"],[309,168.5,2.34,"like abdominal pains and fever."],[309,171.46,2.75,"And it further damages the breached intestinal cells,"],[309,174.21,2.54,"limiting their usual ability to absorb water."],[309,177.13,4.34,"So, whatever’s in the digestive tract gets released in watery diarrhea."],[309,181.89,2.67,"While this inflammatory response may feel unpleasant,"],[309,184.56,4.92,"it effectively purges Salmonella from the body within 2 to 7 days for most people,"],[309,189.52,1.88,"without the need for antibiotics."],[309,191.73,3.54,"But there are times when a Salmonella infection may require more treatment."],[309,195.36,2.13,"It can lead to severe dehydration,"],[309,197.49,2.5,"particularly in children and older patients."],[309,200.24,4.25,"And in some unusual cases, Salmonella can continue to spread through the body,"],[309,204.66,3.71,"hiding inside immune cells, invading other organs and tissues,"],[309,208.54,1.75,"and even poisoning the blood."],[309,210.75,4.46,"These cases occur if a person is infected by a rare but powerful type of Salmonella"],[309,215.25,1.38,"called S. typhi."],[309,216.84,3.38,"Unlike other strains, S. typhi doesn’t infect chickens—"],[309,220.38,1.71,"It spreads from person to person,"],[309,222.3,3.71,"mainly via poor sanitation and untreated drinking water."],[309,226.31,3.29,"Although it’s uncommon in many parts of the world, typhoid fever,"],[309,229.6,4.71,"the disease that S. typhi causes, still kills over 100,000 people yearly."],[309,235.02,3.5,"Thankfully, there are vaccines to prevent infection by S. typhi."],[309,238.73,3.59,"To avoid milder variants, there are steps that everyone can take,"],[309,242.32,3.17,"like washing your hands, avoiding unpasteurized milk,"],[309,245.7,1.96,"and cooking meat and eggs thoroughly."],[309,247.91,1.79,"Cookie dough should be off limits:"],[309,249.95,3.09,"raw eggs and flour both carry a Salmonella risk."],[309,253.71,2.75,"And there are ways to stop Salmonella at its source."],[309,256.46,4.38,"Investigations into the outbreak in 2010 revealed one company's dark history"],[309,260.84,1.92,"of unhygienic farming conditions,"],[309,262.76,2.79,"bribery of health officials, and mislabeled eggs."],[309,265.93,1.08,"Since then,"],[309,267.01,3.59,"the United States has taken steps to put stricter regulations in place."],[309,270.85,3.17,"In Europe, many countries have successfully reduced Salmonella"],[309,274.02,3.92,"by requiring testing on farms and before products reach shelves."],[309,278.23,3.13,"But there's still work to be done if we want to stop the spread"],[309,281.36,2.54,"of this incredibly crafty pathogen."],[310,3.24,2.36,"Avoiding the extreme heat"],[310,5.6,2.72,"imposes a rhythm on desert life."],[310,10.84,1.72,"And many animals here"],[310,12.56,2.4,"choose the simplest option of all."],[310,17.56,1.76,"Staying hidden all day"],[310,19.32,3.28,"and only venturing out in the cool of the night."],[310,30.96,2.24,"As darkness falls,"],[310,33.2,3.4,"animals appear from seemingly nowhere."],[310,43.0,4.68,"And among them, inevitably, are hunters."],[310,62.84,3.28,"One of the most voracious nocturnal predators"],[310,66.12,3.52,"is also one of the hardest to see."],[310,72.0,5.32,"This mysterious creature hardly ever appears on the surface of the dunes."],[310,83.52,2.52,"But there are signs on the sand"],[310,86.04,2.24,"that can give it away."],[310,101.04,4.48,"It lives only here, where the sand grains are so perfectly dry and polished"],[310,105.52,3.16,"that they flow almost like water."],[310,111.44,2.48,"It's no bigger than a ping-pong ball."],[310,118.32,1.68,"A golden mole."],[310,120.88,1.64,"It's totally blind…"],[310,123.52,3.24,"..but there's nothing to see underground anyway."],[310,132.44,2.44,"Instead, it has superb hearing."],[310,137.52,2.92,"Its entire head acts as an amplifier"],[310,140.44,2.16,"that picks up vibrations through the sand."],[310,143.28,3.08,"So to locate prey on the surface of the dune,"],[310,146.36,4.32,"it has, paradoxically, to thrust its face into the dune."],[310,159.4,1.52,"Termites."],[310,161.48,2.04,"Not easy to catch when you're blind."],[310,167.84,2.64,"Far better to go into stealth mode."],[310,172.24,1.76,"Once below the sand,"],[310,174.0,2.56,"it can detect the slightest movement."],[310,179.84,1.52,"Allowing it to strike"],[310,181.36,2.16,"with pinpoint accuracy."],[310,192.48,2.84,"Well, most of the time."],[310,206.92,4.12,"It can travel two-thirds of a mile a night in search of its dinner."],[310,213.76,1.28,"And right now,"],[310,215.04,3.12,"it has just detected its main course."],[310,219.52,4.76,"Little wonder it's sometimes called the shark of the dunes."],[311,6.42,2.71,"It’s 1780 in Paris,"],[311,9.46,4.13,"and the heavy spring rains are saturating the earth."],[311,14.3,2.38,"In a home near the center of town,"],[311,16.8,2.88,"a basement wall collapses under the pressure,"],[311,19.81,3.59,"releasing a flood of decomposing corpses—"],[311,23.52,4.25,"remains from the neighboring Cemetery of the Innocents."],[311,27.9,5.0,"Rumors spread that everyone in the house got sick due to bad air"],[311,32.9,2.88,"emanating from the decaying flesh."],[311,36.07,4.75,"With the overcrowded cemetery housing generations of dead Parisians,"],[311,40.99,3.17,"there was a growing worry that the entire city"],[311,44.16,3.17,"was in grave danger of falling ill."],[311,47.79,4.55,"For centuries, Parisians had buried their dead in the Innocents,"],[311,52.38,1.84,"the city’s largest cemetery."],[311,54.47,4.88,"While cemeteries across Europe were originally placed outside of urban areas,"],[311,59.35,1.33,"in the 9th century,"],[311,60.68,4.05,"the Church began allowing burials directly on its grounds."],[311,65.23,2.79,"As these urban parish cemeteries filled,"],[311,68.19,3.96,"some churches started creating bone chapels,"],[311,72.15,3.29,"like the Sedlec Ossuary in the Czech Republic,"],[311,75.45,2.25,"to make room for new burials."],[311,78.28,4.92,"By the 18th century, public opinion on urban cemeteries shifted,"],[311,83.45,2.79,"as Enlightenment thinkers and physicians"],[311,86.25,4.55,"promoted new scientific ideas that linked hygiene to health."],[311,91.17,3.09,"They didn't yet understand the concept of germs,"],[311,94.3,5.71,"believing instead that disease spread through “miasma,” or bad air."],[311,100.3,4.84,"Consequently, overcrowded cemeteries spewing cadaverous odors"],[311,105.14,3.21,"were cast as public health threats."],[311,108.9,5.76,"Even after the Innocents and other cemeteries closed in the early 1780s,"],[311,114.82,3.79,"residents continued to worry about the foul smells"],[311,118.74,3.25,"and petitioned the government for a long-term solution."],[311,122.33,4.84,"But where could they move the remains of the millions of Parisians?"],[311,127.58,3.04,"The vast abandoned network of quarries"],[311,130.63,3.09,"beneath the city offered a logical solution."],[311,134.09,2.13,"Dating back to Roman times,"],[311,136.22,4.88,"the mines had provided the limestone and plaster to build Paris."],[311,141.55,4.88,"But by the 18th century, they had begun collapsing under the city’s weight,"],[311,146.47,2.17,"creating deadly sinkholes."],[311,148.98,6.3,"So in 1777, King Louis XVI established a team of engineers"],[311,155.44,2.88,"to survey and reinforce the area."],[311,158.86,6.96,"And in 1785, Charles-Axel Guillaumot, the Inspector General of Quarries,"],[311,166.0,5.17,"was tasked with turning sections of the unused tunnels into a massive crypt."],[311,171.88,2.54,"The Catholic Church initially resisted,"],[311,174.59,4.88,"as it stood to lose income from burials and cemetery maintenance fees,"],[311,179.55,2.88,"but public health concerns prevailed."],[311,183.09,4.21,"The transfer of human remains was done mostly at night,"],[311,187.6,5.67,"to avoid upsetting passersby and to limit public exposure to the miasma."],[311,194.02,4.42,"Millions of anonymous bones were dug up by hand,"],[311,198.65,5.46,"carted across Paris, and dumped unceremoniously in the Catacombs."],[311,204.32,2.25,"It was a distinctly modern project,"],[311,206.58,4.38,"focused on efficiency rather than memorialization."],[311,211.37,3.38,"The Cemetery of the Innocents was the largest source,"],[311,214.75,4.09,"though bones were soon brought from cemeteries throughout the city."],[311,219.42,4.42,"The French Revolution temporarily stalled relocation efforts."],[311,224.26,3.84,"Though the Catacombs did offer a space where inconvenient bodies"],[311,228.1,1.29,"could disappear."],[311,229.56,2.67,"Some of the over 1,000 prisoners"],[311,232.23,5.09,"executed in the Revolution’s September Massacres of 1792"],[311,237.32,1.79,"were hastily cast there."],[311,239.36,4.71,"However, the Catacombs weren’t generally used for new burials."],[311,244.2,4.34,"Instead, the newly dead were interred in garden cemeteries"],[311,248.54,3.67,"established, once again, on the city’s outskirts."],[311,252.54,2.0,"By the end of the Revolution,"],[311,254.54,3.38,"the relocation project had sprung back to life."],[311,258.21,6.09,"And in 1809, Napoleon Bonaparte, aware of the unifying power of monuments,"],[311,264.55,3.46,"ordered that a small section of the chaotic space"],[311,268.01,2.71,"be curated and open to the public."],[311,271.18,3.25,"Piles of bones were pushed to the sides,"],[311,274.44,4.25,"facades of skulls and femurs were artfully arranged,"],[311,279.07,4.38,"and quotes about the fleeting nature of life were hung on the walls."],[311,284.49,1.88,"The rest of the Catacombs—"],[311,286.37,4.25,"housing the remains of an estimated 6 million Parisians—"],[311,290.62,3.38,"was, and largely remains, untouched."],[311,294.42,1.92,"After its beautification,"],[311,296.42,4.17,"the Catacombs became a hugely popular tourist destination."],[311,301.17,4.8,"While signs indicated the cemetery where the bones were moved from,"],[311,306.18,2.79,"the remains themselves were anonymous—"],[311,309.06,5.55,"aristocrats lay next to laborers, raiders next to defenders,"],[311,314.81,2.75,"the young next to their elders."],[311,318.06,2.63,"And so, the site became a symbol"],[311,320.69,6.3,"of what one 19th-century photographer called the “confused equality of death.”"],[311,327.57,3.75,"Sources vary, but some say the last remains"],[311,331.33,3.63,"were moved as recently as the 1930s."],[311,335.37,4.88,"Today, people from around the world continue to visit the Catacombs,"],[311,340.59,3.92,"finding amidst its caverns a haunting reminder"],[311,344.76,3.71,"of our shared, inevitable future."],[312,3.62,2.42,"When a community wants to build"],[312,6.04,4.21,"a bridge or transit system, housing,"],[312,10.26,1.63,"sustainable infrastructure --"],[312,11.88,1.75,"where does the money come from?"],[312,14.01,2.92,"Money ultimately decides what gets built."],[312,16.97,1.38,"Money decides what survives."],[312,18.35,3.42,"Money decides what future becomes possible."],[312,21.81,2.29,"So change where money goes and who controls it --"],[312,24.1,2.75,"then you change what becomes possible."],[312,27.57,1.33,"I'm Trinity Tran,"],[312,28.9,3.54,"I lead efforts to build public banks in California and beyond."],[312,32.95,2.75,"Most of the time, our cities don’t have the money upfront"],[312,35.74,5.0,"to be able to pay for a project like a bridge or housing."],[312,40.74,4.17,"And so they will issue a bond,"],[312,44.92,2.17,"which is essentially a long-term loan,"],[312,47.09,3.04,"provided [and] structured by Wall Street banks."],[312,50.17,4.84,"A loan is money that is provided upfront and paid back over time with interest."],[312,55.01,4.09,"Wall Street banks -- private lenders -- end up tacking on fees"],[312,59.1,1.67,"to structure and sell those bonds."],[312,60.81,3.29,"Then the reality is that our local governments are not only paying"],[312,64.14,1.63,"for the cost of the project,"],[312,65.81,3.09,"but decades of interest on top of that."],[312,68.94,2.71,"So for every dollar that we're borrowing,"],[312,71.69,4.3,"we're paying another dollar in interest over time, sometimes more."],[312,76.36,3.42,"And that's all money that's going out the door to private lenders."],[312,79.83,2.38,"We're paying hundreds of millions of dollars"],[312,82.25,1.46,"in the city of Los Angeles."],[312,83.7,4.34,"At the state level, in California, we’re paying $4 billion"],[312,88.08,2.04,"in interest annually."],[312,90.17,3.25,"That's money flowing out of our communities"],[312,93.47,2.33,"rather than being invested locally."],[312,96.47,2.84,"Private banks are owned by shareholders."],[312,99.35,2.75,"They're investing in areas that make them a lot of money,"],[312,102.14,4.17,"and not necessarily what’s in the best interest of our communities."],[312,106.31,2.67,"Public banking asks a different question:"],[312,109.02,3.96,"how do we do more with the dollars that our communities generate"],[312,113.03,2.34,"without raising taxes,"],[312,115.4,2.63,"but by lowering the cost of financing itself?"],[312,118.36,3.0,"A public bank is a bank owned by the people"],[312,121.41,3.75,"through their local government, either city or region or state."],[312,125.16,4.55,"Every time you buy something, like a cup of coffee,"],[312,129.71,3.96,"a portion of that tax will go to your city and your state."],[312,133.67,4.13,"So we're using the money that we already generate that belongs to us."],[312,137.84,3.34,"They don't have shareholders to pay."],[312,141.18,3.0,"So as loans are repaid, it comes back into a public bank."],[312,144.18,4.17,"Its mission is to support the local economic development"],[312,148.35,1.54,"of the community it serves."],[312,149.94,2.33,"That would be designed at a local level."],[312,152.27,3.8,"So the law that we created -- the California Public Banking Act --"],[312,156.07,3.04,"created the legal framework for cities and municipalities"],[312,159.11,1.71,"to be able to form their own banks."],[312,160.87,4.38,"And so the mission of the bank, the mandates, the lending priorities,"],[312,165.29,2.92,"the investment priorities, the capitalization strategies,"],[312,168.25,2.08,"all the details of how this is going to work"],[312,170.33,2.79,"is going to be defined at a local level."],[312,173.88,4.3,"There are 900 public banks globally"],[312,178.22,2.79,"with nearly $50 trillion in assets."],[312,181.05,3.17,"In Germany, a network of 400 municipal banks"],[312,184.22,4.5,"was able to support the country's renewable energy transition."],[312,188.73,3.63,"In the United States, we have a successful model to emulate."],[312,192.4,1.38,"The Bank of North Dakota"],[312,193.81,4.34,"is a 100-year-old public bank founded in 1919."],[312,198.15,2.84,"It was started by socialist farmers"],[312,201.03,5.05,"who were being gouged by out-of-state New York bankers."],[312,206.08,1.38,"During the Great Depression,"],[312,207.45,4.21,"nearly 200,000 farms were being foreclosed on."],[312,211.71,2.59,"Because the state of North Dakota had a public bank,"],[312,214.34,3.59,"it was able to step in and buy the farms"],[312,217.96,2.63,"and lend it back to the families for a dollar."],[312,221.09,3.17,"Once the families were able to stabilize,"],[312,224.26,3.79,"they sold it back at a price they could afford."],[312,228.1,1.5,"For the last century,"],[312,229.64,2.67,"it has supported local economic development,"],[312,232.35,2.75,"supported small businesses, infrastructure"],[312,235.11,4.84,"while still returning very healthy return on equity, 15 to 18 percent."],[312,240.44,2.71,"That means it's returning hundreds of millions of dollars"],[312,243.16,3.09,"back into the general fund year after year."],[312,246.78,3.88,"So that's an example of how a public bank is able to step in and support"],[312,250.71,2.38,"and strengthen the community"],[312,253.12,3.34,"without focusing on just purely profit."],[312,256.5,3.92,"Los Angeles just dealt with a devastating wildfire."],[312,260.42,1.33,"So if we had a public bank,"],[312,261.76,4.63,"it would have been able to step in to offer immediate, low-cost loans."],[312,266.43,4.5,"It's able to be immediately responsive to the needs of the community"],[312,270.93,2.63,"without having to wait for federal aid,"],[312,273.6,2.88,"which can be unstable."],[312,276.48,3.0,"We're moving towards building a system"],[312,279.53,5.17,"that mobilizes public revenue to support our public priorities."],[312,284.74,3.29,"We've seen such an incredible amount of progress"],[312,288.08,1.29,"in such a short time,"],[312,289.41,4.92,"because even without a complex understanding of finance and banking,"],[312,294.33,2.38,"when you start explaining public banking,"],[312,296.71,4.42,"it makes sense on a logical, intuitive level."],[312,301.17,3.21,"Multiple cities and states are working towards it."],[312,304.38,2.71,"We have the ability to create a new system."],[312,307.14,3.17,"This isn't a pie-in-the-sky idea."],[312,311.31,3.63,"This is a model that works for the people,"],[312,314.98,2.71,"our communities and our environment."]];
